window.NPLEX_UNITS = {"CV-1":{"id":"CV-1","summary":"How a single heart tube folds, loops, and divides into four chambers with valves and outflow vessels, and how fetal circulation bypasses the lungs and liver. Exam questions link each embryonic structure to its adult derivative and to the congenital defect that results when a step fails.","notes":[{"h":"Heart tube and looping","points":["Heart forms from splanchnic mesoderm (cardiogenic field) in week 3.","Paired endocardial tubes fuse into one heart tube as the embryo folds.","Heart beats spontaneously by about day 22 (week 4); first functional organ.","Looping begins in week 4 and sets left-right position of the chambers.","Defective left-right dynein (primary ciliary dyskinesia, Kartagener syndrome) can cause dextrocardia with situs inversus."]},{"h":"Atrial septation","points":["Septum primum grows toward the endocardial cushions; the gap below it is the ostium primum.","Ostium secundum opens by apoptosis in the upper septum primum.","Septum secundum grows on the right side of septum primum and leaves an opening, the foramen ovale.","Septum primum acts as a one-way flap valve over the foramen ovale.","After birth, left atrial pressure rises above right and presses the flap shut; the scar is the fossa ovalis.","Failure of the two septa to fuse leaves a patent foramen ovale (about 1 in 4 adults)."]},{"h":"Ventricles, AV canal, and outflow tract","points":["Dorsal and ventral endocardial cushions fuse to split the AV canal into right and left channels.","Muscular interventricular septum grows up from the floor of the ventricle.","Membranous septum closes the last gap using endocardial cushion and outflow ridge tissue; most VSDs are membranous.","Neural crest cells form truncal and bulbar ridges that spiral to make the aorticopulmonary septum.","Mitral and tricuspid valves come from fused AV canal endocardial cushions; aortic and pulmonary valves come from outflow tract endocardial cushions."]},{"h":"Fetal circulation","points":["Umbilical vein carries the most oxygenated fetal blood from the placenta.","Ductus venosus lets most umbilical blood bypass the liver into the IVC.","IVC blood is directed through the foramen ovale to the left atrium, then to the head and upper body.","SVC blood goes RA to RV to pulmonary trunk, then through the ductus arteriosus to the descending aorta.","High fetal pulmonary vascular resistance drives the ductus shunt.","Two umbilical arteries (from the internal iliac arteries) return deoxygenated blood to the placenta."]},{"h":"Changes at birth","points":["First breaths drop pulmonary vascular resistance, so more blood returns to the left atrium.","Higher left atrial pressure closes the foramen ovale.","Rising O2 and falling prostaglandin E2 (placenta removed) constrict the ductus arteriosus.","Clamping the cord stops flow in the umbilical vessels and ductus venosus."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Embryonic heart structures and adult derivatives","cols":["Embryonic structure","Adult derivative"],"rows":[["Truncus arteriosus","Ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk"],["Bulbus cordis","Smooth outflow parts of both ventricles (RV conus arteriosus, LV aortic vestibule)"],["Primitive ventricle","Trabeculated parts of left and right ventricles"],["Primitive atrium","Trabeculated parts of both atria (auricles)"],["Left horn of sinus venosus","Coronary sinus"],["Right horn of sinus venosus","Smooth part of right atrium (sinus venarum)"],["Primitive pulmonary vein","Smooth part of left atrium"],["Right common and anterior cardinal veins","Superior vena cava"]]},{"title":"Fetal shunts and their adult remnants","cols":["Fetal structure","What it does before birth","Adult remnant"],"rows":[["Umbilical vein","Brings oxygenated blood from placenta","Ligamentum teres hepatis (round ligament of liver)"],["Ductus venosus","Shunts umbilical blood past the liver to the IVC","Ligamentum venosum"],["Foramen ovale","Shunts RA blood to LA","Fossa ovalis"],["Ductus arteriosus","Shunts pulmonary trunk blood to the aorta","Ligamentum arteriosum"],["Umbilical arteries","Return blood to the placenta","Medial umbilical ligaments"]]},{"title":"Aortic (pharyngeal) arch artery derivatives","cols":["Arch","Adult derivative"],"rows":[["1st","Part of the maxillary artery"],["2nd","Stapedial and hyoid arteries"],["3rd","Common carotid artery and proximal internal carotid artery"],["4th","Left: part of the aortic arch. Right: proximal right subclavian artery"],["5th","Regresses"],["6th","Proximal pulmonary arteries; left side also forms the ductus arteriosus"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"1st arch is MAXimal, Second = Stapedial, C is the 3rd letter, 4 limbs = systemic, 6th = pulmonary","text":"Arch 1: maxillary artery. Arch 2: stapedial artery. Arch 3: common carotid. Arch 4: systemic vessels (aortic arch, right subclavian). Arch 6: pulmonary arteries and the pulmonary-to-systemic shunt (ductus arteriosus)."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"The 5 Ts of early cyanosis","text":"Truncus arteriosus, Transposition of the great arteries, Tricuspid atresia, Tetralogy of Fallot, TAPVR. The first, second, and fourth are outflow tract (neural crest) septation errors."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Follow the oxygen","text":"Trace one red cell from the placenta: umbilical vein, ductus venosus, IVC, foramen ovale, LA, LV, aorta to the brain. Every shunt exists to send the best blood to the brain and skip the airless lungs and busy liver."}],"highYield":["Neural crest failure in the outflow tract gives persistent truncus arteriosus, transposition, or tetralogy of Fallot.","22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge) is linked to truncus arteriosus and tetralogy of Fallot.","Endocardial cushion defects (AV septal defect) are classic in Down syndrome.","Patent foramen ovale can let a venous clot cross to the systemic side (paradoxical embolism).","The left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the ligamentum arteriosum (6th arch) and aortic arch; the right hooks under the right subclavian artery (4th arch).","Most VSDs are in the membranous septum."],"pitfalls":["Ligamentum venosum comes from the ductus venosus; ligamentum arteriosum comes from the ductus arteriosus.","Medial umbilical ligaments are old umbilical arteries; the median umbilical ligament is the urachus (allantois), which is not a vessel.","Ostium secundum forms in septum primum; the foramen ovale is the hole left in septum secundum.","Failure to form the aorticopulmonary septum gives truncus arteriosus; failure of the septum to spiral gives transposition."],"dumps":[{"t":"Fetal circulation map","p":"Draw the fetal circulation from the placenta and back. Label the three shunts, mark where blood is most and least oxygenated, and write each shunt's adult remnant beside it.","l":["Umbilical vein carries the most oxygenated blood","Ductus venosus bypasses the liver to the IVC (ligamentum venosum)","Foramen ovale moves blood RA to LA (fossa ovalis)","Ductus arteriosus moves blood pulmonary trunk to aorta (ligamentum arteriosum)","High pulmonary vascular resistance explains why lungs are bypassed","Two umbilical arteries from internal iliacs return blood (medial umbilical ligaments)","Umbilical vein becomes ligamentum teres hepatis"]},{"t":"Atrial septum step by step","p":"Draw four panels showing how the atrial septum forms, from septum primum to the closed foramen ovale after birth. Label each opening and septum.","l":["Septum primum grows toward endocardial cushions","Ostium primum is the gap below septum primum, then closes","Ostium secundum forms by apoptosis in septum primum","Septum secundum grows to the right of septum primum","Foramen ovale is the opening in septum secundum","Septum primum serves as the flap valve","At birth LA pressure exceeds RA pressure and closes the flap (fossa ovalis)"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Cardiovascular System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Fetal and Neonatal Physiology (section on circulatory readjustments at birth)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on congenital heart disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Embryology | Development of the Heart ❤️","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RjiPx6Xi-68"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Fetal Circulation | Embryology | Dr Najeeb","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYVGjbzmAtg"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Fetal circulation right before birth | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-IRkisEtzsk"}],"related":["CV-3","CV-18-G","CV-18-G2","CV-18-G3","CV-18-G4"]},"CV-2":{"id":"CV-2","summary":"The microscopic structure of the heart wall, cardiac muscle cells, and the different types of blood vessels. Questions ask you to match a wall feature to its function and to know which layer is affected in common vascular diseases.","notes":[{"h":"Layers of the heart wall","points":["Endocardium: endothelium plus thin connective tissue; the subendocardial layer holds Purkinje fibers, nerves, and small vessels.","Myocardium: thickest layer, made of cardiac muscle cells; thickest in the left ventricle.","Epicardium: visceral layer of serous pericardium (mesothelium) over fat that carries the coronary vessels.","Cardiac skeleton: dense connective tissue rings around the valves; electrically insulates atria from ventricles except at the AV bundle.","Valve leaflets: dense connective tissue core covered by endothelium on both sides."]},{"h":"Cardiac muscle cells","points":["Striated, branched cells with one or two central nuclei.","Intercalated discs join cells end to end: fascia adherens and desmosomes hold, gap junctions pass current.","Gap junctions let the heart contract as a functional syncytium.","T tubules sit at the Z lines and pair with one SR cistern (diads).","Very rich in mitochondria; the heart depends on aerobic metabolism.","Atrial muscle cells contain granules of atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP).","Adult cardiac muscle barely regenerates; dead muscle is replaced by fibrous scar."]},{"h":"Purkinje fibers","points":["Larger and paler than working myocytes, with fewer myofibrils and lots of glycogen.","Lie in the subendocardium and conduct impulses fast."]},{"h":"Blood vessel wall","points":["Tunica intima: endothelium, basal lamina, subendothelial connective tissue; arteries add an internal elastic lamina.","Tunica media: smooth muscle and elastic tissue; thickest layer in arteries.","Tunica adventitia: collagen-rich connective tissue; thickest layer in large veins.","Vasa vasorum supply the outer wall of large vessels and are more plentiful in veins.","Limb veins have valves (folds of intima) that stop backflow."]},{"h":"Endothelial products","points":["Nitric oxide and prostacyclin relax smooth muscle and resist clotting.","Endothelin-1 is a strong vasoconstrictor.","Weibel-Palade bodies store von Willebrand factor and P-selectin."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Vessel types under the microscope","cols":["Vessel","Key wall features","Examples","Main job"],"rows":[["Elastic artery","Media packed with fenestrated elastic laminae","Aorta, pulmonary trunk, their large branches","Stretch in systole and recoil in diastole to keep flow steady"],["Muscular artery","Thick smooth muscle media, clear internal elastic lamina","Coronary, radial, femoral arteries","Distribute blood to organs"],["Arteriole","Thin media of only a few smooth muscle layers","Inside every organ","Main site of vascular resistance; control capillary inflow"],["Capillary","Endothelium and basal lamina, with pericytes","All tissues","Exchange between blood and tissue"],["Postcapillary venule","Endothelium and pericytes; high endothelial venules in lymph nodes","All tissues","Main site where leukocytes leave blood"],["Medium and large vein","Thin media, thick adventitia, valves in limb veins","Great saphenous vein, IVC","Low-pressure return; hold most of the blood volume"]]},{"title":"Capillary types","cols":["Type","Structure","Where found"],"rows":[["Continuous","Unbroken endothelium and basal lamina, tight junctions","Muscle, lung, connective tissue, nervous tissue (blood-brain barrier)"],["Fenestrated","Endothelium with small pores","Kidney glomeruli, intestinal villi, endocrine glands"],["Sinusoidal (discontinuous)","Wide, irregular lumen with gaps in endothelium and basal lamina","Liver, spleen, bone marrow"]]},{"title":"Three muscle types compared","cols":["Feature","Skeletal","Cardiac","Smooth"],"rows":[["Striations","Yes","Yes","No"],["Nuclei","Many, peripheral","One or two, central","One, central"],["Cell-to-cell links","No gap junctions between fibers","Intercalated discs with gap junctions","Gap junctions in single-unit smooth muscle"],["T tubule arrangement","Triads at A-I junctions","Diads at Z lines","No T tubules (caveolae instead)"],["Ca2+ binds to","Troponin C","Troponin C","Calmodulin"],["Regeneration","Limited, through satellite cells","Very little; heals by scar","Good; cells can divide"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Arteries flex, veins hold","text":"Arteries face high pressure, so their media is thick. Veins hold volume at low pressure, so their adventitia is the thickest layer and the lumen is wide."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Leakiness matches the job","text":"Where cells and large proteins must pass (liver, spleen, marrow) capillaries are sinusoidal. Where filtration or hormone release happens (kidney, gut, endocrine) they are fenestrated. Everywhere else they are continuous."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Disc parts by direction","text":"In an intercalated disc, the parts that run across the cell end (fascia adherens, desmosomes) hold cells together; the parts along the side (gap junctions) carry the electrical signal."}],"highYield":["Gap junctions in intercalated discs make the myocardium a functional syncytium.","Infarcted myocardium heals by fibrous scar because cardiac muscle cells barely divide.","Arterioles set most of the systemic vascular resistance.","Postcapillary venules are where neutrophils leave the blood in acute inflammation.","Continuous capillaries with tight junctions form the blood-brain barrier.","Atherosclerosis starts in the intima; Monckeberg medial calcific sclerosis affects the media of muscular arteries."],"pitfalls":["Cardiac T tubules sit at Z lines (diads); skeletal T tubules sit at A-I junctions (triads).","Purkinje fibers are larger and paler than working myocytes; their wide diameter helps them conduct fast.","The epicardium is the same thing as the visceral serous pericardium.","In arteries the media is the thickest layer; in large veins the adventitia is."],"dumps":[{"t":"Artery versus vein","p":"Draw a cross section of a muscular artery next to its companion vein. Label all three tunics in each and add the elastic laminae and a valve.","l":["Intima with endothelium in both","Internal elastic lamina prominent in the artery","Thick smooth muscle media in the artery","Thin media in the vein","Thick adventitia in the vein","Wider, often collapsed lumen in the vein","Valve drawn as a fold of intima in the vein"]},{"t":"Cardiac muscle close up","p":"Draw two cardiac muscle cells joined end to end. Label the nucleus, intercalated disc parts, T tubule, SR, and mitochondria, and write what each part does.","l":["Branched cells with one or two central nuclei","Fascia adherens anchors actin at the cell end","Desmosomes resist pulling apart","Gap junctions on the lateral part pass ions between cells","T tubule at the Z line forming a diad with SR","Many mitochondria between myofibrils","Striations from sarcomeres"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Circulatory System"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Muscle Tissue (section on cardiac muscle)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Histology of Cardiovascular System 🧑🏻‍⚕️","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UiMHnNHmgHM"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Structures and Layers of the Heart","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dgAbpwp9gF8"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Layers of the heart | Human anatomy and physiology | Health & Medicine | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bm65xCS5ivo"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Arteries vs. veins-what's the difference? | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7b6LRebCgb4"}],"related":["CV-5","CV-11","CV-18-I3"]},"CV-3":{"id":"CV-3","summary":"Where the heart sits in the chest, which chambers form each surface and border, how the great vessels connect, and how the pericardium wraps it all. Exam questions use these relations to explain stab wounds, enlarged chambers, referred pain, and tamponade.","notes":[{"h":"Position","points":["Heart lies in the middle mediastinum inside the pericardium, about two thirds left of midline.","Apex sits behind the left 5th intercostal space at about the midclavicular line.","The plane through the sternal angle and the T4/T5 disc separates superior from inferior mediastinum.","Right ventricle is the most anterior chamber, right behind the sternum.","Left atrium is the most posterior chamber, right in front of the esophagus."]},{"h":"Pericardium","points":["Fibrous pericardium: tough outer sac fused to the central tendon of the diaphragm and to the adventitia of the great vessels.","Serous pericardium has a parietal layer (lines the fibrous sac) and a visceral layer (epicardium).","Pericardial cavity holds a thin film of serous fluid between the two serous layers.","Phrenic nerves (C3 to C5) carry pain from the fibrous and parietal layers; pain refers to the shoulder and supraclavicular skin.","Main blood supply is the pericardiacophrenic artery from the internal thoracic artery.","Transverse pericardial sinus lies behind the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk, in front of the SVC.","Oblique pericardial sinus is a blind pouch behind the left atrium, bounded by the pulmonary veins and IVC."]},{"h":"Great vessels","points":["SVC (formed by the two brachiocephalic veins) and IVC enter the right atrium; the azygos vein drains into the SVC.","Pulmonary trunk leaves the RV and divides into right and left pulmonary arteries under the aortic arch.","Usually four pulmonary veins enter the left atrium.","The coronary arteries are the only branches of the ascending aorta.","Aortic arch branches, right to left: brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, left subclavian.","Ligamentum arteriosum links the left pulmonary artery to the underside of the arch; the left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the arch next to it."]},{"h":"Inside the chambers","points":["RA: crista terminalis separates smooth sinus venarum from pectinate muscle; fossa ovalis on the septum; coronary sinus opening.","RV: trabeculae carneae, three papillary muscles, smooth conus arteriosus leading to the pulmonary valve.","Moderator band (septomarginal trabecula) carries part of the right bundle branch to the anterior papillary muscle.","LV wall is about two to three times thicker than the RV wall; two papillary muscles (anterior and posterior)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Heart chambers at a glance","cols":["Chamber","Receives blood from","Sends blood to","Key internal landmarks"],"rows":[["Right atrium","SVC, IVC, coronary sinus","RV through tricuspid valve","Crista terminalis, pectinate muscles, fossa ovalis, SA node near SVC opening"],["Right ventricle","Right atrium","Pulmonary trunk through pulmonary valve","Trabeculae carneae, moderator band, three papillary muscles, conus arteriosus"],["Left atrium","Pulmonary veins (usually four)","LV through mitral valve","Mostly smooth wall; pectinate muscles only in left auricle"],["Left ventricle","Left atrium","Ascending aorta through aortic valve","Thickest wall, two papillary muscles, smooth aortic vestibule"]]},{"title":"Which chamber forms each surface and border","cols":["Surface or border","Formed mainly by"],"rows":[["Anterior (sternocostal) surface","Right ventricle"],["Diaphragmatic (inferior) surface","Left ventricle, partly right ventricle"],["Base (posterior surface)","Left atrium, small part of right atrium"],["Apex","Left ventricle"],["Right border","Right atrium"],["Left border","Left ventricle, small part of left auricle"],["Inferior border","Right ventricle, small part of left ventricle"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive","text":"Phrenic nerve roots are C3 to C5. The same nerve carries pericardial pain, which is why pericarditis can be felt at the shoulder."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Front to back: RV first, LA last","text":"A knife through the sternum hits the RV. An enlarged LA pushes back on the esophagus (trouble swallowing) and can stretch the left recurrent laryngeal nerve (hoarseness)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Transverse sinus = finger behind the arteries","text":"Slide a finger behind the aorta and pulmonary trunk and in front of the SVC. Surgeons pass a clamp or ligature through this sinus to stop arterial outflow during bypass."}],"highYield":["Left atrial enlargement (for example from mitral stenosis) can cause dysphagia and hoarseness.","Cardiac tamponade: fluid in the pericardial sac compresses the heart; Beck triad is hypotension, distended neck veins, and muffled heart sounds.","Pericardial pain refers to the shoulder because of the phrenic nerve.","Phrenic nerve runs anterior to the lung root on the pericardium; vagus runs posterior to the lung root.","The fibrous pericardium does not stretch quickly, so even a small fast bleed can cause tamponade.","A stab wound just left of the sternum most likely enters the right ventricle."],"pitfalls":["The base of the heart is its posterior surface (mostly LA), and the apex points anteroinferiorly to the left.","Epicardium equals visceral serous pericardium; it is part of the heart wall.","Transverse sinus is behind the great arteries; oblique sinus is behind the left atrium.","The right border on a chest X-ray is the right atrium, while the left border is mostly the left ventricle."],"dumps":[{"t":"Anterior view of the heart","p":"Draw the heart as seen from the front. Label each chamber, the borders, the apex, and the great vessels leaving the top.","l":["RV forms most of the anterior surface","RA forms the right border","LV forms the left border and apex","Inferior border mostly RV","SVC entering RA from above","Pulmonary trunk from RV","Ascending aorta and arch with its three branches"]},{"t":"Pericardium and its sinuses","p":"Draw a cross section through the heart and pericardium. Label the fibrous and serous layers, the cavity, and both pericardial sinuses, then add the nerve and artery that supply the pericardium.","l":["Fibrous pericardium outermost, fused to central tendon of diaphragm","Parietal serous layer lines the fibrous sac","Visceral serous layer is the epicardium","Pericardial cavity with thin fluid film","Transverse sinus behind aorta and pulmonary trunk, in front of SVC","Oblique sinus behind LA, bounded by pulmonary veins and IVC","Phrenic nerve (C3 to C5) carries pain","Pericardiacophrenic artery supplies it"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, sections on the pericardium and the heart"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, section on the superior mediastinum and great vessels"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Anatomy of the Heart | Heart Model","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jU9w6w8LwqM"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Introduction to Cardiac (Heart) Anatomy and the Chest Xray","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HYr2NiOvjZE"}],"related":["CV-4","CV-8","CV-18-F","CV-18-F2"]},"CV-4":{"id":"CV-4","summary":"Where the four heart valves sit, how pressure differences open and close them, and how the valve events line up with pressures, volumes, heart sounds, and the ECG in one cardiac cycle. This is the foundation for every murmur and valve disease question.","notes":[{"h":"Valve anatomy","points":["Tricuspid valve (RA to RV): anterior, posterior, and septal cusps.","Mitral valve (LA to LV): anterior and posterior cusps.","Chordae tendineae tie AV cusps to papillary muscles; papillary muscles tighten in systole so cusps do not flip into the atria.","Pulmonary valve: anterior, right, and left cusps.","Aortic valve: right, left, and posterior cusps; right and left aortic sinuses give rise to the coronary arteries.","All four valves open and close passively, driven by pressure differences."]},{"h":"Where to listen","points":["Aortic area: right 2nd intercostal space at the sternal border.","Pulmonic area: left 2nd intercostal space at the sternal border.","Tricuspid area: left lower sternal border.","Mitral area: apex, left 5th intercostal space at the midclavicular line."]},{"h":"Pressures and volumes","points":["Aortic valve opens when LV pressure rises above aortic pressure (about 80 mmHg).","Normal aortic pressure about 120/80 mmHg; pulmonary artery about 25/8 mmHg.","End-diastolic volume about 110 to 120 mL, stroke volume about 70 mL, end-systolic volume about 40 to 50 mL.","Ejection fraction = SV / EDV, normally about 55 to 70%.","Most ventricular filling is passive; atrial contraction adds about the last 20%.","Dicrotic notch (incisura) on the aortic pressure curve marks aortic valve closure."]},{"h":"Jugular venous pulse waves","points":["a wave: right atrial contraction.","c wave: RV contraction bulges the closed tricuspid valve into the RA.","x descent: atrial relaxation and downward pull of the tricuspid ring during ejection.","v wave: RA fills against the closed tricuspid valve.","y descent: RA empties into the RV after the tricuspid valve opens."]},{"h":"Splitting of S2","points":["Inspiration lowers chest pressure, raises venous return to the right heart, lengthens RV ejection, and delays P2.","Normal (physiologic) split widens on inspiration."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Phases of the cardiac cycle (left heart)","cols":["Phase","Valve status","LV volume","Markers"],"rows":[["Atrial contraction","Mitral open, aortic closed","Rises to EDV","Follows the P wave; a wave; S4 if the ventricle is stiff"],["Isovolumetric contraction","All valves closed","Stays at EDV","Starts just after QRS onset with S1; highest myocardial O2 use"],["Ejection","Mitral closed, aortic open","Falls to ESV","During the ST segment and T wave"],["Isovolumetric relaxation","All valves closed","Stays at ESV","Starts with S2 (aortic closure); dicrotic notch"],["Rapid filling","Mitral open, aortic closed","Rises quickly","S3 if present; y descent"],["Reduced filling (diastasis)","Mitral open, aortic closed","Rises slowly","Flat stretch before the next P wave"]]},{"title":"Heart sounds","cols":["Sound","Cause","Timing","What it means"],"rows":[["S1","Mitral and tricuspid closure","Start of systole","Normal; loudest at the apex (mitral area)"],["S2","Aortic (A2) then pulmonic (P2) closure","End of ejection","Normal; loudest at left upper sternal border; splits on inspiration"],["S3","Rapid early filling into a dilated or volume-loaded ventricle","Early diastole, just after S2","Normal in children and pregnancy; in adults over about 40 suggests heart failure or volume overload"],["S4","Atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle","Late diastole, just before S1","Stiff or hypertrophied ventricle; absent in atrial fibrillation"]]},{"title":"Abnormal S2 splitting","cols":["Pattern","What happens","Classic causes"],"rows":[["Wide split","P2 is delayed more than normal; split still widens on inspiration","Pulmonic stenosis, right bundle branch block"],["Fixed split","Split stays the same through the breathing cycle","Atrial septal defect"],["Paradoxical split","A2 is delayed past P2; split narrows on inspiration and widens on expiration","Aortic stenosis, left bundle branch block"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"All Physicians Take Money","text":"Listening areas in order from the right upper chest to the apex: Aortic, Pulmonic, Tricuspid, Mitral."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Kentucky and Tennessee","text":"S3 follows S2 with the rhythm of Kentucky (S1, S2, S3). S4 comes before S1 with the rhythm of Tennessee (S4, S1, S2)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Valves are doors pushed by pressure","text":"A valve opens when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front of it, and shuts when that reverses. Find which pressure line is on top in a Wiggers diagram and you know the valve status."}],"highYield":["Isovolumetric phases are the only times all four valves are closed.","Loss of the atrial kick in atrial fibrillation removes S4 and lowers filling in a stiff ventricle.","Fixed splitting of S2 points to an atrial septal defect.","Paradoxical splitting points to delayed LV emptying, such as aortic stenosis or left bundle branch block.","The c wave is the closed tricuspid valve bulging into the RA as the RV contracts; the x descent is atrial relaxation plus the tricuspid ring pulled down during ejection.","Papillary muscle rupture after MI lets mitral cusps prolapse and causes acute mitral regurgitation."],"pitfalls":["S1 marks the start of isovolumetric contraction, which comes before ejection; the aortic valve opens later.","Papillary muscles do not pull the AV valves open; they hold the cusps shut during systole.","S3 can be normal in a child or pregnant patient; the same sound in an older adult points to disease.","Wide split still varies with breathing; fixed split does not."],"dumps":[{"t":"Wiggers diagram","p":"Draw one cardiac cycle with aortic, LV, and LA pressure curves, the LV volume curve, the ECG, and heart sounds on a shared time axis. Mark when each valve opens and closes.","l":["Mitral closes where LV pressure crosses above LA pressure (S1)","Isovolumetric contraction with volume flat at EDV","Aortic valve opens where LV pressure crosses aortic pressure (about 80 mmHg)","Volume falls to ESV during ejection","Aortic closure at the dicrotic notch (S2)","Isovolumetric relaxation with volume flat at ESV","Mitral opens where LV pressure drops below LA pressure, then rapid filling","P wave before atrial kick; QRS before isovolumetric contraction; T wave near end of ejection"]},{"t":"Listening map","p":"Draw the front of the chest and mark the four auscultation areas. Next to each, write which valve it represents and which heart sound is loudest there.","l":["Aortic area at right 2nd intercostal space, sternal border","Pulmonic area at left 2nd intercostal space, sternal border","Tricuspid area at left lower sternal border","Mitral area at apex, left 5th intercostal space, midclavicular line","S1 loudest at the apex","S2 loudest at the left upper sternal border"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Muscle; The Heart as a Pump and Function of the Heart Valves"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Heart Valves and Heart Sounds; Valvular and Congenital Heart Defects"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, section on the heart valves and areas of auscultation"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Cardiac Cycle","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xamYVlNF5Zo"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Lub Dub | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-4kGMI-qQ3I"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Cardiac Cycle | Systole & Diastole | Dr Najeeb Part 1/8","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XbivIaFPoQI"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Mastering Second Heart Sound | S2 Splitting🩺","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IdRb3OjFAtQ"}],"related":["CV-3","CV-6","CV-18-D","CV-18-D1","CV-18-D2"]},"CV-5":{"id":"CV-5","summary":"How an action potential in a ventricular muscle cell triggers calcium release and contraction, and how calcium handling sets the strength and speed of each beat. Expect questions on ion channels by phase, calcium-induced calcium release, and how sympathetic input raises contractility.","notes":[{"h":"Ventricular action potential","points":["Resting potential is about -90 mV, held by inward rectifier K+ channels.","Upstroke comes from fast voltage-gated Na+ channels.","The plateau comes from L-type Ca2+ influx balanced by K+ efflux.","The action potential lasts about 0.2 to 0.3 s, far longer than in skeletal muscle.","The long refractory period lasts almost as long as contraction, so the heart cannot be tetanized."]},{"h":"Excitation-contraction coupling","points":["The action potential spreads into T tubules and opens L-type Ca2+ channels (dihydropyridine receptors).","Entering Ca2+ opens ryanodine receptors (RyR2) on the SR: calcium-induced calcium release.","Ca2+ binds troponin C, tropomyosin moves, and myosin-binding sites on actin are exposed.","Cross-bridges cycle using ATP until Ca2+ falls.","Cardiac muscle needs extracellular Ca2+ to contract; skeletal muscle does not."]},{"h":"Relaxation","points":["SERCA pumps most Ca2+ back into the SR.","Phospholamban brakes SERCA; when PKA phosphorylates phospholamban, the brake comes off.","The Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (3 Na+ in for 1 Ca2+ out) removes Ca2+ from the cell.","A sarcolemmal Ca2+-ATPase removes a smaller share.","Relaxation uses ATP, so ischemia impairs relaxation as well as contraction."]},{"h":"Controlling force","points":["Every cell contracts with every beat, so force is graded by Ca2+ and sarcomere length, with no motor unit recruitment.","Sympathetic β1 stimulation raises cAMP and activates PKA.","PKA phosphorylates L-type Ca2+ channels (more Ca2+ in), phospholamban (faster SR uptake, bigger SR store), and troponin I (faster relaxation).","Stretch (Frank-Starling) improves filament overlap and raises troponin C sensitivity to Ca2+.","Blocking the Na+/K+-ATPase raises cell Na+, slows Ca2+ removal by the exchanger, and strengthens contraction (cardiac glycoside mechanism)."]},{"h":"Energy","points":["Heart muscle depends almost entirely on aerobic metabolism and burns mostly fatty acids at rest.","It extracts a large share of delivered O2 even at rest, so more demand must be met by more coronary flow."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Ventricular myocyte action potential phases","cols":["Phase","What happens","Main current"],"rows":[["0","Rapid depolarization","Na+ in through fast voltage-gated Na+ channels"],["1","Brief early repolarization","Na+ channels inactivate; transient outward K+ current"],["2","Plateau","Ca2+ in through L-type channels balanced by K+ out"],["3","Rapid repolarization","L-type Ca2+ channels close; delayed rectifier K+ out"],["4","Resting potential (about -90 mV)","Inward rectifier K+ channels; Na+/K+-ATPase keeps gradients"]]},{"title":"Skeletal versus cardiac muscle coupling","cols":["Feature","Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle"],"rows":[["What starts contraction","Motor neuron releases ACh at the neuromuscular junction","Action potential spreads from neighbor cells through gap junctions"],["DHPR to RyR link","Mechanical: DHPR directly pulls RyR1 open","Ca2+ entering through L-type channels opens RyR2"],["Needs extracellular Ca2+","No","Yes"],["Action potential length","A few milliseconds","About 200 to 300 ms"],["Tetanus possible","Yes","No (long refractory period)"],["How force is graded","Recruiting motor units and firing rate","Amount of Ca2+ released and sarcomere length"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Phase story: Na, K, Ca, K, rest","text":"Phase 0 Na+ rushes in, phase 1 K+ starts out, phase 2 Ca2+ holds the plateau, phase 3 K+ pulls it down, phase 4 rests."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Phospholamban is a brake","text":"Unphosphorylated phospholamban slows SERCA. PKA phosphorylation releases the brake, so Ca2+ is pumped back faster: quicker relaxation and a bigger store for the next beat."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Calcium is the volume knob","text":"Anything that raises cytosolic Ca2+ during systole (β1 stimulation, slower Ca2+ removal) turns up force. Anything that lowers it turns force down."}],"highYield":["Calcium-induced calcium release through RyR2 is the core of cardiac excitation-contraction coupling.","The plateau (phase 2) depends on L-type Ca2+ channels.","A refractory period nearly as long as contraction prevents tetany and lets the heart refill.","β1 stimulation raises contractility and speeds relaxation through PKA phosphorylation of L-type channels, phospholamban, and troponin I.","High extracellular K+ makes the resting potential less negative, inactivates some fast Na+ channels, and slows conduction.","Inhibiting the Na+/K+-ATPase raises intracellular Ca2+ through reduced Na+/Ca2+ exchange."],"pitfalls":["Ventricular myocytes use Na+ for the upstroke; SA and AV nodal cells use Ca2+ (covered in CV-7).","In skeletal muscle the DHPR is linked mechanically to RyR1; in cardiac muscle Ca2+ entry is the trigger.","Phosphorylating phospholamban speeds SERCA; it does not block it.","Calcium binds troponin C in both striated muscles; calmodulin is the smooth muscle partner."],"dumps":[{"t":"One beat inside a myocyte","p":"Draw a cardiac muscle cell with its T tubule and SR. Trace Ca2+ from the moment the action potential arrives to full relaxation, numbering each step.","l":["Action potential travels down the T tubule","L-type Ca2+ channels open and Ca2+ enters","Ca2+ opens RyR2 and the SR releases more Ca2+","Ca2+ binds troponin C and tropomyosin shifts","Cross-bridge cycling shortens the sarcomere","SERCA pumps Ca2+ back into the SR (phospholamban regulates it)","Na+/Ca2+ exchanger moves Ca2+ out of the cell"]},{"t":"Action potential with labels","p":"Draw a ventricular myocyte action potential. Label phases 0 to 4, the voltage values, the ion moving in each phase, and the refractory period.","l":["Resting about -90 mV in phase 4","Steep phase 0 from fast Na+ influx","Small notch in phase 1 from transient K+ efflux","Long plateau in phase 2 from L-type Ca2+ in and K+ out","Phase 3 repolarization from delayed rectifier K+","Effective refractory period spans most of the action potential"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Muscle; The Heart as a Pump and Function of the Heart Valves"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Muscle Tissue (section on cardiac muscle)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Action potentials in cardiac myocytes | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rIVCuC-Etc0"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Calcium puts myosin to work | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vv6WBeqw2Nc"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Cardiology - Heart Physiology I (Cardiac Myocyte and Membrane Potential)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FlyKXrY5ak0"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Cardiology - Heart Physiology II (Muscle contraction and Pacemaker activity)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o7L0W3Xb5Eg"}],"related":["CV-2","CV-6","CV-7","CV-18-B"]},"CV-6":{"id":"CV-6","summary":"What sets cardiac output from beat to beat: heart rate plus the three stroke volume controls (preload, afterload, contractility), and the reflexes that adjust them. Exam questions ask you to predict how a change shifts stroke volume, ejection fraction, or a pressure-volume loop.","notes":[{"h":"Core equations","points":["Cardiac output (CO) = stroke volume (SV) x heart rate (HR); about 5 L/min in a resting adult.","Ejection fraction (EF) = SV / EDV.","Mean arterial pressure (MAP) = CO x total peripheral resistance.","MAP is about diastolic pressure plus one third of pulse pressure at resting heart rates.","Fick principle: CO = O2 consumption / (arterial O2 content minus venous O2 content).","Wall stress (Laplace) = pressure x radius / (2 x wall thickness)."]},{"h":"Preload","points":["Preload is ventricular stretch at end of diastole, best judged by end-diastolic volume.","Set mainly by venous return.","Frank-Starling: more filling gives a stronger beat and larger SV, so the heart pumps out what comes in.","Venous return rises with mean systemic filling pressure (about 7 mmHg) and falls as right atrial pressure rises."]},{"h":"Afterload","points":["Afterload is the wall stress the ventricle must overcome to eject.","For the LV it mostly tracks aortic pressure; it rises in hypertension and in aortic stenosis.","Higher afterload raises ESV and lowers SV.","Concentric hypertrophy thickens the wall and lowers wall stress back toward normal."]},{"h":"Contractility and heart rate","points":["Contractility is force at a given preload and afterload; it rises with sympathetic β1 input and falls with ischemia, acidosis, and hypoxia.","Intrinsic SA node rate after autonomic blockade is about 100 bpm, so resting rate shows vagal tone.","Fast heart rates shorten diastole much more than systole, which cuts filling time and LV coronary flow.","Myocardial O2 demand rises with heart rate, contractility, and wall stress."]},{"h":"Reflex control","points":["Carotid sinus baroreceptors signal through CN IX and respond to rises and falls in pressure.","Aortic arch baroreceptors signal through CN X, mainly to rises in pressure.","Both feed the nucleus tractus solitarius in the medulla.","Falling pressure lowers baroreceptor firing, so sympathetic output rises (faster, stronger beats, vasoconstriction) and vagal output falls.","Atrial stretch speeds heart rate (Bainbridge reflex)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"What each change does to the left ventricle","cols":["Change","EDV","ESV","Stroke volume","Pressure-volume loop"],"rows":[["Higher preload","Up","About the same","Up","Wider, shifts right"],["Higher afterload","About the same","Up","Down","Taller and narrower"],["Higher contractility","About the same","Down","Up","Wider, shifts left; steeper end-systolic line"]]},{"title":"Autonomic effects on the heart","cols":["Effect","Sympathetic (β1)","Parasympathetic (vagus, M2)"],"rows":[["SA node rate (chronotropy)","Faster: steeper phase 4","Slower: hyperpolarized cell, flatter phase 4"],["AV node conduction (dromotropy)","Faster; shorter PR interval","Slower; longer PR interval"],["Atrial contractility","Up","Down"],["Ventricular contractility (inotropy)","Up strongly","Small effect (vagal fibers go mainly to atria and nodes)"],["Relaxation (lusitropy)","Faster","Little direct effect"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Preload is volume, afterload is pressure","text":"Preload asks how full the ventricle is before it squeezes. Afterload asks how hard it must push to get blood out."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Read the loop by its corners","text":"The lower right corner is EDV (preload). The upper left corner is ESV and aortic pressure (afterload). The loop width is SV. Move one corner and see what happens to the width."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Baroreceptor direction","text":"Baroreceptors fire more when stretched. Low pressure means less firing, and the brain answers with more sympathetic and less vagal output."}],"highYield":["SV depends on preload, afterload, and contractility; CO = SV x HR.","Normal EF is about 55 to 70%; it falls when contractility falls.","Raising afterload raises ESV and lowers SV.","Tachycardia shortens diastole and reduces LV coronary perfusion.","Carotid sinus uses CN IX; aortic arch uses CN X.","Concentric LV hypertrophy is the adaptation that lowers wall stress in pressure overload."],"pitfalls":["Increasing preload raises SV with little change in ESV; increasing contractility raises SV by lowering ESV.","Contractility is independent of preload; a stronger beat from more filling is the Frank-Starling effect.","The vagus has little direct effect on ventricular force even though it slows the heart a lot.","MAP equals diastolic plus one third of pulse pressure only at normal resting rates."],"dumps":[{"t":"Three pressure-volume loops","p":"Draw a normal LV pressure-volume loop. Then overlay separate loops for higher preload, higher afterload, and higher contractility, and label the EDV, ESV, and SV changes.","l":["Normal loop with four labeled corners (mitral close, aortic open, aortic close, mitral open)","Width of the loop is SV","Higher preload loop extends right with larger SV","Higher afterload loop is taller with larger ESV and smaller SV","Higher contractility loop extends left with smaller ESV","End-systolic pressure-volume line steeper with higher contractility"]},{"t":"Baroreceptor reflex","p":"Trace the baroreceptor reflex after a sudden drop in blood pressure, from receptor to heart and vessels. Label each nerve and the brainstem center.","l":["Stretch receptors in carotid sinus and aortic arch","Less stretch means less firing","Carotid sinus afferents in CN IX","Aortic arch afferents in CN X","Nucleus tractus solitarius in the medulla","Sympathetic output up: faster HR, more contractility, vasoconstriction","Vagal output down","Result: CO and TPR rise and MAP returns toward normal"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Output, Venous Return, and Their Regulation"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Nervous Regulation of the Circulation and Rapid Control of Arterial Pressure"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Muscle; The Heart as a Pump and Function of the Heart Valves (section on regulation of heart pumping)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Cardiac Output","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0O3FfHPE9PU"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Cardiovascular System Physiology - Cardiac Output (stroke volume, heart rate, preload and afterload)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hpQFToprlH8"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Cardiac Cycle | Cardiology | Systole & Diastole | Cardiovascular🫀","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jVvQaqFOJpY"}],"related":["CV-4","CV-5","CV-11","CV-12","CV-18-B"]},"CV-7":{"id":"CV-7","summary":"Where the heart's conduction system lies, how pacemaker cells fire on their own, how the autonomic nervous system speeds or slows them, and how the ECG records the result. Questions ask you to link a wave or interval to a cardiac event and a lead group to the wall and artery it views.","notes":[{"h":"Conduction pathway","points":["SA node: right atrial wall near the SVC opening, at the top of the crista terminalis.","AV node: lower interatrial septum near the opening of the coronary sinus.","AV bundle (His) crosses the fibrous skeleton, the only normal electrical link from atria to ventricles.","Bundle splits into right and left bundle branches; the left has anterior and posterior fascicles.","Right bundle branch reaches the anterior papillary muscle through the moderator band.","Purkinje fibers spread the impulse through the subendocardium."]},{"h":"Blood supply of the nodes","points":["SA nodal artery comes from the RCA in about 60% of people.","AV nodal artery comes from the RCA in about 80% of people (right-dominant hearts)."]},{"h":"Pacemaker action potential","points":["No true resting potential; lowest point about -60 mV.","Phase 4: slow climb from the funny current (If, Na+ in through HCN channels), then T-type Ca2+.","Phase 0: slow upstroke from L-type Ca2+ channels once threshold (about -40 mV) is reached.","Phase 3: K+ efflux repolarizes. There is no phase 1 or 2.","The fastest pacemaker (normally the SA node) suppresses slower ones (overdrive suppression)."]},{"h":"Autonomic control","points":["Norepinephrine on β1 receptors raises cAMP, increases If and Ca2+ currents, and steepens phase 4.","Acetylcholine on M2 receptors lowers cAMP and opens K+ channels, hyperpolarizes the cell, and flattens phase 4.","AV node delay (about 0.1 s) lets the atria finish filling the ventricles; sympathetic input shortens it and vagal input lengthens it."]},{"h":"Reading the ECG","points":["Standard paper at 25 mm/s: small box 0.04 s, large box 0.20 s; 10 mm = 1 mV.","Heart rate is about 300 divided by the number of large boxes between R waves.","Depolarization moving toward a positive electrode gives an upward deflection.","Septum depolarizes first (left to right), then apex, then free walls from endocardium to epicardium; the base is last.","Repolarization runs epicardium to endocardium, so the T wave normally points the same way as the QRS.","Normal QRS axis is about -30° to +90°."]},{"h":"AV blocks","points":["First degree: PR over 0.20 s, every P wave conducts.","Second degree Mobitz I (Wenckebach): PR lengthens beat by beat until a QRS is dropped; usually AV node.","Second degree Mobitz II: QRS dropped without PR lengthening; usually below the AV node and can progress.","Third degree: P waves and QRS complexes beat independently; an escape rhythm drives the ventricles."]}],"charts":[{"title":"ECG waves and intervals","cols":["Component","What it shows","Normal value"],"rows":[["P wave","Atrial depolarization","Under 0.12 s"],["PR interval","Atrial depolarization plus AV nodal delay","0.12 to 0.20 s"],["QRS complex","Ventricular depolarization (hides atrial repolarization)","Under 0.12 s (usually about 0.06 to 0.10 s)"],["ST segment","Ventricles fully depolarized (plateau phase)","Flat at baseline"],["T wave","Ventricular repolarization","Same direction as QRS in most leads"],["QT interval","Ventricular depolarization plus repolarization","Shortens as heart rate rises; read as rate-corrected QTc"],["U wave","Small wave after T","Prominent in hypokalemia"]]},{"title":"Pacemakers and conduction speed","cols":["Site","Intrinsic rate","Conduction speed"],"rows":[["SA node","About 60 to 100 bpm","Slow (nodal tissue)"],["Atrial muscle","None normally","About 0.3 m/s"],["AV node","About 40 to 60 bpm (junctional escape)","Slowest, gives about 0.1 s delay"],["Purkinje fibers","About 20 to 40 bpm (ventricular escape)","Fastest, about 1.5 to 4 m/s"],["Ventricular muscle","None normally","About 0.3 to 0.5 m/s"]]},{"title":"Limb and augmented lead angles","cols":["Lead","Electrodes","Angle"],"rows":[["I","Right arm (-) to left arm (+)","0°"],["II","Right arm (-) to left leg (+)","+60°"],["III","Left arm (-) to left leg (+)","+120°"],["aVR","Augmented, right arm positive","-150°"],["aVL","Augmented, left arm positive","-30°"],["aVF","Augmented, left leg positive","+90°"]]},{"title":"Lead groups, wall, and usual artery","cols":["Wall","Leads","Usual artery"],"rows":[["Septal","V1, V2","LAD"],["Anterior","V3, V4","LAD"],["Lateral","I, aVL, V5, V6","Left circumflex (V5 and V6 may be LAD)"],["Inferior","II, III, aVF","RCA in most people"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Park AT VENtura AVenue","text":"Conduction speed from fastest to slowest: Purkinje, Atria, Ventricles, AV node."},{"kind":"trick","label":"300, 150, 100, 75, 60, 50","text":"Find an R wave on a thick line. Count the next thick lines as 300, 150, 100, 75, 60, 50. Where the next R wave lands is the approximate heart rate."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Leads II, III, aVF look at the floor","text":"All three have their positive pole on the left leg, so they view the inferior wall, which usually gets its blood from the RCA."}],"highYield":["The AV node is the slowest part of the conduction system; that delay lets the atria finish ventricular filling.","SA nodal artery from the RCA in about 60% and AV nodal artery from the RCA in about 80%, so inferior MI often causes bradycardia or AV block.","Phase 0 in nodal cells is Ca2+ driven, so Ca2+ channel blockade slows AV conduction.","Einthoven's law: lead II = lead I + lead III.","Lyme carditis classically causes third-degree AV block.","Mobitz II is more dangerous than Mobitz I because the block is usually below the AV node.","Prominent U waves suggest hypokalemia."],"pitfalls":["The PR interval is measured from the start of P to the start of QRS; the PR segment is only from the end of P.","Atrial repolarization has no visible wave because it is hidden in the QRS.","The T wave is upright even though repolarization is the opposite of depolarization, because it travels in the opposite direction (epicardium to endocardium).","Mobitz I has progressive PR lengthening; Mobitz II has a constant PR before the dropped beat."],"dumps":[{"t":"Conduction system and pacemaker potential","p":"Draw the heart's conduction system in an opened heart, then beside it draw a pacemaker cell action potential. Label each structure and each ion current.","l":["SA node near SVC at the top of the crista terminalis","AV node near coronary sinus opening in the interatrial septum","Bundle of His through the fibrous skeleton","Right bundle branch through moderator band; left bundle with two fascicles","Purkinje fibers in subendocardium","Pacemaker phase 4 slow rise from If (HCN) then T-type Ca2+","Phase 0 slow upstroke from L-type Ca2+","Phase 3 K+ efflux; no plateau"]},{"t":"Label one ECG beat","p":"Draw one normal ECG beat on grid paper at standard speed. Label each wave, segment, and interval with its normal duration and the cardiac event it represents.","l":["Small box 0.04 s and large box 0.20 s","P wave is atrial depolarization","PR interval 0.12 to 0.20 s includes AV nodal delay","QRS under 0.12 s is ventricular depolarization","Atrial repolarization hidden in QRS","ST segment is the plateau, normally flat","T wave is ventricular repolarization","QT interval spans depolarization plus repolarization"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Rhythmical Excitation of the Heart"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Fundamentals of Electrocardiography"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, section on the conducting system of the heart"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Electrophysiology | Intrinsic Cardiac Conduction System","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1kX6Tp8CWFw"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Electrophysiology | Extrinsic Cardiac Conduction System","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2L4FbEEsy1s"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Electrical system of the heart | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7K2icszdxQc"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Cardiology - Relationship of conduction system, ventricular contraction and ECG","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eOX_NmYLKOE"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"EKG or ECG Interpretation | Part 1/20","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ku96IY27CE0"}],"related":["CV-5","CV-6","CV-8","CV-18-C3","CV-18-K3"]},"CV-8":{"id":"CV-8","summary":"This unit covers where the coronary arteries arise, how they branch, which part of the heart each one feeds, and how the heart's veins drain back to the right atrium. The exam uses it to link a blocked artery to the wall that dies, the ECG leads that change, and the complications that follow.","notes":[{"h":"Origins and the left system","points":["Right coronary artery (RCA) arises from the right aortic sinus; left coronary artery (LCA) from the left aortic sinus.","Left main runs between the pulmonary trunk and left auricle, then splits into the LAD and the circumflex (LCx).","LAD (anterior interventricular artery) runs down the anterior interventricular groove to the apex.","LAD feeds the anterior LV wall, the apex, and the anterior two-thirds of the septum (septal perforators); diagonal branches feed the anterolateral wall.","LCx runs in the left atrioventricular (coronary) groove to the back; obtuse marginal branches feed the lateral LV wall."]},{"h":"The right system","points":["RCA runs in the right atrioventricular groove.","Right (acute) marginal branch feeds the right ventricle.","SA nodal artery comes from the RCA in about 60% of hearts and from the LCx in about 40%.","AV nodal artery comes from the RCA in about 80% of hearts.","In a right-dominant heart the RCA gives the posterior descending artery (PDA), which feeds the posterior one-third of the septum and the inferior LV wall."]},{"h":"Dominance and papillary muscles","points":["Dominance is defined by which artery gives the PDA.","Most hearts are right dominant (estimates run from about 67% to 85%); the rest are left dominant (PDA from LCx) or codominant.","Anterolateral papillary muscle has a double supply (LAD and LCx).","Posteromedial papillary muscle depends on the PDA alone, so it is the one that ruptures after an inferior MI."]},{"h":"Venous drainage","points":["Coronary sinus lies in the posterior atrioventricular groove and opens into the right atrium.","Great cardiac vein runs with the LAD, middle cardiac vein with the PDA, small cardiac vein with the right marginal artery; all three reach the coronary sinus.","Anterior cardiac veins drain the front of the RV straight into the right atrium.","Smallest cardiac veins (venae cordis minimae) open directly into the chambers."]},{"h":"How coronary flow works","points":["Resting coronary flow is about 225 mL/min, about 4 to 5% of cardiac output.","LV muscle is perfused mostly in diastole, because systole squeezes the intramyocardial vessels shut.","Tachycardia shortens diastole, so it cuts LV perfusion while raising demand.","The heart extracts about 70% of arterial oxygen at rest, so more demand must be met by more flow.","Adenosine and other local metabolites are the main coronary vasodilators.","The subendocardium has the highest wall stress and the least flow reserve, so it becomes ischemic first."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Coronary arteries and their territories","cols":["Artery","Course","Main territory","Typical infarct ECG leads"],"rows":[["LAD","Anterior interventricular groove to the apex","Anterior LV wall, apex, anterior two-thirds of septum","V1 to V4 (anterior and septal)"],["LCx","Left atrioventricular groove to the posterior heart","Lateral and posterolateral LV wall, left atrium","I, aVL, V5, V6 (lateral)"],["RCA","Right atrioventricular groove","Right atrium, most of the RV, SA node (about 60%), AV node (about 80%)","II, III, aVF (inferior), often with bradycardia or AV block"],["PDA (from RCA in right-dominant hearts)","Posterior interventricular groove","Posterior one-third of septum, inferior LV wall, posteromedial papillary muscle","II, III, aVF (inferior)"]]},{"title":"Cardiac veins","cols":["Vein","Runs with","Drains into"],"rows":[["Great cardiac vein","LAD","Coronary sinus"],["Middle cardiac vein","PDA","Coronary sinus"],["Small cardiac vein","Right marginal artery","Coronary sinus"],["Anterior cardiac veins","Over the anterior RV wall","Right atrium directly"],["Smallest cardiac veins","Within the myocardium","Heart chambers directly"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Who gives the PDA wins","text":"To name the dominant artery, ask only one question: which artery gives the posterior descending artery? Size of the vessel does not matter."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Veins shadow arteries","text":"Pair each vein with its artery: great with LAD, middle with PDA, small with right marginal. All three empty into the coronary sinus, which empties into the right atrium."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Diastole feeds the left","text":"Anything that shortens diastole (fast heart rate) or lowers aortic diastolic pressure (aortic regurgitation, hypotension) starves the LV subendocardium first."}],"highYield":["LAD is the most commonly occluded coronary artery, followed by the RCA, then the LCx.","Inferior MI (II, III, aVF) usually means RCA occlusion and can come with bradycardia or AV block.","Posteromedial papillary muscle rupture after inferior MI causes sudden acute mitral regurgitation.","Coronary perfusion pressure is aortic diastolic pressure minus LV end-diastolic pressure.","Coronary sinus runs in the posterior atrioventricular groove and opens into the right atrium.","Adenosine is the classic local metabolite that dilates coronary arterioles when demand rises."],"pitfalls":["Dominance is about who supplies the PDA; a large LCx can still sit in a right-dominant heart.","The SA node is usually fed by the RCA (about 60%), but in about 40% it comes from the LCx, so an LCx lesion can still cause sinus node problems.","The anterolateral papillary muscle rarely ruptures because it has two arteries; the posteromedial one has only the PDA.","Anterior cardiac veins and smallest cardiac veins bypass the coronary sinus, so the coronary sinus does not carry all cardiac venous blood."],"dumps":[{"t":"Map the coronary arteries","p":"Draw the heart from the front and from below. Label the left main, LAD, diagonal branch, circumflex, obtuse marginal, RCA, right marginal, and PDA. Shade and name the wall each artery feeds.","l":["RCA from the right aortic sinus, LCA from the left aortic sinus","Left main splits into LAD and circumflex","LAD in the anterior interventricular groove reaching the apex","LAD territory: anterior LV wall, apex, anterior two-thirds of septum","Circumflex in the left atrioventricular groove feeding the lateral wall","RCA in the right atrioventricular groove with the right marginal branch to the RV","PDA in the posterior interventricular groove feeding the inferior wall and posterior one-third of septum","Note that the PDA source defines dominance"]},{"t":"Trace coronary blood","p":"Trace a red cell from the aorta through the LAD, across the anterior wall capillaries, and back to the right atrium. Name each vessel and say when in the cardiac cycle the flow is highest.","l":["Left aortic sinus to the left main coronary artery","Left main to LAD, then septal or diagonal branches","Capillaries of the anterior LV wall","Great cardiac vein running back up beside the LAD","Coronary sinus in the posterior atrioventricular groove","Opening into the right atrium","LV flow is highest in diastole because systole compresses intramyocardial vessels","About 70% of the oxygen is extracted on the way through"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax chapter, section on the heart: arterial supply and venous drainage"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Muscle Blood Flow and Cardiac Output During Exercise; the Coronary Circulation and Ischemic Heart Disease"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on ischemic heart disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Circulatory System | Coronary Circulation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TEcPhovaAg4"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Anatomy of the Heart and Coronary Circulation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7ERVxf5eD7k"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Cardiology - Coronary Blood Supply","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3wpT-4bSmoU"}],"related":["CV-3","CV-7","CV-18-C","CV-18-C3"]},"CV-9":{"id":"CV-9","summary":"This unit covers how arterial blood reaches the head, limbs, and abdominal organs, how venous blood returns, and how the portal system connects the gut to the liver. Exam questions name a vessel or a vertebral level and ask what it supplies, or describe a blockage and ask what tissue suffers.","notes":[{"h":"Aortic arch and thoracic aorta","points":["Arch branches, right to left: brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, left subclavian.","Brachiocephalic trunk splits into the right subclavian and right common carotid arteries.","Common carotid divides at about the level of C4 (upper border of the thyroid cartilage) into internal and external carotids.","Descending thoracic aorta gives posterior intercostal, bronchial, and esophageal branches."]},{"h":"Abdominal aorta","points":["Enters the abdomen through the aortic hiatus at T12 and bifurcates into the common iliac arteries at L4.","Unpaired gut arteries: celiac trunk (T12), superior mesenteric artery (L1), inferior mesenteric artery (L3).","Paired branches: inferior phrenic, middle suprarenal, renal (about L1), gonadal (about L2), and four pairs of lumbar arteries.","Celiac trunk gives the left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries.","Splenic flexure sits between SMA and IMA territories, a watershed zone for ischemic colitis."]},{"h":"Brain and limbs","points":["Brain gets anterior flow from the internal carotids and posterior flow from the vertebral arteries (branches of the subclavians), which join as the basilar artery.","Circle of Willis links them: anterior communicating, anterior cerebral, internal carotid, posterior communicating, and posterior cerebral arteries.","Upper limb: subclavian becomes axillary at the lateral border of the first rib, then brachial at the lower border of teres major; brachial splits into radial and ulnar in the cubital fossa.","Lower limb: external iliac becomes femoral under the inguinal ligament, then popliteal at the adductor hiatus; popliteal splits into anterior tibial and the tibiofibular trunk (posterior tibial and fibular).","Dorsalis pedis is the continuation of the anterior tibial artery."]},{"h":"Veins and the portal system","points":["SVC forms from the two brachiocephalic veins; IVC forms from the common iliac veins at about L5.","Left renal vein crosses in front of the aorta just below the SMA; the left gonadal vein drains into it, while the right gonadal vein drains straight into the IVC.","Hepatic portal vein forms behind the neck of the pancreas from the superior mesenteric and splenic veins; the inferior mesenteric vein usually joins the splenic vein.","Portal blood passes through liver sinusoids, then leaves by the hepatic veins into the IVC."]},{"h":"Where cardiac output goes at rest","points":["Liver and gut together receive about a quarter of resting cardiac output, kidneys about a fifth.","Skeletal muscle gets about 15% at rest and the brain about 14%.","Heart muscle gets about 4 to 5% but uses oxygen at a very high rate."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Abdominal aorta landmarks","cols":["Branch or event","Vertebral level","Supplies"],"rows":[["Aorta passes the aortic hiatus","T12","Start of the abdominal aorta"],["Celiac trunk","T12","Foregut: stomach, proximal duodenum, liver, gallbladder, part of the pancreas, spleen"],["Superior mesenteric artery","L1","Midgut: distal duodenum to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon"],["Renal arteries","About L1","Kidneys"],["Gonadal arteries","About L2","Testes or ovaries"],["Inferior mesenteric artery","L3","Hindgut: distal one-third of the transverse colon to the upper anal canal"],["Bifurcation into common iliacs","L4","Pelvis and lower limbs"]]},{"title":"Portosystemic anastomoses","cols":["Site","Portal side","Systemic side","Sign in portal hypertension"],"rows":[["Lower esophagus","Left gastric vein","Esophageal veins draining to the azygos system","Esophageal varices"],["Umbilicus","Paraumbilical veins","Superficial and inferior epigastric veins","Caput medusae"],["Rectum","Superior rectal vein","Middle and inferior rectal veins","Anorectal varices"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ABC'S","text":"Aortic arch gives the Brachiocephalic trunk, left common Carotid, and left Subclavian, in that order from right to left."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Bifourcation","text":"The abdominal aorta bifurcates at L4: bi-FOUR-cation."},{"kind":"trick","label":"One artery per gut segment","text":"Foregut goes with the celiac trunk, midgut with the SMA, hindgut with the IMA. Find which segment the organ came from and you have its artery."}],"highYield":["Celiac T12, SMA L1, renal about L1, gonadal about L2, IMA L3, bifurcation L4.","Left renal vein can be squeezed between the SMA and aorta (nutcracker phenomenon).","Left varicocele is more common because the left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein.","Splenic flexure is a watershed area between SMA and IMA territories.","Portal hypertension opens anastomoses at the esophagus, umbilicus, and rectum.","The vertebral arteries come off the subclavians and join to form the basilar artery."],"pitfalls":["The spleen develops from mesoderm, yet it is supplied by the celiac trunk (splenic artery).","The pancreatic head has a double supply: superior pancreaticoduodenal (celiac) and inferior pancreaticoduodenal (SMA) arteries.","The popliteal artery starts at the adductor hiatus, while the femoral artery starts at the inguinal ligament; mixing these up shifts the level of a lesion.","The right gonadal vein drains into the IVC; only the left one drains into the renal vein."],"dumps":[{"t":"Draw the abdominal aorta","p":"Draw the abdominal aorta from the diaphragm to its bifurcation. Mark the vertebral level of each branch and write what each branch supplies.","l":["Aortic hiatus at T12","Celiac trunk at T12 with left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic branches","SMA at L1 supplying the midgut","Renal arteries at about L1 and gonadal arteries at about L2","IMA at L3 supplying the hindgut","Bifurcation into common iliac arteries at L4","Left renal vein crossing anterior to the aorta below the SMA"]},{"t":"Draw the circle of Willis","p":"Draw the circle of Willis as seen from below the brain. Label every artery that forms the circle, the arteries that feed it, and the middle cerebral arteries.","l":["Internal carotid arteries entering on each side","Anterior cerebral arteries joined by the anterior communicating artery","Middle cerebral arteries coming off each internal carotid","Posterior communicating arteries linking internal carotid to posterior cerebral","Posterior cerebral arteries from the top of the basilar artery","Basilar artery formed by the two vertebral arteries","Vertebral arteries arising from the subclavian arteries"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen chapter, section on the abdominal aorta and the arterial supply of the abdominal viscera"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head chapter, section on the arterial supply of the brain"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Local and Humoral Control of Tissue Blood Flow"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Circulatory System | Arteries of the Thorax & Abdomen | Torso Anatomy Model","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L4vf12QCy70"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Circulatory System | Arteries & Veins of the Upper & Lower Limbs | Wire Man Model","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EmoOicZqXhI"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Arteries, arterioles, venules, and veins | Health & Medicine | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iqRTd1NY-pU"}],"related":["CV-3","CV-11","CV-18-H3","CV-18-I6"]},"CV-10":{"id":"CV-10","summary":"This unit covers how lymph is formed, how it moves, where it drains, and how lymph nodes, spleen, thymus, and mucosal lymphoid tissue are built. The exam asks about drainage routes, the zones of each lymphoid organ, and what goes wrong when lymph flow is blocked.","notes":[{"h":"Lymph formation","points":["Lymphatic capillaries are blind-ended tubes with overlapping endothelial cells that act as one-way flap valves.","Anchoring filaments hold them open when the tissue swells.","They return filtered fluid and leaked plasma protein to the blood, about 2 to 3 L per day.","Lacteals in intestinal villi take up dietary fat as chylomicrons, which makes lymph milky (chyle)."]},{"h":"How lymph moves","points":["Collecting lymphatics have valves and smooth muscle that contract in segments (lymphangions).","Skeletal muscle contraction, breathing, nearby arterial pulsation, and outside compression all push lymph forward.","A rise in interstitial fluid pressure increases lymph flow, up to a maximum."]},{"h":"Drainage routes","points":["Thoracic duct starts at the cisterna chyli (about L1 to L2), passes through the aortic hiatus, and drains into the left venous angle (left internal jugular and left subclavian junction).","Thoracic duct drains everything except the right upper body.","Right lymphatic duct (or its trunks separately) drains the right head, neck, arm, and right side of the thorax into the right venous angle."]},{"h":"Lymphoid organs","points":["Primary lymphoid organs: bone marrow (B cells mature) and thymus (T cells mature).","Secondary lymphoid organs: lymph nodes, spleen, and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (tonsils, Peyer patches in the ileum, appendix).","Lymph node path: afferent vessels, subcapsular sinus, cortical sinuses, medullary sinuses, efferent vessel at the hilum.","Spleen filters blood: red pulp removes old red cells; white pulp mounts immune responses to blood-borne antigens.","Thymus comes from the third pharyngeal pouch; positive selection happens in the cortex, negative selection in the medulla."]},{"h":"When lymph flow fails","points":["Lymphedema is protein-rich edema that becomes firm and nonpitting as fibrosis sets in.","Causes include lymph node removal or radiation (such as after breast cancer surgery), tumor, and filariasis (Wuchereria bancrofti).","Thoracic duct injury leaks chyle into the pleural space (chylothorax)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Zones of lymphoid organs","cols":["Organ","Zone","Main cells","Job"],"rows":[["Lymph node","Cortex follicles","B cells; germinal centers in secondary follicles","B-cell activation and proliferation"],["Lymph node","Paracortex","T cells and dendritic cells; high endothelial venules","T-cell activation; entry point for circulating lymphocytes"],["Lymph node","Medulla","Plasma cells and macrophages in medullary cords","Antibody secretion and filtering of lymph"],["Spleen","White pulp, periarteriolar lymphoid sheath","T cells","T-cell responses to blood antigens"],["Spleen","White pulp, follicles","B cells","B-cell responses to blood antigens"],["Spleen","Red pulp (cords and sinusoids)","Macrophages","Removes old or damaged red cells"],["Thymus","Cortex","Immature T cells and cortical epithelial cells","Positive selection"],["Thymus","Medulla","Maturing T cells; Hassall corpuscles","Negative selection"]]},{"title":"First lymph node group by region","cols":["Region drained","First node group"],"rows":[["Upper limb and lateral breast","Axillary"],["Medial breast","Parasternal (internal thoracic)"],["Scrotum, vulva, anal canal below the pectinate line, most of the superficial lower limb","Superficial inguinal"],["Lateral foot and posterolateral calf","Popliteal"],["Testes and ovaries","Para-aortic (lumbar)"],["Stomach","Celiac"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Right upper corner","text":"Only the right upper corner of the body (right head, neck, arm, and chest) goes to the right lymphatic duct. Every other region drains to the thoracic duct."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"SHiNE SKiS","text":"Encapsulated organisms that threaten people without a spleen: Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae type b, Neisseria meningitidis, Escherichia coli, Salmonella, Klebsiella pneumoniae, group B Streptococcus."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Cortex positive, medulla negative","text":"In the thymus, the cortex keeps T cells that can bind self MHC (positive selection), and the medulla deletes T cells that bind self antigen too strongly (negative selection)."}],"highYield":["Testes drain to para-aortic nodes, while the scrotum drains to superficial inguinal nodes.","Paracortex is underdeveloped in DiGeorge syndrome because T cells are lacking.","Asplenia raises the risk of sepsis from encapsulated bacteria; Howell-Jolly bodies appear on the smear.","Thoracic duct empties into the junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins.","Lymphedema fluid is protein rich, which drives fibrosis and nonpitting swelling.","Peyer patches sit in the ileum and contain M cells that sample gut antigens."],"pitfalls":["Anal canal below the pectinate line drains to superficial inguinal nodes; above it drains to internal iliac nodes.","Lymph node B cells sit in the cortex follicles, while T cells sit in the paracortex; in the spleen T cells sit in the periarteriolar sheath.","Lymphedema and cardiac edema both swell the legs, but lymphedema is protein rich and becomes nonpitting, while early edema from high venous pressure pits easily.","Bone marrow and thymus are primary lymphoid organs; the spleen is secondary."],"dumps":[{"t":"Draw a lymph node","p":"Draw a lymph node in cross section. Label where lymph enters and leaves, every sinus it passes through, and the B-cell, T-cell, and plasma-cell zones.","l":["Afferent lymphatic vessels entering the convex surface","Capsule and subcapsular sinus","Cortex with primary and secondary follicles and germinal centers (B cells)","Paracortex with T cells and high endothelial venules","Medullary cords with plasma cells and macrophages","Medullary sinuses leading to the hilum","Efferent lymphatic vessel, artery, and vein at the hilum"]},{"t":"Trace lymph back to the blood","p":"Trace a drop of interstitial fluid from the left foot back into the blood. Then do the same for a drop from the right hand. Name each vessel and node group.","l":["Lymphatic capillary with overlapping endothelial flaps","Collecting vessels with valves","Left foot: superficial inguinal nodes, then deep inguinal, external iliac, common iliac, and lumbar nodes","Lumbar trunks to the cisterna chyli near L1 to L2","Thoracic duct through the aortic hiatus to the left venous angle","Right hand: axillary nodes, then the subclavian trunk","Right lymphatic duct (or the trunks directly) to the right venous angle"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: Capillary Fluid Exchange, Interstitial Fluid, and Lymph Flow"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Immune System and Lymphoid Organs"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Introduction to the Immune System, section on the tissues of the immune system (lymph nodes, spleen, mucosal lymphoid tissue)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Why we need a lymphatic system | Lymphatic system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_GinTV94hUk"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"How lymphatic vessels move fluid | Lymphatic system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CLneY6SoPi0"}],"related":["CV-11","CV-16","CV-18-H4"]},"CV-11":{"id":"CV-11","summary":"This unit covers the physics of blood flow (pressure, resistance, viscosity, turbulence), the Starling forces that move fluid across capillaries, and how tissues control their own flow. Board questions ask you to predict what happens to flow or pressure when one variable changes.","notes":[{"h":"Pressure, flow, and resistance","points":["Flow equals pressure difference divided by resistance (Q = ΔP / R).","For the whole circulation, mean arterial pressure is about cardiac output times total peripheral resistance.","Poiseuille: resistance = 8ηL / (πr⁴), so radius matters most.","Halving the radius raises resistance 16 times and cuts flow to one-sixteenth at the same pressure.","Resistances in series add; in parallel, 1/R total = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + and so on, so the total is lower than any single branch.","Organs sit in parallel, which lets each one change its own flow without changing the others much."]},{"h":"Velocity, viscosity, turbulence","points":["Velocity = flow divided by total cross-sectional area, so blood moves slowest in capillaries.","Hematocrit is the main determinant of viscosity; polycythemia raises viscosity and resistance, anemia lowers them.","Turbulence becomes more likely with high velocity, low viscosity, large diameter, and rough or narrowed vessels (Reynolds number).","Turbulence makes bruits and murmurs, such as flow murmurs in anemia."]},{"h":"Arterial pressure and compliance","points":["Pulse pressure = systolic minus diastolic; it widens with stiff arteries or a larger stroke volume.","MAP is about diastolic pressure plus one-third of pulse pressure at resting heart rates.","Veins are about 24 times more compliant than their matching arteries and hold about 64% of blood volume.","Law of Laplace: wall tension rises with pressure and radius, so a dilated ventricle or aneurysm faces more wall stress."]},{"h":"Capillary exchange and lymph","points":["Starling: net filtration = Kf × [(Pc − Pi) − σ(πc − πi)].","Edema comes from higher capillary pressure, lower plasma oncotic pressure, leakier capillaries, or blocked lymphatics.","Lymph returns the small net filtrate and leaked protein to the blood.","Venous return is helped by venous valves, the skeletal muscle pump, and the drop in thoracic pressure during inspiration."]},{"h":"Control of blood flow","points":["Metabolic control: adenosine, CO₂, H⁺, K⁺, lactate, and low O₂ dilate arterioles in active tissue.","Myogenic control: stretch of arteriolar smooth muscle triggers contraction, which keeps flow steady when pressure rises (autoregulation).","Endothelium releases nitric oxide (from shear stress) and prostacyclin to dilate, and endothelin to constrict.","Sympathetic nerves constrict most arterioles through α1 receptors.","Constrictors in blood: norepinephrine, angiotensin II, vasopressin. Dilators: bradykinin, histamine, ANP, nitric oxide.","Baroreceptors in the carotid sinus (CN IX) and aortic arch (CN X) report pressure to the nucleus of the solitary tract in the medulla."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Vessel segments","cols":["Segment","Key feature","Why it matters"],"rows":[["Aorta and large arteries","Elastic, store energy in systole","Keep flow going during diastole"],["Arterioles","Largest share of resistance and the biggest pressure drop","Set total peripheral resistance and divide flow among organs"],["Capillaries","Largest total cross-sectional area, slowest velocity","Time for exchange with tissues"],["Venules and veins","Most compliant, hold about 64% of blood volume","Blood reservoir; venoconstriction raises venous return"]]},{"title":"Main control of flow by organ","cols":["Organ","Dominant control"],"rows":[["Heart","Local metabolites (adenosine, NO, CO₂) and low O₂"],["Brain","Arterial PCO₂ (through pH)"],["Kidney","Myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback"],["Lung","Hypoxia causes vasoconstriction"],["Skeletal muscle","Local metabolites during exercise; sympathetic tone at rest"],["Skin","Sympathetic nerves (temperature control)"]]},{"title":"Starling forces","cols":["Force","Pushes fluid","Changes that cause edema"],"rows":[["Capillary hydrostatic pressure (Pc)","Out of the capillary","Rises in heart failure, venous obstruction, volume overload"],["Plasma oncotic pressure (πc)","Into the capillary","Falls in nephrotic syndrome, liver failure, malnutrition"],["Interstitial hydrostatic pressure (Pi)","Into the capillary when positive (it is slightly negative in most loose tissues, which favors filtration)","Rarely a primary cause; it rises as edema builds and opposes further filtration"],["Interstitial oncotic pressure (πi)","Out of the capillary","Rises with leaky capillaries (burns, inflammation) or lymphatic blockage"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Blood follows Ohm's law","text":"ΔP = Q × R works like V = I × R. Pressure is voltage, flow is current, and resistance is resistance. MAP = CO × TPR is the same idea for the whole body."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Radius to the fourth","text":"When a question changes vessel radius, think in powers of two: half the radius gives 16 times the resistance; double the radius gives one-sixteenth."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Four ways to edema","text":"Push harder (high Pc), pull weaker (low πc), leak more (high Kf or πi), or drain less (lymph block). Sort any edema cause into one of the four."}],"highYield":["Arterioles are the main resistance vessels and the main site of blood pressure control.","Capillaries have the greatest total cross-sectional area and the slowest velocity.","Radius is raised to the fourth power in Poiseuille's law, so small changes in caliber cause large changes in flow.","Cerebral flow tracks PCO₂: hyperventilation lowers PCO₂ and constricts cerebral vessels.","Hypoxia dilates systemic vessels but constricts pulmonary vessels.","Nephrotic syndrome causes edema by lowering plasma oncotic pressure.","Isolated systolic hypertension in older adults reflects stiff, less compliant arteries."],"pitfalls":["Adding a parallel vascular bed lowers total resistance; adding a segment in series raises it.","Blood velocity is lowest in capillaries even though each capillary is tiny, because their combined area is the largest.","Pulmonary vessels constrict to hypoxia, the opposite of systemic vessels.","Higher hematocrit raises viscosity and resistance; anemia lowers them and favors turbulence."],"dumps":[{"t":"Pressure, area, and velocity","p":"Draw three stacked line graphs across the aorta, arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, veins, and vena cava: one for pressure, one for total cross-sectional area, one for velocity. Explain each shape.","l":["Pressure is highest in the aorta and pulsatile in large arteries","Steepest pressure drop across the arterioles","Pulsatility is mostly gone by the capillaries","Venous pressure is low and near zero at the right atrium","Cross-sectional area peaks at the capillaries","Velocity is the mirror image of area, lowest in capillaries","Velocity rises again as veins merge into the vena cava"]},{"t":"Starling forces and edema","p":"Build a table on blank paper of the four Starling forces. For each, write which way it pushes fluid and one disease that changes it to cause edema. Add the role of lymph.","l":["Capillary hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid out","Plasma oncotic pressure pulls fluid in","Interstitial hydrostatic pressure opposes filtration when positive","Interstitial oncotic pressure pulls fluid out","Heart failure raises capillary hydrostatic pressure","Nephrotic syndrome or cirrhosis lowers plasma oncotic pressure","Burns or inflammation raise permeability","Lymph removes the net filtrate; blockage causes lymphedema"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Section on the overview of the circulation and the physics of pressure, flow, and resistance"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Local and Humoral Control of Tissue Blood Flow"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: Capillary Fluid Exchange, Interstitial Fluid, and Lymph Flow"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Fundamentals of Blood Pressure","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2g7pFPXG2eE"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Resistance in a tube | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8X7yEzgurP8"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Flow and perfusion | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ePwN6rHhNyU"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Hemodynamics | Circulatory System Physiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cCyTYsxECLw"}],"related":["CV-6","CV-10","CV-18-A2","CV-18-H4"]},"CV-12":{"id":"CV-12","summary":"This unit covers how the heart and vessels respond during a single bout of exercise and how they adapt with regular training. Questions ask you to predict changes in heart rate, stroke volume, cardiac output, blood pressure, and resistance, and to tell an athlete's heart from disease.","notes":[{"h":"Starting exercise","points":["Central command from the motor cortex and reflexes from muscle mechanoreceptors and metaboreceptors raise sympathetic output.","The early rise in heart rate comes from vagal withdrawal; sympathetic drive takes over as intensity rises.","Venoconstriction, the skeletal muscle pump, and deeper breathing push more blood back to the heart."]},{"h":"Heart and output","points":["Heart rate rises roughly in step with workload.","Stroke volume rises from more filling (Frank-Starling) and stronger contraction; in many untrained people it levels off at moderate intensity.","Cardiac output can rise about four- to fivefold in hard exercise in untrained adults, and more in trained endurance athletes.","Oxygen uptake follows the Fick principle: VO₂ = cardiac output × arteriovenous O₂ difference.","The arteriovenous O₂ difference widens because active muscle extracts more oxygen."]},{"h":"Redistribution of flow","points":["Active muscle arterioles dilate to local metabolites (K⁺, adenosine, H⁺, CO₂, lactate) and nitric oxide.","Muscle blood flow can rise up to about 25 times in strenuous exercise.","Sympathetic constriction cuts flow to the gut, kidneys, and inactive muscle.","Skin flow rises as body temperature climbs, to shed heat.","Brain flow stays fairly constant; coronary flow rises with cardiac work."]},{"h":"Blood pressure in two kinds of exercise","points":["Dynamic (aerobic) exercise: systolic pressure rises, diastolic stays about the same or falls slightly, total peripheral resistance falls.","Static (isometric) exercise: contracting muscle squeezes its own vessels, so systolic and diastolic pressure both rise sharply.","Dynamic exercise is mainly a volume load on the heart; static exercise is mainly a pressure load."]},{"h":"Training adaptations","points":["Endurance training enlarges the LV cavity with proportional wall thickening (eccentric remodeling), so stroke volume rises.","Plasma and blood volume increase, which raises preload.","Resting heart rate falls, mostly from higher vagal tone; maximum heart rate changes little.","Maximal cardiac output and VO₂max rise.","Trained muscle has more capillaries and mitochondria, so it extracts more oxygen.","Regular exercise lowers resting blood pressure, raises HDL, lowers triglycerides, and improves insulin sensitivity and endothelial nitric oxide release."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Acute response: dynamic vs static exercise","cols":["Variable","Dynamic (running, cycling)","Static (heavy hold, handgrip)"],"rows":[["Heart rate","Rises a lot","Rises moderately"],["Stroke volume","Rises","Little change"],["Cardiac output","Rises a lot","Rises modestly"],["Systolic pressure","Rises","Rises sharply"],["Diastolic pressure","Same or slightly lower","Rises sharply"],["Total peripheral resistance","Falls","Same or rises"],["Main load on the LV","Volume","Pressure"]]},{"title":"Athlete's heart vs hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","cols":["Feature","Athlete's heart","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"rows":[["Wall thickening","Symmetric and mild","Often asymmetric, septum most"],["LV cavity","Normal or enlarged","Small"],["Diastolic filling","Normal","Impaired (stiff ventricle)"],["After a period of rest from training","Thickening regresses","No regression"],["Family history of sudden death","Usually absent","May be present (autosomal dominant inheritance)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Fick for exercise","text":"VO₂ = CO × (arterial minus venous O₂ content). Exercise raises both terms: the heart pumps more, and muscle pulls more oxygen out of each liter."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Run vs lift","text":"Running opens muscle vessels, so resistance falls and diastolic pressure holds steady. Lifting squeezes them shut, so both systolic and diastolic pressure climb."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Big tank, slow pump","text":"The trained heart has a bigger LV and more blood volume, so each beat moves more blood and the resting rate can be lower for the same output."}],"highYield":["In dynamic exercise total peripheral resistance falls because active muscle beds dilate.","Diastolic pressure stays about the same or falls slightly in dynamic exercise; it rises in static exercise.","Resting bradycardia in trained athletes is mainly from increased vagal tone.","The arteriovenous O₂ difference widens during exercise and after training.","Exercise shunts blood away from the gut and kidneys toward muscle, heart, and skin.","Athlete's heart regresses after training stops; hypertrophic cardiomyopathy does not."],"pitfalls":["Cardiac output at rest stays about the same after training; the lower heart rate is balanced by a larger stroke volume.","Maximum heart rate does not rise with training; the gains in maximal output come from stroke volume.","A rise in systolic pressure during running is normal; a fall in systolic pressure as workload increases is abnormal.","Brain blood flow stays fairly constant in exercise even though cardiac output rises several-fold."],"dumps":[{"t":"Rest vs hard exercise","p":"Build a table with two columns, rest and hard dynamic exercise. Fill in what happens to heart rate, stroke volume, cardiac output, systolic pressure, diastolic pressure, total peripheral resistance, arteriovenous O₂ difference, muscle flow, and gut and kidney flow.","l":["Heart rate up","Stroke volume up","Cardiac output up about four- to fivefold (more in trained athletes)","Systolic pressure up","Diastolic pressure about the same or slightly down","Total peripheral resistance down","Arteriovenous O₂ difference wider","Muscle flow up as much as about 25 times","Gut and kidney flow down from sympathetic constriction"]},{"t":"Trace the reflexes of a run","p":"Trace the steps from the moment someone starts running to the rise in cardiac output. Include the nerve signals, the heart, the veins, and the muscle arterioles.","l":["Central command from the motor cortex","Muscle mechanoreceptor and metaboreceptor input","Medullary cardiovascular centers respond","Vagal withdrawal raises heart rate first","Sympathetic drive raises heart rate and contractility","Venoconstriction and the muscle pump raise venous return","More filling raises stroke volume (Frank-Starling)","Local metabolites dilate active muscle arterioles while inactive beds constrict"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Muscle Blood Flow and Cardiac Output During Exercise; the Coronary Circulation and Ischemic Heart Disease"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Sports Physiology"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Cardiac Output","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0O3FfHPE9PU"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Autonomic nervous system effects on the heart | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KiouveG278Y"}],"related":["CV-6","CV-11","CV-15","CV-18-E1"]},"CV-13":{"id":"CV-13","summary":"This unit covers how lipids travel in the blood, how the heart makes its ATP, and which vitamins, minerals, and cofactors the heart and vessels depend on. The exam links a biochemical defect or deficiency to a heart or vessel problem, such as thiamine and high-output failure or homocysteine and thrombosis.","notes":[{"h":"Lipoprotein transport","points":["Exogenous path: intestine packs dietary triglyceride into chylomicrons (apo B-48), which reach the blood through lymph.","Lipoprotein lipase on capillary endothelium, activated by apo C-II, frees fatty acids from chylomicrons and VLDL.","Endogenous path: liver makes VLDL (apo B-100), which becomes IDL and then LDL as triglyceride is removed.","LDL delivers cholesterol to cells through the LDL receptor, which binds apo B-100.","HDL (apo A-I) carries cholesterol back to the liver (reverse cholesterol transport); apo A-I activates LCAT, which esterifies cholesterol.","CETP moves cholesteryl esters from HDL to VLDL, IDL, and LDL in exchange for triglyceride.","HMG-CoA reductase is the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis."]},{"h":"Lipids and the vessel wall","points":["LDL that enters the intima can be oxidized; macrophages take it up through scavenger receptors that are not downregulated, becoming foam cells.","Lipoprotein(a) is an LDL-like particle with apo(a), which resembles plasminogen; its level is mostly genetic and it adds atherosclerotic risk.","Trans fats raise LDL and lower HDL; omega-3 fatty acids (EPA, DHA) lower triglycerides at higher intakes."]},{"h":"Cardiac energy metabolism","points":["The adult heart at rest makes most of its ATP (roughly 60 to 90%) from fatty acid beta-oxidation; glucose, lactate, and ketones supply the rest.","Mitochondria fill about a third of cardiomyocyte volume, so the heart depends on a steady O₂ supply.","Long-chain fatty acids need carnitine and carnitine palmitoyltransferase I to enter mitochondria; malonyl-CoA inhibits CPT-I.","Creatine kinase moves high-energy phosphate from mitochondria to myofibrils as creatine phosphate.","In ischemia the heart switches to anaerobic glycolysis, lactate builds up, and contraction fails within a minute or two.","Coenzyme Q10 (ubiquinone) carries electrons in the respiratory chain and is made from the same mevalonate pathway that statins block."]},{"h":"Vitamins","points":["Thiamine (B1), as thiamine pyrophosphate, is a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, transketolase, and branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase.","Thiamine deficiency with heart failure (wet beriberi) is a high-output state with dilated heart and peripheral vasodilation; risk is high in alcohol use disorder and malnutrition.","Homocysteine is remethylated to methionine by methionine synthase (needs B12 and folate) or converted to cystathionine by cystathionine beta-synthase (needs B6).","Deficiency of B12, folate, or B6 raises homocysteine, which injures endothelium and favors thrombosis.","Vitamin C is needed to hydroxylate proline and lysine in collagen; scurvy causes fragile vessels, bleeding gums, and perifollicular hemorrhage.","Vitamin K is needed to gamma-carboxylate factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C and S."]},{"h":"Minerals","points":["Potassium (normal about 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L) sets resting membrane potential; both low and high levels cause arrhythmias.","Calcium (normal about 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL) triggers contraction through L-type channels and calcium-induced calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","Magnesium is needed for Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase activity; low magnesium increases renal potassium loss, so hypokalemia is hard to correct, and causes hypocalcemia by impairing PTH release and action.","Iron deficiency anemia causes a high-output, tachycardic state; iron overload (hemochromatosis) causes cardiomyopathy and conduction problems.","Selenium deficiency causes Keshan disease, a cardiomyopathy.","Copper is a cofactor for lysyl oxidase, which cross-links collagen and elastin; deficiency (as in Menkes disease) weakens arteries."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Lipoproteins","cols":["Particle","Made in","Main cargo","Key apolipoproteins","Job"],"rows":[["Chylomicron","Intestine","Dietary triglyceride","B-48, C-II, E","Delivers dietary fat to muscle and adipose tissue; remnant goes to liver"],["VLDL","Liver","Hepatic triglyceride","B-100, C-II, E","Delivers liver-made triglyceride to tissues"],["IDL","From VLDL in plasma","Triglyceride and cholesterol","B-100, E","Taken up by liver or converted to LDL"],["LDL","From IDL in plasma","Cholesteryl ester","B-100","Delivers cholesterol to tissues through the LDL receptor"],["HDL","Liver and intestine","Cholesterol and phospholipid","A-I (also carries C-II and E)","Reverse cholesterol transport; apolipoprotein reservoir"]]},{"title":"Apolipoprotein functions","cols":["Apolipoprotein","Function"],"rows":[["A-I","Activates LCAT"],["B-48","Needed to assemble and secrete chylomicrons"],["B-100","Binds the LDL receptor"],["C-II","Cofactor for lipoprotein lipase"],["E","Mediates uptake of chylomicron remnants and IDL by the liver"]]},{"title":"Electrolytes and the ECG","cols":["Disturbance","ECG findings","Risk"],"rows":[["Hypokalemia","Flattened T waves, U waves","Arrhythmias; raises digoxin toxicity"],["Hyperkalemia","Peaked T waves, wide QRS, loss of P waves, sine wave pattern","Ventricular fibrillation or asystole"],["Hypocalcemia","Prolonged QT","Arrhythmias, tetany"],["Hypercalcemia","Shortened QT","Arrhythmias"],["Hypomagnesemia","Prolonged QT possible","Torsades de pointes; refractory hypokalemia"]]},{"title":"Homocysteine metabolism","cols":["Step","Enzyme","Cofactor needed","Lab clue when deficient"],"rows":[["Homocysteine to methionine","Methionine synthase","Vitamin B12 and folate (as N5-methyl-THF)","B12 deficiency: high homocysteine and high methylmalonic acid. Folate deficiency: high homocysteine, normal methylmalonic acid"],["Homocysteine to cystathionine","Cystathionine beta-synthase","Vitamin B6","High homocysteine; inherited enzyme defect causes homocystinuria"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Thiamine has four enzymes","text":"List them as pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase, and transketolase. All energy production slows without B1, and the heart fails in a high-output pattern."},{"kind":"trick","label":"MMA splits B12 from folate","text":"Both deficiencies raise homocysteine. Only B12 deficiency also raises methylmalonic acid."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Apo letters by job","text":"C-II cuts (activates lipoprotein lipase), E is the entry ticket to the liver for remnants, B-100 docks on the LDL receptor, A-I activates LCAT."}],"highYield":["Wet beriberi: thiamine deficiency causing high-output heart failure.","Apo C-II activates lipoprotein lipase; apo B-100 binds the LDL receptor.","Oxidized LDL taken up by macrophage scavenger receptors forms foam cells.","The resting adult heart burns mostly fatty acids for ATP.","Hypomagnesemia causes refractory hypokalemia and raises the risk of torsades de pointes.","Hyperkalemia shows peaked T waves; hypokalemia shows U waves.","Selenium deficiency causes Keshan cardiomyopathy.","Homocystinuria (cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency) causes arterial and venous thrombosis."],"pitfalls":["LDL receptors on cells are downregulated when cells have enough cholesterol; scavenger receptors on macrophages are not, which is why foam cells overfill.","Lowering homocysteine with B vitamins has not clearly reduced heart attacks in trials, so treat high homocysteine as a marker of risk and a sign of vitamin status.","Thiamine deficiency heart failure is high output with low resistance, unlike the low-output failure of most cardiomyopathies.","Hypocalcemia lengthens QT and hypercalcemia shortens it; potassium changes show up mostly in T and U waves."],"dumps":[{"t":"Lipoprotein pathways","p":"Draw the exogenous and endogenous lipoprotein pathways from the gut and liver to the tissues and back. Label each particle, its main apolipoproteins, and each enzyme or receptor.","l":["Chylomicron from the intestine with B-48, entering blood through lymph","Lipoprotein lipase on capillary endothelium, activated by C-II","Chylomicron remnant taken up by the liver through apo E","VLDL from the liver with B-100","VLDL to IDL to LDL as triglyceride is removed","LDL taken up through the LDL receptor binding B-100","HDL with A-I activating LCAT and returning cholesterol to the liver","CETP swapping cholesteryl ester for triglyceride"]},{"t":"Nutrients the heart needs","p":"Build a table with three columns: nutrient, its biochemical job, and the heart or vessel problem when it is missing or in excess. Include thiamine, B12, folate, B6, vitamin C, potassium, magnesium, iron, and selenium.","l":["Thiamine: TPP for pyruvate dehydrogenase and related enzymes; wet beriberi high-output failure","B12 and folate: methionine synthase; high homocysteine","B6: cystathionine beta-synthase; high homocysteine","Vitamin C: collagen hydroxylation; fragile vessels in scurvy","Potassium: resting membrane potential; arrhythmias when low or high","Magnesium: Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase; torsades and refractory hypokalemia","Iron: deficiency gives high-output state, overload gives cardiomyopathy","Selenium: deficiency gives Keshan cardiomyopathy"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, Lipoprotein, and Steroid Metabolism"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on vitamins (water-soluble and fat-soluble micronutrients)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases, section on nutritional diseases"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Metabolism | Lipoprotein Metabolism | Chylomicrons, VLDL, IDL, LDL, & HDL","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wQY0xpwqPfQ"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Hyperlipidemia | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gE9cIQ9vz0k"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Physiology of Lipoproteins Cholesterol","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PkKH8lTxvzA"}],"related":["CV-5","CV-14","CV-18-I3","CV-18-I4","CV-18-C"]},"CV-14":{"id":"CV-14","summary":"This unit covers single-gene and chromosomal disorders that affect the heart and vessels, plus the gene-expression and cell-cycle ideas behind them. The exam asks you to match a gene or inheritance pattern to a cardiovascular finding and to explain the mechanism.","notes":[{"h":"Genetic principles seen in heart disease","points":["Most single-gene cardiovascular disorders are autosomal dominant (familial hypercholesterolemia, HCM, Marfan, most long QT).","Variable expressivity and incomplete penetrance are common, so relatives with the same variant can look very different.","Locus heterogeneity: one disease, many genes (familial hypercholesterolemia from LDLR, APOB, or PCSK9; long QT from many channel genes).","Pleiotropy: one gene, many organs (fibrillin-1 in Marfan affects aorta, eye, and skeleton).","Gene dosage matters: familial hypercholesterolemia homozygotes are far more severe than heterozygotes.","Mitochondrial DNA disorders can cause cardiomyopathy and conduction block (for example Kearns-Sayre syndrome); heteroplasmy explains variable severity."]},{"h":"Familial hypercholesterolemia","points":["Usually a mutation in the LDL receptor gene; APOB and gain-of-function PCSK9 variants cause the same picture.","Fewer working receptors means less LDL and IDL clearance, so more IDL becomes LDL.","Less cholesterol inside liver cells also takes the brake off cholesterol synthesis.","Heterozygotes have about two to three times normal plasma cholesterol; homozygotes about five to six times, often with MI before age 20.","Signs: tendon xanthomas (often Achilles), xanthelasma, early corneal arcus, premature coronary disease."]},{"h":"Cardiomyopathy and channel genes","points":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy: sarcomere protein genes, most often MYH7 (beta-myosin heavy chain) and MYBPC3 (myosin-binding protein C).","HCM shows myocyte disarray, asymmetric septal thickening, diastolic dysfunction, and a risk of sudden death in young athletes.","Congenital long QT comes from potassium channel loss (KCNQ1, KCNH2) or sodium channel gain (SCN5A), which delays repolarization and invites torsades de pointes.","Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome is autosomal recessive long QT with sensorineural deafness; Romano-Ward syndrome is autosomal dominant without deafness.","Brugada syndrome: loss of function in SCN5A, ST elevation in V1 to V3 with a pseudo-right bundle branch block pattern, and sudden death risk.","Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophy (dystrophin, X-linked) cause dilated cardiomyopathy; Friedreich ataxia (frataxin, GAA repeat) causes HCM."]},{"h":"Connective tissue disorders","points":["Marfan syndrome: FBN1 (fibrillin-1) on chromosome 15; faulty microfibrils weaken elastic tissue and increase TGF-beta signaling.","Marfan cardiovascular findings: aortic root dilation, aortic dissection, aortic regurgitation, mitral valve prolapse.","Loeys-Dietz syndrome (TGFBR1 or TGFBR2): aggressive aortic aneurysms, arterial tortuosity, bifid uvula, widely spaced eyes.","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (COL3A1, type III collagen): arterial, bowel, and uterine rupture."]},{"h":"Gene expression and the cell cycle in the heart","points":["Adult cardiomyocytes have largely left the cell cycle and renew very slowly, so dead myocardium is replaced by scar.","Under pressure or volume load, cardiomyocytes grow larger (hypertrophy) instead of dividing.","Hypertrophy reactivates fetal genes such as ANP, BNP, and beta-myosin heavy chain.","SREBP senses cell sterol levels and turns up LDL receptor and HMG-CoA reductase genes when cholesterol is low.","Smooth muscle cells in the intima proliferate in response to growth factors such as PDGF in atherosclerosis."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Single-gene cardiovascular disorders","cols":["Disorder","Gene and protein","Inheritance","Key cardiovascular features"],"rows":[["Familial hypercholesterolemia","LDLR (most), APOB, or PCSK9 gain of function","Autosomal dominant","Very high LDL, xanthomas, premature coronary disease"],["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Sarcomere genes, most often MYH7 or MYBPC3","Autosomal dominant","Asymmetric septal hypertrophy, outflow obstruction, sudden death"],["Marfan syndrome","FBN1, fibrillin-1","Autosomal dominant","Aortic root dilation and dissection, mitral valve prolapse"],["Loeys-Dietz syndrome","TGFBR1 or TGFBR2","Autosomal dominant","Aortic aneurysm and dissection, arterial tortuosity"],["Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","COL3A1, type III collagen","Autosomal dominant","Arterial rupture"],["Duchenne muscular dystrophy","DMD, dystrophin","X-linked recessive","Dilated cardiomyopathy"],["Friedreich ataxia","FXN, frataxin (GAA repeat)","Autosomal recessive","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],["Homocystinuria (classic)","CBS, cystathionine beta-synthase","Autosomal recessive","Arterial and venous thrombosis"]]},{"title":"Congenital long QT types","cols":["Type","Gene","Channel effect","Classic trigger"],"rows":[["LQT1","KCNQ1","Loss of slow delayed rectifier K⁺ current (IKs)","Exercise, especially swimming"],["LQT2","KCNH2 (hERG)","Loss of rapid delayed rectifier K⁺ current (IKr)","Sudden loud noise or emotional stress"],["LQT3","SCN5A","Gain of function: persistent late Na⁺ current","Rest or sleep"]]},{"title":"Chromosomal syndromes and their heart lesions","cols":["Syndrome","Genetic cause","Typical cardiovascular lesion"],"rows":[["Down syndrome","Trisomy 21","Atrioventricular septal defect (endocardial cushion defect)"],["Turner syndrome","45,X","Bicuspid aortic valve, coarctation of the aorta"],["22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge)","Microdeletion at 22q11.2","Conotruncal defects: truncus arteriosus, tetralogy of Fallot"],["Williams syndrome","7q11.23 microdeletion including the elastin gene","Supravalvular aortic stenosis"],["Noonan syndrome","Often PTPN11","Pulmonary valve stenosis, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CATCH-22","text":"22q11.2 deletion: Cleft palate, Abnormal facies, Thymic aplasia, Cardiac defects (conotruncal), Hypocalcemia from parathyroid aplasia, on chromosome 22."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Lens goes up in Marfan, down in homocystinuria","text":"Both can look tall and thin with a dislocated lens. Marfan lenses usually shift up and out; homocystinuria lenses shift down and in, and homocystinuria adds clots."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Receptor down, LDL up","text":"In familial hypercholesterolemia, picture the liver with half its LDL receptors. LDL piles up in plasma, and the cell, sensing too little cholesterol inside, keeps making more."}],"highYield":["Familial hypercholesterolemia is autosomal dominant; homozygotes can have MI before age 20.","MYH7 and MYBPC3 are the most common HCM genes; inheritance is autosomal dominant.","Marfan syndrome is caused by FBN1 mutations; aortic dissection is the main cause of death.","Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome: long QT plus sensorineural deafness, autosomal recessive.","Down syndrome is most linked to atrioventricular septal defects.","Turner syndrome is linked to bicuspid aortic valve and coarctation.","Williams syndrome causes supravalvular aortic stenosis through elastin gene loss.","Hypertrophied myocytes switch back on fetal genes such as ANP, BNP, and beta-myosin heavy chain."],"pitfalls":["Marfan (FBN1) and Loeys-Dietz (TGFBR1 or TGFBR2) both cause aortic aneurysm; Loeys-Dietz adds bifid uvula, widely spaced eyes, and arterial tortuosity.","Long QT from KCNQ1 and KCNH2 is a loss of potassium current, while SCN5A long QT is a gain of sodium current; Brugada is a loss of SCN5A function.","Cardiomyocytes respond to load by hypertrophy, while vascular smooth muscle cells can still proliferate.","Gain-of-function PCSK9 raises LDL; loss-of-function PCSK9 lowers it, because PCSK9 sends LDL receptors to be degraded."],"dumps":[{"t":"Single-gene heart disorders","p":"Build a table on blank paper for familial hypercholesterolemia, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, Marfan syndrome, congenital long QT, and vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome. Give the gene, protein, inheritance, and main cardiovascular finding for each.","l":["Familial hypercholesterolemia: LDLR (or APOB, PCSK9), autosomal dominant, premature coronary disease","HCM: MYH7 or MYBPC3, autosomal dominant, asymmetric septal hypertrophy and sudden death","Marfan: FBN1, fibrillin-1, autosomal dominant, aortic root dilation and dissection","Long QT: KCNQ1, KCNH2, or SCN5A, mostly autosomal dominant, torsades de pointes","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos: COL3A1, type III collagen, autosomal dominant, arterial rupture","Notes variable expressivity or incomplete penetrance in at least one","Notes that FH homozygotes are much more severe than heterozygotes"]},{"t":"LDL receptor cycle","p":"Draw a liver cell taking up LDL through its receptor. Show what happens to the receptor and to cholesterol inside the cell. Then redraw it for a person with familial hypercholesterolemia.","l":["LDL apo B-100 binds the LDL receptor in clathrin-coated pits","Receptor and LDL are endocytosed and separate in the endosome","Receptor recycles to the cell surface","LDL is broken down in the lysosome, releasing free cholesterol","Free cholesterol lowers SREBP activity, reducing HMG-CoA reductase and LDL receptor synthesis","PCSK9 directs receptors to degradation","In FH, fewer working receptors means less LDL and IDL clearance and more cholesterol synthesis","Result: heterozygotes about two to three times normal cholesterol, homozygotes about five to six times"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic Disorders"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on cardiomyopathies"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, Lipoprotein, and Steroid Metabolism"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy - signs and symptoms, causes, pathophysiology, treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lqn71jvMhQM"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy: Pathophysiology and diagnosis | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VtqrKxJmBn4"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiomyopathy | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Miz9kvrrqk"}],"related":["CV-13","CV-18-E1","CV-18-G","CV-18-I2","CV-18-I4"]},"CV-15":{"id":"CV-15","summary":"The right heart pumps its whole output through the lungs, and the left heart receives it back, so the two organs work as one circuit. Exam items test how the pulmonary circulation differs from the systemic one, how breathing changes venous return, and how disease in one organ overloads the other.","notes":[{"h":"Two circuits in series","points":["Right and left ventricular outputs are equal over time (about 5 L/min at rest).","Pulmonary artery pressure is about 25/8 mmHg (mean about 15 mmHg); systemic is about 120/80 mmHg.","Pulmonary vascular resistance is much lower than systemic, roughly one tenth.","Pulmonary capillary pressure is about 7 mmHg, which keeps fluid out of the alveoli.","Bronchial arteries (systemic) feed the airways; part of their venous blood drains into pulmonary veins, a small normal shunt."]},{"h":"How pulmonary vessels are controlled","points":["Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction: low alveolar O2 constricts nearby arterioles and sends blood to better ventilated alveoli.","Systemic arterioles do the opposite and dilate in hypoxia.","Chronic global hypoxia (COPD, high altitude) constricts vessels throughout the lung and causes pulmonary hypertension.","When cardiac output rises, closed capillaries open (recruitment) and open ones widen (distension), so resistance falls.","Resistance is lowest near FRC; at high lung volume alveolar vessels are stretched and compressed, at low volume extra-alveolar vessels narrow."]},{"h":"Gravity and the zones of the lung","points":["Upright, the base gets more blood flow and more ventilation than the apex; flow rises more steeply.","V/Q is about 3 at the apex and about 0.6 at the base; the whole-lung average is about 0.8.","Zone 1 (no flow) is normally absent; it appears with low arterial pressure (hemorrhage) or high alveolar pressure (positive pressure ventilation)."]},{"h":"Breathing changes the heart","points":["Inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure, raises venous return to the right heart, and raises right ventricular stroke volume.","At the same time pulmonary veins hold more blood, so left ventricular filling and systolic pressure dip slightly (under 10 mmHg).","A drop over 10 mmHg is pulsus paradoxus: cardiac tamponade, severe asthma, or COPD.","Inspiration delays pulmonic valve closure, which gives normal (physiologic) splitting of S2.","Positive pressure ventilation raises intrathoracic pressure, lowers venous return, and can lower cardiac output."]},{"h":"When one organ fails, the other is loaded","points":["Left heart failure or mitral stenosis raises left atrial and pulmonary capillary pressure and causes pulmonary edema.","Alveolar edema appears once pulmonary capillary pressure exceeds plasma colloid osmotic pressure (about 28 mmHg).","Chronic lung disease or hypoxia raises pulmonary artery pressure and causes right ventricular hypertrophy and failure (cor pulmonale).","A large pulmonary embolus suddenly raises right ventricular afterload (acute cor pulmonale).","A right-to-left shunt sends venous blood past the lungs, so hypoxemia barely improves with 100% O2."]},{"h":"The lung as a metabolic organ","points":["ACE on pulmonary capillary endothelium converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II.","The same enzyme inactivates bradykinin.","The pulmonary capillary bed filters small clots and debris from venous blood before it reaches the systemic arteries."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Pulmonary vs systemic circulation","cols":["Feature","Pulmonary circulation","Systemic circulation"],"rows":[["Arterial pressure","About 25/8 mmHg (mean about 15)","About 120/80 mmHg (mean about 93)"],["Resistance","Low","High (roughly 10 times pulmonary)"],["Artery wall","Thin, little smooth muscle, very compliant","Thick muscular media"],["Capillary hydrostatic pressure","About 7 mmHg","About 17 mmHg (average functional value)"],["Response to local hypoxia","Vasoconstriction (matches flow to ventilation)","Vasodilation (matches flow to tissue need)"],["Flow","Entire right ventricular output","Entire left ventricular output, shared among organs"]]},{"title":"Zones of the upright lung","cols":["Zone","Pressure order","Blood flow","Where"],"rows":[["Zone 1","Alveolar > arterial > venous","None (alveolar dead space)","Apex, only if arterial pressure falls or alveolar pressure rises"],["Zone 2","Arterial > alveolar > venous","Intermittent, set by arterial minus alveolar pressure","Upper and middle lung"],["Zone 3","Arterial > venous > alveolar","Continuous, set by arterial minus venous pressure","Lower lung (base)"]]},{"title":"Dead space vs shunt","cols":["Feature","Dead space","Shunt"],"rows":[["V/Q","High (ventilation with no perfusion)","Zero (perfusion with no ventilation)"],["Examples","Pulmonary embolism, zone 1","Atelectasis, lobar pneumonia, pulmonary edema, right-to-left cardiac shunt"],["Effect of 100% O2","Hypoxemia usually improves","Hypoxemia improves very little"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Hypoxia flips in the lung","text":"Everywhere else, low O2 dilates arterioles to bring in more blood. In the lung, low O2 constricts them so blood goes where the air is."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Alveolar pressure walks down the line","text":"Write arterial, venous, alveolar. Zone 1 puts alveolar first, zone 2 puts it second, zone 3 puts it last."},{"kind":"trick","label":"The 100% O2 test","text":"If extra oxygen fixes the hypoxemia, think V/Q mismatch or diffusion. If it barely helps, think shunt."}],"highYield":["Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction is the main local control of pulmonary blood flow.","Chronic hypoxic lung disease leads to pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale.","V/Q is highest at the apex and lowest at the base.","Inspiration raises right heart filling and splits S2.","Pulsus paradoxus over 10 mmHg points to cardiac tamponade or severe obstructive lung disease.","Pulmonary ACE makes angiotensin II and breaks down bradykinin.","A right-to-left shunt causes hypoxemia that 100% O2 barely corrects."],"pitfalls":["Cor pulmonale means right heart disease caused by lung disease or pulmonary vessels; right heart failure from left heart failure does not count.","Pulmonary embolism is a dead-space (high V/Q) problem in the blocked area, yet hypoxemia comes from low V/Q in the overperfused areas.","Both blood flow and ventilation are greater at the base; V/Q is lower there because flow increases more than ventilation."],"dumps":[{"t":"Lung zones","p":"Draw an upright lung. Mark zones 1, 2, and 3, write the pressure order for each, and label where V/Q is highest and lowest.","l":["Zone 1: alveolar > arterial > venous, no flow","Zone 2: arterial > alveolar > venous, flow set by arterial minus alveolar pressure","Zone 3: arterial > venous > alveolar, continuous flow","Zone 1 normally absent; appears with hemorrhage or positive pressure ventilation","V/Q about 3 at apex","V/Q about 0.6 at base","Flow and ventilation both rise toward the base, flow more steeply"]},{"t":"From a sick lung to a failing right heart","p":"Trace the steps from chronic COPD to right heart failure. Then list the signs you would find on exam.","l":["Alveolar hypoxia","Widespread hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","Vessel remodeling (medial hypertrophy) raises pulmonary vascular resistance","Pulmonary hypertension raises right ventricular afterload","Right ventricular hypertrophy, then dilation and failure (cor pulmonale)","Raised jugular venous pressure","Hepatomegaly and peripheral pitting edema","Loud P2 and right ventricular heave"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Circulation, Pulmonary Edema, Pleural Fluid"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Principles of Gas Exchange; Diffusion of Oxygen and Carbon Dioxide Through the Respiratory Membrane (section on ventilation-perfusion ratio)"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Pulmonary Disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Respiratory | External Respiration: Ventilation Perfusion Coupling","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yUtngVm93-c"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Respiratory System Physiology - Ventilation and Perfusion (V:Q Ratio) Physiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mL_NQ3pKnA"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SJ1gu_WRx5o"}],"related":["CV-11","CV-18-A1","CV-18-B2","CV-18-I7"]},"CV-16":{"id":"CV-16","summary":"Many heart and vessel diseases are driven by the immune system: atherosclerosis is chronic inflammation, rheumatic fever is molecular mimicry, and most vasculitis is antibody, immune complex, or T-cell injury. The exam asks you to match each disease to its hypersensitivity type and immune cell.","notes":[{"h":"Atherosclerosis is chronic inflammation","points":["Injured endothelium expresses VCAM-1 and recruits monocytes and T cells into the intima.","LDL trapped in the intima is oxidized; macrophages take it up through scavenger receptors and become foam cells.","Scavenger receptors are not shut off by cell cholesterol, so foam cells keep filling.","Cholesterol crystals activate the NLRP3 inflammasome, which releases IL-1 beta.","Th1 cells release IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages; PDGF draws smooth muscle cells in to build the fibrous cap.","Macrophage metalloproteinases thin the cap and make the plaque prone to rupture.","C-reactive protein (hs-CRP) is a liver-made marker of this inflammation and predicts cardiac risk."]},{"h":"Rheumatic fever: molecular mimicry","points":["Follows group A streptococcal pharyngitis by about 2 to 3 weeks.","Antibodies and T cells against the streptococcal M protein cross-react with heart antigens such as cardiac myosin.","Review books classify it as type II hypersensitivity; CD4 T cells also drive the valve damage.","Acute lesion: Aschoff bodies with Anitschkow (caterpillar) cells; pancarditis.","ASO and anti-DNase B titers show the recent strep infection."]},{"h":"Vasculitis by immune mechanism","points":["Immune complex (type III): polyarteritis nodosa (often hepatitis B), IgA vasculitis, cryoglobulinemic vasculitis (hepatitis C), lupus vasculitis.","ANCA-associated (few immune deposits): granulomatosis with polyangiitis (PR3, c-ANCA), microscopic polyangiitis and eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (MPO, p-ANCA).","ANCA bind neutrophil enzymes on primed neutrophils and trigger degranulation that injures the vessel wall.","Anti-GBM disease is type II: antibodies to type IV collagen in glomerular and alveolar basement membranes.","T-cell granulomatous arteritis (type IV): giant cell arteritis and Takayasu arteritis."]},{"h":"Heart transplant rejection","points":["Hyperacute: preformed antibodies (ABO or HLA) bind graft endothelium within minutes, causing thrombosis.","Acute cellular: CD8 and CD4 T cells attack the myocardium, weeks to months after transplant.","Acute antibody-mediated: antibodies to donor HLA injure graft vessels.","Chronic: cardiac allograft vasculopathy, a diffuse concentric intimal thickening of coronary arteries."]},{"h":"Other immune heart disease","points":["Viral myocarditis: direct viral injury, then a T-cell response that can lead to dilated cardiomyopathy.","Hypersensitivity myocarditis: drug reaction with eosinophils in the myocardium.","Dressler syndrome: autoimmune pericarditis weeks after MI, against myocardial antigens released by necrosis.","Libman-Sacks endocarditis in lupus: sterile vegetations on both sides of the valve, usually mitral.","Maternal anti-Ro (SSA) antibodies cross the placenta and cause congenital heart block.","Rare myocarditis after mRNA COVID-19 vaccines, mostly in young males within days of a dose."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hypersensitivity types in cardiovascular disease","cols":["Type","Mechanism","Cardiovascular examples"],"rows":[["I (immediate)","IgE on mast cells; histamine causes vasodilation and capillary leak","Anaphylactic shock"],["II (antibody to fixed antigen)","IgG or IgM binds cell or matrix antigens; complement and phagocytes injure tissue","Acute rheumatic fever, hyperacute rejection, anti-GBM disease"],["III (immune complex)","Antigen-antibody complexes deposit in vessel walls, fix complement, draw neutrophils","Polyarteritis nodosa, IgA vasculitis, cryoglobulinemic vasculitis, serum sickness, lupus vasculitis"],["IV (T-cell mediated)","CD4 Th1 cells activate macrophages; CD8 cells kill target cells","Acute cellular rejection, giant cell arteritis, Takayasu arteritis"]]},{"title":"Heart transplant rejection","cols":["Type","Timing","Main mechanism","Finding in the graft"],"rows":[["Hyperacute","Minutes to hours","Preformed anti-ABO or anti-HLA antibodies, complement","Graft thrombosis and ischemic necrosis"],["Acute cellular","Weeks to months","Donor-reactive CD8 and CD4 T cells","Lymphocytic infiltrate with myocyte damage"],["Acute antibody-mediated","Weeks to months","New antibodies to donor HLA","Capillary injury with complement (C4d) deposits"],["Chronic","Months to years","T cells, antibodies, and cytokines acting on vessel walls","Cardiac allograft vasculopathy (concentric intimal thickening)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ACID","text":"Hypersensitivity types I to IV: Anaphylactic and Atopic (I), Cytotoxic antibody-mediated (II), Immune complex (III), Delayed cell-mediated (IV)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Throat vs skin","text":"Rheumatic fever follows strep throat only. Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis can follow throat or skin infection."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Rejection clock","text":"Minutes means preformed antibody, weeks means T cells, years means vessel narrowing."}],"highYield":["Rheumatic fever: M protein antibodies cross-react with cardiac myosin (molecular mimicry).","Aschoff bodies are the hallmark of acute rheumatic carditis.","Foam cells form through scavenger receptor uptake of oxidized LDL.","Polyarteritis nodosa is linked to hepatitis B and spares the lungs.","c-ANCA (PR3) points to granulomatosis with polyangiitis; p-ANCA (MPO) to microscopic polyangiitis or EGPA.","Chronic heart transplant rejection appears as accelerated coronary artery narrowing.","Maternal anti-Ro antibodies cause neonatal complete heart block."],"pitfalls":["Rheumatic fever vegetations are small and sterile along the closure line; infective endocarditis vegetations are large, bulky, and contain organisms.","Dressler syndrome comes weeks after MI and is autoimmune; fibrinous pericarditis in the first few days comes from the necrosis itself.","ANCA vasculitis is called pauci-immune because few immune deposits are seen, which separates it from immune complex vasculitis."],"dumps":[{"t":"Hypersensitivity table","p":"On blank paper, build a four-row table for hypersensitivity types I to IV. For each, write the immune effector and two cardiovascular examples.","l":["Type I: IgE and mast cells; anaphylactic shock","Type II: IgG or IgM against fixed antigens","Type II examples: rheumatic fever, hyperacute rejection, anti-GBM","Type III: immune complexes deposit in vessel walls","Type III examples: polyarteritis nodosa, IgA vasculitis, serum sickness","Type IV: T cells and macrophages","Type IV examples: acute cellular rejection, giant cell arteritis"]},{"t":"Plaque as inflammation","p":"Draw a coronary artery wall and show how a plaque forms. Label each immune cell and molecule in the order it acts.","l":["Endothelial injury and VCAM-1 expression","Monocytes enter the intima and become macrophages","LDL is oxidized in the intima","Scavenger receptor uptake makes foam cells","IL-1 and the NLRP3 inflammasome","Th1 cells and IFN-gamma","PDGF draws smooth muscle cells to form the fibrous cap","Metalloproteinases thin the cap and promote rupture"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity: Disorders Caused by Immune Responses"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on transplant rejection"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels (sections on atherosclerosis and vasculitis)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (section on rheumatic fever and rheumatic heart disease)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Hypersensitivity Type II (Cytotoxic reaction) - causes, pathophysiology, complement system","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Em0Wi07dj4E"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Hypersensitivity Type III (Immune complex hypersensitivity) - causes,  pathophysiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NKnAXcM5Ly0"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Vasculitis | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xHRwhK835QE"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Rheumatic Fever & Heart Disease | Pathology | Dr Najeeb🫀","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ivIE8ARRIgM"}],"related":["CV-18-D5","CV-18-I3","CV-18-I12","CV-18-E2","CV-17"]},"CV-17":{"id":"CV-17","summary":"This unit covers the organisms behind the listed cardiovascular infections: the bacteria of infective endocarditis, Trypanosoma cruzi, Borrelia burgdorferi, Rickettsia rickettsii, the hemorrhagic fever viruses, and the myocarditis viruses. Questions pair each organism with its shape, vector, life cycle, and the virulence factor that explains the disease.","notes":[{"h":"Infective endocarditis bacteria","points":["Staphylococcus aureus: gram-positive cocci in clusters, catalase and coagulase positive; acute endocarditis on normal valves and in IV drug use.","S. aureus virulence: protein A binds the Fc end of IgG; clumping factor and fibronectin-binding proteins help it stick to valves.","Viridans streptococci: alpha-hemolytic, optochin resistant; subacute endocarditis on damaged valves after dental work.","Viridans strep make dextran, which binds fibrin-platelet clumps on damaged valves.","Enterococcus faecalis: grows in 6.5% NaCl and bile; endocarditis after GI or GU procedures.","Staphylococcus epidermidis: coagulase negative, makes biofilm on prosthetic valves and devices.","Streptococcus gallolyticus (formerly S. bovis) endocarditis is linked to colon cancer."]},{"h":"Trypanosoma cruzi (Chagas disease)","points":["Flagellated protozoan; spread by triatomine (reduviid, kissing) bugs in Latin America.","The bug defecates while feeding; trypomastigotes in the feces enter the bite wound or the conjunctiva (Romana sign).","Also spread by blood transfusion, organ transplant, mother to fetus, and contaminated food.","Trypomastigotes circulate in blood and do not divide; amastigotes multiply inside heart muscle, smooth muscle, and macrophages.","Chronic disease years later: dilated cardiomyopathy with apical aneurysm and conduction block, megaesophagus, and megacolon."]},{"h":"Borrelia burgdorferi (Lyme disease)","points":["Spirochete; stains poorly with Gram stain, seen with Giemsa or Wright stain.","Vector: Ixodes ticks (black-legged deer tick); white-footed mouse is the main reservoir; deer feed the adult ticks.","Transmission usually needs about 36 to 48 hours of tick attachment.","Switches outer surface proteins: OspA in the tick gut, OspC when it moves to the host.","Antigenic variation of the VlsE surface protein helps it avoid antibodies.","Unusual genome: a linear chromosome plus many linear and circular plasmids.","Lyme carditis (early disseminated stage) causes AV block of varying degree."]},{"h":"Rickettsia rickettsii (Rocky Mountain spotted fever)","points":["Small gram-negative obligate intracellular bacterium; cannot make enough of its own CoA and NAD+.","Vector: Dermacentor ticks (American dog tick, Rocky Mountain wood tick); most US cases are in the southeast and south-central states.","Infects vascular endothelial cells and spreads cell to cell using host actin.","Endothelial infection causes vasculitis, leaky vessels, edema, low blood pressure, and thrombocytopenia.","Rash starts on wrists and ankles, spreads to the trunk, and involves palms and soles."]},{"h":"Viral hemorrhagic fever viruses","points":["Yellow fever and dengue: flaviviruses (enveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA), spread by Aedes aegypti mosquitoes.","Yellow fever damages hepatocytes (Councilman bodies) and causes jaundice and bleeding; a live attenuated vaccine prevents it.","Dengue has four serotypes; a second infection with a different serotype raises the risk of severe dengue through antibody-dependent enhancement.","Ebola and Marburg: filoviruses (enveloped, filamentous, negative-sense single-stranded RNA); fruit bats are the likely reservoir; people spread it through body fluids.","Filoviruses infect macrophages, dendritic cells, and endothelium; Ebola VP35 blocks type I interferon.","Common end path: endothelial leak, cytokine storm, and consumptive coagulopathy (DIC)."]},{"h":"Myocarditis viruses","points":["Coxsackie B virus (enterovirus, picornavirus family) is the classic cause; spread mainly fecal-oral.","Coxsackie B enters cardiomyocytes through the coxsackie-adenovirus receptor (CAR); its protease 2A cleaves dystrophin.","Adenovirus uses the same CAR receptor and is common in children.","Parvovirus B19 and HHV-6 are now frequently found in heart biopsies; parvovirus infects endothelial cells."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Infective endocarditis organisms","cols":["Organism","Morphology and lab clues","Typical setting","Key virulence factor or clue"],"rows":[["Staphylococcus aureus","Gram-positive cocci in clusters; catalase and coagulase positive","Acute endocarditis, normal valves, IV drug use (tricuspid)","Protein A, clumping factor, fibronectin-binding proteins"],["Viridans streptococci","Gram-positive cocci in chains; alpha-hemolytic; optochin resistant","Subacute endocarditis on damaged valves after dental work","Dextran binds fibrin-platelet clumps"],["Enterococcus faecalis","Gram-positive cocci in pairs and chains; grows in 6.5% NaCl and bile","After GI or GU procedures; older adults","Intrinsic resistance to many antibiotics"],["Staphylococcus epidermidis","Gram-positive cocci in clusters; coagulase negative","Prosthetic valves and devices","Biofilm"],["Streptococcus gallolyticus","Gram-positive cocci; group D streptococcus; bile esculin positive","Older adults with colorectal neoplasia","Look for a colon tumor"],["HACEK group","Fastidious gram-negative rods","Culture-negative endocarditis","Slow growth delays diagnosis"]]},{"title":"Vector-borne and other systemic cardiovascular pathogens","cols":["Organism","Type and morphology","Vector or route","Life cycle or spread in the body","Cardiovascular effect"],"rows":[["Trypanosoma cruzi","Flagellated protozoan","Triatomine bug feces; transfusion; congenital","Trypomastigote in blood; amastigote divides inside cells","Dilated cardiomyopathy, apical aneurysm, conduction block"],["Borrelia burgdorferi","Spirochete","Ixodes tick","Spreads from bite site through skin and blood","AV block (Lyme carditis)"],["Rickettsia rickettsii","Gram-negative obligate intracellular rod","Dermacentor tick","Infects endothelium; spreads cell to cell by actin","Vasculitis, capillary leak, hypotension"],["Yellow fever virus","Flavivirus, enveloped, positive-sense ssRNA","Aedes aegypti mosquito","Replicates in liver and lymphoid tissue","Hemorrhage, shock"],["Dengue virus","Flavivirus, enveloped, positive-sense ssRNA (4 serotypes)","Aedes mosquitoes","Infects monocytes and dendritic cells","Plasma leakage, shock, bleeding"],["Ebola and Marburg viruses","Filovirus, enveloped, filamentous, negative-sense ssRNA","Bats to humans; body fluids person to person","Infects macrophages, dendritic cells, endothelium","Capillary leak, DIC, shock"]]},{"title":"Common myocarditis viruses","cols":["Virus","Family and genome","Transmission","Notes"],"rows":[["Coxsackie B","Picornavirus (enterovirus); naked, positive-sense ssRNA","Fecal-oral, respiratory","Uses CAR; protease 2A cleaves dystrophin; also causes pericarditis"],["Adenovirus","Adenovirus; naked, double-stranded DNA","Respiratory droplets, fecal-oral","Uses CAR; common cause in children"],["Parvovirus B19","Parvovirus; naked, single-stranded DNA","Respiratory, blood, mother to fetus","Infects endothelial cells and red cell precursors"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"HACEK","text":"Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter (formerly Actinobacillus), Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella: fastidious gram-negative causes of culture-negative endocarditis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Match the procedure to the bug","text":"Dental work brings viridans strep. GI or GU procedures bring Enterococcus. A new prosthetic valve brings S. epidermidis. IV drug use brings S. aureus to the tricuspid valve."},{"kind":"trick","label":"RMSF rash travels inward","text":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever starts at the wrists and ankles and moves to the trunk. Typhus rash does the reverse, starting on the trunk."}],"highYield":["S. aureus is the most common cause of acute endocarditis and of endocarditis in IV drug users.","Viridans strep dextran lets it stick to fibrin-platelet clumps on damaged valves.","S. gallolyticus endocarditis calls for a colonoscopy to look for colon cancer.","Chagas disease: triatomine bug, Latin America, dilated cardiomyopathy plus megaesophagus and megacolon.","Lyme carditis presents as AV block.","R. rickettsii infects endothelium and causes a vasculitis with a rash on palms and soles.","Dengue: secondary infection with a different serotype causes severe disease through antibody-dependent enhancement.","Coxsackie B is the classic cause of viral myocarditis and pericarditis."],"pitfalls":["Viridans strep and S. pneumoniae are both alpha-hemolytic; S. pneumoniae is optochin sensitive and bile soluble, viridans strep is neither.","Ixodes ticks carry Borrelia; Dermacentor ticks carry Rickettsia rickettsii.","Despite the name, most RMSF cases in the US occur in the southeast and south-central states.","Flaviviruses (yellow fever, dengue) are positive-sense RNA; filoviruses (Ebola, Marburg) are negative-sense RNA."],"dumps":[{"t":"Chagas life cycle","p":"Draw the life cycle of Trypanosoma cruzi between the triatomine bug and a human. Label each form and where it lives.","l":["Triatomine bug takes a blood meal","Bug feces contain metacyclic trypomastigotes","Trypomastigotes enter through the bite wound or conjunctiva","Inside host cells they become amastigotes and divide","Cells burst and release trypomastigotes into the blood","A bug takes up trypomastigotes in a blood meal","In the bug gut they become epimastigotes and multiply","Target tissues: heart muscle, GI smooth muscle and nerve plexus"]},{"t":"Endocarditis organism table","p":"Build a table on blank paper for S. aureus, viridans strep, Enterococcus, and S. epidermidis. Include the lab clues, the typical patient, and one virulence factor for each.","l":["S. aureus: clusters, coagulase positive","S. aureus: acute, normal valves, IV drug use","S. aureus: protein A or coagulase","Viridans strep: alpha-hemolytic, optochin resistant","Viridans strep: damaged valves, dental work, dextran","Enterococcus: grows in 6.5% NaCl and bile; GI or GU procedures","S. epidermidis: coagulase negative; prosthetic valve; biofilm"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Staphylococci"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Spirochetes and Other Spiral Microorganisms"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Rickettsia and Related Genera"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Medical Parasitology (section on Trypanosoma cruzi)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Infective Endocarditis | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iaO8110iSzI"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Staphylococcus: Aureus, Epidermidis, Saprophyticus","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6BkqWKOG8E0"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Infective Endocarditis | Cardiac Pathology | Cardiology🫀","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lyiLcpxa2FU"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"What is myocarditis and pericarditis? | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xL98lBuAn1A"}],"related":["CV-18-K","CV-18-D3","CV-18-E2","CV-16"]},"CV-18-A":{"id":"CV-18-A","summary":"Systemic hypertension loads the left ventricle and damages arterioles in the brain, kidney, retina, and heart. Pulmonary hypertension loads the right ventricle and ends in right heart failure. Know the definitions, the main causes, the vessel changes, and the organ damage for each.","notes":[{"h":"Systemic hypertension: definition and causes","points":["ACC/AHA 2017: stage 1 is 130 to 139 or 80 to 89 mmHg; stage 2 is 140/90 mmHg or higher.","Primary (essential) hypertension is about 90 to 95% of cases; it is polygenic with environmental triggers.","Risk factors: older age, family history, obesity, high sodium intake, excess alcohol, inactivity, diabetes.","Secondary causes: renal artery stenosis, kidney disease, primary aldosteronism, pheochromocytoma, Cushing syndrome, coarctation, sleep apnea.","Renal artery stenosis: atherosclerosis in older adults, fibromuscular dysplasia in younger women."]},{"h":"Systemic hypertension: vessel and heart changes","points":["Hyaline arteriolosclerosis: pink glassy arteriole walls from plasma protein leak; seen in chronic hypertension and diabetes.","Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis: onion-skin wall layers, often with fibrinoid necrosis, in severe (malignant) hypertension.","Pressure overload causes concentric left ventricular hypertrophy (sarcomeres added in parallel).","The thick, stiff ventricle fills poorly (diastolic dysfunction), enlarging the left atrium and raising the risk of atrial fibrillation.","Hypertensive emergency: severe pressure (often 180/120 mmHg or higher) with acute organ damage."]},{"h":"Pulmonary hypertension: definition and groups","points":["Mean pulmonary artery pressure over 20 mmHg at rest by right heart catheterization (older cutoff 25 mmHg).","Left heart disease (group 2) is the most common cause.","Pulmonary arterial hypertension (group 1) includes idiopathic, heritable (BMPR2), drug-related, connective tissue disease, HIV, and portal hypertension.","BMPR2 loss-of-function mutations allow smooth muscle proliferation in small pulmonary arteries.","Endothelial imbalance: low NO and prostacyclin, high endothelin-1."]},{"h":"Pulmonary hypertension: pathology and findings","points":["Medial hypertrophy and intimal fibrosis of small arteries; plexiform lesions in severe pulmonary arterial hypertension.","Atherosclerosis can appear in the large pulmonary arteries.","Symptoms: exertional dyspnea, fatigue, chest pain, syncope.","Signs: loud P2, right ventricular heave, tricuspid regurgitation murmur, raised jugular venous pressure, edema.","ECG: right axis deviation and right ventricular hypertrophy."]},{"h":"Complications","points":["Systemic: stroke (including intracerebral hemorrhage from Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms), coronary disease and MI, heart failure.","Systemic: aortic dissection, abdominal aortic aneurysm, chronic kidney disease (nephrosclerosis), retinopathy.","Pulmonary: right ventricular hypertrophy and failure (cor pulmonale), arrhythmias, sudden death."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hypertensive heart diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Definition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Pulmonary hypertension","Mean pulmonary artery pressure over 20 mmHg at rest","Left heart disease, chronic lung disease and hypoxia, chronic thromboembolism, BMPR2 mutation, drugs, connective tissue disease","Vasoconstriction and remodeling of small pulmonary arteries raise resistance and right ventricular afterload","Exertional dyspnea, syncope, loud P2, right ventricular heave, raised JVP, edema","Cor pulmonale, right heart failure, arrhythmia, sudden death"],["Systemic hypertension","130/80 mmHg or higher (stage 1); 140/90 mmHg or higher (stage 2)","Primary in about 90 to 95%; secondary from kidney, adrenal, or vascular disease","Raised cardiac output or peripheral resistance with sodium retention and RAAS activity","Usually silent; S4, concentric LVH, retinal arteriolar narrowing and AV nicking","Stroke, MI, heart failure, aortic dissection, kidney disease, retinopathy"]]},{"title":"Pulmonary hypertension groups (WHO)","cols":["Group","Cause","Examples"],"rows":[["1","Pulmonary arterial hypertension","Idiopathic, heritable (BMPR2), drugs and toxins, systemic sclerosis, HIV, portal hypertension, congenital shunts"],["2","Left heart disease (most common)","Left ventricular failure, mitral or aortic valve disease"],["3","Lung disease or hypoxia","COPD, interstitial lung disease, sleep apnea, high altitude"],["4","Pulmonary artery obstruction","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension"],["5","Unclear or multiple mechanisms","Sarcoidosis, myeloproliferative disorders"]]},{"title":"Arteriolosclerosis in hypertension","cols":["Type","Look","Setting"],"rows":[["Hyaline","Homogeneous pink thickened wall, narrow lumen","Long-standing mild hypertension, diabetes, aging"],["Hyperplastic","Concentric onion-skin layers, often fibrinoid necrosis","Severe (malignant) hypertension"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Concentric means pressure","text":"Pressure overload (hypertension, aortic stenosis) thickens the wall inward. Volume overload (regurgitation) stretches the chamber outward, which is eccentric hypertrophy."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Order of PH groups","text":"List the groups in order: 1 disease of the pulmonary arteries themselves, 2 left heart disease, 3 lung disease or hypoxia, 4 clots blocking the arteries, 5 everything else. Group 2 is the most common."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Onion skin means emergency","text":"Hyaline arterioles mean long, mild pressure. Onion-skin arterioles with fibrinoid necrosis mean malignant hypertension."}],"highYield":["Essential hypertension makes up about 90 to 95% of cases.","Primary aldosteronism is the most common endocrine cause of secondary hypertension: high blood pressure, high aldosterone, low renin, and sometimes low potassium.","Hypertension is the main risk factor for aortic dissection and intracerebral hemorrhage.","Concentric LVH is the heart's response to chronic pressure overload.","BMPR2 mutations cause heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension.","Plexiform lesions mark severe pulmonary arterial hypertension.","The most common cause of pulmonary hypertension is left heart disease."],"pitfalls":["Cor pulmonale is right heart disease from lung or pulmonary vascular disease; pulmonary hypertension from left heart failure is group 2.","Hyaline arteriolosclerosis appears in both hypertension and diabetes; onion-skin change points to malignant hypertension.","Fibromuscular dysplasia (young women, string of beads) and atherosclerosis (older adults, ostial narrowing) both cause renal artery stenosis."],"dumps":[{"t":"Hypertension end-organ map","p":"Draw a body outline. Mark every organ that long-term systemic hypertension damages, and write the lesion found in each.","l":["Brain: ischemic stroke and intracerebral hemorrhage (Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms)","Eye: retinopathy with AV nicking, hemorrhages, cotton wool spots; papilledema if malignant","Heart: concentric LVH, diastolic dysfunction, coronary disease","Aorta: dissection and aneurysm","Kidney: hyaline arteriolosclerosis and nephrosclerosis","Malignant hypertension: hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis with fibrinoid necrosis","Peripheral arteries: atherosclerosis and PAD"]},{"t":"Pulmonary hypertension pathway","p":"Trace how pulmonary arterial hypertension leads to right heart failure, starting with a BMPR2 mutation.","l":["BMPR2 loss of function","Endothelial imbalance: less NO and prostacyclin, more endothelin-1","Smooth muscle proliferation and medial hypertrophy","Intimal fibrosis and plexiform lesions narrow the lumen","Pulmonary vascular resistance rises","Right ventricular hypertrophy (pressure overload)","Right ventricular dilation and failure","Raised JVP, hepatomegaly, peripheral edema"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (section on hypertensive heart disease, systemic and pulmonary)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels (section on hypertensive vascular disease)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Role of the Kidneys in Long-Term Control of Arterial Pressure and in Hypertension: The Integrated System for Arterial Pressure Regulation"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Vascular Disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Hypertension | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sNCtvsY3TS8"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Pulmonary Artery Hypertension | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S8ehpYrnX2w"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"What is hypertension? | Circulatory system diseases | Health & Medicine | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FBoYigdnLKQ"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Pulmonary hypertension symptoms and causes | Respiratory system diseases | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZVl1M3D2B80"}],"related":["CV-15","CV-11","CV-18-B","CV-18-I2"]},"CV-18-B":{"id":"CV-18-B","summary":"Heart failure means the heart cannot pump enough blood for the body's needs, or can only do so at high filling pressures. Left-sided failure backs blood up into the lungs; right-sided failure backs it up into the body's veins. Know the causes, the compensations that turn harmful, and the classic findings on each side.","notes":[{"h":"Left-sided heart failure","points":["Main causes: ischemic heart disease, hypertension, aortic and mitral valve disease, cardiomyopathy.","Blood backs up into the pulmonary veins and capillaries and causes pulmonary congestion and edema.","Symptoms: dyspnea on exertion, orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea.","Signs: basal crackles, S3, displaced apex beat, pleural effusions.","Lungs show hemosiderin-laden macrophages (heart failure cells) from leaked red cells.","Low forward output reduces kidney perfusion and activates RAAS (prerenal azotemia); low brain perfusion causes confusion."]},{"h":"Right-sided heart failure","points":["Most common cause is left-sided heart failure.","Isolated causes: cor pulmonale (lung disease, pulmonary embolism, pulmonary hypertension), right ventricular infarct, tricuspid or pulmonic valve disease.","Blood backs up into the systemic veins: raised JVP, hepatojugular reflux, dependent pitting edema.","Liver: congestive hepatomegaly with nutmeg pattern (centrilobular congestion and necrosis); long-standing cases develop cardiac cirrhosis.","Also ascites, splenomegaly, and pleural and pericardial effusions."]},{"h":"Ejection fraction types","points":["HFrEF: ejection fraction 40% or lower; the ventricle contracts poorly (systolic failure).","HFpEF: ejection fraction 50% or higher; the stiff ventricle fills poorly (diastolic failure).","Ejection fraction 41 to 49% is called mildly reduced."]},{"h":"Compensation that turns harmful","points":["Frank-Starling: more filling stretches fibers and raises stroke volume until the curve flattens.","Sympathetic activation raises heart rate and contractility but increases O2 demand and arrhythmia risk.","RAAS and ADH retain salt and water, which raises preload and causes edema.","Angiotensin II and aldosterone promote fibrosis and remodeling.","Natriuretic peptides (ANP from atria, BNP from ventricles) are released with stretch and partly oppose RAAS; BNP is a diagnostic marker."]},{"h":"Complications","points":["Acute pulmonary edema and cardiogenic shock.","Arrhythmias (atrial fibrillation, ventricular tachycardia) and sudden death.","Mural thrombi in dilated chambers that can embolize.","Cardiorenal syndrome and cardiac cachexia."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Left vs right heart failure","cols":["Condition","Common causes","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Left-sided","Ischemic heart disease, hypertension, aortic or mitral valve disease, cardiomyopathy","Low forward output with back-pressure into pulmonary veins and capillaries","Dyspnea, orthopnea, PND, crackles, S3, pleural effusion, heart failure cells in lung","Pulmonary edema, cardiogenic shock, arrhythmia, mural thrombus, right heart failure"],["Right-sided","Left heart failure (most common), cor pulmonale, right ventricular infarct, tricuspid or pulmonic valve disease","Back-pressure into systemic veins","Raised JVP, hepatojugular reflux, pitting edema, nutmeg liver, ascites; lungs often clear in pure right failure","Cardiac cirrhosis, ascites, renal congestion, low output"]]},{"title":"HFrEF vs HFpEF","cols":["Feature","HFrEF (systolic)","HFpEF (diastolic)"],"rows":[["Ejection fraction","40% or lower","50% or higher"],["Core problem","Weak contraction","Stiff ventricle, poor relaxation and filling"],["Usual causes","MI, dilated cardiomyopathy","Hypertension, older age, obesity, diabetes, hypertrophic or restrictive cardiomyopathy"],["Ventricle shape","Dilated (eccentric hypertrophy)","Thick walled (concentric hypertrophy)"],["Extra heart sound","S3","S4"]]},{"title":"Compensatory mechanisms","cols":["Mechanism","Short-term help","Long-term harm"],"rows":[["Frank-Starling","More stroke volume from more filling","Fails once the curve flattens; congestion"],["Sympathetic activation","Faster rate, stronger contraction, vasoconstriction","Higher O2 demand, arrhythmia, myocyte death"],["RAAS and ADH","Restore volume and blood pressure","Edema, higher afterload, fibrosis"],["Hypertrophy and remodeling","Spreads wall stress over more muscle","Stiffness, ischemia, dilation"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Left goes to the lungs","text":"Left-sided failure backs up into the lungs. Right-sided failure backs up into the rest of the body (neck veins, liver, legs)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"S3 floods, S4 stiffens","text":"S3 comes from blood rushing into an overfilled, dilated ventricle. S4 comes from the atrium kicking against a stiff, thick ventricle."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Why orthopnea","text":"Lying flat returns fluid from the legs to the chest, and a failing left ventricle cannot handle the extra volume, so the lungs congest."}],"highYield":["Left heart failure is the most common cause of right heart failure.","Heart failure cells are hemosiderin-laden macrophages in the alveoli.","Nutmeg liver shows centrilobular congestion from right heart failure.","BNP is released by stretched ventricular myocytes and is used to diagnose heart failure.","Ischemic heart disease is the most common cause of left-sided heart failure.","HFpEF is linked with hypertension and concentric hypertrophy."],"pitfalls":["Heart failure cells are found in the lungs, not in the heart.","Edema in right heart failure is pitting and dependent; lymphedema and myxedema are nonpitting.","A normal ejection fraction does not rule out heart failure; HFpEF has a normal or high ejection fraction."],"dumps":[{"t":"Two sides of failure","p":"Draw the heart and both circulations. Show where blood backs up in left-sided and in right-sided failure, and label the organ finding at each spot.","l":["Left ventricle fails and left atrial pressure rises","Pulmonary venous and capillary congestion","Pulmonary edema and heart failure cells","Pulmonary hypertension then loads the right ventricle","Right ventricle fails and systemic venous pressure rises","Raised JVP and hepatojugular reflux","Nutmeg liver and ascites","Dependent pitting edema"]},{"t":"Compensation loop","p":"Trace what happens after cardiac output falls in heart failure. Show each compensatory system and how it worsens the failure over time.","l":["Low cardiac output lowers blood pressure and kidney perfusion","Baroreceptors activate sympathetic nerves","Renin release activates angiotensin II and aldosterone","ADH release adds water retention","Salt and water retention raise preload and cause edema","Vasoconstriction raises afterload","Angiotensin II and aldosterone cause fibrosis and remodeling","BNP rises with stretch and partly opposes RAAS"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Failure"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (section on heart failure)"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Congestive Heart Failure | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Gsu8NT1yYes"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Heart failure with reduced and preserved ejection fraction, pathophysiology and treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ItKGeEbzAis"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Heart Failure | CHF | Congestive Heart Failure | Pathophysiology🫀","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xi39GGJA16E"}],"related":["CV-6","CV-15","CV-18-A","CV-18-C","CV-18-E1"]},"CV-18-C":{"id":"CV-18-C","summary":"Ischemic heart disease happens when coronary blood flow cannot meet myocardial O2 demand, nearly always because of coronary atherosclerosis. It ranges from angina to infarction to slow ischemic heart failure. Expect questions on plaque events, the histologic timeline of an infarct, and which complication appears on which day.","notes":[{"h":"Causes and risk factors","points":["Coronary atherosclerosis causes over 90% of cases.","Risk factors: age, male sex, smoking, hypertension, diabetes, high LDL, low HDL, family history, obesity, inactivity.","Other causes: coronary vasospasm, emboli, vasculitis, and high demand states (tachycardia, LVH, severe aortic stenosis).","The subendocardium is most vulnerable because it is last on the coronary supply line and squeezed hardest in systole."]},{"h":"Angina pectoris","points":["Stable angina: fixed narrowing (about 70% or more) causes chest pain with exertion that eases with rest or nitroglycerin.","Stable angina shows ST depression during pain (subendocardial ischemia).","Unstable angina: plaque disruption with a partial clot causes pain at rest or worsening pain, without troponin rise.","Prinzmetal (variant) angina: coronary vasospasm at rest with transient ST elevation; triggers include smoking and cocaine."]},{"h":"Myocardial infarction","points":["Usual sequence: plaque rupture, platelet adhesion, and a thrombus that blocks the artery.","Irreversible myocyte injury begins after about 20 to 40 minutes of severe ischemia.","Transmural infarct (often STEMI) spans the full wall; subendocardial infarct (often NSTEMI) is limited to the inner third to half.","Artery involved: LAD about 40 to 50%, RCA about 30 to 40%, left circumflex about 15 to 20%.","ECG leads: LAD in V1 to V4, left circumflex in I, aVL, V5, V6, RCA in II, III, aVF.","Troponin rises within about 3 to 4 hours and stays elevated about 7 to 10 days; CK-MB returns to normal in about 2 to 3 days, so it helps detect reinfarction.","Reperfusion injury: contraction band necrosis and free radical damage when flow returns."]},{"h":"Chronic ischemic heart disease","points":["Progressive heart failure from ischemic myocardial damage, also called ischemic cardiomyopathy.","Usually follows one or more healed infarcts; scars replace muscle and the remaining muscle hypertrophies.","The left ventricle dilates; diffuse subendocardial myocyte vacuolization and fibrosis are seen.","Complications: progressive heart failure, arrhythmias, sudden cardiac death."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Ischemic heart disease compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Angina pectoris","Coronary atherosclerosis; vasospasm in variant angina","Transient ischemia without myocyte death","Pressure-like chest pain lasting minutes; ST depression (stable) or transient ST elevation (variant)","Progression to unstable angina or MI"],["Chronic ischemic heart disease","Prior MIs and long-standing coronary disease","Loss of muscle with scarring, hypertrophy of the rest, and chamber dilation","Slowly worsening heart failure, enlarged dilated left ventricle","Heart failure, arrhythmia, sudden cardiac death"],["Myocardial infarction (MI)","Plaque rupture with thrombus; same risk factors as atherosclerosis","Prolonged ischemia causes coagulative necrosis","Crushing chest pain over 20 minutes, diaphoresis, ST elevation or depression, rising troponin","Arrhythmia, shock, rupture, aneurysm, mural thrombus, pericarditis"]]},{"title":"Types of angina","cols":["Type","Mechanism","When pain occurs","ECG during pain","Troponin"],"rows":[["Stable","Fixed stenosis about 70% or more","Exertion; eases with rest","ST depression","Normal"],["Unstable","Plaque disruption with nonocclusive thrombus","At rest or with less effort; worsening","ST depression or T wave inversion","Normal"],["Prinzmetal (variant)","Coronary vasospasm","At rest, often early morning","Transient ST elevation","Normal"]]},{"title":"Timeline of an infarct","cols":["Time","Gross","Microscopic","Main risk"],"rows":[["0 to 4 hours","No change","Wavy fibers at the border may appear","Ventricular arrhythmia"],["4 to 24 hours","Dark mottling","Early coagulative necrosis, edema, contraction bands if reperfused","Arrhythmia, heart failure, shock"],["1 to 3 days","Mottled with yellow-tan center","Neutrophils enter, necrosis advances","Fibrinous pericarditis"],["3 to 7 days","Yellow-tan, soft, hyperemic border","Macrophages clear dead cells","Free wall, septal, or papillary muscle rupture"],["1 to 2 weeks","Red-gray depressed border","Granulation tissue","Rupture risk still present"],["2 weeks to 2 months","Gray-white scar","Dense collagen scar","Ventricular aneurysm, mural thrombus, Dressler syndrome"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Cells arrive in order","text":"After an MI the cells arrive in the same order as in any acute inflammation: neutrophils first (days 1 to 3), macrophages next (days 3 to 7), then granulation tissue and scar."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Soft wall, rupture risk","text":"Rupture is most likely when macrophages have eaten the dead muscle and no scar has formed yet, roughly days 3 to 14."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Why the posteromedial papillary muscle","text":"The posteromedial papillary muscle usually has a single supply (posterior descending artery), so an inferior MI can rupture it and cause acute mitral regurgitation."}],"highYield":["Ventricular fibrillation is the main cause of death in the first hours after MI.","LAD occlusion is the most common cause of MI and affects the anterior wall and septum.","Contraction band necrosis is a sign of reperfusion.","Free wall rupture causes sudden cardiac tamponade; septal rupture causes a new left-to-right shunt.","Papillary muscle rupture causes acute mitral regurgitation and pulmonary edema.","Dressler syndrome is autoimmune pericarditis weeks after MI.","Troponin is the most specific marker of myocyte necrosis."],"pitfalls":["Unstable angina and NSTEMI both have rest pain; only NSTEMI has a troponin rise.","Stable angina shows ST depression; Prinzmetal angina shows transient ST elevation.","A true ventricular aneurysm is a scarred bulging wall; a pseudoaneurysm is a rupture sealed off by pericardium and has a narrow neck.","Early fibrinous pericarditis (days 1 to 3) comes from the necrosis itself; Dressler syndrome is a later immune reaction."],"dumps":[{"t":"Infarct timeline","p":"Draw a timeline from hour 0 to 2 months after an MI. Write the microscopic change and the main complication at each stage.","l":["0 to 4 hours: little visible change; arrhythmia risk highest","4 to 24 hours: coagulative necrosis begins; contraction bands if reperfused","1 to 3 days: neutrophils; fibrinous pericarditis","3 to 7 days: macrophages; rupture risk rises","1 to 2 weeks: granulation tissue","2 weeks to 2 months: collagen scar","Late: ventricular aneurysm, mural thrombus, Dressler syndrome"]},{"t":"Coronary territories","p":"Draw the heart with the LAD, left circumflex, and RCA. Label the walls each supplies and the ECG leads that show each infarct.","l":["LAD: anterior wall and anterior two thirds of septum","LAD infarct: V1 to V4","Left circumflex: lateral wall","Left circumflex infarct: I, aVL, V5, V6","RCA: inferior wall, right ventricle, usually SA and AV nodes","RCA infarct: II, III, aVF","Inferior MI can cause bradycardia or AV block and posteromedial papillary muscle rupture"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (section on ischemic heart disease)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Muscle Blood Flow and Cardiac Output During Exercise; the Coronary Circulation and Ischemic Heart Disease"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Coronary Artery Disease | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=67tUtS3y_GA"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Case Study #18: Myocardial Infarction","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JXJEeCZZdQ0"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Heart attack (myocardial infarction) pathophysiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T_b9U5gn_Zk"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Coronary Artery Disease | Ischemic Heart Disease | Angina🫀","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oQ235E1gvrU"}],"related":["CV-8","CV-7","CV-18-I3","CV-18-B","CV-18-F3"]},"CV-18-D":{"id":"CV-18-D","summary":"Valve disease either narrows a valve (stenosis, a pressure load) or lets blood leak back (regurgitation, a volume load). The exam links each lesion to its cause, its murmur, and its effect on the chambers, and adds the special valve diseases: endocarditis, MVP, rheumatic heart disease, and carcinoid heart disease.","notes":[{"h":"Aortic valve","points":["Aortic stenosis: age-related calcification is most common; bicuspid valve causes it at a younger age; rheumatic disease less often.","AS: pressure overload, concentric LVH; syncope, angina, and dyspnea on exertion; weak delayed pulse (pulsus parvus et tardus).","AS can cause hemolysis with schistocytes and GI bleeding from angiodysplasia (Heyde syndrome).","Aortic regurgitation: aortic root dilation (hypertension, age, Marfan, syphilis), bicuspid valve, endocarditis, rheumatic disease.","AR: volume overload, eccentric LVH; wide pulse pressure and bounding pulses; acute AR from dissection or endocarditis causes sudden failure."]},{"h":"Mitral valve","points":["Mitral stenosis is almost always rheumatic.","MS raises left atrial pressure: atrial enlargement, atrial fibrillation, mural thrombi and emboli, pulmonary hypertension, right heart failure.","A large left atrium can compress the esophagus (dysphagia) or the left recurrent laryngeal nerve (hoarseness).","Mitral regurgitation: MVP, ischemic papillary muscle damage or rupture, left ventricular dilation, rheumatic disease, endocarditis.","MR: volume overload of left atrium and ventricle; S3; atrial fibrillation."]},{"h":"Endocarditis","points":["Infective: acute (S. aureus, normal valves, destructive) or subacute (viridans strep, damaged valves, slower).","Mitral valve is involved most often overall; the tricuspid valve in IV drug use.","Nonbacterial thrombotic (marantic) endocarditis: sterile clots on valves in cancer and hypercoagulable states.","Libman-Sacks endocarditis: sterile vegetations on both valve surfaces in lupus.","Signs: fever, new murmur, Janeway lesions (painless, palms and soles), Osler nodes (painful, fingers), Roth spots, splinter hemorrhages."]},{"h":"Mitral valve prolapse","points":["Myxomatous degeneration: excess proteoglycan in the spongiosa makes leaflets floppy and they balloon into the atrium in systole.","Common (about 2 to 3% of adults); linked to Marfan syndrome, Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, and ADPKD.","Mid-systolic click then a late systolic murmur; standing or Valsalva moves the click earlier.","Usually benign; can lead to mitral regurgitation, endocarditis, arrhythmia, and rarely sudden death."]},{"h":"Rheumatic and carcinoid heart disease","points":["Rheumatic heart disease: repeated rheumatic fever scars valves; commissures fuse and chordae thicken and shorten.","The mitral valve is affected in nearly all cases; mitral plus aortic is next most common.","Classic chronic result is mitral stenosis with a fish-mouth valve.","Carcinoid heart disease: serotonin-secreting neuroendocrine tumor with liver metastases lets serotonin reach the right heart.","Plaque-like fibrous thickening of the right heart valves and endocardium causes tricuspid regurgitation and pulmonic stenosis.","The left heart is usually spared because the lungs break down serotonin (monoamine oxidase); urine 5-HIAA is high."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Valvular heart diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Aortic stenosis/insufficiency","AS: calcific aging, bicuspid valve, rheumatic. AI: aortic root dilation, bicuspid valve, endocarditis, rheumatic","AS: pressure overload, concentric LVH. AI: volume overload, eccentric LVH","AS: syncope, angina, dyspnea, slow weak pulse. AI: wide pulse pressure, bounding pulses","Left heart failure, sudden death (AS), hemolysis (AS)"],["Mitral stenosis/insufficiency","MS: rheumatic. MR: MVP, ischemic papillary muscle damage, LV dilation, endocarditis, rheumatic","MS: high left atrial pressure. MR: volume overload of left atrium and ventricle","MS: opening snap, diastolic rumble, dyspnea, hemoptysis. MR: holosystolic apical murmur","Atrial fibrillation, mural thrombus and emboli, pulmonary hypertension, heart failure"],["Endocarditis","Damaged or prosthetic valves, IV drug use, dental and GU procedures, cancer (marantic), lupus (Libman-Sacks)","Vegetations of platelets and fibrin, with organisms in infective endocarditis","Fever, new murmur, Janeway lesions, Osler nodes, Roth spots, splinter hemorrhages","Valve destruction, heart failure, septic emboli, abscess, mycotic aneurysm, glomerulonephritis"],["Mitral valve prolapse (MVP)","Myxomatous degeneration; Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos, ADPKD","Floppy leaflets balloon into the left atrium in systole","Mid-systolic click and late systolic murmur","Mitral regurgitation, endocarditis, arrhythmia, rare sudden death"],["Rheumatic heart disease","Repeated group A strep pharyngitis and rheumatic fever","Molecular mimicry, then valve fibrosis and commissural fusion","Mitral stenosis (fish-mouth valve); history of rheumatic fever","Atrial fibrillation, heart failure, infective endocarditis"],["Carcinoid heart disease","Serotonin-secreting neuroendocrine tumor with liver metastases","Serotonin drives plaque-like fibrosis of right heart valves","Flushing, diarrhea, wheezing, right heart failure; high urine 5-HIAA","Tricuspid regurgitation, pulmonic stenosis, right heart failure"]]},{"title":"Murmurs to know","cols":["Lesion","Timing","Where heard best","Features"],"rows":[["Aortic stenosis","Systolic, crescendo-decrescendo","Right upper sternal border, radiates to carotids","Ejection click possible; soft A2"],["Aortic regurgitation","Early diastolic, decrescendo","Left sternal border","Blowing; wide pulse pressure"],["Mitral stenosis","Mid to late diastolic rumble after an opening snap","Apex","Shorter S2 to snap interval means more severe"],["Mitral regurgitation","Holosystolic","Apex, radiates to axilla","Blowing; S3 possible"],["Mitral valve prolapse","Mid-systolic click, then late systolic murmur","Apex","Standing or Valsalva moves the click earlier"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"SAD","text":"Aortic stenosis symptoms: Syncope, Angina, Dyspnea on exertion."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"J♥NES","text":"Major Jones criteria for rheumatic fever: Joints (migratory polyarthritis), heart (carditis), Nodules (subcutaneous), Erythema marginatum, Sydenham chorea."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"TIPS","text":"Carcinoid heart disease hits the right side: Tricuspid Insufficiency, Pulmonic Stenosis."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Bacteria FROM JANE","text":"Infective endocarditis signs: Bacteremia, Fever, Roth spots, Osler nodes, Murmur, Janeway lesions, Anemia, Nail-bed (splinter) hemorrhages, Emboli."}],"highYield":["Most common cause of aortic stenosis is age-related calcification; bicuspid valve causes it earlier.","Mitral stenosis is almost always rheumatic.","MVP comes from myxomatous degeneration and is linked to Marfan syndrome.","S. aureus causes acute endocarditis; tricuspid involvement suggests IV drug use.","Carcinoid heart disease needs liver metastases and spares the left heart.","Left atrial enlargement in mitral stenosis leads to atrial fibrillation and stroke.","Standing or Valsalva makes the MVP click earlier and the HCM murmur louder."],"pitfalls":["Janeway lesions are painless and from septic emboli; Osler nodes are painful and from immune complexes.","Rheumatic fever vegetations are small and sterile; infective endocarditis vegetations are bulky and full of organisms.","Stenosis is a pressure load (concentric hypertrophy); regurgitation is a volume load (eccentric hypertrophy).","The opening snap belongs to mitral stenosis; the mid-systolic click belongs to MVP."],"dumps":[{"t":"Murmur map","p":"Draw the chest with the four listening areas. For aortic stenosis, aortic regurgitation, mitral stenosis, mitral regurgitation, and MVP, write the timing, location, and one extra clue.","l":["AS: systolic crescendo-decrescendo at right upper sternal border, radiates to carotids","AR: early diastolic decrescendo at left sternal border","AR: wide pulse pressure","MS: opening snap then diastolic rumble at apex","MR: holosystolic at apex, radiates to axilla","MVP: mid-systolic click then late systolic murmur","Standing or Valsalva moves the MVP click earlier"]},{"t":"From strep throat to a stiff valve","p":"Trace the path from group A strep pharyngitis to chronic mitral stenosis and its complications.","l":["Group A strep pharyngitis","M protein antibodies cross-react with heart antigens","Acute rheumatic fever about 2 to 3 weeks later with pancarditis and Aschoff bodies","Repeated attacks scar the valve","Commissural fusion and short, thick chordae make a fish-mouth mitral valve","Left atrial enlargement and atrial fibrillation","Mural thrombus and stroke","Pulmonary hypertension and right heart failure"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (section on valvular heart disease)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Heart Valves and Heart Sounds; Valvular and Congenital Heart Defects"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Valvular Heart Diseases | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KiI9WMXBV_o"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Aortic stenosis and aortic regurgitation | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3t1n5szXriQ"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Mitral valve regurgitation and mitral valve prolapse | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bjnw_jwDt1Q"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Endocarditis | Cardiac Pathology🫀","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Df-FzR7S4SM"}],"related":["CV-4","CV-16","CV-17","CV-18-G1","CV-18-K1"]},"CV-18-E":{"id":"CV-18-E","summary":"Primary myocardial diseases are problems of the heart muscle itself: the three cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive) and myocarditis. Exam questions hinge on telling them apart by chamber shape, systolic versus diastolic failure, and cause.","notes":[{"h":"Dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM)","points":["Most common cardiomyopathy; all four chambers dilate and the walls thin out.","Systolic dysfunction: low ejection fraction, eccentric hypertrophy (sarcomeres added in series).","Causes: familial (often autosomal dominant, TTN titin gene most common), alcohol, prior myocarditis, doxorubicin, peripartum, Chagas, thiamine deficiency.","Findings: signs of heart failure, S3, mitral and tricuspid regurgitation from stretched valve rings, balloon-shaped heart on chest X-ray."]},{"h":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM)","points":["Autosomal dominant sarcomere gene mutations, most often beta-myosin heavy chain (MYH7) or myosin-binding protein C (MYBPC3).","Thick, stiff left ventricle, often asymmetric septal hypertrophy; small cavity; myocyte disarray on histology.","Diastolic dysfunction; ejection fraction normal or high.","Left ventricular outflow obstruction from systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve against the septum.","Classic exam patient: young athlete with exertional syncope or sudden cardiac death from ventricular arrhythmia."]},{"h":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy (RCM)","points":["Stiff ventricle that resists filling; chamber size and systolic function near normal; both atria enlarge.","Causes: amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, hemochromatosis, radiation fibrosis, endomyocardial fibrosis, Loeffler endocarditis.","Amyloid clue: low-voltage ECG despite a thick ventricular wall.","Right-sided failure signs dominate: raised JVP, Kussmaul sign, edema, hepatomegaly."]},{"h":"Myocarditis","points":["Inflammation of the myocardium with myocyte injury.","Most common cause in the US: viruses, classically coxsackie B; also adenovirus, parvovirus B19, HHV-6, SARS-CoV-2.","Other causes: Chagas disease, Lyme disease, diphtheria toxin, drug hypersensitivity, SLE, giant cell myocarditis.","Viral form shows a lymphocytic infiltrate with myocyte necrosis on biopsy.","Presents after a viral illness with chest pain, dyspnea, arrhythmia, raised troponin; can progress to DCM."]},{"h":"How each one fails","points":["DCM: weak squeeze (systolic failure).","HCM and RCM: poor filling (diastolic failure) with preserved ejection fraction.","All three cause arrhythmias and can cause sudden death."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Primary myocardial diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Dilated cardiomyopathy","Familial (TTN), alcohol, prior viral myocarditis, doxorubicin, peripartum, Chagas, wet beriberi, cocaine","Myocyte loss and eccentric hypertrophy; dilated chambers; systolic dysfunction with low EF","Heart failure signs, S3, regurgitant murmurs, balloon-shaped heart on X-ray","Heart failure, arrhythmia, mural thrombus with emboli, sudden death"],["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Autosomal dominant sarcomere mutations (MYH7, MYBPC3); also seen in Friedreich ataxia","Myocyte disarray, thick septum, small stiff LV; diastolic dysfunction; dynamic LV outflow obstruction","S4, systolic murmur that gets louder with Valsalva or standing and softer with squatting; exertional syncope","Sudden cardiac death, atrial fibrillation, heart failure"],["Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, hemochromatosis, radiation, endomyocardial fibrosis, Loeffler endocarditis","Infiltration or fibrosis stiffens the wall; impaired filling with near-normal systolic function","Biatrial enlargement, raised JVP, Kussmaul sign, low-voltage ECG in amyloid","Heart failure (right-sided signs prominent), atrial fibrillation, conduction block"],["Myocarditis","Viruses (coxsackie B classic), Chagas, Lyme, diphtheria, drugs, autoimmune disease","Inflammatory infiltrate with myocyte necrosis; direct viral injury plus T-cell mediated damage","Recent viral illness, chest pain, dyspnea, arrhythmia, raised troponin","Acute heart failure, arrhythmia, sudden death, later dilated cardiomyopathy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ABCCCD (causes of dilated cardiomyopathy)","text":"Alcohol, wet Beriberi, Coxsackie B myocarditis, chronic Cocaine use, Chagas disease, Doxorubicin."},{"kind":"trick","label":"HCM murmur follows LV volume","text":"Anything that shrinks the LV (Valsalva, standing up) pulls the septum and mitral leaflet closer, so the murmur gets louder. Anything that fills the LV (squatting, handgrip, leg raise) makes it softer."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Squeeze versus fill","text":"Ask whether the heart cannot squeeze (DCM, low EF) or cannot fill (HCM and RCM, normal EF). Then use wall thickness and cavity size to split HCM from RCM."}],"highYield":["DCM is the most common cardiomyopathy, about 90 percent of cases.","HCM is a leading cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes.","HCM inheritance is autosomal dominant; screen first-degree relatives.","Amyloid heart: thick walls with low-voltage QRS complexes.","Coxsackie B is the classic viral cause of myocarditis and of later DCM.","Doxorubicin cardiotoxicity is dose-related and causes DCM."],"pitfalls":["HCM versus athlete heart: athlete heart has a normal-sized or larger cavity, normal diastolic function, and regresses with rest from training.","Restrictive cardiomyopathy versus constrictive pericarditis: both impair filling, but constriction shows a thickened or calcified pericardium and a pericardial knock.","Hemochromatosis can cause either a dilated or a restrictive pattern; do not lock it into one box."],"dumps":[{"t":"Three cardiomyopathies side by side","p":"Draw three hearts in cross-section showing dilated, hypertrophic, and restrictive cardiomyopathy. Label wall thickness, cavity size, ejection fraction, and two causes for each.","l":["DCM: thin walls, large cavity, low ejection fraction","HCM: thick (often septal) wall, small cavity, normal or high ejection fraction","RCM: near-normal wall and cavity, enlarged atria, normal or near-normal ejection fraction","DCM causes from ABCCCD or familial TTN","HCM cause: autosomal dominant sarcomere mutation (MYH7 or MYBPC3)","RCM causes: amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, hemochromatosis, radiation","DCM is systolic failure; HCM and RCM are diastolic failure"]},{"t":"HCM outflow obstruction","p":"Trace why the HCM murmur gets louder when the patient stands up from squatting. Start with venous return and end with the murmur.","l":["Standing lowers venous return (preload)","Lower preload means a smaller LV cavity","Smaller cavity brings the thick septum and anterior mitral leaflet closer","Systolic anterior motion of the mitral leaflet narrows the outflow tract","More obstruction gives a louder systolic ejection murmur","Squatting raises preload and afterload and softens the murmur","Mitral regurgitation often accompanies the obstruction"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on cardiomyopathies and myocarditis"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Failure"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiomyopathy | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Miz9kvrrqk"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Dilated cardiomyopathy: Pathophysiology and diagnosis | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ou3a7Htbrvw"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy: Pathophysiology and diagnosis | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VtqrKxJmBn4"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Myocarditis - causes, pathophysiology, investigation and treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=INSa4QQcwFo"}],"related":["CV-18-B","CV-18-K6","CV-18-K2","CV-14"]},"CV-18-F":{"id":"CV-18-F","summary":"Pericardial disease covers fluid in the pericardial sac (effusion and tamponade), inflammation of the pericardium (pericarditis, primary or secondary), and tumor spread to the pericardium. Exams test how each one changes filling of the heart and what the ECG and exam look like.","notes":[{"h":"Pericardium basics","points":["Fibrous outer layer plus a serous sac (parietal and visceral layers).","Normal pericardial fluid is a small volume, about 15 to 50 mL.","The fibrous layer does not stretch quickly, so the rate of fluid build-up matters more than the total amount."]},{"h":"Pericarditis","points":["Primary (acute idiopathic) pericarditis is mostly viral, classically coxsackie B.","Secondary causes: MI (early fibrinous, or Dressler syndrome weeks later), uremia, SLE or RA, radiation, TB, bacterial infection, cancer, cardiac surgery.","Sharp chest pain worse lying flat and with inspiration, better sitting up and leaning forward.","Friction rub; ECG shows diffuse ST elevation with PR depression.","Patterns: fibrinous or serous (most common), purulent (bacterial), hemorrhagic (cancer, TB), caseous (TB)."]},{"h":"Effusion and tamponade","points":["Serous effusion: heart failure, low albumin. Bloody: trauma, cancer, TB, ruptured MI or aortic dissection.","Large effusion: muffled heart sounds, low-voltage QRS, electrical alternans, water-bottle heart on X-ray.","Tamponade: fluid pressure squeezes the heart so it cannot fill; cardiac output falls.","Beck triad: hypotension, distended neck veins, distant heart sounds.","Pulsus paradoxus: systolic pressure falls more than 10 mmHg with inspiration.","Diastolic pressures equalize across all four chambers."]},{"h":"Metastatic disease","points":["Metastases are far more common than primary tumors of the heart and pericardium.","Common sources: lung and breast carcinoma, melanoma, lymphoma, leukemia.","Usually present as a bloody effusion, sometimes with tamponade; malignant cells can be found in the fluid."]},{"h":"Constrictive pericarditis","points":["Chronic scarring, sometimes calcified, encases the heart and limits filling.","Worldwide TB is a major cause; in developed countries it often follows viral pericarditis, cardiac surgery, or radiation.","Findings: Kussmaul sign, pericardial knock in early diastole, pericardial calcification on imaging."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Pericardial diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Metastatic disease","Lung and breast carcinoma, melanoma, lymphoma, leukemia","Direct extension or lymphatic and blood spread to the pericardium; tumor causes exudate and bleeding","Bloody effusion, malignant cells in fluid, new effusion in a cancer patient","Tamponade, constriction"],["Pericardial effusion","Heart failure, low albumin, kidney failure, infection, cancer, trauma, hypothyroidism","Fluid builds in the sac; fast build-up raises pressure and limits filling","Muffled heart sounds, low-voltage QRS, electrical alternans, water-bottle heart","Cardiac tamponade (Beck triad, pulsus paradoxus, equal diastolic pressures)"],["Pericarditis, primary","Viral (coxsackie B classic) or idiopathic","Fibrinous or serous inflammation of the pericardial layers","Positional pleuritic chest pain, friction rub, diffuse ST elevation with PR depression","Effusion, tamponade, recurrence, later constriction"],["Pericarditis, secondary","MI, Dressler syndrome, uremia, SLE or RA, radiation, TB, bacteria, cancer, cardiac surgery","Inflammation driven by the underlying disease (necrosis, toxins, immune complexes, infection, tumor)","Same pain, rub and ECG pattern plus signs of the underlying disease","Purulent or hemorrhagic effusion, tamponade, constrictive pericarditis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Beck triad as three Ds","text":"Decreased blood pressure, Distended neck veins, Distant heart sounds. Add a fourth clue, pulsus paradoxus."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Pericarditis ECG versus STEMI","text":"Pericarditis: ST elevation in almost every lead with PR depression and no reciprocal changes. STEMI: ST elevation in one coronary territory with reciprocal depression."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Tamponade versus constriction","text":"Tamponade is a fluid problem (pulsus paradoxus, Kussmaul sign usually absent). Constriction is a scar problem (Kussmaul sign, pericardial knock, calcification)."}],"highYield":["Pain of pericarditis improves sitting up and leaning forward.","Diffuse ST elevation plus PR depression points to pericarditis.","Electrical alternans means a large effusion with the heart swinging in the fluid.","Rate of fluid build-up matters: a small fast bleed can cause tamponade while a slow large effusion may not.","Dressler syndrome is an autoimmune pericarditis weeks after an MI.","Uremic pericarditis is fibrinous and appears with kidney failure.","TB is a classic cause of caseous pericarditis and constrictive pericarditis."],"pitfalls":["Early post-MI fibrinous pericarditis (first few days, over the infarct) versus Dressler syndrome (weeks later, immune mediated).","Kussmaul sign (JVP rises on inspiration) suggests constriction or restrictive cardiomyopathy more than tamponade.","A primary cardiac tumor is rare; a pericardial mass in an adult with known cancer is most likely metastatic."],"dumps":[{"t":"Tamponade physiology","p":"Draw the heart inside a fluid-filled pericardial sac. Trace what happens to filling, stroke volume and blood pressure as pressure rises, and label the bedside signs.","l":["Rising pericardial pressure limits diastolic filling","Right heart is compressed first (thin walls)","Diastolic pressures equalize across the chambers","Stroke volume and cardiac output fall, so blood pressure drops","Neck veins distend as venous return backs up","Inspiration fills the RV, septum bows left, LV output falls: pulsus paradoxus over 10 mmHg","Heart sounds are muffled"]},{"t":"Causes of pericarditis table","p":"Build a table with columns for type of pericarditis (fibrinous, serous, purulent, hemorrhagic, caseous) and the cause that best fits each.","l":["Fibrinous: MI, uremia, rheumatic fever, radiation, SLE","Serous: viral, autoimmune disease, uremia","Purulent: bacterial infection","Hemorrhagic: malignancy and TB","Caseous: TB","Dressler syndrome is fibrinous and immune mediated weeks after MI","Classic virus: coxsackie B"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on pericardial disease"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease, section on pericardial disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Pericardial Diseases | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dQDd9OgwSV4"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Cardiac Tamponade - pericardial effusion, causes, pathophysiology, investigations and treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9FgpLaCi_KE"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Causes of pericarditis | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ij_ERo1Ashc"}],"related":["CV-18-C3","CV-18-E","CV-3","CV-18-K6"]},"CV-18-G":{"id":"CV-18-G","summary":"This unit covers the congenital heart conditions on the blueprint: bicuspid aortic valve, patent ductus arteriosus, atrial and ventricular septal defects, and tetralogy of Fallot. Expect questions that link the embryology to the shunt direction, the murmur, and the genetic syndrome.","notes":[{"h":"Shunt basics","points":["Left-to-right shunts (VSD, ASD, PDA) send extra blood to the lungs; no cyanosis at first.","Right-to-left shunts (TOF) send blue blood to the body; cyanosis early in life.","Untreated left-to-right shunt raises pulmonary pressure, the shunt reverses, and late cyanosis follows (Eisenmenger syndrome).","Eisenmenger signs: late cyanosis, clubbing, polycythemia, right ventricular hypertrophy."]},{"h":"Septal defects","points":["VSD is the most common congenital heart defect (not counting bicuspid aortic valve); the membranous septum is the usual site.","VSD: harsh holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border; small ones are loud and often close on their own.","Ostium secundum ASD is the most common ASD (septum primum over-resorbed or septum secundum too small).","Ostium primum ASD is an endocardial cushion defect, linked to Down syndrome.","ASD: fixed, widely split S2 and a systolic flow murmur at the left upper sternal border.","ASD allows paradoxical emboli (venous clot crossing to the systemic side)."]},{"h":"Patent ductus arteriosus","points":["Ductus arteriosus (from the sixth aortic arch) normally closes in the first days after birth as oxygen rises and prostaglandin E2 falls.","Risk factors: prematurity, congenital rubella.","Continuous machine-like murmur below the left clavicle; bounding pulses, wide pulse pressure.","With Eisenmenger: differential cyanosis, blue clubbed toes with pink fingers."]},{"h":"Tetralogy of Fallot","points":["Most common cyanotic congenital heart defect.","Cause: anterosuperior displacement of the infundibular (outflow) septum, a neural crest related defect.","Associated with 22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge syndrome).","Degree of right ventricular outflow obstruction sets how blue the child is.","Tet spells improve with squatting (raises systemic resistance, less right-to-left flow); boot-shaped heart on X-ray."]},{"h":"Bicuspid aortic valve","points":["Most common congenital heart anomaly overall, about 1 to 2 percent of people; more common in males.","Associated with Turner syndrome and coarctation of the aorta.","Valve calcifies early, so aortic stenosis appears decades earlier than in a normal valve.","Also linked to aortic regurgitation, endocarditis, and dilation or dissection of the ascending aorta."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Congenital heart conditions compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Bicuspid aortic valve","Male sex, Turner syndrome, coarctation of the aorta, familial cases","Two cusps instead of three; higher leaflet stress leads to early calcification; aortic wall weakness","Ejection click, systolic ejection murmur, aortic stenosis at a younger age","Aortic stenosis or regurgitation, endocarditis, ascending aortic aneurysm and dissection"],["Patent ductus arteriosus","Prematurity, congenital rubella","Ductus stays open; left-to-right flow from aorta to pulmonary artery","Continuous machine-like murmur, bounding pulses, wide pulse pressure","Heart failure, pulmonary hypertension, Eisenmenger with differential cyanosis, endarteritis"],["Ventricular septal defect","Most common defect after bicuspid aortic valve; Down syndrome, fetal alcohol syndrome; part of TOF","Left-to-right shunt between ventricles; usually membranous septum","Holosystolic murmur at left lower sternal border","Heart failure, Eisenmenger syndrome, endocarditis"],["Atrial septal defect","Secundum most common; primum with Down syndrome; Holt-Oram syndrome; fetal alcohol syndrome","Left-to-right shunt between atria; right heart volume overload","Fixed widely split S2, systolic flow murmur at left upper sternal border","Paradoxical embolism, atrial fibrillation, right heart failure, late Eisenmenger"],["Tetralogy of Fallot","22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge syndrome), fetal alcohol exposure","Displaced infundibular septum causes pulmonary stenosis, RVH, overriding aorta, and VSD; right-to-left shunt","Early cyanosis, tet spells eased by squatting, boot-shaped heart, systolic ejection murmur","Hypoxic spells, polycythemia, paradoxical emboli and brain abscess, endocarditis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"PROVe (tetralogy of Fallot)","text":"Pulmonary stenosis (the most important feature), RVH, Overriding aorta, VSD."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"eaRLy versus LateR cyanosis","text":"Right-to-Left shunts cause eaRLy cyanosis (blue babies). Left-to-Right shunts cause LateR cyanosis (blue kids) once Eisenmenger reversal sets in."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Frequency order","text":"Left-to-right shunts by frequency: VSD, then ASD, then PDA. Picture the shunt moving from the ventricles, up to the atria, then out to the great arteries."}],"highYield":["VSD is the most common congenital heart defect after bicuspid aortic valve; TOF is the most common cyanotic one.","Fixed split S2 is the ASD sound.","Continuous machine-like murmur means PDA.","Squatting helps TOF by raising systemic vascular resistance.","Bicuspid aortic valve plus Turner syndrome plus coarctation is a classic cluster.","Down syndrome links to endocardial cushion defects (AV septal defect, ostium primum ASD).","22q11.2 deletion links to TOF and truncus arteriosus."],"pitfalls":["Fixed split S2 (ASD) versus paradoxical split S2 (aortic stenosis or left bundle branch block).","PDA with Eisenmenger causes cyanosis of the lower body (blue toes, pink fingers), because the ductus enters the aorta beyond the left subclavian artery.","A small VSD makes a louder murmur than a large one, so loudness does not equal size.","Patent foramen ovale is a flap that failed to fuse; an ASD is missing septal tissue."],"dumps":[{"t":"Draw the shunts","p":"Draw a four-chamber heart with the aorta and pulmonary artery. Add an ASD, a VSD and a PDA, draw arrows for blood flow through each, and label the murmur for each one.","l":["ASD arrow from left atrium to right atrium","VSD arrow from left ventricle to right ventricle","PDA arrow from aorta to pulmonary artery","ASD murmur: systolic flow murmur at left upper sternal border with fixed split S2","VSD murmur: holosystolic at left lower sternal border","PDA murmur: continuous machine-like below left clavicle","All three are left-to-right and can lead to Eisenmenger syndrome"]},{"t":"Tetralogy of Fallot from embryo to child","p":"Draw the heart in tetralogy of Fallot and label all four defects. Then explain why a child with TOF squats during a spell.","l":["Anterosuperior shift of the infundibular septum is the single root cause","Pulmonary (RV outflow) stenosis","Right ventricular hypertrophy","Overriding aorta sitting over the VSD","Ventricular septal defect","Squatting kinks leg arteries and raises systemic vascular resistance","Higher resistance pushes more RV blood to the lungs and less across the VSD","Associated with 22q11.2 deletion"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Cardiovascular System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Heart Valves and Heart Sounds; Valvular and Congenital Heart Defects"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on congenital heart disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Acyanotic Congenital Heart Defects","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=orjnzBGSNx4"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cyanotic Congenital Heart Defects","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m-PdQwT8mPU"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Tetralogy of fallot | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Be3tuYMgA9I"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Patent ductus arteriosus | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U2fGKvbir24"}],"related":["CV-1","CV-14","CV-18-D1","CV-18-I2"]},"CV-18-H":{"id":"CV-18-H","summary":"Hemodynamic conditions are the general pathology of blood flow: thrombosis, embolism, infarction, hemorrhage, edema and shock. They link to each other in chains (clot, then embolus, then infarct) and show up in almost every organ system on the exam.","notes":[{"h":"Thrombosis","points":["Virchow triad: endothelial injury, abnormal flow (stasis or turbulence), hypercoagulability.","Arterial thrombi: platelet-rich, form on ruptured atherosclerotic plaque.","Venous thrombi: red, form in stasis, usually deep leg veins.","Lines of Zahn (layers of platelets and fibrin alternating with red cells) mark a clot formed in flowing blood before death.","Inherited hypercoagulability: factor V Leiden (most common), prothrombin G20210A, antithrombin, protein C or S deficiency.","Fates: propagation, embolization, dissolution, organization and recanalization."]},{"h":"Embolism","points":["Most emboli are thromboemboli.","Venous emboli (mostly from deep leg veins) lodge in the lungs.","Systemic arterial emboli mostly come from intracardiac mural thrombi and lodge in legs, brain, gut, kidney and spleen.","Fat embolism after long-bone fracture: low oxygen, confusion, petechial rash, about 1 to 3 days after injury.","Amniotic fluid embolism: sudden respiratory failure, shock and DIC during or after labor.","Gas embolism includes decompression sickness (nitrogen bubbles on fast ascent)."]},{"h":"Infarction","points":["Area of ischemic necrosis from blocked arterial supply or venous drainage.","White (pale) infarcts: solid organs with a single arterial supply (heart, kidney, spleen).","Red (hemorrhagic) infarcts: venous occlusion, loose tissue, dual blood supply (lung, small bowel), or reperfusion.","Coagulative necrosis in most organs; liquefactive necrosis in the brain.","Infarcts are often wedge-shaped with the blocked vessel at the apex."]},{"h":"Hemorrhage and edema","points":["Hemorrhage sizes: petechiae (1 to 2 mm), purpura (3 mm or more), ecchymoses (larger bruises), hematoma (collected mass of blood).","Acute loss of more than about 20 percent of blood volume can cause hypovolemic shock; chronic loss causes iron deficiency anemia.","Edema forms when Starling forces push fluid out faster than lymphatics remove it.","Transudate (low protein): high hydrostatic pressure or low oncotic pressure. Exudate (high protein): inflammation."]},{"h":"Shock","points":["Shock is widespread poor tissue perfusion from low cardiac output or low effective blood volume.","Types: hypovolemic, cardiogenic, obstructive, distributive (septic, anaphylactic, neurogenic).","Stages: nonprogressive (compensated), progressive (lactic acidosis), irreversible.","End-organ damage: acute tubular necrosis, ARDS, DIC, ischemic bowel, multiorgan failure."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hemodynamic conditions compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Thrombosis","Immobility, surgery, cancer, pregnancy, estrogen, factor V Leiden, antiphospholipid syndrome, atherosclerosis","Virchow triad activates platelets and coagulation inside an intact vessel","Venous: swollen painful limb. Arterial: sudden ischemia of the supplied tissue","Embolism, infarction, chronic venous insufficiency"],["Embolism","Deep vein thrombosis, atrial fibrillation, mural thrombus, long-bone fracture, labor, rapid ascent from a dive","Detached mass (clot, fat, gas, amniotic fluid, tumor, bacteria) travels and blocks a downstream vessel","Depends on site: pulmonary embolism, stroke, cold pulseless limb, bowel ischemia","Infarction, sudden death, DIC (amniotic fluid)"],["Hemorrhage","Trauma, vessel rupture, aneurysm, coagulation or platelet disorders","Blood escapes the vessel into tissue, a body cavity, or outside the body","Petechiae, purpura, ecchymoses, hematoma","Hypovolemic shock (acute), iron deficiency anemia (chronic), mass effect in the skull"],["Infarction","Arterial thrombosis or embolism, venous occlusion, vasospasm, torsion","Ischemic necrosis of the tissue supplied","White wedge in heart, kidney, spleen; red infarct in lung, bowel, testis","Organ dysfunction, rupture, scar formation"],["Edema","Heart failure, nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, malnutrition, lymphatic blockage, inflammation","Raised hydrostatic pressure, lowered oncotic pressure, lymphatic obstruction, sodium retention, or leaky vessels","Pitting leg edema, pulmonary crackles, ascites, periorbital swelling","Pulmonary edema with hypoxemia, cerebral edema with herniation, skin breakdown"],["Shock","Bleeding or fluid loss, MI, tamponade, massive PE, sepsis, anaphylaxis, spinal cord injury","Systemic hypoperfusion leads to anaerobic metabolism and cell injury","Low blood pressure, fast pulse, low urine output, confusion, raised lactate","Acute tubular necrosis, ARDS, DIC, multiorgan failure, death"]]},{"title":"Types of shock by hemodynamics","cols":["Type","Example causes","Cardiac output","Systemic vascular resistance","Wedge pressure (PCWP)","Skin"],"rows":[["Hypovolemic","Hemorrhage, burns, vomiting, diarrhea","Low","High","Low","Cold, clammy"],["Cardiogenic","MI, arrhythmia, acute valve failure","Low","High","High","Cold, clammy"],["Obstructive","Tamponade, tension pneumothorax, massive PE","Low","High","Varies (high in tamponade, low in massive PE)","Cold, clammy"],["Distributive: septic","Gram-negative or gram-positive sepsis","High early, may fall later","Low","Low","Warm early"],["Distributive: anaphylactic","IgE-mediated mast cell release (type I hypersensitivity)","High or normal","Low","Low","Warm, flushed, hives"],["Distributive: neurogenic","Spinal cord or brainstem injury","Low","Low","Low","Warm, dry, with slow heart rate"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"An embolus moves like a FAT BAT","text":"Types of emboli: Fat, Air, Thrombus, Bacteria, Amniotic fluid, Tumor."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Red or white infarct","text":"Red infarcts happen where blood can still get in or pool: veins blocked, loose tissue, two blood supplies, or reperfusion. Otherwise expect a white infarct."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Reading a shock profile","text":"Start with SVR. High SVR means the body is compensating for a pump or volume problem, so check PCWP (high in cardiogenic, low in hypovolemic). Low SVR means distributive shock."}],"highYield":["Factor V Leiden resists inactivation by activated protein C and is the most common inherited thrombophilia.","Lines of Zahn tell an antemortem clot from a postmortem clot.","Most pulmonary emboli start in deep veins of the thigh and pelvis.","Brain infarcts show liquefactive necrosis; other solid organs show coagulative necrosis.","Lung infarcts are red because of the dual bronchial and pulmonary supply.","Septic shock: low SVR, low PCWP, and high cardiac output early.","Neurogenic shock is the type with a slow heart rate."],"pitfalls":["Postmortem clots are gelatinous with a chicken-fat upper layer and no lines of Zahn; do not mistake them for antemortem thrombi.","Transudate versus exudate: inflammation leaks protein (exudate); heart failure and low albumin do not (transudate).","Obstructive shock looks like cardiogenic shock on SVR and output; the cause is outside the heart muscle.","Paradoxical embolism (venous clot causing a stroke) needs a right-to-left path such as a patent foramen ovale."],"dumps":[{"t":"Clot to infarct chain","p":"Trace a deep leg vein clot from formation to its effect on the lung. Label each step with the Virchow triad element or pathology term involved.","l":["Stasis from immobility starts the clot (Virchow triad)","Hypercoagulability or vessel injury adds risk","Venous thrombus forms in deep calf or thigh veins and propagates","Fragment breaks off and becomes a thromboembolus","Travels through the right heart to the pulmonary arteries","Blocks flow and creates dead space (V/Q mismatch)","Most do not infarct because of bronchial artery supply; an infarct is red and wedge-shaped"]},{"t":"Shock table","p":"Build a table on blank paper with rows for hypovolemic, cardiogenic, obstructive, septic and neurogenic shock, and columns for one cause, cardiac output, SVR, and PCWP.","l":["Hypovolemic: low CO, high SVR, low PCWP","Cardiogenic: low CO, high SVR, high PCWP","Obstructive: low CO, high SVR, PCWP depends on cause","Septic: high CO early, low SVR, low PCWP","Neurogenic: low CO, low SVR, low PCWP, slow heart rate","One correct cause for each type","Lactic acidosis marks progressive shock"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Hemodynamic Disorders, Thromboembolic Disease, and Shock"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Circulatory Shock and Physiology of Its Treatment"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: Capillary Fluid Exchange, Interstitial Fluid, and Lymph Flow"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Hemostasis and Blood Coagulation"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Shock | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=07N42Ragqas"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Approach to Shock - types, hypovolemic, cardiogenic, distributive, anaphylaxis, pathology, treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NqkdqJtxIfs"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Shock | Types And Mechanisms of Shock | Dr Najeeb🧠","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hvoYD2Qu_ms"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"What is shock? | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AvtS_IrlbYk"},{"channel":"Khan Academy","title":"Thromboemboli and thromboembolisms | Miscellaneous | Heatlh & Medicine | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=h0207xMD6b8"}],"related":["CV-11","CV-18-I7","CV-18-I10","CV-18-C3"]},"CV-18-I":{"id":"CV-18-I","summary":"Vascular conditions cover disease of the arteries (atherosclerosis, aneurysm, dissection, PAD, familial hypercholesterolemia), the veins (DVT, PE, varicose veins), and vessel inflammation or spasm (vasculitis, giant cell arteritis, Buerger disease, Raynaud). The exam favors risk factors, vessel size, and the classic patient for each.","notes":[{"h":"Arteriosclerosis and atherosclerosis","points":["Arteriosclerosis means hardening of arteries; three patterns: atherosclerosis, arteriolosclerosis, and Monckeberg medial calcific sclerosis.","Hyaline arteriolosclerosis: hypertension and diabetes. Hyperplastic (onion-skin): severe hypertension.","Monckeberg: calcification of the media in muscular arteries; does not narrow the lumen.","Atherosclerosis steps: endothelial injury, LDL entry and oxidation, monocytes become foam-cell macrophages, fatty streak, smooth muscle migration (PDGF), fibrous cap over a necrotic core.","Most affected: abdominal aorta, then coronary, popliteal and carotid arteries.","Modifiable risks: smoking, hypertension, high LDL, diabetes. Fixed: age, male sex, family history."]},{"h":"Familial hypercholesterolemia","points":["Autosomal dominant; most often LDL receptor (LDLR) mutation; also APOB or gain-of-function PCSK9.","Heterozygotes (about 1 in 250) have LDL often above 190 mg/dL and early coronary disease.","Homozygotes can have LDL above 500 mg/dL and MI in childhood or the teen years.","Tendon xanthomas (Achilles), xanthelasma, and early corneal arcus."]},{"h":"Aneurysm and dissection","points":["Abdominal aortic aneurysm: atherosclerosis; older male smokers; usually below the renal arteries; rupture risk rises with diameter.","Thoracic aortic aneurysm: cystic medial degeneration (hypertension, Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos); tertiary syphilis damages the vasa vasorum.","Berry aneurysms in the circle of Willis link to ADPKD and Ehlers-Danlos.","Aortic dissection: intimal tear lets blood split the media; hypertension is the main risk; also Marfan, bicuspid aortic valve, Turner, cocaine.","Dissection pain is sudden, tearing, radiates to the back; unequal arm pressures; widened mediastinum.","Stanford A involves the ascending aorta; Stanford B does not."]},{"h":"Peripheral arterial disease","points":["Atherosclerosis of leg arteries; smoking and diabetes are the strongest risks.","Claudication (calf pain with walking that stops with rest), then rest pain, ulcers and gangrene.","Weak pulses, shiny hairless skin, cool feet; ankle-brachial index 0.90 or lower (normal about 1.0 to 1.4).","Leriche syndrome (aortoiliac block): buttock claudication, absent femoral pulses, erectile dysfunction."]},{"h":"Veins: DVT, PE, varicose veins","points":["DVT: Virchow triad; one swollen, warm, painful leg, though many are silent.","PE: sudden dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, hypoxemia with a raised A-a gradient; saddle embolus can cause sudden death.","Varicose veins: incompetent valves in superficial veins (great saphenous); risks are age, female sex, pregnancy, obesity, long standing.","Chronic venous disease: edema, stasis dermatitis, ulcers near the medial malleolus; superficial varicose veins rarely embolize."]},{"h":"Vasospasm and vasculitis","points":["Raynaud: cold or stress triggers white, then blue, then red fingers; primary is benign in young women, secondary goes with scleroderma, SLE, Buerger disease.","Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger): heavy smokers, often young men; segmental inflammation and thrombosis of small and medium arteries spreading to veins and nerves; stopping tobacco is the key step.","Giant cell arteritis: over age 50, women more than men; headache, jaw claudication, scalp tenderness, high ESR; can cause sudden blindness.","Half or so of GCA patients have polymyalgia rheumatica (shoulder and hip girdle stiffness).","Vasculitis is grouped by vessel size: large, medium, small (see chart)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Arterial conditions","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Arteriosclerosis and atherosclerosis","Smoking, hypertension, high LDL, diabetes, age, male sex, family history","Endothelial injury, LDL oxidation, foam cells, smooth muscle proliferation, fibrous plaque","Often silent until narrowing or rupture; angina, claudication, TIA","MI, stroke, PAD, aneurysm, mesenteric and renal ischemia"],["Familial hypercholesterolemia","Autosomal dominant LDLR mutation (also APOB, PCSK9)","Poor LDL clearance by the liver raises plasma LDL from birth","Very high LDL, tendon xanthomas, xanthelasma, early corneal arcus","Premature coronary disease and MI"],["Aneurysm","Abdominal: atherosclerosis, smoking, male, older age. Thoracic: hypertension, Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos, syphilis","Weakened wall (media damage or matrix defect) bulges outward","Pulsatile abdominal mass; often found by chance on imaging","Rupture, thrombosis, embolism, pressure on nearby structures"],["Aortic dissection","Hypertension, Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos, bicuspid aortic valve, Turner syndrome, cocaine, pregnancy","Intimal tear; blood splits the media and forms a false lumen","Sudden tearing chest pain to the back, unequal arm pressures, widened mediastinum","Tamponade, aortic regurgitation, MI, stroke, organ ischemia, rupture"],["Peripheral arterial disease","Smoking, diabetes, hypertension, high LDL, age","Atherosclerotic narrowing of leg arteries limits flow during exercise","Claudication, weak pulses, hairless shiny skin, ABI 0.90 or lower","Rest pain, nonhealing ulcers, gangrene, amputation"]]},{"title":"Venous and embolic conditions","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Thrombosis, deep vein (DVT)","Immobility, surgery, trauma, pregnancy, estrogen, cancer, inherited thrombophilia","Virchow triad: stasis, endothelial injury, hypercoagulability","Unilateral leg swelling, pain, warmth; often silent","Pulmonary embolism, post-thrombotic syndrome"],["Pulmonary embolism (PE)","Source is usually a deep vein of the thigh or pelvis; same risks as DVT","Clot blocks pulmonary arteries, creating dead space and raising RV afterload","Sudden dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, hypoxemia, respiratory alkalosis","Acute right heart failure, sudden death, pulmonary infarct, chronic pulmonary hypertension"],["Varicose veins","Age, female sex, pregnancy, obesity, long standing, family history","Incompetent valves raise pressure in superficial veins, which dilate and twist","Visible tortuous leg veins, aching, edema","Stasis dermatitis, venous ulcers, superficial thrombophlebitis"]]},{"title":"Vasospastic and vasculitic conditions","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Giant cell arteritis (temporal arteritis)","Age over 50, female, northern European ancestry; polymyalgia rheumatica","T-cell driven granulomatous inflammation of large and medium arteries (carotid branches); patchy skip lesions","Headache, scalp tenderness, jaw claudication, visual symptoms, high ESR and CRP","Permanent blindness (ophthalmic artery), aortic aneurysm"],["Raynaud phenomenon (primary and secondary)","Primary: young women, no cause. Secondary: scleroderma, SLE, Buerger disease, some drugs","Exaggerated vasospasm of digital arteries and arterioles","White, then blue, then red fingers or toes on cold or stress","Primary: none serious. Secondary: digital ulcers and gangrene"],["Thromboangiitis obliterans","Heavy tobacco use; usually young men","Segmental inflammation and thrombosis of small and medium arteries, spreading to veins and nerves","Instep claudication, superficial migratory phlebitis, Raynaud, severe ischemic pain","Ulcers, gangrene, digit amputation"],["Vasculitis","Immune complexes (hepatitis B in PAN, hepatitis C in cryoglobulinemia), ANCA, T-cell granulomas, infection","Inflammation and necrosis of the vessel wall narrow or weaken the vessel","Fever, weight loss, palpable purpura (small vessel), organ ischemia by vessel size","Organ infarction, aneurysm, hemorrhage, glomerulonephritis"]]},{"title":"Vasculitis by vessel size","cols":["Vessel size","Disease","Classic patient and clues"],"rows":[["Large","Giant cell arteritis","Over 50, headache, jaw claudication, high ESR, granulomas"],["Large","Takayasu arteritis","Asian woman under 40, weak arm pulses (pulseless disease), granulomatous aortic arch disease"],["Medium","Polyarteritis nodosa","Hepatitis B link, spares the lungs, renal and mesenteric microaneurysms, transmural fibrinoid necrosis"],["Medium","Kawasaki disease","Child under 5, fever 5 days or more, rash, red eyes, strawberry tongue; coronary aneurysms"],["Medium","Thromboangiitis obliterans","Young heavy smoker, distal limb ischemia"],["Small","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","PR3-ANCA (c-ANCA); sinus, lung and kidney necrotizing granulomas"],["Small","Microscopic polyangiitis","MPO-ANCA (p-ANCA); lung and kidney, no granulomas"],["Small","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Asthma, eosinophilia, MPO-ANCA (p-ANCA), neuropathy"],["Small","IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein purpura)","Child after an upper respiratory infection; palpable purpura on buttocks and legs, joint pain, abdominal pain, IgA nephropathy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CRASH and Burn (Kawasaki disease)","text":"Conjunctival injection, Rash, Adenopathy (cervical), Strawberry tongue, Hand and foot redness and swelling, and Burn (fever lasting 5 days or more)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Raynaud colors in order","text":"White (spasm, no flow), blue (deoxygenated blood sitting still), red (blood rushing back)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Arterial versus venous leg ulcer","text":"Arterial: toes or pressure points, punched out, painful, pale cold foot, weak pulses. Venous: above the medial malleolus, shallow, edema and brown skin, pulses present."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Smoker with a cold limb","text":"Older smoker with claudication and calf pain points to PAD. Young heavy smoker with digit ischemia and migrating phlebitis points to Buerger disease."}],"highYield":["Hypertension is the most important risk factor for aortic dissection.","Abdominal aortic aneurysm is mainly atherosclerotic; thoracic aneurysm is mainly hypertension or connective tissue disease.","Familial hypercholesterolemia: LDL receptor defect, tendon xanthomas, early MI.","Giant cell arteritis needs prompt treatment to prevent blindness; biopsy can miss skip lesions.","Most PEs come from proximal deep leg veins; sinus tachycardia is the most common ECG finding.","c-ANCA (PR3) goes with granulomatosis with polyangiitis; p-ANCA (MPO) with microscopic polyangiitis and EGPA.","PAN spares the lungs and links to hepatitis B.","Buerger disease is treated by stopping tobacco completely."],"pitfalls":["Monckeberg sclerosis calcifies the media without narrowing the lumen, so it gives falsely high ankle pressures; it differs from atherosclerosis.","Primary Raynaud is symmetric and benign; asymmetric attacks, ulcers, or an older patient suggest secondary Raynaud.","Aortic dissection versus MI: dissection gives tearing back pain and unequal arm pressures, and thrombolytics are dangerous.","Takayasu and giant cell arteritis share granulomas; age (under 40 versus over 50) is the main split."],"dumps":[{"t":"Atherosclerotic plaque","p":"Draw an artery wall in cross-section and build an atherosclerotic plaque step by step. Label each cell type and the order of events.","l":["Endothelial injury from smoking, hypertension, high LDL or high glucose","LDL enters the intima and is oxidized","Monocytes stick, enter, and become macrophages","Macrophages eat oxidized LDL and become foam cells (fatty streak)","Smooth muscle cells migrate from the media (PDGF) and lay down matrix","Fibrous cap over a necrotic lipid core","Cap rupture exposes contents and triggers thrombosis"]},{"t":"Vasculitis map","p":"Draw three vessels of decreasing size (aorta, medium artery, capillary). Write two vasculitides next to each, with one lab or clinical clue for each disease.","l":["Large: giant cell arteritis (over 50, jaw claudication, high ESR)","Large: Takayasu arteritis (young woman, weak arm pulses)","Medium: polyarteritis nodosa (hepatitis B, spares lung, microaneurysms)","Medium: Kawasaki disease (child, coronary aneurysm)","Small: granulomatosis with polyangiitis (PR3-ANCA, sinus, lung, kidney)","Small: IgA vasculitis (child, palpable purpura, IgA nephropathy)","Small vessel disease gives palpable purpura"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Vascular Disease"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, Lipoprotein, and Steroid Metabolism"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Aortic Diseases | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4QvHj5wXdWA"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Venous Thromboembolism (DVT, PE) | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0SVP95BOUNI"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Vasculitis Overview - types and classification","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YwW_Wwkhxnk"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Giant cell Arteritis and Takayasu arteritis (Large Vessel Vasculitis) - signs, pathophysiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ck8W4GcYYe8"}],"related":["CV-18-H","CV-18-C","CV-13","CV-18-G1"]},"CV-18-J":{"id":"CV-18-J","summary":"Vascular neoplasms on the blueprint are hemangiomas (benign, very common, especially in infants) and Kaposi sarcoma (an HHV-8 driven tumor seen with immune suppression). Exam questions focus on the virus, the patient group, and telling these lesions from look-alikes.","notes":[{"h":"Hemangioma basics","points":["Benign tumors made of blood vessels; the most common tumors of infancy and childhood.","Most occur in the skin of the head and neck; cavernous hemangioma is the most common benign tumor of the liver.","Capillary type: small, thin-walled capillary-sized vessels in skin and mucosa.","Cavernous type: large dilated blood-filled channels; less well circumscribed; does not regress."]},{"h":"Infantile hemangioma","points":["Appears in the first weeks of life, grows fast for months, then slowly fades.","Most regress by about age 7.","Bright red raised lesion, often called a strawberry hemangioma.","GLUT1 positive on staining, which separates it from vascular malformations."]},{"h":"Other benign vascular lesions","points":["Pyogenic granuloma (lobular capillary hemangioma): fast-growing red polyp on skin, gums or mouth that bleeds easily, often after minor trauma.","Pregnancy tumor (granuloma gravidarum) is a pyogenic granuloma of the gingiva during pregnancy.","Cherry hemangiomas are small red papules that increase with age in adults."]},{"h":"Kaposi sarcoma","points":["Caused by human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8, also called KSHV), a DNA virus.","Spindle cells form slit-like vascular spaces with red cells leaking between them.","Lesions progress from purple patches to plaques to nodules.","HHV-8 latent proteins push cell growth: a viral cyclin D homolog, and LANA, which interferes with p53 and Rb.","Immune suppression lets the infected cells grow; tumors may shrink when immunity improves.","HHV-8 also causes primary effusion lymphoma and multicentric Castleman disease."]},{"h":"Four forms of Kaposi sarcoma","points":["Classic: older men of Mediterranean, Eastern European or Ashkenazi Jewish background; slow; lower legs.","Endemic African: children and young adults; children can have aggressive lymph node disease.","Transplant-associated: from immunosuppressive drugs; often regresses when the drugs are reduced.","AIDS-associated: an AIDS-defining illness; spreads widely to skin, mouth, gut, lungs and lymph nodes."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hemangiomas and Kaposi sarcoma compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Hemangiomas (capillary and cavernous)","Unknown cause; very common in infants and children","Benign proliferation of endothelial cells forming capillary or large cavernous channels","Red to blue skin lesion in head and neck; cavernous hemangioma as a liver mass on imaging","Usually none; large or deep ones can bleed, compress structures, or cause cosmetic or vision problems"],["Infantile (strawberry) hemangioma","Infants, more often girls and preterm babies","Rapid endothelial growth phase then involution; GLUT1 positive","Bright red raised lesion appearing in the first weeks of life","Ulceration, bleeding, airway or eye obstruction; most regress by about age 7"],["Pyogenic granuloma (lobular capillary hemangioma)","Minor trauma, pregnancy","Rapidly growing lobules of capillaries with inflamed, often ulcerated surface","Red polypoid lesion on skin, gums or lips that bleeds easily","Repeated bleeding; may recur after removal"],["Kaposi sarcoma","HHV-8 infection plus immune suppression (AIDS, transplant drugs) or older age in the classic form","HHV-8 latent genes drive proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like vessels","Purple patches, plaques and nodules on skin; lesions in mouth, gut, lungs","GI bleeding, lung involvement with respiratory failure, lymphedema"]]},{"title":"Forms of Kaposi sarcoma","cols":["Form","Who gets it","Typical sites","Behavior"],"rows":[["Classic","Older men of Mediterranean, Eastern European or Ashkenazi Jewish background","Lower legs","Slow, rarely spreads to organs"],["Endemic African","Children and young adults in sub-Saharan Africa","Skin; lymph nodes in children","Can be aggressive in children"],["Transplant-associated","Organ transplant patients on immunosuppressive drugs","Skin, lymph nodes, organs","Often regresses when immunosuppression is reduced"],["AIDS-associated","People with untreated HIV and low CD4 counts","Skin, mouth, gut, lungs, lymph nodes","Widespread and aggressive; improves with antiretroviral therapy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Purple lesion in a patient with HIV","text":"Ask whether it responds to antibiotics. Bacillary angiomatosis (Bartonella) has neutrophils and clears with antibiotics. Kaposi sarcoma has lymphocytes and spindle cells and is driven by HHV-8."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Which hemangioma goes away","text":"Infantile hemangioma grows then shrinks on its own. Cavernous hemangioma and cherry hemangioma stay."},{"kind":"trick","label":"HHV-8 three diseases","text":"Link HHV-8 to three things: Kaposi sarcoma, primary effusion lymphoma, and multicentric Castleman disease."}],"highYield":["Kaposi sarcoma is caused by HHV-8 (KSHV).","Kaposi sarcoma is an AIDS-defining illness.","Histology: spindle cells, slit-like vascular spaces, extravasated red cells.","Infantile hemangiomas grow fast and then regress in most children.","Cavernous hemangioma is the most common benign liver tumor.","Pyogenic granuloma bleeds easily and can appear in pregnancy."],"pitfalls":["Kaposi sarcoma versus bacillary angiomatosis: both are red-purple lesions in AIDS; bacillary angiomatosis has neutrophils and is caused by Bartonella.","Pyogenic granuloma is a vascular lesion and is neither infectious nor granulomatous, despite its name.","Angiosarcoma is a separate malignant endothelial tumor (linked to vinyl chloride, arsenic, and chronic lymphedema); do not confuse it with Kaposi sarcoma."],"dumps":[{"t":"HHV-8 to Kaposi sarcoma","p":"Trace how HHV-8 infection becomes a Kaposi sarcoma lesion in a person with untreated HIV. Draw the lesion stages and label the cell types.","l":["HHV-8 is a DNA herpesvirus that infects endothelial-lineage cells","Low CD4 count weakens T-cell control of the virus","Latent proteins drive growth: viral cyclin D homolog and LANA blocking p53 and Rb","Spindle cells form slit-like vessels with leaking red cells","Lesions progress: patch, plaque, nodule","Spread to mouth, gut, lungs and lymph nodes in the AIDS form","Restoring immunity with antiretroviral therapy can shrink lesions"]},{"t":"Vascular lesion comparison","p":"Build a table comparing infantile hemangioma, cavernous hemangioma, pyogenic granuloma, and Kaposi sarcoma. Use columns for patient, appearance, behavior, and cause.","l":["Infantile: infant, bright red raised lesion, grows then regresses","Cavernous: large dilated channels, skin or liver, does not regress","Pyogenic granuloma: red polyp that bleeds, after trauma or in pregnancy","Kaposi sarcoma: purple patches to nodules, HHV-8","Only Kaposi sarcoma is malignant","Kaposi sarcoma forms: classic, endemic, transplant, AIDS"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels, section on vascular tumors"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Herpesviruses, section on human herpesvirus 8"}],"videos":[],"related":["CV-18-I","CV-16","CV-17"]},"CV-18-K":{"id":"CV-18-K","summary":"Infectious vascular diseases are infections that damage the heart or blood vessels: bacterial endocarditis, Chagas disease, Lyme disease, Rocky Mountain spotted fever, viral hemorrhagic fevers, and viral myocarditis. Exams test the organism, how it spreads, and the cardiovascular damage it causes.","notes":[{"h":"Bacterial endocarditis","points":["Acute: Staphylococcus aureus, high virulence, attacks normal valves; IV drug use favors the tricuspid valve.","Subacute: viridans streptococci, low virulence, attacks damaged valves, often after dental work; dextrans help them stick to fibrin-platelet clumps.","Prosthetic valves: Staphylococcus epidermidis. Streptococcus gallolyticus (bovis) suggests colon cancer.","Culture-negative causes: HACEK organisms, Coxiella, Bartonella.","Signs: fever, new murmur, Janeway lesions, Osler nodes, Roth spots, splinter hemorrhages.","Complications: valve destruction, heart failure, septic emboli, glomerulonephritis, mycotic aneurysm."]},{"h":"Chagas disease","points":["Trypanosoma cruzi, a flagellated protozoan; Mexico, Central and South America.","Triatomine (reduviid, kissing) bug bites, defecates, and parasites in the feces enter the bite or mucosa.","Also spread by transfusion, transplant, mother to baby, and contaminated food.","Acute: often mild; Romana sign (one swollen eyelid) when entry is through the conjunctiva.","Chronic (in about 20 to 30 percent, years later): dilated cardiomyopathy with conduction block and apical aneurysm, megaesophagus, megacolon."]},{"h":"Lyme disease","points":["Borrelia burgdorferi, a spirochete; Ixodes tick; Northeast and upper Midwest US.","Early localized: erythema migrans (expanding bull's-eye rash) plus flu-like illness.","Early disseminated: facial nerve palsy (can be bilateral), meningitis, carditis with AV block.","Late: arthritis of large joints such as the knee, and neurologic disease."]},{"h":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever","points":["Rickettsia rickettsii, an obligate intracellular gram-negative bacterium; Dermacentor tick.","Most US cases are in the southeastern and south-central states despite the name.","Infects endothelial cells, causing vasculitis, leaky vessels, edema and microhemorrhage.","Fever, headache, and a rash that starts at wrists and ankles, spreads to the trunk, and involves palms and soles.","Untreated: shock, pulmonary edema, encephalitis, DIC, death."]},{"h":"Viral hemorrhagic fevers","points":["Enveloped RNA viruses that damage endothelium and clotting, causing leaky vessels, bleeding and shock.","Yellow fever: flavivirus, Aedes mosquito; liver necrosis with Councilman bodies, jaundice, black vomit.","Dengue: flavivirus, Aedes mosquito, four serotypes; fever, severe muscle and joint pain, low platelets.","Severe dengue is more likely with a second infection by a different serotype (antibody-dependent enhancement).","Filoviruses (Ebola, Marburg): filamentous negative-sense RNA viruses; spread by body fluids; fruit bats are the likely reservoir; high fatality."]},{"h":"Viral myocarditis","points":["Coxsackie B is the classic cause; also adenovirus, parvovirus B19, HHV-6, influenza, SARS-CoV-2.","Coxsackie B and adenovirus enter myocytes through the coxsackie-adenovirus receptor (CAR).","Injury comes from direct viral damage plus cytotoxic T-cell attack.","Young adult after a cold or GI illness with chest pain, arrhythmia, raised troponin, heart failure."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Infectious vascular diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Organism and spread","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Cardiovascular complications"],"rows":[["Bacterial endocarditis","S. aureus (acute, IV drug use), viridans strep (subacute, dental), S. epidermidis (prosthetic valve), enterococci; spread through the bloodstream","Bacteria colonize valve vegetations; acute forms destroy valve tissue","Fever, new murmur, Janeway lesions, Osler nodes, Roth spots, splinter hemorrhages","Valve regurgitation, heart failure, septic emboli, mycotic aneurysm, ring abscess"],["Chagas disease","Trypanosoma cruzi (protozoan); triatomine bug feces, transfusion, transplant, congenital, food","Intracellular amastigotes in heart muscle and gut nerves; parasite persistence plus immune damage","Romana sign or chagoma early; decades later heart failure, megaesophagus, megacolon","Dilated cardiomyopathy, apical aneurysm, conduction block, arrhythmia, emboli"],["Lyme disease","Borrelia burgdorferi (spirochete); Ixodes tick","Spirochete spreads from the bite to skin, nerves, heart and joints","Erythema migrans, facial palsy, migratory or knee arthritis","Lyme carditis with AV block, myocarditis"],["Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Rickettsia rickettsii (obligate intracellular gram-negative); Dermacentor tick","Infects vascular endothelium causing vasculitis and leaky vessels","Fever, headache, rash from wrists and ankles inward including palms and soles","Hypotension and shock, edema, DIC, myocarditis"],["Viral hemorrhagic fever (yellow fever, dengue, filoviruses)","Yellow fever and dengue: flaviviruses, Aedes mosquito. Ebola and Marburg: filoviruses, body fluids","Endothelial and immune cell infection causes capillary leak and clotting failure","High fever, bleeding, low platelets; jaundice in yellow fever; bone-breaking pain in dengue","Hypovolemic shock, DIC, multiorgan failure"],["Viral myocarditis","Coxsackie B (classic), adenovirus, parvovirus B19, HHV-6, SARS-CoV-2; respiratory or fecal-oral spread","Direct viral injury plus cytotoxic T-cell attack on myocytes","Recent viral illness, chest pain, arrhythmia, raised troponin","Acute heart failure, arrhythmia, sudden death, dilated cardiomyopathy"]]},{"title":"Viral hemorrhagic fevers compared","cols":["Virus","Family and genome","Spread","Key features"],"rows":[["Yellow fever","Flavivirus; enveloped positive-sense single-stranded RNA","Aedes aegypti mosquito; monkeys in the jungle cycle","Fever, jaundice from midzonal liver necrosis, Councilman bodies, black vomit; live vaccine available"],["Dengue","Flavivirus; enveloped positive-sense single-stranded RNA; four serotypes","Aedes mosquitoes","Fever, retro-orbital pain, severe muscle and joint pain, rash, low platelets; severe dengue with plasma leak more likely on reinfection"],["Ebola and Marburg","Filoviruses; enveloped negative-sense single-stranded RNA, filament-shaped","Contact with blood or body fluids; fruit bats likely reservoir","Fever, vomiting and diarrhea, bleeding, DIC, high case fatality"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Bacteria FROM JANE (endocarditis signs)","text":"Fever, Roth spots, Osler nodes, Murmur, Janeway lesions, Anemia, Nail-bed (splinter) hemorrhages, Emboli."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"FAKE a Key Lyme pie (Lyme disease)","text":"Facial nerve palsy, Arthritis, Kardiac block, Erythema migrans."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"You drive CARS using your palms and soles","text":"Rashes on palms and soles: Coxsackie A (hand-foot-mouth disease), Rocky Mountain spotted fever, Syphilis (secondary)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Osler versus Janeway","text":"Osler nodes are Ouch: painful, on finger pads, immune complex. Janeway lesions are painless, on palms and soles, from septic microemboli."}],"highYield":["S. aureus is the most common cause of endocarditis in IV drug users and attacks the tricuspid valve.","S. gallolyticus (bovis) endocarditis means look for colon cancer.","Chagas disease is a classic infectious cause of dilated cardiomyopathy in Latin America.","Lyme carditis usually shows AV block.","RMSF rash moves from wrists and ankles toward the trunk and involves palms and soles.","Second dengue infection with a new serotype raises the risk of severe dengue.","Yellow fever liver shows Councilman bodies (apoptotic hepatocytes).","Coxsackie B is the classic viral cause of myocarditis."],"pitfalls":["RMSF rash moves from the limbs inward (centripetal); typhus rash starts on the trunk and spreads outward and spares palms and soles.","Lyme erythema migrans appears at the bite site days after a tick bite; a small red bump right after the bite is a local reaction.","Libman-Sacks endocarditis in SLE is sterile, so it does not count as bacterial endocarditis.","Romana sign is unilateral eyelid swelling; bilateral eyelid swelling points to other causes such as nephrotic syndrome."],"dumps":[{"t":"Endocarditis pathway","p":"Trace subacute bacterial endocarditis from a dental procedure to a kidney complication. Draw the valve vegetation and label each step.","l":["Damaged valve (for example after rheumatic disease) has a sterile platelet-fibrin clump","Dental work causes transient bacteremia with viridans streptococci","Dextrans help the bacteria stick to the fibrin-platelet clump","Vegetation grows on the valve, usually the mitral valve","Pieces break off as septic emboli to brain, kidney, spleen","Immune complexes cause glomerulonephritis","Osler nodes and Roth spots reflect immune complex and embolic damage"]},{"t":"Tick and bug map","p":"Build a table of the vector-borne diseases in this unit. Columns: disease, organism, vector, main US or world region, and cardiovascular effect.","l":["Lyme: Borrelia burgdorferi, Ixodes tick, Northeast and upper Midwest, AV block","RMSF: Rickettsia rickettsii, Dermacentor tick, southeastern and south-central US, vasculitis and shock","Chagas: Trypanosoma cruzi, triatomine bug, Latin America, dilated cardiomyopathy","Yellow fever: flavivirus, Aedes mosquito, Africa and South America, hemorrhage and shock","Dengue: flavivirus, Aedes mosquito, tropics worldwide, plasma leak and shock","Ebola has no insect vector; spread by body fluids"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on infective endocarditis and myocarditis"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Spirochetes and Other Spiral Microorganisms"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Arthropod-Borne and Rodent-Borne Viral Diseases"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"section on Trypanosoma cruzi in the medical parasitology chapter"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Infective Endocarditis | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iaO8110iSzI"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Myocarditis - causes, pathophysiology, investigation and treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=INSa4QQcwFo"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"What is myocarditis and pericarditis? | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xL98lBuAn1A"}],"related":["CV-17","CV-16","CV-18-D3","CV-18-E2"]},"GI-1":{"id":"GI-1","summary":"How the gut tube is divided into foregut, midgut, and hindgut, how the midgut herniates and rotates, and how the liver, pancreas, and enteric nerves form. Exam questions link each step to the birth defect that results when it fails, and use the gut divisions to explain blood supply and referred pain.","notes":[{"h":"Three parts of the gut tube","points":["Foregut: pharynx to the second part of the duodenum (at the major papilla), plus liver, gallbladder, and pancreas. Artery: celiac trunk.","Midgut: distal duodenum to the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon. Artery: superior mesenteric (SMA).","Hindgut: distal third of the transverse colon to the anal canal above the pectinate line. Artery: inferior mesenteric (IMA).","Visceral pain is dull and poorly localized and refers to the midline by gut division: foregut to the epigastrium, midgut to the umbilicus, hindgut to the suprapubic region.","Early appendicitis hurts at the umbilicus (midgut, T10) before it moves to the right lower quadrant once the parietal peritoneum is irritated."]},{"h":"Midgut herniation and rotation","points":["In week 6 the midgut loop grows faster than the abdomen and herniates into the umbilical cord (physiologic herniation).","The loop rotates 270 degrees counterclockwise (viewed from the front) around the axis of the SMA: 90 degrees while herniated, 180 degrees as it returns.","The loops return to the abdomen by about week 10, and the cecum descends to the right lower quadrant.","Malrotation leaves the cecum high and Ladd bands crossing the duodenum, and the narrow mesentery can twist (midgut volvulus) with bilious vomiting in a newborn.","Omphalocele: herniated loops fail to return, so viscera sit at the umbilicus in a sac of peritoneum and amnion."]},{"h":"Foregut septation and glands","points":["In week 4 the respiratory diverticulum buds from the ventral foregut; the tracheoesophageal septum then separates trachea in front from esophagus behind.","A septum that deviates or fails gives esophageal atresia, tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF), or both.","Most common type: proximal esophageal atresia with a distal TEF, causing drooling, choking on first feeds, an NG tube that coils, and air in the stomach.","The hepatic diverticulum grows from the distal foregut into the septum transversum; endoderm forms hepatocytes and bile duct lining, and its caudal part forms the gallbladder and cystic duct.","Pancreas: dorsal bud forms most of the gland; ventral bud rotates behind the duodenum and forms the uncinate process and part of the head.","Annular pancreas: a bifid ventral bud wraps around the second part of the duodenum and can obstruct it.","Pancreas divisum: dorsal and ventral ducts fail to fuse, so most juice drains through the minor papilla."]},{"h":"Enteric nerves and duodenal lumen","points":["Neural crest cells migrate along the gut from cranial to caudal to form the myenteric and submucosal plexuses.","Hirschsprung disease: failed migration (often RET mutation) leaves a distal aganglionic segment that always includes the rectum and cannot relax.","Hirschsprung signs: no meconium in the first 48 hours, a dilated colon above the narrow segment, and an explosive stool after rectal exam; biopsy shows no ganglion cells.","The duodenal lumen is filled in by epithelial growth around weeks 5 to 6 and opens again (recanalizes) by the end of the embryonic period.","Failed recanalization gives duodenal atresia: bilious vomiting, a double bubble on X-ray, polyhydramnios, and a strong link to Down syndrome."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Gut tube divisions","cols":["Division","Adult structures","Artery","Parasympathetic supply","Pain referred to"],"rows":[["Foregut","Esophagus, stomach, duodenum to the major papilla, liver, gallbladder, pancreas","Celiac trunk","Vagus nerve","Epigastrium"],["Midgut","Distal duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, proximal two thirds of transverse colon","Superior mesenteric artery","Vagus nerve","Periumbilical region"],["Hindgut","Distal third of transverse colon, descending and sigmoid colon, rectum, anal canal above the pectinate line","Inferior mesenteric artery","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2 to S4)","Suprapubic region"]]},{"title":"Developmental errors and their signs","cols":["Defect","What went wrong","Classic finding"],"rows":[["Esophageal atresia with distal TEF","Tracheoesophageal septum deviates","Drooling and choking on first feed; gas in the stomach"],["Malrotation with volvulus","Incomplete 270 degree rotation","Bilious vomiting; Ladd bands over the duodenum"],["Omphalocele","Midgut fails to return from the cord","Midline sac covered by peritoneum and amnion"],["Annular pancreas","Bifid ventral bud encircles the duodenum","Duodenal obstruction in a newborn"],["Pancreas divisum","Dorsal and ventral ducts fail to fuse","Usually silent; minor papilla drains most juice"],["Duodenal atresia","Duodenal lumen fails to recanalize","Double bubble; linked to Down syndrome"],["Hirschsprung disease","Neural crest fails to reach the distal colon","Delayed meconium; aganglionic rectum"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"90 out, 180 back","text":"The midgut turns 90 degrees counterclockwise while it is in the cord and 180 degrees more as it comes home, for 270 degrees in all around the SMA."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"VACTERL","text":"Vertebral defects, Anal atresia, Cardiac defects, TracheoEsophageal fistula, Renal anomalies, Limb anomalies. A baby with a TEF gets checked for the rest."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Follow the artery to the pain","text":"Name the artery that feeds the organ and you know where its pain refers: celiac to the epigastrium, SMA to the umbilicus, IMA to the suprapubic area."}],"highYield":["Duodenal atresia with a double bubble points to Down syndrome; jejunal and ileal atresia come from a vascular accident instead.","Hirschsprung disease is also more common in Down syndrome, and the rectum is always involved.","Omphalocele is linked to trisomies 13 and 18 and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome; gastroschisis has no covering sac.","The H-type TEF (no atresia) shows up later as recurrent aspiration pneumonia.","An annular pancreas and duodenal atresia both obstruct the second part of the duodenum; the vomiting is bilious when the block lies below the major papilla, as it usually does.","The spleen comes from mesoderm in the dorsal mesogastrium yet is supplied by the celiac trunk (splenic artery)."],"pitfalls":["Omphalocele is midline and covered by a sac; gastroschisis is usually right of the umbilicus with bare bowel and no sac.","Malrotation is a rotation problem; omphalocele is a return problem. Both involve the midgut.","The foregut ends at the major duodenal papilla, so the duodenum above the papilla hurts like the stomach and the duodenum below it like the small bowel.","The ventral pancreatic bud forms the uncinate process; the dorsal bud forms the body and tail."],"dumps":[{"t":"Gut tube map","p":"Draw the gut tube from esophagus to anal canal. Mark where the foregut, midgut, and hindgut begin and end, and write each division's artery, nerve, and pain referral site beside it.","l":["Foregut ends at the major duodenal papilla","Midgut ends at the junction of the proximal two thirds and distal third of the transverse colon","Hindgut ends at the pectinate line","Celiac, SMA, and IMA matched to each division","Vagus to foregut and midgut; pelvic splanchnics to hindgut","Pain to epigastrium, umbilicus, and suprapubic region","Liver, gallbladder, and pancreas drawn as foregut buds"]},{"t":"Midgut rotation and its errors","p":"Draw the midgut loop in three panels: herniated into the cord at week 6, rotating, and back in the abdomen at week 10. Then list the defect that results when each step fails.","l":["SMA drawn as the axis of rotation","Herniation into the umbilical cord in week 6","90 degrees counterclockwise in the cord","180 degrees more during return, 270 in total","Return by about week 10 with the cecum moving to the right lower quadrant","Incomplete rotation gives malrotation with Ladd bands and volvulus risk","Failed return gives omphalocele in a peritoneal and amniotic sac"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Digestive System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract (section on congenital abnormalities)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Development & Embryology of the GI Tract: Part 1","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4it-uI9e_I8"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Development & Embryology of the GI Tract: Part 2","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PAMaaKocIsk"}],"related":["GI-5","GI-13-H","GI-13-H1","GI-13-H3","GI-13-F5","GI-13-B"]},"GI-2":{"id":"GI-2","summary":"The microscopic structure of the gut wall, stomach glands, small and large intestine, liver, pancreas, and salivary glands. Exam questions show a cell or a section and ask what it secretes, where it is, and what happens when it is damaged.","notes":[{"h":"Four layers of the gut wall","points":["Mucosa: epithelium, lamina propria, and a thin muscularis mucosae.","Submucosa: dense connective tissue with vessels and the submucosal (Meissner) plexus, which controls secretion and local blood flow.","Muscularis externa: inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle, with the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus between them controlling motility.","Outer layer: serosa (mesothelium) over intraperitoneal parts; adventitia over the thoracic esophagus and retroperitoneal parts."]},{"h":"Epithelium along the tube","points":["Esophagus: nonkeratinized stratified squamous to resist abrasion.","Stomach through colon: simple columnar for secretion and absorption.","Anal canal: columnar above the pectinate line and stratified squamous below it.","Barrett esophagus: chronic acid reflux turns distal esophageal squamous epithelium into intestinal-type columnar epithelium with goblet cells, which raises adenocarcinoma risk."]},{"h":"Gastric gland cells","points":["Parietal cells (body and fundus): pink, fried-egg look; secrete HCl and intrinsic factor.","Chief cells (base of body and fundus glands): basophilic; secrete pepsinogen.","Mucous neck cells: mucus; surface mucous cells also add bicarbonate.","G cells (antrum): gastrin. D cells: somatostatin. ECL cells: histamine.","Autoimmune gastritis destroys body and fundus mucosa, so acid, intrinsic factor, and pepsinogen all fall while the antrum is spared.","Without acid feedback, antral G cells keep releasing gastrin (hypergastrinemia), and lost intrinsic factor leads to vitamin B12 deficiency (pernicious anemia)."]},{"h":"Small intestine versus colon","points":["Duodenum: Brunner glands in the submucosa secrete alkaline mucus to neutralize acid.","Jejunum: tall, closely spaced plicae circulares and long villi; the main absorptive segment.","Ileum: Peyer patches (lymphoid follicles with overlying M cells) in the lamina propria and submucosa.","Small intestine crypts hold stem cells and Paneth cells (lysozyme and defensins).","Colon: no villi, straight crypts packed with goblet cells, and outer longitudinal muscle gathered into taeniae coli."]},{"h":"Liver lobule and acinus","points":["Classic lobule: a hexagon with a central vein in the middle and portal triads (portal vein branch, hepatic artery branch, bile duct) at the corners.","Blood runs from the triads through fenestrated sinusoids to the central vein; bile runs the other way in canaliculi toward the triad.","Space of Disse: the gap between hepatocytes and sinusoid lining, where plasma meets hepatocyte microvilli.","Kupffer cells are sinusoid macrophages; stellate (Ito) cells in the space of Disse store vitamin A and make collagen in fibrosis.","Acinus zones: zone 1 near the triad gets the most oxygen; zone 3 near the central vein gets the least and has the most cytochrome P450.","Zone 3 dies first in shock or heart failure and in acetaminophen overdose (toxic metabolite NAPQI made by P450)."]},{"h":"Pancreas and salivary glands","points":["Pancreatic acini: basophilic base and apical eosinophilic zymogen granules; centroacinar cells start the ducts.","Islets of Langerhans: pale clusters of endocrine cells scattered among the acini.","Serous acini stain dark and make watery protein-rich (amylase) secretion; mucous acini look pale and foamy.","Parotid is mainly serous, submandibular is mixed (mostly serous), and sublingual is mostly mucous."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Gastric gland cells","cols":["Cell","Location","Product","Loss causes"],"rows":[["Parietal","Body and fundus","HCl, intrinsic factor","Achlorhydria, B12 deficiency"],["Chief","Body and fundus, gland base","Pepsinogen","Low pepsin activity"],["Mucous neck and surface","Throughout stomach","Mucus, bicarbonate","Weaker mucosal barrier"],["G cell","Antrum","Gastrin","Less acid drive"],["D cell","Antrum and body","Somatostatin","Less brake on acid"],["ECL cell","Body and fundus","Histamine","Less acid drive"]]},{"title":"Telling gut segments apart","cols":["Segment","Epithelium","Key feature"],"rows":[["Esophagus","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous","Skeletal muscle above, smooth muscle below"],["Stomach body","Simple columnar","Gastric pits and glands with parietal and chief cells"],["Duodenum","Simple columnar with villi","Brunner glands in the submucosa"],["Jejunum","Simple columnar with villi","Tall plicae circulares"],["Ileum","Simple columnar with villi","Peyer patches"],["Colon","Simple columnar, no villi","Crypts rich in goblet cells; taeniae coli"]]},{"title":"Liver acinus zones","cols":["Zone","Position","Oxygen","Most vulnerable to"],"rows":[["Zone 1","Periportal","Highest","Viral hepatitis, toxins that arrive in portal blood"],["Zone 2","Middle","Intermediate","Yellow fever"],["Zone 3","Around the central vein","Lowest","Ischemia, acetaminophen, alcohol"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Brunner in the bulb","text":"Brunner glands mean duodenum. Think B for bicarbonate-rich mucus that buffers acid as it leaves the stomach."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Zone 3 is last in line","text":"Blood reaches zone 3 last, after the oxygen is used up, and zone 3 holds the most P450. Low oxygen and toxic drug metabolites both hit it first."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Parotid is pure","text":"The parotid is almost entirely serous, so its acini all stain dark. Pale foamy mucous acini mean submandibular or sublingual."}],"highYield":["Autoimmune gastritis: anti-parietal cell and anti-intrinsic factor antibodies, achlorhydria, high gastrin, and B12 deficiency.","Barrett esophagus is intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells and is the precursor of esophageal adenocarcinoma.","Stellate (Ito) cells drive liver fibrosis and cirrhosis by laying down collagen in the space of Disse.","Acetaminophen overdose causes centrilobular (zone 3) necrosis.","Peyer patches in the ileum are the entry point for some invasive bacteria (for example Salmonella Typhi) through M cells.","Paneth cells at crypt bases release defensins and lysozyme."],"pitfalls":["Meissner (submucosal) plexus controls secretion; Auerbach (myenteric) plexus controls motility.","Zone 1 is near the portal triad, and zone 3 is near the central vein; the lobule is drawn around the central vein while the acinus is drawn around the triad.","Autoimmune gastritis spares the antrum, so gastrin rises; H. pylori gastritis usually starts in the antrum.","Brunner glands sit in the submucosa; crypts of Lieberkuhn sit in the mucosa."],"dumps":[{"t":"Gut wall and gastric gland","p":"Draw a cross section of the stomach wall and enlarge one gastric gland beside it. Label the four layers, both nerve plexuses, and each gland cell with its product.","l":["Mucosa with epithelium, lamina propria, and muscularis mucosae","Submucosa with Meissner plexus","Muscularis externa with Auerbach plexus between layers","Serosa on the outside","Surface and mucous neck cells make mucus","Parietal cells make HCl and intrinsic factor","Chief cells at the base make pepsinogen","G cells in the antrum make gastrin"]},{"t":"Liver lobule and acinus","p":"Draw a hexagonal liver lobule. Label the central vein, portal triads, sinusoids, space of Disse, Kupffer cell, and stellate cell, then shade zones 1 to 3 of one acinus and write what injures each zone.","l":["Central vein in the center","Portal triad with portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile duct at the corners","Blood flows from triad to central vein","Bile flows from hepatocytes toward the triad","Space of Disse between hepatocytes and sinusoid lining","Kupffer cell in the sinusoid; stellate cell in the space of Disse","Zone 1 periportal, zone 3 around the central vein","Zone 3 hit by ischemia and acetaminophen"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Digestive Tract"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Organs Associated with the Digestive Tract"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder (section on patterns of hepatic injury)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Liver Histology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9CTyKLefn9E"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Hepatocytes and Portal Vein | Liver Histology | Dr Najeeb","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QEY_hCx-t4w"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Small intestine 1: Structure | Gastrointestinal system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UgnF1w-H8vw"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Hepatic lobule | Gastrointestinal system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nJfB32MtqBA"}],"related":["GI-1","GI-6","GI-13-G2","GI-13-G7","GI-13-E1","GI-13-C"]},"GI-3":{"id":"GI-3","summary":"The gross anatomy of the stomach, duodenum, liver, biliary tree, colon, and salivary glands, and which organs lie behind the peritoneum. Exam questions use location to explain where a stone blocks, where an injury bleeds, and which nerve is at risk.","notes":[{"h":"Stomach and duodenum","points":["Stomach parts: cardia, fundus, body, antrum, and pylorus, with a lesser and a greater curvature.","Duodenum is about 25 cm long and wraps in a C around the head of the pancreas.","First (superior) part: the duodenal bulb, the most common site of duodenal ulcers.","Second (descending) part: the major duodenal papilla, where bile and pancreatic juice enter.","Third (horizontal) part: crossed in front by the superior mesenteric vessels, with the aorta behind.","Fourth (ascending) part: ends at the duodenojejunal flexure, held by the suspensory ligament (of Treitz)."]},{"h":"Intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal","points":["Intraperitoneal organs hang on a mesentery and are wrapped in visceral peritoneum: stomach, jejunum, ileum, transverse and sigmoid colon, liver, spleen.","Retroperitoneal gut structures: duodenum parts 2 to 4, pancreas except the tail, ascending and descending colon, and part of the rectum.","Retroperitoneal organs are fixed to the back wall, so bleeding from them can collect without peritoneal signs.","The first part of the duodenum is mostly intraperitoneal; the pancreatic tail runs in the splenorenal ligament."]},{"h":"Liver and bile ducts","points":["Anatomic lobes: right and left (split by the falciform ligament), plus the caudate and quadrate lobes on the underside.","Bare area: the posterior surface touching the diaphragm, with no peritoneal cover.","Porta hepatis: the hilum where the portal vein and hepatic artery enter and the hepatic ducts leave.","Bile path: right and left hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct, joined by the cystic duct to form the common bile duct.","The common bile duct runs behind the first part of the duodenum and through the pancreatic head, then joins the main pancreatic duct at the ampulla of Vater.","The sphincter of Oddi controls flow from the ampulla into the second part of the duodenum."]},{"h":"Colon and appendix","points":["Colon has three taeniae coli (bands of longitudinal muscle), haustra (sacculations), and omental appendices (fat tags); small intestine has none of these.","The taeniae converge at the base of the appendix, which surgeons follow to find it.","McBurney point: one third of the way from the right anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus, the surface mark for the appendix base."]},{"h":"Salivary glands","points":["Parotid: largest, in front of the ear over the masseter; its duct crosses the masseter, pierces the buccinator, and opens opposite the upper second molar.","Submandibular: under the mandible; its duct opens beside the lingual frenulum. Most salivary stones form here.","Sublingual: in the floor of the mouth, with many small ducts.","The facial nerve (CN VII) runs through the parotid and splits into its five branches there, so a parotid tumor or surgery can cause facial weakness."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Duodenum by part","cols":["Part","Peritoneal status","Key relation or feature"],"rows":[["First (superior)","Mostly intraperitoneal","Duodenal bulb; gastroduodenal artery behind it"],["Second (descending)","Retroperitoneal","Major papilla with the ampulla of Vater"],["Third (horizontal)","Retroperitoneal","Between the superior mesenteric vessels in front and the aorta behind"],["Fourth (ascending)","Retroperitoneal","Duodenojejunal flexure and suspensory ligament"]]},{"title":"Salivary glands","cols":["Gland","Secretion type","Duct opening","Parasympathetic supply"],"rows":[["Parotid","Serous","Opposite upper second molar","CN IX (via otic ganglion)"],["Submandibular","Mixed, mostly serous","Sublingual papilla beside the frenulum","CN VII (chorda tympani, submandibular ganglion)"],["Sublingual","Mixed, mostly mucous","Many small ducts in the floor of the mouth","CN VII (chorda tympani, submandibular ganglion)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"SAD PUCKER","text":"Retroperitoneal structures: Suprarenal glands, Aorta and IVC, Duodenum (parts 2 to 4), Pancreas (except tail), Ureters, Colon (ascending and descending), Kidneys, Esophagus (lower part), Rectum (partly)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Follow the bands","text":"Taeniae coli mean colon. Trace them down the cecum and they meet at the base of the appendix, wherever its tip has wandered."},{"kind":"trick","label":"One stone, two blocks","text":"A stone stuck at the ampulla blocks the common bile duct and the pancreatic duct at once, so jaundice and pancreatitis can arrive together."}],"highYield":["A stone in the common bile duct gives obstructive jaundice; a stone at the ampulla can also cause gallstone pancreatitis.","Pancreatic head cancer compresses the common bile duct and causes painless jaundice with a palpable gallbladder (Courvoisier sign).","Compression of the third part of the duodenum between the SMA and aorta (SMA syndrome) can follow rapid weight loss.","Facial nerve weakness with a parotid mass suggests a malignant tumor; the common pleomorphic adenoma rarely affects the nerve.","Blunt trauma can crush the retroperitoneal duodenum or pancreas against the spine.","The appendix base is constant at the taeniae meeting point; the tip is often retrocecal."],"pitfalls":["The major papilla (bile and main pancreatic duct) is in the second part of the duodenum; the minor papilla, slightly higher, drains the accessory duct.","Ascending and descending colon are retroperitoneal; transverse and sigmoid colon have mesenteries and are intraperitoneal.","The bare area of the liver touches the diaphragm directly, with no peritoneum between them.","The facial nerve passes through the parotid without supplying it; the parotid gets secretomotor fibers from CN IX."],"dumps":[{"t":"Biliary tree","p":"Draw the liver, gallbladder, duodenum, and pancreas. Trace bile from the hepatic ducts into the duodenum and label every duct, the ampulla, and the sphincter of Oddi.","l":["Right and left hepatic ducts join as the common hepatic duct","Cystic duct from the gallbladder","Common bile duct formed by common hepatic plus cystic duct","Common bile duct passes behind the first part of the duodenum","Runs through the head of the pancreas","Joins the main pancreatic duct at the ampulla of Vater","Opens at the major papilla in the second part of the duodenum","Sphincter of Oddi around the ampulla"]},{"t":"Peritoneal status table","p":"Build a two-column table: intraperitoneal on one side and retroperitoneal on the other. Place every gut organ, then circle the ones that change status partway along.","l":["Stomach, jejunum, ileum intraperitoneal","Transverse and sigmoid colon intraperitoneal","Liver and spleen intraperitoneal","Duodenum parts 2 to 4 retroperitoneal","Pancreas retroperitoneal except the tail","Ascending and descending colon retroperitoneal","Duodenum circled: first part mostly intraperitoneal","Rectum circled: upper and middle thirds covered in front, lower third below the peritoneum"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen (sections on the abdominal viscera)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head (section on the parotid region)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Digestive System Anatomy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1-m3z_d531E"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Salivary glands - Anatomy and Physiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EYZPE5-7vZ8"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Biliary tree | Gastrointestinal system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jmfcACEGW-0"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Colon, rectum, and anus | Gastrointestinal system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lzr9GGjh6YQ"}],"related":["GI-4","GI-5","GI-13-A1","GI-13-D2","GI-13-G1","GI-13-K6"]},"GI-4":{"id":"GI-4","summary":"Where the gut sits in the mouth, mediastinum, and abdominopelvic cavity: the path and narrowings of the esophagus, nerve supply of the tongue, peritoneal sacs and ligaments, the inguinal region, and the low spaces where fluid pools. Exam questions give a lesion or a collection and ask which structure or space is involved.","notes":[{"h":"Esophagus through the thorax","points":["Starts at the cricoid cartilage (C6) as a continuation of the pharynx.","Runs through the superior and then posterior mediastinum, behind the trachea and the left atrium.","Passes through the esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm at T10 with the vagal trunks.","Three narrowings: the cricopharyngeus (upper esophageal sphincter), the crossing of the aortic arch and left main bronchus, and the diaphragmatic hiatus.","Swallowed objects and pills tend to lodge at these narrowings, and an enlarged left atrium can press on the esophagus."]},{"h":"Tongue innervation","points":["Anterior two thirds: touch by CN V3 (lingual nerve); taste by CN VII (chorda tympani riding with the lingual nerve).","Posterior third: touch and taste by CN IX.","Motor: CN XII to all tongue muscles except palatoglossus, which is CN X.","A lower motor neuron CN XII lesion makes the protruded tongue deviate toward the injured side, with atrophy and fasciculations on that side.","Lingual nerve injury above where the chorda tympani joins it removes touch only; injury below the junction removes both touch and taste from the anterior two thirds."]},{"h":"Peritoneal sacs and ligaments","points":["Greater sac: most of the peritoneal cavity. Lesser sac (omental bursa): behind the stomach and lesser omentum.","The two sacs connect through the omental (epiploic) foramen, whose front edge is the hepatoduodenal ligament.","Lesser omentum: hepatogastric (gastrohepatic) plus hepatoduodenal ligaments. Greater omentum hangs from the greater curvature.","Hepatoduodenal ligament carries the portal triad: portal vein behind, proper hepatic artery in front on the left, common bile duct in front on the right.","Squeezing this ligament (Pringle maneuver) controls bleeding from the liver."]},{"h":"Inguinal region and hernias","points":["The inguinal canal runs from the deep ring (in transversalis fascia, lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels) to the superficial ring (in external oblique aponeurosis).","Hesselbach triangle: inferior epigastric vessels laterally, rectus abdominis medially, inguinal ligament below.","Indirect hernia: enters the deep ring lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels through a patent processus vaginalis and can reach the scrotum; most common type, often in young males.","Direct hernia: pushes through a weak Hesselbach triangle medial to the vessels; usually older men.","Femoral hernia: below the inguinal ligament through the femoral canal; more common in women and most likely to strangulate."]},{"h":"Where fluid collects","points":["Supine: the hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch) is the lowest part of the upper abdomen, so blood and pus pool there.","Upright: the rectouterine pouch (Douglas) in women or the rectovesical pouch in men is the lowest point of the peritoneal cavity.","Subphrenic spaces under the diaphragm collect abscesses after perforation or surgery, sometimes with shoulder-tip pain from phrenic nerve irritation.","The paracolic gutters let fluid travel between the pelvis and the upper abdomen."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Peritoneal ligaments and what they carry","cols":["Ligament","Connects","Contents"],"rows":[["Falciform","Liver to anterior abdominal wall","Ligamentum teres (old umbilical vein), paraumbilical veins"],["Hepatoduodenal","Liver to duodenum","Portal vein, proper hepatic artery, common bile duct"],["Gastrohepatic","Liver to lesser curvature","Left and right gastric vessels"],["Gastrocolic","Greater curvature to transverse colon","Gastroepiploic (gastro-omental) vessels"],["Gastrosplenic","Greater curvature to spleen","Short gastric and left gastroepiploic vessels"],["Splenorenal","Spleen to posterior abdominal wall","Splenic artery and vein, tail of pancreas"]]},{"title":"Groin hernias","cols":["Type","Path","Relation to inferior epigastric vessels","Typical patient"],"rows":[["Indirect inguinal","Deep ring, canal, superficial ring, may reach scrotum","Lateral","Infants and young males; most common overall"],["Direct inguinal","Through Hesselbach triangle to superficial ring","Medial","Older men"],["Femoral","Femoral canal below the inguinal ligament","Below the inguinal ligament","Women; highest strangulation risk"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"I 8 10 EGGs AT 12","text":"Diaphragm openings: IVC at T8; Esophagus and vaGus at T10; Aorta, Thoracic duct, and azygos at T12."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"MDs don't LIe","text":"Medial to the inferior epigastric vessels is a Direct hernia; Lateral is Indirect."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Lick your wounds","text":"A hypoglossal lesion pushes the tongue toward the hurt side, because the healthy genioglossus pushes forward alone."}],"highYield":["Gag reflex: afferent CN IX, efferent CN X.","An indirect inguinal hernia follows the path of testicular descent and is covered by all three layers of spermatic fascia.","In a supine trauma patient, free fluid on a FAST exam most often shows up first in the hepatorenal recess.","A ruptured appendix can track pus up the right paracolic gutter to the right subphrenic space.","The omental foramen lies behind the hepatoduodenal ligament and in front of the IVC.","Third molar extraction can injure the lingual nerve and cause loss of touch and taste on the front of the tongue."],"pitfalls":["Palatoglossus is the one tongue muscle supplied by CN X; every other tongue muscle is CN XII.","Taste and touch on the anterior two thirds travel in different nerves (VII and V3), while the posterior third uses CN IX for both.","Femoral hernias are more common in women than in men, yet indirect inguinal hernia is still the most common hernia in women.","The hepatorenal recess is lowest when lying flat; the rectouterine or rectovesical pouch is lowest when upright."],"dumps":[{"t":"Inguinal region","p":"Draw the inner surface of the lower anterior abdominal wall. Label Hesselbach triangle, the deep ring, the inferior epigastric vessels, the inguinal ligament, and the femoral canal, then draw arrows for direct, indirect, and femoral hernias.","l":["Inferior epigastric vessels as the lateral border of Hesselbach triangle","Rectus abdominis as the medial border","Inguinal ligament as the inferior border","Deep ring lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels","Indirect arrow through the deep ring","Direct arrow through the triangle","Femoral arrow below the inguinal ligament, medial to the femoral vein"]},{"t":"Tongue nerve map","p":"Draw the tongue from above, divided into anterior two thirds and posterior third. Label the nerve for touch, taste, and movement in each region, and write the deficit from a CN XII lesion and a lingual nerve lesion.","l":["Anterior two thirds touch: CN V3 lingual nerve","Anterior two thirds taste: CN VII chorda tympani","Posterior third touch and taste: CN IX","Motor: CN XII to all but palatoglossus","Palatoglossus: CN X","CN XII lesion: deviation toward the lesion with atrophy","Lingual nerve lesion: loss of touch and taste on the anterior two thirds"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen (sections on the inguinal region and the peritoneum)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax (section on the posterior mediastinum)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head (section on the oral region and tongue)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"The Oesophagus (Esophagus) - Clinical Anatomy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BZ0JBiPeek8"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Esophagus | Gastrointestinal system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wU9_daCwr_w"}],"related":["GI-3","GI-5","GI-13-F3","GI-13-I","GI-13-I2","GI-13-H2"]},"GI-5":{"id":"GI-5","summary":"The arteries that feed the gut, the portal vein that drains it, the shortcuts portal blood takes when the liver is blocked, and the two blood supplies of the anal canal and liver. Exam questions ask which vessel bleeds, which segment of colon dies in shock, and where varices form.","notes":[{"h":"Celiac trunk","points":["Leaves the aorta at about T12 and splits into the left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries.","Left gastric supplies the lesser curvature and lower esophagus.","Splenic runs along the top of the pancreas and gives short gastric, left gastroepiploic, and pancreatic branches.","Common hepatic gives the proper hepatic artery (to the liver and, through the cystic artery, the gallbladder) and the gastroduodenal artery.","The gastroduodenal artery runs behind the first part of the duodenum, so a posterior duodenal ulcer can erode it and cause major bleeding."]},{"h":"Superior and inferior mesenteric arteries","points":["SMA (about L1) supplies the distal duodenum to the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon through jejunal, ileal, ileocolic, right colic, and middle colic branches.","IMA (about L3) supplies the distal transverse colon to the upper rectum through left colic, sigmoid, and superior rectal branches.","The marginal artery runs along the colon and links SMA and IMA branches.","Watershed zones sit where two territories meet: the splenic flexure (SMA and IMA) and the rectosigmoid junction (sigmoid and superior rectal arteries).","Hypotension starves these watershed zones first and causes ischemic colitis: cramping pain followed by bloody diarrhea."]},{"h":"Portal venous system","points":["The superior mesenteric vein and splenic vein join behind the neck of the pancreas to form the portal vein; the inferior mesenteric vein usually drains into the splenic vein.","The portal vein rises in the hepatoduodenal ligament to the porta hepatis.","Portal blood mixes with hepatic arterial blood in the sinusoids, drains to central veins, then hepatic veins, then the IVC.","Portal hypertension (often from cirrhosis) forces blood through portosystemic anastomoses back to the systemic veins."]},{"h":"Portosystemic anastomoses","points":["Esophagus: left gastric vein to esophageal veins draining to the azygos system. Result: esophageal varices that can bleed massively.","Umbilicus: paraumbilical veins to superficial veins of the abdominal wall. Result: caput medusae.","Rectum: superior rectal vein (portal) to middle and inferior rectal veins (systemic). Result: anorectal varices."]},{"h":"Anal canal above and below the pectinate line","points":["Above: columnar epithelium, superior rectal artery, portal drainage, internal iliac nodes, visceral (painless) nerves.","Below: stratified squamous epithelium, inferior rectal artery, systemic drainage, superficial inguinal nodes, somatic pudendal nerve (painful).","Internal hemorrhoids arise above the line and bleed without pain; external hemorrhoids arise below and hurt."]},{"h":"Two blood supplies to the liver","points":["About 75% of liver blood flow comes from the portal vein and about 25% from the hepatic artery; each supplies roughly half of the oxygen.","Because two sources feed the liver, a block in one rarely causes hepatic infarction.","Bile ducts depend on the hepatic artery alone, so hepatic artery thrombosis (for example after liver transplant) causes bile duct ischemia and strictures."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Gut arteries","cols":["Artery","Aortic level","Main branches","Territory"],"rows":[["Celiac trunk","T12","Left gastric, splenic, common hepatic","Foregut: lower esophagus to the second part of the duodenum, liver, gallbladder, pancreas, spleen"],["Superior mesenteric","L1","Inferior pancreaticoduodenal, jejunal, ileal, ileocolic, right colic, middle colic","Midgut: distal duodenum to proximal two thirds of transverse colon"],["Inferior mesenteric","L3","Left colic, sigmoid, superior rectal","Hindgut: distal third of transverse colon to upper rectum"]]},{"title":"Portosystemic anastomoses","cols":["Site","Portal side","Systemic side","Clinical sign"],"rows":[["Lower esophagus","Left gastric vein","Esophageal veins to azygos","Esophageal varices"],["Umbilicus","Paraumbilical veins","Superficial and inferior epigastric veins","Caput medusae"],["Rectum","Superior rectal vein","Middle and inferior rectal veins","Anorectal varices"]]},{"title":"Anal canal by the pectinate line","cols":["Feature","Above the line","Below the line"],"rows":[["Epithelium","Simple columnar","Stratified squamous"],["Artery","Superior rectal (from IMA)","Inferior rectal (from internal pudendal)"],["Venous drainage","Superior rectal to IMV to portal vein","Inferior rectal to internal iliac to IVC"],["Lymph drainage","Internal iliac nodes","Superficial inguinal nodes"],["Pain","Visceral, so internal hemorrhoids are painless","Somatic (pudendal), so external hemorrhoids hurt"],["Typical cancer","Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Three exits for trapped portal blood","text":"Portal blood escapes at the top (esophagus), the middle (umbilicus), and the bottom (rectum) of the gut. Varices, caput medusae, and anorectal varices are the same problem at three sites."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Watershed = border towns","text":"Ischemia hits where two arterial territories meet and neither supplies much: splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Posterior bleeds, anterior perforates","text":"A posterior duodenal ulcer erodes the gastroduodenal artery and bleeds; an anterior ulcer perforates into the peritoneal cavity and gives free air."}],"highYield":["Massive upper GI bleeding from a posterior duodenal ulcer comes from the gastroduodenal artery.","A gastric ulcer on the lesser curvature can erode the left gastric artery.","Ischemic colitis after hypotension favors the splenic flexure; the rectum is spared by its dual supply.","Acute mesenteric ischemia (often an embolus to the SMA in atrial fibrillation) causes pain out of proportion to the exam.","Bleeding esophageal varices are a leading cause of death in cirrhosis.","Anal cancer below the pectinate line spreads to superficial inguinal nodes."],"pitfalls":["Anorectal varices come from portal hypertension; internal hemorrhoids are enlarged anal vascular cushions with other causes such as straining.","The inferior mesenteric vein drains into the splenic vein, while the SMV and splenic vein form the portal vein.","The portal vein brings most of the liver's blood flow, yet the hepatic artery is the only supply to the bile ducts.","The superior rectal artery comes from the IMA; the middle and inferior rectal arteries come from the internal iliac system."],"dumps":[{"t":"Arterial supply of the gut","p":"Draw the abdominal aorta with the celiac trunk, SMA, and IMA. Draw each main branch to its organ, then circle the two watershed zones of the colon.","l":["Celiac trunk at T12 with left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries","Gastroduodenal artery behind the first part of the duodenum","SMA at L1 with jejunal, ileal, ileocolic, right colic, and middle colic arteries","IMA at L3 with left colic, sigmoid, and superior rectal arteries","Marginal artery connecting SMA and IMA along the colon","Splenic flexure circled as a watershed zone","Rectosigmoid junction circled as a watershed zone"]},{"t":"Portal system and its escape routes","p":"Draw the portal vein forming behind the pancreas and entering the liver. Then draw the three portosystemic anastomoses and write the sign each one produces in portal hypertension.","l":["SMV and splenic vein join behind the pancreatic neck","IMV drains into the splenic vein","Portal vein in the hepatoduodenal ligament to the porta hepatis","Sinusoids to central veins to hepatic veins to IVC","Left gastric to esophageal veins to azygos: esophageal varices","Paraumbilical to abdominal wall veins: caput medusae","Superior rectal to middle and inferior rectal veins: anorectal varices"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen (sections on the vasculature of the abdominal viscera and the hepatic portal system)"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Liver Disease (section on portal hypertension)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Section on hepatic blood flow"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Liver Circulation & Portal Hypertension","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CqtQALXskDU"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Liver | Gastrointestinal system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rDjWrNRKfvg"}],"related":["GI-1","GI-4","GI-13-C5","GI-13-J1","GI-13-J2","GI-13-J3"]},"GI-6":{"id":"GI-6","summary":"How food is chewed, swallowed, moved, broken down, and absorbed, how acid and gut hormones are controlled, and how defecation and vomiting work. Exam questions give a meal, a drug, or a lesion and ask you to predict the hormone, secretion, or acid-base change that follows.","notes":[{"h":"Chewing and swallowing","points":["Masseter, temporalis, medial and lateral pterygoids chew; all are supplied by CN V3.","Oral phase is voluntary: the tongue pushes the bolus into the pharynx.","Pharyngeal and esophageal phases are reflexes run by the swallowing center in the medulla: the soft palate seals the nose, the larynx rises, breathing pauses, and the upper esophageal sphincter opens.","Vagal inhibitory neurons release nitric oxide and VIP to relax the lower esophageal sphincter and the proximal stomach (receptive relaxation)."]},{"h":"Slow waves, peristalsis, and the MMC","points":["Interstitial cells of Cajal make slow waves that set the maximum contraction rate (about 3 per minute in the stomach, about 12 per minute in the duodenum).","Contraction happens only when spike potentials fire on top of a slow wave; ACh and stretch make this more likely.","Peristalsis needs the myenteric plexus: contraction above the bolus (ACh, substance P) and relaxation below it (VIP, nitric oxide).","Between meals, motilin from M cells drives the migrating motor complex about every 90 minutes to sweep the stomach and small bowel clean; eating stops it."]},{"h":"Gastric acid control","points":["Parietal cell stimulators: ACh (M3), gastrin (CCK-B), and histamine (H2); each makes the others stronger.","Gastrin also acts by making ECL cells release histamine.","Final step: the H+/K+ ATPase pumps H+ into the lumen in exchange for K+.","Somatostatin from D cells, released when antral pH falls, inhibits G cells and parietal cells.","Cephalic phase (about 30%): sight, smell, and chewing drive the vagus, which releases ACh and GRP (gastrin releasing peptide).","Gastric phase (about 60%): stretch and peptides in the stomach release gastrin.","Intestinal phase (about 10%): protein in the duodenum releases a little gastrin, but acid and fat in the duodenum mainly inhibit acid through secretin, GIP, and nerve reflexes."]},{"h":"Digestion of carbohydrate, protein, and fat","points":["Salivary and pancreatic amylase cut starch into maltose, maltotriose, and limit dextrins; brush border enzymes (lactase, sucrase, maltase) finish to monosaccharides.","Glucose and galactose enter by SGLT1 with sodium; fructose by GLUT5; all leave the cell by GLUT2.","Enteropeptidase on the duodenal brush border turns trypsinogen into trypsin, which activates the other pancreatic proteases.","Amino acids and di- and tripeptides are absorbed; peptides are split to amino acids inside the cell.","Bile salts emulsify fat; pancreatic lipase with colipase makes fatty acids and monoglycerides, which ride in micelles to the brush border.","Enterocytes rebuild triglycerides and pack them into chylomicrons that enter the lacteals (lymph)."]},{"h":"Defecation and the gastrocolic reflex","points":["Rectal stretch triggers reflex relaxation of the internal anal sphincter (smooth muscle, myenteric plexus) and the urge to defecate.","The external anal sphincter (skeletal muscle, pudendal nerve S2 to S4) stays closed by choice until the time is right.","Gastrocolic reflex: a filling stomach increases colonic mass movements, which is why the urge often comes after meals.","Pudendal nerve injury weakens the external sphincter and gives urge incontinence; internal sphincter damage gives passive leaking at rest."]},{"h":"Vomiting","points":["The chemoreceptor trigger zone sits in the area postrema, outside the blood-brain barrier, and senses drugs and toxins in blood (D2, 5-HT3, NK1 receptors).","It signals the vomiting center in the medulla, which also gets input from gut vagal afferents, the vestibular system, and the cortex.","Prolonged vomiting loses HCl, causing hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis.","Volume loss turns on aldosterone, which wastes K+ and H+ in the urine, causing hypokalemia and keeping the alkalosis going."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Gut hormones","cols":["Hormone","Cell and site","Main stimulus","Main actions"],"rows":[["Gastrin","G cells, antrum and duodenum","Peptides and amino acids, stomach stretch, vagus (GRP)","Raises acid secretion, mucosal growth, gastric motility"],["Cholecystokinin (CCK)","I cells, duodenum and jejunum","Fatty acids and amino acids","Pancreatic enzyme release, gallbladder contraction, sphincter of Oddi relaxation, slower gastric emptying"],["Secretin","S cells, duodenum","Acid (and fatty acids) in the duodenum","Pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate, lower gastric acid"],["GIP","K cells, duodenum and jejunum","Glucose, fatty acids, amino acids","Insulin release, lower gastric acid"],["Motilin","M cells, duodenum and jejunum","Fasting","Migrating motor complex"],["Somatostatin","D cells, stomach and duodenum","Low pH","Inhibits gastrin, acid, and most gut hormones"]]},{"title":"Response to a meal that puts acid and fat into the duodenum","cols":["Signal","Change","Effect"],"rows":[["Secretin","Rises","Bicarbonate from pancreas neutralizes acid"],["CCK","Rises","Enzymes and bile arrive; stomach empties more slowly"],["GIP","Rises","Insulin release; less gastric acid"],["Gastric acid secretion","Falls","Less acid sent to the duodenum"],["Motilin and MMC","Fall","Fasting sweep is switched off"]]},{"title":"Digestion by nutrient","cols":["Nutrient","Key enzymes","Absorbed as","Into"],"rows":[["Carbohydrate","Salivary and pancreatic amylase, brush border disaccharidases","Glucose, galactose (SGLT1), fructose (GLUT5)","Portal blood"],["Protein","Pepsin, trypsin (activated by enteropeptidase), other pancreatic proteases, brush border peptidases","Amino acids, di- and tripeptides","Portal blood"],["Fat","Pancreatic lipase with colipase, aided by bile salts","Fatty acids and monoglycerides in micelles, packed into chylomicrons","Lacteals (lymph)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Cell letters and their hormones","text":"G cells make Gastrin, S cells make Secretin, and M cells make Motilin, so those letters match. K cells make GIP and I cells make CCK, so those two need extra practice."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Three keys, one lock","text":"ACh, gastrin, and histamine are three keys that all open the same lock, the H+/K+ ATPase. Block the pump and all three lose their effect."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Lose acid, lose volume, lose potassium","text":"Vomiting removes HCl (alkalosis, low chloride), shrinks volume (aldosterone), and aldosterone makes the kidney dump K+ and H+ (low potassium, worse alkalosis)."}],"highYield":["Achalasia: loss of inhibitory (NO and VIP) myenteric neurons, so the lower esophageal sphincter cannot relax and peristalsis is lost.","Erythromycin acts on motilin receptors and speeds gastric emptying.","Enteropeptidase deficiency leaves trypsinogen inactive and causes protein malabsorption.","Oral glucose releases more insulin than the same glucose given by vein because gut incretins (GIP and GLP-1) add to the insulin response.","Fat malabsorption causes steatorrhea and deficiency of vitamins A, D, E, and K.","Bile salts are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum; ileal resection causes bile salt loss and fat malabsorption.","In prolonged vomiting, urine chloride is low and the alkalosis corrects with saline."],"pitfalls":["Spike potentials riding on slow waves trigger contraction; a slow wave by itself only sets the rhythm.","Secretin brings bicarbonate; CCK brings enzymes and bile.","The internal anal sphincter relaxes by reflex; only the external sphincter is under voluntary control.","Most K+ lost in vomiting leaves through the kidney; gastric fluid itself holds little K+.","The intestinal phase is mostly inhibitory even though a small amount of gastrin comes from the duodenum."],"dumps":[{"t":"Parietal cell control","p":"Draw a parietal cell with its receptors and the H+/K+ ATPase. Add the G cell, ECL cell, D cell, and vagal fiber around it, and use arrows to show what turns acid on and off.","l":["M3 receptor for ACh from the vagus","CCK-B receptor for gastrin","H2 receptor for histamine from ECL cells","Gastrin also drives ECL histamine release","H+/K+ ATPase on the luminal side","Bicarbonate leaving the basolateral side (alkaline tide)","D cell somatostatin inhibits G cells and parietal cells","Vagal GRP stimulates G cells"]},{"t":"From meal to absorption","p":"Trace one meal with starch, protein, and fat from mouth to enterocyte. For each nutrient, write the enzymes, the hormones released, and how the product is absorbed.","l":["Salivary amylase starts starch digestion","Pancreatic amylase and brush border disaccharidases finish carbohydrate","SGLT1, GLUT5, and GLUT2 for monosaccharides","Enteropeptidase activates trypsinogen, trypsin activates the rest","Amino acids and small peptides absorbed","CCK releases enzymes and bile; secretin releases bicarbonate","Bile salts and lipase with colipase form micelles","Chylomicrons enter the lacteals"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Propulsion and Mixing of Food in the Alimentary Tract"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Digestion and Absorption in the Gastrointestinal Tract"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Gastric Secretion: The Cephalic & Gastric Phase","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NIbclTo3duU"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | GI Motility of the Esophagus & Stomach","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DXPwCphPsmU"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Gastric Acid Physiology (Secretion, Ulcers, Acid Reflux and Treatment)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rcb6I7gsl-Y"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Gastrointestinal Motility | GI Motility | Dr Najeeb🩺","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pWGhGx_mNXc"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Small intestine 2: Digestion | Gastrointestinal system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nLCNoT79_-o"}],"related":["GI-7","GI-8","GI-13-F1","GI-13-E","GI-13-E4","GI-13-K3"]},"GI-7":{"id":"GI-7","summary":"How the end products of digestion (monosaccharides, amino acids and small peptides, fatty acids and monoglycerides, vitamins, and minerals) cross the intestinal wall, where each is absorbed, and how bile acids recycle. The exam asks you to predict what goes wrong when a transporter is missing, a gut segment is diseased or removed, or a toxin turns on secretion.","notes":[{"h":"Carbohydrates","points":["Only monosaccharides are absorbed; brush border lactase, sucrase-isomaltase, and maltase finish digestion.","SGLT1 (apical) moves glucose and galactose in with 2 Na+ (secondary active transport).","GLUT5 (apical) lets fructose in by facilitated diffusion, no sodium needed.","GLUT2 (basolateral) carries all three monosaccharides out to portal blood.","Oral rehydration solution: glucose pulls Na+ in through SGLT1, and water follows osmotically.","SGLT1 keeps working in cholera because the toxin does not block it, so ORS still works."]},{"h":"Proteins","points":["Enteropeptidase (enterokinase) activates trypsinogen; trypsin activates the other pancreatic proteases.","Free amino acids enter enterocytes on several Na+-linked carriers grouped by amino acid class.","Dipeptides and tripeptides enter on PepT1 with H+, then cytosolic peptidases split them.","Hartnup disease: autosomal recessive defect of the neutral amino acid transporter in gut and proximal tubule.","Lost tryptophan means less niacin made, so pellagra-like rash, diarrhea, and ataxia appear; urine shows neutral aminoaciduria.","Dipeptide uptake on PepT1 is spared, which is why patients still absorb some tryptophan."]},{"h":"Fats","points":["Pancreatic lipase plus colipase splits triglyceride into 2-monoglyceride and free fatty acids.","Bile acids form micelles that carry these products to the brush border.","In the enterocyte smooth ER, they are re-esterified to triglyceride.","MTP loads triglyceride onto apoB-48 to make chylomicrons, which exit into lacteals and the thoracic duct.","Short and medium chain fatty acids skip chylomicrons and go straight to portal blood.","Abetalipoproteinemia (autosomal recessive MTP defect): no chylomicrons or VLDL, fat-filled enterocytes, steatorrhea.","Abetalipoproteinemia also gives acanthocytes and vitamin E deficiency with ataxia and retinal degeneration."]},{"h":"Where things are absorbed","points":["Duodenum: iron (Fe2+ on DMT1) and active calcium uptake.","Jejunum: folate and most sugars, amino acids, and fats.","Terminal ileum: vitamin B12 bound to intrinsic factor, and bile acids.","Duodenal disease (celiac disease) causes iron deficiency first.","Ileal resection or Crohn ileitis causes B12 deficiency and bile acid loss."]},{"h":"Enterohepatic circulation","points":["About 95% of bile acids are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum by the apical Na+-bile acid cotransporter (ASBT).","Bile acids return in portal blood and the liver re-secretes them; the pool cycles several times a day.","Short ileal loss (under about 100 cm): bile acids reach the colon and cause watery bile acid diarrhea.","Long ileal loss (over about 100 cm): the pool runs out and steatorrhea follows.","Fewer bile acids per unit of cholesterol in bile lets cholesterol crystallize, so cholesterol gallstones form."]},{"h":"Three kinds of diarrhea","points":["Secretory: cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates Gs, cAMP rises, PKA opens CFTR, and Cl- with water pours out.","Osmotic: unabsorbed solute (lactose in lactase deficiency) holds water in the lumen.","Inflammatory: mucosal damage with blood, leukocytes, and often fever (invasive bacteria, IBD).","Stool osmotic gap = 290 minus 2 x (stool Na+ plus K+), in mOsm/kg.","Gap below about 50 means secretory; gap above about 100 to 125 means osmotic.","Fasting stops osmotic diarrhea; secretory diarrhea continues."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Monosaccharide transporters","cols":["Transporter","Membrane","Carries","Mechanism"],"rows":[["SGLT1","Apical","Glucose, galactose","Secondary active, with 2 Na+"],["GLUT5","Apical","Fructose","Facilitated diffusion"],["GLUT2","Basolateral","Glucose, galactose, fructose","Facilitated diffusion to blood"]]},{"title":"Absorption sites and what happens when they fail","cols":["Nutrient","Main site","Deficiency after loss"],"rows":[["Iron","Duodenum","Microcytic anemia"],["Calcium","Duodenum","Low bone mineral, secondary hyperparathyroidism"],["Folate","Jejunum","Megaloblastic anemia with normal methylmalonic acid"],["Vitamin B12","Terminal ileum","Megaloblastic anemia with neuropathy"],["Bile acids","Terminal ileum","Bile acid diarrhea, then steatorrhea and fat-soluble vitamin loss"]]},{"title":"Diarrhea types","cols":["Type","Classic cause","Stool osmotic gap","With fasting","Stool findings"],"rows":[["Secretory","Cholera toxin, ETEC, VIPoma","Low (under about 50 mOsm/kg)","Continues","Large volume, watery"],["Osmotic","Lactase deficiency, laxatives","High (over about 100 to 125 mOsm/kg)","Stops","Acidic stool in lactose intolerance"],["Inflammatory","Shigella, Campylobacter, IBD","Variable","Often continues","Blood, leukocytes, mucus"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Iron Fist, Bro","text":"Iron in the duodenum, Folate in the jejunum, B12 in the ileum, going down the gut in order."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Ride the sodium","text":"Glucose and sodium enter together on SGLT1, so giving both drags water in. That is the whole logic of oral rehydration."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Fast them","text":"If the diarrhea stops when the patient stops eating, the cause is something in the food (osmotic). If it keeps going, the gut is secreting."}],"highYield":["Cholera toxin raises cAMP and opens CFTR; SGLT1 still works, so oral rehydration with glucose and sodium saves lives.","Hartnup disease: neutral amino acid transporter loss gives pellagra-like findings that respond to niacin.","Abetalipoproteinemia: steatorrhea, acanthocytes, very low cholesterol and triglyceride, and vitamin E deficiency.","Chylomicrons enter lacteals; medium chain triglycerides go to portal blood and help in fat malabsorption.","Terminal ileal resection: B12 deficiency, bile acid diarrhea or steatorrhea, and cholesterol gallstones.","Fat malabsorption also raises oxalate absorption, because free fatty acids bind calcium that would normally trap oxalate, leading to oxalate kidney stones."],"pitfalls":["GLUT5 brings fructose in; GLUT2 takes all three sugars out. Do not put GLUT2 on the apical side for exam purposes.","Folate is absorbed in the jejunum, B12 in the terminal ileum; ileal resection spares folate.","A small ileal resection causes watery bile acid diarrhea; a large one causes steatorrhea from a depleted pool.","Lactase deficiency stool is acidic with a high osmotic gap; cholera stool has a low gap."],"dumps":[{"t":"Enterocyte transport map","p":"Draw one enterocyte with its apical and basolateral sides. Show how glucose, galactose, fructose, amino acids, dipeptides, and fatty acids get in and out, labeling each transporter.","l":["SGLT1 apical with 2 Na+ for glucose and galactose","GLUT5 apical for fructose","GLUT2 basolateral exit for all monosaccharides","Na+/K+-ATPase basolateral keeps intracellular Na+ low","Na+-linked amino acid carriers and H+-linked PepT1 for di- and tripeptides","Micelles deliver monoglyceride and fatty acids; re-esterified in smooth ER","Chylomicrons with apoB-48 leave into the lacteal"]},{"t":"Ileal resection consequences","p":"A patient loses 120 cm of terminal ileum. Make a flowchart from the resection to every deficiency and complication that follows.","l":["Loss of intrinsic factor-B12 uptake leads to B12 deficiency","Megaloblastic anemia with high methylmalonic acid and neuropathy","Bile acids not reabsorbed, so the pool shrinks","Micelles fail, causing steatorrhea","Fat-soluble vitamin A, D, E, K deficiency","Bile supersaturated with cholesterol, so cholesterol gallstones","Calcium bound by fat frees oxalate, so oxalate kidney stones"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Digestion and Absorption in the Gastrointestinal Tract"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Section on malabsorption and diarrhea"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on digestion and absorption of dietary lipids"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Digestion & Absorption of Carbohydrates","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BXXxtEW5v3o"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Digestion & Absorption of Lipids","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ja-MWkFNvDg"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Diarrhea | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7ylgYbLCEA0"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Malabsorption | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kx1UgaqpYkc"}],"related":["GI-6","GI-8","GI-13-E4","GI-13-D2","GI-12"]},"GI-8":{"id":"GI-8","summary":"What the body must get from food (essential amino acids, essential fatty acids, vitamins, and minerals), what each vitamin does as a cofactor, and how the liver keeps energy flowing between meals. Questions give you a deficiency or toxicity picture and ask you to name the missing cofactor, the blocked enzyme, or the lab that tells two deficiencies apart.","notes":[{"h":"Essential nutrients and malnutrition","points":["Essential amino acids: phenylalanine, valine, threonine, tryptophan, isoleucine, methionine, histidine, leucine, lysine.","Essential fatty acids: linoleic acid (omega-6) and alpha-linolenic acid (omega-3); lack causes scaly dermatitis and poor healing.","Kwashiorkor: protein lack with enough calories; low albumin gives edema and ascites.","Kwashiorkor fatty liver: too little apolipoprotein to export VLDL from hepatocytes.","Marasmus: total calorie lack; muscle wasting and lost subcutaneous fat, no edema."]},{"h":"Fat-soluble vitamins","points":["Fat malabsorption (pancreatic insufficiency, cholestasis, celiac disease) drops vitamins A, D, E, and K together.","A: night blindness, dry eyes with Bitot spots, follicular hyperkeratosis.","D: osteomalacia in adults, rickets in children; low calcium and phosphate, high PTH and alkaline phosphatase.","E: antioxidant loss gives hemolytic anemia, spinocerebellar ataxia, posterior column loss, and neuropathy.","K: no gamma-carboxylation of factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C and S; PT prolongs first because factor VII has the shortest half-life."]},{"h":"Vitamin B12 and folate","points":["Acid and pepsin free B12 from food protein; it binds haptocorrin (R binder) from saliva.","Pancreatic proteases digest haptocorrin in the duodenum, freeing B12 to bind intrinsic factor.","The IF-B12 complex is taken up in the terminal ileum; transcobalamin carries it in blood; liver stores last years.","B12 is a cofactor for methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase.","B12 deficiency: homocysteine high and methylmalonic acid high, with subacute combined degeneration.","Folate deficiency: homocysteine high, methylmalonic acid normal, no neurologic findings."]},{"h":"Water-soluble vitamin cofactors","points":["Thiamine (B1) as TPP: pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, transketolase, branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase.","Thiamine lack: Wernicke encephalopathy (confusion, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia), dry beriberi (neuropathy), wet beriberi (high-output heart failure).","Niacin (B3) makes NAD+ and NADP+; lack gives pellagra: dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia.","Pyridoxine (B6) as PLP: ALA synthase (sideroblastic anemia), glutamate decarboxylase for GABA (seizures), transaminases.","Vitamin C: cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase; scurvy gives bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhage, corkscrew hairs, poor wound healing."]},{"h":"Fed and fasting energy","points":["Fed: insulin drives glycogen synthesis, glycolysis, and fat synthesis in the liver.","Early fasting: glucagon drives hepatic glycogenolysis; liver glycogen is largely used up within about 24 hours.","Gluconeogenesis uses alanine (glucose-alanine cycle), lactate (Cori cycle), and glycerol from fat breakdown.","Even-chain fatty acids cannot make net glucose; acetyl-CoA cannot be turned back into pyruvate.","Prolonged fasting: liver makes acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate from fatty acid acetyl-CoA; the brain shifts to ketones over days.","The liver cannot use ketones because it lacks succinyl-CoA:acetoacetate CoA transferase (thiophorase)."]},{"h":"Minerals and toxicity","points":["Iron deficiency: microcytic hypochromic anemia, low ferritin, high TIBC.","Zinc deficiency: poor wound healing, dysgeusia, alopecia, hypogonadism, acrodermatitis around mouth and on hands and feet.","Copper deficiency: anemia, neutropenia, and a B12-like myelopathy with ataxia.","Excess zinc induces enterocyte metallothionein, which traps copper; gastric bypass also causes copper deficiency.","Vitamin A toxicity: raised intracranial pressure (pseudotumor cerebri), liver injury, dry skin, hair loss; it is a strong teratogen.","Vitamin D toxicity: hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, kidney stones, with suppressed PTH."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Vitamin deficiencies by cofactor role","cols":["Vitamin","Active form or role","Deficiency findings"],"rows":[["A (retinol)","Retinal in rhodopsin; epithelial differentiation","Night blindness, Bitot spots, xerophthalmia"],["D","Calcitriol raises gut calcium and phosphate uptake","Rickets, osteomalacia"],["E","Antioxidant in cell membranes","Hemolytic anemia, ataxia, neuropathy"],["K","Gamma-carboxylation of II, VII, IX, X, C, S","Bleeding, prolonged PT"],["B1 (thiamine)","TPP for dehydrogenases and transketolase","Wernicke encephalopathy, beriberi"],["B3 (niacin)","NAD+ and NADP+","Pellagra"],["B6 (pyridoxine)","PLP for ALA synthase, transaminases, GABA synthesis","Sideroblastic anemia, seizures, neuropathy"],["B9 (folate)","THF carries one-carbon units","Megaloblastic anemia, high homocysteine only"],["B12 (cobalamin)","Methionine synthase, methylmalonyl-CoA mutase","Megaloblastic anemia, high homocysteine and MMA, neuropathy"],["C (ascorbate)","Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase","Scurvy"]]},{"title":"Kwashiorkor and marasmus","cols":["Feature","Kwashiorkor","Marasmus"],"rows":[["Main lack","Protein","Total calories"],["Edema","Yes, from low albumin","No"],["Liver","Fatty, enlarged","Usually normal"],["Muscle and fat","Some muscle loss, fat partly kept","Severe wasting of both"]]},{"title":"Fuel sources across a fast","cols":["Phase","Main hormone","Main glucose or fuel source"],"rows":[["Fed","Insulin","Dietary glucose; liver stores glycogen and makes fat"],["Early fast (hours)","Glucagon","Hepatic glycogenolysis"],["Fast past about a day","Glucagon, cortisol","Gluconeogenesis from alanine, lactate, glycerol"],["Prolonged fast (days to weeks)","Glucagon","Ketone bodies for brain; less muscle protein breakdown"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"PVT TIM HaLL","text":"Phenylalanine, Valine, Threonine, Tryptophan, Isoleucine, Methionine, Histidine, Leucine, Lysine: the essential amino acids."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Kwashiorkor is a protein-poor MEAL","text":"Malnutrition, Edema, Anemia, Liver (fatty)."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"The 3 Ds of pellagra","text":"Dermatitis (sun-exposed skin), Diarrhea, Dementia; untreated it can lead to death."},{"kind":"trick","label":"B12 needs two jobs done","text":"B12 runs two enzymes, so both products back up (homocysteine and MMA). Folate runs only the methionine synthase step, so only homocysteine rises."}],"highYield":["Give thiamine before glucose in a malnourished patient with alcohol use disorder to avoid triggering Wernicke encephalopathy.","Isoniazid causes B6 deficiency, which can also cause pellagra because B6 is needed to make niacin from tryptophan.","Carcinoid syndrome and Hartnup disease both cause niacin deficiency by diverting or losing tryptophan.","Copper deficiency after gastric bypass or zinc supplements can look exactly like B12 subacute combined degeneration.","Vitamin K deficiency in fat malabsorption corrects with parenteral vitamin K; in liver failure it does not.","Isotretinoin (a vitamin A analog) is teratogenic; vitamin A excess can cause pseudotumor cerebri with papilledema."],"pitfalls":["High homocysteine alone does not tell B12 from folate deficiency; methylmalonic acid does.","Kwashiorkor has edema; marasmus does not.","Vitamin E deficiency neurologic signs mimic B12 deficiency; look for fat malabsorption and hemolysis.","Ketones are made in the liver but used by brain, heart, and muscle; red cells and liver cannot use them."],"dumps":[{"t":"Vitamin B12 path","p":"Draw the path of vitamin B12 from a bite of meat to a cell in bone marrow. Label each binding protein and the organ that makes it.","l":["Acid and pepsin in the stomach release B12 from food protein","Haptocorrin (R binder) from saliva binds B12 in the stomach","Intrinsic factor comes from gastric parietal cells","Pancreatic proteases cleave haptocorrin in the duodenum","B12 transfers to intrinsic factor","IF-B12 taken up in the terminal ileum","Transcobalamin carries B12 in blood; liver stores last years","Name one cause of failure at each step (pernicious anemia, pancreatic insufficiency, ileal resection)"]},{"t":"Fasting timeline","p":"Draw a timeline from a meal through 3 weeks of fasting. Under each phase, write the main hormone, the main source of blood glucose, and the main fuel used by the brain.","l":["Fed: insulin, dietary glucose, glycogen and fat storage","Hours after the meal: glucagon and hepatic glycogenolysis","Liver glycogen largely gone within about 24 hours","Gluconeogenesis from alanine, lactate, and glycerol","Even-chain fatty acids cannot give net glucose","Liver makes ketones from fatty acids but cannot use them (no thiophorase)","Brain adapts to ketones over days, sparing muscle protein"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on vitamins"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on the feed/fast cycle"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Vitamin B12 Digestion and Absorption & Pernicious Anaemia","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aww5NiahRb8"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Metabolism | Gluconeogenesis","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YpV96faQOZc"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Metabolism | Ketone Metabolism","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BlR9DkUAUkI"}],"related":["GI-7","GI-13-E","HE-9-A1","HE-9-C5"]},"GI-9":{"id":"GI-9","summary":"The liver's work beyond digestion: clearing bilirubin, transforming drugs and toxins, making albumin and clotting factors, and turning ammonia into urea, along with how saliva protects teeth and how the gallbladder concentrates bile. Questions hand you a jaundice lab panel, an overdose, or a failing liver and ask which step broke.","notes":[{"h":"Bilirubin pathway","points":["Macrophages (mainly spleen) break down heme: heme oxygenase makes biliverdin, CO, and iron.","Biliverdin reductase makes unconjugated bilirubin, which is fat-soluble and rides on albumin.","Hepatocytes take it up and UGT1A1 conjugates it with glucuronic acid, making it water-soluble.","MRP2 pumps conjugated bilirubin into the canaliculus; this ATP-dependent step is rate-limiting.","Gut bacteria turn it into urobilinogen; most becomes stercobilin (brown stool).","Some urobilinogen is reabsorbed; a little is excreted in urine, where it oxidizes to yellow urobilin."]},{"h":"Reading jaundice labs","points":["Only conjugated bilirubin can appear in urine; unconjugated is bound to albumin.","Hemolysis: unconjugated rises, no urine bilirubin, urine urobilinogen goes up.","Obstruction: conjugated rises, dark urine bilirubin, pale stool, urine urobilinogen low or absent.","Hepatocellular injury: both fractions rise, urine bilirubin present, urobilinogen normal or high.","Gilbert syndrome (mild UGT1A1 reduction) and Crigler-Najjar (absent or very low UGT1A1) raise unconjugated bilirubin.","Dubin-Johnson (MRP2 defect, black liver) and Rotor syndrome raise conjugated bilirubin."]},{"h":"Drug and toxin metabolism","points":["Phase I: cytochrome P450 oxidation, reduction, or hydrolysis in smooth ER; products can be more reactive.","Phase II: conjugation (glucuronidation, sulfation, glutathione, acetylation) makes a polar product to excrete.","Acetaminophen is mostly glucuronidated and sulfated; a small fraction goes through CYP2E1 to NAPQI.","Glutathione normally neutralizes NAPQI; in overdose glutathione runs out and NAPQI binds cell proteins.","Injury is centrilobular (zone 3) because zone 3 has the most CYP2E1 and the least oxygen.","N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione; chronic alcohol use raises risk by inducing CYP2E1 and lowering glutathione.","Carbon tetrachloride: CYP450 makes the trichloromethyl radical, which causes lipid peroxidation, fatty change, and centrilobular necrosis."]},{"h":"Synthesis and ammonia","points":["Liver makes albumin, most clotting factors, and proteins C and S.","PT prolongs early in liver failure because factor VII has a half-life of about 6 hours.","Albumin falls more slowly (half-life about 3 weeks) and causes edema and ascites.","The urea cycle in hepatocytes turns ammonia into urea; CPS I is the first committed step.","Liver failure or portosystemic shunting lets ammonia rise, causing hepatic encephalopathy with asterixis.","Factor VIII is made by endothelium, so it stays normal in liver disease."]},{"h":"Saliva and gallbladder","points":["Saliva bicarbonate buffers plaque acid; calcium and phosphate remineralize early enamel lesions.","Saliva defenses: secretory IgA, lysozyme (cleaves peptidoglycan), lactoferrin (binds iron).","Low salivary flow (Sjogren syndrome, anticholinergics, head and neck radiation) leads to rampant caries.","The gallbladder absorbs Na+ actively; Cl- and water follow, concentrating bile about 5-fold, up to about 20-fold.","CCK from duodenal I cells (after fat and protein) contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi.","Stasis (fasting, TPN, pregnancy, rapid weight loss) plus concentrated bile lets cholesterol crystals nucleate into stones."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Jaundice by mechanism","cols":["Type","Serum bilirubin","Urine bilirubin","Urine urobilinogen","Stool"],"rows":[["Hemolytic (prehepatic)","Unconjugated high","Absent","Increased","Normal or dark"],["Hepatocellular","Both high","Present","Normal or increased","Normal or pale"],["Obstructive (posthepatic)","Conjugated high","Present (dark urine)","Decreased or absent","Pale, clay-colored"]]},{"title":"Inherited hyperbilirubinemias","cols":["Syndrome","Defect","Bilirubin type","Key feature"],"rows":[["Gilbert","Mildly reduced UGT1A1","Unconjugated","Mild jaundice with fasting, stress, illness"],["Crigler-Najjar type I","Absent UGT1A1","Unconjugated","Kernicterus in infancy"],["Dubin-Johnson","MRP2 defect","Conjugated","Black liver"],["Rotor","Impaired hepatic uptake and storage","Conjugated","Normal liver color, milder"]]},{"title":"Liver failure findings and their cause","cols":["Finding","Lost function"],"rows":[["Prolonged PT, bleeding","Clotting factor synthesis"],["Edema, ascites","Albumin synthesis"],["Confusion, asterixis","Urea cycle disposal of ammonia"],["Hypoglycemia","Glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis"],["Jaundice","Bilirubin conjugation and excretion"],["Gynecomastia, spider angiomas","Estrogen breakdown"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Only the conjugated pees","text":"Unconjugated bilirubin is stuck to albumin and cannot be filtered. If there is bilirubin in the urine, the conjugated fraction is up."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Follow the urobilinogen","text":"More heme in means more urobilinogen out (hemolysis). A blocked duct means nothing reaches the gut, so urobilinogen vanishes and the stool turns pale."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Zone 3 takes the hit","text":"Zone 3 sits by the central vein, gets the least oxygen, and holds the most CYP2E1. Toxins activated by P450 (acetaminophen, CCl4) and ischemia all injure zone 3 first."}],"highYield":["Acetaminophen overdose: glutathione depletion, NAPQI, centrilobular necrosis; treat with N-acetylcysteine.","CCl4 is the classic model of free radical injury through lipid peroxidation.","Dark urine and pale stools mean obstruction; jaundice with no urine bilirubin means hemolysis or Gilbert.","PT is the fastest lab marker of hepatic synthetic failure; vitamin K does not correct it in liver failure.","Hyperammonemia from liver failure causes hepatic encephalopathy and asterixis.","Xerostomia from Sjogren syndrome or radiation causes caries at the gum line and root surfaces."],"pitfalls":["Heme oxygenase makes biliverdin; biliverdin reductase makes bilirubin. Do not reverse them.","Phase II conjugation usually detoxifies; phase I can create the toxic metabolite.","Albumin is a poor marker of acute liver failure because of its long half-life; use PT.","Gilbert syndrome is unconjugated; Dubin-Johnson and Rotor are conjugated."],"dumps":[{"t":"Bilirubin from red cell to stool","p":"Draw the path of bilirubin from an old red cell in the spleen to the stool and urine. Label every enzyme, carrier, and site.","l":["Heme oxygenase makes biliverdin, CO, and iron","Biliverdin reductase makes unconjugated bilirubin","Albumin carries unconjugated bilirubin in blood","UGT1A1 conjugates with glucuronic acid in the hepatocyte","MRP2 excretes into bile (rate-limiting)","Gut bacteria make urobilinogen; most becomes stercobilin","Some urobilinogen reabsorbed, a little excreted in urine as urobilin"]},{"t":"Acetaminophen overdose","p":"Draw a hepatocyte and show the three routes acetaminophen takes at a normal dose, then redraw it after an overdose. Mark where N-acetylcysteine acts.","l":["Most of a normal dose is glucuronidated and sulfated (phase II)","A small fraction goes through CYP2E1 to NAPQI (phase I)","Glutathione conjugates NAPQI to a safe product","In overdose, phase II routes saturate and more NAPQI forms","Glutathione runs out; NAPQI binds proteins and kills cells","Zone 3 centrilobular necrosis","N-acetylcysteine restores glutathione"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Section on chemical (toxic) injury in cell injury"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on heme degradation and bilirubin"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Bilirubin Metabolism - unconjugated and conjugated bilirubin","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_-JFqnBYmzc"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Liver Metabolism, Protein Synthesis & Storage","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wRkanE05ICo"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Bile Synthesis","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-L4i_2EHWQU"}],"related":["GI-13-C1","GI-13-C2","GI-13-C3","GI-13-D2","GI-13-A"]},"GI-10":{"id":"GI-10","summary":"Single-gene diseases that damage the liver, pancreas, and colon: iron and copper overload, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, cystic fibrosis, and the inherited colon cancer and polyposis syndromes. Questions give you the gene or inheritance pattern and ask for the mechanism and the organs that pay for it, or give you the findings and ask for the gene.","notes":[{"h":"Hereditary hemochromatosis","points":["Autosomal recessive; most often homozygous HFE C282Y (chromosome 6).","Faulty iron sensing keeps hepcidin inappropriately low.","Low hepcidin leaves ferroportin active, so the gut absorbs too much iron for years.","Labs: high transferrin saturation and ferritin; liver iron stains with Prussian blue.","Iron free radicals cause cirrhosis, diabetes, skin bronzing, cardiomyopathy, arthropathy, and hypogonadism.","Cirrhosis carries a high risk of hepatocellular carcinoma; women present later because of menstrual iron loss."]},{"h":"Wilson disease","points":["Autosomal recessive ATP7B mutation (chromosome 13), a hepatocyte copper-transporting ATPase.","Copper is not loaded onto ceruloplasmin and not excreted into bile, so it builds up in the liver.","Spillover copper reaches brain (basal ganglia), cornea (Kayser-Fleischer rings), and kidney.","Labs: low serum ceruloplasmin, high urinary copper, high liver copper; may have Coombs-negative hemolysis.","Presents in children and young adults with hepatitis, cirrhosis, or acute liver failure, plus tremor, parkinsonism, or psychiatric change."]},{"h":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","points":["SERPINA1 gene, codominant alleles; PiMM is normal, PiZZ is severe.","The Z protein misfolds and polymerizes in the hepatocyte ER instead of being secreted.","Retained protein shows as PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules; it causes neonatal hepatitis, cirrhosis, and HCC.","Low serum levels leave neutrophil elastase unopposed, causing panacinar emphysema at the lung bases, worse with smoking."]},{"h":"Colorectal cancer pathways","points":["Chromosomal instability (adenoma to carcinoma): APC loss, then KRAS activation, then TP53 loss.","APC loss frees beta-catenin, which turns on Wnt target genes and starts the adenoma.","Microsatellite instability: mismatch repair genes (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2) fail, so repeats mutate.","Lynch syndrome is a germline mismatch repair mutation, autosomal dominant, with few polyps.","Lynch cancers tend to be right-sided and early; endometrial, ovarian, and gastric cancers also rise.","Sporadic MSI cancers usually come from MLH1 promoter hypermethylation."]},{"h":"Polyposis syndromes","points":["FAP: autosomal dominant APC (chromosome 5q), hundreds to thousands of adenomas, near-certain cancer without colectomy.","Gardner syndrome: FAP plus osteomas, desmoid tumors, epidermoid cysts, and extra teeth.","Turcot syndrome: polyposis plus a brain tumor (medulloblastoma or glioma).","MUTYH-associated polyposis: autosomal recessive base excision repair defect, tens to hundreds of adenomas.","Peutz-Jeghers: autosomal dominant STK11, hamartomatous polyps, dark spots on lips and buccal mucosa, intussusception, raised cancer risk."]},{"h":"Cystic fibrosis in the gut","points":["Autosomal recessive CFTR (chromosome 7); the common F508del protein misfolds and is degraded.","CFTR is a Cl- channel opened by cAMP and protein kinase A; without it, Cl- and bicarbonate secretion fall and secretions thicken.","Newborn: meconium ileus from thick meconium blocking the distal ileum.","Pancreatic ducts plug, acini are destroyed, and exocrine insufficiency causes steatorrhea and A, D, E, K deficiency.","Inspissated bile causes focal biliary cirrhosis; sweat chloride is high because the sweat duct cannot reabsorb Cl-."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Inherited liver diseases","cols":["Disease","Gene and inheritance","Mechanism","Key findings"],"rows":[["Hemochromatosis","HFE C282Y, autosomal recessive","Low hepcidin, excess iron absorption","Cirrhosis, diabetes, bronze skin, cardiomyopathy, high transferrin saturation"],["Wilson disease","ATP7B, autosomal recessive","No biliary copper excretion or ceruloplasmin loading","Low ceruloplasmin, Kayser-Fleischer rings, basal ganglia signs, hepatitis"],["Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","SERPINA1 PiZZ, codominant","Misfolded protein trapped in ER; low serum level","PAS-positive globules, cirrhosis, panacinar emphysema"]]},{"title":"Hereditary colorectal syndromes","cols":["Syndrome","Gene","Inheritance","Polyps","Extra features"],"rows":[["FAP","APC","Autosomal dominant","Hundreds to thousands of adenomas","Cancer by about age 40 without colectomy"],["Gardner","APC","Autosomal dominant","Many adenomas","Osteomas, desmoids, epidermoid cysts, dental anomalies"],["Turcot","APC or mismatch repair","Depends on the gene","Adenomas","Medulloblastoma or glioma"],["MUTYH-associated polyposis","MUTYH","Autosomal recessive","Tens to hundreds of adenomas","Base excision repair defect"],["Lynch","MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2","Autosomal dominant","Few","Right-sided cancer, endometrial and ovarian cancer"],["Peutz-Jeghers","STK11","Autosomal dominant","Hamartomas, mostly small bowel","Mucocutaneous pigmentation, intussusception"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Iron is absorbed, copper is excreted","text":"Hemochromatosis is a problem of taking in too much (gut absorption, hepcidin). Wilson disease is a problem of getting rid of too little (bile excretion, ATP7B)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"AKP in order","text":"Walk the adenoma to carcinoma sequence as A, K, P: APC first (polyp starts), KRAS next (polyp grows), p53 last (cancer)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Amsterdam 3-2-1","text":"Suspect Lynch syndrome when 3 relatives have Lynch cancers, across 2 generations, with 1 diagnosed before age 50."}],"highYield":["Hemochromatosis: high transferrin saturation is the best first screen; phlebotomy is the treatment.","Wilson disease: low ceruloplasmin and Kayser-Fleischer rings in a young patient with liver and movement problems.","PiZZ alpha-1 antitrypsin: liver disease from retained protein, lung disease from absent protein.","Lynch tumors show microsatellite instability and loss of mismatch repair protein staining.","Peutz-Jeghers: freckle-like spots on the lips with small bowel hamartomas and intussusception.","CF: meconium ileus at birth, then pancreatic insufficiency with fat-soluble vitamin deficiency."],"pitfalls":["Ceruloplasmin is low in Wilson disease even though total body copper is high.","FAP is autosomal dominant APC; MUTYH polyposis looks similar but is autosomal recessive.","Lynch syndrome has few polyps; FAP has hundreds.","Peutz-Jeghers polyps are hamartomas, yet cancer risk still rises in the gut and other organs."],"dumps":[{"t":"Iron and copper overload side by side","p":"Draw a hepatocyte for hemochromatosis and a second one for Wilson disease. Show where the metal comes in, where it should leave, and where the defect is. List the organs damaged by each.","l":["Hemochromatosis: HFE C282Y, autosomal recessive","Low hepcidin keeps ferroportin active, so gut absorbs excess iron","Iron damages liver, pancreas, heart, joints, skin, pituitary","Wilson: ATP7B, autosomal recessive","Copper not put into ceruloplasmin and not excreted into bile","Copper spills to brain basal ganglia, cornea, kidney","Labs: high transferrin saturation versus low ceruloplasmin"]},{"t":"Two roads to colon cancer","p":"Draw two parallel flowcharts, one for the adenoma to carcinoma pathway and one for the mismatch repair pathway. Name the genes at each step and the inherited syndrome tied to each road.","l":["APC loss raises beta-catenin and starts the adenoma","KRAS activation drives growth","TP53 loss allows invasion and carcinoma","FAP is germline APC","Mismatch repair genes MLH1, MSH2 lost","Microsatellite instability in repeat sequences","Lynch syndrome is germline mismatch repair, autosomal dominant","Sporadic MSI comes from MLH1 promoter methylation"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Section on colonic polyps and neoplastic disease"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic Disorders"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Haemochromatosis (Iron Overload) - iron physiology, causes and pathophysiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hbs_GaWX5PI"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Colorectal Cancer | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HjIQy8FycKA"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Liver Cirrhosis","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=f-Fxlsb2dtQ"}],"related":["GI-13-C2","GI-13-K4","GI-13-K7","GI-13-B","GI-8"]},"GI-11":{"id":"GI-11","summary":"How the gut immune system tolerates food and flora but still fights pathogens, and how that balance fails in celiac disease, Crohn disease, autoimmune liver and stomach disease, MALT lymphoma, and hepatitis B. Questions ask which cell, antibody, HLA type, or immune mechanism explains the damage, and how to read hepatitis B serology.","notes":[{"h":"Mucosal immunity","points":["M cells over Peyer patches (ileum) sample luminal antigen and hand it to dendritic cells.","TGF-beta in the gut drives B cells to switch to IgA; lamina propria plasma cells make dimeric IgA with a J chain.","The polymeric Ig receptor carries dimeric IgA across the epithelium; its cleaved piece stays on as secretory component.","Secretory IgA blocks microbe attachment without activating much complement or inflammation.","Oral tolerance: regulatory T cells (IL-10, TGF-beta) and anergy keep reactions to food and flora quiet.","Selective IgA deficiency: most common primary immunodeficiency; often silent, but recurrent sinopulmonary and GI infections (Giardia) occur."]},{"h":"Celiac disease","points":["Gliadin (from gluten) resists digestion; tissue transglutaminase deamidates its glutamines to glutamate.","Deamidated gliadin binds HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 tightly and activates CD4 T cells, which release IFN-gamma.","Epithelial IL-15 activates intraepithelial CD8 T cells that kill enterocytes.","Biopsy: villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, intraepithelial lymphocytes, worst in the duodenum.","Serology: IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase and anti-endomysial antibodies; check total IgA because IgA deficiency gives false negatives.","Associations: dermatitis herpetiformis, type 1 diabetes, and enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma."]},{"h":"Crohn disease","points":["NOD2 is a cytosolic sensor of bacterial peptidoglycan (muramyl dipeptide) in Paneth cells and monocytes.","NOD2 variants impair handling of commensal bacteria, which then breach the mucosa.","Th1 (IFN-gamma) and Th17 (IL-17, sustained by IL-23) responses drive the inflammation.","Result: transmural inflammation, noncaseating granulomas, skip lesions, fistulas, and strictures."]},{"h":"Autoimmune gut and liver disease","points":["Autoimmune gastritis: antibodies to parietal cells (H+/K+-ATPase) and intrinsic factor; T cells destroy the body and fundus.","Autoimmune gastritis gives achlorhydria, high gastrin, and pernicious anemia.","Autoimmune hepatitis type 1: ANA and anti-smooth muscle antibodies; type 2 (often children): anti-LKM1.","Primary biliary cholangitis: antimitochondrial antibodies, middle-aged women, destruction of small intrahepatic bile ducts."]},{"h":"MALT lymphoma","points":["Chronic H. pylori gastritis builds lymphoid tissue in a stomach that normally has little.","H. pylori-specific T cells keep stimulating B cells until a monoclonal marginal zone lymphoma emerges.","Early tumors still depend on that T cell help, so eradicating H. pylori can make them regress.","Tumors with t(11;18) usually no longer respond to eradication."]},{"h":"Hepatitis B immunity","points":["HBV itself barely harms hepatocytes; CD8 cytotoxic T cells kill infected cells displaying viral peptides on MHC class I.","A strong T cell response causes acute hepatitis and clearance; a weak one allows chronic infection.","Infection at birth becomes chronic about 90% of the time; adult infection only about 5%.","HBsAg-antibody immune complexes cause polyarteritis nodosa and membranous nephropathy (type III hypersensitivity).","The vaccine is recombinant HBsAg only, so a vaccinated person makes anti-HBs but never anti-HBc."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hepatitis B serology","cols":["State","HBsAg","Anti-HBc","Anti-HBs"],"rows":[["Vaccinated","Negative","Negative","Positive"],["Resolved infection","Negative","Positive (IgG)","Positive"],["Acute infection","Positive","Positive (IgM)","Negative"],["Window period","Negative","Positive (IgM)","Negative"],["Chronic infection","Positive over 6 months","Positive (IgG)","Negative"]]},{"title":"Autoantibodies in GI disease","cols":["Disease","Autoantibody","Target or note"],"rows":[["Celiac disease","IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase, anti-endomysial","Tissue transglutaminase"],["Autoimmune gastritis","Anti-parietal cell, anti-intrinsic factor","H+/K+-ATPase; intrinsic factor"],["Autoimmune hepatitis type 1","ANA, anti-smooth muscle","Smooth muscle antibody targets actin"],["Autoimmune hepatitis type 2","Anti-LKM1","CYP2D6"],["Primary biliary cholangitis","Antimitochondrial","Pyruvate dehydrogenase E2 subunit"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Core means caught","text":"Anti-HBc only appears after real infection because the vaccine has no core protein. Anti-HBs alone means vaccine; anti-HBs plus anti-HBc means recovered from infection."},{"kind":"trick","label":"The enzyme makes the antigen and the target","text":"In celiac disease tissue transglutaminase does both jobs: it modifies gliadin so DQ2 or DQ8 can present it, and it becomes the antibody target used in testing."},{"kind":"trick","label":"MALT lymphoma still listens","text":"Early gastric MALT lymphoma still needs H. pylori-driven T cell help to grow. Take away the bug and the help disappears, so antibiotics can cure an early lymphoma."}],"highYield":["Celiac disease is tied to HLA-DQ2 and DQ8; screen with IgA anti-tTG and check total IgA.","Selective IgA deficiency can cause anaphylaxis to blood products that contain IgA.","NOD2 variants are the classic genetic link to Crohn disease.","Antimitochondrial antibody points to primary biliary cholangitis; anti-LKM1 points to type 2 autoimmune hepatitis.","Hepatitis B liver damage is mostly immune-mediated by CD8 T cells.","HBsAg immune complexes cause polyarteritis nodosa, which spares the lungs."],"pitfalls":["A negative anti-tTG IgA does not rule out celiac disease in a patient with IgA deficiency.","IgM anti-HBc is the only marker positive in the window period; HBsAg and anti-HBs are both negative.","Autoimmune gastritis hits the body and fundus; H. pylori gastritis usually starts in the antrum.","Crohn granulomas are noncaseating; caseating granulomas suggest tuberculosis."],"dumps":[{"t":"Secretory IgA on the move","p":"Draw the intestinal lining over a Peyer patch. Show how an antigen is sampled and how secretory IgA is made and delivered to the lumen. Label each cell and molecule.","l":["M cell transports antigen across the epithelium","Dendritic cell presents antigen to T cells in the Peyer patch","TGF-beta drives B cell class switching to IgA","Plasma cell in lamina propria makes dimeric IgA joined by J chain","Polymeric Ig receptor binds IgA on the basolateral side and carries it across","Cleavage leaves secretory component attached in the lumen","Secretory IgA blocks microbial attachment"]},{"t":"Celiac disease mechanism","p":"Draw a flowchart from eating bread to villous atrophy in celiac disease. Mark where the HLA molecule, the enzyme, and each T cell type act, and where the test antibodies come from.","l":["Gliadin resists digestion","Tissue transglutaminase deamidates gliadin","HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 presents deamidated gliadin","CD4 T cells release IFN-gamma","IL-15 activates intraepithelial CD8 T cells that kill enterocytes","Villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, intraepithelial lymphocytes","IgA anti-tTG and anti-endomysial antibodies appear","Gluten-free diet reverses the lesion"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on mucosal immunity and secretory IgA"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on immunological tolerance and autoimmunity"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Section on the gastrointestinal tract"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Coeliac (Celiac) Disease - Overview (signs and symptoms, pathophysiology, diagnosis, treatment)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Hxd0msD954I"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Pathophysiology and Complications","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=chSyQPKsNk4"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD) | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PBZZ_Cu64aQ"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Hepatitis B | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e-P-L0oGXgo"}],"related":["GI-13-E2","GI-13-G9","GI-13-G7","GI-13-L7","IM-15-A3","IM-15-C3"]},"GI-12":{"id":"GI-12","summary":"The bacteria, viruses, and parasites that cause diarrhea, gastritis, and hepatitis, organized by how they cause disease: preformed toxin, secretory toxin, invasion, villus damage, or immune injury. Questions give you the food, the timing, the stool, or a lab clue and ask for the organism or its virulence factor.","notes":[{"h":"Toxin-mediated","points":["Staphylococcus aureus: preformed heat-stable enterotoxin in mayonnaise salads, custards, and meats; vomiting within about 1 to 6 hours.","Bacillus cereus emetic toxin: preformed in reheated fried rice; vomiting within about 1 to 6 hours.","Cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates Gs, locking adenylyl cyclase on; cAMP rises and rice-water stool follows.","ETEC heat-labile toxin works like cholera toxin (cAMP); its heat-stable toxin raises cGMP; classic traveler's diarrhea.","Clostridioides difficile: spore-forming gram-positive anaerobe; toxins A and B glucosylate Rho GTPases and kill colonocytes.","C. difficile follows antibiotics (clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, cephalosporins) and forms pseudomembranes."]},{"h":"Invasive bacteria","points":["Shigella: nonmotile, no H2S, infectious dose as low as about 10 to 100 organisms; invades M cells and spreads cell to cell.","Salmonella: motile, H2S-producing, needs a large dose (acid sensitive); poultry, eggs, reptiles.","Salmonella Typhi causes typhoid fever with rose spots and can persist in the gallbladder.","Campylobacter jejuni: comma or S-shaped, grows at 42 °C (107.6 °F), from undercooked poultry; linked to Guillain-Barre syndrome.","Yersinia enterocolitica: pork and unpasteurized milk; mesenteric adenitis and ileitis mimic appendicitis.","EHEC O157:H7: Shiga toxin inactivates the 60S ribosome; bloody diarrhea from undercooked beef; hemolytic uremic syndrome.","Listeria monocytogenes: deli meats and soft cheese, grows in the cold; listeriolysin O and ActA actin tails for cell-to-cell spread."]},{"h":"Enteric viruses","points":["Rotavirus: reovirus, segmented double-stranded RNA, nonenveloped; destroys villus tip cells in infants and young children.","Norovirus: calicivirus, single-stranded RNA, nonenveloped; outbreaks on cruise ships and in nursing homes, low infectious dose.","Coxsackievirus and echovirus: picornaviruses (single-stranded RNA), fecal-oral, peak in summer and fall."]},{"h":"Hepatitis viruses","points":["HAV: picornavirus, fecal-oral, acute only, never chronic.","HBV: hepadnavirus with partially double-stranded DNA; it replicates through an RNA intermediate using reverse transcriptase.","HCV: flavivirus; error-prone RNA polymerase gives high antigenic variation, so no vaccine and frequent chronic infection.","HDV: defective RNA virus that needs HBsAg as its envelope; superinfection of an HBV carrier is the most severe.","HEV: fecal-oral from contaminated water; fulminant hepatitis with high mortality in pregnancy."]},{"h":"Helicobacter pylori","points":["Curved, flagellated, microaerophilic gram-negative rod; urease, catalase, and oxidase positive.","Urease splits urea to ammonia, buffering acid around the organism in the antral mucus.","Causes chronic gastritis, duodenal and gastric ulcers, gastric adenocarcinoma, and MALT lymphoma.","Active infection tests: urea breath test and stool antigen (both used for test of cure); biopsy with rapid urease test or histology.","Serology stays positive after cure, so it cannot confirm eradication."]},{"h":"Intestinal parasites","points":["Giardia: cysts in untreated water (campers); trophozoites stick to villi and cause foul, fatty diarrhea without blood.","Entamoeba histolytica: trophozoites with ingested red cells; flask-shaped colon ulcers, bloody diarrhea, liver abscess.","Cryptosporidium: acid-fast oocysts, chlorine resistant; severe watery diarrhea in advanced HIV, self-limited otherwise.","Cyclospora (contaminated produce) and Cystoisospora also have acid-fast oocysts.","Strongyloides: larvae penetrate skin, migrate through lungs, and live in the duodenum; autoinfection lets it persist for decades.","Corticosteroids or HTLV-1 trigger Strongyloides hyperinfection with gram-negative sepsis."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Toxin-mediated GI infections","cols":["Organism","Toxin and mechanism","Typical source","Onset and stool"],"rows":[["Staphylococcus aureus","Preformed heat-stable enterotoxin","Mayonnaise salads, custards, meats","About 1 to 6 hours; vomiting predominant"],["Bacillus cereus (emetic)","Preformed heat-stable cereulide","Reheated fried rice","About 1 to 6 hours; vomiting"],["Vibrio cholerae","Cholera toxin activates Gs, raises cAMP","Contaminated water, shellfish","Rice-water stool, massive volume"],["ETEC","Heat-labile toxin raises cAMP; heat-stable toxin raises cGMP","Food and water in travel","Watery diarrhea"],["Clostridioides difficile","Toxins A and B inactivate Rho GTPases","Recent antibiotics, hospitals","Watery diarrhea, pseudomembranes"]]},{"title":"Invasive enteric bacteria","cols":["Organism","Key lab or structural clue","Source","Classic complication"],"rows":[["Shigella","Nonmotile, no H2S, very low infectious dose","Person to person, daycare","Bloody dysentery; HUS with S. dysenteriae"],["Salmonella (nontyphoidal)","Motile, H2S producing","Poultry, eggs, reptiles","Bacteremia, osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease"],["Campylobacter jejuni","Comma or S-shaped, grows at 42 °C (107.6 °F)","Undercooked poultry, raw milk","Guillain-Barre syndrome, reactive arthritis"],["Yersinia enterocolitica","Grows in the cold","Pork, raw milk","Pseudoappendicitis from mesenteric adenitis"],["EHEC O157:H7","Shiga toxin, does not ferment sorbitol","Undercooked beef, produce","Hemolytic uremic syndrome"],["Listeria monocytogenes","Gram-positive rod, tumbling motility, actin tails","Deli meats, soft cheese","Meningitis in newborns, elderly; fetal infection"]]},{"title":"Hepatitis viruses","cols":["Virus","Family and genome","Spread","Chronic?","Key point"],"rows":[["HAV","Picornavirus, ssRNA","Fecal-oral","No","Travel, shellfish"],["HBV","Hepadnavirus, partially dsDNA","Blood, sex, birth","Yes","Reverse transcriptase; HCC risk"],["HCV","Flavivirus, ssRNA","Blood","Yes, most cases","High antigenic variation, no vaccine"],["HDV","Deltavirus, defective ssRNA","Blood, sex","Yes","Needs HBsAg to make particles"],["HEV","Hepevirus, ssRNA","Fecal-oral, water","No in healthy hosts","Severe in pregnancy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"The vowels hit your bowels","text":"Hepatitis A and E spread by the fecal-oral route; B, C, and D spread through blood and body fluids."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Count the hours","text":"Vomiting within about 6 hours of eating means the toxin was already in the food (S. aureus, B. cereus). Symptoms a day or more later mean the organism had to grow in the gut first."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Acid-fast in the stool","text":"Acid-fast oocysts in diarrhea point to the coccidia: Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, and Cystoisospora, especially in advanced HIV."}],"highYield":["EHEC O157:H7 plus bloody diarrhea, then anemia, low platelets, and kidney failure means hemolytic uremic syndrome.","Rotavirus infects infants; norovirus causes adult outbreaks.","HDV can only replicate where HBV supplies HBsAg; vaccinating for HBV also prevents HDV.","Urea breath test and stool antigen detect active H. pylori; hold proton pump inhibitors beforehand to avoid false negatives.","Strongyloides hyperinfection follows corticosteroids; screen people from endemic areas before immunosuppression.","Entamoeba causes flask-shaped ulcers and a liver abscess; Giardia causes fatty diarrhea without invasion."],"pitfalls":["Shigella dysenteriae and EHEC both make Shiga or Shiga-like toxin; Shigella invades and is nonmotile, EHEC does not invade.","Salmonella is motile and makes H2S; Shigella is neither.","Cholera toxin and ETEC heat-labile toxin raise cAMP; ETEC heat-stable toxin raises cGMP.","HBV is a DNA virus but uses reverse transcriptase; do not file it with the RNA hepatitis viruses."],"dumps":[{"t":"Diarrhea by mechanism","p":"Build a comparison table on blank paper with rows for preformed toxin, secretory toxin, cytotoxin, invasive bacteria, virus, and parasite. For each row, name two organisms, the onset, and whether the stool is watery or bloody.","l":["Preformed toxin: S. aureus, B. cereus; onset about 1 to 6 hours; vomiting","Secretory toxin: V. cholerae, ETEC; cAMP rise; watery","Cytotoxin: C. difficile, EHEC; colon damage","Invasive: Shigella, Campylobacter, Salmonella; bloody with fever","Viral: rotavirus in infants, norovirus in outbreaks; watery","Parasite: Giardia fatty and nonbloody, Entamoeba bloody","EHEC links to hemolytic uremic syndrome"]},{"t":"Hepatitis virus map","p":"Draw five boxes, one for each hepatitis virus. In each, write the genome type, the virus family, how it spreads, whether it becomes chronic, and one fact the exam loves.","l":["HAV picornavirus, fecal-oral, never chronic","HBV hepadnavirus, partially dsDNA, reverse transcriptase","HCV flavivirus, high antigenic variation, often chronic","HDV defective, requires HBsAg","HEV fecal-oral, severe in pregnancy","A and E spread fecal-oral; B, C, D spread by blood and body fluids","HBV and HCV raise hepatocellular carcinoma risk"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Section on enteric gram-negative rods (Enterobacteriaceae)"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Hepatitis Viruses"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Section on intestinal protozoa and nematodes"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Microbiology - Helicobacter Pylori (Ulcer)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y-shOXdsJeA"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Hepatitis | Pathophysiology of Viral Hepatitis","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R4OG0wHkPcM"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Hepatitis | Diagnosis of Viral Hepatitis","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B0pFnLbo6eM"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Case Study #16: Clostridium Difficile | C. Diff","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nn34qoj1hh0"}],"related":["GI-13-L1","GI-13-L3","GI-13-L7","GI-13-G7","GI-13-G5","GI-11"]},"GI-13-A":{"id":"GI-13-A","summary":"Parotitis is inflammation and swelling of the parotid gland. Exam questions ask you to separate viral mumps parotitis from bacterial parotitis using the patient, the duct findings, and the complications, and to explain why salivary stones favor the submandibular gland.","notes":[{"h":"Salivary gland basics","points":["Parotid gland is purely serous; its duct (Stensen duct) opens opposite the upper second molar.","Submandibular gland is mixed, mostly serous; its duct (Wharton duct) opens at the sublingual papilla beside the frenulum.","Sublingual gland is mostly mucous and drains by many small ducts into the floor of the mouth.","Parotid secretion is driven by CN IX through the otic ganglion; submandibular and sublingual by CN VII (chorda tympani) through the submandibular ganglion.","Serous cells make salivary amylase, so parotid inflammation raises serum amylase."]},{"h":"Mumps parotitis","points":["Caused by mumps virus, an enveloped negative-sense RNA paramyxovirus spread by respiratory droplets.","Fever and malaise, then painful parotid swelling that is often bilateral (it may start on one side).","Pain with chewing and an earache; the duct opening may be red, but no pus comes out.","Serum amylase is raised from the salivary gland; lipase stays normal unless the pancreas is involved.","Complications: orchitis after puberty (testicular atrophy, rarely sterility), oophoritis, pancreatitis, aseptic meningitis, sensorineural hearing loss.","Prevented by the live attenuated MMR vaccine."]},{"h":"Acute bacterial parotitis","points":["Staphylococcus aureus is the most common cause.","Low salivary flow lets mouth bacteria climb up Stensen duct.","Risk: dehydration, older age, the days after surgery, poor oral hygiene, anticholinergic drugs, duct obstruction.","Usually one side: hot, red, very tender gland, fever, and leukocytosis.","Massaging the gland pushes pus out of the Stensen duct opening.","Can form an abscess and spread into the deep spaces of the neck."]},{"h":"Salivary stones (sialolithiasis)","points":["About 80 to 90 percent of stones form in the submandibular gland.","Its saliva is thicker (more mucin) and more alkaline, with more calcium and phosphate.","Wharton duct is long and runs upward against gravity, bending around the mylohyoid, so saliva pools.","Stone blocks flow, so the gland swells painfully at meals when secretion is stimulated, then settles.","Stagnant saliva gets infected, causing secondary bacterial sialadenitis."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Mumps parotitis vs bacterial parotitis vs salivary stone","cols":["Feature","Mumps parotitis","Acute bacterial parotitis","Sialolithiasis"],"rows":[["Cause","Mumps virus (paramyxovirus)","Staphylococcus aureus most often","Calcium phosphate stone blocking a duct"],["Typical patient","Unvaccinated child or young adult","Dehydrated, older, or postoperative patient","Adult with meal-related swelling"],["Gland and side","Parotid, often both sides","Parotid, usually one side","Submandibular, one side"],["Duct opening","May be red, no pus","Pus on gland massage","Stone may be felt in floor of mouth"],["Pain pattern","Constant, worse with chewing","Constant, severe, with fever","Comes and goes with meals"],["Key labs","High amylase, normal lipase","Leukocytosis","Usually normal"],["Complications","Orchitis, pancreatitis, aseptic meningitis, deafness","Abscess, deep neck spread","Secondary bacterial sialadenitis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Mumps makes glands as big as POM-POMs","text":"Parotitis, Orchitis, aseptic Meningitis, and Pancreatitis are the classic sites of mumps disease."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Wharton duct flows uphill","text":"Thick saliva that has to climb a long duct against gravity sits still and crystallizes. That is why stones form in the submandibular gland."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Pus means bacteria","text":"If pressing the parotid squeezes pus from the duct opening, think bacterial parotitis. Mumps gives a swollen gland with a clean duct."}],"highYield":["Unvaccinated young adult with bilateral parotid swelling and testicular pain: mumps orchitis.","High serum amylase with normal lipase in mumps comes from the salivary gland.","Older, dehydrated, or postoperative patient with one hot parotid and pus at the duct: Staphylococcus aureus parotitis.","Salivary stones form mostly in the submandibular gland because of thick saliva and an upward duct.","Gland swelling and pain that start with eating point to a duct stone.","Mumps can cause aseptic meningitis with a lymphocytic CSF."],"pitfalls":["A raised amylase in mumps does not prove pancreatitis; check lipase, which is pancreas-specific.","Mumps can begin on one side, so one-sided swelling does not rule it out; pus at the duct is what points to bacteria.","Stensen duct drains the parotid; Wharton duct drains the submandibular gland.","Stone pain is intermittent and tied to meals; constant pain with fever means the stagnant gland is now infected."],"dumps":[{"t":"Mumps vs bacterial parotitis","p":"Make a two-column table comparing mumps parotitis and acute bacterial parotitis. Include cause, who gets it, sides involved, duct findings, labs, and complications.","l":["Mumps is a paramyxovirus spread by droplets","Bacterial parotitis is usually Staphylococcus aureus","Mumps is often bilateral; bacterial is usually one side","Pus at Stensen duct means bacterial","Bacterial risk: dehydration, older age, after surgery, low saliva flow","Mumps raises serum amylase with normal lipase","Mumps complications: orchitis, oophoritis, pancreatitis, aseptic meningitis, hearing loss","Mumps is prevented by the MMR vaccine"]},{"t":"Salivary glands and their ducts","p":"Draw the parotid, submandibular, and sublingual glands with their ducts and where each duct opens in the mouth. Mark where stones usually form and write the reasons beside it.","l":["Stensen duct opens opposite the upper second molar","Wharton duct opens at the sublingual papilla beside the frenulum","Parotid is serous; submandibular is mixed; sublingual is mostly mucous","Parotid nerve supply is CN IX via the otic ganglion","About 80 to 90 percent of stones are submandibular","Reasons: thick alkaline saliva rich in calcium, long duct running upward","Blocked duct causes painful swelling at meals and can become infected"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Head and Neck, section on salivary glands"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on mumps virus (paramyxoviruses)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Section on the parotid region and salivary glands"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Salivation: Parotid, Submandibular, & Sublingual Glands","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3tasuHt6jKg"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Salivary glands - Anatomy and Physiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EYZPE5-7vZ8"}],"related":["GI-3","GI-12","GI-13-B1"]},"GI-13-B":{"id":"GI-13-B","summary":"Pancreatitis is the pancreas digesting itself after its enzymes switch on too early. The exam tests the causes, the lab and exam findings of acute pancreatitis, its complications, and how chronic pancreatitis leads to malabsorption and diabetes.","notes":[{"h":"How acute pancreatitis starts","points":["Normally trypsinogen is switched on only in the duodenum, by enteropeptidase.","Safeguards: enzymes are made as inactive zymogens, SPINK1 inhibits stray trypsin, and trypsin can destroy itself.","Acinar injury or duct blockage activates trypsin inside the pancreas.","Trypsin then activates phospholipase A2, elastase, and other enzymes, and the gland digests itself.","Results: protein breakdown, fat necrosis, vessel damage with bleeding, and intense inflammation."]},{"h":"Causes","points":["Gallstones and alcohol cause most cases.","A gallstone at the ampulla blocks the duct and raises pressure in it.","Hypertriglyceridemia (usually above 1,000 mg/dL) and hypercalcemia (calcium activates trypsinogen).","After ERCP, after blunt abdominal trauma, and with mumps.","Drugs include thiazides, azathioprine, valproate, and sulfonamides."]},{"h":"Findings of acute pancreatitis","points":["Sudden epigastric pain boring through to the back, with nausea and vomiting; leaning forward may ease it.","Diagnosis needs two of three: typical pain, lipase or amylase above 3 times the upper limit of normal, typical imaging.","Lipase is more specific than amylase and stays high longer.","Lipase frees fatty acids that bind calcium and form chalky calcium soaps (fat necrosis, saponification).","Calcium used up in soaps causes hypocalcemia, a sign of severe disease.","Cullen sign (periumbilical bruising) and Grey Turner sign (flank bruising) mean blood is tracking from the retroperitoneum."]},{"h":"Complications","points":["Pseudocyst: enzyme-rich fluid walled off by granulation and fibrous tissue, with no epithelial lining, usually after about 4 weeks.","Pseudocysts can compress nearby organs, rupture, bleed, or become infected.","Necrotic pancreas can become infected by gut bacteria, forming an abscess.","ARDS: circulating enzymes (phospholipase A2 breaks down surfactant) and cytokines injure the lungs.","DIC from release of procoagulant tissue factors into the blood.","Hypovolemic shock from massive fluid loss into the retroperitoneum and from bleeding."]},{"h":"Chronic pancreatitis","points":["Repeated injury causes permanent fibrosis, loss of acini, and strictured, dilated ducts.","Calcifications on imaging are typical, especially with alcohol.","Causes: alcohol in adults, cystic fibrosis (thick secretions plug ducts) in children, duct obstruction.","Hereditary: PRSS1 gain-of-function (trypsin resists inactivation, autosomal dominant) and SPINK1 loss of function.","Exocrine failure (after about 90 percent loss): steatorrhea, weight loss, deficiency of vitamins A, D, E, and K.","Islet loss causes diabetes; lipase and amylase may be normal late in disease."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Acute vs chronic pancreatitis","cols":["Feature","Acute pancreatitis","Chronic pancreatitis"],"rows":[["Main lesion","Enzyme autodigestion, edema, fat necrosis, hemorrhage","Fibrosis, acinar loss, duct strictures, calcification"],["Leading causes","Gallstones, alcohol","Alcohol (adults), cystic fibrosis (children)"],["Pain","Sudden severe epigastric pain to the back","Recurrent or constant epigastric pain"],["Lipase and amylase","Above 3 times normal","Often normal"],["Reversible","Usually, if the cause is removed","No"],["Key complications","Pseudocyst, infected necrosis, ARDS, DIC, shock, hypocalcemia","Steatorrhea, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency, diabetes, pancreatic cancer risk"]]},{"title":"Complications of acute pancreatitis and their mechanism","cols":["Complication","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Hypocalcemia","Calcium binds free fatty acids in fat necrosis (calcium soaps)"],["Pseudocyst","Leaked enzymes and debris walled off by granulation tissue, no epithelium"],["Infected necrosis or abscess","Gut bacteria seed dead pancreatic tissue"],["ARDS","Phospholipase A2 and cytokines damage alveoli and surfactant"],["DIC","Tissue factor and activated enzymes trigger clotting"],["Hypovolemic shock","Fluid shifts into the retroperitoneum plus bleeding"],["Cullen and Grey Turner signs","Retroperitoneal hemorrhage tracking to umbilicus or flanks"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"I GET SMASHED","text":"Idiopathic, Gallstones, Ethanol, Trauma, Steroids, Mumps, Autoimmune, Scorpion sting, Hypercalcemia or Hypertriglyceridemia, ERCP, Drugs (such as thiazides and azathioprine)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Pseudo means no lining","text":"A true cyst is lined by epithelium. A pseudocyst is a walled-off pocket lined only by granulation and fibrous tissue."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Calcium goes into soap","text":"Picture lipase making fatty acids that grab calcium like soap scum. The blood calcium falls as it is locked away in dead fat."}],"highYield":["Epigastric pain to the back plus lipase above 3 times normal: acute pancreatitis.","Gallstones and alcohol are the two leading causes.","Triglycerides above about 1,000 mg/dL can cause pancreatitis.","Low calcium in pancreatitis comes from calcium soap formation in fat necrosis.","Pseudocyst wall is granulation tissue with no epithelium.","Cullen and Grey Turner signs mean retroperitoneal hemorrhage.","PRSS1 mutation makes trypsin resist inactivation, causing hereditary pancreatitis.","Chronic pancreatitis gives steatorrhea, vitamin A, D, E, K deficiency, and diabetes."],"pitfalls":["Amylase rises in salivary disease and some bowel disorders; lipase is the more specific pancreatic test.","Pseudocyst vs cystic tumor: pseudocyst follows pancreatitis and has no epithelial lining.","In chronic pancreatitis the enzymes may be normal because little gland is left; diagnosis relies on imaging and function.","Hypercalcemia causes pancreatitis, but pancreatitis causes hypocalcemia."],"dumps":[{"t":"Trypsin cascade gone wrong","p":"Draw an acinar cell and the duodenum. Show where trypsinogen is normally activated, the safeguards, and what happens when trypsin is activated inside the pancreas.","l":["Enteropeptidase in the duodenum normally activates trypsinogen","Safeguards: zymogens, SPINK1 inhibitor, trypsin self-destruction","Early activation inside the gland starts autodigestion","Trypsin activates phospholipase A2 and elastase","Lipase causes fat necrosis with calcium soaps","Elastase damages vessels, causing hemorrhage","PRSS1 mutation blocks trypsin inactivation"]},{"t":"Acute vs chronic pancreatitis table","p":"Build a table comparing acute and chronic pancreatitis: causes, tissue changes, labs, signs, and complications.","l":["Acute: gallstones and alcohol lead the causes","Acute: lipase above 3 times normal; chronic: enzymes often normal","Acute: fat necrosis, hypocalcemia, Cullen and Grey Turner signs","Acute complications: pseudocyst, infected necrosis, ARDS, DIC, shock","Chronic: fibrosis and calcifications","Chronic causes include alcohol, cystic fibrosis, PRSS1, SPINK1","Chronic: steatorrhea, fat-soluble vitamin loss, diabetes"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Pancreas"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Disorders of the Exocrine Pancreas"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract, section on pancreatic secretion"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Pancreatitis | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9j9G3d2gRs4"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Acute Pancreatitis - Overview (signs and symptoms, pathophysiology, investigations, treatment)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UVof2fZdfjY"}],"related":["GI-6","GI-7","GI-10","GI-13-D2","GI-13-E3","GI-13-K6"]},"GI-13-C":{"id":"GI-13-C","summary":"This unit covers the main noninfectious liver diseases: blocked bile flow, inherited bilirubin disorders, alcohol, fatty liver, autoimmune and drug hepatitis, and the cirrhosis and portal hypertension they can lead to. Exam questions give you liver tests and physical signs and ask you to name the process and predict what happens next.","notes":[{"h":"Cholestasis","points":["Intrahepatic causes: drugs (estrogens, anabolic steroids), pregnancy (third trimester itching), total parenteral nutrition, sepsis, primary biliary cholangitis.","Extrahepatic causes: common bile duct stone, pancreatic head cancer, cholangiocarcinoma, strictures; ultrasound shows dilated ducts.","Labs: conjugated bilirubin, alkaline phosphatase, and GGT rise; GGT confirms the ALP comes from the liver.","Retained bile acids cause itching; high cholesterol causes xanthomas.","No bile reaches the gut: pale stools, dark urine (conjugated bilirubin), poor fat and vitamin K absorption."]},{"h":"Cirrhosis: how it forms","points":["Hepatic stellate (Ito) cells sit in the space of Disse and normally store vitamin A.","Chronic injury releases TGF-beta and other signals that turn stellate cells into collagen-making myofibroblasts.","Collagen in the space of Disse closes sinusoid fenestrations, so exchange with hepatocytes falls.","Bridging fibrous septa and regenerative nodules replace the normal lobules across the whole liver.","Main causes: alcohol, chronic HBV and HCV, fatty liver disease, and metabolic disease (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency)."]},{"h":"Cirrhosis: findings from liver failure","points":["Jaundice from failed conjugation and excretion of bilirubin.","Coagulopathy: the liver makes fewer clotting factors (II, V, VII, IX, X), so PT and INR rise and vitamin K does not correct them.","Low albumin causes edema and adds to ascites.","Poor estrogen breakdown: spider angiomas, palmar erythema, gynecomastia, testicular atrophy.","Hepatic encephalopathy: ammonia escapes the urea cycle and shunts past the liver; confusion and asterixis."]},{"h":"Portal hypertension","points":["Prehepatic: portal vein thrombosis; varices and splenomegaly with a normal liver.","Intrahepatic: cirrhosis (most common) and schistosomiasis (presinusoidal periportal fibrosis).","Posthepatic: Budd-Chiari syndrome (hepatic vein thrombosis) and right heart failure (nutmeg liver).","Findings: ascites, congestive splenomegaly with low platelets, varices, caput medusae."]},{"h":"Hepatorenal and hepatopulmonary syndromes","points":["Hepatorenal: splanchnic vasodilation lowers effective arterial volume and triggers RAAS and sympathetic drive.","The kidneys clamp down (renal vasoconstriction), so GFR falls and urine sodium is very low, yet the kidneys look normal under the microscope.","Hepatopulmonary: nitric oxide widens lung capillaries, so oxygen cannot diffuse to blood in the middle of the vessel, causing hypoxemia.","Dilations are at the lung bases, so oxygen saturation drops on standing (orthodeoxia)."]},{"h":"Inherited bilirubin disorders","points":["Gilbert: mildly reduced UGT1A1; unconjugated bilirubin rises with fasting, stress, illness; benign.","Crigler-Najjar type I: absent UGT1A1; severe unconjugated jaundice and kernicterus in infancy.","Crigler-Najjar type II: partial deficiency, milder; bilirubin falls with phenobarbital, which induces UGT1A1.","Dubin-Johnson: MRP2 defect blocks secretion of conjugated bilirubin; black liver; benign.","Rotor: similar conjugated rise, milder, and the liver is not pigmented."]},{"h":"Noninfectious hepatitis","points":["Alcohol: steatosis (reversible) to alcoholic hepatitis to cirrhosis.","Alcoholic hepatitis: ballooned hepatocytes, Mallory-Denk bodies (clumped keratin), neutrophils; AST to ALT ratio above 2.","MASLD: insulin resistance and obesity flood the liver with fatty acids; ALT usually above AST.","Simple steatosis is fat only; MASH adds inflammation and ballooning and can progress to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma.","Autoimmune hepatitis: young women, high IgG, ANA and anti-smooth muscle antibody, interface hepatitis with plasma cells.","Acetaminophen overdose: CYP2E1 makes NAPQI, glutathione runs out, zone 3 (centrilobular) necrosis; N-acetylcysteine replaces glutathione.","Isoniazid causes hepatitis that is more common with older age and alcohol use."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Primary biliary cholangitis vs primary sclerosing cholangitis","cols":["Feature","Primary biliary cholangitis","Primary sclerosing cholangitis"],"rows":[["Typical patient","Middle-aged woman","Young or middle-aged man"],["Ducts affected","Small intrahepatic ducts","Intrahepatic and extrahepatic ducts"],["Histology","Granulomatous, lymphocytic destruction of small ducts","Onion-skin periductal fibrosis"],["Imaging","Usually normal large ducts","Beaded ducts (strictures and dilations) on cholangiography"],["Antibody","Antimitochondrial antibody","p-ANCA in many"],["Associations","Other autoimmune disease (Sjogren syndrome, thyroiditis)","Ulcerative colitis"],["Cancer risk","Hepatocellular carcinoma after cirrhosis","Cholangiocarcinoma"]]},{"title":"Inherited disorders of bilirubin","cols":["Disorder","Defect","Bilirubin type","Key features"],"rows":[["Gilbert syndrome","Mildly reduced UGT1A1","Unconjugated","Mild jaundice with fasting, stress, illness; benign"],["Crigler-Najjar type I","Absent UGT1A1","Unconjugated","Severe neonatal jaundice, kernicterus; no response to phenobarbital"],["Crigler-Najjar type II","Partial UGT1A1 deficiency","Unconjugated","Milder; bilirubin falls with phenobarbital"],["Dubin-Johnson syndrome","MRP2 canalicular transporter","Conjugated","Black pigmented liver; benign"],["Rotor syndrome","Hepatic bilirubin reuptake defect (OATP1B1 and OATP1B3)","Conjugated","Milder; liver not pigmented; benign"]]},{"title":"Causes of portal hypertension by site","cols":["Site","Examples","Notes"],"rows":[["Prehepatic","Portal vein thrombosis","Varices and splenomegaly; liver itself normal"],["Intrahepatic","Cirrhosis, schistosomiasis","Cirrhosis is the most common cause overall"],["Posthepatic","Budd-Chiari syndrome, right heart failure, constrictive pericarditis","Congested, enlarged liver (nutmeg liver)"]]},{"title":"Portosystemic anastomoses","cols":["Site","Portal side","Systemic side","Result"],"rows":[["Esophagus","Left gastric vein","Esophageal veins to azygos","Esophageal varices"],["Umbilicus","Paraumbilical veins","Superficial epigastric veins","Caput medusae"],["Rectum","Superior rectal vein","Middle and inferior rectal veins","Anorectal varices"]]},{"title":"Noninfectious hepatitis at a glance","cols":["Type","Key mechanism","Histology","Lab clue"],"rows":[["Alcoholic hepatitis","Alcohol metabolism injures hepatocytes and mitochondria","Ballooning, Mallory-Denk bodies, neutrophils","AST to ALT above 2"],["MASH","Insulin resistance and fatty acid overload","Steatosis, ballooning, inflammation","ALT usually above AST"],["Autoimmune hepatitis","T cell attack on hepatocytes","Interface hepatitis with plasma cells","High IgG, ANA, anti-smooth muscle antibody"],["Acetaminophen","NAPQI after glutathione is used up","Zone 3 (centrilobular) necrosis","Very high AST and ALT"],["Isoniazid","Toxic metabolite injury","Hepatitis pattern","Rising AST and ALT"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"ToAST","text":"In alcoholic liver disease AST runs higher than ALT, usually more than twice as high. Picture the liver toasted by alcohol."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Dubin-Johnson is dark","text":"Dubin-Johnson makes the liver black. Rotor is the look-alike with a normal colored liver."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Before, in, after the liver","text":"Sort portal hypertension by following blood: portal vein (before), sinusoids (in), hepatic veins and right heart (after)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Gilbert skips breakfast","text":"A healthy person who turns slightly yellow after fasting or a cold, with normal liver enzymes and no hemolysis, has Gilbert syndrome."}],"highYield":["Middle-aged woman, itching, high ALP, antimitochondrial antibody: primary biliary cholangitis.","Ulcerative colitis with beaded bile ducts: primary sclerosing cholangitis, with cholangiocarcinoma risk.","Stellate cells in the space of Disse make the collagen of cirrhosis.","Spider angiomas, palmar erythema, and gynecomastia come from high estrogen in liver failure.","Asterixis and confusion in cirrhosis: hepatic encephalopathy from ammonia.","Hepatorenal syndrome: kidney failure with very low urine sodium and normal kidney histology.","Acetaminophen overdose injures zone 3 first; N-acetylcysteine restores glutathione.","Mallory-Denk bodies plus AST to ALT above 2: alcoholic hepatitis."],"pitfalls":["Gilbert and Crigler-Najjar raise unconjugated bilirubin; Dubin-Johnson and Rotor raise conjugated bilirubin.","High PT from cholestasis corrects with vitamin K; high PT from liver failure does not.","PBC damages small intrahepatic ducts; PSC damages large and small ducts with beading.","Budd-Chiari is posthepatic (hepatic veins); portal vein thrombosis is prehepatic.","MASLD has ALT higher than AST; alcohol has AST higher than ALT."],"dumps":[{"t":"Cirrhosis from cell to patient","p":"Draw a liver sinusoid with a hepatocyte, the space of Disse, and a stellate cell. Show how injury leads to fibrosis, then list the signs from liver failure and from portal hypertension.","l":["Stellate cell in space of Disse stores vitamin A","TGF-beta activates it into a collagen-making myofibroblast","Collagen closes sinusoid fenestrations","Bridging fibrosis and regenerative nodules","Liver failure: jaundice, coagulopathy, low albumin, encephalopathy","High estrogen: spider angiomas, palmar erythema, gynecomastia","Portal hypertension: ascites, splenomegaly, varices, caput medusae","Complications include hepatorenal and hepatopulmonary syndromes"]},{"t":"Jaundice sorting table","p":"Make a table of Gilbert, Crigler-Najjar I and II, Dubin-Johnson, Rotor, and cholestasis. For each give the defect, bilirubin type, and one key finding.","l":["Gilbert: mild UGT1A1 drop, unconjugated, worse with fasting","Crigler-Najjar I: absent UGT1A1, kernicterus","Crigler-Najjar II: partial, responds to phenobarbital","Dubin-Johnson: MRP2 defect, conjugated, black liver","Rotor: conjugated, milder, liver not pigmented","Cholestasis: conjugated bilirubin with high ALP and GGT","Cholestasis signs: itching, pale stools, dark urine, xanthomas"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Liver Disease"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on bilirubin metabolism and jaundice"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cirrhosis | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R1f1gWCyutA"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Types of Jaundice","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2qgtG_KGz9A"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Liver Cirrhosis","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=f-Fxlsb2dtQ"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Approach to Jaundice - types and causes of hyperbilirubinemia, investigations","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdZFGEJ3bqQ"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Pathophysiology Of Jaundice | Dr Najeeb Lectures","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6tU6BS6x_cs"}],"related":["GI-9","GI-5","GI-13-I1","GI-13-J1","GI-13-K4","GI-13-L7","GI-13-G9"]},"GI-13-D":{"id":"GI-13-D","summary":"Gallstones are common, and where a stone ends up decides the disease it causes. The exam tests stone types and risk factors, acute cholecystitis and its calculous and acalculous forms, and complications such as cholangitis, gallstone pancreatitis, gallstone ileus, and Mirizzi syndrome.","notes":[{"h":"How stones form","points":["Bile salts and lecithin hold cholesterol in micelles.","Cholesterol stones form when bile has too much cholesterol or too few bile salts, plus gallbladder stasis.","Cholesterol stone risks: female sex, obesity, pregnancy, estrogen or oral contraceptives, rapid weight loss, older age, fibrates.","Estrogen increases cholesterol secretion into bile; progesterone slows gallbladder emptying.","Black pigment stones (calcium bilirubinate) form with chronic hemolysis such as sickle cell disease and hereditary spherocytosis.","Brown pigment stones form in infected bile ducts: bacterial beta-glucuronidase frees unconjugated bilirubin."]},{"h":"Where the stone goes, what it causes","points":["In the gallbladder: often silent.","Brief block of the cystic duct: biliary colic, pain after a fatty meal (CCK makes the gallbladder contract) that settles within hours, normal labs.","Stuck in the cystic duct: acute cholecystitis.","In the common bile duct (choledocholithiasis): obstructive jaundice with high conjugated bilirubin and ALP.","Common duct stone plus infection: ascending cholangitis with fever, jaundice, and right upper quadrant pain (Charcot triad).","At the ampulla: gallstone pancreatitis.","Through a cholecystoenteric fistula: gallstone ileus, small bowel obstruction usually at the ileocecal valve, with air in the biliary tree."]},{"h":"Acute cholecystitis","points":["Calculous: a stone stays stuck in the cystic duct and the gallbladder distends.","First chemical inflammation (lysolecithin and prostaglandins irritate the wall), then bacterial infection by gut organisms such as E. coli.","Right upper quadrant pain lasting more than about 6 hours, fever, leukocytosis.","Murphy sign: the patient stops breathing in when the examiner presses under the right costal margin.","Acalculous: no stone; occurs in critically ill patients (sepsis, burns, trauma, major surgery, TPN) from stasis and ischemia.","Acalculous disease has a higher risk of gangrene and perforation."]},{"h":"Chronic cholecystitis and cancer risk","points":["Repeated attacks scar and thicken the gallbladder wall.","Calcium deposits in the scarred wall give a porcelain gallbladder.","Porcelain gallbladder and long-standing gallstones raise the risk of gallbladder adenocarcinoma."]},{"h":"Mirizzi syndrome","points":["A stone stuck in the cystic duct or gallbladder neck presses on the common hepatic duct from outside.","Result: obstructive jaundice even though no stone sits in the common bile duct."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Gallstone types","cols":["Type","Made of","Main cause or risk","Where it forms"],"rows":[["Cholesterol","Cholesterol (most common type)","Supersaturated bile and stasis: female, obesity, pregnancy, estrogen, rapid weight loss","Gallbladder"],["Black pigment","Calcium bilirubinate","Chronic hemolysis (sickle cell disease, hereditary spherocytosis)","Gallbladder"],["Brown pigment","Calcium bilirubinate with fatty acids","Biliary infection (E. coli, liver flukes)","Bile ducts"]]},{"title":"Gallstone complications by location","cols":["Stone location","Condition","Key findings"],"rows":[["Cystic duct, brief","Biliary colic","Postprandial pain, settles within hours, normal labs"],["Cystic duct, persistent","Acute cholecystitis","Pain over 6 hours, fever, Murphy sign, leukocytosis"],["Cystic duct or gallbladder neck pressing on common hepatic duct","Mirizzi syndrome","Obstructive jaundice"],["Common bile duct","Choledocholithiasis","Jaundice, high conjugated bilirubin and ALP"],["Common bile duct with infection","Ascending cholangitis","Fever, jaundice, right upper quadrant pain"],["Ampulla of Vater","Gallstone pancreatitis","Epigastric pain to back, high lipase"],["Ileocecal valve via fistula","Gallstone ileus","Small bowel obstruction, air in biliary tree"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"The 4 Fs","text":"Female, Fat (obese), Fertile (pregnancy, multiparity), Forty: classic risk factors for cholesterol gallstones."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Black is blood, brown is bugs","text":"Black pigment stones come from hemolysis. Brown pigment stones come from bacteria or parasites in the bile ducts."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Add one finding, change one diagnosis","text":"Pain under 6 hours with normal labs is biliary colic. Pain over 6 hours with fever and leukocytosis is cholecystitis. Jaundice points to a stone in the common bile duct, and fever with jaundice and right upper quadrant pain is cholangitis."}],"highYield":["Charcot triad (fever, jaundice, right upper quadrant pain) means ascending cholangitis.","Reynolds pentad adds hypotension and confusion to Charcot triad.","Air in the biliary tree with small bowel obstruction in an older woman: gallstone ileus.","Critically ill patient with gallbladder inflammation and no stones: acalculous cholecystitis.","Porcelain gallbladder is linked to gallbladder adenocarcinoma.","Hemolytic anemia in a child with gallstones: black pigment stones.","Mirizzi syndrome causes jaundice from outside compression of the common hepatic duct."],"pitfalls":["Biliary colic lasts a few hours with normal labs; cholecystitis lasts longer with fever and leukocytosis.","Plain cholecystitis rarely causes marked jaundice; jaundice suggests a duct stone, cholangitis, or Mirizzi syndrome.","Gallstone ileus is a mechanical obstruction of the small bowel, usually at the ileocecal valve.","Black stones form in the gallbladder; brown stones form in the bile ducts."],"dumps":[{"t":"Biliary tree stone map","p":"Draw the gallbladder, cystic duct, common hepatic duct, common bile duct, pancreatic duct, and duodenum. Place a stone at each site and write the condition it causes.","l":["Brief cystic duct block: biliary colic","Persistent cystic duct block: acute cholecystitis","Gallbladder neck stone pressing common hepatic duct: Mirizzi syndrome","Common bile duct: choledocholithiasis with obstructive jaundice","Duct stone plus infection: ascending cholangitis (Charcot triad)","Ampulla: gallstone pancreatitis","Fistula to bowel: gallstone ileus at the ileocecal valve"]},{"t":"Stone types table","p":"Make a table of cholesterol, black pigment, and brown pigment stones with what they are made of, the cause, and where they form.","l":["Cholesterol stones are the most common","Cholesterol stones need supersaturated bile and stasis","Risks: female, obesity, pregnancy, estrogen, rapid weight loss","Black stones: calcium bilirubinate from chronic hemolysis","Brown stones: infection, bacterial beta-glucuronidase","Brown stones form in the bile ducts"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder, section on gallbladder diseases"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract, section on bile secretion"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, Lipoprotein, and Steroid Metabolism"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Biliary Tract Diseases | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSiifa2PR_Y"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Acute Cholecystitis - Overview (signs and symptoms, pathophysiology, treatment)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dGdQ-xWXOTE"}],"related":["GI-3","GI-6","GI-9","GI-13-B1","GI-13-C1","GI-13-F"]},"GI-13-E":{"id":"GI-13-E","summary":"Malabsorption happens when food is not broken down (enzyme or acid problems) or not taken up by a damaged or bypassed bowel. The exam asks you to match the stool, the labs, and the vitamin pattern to the cause: achlorhydria, celiac disease, pancreatic or brush border enzyme loss, lactase deficiency, short bowel, or bacterial overgrowth.","notes":[{"h":"Achlorhydria","points":["Causes: autoimmune atrophic gastritis (antibodies to parietal cells and intrinsic factor), chronic H. pylori pangastritis with atrophy, gastrectomy.","No acid means no feedback on antral G cells, so gastrin rises.","Gastrin drives ECL cell hyperplasia, which can progress to gastric carcinoid (neuroendocrine) tumors.","Iron needs acid to stay soluble and reduced (Fe2+), so iron deficiency follows.","Acid and pepsin free B12 from food; autoimmune gastritis also loses intrinsic factor, causing pernicious anemia.","Without the acid barrier, bacteria overgrow in the stomach and small bowel."]},{"h":"Celiac disease","points":["Tissue transglutaminase deamidates gliadin, which binds HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 and activates CD4 T cells.","Biopsy: villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, more intraepithelial lymphocytes.","Worst in the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum, where iron and folate are absorbed.","Iron deficiency anemia that does not respond to oral iron is a common first sign.","Antibodies: IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase and antiendomysial; check total IgA because IgA deficiency is linked.","Dermatitis herpetiformis: itchy vesicles on elbows and knees with IgA in the dermal papillae.","Long-term risk of enteropathy-associated T cell lymphoma."]},{"h":"Enzyme deficiency vs mucosal disease","points":["Pancreatic insufficiency (chronic pancreatitis, cystic fibrosis) blocks digestion of fat and protein: steatorrhea, weight loss, low vitamins A, D, E, K.","Fecal elastase is low in pancreatic insufficiency.","D-xylose needs no digestion, so it tests the mucosa: normal in pancreatic disease, low in mucosal disease such as celiac.","Enteropeptidase (brush border) normally activates trypsinogen; congenital deficiency stops all protease activation.","Enteropeptidase deficiency causes protein malabsorption in infants: diarrhea, failure to thrive, low protein, edema."]},{"h":"Lactase deficiency","points":["Primary (adult type): lactase falls after childhood; common in people of East Asian, African, and Native American ancestry; villi are normal.","Secondary: villus tips are injured, as after viral enteritis or in celiac disease.","Unabsorbed lactose pulls water into the gut: osmotic diarrhea with a high stool osmotic gap and acidic stool.","Colon bacteria ferment lactose to gas and acids: bloating, cramps, flatulence after dairy.","Hydrogen breath test rises after a lactose load; diarrhea stops with fasting."]},{"h":"Short bowel and blind loop","points":["Short bowel after extensive resection: watery diarrhea, steatorrhea, weight loss.","Losing the terminal ileum loses B12 uptake and bile acid recycling, so B12 deficiency and fat malabsorption follow.","Unabsorbed fat binds calcium, leaving oxalate free to be absorbed in the colon: calcium oxalate kidney stones.","Blind loop after gastric surgery (stasis) lets bacteria overgrow.","Bacteria deconjugate bile acids (micelles fail, steatorrhea) and consume B12, while making folate (normal or high folate)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Sorting malabsorption","cols":["Condition","Main defect","D-xylose test","Key clue"],"rows":[["Pancreatic insufficiency","No pancreatic enzymes, no digestion","Normal","Low fecal elastase, steatorrhea"],["Celiac disease","Damaged villi in duodenum and jejunum","Low","Anti-tTG IgA, iron deficiency, dermatitis herpetiformis"],["Lactase deficiency","Missing brush border lactase","Normal (primary form)","Positive hydrogen breath test after lactose"],["Bacterial overgrowth","Bacteria deconjugate bile acids and use B12","Low, corrects with antibiotics","Low B12, normal or high folate"],["Terminal ileal resection","Loss of B12 and bile acid uptake","Normal if jejunum is intact","B12 deficiency, oxalate stones"],["Enteropeptidase deficiency","Trypsinogen never activated","Normal","Infant with protein malabsorption and edema"]]},{"title":"Consequences of achlorhydria","cols":["Loss of acid leads to","Why"],"rows":[["Hypergastrinemia","No acid feedback on antral G cells"],["ECL hyperplasia and carcinoid tumors","Gastrin is trophic to ECL cells"],["Iron deficiency","Iron needs acid to stay soluble and reduced"],["B12 deficiency","Acid and pepsin free B12 from food; intrinsic factor lost in autoimmune gastritis"],["Bacterial overgrowth","No acid barrier to swallowed bacteria"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"D-xylose skips digestion","text":"D-xylose is absorbed without enzymes, so it only tests the bowel wall. Low result means mucosal disease (or bacteria eating it); normal result points to the pancreas."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Bacteria eat B12 and make folate","text":"In bacterial overgrowth B12 falls and folate stays normal or rises. Two vitamins moving in opposite directions is the clue."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Tips go first","text":"Lactase sits at the villus tips, so any mucosal injury (viral enteritis, celiac) knocks it out first."}],"highYield":["Autoimmune gastritis: achlorhydria, high gastrin, pernicious anemia, and carcinoid risk.","Celiac disease: villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, intraepithelial lymphocytes, worst in distal duodenum.","Iron deficiency anemia not fixed by oral iron should prompt testing for celiac disease.","Dermatitis herpetiformis is a skin sign of celiac disease.","Steatorrhea with normal D-xylose and low fecal elastase: pancreatic insufficiency.","Bloating and diarrhea after dairy with normal villi: primary lactase deficiency.","Ileal resection leads to B12 deficiency, bile acid loss, and calcium oxalate stones.","Blind loop: low B12 with normal or high folate."],"pitfalls":["Pernicious anemia lacks intrinsic factor; bacterial overgrowth has intrinsic factor but bacteria use up the B12.","Primary lactase deficiency has normal villi; secondary lactase deficiency follows mucosal injury.","Oxalate stones after bowel resection need the colon to be present, because oxalate is absorbed there.","Celiac IgA tests can be falsely negative in IgA deficiency, so total IgA is checked."],"dumps":[{"t":"Malabsorption sorting table","p":"Make a table of pancreatic insufficiency, celiac disease, lactase deficiency, and bacterial overgrowth. Give the defect, the D-xylose result, and the key test or clue for each.","l":["Pancreatic insufficiency: normal D-xylose, low fecal elastase","Celiac: low D-xylose, anti-tTG IgA, villous atrophy","Celiac distribution: distal duodenum and proximal jejunum","Lactase deficiency: osmotic diarrhea, positive hydrogen breath test","Primary lactase deficiency: normal villi","Bacterial overgrowth: deconjugated bile acids, steatorrhea","Bacterial overgrowth: low B12, normal or high folate"]},{"t":"Achlorhydria chain","p":"Draw the stomach with parietal cells, G cells, and ECL cells. Trace what happens when acid is lost, ending with each nutrient and tumor consequence.","l":["Causes: autoimmune gastritis, H. pylori pangastritis, gastrectomy","No acid feedback on G cells, gastrin rises","Gastrin causes ECL hyperplasia and carcinoid risk","Iron deficiency from loss of acid","B12 deficiency, plus intrinsic factor loss in autoimmune gastritis","Bacterial overgrowth from loss of the acid barrier"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Digestion and Absorption in the Gastrointestinal Tract"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Gastrointestinal Disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Malabsorption | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kx1UgaqpYkc"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Coeliac (Celiac) Disease - Overview (signs and symptoms, pathophysiology, diagnosis, treatment)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Hxd0msD954I"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"What is celiac disease | Gastrointestinal system diseases | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gZeliSjhkpg"}],"related":["GI-6","GI-7","GI-11","GI-13-B1","GI-13-G7"]},"GI-13-F":{"id":"GI-13-F","summary":"This unit covers disorders that block or stall movement through the gut, from the esophagus to the rectum. The exam asks you to tell a nerve or muscle failure (achalasia, ileus, Hirschsprung disease) from a mechanical block (hernia, intussusception, volvulus) and to predict the findings and complications of each.","notes":[{"h":"Achalasia and Zenker diverticulum","points":["Achalasia: loss of inhibitory (nitric oxide and VIP) neurons in the myenteric plexus.","Result: the LES cannot relax and the distal esophagus loses peristalsis.","Dysphagia to solids and liquids from the start, regurgitation, weight loss.","Barium swallow shows a dilated esophagus tapering to a bird beak; manometry shows incomplete LES relaxation and no peristalsis.","Chagas disease (Trypanosoma cruzi) destroys the plexus and causes secondary achalasia.","Zenker diverticulum: a false diverticulum (mucosa and submucosa) pushing out above the cricopharyngeus.","Cricopharyngeal dysfunction raises pressure; regurgitation of undigested food, halitosis, aspiration in older adults."]},{"h":"Adynamic ileus vs mechanical obstruction","points":["Adynamic (paralytic) ileus: the bowel stops moving with no physical block.","Causes: abdominal surgery, peritonitis, hypokalemia, opioids.","Adynamic signs: distension, absent bowel sounds, gas spread through small and large bowel.","Mechanical obstruction causes: adhesions (most common in the small bowel), hernias, tumors, impaction.","Mechanical signs: colicky pain, vomiting, high-pitched bowel sounds early, dilated loops above a transition point with little gas below."]},{"h":"Hernias","points":["Sliding hiatal hernia: the GE junction slides up into the chest; most common type; linked to reflux.","Paraesophageal hiatal hernia: GE junction stays in place and the fundus rolls up beside the esophagus; can incarcerate or strangulate.","Incarcerated hernia: bowel is trapped and cannot be pushed back.","Strangulation: the tight neck squeezes thin-walled veins first, then swelling raises pressure until arterial inflow stops and the bowel infarcts.","Femoral hernias have the highest risk of strangulation."]},{"h":"Intussusception and volvulus","points":["Intussusception: one segment telescopes into the next, most often ileocolic in children about 6 months to 3 years old.","Lead points: hypertrophied Peyer patches after viral infection, Meckel diverticulum, polyps; in adults, a tumor.","Findings: bouts of colicky pain with legs drawn up, sausage-shaped mass, currant jelly stool (mucosal ischemia), target sign on ultrasound.","Volvulus: bowel twists on its mesentery, blocking the lumen and its blood supply.","Sigmoid volvulus is most common in older adults with chronic constipation (coffee bean sign).","Midgut volvulus occurs in infants with malrotation and presents with bilious vomiting."]},{"h":"Megacolon","points":["Hirschsprung disease: neural crest cells fail to migrate, so ganglion cells are missing from the rectum and a continuous segment above it.","The aganglionic segment stays contracted; colon above it dilates.","Newborn fails to pass meconium within 48 hours, distended abdomen, empty rectal vault, then explosive stool after a rectal exam.","Linked to RET mutations and Down syndrome; diagnosed by rectal suction biopsy.","Chagas disease destroys the myenteric plexus later in life (acquired megacolon and megaesophagus).","Toxic megacolon: acute colon dilation with systemic toxicity complicating ulcerative colitis or C. difficile colitis; risk of perforation."]},{"h":"Functional motility disorders","points":["Gastroparesis: delayed gastric emptying with no blockage, most often from diabetic autonomic (vagal) neuropathy.","Gastroparesis symptoms: early satiety, nausea, vomiting of undigested food hours after meals, bloating.","Irritable bowel syndrome: recurrent abdominal pain linked to defecation and a change in stool frequency or form.","IBS has no structural disease; weight loss, bleeding, or anemia suggest another diagnosis."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Adynamic ileus vs mechanical obstruction","cols":["Feature","Adynamic (paralytic) ileus","Mechanical obstruction"],"rows":[["Problem","Bowel stops contracting","Physical block in or around the lumen"],["Common causes","Surgery, peritonitis, hypokalemia, opioids","Adhesions, hernias, tumors, impaction, volvulus"],["Pain","Mild, diffuse","Colicky"],["Bowel sounds","Absent or reduced","High-pitched and tinkling early, absent late"],["Imaging","Gas spread through small and large bowel","Dilated loops above a transition point, little gas below"]]},{"title":"Intussusception vs volvulus","cols":["Feature","Intussusception","Volvulus"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Segment telescopes into the next segment","Bowel twists around its mesentery"],["Typical patient","Child about 6 months to 3 years","Older adult (sigmoid) or infant with malrotation (midgut)"],["Usual site","Ileocolic","Sigmoid colon in adults; midgut in infants"],["Lead point or risk","Peyer patches, Meckel diverticulum, polyp, tumor in adults","Redundant sigmoid, chronic constipation, malrotation"],["Classic findings","Colicky pain, sausage mass, currant jelly stool, target sign","Distension, coffee bean sign, bilious vomiting in infants"]]},{"title":"Causes of megacolon","cols":["Cause","Mechanism","Classic picture"],"rows":[["Hirschsprung disease","Failed neural crest migration, no ganglion cells in rectum and segment above","Newborn with delayed meconium, empty rectal vault"],["Chagas disease","Trypanosoma cruzi destroys the myenteric plexus","Adult from Latin America with megacolon, megaesophagus, cardiomyopathy"],["Toxic megacolon","Severe colitis paralyzes colon muscle","Ulcerative colitis or C. difficile colitis with fever, tachycardia, dilated colon"]]},{"title":"Hiatal hernia types","cols":["Feature","Sliding","Paraesophageal"],"rows":[["GE junction","Moves up into the chest","Stays below the diaphragm"],["What herniates","GE junction and cardia","Fundus beside the esophagus"],["Frequency","Most common","Less common"],["Main problem","Reflux","Incarceration, strangulation, volvulus"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"A-chalasia means no relaxing","text":"Chalasia means relaxation. Achalasia is the LES that cannot relax because its nitric oxide neurons are gone."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Solids first or both at once","text":"A mechanical narrowing (stricture, cancer) blocks solids first. A motility problem like achalasia blocks solids and liquids from the start."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Veins before arteries","text":"In strangulation, soft veins collapse first, the bowel swells, and only then are the arteries squeezed shut. That is why trapped bowel turns dark and swollen before it dies."}],"highYield":["Bird-beak esophagus with dysphagia to solids and liquids: achalasia.","Achalasia in a patient from Latin America: think Chagas disease.","Older adult with halitosis and regurgitation of old food: Zenker diverticulum.","Absent bowel sounds after surgery or with low potassium: adynamic ileus.","Currant jelly stool and a sausage-shaped mass in a toddler: intussusception.","Coffee bean sign in an older constipated patient: sigmoid volvulus.","Newborn with no meconium by 48 hours and an empty rectum: Hirschsprung disease.","Diabetic with early satiety and vomiting of food eaten hours ago: gastroparesis."],"pitfalls":["Hirschsprung always involves the rectum; the dilated part is the normal colon above the aganglionic segment.","Zenker is a false diverticulum (no muscle layer); Meckel diverticulum is a true diverticulum.","A sliding hernia moves the GE junction up; a paraesophageal hernia leaves it in place.","Ileus has diffuse gas with no transition point; mechanical obstruction has a transition point."],"dumps":[{"t":"Obstruction types table","p":"Make a table comparing adynamic ileus, intussusception, volvulus, and strangulated hernia. Include cause, typical patient, and key findings.","l":["Adynamic ileus: surgery, peritonitis, hypokalemia, opioids","Adynamic ileus: absent bowel sounds, diffuse gas","Intussusception: ileocolic in young children with a lead point","Intussusception: currant jelly stool, sausage mass, target sign","Volvulus: sigmoid in older adults, midgut with malrotation in infants","Strangulation: venous block, then swelling, then arterial block and infarction","Mechanical obstruction: high-pitched bowel sounds and a transition point"]},{"t":"Enteric nervous system failures","p":"Draw the gut wall layers and label the myenteric and submucosal plexuses. Then show what goes wrong in achalasia, Hirschsprung disease, Chagas disease, and gastroparesis.","l":["Myenteric plexus lies between the muscle layers; submucosal plexus in the submucosa","Achalasia: loss of nitric oxide and VIP inhibitory neurons","Achalasia: LES fails to relax, no peristalsis, bird beak","Hirschsprung: neural crest migration failure, no ganglion cells in rectum","Hirschsprung: delayed meconium, empty rectal vault","Chagas: Trypanosoma cruzi destroys the plexus, megaesophagus and megacolon","Gastroparesis: diabetic vagal neuropathy slows gastric emptying"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Physiology of Gastrointestinal Disorders"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Section on the neural crest and enteric nervous system"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Section on the inguinal region and hernias"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Bowel Obstruction | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6GhdESRDkqY"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Esophageal Motility Disorders | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rc-ZnGg4QMA"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Achalasia (esophageal) - signs and symptoms, pathophysiology, investigations and treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IthD_e6SIio"}],"related":["GI-1","GI-6","GI-13-G8","GI-13-G9","GI-13-H3","GI-13-I2"]},"GI-13-G":{"id":"GI-13-G","summary":"Inflammatory GI diseases run from reflux, esophagitis, gastritis, and ulcers in the upper tract to appendicitis, diverticular disease, and inflammatory bowel disease lower down. Exams test what injures the mucosa, where the lesion sits, and which complication follows.","notes":[{"h":"Reflux and Barrett esophagus","points":["GERD comes from transient LES relaxations, low resting LES tone, and hiatal hernia.","Risks: obesity and pregnancy (raised abdominal pressure; progesterone relaxes the LES), smoking, alcohol, fatty meals.","Symptoms: heartburn and regurgitation; aspiration and vagal reflexes add chronic cough, hoarseness, and wheeze.","Reflux esophagitis biopsy: basal cell hyperplasia, elongated lamina propria papillae, a few eosinophils.","Complications: peptic stricture with dysphagia, ulcer, bleeding, and Barrett esophagus.","Barrett: distal squamous epithelium replaced by intestinal-type columnar epithelium with goblet cells; then low-grade dysplasia, high-grade dysplasia, adenocarcinoma."]},{"h":"Other esophageal injury","points":["Eosinophilic esophagitis: atopic young men, dysphagia and food impaction, 15 or more eosinophils per high-power field, rings and linear furrows.","Pill esophagitis: doxycycline, NSAIDs, potassium chloride, bisphosphonates; a pill taken with little water while lying down sits and burns a focal ulcer.","Mallory-Weiss: longitudinal mucosal tear at the gastroesophageal junction after forceful retching (alcohol use, bulimia); hematemesis.","Boerhaave: full-thickness rupture, usually the distal left wall; severe chest pain, pneumomediastinum, subcutaneous emphysema, mediastinitis."]},{"h":"Gastritis","points":["NSAIDs block COX, so less prostaglandin: less mucus and bicarbonate, less mucosal blood flow, more acid.","Alcohol, uremia, and shock also cause acute erosive gastritis.","Curling ulcer: severe burns cause hypovolemia and mucosal ischemia. Cushing ulcer: brain injury raises vagal output, so acid rises.","H. pylori gastritis starts in the antrum; urease makes ammonia; leads to intestinal metaplasia, adenocarcinoma, MALT lymphoma, and duodenal ulcer.","Autoimmune gastritis hits the body and fundus; T cells and antibodies destroy parietal cells, giving achlorhydria, high gastrin, and pernicious anemia."]},{"h":"Peptic ulcers","points":["H. pylori and NSAIDs cause most ulcers; Zollinger-Ellison gives multiple, refractory, or distal ulcers.","H. pylori in the antrum lowers somatostatin, so gastrin and acid rise and the duodenum takes the hit.","Posterior duodenal ulcers erode the gastroduodenal artery; lesser curvature gastric ulcers erode the left gastric artery.","Anterior duodenal ulcers perforate: sudden severe pain, rigid abdomen, free air under the diaphragm on an upright film."]},{"h":"Appendicitis and diverticular disease","points":["Appendicitis: a fecalith (adults) or lymphoid hyperplasia (children) blocks the lumen; mucus builds, pressure squeezes veins then arteries, and bacteria invade the ischemic wall.","Early pain is visceral (T10 afferents), vague, and periumbilical; it moves to McBurney point once the parietal peritoneum is inflamed.","Untreated appendicitis leads to gangrene, perforation, abscess, and peritonitis.","Diverticula: a low-fiber diet makes small stools and high segmental pressure; mucosa and submucosa herniate where vasa recta pierce the muscle, mostly in the sigmoid.","Diverticulosis bleeds painlessly when a stretched vas rectum ruptures; diverticulitis gives LLQ pain, fever, leukocytosis, and can form an abscess, perforate, or make a colovesical fistula."]},{"h":"Inflammatory bowel disease","points":["Crohn: anywhere mouth to anus, usually terminal ileum; skip lesions, transmural, noncaseating granulomas, cobblestone mucosa.","Ulcerative colitis: rectum first, extends proximally, continuous; mucosa and submucosa only; crypt abscesses, pseudopolyps.","Crohn complications: fistulas, strictures, B12 and bile acid malabsorption, gallstones, calcium oxalate kidney stones.","UC complications: toxic megacolon, primary sclerosing cholangitis. Both: colorectal cancer, erythema nodosum, uveitis, arthritis."]},{"h":"Noninfectious enteritis and anorectal disease","points":["Microscopic colitis: older women, chronic watery diarrhea, normal-looking colonoscopy; only a biopsy shows it.","Collagenous colitis has a thick subepithelial collagen band; lymphocytic colitis has excess intraepithelial lymphocytes. NSAIDs, PPIs, and SSRIs are linked.","Radiation enteritis: acute crypt injury; later, obliterative endarteritis causes fibrosis, stricture, and bleeding.","Anal fissure: posterior midline tear, tearing pain with stools, bright red blood on the paper; an off-midline fissure suggests Crohn disease.","Anorectal abscess starts in an anal gland and can track to skin as a fistula (classic in Crohn). Proctitis: UC, radiation, sexually transmitted infection. Rectal prolapse: concentric rings, older women who strain, children with cystic fibrosis."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Inflammatory GI diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Appendicitis","Fecalith in adults, lymphoid hyperplasia in children","Luminal obstruction, distension, ischemia, bacterial invasion","Periumbilical pain moving to McBurney point, fever, vomiting, leukocytosis","Gangrene, perforation, abscess, peritonitis"],["Barrett esophagus","Chronic GERD, obesity, white men, smoking","Intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells replaces distal squamous epithelium","Salmon-pink mucosa above the GE junction; may have no symptoms beyond reflux","Dysplasia, esophageal adenocarcinoma"],["Diverticular disease","Low-fiber diet, age, obesity","High segmental pressure pushes mucosa through weak spots at vasa recta (false diverticula)","Diverticulosis: painless hematochezia. Diverticulitis: LLQ pain, fever","Abscess, perforation, colovesical fistula, stricture"],["Enteritis (noninfectious)","Radiation, NSAIDs, microscopic colitis (drugs, autoimmune links)","Direct mucosal injury; radiation endarteritis; mucosal collagen or lymphocyte infiltration","Diarrhea; microscopic colitis: chronic watery diarrhea with normal-looking mucosa","Radiation strictures and bleeding; NSAID strictures"],["Esophageal, gastric, duodenal ulcers","H. pylori, NSAIDs, Zollinger-Ellison, smoking","Acid and pepsin overwhelm mucosal defense","Epigastric pain tied to meals","Hemorrhage, perforation with free air, gastric outlet obstruction"],["Esophagitis (noninfectious)","Reflux, atopy (eosinophilic), pills, corrosives","Acid, allergic (Th2) inflammation, or direct chemical injury","Heartburn, odynophagia, dysphagia, food impaction in eosinophilic type","Stricture, Barrett esophagus (reflux)"],["Gastritis","H. pylori, autoimmunity, NSAIDs, alcohol, burns, brain injury","Infection, parietal cell destruction, or loss of prostaglandin protection and ischemia","Dyspepsia, bleeding erosions; B12 deficiency in autoimmune type","Ulcer, adenocarcinoma, MALT lymphoma, pernicious anemia, carcinoid"],["GERD","Obesity, pregnancy, hiatal hernia, smoking, alcohol","Transient LES relaxation, low LES tone","Heartburn, regurgitation, chronic cough, hoarseness","Esophagitis, stricture, Barrett esophagus, adenocarcinoma"],["Inflammatory bowel disease","Genetic susceptibility (NOD2 in Crohn), abnormal immune response to gut flora; smoking raises Crohn risk and lowers UC risk","Crohn: transmural, Th1 and Th17. UC: mucosal","Crohn: RLQ pain, diarrhea, perianal disease. UC: bloody diarrhea, tenesmus","Fistula and stricture (Crohn), toxic megacolon and PSC (UC), colorectal cancer"]]},{"title":"Crohn disease versus ulcerative colitis","cols":["Feature","Crohn disease","Ulcerative colitis"],"rows":[["Location","Mouth to anus, terminal ileum most often, rectum often spared","Rectum always, extends proximally in the colon"],["Distribution","Skip lesions","Continuous"],["Depth","Transmural","Mucosa and submucosa"],["Gross look","Cobblestone mucosa, knife-like linear ulcers, creeping fat, strictures","Pseudopolyps, friable bleeding mucosa, loss of haustra"],["Microscopy","Noncaseating granulomas, lymphoid aggregates","Crypt abscesses, crypt distortion"],["Bowel complications","Fistulas, strictures, perianal abscess","Toxic megacolon, severe bleeding"],["Absorption","B12 and bile acid loss when ileum is diseased; gallstones; calcium oxalate stones","Malabsorption uncommon"],["Liver link","Primary sclerosing cholangitis less common","Primary sclerosing cholangitis (often p-ANCA positive)"],["Shared","Colorectal cancer risk, erythema nodosum, pyoderma gangrenosum, uveitis, arthritis","Same"]]},{"title":"Gastric versus duodenal ulcer","cols":["Feature","Gastric ulcer","Duodenal ulcer"],"rows":[["Usual site","Lesser curvature near the antrum","First part of the duodenum"],["Main mechanism","Weak mucosal defense (H. pylori, NSAIDs)","Too much acid (H. pylori raising gastrin, Zollinger-Ellison)"],["Pain and meals","Worse with eating; weight loss","Better with eating, returns 2 to 3 hours later"],["Malignancy","Can be malignant, so biopsy","Almost never malignant"],["Bleeding vessel","Left gastric artery","Gastroduodenal artery (posterior wall)"],["Perforation","Free air under the diaphragm","Anterior wall ulcer; free air under the diaphragm"]]},{"title":"Chronic gastritis types","cols":["Feature","H. pylori gastritis","Autoimmune gastritis"],"rows":[["Region","Antrum","Body and fundus"],["Cause","Infection with a urease-positive curved gram-negative rod","T cells and antibodies against parietal cells (H+/K+ ATPase) and intrinsic factor"],["Acid","Often raised (antral type)","Low to absent (achlorhydria)"],["Gastrin","Normal or raised","Very high (G-cell hyperplasia)"],["Key risks","Peptic ulcer, intestinal-type adenocarcinoma, MALT lymphoma","Pernicious anemia, adenocarcinoma, gastric carcinoid"]]},{"title":"Esophageal injury compared","cols":["Condition","Cause","Key finding","Typical patient"],"rows":[["Reflux esophagitis","Acid and pepsin reflux","Distal; basal cell hyperplasia, elongated papillae","Obese adult with heartburn"],["Eosinophilic esophagitis","Allergic (Th2) reaction to food antigens","15 or more eosinophils per high-power field; rings and furrows","Young atopic man with food impaction"],["Pill esophagitis","Doxycycline, NSAIDs, potassium chloride, bisphosphonates","Focal ulcer where the pill lodged","Took a pill at bedtime with little water"],["Mallory-Weiss syndrome","Forceful retching","Mucosal tear at the GE junction; hematemesis","Binge drinker or bulimia"],["Boerhaave syndrome","Forceful vomiting","Transmural rupture; pneumomediastinum, subcutaneous emphysema","Severe chest pain after vomiting"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ULCCCERS (ulcerative colitis)","text":"Ulcers, Large intestine, Continuous, Colorectal carcinoma, Crypt abscesses, Extends proximally, Red diarrhea, Sclerosing cholangitis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Meals sort the ulcer","text":"Gastric ulcer pain gets worse when food arrives, so patients eat less and lose weight. Duodenal ulcer pain eases with food because the meal buffers acid, then it returns hours later."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Appendicitis pain moves","text":"Visceral fibers from the appendix enter at T10, so early pain is vague and around the navel. When the inflamed tip touches the parietal peritoneum, which has somatic nerves, pain localizes to McBurney point."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Tear versus rupture","text":"Mallory-Weiss tears the mucosa only, so blood comes up. Boerhaave goes all the way through, so air escapes into the mediastinum and under the skin."}],"highYield":["Barrett esophagus is intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells and is the precursor of esophageal adenocarcinoma.","Autoimmune gastritis in the body and fundus causes achlorhydria, high gastrin, and pernicious anemia.","Posterior duodenal ulcers bleed from the gastroduodenal artery; anterior ones perforate.","Curling ulcer follows burns from ischemia; Cushing ulcer follows brain injury from vagal acid drive.","Colonic diverticula are false diverticula at sites where vasa recta pierce the muscle.","Noncaseating granulomas and transmural inflammation point to Crohn disease.","Primary sclerosing cholangitis and toxic megacolon go with ulcerative colitis.","Microscopic colitis causes chronic watery diarrhea with normal-looking mucosa on colonoscopy."],"pitfalls":["Calcium oxalate stones in Crohn disease come from fat binding calcium in the gut, which leaves oxalate free to be absorbed; they do not mean a calcium excess.","Eosinophils appear in both reflux and eosinophilic esophagitis; the eosinophilic type has 15 or more per high-power field and involves the upper esophagus too.","Diverticulosis bleeding is painless; pain with fever means diverticulitis.","Autoimmune gastritis has high gastrin because acid is absent; a high gastrin with high acid points to Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","A fissure off the posterior midline is atypical and should raise concern for Crohn disease or another cause."],"dumps":[{"t":"Crohn versus UC table","p":"On blank paper, build a two-column table comparing Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis. Rows: location, spread, depth, gross look, microscopy, complications, extraintestinal links.","l":["Crohn: mouth to anus, terminal ileum; UC: rectum extending proximally","Crohn: skip lesions; UC: continuous","Crohn: transmural; UC: mucosa and submucosa","Crohn: cobblestone, creeping fat, strictures; UC: pseudopolyps, loss of haustra","Crohn: noncaseating granulomas; UC: crypt abscesses","Crohn: fistulas, B12 loss, oxalate stones; UC: toxic megacolon","UC more linked to primary sclerosing cholangitis","Both: colorectal cancer, erythema nodosum, uveitis, arthritis"]},{"t":"Ulcer pathways","p":"Draw the stomach and duodenum. Mark where gastric and duodenal ulcers form, trace how H. pylori and NSAIDs each cause an ulcer, and label the artery that bleeds and the sign of perforation.","l":["Gastric ulcer on the lesser curvature; duodenal ulcer in the first part of the duodenum","H. pylori urease and inflammation damage the antral mucosa","H. pylori lowers somatostatin, raising gastrin and acid","NSAIDs block COX and cut prostaglandin protection (mucus, bicarbonate, blood flow)","Left gastric artery bleeds from lesser curvature ulcers","Gastroduodenal artery bleeds from posterior duodenal ulcers","Perforation shows free air under the diaphragm on an upright film"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Oral Cavity and Gastrointestinal Tract"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Gastrointestinal Disease"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Physiology of Gastrointestinal Disorders"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Stomach Disorders | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mFOz0C6sI_I"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD) | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PBZZ_Cu64aQ"}],"related":["GI-13-K1","GI-13-K2","GI-13-K3","GI-13-L1","GI-11"]},"GI-13-H":{"id":"GI-13-H","summary":"Congenital GI disease comes from steps in gut, face, and abdominal wall development that fail or go wrong. Exams test the defect behind each condition, the age it shows up, and the clue that names it, such as bilious versus nonbilious vomiting.","notes":[{"h":"Esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula","points":["Faulty division of the foregut by the tracheoesophageal septum.","Most common type: blind upper esophageal pouch with a fistula from the trachea to the distal esophagus.","Fetus cannot swallow amniotic fluid, so polyhydramnios.","Newborn drools, chokes, coughs, and turns cyanotic with the first feeds; a nasogastric tube coils in the pouch.","Air in the stomach on x-ray means a distal fistula; a gasless abdomen means atresia without a distal fistula.","Look for VACTERL defects (vertebral, anal, cardiac, TE fistula, renal, limb)."]},{"h":"Webs, rings, and Meckel diverticulum","points":["Esophageal web: thin mucosal fold in the upper esophagus; dysphagia to solids.","Plummer-Vinson: web plus iron deficiency anemia and dysphagia; raises squamous cell carcinoma risk.","Schatzki ring: lower esophageal ring at the squamocolumnar junction; intermittent dysphagia with solid food such as meat.","Meckel: persistent vitelline duct forming a true diverticulum (all wall layers) on the antimesenteric ileum.","Ectopic gastric mucosa secretes acid and ulcerates nearby ileum, giving painless rectal bleeding in a young child.","Meckel can also act as a lead point for intussusception, twist into volvulus, or inflame and mimic appendicitis."]},{"h":"Pyloric stenosis","points":["Hypertrophy of pyloric smooth muscle narrows the outlet.","Starts at 2 to 6 weeks: nonbilious projectile vomiting after feeds, hungry infant, olive-shaped epigastric mass.","Loss of gastric HCl gives hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis; volume loss drives aldosterone, so the kidney wastes potassium (hypokalemia).","Risks: first-born males, macrolide exposure (erythromycin, azithromycin) in early infancy."]},{"h":"Biliary atresia","points":["Progressive inflammatory obliteration of the extrahepatic bile ducts.","First weeks of life: persistent conjugated jaundice, acholic (pale) stools, dark urine, hepatomegaly.","Without surgery it progresses to biliary cirrhosis; it is the most common reason for liver transplant in children."]},{"h":"Abdominal wall and rotation","points":["Gastroschisis: full-thickness defect, usually right of the umbilicus; bowel lies free with no covering sac.","Omphalocele: midline; midgut fails to return after physiologic herniation; covered by peritoneum and amnion; linked to trisomy 13 and 18 and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome.","Malrotation: incomplete 270 degree counterclockwise rotation leaves the cecum in the upper right abdomen.","Ladd bands run from the misplaced cecum across the duodenum and obstruct it; the narrow mesentery can twist (midgut volvulus), causing bilious vomiting and bowel ischemia."]},{"h":"Cleft lip and palate","points":["Cleft lip: maxillary prominence fails to fuse with the medial nasal prominence.","Cleft palate: palatine shelves fail to fuse with each other or with the primary palate.","Infant cannot seal and suck well: poor feeding, nasal regurgitation, aspiration; later ear infections and speech problems."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Congenital GI conditions compared","cols":["Condition","Defect","Age and presentation","Key clue"],"rows":[["Esophageal atresia with distal TE fistula","Incomplete division by the tracheoesophageal septum","Newborn; drooling, choking, cyanosis with feeds","Polyhydramnios; tube coils in pouch; air in stomach"],["Esophageal web","Mucosal fold, upper esophagus","Adult (often a woman) with dysphagia to solids","Iron deficiency anemia (Plummer-Vinson)"],["Schatzki ring","Mucosal ring at the squamocolumnar junction","Adult with intermittent solid food dysphagia","Lower esophagus; food bolus impaction"],["Meckel diverticulum","Persistent vitelline duct; true diverticulum","Child under about 2 years with painless rectal bleeding","Ectopic gastric mucosa; technetium-99m pertechnetate scan"],["Pyloric stenosis","Hypertrophy of pyloric smooth muscle","2 to 6 weeks; projectile nonbilious vomiting","Olive mass; hypochloremic hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis"],["Biliary atresia","Obliterated extrahepatic bile ducts","First weeks; jaundice persisting past 2 weeks","Conjugated bilirubin high; acholic stools"],["Gastroschisis","Full-thickness wall defect beside the umbilicus","At birth; exposed bowel","Right of umbilicus; no covering sac"],["Omphalocele","Midgut fails to return to the abdomen","At birth; midline sac with bowel or liver","Covered by peritoneum and amnion; trisomies, Beckwith-Wiedemann"],["Malrotation with Ladd bands","Incomplete midgut rotation","Often first month; bilious vomiting","Cecum in upper right abdomen; volvulus risk"],["Cleft lip","Maxillary and medial nasal prominences do not fuse","At birth; split upper lip","Feeding difficulty"],["Cleft palate","Palatine shelves do not fuse","At birth; opening into nasal cavity","Nasal regurgitation, ear infections"]]},{"title":"Vomiting in the newborn and infant","cols":["Vomit type","Where the block is","Examples"],"rows":[["Nonbilious","Above the ampulla of Vater","Pyloric stenosis, esophageal atresia (feeds come straight back)"],["Bilious","Below the ampulla of Vater","Malrotation with volvulus or Ladd bands, duodenal atresia, intestinal atresia"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Rule of 2s (Meckel diverticulum)","text":"About 2 percent of people, 2 feet from the ileocecal valve, 2 inches long, 2 types of ectopic tissue (gastric and pancreatic), often shows up by age 2, about 2 times more common in males."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"VACTERL","text":"Vertebral defects, Anal atresia, Cardiac defects, Tracheoesophageal fistula, Renal defects, Limb defects (often radial)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Sac or no sac","text":"Omphalocele has a sac and sits in the midline, and it often comes with other defects. Gastroschisis has no sac and sits beside the cord, and the bowel is usually the only problem."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Green means emergency","text":"Bile in a newborn's vomit means the block is past the ampulla. Treat it as malrotation with volvulus until proven otherwise."}],"highYield":["The most common esophageal atresia has a distal tracheoesophageal fistula, so the stomach contains air.","Polyhydramnios points to a fetus that cannot swallow, as in esophageal atresia.","Pyloric stenosis gives hypochloremic hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis.","Meckel diverticulum is a true diverticulum; painless bleeding comes from acid made by ectopic gastric mucosa.","Plummer-Vinson syndrome: esophageal web, iron deficiency anemia, dysphagia, and squamous cell carcinoma risk.","Biliary atresia causes persistent conjugated jaundice with pale stools in the first weeks of life.","Omphalocele is covered by peritoneum and amnion and goes with trisomies and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome."],"pitfalls":["Physiologic newborn jaundice is unconjugated and fades in the first two weeks; jaundice that stays and is conjugated needs a biliary atresia workup.","Pyloric stenosis vomit has no bile because the block is above the ampulla; bilious vomit points to malrotation or atresia below it.","Esophageal web sits high (upper esophagus); Schatzki ring sits low at the squamocolumnar junction.","Cleft lip and cleft palate come from different fusion failures and can occur alone or together."],"dumps":[{"t":"TE fistula sketch","p":"Draw the most common form of esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula. Label the blind pouch, trachea, fistula, and stomach, and write the newborn findings beside it.","l":["Upper esophagus ends in a blind pouch","Fistula connects the trachea to the distal esophagus","Air reaches the stomach through the fistula","Polyhydramnios before birth","Drooling, choking, cyanosis with feeds","Nasogastric tube coils in the pouch","Screen for VACTERL defects"]},{"t":"Pyloric stenosis acid-base","p":"Trace how repeated vomiting in pyloric stenosis leads to low chloride, low potassium, and metabolic alkalosis. Draw arrows from the stomach to the kidney.","l":["Vomiting removes HCl from the stomach","Loss of H+ raises plasma bicarbonate","Loss of Cl- gives hypochloremia","Volume loss activates renin, angiotensin, and aldosterone","Aldosterone makes the kidney reabsorb sodium and secrete potassium and H+","Hypokalemia and paradoxical acidic urine sustain the alkalosis","Infant aged 2 to 6 weeks with an olive mass"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Digestive System"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and Neck"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Oral Cavity and Gastrointestinal Tract, section on congenital anomalies"}],"videos":[],"related":["GI-1","GI-13-F4","GI-13-C1","GI-13-K1"]},"GI-13-I":{"id":"GI-13-I","summary":"The abdominal cavity can fill with fluid (ascites), become infected (peritonitis), or scar after injury (adhesions). Exams test why fluid builds in cirrhosis, how to read the ascitic fluid, and how to tell spontaneous from secondary peritonitis.","notes":[{"h":"Ascites in cirrhosis","points":["Portal hypertension raises hydrostatic pressure in the hepatic sinusoids and splanchnic capillaries.","Low albumin from liver failure lowers plasma oncotic pressure.","Splanchnic vasodilation (nitric oxide) makes arteries feel underfilled, so renin, angiotensin, aldosterone, ADH, and sympathetic tone rise.","The kidney retains sodium and water, and the extra fluid leaks into the peritoneum.","Exam: bulging flanks, shifting dullness, fluid wave."]},{"h":"Reading ascitic fluid","points":["SAAG = serum albumin minus ascitic fluid albumin.","SAAG of 1.1 g/dL or more means portal hypertension: cirrhosis, heart failure, Budd-Chiari syndrome.","SAAG under 1.1 g/dL means another cause: peritoneal carcinomatosis, tuberculous peritonitis, nephrotic syndrome, pancreatitis.","Ascitic neutrophils of 250/mm3 or more mean infection."]},{"h":"Peritonitis","points":["Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP): infected cirrhotic ascites with no surgical source; gut bacteria cross the wall.","SBP organisms: usually one gram-negative enteric rod, E. coli or Klebsiella.","Low ascitic protein (under about 1.5 g/dL) means little complement and opsonin, which raises SBP risk.","SBP can be subtle: fever, mild diffuse tenderness, new confusion, or worsening kidney function.","Secondary peritonitis: perforated ulcer, appendix, or diverticulum; polymicrobial with anaerobes such as Bacteroides fragilis.","Secondary peritonitis gives a rigid, board-like abdomen, rebound, guarding, and free air on imaging."]},{"h":"Adhesions","points":["Surgery, infection, or blood injures the mesothelium, and a fibrin exudate forms.","Injured mesothelium makes less plasminogen activator, so fibrin persists and fibroblasts turn it into fibrous bands.","Main complication: small bowel obstruction with colicky pain, vomiting, distension, and no flatus.","Adhesions are the most common cause of small bowel obstruction in adults; a closed loop can strangulate."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Conditions of the abdominal cavity","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Ascites","Cirrhosis, heart failure, cancer, nephrotic syndrome","Portal hypertension, low albumin, renal sodium retention","Bulging flanks, shifting dullness, fluid wave","Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, hepatorenal syndrome, hernia"],["Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis","Cirrhotic ascites with low protein","Bacterial translocation from the gut","Fever, mild tenderness, confusion; neutrophils 250/mm3 or more","Hepatorenal syndrome, sepsis, death"],["Secondary peritonitis","Perforated viscus, ruptured appendix or diverticulum","Gut contents spill into the peritoneum; polymicrobial","Rigid abdomen, rebound, free air","Abscess, sepsis, adhesions"],["Adhesions","Prior surgery, peritonitis, endometriosis","Persistent fibrin organized into fibrous bands","Often silent until obstruction","Small bowel obstruction, strangulation, female infertility"]]},{"title":"Serum-ascites albumin gradient","cols":["SAAG","Meaning","Causes"],"rows":[["1.1 g/dL or more","Portal hypertension present","Cirrhosis, heart failure, Budd-Chiari syndrome, massive liver metastases"],["Under 1.1 g/dL","Portal pressure normal","Peritoneal carcinomatosis, tuberculous peritonitis, nephrotic syndrome, pancreatitis"]]},{"title":"Spontaneous versus secondary peritonitis","cols":["Feature","Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis","Secondary peritonitis"],"rows":[["Setting","Cirrhosis with ascites","Perforated or ruptured abdominal organ"],["Organisms","One organism, usually E. coli or Klebsiella","Several, including anaerobes"],["Abdomen","Soft or mildly tender","Rigid with rebound and guarding"],["Imaging","No free air","Free air if a viscus perforated"],["Treatment","Antibiotics","Surgery plus antibiotics"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"High gradient, high pressure","text":"A SAAG of 1.1 g/dL or more means fluid was pushed out by high portal pressure. A low SAAG means something on the peritoneum itself, such as tumor or infection, is leaking protein-rich fluid."},{"kind":"trick","label":"One bug or many","text":"One gram-negative rod in ascitic fluid fits spontaneous bacterial peritonitis. Many organisms, including anaerobes, mean a hole in the bowel."}],"highYield":["A SAAG of 1.1 g/dL or more means portal hypertension.","Ascitic neutrophils of 250/mm3 or more diagnose spontaneous bacterial peritonitis.","E. coli and Klebsiella are the usual causes of spontaneous bacterial peritonitis.","Splanchnic vasodilation and RAAS activation make the kidney retain sodium in cirrhosis.","Secondary peritonitis is polymicrobial and causes a rigid abdomen.","Adhesions are the leading cause of small bowel obstruction in adults."],"pitfalls":["Cirrhosis has total body sodium overload even when serum sodium is low; the low serum sodium reflects ADH-driven water retention.","Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis may show no rigidity; new confusion or kidney injury in a cirrhotic patient is enough to tap the ascites.","Heart failure ascites also has a high SAAG; a high ascitic protein helps separate it from cirrhosis."],"dumps":[{"t":"Ascites flowchart","p":"Draw a flowchart showing how cirrhosis leads to ascites. Start at the scarred liver and end at fluid in the peritoneal cavity.","l":["Scarred liver raises resistance to portal flow","Portal hypertension raises sinusoidal hydrostatic pressure","Low albumin lowers plasma oncotic pressure","Nitric oxide causes splanchnic vasodilation","Effective arterial underfilling activates RAAS, ADH, and sympathetic nerves","Kidney retains sodium and water","Fluid leaks into the peritoneum; SAAG 1.1 g/dL or more"]},{"t":"Peritonitis comparison","p":"Make a table comparing spontaneous bacterial peritonitis and secondary peritonitis. Rows: setting, organisms, exam, fluid findings, imaging, treatment.","l":["SBP: cirrhotic ascites; secondary: perforated organ","SBP: one gram-negative rod (E. coli, Klebsiella); secondary: polymicrobial with anaerobes","SBP: mild tenderness, fever, confusion; secondary: rigid abdomen","Both: neutrophils 250/mm3 or more in fluid","Secondary: free air on imaging","SBP: antibiotics; secondary: surgery plus antibiotics"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Liver Disease, section on cirrhosis and ascites"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"section on portal hypertension and ascites"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"section on the peritoneal cavity"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Ascites | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GdGEiVDvCtU"}],"related":["GI-13-C5","GI-13-C2","GI-4","GI-13-F3"]},"GI-13-J":{"id":"GI-13-J","summary":"GI vascular disease covers dilated veins (varices, hemorrhoids), dilated mucosal vessels (angiodysplasia), and lost blood supply (mesenteric ischemia, necrotizing enterocolitis). Exams test the vessel involved, where it bleeds or infarcts, and the setting that predicts it.","notes":[{"h":"Esophageal varices","points":["Portal hypertension opens portosystemic shunts.","Left gastric vein (portal) connects to esophageal veins that drain to the azygos system (systemic).","Distal esophageal submucosal veins dilate and can rupture.","Rupture causes painless, massive hematemesis, a major cause of death in cirrhosis.","Low platelets from splenic sequestration and low clotting factors make bleeding worse."]},{"h":"Hemorrhoids","points":["Dilated, sliding anal vascular cushions.","Internal: above the pectinate line, visceral nerves, so painless bright red bleeding and prolapse.","External: below the pectinate line, somatic nerves (inferior rectal), so painful when thrombosed.","Risks: straining, constipation, low-fiber diet, pregnancy, prolonged sitting."]},{"h":"Mesenteric ischemia and infarction","points":["Acute: embolus from atrial fibrillation or mural thrombus lodges in the superior mesenteric artery (SMA).","Pain out of proportion to the exam, then raised lactate, metabolic acidosis, bloody stool, and peritonitis.","Other acute causes: SMA thrombosis on atherosclerosis, low-flow states (shock), mesenteric vein thrombosis.","Mucosa dies first; full-thickness infarction leads to perforation and sepsis.","Chronic: atherosclerosis of the mesenteric arteries; pain soon after meals, food fear, weight loss.","Ischemic colitis: low flow to watershed zones (splenic flexure, rectosigmoid junction); older adult with crampy pain then bloody diarrhea."]},{"h":"Angiodysplasia","points":["Dilated, tortuous submucosal vessels, mostly in the cecum and right colon of adults over 60.","High wall tension in the wide cecum partly blocks submucosal veins during contraction, so they dilate over years.","Painless, intermittent lower GI bleeding and iron deficiency anemia.","Heyde syndrome: aortic stenosis shear breaks up large von Willebrand factor multimers, so angiodysplasia bleeds more.","Also linked to end-stage kidney disease."]},{"h":"Necrotizing enterocolitis","points":["Premature infant, usually after enteral (often formula) feeds start; breast milk lowers risk.","Immature mucosa, poor blood flow, and bacterial invasion cause mucosal necrosis, often terminal ileum and colon.","Feeding intolerance, abdominal distension, bloody stools.","X-ray: pneumatosis intestinalis (gas in the bowel wall), portal venous gas, free air if perforated."]}],"charts":[{"title":"GI vascular diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Esophageal varices","Portal hypertension, mostly cirrhosis","Left gastric vein backs up into esophageal veins draining to the azygos system","Painless massive hematemesis","Hemorrhagic shock, death, hepatic encephalopathy after bleeding"],["Hemorrhoids","Straining, constipation, low fiber, pregnancy","Anal vascular cushions dilate and slide down","Internal: painless bright red bleeding. External: painful lump when thrombosed","Prolapse, thrombosis, iron deficiency (rare)"],["Acute mesenteric ischemia","Atrial fibrillation, recent MI, atherosclerosis, hypercoagulable state, shock","Embolus or thrombus blocks the SMA","Pain out of proportion to exam, raised lactate","Transmural infarction, perforation, sepsis"],["Chronic mesenteric ischemia","Atherosclerosis of mesenteric arteries","Blood flow cannot rise after meals","Postprandial pain, food fear, weight loss","Acute thrombosis and infarction"],["Ischemic colitis","Older age, hypotension, vascular disease","Low flow to watershed zones","Crampy pain then bloody diarrhea, splenic flexure or rectosigmoid","Stricture, gangrene"],["Angiodysplasia (vascular ectasia)","Age over 60, aortic stenosis, end-stage kidney disease","Wall tension slowly dilates submucosal veins and capillaries","Painless intermittent bleeding from the cecum and right colon","Iron deficiency anemia, recurrent bleeding"],["Necrotizing enterocolitis","Prematurity, formula feeds","Mucosal ischemia plus bacterial invasion","Distension, bloody stools, pneumatosis intestinalis","Perforation, sepsis, short bowel"]]},{"title":"Portosystemic anastomoses","cols":["Site","Portal side","Systemic side","Result in portal hypertension"],"rows":[["Distal esophagus","Left gastric vein","Esophageal veins to the azygos system","Esophageal varices"],["Umbilicus","Paraumbilical veins","Superficial and inferior epigastric veins","Caput medusae"],["Rectum","Superior rectal vein","Middle and inferior rectal veins","Anorectal varices"]]},{"title":"Internal versus external hemorrhoids","cols":["Feature","Internal","External"],"rows":[["Position","Above the pectinate line","Below the pectinate line"],["Covering","Rectal and transitional mucosa","Squamous anoderm and skin"],["Nerve supply","Visceral (autonomic)","Somatic (inferior rectal nerve)"],["Pain","Painless","Painful when thrombosed"],["Main sign","Bright red bleeding, prolapse","Tender bluish perianal lump"],["Venous drainage","Superior rectal vein to the portal system","Inferior rectal vein to the internal iliac (systemic)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Pectinate line sorts pain","text":"Above the line has visceral nerves, so internal hemorrhoids do not hurt. Below the line has somatic nerves, so external hemorrhoids hurt when they clot."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Watershed zones of the colon","text":"The splenic flexure sits between SMA and IMA territory, and the rectosigmoid sits between IMA and internal iliac territory. Low flow injures these edges first."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Heyde: murmur plus bleeding","text":"An older patient with an aortic stenosis murmur and iron deficiency from painless GI bleeding has Heyde syndrome until proven otherwise."}],"highYield":["Esophageal varices form where the left gastric vein meets esophageal veins draining to the azygos system.","Variceal bleeding is painless massive hematemesis in a patient with cirrhosis.","Internal hemorrhoids bleed painlessly; thrombosed external hemorrhoids hurt.","Acute mesenteric ischemia gives pain out of proportion to the exam; atrial fibrillation is the classic embolic source.","Chronic mesenteric ischemia gives postprandial pain and weight loss from food fear.","Angiodysplasia is in the cecum and right colon and links to aortic stenosis (Heyde syndrome).","Pneumatosis intestinalis in a premature infant means necrotizing enterocolitis."],"pitfalls":["Hemorrhoids are not caused by portal hypertension; portal hypertension makes anorectal varices, a different lesion.","Mallory-Weiss tears also cause hematemesis after retching, but the bleed is usually smaller and the patient may have no liver disease.","Early acute mesenteric ischemia has a soft abdomen; waiting for peritoneal signs means the bowel is already dying.","Ischemic colitis affects the colon watershed areas and usually recovers; acute mesenteric ischemia involves the SMA and small bowel and is more lethal."],"dumps":[{"t":"Portal-systemic map","p":"Draw the portal vein and its tributaries. Mark the three main portosystemic anastomoses and label what forms at each one in portal hypertension.","l":["Portal vein forms from the superior mesenteric and splenic veins","Inferior mesenteric vein usually drains to the splenic vein","Left gastric to esophageal veins (azygos): esophageal varices","Paraumbilical to epigastric veins: caput medusae","Superior rectal to middle and inferior rectal veins: anorectal varices","Variceal rupture causes painless massive hematemesis"]},{"t":"Gut ischemia table","p":"Build a table comparing acute mesenteric ischemia, chronic mesenteric ischemia, ischemic colitis, and necrotizing enterocolitis. Columns: patient, cause, key symptom, key test finding.","l":["Acute: older adult with atrial fibrillation; SMA embolus; pain out of proportion; raised lactate","Chronic: atherosclerosis; postprandial pain and food fear; weight loss","Ischemic colitis: low flow to watershed zones; crampy pain then bloody diarrhea","Splenic flexure and rectosigmoid are the watershed zones","Necrotizing enterocolitis: premature infant after feeds; bloody stools","Necrotizing enterocolitis: pneumatosis intestinalis on x-ray"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Oral Cavity and Gastrointestinal Tract, section on vascular disorders of the bowel"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, section on the hepatic portal venous system"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and Perineum, section on the anal canal"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Intestinal Ischemia | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y6k7OEtoSXQ"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"GI Bleed | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zERmfobGF70"}],"related":["GI-5","GI-13-C5","GI-13-G5","GI-13-I1"]},"GI-13-K":{"id":"GI-13-K","summary":"GI neoplasms run from the mouth and salivary glands to the anus, plus the liver, pancreas, and bile ducts. Exams test each tumor's risk factors and precursor lesion, where it grows, its marker, and the sign that shows it has spread.","notes":[{"h":"Esophagus and stomach","points":["Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma: upper and middle third; alcohol, tobacco, achalasia, caustic injury, Plummer-Vinson.","Esophageal adenocarcinoma: distal third; GERD, Barrett esophagus, obesity.","Both cause progressive dysphagia, solids then liquids, with weight loss.","Intestinal-type gastric cancer: H. pylori, nitrosamines in smoked foods, atrophic gastritis; forms glands.","Diffuse-type gastric cancer: signet ring cells, thick rigid stomach (linitis plastica), loss of E-cadherin (CDH1).","Spread: Virchow node (left supraclavicular), Sister Mary Joseph nodule (umbilicus), Krukenberg tumor (ovaries).","MALT lymphoma: extranodal marginal zone B cell lymphoma driven by H. pylori; early lesions can regress when H. pylori is cleared.","GIST: KIT (CD117) mutant tumor of the interstitial cells of Cajal, most often in the stomach."]},{"h":"Neuroendocrine tumors of the gut","points":["Zollinger-Ellison: gastrinoma in the duodenum or pancreas; about a quarter occur in MEN1.","Gastrin drives acid: multiple, refractory, or distal (jejunal) ulcers.","Acid inactivates pancreatic lipase and precipitates bile acids, so diarrhea and steatorrhea.","Secretin normally lowers gastrin; in gastrinoma it paradoxically raises it.","Small bowel carcinoid makes serotonin; the liver breaks it down, so carcinoid syndrome appears mainly after liver metastasis.","Carcinoid syndrome: flushing, diarrhea, wheezing, right-sided valve fibrosis; raised urinary 5-HIAA."]},{"h":"Liver","points":["Hepatocellular carcinoma risks: chronic HBV (even without cirrhosis), HCV, alcohol, hemochromatosis, aflatoxin B1 (TP53 mutation).","Findings: raised alpha-fetoprotein, enlarging liver, weight loss; spreads through blood vessels (portal and hepatic veins).","Metastases are the most common malignancy in the liver and on the peritoneum.","Cavernous hemangioma is the most common benign liver tumor.","Hepatocellular adenoma: oral contraceptive or anabolic steroid use; can rupture and bleed."]},{"h":"Mouth and salivary glands","points":["Oral leukoplakia: white plaque that does not scrape off; may hide dysplasia, so biopsy.","Oral squamous cell carcinoma: tobacco and alcohol. Oropharyngeal carcinoma: HPV-16.","Pleomorphic adenoma: most common salivary tumor, usually parotid, epithelial plus stromal tissue; recurs if incompletely removed.","Warthin tumor: parotid, smokers, cysts with lymphoid stroma. Mucoepidermoid carcinoma: most common salivary malignancy."]},{"h":"Pancreas, bile ducts, and anus","points":["Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma: KRAS mutation; risks smoking and chronic pancreatitis.","Head tumors: painless jaundice with a palpable nontender gallbladder (Courvoisier sign); also migratory thrombophlebitis, weight loss, raised CA 19-9.","Cholangiocarcinoma: primary sclerosing cholangitis, liver flukes such as Clonorchis sinensis; painless obstructive jaundice.","Gallbladder adenocarcinoma: gallstones. Anal squamous cell carcinoma: below the pectinate line, HPV-related."]},{"h":"Colon","points":["Right-sided cancer: bulky mass, occult bleeding, iron deficiency anemia.","Left-sided cancer: napkin-ring narrowing, change in bowel habit, obstruction, visible blood.","Risks: age, IBD, familial adenomatous polyposis (APC), Lynch syndrome (mismatch repair genes).","CEA tracks recurrence after surgery; it is not a screening test.","Adenoma malignancy risk rises with size and villous component."]}],"charts":[{"title":"GI neoplasms by site","cols":["Site","Main tumors","Key risk factors","Classic findings","Marker or spread"],"rows":[["Esophageal","Squamous cell carcinoma, adenocarcinoma","SCC: alcohol, tobacco, achalasia, caustics. Adeno: GERD, Barrett, obesity","Progressive dysphagia, weight loss","Local invasion; lymph nodes"],["Gastric","Adenocarcinoma, MALT lymphoma, GIST","H. pylori, nitrosamines, atrophic gastritis, CDH1 loss, KIT mutation","Early satiety, weight loss, epigastric mass","Virchow node, Sister Mary Joseph nodule, Krukenberg tumor"],["Intestinal (gastrinoma, carcinoid)","Gastrinoma, carcinoid (neuroendocrine) tumor","MEN1 for gastrinoma","Refractory ulcers and diarrhea; flushing, wheeze, diarrhea","Paradoxical gastrin rise with secretin; urinary 5-HIAA"],["Liver","Metastases, hepatocellular carcinoma, hemangioma, adenoma","HBV, HCV, alcohol, hemochromatosis, aflatoxin; oral contraceptives for adenoma","Hepatomegaly, weight loss, decompensated cirrhosis","Alpha-fetoprotein; hematogenous spread"],["Oral (leukoplakia)","Squamous cell carcinoma arising from leukoplakia","Tobacco, alcohol; HPV-16 for oropharynx","White plaque that does not scrape off; nonhealing ulcer","Cervical lymph nodes"],["Pancreas","Ductal adenocarcinoma","Smoking, chronic pancreatitis, KRAS mutation","Painless jaundice, Courvoisier sign, back pain, weight loss","CA 19-9; migratory thrombophlebitis"],["Colorectal","Adenocarcinoma from adenomas or serrated polyps","Age, IBD, FAP, Lynch syndrome","Right: anemia. Left: obstruction, change in bowel habit","CEA for recurrence; liver metastases"]]},{"title":"Esophageal cancer types","cols":["Feature","Squamous cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma"],"rows":[["Location","Upper and middle third","Distal third"],["Precursor","Squamous dysplasia","Barrett esophagus"],["Risks","Alcohol, tobacco, hot liquids, achalasia, caustic strictures, Plummer-Vinson","Chronic GERD, obesity, tobacco"],["Where most common","Worldwide","United States and other Western countries"]]},{"title":"Gastric tumors","cols":["Tumor","Cell or origin","Driver","Key feature"],"rows":[["Intestinal-type adenocarcinoma","Gastric epithelium forming glands","H. pylori, nitrosamines, atrophic gastritis with intestinal metaplasia","Ulcerated mass, often lesser curvature"],["Diffuse-type adenocarcinoma","Signet ring cells infiltrating the wall","Loss of E-cadherin (CDH1)","Linitis plastica (leather-bottle stomach)"],["MALT lymphoma","Marginal zone B cells","Chronic H. pylori antigen stimulation","May regress after H. pylori is cleared"],["GIST","Interstitial cells of Cajal","Activating KIT (CD117) mutation","Submucosal mass, stomach most often"]]},{"title":"Colonic polyps","cols":["Polyp","Features","Malignant potential"],"rows":[["Hyperplastic","Most common non-neoplastic polyp; small, rectosigmoid; serrated crypts","Very low"],["Tubular adenoma","Most common adenoma; tubular glands","Present; lowest of the adenomas"],["Tubulovillous adenoma","Mixed tubular and villous parts","Intermediate"],["Villous adenoma","Finger-like villi; can secrete mucus and potassium","Highest of the adenomas; rises with size"],["Sessile serrated lesion","Flat, right colon; BRAF mutation and CpG island methylation","Yes, through the serrated pathway"],["Juvenile polyp","Hamartoma; child, rectum, bleeding","Solitary: very low; juvenile polyposis syndrome: raised"],["Peutz-Jeghers polyp","Hamartoma; STK11; lip and mouth hyperpigmentation; small bowel intussusception","Raised risk of GI and other cancers"]]},{"title":"Liver masses","cols":["Lesion","Who gets it","Key features"],"rows":[["Metastases","Patients with colon, breast, lung, or pancreatic cancer","Most common liver malignancy; multiple nodules"],["Hepatocellular carcinoma","Chronic HBV or HCV, cirrhosis, aflatoxin exposure","Raised alpha-fetoprotein; invades veins"],["Cavernous hemangioma","Any adult","Most common benign tumor; avoid needle biopsy (bleeding)"],["Hepatocellular adenoma","Young women on oral contraceptives; anabolic steroid users","Can rupture and bleed, especially in pregnancy"]]},{"title":"White lesions of the mouth","cols":["Lesion","Cause","Scrapes off?","Clue"],"rows":[["Leukoplakia","Tobacco, alcohol; may hide dysplasia","No","Biopsy to exclude dysplasia or carcinoma"],["Oral candidiasis","Candida albicans","Yes, leaving a red base","Infants, antibiotics, inhaled steroids, HIV"],["Hairy leukoplakia","Epstein-Barr virus","No","Lateral tongue, immunosuppression, not premalignant"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Squamous up top, adeno at the bottom","text":"Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma sits high where the lining is squamous and the irritants pass. Adenocarcinoma sits low where acid makes Barrett glandular lining."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Why carcinoid needs the liver","text":"Gut serotonin goes to the liver through the portal vein and is broken down there. Once tumor is in the liver, serotonin reaches the systemic circulation, and the lungs clear it before the left heart, so the right-sided valves scar."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Right bleeds, left blocks","text":"The right colon is wide with liquid stool, so tumors grow large and bleed slowly. The left colon is narrow with formed stool, so tumors obstruct."}],"highYield":["Barrett esophagus leads to distal esophageal adenocarcinoma; alcohol and tobacco lead to upper and middle squamous cell carcinoma.","Signet ring cells and linitis plastica mean diffuse gastric cancer with CDH1 loss.","Gastric MALT lymphoma is driven by H. pylori; GIST carries a KIT (CD117) mutation.","Gastrinoma raises gastrin after secretin and links to MEN1.","Carcinoid syndrome needs liver metastasis and raises urinary 5-HIAA.","Aflatoxin B1 causes TP53 mutation and hepatocellular carcinoma; HBV can cause it without cirrhosis.","Courvoisier sign: painless jaundice with a palpable gallbladder points to a tumor blocking the distal bile duct, classically a pancreatic head cancer.","Villous adenomas carry the highest malignant risk of colon adenomas."],"pitfalls":["Metastases outnumber primary tumors in the liver; a liver mass in a patient with colon cancer is a metastasis until proven otherwise.","Leukoplakia and candidiasis both look white; candidiasis scrapes off and leukoplakia does not.","CEA helps follow colon cancer after surgery and should not be used to screen.","Hyperplastic polyps are usually harmless; sessile serrated lesions look similar but are premalignant.","Warthin tumor is benign and occurs in smokers; mucoepidermoid carcinoma is the most common salivary malignancy."],"dumps":[{"t":"GI tumor map","p":"Draw the GI tract from mouth to anus with the liver and pancreas. At each site, write the main tumor, its biggest risk factor, and one sign or marker.","l":["Mouth: squamous cell carcinoma, tobacco and alcohol; leukoplakia precursor","Upper esophagus: squamous cell carcinoma; distal esophagus: adenocarcinoma from Barrett","Stomach: adenocarcinoma with H. pylori; Virchow node, Krukenberg tumor","Small bowel: carcinoid with 5-HIAA after liver metastasis","Liver: hepatocellular carcinoma with HBV or HCV; alpha-fetoprotein","Pancreatic head: adenocarcinoma with KRAS; Courvoisier sign, CA 19-9","Colon: adenocarcinoma from adenomas; CEA for recurrence","Anus below the pectinate line: squamous cell carcinoma with HPV"]},{"t":"Polyp to cancer","p":"Draw a colonic polyp progressing to invasive cancer. Label each step of the adenoma-carcinoma sequence and list which polyps carry the most risk.","l":["Loss of APC starts the adenoma","KRAS mutation helps the adenoma grow","TP53 loss allows invasive carcinoma","Risk rises with size and villous component","Villous adenoma has the highest adenoma risk; tubular the lowest","Sessile serrated lesions follow a separate pathway with BRAF and methylation","Lynch syndrome tumors have mismatch repair loss and microsatellite instability"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Oral Cavity and Gastrointestinal Tract"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"section on hepatic neoplasms"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Pancreas"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Neoplasia"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Esophageal and Gastric Cancer | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ahvwhYMk3Ng"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Colorectal Cancer | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HjIQy8FycKA"}],"related":["GI-10","GI-13-G2","GI-13-G7","GI-13-L7","GI-13-C2"]},"GI-13-L":{"id":"GI-13-L","summary":"Infectious GI diseases include diarrheal illness, infections of the mouth and esophagus, viral hepatitis, and liver abscess. Exams link each organism to its setting, the way it causes damage, and the complication it is known for.","notes":[{"h":"Clostridioides difficile colitis","points":["Antibiotics (clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, cephalosporins) wipe out normal colonic flora.","Spore-forming gram-positive anaerobe; spores survive alcohol hand rubs, so wash with soap and water.","Toxin A (enterotoxin) draws fluid and neutrophils; toxin B (cytotoxin) breaks down the actin cytoskeleton.","Yellow-white pseudomembranes of fibrin, mucus, and neutrophils; watery diarrhea, cramps, high white count.","Diagnosis: stool toxin or toxin gene (PCR) testing. Severe cases cause toxic megacolon."]},{"h":"Bloody and watery diarrhea","points":["Bloody (invasive or cytotoxic): Shigella, Campylobacter, Salmonella, EHEC, Entamoeba histolytica.","EHEC O157:H7 (undercooked beef): Shiga toxin damages renal endothelium, causing hemolytic uremic syndrome.","Campylobacter (poultry) can trigger Guillain-Barre syndrome by molecular mimicry.","Shigella, Campylobacter, Salmonella, and Yersinia can trigger HLA-B27 reactive arthritis.","Watery: rotavirus (unvaccinated infants), norovirus (cruise ships, institutions), ETEC (traveler's diarrhea).","Cholera toxin keeps Gs on, cAMP rises, and CFTR pumps chloride and water out: rice-water stool.","Bacillus cereus emetic toxin is preformed in reheated rice and causes vomiting within hours."]},{"h":"Mouth and esophagus","points":["Plaque biofilm at the gum line causes gingivitis: red, swollen gums that bleed; reversible.","Periodontitis: Porphyromonas gingivalis and host inflammation destroy periodontal ligament and alveolar bone.","Streptococcus mutans ferments sugar to acid, dissolving enamel (caries) and leading to tooth abscess.","Oral thrush: Candida albicans; white plaques that scrape off; infants, inhaled steroids, antibiotics, diabetes, HIV.","Herpetic gingivostomatitis (primary HSV-1): fever with vesicles on gums and lips. Herpangina (coxsackie A): vesicles on the soft palate and posterior pharynx.","Hand-foot-and-mouth disease: coxsackie A16, mouth ulcers plus palm and sole vesicles. Aphthous ulcers: Crohn disease, Behcet disease.","Infectious esophagitis in immunosuppression: Candida (white plaques), HSV-1 (punched-out ulcers), CMV (linear ulcers)."]},{"h":"Viral hepatitis and liver abscess","points":["HBV chronic: most infected neonates, few infected adults. HCV chronic: most infected adults.","HBV and HCV lead to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma; HBV can cause carcinoma without cirrhosis.","HCV causes mixed cryoglobulinemia (vasculitis, glomerulonephritis, purpura).","Pyogenic liver abscess: biliary or portal spread, often E. coli or Klebsiella.","Amebic liver abscess: Entamoeba histolytica, travel history, anchovy paste fluid."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Infectious GI diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Main organisms","Setting or risk","Key mechanism or finding","Complications"],"rows":[["Enterocolitis","C. difficile, Shigella, Campylobacter, Salmonella, EHEC, Entamoeba","Antibiotics, contaminated food or water","Toxins or invasion damage the colonic mucosa; bloody or watery diarrhea","Toxic megacolon, HUS, Guillain-Barre, reactive arthritis"],["Esophagitis","Candida, HSV-1, CMV","HIV, transplant, chemotherapy, steroids","White plaques, punched-out ulcers, or linear ulcers","Odynophagia, poor intake, bleeding"],["Gastroenteritis","Rotavirus, norovirus, ETEC, Vibrio cholerae, Bacillus cereus","Infants, outbreaks, travel, bad water, reheated rice","Watery diarrhea from toxins or villous injury","Dehydration, electrolyte loss"],["Gingivitis and periodontitis","Plaque bacteria, Porphyromonas gingivalis; Streptococcus mutans for caries","Poor oral hygiene, smoking, diabetes","Biofilm inflammation; bone and ligament loss","Tooth loss, tooth abscess"],["Oral thrush","Candida albicans","Infants, inhaled steroids, antibiotics, diabetes, HIV","Budding yeast and pseudohyphae; plaques scrape off","Esophageal spread in immunosuppression"],["Stomatitis","HSV-1, coxsackie A, coxsackie A16; noninfectious aphthae","Young children; Crohn or Behcet disease for aphthae","Vesicles and painful ulcers","Dehydration from painful swallowing; HSV latency"],["Viral hepatitis","HAV, HBV, HCV, HDV, HEV","Fecal-oral (A, E); blood, sex, birth (B, C, D)","Immune attack on infected hepatocytes","Chronic hepatitis, cirrhosis, carcinoma, cryoglobulinemia"]]},{"title":"Hepatitis B serology","cols":["Stage","HBsAg","Anti-HBs","Anti-HBc"],"rows":[["Acute infection","Positive","Negative","IgM"],["Window period","Negative","Negative","IgM"],["Chronic infection","Positive for over 6 months","Negative","IgG"],["Resolved infection","Negative","Positive","IgG"],["Vaccinated","Negative","Positive","Negative"]]},{"title":"Bloody diarrhea organisms","cols":["Organism","Source","Key feature","Known complication"],"rows":[["Shigella","Person to person, very low infectious dose","Invades M cells, spreads cell to cell","Reactive arthritis, seizures in children"],["Campylobacter jejuni","Undercooked poultry, raw milk","Curved gram-negative rod","Guillain-Barre syndrome, reactive arthritis"],["Nontyphoidal Salmonella","Poultry, eggs, reptiles","Invades mucosa; flagellated","Bacteremia, osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease"],["EHEC O157:H7","Undercooked ground beef, produce","Shiga toxin; usually little fever","Hemolytic uremic syndrome"],["Entamoeba histolytica","Cysts in contaminated water","Flask-shaped ulcers; trophozoites with ingested red cells","Amebic liver abscess"]]},{"title":"Watery gastroenteritis by setting","cols":["Organism","Setting","Mechanism or clue"],"rows":[["Rotavirus","Unvaccinated infants and young children","Destroys villus tip cells; winter outbreaks"],["Norovirus","Cruise ships, nursing homes, schools","Very low infectious dose; vomiting prominent"],["ETEC","Traveler's diarrhea","Heat-labile toxin raises cAMP; heat-stable toxin raises cGMP"],["Vibrio cholerae","Contaminated water","Toxin keeps Gs active; rice-water stool"],["Bacillus cereus","Reheated fried rice","Preformed emetic toxin; vomiting within 1 to 6 hours"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Read HBV serology in order","text":"First ask about HBsAg (virus present now). Then anti-HBc (ever infected; IgM recent, IgG old). Then anti-HBs (immune). Anti-HBs alone means vaccine; IgM anti-HBc alone means window period."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Scrape test","text":"White plaque that wipes off is thrush. White plaque that stays is leukoplakia or hairy leukoplakia."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Which way the chronic odds run","text":"HBV becomes chronic in most newborns and few adults because the newborn immune system tolerates the virus. HCV becomes chronic in most adults because its envelope proteins keep changing and escape antibodies."}],"highYield":["Clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, and cephalosporins predispose to C. difficile colitis; diagnose by stool toxin or toxin gene testing.","EHEC O157:H7 leads to hemolytic uremic syndrome; Campylobacter leads to Guillain-Barre syndrome.","Rice-water stool from contaminated water means Vibrio cholerae.","IgM anti-HBc alone means the hepatitis B window period; anti-HBs alone means vaccination.","HCV becomes chronic in most adults and causes mixed cryoglobulinemia.","CMV esophagitis has linear ulcers and inclusions in endothelial and stromal cells; HSV has punched-out ulcers with intranuclear inclusions in squamous cells.","Herpangina (coxsackie A) affects the posterior mouth; primary HSV-1 gingivostomatitis affects the gums and lips.","Amebic liver abscess fluid looks like anchovy paste."],"pitfalls":["Alcohol hand gel does not kill C. difficile spores; soap and water does.","Anti-HBs alone means vaccine immunity; anti-HBs with IgG anti-HBc means past infection that cleared.","Gingivitis is reversible; periodontitis means bone and ligament are already lost.","Aphthous ulcers are not preceded by vesicles; vesicles that break into ulcers point to HSV or coxsackie virus.","A single gram-negative rod in a liver abscess with biliary disease suggests pyogenic abscess; a young traveler with a single right-lobe abscess suggests amebic abscess."],"dumps":[{"t":"HBV serology timeline","p":"Draw a timeline of acute hepatitis B that resolves. Plot HBsAg, IgM anti-HBc, IgG anti-HBc, and anti-HBs, and mark the window period.","l":["HBsAg rises first and falls as the infection clears","IgM anti-HBc appears during acute illness","Window period: HBsAg gone, anti-HBs not yet present, IgM anti-HBc positive","Anti-HBs appears after HBsAg is gone and gives immunity","IgG anti-HBc stays for life after infection","Chronic infection: HBsAg persists over 6 months with IgG anti-HBc","Vaccination gives anti-HBs only"]},{"t":"Diarrhea organism table","p":"Make a table of diarrhea organisms with columns for organism, source, bloody or watery, mechanism, and known complication. Include C. difficile, Shigella, Campylobacter, EHEC, cholera, ETEC, rotavirus, and norovirus.","l":["C. difficile: antibiotics; watery; toxins A and B; toxic megacolon","Shigella: low infectious dose; bloody; invasion; reactive arthritis","Campylobacter: poultry; bloody; invasion; Guillain-Barre syndrome","EHEC: undercooked beef; bloody; Shiga toxin; hemolytic uremic syndrome","Cholera: contaminated water; watery; cAMP rise; severe dehydration","ETEC: travelers; watery; heat-labile and heat-stable toxins","Rotavirus: infants; watery; villus injury","Norovirus: outbreaks; watery with vomiting"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Hepatitis Viruses"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Enteric Gram-Negative Rods (Enterobacteriaceae)"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Oral Cavity and Gastrointestinal Tract, section on infectious enterocolitis"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Diarrhea | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7ylgYbLCEA0"}],"related":["GI-12","GI-11","GI-13-K4","GI-13-G9","GI-13-C2"]},"MS-1":{"id":"MS-1","summary":"How somites, lateral plate mesoderm, and the pharyngeal arches build the bones, muscles, and joints, and how limb buds grow, rotate, and ossify. Exam questions link an embryonic step to its adult nerve supply, curve, or birth defect.","notes":[{"h":"Somites","points":["Paraxial mesoderm segments into somites from about day 20 (week 3 to 5).","Sclerotome (ventromedial) surrounds the notochord and neural tube to form vertebrae and ribs.","Dermomyotome splits into dermatome (dermis of the back) and myotome (skeletal muscle).","Epaxial myotome forms the deep back muscles (erector spinae), supplied by dorsal rami.","Hypaxial myotome forms limb and body wall muscles, supplied by ventral rami.","Each segment keeps its own spinal nerve, which is the basis of dermatomes and myotomes."]},{"h":"Vertebrae and discs","points":["Resegmentation: each vertebral body forms from the caudal half of one sclerotome fused to the cranial half of the next.","Because of this shift, segmental arteries end up running across the middle of each vertebral body.","The spinal nerve exits at the level of the disc, and each myotome bridges two vertebrae so muscles can move the spine.","Notochord persists between bodies as the nucleus pulposus; the annulus fibrosus forms from sclerotome cells.","A notochord remnant can give rise to a chordoma (often sacral or clival)."]},{"h":"Limb buds and patterning","points":["Limb buds appear in week 4 (upper about day 24, lower about day 28): lateral plate mesoderm core covered by ectoderm.","Limb muscle comes from hypaxial myotome cells that migrate into the bud; limb bone, cartilage, and tendon come from lateral plate mesoderm.","Apical ectodermal ridge (AER) secretes FGFs that drive proximal to distal outgrowth; loss of AER gives limb truncation.","Zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) at the posterior bud secretes sonic hedgehog for anterior to posterior (thumb to little finger) pattern.","Extra Shh activity on the anterior side gives mirror digits and preaxial polydactyly.","HOX genes set the identity of each segment; HOXD13 mutation causes synpolydactyly.","Thalidomide during limb bud formation (about days 24 to 36 after fertilization) causes phocomelia, with hands or feet attached close to the trunk."]},{"h":"Limb rotation","points":["In week 7 to 8 the upper limb rotates 90 degrees laterally and the lower limb rotates about 90 degrees medially.","Upper limb: extensors end up posterior and the thumb lateral.","Lower limb: extensors end up anterior, flexors posterior, and the big toe medial.","Medial rotation twists lower limb dermatomes into a spiral (L4 medial leg, L5 dorsum of foot, S1 lateral foot)."]},{"h":"Bone and joint formation","points":["Intramembranous: mesenchyme turns straight into bone (skull vault, facial bones, most of the clavicle).","Endochondral: a hyaline cartilage model is replaced by bone (long bones, vertebrae, skull base).","Primary ossification centers appear in the diaphysis of most long bones by about week 12; secondary centers form in the epiphyses, mostly after birth.","Clavicle is the first bone to start ossifying (about week 5 to 6).","Joints form from interzone mesenchyme between bone models; synovial joints form when the interzone cavitates into a joint cavity."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Somite parts and their fates","cols":["Part","Adult derivative","Nerve supply"],"rows":[["Sclerotome","Vertebrae, ribs, annulus fibrosus","Not applicable"],["Dermatome","Dermis of the back","Segmental cutaneous branches"],["Epaxial myotome","Deep (intrinsic) back muscles","Dorsal rami"],["Hypaxial myotome","Limb, intercostal, and abdominal wall muscles","Ventral rami"],["Notochord (not a somite)","Nucleus pulposus","Not applicable"]]},{"title":"Limb patterning signals","cols":["Signal center","Molecule","Axis or job","Defect when disrupted"],"rows":[["Apical ectodermal ridge","FGFs (FGF8)","Proximal to distal outgrowth","Truncated limb (amelia or meromelia)"],["Zone of polarizing activity","Sonic hedgehog","Anterior to posterior (digit identity)","Ectopic anterior Shh gives polydactyly"],["Dorsal ectoderm","WNT7","Dorsal to ventral","Ventralized limb (dorsal side takes on ventral features)"],["Mesenchyme","HOX genes","Segment identity","Synpolydactyly with HOXD13 mutation"]]},{"title":"Pharyngeal arch muscles and nerves","cols":["Arch","Muscles","Nerve"],"rows":[["1st","Masseter, temporalis, medial and lateral pterygoids, mylohyoid, anterior belly of digastric, tensor tympani, tensor veli palatini","CN V3"],["2nd","Muscles of facial expression, stapedius, stylohyoid, posterior belly of digastric","CN VII"],["3rd","Stylopharyngeus","CN IX"],["4th","Most pharyngeal constrictors, cricothyroid, levator veli palatini","CN X (pharyngeal branches; superior laryngeal nerve to cricothyroid)"],["6th","Intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid","CN X (recurrent laryngeal)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Epaxial goes to the back door","text":"Epaxial muscles sit behind the vertebral axis and are reached by the dorsal ramus. Everything in front (limbs, body wall) is hypaxial and comes off the ventral ramus."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Count the arches by nerve","text":"Arches 1, 2, 3 go with cranial nerves 5, 7, 9 (skip one each time). Arches 4 and 6 both use CN X: superior laryngeal for 4, recurrent laryngeal for 6."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Thumbs out, big toes in","text":"Upper limb turns out (lateral) and lower limb turns in (medial). That is why the thumb is lateral, the big toe is medial, and the knee faces forward while the elbow faces back."}],"highYield":["Erector spinae are epaxial and supplied by dorsal rami; limb muscles are hypaxial and supplied by ventral rami.","Nucleus pulposus is the notochord remnant; chordoma arises from notochord rests.","AER makes FGF for length; ZPA makes Shh for thumb to pinky order.","Ectopic anterior Shh expression causes polydactyly.","Thalidomide exposure during limb bud formation causes phocomelia.","Skull vault and most of the clavicle form by intramembranous ossification; long bones form by endochondral ossification.","First arch muscles chew (CN V3); second arch muscles make facial expression (CN VII).","Medial rotation of the lower limb puts the knee extensors in front and twists the dermatomes into a spiral."],"pitfalls":["Dermatome in a somite means the embryonic block that forms dermis; in the clinic it means a skin area supplied by one spinal nerve.","Limb muscles come from somites (myotome), while limb bones and tendons come from lateral plate mesoderm.","The vertebral body is built from two neighboring sclerotome halves, so it does not match a single somite.","Stylopharyngeus is the only third arch muscle (CN IX) and is not a constrictor; the pharyngeal constrictors are fourth arch muscles (CN X)."],"dumps":[{"t":"Somite to adult","p":"Draw a cross section of a week 4 embryo showing the neural tube, notochord, and one somite. Label sclerotome, dermatome, and myotome, then draw arrows to what each becomes and which ramus supplies the muscles.","l":["Somite comes from paraxial mesoderm","Sclerotome moves around notochord and neural tube to form vertebrae and ribs","Dermatome forms dermis of the back","Epaxial myotome forms deep back muscles supplied by dorsal rami","Hypaxial myotome forms limb and body wall muscles supplied by ventral rami","Notochord becomes the nucleus pulposus","Vertebral body forms from caudal half of one sclerotome plus cranial half of the next"]},{"t":"Limb bud signals","p":"Draw a limb bud and label the AER, the ZPA, and the dorsal ectoderm. Beside each, write its signal molecule, the axis it controls, and the defect you see when it goes wrong.","l":["AER at the distal tip secretes FGF","AER drives proximal to distal growth; loss gives truncation","ZPA at the posterior margin secretes sonic hedgehog","Shh sets anterior to posterior digit order; ectopic anterior Shh gives polydactyly","Dorsal ectoderm WNT7 sets dorsal to ventral pattern","HOX genes set segment identity","Thalidomide during limb bud formation causes phocomelia","Upper limb rotates laterally, lower limb medially in weeks 7 to 8"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Axial Skeleton"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Muscular System"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Limbs"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and Neck (section on pharyngeal arches)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Embryology | Development of Muscular System","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tuhv_JrmaXg"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Embryology | Development of Skeletal System","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WmlbqVyhMts"}],"related":["MS-2","MS-6","MS-9","MS-3"]},"MS-2":{"id":"MS-2","summary":"The microscopic structure of the three muscle types, compact and spongy bone, the bone cells, the growth plate, and the three joint classes. Exam questions ask you to predict what changes in a sarcomere, a growth plate, or a joint when something goes wrong.","notes":[{"h":"Sarcomere bands","points":["Sarcomere runs from one Z line to the next; thin (actin) filaments anchor at the Z line.","A band is the full length of the thick (myosin) filaments; it stays the same length during contraction.","I band holds thin filaments only and shortens.","H zone holds thick filaments only and shortens; the M line sits in its center and links thick filaments.","Z lines move closer together because filaments slide past each other; no filament changes length."]},{"h":"Three muscle types","points":["Skeletal: long multinucleate fibers with peripheral nuclei, striated, triads (T tubule plus two terminal cisternae) at the A to I junction.","Skeletal regeneration comes from satellite cells.","Cardiac: branched cells with one or two central nuclei, striated, dyads at the Z line.","Intercalated discs join cardiac cells: gap junctions for electrical spread, desmosomes and fascia adherens for mechanical strength.","Smooth: fusiform cells with one central nucleus, no striations, dense bodies anchor actin (Z line equivalent).","Smooth muscle uses caveolae instead of a T tubule system and can divide (hyperplasia)."]},{"h":"Bone structure","points":["Compact bone is built of osteons: concentric lamellae around a central (Haversian) canal with vessels and nerves.","Volkmann canals run across the bone and connect Haversian canals to each other and to the periosteum.","Osteocytes sit in lacunae and touch each other through canaliculi joined by gap junctions.","Spongy (cancellous) bone is a lattice of trabeculae with marrow in the spaces; no osteons.","Woven bone has random collagen and is weak (fetal bone, fracture callus, Paget disease); lamellar bone has organized layers and replaces it."]},{"h":"Bone cells","points":["Osteoblasts come from mesenchymal stem cells (RUNX2) and secrete type I collagen osteoid and alkaline phosphatase.","Osteoblasts express RANKL and OPG, so they control osteoclast activity; PTH receptors are on osteoblasts.","Osteocytes are osteoblasts trapped in matrix; they sense load and secrete FGF23 (phosphate loss in urine) and sclerostin.","Osteoclasts come from the monocyte and macrophage line, fuse into multinucleated cells, and need M-CSF and RANKL.","Osteoclast ruffled border pumps acid (H+ made by carbonic anhydrase II) and releases cathepsin K into Howship lacunae."]},{"h":"Growth plate and joints","points":["Zones from epiphysis to diaphysis: reserve, proliferative, hypertrophic, calcification, ossification.","Achondroplasia: activating FGFR3 mutation slows chondrocyte proliferation, giving a thin proliferative zone and short long bones.","Rickets: poor mineralization lets the hypertrophic zone pile up, giving a wide, irregular plate with frayed, cupped metaphyses.","Fibrous joints: sutures, syndesmoses (distal tibiofibular), gomphoses (tooth in socket).","Cartilaginous joints: synchondroses use hyaline cartilage (growth plate, first sternocostal joint); symphyses use fibrocartilage (pubic symphysis, intervertebral disc).","Synovial joints: avascular articular hyaline cartilage with no perichondrium; synoviocytes make hyaluronan and lubricin for synovial fluid."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle","cols":["Feature","Skeletal","Cardiac","Smooth"],"rows":[["Cell shape","Long cylinder","Branched","Fusiform (spindle)"],["Nuclei","Many, peripheral","One or two, central","One, central"],["Striations","Yes","Yes","No"],["Cell junctions","None between fibers","Intercalated discs (gap junctions, desmosomes)","Gap junctions in single unit muscle"],["T tubule pattern","Triads at A to I junction","Dyads at Z line","No T tubules (caveolae)"],["Actin anchor","Z line","Z line","Dense bodies"],["Repair","Satellite cells","Very limited (scar)","Cell division"]]},{"title":"Growth plate zones","cols":["Zone (epiphysis to diaphysis)","What happens","Disease effect"],"rows":[["Reserve (resting)","Small scattered chondrocytes","Little change"],["Proliferative","Chondrocytes divide in stacked columns","Thin in achondroplasia (FGFR3 activation)"],["Hypertrophic","Chondrocytes enlarge and release VEGF","Expanded and disorganized in rickets"],["Calcification","Cartilage matrix mineralizes, chondrocytes die","Fails in rickets"],["Ossification","Osteoblasts lay bone on calcified cartilage","Fails in rickets, leaving excess osteoid"]]},{"title":"Joint classes","cols":["Class","Subtype","Tissue","Example"],"rows":[["Fibrous","Suture","Dense connective tissue","Sagittal suture"],["Fibrous","Syndesmosis","Ligament or interosseous membrane","Distal tibiofibular joint"],["Fibrous","Gomphosis","Periodontal ligament","Tooth in its socket"],["Cartilaginous","Synchondrosis (primary)","Hyaline cartilage","Epiphyseal plate, first sternocostal joint"],["Cartilaginous","Symphysis (secondary)","Fibrocartilage","Pubic symphysis, intervertebral disc"],["Synovial","Diarthrosis","Articular hyaline cartilage, capsule, synovium","Knee, shoulder"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"HIZ shrinkage","text":"During contraction the H zone, the I band, and the Z to Z distance (sarcomere) all shorten. The A band is the one that stays the same."},{"kind":"trick","label":"A is for Always","text":"The A band is set by thick filament length, and myosin never changes length, so the A band always stays constant."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Blasts build, clasts cut","text":"Osteoblasts build bone and come from mesenchyme. Osteoclasts cut bone and come from blood (monocytes), which is why they are multinucleated like other fused macrophages."}],"highYield":["I band and H zone shorten during contraction; the A band stays constant.","Intercalated discs with gap junctions let cardiac muscle contract as a functional syncytium.","Osteoclasts are monocyte lineage cells that use cathepsin K and acid in Howship lacunae.","Osteocytes secrete FGF23, which increases urinary phosphate loss.","Achondroplasia (FGFR3 gain of function) shortens the proliferative zone, so endochondral bones are short while intramembranous bones grow normally.","Rickets widens the growth plate because hypertrophic cartilage is not mineralized and removed.","Articular cartilage has no blood supply and no perichondrium; it is fed by synovial fluid."],"pitfalls":["Triads belong to skeletal muscle; dyads belong to cardiac muscle.","Haversian canals run along the bone; Volkmann canals cross it.","Synchondroses are hyaline; symphyses are fibrocartilage.","Woven bone in an adult is abnormal (fracture callus, Paget disease, tumors); lamellar bone is the normal mature form."],"dumps":[{"t":"Sarcomere before and after","p":"Draw one sarcomere relaxed and the same sarcomere contracted. Label the Z lines, M line, A band, I band, and H zone in both, and mark which ones got shorter.","l":["Sarcomere spans Z line to Z line","Thin actin filaments attach at Z lines","Thick myosin filaments are centered on the M line","A band equals thick filament length and stays constant","I band (thin only) shortens","H zone (thick only) shortens","Filaments slide; they do not change length"]},{"t":"Growth plate column","p":"Draw a column of the epiphyseal growth plate from epiphysis to diaphysis and label the five zones. Next to it, show how the plate looks in achondroplasia and in rickets.","l":["Reserve zone sits nearest the epiphysis","Proliferative zone has stacked dividing chondrocytes","Hypertrophic zone has large chondrocytes","Calcification zone mineralizes matrix","Ossification zone has osteoblasts laying bone on calcified cartilage","Achondroplasia: thin proliferative zone from FGFR3 activation","Rickets: wide, disorganized hypertrophic zone from failed mineralization"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Muscle Tissue"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Bone"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Cartilage"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Musculoskeletal System | Sarcomere Structure: Actin & Myosin","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nrf_g5m8fVM"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Musculoskeletal System | Smooth Muscle","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2EeUy7xopdo"}],"related":["MS-7","MS-13-A2","MS-9","MS-1"]},"MS-3":{"id":"MS-3","summary":"The bones of the skull, spine, and limbs, the skull foramina and what passes through them, and the nerves that run against bone where fractures happen. Exam questions give a fracture or a curve and ask what structure or function is lost.","notes":[{"h":"Vertebrae by region","points":["Cervical: transverse foramina carry the vertebral artery (usually C6 to C1); C3 to C6 have bifid spines; C7 is the vertebra prominens.","Atlas (C1) has no body and no spinous process; it holds the skull and allows nodding.","Axis (C2) has the dens, the pivot for head rotation at the atlantoaxial joint.","Thoracic: costal facets on bodies and transverse processes for ribs; long downward spines; ribs limit motion.","Lumbar: large kidney-shaped bodies for weight bearing; sagittal facets favor flexion and extension."]},{"h":"Spinal curves","points":["Thoracic and sacral kyphoses are primary curves present before birth.","Cervical lordosis appears with head control and lumbar lordosis with standing and walking (secondary curves).","Scoliosis is lateral curvature plus vertebral rotation; adolescent idiopathic scoliosis is most common in girls.","Excess kyphosis can follow osteoporotic compression fractures.","Severe thoracic scoliosis stiffens the chest wall, causing restrictive lung disease (low TLC and vital capacity, normal or high FEV1/FVC)."]},{"h":"Skull foramina and pterion","points":["Superior orbital fissure: CN III, IV, V1, VI and the superior ophthalmic vein.","Foramen rotundum: V2. Foramen ovale: V3. Foramen spinosum: middle meningeal artery.","Internal acoustic meatus: CN VII and VIII. Jugular foramen: CN IX, X, XI and the internal jugular vein.","Foramen magnum: medulla, vertebral arteries, spinal roots of CN XI.","Pterion is where frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones meet; it is thin.","The anterior branch of the middle meningeal artery runs deep to the pterion, so a fracture there causes an epidural hematoma."]},{"h":"Upper limb bones","points":["Clavicle fractures most often in the middle third.","Scapula: glenoid fossa for the humeral head, acromion and coracoid process for muscle and ligament attachment.","Radius is lateral (thumb side) and widest at the wrist; ulna is medial and widest at the elbow (olecranon).","Humeral surgical neck: axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery.","Midshaft humerus (radial groove): radial nerve and deep brachial artery.","Medial epicondyle: ulnar nerve. Supracondylar fracture: median nerve and brachial artery.","Scaphoid is the most fractured carpal (snuffbox tenderness); its blood enters distally, so the proximal pole can undergo avascular necrosis.","Lunate is the most dislocated carpal and can compress the median nerve."]},{"h":"Lower limb bones","points":["Hip bone: ilium, ischium, and pubis meet and fuse at the acetabulum.","Femur carries body weight to the tibia; the fibula bears little weight but anchors muscles and forms the lateral malleolus.","Femoral neck fracture can tear the medial circumflex femoral artery and cause avascular necrosis of the femoral head.","Fibular neck fracture injures the common fibular nerve: foot drop, weak eversion, numb dorsum of foot.","Foot has medial longitudinal, lateral longitudinal, and transverse arches.","Medial longitudinal arch is held by the spring ligament, tibialis posterior tendon, and plantar aponeurosis; collapse gives flat foot (pes planus)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Regional vertebral features","cols":["Region","Key features","Functional result"],"rows":[["Cervical","Small bodies, transverse foramina, bifid spines (C3 to C6)","Wide range of motion, protects vertebral artery"],["C1 atlas","No body, no spinous process, lateral masses","Nodding at atlanto-occipital joint"],["C2 axis","Dens","Rotation at atlantoaxial joint"],["Thoracic","Costal facets, long downward spines","Rib attachment, limited flexion"],["Lumbar","Large bodies, sagittal facets","Weight bearing, flexion and extension, little rotation"]]},{"title":"Skull openings and contents","cols":["Opening","Contents"],"rows":[["Optic canal","CN II, ophthalmic artery"],["Superior orbital fissure","CN III, IV, V1, VI, superior ophthalmic vein"],["Foramen rotundum","CN V2"],["Foramen ovale","CN V3"],["Foramen spinosum","Middle meningeal artery"],["Internal acoustic meatus","CN VII, CN VIII"],["Jugular foramen","CN IX, X, XI, internal jugular vein"],["Hypoglossal canal","CN XII"],["Foramen magnum","Medulla, vertebral arteries, spinal roots of CN XI"]]},{"title":"Fracture site and structure at risk","cols":["Fracture site","Nerve at risk","Main deficit"],"rows":[["Humeral surgical neck","Axillary","Weak abduction (deltoid), numb lateral shoulder"],["Midshaft humerus (radial groove)","Radial","Wrist drop"],["Supracondylar humerus","Median","Weak thumb opposition and finger flexion"],["Medial epicondyle","Ulnar","Weak finger abduction, clawing of 4th and 5th digits"],["Fibular neck","Common fibular","Foot drop, weak eversion"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Standing Room Only","text":"V1 passes through the Superior orbital fissure, V2 through foramen Rotundum, V3 through foramen Ovale."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"So Long To Pinky, Here Comes The Thumb","text":"Proximal row lateral to medial: scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform. Distal row medial to lateral: hamate, capitate, trapezoid, trapezium."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Bone touches nerve","text":"Picture where a nerve lies directly on bone: axillary around the surgical neck, radial in the spiral groove, ulnar behind the medial epicondyle, common fibular around the fibular neck. The fracture site predicts the nerve."}],"highYield":["Surgical neck fracture of the humerus injures the axillary nerve; midshaft fracture injures the radial nerve.","Pterion fracture tears the middle meningeal artery, causing an epidural hematoma with a lucid interval.","Severe thoracic scoliosis causes restrictive lung disease from reduced chest wall expansion.","Fibular neck fracture causes foot drop from common fibular nerve injury.","Scaphoid fracture risks avascular necrosis of the proximal pole because blood enters distally.","Femoral neck fracture risks avascular necrosis from damage to the medial circumflex femoral artery.","The dens of C2 is the pivot for head rotation."],"pitfalls":["Anatomical neck fractures of the humerus are rare; the surgical neck is the classic site for axillary nerve injury.","Foramen spinosum carries the middle meningeal artery; foramen ovale carries V3.","Kyphosis is a front to back curve; scoliosis is a side to side curve with rotation.","CN VII enters the internal acoustic meatus but exits the skull through the stylomastoid foramen."],"dumps":[{"t":"Cranial base map","p":"Draw the floor of the skull from above and label the openings from the optic canal back to the foramen magnum. Write what passes through each one.","l":["Optic canal: CN II and ophthalmic artery","Superior orbital fissure: CN III, IV, V1, VI","Foramen rotundum: V2","Foramen ovale: V3","Foramen spinosum: middle meningeal artery","Internal acoustic meatus: CN VII and VIII","Jugular foramen: CN IX, X, XI and internal jugular vein","Foramen magnum: medulla and vertebral arteries"]},{"t":"Limb fracture table","p":"Make a table with three columns: fracture site, nerve or vessel at risk, and the deficit you would test. Fill it for the upper and lower limb.","l":["Humeral surgical neck: axillary nerve, weak abduction","Midshaft humerus: radial nerve, wrist drop","Supracondylar humerus: median nerve and brachial artery","Medial epicondyle: ulnar nerve, claw hand","Scaphoid: avascular necrosis of proximal pole","Femoral neck: medial circumflex femoral artery, avascular necrosis","Fibular neck: common fibular nerve, foot drop"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower Limb"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head (section on the cranium)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Scapula Anatomy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=70j06vbmclM"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Coxa & Pelvis Anatomy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tdlmHi0n0Ss"}],"related":["MS-5","MS-4","MS-13-H1","MS-13-E4"]},"MS-4":{"id":"MS-4","summary":"How joints are classified, how they are innervated, and how the shoulder, knee, and jaw stay stable. Exam questions give a dislocation, a positive exam test, or referred pain and ask which structure is involved.","notes":[{"h":"Joint classification","points":["By movement: synarthrosis (immovable), amphiarthrosis (slightly movable), diarthrosis (freely movable, all synovial).","Hinge: humeroulnar joint, interphalangeal joints (flexion and extension).","Pivot: median atlantoaxial joint, proximal radioulnar joint (rotation).","Ball and socket: shoulder, hip (movement in all axes).","Saddle: first carpometacarpal joint (thumb opposition). Condyloid: wrist, metacarpophalangeal joints.","Plane: acromioclavicular and intercarpal joints (gliding)."]},{"h":"Joint innervation","points":["Hilton law: nerves to the muscles that move a joint also supply that joint and the skin over it.","Joint nerves carry proprioception (position sense) and nociception (pain).","The hip is supplied by the femoral, obturator, and sciatic nerves; the obturator and femoral nerves also supply the knee region.","Hip disease (for example slipped capital femoral epiphysis) can therefore present as knee pain."]},{"h":"Shoulder","points":["Glenoid fossa is shallow, and the labrum only deepens it slightly, so bone gives little stability.","Rotator cuff tendons hold the humeral head in the glenoid (dynamic stability).","Anterior dislocation is most common, usually with the arm abducted and externally rotated.","The axillary nerve wraps the surgical neck and is stretched in anterior dislocation.","Axillary nerve injury: weak abduction past about 15 degrees (deltoid), weak lateral rotation (teres minor), numb skin over the lower deltoid."]},{"h":"Knee","points":["ACL runs from the anterior tibia to the lateral femoral condyle and stops forward slide of the tibia.","PCL runs from the posterior tibia to the medial femoral condyle and stops backward slide of the tibia.","MCL resists valgus stress (blow to the outside of the knee); LCL resists varus stress.","Medial meniscus is attached to the MCL and capsule, so it moves less and tears more often.","Classic unhappy triad from a lateral blow: ACL, MCL, and medial meniscus."]},{"h":"Temporomandibular joint","points":["Synovial joint between the mandibular condyle and the temporal bone, divided by an articular disc.","Lower compartment allows hinge rotation; upper compartment allows gliding (translation) onto the articular eminence.","Lateral pterygoid pulls the condyle and disc forward to open and protrude the jaw.","Innervated by the auriculotemporal and masseteric branches of CN V3.","Anterior disc displacement: on opening, the condyle snaps forward under the displaced disc and clicks; jaw pain and limited opening can follow."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Synovial joint types","cols":["Type","Movement","Example"],"rows":[["Hinge","Flexion and extension in one plane","Humeroulnar, interphalangeal"],["Pivot","Rotation around one axis","Median atlantoaxial, proximal radioulnar"],["Ball and socket","Movement in all axes","Shoulder, hip"],["Saddle","Two axes plus opposition","First carpometacarpal"],["Condyloid","Two axes, no rotation","Radiocarpal (wrist), metacarpophalangeal"],["Plane","Gliding","Acromioclavicular, intercarpal"]]},{"title":"Knee structures and their tests","cols":["Structure","Job","Test","Positive finding"],"rows":[["ACL","Stops anterior tibial slide","Anterior drawer, Lachman","Tibia pulls forward excessively"],["PCL","Stops posterior tibial slide","Posterior drawer","Tibia pushes back excessively"],["MCL","Resists valgus force","Valgus stress","Medial joint line opens"],["LCL","Resists varus force","Varus stress","Lateral joint line opens"],["Menisci","Cushion and spread load","McMurray","Painful click on rotation"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Ligament letters","text":"ACL attaches to the Anterior tibia and runs to the Lateral femur. PCL is the opposite: posterior tibia to medial femur. The name tells you where it sits on the tibia."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Valgus pushes the knee in","text":"A valgus force pushes the knee toward the midline and stretches the medial side, so it tests the MCL. Varus stretches the lateral side and tests the LCL."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Knee pain, check the hip","text":"Because of Hilton law, the obturator and femoral nerves carry pain from the hip and the knee region. Always examine the hip in a child or adolescent with knee pain."}],"highYield":["Anterior shoulder dislocation stretches the axillary nerve, causing deltoid weakness and numbness over the lower deltoid.","The rotator cuff provides most glenohumeral stability because the glenoid is shallow.","Lachman test is the most sensitive exam test for an ACL tear.","Medial meniscus tears more often because it is fixed to the MCL.","Hip disease can present as knee pain through the obturator and femoral nerves.","TMJ clicking points to articular disc displacement.","The first carpometacarpal joint is a saddle joint; the median atlantoaxial joint is a pivot."],"pitfalls":["A positive anterior drawer points to the ACL; a positive posterior drawer points to the PCL.","Valgus stress tests the MCL, even though the force is applied to the lateral knee.","Posterior shoulder dislocation is rare and classically follows seizures or electric shock.","The TMJ disc sits in a synovial joint; TMJ articular surfaces are covered with fibrocartilage."],"dumps":[{"t":"Knee from above","p":"Draw the top of the tibia viewed from above. Place the medial and lateral menisci, the ACL, the PCL, and the collateral ligaments, then write the clinical test for each.","l":["ACL attaches anteriorly on the tibia","PCL attaches posteriorly on the tibia","Medial meniscus is C-shaped and attached to the MCL","Lateral meniscus is more circular and more mobile","Anterior drawer and Lachman test the ACL","Posterior drawer tests the PCL","Valgus stress tests the MCL; varus stress tests the LCL","Unhappy triad: ACL, MCL, medial meniscus"]},{"t":"Shoulder dislocation","p":"Draw the glenohumeral joint from the front, then draw an anterior dislocation. Trace the axillary nerve and list what the patient loses.","l":["Glenoid is shallow; labrum deepens it","Rotator cuff provides dynamic stability","Anterior dislocation is most common","Mechanism is abduction plus external rotation","Axillary nerve wraps the surgical neck of the humerus","Deltoid weakness gives weak abduction","Teres minor weakness gives weak lateral rotation","Numb skin over the lower deltoid"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb (section on joints of the upper limb)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower Limb (section on the knee joint)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head (section on the temporomandibular joint)"}],"videos":[],"related":["MS-2","MS-5","MS-13-H1","MS-13-E1"]},"MS-5":{"id":"MS-5","summary":"The action and nerve supply of the rotator cuff, the limb muscles, the head and neck muscles, and the trunk muscles. Exam questions give a weakness or a deformity and ask which nerve or muscle is injured.","notes":[{"h":"Rotator cuff","points":["Supraspinatus: starts abduction (first about 15 degrees); suprascapular nerve.","Infraspinatus: lateral rotation; suprascapular nerve.","Teres minor: lateral rotation; axillary nerve.","Subscapularis: medial rotation; upper and lower subscapular nerves.","Supraspinatus is injured most often, from impingement under the acromion; test with the empty can test."]},{"h":"Upper limb nerve injuries","points":["Musculocutaneous: weak elbow flexion and supination (biceps, brachialis), numb lateral forearm.","Radial: wrist drop from paralysis of wrist and finger extensors; high lesions also weaken the triceps.","Median at the wrist: thenar atrophy and loss of thumb opposition (ape hand), numb lateral three and a half fingers (palm spared in carpal tunnel syndrome).","Median at the elbow: also loses lateral finger flexors, so a fist shows the hand of benediction.","Ulnar: claw hand (4th and 5th digits) and weak finger abduction and adduction (interossei).","Long thoracic: serratus anterior paralysis gives a winged scapula; injured in axillary surgery."]},{"h":"Lower limb nerve injuries","points":["Femoral: weak knee extension (quadriceps), lost patellar reflex, numb anterior thigh.","Obturator: weak thigh adduction, numb medial thigh.","Superior gluteal: weak gluteus medius and minimus; standing on the injured leg, the pelvis drops on the opposite side (Trendelenburg sign).","Inferior gluteal: weak gluteus maximus, trouble rising from a chair or climbing stairs.","Common fibular: foot drop, steppage gait, weak eversion, numb dorsum of foot.","Tibial: weak plantarflexion, inversion, and toe flexion; numb sole."]},{"h":"Head and neck","points":["Muscles of mastication (masseter, temporalis, medial and lateral pterygoids): CN V3.","Masseter, temporalis, and medial pterygoid close the jaw; lateral pterygoid opens and protrudes it.","Muscles of facial expression: CN VII.","All tongue muscles are CN XII except palatoglossus (CN X); a CN XII lesion makes the tongue deviate toward the injured side.","Sternocleidomastoid (turns the head to the opposite side) and trapezius (shrugs the shoulder): CN XI."]},{"h":"Trunk","points":["Erector spinae extend the back and are supplied by dorsal rami.","Diaphragm is the main muscle of inspiration; phrenic nerve C3 to C5.","Abdominal wall (external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, rectus abdominis): thoracoabdominal nerves (T7 to T11), subcostal nerve (T12), and L1 branches.","Levator ani (puborectalis, pubococcygeus, iliococcygeus) forms the pelvic floor and supports pelvic organs; puborectalis helps fecal continence."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Rotator cuff","cols":["Muscle","Main action","Nerve"],"rows":[["Supraspinatus","Initial abduction (about 0 to 15 degrees)","Suprascapular"],["Infraspinatus","Lateral rotation","Suprascapular"],["Teres minor","Lateral rotation","Axillary"],["Subscapularis","Medial rotation","Upper and lower subscapular"]]},{"title":"Limb nerve injuries","cols":["Nerve","Key muscles","Classic sign"],"rows":[["Musculocutaneous","Biceps, brachialis","Weak elbow flexion and supination"],["Radial","Wrist and finger extensors","Wrist drop"],["Median","Thenar muscles, lateral finger flexors","Ape hand; hand of benediction if lesion is proximal"],["Ulnar","Interossei, medial lumbricals, hypothenar","Claw hand of 4th and 5th digits"],["Long thoracic","Serratus anterior","Winged scapula"],["Femoral","Quadriceps","Weak knee extension, lost patellar reflex"],["Obturator","Thigh adductors","Weak adduction"],["Superior gluteal","Gluteus medius and minimus","Trendelenburg sign (opposite hip drops)"],["Common fibular","Dorsiflexors and evertors","Foot drop"],["Tibial","Plantarflexors, tibialis posterior","Weak plantarflexion and inversion"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"SItS","text":"Rotator cuff: Supraspinatus, Infraspinatus, teres minor, Subscapularis. The small t reminds you teres minor is the one with a different nerve (axillary)."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive","text":"The phrenic nerve comes from C3 to C5. A spinal cord injury above C3 stops spontaneous breathing."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"M's munch, lateral lowers","text":"Masseter, teMporalis, and Medial pterygoid close the jaw (they munch). The Lateral pterygoid Lowers the jaw (opens it)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Trendelenburg: the good side sags","text":"The gluteus medius on the stance leg holds the pelvis level. If it is weak, the pelvis drops on the opposite, lifted side. The lesion is on the side of the leg the patient stands on."}],"highYield":["Supraspinatus is the most commonly injured rotator cuff muscle.","Teres minor is the only rotator cuff muscle supplied by the axillary nerve.","Midshaft humerus fracture or crutch pressure in the axilla gives wrist drop (radial nerve).","Long thoracic nerve injury after mastectomy causes a winged scapula.","Superior gluteal nerve injury gives a positive Trendelenburg sign with drop of the opposite hip.","Common fibular nerve injury gives foot drop and loss of eversion.","Tongue deviates toward the side of a CN XII lesion.","Diaphragm irritation refers pain to the shoulder through C3 to C5."],"pitfalls":["Supraspinatus starts abduction; the deltoid takes over after about 15 degrees.","Infraspinatus and teres minor both laterally rotate but have different nerves (suprascapular versus axillary).","Ape hand comes from median nerve injury; claw hand comes mainly from ulnar nerve injury.","In Trendelenburg sign the pelvis drops on the side opposite the injured nerve.","Palatoglossus is the one tongue muscle supplied by CN X."],"dumps":[{"t":"Upper limb nerve table","p":"Make a table of the musculocutaneous, radial, median, ulnar, axillary, and long thoracic nerves. For each, list the key muscles, the classic sign of injury, and a common injury site.","l":["Musculocutaneous: biceps and brachialis, weak elbow flexion","Radial: extensors, wrist drop, midshaft humerus","Median: thenar muscles, ape hand, carpal tunnel or supracondylar fracture","Ulnar: interossei, claw hand, medial epicondyle","Axillary: deltoid and teres minor, weak abduction, surgical neck or dislocation","Long thoracic: serratus anterior, winged scapula, axillary surgery"]},{"t":"Lower limb nerve table","p":"Make a table of the femoral, obturator, superior gluteal, inferior gluteal, common fibular, and tibial nerves. For each, list the key muscles, the movement lost, and the sign you would see.","l":["Femoral: quadriceps, knee extension, lost patellar reflex","Obturator: adductors, thigh adduction","Superior gluteal: gluteus medius and minimus, Trendelenburg sign","Inferior gluteal: gluteus maximus, trouble climbing stairs","Common fibular: dorsiflexors and evertors, foot drop","Tibial: plantarflexors and tibialis posterior, weak plantarflexion and inversion"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower Limb"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Neck"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen (section on the anterolateral abdominal wall)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Muscles of the Upper Limb","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ToNL6jMEjuE"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Muscles of the Lower Limb | Anatomy Model","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q0WDD704P1M"}],"related":["MS-3","MS-4","MS-10","MS-13-H1"]},"MS-6":{"id":"MS-6","summary":"Where bone, cartilage, and tendon come from, which collagen types sit in which tissues, how collagen and elastic fibers are built, and why cartilage and tendons heal slowly. Exam questions name a vitamin, mineral, or gene defect and ask which collagen step fails.","notes":[{"h":"Embryonic origins","points":["Limb bones, cartilage, and tendons come from lateral plate (somatic) mesoderm.","Vertebrae and ribs come from sclerotome (paraxial mesoderm).","Most of the face and the pharyngeal arch cartilages come from neural crest mesenchyme.","Skeletal muscle itself comes from somite myotomes, so a limb's muscles and its tendons have different origins."]},{"h":"Collagen types","points":["Type I: bone, tendon, ligament, skin, fibrocartilage (annulus fibrosus); about 90 percent of body collagen.","Type II: hyaline cartilage, nucleus pulposus, vitreous humor.","Type III: reticular fibers, blood vessels, skin, early granulation tissue.","Type IV: basement membrane (network, not fibrils)."]},{"h":"Collagen synthesis","points":["Inside the cell (RER): alpha chains are made with repeating Gly-X-Y, where X and Y are often proline and lysine.","Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases add OH groups; they need vitamin C (ascorbate) and iron.","Some hydroxylysines are glycosylated.","Three chains wind into a triple helix (procollagen), lined up by disulfide bonds between their C-terminal propeptides, and are secreted.","Outside the cell: procollagen peptidases cut off the end propeptides to make tropocollagen.","Lysyl oxidase (needs copper) forms covalent cross-links between tropocollagen molecules in the fibril."]},{"h":"Defects by step","points":["Scurvy: no vitamin C, poor hydroxylation, weak triple helix; bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhage, poor wound healing.","Osteogenesis imperfecta: COL1A1 or COL1A2 mutation (often glycine substitution) gives too little or faulty type I collagen; fragile bones, blue sclerae, hearing loss.","Menkes disease: X-linked ATP7A mutation blocks copper transport, so lysyl oxidase cannot cross-link; kinky hair, hypotonia, neurodegeneration."]},{"h":"Cartilage, tendon, and elastic fibers","points":["Aggrecan carries chondroitin and keratan sulfate chains and binds hyaluronan through link protein.","These negative GAGs pull in cations and water, which pushes back against compression; type II collagen resists stretch.","Articular cartilage has no blood vessels, so chondrocytes get nutrients by diffusion and repair is poor.","Tendons have few cells and a poor blood supply, so they heal slowly.","Elastic fibers have an elastin core laid on fibrillin-1 microfibrils; elastin is cross-linked by desmosine.","Elastic fibers fill the ligamentum flavum (yellow ligament), which recoils when the spine straightens.","Fibrillin-1 binds latent TGF-beta; in Marfan syndrome (FBN1 mutation) TGF-beta is released and overactive."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Collagen types","cols":["Type","Main tissues","Linked disorder"],"rows":[["I","Bone, tendon, ligament, skin, fibrocartilage","Osteogenesis imperfecta"],["II","Hyaline cartilage, nucleus pulposus, vitreous","Some chondrodysplasias"],["III","Reticular fibers, vessels, granulation tissue","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome"],["IV","Basement membranes","Alport syndrome, Goodpasture syndrome"]]},{"title":"Collagen synthesis steps and defects","cols":["Step","Where","Requirement","Disease when it fails"],"rows":[["Gly-X-Y chain translation","RER","Normal COL genes","Osteogenesis imperfecta (glycine substitution)"],["Proline and lysine hydroxylation","RER","Vitamin C, iron","Scurvy"],["Glycosylation of hydroxylysine","RER","Glycosyltransferases","No classic exam disease"],["Triple helix formation","RER","Intact chains, disulfide bonds","Osteogenesis imperfecta"],["Propeptide cleavage","Outside cell","Procollagen peptidases","Some Ehlers-Danlos types"],["Cross-linking","Outside cell","Lysyl oxidase, copper","Menkes disease"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Be So Totally Cool, Read Books","text":"Type I: Bone, Skin, Tendon. Type II: Cartilage. Type III: Reticulin. Type IV: Basement membrane."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Type II: cartwolage","text":"Type II collagen is in cartilage, including the nucleus pulposus and vitreous body."},{"kind":"trick","label":"C for collagen, Cu for cross-links","text":"Vitamin C works inside the cell (hydroxylation). Copper works outside the cell (lysyl oxidase cross-links). Scurvy breaks the inside step; Menkes breaks the outside step."}],"highYield":["Vitamin C is a cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase; deficiency weakens the triple helix.","Lysyl oxidase needs copper; Menkes disease (ATP7A) blocks cross-linking.","Osteogenesis imperfecta is most often a type I collagen defect (COL1A1 or COL1A2).","Hyaline cartilage and nucleus pulposus are type II collagen; annulus fibrosus is type I.","Aggrecan GAGs hold water, which lets articular cartilage resist compression.","Fibrillin-1 sequesters TGF-beta; FBN1 mutation in Marfan syndrome raises TGF-beta signaling.","Ligamentum flavum is rich in elastic fibers."],"pitfalls":["Hydroxylation happens inside the cell; cross-linking happens outside the cell.","Type IV collagen forms sheets in basement membranes, not fibrils.","Limb tendons come from lateral plate mesoderm even though limb muscles come from somites.","Fibrillin-1 is the scaffold that is defective in Marfan syndrome; elastin is the stretchy core laid on it. Lysyl oxidase cross-links both collagen and elastin (desmosine)."],"dumps":[{"t":"Collagen assembly line","p":"Draw a fibroblast and trace collagen synthesis from the RER to a cross-linked fibril outside the cell. At each step, write what it needs and the disease when it fails.","l":["Alpha chains are made in the RER with Gly-X-Y repeats","Proline and lysine hydroxylation needs vitamin C and iron (scurvy)","Hydroxylysine glycosylation","Triple helix forms as procollagen","Procollagen is secreted","Peptidases cleave propeptides to form tropocollagen","Lysyl oxidase with copper cross-links fibrils (Menkes)","Faulty or reduced type I chains cause osteogenesis imperfecta"]},{"t":"Why cartilage resists squeezing","p":"Draw a piece of articular cartilage matrix showing hyaluronan, aggrecan, GAG chains, and type II collagen. Explain how it resists compression and why it heals poorly.","l":["Hyaluronan backbone","Aggrecan core proteins attached through link protein","Chondroitin sulfate and keratan sulfate chains are negatively charged","Negative charges draw in cations and water","Bound water resists compression","Type II collagen network resists tension","No blood vessels, so nutrients arrive by diffusion and repair is slow"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous Proteins"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Glycosaminoglycans, Proteoglycans, and Glycoproteins"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Connective Tissue"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Cartilage"}],"videos":[],"related":["MS-8","MS-9","MS-13-A3","MS-13-D1","MS-13-D3"]},"MS-7":{"id":"MS-7","summary":"How skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle turn an electrical or chemical signal into force, and how force is graded. Exam questions give a channel, drug, or training change and ask how contraction or relaxation will change.","notes":[{"h":"Skeletal excitation-contraction coupling","points":["An action potential spreads along the sarcolemma and down the T tubules.","The dihydropyridine receptor (L-type voltage sensor) in the T tubule changes shape.","It is mechanically linked to ryanodine receptor 1 (RyR1) on the sarcoplasmic reticulum, which opens and releases calcium.","Skeletal muscle does not need calcium entry from outside the cell to contract.","Calcium binds troponin C, which moves tropomyosin off the myosin binding sites on actin.","SERCA pumps calcium back into the SR (uses ATP), and the muscle relaxes."]},{"h":"Cross-bridge cycle","points":["Starting point: myosin head tightly bound to actin with no nucleotide (rigor state).","ATP binds myosin and releases it from actin.","ATP hydrolysis cocks the head; ADP and phosphate stay bound.","Head binds actin; phosphate release drives the power stroke; then ADP leaves.","Rigor mortis: without ATP, myosin cannot detach from actin, so muscles lock a few hours after death."]},{"h":"Grading skeletal force","points":["A motor unit is one motor neuron plus all the fibers it supplies.","Size principle: small motor units (slow, type I) are recruited first and large ones (fast, type II) last.","Summation: a second action potential before relaxation adds force, because the twitch lasts longer than the action potential.","At high firing rates twitches fuse into tetanus, the maximal sustained force.","Length-tension: active force is greatest at the resting sarcomere length with optimal actin-myosin overlap; too stretched or too short lowers force.","Force-velocity: the heavier the load, the slower the shortening; velocity is maximal at zero load and zero at maximal (isometric) force."]},{"h":"Cardiac muscle","points":["During the plateau, calcium enters through L-type calcium channels.","This calcium triggers RyR2 to release more calcium from the SR (calcium-induced calcium release).","The long refractory period lasts nearly as long as contraction, so cardiac muscle cannot summate or go into tetany.","Sympathetic beta-1 stimulation raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A.","PKA phosphorylates L-type channels (more calcium entry) and phospholamban.","Phosphorylated phospholamban stops inhibiting SERCA2, so the SR refills faster: faster relaxation and stronger next beat."]},{"h":"Smooth muscle","points":["Calcium enters through L-type channels or is released from the SR through IP3 receptors.","Calcium binds calmodulin; calcium-calmodulin activates myosin light chain kinase (MLCK).","MLCK phosphorylates the myosin light chain, which turns on cross-bridge cycling; smooth muscle has no troponin.","Myosin light chain phosphatase removes the phosphate and causes relaxation.","Latch state: dephosphorylated myosin stays attached and cycles slowly, holding tone with little ATP.","Nitric oxide activates guanylyl cyclase; cGMP activates protein kinase G, lowers calcium, and activates the phosphatase, causing relaxation."]},{"h":"Fiber types and training","points":["Type I: slow, oxidative, red (myoglobin), many mitochondria and capillaries, fatigue resistant; posture muscles such as soleus.","Type II: fast, glycolytic, white, large glycogen stores, high force, fatigable.","Endurance training raises mitochondria, capillaries, and oxidative enzymes.","Resistance training enlarges type II fibers by adding myofibrils (hypertrophy)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Contraction in three muscle types","cols":["Feature","Skeletal","Cardiac","Smooth"],"rows":[["Trigger","ACh at the neuromuscular junction","Pacemaker action potential","Nerves, hormones, stretch"],["Calcium source","SR only (RyR1)","Extracellular calcium triggers SR release (RyR2)","Extracellular and SR (IP3 receptor)"],["DHPR to RyR link","Mechanical","Calcium-induced calcium release","No T tubules"],["Calcium sensor","Troponin C","Troponin C","Calmodulin"],["Switch for cross-bridges","Tropomyosin moves off actin","Tropomyosin moves off actin","MLCK phosphorylates myosin light chain"],["Tetany possible","Yes","No (long refractory period)","Sustained tone (latch state)"]]},{"title":"Cross-bridge cycle","cols":["Step","Event","Result"],"rows":[["1","ATP binds myosin","Myosin releases actin"],["2","ATP is hydrolyzed","Head cocks with ADP and phosphate bound"],["3","Head binds exposed actin","Cross-bridge forms"],["4","Phosphate is released","Power stroke pulls actin toward the M line"],["5","ADP is released","Rigor state until new ATP binds"]]},{"title":"Muscle fiber types","cols":["Feature","Type I","Type II"],"rows":[["Speed","Slow twitch","Fast twitch"],["Energy","Oxidative phosphorylation","Glycolysis"],["Color","Red (myoglobin)","White"],["Mitochondria and capillaries","Many","Few"],["Fatigue","Resistant","Fatigable"],["Training response","Endurance raises oxidative capacity","Resistance causes hypertrophy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"One slow red ox","text":"Type 1 fibers are slow twitch, red, and use oxidative phosphorylation."},{"kind":"trick","label":"ATP lets go","text":"ATP is needed to release myosin from actin and to pump calcium back into the SR. With no ATP, muscle cannot relax, which is rigor mortis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Smooth muscle switch is on myosin","text":"Skeletal and cardiac muscle regulate the thin filament (troponin and tropomyosin). Smooth muscle regulates the thick filament (phosphorylating the myosin light chain)."}],"highYield":["Skeletal DHPR is mechanically coupled to RyR1; cardiac contraction depends on calcium entry triggering RyR2.","RyR1 mutation causes malignant hyperthermia with volatile anesthetics; dantrolene blocks RyR1.","ATP binding detaches myosin from actin; loss of ATP causes rigor mortis.","Small motor units are recruited first (size principle).","Phospholamban phosphorylation by PKA speeds SERCA2, improving relaxation and contractility.","The long cardiac refractory period prevents tetany.","Nitric oxide relaxes smooth muscle through cGMP; PDE5 inhibitors prolong this effect.","Endurance training raises oxidative capacity; resistance training enlarges type II fibers."],"pitfalls":["The power stroke happens with phosphate release; ATP binding causes detachment.","Smooth muscle uses calmodulin; skeletal and cardiac muscle use troponin C.","Phosphorylating the myosin light chain contracts smooth muscle; the phosphatase relaxes it.","Tetanus in muscle physiology means fused maximal contraction from rapid firing; the disease tetanus is caused by Clostridium tetani toxin.","Phospholamban inhibits SERCA when unphosphorylated; PKA phosphorylation removes that brake."],"dumps":[{"t":"Skeletal versus cardiac coupling","p":"Draw a T tubule next to the SR for a skeletal fiber and for a cardiac cell. Show how calcium is released in each, how it is removed, and why only one can go into tetany.","l":["Action potential travels down the T tubule","Skeletal: DHPR mechanically opens RyR1","Cardiac: L-type calcium entry triggers RyR2 (calcium-induced calcium release)","Calcium binds troponin C and moves tropomyosin","SERCA returns calcium to the SR","Phospholamban regulates SERCA2 in the heart; PKA phosphorylation speeds it","Cardiac long refractory period prevents tetany","Skeletal summation and tetanus come from rapid firing"]},{"t":"Smooth muscle on and off","p":"Draw a smooth muscle cell and trace the pathway from a rise in calcium to contraction, then from nitric oxide to relaxation.","l":["Calcium enters through L-type channels or SR IP3 receptors","Calcium binds calmodulin","Calcium-calmodulin activates MLCK","MLCK phosphorylates the myosin light chain and starts cross-bridge cycling","Myosin light chain phosphatase dephosphorylates and relaxes","Latch state holds tone with little ATP","Nitric oxide activates guanylyl cyclase and raises cGMP","Protein kinase G lowers calcium and activates the phosphatase"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Contraction of Skeletal Muscle"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Excitation of Skeletal Muscle: Neuromuscular Transmission and Excitation-Contraction Coupling"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Excitation and Contraction of Smooth Muscle"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Muscle; The Heart as a Pump and Function of the Heart Valves"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Musculoskeletal System | Neuromuscular Junction | Excitation Contraction Coupling: Part 2","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A96kAMPZXv8"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Musculoskeletal System | Neuromuscular Junction | Sliding Filament Theory: Part 3","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UW47uSLcM2g"}],"related":["MS-2","MS-10","MS-13-D2","MS-9"]},"MS-8":{"id":"MS-8","summary":"How vitamin D, PTH, calcitonin, and FGF23 keep calcium and phosphate in range, how muscle switches fuels as exercise goes on, and which minerals and cofactors bone and muscle depend on. Exam questions pair a lab pattern or an exercise history with the enzyme or hormone step that explains it, such as McArdle disease versus CPT II deficiency.","notes":[{"h":"Vitamin D activation","points":["Skin: UVB converts 7-dehydrocholesterol to cholecalciferol (vitamin D3); diet adds D2 and D3","Liver: 25-hydroxylase makes 25-hydroxyvitamin D (calcidiol), the main circulating and storage form","Kidney proximal tubule: 1-alpha-hydroxylase makes 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol), the active hormone","Serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D measures status: long half-life (weeks), loosely regulated, reflects skin plus diet supply","Calcitriol has a half-life of hours and is tightly controlled, so it can look normal even in deficiency","1-alpha-hydroxylase is turned up by PTH and low phosphate, and turned down by FGF23 and calcitriol itself"]},{"h":"Calcium and phosphate hormones","points":["Low ionized calcium is sensed by the calcium-sensing receptor on parathyroid chief cells, which then release PTH","PTH acts on osteoblasts (they carry the PTH receptor), which raise RANKL and lower OPG, so osteoclasts resorb bone","PTH increases calcium reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule and blocks phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule","Calcitriol raises gut absorption of both calcium and phosphate","Calcitonin from thyroid C cells inhibits osteoclasts; minor role in adult calcium balance","FGF23 from osteocytes rises with high phosphate or calcitriol, causes phosphaturia, and suppresses 1-alpha-hydroxylase","Osteoprotegerin (OPG) from osteoblasts is a decoy receptor that binds RANKL and limits osteoclast activation"]},{"h":"Muscle fuel and exercise","points":["First seconds: stored ATP plus phosphocreatine; creatine kinase moves phosphate from phosphocreatine to ADP","Up to about 2 minutes of hard effort: anaerobic glycolysis of muscle glycogen to lactate","Longer effort: oxidative metabolism of glucose, then a rising share from fatty acid beta-oxidation","Cori cycle: muscle lactate goes to the liver, becomes glucose by gluconeogenesis, and returns to muscle (net cost 4 ATP)","Creatinine forms nonenzymatically from creatine and phosphocreatine at a steady daily rate, so it tracks muscle mass"]},{"h":"Metabolic myopathies","points":["McArdle disease (glycogen storage disease type V): autosomal recessive loss of muscle glycogen phosphorylase (myophosphorylase)","McArdle: cramps and fatigue in brief intense exercise, myoglobinuria, high CK, blood lactate fails to rise with exercise","McArdle second wind: after about 10 minutes, blood flow brings glucose and fatty acids and the patient can continue","Carnitine shuttle: CPT I on the outer membrane, translocase, CPT II on the inner membrane move long-chain fatty acids into mitochondria","CPT II deficiency: weakness, muscle pain, and rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria after prolonged exercise, fasting, cold, or illness"]},{"h":"Minerals and cofactors","points":["Bone mineral is calcium hydroxyapatite, Ca10(PO4)6(OH)2, laid down on type I collagen","Magnesium is needed for PTH secretion and action; severe hypomagnesemia causes hypocalcemia that persists until magnesium is replaced","Copper is the cofactor for lysyl oxidase, which cross-links collagen and elastin","Vitamin K drives gamma-carboxylation of glutamate on osteocalcin so it can bind calcium in bone matrix","Vitamin C drives prolyl and lysyl hydroxylation of collagen (see scurvy)"]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hormones that set serum calcium and phosphate","cols":["Hormone","Source and trigger","Bone","Kidney","Gut","Net serum effect"],"rows":[["PTH","Parathyroid chief cells; low ionized calcium","Resorption via osteoblast RANKL","Calcium reabsorption up (DCT), phosphate excretion up (PCT), 1-alpha-hydroxylase up","Indirect, through calcitriol","Calcium up, phosphate down"],["Calcitriol","Kidney proximal tubule; PTH and low phosphate","Supplies mineral; resorption at high levels","Modest calcium and phosphate reabsorption","Calcium and phosphate absorption up","Calcium up, phosphate up"],["Calcitonin","Thyroid parafollicular C cells; high calcium","Inhibits osteoclasts","Minor increase in calcium excretion","None","Calcium down (minor)"],["FGF23","Osteocytes; high phosphate or calcitriol","No major direct effect","Phosphate excretion up, 1-alpha-hydroxylase down","Less absorption through lower calcitriol","Phosphate down"]]},{"title":"Muscle fuel across exercise duration","cols":["Time frame","Main ATP source","Key enzyme or pathway","Example"],"rows":[["0 to about 10 seconds","Stored ATP and phosphocreatine","Creatine kinase","100 m sprint, heavy lift"],["About 10 seconds to 2 minutes","Anaerobic glycolysis of muscle glycogen","Glycogen phosphorylase, glycolysis, lactate dehydrogenase","400 m run"],["Minutes to about an hour","Oxidative metabolism of glucose and glycogen, rising fat use","TCA cycle and oxidative phosphorylation","10 km run"],["Hours, or fasting","Fatty acid beta-oxidation","Carnitine shuttle (CPT I, CPT II), beta-oxidation","Marathon, long hike without food"]]},{"title":"McArdle disease versus CPT II deficiency","cols":["Feature","McArdle disease","CPT II deficiency"],"rows":[["Defect","Muscle glycogen phosphorylase","Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II (inner mitochondrial membrane)"],["Inheritance","Autosomal recessive","Autosomal recessive"],["Trigger","Brief, intense, or isometric exercise","Prolonged exercise, fasting, cold, infection"],["Fuel that fails","Muscle glycogen","Long-chain fatty acids"],["Second wind","Yes","No"],["Lactate rise with exercise","Absent","Normal"],["Myoglobinuria","Yes","Yes, often the presenting sign"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"PTH = Phosphate Trashing Hormone","text":"PTH raises serum calcium but makes the proximal tubule dump phosphate, so serum phosphate falls."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Very Poor Carbohydrate Metabolism","text":"Glycogen storage diseases I, II, III, V: Von Gierke, Pompe, Cori, McArdle. McArdle (type V) is the Muscle one."},{"kind":"trick","label":"25 for status, 1,25 for action","text":"Order the 25-hydroxyvitamin D level to judge vitamin D stores. Calcitriol is the active hormone but is held steady by PTH, so it misleads."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Sprint versus marathon","text":"Cramps in a short sprint point to glycogen (McArdle). Dark urine after a long run or a missed meal points to fat (CPT II)."}],"highYield":["Serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D is the best test of vitamin D status","PTH acts on osteoclasts indirectly, through RANKL from osteoblasts","FGF23 lowers phosphate and calcitriol; excess FGF23 causes X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets","McArdle: flat lactate curve with exercise, myoglobinuria, and a second wind","CPT II deficiency: rhabdomyolysis after prolonged exercise or fasting, normal lactate rise","Hypomagnesemia causes hypocalcemia by impairing PTH release and action","Copper deficiency weakens collagen cross-links through lysyl oxidase","Serum creatinine depends on muscle mass as well as GFR"],"pitfalls":["Calcitriol level is a poor marker of vitamin D stores; high PTH can keep it normal while 25-hydroxyvitamin D is low.","Osteoclasts lack the PTH receptor; PTH signals through osteoblasts and RANKL.","McArdle and CPT II both cause myoglobinuria; the trigger (short burst versus prolonged effort or fasting) and the lactate response separate them.","A low serum creatinine in a frail older adult may reflect low muscle mass rather than excellent kidney function."],"dumps":[{"t":"Vitamin D and PTH map","p":"Draw skin, liver, kidney, gut, bone, and parathyroid. Trace vitamin D from 7-dehydrocholesterol to calcitriol, then add arrows for PTH, calcitonin, FGF23, and OPG with their effects on calcium and phosphate.","l":["UVB converts 7-dehydrocholesterol to cholecalciferol in skin","Liver 25-hydroxylation makes 25-hydroxyvitamin D, the status marker","Kidney 1-alpha-hydroxylation makes calcitriol","PTH raises RANKL on osteoblasts to activate osteoclasts","PTH increases distal tubule calcium reabsorption and causes phosphaturia","PTH and low phosphate turn on 1-alpha-hydroxylase; FGF23 turns it off","Calcitonin from C cells inhibits osteoclasts","OPG binds RANKL and blocks osteoclast activation"]},{"t":"Exercise fuel timeline","p":"Draw a timeline of exercise from the first second to several hours. Under each phase write the main fuel and enzyme, then mark where McArdle disease and CPT II deficiency each break down.","l":["Phosphocreatine and creatine kinase cover the first seconds","Anaerobic glycolysis of glycogen to lactate covers about 10 seconds to 2 minutes","Oxidative metabolism of glucose, then fatty acids, covers longer effort","Cori cycle returns lactate to the liver for gluconeogenesis","McArdle fails early: no glycogen breakdown, no lactate rise, second wind later","CPT II fails late: no long-chain fatty acid entry into mitochondria","Both can cause rhabdomyolysis and myoglobinuria"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Sports Physiology"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Glycogen Metabolism"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fatty Acid, Triacylglycerol, and Ketone Body Metabolism"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Endocrinology | Parathyroid Gland | Calcitonin","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y64aXKCkHk4"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Endocrinology - Calcium and Phosphate Regulation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EEM0iRJNhU8"}],"related":["MS-7","MS-13-A","MS-13-A1","MS-13-A2","MS-13-A3","MS-13-E2"]},"MS-9":{"id":"MS-9","summary":"The genes that build the skeleton and muscle, and what happens when they fail: transcription factor defects, collagen and dystrophin mutations, repeat expansions, mitochondrial inheritance, and the tumor suppressor and fusion gene changes behind bone sarcomas. Exam questions ask you to predict severity from the type of mutation and to name the inheritance pattern from a pedigree or family history.","notes":[{"h":"Skeletal development genes","points":["RUNX2 is the master switch for osteoblast differentiation","RUNX2 haploinsufficiency (autosomal dominant) causes cleidocranial dysplasia: absent or small clavicles, open fontanelles, delayed suture closure, extra teeth","SOX9 drives chondrocyte differentiation from mesenchyme","HOX genes give each body segment its identity along the head to tail axis; mutations put structures in the wrong place (for example HOXD13 synpolydactyly)"]},{"h":"Achondroplasia and osteogenesis imperfecta","points":["Achondroplasia: FGFR3 gain-of-function mutation keeps the receptor active and slows chondrocyte proliferation in the growth plate","Endochondral bone is short (limbs) while membranous bone is normal, so the head is large and the trunk near normal","Most achondroplasia cases are new autosomal dominant mutations linked to advanced paternal age","Type I collagen is two alpha-1 chains (COL1A1) and one alpha-2 chain (COL1A2), with glycine at every third residue","Glycine substitution: the bad chain enters many triple helices (3 of 4 when COL1A1 is hit) and spoils them (dominant negative), giving severe OI types II to IV","Null allele: no bad chain is made, only half the normal amount of collagen, giving mild OI type I (blue sclerae, fractures, hearing loss)"]},{"h":"Dystrophin and the reading frame","points":["DMD gene (Xp21) is the largest human gene, a big target, so about one third of cases are new mutations","Frameshift deletion leads to an early stop codon and no dystrophin: Duchenne, onset by about age 5, Gowers sign, calf pseudohypertrophy","In-frame deletion leaves a shorter, partly working dystrophin: Becker, later onset, slower course","Severity follows the reading frame, regardless of deletion size","Dystrophin anchors actin to the dystroglycan complex and extracellular matrix; without it the sarcolemma tears with contraction"]},{"h":"Repeat expansion and mitochondrial disease","points":["Myotonic dystrophy type 1: autosomal dominant CTG expansion in the DMPK gene, toxic RNA disrupts splicing","Anticipation: the repeat grows each generation; maternal transmission can cause a severe congenital form","Features: myotonia (slow grip release), distal weakness, cataracts, frontal balding, testicular atrophy, cardiac conduction block","Mitochondrial DNA comes only from the mother: all children of an affected mother can be affected, none from an affected father","Heteroplasmy: cells carry a mix of normal and mutant mtDNA, so severity varies widely within a family","Ragged red fibers on modified Gomori trichrome are clumps of abnormal mitochondria under the sarcolemma (MERRF, MELAS)"]},{"h":"Sarcoma genetics","points":["RB1 (13q14) controls the G1 to S checkpoint; a germline RB1 mutation (hereditary retinoblastoma) raises osteosarcoma risk","Germline TP53 loss (Li-Fraumeni syndrome) causes sarcomas, breast cancer, brain tumors, leukemia, and adrenocortical carcinoma at young ages","Ewing sarcoma: t(11;22) fuses EWSR1 to FLI1, making an abnormal transcription factor"]}],"charts":[{"title":"Musculoskeletal genetic disorders","cols":["Disorder","Gene","Inheritance","Mechanism","Key features"],"rows":[["Cleidocranial dysplasia","RUNX2","Autosomal dominant","Haploinsufficiency","Absent clavicles, open fontanelles, extra teeth"],["Achondroplasia","FGFR3","Autosomal dominant, mostly new mutations","Gain of function","Short limbs, large head, normal intelligence"],["Osteogenesis imperfecta type I","COL1A1 null allele","Autosomal dominant","Half the normal collagen (quantitative)","Blue sclerae, fractures, hearing loss"],["Osteogenesis imperfecta types II to IV","COL1A1 or COL1A2 glycine substitution","Mostly autosomal dominant","Dominant negative (qualitative)","Severe fractures and deformity, perinatal death in type II"],["Duchenne muscular dystrophy","DMD","X-linked recessive","Frameshift, no dystrophin","Gowers sign, pseudohypertrophy, dilated cardiomyopathy"],["Becker muscular dystrophy","DMD","X-linked recessive","In-frame, shortened dystrophin","Later onset, milder weakness"],["Myotonic dystrophy type 1","DMPK (CTG repeat)","Autosomal dominant with anticipation","Toxic RNA gain of function","Myotonia, cataracts, balding, testicular atrophy, conduction block"],["MERRF and MELAS","Mitochondrial tRNA genes","Maternal","Heteroplasmy, failed oxidative phosphorylation","Ragged red fibers, lactic acidosis, seizures or stroke-like episodes"],["Spinal muscular atrophy","SMN1 deletion","Autosomal recessive","Anterior horn cell loss","Floppy infant, tongue fasciculations"]]},{"title":"Bone and soft tissue sarcoma genetics","cols":["Tumor or syndrome","Genetic change","Normal role of the gene","Clinical clue"],"rows":[["Osteosarcoma after hereditary retinoblastoma","Germline RB1 loss plus second hit","Holds cells at the G1 to S checkpoint","Retinoblastoma in infancy, bone tumor in adolescence"],["Li-Fraumeni syndrome","Germline TP53 loss","Arrests the cell cycle or triggers apoptosis after DNA damage","Several early cancers in one family"],["Ewing sarcoma","t(11;22), EWSR1-FLI1 fusion","Fusion protein acts as an abnormal transcription factor","Boy with diaphyseal tumor, onion-skin periosteal reaction"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"My Tonia, My Testicles, My Toupee, My Cataracts","text":"Myotonic dystrophy: myotonia, testicular atrophy, frontal balding, cataracts. Add the heart: conduction block."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"11 + 22 = 33, Patrick Ewing's jersey number","text":"Ewing sarcoma carries t(11;22), the EWSR1-FLI1 fusion."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Count in threes","text":"Codons are read in threes. A deletion that is a multiple of 3 keeps the frame (Becker). Any other size shifts the frame and ends the protein early (Duchenne)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Three out of four","text":"Each collagen I molecule has two alpha-1 chains. If half the alpha-1 chains are faulty, only 1 in 4 molecules is fully normal, so a bad chain hurts more than a missing one."}],"highYield":["RUNX2 loss: cleidocranial dysplasia with absent clavicles","FGFR3 gain of function: achondroplasia, new mutations with advanced paternal age","OI: a bad collagen chain (dominant negative) is worse than no chain (null allele)","Duchenne versus Becker depends on the reading frame; deletion size does not decide it","Myotonic dystrophy: CTG repeat in DMPK with anticipation","Mitochondrial disease: maternal inheritance, heteroplasmy, ragged red fibers","Germline RB1 mutation raises osteosarcoma risk","Ewing sarcoma: EWSR1-FLI1 fusion from t(11;22)"],"pitfalls":["A large in-frame deletion causes Becker while a single-base deletion can cause Duchenne; judge by the frame.","Mild OI type I comes from too little normal collagen; the severe types come from abnormal collagen that poisons the helix.","Mitochondrial disorders skip transmission through fathers; an affected man's children are unaffected.","Achondroplasia spares membranous bone; the skull vault and face grow, so the head looks large."],"dumps":[{"t":"Collagen helix and OI","p":"Draw a type I collagen triple helix with two alpha-1 chains and one alpha-2 chain. Then draw what happens with a COL1A1 null allele and with a COL1A1 glycine substitution, and label which gives mild and which gives severe OI.","l":["Two alpha-1 chains (COL1A1) and one alpha-2 chain (COL1A2)","Glycine every third residue fits the tight center of the helix","Null allele: half as much normal collagen (quantitative defect)","Null allele gives mild type I OI with blue sclerae and hearing loss","Glycine substitution chain enters and distorts most helices","Dominant negative effect leaves only about 1 in 4 molecules normal","Glycine substitutions cause severe types II to IV"]},{"t":"Inheritance pattern table","p":"Make a table with columns for inheritance pattern, how it passes in a pedigree, and one musculoskeletal example. Fill rows for autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, X-linked recessive, repeat expansion with anticipation, and mitochondrial.","l":["Autosomal dominant: every generation, both sexes (achondroplasia, cleidocranial dysplasia)","Autosomal recessive: carrier parents, siblings at 1 in 4 risk (SMA, McArdle)","X-linked recessive: affected males, carrier mothers, no father to son spread (Duchenne, Becker)","Anticipation: earlier, more severe disease in later generations (myotonic dystrophy)","Mitochondrial: from mother to all children, never through fathers (MERRF, MELAS)","Heteroplasmy explains variable severity in mitochondrial disease","New mutations are common in achondroplasia and Duchenne"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic Disorders"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous Proteins"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Muscular Dystrophy - Duchenne, Becker and Mytonic","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o1uhhpjmzkw"}],"related":["MS-1","MS-6","MS-13-D","MS-13-D2","MS-13-D3","MS-13-F2","MS-13-F4"]},"MS-10":{"id":"MS-10","summary":"How nerves drive muscle: transmission at the neuromuscular junction, the stretch and tendon reflexes, and how electrolytes change excitability. Exam questions ask you to place a weakness at the nerve, the junction, or the muscle, and to separate myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton syndrome, and botulism by their mechanism.","notes":[{"h":"Neuromuscular transmission","points":["Action potential reaches the terminal and opens presynaptic voltage-gated (P/Q-type) calcium channels","Calcium entry triggers SNARE proteins (synaptobrevin, syntaxin, SNAP-25) to fuse vesicles and release acetylcholine","Acetylcholine binds postsynaptic nicotinic receptors, ligand-gated cation channels, producing the end plate potential","The end plate potential normally exceeds threshold by a wide safety margin and fires a muscle action potential","Acetylcholinesterase in the cleft splits acetylcholine into acetate and choline, ending the signal"]},{"h":"Junction disorders","points":["Botulism: toxin cleaves SNARE proteins, so no acetylcholine release; descending flaccid paralysis with dilated pupils","Lambert-Eaton syndrome: antibodies against presynaptic calcium channels reduce release; often paraneoplastic with small cell lung cancer","Lambert-Eaton: proximal limb weakness that improves with repeated effort, low reflexes, dry mouth and other autonomic signs","Myasthenia gravis: antibodies against postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors block them and cause complement damage","Myasthenia gravis: weakness worsens with use and late in the day, ptosis and diplopia, normal reflexes and pupils, thymus abnormalities"]},{"h":"Spindles, tendon organs, and reflexes","points":["Muscle spindles lie in parallel with muscle fibers and sense length and rate of stretch","Ia afferents from spindles excite alpha motor neurons to the same muscle (monosynaptic stretch reflex) and inhibit the antagonist","Gamma motor neurons contract the spindle ends to keep it taut and set its sensitivity","Golgi tendon organs lie in series at the muscle-tendon junction and sense tension","Ib afferents act through inhibitory interneurons to relax the same muscle under high tension","Reflex roots: biceps C5 to C6, triceps C7, patellar L4, Achilles S1"]},{"h":"Neurogenic versus myopathic weakness","points":["Neurogenic (lower motor neuron): fasciculations, atrophy, low reflexes, often distal; CK normal or mildly high","Biopsy in denervation: grouped atrophy; after reinnervation, fiber type grouping","Spinal muscular atrophy: SMN1 deletion, anterior horn cell loss, floppy infant with tongue fasciculations","Myopathic: proximal weakness, high CK, reflexes kept until late","Type II fiber atrophy with normal CK points to disuse or corticosteroids"]},{"h":"Electrolytes and excitability","points":["Hypocalcemia lowers the threshold for sodium channel opening, so nerves fire on their own: tetany, perioral tingling, carpopedal spasm","Chvostek sign: tapping the facial nerve in front of the ear twitches the face","Trousseau sign: a blood pressure cuff inflated above systolic for 3 minutes causes carpal spasm","Hypokalemia hyperpolarizes the membrane, so threshold is harder to reach: weakness and cramps","Hyperkalemia depolarizes the membrane and inactivates sodium channels: weakness and cardiac arrhythmias"]}],"charts":[{"title":"Junction disorders side by side","cols":["Feature","Myasthenia gravis","Lambert-Eaton syndrome","Botulism"],"rows":[["Site","Postsynaptic","Presynaptic","Presynaptic"],["Target","Nicotinic acetylcholine receptor (antibody)","Voltage-gated calcium channel (antibody)","SNARE proteins (toxin protease)"],["Repeated effort","Weakness worsens","Strength improves","Not a useful bedside clue"],["Muscles first affected","Eyelids and eye muscles, then bulbar","Proximal legs","Cranial nerves, then descending"],["Reflexes","Normal","Reduced","Normal or reduced"],["Autonomic signs and pupils","Absent; pupils spared","Dry mouth, constipation","Dilated pupils, dry mouth"],["Association","Thymoma or thymic hyperplasia","Small cell lung cancer","Home canned food, wounds, honey in infants"],["Repetitive nerve stimulation","Decrement","Increment at high rates","Increment at high rates"]]},{"title":"Muscle spindle versus Golgi tendon organ","cols":["Feature","Muscle spindle","Golgi tendon organ"],"rows":[["Location","In parallel with extrafusal fibers","In series at the muscle-tendon junction"],["Senses","Length and rate of stretch","Tension"],["Afferent","Ia (and II)","Ib"],["Reflex effect","Contracts the stretched muscle","Relaxes the tensed muscle"],["Efferent tuning","Gamma motor neurons","None"]]},{"title":"Deep tendon reflexes","cols":["Reflex","Main root","Muscle","Nerve"],"rows":[["Biceps","C5 to C6","Biceps brachii","Musculocutaneous"],["Triceps","C7","Triceps brachii","Radial"],["Patellar","L4","Quadriceps femoris","Femoral"],["Achilles","S1","Gastrocnemius and soleus","Tibial"]]},{"title":"Neurogenic versus myopathic weakness","cols":["Feature","Neurogenic","Myopathic"],"rows":[["Pattern","Often distal, asymmetric","Proximal, symmetric"],["Fasciculations","Present","Absent"],["Reflexes","Reduced","Normal until late"],["CK","Normal or mildly high","High"],["Biopsy","Grouped atrophy, fiber type grouping","Necrosis, regeneration, varied fiber size"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"S1, 2 buckle my shoe; L3, 4 kick the door; C5, 6 pick up sticks; C7, 8 lay them straight","text":"Achilles S1 to S2, patellar L3 to L4, biceps C5 to C6, triceps C7 to C8. The main roots to name are S1, L4, C5 to C6, and C7."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Lambert-Eaton gets Easier with effort","text":"Repeated firing lets calcium build up in the terminal, so more acetylcholine is released and strength rises. In myasthenia the receptors are few, so strength falls."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Calcium guards the gate","text":"Calcium on the outside of the membrane stabilizes sodium channels. Take calcium away and the gate opens too easily, giving tetany."}],"highYield":["Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins and blocks acetylcholine release","Lambert-Eaton: anti-calcium channel antibodies, small cell lung cancer, improves with use","Myasthenia gravis: anti-acetylcholine receptor antibodies, ptosis and diplopia, worsens with use","Ia afferents carry spindle stretch; Ib afferents carry tendon organ tension","Grouped atrophy and fiber type grouping mean denervation and reinnervation","Type II fiber atrophy: disuse or corticosteroids","Hypocalcemia: Chvostek and Trousseau signs from increased excitability","Both low and high potassium cause weakness"],"pitfalls":["Ptosis with dilated pupils and descending paralysis is botulism; myasthenia spares the pupils.","Lambert-Eaton has low reflexes that can briefly return after exercise; myasthenia keeps normal reflexes.","Hyperkalemia causes weakness even though it depolarizes the membrane, because inactivated sodium channels cannot fire.","Steroid myopathy shows normal CK and type II atrophy, unlike inflammatory myositis with high CK."],"dumps":[{"t":"Neuromuscular junction","p":"Draw a neuromuscular junction from the motor nerve terminal to the muscle fiber. Label each step of transmission, then mark where botulinum toxin, Lambert-Eaton antibodies, and myasthenia antibodies act.","l":["Action potential opens presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels","Calcium triggers SNARE-mediated vesicle fusion","Acetylcholine binds postsynaptic nicotinic receptors","End plate potential triggers the muscle action potential","Acetylcholinesterase ends the signal","Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins","Lambert-Eaton antibodies block presynaptic calcium channels","Myasthenia antibodies block or destroy acetylcholine receptors"]},{"t":"Stretch reflex arc","p":"Draw the knee jerk reflex arc. Label the muscle spindle, afferent fiber type, spinal level, motor neurons, and the antagonist pathway. Add a Golgi tendon organ and its pathway in a second color.","l":["Tap stretches the quadriceps and its spindles","Ia afferent enters the cord at L4","Monosynaptic excitation of the quadriceps alpha motor neuron","Inhibitory interneuron relaxes the hamstrings","Gamma motor neurons keep the spindle taut","Golgi tendon organ sits in series at the tendon and senses tension","Ib afferent inhibits the same muscle through an interneuron"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Excitation of Skeletal Muscle: Neuromuscular Transmission and Excitation-Contraction Coupling"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Motor Functions of the Spinal Cord; the Cord Reflexes"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Musculoskeletal System | Neuromuscular Junction Pathologies: Part 4","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=31ri-1Nv61M"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Neurology | Spinal Cord: Stretch Reflex | Muscle Spindle","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXV4fRC6nPc"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Neuromuscular Junction | Dr Najeeb | Part 1/2","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RKhr_YXUVLE"}],"related":["MS-5","MS-7","MS-8","MS-11","MS-13-D2"]},"MS-11":{"id":"MS-11","summary":"How the immune system attacks joints, muscle, and connective tissue: autoimmunity in rheumatoid arthritis and the connective tissue diseases, hypersensitivity types, crystal-driven innate inflammation, and arthritis after infection or vaccination. Exam questions match an autoantibody to its disease and a joint picture to its immune mechanism.","notes":[{"h":"Rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis","points":["Genetic risk: HLA-DR4 (shared epitope); environmental risk: smoking","Peptidyl arginine deiminase converts arginine to citrulline; citrullinated peptides break tolerance","CD4 T cells (Th1, Th17) and B cells drive synovitis; anti-CCP and rheumatoid factor (IgM against the Fc of IgG) appear","TNF, IL-1, and IL-6 inflame the synovium and cause systemic symptoms","Pannus (proliferating synovium with inflammatory cells and granulation tissue) erodes cartilage","RANKL from T cells and synovial fibroblasts activates osteoclasts, giving marginal erosions and periarticular osteopenia"]},{"h":"Rheumatoid arthritis clinical picture","points":["Symmetric MCP, PIP, and wrist synovitis; DIP joints spared","Morning stiffness over 1 hour that eases with use; fatigue and low-grade fever","Ulnar deviation, swan neck (PIP hyperextension, DIP flexion), boutonniere (PIP flexion, DIP hyperextension)","Rheumatoid nodules: central fibrinoid necrosis ringed by palisading histiocytes","Felty syndrome: rheumatoid arthritis, splenomegaly, and neutropenia with recurrent infections"]},{"h":"Other connective tissue diseases","points":["SLE arthritis is nonerosive; hand deformities can be correctable (Jaccoud arthropathy)","Systemic sclerosis: sclerodactyly, calcinosis, flexion contractures, Raynaud phenomenon","Mixed connective tissue disease: overlap of SLE, scleroderma, and myositis features with high anti-U1 RNP"]},{"h":"Hypersensitivity in joints and muscle","points":["Type II (antibody against tissue): rheumatic fever polyarthritis from anti-M protein cross-reaction (molecular mimicry)","Type II: Lambert-Eaton, where antibodies made against tumor calcium channels cross-react with nerve terminals","Type III (immune complex): SLE arthritis and nephritis","Type IV (T cell): polymyositis, with CD8 T cells killing muscle fibers"]},{"h":"Innate inflammation and infection-related arthritis","points":["Gout: macrophages take up urate crystals, NLRP3 inflammasome activates caspase-1, which releases IL-1 beta and recruits neutrophils","Septic arthritis: neutrophil proteases and bacterial enzymes destroy articular cartilage within days","Reactive arthritis: HLA-B27 linked sterile arthritis 1 to 4 weeks after enteric or Chlamydia infection","Parvovirus B19 and rubella infection: symmetric small joint arthritis in adults, mostly women, that mimics rheumatoid arthritis","Rubella vaccination: transient arthralgia or arthritis, most often in adult women"]}],"charts":[{"title":"Autoantibodies in rheumatic disease","cols":["Antibody","Disease","Clue"],"rows":[["Anti-CCP","Rheumatoid arthritis","Most specific for RA; can precede symptoms"],["Rheumatoid factor","Rheumatoid arthritis","IgM against IgG Fc; also seen in Sjogren syndrome and chronic infection"],["ANA","SLE and many others","Sensitive screening test, low specificity"],["Anti-dsDNA","SLE","Tracks disease activity and lupus nephritis"],["Anti-Smith","SLE","Most specific for SLE"],["Anti-Scl-70 (topoisomerase I)","Diffuse systemic sclerosis","Interstitial lung disease risk"],["Anticentromere","Limited systemic sclerosis (CREST)","Pulmonary hypertension risk"],["Anti-U1 RNP","Mixed connective tissue disease","Overlap features, Raynaud, puffy hands"],["Anti-Jo-1","Polymyositis and dermatomyositis","Interstitial lung disease, mechanic's hands"],["Anti-SSA (Ro) and anti-SSB (La)","Sjogren syndrome","Anti-SSA in pregnancy risks neonatal heart block"]]},{"title":"Rheumatoid arthritis versus osteoarthritis","cols":["Feature","Rheumatoid arthritis","Osteoarthritis"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Autoimmune synovitis with pannus","Cartilage wear and repair failure"],["Joints","Symmetric MCP, PIP, wrist; DIP spared","DIP, PIP, hips, knees, first CMC"],["Morning stiffness","Over 1 hour","Under 30 minutes"],["Effect of use","Improves","Worsens"],["Hand findings","Ulnar deviation, swan neck, boutonniere","Heberden (DIP) and Bouchard (PIP) nodes"],["Radiograph","Marginal erosions, periarticular osteopenia, even joint space loss","Osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis, uneven joint space loss"],["Systemic signs","Fatigue, nodules, anemia, high ESR","None"]]},{"title":"Hypersensitivity types in musculoskeletal disease","cols":["Type","Effector","Example"],"rows":[["II","IgG or IgM against tissue antigens","Rheumatic fever arthritis, Lambert-Eaton, myasthenia gravis"],["III","Immune complexes and complement","SLE arthritis, serum sickness arthralgia"],["IV","T cells","Polymyositis (CD8 T cells)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CREST","text":"Limited systemic sclerosis: Calcinosis, Raynaud phenomenon, Esophageal dysmotility, Sclerodactyly, Telangiectasia. Anticentromere antibody."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"HLA-B27: PAIR","text":"Psoriatic arthritis, Ankylosing spondylitis, IBD-associated arthritis, Reactive arthritis."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ACID","text":"Hypersensitivity types I to IV: Anaphylactic, Cytotoxic (antibody), Immune complex, Delayed (T cell)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Swan neck versus boutonniere","text":"Look at the PIP. A swan's neck arches up (PIP hyperextended). A buttonhole lets the PIP poke through bent (PIP flexed)."}],"highYield":["Anti-CCP is the most specific test for rheumatoid arthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis spares the DIP; osteoarthritis and psoriatic arthritis involve it","RANKL-driven osteoclasts cause RA bone erosions","Felty syndrome: RA, splenomegaly, neutropenia","Anti-U1 RNP: mixed connective tissue disease","Gout inflammation runs through the NLRP3 inflammasome and IL-1 beta","Polymyositis is type IV (CD8 T cell) injury","Parvovirus B19 in adults can mimic early rheumatoid arthritis"],"pitfalls":["Rheumatoid factor alone is not specific for RA; anti-CCP is the better confirmatory antibody.","SLE arthritis does not erode bone; erosions point toward RA.","Lambert-Eaton is type II hypersensitivity even though it starts from a tumor, because antibodies attack a tissue antigen.","Reactive arthritis follows infection by weeks and the joint fluid is sterile; septic arthritis has organisms in the joint."],"dumps":[{"t":"RA joint drawing","p":"Draw a normal synovial joint and an RA joint side by side. Label the cells, cytokines, and structures that lead from citrullinated peptides to bone erosion.","l":["HLA-DR4 presents citrullinated peptides","CD4 T cells activate B cells and macrophages","Anti-CCP and rheumatoid factor are produced","TNF, IL-1, and IL-6 drive synovitis","Pannus grows over and erodes cartilage","RANKL activates osteoclasts at the joint margins","Periarticular osteopenia and marginal erosions on radiograph"]},{"t":"Autoantibody table","p":"From memory, make a two-column table matching each autoantibody to its disease: anti-CCP, anti-dsDNA, anti-Smith, anti-Scl-70, anticentromere, anti-U1 RNP, anti-Jo-1, anti-SSA, anti-SSB. Add one clinical clue for each.","l":["Anti-CCP: rheumatoid arthritis","Anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith: SLE","Anti-Scl-70: diffuse systemic sclerosis","Anticentromere: limited systemic sclerosis (CREST)","Anti-U1 RNP: mixed connective tissue disease","Anti-Jo-1: myositis with interstitial lung disease","Anti-SSA and anti-SSB: Sjogren syndrome"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors (section on joints)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Rheumatoid Arthritis Pathophysiology (signs and symptoms)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ld8PhyAHov8"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Hypersensitivity Type III (Immune complex hypersensitivity) - causes,  pathophysiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NKnAXcM5Ly0"}],"related":["MS-10","MS-13-B","MS-13-B4","MS-13-B5","MS-13-B7","MS-13-E1","MS-13-G1"]},"MS-12":{"id":"MS-12","summary":"The organisms that infect bone, joints, fascia, and muscle, and the virulence factors that let them do it. Exam questions give a setting (sickle cell disease, a nail through a sneaker, a deep dirty wound, a tick bite) and ask for the organism, its lab features, or the toxin behind the damage.","notes":[{"h":"Staphylococcus aureus","points":["Gram-positive cocci in clusters; catalase positive, coagulase positive","Protein A binds the Fc of IgG, blocking opsonization and phagocytosis","Surface adhesins bind collagen, fibronectin, and fibrinogen in bone and joint tissue","Biofilm on hardware and prostheses shields bacteria from antibodies and antibiotics","Most common cause of osteomyelitis and septic arthritis; in children it seeds the metaphysis of long bones"]},{"h":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae","points":["Gram-negative diplococci, often inside neutrophils; ferments glucose only","Pili attach to mucosa and change antigens, so immunity does not last and no vaccine exists","IgA protease cleaves mucosal IgA","Disseminated infection in sexually active young adults: tenosynovitis, pustular skin lesions, migratory polyarthralgia","Or a purulent monoarthritis, often the knee; terminal complement (C5 to C9) deficiency raises risk"]},{"h":"Clostridium perfringens and Streptococcus pyogenes","points":["C. perfringens: gram-positive, anaerobic, spore-forming rod in deep, dirty, low-oxygen wounds","Alpha toxin is a phospholipase C (lecithinase) that destroys cell membranes, causing myonecrosis and hemolysis","Gas from bacterial fermentation produces crepitus in gas gangrene","S. pyogenes: beta-hemolytic gram-positive cocci in chains, catalase negative, bacitracin sensitive","M protein resists phagocytosis and complement; hyaluronidase and streptokinase help spread along fascial planes","Pyrogenic exotoxins act as superantigens, linking MHC II to T cell receptors outside the peptide groove and causing toxic shock"]},{"h":"Borrelia burgdorferi","points":["Spirochete carried by Ixodes ticks in the northeastern and upper midwestern United States","Larvae pick up Borrelia from white-footed mice (the reservoir); nymphs transmit it to people","Transmission usually needs about 36 hours or more of tick attachment","Deer feed adult ticks and keep the tick population going but are not the bacterial reservoir","Late Lyme disease: intermittent or persistent arthritis of large joints, most often the knee"]},{"h":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis in the spine","points":["Spreads through blood from the lung to vertebral bodies, most often lower thoracic and upper lumbar (Pott disease)","Caseating granulomas destroy the anterior vertebral body, spread to the disc and next vertebra, and collapse them","Collapse makes an angular kyphosis (gibbus deformity)","Pus can track along the psoas sheath to form a psoas abscess with groin or hip pain"]}],"charts":[{"title":"Bone and joint infection by setting","cols":["Setting","Classic organism","Clue"],"rows":[["Most cases at any age","Staphylococcus aureus","Hematogenous spread to metaphysis in children"],["Sickle cell disease","Salmonella","Bone infarcts and functional asplenia"],["Injection drug use","Pseudomonas aeruginosa (also S. aureus)","Vertebrae or sternoclavicular joint"],["Nail puncture through a sneaker sole","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Foam in the shoe holds the organism"],["Cat or dog bite","Pasteurella multocida","Cellulitis within a day of the bite"],["Prosthetic joint or hardware","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Biofilm on foreign material"],["Vertebrae with slow course","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Pott disease, gibbus, psoas abscess"],["Sexually active young adult with septic joint","Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Tenosynovitis and pustules"]]},{"title":"Organisms and virulence factors","cols":["Organism","Morphology and tests","Key virulence factors","Musculoskeletal disease"],"rows":[["Staphylococcus aureus","Gram-positive cocci in clusters; catalase and coagulase positive","Protein A, collagen and fibronectin adhesins, biofilm","Osteomyelitis, septic arthritis, septic bursitis"],["Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Gram-negative diplococci; oxidase positive, glucose fermenter","Pili with antigenic variation, IgA protease","Disseminated gonococcal infection"],["Clostridium perfringens","Gram-positive anaerobic spore-forming rod","Alpha toxin (phospholipase C, lecithinase)","Gas gangrene (myonecrosis)"],["Streptococcus pyogenes","Gram-positive cocci in chains; beta-hemolytic, bacitracin sensitive","M protein, hyaluronidase, streptokinase, pyrogenic exotoxins","Necrotizing fasciitis, toxic shock"],["Borrelia burgdorferi","Spirochete","Outer surface protein switching","Lyme arthritis of the knee"],["Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Acid-fast rod","Cord factor, mycolic acid cell wall","Pott disease of the spine"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"FAKE a Key Lyme pie","text":"Lyme disease: Facial nerve palsy, Arthritis, Kardiac block, Erythema migrans."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Mice hold it, deer feed it","text":"White-footed mice are the Borrelia reservoir for tick larvae. Deer feed adult ticks. Nymphs bite people."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Lecithinase eats lecithin","text":"Cell membranes are rich in lecithin (phosphatidylcholine). An enzyme that cuts it destroys muscle and red cell membranes, the core of gas gangrene."}],"highYield":["S. aureus is the most common cause of osteomyelitis and septic arthritis","Protein A binds IgG Fc and blocks opsonization","Salmonella osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease","Pseudomonas after a puncture through a sneaker sole","Pasteurella multocida after cat or dog bites","C. perfringens alpha toxin is a lecithinase (phospholipase C)","Streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins are superantigens","Pott disease: vertebral collapse, gibbus deformity, psoas abscess"],"pitfalls":["Salmonella is the classic association in sickle cell disease, but S. aureus still causes many of those infections.","Gonococcal joint fluid cultures are often negative; culture or test the urethra, cervix, pharynx, and rectum too.","Deer are the host for adult ticks; mice are the Borrelia reservoir.","Gas gangrene toxin destroys membranes; streptococcal superantigens act by overactivating T cells."],"dumps":[{"t":"Lyme transmission cycle","p":"Draw the two-year Ixodes tick life cycle. Label each stage, the animal it feeds on, when it picks up or passes on Borrelia, and the stage of Lyme disease that causes knee arthritis.","l":["Eggs hatch into uninfected larvae","Larvae feed on white-footed mice and acquire Borrelia","Larvae molt into nymphs that keep the infection","Nymphs bite humans and transmit Borrelia","Transmission usually needs about 36 hours or more of attachment","Adults feed on deer","Late disease months later causes large joint arthritis, often the knee"]},{"t":"Setting to organism table","p":"Make a table listing each setting for bone or joint infection and the organism it points to. Add the Gram stain or morphology for each organism.","l":["S. aureus: gram-positive cocci in clusters, most common overall","Salmonella: gram-negative rod, sickle cell disease","Pseudomonas aeruginosa: gram-negative rod, sneaker puncture or injection drug use","Pasteurella multocida: gram-negative coccobacillus, animal bites","S. epidermidis: gram-positive cocci, coagulase negative, prosthetic material","M. tuberculosis: acid-fast rod, vertebrae","N. gonorrhoeae: gram-negative diplococci, sexually active young adults"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"The Staphylococci"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"The Neisseriae"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Mycobacteria"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors (section on osteomyelitis)"}],"videos":[],"related":["MS-11","MS-13-G","MS-13-G1","MS-13-G2","MS-13-G3","MS-13-G4"]},"MS-13-A":{"id":"MS-13-A","summary":"Rickets, osteomalacia, and scurvy: two problems of mineralizing bone and one of building collagen. Exam questions give a child's skeletal findings or an adult's bone pain with lab values and ask for the deficiency, its mechanism, or the risk factor behind it.","notes":[{"h":"Rickets and osteomalacia: one mechanism","points":["Osteoid is made normally but does not mineralize, leaving wide seams of soft osteoid","Cause is usually vitamin D deficiency; phosphate loss (renal wasting) does the same","Before the growth plates close it is rickets (growth plate and bone); after they close it is osteomalacia (bone only)","Low vitamin D lowers gut calcium uptake, PTH rises, PTH wastes phosphate, and low phosphate stalls mineralization"]},{"h":"Risk factors for vitamin D deficiency","points":["Limited sun exposure, high latitude, covering clothing, and darker skin (melanin blocks UVB)","Exclusive breastfeeding without vitamin D supplements (breast milk is low in vitamin D)","Fat malabsorption: celiac disease, Crohn disease, cystic fibrosis, cholestasis, bariatric surgery","Liver disease (less 25-hydroxylation) and chronic kidney disease (less 1-alpha-hydroxylation)","Anticonvulsants such as phenytoin, phenobarbital, and carbamazepine speed vitamin D breakdown"]},{"h":"Osteomalacia","points":["Diffuse bone pain and tenderness, proximal muscle weakness, waddling gait, fractures","Looser zones (pseudofractures): thin lucent bands across the cortex at the femoral neck, pubic rami, ribs, and scapula","Labs: calcium low or normal, phosphate low, alkaline phosphatase high, PTH high, 25-hydroxyvitamin D low"]},{"h":"Rickets","points":["Craniotabes (soft skull), frontal bossing, delayed fontanelle closure","Rachitic rosary: swelling at the costochondral junctions","Bowed legs (genu varum) once walking, widened wrists, delayed teeth","Radiograph: widened, cupped, frayed metaphyses with a wide growth plate","X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets: excess FGF23 wastes phosphate; vitamin D level normal, calcitriol inappropriately low"]},{"h":"Scurvy","points":["Vitamin C is the cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase in collagen synthesis","Underhydroxylated chains cannot form stable triple helices and are degraded, leaving weak vessels and scars","Perifollicular hemorrhage, corkscrew hairs, swollen bleeding gums, easy bruising, hemarthrosis, poor wound healing","Children: painful subperiosteal hemorrhage, refusal to walk, and leg tenderness","Risks: diets without fruit and vegetables, alcohol use disorder, older adults living alone, very restricted diets"]}],"charts":[{"title":"Nutritional bone and collagen disorders","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Osteomalacia","Vitamin D deficiency after growth plate closure; renal phosphate loss","Failed mineralization of osteoid","Bone pain, proximal weakness, Looser zones","Fractures, falls, hypocalcemia"],["Rickets","Vitamin D deficiency in a growing child; X-linked hypophosphatemia","Failed mineralization of osteoid and growth plate cartilage","Rachitic rosary, craniotabes, frontal bossing, genu varum, cupped metaphyses","Short stature, deformity, hypocalcemic seizures"],["Scurvy","Vitamin C deficiency from poor diet or alcohol use disorder","Failed prolyl and lysyl hydroxylation of collagen","Perifollicular hemorrhage, corkscrew hairs, bleeding gums, poor healing","Hemarthrosis, subperiosteal hemorrhage, anemia"]]},{"title":"Lab patterns in mineralization disorders","cols":["Disorder","Calcium","Phosphate","Alkaline phosphatase","PTH","25-hydroxyvitamin D"],"rows":[["Vitamin D deficiency (rickets or osteomalacia)","Low or normal","Low","High","High","Low"],["X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets","Normal","Low","High","Normal or mildly high","Normal"],["Chronic kidney disease","Low","High","Normal or high","High","Variable"],["Osteoporosis (for contrast)","Normal","Normal","Normal","Normal","Normal"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Same disease, different age","text":"If the growth plates are open, failed mineralization is rickets. If they are closed, it is osteomalacia."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Follow the PTH","text":"Low vitamin D, low calcium absorption, high PTH, phosphate dumped in urine. That chain explains why phosphate is low and alkaline phosphatase is high."},{"kind":"trick","label":"C makes the collagen hold","text":"Vitamin C adds the hydroxyl groups that let collagen chains bond into a firm triple helix. Without it, vessels leak and wounds reopen."}],"highYield":["Rickets and osteomalacia: defective mineralization with excess osteoid","Osteomalacia labs: low phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase, high PTH, low 25-hydroxyvitamin D","Looser zones are pseudofractures of osteomalacia","Rachitic rosary and craniotabes are signs of rickets","X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets: FGF23 excess with normal 25-hydroxyvitamin D","Anticonvulsants, fat malabsorption, and darker skin raise vitamin D deficiency risk","Scurvy: failed prolyl and lysyl hydroxylation of collagen","Perifollicular hemorrhage with corkscrew hairs points to scurvy"],"pitfalls":["Osteoporosis has normal labs and normally mineralized bone; osteomalacia has abnormal labs and soft, undermineralized bone.","Calcium can be normal in vitamin D deficiency because high PTH pulls calcium from bone.","X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets does not respond to ordinary vitamin D because the problem is phosphate wasting.","Scurvy bleeding comes from weak vessel walls; platelet counts and clotting tests are usually normal."],"dumps":[{"t":"Vitamin D deficiency cascade","p":"Draw a flowchart from low vitamin D to soft bone. At each step write which lab rises or falls, then add the bedside findings in a child and in an adult.","l":["Low vitamin D lowers gut calcium and phosphate absorption","Low calcium raises PTH (secondary hyperparathyroidism)","PTH keeps calcium low-normal by resorbing bone","PTH wastes phosphate in urine, so phosphate falls","Alkaline phosphatase rises with osteoblast activity","Osteoid stays unmineralized","Child: rachitic rosary, craniotabes, genu varum, cupped metaphyses","Adult: bone pain, proximal weakness, Looser zones"]},{"t":"Collagen synthesis and scurvy","p":"Draw collagen synthesis from the ribosome to the fibril. Mark the step that needs vitamin C and list the scurvy findings that follow when it fails.","l":["Pro-alpha chains made on rough ER","Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase need vitamin C","Hydroxyproline stabilizes the triple helix","Hydroxylysine allows glycosylation and later cross-links","Unstable helices are degraded, so collagen is weak","Fragile vessels: perifollicular hemorrhage, bleeding gums, hemarthrosis","Poor wound healing and subperiosteal hemorrhage in children"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous Proteins"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Calcium Disorders | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_gD5XWvONik"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Endocrinology - Calcium and Phosphate Regulation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EEM0iRJNhU8"}],"related":["MS-6","MS-8","MS-13-E","MS-13-E2"]},"MS-13-B":{"id":"MS-13-B","summary":"The inflammatory and overuse disorders of joints, tendons, bursae, and muscle: spondyloarthropathies, crystal arthritis, myositis, polymyalgia rheumatica, fibromyalgia, bursitis, and tendinopathy. Exam questions turn on a few sharp clues, such as crystal shape, which joints are involved, whether CK is high, and which HLA type or infection came first.","notes":[{"h":"Seronegative spondyloarthropathies","points":["Ankylosing spondylitis: HLA-B27, young men, enthesitis and bilateral sacroiliitis","AS back pain is worse in the morning and with rest, better with exercise; chest expansion and lumbar flexion fall","Syndesmophytes bridge vertebrae into a bamboo spine","AS complications: anterior uveitis, aortitis with aortic regurgitation, restrictive lung disease, fractures of the fused spine","Reactive arthritis: HLA-B27, 1 to 4 weeks after Chlamydia trachomatis or Shigella, Salmonella, Yersinia, Campylobacter; joint fluid sterile","Reactive arthritis: asymmetric leg arthritis, conjunctivitis, urethritis, enthesitis, keratoderma blennorrhagicum","Psoriatic arthritis: DIP involvement, dactylitis (sausage digits), nail pitting, pencil-in-cup deformity; axial disease linked to HLA-B27"]},{"h":"Gout and pseudogout","points":["Purine breakdown: hypoxanthine to xanthine to uric acid, both steps by xanthine oxidase","Underexcretion causes most gout: kidney disease, thiazide and loop diuretics, alcohol, low-dose aspirin","Overproduction: Lesch-Nyhan (HGPRT deficiency), tumor lysis, glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency","Acute gout: hot, red first MTP (podagra) after alcohol or a purine-rich meal","Chronic gout: tophi, punched-out erosions with overhanging edges, uric acid kidney stones","Pseudogout: calcium pyrophosphate crystals in the knee or wrist, chondrocalcinosis; linked to hyperparathyroidism and hemochromatosis"]},{"h":"Myositis and polymyalgia rheumatica","points":["Dermatomyositis: complement-mediated capillary injury, perifascicular atrophy, heliotrope rash, Gottron papules; screen for internal cancer","Polymyositis: endomysial CD8 T cells invading muscle fibers, no rash","Both: symmetric proximal weakness, high CK, myofiber necrosis; anti-Jo-1 marks interstitial lung disease risk","Polymyalgia rheumatica: adults over 50 with shoulder and hip girdle pain and stiffness, high ESR, normal CK and strength","PMR travels with giant cell arteritis; new headache, jaw claudication, or visual change signals risk of vision loss","Steroid myopathy: proximal weakness with normal CK and type II fiber atrophy without inflammation"]},{"h":"Bursitis and tendinopathy","points":["Prepatellar bursitis from kneeling; olecranon bursitis from leaning or trauma; subacromial and trochanteric from overuse","Septic bursitis (most often Staphylococcus aureus) follows a skin break, most often prepatellar or olecranon","Tendinopathy (tendinosis): overuse causes collagen disorganization and neovascularization with few inflammatory cells","Lateral epicondylitis: extensor carpi radialis brevis origin; medial epicondylitis: common flexor-pronator origin","Supraspinatus impingement: painful arc from about 60 to 120 degrees of abduction","De Quervain tenosynovitis: abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis, positive Finkelstein test","Plantar fasciitis: heel pain with first morning steps; fluoroquinolones raise Achilles tendinopathy and rupture risk"]},{"h":"Fibromyalgia, JIA, and other soft tissue","points":["Fibromyalgia: central pain sensitization; widespread pain for at least 3 months, fatigue, unrefreshing sleep, mostly women","Fibromyalgia labs are normal: ESR, CRP, and CK","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis: arthritis before age 16 lasting at least 6 weeks","Oligoarticular JIA: young girls, ANA positive, silent anterior uveitis; systemic JIA: daily spiking fever and salmon-colored rash","Adhesive capsulitis: capsular inflammation and fibrosis, loss of both active and passive shoulder motion, linked to diabetes","Ganglion cyst: myxoid degeneration near a joint capsule or tendon sheath, often dorsal wrist, no true lining"]}],"charts":[{"title":"Inflammatory musculoskeletal conditions","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Ankylosing spondylitis","HLA-B27, young men","Enthesitis and sacroiliitis with new bone formation","Inflammatory back pain better with exercise, low chest expansion, bamboo spine","Anterior uveitis, aortic regurgitation, restrictive lung disease, spinal fracture"],["Bursitis","Kneeling, leaning on elbow, overuse, skin break","Inflammation of a synovial-lined bursa; S. aureus if septic","Local swelling and tenderness over the bursa","Septic bursitis, chronic bursitis"],["Fibromyalgia","Women, sleep disturbance, depression, anxiety","Central pain sensitization","Widespread pain at least 3 months, fatigue, unrefreshing sleep, normal labs","Chronic pain and disability without tissue damage"],["Gout","Men, alcohol, purine-rich food, diuretics, kidney disease","Urate crystals activate NLRP3 inflammasome and IL-1 beta","Podagra, needle-shaped negatively birefringent crystals, tophi","Erosions, uric acid stones, urate nephropathy"],["Myositis (dermatomyositis, polymyositis)","Adults; dermatomyositis also in children and with internal cancer","DM: complement injury to capillaries; PM: CD8 T cells","Proximal weakness, high CK; DM rash (heliotrope, Gottron papules)","Interstitial lung disease (anti-Jo-1), dysphagia, myocarditis"],["Polymyalgia rheumatica","Adults over 50, women more often","Inflammation of shoulder and hip girdle bursae and synovium","Girdle pain and stiffness, high ESR, normal CK and strength","Giant cell arteritis with vision loss"],["Reactive arthritis","HLA-B27; Chlamydia or Shigella, Salmonella, Yersinia, Campylobacter","Sterile immune arthritis triggered by bacterial antigens","Arthritis, conjunctivitis, urethritis, enthesitis, keratoderma blennorrhagicum","Chronic or recurrent arthritis, uveitis"],["Tendinopathy","Repetitive overuse, age, fluoroquinolones","Collagen disorganization with little inflammation (tendinosis)","Epicondylitis, painful arc, Finkelstein test, heel pain","Tendon rupture (Achilles), rotator cuff tear"]]},{"title":"Gout versus pseudogout","cols":["Feature","Gout","Pseudogout (CPPD)"],"rows":[["Crystal","Monosodium urate","Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate"],["Shape","Needle-shaped","Rhomboid"],["Birefringence","Strongly negative (yellow when parallel)","Weakly positive (blue when parallel)"],["Typical joint","First MTP, ankle, knee","Knee, wrist"],["Imaging","Punched-out erosions, tophi","Chondrocalcinosis"],["Associations","Alcohol, diuretics, kidney disease, high cell turnover","Older age, hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypomagnesemia"]]},{"title":"Separating the arthritides","cols":["Feature","Ankylosing spondylitis","Reactive arthritis","Psoriatic arthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis"],"rows":[["HLA link","HLA-B27","HLA-B27","HLA-B27 with axial disease","HLA-DR4"],["Joints","Sacroiliac joints and spine","Asymmetric large leg joints","DIP joints, asymmetric; dactylitis","Symmetric MCP, PIP, wrist; DIP spared"],["Distinctive signs","Bamboo spine, low chest expansion","Conjunctivitis, urethritis, keratoderma","Nail pitting, pencil-in-cup","Ulnar deviation, swan neck, nodules"],["RF and anti-CCP","Negative","Negative","Negative","Positive"]]},{"title":"Weakness or girdle pain: telling them apart","cols":["Feature","Dermatomyositis","Polymyositis","Polymyalgia rheumatica","Steroid myopathy"],"rows":[["Strength","Proximal weakness","Proximal weakness","Normal (pain limits movement)","Proximal weakness, legs more"],["CK","High","High","Normal","Normal"],["ESR","Normal or high","Normal or high","High","Not raised by the myopathy itself"],["Biopsy","Perifascicular atrophy, perivascular inflammation, capillary loss","Endomysial CD8 T cells, fiber necrosis","Normal muscle","Type II fiber atrophy, no inflammation"],["Skin","Heliotrope rash, Gottron papules","Normal","Normal","Cushingoid features"]]},{"title":"Tendon and soft tissue disorders","cols":["Disorder","Structure","Key finding","Risk"],"rows":[["Lateral epicondylitis","Extensor carpi radialis brevis origin","Pain with resisted wrist extension","Repetitive gripping and wrist extension"],["Medial epicondylitis","Common flexor-pronator origin","Pain with resisted wrist flexion and pronation","Golf, throwing"],["Supraspinatus impingement","Supraspinatus tendon under the acromion","Painful arc about 60 to 120 degrees","Overhead work and sports"],["De Quervain tenosynovitis","Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis","Positive Finkelstein test","Repetitive thumb use, new parents lifting infants"],["Plantar fasciitis","Plantar fascia at the medial calcaneal tubercle","Heel pain worst with first morning steps","Running, prolonged standing, obesity"],["Achilles tendinopathy","Achilles tendon","Pain and thickening above the heel","Running, fluoroquinolones"],["Adhesive capsulitis","Glenohumeral joint capsule","Loss of active and passive motion","Diabetes"],["Ganglion cyst","Joint capsule or tendon sheath","Firm cyst, often dorsal wrist, no true lining","Young adults, women"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Can't see, can't pee, can't climb a tree","text":"Reactive arthritis: conjunctivitis, urethritis, arthritis."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"HLA-B27: PAIR","text":"Psoriatic arthritis, Ankylosing spondylitis, IBD-associated arthritis, Reactive arthritis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"P for Pseudogout","text":"Pseudogout crystals are calcium Pyrophosphate, Positively birefringent, and rhomboid (Parallelogram-like). Gout crystals are the needles."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Weak or just sore?","text":"Weakness with high CK means myositis. Stiffness and pain with normal strength, normal CK, and high ESR means PMR. Pain everywhere with all labs normal means fibromyalgia."}],"highYield":["Ankylosing spondylitis: HLA-B27, sacroiliitis, bamboo spine, uveitis, aortic regurgitation","Gout: needle-shaped, negatively birefringent urate crystals; podagra","Pseudogout: rhomboid, weakly positively birefringent crystals; chondrocalcinosis","Dermatomyositis: perifascicular atrophy and association with internal cancer","Polymyositis: endomysial CD8 T cells","PMR: over 50, high ESR, normal CK; watch for giant cell arteritis","Fluoroquinolones raise Achilles tendon rupture risk","Oligoarticular JIA with positive ANA needs eye screening for silent uveitis"],"pitfalls":["AS back pain improves with exercise; mechanical back pain gets worse with activity.","Gout crystals are negatively birefringent; pseudogout crystals are weakly positive. Shape (needle versus rhomboid) helps settle it.","PMR patients feel weak because of pain, but their strength and CK are normal; myositis has true weakness and high CK.","Tendinopathy is mostly degenerative (tendinosis), so inflammatory cells are scarce on histology.","Psoriatic arthritis can appear before the skin rash; DIP disease with nail pitting points to it over RA."],"dumps":[{"t":"Purine breakdown and gout","p":"Draw the purine degradation pathway from AMP and GMP to uric acid. Mark the enzyme xanthine oxidase, the HGPRT salvage step, and write the causes of overproduction and underexcretion beside the pathway.","l":["AMP to IMP to inosine to hypoxanthine","GMP to guanosine to guanine to xanthine","Xanthine oxidase converts hypoxanthine to xanthine and xanthine to uric acid","HGPRT salvages hypoxanthine and guanine; loss (Lesch-Nyhan) raises uric acid","Overproduction: tumor lysis, glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency","Underexcretion causes most gout: kidney disease, thiazides, alcohol","Urate crystals activate the NLRP3 inflammasome and IL-1 beta"]},{"t":"Weakness and pain comparison","p":"Build a table comparing dermatomyositis, polymyositis, polymyalgia rheumatica, steroid myopathy, and fibromyalgia. Use rows for strength, CK, ESR, biopsy findings, and one extra clue for each.","l":["Dermatomyositis: weakness, high CK, perifascicular atrophy, heliotrope rash and Gottron papules","Polymyositis: weakness, high CK, endomysial CD8 T cells","PMR: normal strength and CK, high ESR, giant cell arteritis link","Steroid myopathy: weakness, normal CK, type II fiber atrophy","Fibromyalgia: normal strength and labs, widespread pain, poor sleep","Anti-Jo-1 points to interstitial lung disease in myositis","Dermatomyositis calls for a search for internal cancer"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors (section on joints)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles (section on inflammatory myopathies)"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Nucleotide Metabolism"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb (sections on the rotator cuff and elbow region)"}],"videos":[],"related":["MS-4","MS-5","MS-11","MS-12","MS-13-E1","MS-13-G1"]},"MS-13-C":{"id":"MS-13-C","summary":"Metabolic bone disease covers conditions where bone building or bone breakdown goes wrong at the cell or hormone level. This unit centers on osteopetrosis (osteoclasts cannot resorb bone) and renal osteodystrophy, and teaches the lab pattern for each major bone disease. Exams love the calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, and PTH table.","notes":[{"h":"Osteopetrosis: the mechanism","points":["Osteoclasts fail to resorb bone, so old bone is never removed and remodeled.","Osteoclasts need an acid pump and a chloride channel to dissolve mineral; loss of either stops resorption.","Causes: carbonic anhydrase II deficiency (no H+ supply), CLCN7 chloride channel mutations, TCIRG1 proton pump subunit mutations.","Carbonic anhydrase II deficiency also causes renal tubular acidosis and cerebral calcification.","Result: thick, dense, marble-like bone that is brittle and fractures easily."]},{"h":"Osteopetrosis: forms and complications","points":["Infantile autosomal recessive form: severe, presents in infancy, often fatal in childhood without treatment.","Adult autosomal dominant form (Albers-Schonberg disease): milder, often found by fractures or on x-ray.","Bone fills the marrow space, causing anemia, thrombocytopenia, and infections (pancytopenia).","Extramedullary hematopoiesis enlarges the liver and spleen.","Narrowed skull foramina compress cranial nerves II, VII, and VIII: vision loss, facial palsy, hearing loss.","Osteomyelitis of the jaw is common because dense bone has poor blood supply.","X-ray: diffusely dense bones, bone-in-bone, Erlenmeyer flask flaring of the distal femur.","Osteoclasts come from the monocyte-macrophage line, so stem cell transplant can cure the infantile form."]},{"h":"Reading bone labs","points":["Osteoporosis: calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, and PTH all normal.","Paget disease: only alkaline phosphatase is high.","Osteomalacia and rickets (vitamin D deficiency): low calcium and phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase and PTH.","Primary hyperparathyroidism: high calcium and PTH, low phosphate.","Renal osteodystrophy: low calcium, high phosphate, high PTH, high alkaline phosphatase.","Alkaline phosphatase rises when osteoblasts are busy; it stays normal when bone is only lost."]},{"h":"Renal osteodystrophy","points":["Chronic kidney disease cannot excrete phosphate, so phosphate rises and binds calcium.","Damaged kidneys make less 1-alpha-hydroxylase, so calcitriol falls and gut calcium absorption drops; high FGF23 lowers calcitriol further.","Low calcium, high phosphate, and low calcitriol drive parathyroid hyperplasia (secondary hyperparathyroidism).","High PTH causes osteitis fibrosa cystica: subperiosteal resorption (radial side of the middle phalanges), salt and pepper skull, bone cysts.","Brown tumors are masses of osteoclast-like giant cells, fibrous tissue, and hemorrhage; hemosiderin makes them brown.","Low calcitriol also causes defective mineralization (osteomalacia), and chronic acidosis leaches bone buffer.","Long-standing secondary disease can become autonomous (tertiary hyperparathyroidism) with high calcium."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Lab patterns in bone disease","cols":["Condition","Serum calcium","Serum phosphate","Alkaline phosphatase","PTH"],"rows":[["Osteoporosis","Normal","Normal","Normal","Normal"],["Osteopetrosis","Usually normal (can be low in the infantile form)","Normal","Normal","Usually normal (can rise if calcium is low)"],["Osteomalacia or rickets (vitamin D deficiency)","Low or low-normal","Low","High","High"],["Paget disease of bone","Normal","Normal","High (isolated)","Normal"],["Primary hyperparathyroidism","High","Low","Normal or high","High"],["Renal osteodystrophy (secondary hyperparathyroidism)","Low or normal","High","High","High"]]},{"title":"Osteopetrosis forms","cols":["Feature","Infantile (malignant) form","Adult form"],"rows":[["Inheritance","Autosomal recessive","Autosomal dominant (Albers-Schonberg disease)"],["Example genes","TCIRG1, CLCN7; carbonic anhydrase II in a milder recessive variant with renal tubular acidosis","CLCN7"],["Onset","Infancy","Adolescence or adulthood"],["Marrow","Pancytopenia, hepatosplenomegaly from extramedullary hematopoiesis","Usually normal or mildly affected"],["Cranial nerves","Blindness, deafness, facial palsy common","Less common"],["Course","Often fatal in childhood without stem cell transplant","Fractures, osteoarthritis, jaw osteomyelitis; normal lifespan"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Marble bone","text":"Marble is hard, heavy, and shatters. Osteopetrosis bone is dense, fills the marrow cavity, and breaks easily."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Who uses the ALP?","text":"Alkaline phosphatase tracks osteoblast work. Bone that is only being lost (osteoporosis) has normal ALP; bone being rebuilt or poorly mineralized (Paget, osteomalacia, hyperparathyroid bone) has high ALP."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Kidney failure chain","text":"Phosphate up, calcitriol down, calcium down, PTH up, bone eaten. Trace these five steps in order and the lab pattern writes itself."}],"highYield":["Osteopetrosis is a failure of osteoclast resorption, giving dense but brittle bone.","Carbonic anhydrase II deficiency combines osteopetrosis with renal tubular acidosis.","Marrow obliteration in osteopetrosis causes pancytopenia with hepatosplenomegaly from extramedullary hematopoiesis.","Narrow foramina in osteopetrosis cause blindness and deafness.","Osteoporosis has all-normal bone labs; Paget disease has an isolated high alkaline phosphatase.","Renal osteodystrophy shows high phosphate, low calcium, and high PTH.","Brown tumors and subperiosteal resorption of the phalanges mark osteitis fibrosa cystica from high PTH."],"pitfalls":["Osteopetrosis and osteoporosis sound alike but are opposites: too much bone versus too little bone.","Primary hyperparathyroidism has low phosphate; secondary hyperparathyroidism from kidney failure has high phosphate.","Dense bone on x-ray does not mean strong bone; osteopetrotic bone fractures easily.","A brown tumor is a reactive lesion of hyperparathyroidism and is easy to mistake for a true giant cell tumor of bone."],"dumps":[{"t":"Bone lab table","p":"Build a table from memory with rows for osteoporosis, osteopetrosis, osteomalacia, Paget disease, primary hyperparathyroidism, and renal osteodystrophy. Columns: calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, PTH.","l":["Osteoporosis: all four normal","Osteopetrosis: usually all normal","Osteomalacia: low calcium and phosphate, high ALP and PTH","Paget: isolated high ALP","Primary hyperparathyroidism: high calcium, low phosphate, high PTH","Renal osteodystrophy: low calcium, high phosphate, high ALP and PTH","Explains that ALP reflects osteoblast activity"]},{"t":"CKD to bone","p":"Draw the kidney, gut, parathyroid gland, and bone. Use arrows to trace how chronic kidney disease leads to osteitis fibrosa cystica.","l":["Low GFR causes phosphate retention","Phosphate binds calcium and lowers ionized calcium","Less 1-alpha-hydroxylase means low calcitriol","Low calcitriol cuts gut calcium absorption","Low calcium and high phosphate stimulate PTH secretion and parathyroid hyperplasia","High PTH drives osteoclast resorption: subperiosteal erosions, cysts, brown tumors","Low calcitriol also causes poor mineralization"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"section on chronic kidney disease and its effects on bone"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Calcium Disorders | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_gD5XWvONik"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Parathyroid Disorders | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DQk1LfxcB1U"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ywe5jjiJJJo"}],"related":["MS-8","MS-13-A1","MS-13-E2","MS-13-E3","EN-12-A2","UR-12-A4"]},"MS-13-D":{"id":"MS-13-D","summary":"Congenital musculoskeletal diseases come from single-gene defects in structural proteins: fibrillin (Marfan), dystrophin (muscular dystrophy), collagen (osteogenesis imperfecta, Ehlers-Danlos), and a growth plate receptor (achondroplasia). Exams link each gene to its protein, its inheritance, and its classic physical findings.","notes":[{"h":"Marfan syndrome","points":["Autosomal dominant FBN1 mutation on chromosome 15; fibrillin-1 forms microfibrils that scaffold elastic fibers.","Fibrillin normally holds latent TGF-beta; defective fibrillin releases it, so excess TGF-beta signaling weakens tissue.","Tall and thin, long limbs, arm span greater than height, arachnodactyly, pectus excavatum or carinatum, joint hypermobility, scoliosis.","Lens dislocates upward (superotemporal ectopia lentis).","Aortic root dilation from medial degeneration (cystic medial necrosis) leads to aortic regurgitation and dissection, the main cause of death.","Mitral valve prolapse and spontaneous pneumothorax are also common."]},{"h":"Homocystinuria versus Marfan","points":["Homocystinuria: autosomal recessive, usually cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency, with high homocysteine.","Shares a tall, Marfan-like body build.","Lens dislocates downward (inferonasal); intellectual disability and arterial and venous thrombosis are present.","Marfan has normal intelligence and no clotting tendency."]},{"h":"Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophy","points":["X-linked recessive DMD gene; dystrophin links the actin cytoskeleton to the dystroglycan complex and extracellular matrix.","Duchenne: frameshift deletions or nonsense mutations give absent dystrophin, so the membrane tears with each contraction and fibers die.","Boys under 5: proximal weakness, waddling gait, Gowers sign (uses hands to climb up the legs to stand).","Calf pseudohypertrophy: muscle replaced by fat and fibrous tissue.","CK very high (often 10 to 100 times normal) from birth.","Death in early adulthood from dilated cardiomyopathy or respiratory failure; scoliosis worsens breathing.","Becker: in-frame deletions give a shorter, partly working dystrophin; onset later, slower course, walking into adulthood.","Immunostaining: dystrophin absent in Duchenne, reduced or patchy in Becker; genetic deletion and duplication testing confirms the type."]},{"h":"Osteogenesis imperfecta","points":["Mostly autosomal dominant COL1A1 or COL1A2 mutations: defective or too little type I collagen.","Glycine substitutions break the Gly-X-Y triple helix and act as dominant negatives; type I (mildest) makes too little normal collagen; type II is lethal around birth.","Multiple fractures with minimal trauma, bowed bones, short stature, wormian bones.","Blue sclerae (thin collagen lets the choroid show), conductive hearing loss (abnormal ossicles), dentinogenesis imperfecta (opalescent, fragile teeth), joint laxity."]},{"h":"Osteogenesis imperfecta versus child abuse","points":["Favors abuse: posterior rib fractures, metaphyseal corner fractures, fractures of different ages, bruises in a non-mobile infant, retinal hemorrhage, a story that does not fit.","Favors osteogenesis imperfecta: blue sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta, wormian bones, low bone density, bowing, family history of fractures or hearing loss."]},{"h":"Ehlers-Danlos syndrome and achondroplasia","points":["Ehlers-Danlos: a group of collagen disorders with stretchy skin, hypermobile and dislocating joints, easy bruising, poor wound healing.","Classic type: type V collagen (COL5A1, COL5A2). Vascular type: type III collagen (COL3A1), with arterial, bowel, and uterine rupture.","Achondroplasia: autosomal dominant FGFR3 gain of function that slows growth plate chondrocytes (endochondral bone).","Rhizomelic shortening (upper arms and thighs) with normal trunk length, macrocephaly, frontal bossing, normal intelligence.","Most cases are new mutations linked to older paternal age."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Congenital musculoskeletal diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Gene and protein","Inheritance","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Marfan syndrome","FBN1, fibrillin-1","Autosomal dominant","Weak microfibrils plus excess TGF-beta signaling","Tall, arachnodactyly, arm span over height, pectus, hypermobility, upward lens dislocation","Aortic root dilation, aortic dissection, mitral valve prolapse, pneumothorax"],["Duchenne muscular dystrophy","DMD, dystrophin absent (frameshift or nonsense)","X-linked recessive","Unstable sarcolemma tears during contraction; fibers die and are replaced by fat and fibrous tissue","Onset under age 5, Gowers sign, calf pseudohypertrophy, very high CK","Dilated cardiomyopathy, scoliosis, respiratory failure"],["Becker muscular dystrophy","DMD, truncated dystrophin (in-frame)","X-linked recessive","Partly working dystrophin","Later onset, milder proximal weakness, high CK","Cardiomyopathy, sometimes out of proportion to weakness"],["Osteogenesis imperfecta","COL1A1 or COL1A2, type I collagen","Mostly autosomal dominant","Too little or structurally abnormal type I collagen","Fractures with minimal trauma, blue sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta, joint laxity","Conductive hearing loss, deformity, short stature"],["Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Classic: COL5A1 or COL5A2 (type V). Vascular: COL3A1 (type III)","Mostly autosomal dominant","Faulty collagen in skin, joints, and vessels","Hyperextensible skin, hypermobile joints, easy bruising, wide scars","Joint dislocation; vascular type: arterial, bowel, or uterine rupture"],["Achondroplasia","FGFR3, gain of function","Autosomal dominant (most cases new mutations)","Reduced chondrocyte proliferation in growth plates","Rhizomelic limbs, normal trunk, macrocephaly, frontal bossing, normal intelligence","Foramen magnum and spinal stenosis"]]},{"title":"Lens dislocation","cols":["Condition","Direction","Other clues"],"rows":[["Marfan syndrome","Upward (superotemporal)","Normal intelligence, aortic root disease"],["Homocystinuria","Downward (inferonasal)","Intellectual disability, thrombosis, high homocysteine"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Patients can't BITE","text":"Osteogenesis imperfecta: Bones (multiple fractures), I (eye: blue sclerae), Teeth (dentinogenesis imperfecta), Ear (hearing loss)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Marfan looks up, homocystinuria looks down","text":"Marfan lens goes up and out; homocystinuria lens goes down and in. The patient with clots and intellectual disability is the one looking down."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Reading frame rule","text":"Frameshift deletion scrambles every codon after it, so no dystrophin (Duchenne). In-frame deletion skips a chunk but keeps reading, so a short dystrophin (Becker)."}],"highYield":["Marfan syndrome is an autosomal dominant FBN1 (fibrillin-1) defect with excess TGF-beta signaling.","Aortic root dilation and dissection are the main threats to life in Marfan syndrome.","Upward lens dislocation suggests Marfan; downward lens dislocation suggests homocystinuria.","Duchenne: absent dystrophin, Gowers sign, calf pseudohypertrophy, very high CK, death from cardiomyopathy or respiratory failure.","Becker: truncated dystrophin from an in-frame deletion, later onset, milder course.","Osteogenesis imperfecta is a type I collagen defect with fractures, blue sclerae, hearing loss, and dentinogenesis imperfecta.","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos (type III collagen) causes arterial and bowel rupture.","Achondroplasia is an FGFR3 gain of function with rhizomelic shortening and normal intelligence."],"pitfalls":["Calf pseudohypertrophy is fat and fibrous tissue, so big calves do not mean strong muscle.","Becker is not just mild Duchenne in time; check dystrophin staining (reduced versus absent) and the type of deletion.","Multiple fractures in a child are not automatically abuse; look for blue sclerae, abnormal teeth, and family history before deciding.","Osteogenesis imperfecta is type I collagen (bone); classic Ehlers-Danlos is type V and vascular Ehlers-Danlos is type III.","A Marfan-like build with thrombosis and learning problems points to homocystinuria."],"dumps":[{"t":"Structural protein table","p":"Make a table of Marfan syndrome, Duchenne, Becker, osteogenesis imperfecta, vascular Ehlers-Danlos, and achondroplasia. Columns: gene, protein, inheritance, two classic findings.","l":["Marfan: FBN1, fibrillin-1, autosomal dominant","Duchenne: DMD, absent dystrophin, X-linked recessive","Becker: DMD, truncated dystrophin, X-linked recessive","Osteogenesis imperfecta: COL1A1 or COL1A2, type I collagen, mostly autosomal dominant","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos: COL3A1, type III collagen","Achondroplasia: FGFR3 gain of function, autosomal dominant","Two correct findings listed for each"]},{"t":"Dystrophin and the muscle membrane","p":"Draw a muscle fiber membrane. Label actin, dystrophin, the dystroglycan complex, and the extracellular matrix. Then show what happens in Duchenne.","l":["Dystrophin links actin inside the cell to the dystroglycan complex in the membrane","Dystroglycan links to laminin in the extracellular matrix","Without dystrophin the membrane tears during contraction","Calcium enters and the fiber dies; CK leaks into blood","Dead fibers are replaced by fat and fibrous tissue (pseudohypertrophy)","Heart muscle and diaphragm are affected, causing cardiomyopathy and respiratory failure"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Genetic and Pediatric Diseases, section on Marfan syndrome and Ehlers-Danlos syndromes"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles, section on muscular dystrophies"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors, section on osteogenesis imperfecta and achondroplasia"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous Proteins"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Muscular Dystrophy - Duchenne, Becker and Mytonic","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o1uhhpjmzkw"}],"related":["MS-6","MS-7","MS-9","CV-18-I2","IN-13-E2"]},"MS-13-E":{"id":"MS-13-E","summary":"Degenerative musculoskeletal diseases come from wear, aging, hormone loss, or lost blood supply: osteoarthritis, osteoporosis, Paget disease, and avascular necrosis. This unit also covers pediatric hip and knee disorders, disc herniation, and Dupuytren contracture. Exams test the mechanism, the typical patient, and the findings that separate look-alikes.","notes":[{"h":"Osteoarthritis","points":["Chondrocyte injury and mechanical stress cause loss of proteoglycan and type II collagen.","Cartilage fibrillates and cracks; exposed bone polishes smooth (eburnation).","Subchondral sclerosis and cysts form; osteophytes grow at the joint margins.","Risk: age, obesity, prior joint injury, heavy mechanical load, joint malalignment.","Pain worse with use and better with rest; morning stiffness under 30 minutes.","Asymmetric weight-bearing joints (knees, hips, spine), first CMC, Heberden (DIP) and Bouchard (PIP) nodes; MCPs and wrists usually spared.","X-ray: joint space narrowing, osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis and cysts; synovial fluid noninflammatory."]},{"h":"Osteoporosis","points":["Reduced bone mass with normal mineralization; trabecular bone is hit first.","Low peak bone mass plus age-related loss; estrogen loss raises RANKL and lowers osteoprotegerin, so osteoclasts resorb more.","Secondary causes: glucocorticoids, hyperthyroidism, immobilization, alcohol, smoking.","Fractures: vertebral compression (height loss, kyphosis), hip, distal radius.","Serum calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase normal.","DEXA T-score minus 2.5 or lower is osteoporosis; minus 1 to minus 2.5 is osteopenia."]},{"h":"Paget disease of bone","points":["Overactive, huge osteoclasts start the disease, then osteoblasts lay down bone in a disorganized way.","Phases: lytic, mixed, then sclerotic (burned out); end result is thick but weak mosaic lamellar bone.","Older adults; skull, pelvis, spine, femur, tibia.","Isolated high alkaline phosphatase with normal calcium, phosphate, and PTH.","Complications: bone pain, enlarging skull (hat size), hearing loss, bowed tibia, fractures, high-output heart failure from arteriovenous shunting, osteosarcoma."]},{"h":"Avascular necrosis","points":["Bone infarction when blood supply is cut off; femoral head is the most common site.","Causes: glucocorticoids, alcohol, sickle cell disease, SLE, decompression sickness, femoral neck and scaphoid fractures.","Femoral head depends on retinacular branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery.","MRI is the most sensitive test; early x-rays can be normal."]},{"h":"Pediatric hip and adolescent knee","points":["Developmental dysplasia of the hip: newborn, often girl, breech; Ortolani test reduces a dislocated hip, Barlow test dislocates an unstable one.","Legg-Calve-Perthes: idiopathic femoral head osteonecrosis in young children (often boys about 4 to 8), painless or mildly painful limp.","Slipped capital femoral epiphysis: obese adolescent; the epiphysis slips off the femoral neck; hip or referred knee pain.","Osgood-Schlatter: traction apophysitis of the tibial tubercle from patellar tendon pull in active teens.","Chondromalacia patellae: softened patellar cartilage, anterior knee pain worse with stairs or long sitting.","Osteochondritis dissecans: a fragment of subchondral bone and cartilage separates, causing pain, effusion, or locking."]},{"h":"Spine and hand","points":["Degenerated disc lets the nucleus pulposus herniate posterolaterally, where the posterior longitudinal ligament is thin.","Lumbar herniation compresses the lower root: L4 to L5 disc hits L5 (foot dorsiflexion, dorsum of foot).","Spondylosis is degenerative disc and facet change; spondylolisthesis is forward slip of one vertebra on the one below.","Dupuytren contracture: myofibroblast proliferation in the palmar fascia pulls the ring and little fingers into flexion."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Degenerative bone and joint diseases","cols":["Condition","Risk factors or cause","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Osteoarthritis","Age, obesity, prior injury, mechanical load","Chondrocyte injury, proteoglycan loss, cartilage fibrillation, reactive bone","Use-related pain, stiffness under 30 minutes, Heberden and Bouchard nodes, asymmetric weight-bearing joints","Deformity, loss of function, loose bodies"],["Osteoporosis","Menopause, age, low peak bone mass, glucocorticoids, hyperthyroidism, immobility, alcohol, smoking","Resorption exceeds formation (RANKL up with estrogen loss); mineralization normal","Normal labs, T-score minus 2.5 or lower, height loss, kyphosis","Vertebral compression, hip, and distal radius fractures"],["Paget disease of bone","Older adults; genetic factors (for example SQSTM1)","Osteoclast overactivity then chaotic osteoblast repair; mosaic lamellar bone","Bone pain, enlarging skull, bowed tibia, isolated high ALP","Hearing loss, fractures, high-output heart failure, osteosarcoma"],["Avascular necrosis","Glucocorticoids, alcohol, sickle cell, SLE, decompression sickness, femoral neck or scaphoid fracture","Interrupted blood supply causes bone infarction","Deep groin or joint pain; MRI changes before x-ray","Subchondral collapse, secondary osteoarthritis"]]},{"title":"Pediatric hip disorders","cols":["Disorder","Typical patient","What happens","Key clue"],"rows":[["Developmental dysplasia of the hip","Newborn, girl, breech, family history","Shallow acetabulum lets the femoral head slip out","Positive Ortolani or Barlow test"],["Legg-Calve-Perthes disease","Young child, usually boy, about 4 to 8 years","Idiopathic osteonecrosis of the femoral head","Painless or mild limp, reduced hip motion"],["Slipped capital femoral epiphysis","Obese adolescent","Epiphysis slips off the femoral neck through the growth plate","Hip or knee pain, limp, externally rotated leg"]]},{"title":"Adolescent knee disorders","cols":["Disorder","Site","Mechanism","Clue"],"rows":[["Osgood-Schlatter disease","Tibial tubercle","Traction apophysitis from patellar tendon pull","Tender bump below the kneecap in an active teen"],["Chondromalacia patellae","Patellar articular cartilage","Softening of cartilage","Anterior knee pain with stairs or long sitting"],["Osteochondritis dissecans","Subchondral bone, often the medial femoral condyle","Bone and cartilage fragment separates","Pain, effusion, locking or catching"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"LOSS (osteoarthritis x-ray)","text":"Loss of joint space, Osteophytes, Subchondral sclerosis, Subchondral cysts."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CASTS Bend LEGS (avascular necrosis)","text":"Corticosteroids, Alcohol, Sickle cell, Trauma, SLE, the Bends (decompression), LEgg-Calve-Perthes, Gaucher disease, Slipped capital femoral epiphysis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Heberden at the fingertip","text":"Heberden nodes sit on the DIP joints, out at the fingertip. Bouchard nodes sit on the PIP joints, closer to the body. Both are osteophytes."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Lumbar disc names the lower root","text":"In the lumbar spine the herniated disc usually hits the root that exits one level down: L4 to L5 disc, L5 root; L5 to S1 disc, S1 root."}],"highYield":["Osteoarthritis is noninflammatory cartilage failure with eburnation, subchondral cysts, and osteophytes.","Osteoarthritis stiffness lasts under 30 minutes; rheumatoid arthritis stiffness lasts longer than an hour.","Osteoporosis has normal calcium, phosphate, and alkaline phosphatase; DEXA T-score of minus 2.5 or lower confirms it.","Estrogen loss raises RANKL-driven osteoclast resorption.","Paget disease: isolated high alkaline phosphatase, mosaic bone, high-output heart failure, osteosarcoma risk.","The femoral head is the most common site of avascular necrosis; MRI detects it first.","Obese adolescent with hip or knee pain suggests slipped capital femoral epiphysis.","L4 to L5 disc herniation compresses the L5 root."],"pitfalls":["Osteoporosis is less bone with normal mineral; osteomalacia is soft bone with poor mineral and abnormal labs.","Paget bone looks thick on x-ray but breaks easily.","Legg-Calve-Perthes affects younger children and is not linked to obesity; slipped capital femoral epiphysis affects obese teens.","Hip disease in children can show up as knee pain, so examine the hip in a limping child.","Spondylosis is wear and tear; spondylolysis is a pars defect; spondylolisthesis is the slip."],"dumps":[{"t":"Osteoarthritic joint","p":"Draw a normal synovial joint next to an osteoarthritic joint. Label the changes in the cartilage and bone.","l":["Normal smooth hyaline cartilage and even joint space","Fibrillated and thinned cartilage","Joint space narrowing","Eburnation of exposed bone","Subchondral sclerosis","Subchondral cysts","Osteophytes at the joint margins","Loose cartilage or bone fragments (joint mice)"]},{"t":"Bone loss versus bone chaos","p":"Build a comparison table for osteoporosis, Paget disease, and avascular necrosis. Columns: cell or blood supply problem, typical patient, lab findings, imaging, two complications.","l":["Osteoporosis: resorption exceeds formation, postmenopausal or older adult","Osteoporosis: normal labs, low T-score, compression and hip fractures","Paget: osteoclast overactivity then disorganized formation, older adult","Paget: isolated high ALP, thick mosaic bone","Paget: hearing loss, heart failure, osteosarcoma","Avascular necrosis: loss of blood supply, femoral head most common","Avascular necrosis: MRI most sensitive, collapse and arthritis later"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower Limb, section on the hip joint and blood supply of the femoral head"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back, section on intervertebral discs and herniation"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Osteoporosis (Part I)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2cz2rkBy2rg"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Paget's Disease of the bone","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NVcYG3_B8HU"}],"related":["MS-4","MS-13-C","MS-13-B4","MS-13-H1","HE-9-B1"]},"MS-13-F":{"id":"MS-13-F","summary":"Musculoskeletal neoplasms include primary bone tumors, soft tissue sarcomas, and metastases. Exams test the patient's age, the location in the bone, the x-ray pattern, the tissue the tumor makes, and the gene or marker that confirms it.","notes":[{"h":"Bone-forming tumors","points":["Osteosarcoma: malignant cells make osteoid; most common primary malignant bone tumor apart from myeloma.","Peaks in adolescents and again in older adults with Paget disease or prior radiation.","Metaphysis around the knee (distal femur, proximal tibia).","X-ray: Codman triangle (lifted periosteum) and sunburst pattern.","Risk with germline RB1 (hereditary retinoblastoma) and TP53 (Li-Fraumeni) loss.","Spreads through blood, most often to the lungs.","Osteoid osteoma: under 2 cm cortical nidus with a sclerotic rim, young males, night pain relieved by NSAIDs because the nidus makes prostaglandin E2.","Osteoblastoma: larger, often in the vertebrae, pain not relieved by NSAIDs."]},{"h":"Cartilage tumors","points":["Osteochondroma: most common benign bone tumor; metaphyseal bony stalk with a cartilage cap, continuous with the marrow; EXT1 or EXT2 (multiple in hereditary exostoses).","Enchondroma: hyaline cartilage inside the medullary cavity, often in hand bones; multiple in Ollier disease and Maffucci syndrome (with hemangiomas).","Chondrosarcoma: malignant cartilage in older adults; pelvis, proximal femur, shoulder girdle.","Chondrosarcoma can arise from an enchondroma or osteochondroma and often carries IDH1 or IDH2 mutations."]},{"h":"Ewing sarcoma and giant cell tumors","points":["Ewing sarcoma: children and adolescents; diaphysis of long bones or pelvis.","Small round blue cells, CD99 positive, EWSR1-FLI1 fusion from t(11;22).","Onion-skin periosteal reaction; fever, high ESR, and leukocytosis can mimic osteomyelitis.","Giant cell tumor of bone: young adults, epiphysis near the knee, soap-bubble lytic lesion.","Neoplastic stromal cells express RANKL and recruit osteoclast-like giant cells.","Tenosynovial giant cell tumor: tumor of synovium or tendon sheath driven by CSF1 overexpression."]},{"h":"Soft tissue sarcomas","points":["Rhabdomyosarcoma: most common soft tissue sarcoma of childhood; head and neck (orbit) or genitourinary tract.","Sarcoma botryoides: grape-like embryonal variant in the vagina or bladder of young children.","Rhabdomyoblasts stain for desmin and myogenin; alveolar type has PAX3 or PAX7 to FOXO1 fusions.","Leiomyosarcoma: adults; uterus and retroperitoneum; smooth muscle actin and desmin positive.","Sarcomas spread mainly through blood to the lungs; rhabdomyosarcoma can also reach lymph nodes."]},{"h":"Metastases and paraneoplastic bone disease","points":["Metastases are the most common malignant bone tumors; they seed red marrow in the spine, pelvis, ribs, and proximal long bones.","Osteoblastic: prostate. Osteolytic: kidney, thyroid, lung. Mixed: breast.","Complications: pathologic fracture, hypercalcemia, pain, spinal cord compression.","Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy: clubbing, periosteal new bone on long bones, and arthritis as a paraneoplastic sign of lung cancer."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Named musculoskeletal neoplasms","cols":["Tumor","Age and site","Tissue or cell","Imaging or gross","Genetics or marker","Behavior"],"rows":[["Osteosarcoma","Teens and older adults (Paget, radiation); metaphysis around the knee","Malignant osteoid","Codman triangle, sunburst","RB1, TP53","Malignant; blood spread to lung"],["Ewing sarcoma","Children and teens; diaphysis, pelvis","Small round blue cells","Onion-skin periosteal reaction, lytic","CD99, t(11;22) EWSR1-FLI1","Malignant; early metastasis; can mimic infection"],["Chondrosarcoma","Older adults; pelvis, proximal femur, shoulder girdle","Malignant hyaline cartilage","Lytic with ring and arc calcification","IDH1 or IDH2","Malignant; may arise from enchondroma or osteochondroma"],["Osteoid osteoma","Young males; cortex of long bones","Osteoid nidus under 2 cm","Lucent nidus with sclerotic rim","Nidus makes prostaglandin E2","Benign; night pain relieved by NSAIDs"],["Rhabdomyosarcoma","Children; head and neck, genitourinary tract","Rhabdomyoblasts (skeletal muscle lineage)","Botryoid grape-like mass in hollow organs","Desmin, myogenin; alveolar PAX3 or PAX7 to FOXO1","Malignant; blood and lymph spread"]]},{"title":"Other bone and soft tissue tumors","cols":["Tumor","Typical patient and site","Key feature","Note"],"rows":[["Osteochondroma","Adolescent; metaphysis near the knee","Bony stalk with cartilage cap, grows away from the joint","EXT1 or EXT2; rare chondrosarcoma"],["Enchondroma","Hand and foot bones","Intramedullary hyaline cartilage","Multiple in Ollier and Maffucci; chondrosarcoma risk"],["Osteoblastoma","Young adults; vertebrae","Larger than 2 cm","Pain not relieved by NSAIDs"],["Giant cell tumor of bone","Young adults; epiphysis near the knee","Soap-bubble lytic lesion","RANKL-expressing stromal cells recruit giant cells"],["Tenosynovial giant cell tumor","Synovium or tendon sheath","Hemosiderin-laden synovial mass","CSF1 overexpression"],["Leiomyosarcoma","Adults; uterus, retroperitoneum","Malignant spindle smooth muscle cells","Smooth muscle actin and desmin; lung metastasis"],["Bone metastases","Older adults; spine, pelvis, ribs","Most common malignant bone tumor","Blastic prostate, lytic kidney, thyroid, lung, mixed breast"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Where in the bone","text":"Epiphysis: giant cell tumor. Metaphysis: osteosarcoma and osteochondroma. Diaphysis: Ewing sarcoma. Location alone narrows most bone tumor questions."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Aspirin at night","text":"A young man woken by bone pain that aspirin or ibuprofen relieves has osteoid osteoma; the nidus makes prostaglandin E2, which NSAIDs block."}],"highYield":["Metastases are the most common malignant tumors in bone; prostate makes blastic lesions.","Osteosarcoma: teen with a metaphyseal knee tumor, Codman triangle, sunburst, lung metastasis.","Hereditary retinoblastoma (RB1) and Li-Fraumeni (TP53) raise osteosarcoma risk; Paget disease does in older adults.","Ewing sarcoma: diaphysis, onion skin, CD99, t(11;22) EWSR1-FLI1, may mimic osteomyelitis.","Chondrosarcoma: older adult, pelvis or shoulder, IDH1 or IDH2.","Rhabdomyosarcoma: most common childhood soft tissue sarcoma; desmin and myogenin.","Giant cell tumor of bone: epiphyseal soap bubble; stromal cells make RANKL.","Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy is a paraneoplastic sign of lung cancer."],"pitfalls":["Fever and high ESR with a child's bone lesion can be Ewing sarcoma, not just osteomyelitis.","Osteoid osteoma pain responds to NSAIDs; osteoblastoma pain does not.","Codman triangle shows aggressive growth but is not specific for osteosarcoma.","Multiple myeloma is the most common primary malignancy in bone, but it arises from plasma cells; that is why osteosarcoma is called the most common primary bone tumor apart from myeloma.","Myogenin marks skeletal muscle (rhabdomyosarcoma); smooth muscle actin marks smooth muscle (leiomyosarcoma)."],"dumps":[{"t":"Long bone tumor map","p":"Draw a long bone and label the epiphysis, metaphysis, diaphysis, and medullary cavity. Place each tumor where it usually arises.","l":["Giant cell tumor in the epiphysis","Osteosarcoma in the metaphysis","Osteochondroma as a metaphyseal stalk with a cartilage cap","Ewing sarcoma in the diaphysis","Enchondroma inside the medullary cavity","Osteoid osteoma in the cortex with a sclerotic rim","Labels Codman triangle at a lifted periosteum"]},{"t":"Sarcoma marker table","p":"Build a table for osteosarcoma, Ewing sarcoma, chondrosarcoma, rhabdomyosarcoma, and leiomyosarcoma. Columns: age, site, marker or gene, and where it spreads.","l":["Osteosarcoma: teens or older adults, knee metaphysis, RB1 or TP53, lung","Ewing: children and teens, diaphysis or pelvis, CD99 and EWSR1-FLI1, lung and bone","Chondrosarcoma: older adults, pelvis and shoulder, IDH1 or IDH2, lung","Rhabdomyosarcoma: children, head and neck or genitourinary, desmin and myogenin","Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma: PAX3 or PAX7 to FOXO1","Leiomyosarcoma: adults, uterus or retroperitoneum, smooth muscle actin and desmin","Sarcomas spread mainly through blood to the lungs"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia, section on paraneoplastic syndromes"}],"videos":[],"related":["MS-13-E3","MS-13-G3","RE-13-J6","HE-9-D3","MS-9"]},"MS-13-G":{"id":"MS-13-G","summary":"Infectious musculoskeletal diseases include septic arthritis, osteomyelitis, necrotizing fasciitis, gangrene, and infective myositis. Exams test the likely organism for each patient, how the infection reaches the tissue, and the findings in joint fluid, bone, and skin.","notes":[{"h":"Septic arthritis","points":["Bacteria reach the joint through blood (most cases) or by direct inoculation (injection, surgery, bite).","The synovium is richly vascular with no basement membrane, so bacteria seed it easily.","Staphylococcus aureus is the most common cause; Neisseria gonorrhoeae in sexually active young adults.","Risk: rheumatoid arthritis, prosthetic joints, injection drug use, diabetes, older age.","Neutrophil enzymes destroy cartilage within days, so a hot swollen joint is an emergency.","Gonococcal arthritis can be migratory with tenosynovitis and pustular skin lesions; joint cultures are often negative."]},{"h":"Synovial fluid","points":["Noninflammatory (osteoarthritis, trauma): clear, under 2,000 WBC/mm3, few neutrophils.","Inflammatory (rheumatoid arthritis, gout, pseudogout, reactive arthritis): cloudy, about 2,000 to 50,000 WBC/mm3, mostly neutrophils.","Septic: purulent, usually over 50,000 WBC/mm3, over 75 percent neutrophils, positive Gram stain or culture.","Gout: needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals. Pseudogout: rhomboid, weakly positively birefringent crystals.","Crystals do not rule out infection; both can be present together."]},{"h":"Osteomyelitis","points":["Children: blood-borne seeding of the long bone metaphysis, where slow flow in looping vessels and few phagocytes let bacteria settle.","Adults: vertebral osteomyelitis with discitis from blood spread, and contiguous spread from diabetic foot ulcers.","S. aureus is most common; Salmonella in sickle cell disease; Pseudomonas after a nail through a sneaker or in injection drug use; Mycobacterium tuberculosis in the spine (Pott disease).","Sequestrum is dead bone; involucrum is new bone around it; Brodie abscess is a walled-off bone abscess.","ESR and CRP are high; MRI is the most sensitive imaging; x-ray changes lag about 2 weeks.","Chronic osteomyelitis can drain through a sinus tract, which may develop squamous cell carcinoma."]},{"h":"Necrotizing fasciitis","points":["Infection spreads rapidly along fascial planes and clots the vessels that feed the overlying skin.","Type I: polymicrobial (anaerobes plus aerobes), in diabetes or after surgery; Fournier gangrene of the perineum.","Type II: Streptococcus pyogenes (group A), alone or with S. aureus, can strike healthy people after minor injury.","Pain out of proportion to exam, swelling past the redness, crepitus, bullae, dusky skin, and systemic toxicity."]},{"h":"Gangrene","points":["Dry gangrene: arterial occlusion causes coagulative necrosis; black, shriveled, sharply demarcated.","Wet gangrene: bacteria infect the dead tissue, giving liquefactive necrosis; swollen, foul, spreading, risk of sepsis.","Diabetes and peripheral artery disease raise risk through poor perfusion, neuropathy, and weak neutrophil function.","Gas gangrene: Clostridium perfringens infects deep contaminated wounds; alpha toxin (lecithinase) destroys cell membranes.","Gas gangrene signs: rapid spread, severe pain, crepitus, bronze skin, hemolysis, and shock."]},{"h":"Infective myositis","points":["Pyomyositis: S. aureus abscess in a large muscle such as the thigh or buttock.","Trichinellosis: Trichinella spiralis larvae from undercooked pork or wild game encyst in skeletal muscle.","Trichinellosis findings: myalgia, fever, periorbital edema, and eosinophilia."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Infectious musculoskeletal diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Organisms","Route and risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Septic arthritis","S. aureus; N. gonorrhoeae in sexually active adults; streptococci; gram-negatives in older adults and drug users","Blood spread or direct inoculation; rheumatoid arthritis, prosthetic joint, injection drug use, diabetes","Neutrophil enzymes and cytokines destroy cartilage","Acute hot swollen joint, fever, synovial WBC over 50,000/mm3","Cartilage loss, osteomyelitis, sepsis"],["Osteomyelitis","S. aureus; Salmonella (sickle cell); Pseudomonas (puncture, drug use); M. tuberculosis (spine)","Blood spread (child metaphysis, adult spine) or contiguous (diabetic foot, open fracture)","Pus raises pressure in bone, cuts blood supply, and kills bone","Bone pain, fever, high ESR and CRP, MRI changes","Sequestrum, chronic draining sinus, squamous cell carcinoma, pathologic fracture"],["Necrotizing fasciitis","Type I polymicrobial; type II Streptococcus pyogenes","Diabetes, surgery, perineal wounds (type I); minor skin injury (type II)","Spread along fascia with vessel thrombosis and toxins","Pain out of proportion, crepitus, bullae, systemic toxicity","Septic shock, toxic shock syndrome, limb loss, death"],["Wet gangrene","Mixed skin and gut bacteria","Ischemic tissue in diabetes or peripheral artery disease","Bacteria convert coagulative necrosis to liquefactive necrosis","Swollen, moist, foul, poorly demarcated tissue","Sepsis, amputation"],["Gas gangrene (clostridial myonecrosis)","Clostridium perfringens","Deep contaminated wounds, crush injury","Alpha toxin (lecithinase) destroys membranes of muscle and red cells","Severe pain, crepitus, bronze skin, thin brown discharge","Hemolysis, shock, renal failure, death"]]},{"title":"Synovial fluid categories","cols":["Category","Appearance","WBC per mm3","Neutrophils","Examples"],"rows":[["Normal","Clear","Under 200","Under 25 percent","Healthy joint"],["Noninflammatory","Clear, yellow","200 to 2,000","Under 25 percent","Osteoarthritis, trauma"],["Inflammatory","Cloudy","About 2,000 to 50,000","Over 50 percent","Rheumatoid arthritis, gout, pseudogout, reactive arthritis"],["Septic","Purulent","Usually over 50,000","Over 75 percent","Bacterial arthritis; Gram stain or culture often positive"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Dead bone, new bone","text":"Sequestrum is sequestered (dead and isolated). Involucrum is the envelope of new bone that wraps around it."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Dry is clean, wet is infected","text":"Dry gangrene stays coagulative and sharply edged. Add bacteria and enzymes and it turns wet, liquefied, and spreading."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Pain out of proportion","text":"Severe pain with skin that looks only mildly red points to a deep process: necrotizing fasciitis, gas gangrene, or compartment syndrome."}],"highYield":["S. aureus is the top cause of septic arthritis and osteomyelitis.","Septic joint fluid usually has more than 50,000 WBC/mm3 with mostly neutrophils.","Childhood hematogenous osteomyelitis settles in the metaphysis because blood flow there is slow.","Salmonella osteomyelitis is classic in sickle cell disease; Pseudomonas follows a nail through a sneaker.","MRI is the most sensitive imaging for osteomyelitis.","Necrotizing fasciitis: pain out of proportion, crepitus, bullae; type II is group A streptococcus.","Clostridium perfringens alpha toxin (lecithinase) causes gas gangrene with hemolysis and shock.","Trichinella from undercooked pork or game causes myalgia, periorbital edema, and eosinophilia."],"pitfalls":["Finding crystals in a joint does not exclude infection; a gouty joint can also be septic.","Crepitus can occur in type I necrotizing fasciitis and in gas gangrene; Gram stain and the deep tissue involved (fascia versus muscle) help separate them.","Early osteomyelitis can have a normal x-ray; order MRI if suspicion is high.","Gonococcal arthritis often has negative joint cultures; test the urethra, cervix, rectum, and throat."],"dumps":[{"t":"Osteomyelitis in a child's bone","p":"Draw a child's long bone and trace how blood-borne bacteria cause osteomyelitis. Label the metaphysis, growth plate, sequestrum, and involucrum.","l":["Bacteria arrive through the nutrient artery","Slow flow in metaphyseal vessel loops lets bacteria settle","Neutrophils and pus raise pressure inside the bone","Blood supply is cut off and bone dies (sequestrum)","Pus lifts the periosteum, which lays down new bone (involucrum)","Sinus tract can drain to the skin","S. aureus is the most common organism"]},{"t":"Hot joint workup","p":"Build a table of synovial fluid findings for noninflammatory, inflammatory, septic, gout, and pseudogout joints. Include appearance, WBC count, neutrophil percent, and crystals.","l":["Noninflammatory: clear, under 2,000 WBC/mm3","Inflammatory: cloudy, about 2,000 to 50,000 WBC/mm3, mostly neutrophils","Septic: purulent, usually over 50,000 WBC/mm3, over 75 percent neutrophils","Septic: Gram stain or culture positive","Gout: needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals","Pseudogout: rhomboid, weakly positively birefringent crystals","Crystals and infection can coexist"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors, sections on osteomyelitis and infectious arthritis"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Staphylococci"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"section on Clostridium perfringens and gas gangrene"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Cell Injury, Cell Death, and Adaptations, section on gangrenous necrosis"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Septic Arthritis - Overview (causes, pathophysiology, treatment)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Tz07Uqx7_VY"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Bone Fracture - Types, Fracture Repair and Osteomyelitis","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MNkI6Of2PRs"}],"related":["MS-12","MS-13-B4","IN-13-H3","CV-18-I6","HE-9-B1"]},"MS-13-H":{"id":"MS-13-H","summary":"Musculoskeletal trauma covers fractures and how they heal, soft tissue injuries, and the dangerous complications of injury: avascular necrosis, nerve and artery damage, fat embolism, compartment syndrome, rhabdomyolysis, and myositis ossificans. Exams link each injury to the structure at risk and the mechanism of harm.","notes":[{"h":"Fracture healing","points":["Hematoma and inflammation (first days): clot forms a fibrin scaffold; platelets and macrophages release growth factors.","Soft callus (about 1 to 3 weeks): granulation tissue and fibrocartilage bridge the gap.","Hard callus (weeks to months): woven bone replaces the cartilage by endochondral ossification.","Remodeling (months to years): woven bone becomes lamellar bone shaped along lines of stress.","Delayed union or nonunion: poor blood supply, infection, motion at the fracture, smoking, diabetes, malnutrition."]},{"h":"Fracture complications","points":["Scaphoid: blood enters distally and flows backward, so the proximal pole can die (avascular necrosis, nonunion).","Femoral neck: displaced fractures tear the retinacular branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery, causing femoral head necrosis.","Supracondylar humerus (child): brachial artery and median nerve injury; can lead to forearm compartment syndrome.","Long bone and pelvic fractures: fat embolism about 1 to 3 days later.","Fat embolism triad: hypoxemia, confusion, and petechiae (chest, axillae, conjunctivae)."]},{"h":"Soft tissue injury","points":["Sprain: ligament stretch or tear. Strain: muscle or tendon stretch or tear.","Contusion: bleeding into soft tissue from a blow. Dislocation: joint surfaces lose contact.","Ligaments heal slowly: dense regular tissue with poor blood supply and few fibroblasts; repair forms weaker scar."]},{"h":"Compartment syndrome","points":["Swelling in a closed fascial compartment raises pressure until capillary and venous flow stop.","Causes: tibial and forearm fractures, crush injury, reperfusion after ischemia, tight casts, circumferential burns.","Earliest signs: pain out of proportion and pain with passive stretch of the compartment's muscles.","Pulses are usually preserved early because arterial pressure is higher than compartment pressure.","Untreated forearm compartment syndrome leaves Volkmann ischemic contracture (fibrosed forearm flexors, flexed wrist, and clawed fingers)."]},{"h":"Rhabdomyolysis","points":["Muscle breakdown from crush injury, extreme exertion, heat, seizures, statins, or drugs and alcohol.","Dying muscle releases CK, myoglobin, potassium, and phosphate.","CK very high (often over 5 times normal, frequently many thousands of U/L).","Urine dipstick positive for blood with few or no red cells on microscopy (myoglobinuria); tea-colored urine.","Hyperkalemia can cause arrhythmia; calcium is often low early as it deposits in injured muscle.","Acute kidney injury from myoglobin casts, heme toxicity to tubules, and renal vasoconstriction."]},{"h":"Myositis ossificans","points":["Heterotopic bone forms in muscle after trauma, often in the thigh or upper arm of young athletes.","Zonal pattern: mature bone at the periphery, immature cells in the center.","Osteosarcoma shows the opposite: the most mature, mineralized tissue centrally with an infiltrative, atypical edge."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Fracture healing stages","cols":["Stage","Approximate timing","What happens"],"rows":[["Hematoma and inflammation","Hours to about 1 week","Clot forms; platelets and macrophages release growth factors; dead bone ends removed"],["Soft callus","About 1 to 3 weeks","Granulation tissue and fibrocartilage bridge the gap"],["Hard callus","Weeks to about 3 months","Woven bone replaces cartilage by endochondral ossification"],["Remodeling","Months to years","Woven bone replaced by lamellar bone along stress lines"]]},{"title":"Injuries and what they put at risk","cols":["Injury","Structure at risk","Mechanism","Result"],"rows":[["Scaphoid fracture","Proximal pole blood supply","Retrograde flow from distal vessels is cut","Avascular necrosis, nonunion"],["Displaced femoral neck fracture","Retinacular branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery","Vessels along the neck tear","Femoral head avascular necrosis"],["Supracondylar humerus fracture","Brachial artery, median nerve","Displaced fragment injures the neurovascular bundle","Ischemia, compartment syndrome, Volkmann contracture"],["Long bone or pelvic fracture","Lungs, brain, skin","Marrow fat enters the veins","Fat embolism: hypoxemia, confusion, petechiae"],["Crush injury","Muscle and kidneys","Muscle necrosis releases myoglobin and potassium","Compartment syndrome, rhabdomyolysis, acute kidney injury"]]},{"title":"Soft tissue injuries","cols":["Injury","Tissue","Description"],"rows":[["Sprain","Ligament","Stretch or tear of a ligament"],["Strain","Muscle or tendon","Stretch or tear of muscle fibers or tendon"],["Contusion","Soft tissue","Bleeding into tissue from a blunt blow"],["Dislocation","Joint","Complete loss of contact between joint surfaces"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Sprain versus strain","text":"Strain has a T for tendon (and the muscle it belongs to). Sprain is the ligament."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"The 6 Ps","text":"Pain, Pallor, Paresthesia, Paralysis, Pulselessness, Poikilothermia. In compartment syndrome pain comes first; pulselessness comes late."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Blood but no red cells","text":"A dipstick positive for blood with no red cells on microscopy means heme pigment: myoglobin (rhabdomyolysis) or free hemoglobin (hemolysis)."}],"highYield":["Fracture healing order: hematoma, soft callus, hard callus, remodeling.","Scaphoid fractures risk proximal pole avascular necrosis because blood flows in from the distal end.","Displaced femoral neck fractures risk femoral head necrosis.","Supracondylar humerus fractures injure the brachial artery and median nerve.","Fat embolism after long bone fracture: hypoxemia, confusion, petechiae.","Compartment syndrome: pain out of proportion and pain on passive stretch, with pulses often still present.","Rhabdomyolysis: very high CK, heme-positive urine without red cells, hyperkalemia, acute kidney injury.","Myositis ossificans matures from the outside in; osteosarcoma does the reverse."],"pitfalls":["A palpable pulse does not rule out compartment syndrome.","A normal first x-ray does not rule out a scaphoid fracture when the anatomic snuffbox is tender.","Fat embolism is delayed by a day or more, which helps separate it from immediate hemorrhage or pneumothorax.","Myositis ossificans can look worrying on biopsy; the orderly zonal pattern points away from osteosarcoma."],"dumps":[{"t":"Fracture healing timeline","p":"Draw a fractured long bone at four stages of healing. Label the cells and tissue present at each stage.","l":["Stage 1: hematoma with fibrin, platelets, and inflammatory cells","Growth factors from platelets and macrophages recruit repair cells","Stage 2: soft callus of granulation tissue and fibrocartilage","Stage 3: hard callus of woven bone by endochondral ossification","Stage 4: remodeling to lamellar bone by osteoclasts and osteoblasts","Lists two causes of delayed union (for example poor blood supply, smoking, infection, motion)"]},{"t":"Crush injury cascade","p":"Trace what happens after a leg is crushed under a heavy object for several hours. Draw arrows from the muscle to the heart and kidney.","l":["Swollen muscle raises compartment pressure","Capillary and venous flow stop, causing muscle and nerve ischemia","Muscle cells break down and release CK, myoglobin, potassium, and phosphate","Hyperkalemia threatens cardiac arrhythmia","Myoglobin forms tubular casts and injures tubules","Acute kidney injury follows","Urine is heme positive with few red cells"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors, section on fractures and fracture healing"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb, section on the scaphoid and supracondylar fractures"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower Limb, section on fractures of the femoral neck"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Bone, section on fracture repair"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Bone Fracture - Types, Fracture Repair and Osteomyelitis","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MNkI6Of2PRs"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Acute Compartment Syndrome","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=txAugSNF4Jk"}],"related":["MS-2","MS-13-E4","MS-13-F4","UR-12-A4"]}};
window.NPLEX_CASES = [{"id":"CV-C01","system":"CV","dx":"Heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension","codes":["CV-18-A1"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 14-month history of progressive shortness of breath on exertion and one episode of near-syncope while climbing stairs. Her father died of an undiagnosed cardiopulmonary illness at the age of 45 years. Oxygen saturation is 94% on room air. Physical examination shows a loud pulmonic component of the second heart sound, a lift at the left lower sternal border, and mild ankle edema. Echocardiography shows a dilated, hypertrophied right ventricle and a normal left ventricle. Right heart catheterization shows a mean pulmonary artery pressure of 46 mm Hg (normal about 14; pulmonary hypertension is above 20), a pulmonary capillary wedge pressure of 9 mm Hg (normal 6 to 12), and increased pulmonary vascular resistance. CT angiography and a ventilation-perfusion scan show no emboli or lung disease. Diagnosis: heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C01-Q1","stem":"Genetic testing shows a heterozygous loss-of-function mutation in BMPR2, the gene most often mutated in heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension. Which of the following best describes the normal function of the protein encoded by this gene?","options":["A serine/threonine kinase receptor that restrains vascular smooth muscle growth","A G protein-coupled receptor for endothelin-1 that drives vasoconstriction","An extracellular microfibril protein that holds TGF-beta inactive in the vessel wall","A receptor tyrosine kinase for PDGF that promotes smooth muscle proliferation"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-A1","obj":"CV-18-A.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize BMPR2 as bone morphogenetic protein receptor type 2, a member of the TGF-beta receptor family.","Recall that TGF-beta family receptors are serine/threonine kinases that signal through SMAD proteins.","Link loss of this growth-restraining signal to cell proliferation in small pulmonary arteries."],"why":"BMPR2 encodes bone morphogenetic protein receptor type 2, a serine/threonine kinase receptor in the TGF-beta superfamily. Its SMAD signaling normally inhibits proliferation of pulmonary vascular smooth muscle and supports endothelial survival. With one working copy lost, endothelial and smooth muscle cells proliferate, thickening the media and intima of small pulmonary arteries and raising resistance. Inheritance is autosomal dominant with incomplete penetrance, so many carriers never develop disease.","whyNot":["Correct.","Endothelin-1 constricts and remodels pulmonary vessels and is high in this disease, but its receptors are G protein-coupled receptors and are not encoded by BMPR2.","This describes fibrillin-1. FBN1 mutations cause Marfan syndrome, with aortic root dilation and dissection.","PDGF does drive smooth muscle growth in pulmonary hypertension, but it acts through a receptor tyrosine kinase. BMPR2 signaling normally opposes this growth."],"study":{"topics":["BMPR2 and heritable PAH","TGF-beta receptor signaling"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-A1"]}},{"id":"CV-C01-Q2","stem":"During a commercial airline flight, the reduced cabin pressure lowers her alveolar oxygen tension, and her shortness of breath worsens. Which of the following events in pulmonary arteriolar smooth muscle initiates the vascular response to alveolar hypoxia?","options":["Adenosine binding A2A receptors, raising cyclic AMP in the cell","Inhibition of oxygen-sensitive K+ channels, depolarizing the cell","More nitric oxide release, raising cGMP and lowering cytosolic calcium","Opening of ATP-sensitive K+ channels, hyperpolarizing the cell"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","obj":"CV-15.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that alveolar hypoxia constricts pulmonary arterioles, the opposite of the dilation seen in most systemic arterioles.","Constriction needs a rise in cytosolic calcium, which follows membrane depolarization.","Pick the option that depolarizes the cell: closing potassium channels."],"why":"Pulmonary arterioles constrict when alveolar oxygen falls, which normally diverts blood toward better-ventilated alveoli. Hypoxia inhibits oxygen-sensitive potassium channels in the smooth muscle, the membrane depolarizes, voltage-gated L-type calcium channels open, and calcium entry triggers contraction. At altitude the whole lung is hypoxic, so the constriction is widespread and adds to a pulmonary vascular resistance that is already high in her disease.","whyNot":["Adenosine is a key metabolic vasodilator in the coronary circulation during hypoxia. Rising cyclic AMP relaxes vascular smooth muscle.","Correct.","Nitric oxide and cGMP relax smooth muscle. Hypoxia does not raise this pathway, and reduced nitric oxide signaling is part of the problem in pulmonary arterial hypertension.","This is how hypoxia dilates many systemic arterioles, such as those in skeletal muscle and the heart. Hyperpolarization closes calcium channels and relaxes the cell."],"study":{"topics":["Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","Pulmonary and systemic responses to hypoxia"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-11"]}},{"id":"CV-C01-Q3","stem":"The lift on her anterior chest wall is palpated best at the left lower sternal border. Which of the following anatomic features best explains why enlargement of the affected chamber is palpable at this site?","options":["The right ventricle forms most of the sternocostal surface","The left atrium forms most of the base on the posterior surface","The right atrium forms the right border of the heart shadow","The left ventricle forms the apex and most of the left border"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","obj":"CV-3.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the chamber under strain: pulmonary hypertension loads the right ventricle.","Recall which chamber faces the anterior chest wall.","Match the right ventricle to the left parasternal location of the heave."],"why":"Pulmonary hypertension raises right ventricular afterload, so the right ventricle hypertrophies and dilates. The right ventricle lies just behind the sternum and left parasternal region and forms most of the anterior (sternocostal) surface of the heart. A forceful, enlarged right ventricle therefore lifts the chest wall at the left lower sternal border, called a parasternal heave.","whyNot":["Correct.","The left atrium forms the base and lies against the esophagus. When it enlarges, as in mitral stenosis, it can push the esophagus back, but it cannot be felt on the front of the chest.","The right atrium forms the right heart border on a frontal chest film. It does not lie under the left lower sternum.","Left ventricular enlargement moves the apex beat down and to the left, near or beyond the left midclavicular line. Her left ventricle is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Surfaces and borders of the heart","Parasternal heave"],"comps":["CV-3"]}},{"id":"CV-C01-Q4","stem":"Two years later, she dies of progressive right-sided heart failure. Which of the following findings in the lungs at autopsy is most characteristic of her disease?","options":["Hemosiderin-laden macrophages filling the alveolar spaces","Hyaline membranes lining the alveolar ducts","Noncaseating granulomas along bronchovascular bundles","Plexiform lesions in small muscular pulmonary arteries"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-A1","obj":"CV-18-A.6","dok":3,"approach":["Classify her disease as precapillary: high pulmonary artery pressure with a normal wedge pressure.","Recall the vascular changes of severe pulmonary arterial hypertension.","Rule out lesions that point to left heart failure, acute alveolar injury, or granulomatous lung disease."],"why":"Severe pulmonary arterial hypertension remodels small muscular pulmonary arteries and arterioles with medial hypertrophy and intimal fibrosis, and in advanced idiopathic and heritable disease it produces plexiform lesions. A plexiform lesion is a tuft of small, slit-like capillary channels inside a dilated arterial segment, formed by proliferating endothelial cells. It reflects the uncontrolled endothelial and smooth muscle growth that follows loss of BMPR2 signaling.","whyNot":["These heart failure cells form when high left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure leaks red cells into the alveoli, as in chronic left heart failure or mitral stenosis. Her normal wedge pressure rules out that cause.","Hyaline membranes mark diffuse alveolar damage, as in acute respiratory distress syndrome. They reflect acute injury to the alveolar wall.","Noncaseating granulomas suggest sarcoidosis, which can cause pulmonary hypertension through lung fibrosis. Her imaging showed no lung disease, and granulomas are not a feature of heritable disease.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pulmonary vascular remodeling","Plexiform lesions"],"comps":["CV-18-A1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C02","system":"CV","dx":"Systemic hypertension with concentric left ventricular hypertrophy","codes":["CV-18-A2"],"case":"A 58-year-old man who works as a delivery truck driver comes to the physician for a required work physical examination. He was diagnosed with hypertension 12 years ago but stopped treatment many years ago. He reports mild shortness of breath when climbing two flights of stairs. Blood pressure is 174/104 mm Hg on repeated measurements. Physical examination shows a sustained, nondisplaced apical impulse and a fourth heart sound at the apex. An ECG shows increased QRS voltage with a left ventricular strain pattern. Echocardiography shows concentric hypertrophy with an interventricular septal thickness of 15 mm (normal 6 to 10), normal chamber size, an ejection fraction of 62% (normal 52 to 72%), and impaired relaxation. Serum creatinine concentration is 1.1 mg/dL (normal 0.7 to 1.3). Diagnosis: hypertensive heart disease with concentric left ventricular hypertrophy.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C02-Q1","stem":"One year later, he develops atrial fibrillation. With medication, his ventricular rate is maintained at about 75/min, but his exercise tolerance decreases further and the fourth heart sound is no longer heard. Loss of which of the following phases of the cardiac cycle best explains this decline?","options":["Isovolumetric relaxation before the mitral valve opens","Reduced ejection late in ventricular systole","Atrial systole at the end of ventricular filling","Rapid ventricular filling just after mitral opening"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-4","obj":"CV-4.6","dok":3,"approach":["Note what changed: atrial fibrillation with a controlled rate, so a short diastole is not the explanation.","Recall that the fourth heart sound comes from atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle.","Conclude that a stiff ventricle relies on atrial systole for filling, which atrial fibrillation removes."],"why":"A stiff, concentrically hypertrophied left ventricle fills poorly in early diastole, so it depends more on atrial contraction at the end of diastole. In a normal heart the atrial kick adds about 20 percent of filling, and in a stiff ventricle its share is larger. Atrial fibrillation removes organized atrial contraction, so end-diastolic volume and stroke volume fall. The fourth heart sound is made by atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle, which is why it disappears.","whyNot":["Isovolumetric relaxation still occurs with every beat, and no blood enters the ventricle during it. Slow relaxation in this phase is part of his baseline disease.","Ejection depends on ventricular contraction, which continues in atrial fibrillation. This phase is not lost.","Correct.","Rapid filling still happens in atrial fibrillation because it is driven by the pressure gradient when the mitral valve opens. It was already reduced by his impaired relaxation and does not explain the new decline."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of the cardiac cycle","Atrial kick and diastolic dysfunction"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-A2"]}},{"id":"CV-C02-Q2","stem":"Echocardiography shows slowed early diastolic relaxation. Myocardial relaxation requires ATP and is delayed when myocardial ATP is depleted. Which of the following steps in the cross-bridge cycle directly detaches the myosin head from actin?","options":["Hydrolysis of ATP to ADP and phosphate on the myosin head","Binding of an ATP molecule to the myosin head","Release of ADP at the end of the power stroke","Release of phosphate, which triggers the power stroke"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-5","obj":"CV-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["Picture the head after the power stroke: attached to actin with no nucleotide (the rigor state).","Ask what must happen for it to let go: a new ATP must bind.","Keep the roles apart: binding detaches, hydrolysis recocks, phosphate release powers the stroke."],"why":"After the power stroke, a myosin head bound to actin with no nucleotide is in the rigor state. Binding of a new ATP molecule lowers myosin's affinity for actin, and the head detaches. Hydrolysis of that ATP then recocks the head for the next cycle. When ATP is scarce, as in energy-starved hypertrophied muscle, heads stay attached longer and relaxation slows. Rigor mortis is the extreme case, with no ATP left to release the heads.","whyNot":["Hydrolysis happens after detachment and recocks the head into its high-energy position. The head is already free of actin at this step.","Correct.","ADP release ends the power stroke and leaves the head tightly bound to actin in the rigor state. A new ATP must bind before the head can let go.","Phosphate release strengthens binding to actin and starts the power stroke. It moves the filaments and does not separate them."],"study":{"topics":["Cross-bridge cycle","Energy use in diastolic relaxation"],"comps":["CV-5"]}},{"id":"CV-C02-Q3","stem":"He later reports pressure-like chest discomfort that occurs with heavy exertion and resolves within minutes of rest. Coronary angiography shows no significant stenosis of the epicardial arteries. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of his exertional ischemia?","options":["Rupture of a small plaque with a nonocclusive thrombus","Spasm of a large epicardial coronary artery","Vasculitis narrowing small coronary arteries","Myocyte mass outgrowing its capillary supply"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-A2","obj":"CV-18-A.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note the clues: exertional pain, thick ventricle, clean epicardial arteries.","Compare oxygen supply and demand in a hypertrophied wall.","Choose the mechanism tied to hypertrophy itself."],"why":"In concentric hypertrophy, myocytes enlarge but capillary density does not keep pace, so the distance oxygen must diffuse increases. Oxygen demand rises with the larger muscle mass and high systolic wall stress. A thick, stiff wall with high diastolic pressure also compresses subendocardial vessels, which fill mainly in diastole. During exertion, demand outruns supply and the subendocardium becomes ischemic even with normal epicardial arteries.","whyNot":["Plaque rupture with thrombus causes unstable angina or infarction, and angiography would show a culprit lesion. His arteries are clear.","Vasospastic (Prinzmetal) angina typically occurs at rest, often in the early morning, and is not reliably brought on by exertion.","Vasculitis such as polyarteritis nodosa or Kawasaki disease can damage coronary arteries, but he has no systemic features, and his symptoms track with the work of a thickened ventricle.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of left ventricular hypertrophy","Subendocardial ischemia"],"comps":["CV-18-A2","CV-8"]}},{"id":"CV-C02-Q4","stem":"Eight years later, still untreated, he has the sudden onset of right-sided weakness and slurred speech. CT of the head shows a hemorrhage in the left putamen and internal capsule. Rupture of which of the following vessels most likely caused this hemorrhage?","options":["Bridging veins crossing the subdural space","A saccular aneurysm at the anterior communicating artery","Lenticulostriate branches of the middle cerebral artery","The middle meningeal artery beneath the pterion"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.2","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the bleed: putamen and internal capsule, deep in the brain.","Recall which arteries supply that region: lenticulostriate branches of the middle cerebral artery.","Link chronic hypertension to damage in small penetrating arteries."],"why":"Long-standing hypertension damages small penetrating arteries and can form tiny microaneurysms in their walls. The lenticulostriate arteries arise from the first part of the middle cerebral artery and enter the brain to supply the basal ganglia and internal capsule. Their rupture causes intraparenchymal hemorrhage, and the putamen is the most common site of hypertensive hemorrhage. Damage to the internal capsule produces weakness on the opposite side of the body.","whyNot":["Torn bridging veins cause a subdural hematoma, usually after head trauma in older adults or people with brain atrophy. Blood collects over the brain surface.","A ruptured berry aneurysm causes subarachnoid hemorrhage with a sudden, severe headache. Blood fills the subarachnoid space around the circle of Willis.","Correct.","A torn middle meningeal artery follows a skull fracture at the pterion and causes an epidural hematoma, often with a lucid interval."],"study":{"topics":["Blood supply of the basal ganglia and internal capsule","Hypertensive intracerebral hemorrhage"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-A2"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C03","system":"CV","dx":"Renovascular hypertension from atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis","codes":["CV-18-A2"],"case":"A 66-year-old woman is referred to the physician because of a blood pressure of 182/98 mm Hg despite treatment with three antihypertensive medications. Her blood pressure was normal until the age of 62 years. She has peripheral artery disease and has smoked one pack of cigarettes daily for 40 years. Physical examination shows an epigastric bruit. Funduscopic examination shows arteriolar narrowing without papilledema. Serum potassium concentration is 3.2 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0), serum creatinine concentration is 1.3 mg/dL (normal 0.6 to 1.1), and plasma renin activity is increased. Ultrasonography shows a right kidney 8.6 cm long and a left kidney 11.2 cm long (normal 10 to 12). CT angiography shows an 80% stenosis at the origin of the right renal artery caused by an atherosclerotic plaque. Diagnosis: renovascular hypertension due to right renal artery stenosis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C03-Q1","stem":"Assume that flow through the stenosis is laminar and follows Poiseuille's law. If the luminal radius at the stenosis is half its normal value, with length, viscosity, and all other factors unchanged, resistance in that segment increases by approximately which of the following factors?","options":["4-fold","8-fold","16-fold","32-fold"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.1","dok":2,"approach":["Write Poiseuille's law: resistance = 8 x viscosity x length / (pi x radius to the fourth power).","Halve the radius: radius to the fourth power falls to 1/16.","Resistance therefore rises 16-fold."],"why":"Poiseuille's law states that resistance is proportional to vessel length and blood viscosity and inversely proportional to the fourth power of the radius. Halving the radius raises resistance by 2 to the fourth power, which is 16. This is why a moderate narrowing can sharply cut flow and pressure beyond it, and why the kidney beyond a tight stenosis senses low perfusion pressure.","whyNot":["A 4-fold rise would follow if resistance depended on cross-sectional area (radius squared). Poiseuille's law uses the fourth power.","An 8-fold rise would follow from the third power of the radius, which does not appear in Poiseuille's law.","Correct.","A 32-fold rise would need a fifth-power relationship. Resistance varies with the fourth power of the radius."],"study":{"topics":["Poiseuille's law","Resistance and vessel radius"],"comps":["CV-11"]}},{"id":"CV-C03-Q2","stem":"Decreased perfusion pressure distal to the stenosis is sensed within the right kidney and stimulates renin release. Which of the following cells secretes the renin?","options":["Modified smooth muscle cells in the afferent arteriole","Podocytes on the outer surface of glomerular capillaries","Macula densa cells in the wall of the distal tubule","Interstitial fibroblasts near the peritubular capillaries"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-2","obj":"UR-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["Connect the clue (low pressure in the renal arterial tree) to the renin-angiotensin system.","Recall the juxtaglomerular apparatus and which of its parts stores renin.","Place the juxtaglomerular cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole."],"why":"Juxtaglomerular (granular) cells are modified smooth muscle cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole near the glomerular vascular pole. They store renin in granules and release it when stretch in the arteriole falls, when the macula densa senses low sodium chloride delivery, and with sympathetic beta-1 stimulation. In renal artery stenosis, low afferent arteriolar pressure in the affected kidney increases renin, which raises angiotensin II and aldosterone. Excess aldosterone explains her low potassium.","whyNot":["Correct.","Podocytes form the filtration slits of the glomerular barrier. They do not make renin.","Macula densa cells sense sodium chloride in the tubular fluid and signal the juxtaglomerular cells, but they do not secrete renin themselves.","Peritubular interstitial fibroblasts in the cortex make erythropoietin in response to low oxygen delivery."],"study":{"topics":["Juxtaglomerular apparatus","Renin release"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-18-A2"]}},{"id":"CV-C03-Q3","stem":"The plaque at the origin of the renal artery contains many lipid-laden macrophages (foam cells). Which of the following features of macrophage lipid handling best explains why these cells continue to take up cholesterol until they are engorged?","options":["Scavenger receptors for oxidized LDL escape feedback by cell cholesterol","LDL receptors increase as cell cholesterol rises, through SREBP activation","ABCA1 transporters move cholesterol from HDL into the macrophage","ACAT is inhibited, so free cholesterol cannot be stored as ester"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall how native LDL uptake is limited by feedback on the LDL receptor.","Note that LDL trapped in the intima is oxidized.","Identify the uptake route that lacks feedback control: scavenger receptors."],"why":"Native LDL enters cells through the LDL receptor, and rising cell cholesterol shuts off SREBP and lowers LDL receptor numbers, which limits uptake. LDL trapped in the intima becomes oxidized. Macrophages take up oxidized LDL through scavenger receptors such as SR-A and CD36, and these receptors are not reduced as cholesterol builds up. ACAT stores the excess as cholesteryl ester droplets, which give the cells their foamy look.","whyNot":["Correct.","SREBP is activated when cell cholesterol is low. High cell cholesterol suppresses LDL receptor synthesis, which limits native LDL uptake.","ABCA1 does the reverse: it exports cholesterol from the macrophage to apolipoprotein A-I to form HDL. Loss of ABCA1 (Tangier disease) causes cholesterol buildup in macrophages.","ACAT is active in foam cells. It esterifies cholesterol, and the stored cholesteryl ester droplets give the cells their foamy appearance."],"study":{"topics":["Foam cell formation","LDL receptor regulation"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C03-Q4","stem":"Her hypertension has been present for about 4 years. Histologic examination of the left (unaffected) kidney would most likely show which of the following findings?","options":["Ischemic atrophy with shrunken tubules and crowded glomeruli","Hyperplastic onion-skin thickening with fibrinoid necrosis","Concentric ring-like calcification of the arteriolar media","Hyaline arteriolosclerosis from chronic high pressure"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-A2","obj":"CV-18-A.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note that the stenosis shields the right kidney from high pressure while the left kidney receives it in full.","Recall the arteriolar change of chronic, non-malignant hypertension.","Rule out malignant hypertension: no papilledema and pressures below that range."],"why":"The stenosis protects the right kidney's small vessels from high pressure, while the left kidney receives the full systemic pressure. Over years, plasma proteins leak into the walls of its arterioles and smooth muscle cells lay down extra matrix, producing hyaline arteriolosclerosis. The arterioles narrow, and the kidney develops benign nephrosclerosis. The right kidney instead shrinks from ischemia, which matches its small size on ultrasound.","whyNot":["Ischemic atrophy is the expected change in the right kidney, downstream of the stenosis, and it explains that kidney's small size.","Onion-skin hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis with fibrinoid necrosis marks malignant hypertension, usually with pressures above about 200/120 mm Hg and papilledema. She has neither.","Ring-like medial calcification describes Mönckeberg medial sclerosis, which affects medium-sized muscular arteries in older adults and does not narrow the lumen.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hyaline and hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis","Renal artery stenosis pathology"],"comps":["CV-18-A2","CV-18-I3"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C04","system":"CV","dx":"Acute left-sided heart failure with pulmonary edema due to viral myocarditis","codes":["CV-18-B1"],"case":"A 29-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of a 3-day history of progressive shortness of breath. He cannot lie flat and is coughing up pink, frothy sputum. Two weeks ago, he had fever, sore throat, and diarrhea. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 98/64 mm Hg. Oxygen saturation is 86% on room air. Physical examination shows crackles over both lung fields and a third heart sound. A chest x-ray shows bilateral perihilar alveolar opacities and Kerley B lines. Serum troponin I concentration is increased. Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 25% (normal 52 to 72%). Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure is 28 mm Hg (normal 6 to 12). Diagnosis: acute left-sided heart failure with pulmonary edema due to viral myocarditis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C04-Q1","stem":"Which of the following changes in the forces across the pulmonary capillary wall is the primary cause of the fluid in his alveoli?","options":["Fall in plasma colloid osmotic pressure","Rise in pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure","Rise in capillary permeability to plasma proteins","Blockage of lymph drainage from the lung interstitium"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.5","dok":2,"approach":["Read the wedge pressure: 28 mm Hg means high left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure.","Apply the Starling forces: high capillary hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid out.","Separate cardiogenic edema (high wedge pressure) from permeability edema (normal wedge pressure)."],"why":"Pulmonary capillary pressure is normally about 7 mm Hg, well below the plasma colloid osmotic pressure of about 28 mm Hg, so the lungs stay dry. A failing left ventricle raises left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure, shown by his wedge pressure of 28 mm Hg. When capillary pressure reaches the colloid osmotic pressure and lymph flow can no longer keep up, fluid fills the interstitium (Kerley B lines) and then the alveoli.","whyNot":["Low oncotic pressure from low albumin, as in nephrotic syndrome or cirrhosis, favors edema, but nothing suggests low albumin here, and his wedge pressure explains the edema.","Correct.","Increased permeability causes noncardiogenic edema, as in acute respiratory distress syndrome or sepsis. In that setting the wedge pressure is not elevated.","Lymphatic blockage, as from tumor spread, causes interstitial fluid buildup. His lymphatics are working and are simply overwhelmed."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces in the lung","Cardiogenic pulmonary edema"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-B1"]}},{"id":"CV-C04-Q2","stem":"PCR of an endomyocardial biopsy specimen detects coxsackievirus B3 RNA. Which of the following best describes this virus and its most likely route of transmission to this patient?","options":["Enveloped, negative-sense single-stranded RNA, spread by respiratory droplets","Nonenveloped, double-stranded DNA, spread by droplets and fomites","Enveloped, double-stranded DNA, spread by close contact with saliva","Nonenveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA, spread fecal-orally"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.6","dok":2,"approach":["Place coxsackievirus in its family: an enterovirus within the picornaviruses.","Recall picornavirus structure: small, nonenveloped, positive-sense RNA.","Match the route to his diarrheal prodrome: fecal-oral."],"why":"Coxsackievirus B is an enterovirus in the picornavirus family. It is small and nonenveloped, with a positive-sense, single-stranded RNA genome that can be translated directly as mRNA. Without an envelope it survives stomach acid and spreads mainly by the fecal-oral route, which fits his diarrheal illness two weeks earlier. It enters cardiac myocytes through the coxsackievirus and adenovirus receptor (CAR).","whyNot":["This describes influenza, which spreads by droplets and can occasionally cause myocarditis. It is not an enterovirus.","This describes adenovirus, another cause of viral myocarditis, especially in children. It is a DNA virus.","This describes herpesviruses such as Epstein-Barr virus and cytomegalovirus, which spread through saliva and other close contact.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Picornaviruses and enteroviruses","Viral causes of myocarditis"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K6"]}},{"id":"CV-C04-Q3","stem":"His wedge pressure was measured with a balloon-tipped catheter advanced into a small branch of the pulmonary artery and inflated until flow stopped. Which of the following best explains why this measurement approximates his left ventricular filling pressure?","options":["The catheter tip senses pressure in the bronchial arteries","A still column of blood links the tip to the left atrium","The balloon transmits right ventricular pressure forward","The tip lies in a pulmonary vein beside the left atrium"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-15","obj":"CV-15.4","dok":3,"approach":["Trace the path from a pulmonary artery branch through capillaries and pulmonary veins to the left atrium.","Apply the rule that with no flow there is no pressure drop along a vessel.","Link left atrial pressure to left ventricular pressure in diastole through the open mitral valve."],"why":"Inflating the balloon blocks flow in that branch. With no flow there is no pressure drop along the vessels downstream, so the blood in the capillaries and pulmonary veins forms a static column connected to the left atrium. The tip therefore reads left atrial pressure. In diastole the mitral valve is open, so left atrial pressure approximates left ventricular end-diastolic pressure, unless a problem such as mitral stenosis separates them.","whyNot":["Bronchial arteries come from the systemic circulation (the aorta or intercostal arteries) and supply the airways. The catheter lies in a pulmonary artery branch.","Correct.","The inflated balloon blocks the pressure coming from the right ventricle. Before inflation the catheter reads pulmonary artery pressure.","The catheter stays in the pulmonary artery tree and cannot pass through the capillaries. It reflects venous pressure only through the static column of blood."],"study":{"topics":["Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure","Pulmonary circulation pathway"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-3"]}},{"id":"CV-C04-Q4","stem":"The biopsy specimen also shows lymphocytes infiltrating the myocardium adjacent to necrotic myocytes. The infected myocytes display viral peptides on their surface. Which of the following immune mechanisms most directly kills these infected myocytes?","options":["Immune complexes depositing in small vessel walls","CD4 T cells recognizing viral peptide on MHC class I","IgE on mast cells triggering histamine release","CD8 T cells binding viral peptide on MHC class I"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.6","dok":2,"approach":["Note that viral proteins made inside the myocyte are presented on MHC class I.","Recall which T cells recognize MHC class I: CD8 cytotoxic T cells.","Link the lymphocytic infiltrate with myocyte necrosis to cell-mediated killing."],"why":"Cardiac myocytes, like nearly all nucleated cells, present peptides from viral proteins made in their cytoplasm on MHC class I. Cytotoxic CD8 T cells recognize these complexes and kill the cell with perforin and granzymes or Fas ligand. This clears the virus but also destroys myocytes, and a lymphocytic infiltrate with myocyte necrosis is the hallmark of viral myocarditis. Loss of myocytes weakens contraction and leads to acute left heart failure.","whyNot":["Immune complex deposition (type III hypersensitivity) causes vasculitis, as in serum sickness or lupus. It inflames vessel walls and does not target infected myocytes.","CD4 T cells recognize peptide on MHC class II, found mainly on antigen-presenting cells. They help the response but do not directly kill infected myocytes.","IgE on mast cells drives type I hypersensitivity, such as anaphylaxis and allergic asthma. It plays no direct part in killing virus-infected myocytes.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["MHC class I and cytotoxic T cells","Viral myocarditis immunopathology"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-K6"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C05","system":"CV","dx":"Right-sided heart failure (cor pulmonale) due to cystic fibrosis lung disease","codes":["CV-18-B2"],"case":"A 24-year-old woman with cystic fibrosis (homozygous for F508del) comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of ankle swelling, abdominal fullness, and fatigue. She has advanced lung disease with chronic airway infection by a mucoid gram-negative rod, and her FEV1 is 28% of predicted. Physical examination shows jugular venous distention to 7 cm above the sternal angle (normal 3 cm or less), a loud pulmonic component of the second heart sound, a tender, enlarged liver, and pitting edema to the mid-shins. On room air, arterial PO2 is 54 mm Hg (normal 80 to 100). Hematocrit is 52% (normal 36 to 46), and serum ALT activity is 88 U/L (normal 7 to 35). Echocardiography shows a dilated right ventricle, a flattened septum, and normal left ventricular function. Diagnosis: right-sided heart failure (cor pulmonale) due to cystic fibrosis lung disease.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C05-Q1","stem":"Her F508del mutation deletes one phenylalanine residue from the CFTR protein. Which of the following best explains the loss of CFTR function in her airway epithelial cells?","options":["A premature stop codon that produces a short, truncated protein","A protein that reaches the cell membrane but rarely opens its channel","A misfolded protein held in the endoplasmic reticulum and degraded","A promoter mutation that sharply lowers transcription of the gene"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-10","obj":"PU-10.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note the mutation type: an in-frame deletion of one amino acid.","Recall what happens to misfolded membrane proteins: endoplasmic reticulum quality control and proteasomal degradation.","Connect the missing chloride channel to thick mucus, chronic lung disease, and pulmonary hypertension."],"why":"F508del is an in-frame deletion of three nucleotides that removes phenylalanine at position 508. The protein misfolds, is caught by quality control in the endoplasmic reticulum, and is degraded by the proteasome, so little CFTR reaches the apical membrane (a class II processing defect). Without this chloride channel, airway surface liquid becomes dehydrated and mucus thickens, leading to chronic infection, bronchiectasis, hypoxemia, and eventually pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale.","whyNot":["Nonsense mutations such as G542X create a premature stop codon (class I). F508del removes one amino acid and keeps the reading frame.","Gating mutations such as G551D (class III) produce a protein that reaches the membrane but opens poorly.","Correct.","Promoter or splicing mutations reduce the amount of normal protein made. F508del leaves transcription intact and changes how the protein folds."],"study":{"topics":["CFTR mutation classes","Protein folding and ER quality control"],"comps":["CV-14"]}},{"id":"CV-C05-Q2","stem":"Her mucoid gram-negative rod is oxidase positive, produces a blue-green pigment, and has persisted for years despite a dense neutrophilic infiltrate in her airways. Which of the following bacterial products best explains this persistence?","options":["Alginate, which forms a protective biofilm matrix","Lipid A, which binds Toll-like receptor 4","Flagellin, which drives motility through airway mucus","Exotoxin A, which ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-13","obj":"PU-13.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the organism: an oxidase-positive, pigment-making, mucoid gram-negative rod in cystic fibrosis is Pseudomonas aeruginosa.","Ask which product shields bacteria from phagocytes over the long term.","Choose the exopolysaccharide that defines the mucoid form: alginate."],"why":"The organism is Pseudomonas aeruginosa. In the cystic fibrosis lung it switches to a mucoid form that overproduces alginate, an exopolysaccharide. Alginate builds a biofilm that blocks phagocytosis by neutrophils and macrophages, scavenges oxidants, and limits antibody and antibiotic penetration. Neutrophils keep arriving but cannot clear the bacteria, and their proteases and oxidants damage the airways, driving bronchiectasis, hypoxemia, and pulmonary hypertension.","whyNot":["Correct.","Lipid A is the toxic part of endotoxin. It activates TLR4 and drives inflammation and septic shock, which helps the host detect the bacteria.","Flagellin is detected by TLR5, and Pseudomonas from chronic cystic fibrosis infection often loses its flagella, which helps it avoid detection.","Exotoxin A blocks host protein synthesis and kills tissue cells, much like diphtheria toxin. It damages tissue but does not shield the bacteria from clearance."],"study":{"topics":["Pseudomonas aeruginosa virulence factors","Biofilms and immune evasion"],"comps":["CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C05-Q3","stem":"Her jugular venous pressure is assessed using the right internal jugular vein with the head of the bed elevated to 45 degrees. Which of the following best explains why this vein is preferred for estimating right atrial pressure?","options":["It lies superficial to sternocleidomastoid, where it is easy to see","It drains straight into the right atrium through its own opening","It lines up almost straight with the SVC and right atrium","It reaches the heart by crossing the midline in front of the aorta"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","obj":"CV-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the venous path from the right internal jugular vein to the right atrium.","Note that a short, straight column transmits right atrial pressure best.","Rule out options that describe the external jugular or the left-sided veins."],"why":"The right internal jugular vein joins the right subclavian vein to form the right brachiocephalic vein, which runs almost straight down into the superior vena cava (SVC) and right atrium. This short, direct path lets the height of the pulsating column reflect right atrial pressure. In right heart failure, rising right atrial pressure shows as distended neck veins. The external jugular vein is easier to see but takes a more angled course through the deep fascia, so it is less reliable.","whyNot":["This describes the external jugular vein. The internal jugular lies deep to sternocleidomastoid, so its pulsation is seen as movement in the neck rather than as a visible vein.","No neck vein opens directly into the right atrium. Blood reaches it through the brachiocephalic veins and the SVC.","Correct.","This describes the left brachiocephalic vein, which carries left internal jugular blood across the midline. That longer route makes the left side less reliable."],"study":{"topics":["Veins of the neck and thorax","Jugular venous pressure"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-B2"]}},{"id":"CV-C05-Q4","stem":"A liver biopsy specimen would most likely show which of the following patterns?","options":["Fatty change with large lipid droplets in hepatocytes","Congestion and hepatocyte loss around central veins","Bile duct destruction by granulomas in portal tracts","Portal tract lymphocytes with interface hepatitis"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-B2","obj":"CV-18-B.2","dok":2,"approach":["Trace back-pressure from the right atrium through the inferior vena cava and hepatic veins to the central veins.","Recall that the central (zone 3) hepatocytes are also the least oxygenated.","Choose congestion and injury around central veins."],"why":"Right heart failure raises right atrial pressure, which passes back through the inferior vena cava and hepatic veins into the central veins of the liver lobules. Blood pools around the central veins (zone 3), the zone farthest from the oxygen-rich portal inflow. Hepatocytes there atrophy and may die, while better-oxygenated periportal cells survive. The dark, congested centers against paler surrounding tissue give the cut liver a nutmeg look.","whyNot":["Fatty change (steatosis) comes from alcohol, obesity, insulin resistance, or other metabolic causes. It does not reflect venous congestion and would not explain a tender, swollen liver with distended neck veins.","Correct.","Granulomatous bile duct destruction is the classic lesion of primary biliary cholangitis.","Interface hepatitis with portal lymphocytes is the pattern of chronic viral or autoimmune hepatitis."],"study":{"topics":["Chronic passive congestion of the liver","Hepatic lobule zones"],"comps":["CV-18-B2"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C06","system":"CV","dx":"Chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction from Chagas cardiomyopathy, with neurohormonal activation","codes":["CV-18-B1"],"case":"A 54-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of progressive fatigue, shortness of breath after walking one block, and leg swelling. He moved to the United States from rural Bolivia 25 years ago. Pulse is 96/min, and blood pressure is 102/68 mm Hg. Physical examination shows bibasilar crackles, a third heart sound, and pitting edema of the legs. An ECG shows right bundle branch block and left anterior fascicular block. Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle, an ejection fraction of 27% (normal 52 to 72%), and a small apical aneurysm. Serum BNP is 1,240 pg/mL (normal less than 100), sodium 130 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145), and creatinine 1.4 mg/dL (normal 0.7 to 1.3). IgG antibodies to Trypanosoma cruzi are positive on two assays. Diagnosis: Chagas cardiomyopathy with chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C06-Q1","stem":"He most likely acquired the infection during childhood. Which of the following is the usual route of transmission of this organism to humans in endemic areas?","options":["Bite of an infected female sandfly","Feces of an infected triatomine bug","Bite of an infected Anopheles mosquito","Bite of an infected tsetse fly"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism from the serology: Trypanosoma cruzi, the cause of Chagas disease.","Recall its vector: the triatomine (reduviid, kissing) bug.","Note that the parasite passes in the bug's feces during or after the blood meal."],"why":"Trypanosoma cruzi is carried by triatomine (reduviid, or kissing) bugs that feed at night, often on the face. The bug defecates while feeding, and trypomastigotes in its feces enter through the bite wound or the conjunctiva when the person rubs the area. Inside host cells, including cardiac myocytes, the parasite multiplies as amastigotes. Decades later, about 20 to 30 percent of infected people develop chronic heart disease. Blood transfusion, organ transplant, mother-to-child spread, and contaminated food are less common routes.","whyNot":["Sandflies transmit Leishmania, which causes cutaneous or visceral leishmaniasis.","Correct.","Anopheles mosquitoes transmit Plasmodium species, the cause of malaria.","Tsetse flies transmit Trypanosoma brucei, the cause of African sleeping sickness. That parasite passes in the fly's saliva during the bite."],"study":{"topics":["Trypanosoma cruzi life cycle","Vector-borne protozoa"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K2"]}},{"id":"CV-C06-Q2","stem":"Myocytes from failing hearts such as his show smaller calcium transients with each beat and slower removal of calcium from the cytosol. Which of the following changes most directly reduces the calcium available for each contraction?","options":["More phospholamban phosphorylation by protein kinase A","Less sodium-calcium exchange, trapping calcium in the cytosol","Lower SERCA2a activity refilling the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Tighter closure of ryanodine receptors during diastole"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-5","obj":"CV-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that each beat releases calcium stored in the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","Ask which pump refills that store: SERCA2a.","Link less refilling to smaller release, weaker contraction, and slower relaxation."],"why":"With each beat, calcium released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum through ryanodine receptors binds troponin C. Relaxation and the next beat depend on SERCA2a pumping that calcium back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum. In heart failure, SERCA2a expression and activity fall, and its inhibitor phospholamban is less phosphorylated, so it inhibits SERCA2a more. Less calcium is stored, less is released with the next beat, and contraction weakens. Slower reuptake also slows relaxation.","whyNot":["Protein kinase A phosphorylation of phospholamban relieves its inhibition of SERCA2a and raises stored calcium, as during sympathetic stimulation. In failing hearts phospholamban phosphorylation is reduced.","The sodium-calcium exchanger is increased in failing myocytes and removes more calcium from the cell. If exchange were reduced, more calcium would stay in the cell.","Correct.","In heart failure, ryanodine receptors tend to leak calcium during diastole, which further drains the stores. Tighter closure would preserve them."],"study":{"topics":["Excitation-contraction coupling","Calcium handling in heart failure"],"comps":["CV-5","CV-18-B1"]}},{"id":"CV-C06-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best describes the change in his left ventricular pressure-volume loop compared with that of a healthy adult?","options":["Flatter end-systolic line, loop at larger volumes","Narrower loop shifted to a smaller end-diastolic volume","Steeper end-diastolic curve, normal end-systolic volume","No isovolumetric relaxation, with a much wider loop"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","obj":"CV-6.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the primary defect: reduced contractility (ejection fraction 27%).","Recall that contractility is shown by the slope of the end-systolic pressure-volume line.","Predict the result: larger end-systolic and end-diastolic volumes, with a small stroke volume."],"why":"Contractility is shown by the slope of the end-systolic pressure-volume relationship. In systolic heart failure that slope falls, so at any afterload the ventricle empties less and end-systolic volume rises. The ventricle dilates and end-diastolic volume rises as the body retains fluid to raise preload. The loop moves to the right and becomes narrow, because stroke volume is small compared with the large end-diastolic volume, giving his ejection fraction of 27%.","whyNot":["Correct.","A narrow loop at small volumes reflects low preload, as in hemorrhage or dehydration. His ventricle is dilated.","A steep end-diastolic curve with normal systolic function describes a stiff ventricle, as in heart failure with preserved ejection fraction from hypertensive hypertrophy.","Loss of a true isovolumetric relaxation phase occurs in aortic regurgitation, because blood leaks back into the ventricle as soon as ejection ends. That loop widens from a large stroke volume."],"study":{"topics":["Pressure-volume loops","Contractility and the ESPVR"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-B1"]}},{"id":"CV-C06-Q4","stem":"His serum sodium concentration is 130 mEq/L although his total body sodium is increased. Which of the following hormones most directly causes the low serum sodium concentration?","options":["Norepinephrine acting on renal tubular alpha-1 receptors","Aldosterone acting on collecting duct principal cells","Cortisol acting on renal mineralocorticoid receptors","Vasopressin acting on collecting duct V2 receptors"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-B1","obj":"CV-18-B.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize hyponatremia with volume overload as a sign of excess free water.","Ask which hormone adds free water: vasopressin (ADH).","Explain its release despite low osmolality: baroreceptors sense low effective arterial volume."],"why":"In heart failure, low cardiac output reduces effective arterial blood volume. Arterial baroreceptors sense this and trigger nonosmotic release of vasopressin (ADH), which overrides the normal suppression of ADH by low plasma osmolality. Vasopressin binds V2 receptors on collecting duct principal cells and inserts aquaporin-2 channels, so free water is reabsorbed. Water retained in excess of sodium dilutes plasma sodium. Low serum sodium in heart failure signals strong neurohormonal activation and a worse prognosis.","whyNot":["Norepinephrine raises heart rate, contractility, renin release, and tubular sodium reabsorption, which adds to fluid retention. It does not directly add free water.","Aldosterone increases sodium reabsorption, and water follows, which expands volume and causes edema. On its own it does not dilute plasma sodium, and it also increases potassium secretion.","At high levels cortisol can act on mineralocorticoid receptors and retain sodium, which tends to keep sodium up. Low sodium is a feature of cortisol deficiency, through increased vasopressin.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Neurohormonal activation in heart failure","Hyponatremia in heart failure"],"comps":["CV-18-B1","CV-11"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C55","system":"CV","dx":"Acute pulmonary embolism","codes":["CV-18-I7"],"case":"A 46-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of sudden shortness of breath and sharp right-sided chest pain that worsens with inspiration. He returned from a 14-hour flight 3 days ago, and his right calf has been sore and swollen since the trip. Pulse is 116/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 104/68 mmHg. Pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 89%. Arterial blood gas analysis on room air at sea level shows pH 7.49 (7.35 to 7.45), PaCO2 30 mmHg (35 to 45), and PaO2 62 mmHg (80 to 100). D-dimer is 2,400 ng/mL FEU (below 500). CT pulmonary angiography shows filling defects in both lower lobe pulmonary arteries. Echocardiography shows a dilated right ventricle. The diagnosis is acute pulmonary embolism.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C55-Q1","stem":"Using the alveolar gas equation (PAO2 = 150 mmHg minus PaCO2/0.8), which of the following best describes this patient's alveolar-arterial (A-a) oxygen gradient and the primary mechanism of his hypoxemia?","options":["About 10 mmHg, caused by alveolar hypoventilation","About 10 mmHg, caused by low inspired oxygen pressure","About 50 mmHg, caused by ventilation-perfusion mismatch","About 50 mmHg, caused by thickening of the alveolar membrane"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","obj":"CV-15.5","dok":3,"approach":["Calculate PAO2: 150 minus (30 divided by 0.8) is about 112 mmHg.","Subtract PaO2: 112 minus 62 gives an A-a gradient of about 50 mmHg, well above the normal 5 to 15 mmHg.","A wide gradient with a low PaCO2 rules out hypoventilation and low inspired oxygen; in PE the cause is ventilation-perfusion mismatch."],"why":"The clots block flow to ventilated lung, so blood is diverted to the remaining vessels, which are now overperfused relative to their ventilation. Blood leaving those low V/Q units is poorly oxygenated, and the A-a gradient widens to about 50 mmHg. Hypoxemia and pain drive hyperventilation, which explains the low PaCO2 and respiratory alkalosis.","whyNot":["Hypoventilation raises PaCO2 and keeps the A-a gradient normal. His PaCO2 is low. This fits opioid overdose or neuromuscular weakness.","Low inspired oxygen (high altitude) lowers PaO2 with a normal gradient. He was tested at sea level, and his gradient is wide.","Correct.","Membrane thickening (pulmonary fibrosis) does widen the gradient, but nothing in this case thickens the alveolar wall. The problem here is blocked blood flow."],"study":{"topics":["Alveolar gas equation and A-a gradient","Causes of hypoxemia","V/Q mismatch in pulmonary embolism"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-18-I7"]}},{"id":"CV-C55-Q2","stem":"CT pulmonary angiography shows no infarction of the lung parenchyma distal to the occluded arteries. Which of the following vessels most likely maintains the viability of this lung tissue?","options":["Bronchial veins draining lung tissue into the azygos system","Pericardiophrenic arteries supplying the lower lobes","Internal thoracic arteries entering through the visceral pleura","Bronchial arteries arising from the systemic circulation"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-18-I7","obj":"CV-18-I.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize that the lung has a dual blood supply.","Identify the second, systemic source: the bronchial arteries, mostly from the descending thoracic aorta.","Link this to the fact that most emboli in healthy people do not cause infarction."],"why":"The bronchial arteries arise from the systemic circulation, mainly the descending thoracic aorta, and supply the bronchi and supporting lung tissue. When a pulmonary artery branch is blocked, bronchial flow keeps the tissue alive. Infarction becomes likely when this backup is weak, as in left heart failure or existing lung disease.","whyNot":["Veins drain blood away from tissue and cannot deliver oxygen. Bronchial veins do drain into the azygos system, but that is irrelevant to survival.","The pericardiophrenic arteries run with the phrenic nerves to the pericardium and diaphragm. They do not supply lung parenchyma.","The internal thoracic arteries supply the anterior chest wall and breast. They do not enter the lung through the visceral pleura.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Dual blood supply of the lung","Bronchial arteries","Pulmonary infarction"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-H3"]}},{"id":"CV-C55-Q3","stem":"One hour later, his blood pressure decreases to 86/54 mmHg. Repeat echocardiography shows flattening of the interventricular septum with bowing toward the left ventricle during diastole. Which of the following mechanisms most likely explains the decrease in his cardiac output?","options":["Pericardial fluid compressing all four chambers of the heart","Systemic vasoconstriction raising left ventricular afterload","Septal bowing into the left ventricle that limits its filling","Coronary artery embolism reducing left ventricular contraction"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","obj":"CV-15.4","dok":3,"approach":["Clots suddenly raise pulmonary vascular resistance, so right ventricular afterload rises.","The thin-walled right ventricle dilates, and because both ventricles share the septum and pericardium, the septum shifts left.","A smaller left ventricle fills less, so stroke volume and cardiac output fall (ventricular interdependence)."],"why":"Acute right ventricular pressure overload dilates the right ventricle. Inside the fixed pericardial space, the septum bows into the left ventricle and reduces its preload. Left ventricular stroke volume falls, causing hypotension. Low aortic pressure and high right ventricular wall stress also reduce right coronary perfusion, which explains the troponin rise often seen in PE with right ventricular strain.","whyNot":["Tamponade would show a pericardial effusion on echocardiography. His echo shows a dilated right ventricle and septal shift instead.","Higher afterload would raise blood pressure. His problem is low left ventricular preload, and his pressure is falling.","Correct.","Venous clots travel to the lungs and cannot reach the coronary arteries unless they cross a right-to-left shunt. Nothing here suggests that."],"study":{"topics":["Acute right heart strain","Ventricular interdependence","Obstructive shock"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-18-B2"]}},{"id":"CV-C55-Q4","stem":"Which of the following sequences of enzymatic steps produces the fragment measured by this patient's D-dimer assay?","options":["Factor XIIIa cross-links fibrin, then plasmin cleaves the clot","Plasmin cleaves circulating fibrinogen that was never cross-linked","Tissue plasminogen activator directly digests cross-linked fibrin","Thrombin cleaves fibrinogen, then antithrombin breaks down fibrin"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-H6","obj":"CV-18-H.1","dok":2,"approach":["D-dimer is two D domains held together by a covalent cross-link.","That cross-link is made only by factor XIIIa in a formed clot.","Plasmin must then break the cross-linked fibrin to release the fragment."],"why":"Thrombin converts fibrinogen to fibrin and activates factor XIII. Factor XIIIa, a transglutaminase, covalently links neighboring D domains of fibrin. When plasmin degrades this stabilized clot, the linked D domains are released as D-dimer. A high value shows that a clot formed and is being broken down, so it is sensitive but not specific.","whyNot":["Correct.","Plasmin acting on fibrinogen alone makes fibrinogen degradation products without D-dimer, because no cross-link exists. This pattern occurs in primary fibrinogenolysis.","Tissue plasminogen activator converts plasminogen to plasmin. Plasmin then does the work of digesting the fibrin.","Antithrombin inhibits thrombin and factor Xa. It does not degrade fibrin."],"study":{"topics":["Fibrin stabilization by factor XIII","Fibrinolysis","D-dimer"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-H6"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C56","system":"CV","dx":"Deep vein thrombosis after surgery and immobility","codes":["CV-18-I10"],"case":"A 71-year-old woman is evaluated because of aching and swelling of the left leg 5 days after surgical repair of a left hip fracture. Because of pain, she has been mostly confined to bed since the operation. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F). Physical examination shows a warm, tender left calf with pitting edema; its circumference is 4 cm greater than that of the right calf. Compression duplex ultrasonography shows noncompressible left popliteal and femoral veins containing echogenic thrombus. Platelet count is 260,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000). The diagnosis is proximal deep vein thrombosis after surgery and immobility. Treatment with unfractionated heparin is started.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C56-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the composition of this patient's venous thrombus?","options":["Many trapped red cells in a fibrin mesh, formed where flow is slow","Mostly platelets with little fibrin, formed where shear stress is high","Platelets and fibrin attached to a damaged heart valve leaflet","Separated layers of red cells and plasma, formed after flow stops"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H6","obj":"CV-18-H.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note the setting: a vein with stasis.","Low flow lets coagulation factors build up, so fibrin forms and traps red cells.","Choose the red (stasis) thrombus."],"why":"Venous thrombi form under low flow, where activated clotting factors are not washed away. Fibrin forms a mesh that traps many red cells, so the thrombus is red and gelatinous and often builds a long tail in the direction of flow. This fibrin-rich makeup is why venous thrombosis is treated with anticoagulants.","whyNot":["Correct.","This is an arterial (pale) thrombus, which forms on a ruptured plaque under high shear and is platelet rich. That is why arterial disease is treated with antiplatelet drugs.","This describes a valve vegetation, as in endocarditis. Her clot is in a leg vein.","This describes a postmortem clot, which settles into layers after circulation stops and is not attached to the vessel wall."],"study":{"topics":["Venous versus arterial thrombi","Virchow triad"],"comps":["CV-18-H6","CV-18-I10"]}},{"id":"CV-C56-Q2","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms that normally promotes venous return from the deep veins of the leg was most reduced by her bed rest?","options":["Sympathetic venoconstriction that keeps the deep veins narrowed","Plasma oncotic pressure that pulls tissue fluid into the leg veins","Arteriolar constriction that limits inflow of blood into the leg","Calf muscle contraction that squeezes blood through one-way valves"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-I10","obj":"CV-18-I.8","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the factor in Virchow triad that immobility causes: stasis.","Ask what normally moves venous blood upward from the legs against gravity.","The skeletal muscle pump, with venous valves, drives venous return from the legs."],"why":"The deep leg veins run within muscle compartments. Each calf contraction squeezes them, and the one-way valves let blood move only toward the heart. Without movement this pump stops, blood pools in the valve pockets and soleal sinuses, and stasis allows clotting factors to accumulate.","whyNot":["Sympathetic venous tone is still present during bed rest. Venoconstriction actually pushes blood toward the heart, so it is not the force she lost.","Plasma oncotic pressure is unchanged by immobility. It governs fluid shifts across capillaries, not forward flow in large veins.","Arteriolar tone controls inflow to capillaries. Bed rest does not remove it, and less inflow would not explain pooling in the veins.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Skeletal muscle pump","Venous return","Venous valves"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-12"]}},{"id":"CV-C56-Q3","stem":"Iliofemoral deep vein thrombosis occurs more often in the left leg than in the right leg. Which of the following anatomic relationships best explains this pattern?","options":["The left renal vein crossing between the aorta and superior mesenteric artery","The right common iliac artery crossing anterior to the left common iliac vein","The left common iliac artery crossing anterior to the right common iliac vein","The inguinal ligament pressing on the left femoral vein during hip flexion"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.4","dok":3,"approach":["Picture the aortic bifurcation at about L4 and the IVC formed to the right of midline at about L5.","The left common iliac vein must cross to the right to reach the IVC.","On its way it passes behind the right common iliac artery, which presses it against the lower lumbar spine."],"why":"The IVC lies to the right of the aorta, so the left common iliac vein takes a longer course across the midline. The right common iliac artery crosses in front of it and can compress it against the fifth lumbar vertebra. Chronic pulsatile compression narrows the vein and causes stasis. When this compression causes symptoms, it is called May-Thurner syndrome.","whyNot":["Compression of the left renal vein between the aorta and SMA (nutcracker) causes left flank pain, hematuria, or a left varicocele, not leg DVT.","Correct.","The right common iliac vein runs a short, nearly straight course to the IVC and is not crossed by the left common iliac artery.","The inguinal ligament is symmetric on both sides and does not explain a left-sided pattern."],"study":{"topics":["Iliac vessel relationships","May-Thurner anatomy","Formation of the IVC"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I10"]}},{"id":"CV-C56-Q4","stem":"On day 7 of heparin therapy, her platelet count is 98,000/µL, and duplex ultrasonography shows a new thrombus in the right femoral vein. Which of the following mechanisms best explains both findings?","options":["IgM cold agglutinins fixing complement on platelet surfaces","IgE on mast cells binding heparin and releasing histamine","IgG against platelet factor 4 and heparin activating platelets","Cytotoxic T cells destroying megakaryocytes in the bone marrow"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-H6","obj":"CV-18-H.2","dok":3,"approach":["Platelets fell by more than half about 5 to 10 days after heparin started, and a new clot formed.","Low platelets plus new thrombosis points to heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT).","Name the mechanism: IgG antibodies to platelet factor 4 bound to heparin."],"why":"Heparin binds platelet factor 4 (PF4), creating a new epitope. IgG antibodies form against the PF4-heparin complex, and the immune complexes bind platelet Fc receptors. This activates platelets, which releases more PF4 and procoagulant microparticles, so platelets are consumed and cleared while thrombin generation rises. The result is a low count with new clots, so heparin must be stopped and replaced with a non-heparin anticoagulant.","whyNot":["Cold agglutinins are IgM antibodies against red cells, as in Mycoplasma infection. They cause hemolysis, not falling platelets with clots.","An IgE-mediated reaction causes hives, wheezing, or anaphylaxis within minutes. It does not lower platelets or cause clots.","Correct.","T cells attacking megakaryocytes would reduce platelet production but would not cause new thrombosis."],"study":{"topics":["Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","Antibody-mediated platelet activation"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-H6"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C57","system":"CV","dx":"Deep vein thrombosis in a patient with factor V Leiden","codes":["CV-18-I10"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 3-day history of pain and swelling of the right leg. She has had no recent surgery, trauma, or prolonged travel. She began taking a combined estrogen-progestin oral contraceptive 4 months ago. Her mother had a deep vein thrombosis at age 40 years. Duplex ultrasonography shows thrombus in the right femoral vein. Prothrombin time is 12 seconds (11 to 13.5), and activated partial thromboplastin time is 30 seconds (25 to 35). An activated protein C resistance assay is abnormal, and genetic testing shows heterozygosity for factor V Leiden (F5 R506Q). The diagnosis is deep vein thrombosis in a patient with factor V Leiden.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C57-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the molecular mechanism by which this patient's variant promotes thrombosis?","options":["Activated factor V resists cleavage by activated protein C","Thrombomodulin fails to bind thrombin on the endothelial surface","Antithrombin cannot bind heparan sulfate on endothelial cells","A 3' untranslated region change raises plasma prothrombin levels"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-H6","obj":"CV-18-H.2","dok":2,"approach":["R506Q is a single amino acid change: arginine 506 becomes glutamine.","Arginine 506 is a cleavage site where activated protein C inactivates factor Va.","Factor Va lasts longer, so more thrombin is made."],"why":"Factor V Leiden is a missense point mutation (G to A) that replaces arginine 506 with glutamine. Activated protein C, with protein S as cofactor, normally shuts off factor Va by cleaving it, and arginine 506 is the first and fastest cleavage site. The mutant factor Va resists that cleavage, so prothrombinase stays active longer and thrombin generation rises. Routine PT and aPTT are normal because the clotting factors are present in normal amounts and work normally.","whyNot":["Correct.","Thrombomodulin defects are rare and would reduce protein C activation. Her abnormal test is resistance of factor Va to protein C that is already activated.","Antithrombin deficiency or dysfunction is a separate inherited thrombophilia. It does not explain APC resistance.","This describes the prothrombin G20210A variant, the second most common inherited thrombophilia. Her test shows a factor V variant."],"study":{"topics":["Factor V Leiden","Protein C pathway","Inherited thrombophilias"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C57-Q2","stem":"Her husband is tested and is also heterozygous for factor V Leiden. Which of the following is the probability that their first child will be homozygous for the variant?","options":["12.5%","25%","50%","75%"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.1","dok":2,"approach":["Each heterozygous parent passes the variant allele with a probability of 1/2.","A homozygous child must receive the variant from both parents.","Multiply: 1/2 times 1/2 equals 1/4, or 25%."],"why":"Factor V Leiden is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern for thrombosis risk, with incomplete penetrance. Two heterozygous parents give a 25% chance of a homozygous child, a 50% chance of a heterozygous child, and a 25% chance of an unaffected child. Homozygotes have a much higher lifetime risk of clotting than heterozygotes, which is why this calculation matters for counseling.","whyNot":["12.5% would apply if one more independent 1/2 event were required. With two heterozygous parents there are only two transmissions.","Correct.","50% is the chance of a heterozygous child, or of any affected child if only one parent carried the variant.","75% is the chance that a child carries at least one copy (heterozygous or homozygous), not the chance of being homozygous."],"study":{"topics":["Punnett square for two heterozygotes","Autosomal dominant inheritance with incomplete penetrance"],"comps":["CV-14"]}},{"id":"CV-C57-Q3","stem":"Two days later, she develops sudden weakness of the left arm. MRI of the brain shows a small acute infarct in the territory of the right middle cerebral artery. Echocardiography with agitated saline shows bubbles passing from the right atrium to the left atrium during a Valsalva maneuver through a flap-like opening in the atrial septum; no septal tissue is missing. Failure of which of the following normal processes most likely allowed a leg thrombus to reach her brain?","options":["Obliteration of the ductus venosus to form the ligamentum venosum","Sealing of the opening between septum primum and septum secundum","Division of the truncus arteriosus by the aorticopulmonary septum","Closure of the ductus arteriosus soon after the first breaths"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","obj":"CV-1.2","dok":3,"approach":["A venous clot causing a stroke means it crossed from the right side of the heart to the left (paradoxical embolism).","A flap-like atrial opening that opens with straining is a patent foramen ovale.","The foramen ovale closes when the septum primum seals against the septum secundum after birth."],"why":"Before birth, the foramen ovale lets blood pass from the right to the left atrium through the gap between the septum secundum and the septum primum. After birth, left atrial pressure rises above right, pressing the septum primum against the septum secundum, and the two usually fuse. In about a quarter of adults they never fuse, leaving a patent foramen ovale. Straining raises right atrial pressure, opens the flap, and lets a venous clot reach the systemic arteries.","whyNot":["The ductus venosus bypasses the fetal liver. Its persistence would not create an atrial-level shunt.","Correct.","Failed truncal division causes persistent truncus arteriosus, a cyanotic defect present from infancy, not an adult atrial flap.","A patent ductus arteriosus connects the aorta and pulmonary artery, not the atria, and usually shunts left to right after birth."],"study":{"topics":["Patent foramen ovale","Paradoxical embolism","Atrial septation"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-G3"]}},{"id":"CV-C57-Q4","stem":"Her thrombosis risk increased sharply after she began taking the combined oral contraceptive. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this added risk?","options":["Progestin destroys the valves in the deep veins of the lower leg","Estrogen causes thrombocytosis by raising thrombopoietin output","Estrogen stimulates erythropoietin release and thickens the blood","Estrogen raises hepatic clotting factors and lowers protein S"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I10","obj":"CV-18-I.8","dok":3,"approach":["Estrogen acts on the liver, where most clotting proteins are made.","It increases procoagulant factors and decreases natural anticoagulants such as protein S and antithrombin.","This acquired hypercoagulable state adds to the inherited APC resistance."],"why":"Oral estrogen passes through the liver and raises synthesis of fibrinogen and factors II, VII, VIII, and X while lowering protein S and antithrombin. Lower free protein S also produces an acquired resistance to activated protein C. Combined with factor V Leiden, these two hits on the protein C pathway multiply her risk well beyond either one alone.","whyNot":["No hormone destroys venous valves. Valve damage follows a prior DVT and leads to post-thrombotic syndrome.","Estrogen does not cause thrombocytosis. High platelet counts arise from inflammation, iron deficiency, or myeloproliferative disease.","Estrogen does not raise erythropoietin. Androgens and hypoxia do, and the higher hematocrit raises viscosity.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Estrogen and coagulation","Acquired hypercoagulable states"],"comps":["CV-18-I10","CV-18-H6"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C58","system":"CV","dx":"Varicose veins with chronic venous insufficiency","codes":["CV-18-I11"],"case":"A 56-year-old man comes to the physician because of heaviness and aching of the right leg that worsen through the day and improve with elevation. He has worked as a line cook for 30 years, standing about 10 hours a day. Physical examination shows dilated, tortuous superficial veins along the medial thigh and calf of the right leg, ankle edema, and brown, thickened skin above the medial malleolus. The ankle-brachial index is 1.05 (0.9 to 1.3). Duplex ultrasonography shows reflux in the main superficial vein of the medial leg and at its junction with the deep system, and incompetent valves in the deep veins. The diagnosis is varicose veins with chronic venous insufficiency.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C58-Q1","stem":"His varicosities follow the medial thigh and calf. Which of the following best describes the incompetent superficial vein and its normal site of termination in the deep system?","options":["Small saphenous vein into the femoral vein in the groin","Great saphenous vein into the popliteal vein behind the knee","Great saphenous vein into the femoral vein in the groin","Small saphenous vein into the popliteal vein behind the knee"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.4","dok":2,"approach":["Medial leg and thigh varicosities follow the great saphenous vein.","Trace its course: anterior to the medial malleolus, up the medial leg, behind the medial femoral condyle, and up the medial thigh.","It passes through the saphenous opening in the fascia lata to join the femoral vein."],"why":"The great saphenous vein begins at the medial end of the dorsal venous arch, passes anterior to the medial malleolus, and ascends the medial leg and thigh. It ends by passing through the saphenous opening in the fascia lata, just below and lateral to the pubic tubercle, to join the femoral vein. Valve failure at this saphenofemoral junction is the most common source of reflux in varicose veins.","whyNot":["The small saphenous vein ends in the popliteal fossa, not the groin.","The great saphenous vein passes behind the knee on its medial side but continues up to the groin. It does not end in the popliteal vein.","Correct.","This is the correct course of the small saphenous vein, which runs behind the lateral malleolus and up the back of the calf. Reflux there gives posterior calf varicosities."],"study":{"topics":["Great and small saphenous veins","Saphenous opening"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I11"]}},{"id":"CV-C58-Q2","stem":"In a healthy adult, foot venous pressure is about 90 mmHg during quiet standing and falls sharply within a few steps of walking. Which of the following best describes this patient's foot venous pressure during walking?","options":["It rises above arterial pressure because contracting muscles block outflow","It falls as far as in a healthy leg because his calf muscles still contract","It falls below normal because his dilated veins act as a large reservoir","It falls only a little because blood refluxes through incompetent valves"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-I11","obj":"CV-18-I.9","dok":3,"approach":["Standing still, foot venous pressure equals the weight of the blood column up to the heart, about 90 mmHg.","During walking, each calf contraction empties the veins, and competent valves break the column into short segments, so pressure falls.","With incompetent valves, blood refluxes back between contractions and the column stays intact."],"why":"During exercise, the muscle pump plus competent valves lowers foot venous pressure, which reduces capillary filtration and edema. When valves fail, the veins refill from above almost as fast as they are emptied, so pressure falls only slightly and stays high during walking (ambulatory venous hypertension). This sustained pressure drives the edema, skin changes, and ulcers of chronic venous insufficiency.","whyNot":["Venous pressure cannot rise above arterial pressure in a leg with intact arterial inflow, and muscle contraction pushes blood toward the heart.","Muscle contraction alone does not lower pressure. Without working valves, blood flows back as soon as the muscle relaxes.","Dilated veins hold more blood, which keeps pressure high. They do not lower it below normal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Ambulatory venous pressure","Muscle pump during exercise"],"comps":["CV-12","CV-11"]}},{"id":"CV-C58-Q3","stem":"Two years later, a shallow, irregular, weeping ulcer develops on the same leg. Which of the following best describes the most likely location and mechanism of this ulcer?","options":["Above the medial ankle, from sustained venous hypertension","Back of the heel, from prolonged pressure while lying in bed","Tips of the toes, from reduced arterial inflow and ischemia","Under the metatarsal heads, from repeated pressure on numb skin"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I11","obj":"CV-18-I.9","dok":2,"approach":["Link the disease to its pressure problem: high venous pressure in the lower leg.","That pressure is highest and the skin most affected in the gaiter area above the medial malleolus.","Choose the location and mechanism that match venous disease."],"why":"Sustained venous hypertension distends skin capillaries and lets fluid, fibrinogen, and red cells leak into the tissue. Trapped leukocytes, fibrosis (lipodermatosclerosis), and poor nutrient exchange make the skin fragile, and it breaks down into a shallow, irregular ulcer. These ulcers most often occur above the medial malleolus, where the brown, thickened skin already appears.","whyNot":["Correct.","Heel pressure injuries occur in bedbound patients. He is ambulatory, and his skin changes point to venous disease.","Painful, punched-out ulcers on the toe tips come from peripheral arterial disease. His ankle-brachial index is normal.","Ulcers under the metatarsal heads are neuropathic, as in diabetic neuropathy. Nothing here suggests sensory loss."],"study":{"topics":["Venous stasis ulcers","Arterial versus venous versus neuropathic ulcers"],"comps":["CV-18-I11","CV-18-I6"]}},{"id":"CV-C58-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best describes the brown pigment in the skin above his medial malleolus and its source?","options":["Lipofuscin from lipid peroxidation in aging dermal fibroblasts","Homogentisic acid polymer bound to collagen fibers of the dermis","Iron from leaked red cells, stored by macrophages as hemosiderin","Bilirubin carried in the blood and deposited in the dermis"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-I11","obj":"CV-18-I.9","dok":3,"approach":["High capillary pressure lets red cells leak into the dermis.","Macrophages engulf the red cells and break down hemoglobin.","The released iron is stored as ferritin, which aggregates into brown hemosiderin."],"why":"Macrophages break down the hemoglobin of leaked red cells. Heme oxygenase opens the heme ring, releasing iron and producing biliverdin, which becomes bilirubin and is carried away. The iron stays in the macrophage, bound to ferritin, and excess ferritin aggregates into golden-brown hemosiderin granules that stain blue with Prussian blue. This local iron deposit is what gives stasis skin its brown color.","whyNot":["Lipofuscin is a yellow-brown wear-and-tear pigment inside aging cells such as neurons and cardiac myocytes. It does not explain a localized patch above the ankle.","Homogentisic acid deposits occur in alkaptonuria (ochronosis) and darken cartilage, ears, and sclerae.","Correct.","Bilirubin is cleared from tissue and colors skin diffusely yellow in jaundice. It does not form a fixed brown patch over the ankle."],"study":{"topics":["Heme degradation","Ferritin and hemosiderin","Stasis dermatitis"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I11"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C59","system":"CV","dx":"Kaposi sarcoma in advanced HIV infection","codes":["CV-18-J2"],"case":"A 38-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of painless purple plaques and nodules on both lower legs. He has HIV infection and stopped taking antiretroviral therapy 3 years ago. Physical examination also shows a flat violaceous lesion on the hard palate and mild edema of both legs. CD4+ T-lymphocyte count is 42/µL (500 to 1,500), and HIV RNA is 210,000 copies/mL. Biopsy of a leg nodule is positive on immunostaining for a latency protein of a virus known to cause this tumor. The diagnosis is Kaposi sarcoma in advanced HIV infection (AIDS).","questions":[{"id":"CV-C59-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the virus that causes this patient's tumor?","options":["Nonenveloped double-stranded DNA virus in the papillomavirus family","Enveloped double-stranded DNA virus in the gammaherpesvirus subfamily","Enveloped single-stranded RNA retrovirus that uses reverse transcriptase","Enveloped double-stranded DNA virus in the betaherpesvirus subfamily"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-J2","obj":"CV-18-J.3","dok":2,"approach":["Kaposi sarcoma is caused by human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8, also called KSHV).","All herpesviruses are enveloped double-stranded DNA viruses that establish latency.","HHV-8 belongs with Epstein-Barr virus in the gammaherpesvirus subfamily, which infects lymphocytes and drives cell growth."],"why":"HHV-8 is an enveloped, linear double-stranded DNA gammaherpesvirus. It spreads mainly through saliva and also through sexual contact and transplanted organs. It stays latent in B cells and endothelial cells, where latency proteins such as LANA keep the viral genome and push cell growth. HHV-8 also causes primary effusion lymphoma and multicentric Castleman disease.","whyNot":["Papillomaviruses are nonenveloped dsDNA viruses that cause warts and cervical, anal, and oropharyngeal cancers, not Kaposi sarcoma.","Correct.","This describes HIV itself. HIV causes the immune deficiency that allows Kaposi sarcoma but is not the tumor virus.","Cytomegalovirus (HHV-5) is a betaherpesvirus. In AIDS it causes retinitis and colitis, not Kaposi sarcoma."],"study":{"topics":["Herpesvirus subfamilies","HHV-8 transmission and latency"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-J2"]}},{"id":"CV-C59-Q2","stem":"After antiretroviral therapy is restarted, his CD4+ T-lymphocyte count increases over several months and his lesions regress. Restoration of which of the following immune responses best explains the regression?","options":["Virus-specific CD8 T-cell killing aided by CD4 T cells","IgE-triggered mast cell degranulation in the dermis","Complement lysis of infected cells by the attack complex","Neutrophil phagocytosis of the infected spindle cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.7","dok":3,"approach":["Latent virus sits inside cells, out of reach of antibodies and complement.","Cells infected with virus show viral peptides on MHC class I, which CD8 cytotoxic T cells recognize.","CD8 responses need CD4 helper T cells, so restoring CD4 cells restores control."],"why":"Intracellular viral infection is controlled by cell-mediated immunity. CD8 cytotoxic T cells recognize HHV-8 peptides on MHC class I and kill infected cells with perforin and granzymes. CD4 helper T cells make IL-2 and license dendritic cells, which sustains effective CD8 responses. When HIV depletes CD4 cells, HHV-8 escapes this control, and when ART restores them, lesions often regress.","whyNot":["Correct.","IgE and mast cells defend against parasites and cause allergy. They play no role in controlling herpesvirus-infected cells.","The membrane attack complex is most important against Neisseria and other extracellular bacteria. Latent virus inside cells escapes it.","Neutrophils mainly kill extracellular bacteria and fungi. They do not target virus-infected tumor cells."],"study":{"topics":["Cell-mediated immunity to viruses","CD4 help for CD8 T cells","Immune reconstitution"],"comps":["CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C59-Q3","stem":"Which of the following histologic findings is most likely in the biopsy specimen of the leg nodule?","options":["Atypical melanocytes spreading upward through the epidermis","Spindle cells forming slitlike spaces with extravasated red cells","Thin-walled capillaries lined by a single layer of flat endothelium","Lobular capillaries with neutrophils and silver-staining bacilli"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-J2","obj":"CV-18-J.3","dok":2,"approach":["Kaposi sarcoma is a tumor of endothelial-type spindle cells.","The spindle cells form jagged, slitlike vascular channels.","Red cells leak out of these channels, leaving hemosiderin, which gives the lesion its purple color."],"why":"Nodular Kaposi sarcoma shows sheets of plump spindle cells forming slitlike vascular spaces packed with red cells, along with extravasated red cells and hemosiderin-laden macrophages. The spindle cells carry HHV-8, which is shown by immunostaining for LANA. The leaked blood and slow flow give the lesions their purple to brown color.","whyNot":["Upward (pagetoid) spread of atypical melanocytes describes melanoma, a pigmented tumor of melanocytes with no vascular channels.","Correct.","Well-formed capillaries lined by normal flat endothelium describe a benign capillary hemangioma, which lacks spindle cells and slitlike spaces.","This describes bacillary angiomatosis, caused by Bartonella henselae or quintana in AIDS. It can look like Kaposi sarcoma on the skin but responds to antibiotics."],"study":{"topics":["Kaposi sarcoma histology","Bacillary angiomatosis","Vascular tumors"],"comps":["CV-18-J2","CV-18-J1"]}},{"id":"CV-C59-Q4","stem":"The causative virus encodes a homolog of cellular cyclin D. Which of the following best describes how this viral cyclin promotes proliferation of infected cells?","options":["It activates p53, which induces p21 and arrests the cycle","It pairs with CDK6 to phosphorylate Rb and free E2F","It inhibits CDK1 and blocks the cell from entering mitosis","It stabilizes the Rb and E2F complex to hold cells in G1"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-J2","obj":"CV-18-J.3","dok":3,"approach":["Cyclin D normally binds CDK4 or CDK6 in G1.","The cyclin-CDK complex phosphorylates Rb, which releases E2F.","Free E2F turns on S-phase genes, so the cell passes the G1 to S checkpoint."],"why":"In G1, cyclin D partners with CDK4 or CDK6 to phosphorylate the retinoblastoma protein (Rb). Phosphorylated Rb releases E2F transcription factors, which turn on genes needed for DNA synthesis. The HHV-8 viral cyclin binds CDK6 and resists normal CDK inhibitors, so it keeps Rb phosphorylated and drives continuous cell cycle entry. Other HHV-8 latency proteins, such as LANA, also block p53 and Rb.","whyNot":["p53 activation and p21 production stop the cell cycle. Viruses that cause cancer block p53 instead.","Correct.","Blocking CDK1 would arrest cells at G2. Cyclin D partners act earlier, at the G1 to S transition.","Hypophosphorylated Rb bound to E2F holds cells in G1. A cyclin that promoted this would stop growth, the opposite of an oncogenic effect."],"study":{"topics":["Cyclins, CDKs, and Rb","Viral oncogenesis"],"comps":["CV-18-J2","CV-14"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C60","system":"CV","dx":"Secondary lymphedema after axillary lymph node dissection","codes":["CV-10","CV-18-H4"],"case":"A 52-year-old woman comes to the physician because of gradually increasing swelling and heaviness of the right arm and hand; her rings no longer fit. Ten months ago, she underwent right modified radical mastectomy with axillary lymph node dissection, followed by axillary radiation, for invasive ductal carcinoma. The right forearm circumference is 5 cm greater than the left. The swelling was pitting at first but is now firm and nonpitting, with thickened skin. Venous duplex ultrasonography shows no thrombosis. Serum albumin is 4.1 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0). Lymphoscintigraphy shows delayed lymph transport, dermal backflow, and no axillary node uptake. The diagnosis is secondary lymphedema of the right arm.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C60-Q1","stem":"Before her surgery, lymph from her right hand entered the venous circulation at which of the following sites?","options":["Cisterna chyli, anterior to the first and second lumbar vertebrae","Azygos vein, through lymphatics that follow the intercostal spaces","Junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins","Junction of the right subclavian and right internal jugular veins"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-10","obj":"CV-10.2","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the path: hand lymphatics, then axillary nodes, then apical axillary nodes.","The right subclavian lymph trunk carries this lymph to the right lymphatic duct or directly to the vein.","It enters the blood at the right venous angle, where the right subclavian and internal jugular veins meet."],"why":"Lymph from the upper limb drains through the axillary nodes to the apical group, then into the subclavian lymph trunk. On the right, this trunk joins the right lymphatic duct or opens on its own at the right venous angle. The right lymphatic duct drains only the right upper limb and the right side of the head, neck, and thorax. All other lymph reaches the left venous angle through the thoracic duct.","whyNot":["The cisterna chyli collects lymph from the abdomen and lower limbs and is the start of the thoracic duct. It is not a point of entry into the blood.","Intercostal lymphatics drain the chest wall to parasternal and intercostal nodes. The azygos vein does not receive upper limb lymph.","The left venous angle receives the thoracic duct, which drains everything below the diaphragm plus the left side of the head, neck, thorax, and left upper limb. It would be correct for her left arm.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Lymphatic drainage of the upper limb","Right lymphatic duct and thoracic duct"],"comps":["CV-10"]}},{"id":"CV-C60-Q2","stem":"Her serum albumin concentration is normal, and her veins are patent. Which of the following changes in Starling forces most likely sustains the fluid accumulation in her arm?","options":["Higher interstitial oncotic pressure from retained tissue protein","Lower plasma oncotic pressure from loss of albumin in the urine","Higher capillary hydrostatic pressure from venous outflow blockage","Higher capillary permeability from widened endothelial cell gaps"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.5","dok":3,"approach":["Capillaries normally filter a small net amount of fluid and protein that only lymphatics can return.","With lymphatics removed, the protein stays in the interstitium.","Interstitial oncotic pressure rises, which increases net filtration and traps more water."],"why":"Even with normal Starling forces, capillaries filter a little more fluid than they reabsorb, along with small amounts of plasma protein. Lymphatics normally return this fluid and protein to the blood, about 2 to 3 L per day for the whole body. Without lymph drainage, protein accumulates in the tissue, raising interstitial oncotic pressure and drawing still more fluid out of the capillaries. This is why lymphedema fluid is high in protein, unlike most other edemas.","whyNot":["Correct.","Low plasma oncotic pressure occurs in nephrotic syndrome and liver failure. Her albumin is normal.","Venous obstruction raises capillary hydrostatic pressure, as in DVT. Her duplex scan shows open veins.","Increased permeability occurs in inflammation, burns, and sepsis. Her edema came on gradually after node removal, with no sign of inflammation."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces","Lymph flow","Mechanisms of edema"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H4"]}},{"id":"CV-C60-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains the change in her swelling from soft and pitting to firm and nonpitting?","options":["Hemosiderin deposited from repeated bleeding into soft tissue","Glycosaminoglycans deposited in the dermis from thyroid disease","Protein-rich fluid driving fibrosis and fat buildup in tissue","Clot organizing in the deep veins and stiffening the arm tissue"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H4","obj":"CV-18-H.6","dok":2,"approach":["Early lymphedema is free fluid that can be pushed aside, so it pits.","Long-standing protein-rich fluid causes chronic inflammation that stimulates fibroblasts and fat cells.","Fibrosis and fat do not move under pressure, so the swelling becomes firm and nonpitting."],"why":"Stagnant, protein-rich interstitial fluid triggers chronic low-grade inflammation. Fibroblasts lay down collagen, adipose tissue expands, and the skin thickens. Once fibrosis and fat replace free fluid, pressing on the arm no longer leaves a pit, and elevation alone no longer reduces the swelling. Skin may take on a thick, dimpled texture (peau d'orange), and long-standing cases carry a small risk of angiosarcoma.","whyNot":["Hemosiderin causes brown discoloration, as in venous stasis. It does not make tissue firm, and she has no history of bleeding.","Glycosaminoglycan deposition causes nonpitting myxedema in hypothyroidism or pretibial myxedema in Graves disease. Nothing suggests thyroid disease here.","Correct.","Her duplex scan shows no venous thrombosis."],"study":{"topics":["Chronic lymphedema","Pitting versus nonpitting edema"],"comps":["CV-18-H4","CV-10"]}},{"id":"CV-C60-Q4","stem":"Eight months later, she has a temperature of 38.6 °C (101.5 °F) and a warm, spreading area of erythema without pus on the swollen forearm. Blood cultures grow beta-hemolytic, gram-positive cocci in chains that are sensitive to bacitracin. Which of the following surface proteins of this organism resists phagocytosis and is the main target of type-specific antibodies?","options":["Protein A, which binds the Fc portion of IgG and blocks opsonization","Internalin, which binds E-cadherin to enter epithelial cells","Coagulase, which clots plasma and wraps the organism in fibrin","M protein, which recruits factor H and limits C3b deposition"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.2","dok":3,"approach":["Beta-hemolytic cocci in chains that are bacitracin sensitive are Streptococcus pyogenes (group A).","Lymphedema impairs local immune surveillance and is a strong risk factor for recurrent streptococcal cellulitis.","The major group A antiphagocytic surface protein, and the basis for typing, is M protein."],"why":"Streptococcus pyogenes is a common cause of nonpurulent cellulitis, and a lymphedematous limb is especially prone to repeated attacks. M protein extends from the cell wall and binds host factor H, which speeds breakdown of C3b, so less complement opsonin coats the bacterium and phagocytes cannot grab it. Antibodies against a given M type protect against that type, and M protein also shows molecular mimicry with cardiac proteins, the basis of rheumatic fever.","whyNot":["Protein A is the antiphagocytic surface protein of Staphylococcus aureus, which grows in clusters and is catalase positive.","Internalin lets Listeria monocytogenes enter gut epithelial cells. Listeria is a gram-positive rod.","Coagulase identifies Staphylococcus aureus. It is an enzyme that converts fibrinogen to fibrin, not a streptococcal surface protein.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Streptococcus pyogenes virulence factors","Cellulitis risk in lymphedema"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-16"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C07","system":"CV","dx":"Stable angina pectoris from a fixed atherosclerotic coronary narrowing","codes":["CV-18-C1"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman who works as a letter carrier comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of pressure-like substernal chest discomfort. The discomfort occurs when she walks uphill carrying her mail bag and resolves within 5 minutes of rest. It has never occurred at rest. She has type 2 diabetes mellitus and smokes cigarettes. Pulse is 76/min and blood pressure is 146/88 mm Hg. An ECG at rest shows no abnormalities. Serum LDL cholesterol is 168 mg/dL (reference below 100 mg/dL), and HDL cholesterol is 38 mg/dL (reference 50 mg/dL or higher for women). During treadmill exercise testing, at a heart rate of 142/min, she develops her usual discomfort and 2-mm horizontal ST-segment depression in leads V4 to V6 that resolves during recovery. Diagnosis: stable angina pectoris from a fixed atherosclerotic coronary narrowing.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C07-Q1","stem":"During exercise, rising shear stress on healthy coronary endothelium triggers release of nitric oxide, which dilates the artery. In atherosclerotic segments like hers, nitric oxide output is reduced and the artery dilates poorly. Which of the following reactions produces nitric oxide in endothelial cells?","options":["Guanylyl cyclase converting GTP into nitric oxide and cGMP","Nitric oxide synthase oxidizing L-arginine to L-citrulline","Arginase splitting L-arginine into ornithine and nitric oxide","Heme oxygenase breaking down heme into biliverdin and nitric oxide"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-2","obj":"CV-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["The clue is a gas made by endothelium that relaxes smooth muscle.","Recall the enzyme that makes it and its amino acid substrate.","Separate the enzyme that makes nitric oxide from the enzyme nitric oxide activates."],"why":"Endothelial nitric oxide synthase (eNOS) oxidizes L-arginine, using oxygen and NADPH with tetrahydrobiopterin as a cofactor, to form nitric oxide and L-citrulline. Shear stress and calcium-calmodulin activate eNOS. Nitric oxide diffuses into vascular smooth muscle, activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, and raises cGMP, which lowers cytosolic calcium and relaxes the vessel. Atherosclerosis and its risk factors reduce nitric oxide availability (endothelial dysfunction), so the narrowed artery cannot dilate well when demand rises.","whyNot":["Guanylyl cyclase is the target of nitric oxide in smooth muscle. It converts GTP to cGMP and does not make nitric oxide.","Correct.","Arginase converts L-arginine to ornithine and urea in the urea cycle. It competes with nitric oxide synthase for arginine but makes no nitric oxide.","Heme oxygenase breaks heme into biliverdin, iron, and carbon monoxide. Carbon monoxide is a different gas signal."],"study":{"topics":["Endothelial nitric oxide synthesis","Endothelial dysfunction in atherosclerosis"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-11","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C07-Q2","stem":"Her high LDL enters the intima of her coronary arteries, where some of it is oxidized. Macrophages take up the oxidized LDL and become lipid-filled foam cells. Which of the following best explains why macrophages continue to take up cholesterol after their cholesterol content is high?","options":["Rising cell cholesterol activates SREBP and raises LDL receptor numbers","Oxidized LDL crosses the cell membrane only by passive diffusion","Macrophages lack the enzyme that esterifies cholesterol for storage","Scavenger receptors for oxidized LDL are not suppressed by cholesterol"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall how most cells stop LDL uptake when cholesterol is high.","Identify which receptor macrophages use for oxidized LDL.","Ask whether that receptor is under the same sterol feedback."],"why":"Most cells take up native LDL through the LDL receptor. When cell cholesterol rises, SREBP stays in the endoplasmic reticulum, so transcription of the LDL receptor and HMG-CoA reductase falls and uptake stops. Macrophages take up oxidized LDL through scavenger receptors such as SR-A and CD36, which lack this feedback. Uptake therefore continues, the cholesterol is esterified by ACAT and stored in droplets, and the cell becomes a foam cell.","whyNot":["High cell cholesterol does the opposite: it keeps SREBP inactive and lowers LDL receptor numbers. This feedback is why the LDL receptor pathway cannot make foam cells.","Oxidized LDL is a large particle taken up by receptor-mediated endocytosis. It does not diffuse across the membrane.","Macrophages do esterify cholesterol with ACAT. The stored cholesteryl ester droplets are what give foam cells their foamy look.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Scavenger receptors and foam cell formation","SREBP regulation of the LDL receptor"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C07-Q3","stem":"Eight months later she has chest discomfort at rest that lasts about 20 minutes and recurs several times over 2 days. During pain her ECG shows ST depression in V3 to V6. Serial troponin levels stay within the reference range. Which of the following changes in her coronary plaque most likely caused this new pattern?","options":["Dystrophic calcification of the core with an intact plaque surface","Slow plaque growth that thickens its collagen-rich fibrous cap","A fissured plaque cap with a thrombus partly blocking the lumen","Complete thrombotic occlusion causing full-thickness necrosis"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C1","obj":"CV-18-C.2","dok":3,"approach":["Rest pain that is new and recurring with normal troponin defines unstable angina.","Unstable angina comes from an acute change in the plaque, not gradual growth.","Normal troponin rules out myocardial necrosis."],"why":"Pain at rest that is new or increasing, with ST depression and no troponin rise, is unstable angina. In most cases a plaque fissures or ruptures, platelets aggregate, and a thrombus forms that narrows the lumen without completely blocking it. Flow can fall at rest, and fragments may embolize downstream. Because troponin stays normal, the ischemia has not caused myocyte necrosis.","whyNot":["Calcification makes a plaque stiffer but does not by itself cause a sudden change in symptoms. An intact surface does not trigger thrombosis.","Slow growth with a thick fibrous cap describes a stable plaque. It causes symptoms only when demand rises, which is her original pattern.","Correct.","Complete occlusion with full-thickness necrosis is an ST-elevation MI. Troponin would rise and the ECG would show ST elevation."],"study":{"topics":["Unstable angina versus stable angina","Acute plaque change"],"comps":["CV-18-C1","CV-18-C3","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C07-Q4","stem":"Her exercise ECG showed ST depression, which reflects ischemia limited to the inner layer of the left ventricular wall. Her heart rate rose to 142/min during the test. Which of the following best explains why the subendocardium is to become ischemic when demand rises past what a narrowed artery can supply?","options":["It is supplied by end arteries that arise from the coronary sinus","Its myocytes depend on anaerobic glycolysis even when oxygen is plentiful","It is squeezed in systole and perfused mainly during diastole","It is fed by blood from the ventricular cavity that is low in oxygen"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-8","obj":"CV-8.4","dok":3,"approach":["Note where the ischemia is: the inner layer of the left ventricle.","Recall when left ventricular myocardium receives most of its flow.","Link a faster heart rate to a shorter diastole."],"why":"Coronary arteries run on the epicardial surface and send branches inward, so the subendocardium is farthest from the supply. During systole, pressure inside the left ventricular wall is highest in the subendocardium and compresses its vessels, so most of its flow occurs during diastole. Tachycardia shortens diastole more than systole, which cuts subendocardial perfusion just as oxygen demand rises. With a fixed stenosis, this layer becomes ischemic first, producing ST depression on the surface ECG.","whyNot":["The coronary sinus is a vein that drains the heart into the right atrium. It does not supply any myocardium.","Cardiac myocytes rely on aerobic metabolism, mostly fatty acid oxidation, when oxygen is available. Anaerobic glycolysis takes over only during ischemia.","Correct.","Small veins (Thebesian veins) drain into the chambers, but the ventricular cavity does not supply the myocardium in a normal adult heart. The subendocardium is fed by coronary branches."],"study":{"topics":["Subendocardial perfusion","Effect of heart rate on diastolic time"],"comps":["CV-8","CV-11","CV-18-C1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C08","system":"CV","dx":"Chronic ischemic heart disease (ischemic cardiomyopathy) with heart failure","codes":["CV-18-C2"],"case":"A 71-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 1-year history of increasing shortness of breath when climbing one flight of stairs, orthopnea, and ankle swelling. He has had no recent chest pain. He had myocardial infarctions at ages 60 and 66 years. Pulse is 88/min and blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg. Physical examination shows crackles at both lung bases, an S3 gallop, and edema of both ankles. Serum BNP is 820 pg/mL (reference below 100 pg/mL). Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 30% (reference 55% to 70%), a thin, akinetic scar in the inferior wall, and a hypokinetic anterior wall. Coronary angiography shows severe stenosis of all three major coronary arteries. Diagnosis: chronic ischemic heart disease (ischemic cardiomyopathy) with heart failure.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C08-Q1","stem":"Further imaging shows that his hypokinetic anterior wall has normal thickness and takes up a metabolic tracer, so its myocytes are alive. After bypass surgery restores blood flow to it, the anterior wall contracts normally over the following weeks. Which of the following best describes the anterior wall before surgery?","options":["Hibernating myocardium","Stunned myocardium","Transmural collagen scar","Ventricular aneurysm"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C2","obj":"CV-18-C.7","dok":2,"approach":["The muscle is alive but weak while flow is chronically low.","Function returns only after flow is restored.","Match that pattern to the term, and separate it from stunning, which follows a brief ischemic episode."],"why":"Hibernating myocardium is living muscle that lowers its contraction to match a chronically reduced blood supply. It stays weak as long as flow stays low and recovers after revascularization. It is common in chronic ischemic heart disease and is one reason bypass or stenting can improve ejection fraction. Detecting viable tissue with metabolic or perfusion imaging helps predict which segments will recover.","whyNot":["Correct.","Stunned myocardium follows a brief episode of severe ischemia after flow has already returned. It recovers on its own over hours to days without revascularization.","A scar is dead tissue replaced by collagen. It is thin, does not take up a metabolic tracer, and does not regain contraction, like his inferior wall.","A ventricular aneurysm is a thin scar that bulges outward during systole. It has no living muscle to recover."],"study":{"topics":["Hibernating versus stunned myocardium","Chronic ischemic heart disease"],"comps":["CV-18-C2","CV-18-C3"]}},{"id":"CV-C08-Q2","stem":"His healthy myocardium gets most of its ATP from oxidizing fatty acids. In his poorly perfused but living segments, a shift toward oxidizing glucose instead would help those cells keep working. Which of the following best explains why this shift helps when oxygen supply is limited?","options":["Glucose oxidation avoids the citric acid cycle and the NADH it forms","Glucose oxidation yields more ATP per gram of fuel than fatty acids","Glucose oxidation makes all of its ATP without using mitochondria","Glucose oxidation yields more ATP for each molecule of oxygen used"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.4","dok":3,"approach":["The limiting factor in ischemia is oxygen, not fuel.","Compare fuels by ATP made per oxygen consumed.","Fatty acids are more reduced and use more oxygen for each ATP they produce."],"why":"Fatty acids are more reduced than glucose, so their oxidation passes a larger share of electrons through FADH2 and uses more oxygen per carbon. As a result, complete oxidation of glucose produces more ATP for each molecule of oxygen consumed. When oxygen delivery is the limit, as in chronically ischemic myocardium, shifting fuel use toward glucose gets more work from the same oxygen. This is the rationale behind metabolic approaches that partly inhibit fatty acid oxidation in angina.","whyNot":["Pyruvate from glucose enters the citric acid cycle as acetyl-CoA and generates NADH, just as fatty acids do. Both fuels depend on the cycle.","Fatty acids yield more ATP per gram than glucose, which is why fat is the body's main energy store. The advantage of glucose is per oxygen, not per gram.","Only the glycolysis portion of glucose metabolism runs outside the mitochondria and yields 2 ATP. Complete oxidation needs the citric acid cycle and electron transport chain in mitochondria.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Myocardial fuel selection","ATP yield per oxygen for glucose and fatty acids"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-C2"]}},{"id":"CV-C08-Q3","stem":"His left ventricle has dilated over several years. At the same systolic pressure, which of the following best describes the effect of the larger chamber on the oxygen demand of the remaining viable myocardium?","options":["Demand rises, because a larger radius raises the tension in the wall","Demand falls, because stretched fibers contract with greater mechanical efficiency","Demand stays the same, because myocardial oxygen use depends only on heart rate","Demand falls, because each fiber shortens less to eject the same stroke volume"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","obj":"CV-6.4","dok":3,"approach":["Wall tension is a major driver of myocardial oxygen use.","Apply the law of Laplace: wall stress rises with pressure and radius and falls with wall thickness.","A dilated, thinned ventricle has a larger radius and a thinner wall."],"why":"By the law of Laplace, wall stress is about pressure times radius divided by twice the wall thickness. A dilated ventricle has a larger radius and often a thinner wall, so each fiber must generate more tension to produce the same pressure. Wall tension, heart rate, and contractility are the main determinants of myocardial oxygen demand. Dilation therefore raises demand in a heart whose supply is already limited, which feeds the decline in chronic ischemic heart disease.","whyNot":["Correct.","Moderate stretch improves force through the Frank-Starling mechanism, but in a chronically dilated heart this benefit is exhausted and wall stress dominates.","Heart rate is one determinant of oxygen demand, but wall tension and contractility matter as well.","A larger chamber does need less fiber shortening per stroke volume, but each fiber must produce more tension. The rise in tension raises oxygen use."],"study":{"topics":["Law of Laplace and wall stress","Determinants of myocardial oxygen demand"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-B1","CV-18-C2"]}},{"id":"CV-C08-Q4","stem":"A plaque removed from one of his coronary arteries contains many activated CD4+ T cells that secrete interferon-gamma. Which of the following best explains how this cytokine makes a plaque more likely to rupture?","options":["It drives B cells to switch to IgE production against plaque lipids","It blocks collagen synthesis by smooth muscle cells in the cap","It shifts macrophages toward a repair state that lays down matrix","It stimulates fibroblasts to cross-link type I collagen in the cap"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.1","dok":2,"approach":["Interferon-gamma is the main Th1 cytokine.","Plaque strength depends on collagen in the fibrous cap, made by smooth muscle cells.","Ask whether the cytokine adds or removes cap collagen."],"why":"Th1 cells in plaques secrete interferon-gamma. It inhibits collagen synthesis by intimal smooth muscle cells and activates macrophages, which release matrix metalloproteinases that break down collagen. Less collagen is made and more is destroyed, so the fibrous cap thins and becomes vulnerable to rupture. Plaque rupture with thrombosis is the usual trigger for acute coronary syndromes in patients like him.","whyNot":["Class switching to IgE is driven by IL-4 from Th2 cells, and interferon-gamma inhibits it. This is not how interferon-gamma weakens the cap.","Correct.","Interferon-gamma promotes classically activated (M1) macrophages that are pro-inflammatory. Repair-type (M2) macrophages are driven by IL-4 and IL-13.","Interferon-gamma reduces collagen production in the cap. Collagen cross-linking would strengthen the cap, the opposite effect."],"study":{"topics":["Inflammation and plaque stability","Th1 cytokines and macrophage activation"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-I3","CV-18-C3"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C09","system":"CV","dx":"Acute anterior wall ST-elevation myocardial infarction from proximal LAD occlusion","codes":["CV-18-C3"],"case":"A 46-year-old man who is a high school teacher is brought to the emergency department 1 hour after the onset of crushing chest pain and diaphoresis that began while he was shoveling snow. He does not smoke. His father had a myocardial infarction at age 50 years. Pulse is 98/min and blood pressure is 132/84 mm Hg. An ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads V1 to V4. Serum troponin I is 4.8 ng/mL (reference below 0.04 ng/mL). LDL cholesterol is 118 mg/dL (reference below 100 mg/dL), and lipoprotein(a) is 210 nmol/L (reference below 75 nmol/L). Coronary angiography shows a thrombus occluding the proximal left anterior descending (LAD) artery, which is opened with a stent 90 minutes after the onset of pain. Diagnosis: acute anterior wall ST-elevation myocardial infarction.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C09-Q1","stem":"On day 2, his ECG shows a new right bundle branch block. Which of the following vessels most likely supplied the injured conduction tissue?","options":["AV nodal branch of the right coronary artery","Sinoatrial nodal branch of the right coronary artery","Septal perforating branches of the LAD artery","Obtuse marginal branches of the circumflex artery"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-8","obj":"CV-8.1","dok":3,"approach":["The right bundle branch runs down the interventricular septum.","The anterior two-thirds of the septum is supplied by septal branches of the LAD.","His occluded artery is the LAD, which fits."],"why":"The LAD runs in the anterior interventricular groove and gives off septal perforating branches that supply the anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum. The right bundle branch and the anterior fascicle of the left bundle branch run through this territory and depend mainly on these branches. A new bundle branch block in anterior MI signals a large septal infarct and a risk of progression to complete heart block below the AV node.","whyNot":["The AV nodal artery supplies the AV node and the start of the bundle of His, not the length of the right bundle branch. It is usually involved in inferior MI from right coronary occlusion.","The sinoatrial nodal artery supplies the SA node in the right atrium. Its loss causes sinus node dysfunction, not bundle branch block.","Correct.","Obtuse marginal branches supply the lateral wall of the left ventricle. They do not reach the septum."],"study":{"topics":["Septal blood supply and the bundle branches","Conduction blocks in anterior MI"],"comps":["CV-8","CV-7","CV-18-C3"]}},{"id":"CV-C09-Q2","stem":"Flow was restored about 90 minutes after occlusion. Which of the following microscopic findings is most characteristic of irreversibly injured myocytes that are reperfused?","options":["Dense eosinophilic cross bands of hypercontracted sarcomeres","Granulation tissue with new capillaries and loose collagen","Wavy fibers at the infarct border stretched by nearby beating cells","Myocyte nuclei dividing to replace the cells that were lost"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C3","obj":"CV-18-C.5","dok":3,"approach":["The key clue is reperfusion of tissue that is already dying.","Restored flow brings a surge of calcium into cells with damaged membranes.","Calcium overload causes the myofibrils to hypercontract."],"why":"When blood flow returns to myocytes that are already irreversibly injured, calcium floods in through damaged membranes. The sarcomeres contract intensely and lock, forming dense, eosinophilic transverse bands called contraction bands. This contraction band necrosis is typical of reperfused infarcts, which may also be hemorrhagic because injured vessels leak when flow returns. Reperfusion still salvages myocytes that were only reversibly injured, so it limits infarct size overall.","whyNot":["Correct.","Granulation tissue appears at the infarct margins at about 7 to 14 days. It is far too late for the first hours after reperfusion.","Wavy fibers appear at the border of an early infarct as noncontracting dead fibers are stretched. They can appear within hours with or without reperfusion, so they do not mark reperfusion.","Adult cardiac myocytes are permanent cells and do not divide to replace lost tissue in any meaningful amount. Infarcts heal by scar."],"study":{"topics":["Contraction band necrosis and reperfusion injury","Infarct histology"],"comps":["CV-18-C3","CV-2"]}},{"id":"CV-C09-Q3","stem":"His lipoprotein(a) is high. Lp(a) is an LDL-like particle in which apolipoprotein B-100 is linked to apolipoprotein(a). In addition to carrying cholesterol into the artery wall, which of the following best explains how apolipoprotein(a) promotes coronary thrombosis?","options":["It binds von Willebrand factor and increases platelet adhesion","It activates factor X directly and speeds thrombin formation","It inhibits antithrombin, leaving thrombin unopposed","It resembles plasminogen and interferes with fibrinolysis"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the structure of apolipoprotein(a).","Its repeated kringle domains are similar to those in plasminogen.","A plasminogen look-alike competes with plasminogen and slows clot breakdown."],"why":"Apolipoprotein(a) contains many kringle domains that closely resemble those of plasminogen. It competes with plasminogen for binding sites on fibrin and cells, so less plasmin is formed and clots are broken down more slowly. Lp(a) level is mostly set by inheritance at the LPA gene and changes little with diet. A high level is an independent risk factor for premature coronary disease, as in this patient with only modestly raised LDL.","whyNot":["Von Willebrand factor links platelets to exposed collagen through glycoprotein Ib. That is not the mechanism of Lp(a).","Factor X is activated by the tissue factor and intrinsic tenase complexes. Apolipoprotein(a) is not a known activator.","Lp(a) does not inhibit antithrombin. Low antithrombin activity comes from inherited deficiency, liver disease, or protein loss.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Lipoprotein(a) structure and function","Fibrinolysis and plasminogen"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-14","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C09-Q4","stem":"On day 4 he develops new chest pain with fresh ST elevation in V1 to V4. His troponin I is still elevated from the first infarct. Which of the following serum markers is most useful for detecting a new infarct at this point?","options":["Creatine kinase MB","Lactate dehydrogenase","Aspartate aminotransferase","B-type natriuretic peptide"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C3","obj":"CV-18-C.4","dok":3,"approach":["Troponin stays high for about 7 to 10 days, so it cannot easily show a second event on day 4.","Look for a cardiac marker that returns to normal within a few days.","A fresh rise in that marker points to new necrosis."],"why":"Troponin I and T begin to rise about 2 to 4 hours after infarction and stay elevated for about 7 to 10 days. CK-MB rises over a similar early period, peaks at about 24 to 48 hours, and returns to normal within about 72 hours. By day 4 his CK-MB should be back near baseline, so a new rise strongly suggests reinfarction. This timing difference is the classic reason CK-MB is used to detect early reinfarction. Current practice can also detect reinfarction by a further rise in troponin of about 20% or more, but among these markers CK-MB is the one that has already returned to baseline.","whyNot":["Correct.","Lactate dehydrogenase rises later than CK-MB and stays high for a week or more. It is also nonspecific, so it cannot separate a new infarct from the first.","AST is found in liver and muscle as well as heart and is no longer used to diagnose MI. It is too nonspecific to confirm reinfarction.","BNP rises with ventricular wall stress and heart failure. It does not measure myocyte necrosis."],"study":{"topics":["Cardiac biomarker timing","Diagnosis of reinfarction"],"comps":["CV-18-C3"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C10","system":"CV","dx":"Inferior wall MI with right ventricular involvement from right coronary artery occlusion, with bradycardia and second-degree AV block","codes":["CV-18-C3"],"case":"A 66-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 2 hours after the onset of heavy pressure in her lower chest and upper abdomen with nausea and diaphoresis. Pulse is 44/min and blood pressure is 84/56 mm Hg. Jugular venous pressure is increased, and the lungs are clear to auscultation. An ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF and in the right-sided lead V4R, with ST-segment depression in leads I and aVL. A rhythm strip shows progressive lengthening of the PR interval until a P wave is not followed by a QRS complex. Serum troponin I is 2.6 ng/mL (reference below 0.04 ng/mL). Coronary angiography shows occlusion of a dominant right coronary artery (RCA) proximal to its right ventricular branches. Diagnosis: inferior wall MI with right ventricular involvement from RCA occlusion, with bradycardia and second-degree AV block.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C10-Q1","stem":"Which of the following anatomic features best explains why her coronary occlusion has disturbed AV conduction?","options":["The AV nodal artery comes from the circumflex in right-dominant hearts","The AV nodal artery usually arises from the RCA near the crux","The AV node lies in the free wall of the right ventricle","The AV node is supplied by the first diagonal branch of the LAD"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-7","obj":"CV-7.3","dok":2,"approach":["She has an RCA occlusion and a conduction block at the AV node.","Recall where the AV nodal artery arises.","Link the artery to the node it supplies."],"why":"The AV node lies in the lower part of the interatrial septum, in the triangle of Koch near the opening of the coronary sinus. Its artery usually arises from the RCA near the crux of the heart, the point where the posterior interventricular groove meets the atrioventricular groove. In most people the RCA is dominant, giving rise to the posterior descending artery and the AV nodal artery. RCA occlusion can therefore cause AV block along with inferior wall infarction.","whyNot":["In right-dominant hearts, the RCA gives the AV nodal artery. The circumflex supplies it mainly in left-dominant hearts.","Correct.","The AV node lies in the lower interatrial septum, in the triangle of Koch near the coronary sinus opening. It is not in the ventricular wall.","Diagonal branches of the LAD supply the anterolateral left ventricle. They do not supply the AV node."],"study":{"topics":["AV nodal blood supply","Coronary dominance"],"comps":["CV-8","CV-7"]}},{"id":"CV-C10-Q2","stem":"Her rhythm strip shows Mobitz type I (Wenckebach) second-degree AV block. This block often improves with atropine after inferior myocardial infarction. Which of the following best describes the site of the conduction delay and the reason for this response?","options":["In the His-Purkinje system, which has dense vagal innervation","In the bundle branches, which are supplied by the LAD","In the SA node, where vagal tone delays each atrial impulse","In the AV node, where vagal tone and ischemia slow conduction"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-7","obj":"CV-7.5","dok":3,"approach":["A PR interval that lengthens before a dropped beat places the block in the AV node.","The AV node is strongly influenced by the vagus nerve.","Atropine blocks muscarinic receptors and removes vagal slowing."],"why":"In Mobitz type I block, conduction through the AV node slows a little more with each beat until one impulse fails to pass, and the cycle restarts. The AV node receives dense vagal innervation, and inferior MI often increases vagal tone through reflexes from the inferior wall, along with ischemia of the node itself. Acetylcholine acting on M2 receptors slows nodal conduction, so atropine often improves the block. Block at this level is usually transient after inferior MI.","whyNot":["The His-Purkinje system has little vagal innervation. Block there causes Mobitz type II or complete block with a fixed PR interval and responds poorly to atropine.","Bundle branch block widens the QRS but does not cause a progressively lengthening PR interval. The bundle branches are supplied mainly by the LAD and are usually affected in anterior MI.","SA node slowing causes sinus bradycardia or sinus pauses in which the P wave itself is missing. Here P waves appear on time but fail to conduct.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Mobitz type I versus type II block","Autonomic control of the AV node"],"comps":["CV-7","CV-18-C3"]}},{"id":"CV-C10-Q3","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains her low blood pressure with raised jugular venous pressure and clear lungs?","options":["Rupture of a papillary muscle with acute mitral regurgitation","Loss of over 40% of left ventricular mass with pulmonary congestion","Right ventricular failure that underfills the left ventricle","Cytokine-driven vasodilation with a low systemic vascular resistance"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-B2","obj":"CV-18-B.2","dok":3,"approach":["Combine the findings: hypotension, high venous pressure, and clear lungs.","High venous pressure with clear lungs points to a problem on the right side.","ST elevation in V4R confirms right ventricular infarction."],"why":"Proximal RCA occlusion can infarct the right ventricle as well as the inferior left ventricle. The weak right ventricle cannot pump venous return forward, so blood backs up in the systemic veins (raised jugular venous pressure) while the left ventricle receives too little preload. Left ventricular output and blood pressure fall, yet the lungs stay clear because the left side is not congested. These patients depend on preload, so lowering it with venodilators can worsen the hypotension.","whyNot":["Acute mitral regurgitation from papillary muscle rupture causes sudden pulmonary edema, so the lungs would not be clear. It also usually occurs a few days after MI, not in the first hours.","Massive left ventricular loss causes cardiogenic shock with pulmonary congestion and crackles. Her lungs are clear.","Correct.","Vasodilatory shock, as in sepsis, has a low or normal venous pressure. Her raised jugular venous pressure points to pump failure on the right."],"study":{"topics":["Right ventricular infarction","Preload dependence"],"comps":["CV-18-C3","CV-18-B2","CV-18-H5"]}},{"id":"CV-C10-Q4","stem":"Within the first day after infarction, neutrophils begin to enter her necrotic myocardium. Which of the following most likely initiates this inflammatory response?","options":["Necrotic myocyte contents activating pattern recognition receptors","Cytotoxic T cells recognizing myocyte antigens shown on MHC class I","Antibodies to cardiac myosin forming immune complexes in the wall","IgE on mast cells binding cardiac proteins released from the infarct"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-6","obj":"IM-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["Necrosis releases cell contents that are normally hidden inside cells.","These molecules are danger signals recognized by innate immune receptors.","Innate immunity acts within hours, faster than any antibody or T-cell response."],"why":"Necrotic myocytes release damage-associated molecular patterns such as HMGB1, ATP, heat shock proteins, and mitochondrial DNA. These bind pattern recognition receptors, including Toll-like receptors and the NLRP3 inflammasome, on resident macrophages and endothelial cells. The cells release IL-1, TNF, and chemokines and display adhesion molecules, and complement is also activated. Neutrophils are recruited within hours and peak at about 1 to 3 days.","whyNot":["Correct.","A CD8 T-cell response needs priming and takes days. It does not explain neutrophil entry within hours.","Antibodies against heart antigens take weeks to form. They are linked to later immune complications such as post-MI (Dressler) pericarditis, not to early neutrophil entry.","IgE and mast cells drive immediate (type I) hypersensitivity to allergens. They do not start the response to necrotic tissue."],"study":{"topics":["Damage-associated molecular patterns","Acute inflammation after infarction"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-2","CV-18-C3"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C11","system":"CV","dx":"Post-myocardial infarction (Dressler) syndrome","codes":["CV-18-C3","CV-18-F3"],"case":"A 63-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 4-day history of sharp chest pain that worsens with inspiration and when lying supine and improves when she sits up and leans forward. Three weeks ago, she had an anterior wall myocardial infarction treated with a coronary stent. Temperature is 38.2 °C (100.8 °F), pulse is 96/min, and blood pressure is 128/76 mm Hg. A three-component friction rub is heard at the left sternal border. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 68 mm/h (reference below 30 mm/h), and leukocyte count is 11,800/µL (reference 4,500 to 11,000/µL). Echocardiography shows a small pericardial effusion and no new wall-motion abnormality. Diagnosis: post-myocardial infarction (Dressler) syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C11-Q1","stem":"Some patients develop pericarditis 1 to 3 days after a transmural infarct, but hers began about 3 weeks later. Which mechanism best explains her pericarditis?","options":["Fibrinous inflammation spreading from the necrotic wall to the overlying pericardium","An immune response to myocardial antigens exposed by the infarct","Coxsackievirus B infection picked up during her hospital stay","Bacterial seeding of the pericardium during the stent procedure"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.4","dok":3,"approach":["Use the timing: weeks after MI, not days.","A delay of weeks fits an adaptive immune response.","Necrosis exposes heart proteins the immune system does not normally see."],"why":"Dressler syndrome usually appears a few weeks after MI or heart surgery. Necrosis releases myocardial antigens, the immune system forms antibodies against them, and immune complexes are thought to deposit on the pericardium and pleura. The result is fever, pericarditis, sometimes pleuritis, and high inflammatory markers. The delay reflects the time needed for an adaptive immune response.","whyNot":["Early post-MI pericarditis comes from inflammation over a transmural infarct and appears in the first few days. Her onset at 3 weeks does not fit.","Correct.","Coxsackievirus B is a common cause of viral pericarditis and myocarditis. Nothing here links a new viral infection to her infarct, and the timing fits Dressler syndrome.","Purulent bacterial pericarditis is rare after a stent and causes a much sicker patient with a purulent effusion. Her small effusion and recent MI fit an immune cause."],"study":{"topics":["Dressler syndrome","Early versus late pericarditis after MI"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-F3","CV-18-C3"]}},{"id":"CV-C11-Q2","stem":"If the infarcted region of her anterior wall were examined now, 3 weeks after the infarct, which of the following microscopic findings would most likely be seen?","options":["Dense collagen scar that contains very few cells","Macrophages engulfing dead myocytes at the infarct edge","Maturing granulation tissue with increasing collagen","Coagulation necrosis with a dense band of neutrophils"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C3","obj":"CV-18-C.5","dok":2,"approach":["Place 3 weeks on the healing timeline.","Neutrophils peak at about 1 to 3 days and macrophages at about 3 to 7 days.","Granulation tissue forms at about 7 to 14 days, and a dense scar forms by about 2 months."],"why":"Infarcts heal in a predictable order. Neutrophils peak at about 1 to 3 days, macrophages clear dead cells at about 3 to 7 days, and granulation tissue with new capillaries forms at about 7 to 14 days. From about 2 to 8 weeks, collagen steadily increases and cellularity falls. By about 2 months the area is a dense collagen scar, so at 3 weeks she has maturing granulation tissue.","whyNot":["A dense, cell-poor collagen scar is expected by about 2 months. At 3 weeks healing is still in progress.","Macrophage removal of dead myocytes peaks at about 3 to 7 days. This is also when the wall is softest and most likely to rupture.","Correct.","Coagulation necrosis with a neutrophil band is the picture at about 1 to 3 days."],"study":{"topics":["Histologic timeline of myocardial infarction","Repair by connective tissue"],"comps":["CV-18-C3","CV-2"]}},{"id":"CV-C11-Q3","stem":"She also has pain along the ridge of her left trapezius muscle. Which of the following nerves carries pain from the parietal pericardium that is referred to this area?","options":["Vagus nerve","Phrenic nerve","Second intercostal nerve","Cardiopulmonary splanchnic nerves"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","obj":"CV-3.3","dok":2,"approach":["Trapezius ridge pain is referred to the C3 to C5 dermatomes.","Find the nerve with C3 to C5 roots that supplies the pericardium.","Separate pericardial pain from pain arising in the heart muscle itself."],"why":"The fibrous pericardium and the parietal layer of serous pericardium receive sensory fibers from the phrenic nerves (C3 to C5). Pain from inflamed parietal pericardium is referred to skin supplied by the same spinal segments, including the trapezius ridge and shoulder. Trapezius ridge pain is a classic clue to pericarditis. Myocardial ischemic pain travels with sympathetic fibers instead.","whyNot":["The vagus nerve carries some visceral sensation from the heart and lungs, mainly for reflexes, and does not cause referred pain to the trapezius.","Correct.","Intercostal nerves supply the chest wall and parietal pleura of the ribs, not the parietal pericardium. Their pain stays over the chest wall.","The cardiopulmonary splanchnic nerves carry sympathetic fibers and the visceral pain fibers that run with them from the heart to spinal segments about T1 to T4. That ischemic pain is referred to the chest and inner arm, not the trapezius."],"study":{"topics":["Innervation of the pericardium","Referred pain from the thorax"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-F3"]}},{"id":"CV-C11-Q4","stem":"A new myocardial infarction must be excluded. Which of the following ECG findings most strongly supports pericarditis rather than a new ST-elevation myocardial infarction?","options":["Horizontal ST depression in V4 to V6 that resolves with rest","ST elevation in V1 to V4 with reciprocal depression in II, III, aVF","ST elevation across many territories with PR-segment depression","Convex ST elevation in II, III, and aVF with new Q waves there"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-F3","obj":"CV-18-F.1","dok":3,"approach":["Pericarditis inflames the whole epicardial surface, not one artery's territory.","An MI produces ST elevation in one territory, often with reciprocal depression.","Inflamed atrial epicardium shifts the PR segment."],"why":"Pericarditis injures the epicardium over most of the heart, so concave ST elevation appears in leads from several coronary territories, usually without reciprocal ST depression except in aVR. Inflammation of the atrial epicardium creates an atrial injury current that depresses the PR segment. An ST-elevation MI follows the territory of one artery, often shows reciprocal depression, and may develop new Q waves. These differences let clinicians separate the two at the bedside.","whyNot":["Horizontal ST depression that resolves with rest fits exercise-induced subendocardial ischemia, as in stable angina.","ST elevation in V1 to V4 with reciprocal inferior depression follows the LAD territory. That pattern points to a new anterior infarct.","Correct.","Convex ST elevation limited to the inferior leads with new Q waves points to an RCA or circumflex infarct."],"study":{"topics":["ECG in pericarditis versus STEMI","Atrial injury current and PR depression"],"comps":["CV-18-F3","CV-7","CV-18-C3"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C12","system":"CV","dx":"Cardiogenic shock from left ventricular pump failure after a large anterior myocardial infarction","codes":["CV-18-H5","CV-18-C3"],"case":"A 74-year-old man in the coronary care unit is evaluated 14 hours after a large anterior wall myocardial infarction. He is confused, and his skin is cool, pale, and clammy. Pulse is 122/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 78/52 mm Hg. Crackles are heard over both lung fields. Urine output has been 10 mL/h for 4 hours. Serum lactate is 6.1 mmol/L (reference 0.5 to 2.2 mmol/L), and creatinine has increased from 1.0 to 2.3 mg/dL (reference 0.7 to 1.3 mg/dL). A pulmonary artery catheter shows a cardiac index of 1.6 L/min/m² (reference 2.5 to 4.0 L/min/m²), a pulmonary capillary wedge pressure of 28 mm Hg (reference 6 to 12 mm Hg), and an increased systemic vascular resistance. Diagnosis: cardiogenic shock from left ventricular pump failure.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C12-Q1","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains his cool, pale skin and high systemic vascular resistance?","options":["Nitric oxide released from activated endothelium in the skin","More carotid baroreceptor firing, which stimulates the vagus","Direct constriction of skin vessels by high lactate levels","Less baroreceptor firing, which raises sympathetic tone"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","obj":"CV-6.5","dok":2,"approach":["Low arterial pressure reduces stretch on the carotid sinus and aortic arch.","Less baroreceptor firing removes inhibition of the sympathetic outflow.","Sympathetic alpha-1 stimulation constricts skin arterioles."],"why":"When blood pressure falls, the carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptors fire less. The medullary centers respond by raising sympathetic output and lowering vagal output. Norepinephrine acting on alpha-1 receptors constricts arterioles in the skin, kidneys, and gut, which raises systemic vascular resistance and makes the skin cool and pale, while sympathetic stimulation of sweat glands makes it clammy. The response protects brain and heart flow but adds afterload to a failing ventricle.","whyNot":["Nitric oxide dilates vessels. Widespread nitric oxide release causes the warm skin and low resistance of septic shock.","Increased baroreceptor firing happens when pressure rises, and it slows the heart and dilates vessels. His pressure is low.","Lactate and acidosis tend to dilate local vessels. They cannot explain the high systemic resistance.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Baroreceptor reflex","Hemodynamic profiles of shock"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H5"]}},{"id":"CV-C12-Q2","stem":"His lactate is 6.1 mmol/L. In poorly perfused tissues, which of the following best explains why converting pyruvate to lactate allow ATP production to continue?","options":["It makes ATP directly by substrate-level phosphorylation","It restores the NAD+ that glycolysis needs to continue","It feeds acetyl-CoA into the citric acid cycle without oxygen","It reoxidizes FADH2 so the electron transport chain can work"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.4","dok":2,"approach":["Without oxygen, the electron transport chain cannot reoxidize NADH.","Glycolysis needs NAD+ at the glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase step.","Lactate dehydrogenase turns NADH back into NAD+ as it reduces pyruvate."],"why":"Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase needs NAD+ to keep glycolysis running. Normally the NADH it forms is reoxidized by the mitochondria, but that requires oxygen. In hypoperfused tissue, lactate dehydrogenase reduces pyruvate to lactate and regenerates NAD+, so glycolysis can keep making its 2 ATP per glucose. Lactate builds up in the blood, and a high level is a marker of tissue hypoperfusion and a poor prognosis in shock.","whyNot":["The lactate dehydrogenase reaction makes no ATP. The ATP comes from earlier glycolytic steps (phosphoglycerate kinase and pyruvate kinase).","Correct.","Pyruvate enters the citric acid cycle as acetyl-CoA through pyruvate dehydrogenase. Making lactate diverts pyruvate away from that pathway.","FADH2 is reoxidized only by the electron transport chain, which needs oxygen. Lactate formation does not involve FAD."],"study":{"topics":["Anaerobic glycolysis and lactate","NAD+ regeneration"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-H5"]}},{"id":"CV-C12-Q3","stem":"His creatinine continues to rise. Urine dipstick is negative for blood, and urine microscopy shows many muddy brown granular casts. Which process is most likely occurring in his kidneys?","options":["Prerenal azotemia with intact tubules that reabsorb sodium avidly","Tubules blocked by myoglobin released from injured skeletal muscle","Immune complex deposition in the glomerular basement membrane","Ischemic injury of tubular epithelium from low renal blood flow"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H5","obj":"CV-18-H.8","dok":3,"approach":["Shock lowers renal perfusion.","Muddy brown granular casts are made of shed tubular epithelial cells.","A dipstick negative for blood argues against myoglobin."],"why":"In shock, low cardiac output and intense renal vasoconstriction reduce blood flow to the kidneys. Tubular epithelial cells, especially in the proximal tubule and thick ascending limb, have high energy needs and are injured first. Injured cells detach and form muddy brown granular casts, tubules become blocked, and glomerular filtration falls. This acute tubular necrosis is one of the main forms of organ damage in progressive shock.","whyNot":["Prerenal azotemia shows bland urine or hyaline casts. Muddy brown granular casts mean the tubular cells themselves are injured.","Myoglobin causes a dipstick that is positive for blood without red cells on microscopy. His dipstick is negative.","Immune complex glomerulonephritis causes blood and red cell casts in the urine. His dipstick is negative for blood.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Acute tubular necrosis in shock","Stages of shock"],"comps":["CV-18-H5"]}},{"id":"CV-C12-Q4","stem":"On day 2, his C-reactive protein is 160 mg/L (reference below 10 mg/L). Which of the following cytokines, released by macrophages in the infarct, is the main stimulus for hepatocytes to make this protein?","options":["Interleukin-6","Interleukin-10","Interleukin-4","Interleukin-2"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-6","obj":"IM-6.3","dok":2,"approach":["C-reactive protein is an acute-phase protein made by the liver.","Identify the cytokine that most strongly drives acute-phase protein synthesis.","Rule out anti-inflammatory and lymphocyte growth cytokines."],"why":"C-reactive protein is an acute-phase reactant made by hepatocytes. Tissue injury, such as a large infarct, activates macrophages to release IL-1, TNF, and especially IL-6. IL-6 is the main stimulus for hepatocytes to make C-reactive protein and other acute-phase proteins such as fibrinogen and serum amyloid A. C-reactive protein binds damaged cells and microbes and activates complement.","whyNot":["Correct.","IL-10 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that suppresses macrophages. It does not drive acute-phase protein production.","IL-4 drives Th2 responses and IgE class switching. It does not stimulate C-reactive protein production.","IL-2 is a T-cell growth factor made mainly by activated T cells. It does not drive the acute-phase response."],"study":{"topics":["Acute-phase response","IL-6 and C-reactive protein"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-H5"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C13","system":"CV","dx":"Chronic aortic regurgitation from aortic root dilation","codes":["CV-18-D1"],"case":"A 34-year-old man comes to the physician because of fatigue and a pounding sensation in his chest when he lies on his left side. He is 196 cm tall, and his arm span exceeds his height. Ophthalmologic examination 1 year ago showed bilateral upward subluxation of the lenses. His father died suddenly of an aortic dissection at age 41 years. Blood pressure is 152/48 mm Hg, and peripheral pulses are bounding. A high-pitched, early diastolic decrescendo murmur is heard best with the patient sitting up and leaning forward at end-expiration. Echocardiography shows an aortic root diameter of 5.1 cm (reference under about 4.0 cm), moderate to severe aortic regurgitation, and a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 58% (reference 55% to 70%). Diagnosis: chronic aortic regurgitation due to aortic root dilation.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C13-Q1","stem":"Genetic testing in this patient is most likely to show a mutation in the gene encoding which of the following proteins?","options":["Fibrillin-1, a glycoprotein of elastic fiber microfibrils","Cystathionine beta-synthase, an enzyme of homocysteine use","Elastin, the core protein of arterial elastic lamellae","Type III collagen, a fibrillar collagen of vessel walls"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.3","dok":2,"approach":["Group the clues: tall build, long arm span, upward lens displacement, aortic root dilation, father with dissection.","Recognize Marfan syndrome, autosomal dominant.","Recall the gene: FBN1 on chromosome 15, which encodes fibrillin-1."],"why":"This is Marfan syndrome. FBN1 encodes fibrillin-1, which forms the microfibril scaffold that elastic fibers are built on. Defective fibrillin-1 weakens the aortic media (cystic medial degeneration) and the suspensory ligaments of the lens. Fibrillin-1 also normally holds TGF-beta in an inactive form, so loss of it increases TGF-beta signaling, which adds to aortic root enlargement.","whyNot":["Correct.","Homocystinuria (CBS deficiency) can give a tall build and lens dislocation, but the lenses usually shift downward, inheritance is autosomal recessive, and the main vascular problem is thrombosis.","Elastin (ELN) loss in Williams syndrome causes supravalvular aortic stenosis, not root dilation with lens displacement.","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (COL3A1) causes arterial and bowel rupture with thin, translucent skin. It does not cause tall stature or lens dislocation."],"study":{"topics":["Marfan syndrome genetics","Elastic fiber structure"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-I2"]}},{"id":"CV-C13-Q2","stem":"Which of the following changes in his left ventricular pressure-volume loop, compared with a normal loop, is most likely in this chronic, compensated state?","options":["Normal end-diastolic volume and smaller stroke volume, with a much higher peak systolic pressure","Larger end-diastolic and end-systolic volumes, with a smaller stroke volume","Larger end-diastolic volume and stroke volume, with a higher peak systolic pressure","Smaller end-diastolic volume and smaller stroke volume, with a normal peak pressure"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-4","obj":"CV-4.3","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the lesion: wide pulse pressure and early diastolic decrescendo murmur mean aortic regurgitation.","Ask which phase is lost: the leaky aortic valve lets blood enter the ventricle from the aorta as soon as ejection ends.","Add the volume load: regurgitant blood plus normal atrial filling raises end-diastolic volume and total stroke volume."],"why":"In aortic regurgitation, blood flows back into the left ventricle from the aorta during diastole, so the ventricle starts filling the moment ejection stops. Because the ventricle is never a closed chamber at that point, there is no true isovolumetric relaxation. The regurgitant volume adds to normal filling from the atrium, so end-diastolic volume rises, and the Frank-Starling mechanism produces a large total stroke volume. That large stroke volume raises systolic pressure, and the back-leak lowers diastolic pressure, which explains the 152/48 reading.","whyNot":["This is aortic stenosis: the ventricle must generate a much higher systolic pressure to push blood through a narrow valve, while stroke volume does not rise.","This is systolic heart failure, as in dilated cardiomyopathy. His ejection fraction of 58% shows that contraction is still preserved.","Correct.","This is mitral stenosis: a narrow mitral valve limits filling, so end-diastolic volume and stroke volume both fall."],"study":{"topics":["Pressure-volume loops in valve disease","Frank-Starling mechanism"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-4"]}},{"id":"CV-C13-Q3","stem":"His murmur is produced by blood flowing across one valve during part of the cardiac cycle. Which of the following best describes when this flow begins?","options":["At S1, when left ventricular pressure rises above left atrial pressure","At A2, when left ventricular pressure falls below aortic pressure","After an opening snap, when left atrial pressure exceeds ventricular pressure","After an ejection click, when ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-4","obj":"CV-4.2","dok":2,"approach":["Place the murmur in the cycle: early diastolic and decrescendo.","Name the valve: the aortic valve should be closed during diastole.","Link it to pressures: once ejection ends, aortic pressure exceeds ventricular pressure, and a leaky aortic valve lets blood flow back."],"why":"At the end of ejection, left ventricular pressure falls below aortic pressure and the aortic valve closes, producing A2. In aortic regurgitation the cusps do not seal, so blood flows back from the high-pressure aorta into the relaxing ventricle starting at that moment. The gradient is largest early in diastole and narrows as the ventricle fills and aortic pressure falls, which is why the murmur is decrescendo.","whyNot":["This describes mitral regurgitation, a holosystolic murmur that starts with S1 and is heard best at the apex.","Correct.","This describes mitral stenosis, a low-pitched diastolic rumble that starts after the opening snap of a stiff mitral valve.","This describes forward flow through a stenotic aortic valve (often a bicuspid valve with an ejection click), which produces a systolic crescendo-decrescendo murmur."],"study":{"topics":["Timing of murmurs in the cardiac cycle","Wiggers diagram"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-18-D1"]}},{"id":"CV-C13-Q4","stem":"His ventricle has handled the extra volume for years with a near-normal ejection fraction. Which of the following structural adaptations of the left ventricle best explains this?","options":["Concentric hypertrophy, with new sarcomeres added in parallel","Interstitial amyloid deposition with a stiff, thick wall","Eccentric hypertrophy, with new sarcomeres added in series","Asymmetric septal hypertrophy with myocyte disarray"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-D1","obj":"CV-18-D.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the load: aortic regurgitation causes volume overload.","Match load to remodeling: volume overload leads to eccentric hypertrophy, pressure overload leads to concentric hypertrophy.","Link structure to function: a larger cavity can eject a larger stroke volume at the same ejection fraction."],"why":"Chronic aortic regurgitation is a volume overload. Myocytes respond by adding sarcomeres in series, so the cells lengthen and the chamber enlarges, with a modest increase in wall thickness. The larger chamber holds the extra volume at a tolerable filling pressure and ejects a large stroke volume, so forward output is maintained for years. Over time, rising wall stress and fibrosis lead to systolic dysfunction and heart failure.","whyNot":["Concentric hypertrophy is the response to pressure overload, as in aortic stenosis or systemic hypertension. Wall thickness rises while the cavity stays normal or small.","Amyloid infiltration causes restrictive cardiomyopathy with a stiff, non-dilated ventricle.","Correct.","This is hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, a genetic sarcomere disease, not an adaptation to valve leakage."],"study":{"topics":["Ventricular remodeling: volume versus pressure overload"],"comps":["CV-18-D1","CV-18-B1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C14","system":"CV","dx":"Rheumatic mitral stenosis","codes":["CV-18-D2"],"case":"A 29-year-old woman, gravida 1, para 0, at 26 weeks' gestation comes to the physician because of worsening shortness of breath, two-pillow orthopnea, and cough with blood-streaked sputum. She grew up in a rural area with limited access to medical care. At age 10 years, she had an illness with fever and painful, swollen knees and ankles. Pulse is 108/min and regular. Cardiac examination shows a loud S1, an opening snap after S2, and a low-pitched diastolic rumble at the apex. Echocardiography shows thickened mitral leaflets with commissural fusion, a mitral valve area of 1.1 cm2 (reference 4 to 6 cm2), and an enlarged left atrium. Diagnosis: rheumatic mitral stenosis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C14-Q1","stem":"The valve damage began years ago after a streptococcal pharyngitis. Which immune mechanism best explains how that infection injured her heart?","options":["Type III hypersensitivity, with immune complexes trapped in valve capillaries","Persistent bacterial infection of the valve leaflets with local complement activation","Type I hypersensitivity, with IgE-armed mast cells degranulating in the valve","Type II hypersensitivity, with antibodies that cross-react with heart proteins"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.2","dok":2,"approach":["Link the history: fever and migrating large-joint arthritis in childhood suggest acute rheumatic fever.","Recall the cause: group A streptococcal pharyngitis followed by an immune reaction, not direct infection.","Classify it: antibodies against streptococcal antigens (M protein) bind similar host proteins, which is molecular mimicry and type II hypersensitivity."],"why":"Acute rheumatic fever follows group A streptococcal pharyngitis by about 2 to 4 weeks. Antibodies made against streptococcal antigens, especially M protein, cross-react with host proteins in the heart such as cardiac myosin and valve components. This is molecular mimicry, classified as type II hypersensitivity, and T cells reacting to the same shared antigens add to the damage. Repeated inflammation heals by fibrosis, producing thickened leaflets with commissural fusion years later.","whyNot":["Immune complex deposition is the mechanism of poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, which follows the same organism but affects the kidney.","Rheumatic fever is a sterile, immune-mediated disease. Live bacteria growing on a valve describes infective endocarditis.","Type I reactions cause allergy and anaphylaxis within minutes of antigen exposure. They do not cause delayed valve scarring.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Molecular mimicry","Hypersensitivity types"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-D5"]}},{"id":"CV-C14-Q2","stem":"Before pregnancy, her resting pulse was 72/min and she had no symptoms. It is now 108/min. Which of the following best explains how this change in heart rate produced her symptoms?","options":["A faster rate increases backflow from the ventricle into the left atrium in systole","A faster rate weakens ventricular contraction and lowers forward stroke volume","A shorter systole raises aortic pressure and the afterload on the left ventricle","A shorter diastole raises the left atrial pressure needed to fill the ventricle"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-D2","obj":"CV-18-D.3","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the lesion: a narrow mitral valve limits flow from the left atrium to the left ventricle.","Recall that tachycardia shortens diastole much more than systole.","Apply it: the same filling volume must cross the valve in less time, so the pressure gradient and left atrial pressure rise and back up into the lungs."],"why":"Flow across a stenotic mitral valve happens only in diastole and depends on the pressure gradient and the time available. As heart rate rises, diastole shortens much more than systole. To fill the ventricle in less time, the gradient across the valve must rise, so left atrial pressure climbs. That pressure passes back to the pulmonary veins and capillaries, causing dyspnea, orthopnea, and blood-streaked sputum. This is why pregnancy, fever, exercise, and atrial fibrillation with a fast rate often unmask mitral stenosis.","whyNot":["Systolic backflow is mitral regurgitation. In pure mitral stenosis the valve closes, so there is no systolic leak to increase.","Healthy myocardium contracts more strongly at faster rates (the force-frequency effect), and in mitral stenosis the left ventricle itself is normal. The problem is filling.","Heart rate does not raise afterload in this way, and afterload is not the limiting step in mitral stenosis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Diastolic filling time and heart rate","Hemodynamics of mitral stenosis"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-D2"]}},{"id":"CV-C14-Q3","stem":"Months later she develops atrial fibrillation, and transesophageal echocardiography shows a thrombus in the left atrial appendage. This part of the left atrium develops from which embryonic structure?","options":["Septum primum","Primitive atrium","Right horn of the sinus venosus","Common pulmonary vein"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","obj":"CV-1.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the typical site of thrombus in atrial fibrillation: the left atrial appendage.","Recall the two embryonic sources of the adult left atrium.","Match them: trabeculated appendage from the primitive atrium, smooth-walled part from the pulmonary veins."],"why":"The adult left atrium has two embryonic sources. The smooth-walled main portion forms when the common pulmonary vein and its branches are absorbed into the atrial wall. The original primitive atrium is left behind as the trabeculated, pouch-like appendage. Blood stagnates in this narrow, ridged pouch when the atrium no longer contracts, so it is the usual site of thrombus in atrial fibrillation and mitral stenosis.","whyNot":["The septum primum forms part of the interatrial septum (including the flap of the foramen ovale), not the appendage.","Correct.","The right sinus horn forms the smooth-walled part of the right atrium (sinus venarum), not any part of the left atrium.","The common pulmonary vein is absorbed into the left atrial wall to form its smooth-walled main part, not the trabeculated appendage."],"study":{"topics":["Development of the atria","Atrial fibrillation and thrombus"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-H6"]}},{"id":"CV-C14-Q4","stem":"Her sputum contains macrophages filled with golden-brown granules that stain blue with Prussian blue. Which process best explains these cells?","options":["Peroxidation of organelle membranes inside the macrophages, leaving lipofuscin granules","Small bleeds from congested alveolar capillaries, with hemoglobin stored as hemosiderin","Bilirubin crystals formed in macrophages from hemolysis within the circulation","Inhaled carbon particles from air pollution taken up by alveolar macrophages"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-B1","obj":"CV-18-B.1","dok":3,"approach":["Read the stain: Prussian blue positive means iron, which in tissue is hemosiderin.","Ask where the iron came from: red cells that leaked into alveoli.","Link to the lesion: mitral stenosis raises left atrial and pulmonary capillary pressure, causing chronic passive congestion and small bleeds."],"why":"In mitral stenosis, high left atrial pressure passes back to the pulmonary veins and capillaries, causing chronic passive congestion. Engorged capillaries leak red cells into the alveoli. Alveolar macrophages engulf these red cells and break hemoglobin down, storing the iron as hemosiderin, which is golden-brown and Prussian blue positive. These hemosiderin-laden macrophages are often called heart failure cells, and the same small bleeds explain her blood-streaked sputum.","whyNot":["Lipofuscin is a wear-and-tear pigment of aging cells. It is yellow-brown but does not stain with Prussian blue because it holds no iron.","Correct.","Bilirubin does not contain iron, so it is Prussian blue negative, and intravascular hemolysis does not load alveolar macrophages with pigment.","Anthracotic pigment is black and does not contain iron, so it does not stain with Prussian blue."],"study":{"topics":["Chronic passive congestion of the lung","Hemosiderin and other pigments"],"comps":["CV-18-D2","CV-18-B1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C15","system":"CV","dx":"Mitral valve prolapse","codes":["CV-18-D4"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the physician because of brief episodes of palpitations and occasional sharp chest pain unrelated to exertion. Pulse is 74/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Auscultation at the apex shows a midsystolic click followed by a short, late systolic murmur. She does not have tall stature, lens subluxation, or joint hypermobility. Echocardiography shows thickened, redundant mitral leaflets, with the posterior leaflet billowing more than 2 mm above the annulus into the left atrium during systole, and mild mitral regurgitation. Ejection fraction is 63% (reference 55% to 70%). Diagnosis: mitral valve prolapse.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C15-Q1","stem":"She is examined while squatting and again immediately after she stands up. Which of the following changes in her auscultatory findings is most likely on standing?","options":["Earlier click and longer murmur, because left ventricular end-diastolic volume falls","Later click and shorter murmur, because left ventricular end-diastolic volume rises","Earlier click and longer murmur, because left ventricular end-diastolic volume rises","Later click and shorter murmur, because left ventricular end-diastolic volume falls"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-D4","obj":"CV-18-D.7","dok":3,"approach":["Recall what standing does: blood pools in the legs, venous return and preload fall.","Link volume to prolapse: a smaller ventricle leaves the redundant leaflet and chordae relatively longer, so the leaflet prolapses earlier in systole.","Predict: earlier click, longer murmur."],"why":"Standing suddenly reduces venous return, so left ventricular end-diastolic volume falls. In a smaller ventricle the redundant leaflet and its chordae are relatively too long for the chamber, so the leaflet reaches its prolapse point earlier in systole. The click moves closer to S1 and the late systolic murmur lengthens. Squatting, which raises venous return and afterload, does the reverse. Valsalva strain acts like standing.","whyNot":["Correct.","This is the effect of squatting: more venous return and higher afterload enlarge the ventricle, so the click moves later. Standing does the opposite.","Standing lowers venous return, so ventricular volume falls rather than rises.","Smaller volume makes the prolapse happen sooner, so the click moves earlier, not later."],"study":{"topics":["Bedside maneuvers and murmurs","Preload and ventricular volume"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-4"]}},{"id":"CV-C15-Q2","stem":"The midsystolic click is produced when the prolapsing leaflet is suddenly checked during ventricular contraction. Sudden tensing of which structure produces the click?","options":["Pectinate muscles","Crista terminalis","Septomarginal trabecula","Chordae tendineae"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-4","obj":"CV-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall how the mitral valve is held shut in systole: papillary muscles contract and pull on chordae tendineae.","Apply it to prolapse: the redundant leaflet balloons back until its chordae snap taut.","Choose the structure that tenses at that moment."],"why":"During ventricular systole the papillary muscles contract and their chordae tendineae hold the mitral leaflets so they meet without everting into the left atrium. In mitral valve prolapse the leaflets and chordae are redundant, so the leaflet billows backward until the slack chordae suddenly tighten. That sudden tensing produces the midsystolic click. The murmur that follows is the mitral regurgitation that starts once the leaflets no longer meet.","whyNot":["Pectinate muscles are ridges in the atrial walls and appendages. They do not connect to the valve leaflets.","The crista terminalis is a ridge in the right atrium between its smooth and trabeculated parts, unrelated to the mitral valve.","The septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) is in the right ventricle and carries the right bundle branch to the anterior papillary muscle. It is not attached to the mitral valve.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Mitral valve apparatus","Heart sounds"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-3"]}},{"id":"CV-C15-Q3","stem":"A resected leaflet from a patient with the same disease shows a markedly expanded middle (spongiosa) layer. The material that expands this layer and draws water into it is best described as which of the following?","options":["Proteoglycans with glycosaminoglycan side chains","Hydroxyapatite crystals of calcium phosphate","Amyloid fibrils with a beta-pleated sheet structure","Cholesterol esters stored within foam cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-D4","obj":"CV-18-D.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the histology of mitral valve prolapse: myxomatous degeneration of the spongiosa.","Ask which matrix molecules hold water: glycosaminoglycans are highly negatively charged and attract water.","Pick proteoglycans, the core-protein molecules that carry glycosaminoglycan chains."],"why":"Mitral valve prolapse is caused by myxomatous degeneration. The spongiosa layer fills with proteoglycans, whose glycosaminoglycan side chains (such as chondroitin sulfate and dermatan sulfate) carry many negative charges and bind water. At the same time collagen and elastic fibers in the fibrosa become fragmented. The leaflets become thick, soft, and redundant, so they balloon into the atrium under systolic pressure.","whyNot":["Correct.","Calcium phosphate deposits make valve cusps rigid, as in calcific aortic stenosis.","Amyloid is a misfolded protein deposit that stiffens tissue. It is seen in restrictive cardiomyopathy, not floppy leaflets.","Lipid-laden foam cells are a feature of atherosclerotic plaques."],"study":{"topics":["Myxomatous degeneration","Proteoglycans and glycosaminoglycans"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-D4"]}},{"id":"CV-C15-Q4","stem":"Six years later she has had fever up to 38.4 °C (101.1 °F) and fatigue for three weeks. The fever began about two weeks after a tooth extraction. Blood cultures grow alpha-hemolytic, optochin-resistant, gram-positive cocci in chains. Which property of this organism best explains how it settles on her mitral valve?","options":["IgA protease that cleaves secretory antibody at mucosal surfaces","M protein that blocks complement deposition and neutrophil phagocytosis","Dextran that binds fibrin-platelet deposits on the damaged leaflet","Coagulase that converts fibrinogen to fibrin around the bacteria"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.1","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the organism: alpha-hemolytic, optochin-resistant streptococci after dental work are viridans group streptococci.","Recall the setting: subacute endocarditis on a previously abnormal valve.","Link virulence to site: dextran lets the bacteria stick to fibrin-platelet deposits on the damaged leaflet."],"why":"Viridans group streptococci live in the mouth and enter the blood during dental procedures. An abnormal, regurgitant valve develops small sterile fibrin-platelet deposits where flow is turbulent. Strains such as Streptococcus sanguinis make dextran, an extracellular polysaccharide that binds these fibrin-platelet deposits, so the bacteria adhere and grow into vegetations. The course is subacute, with weeks of low-grade fever and fatigue.","whyNot":["IgA protease helps Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria, and Haemophilus colonize mucosa. Pneumococci are optochin sensitive.","M protein is the main virulence factor of group A streptococci, which are beta-hemolytic and rarely cause endocarditis.","Correct.","Coagulase is the marker of Staphylococcus aureus, a gram-positive coccus in clusters that causes acute endocarditis, often on normal valves."],"study":{"topics":["Viridans streptococci","Subacute infective endocarditis"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C16","system":"CV","dx":"Chronic rheumatic heart disease with mitral stenosis and aortic regurgitation","codes":["CV-18-D5"],"case":"A 52-year-old man is referred to a cardiac surgeon because of worsening exertional dyspnea and ankle swelling. He had acute rheumatic fever at age 9 years and a recurrence at age 14 years after another episode of pharyngitis. Pulse is 84/min and irregularly irregular. Cardiac examination shows a low-pitched diastolic rumble at the apex, a soft early diastolic decrescendo murmur at the left sternal border, a loud P2, and a left parasternal heave. Echocardiography shows thickened, restricted mitral leaflets with a valve area of 1.3 cm2 (reference 4 to 6 cm2), mild aortic regurgitation, right ventricular hypertrophy, and an estimated pulmonary artery systolic pressure of 58 mm Hg (reference 15 to 30 mm Hg). Diagnosis: chronic rheumatic heart disease.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C16-Q1","stem":"He undergoes mitral valve replacement. Which of the following gross findings is most likely in the excised mitral valve?","options":["Thick leaflets, fused commissures, and short, thick, fused chordae","Bulky, friable vegetations that have eroded through the leaflets","Thin, ballooning leaflets with long, thin, stretched chordae","Calcium nodules at the leaflet bases with free, unfused commissures"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-D5","obj":"CV-18-D.8","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that chronic rheumatic disease follows repeated inflammation that heals by fibrosis.","Picture where scarring occurs: leaflet edges and commissures fuse, and chordae shorten and fuse.","Match the gross picture: a narrow, fish-mouth orifice."],"why":"Chronic rheumatic heart disease is the scarred end stage of repeated bouts of valvulitis. Fibrosis thickens the leaflets, fuses them at the commissures, and shortens, thickens, and fuses the chordae tendineae. The result is a rigid, funnel-shaped mitral orifice often called a fish-mouth or buttonhole deformity. The mitral valve is involved most often, followed by the aortic valve, as in this patient.","whyNot":["Correct.","Destructive vegetations are typical of acute infective endocarditis, often caused by Staphylococcus aureus.","Ballooning leaflets with elongated chordae describe myxomatous mitral valve prolapse.","Calcium at the base with free commissures describes degenerative calcification, such as mitral annular calcification or calcific aortic stenosis."],"study":{"topics":["Gross pathology of rheumatic valve disease"],"comps":["CV-18-D5","CV-18-D2"]}},{"id":"CV-C16-Q2","stem":"His second attack at 14 followed a new streptococcal pharyngitis and added to the damage from the first. Which feature of adaptive immunity best explains why a later infection can quickly re-trigger the cross-reactive response?","options":["Plasma cells switch from IgG back to IgM production to respond faster on re-exposure","Memory B and T cells formed in the first attack respond rapidly on re-exposure","Mast cells coated with IgE in the first attack degranulate on re-exposure","Natural killer cells recognize streptococcal peptides presented on MHC class I"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that rheumatic fever is an adaptive, antigen-specific response that cross-reacts with heart proteins.","Apply the principle of immunologic memory: the first response leaves memory B and T cells.","On a new infection those cells respond faster and more strongly, so recurrences add cumulative damage."],"why":"The first episode of rheumatic fever creates long-lived memory B and T cells specific for streptococcal antigens that also cross-react with heart proteins. A later group A streptococcal pharyngitis reactivates these cells, so the secondary response is faster and larger. Each recurrence adds new valve inflammation, and the scarring is cumulative. This is why patients with a history of rheumatic fever are given long-term antibiotic prophylaxis to prevent new streptococcal infections.","whyNot":["Class switching goes from IgM to IgG, IgA, or IgE and cannot be reversed. A secondary response is mainly IgG.","Correct.","IgE-coated mast cells mediate type I hypersensitivity, which is not the mechanism of rheumatic fever.","Natural killer cells do not recognize specific peptides on MHC class I. They kill cells that have lost MHC class I or show stress signals."],"study":{"topics":["Primary versus secondary immune response","Recurrent rheumatic fever"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-D5"]}},{"id":"CV-C16-Q3","stem":"A throat culture from his original pharyngitis would most likely have shown which laboratory profile?","options":["Alpha-hemolytic, optochin-sensitive, bile-soluble gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci","Beta-hemolytic, bacitracin-resistant, CAMP-positive gram-positive cocci in chains","Catalase-positive, coagulase-positive gram-positive cocci in grape-like clusters","Beta-hemolytic, bacitracin-sensitive, PYR-positive gram-positive cocci in chains"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-14","obj":"IM-14.6","dok":2,"approach":["Name the trigger of rheumatic fever: Streptococcus pyogenes (group A) pharyngitis.","Recall its identifying tests: beta-hemolysis, bacitracin sensitivity, and PYR positivity.","Rule out the other streptococci and staphylococci by their own tests."],"why":"Rheumatic fever follows pharyngitis caused by Streptococcus pyogenes, the group A streptococcus. In the lab it grows as gram-positive cocci in chains that are catalase negative, produce complete (beta) hemolysis on blood agar, are inhibited by bacitracin, and are PYR positive. Skin infections with group A strains can cause poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis but are not the classic trigger of rheumatic fever.","whyNot":["This is Streptococcus pneumoniae, which causes pneumonia, otitis media, and meningitis.","This is Streptococcus agalactiae (group B), a cause of neonatal sepsis and meningitis, not rheumatic fever.","This is Staphylococcus aureus. It does not trigger rheumatic fever.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Streptococcus identification","Group A streptococcus"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C16-Q4","stem":"Which of the following sequences best explains his increased pulmonary artery pressure and right ventricular hypertrophy?","options":["Recurrent thromboemboli from leg veins lodge in and gradually obstruct the pulmonary arteries","A left-to-right shunt delivers excess blood flow into the pulmonary circulation","High left atrial pressure backs up into the lungs, then pulmonary arterioles constrict and remodel","Chronic alveolar hypoxia from obstructive lung disease constricts and then remodels pulmonary arterioles"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-A1","obj":"CV-18-A.5","dok":3,"approach":["Start upstream at the obstruction: mitral stenosis raises left atrial pressure.","Follow the pressure back: pulmonary veins, capillaries, and then pulmonary arteries.","Add the arterial response and its result: reactive constriction and remodeling raise the right ventricle's afterload, causing hypertrophy, a loud P2, and a parasternal heave."],"why":"Mitral stenosis keeps left atrial pressure high. With no valves in the pulmonary veins, that pressure passes straight back to the pulmonary capillaries. The pulmonary arteries must then run at a higher pressure to keep blood moving forward, and over time the arterioles constrict and their walls thicken, raising pulmonary artery pressure further. The right ventricle faces this higher afterload and develops concentric hypertrophy, which produces the parasternal heave, while the loud P2 reflects forceful closure of the pulmonic valve.","whyNot":["Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension needs a history of clots. His valve disease already explains the finding.","A shunt such as an atrial or ventricular septal defect causes pulmonary hypertension from excess flow. He has no shunt.","Correct.","Hypoxic vasoconstriction from lung disease is a cause of pulmonary hypertension, but nothing in his case suggests chronic lung disease."],"study":{"topics":["Pulmonary hypertension due to left heart disease","Right ventricular response to afterload"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-18-A1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C17","system":"CV","dx":"Carcinoid heart disease","codes":["CV-18-D6"],"case":"A 61-year-old woman is admitted to the hospital because of a 1-year history of episodic facial flushing, watery diarrhea, and wheezing, and more recent leg swelling and abdominal fullness. Imaging shows a small tumor in the terminal ileum and multiple liver metastases. Her 24-hour urine 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA) is 180 mg/24 h (reference about 2 to 9 mg/24 h). Physical examination shows jugular venous distention and a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration. Echocardiography shows thickened, retracted, poorly mobile tricuspid leaflets with severe tricuspid regurgitation, a thickened pulmonic valve, and normal mitral and aortic valves. Diagnosis: carcinoid heart disease from a metastatic ileal neuroendocrine tumor.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C17-Q1","stem":"She also has a scaly rash on sun-exposed skin of her hands and neck and has become forgetful. Reduced synthesis of which cofactor best explains these new findings?","options":["Tetrahydrobiopterin, made from guanosine triphosphate","FAD and FMN, made from riboflavin","Thiamine pyrophosphate, made from thiamine","NAD+ and NADP+, made in part from tryptophan"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-5","obj":"IN-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the new findings as pellagra: dermatitis on sun-exposed skin, diarrhea, and dementia.","Link to the tumor: carcinoid cells convert a large share of dietary tryptophan into serotonin.","Recall that tryptophan is also a precursor for niacin, which forms NAD+ and NADP+."],"why":"Tryptophan normally supplies part of the body's niacin, which is built into NAD+ and NADP+. A carcinoid tumor diverts a much larger share of tryptophan than normal into serotonin, so less is left for niacin synthesis. The result can be pellagra: dermatitis in sun-exposed areas, diarrhea, and dementia. Her diarrhea may come from both serotonin excess and pellagra.","whyNot":["Tetrahydrobiopterin is a cofactor for tryptophan hydroxylase, but it is made from GTP, and its deficiency causes a form of hyperphenylalaninemia with neurologic signs rather than a photosensitive rash.","Riboflavin deficiency causes cheilosis, angular stomatitis, and glossitis. Riboflavin is not made from tryptophan.","Thiamine deficiency causes beriberi and Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, not a photosensitive dermatitis, and thiamine is not made from tryptophan.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Tryptophan metabolism","Niacin and pellagra"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-D6"]}},{"id":"CV-C17-Q2","stem":"An ileal carcinoid often causes no systemic symptoms until it has spread to the liver. Which venous route best explains why her symptoms appeared only after liver metastases formed?","options":["Left gastric vein draining into the portal vein","Superior mesenteric vein draining into the portal vein","Hepatic veins draining into the inferior vena cava","Inferior mesenteric vein draining into the splenic vein"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.4","dok":3,"approach":["Trace venous drainage of the ileum: superior mesenteric vein to portal vein to liver.","Recall that the liver inactivates serotonin (by monoamine oxidase) on this first pass.","Ask how liver metastases differ: they release products into the hepatic veins, which drain straight into the inferior vena cava and right heart."],"why":"Blood from the ileum travels through the superior mesenteric and portal veins to the liver, which breaks down serotonin and other vasoactive products before they reach the systemic circulation. Liver metastases secrete these products downstream of that filter, into the hepatic veins, which empty directly into the inferior vena cava. The products reach the right heart in high concentration, causing flushing, diarrhea, bronchospasm, and right-sided valve damage. Primary tumors outside the portal drainage, such as bronchial or ovarian carcinoids, can cause the syndrome without liver spread.","whyNot":["The left gastric vein drains the stomach into the portal vein, so its blood is filtered by the liver as well.","The ileum drains through the superior mesenteric vein into the portal vein, so serotonin from the primary tumor passes through the liver and is inactivated there.","Correct.","The inferior mesenteric vein drains the distal colon and reaches the portal system through the splenic vein, so its blood also passes through the liver."],"study":{"topics":["Portal and systemic venous drainage","First-pass hepatic metabolism"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-D6"]}},{"id":"CV-C17-Q3","stem":"A biopsy of the thickened tricuspid tissue would most likely show which of the following?","options":["Surface plaques of smooth muscle cells in a mucopolysaccharide-rich matrix","Small, sterile fibrin-platelet vegetations along the lines of leaflet closure","Granulomas with plump macrophages, giant cells, and foci of fibrinoid necrosis","Ballooning leaflets with an expanded spongiosa and fragmented collagen fibers"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-D6","obj":"CV-18-D.9","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the lesion: right-sided valve thickening and retraction in a patient with carcinoid syndrome.","Recall that carcinoid plaques are fibrous deposits laid on top of the valve and endocardium.","Match the histology: smooth muscle cells and myofibroblasts in a matrix rich in mucopolysaccharides."],"why":"Carcinoid heart disease produces plaque-like fibrous thickenings on the endocardium of the right heart chambers and on the tricuspid and pulmonic valves. They are made of smooth muscle cells and myofibroblasts in a matrix rich in acid mucopolysaccharides, laid on top of the existing valve. Serotonin and related products are thought to drive this fibrosis. The plaques stiffen and retract the leaflets, which typically causes tricuspid regurgitation and pulmonic stenosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Small sterile vegetations describe nonbacterial thrombotic (marantic) endocarditis, seen with hypercoagulable states such as advanced cancer, mainly on left-sided valves.","Granulomas with activated macrophages (Anitschkow cells) and giant cells are Aschoff bodies of acute rheumatic carditis.","An expanded spongiosa in floppy leaflets describes myxomatous degeneration in mitral valve prolapse."],"study":{"topics":["Carcinoid heart disease pathology"],"comps":["CV-18-D6","CV-18-D3"]}},{"id":"CV-C17-Q4","stem":"Her mitral and aortic valves are normal even though the tumor products are reaching her right heart in high amounts. Which enzyme and location best explain why the left-sided valves are spared?","options":["Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase in the lung","Catechol-O-methyltransferase in the left atrial wall","Tryptophan hydroxylase in the alveolar epithelium","Monoamine oxidase in the pulmonary vascular bed"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-15","obj":"CV-15.6","dok":3,"approach":["Note the pattern: right-sided lesions with left-sided sparing.","Ask what lies between the right and left heart: the pulmonary circulation.","Recall that monoamine oxidase in the lung vascular bed breaks serotonin down to 5-HIAA before blood reaches the left heart."],"why":"Blood from the right heart passes through the lungs before reaching the left heart. The pulmonary vascular bed takes up serotonin and breaks it down with monoamine oxidase to 5-HIAA, so much less reaches the mitral and aortic valves. Left-sided lesions do occur when this filter is bypassed, such as with a patent foramen ovale and right-to-left shunting, a primary bronchial carcinoid, or very high hormone levels.","whyNot":["Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase makes serotonin from 5-hydroxytryptophan, so it would add serotonin rather than remove it.","COMT mainly breaks down catecholamines such as epinephrine and dopamine, not serotonin, and the left atrial wall is not a major site of serotonin clearance.","Tryptophan hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme for serotonin synthesis, so it would also add serotonin.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Serotonin metabolism","Lung as a metabolic filter"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-15"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C18","system":"CV","dx":"Calcific aortic stenosis","codes":["CV-18-D1"],"case":"A 79-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after he lost consciousness while climbing two flights of stairs. For 6 months, he has had chest pressure and shortness of breath with exertion. Blood pressure is 118/84 mm Hg. A harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur is heard at the right upper sternal border and radiates to both carotid arteries. S2 is soft and single, and the carotid upstroke is weak and delayed. Echocardiography shows a three-cusp aortic valve with heavily calcified, poorly mobile cusps, an aortic valve area of 0.7 cm2 (reference about 3 to 4 cm2), a mean gradient of 48 mm Hg, concentric left ventricular hypertrophy, and an ejection fraction of 60% (reference 55% to 70%). Serum calcium is 9.4 mg/dL (reference 8.5 to 10.2 mg/dL). Diagnosis: severe calcific aortic stenosis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C18-Q1","stem":"Which process best explains the calcium deposits in his aortic valve?","options":["Metastatic calcification of normal tissue due to raised serum calcium and phosphate","Dystrophic calcification of damaged valve tissue despite normal serum calcium","Fibrous fusion of the commissures from earlier rheumatic valve inflammation","Myxomatous expansion of the spongiosa with proteoglycan accumulation"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-D1","obj":"CV-18-D.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note the normal serum calcium.","Recall the two types of pathologic calcification: dystrophic (damaged tissue, normal calcium) and metastatic (normal tissue, high calcium).","Link to age-related wear on a three-cusp aortic valve: dystrophic."],"why":"Calcific aortic stenosis of older adults results from years of mechanical stress and low-grade injury to the cusps, with lipid deposition, inflammation, and calcification that resembles bone formation. Because the calcium is laid down in damaged tissue while serum calcium is normal, it is dystrophic calcification. The calcium masses form in the cusps from the aortic side and stiffen them, typically without fusing the commissures.","whyNot":["Metastatic calcification needs high serum calcium or phosphate, as in hyperparathyroidism or kidney failure. His calcium is normal.","Correct.","Rheumatic aortic stenosis shows commissural fusion and almost always involves the mitral valve too. His cusps are calcified with no mention of fusion or mitral disease.","Myxomatous change makes leaflets soft and floppy, causing prolapse and regurgitation rather than stenosis."],"study":{"topics":["Dystrophic versus metastatic calcification","Calcific aortic stenosis"],"comps":["CV-18-D1","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C18-Q2","stem":"The cusps of the diseased valve developed from which of the following embryonic structures?","options":["Endocardial cushion swellings of the truncus arteriosus","Atrioventricular endocardial cushions surrounding the atrioventricular canal","Septum primum tissue at the upper border of the primitive atrium","Mesenchyme of the right and left horns of the sinus venosus"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","obj":"CV-1.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the valve from the murmur: systolic ejection murmur at the right upper sternal border radiating to the carotids means the aortic valve.","Recall that the aortic and pulmonic (semilunar) valves form in the outflow tract.","Pick the outflow tract cushions, which also receive neural crest cells."],"why":"The aortic and pulmonic valves develop from endocardial cushion tissue in the outflow tract (truncal swellings), with a contribution from cardiac neural crest cells. As the outflow tract divides into the aorta and pulmonary trunk, small swellings on the cushions are hollowed out to form three cusps for each valve. Abnormal formation or fusion of these swellings produces a bicuspid aortic valve, which calcifies and becomes stenotic at a younger age than a three-cusp valve.","whyNot":["Correct.","The atrioventricular cushions give rise to tissue of the mitral and tricuspid valves and the atrioventricular septum, not the semilunar valves.","The septum primum forms part of the interatrial septum and the flap of the foramen ovale.","The sinus venosus forms the smooth part of the right atrium, the coronary sinus, and part of the sinoatrial region."],"study":{"topics":["Development of the semilunar valves","Bicuspid aortic valve"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-G1"]}},{"id":"CV-C18-Q3","stem":"Two days later, he develops atrial fibrillation. His shortness of breath worsens and his blood pressure falls abruptly. Which of the following best explains why loss of atrial contraction harms him more than it would harm a person with a normal heart?","options":["Losing the atrial contraction raises the gradient across his narrow aortic valve directly","His stiff, thick ventricle relies on atrial contraction for a larger share of filling","Atrial fibrillation removes the main source of coronary blood flow to his thick ventricle","Atrial fibrillation lengthens diastole and lets too much blood fill his thick ventricle"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-4","obj":"CV-4.6","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the remodeling: aortic stenosis causes pressure overload and concentric hypertrophy.","Recall the cost: a thick, stiff ventricle relaxes and fills poorly in early diastole.","Apply it: atrial contraction supplies a larger share of filling, so its loss drops preload and stroke volume sharply."],"why":"In a normal heart, most ventricular filling happens passively in early diastole, and atrial contraction adds a smaller share at the end. A concentrically hypertrophied ventricle from aortic stenosis is stiff and relaxes slowly, so passive filling falls and atrial contraction supplies a much larger part of the end-diastolic volume. When atrial fibrillation removes that contraction, and the fast rate also shortens diastole, preload and stroke volume drop. Pressure backs up into the lungs and cardiac output falls.","whyNot":["The atrium sits upstream of the mitral valve. Losing its contraction lowers ventricular filling and stroke volume, so the aortic gradient tends to fall rather than rise.","Correct.","Coronary flow is driven by aortic pressure during diastole. Atrial contraction does not supply coronary blood flow.","Atrial fibrillation usually brings a fast ventricular rate, which shortens diastole and reduces filling, the opposite of this option."],"study":{"topics":["Atrial contribution to ventricular filling","Diastolic dysfunction"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-B1"]}},{"id":"CV-C18-Q4","stem":"Over the past year he has had recurrent melena from bleeding gastrointestinal angiodysplasia. He had no unusual bleeding earlier in life, including after a tooth extraction at age 60. Testing now shows loss of the largest von Willebrand factor (vWF) multimers. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this change?","options":["Autoantibodies against ADAMTS13 block normal cleavage of the largest vWF multimers","Congestive liver injury reduces hepatic synthesis and secretion of vWF","High shear stress across the narrow valve unfolds vWF so ADAMTS13 cleaves it","A germline VWF mutation prevents normal assembly of multimers in endothelial cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-9-B","obj":"HE-9-B.9","dok":3,"approach":["Note the timing: no bleeding problems until recently, so the defect is acquired.","Link it to the valve: blood jets through a narrow aortic valve at high shear.","Recall that shear unfolds large vWF multimers and exposes the site cleaved by the protease ADAMTS13."],"why":"Von Willebrand factor circulates as multimers, and the largest ones are the most effective at binding platelets to injured vessel walls. High shear stress across a stenotic aortic valve stretches these large multimers open and exposes the site that the plasma protease ADAMTS13 cleaves. The largest multimers are lost, which resembles type 2A von Willebrand disease. Together with bleeding from angiodysplasia this is called Heyde syndrome, and it often improves after the valve is replaced.","whyNot":["Antibodies that block ADAMTS13 cause acquired thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, in which very large vWF multimers build up, the opposite of this finding.","vWF is made by endothelial cells and megakaryocytes, not the liver.","Correct.","An inherited defect usually causes bleeding from early life. His normal bleeding history until recently points to an acquired cause."],"study":{"topics":["Von Willebrand factor structure and function","Acquired von Willebrand syndrome"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-D1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C19","system":"CV","dx":"Subacute infective endocarditis (viridans group streptococci) on a rheumatic mitral valve","codes":["CV-18-D3","CV-18-K1"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman with mitral regurgitation from childhood rheumatic fever comes to the physician because of low-grade fevers, night sweats, fatigue, and a 4-kg weight loss over 5 weeks. Two molars were extracted 7 weeks ago. Temperature is 38.1 °C (100.6 °F). Physical examination shows splinter hemorrhages, tender nodules on the pads of two fingers, and a palpable spleen tip. A holosystolic murmur that radiates to the left axilla is louder than it was last year. Hemoglobin is 10.6 g/dL (12.0 to 16.0), and MCV is 84 fL (80 to 100). Three blood cultures grow alpha-hemolytic, gram-positive cocci in chains that are optochin resistant and bile insoluble. Echocardiography shows a 9-mm vegetation on the atrial surface of the anterior mitral leaflet. Diagnosis: subacute infective endocarditis caused by viridans group streptococci.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C19-Q1","stem":"The organism adheres to fibrin-platelet deposits on the scarred leaflet by means of an extracellular polysaccharide. Which of the following dietary sugars is the substrate for synthesis of this polysaccharide?","options":["Sucrose","Galactose","Lactose","Maltose"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism: alpha-hemolytic, optochin-resistant, bile-insoluble streptococci are viridans streptococci.","Recall their adhesin: dextran (a glucose polymer) that binds fibrin-platelet deposits on damaged valves.","Name the substrate: glucosyltransferase builds dextran from sucrose."],"why":"Viridans streptococci such as S. mutans and S. sanguinis carry glucosyltransferase (dextransucrase). This enzyme splits sucrose, links the glucose units into dextran, and releases fructose. Dextran helps the bacteria stick to tooth surfaces and to the sterile fibrin-platelet deposits that form on valves scarred by rheumatic disease. Bacteremia after dental work lets them seed those deposits.","whyNot":["Correct.","Galactose is a monosaccharide from lactose. It is not used to build dextran, which is a glucose polymer.","Lactose is a glucose-galactose disaccharide from milk. Many bacteria ferment it, but it is not the substrate for dextran synthesis.","Maltose is a glucose-glucose disaccharide from starch breakdown. Glucosyltransferase does not use it to build dextran."],"study":{"topics":["Viridans streptococci virulence","Dextran and glucosyltransferase"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-D3"]}},{"id":"CV-C19-Q2","stem":"The mitral valve lies posterior to the left half of the sternum at the level of the fourth costal cartilage. At which of the following sites is her murmur most likely to be heard best?","options":["Second right intercostal space at the sternal border","Fifth left intercostal space at the midclavicular line","Fourth left intercostal space at the lower sternal border","Second left intercostal space at the sternal border"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-4","obj":"CV-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Link the murmur to the valve: a holosystolic murmur radiating to the axilla in this case is mitral regurgitation.","Separate the valve's position from its listening area: sound travels with blood flow toward the left ventricle.","Pick the mitral area: the cardiac apex, fifth left intercostal space at the midclavicular line."],"why":"Each valve is heard best downstream of it, in the direction its blood flows. Mitral sounds travel toward the left ventricular apex, so the mitral area is the fifth left intercostal space at the midclavicular line. Mitral regurgitation is holosystolic, loudest at the apex, and radiates to the left axilla. A vegetation that erodes a leaflet or chordae makes the regurgitation and murmur worse.","whyNot":["This is the aortic area. Aortic stenosis would be heard best here, with radiation to the carotids.","Correct.","This is the tricuspid area. Tricuspid regurgitation, common in endocarditis in people who inject drugs, is heard best here.","This is the pulmonic area. Pulmonic stenosis or a split S2 is heard best here."],"study":{"topics":["Auscultation areas of the heart valves","Mitral regurgitation murmur"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-18-D2"]}},{"id":"CV-C19-Q3","stem":"Urinalysis shows red blood cells, red blood cell casts, and 1+ protein. Serum C3 concentration is 58 mg/dL (90 to 180). Which of the following mechanisms best explains her renal findings?","options":["Septic emboli causing wedge-shaped infarcts in the renal cortex","Antibodies binding type IV collagen in the glomerular basement membrane","Immune complexes deposited in glomeruli, activating complement","Bacteria invading the renal tubules and interstitium from the blood"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-K1","obj":"CV-18-K.2","dok":3,"approach":["Read red cell casts as glomerular bleeding, which means glomerulonephritis.","Read the low C3 as complement being consumed.","Match a long-standing infection with low complement and glomerulonephritis to immune complex deposition (type III hypersensitivity)."],"why":"Weeks of bacteremia produce antibodies that bind circulating bacterial antigens. These immune complexes lodge in the glomeruli and activate complement, which lowers serum C3 and recruits inflammatory cells. The result is a glomerulonephritis with red cell casts, a type III hypersensitivity reaction.","whyNot":["Renal infarcts from emboli cause flank pain and hematuria, but they do not produce red cell casts or low C3.","Anti-GBM disease is type II hypersensitivity. It often affects the lungs as well, and serum complement is usually normal.","Correct.","Bacterial invasion of the tubules and interstitium is pyelonephritis, which produces white cell casts."],"study":{"topics":["Type III hypersensitivity","Glomerulonephritis in endocarditis"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-D3"]}},{"id":"CV-C19-Q4","stem":"Additional laboratory studies show serum iron 35 µg/dL (60 to 170), total iron-binding capacity 210 µg/dL (250 to 450), and ferritin 380 ng/mL (12 to 150). Which of the following mechanisms best explains her anemia?","options":["Chronic blood loss that has depleted total body iron stores","Hepcidin release driven by IL-6 that causes ferroportin breakdown","Antibody-mediated destruction of circulating red blood cells","Vitamin B12 deficiency slowing DNA synthesis in red cell precursors"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-9-A1","obj":"HE-9-A.2","dok":3,"approach":["Read the pattern: low iron, low TIBC, and high ferritin with a normal MCV.","Recognize anemia of chronic disease (anemia of inflammation) from a long infection.","Name the mechanism: IL-6 raises hepcidin, which makes cells internalize and degrade ferroportin, trapping iron in macrophages and enterocytes."],"why":"Ongoing infection raises IL-6, which makes the liver release hepcidin. Hepcidin binds ferroportin, the iron exporter on enterocytes and macrophages, and causes it to be taken in and degraded. Iron stays locked in storage, so ferritin is high while serum iron falls. The liver also makes less transferrin, so TIBC is low. Red cell production is limited by the iron supply.","whyNot":["Iron deficiency from blood loss gives low ferritin and high TIBC, the opposite of this pattern.","Correct.","Immune hemolysis would raise reticulocytes, LDH, and indirect bilirubin. It does not trap iron in stores.","B12 deficiency gives a macrocytic anemia with a high MCV. Her MCV is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Anemia of chronic disease","Hepcidin and ferroportin"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-16"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C20","system":"CV","dx":"Acute bacterial endocarditis of the aortic valve (Staphylococcus aureus)","codes":["CV-18-K1","CV-18-D3"],"case":"A 63-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus and an infected foot ulcer is brought to the emergency department because of 4 days of fever, rigors, and shortness of breath. An echocardiogram 1 year ago showed normal valves. Temperature is 39.7 °C (103.5 °F), pulse is 118/min, and blood pressure is 104/52 mm Hg. Physical examination shows painless erythematous macules on both soles and a new early diastolic decrescendo murmur at the left sternal border. Serum lactate is 4.6 mmol/L (0.5 to 2.2). Blood cultures grow gram-positive cocci in clusters that are catalase positive and coagulase positive. Echocardiography shows a 14-mm vegetation on the aortic valve with a perforated leaflet and moderate to severe regurgitation. Diagnosis: acute bacterial endocarditis caused by Staphylococcus aureus.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C20-Q1","stem":"His new murmur begins immediately after S2 and decreases in intensity through diastole. Which of the following pressure relationships best explains the timing and shape of this murmur?","options":["Atrium-to-ventricle gradient is highest at the end of diastole","Ventricle-to-atrium gradient stays high throughout all of systole","Ventricle-to-aorta gradient is highest in mid systole","Aorta-to-ventricle gradient is highest just after S2, then falls"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-4","obj":"CV-4.7","dok":3,"approach":["Connect the early diastolic decrescendo murmur and the perforated aortic leaflet to aortic regurgitation.","Ask when the leak is driven hardest: right after the aortic valve closes, when aortic pressure is far above left ventricular pressure.","Follow the gradient through diastole: aortic pressure falls and ventricular pressure rises as the ventricle fills, so flow and loudness fade."],"why":"A murmur is loudest when the pressure gradient driving the abnormal flow is largest. In aortic regurgitation, backflow begins as soon as the aortic valve closes (S2), when aortic pressure is far above left ventricular pressure. Through diastole the aorta drains backward and the ventricle fills, so the gradient narrows and the murmur fades. This produces a high-pitched, blowing, early diastolic decrescendo murmur.","whyNot":["A gradient that peaks at the end of diastole fits mitral stenosis, where atrial contraction causes presystolic accentuation.","A gradient that stays high through all of systole fits mitral regurgitation, a holosystolic murmur.","A gradient that peaks in mid systole fits aortic stenosis, a crescendo-decrescendo systolic ejection murmur.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Aortic regurgitation murmur","Pressure gradients in the cardiac cycle"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-18-D1"]}},{"id":"CV-C20-Q2","stem":"Susceptibility testing shows that the isolate grows in the presence of oxacillin and cefoxitin. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this resistance?","options":["D-Ala-D-Lac replacing D-Ala-D-Ala in cell wall precursors","Methylation of the binding site on 23S ribosomal RNA","A mecA-encoded penicillin-binding protein with low drug affinity","Hydrolysis of the beta-lactam ring by a plasmid-encoded penicillinase"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-12","obj":"IN-12.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize oxacillin and cefoxitin resistance in S. aureus as methicillin resistance (MRSA).","Recall that oxacillin resists staphylococcal penicillinase, so penicillinase cannot explain it.","Name the mechanism: mecA codes for PBP2a, which keeps building cell wall when beta-lactams are present."],"why":"Methicillin-resistant S. aureus carries the mecA gene on the SCCmec element. mecA encodes PBP2a, a transpeptidase that binds beta-lactams poorly, so cell wall cross-linking continues. Laboratories use cefoxitin or oxacillin to detect it. The plain penicillinase most S. aureus strains make cannot break down oxacillin.","whyNot":["This is the vancomycin resistance mechanism of enterococci (vanA). It does not affect beta-lactams.","This (erm) methylation causes resistance to macrolides and clindamycin.","Correct.","Most S. aureus make penicillinase, which explains resistance to penicillin G. Oxacillin and nafcillin are built to resist it."],"study":{"topics":["MRSA and mecA","Beta-lactam resistance mechanisms"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K1"]}},{"id":"CV-C20-Q3","stem":"His elevated serum lactate reflects poor tissue perfusion. Which of the following best describes the reaction that produces lactate in hypoxic cells and its purpose?","options":["Pyruvate dehydrogenase converting pyruvate to acetyl-CoA for the citric acid cycle","Alanine aminotransferase converting pyruvate to alanine for transport to liver","Pyruvate carboxylase converting pyruvate to oxaloacetate for gluconeogenesis","Lactate dehydrogenase reducing pyruvate to regenerate NAD+ for glycolysis"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.4","dok":3,"approach":["Without enough oxygen, the electron transport chain slows and NADH builds up.","Glycolysis needs NAD+ at the glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase step.","Lactate dehydrogenase reduces pyruvate to lactate and oxidizes NADH back to NAD+, so glycolysis can keep making ATP."],"why":"When oxygen delivery falls, mitochondria cannot reoxidize NADH. Glycolysis would stop at glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase without NAD+. Lactate dehydrogenase solves this by reducing pyruvate to lactate while turning NADH into NAD+. The cell keeps a small ATP supply, and lactate spills into the blood. A rising lactate is a marker of tissue hypoperfusion in sepsis.","whyNot":["Pyruvate dehydrogenase sends pyruvate into aerobic metabolism. Its flux falls when oxygen is short, which is why pyruvate is diverted to lactate.","Alanine aminotransferase makes alanine, used in the glucose-alanine cycle. It does not make lactate.","Pyruvate carboxylase is a mitochondrial gluconeogenic enzyme. It does not make lactate.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Anaerobic glycolysis","Lactic acidosis in shock"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-H5"]}},{"id":"CV-C20-Q4","stem":"On hospital day 3, he has no chest pain, and ECG shows no ST-segment changes. The PR interval has lengthened to 0.30 s (0.12 to 0.20), and intermittent second-degree AV block develops later. Which of the following complications most likely explains these findings?","options":["Septic embolus occluding the left anterior descending artery","Immune complex deposition damaging the sinoatrial node","Rupture of mitral chordae tendineae causing acute regurgitation","Aortic annular abscess invading nearby conduction tissue"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K1","obj":"CV-18-K.2","dok":3,"approach":["Notice new and progressing AV conduction delay in a patient with aortic valve endocarditis.","Recall the anatomy: the aortic annulus sits beside the membranous septum, where the AV bundle (bundle of His) passes.","Conclude that infection has spread beyond the leaflet into a perivalvular (ring) abscess, a typical S. aureus complication."],"why":"S. aureus is destructive and often spreads from the leaflets into the valve ring. The aortic annulus lies next to the membranous interventricular septum and the AV node and bundle of His. An abscess there causes new PR prolongation or higher-grade AV block. In aortic valve endocarditis, a new conduction block suggests a perivalvular abscess until proven otherwise.","whyNot":["An embolus to a coronary artery would cause an infarct, usually with chest pain or ST-segment changes. Neither is present.","Immune complex disease in endocarditis affects the kidneys and skin. SA node damage would cause sinus pauses or sinus bradycardia. The PR interval reflects AV conduction.","Chordal rupture causes acute mitral regurgitation and pulmonary edema. It does not damage conduction tissue.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Perivalvular abscess","AV node and bundle of His location"],"comps":["CV-18-K1","CV-7"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C21","system":"CV","dx":"Tricuspid valve endocarditis in a person who injects drugs (Staphylococcus aureus)","codes":["CV-18-K1","CV-18-D3"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman who injects heroin comes to the emergency department because of 9 days of fever, chills, pleuritic chest pain, and cough with blood-streaked sputum. Temperature is 39.2 °C (102.6 °F). Physical examination shows injection marks over both antecubital fossae. A holosystolic murmur is heard at the left lower sternal border, and the jugular venous pulse shows large v waves. Blood cultures grow catalase-positive, coagulase-positive, gram-positive cocci in clusters that are oxacillin susceptible. Echocardiography shows a 15-mm vegetation on the tricuspid valve with severe regurgitation. CT scan of the chest shows multiple peripheral nodules in both lungs, several with central cavitation. Diagnosis: tricuspid valve endocarditis caused by methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C21-Q1","stem":"Which of the following processes best explains the multiple peripheral, cavitating lung nodules on her CT scan?","options":["Reactivated tuberculosis spreading through the bronchial tree","Vegetation fragments crossing a patent foramen ovale to systemic arteries","Vegetation fragments carried by the pulmonary arteries to the lungs","Aspirated oral anaerobes forming a single dependent abscess"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K1","obj":"CV-18-K.2","dok":3,"approach":["Locate the source: a right-sided (tricuspid) vegetation.","Trace where its fragments go: right ventricle, pulmonary trunk, pulmonary arteries.","Match the result: many peripheral nodules that cavitate as the infected emboli form small abscesses (septic pulmonary emboli)."],"why":"Fragments that break off a tricuspid vegetation travel with venous blood through the right ventricle into the pulmonary arteries. They lodge in small peripheral branches of both lungs, cause infarcts, and form abscesses that cavitate. This explains her pleuritic pain and blood-streaked sputum. Left-sided vegetations embolize instead to the brain, kidneys, spleen, and limbs.","whyNot":["Reactivation tuberculosis usually causes upper lobe cavitation with weeks to months of symptoms. It does not explain a tricuspid vegetation with many bilateral peripheral nodules.","Crossing a patent foramen ovale sends emboli into the systemic circulation (paradoxical embolism), so they would bypass the lungs.","Correct.","Aspiration causes a lung abscess in a dependent segment, usually a single lesion. Her lesions are multiple and peripheral."],"study":{"topics":["Septic pulmonary emboli","Right-sided versus left-sided embolization"],"comps":["CV-18-K1","CV-18-H1"]}},{"id":"CV-C21-Q2","stem":"Her murmur at the left lower sternal border increases in intensity during inspiration. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this change?","options":["Decreased intrathoracic pressure that augments right heart preload","Lung expansion that displaces the left ventricle toward the chest wall","Increased right ventricular contractility during inspiration","Increased intrathoracic pressure that augments left ventricular filling"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","obj":"CV-15.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the murmur: holosystolic at the left lower sternal border with large v waves means tricuspid regurgitation.","Recall that inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure.","Apply it: lower intrathoracic pressure draws more venous blood into the right heart, so the right ventricle ejects and regurgitates more, and the murmur gets louder."],"why":"During inspiration the diaphragm descends and intrathoracic pressure falls. More blood returns from the venae cavae to the right atrium and right ventricle. A larger right ventricular volume means more blood flows back through the leaky tricuspid valve in systole, so the murmur grows louder (Carvallo sign). Right-sided murmurs tend to increase with inspiration, while left-sided murmurs do not.","whyNot":["Correct.","Inflating lungs move between the heart and the chest wall, which tends to soften heart sounds.","Inspiration does not raise right ventricular contractility. The louder murmur comes from greater right ventricular filling (preload).","Inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure, and it briefly reduces left heart filling."],"study":{"topics":["Heart sounds and respiration","Tricuspid regurgitation"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-4"]}},{"id":"CV-C21-Q3","stem":"Which of the following factors best explains why the infection involves the tricuspid valve rather than a left-sided valve?","options":["Injected particles injure the first valve that venous blood crosses","The tricuspid valve closes against the highest pressure in the heart","Rheumatic damage, which favors the tricuspid valve, was already present","Low oxygen tension in venous blood selects for anaerobic organisms"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K1","obj":"CV-18-K.1","dok":3,"approach":["Trace injected material: from an arm vein to the superior vena cava, right atrium, and then the tricuspid valve.","Recall that particles and drug additives in injected material are thought to damage the endothelium of the tricuspid valve.","Add skin flora (S. aureus) introduced with each injection, which can then attach to the injured valve."],"why":"In people who inject drugs, every injection delivers particles, drug additives, and skin bacteria into venous blood. The tricuspid valve is the first valve this material crosses, and repeated endothelial injury there is the accepted explanation for the right-sided preference. S. aureus from the skin is the most common organism. It is virulent enough to infect even a previously normal valve.","whyNot":["Correct.","The tricuspid valve closes against right ventricular pressure, which is far lower than left ventricular pressure. Higher pressure stress on left-sided valves would favor the mitral and aortic valves.","Rheumatic heart disease mostly affects the mitral valve, then the aortic valve.","S. aureus is a facultative anaerobe that grows well in oxygenated blood, and anaerobes are rare causes of endocarditis."],"study":{"topics":["Endocarditis in people who inject drugs","Valve involvement patterns in endocarditis"],"comps":["CV-18-K1","CV-18-D3"]}},{"id":"CV-C21-Q4","stem":"She injects into her right median cubital vein. Which of the following sequences of veins most directly carries the injected bacteria to the right atrium?","options":["Basilic, axillary, subclavian, right brachiocephalic, inferior vena cava","Basilic, axillary, subclavian, right brachiocephalic, superior vena cava","Cephalic, external jugular, azygos, right brachiocephalic, superior vena cava","Basilic, brachial, internal jugular, right brachiocephalic, superior vena cava"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.4","dok":2,"approach":["The median cubital vein links the cephalic and basilic veins in the cubital fossa.","The basilic vein joins the brachial veins to form the axillary vein, which becomes the subclavian vein at the first rib.","The subclavian joins the internal jugular to form the brachiocephalic vein, and the two brachiocephalic veins form the superior vena cava."],"why":"Blood from the median cubital vein drains into the basilic vein, which joins the brachial veins at the lower border of teres major to form the axillary vein. The axillary vein becomes the subclavian vein at the outer border of the first rib. The subclavian and internal jugular veins join to form the brachiocephalic vein. Both brachiocephalic veins form the superior vena cava, which empties into the right atrium.","whyNot":["The inferior vena cava drains the lower body. Upper limb blood returns through the superior vena cava.","Correct.","The cephalic vein drains into the axillary vein, and the azygos vein drains the posterior thoracic wall into the superior vena cava. This route is wrong.","The internal jugular vein drains the head and neck. Arm blood reaches the brachiocephalic vein through the subclavian vein."],"study":{"topics":["Venous drainage of the upper limb","Formation of the superior vena cava"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-3"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C22","system":"CV","dx":"Chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy (Trypanosoma cruzi)","codes":["CV-18-K2","CV-18-E1"],"case":"A 54-year-old man comes to the physician because of 6 months of palpitations, exertional dyspnea, and ankle swelling. He also has dysphagia to both solids and liquids. He grew up in a rural adobe home in Bolivia and moved to California 20 years ago. ECG shows right bundle branch block with left anterior fascicular block and frequent premature ventricular contractions. Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 30% (55 to 70) and a thin-walled apical aneurysm. Two different serologic tests detect IgG antibodies against Trypanosoma cruzi. Diagnosis: chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C22-Q1","stem":"He most likely acquired the infection as a child in his home. Which of the following was the most likely mode of transmission?","options":["Triatomine bug feces rubbed into the bite wound or eye","Bite of a female Anopheles mosquito injecting sporozoites","Bite of a female sandfly that injects promastigotes","Bite of a tsetse fly that injects parasites in its saliva"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism from the diagnosis: Trypanosoma cruzi.","Recall its vector: triatomine (reduviid, kissing) bugs that live in cracks of adobe walls and thatched roofs in Latin America.","Recall the route: the bug defecates while feeding, and parasites in the feces enter through the bite wound or the conjunctiva."],"why":"T. cruzi is spread by triatomine bugs that hide in the walls of poorly built rural homes and feed at night. The bug passes infective trypomastigotes in its feces while feeding. Scratching rubs the feces into the bite wound, or the parasites enter through the conjunctiva, which can cause one-sided eyelid swelling (Romaña sign). Blood transfusion, organ transplant, mother-to-baby spread, and contaminated food are less common routes.","whyNot":["Correct.","Anopheles mosquitoes transmit Plasmodium species, which cause malaria.","Sandflies transmit Leishmania species.","Tsetse flies transmit Trypanosoma brucei, the cause of African sleeping sickness, through their saliva."],"study":{"topics":["Trypanosoma cruzi life cycle","Protozoan vectors"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K2"]}},{"id":"CV-C22-Q2","stem":"Right bundle branch block is the most common conduction abnormality in chronic Chagas heart disease. In the right ventricle, the right bundle branch normally reaches the base of the anterior papillary muscle by way of which of the following structures?","options":["Chordae tendineae of the tricuspid leaflets","Crista terminalis of the right atrium","Pectinate muscles of the right auricle","Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-7","obj":"CV-7.1","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the right bundle branch: it runs down the right side of the interventricular septum.","Recall the muscular bridge from the septum to the anterior papillary muscle in the right ventricle.","Name it: the septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) carries the right bundle branch, so the anterior papillary muscle contracts in time."],"why":"The right bundle branch descends on the right side of the interventricular septum. Much of it crosses the right ventricular cavity in the septomarginal trabecula, also called the moderator band, to reach the base of the anterior papillary muscle. This shortcut lets the papillary muscle tense the tricuspid chordae before the ventricle ejects. Chagas inflammation and fibrosis commonly involve the right bundle branch, so right bundle branch block is a classic finding.","whyNot":["Chordae tendineae are fibrous cords from papillary muscles to valve leaflets. They contain no conduction tissue.","The crista terminalis is a ridge in the right atrium that separates its smooth and rough parts. The SA node lies near its upper end.","Pectinate muscles are ridges in the atrial walls and auricles. They carry no bundle branch.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Right bundle branch and moderator band","Right ventricle internal anatomy"],"comps":["CV-7","CV-3"]}},{"id":"CV-C22-Q3","stem":"His dilated, thin-walled left ventricle generates about the same systolic pressure as a normal ventricle, yet its wall stress and oxygen demand per beat are higher. Which of the following relationships best explains this?","options":["Force of contraction rises as end-diastolic fiber length increases","Fluid movement depends on hydrostatic and oncotic pressure gradients","Wall stress rises with chamber radius and falls with wall thickness","Flow through a vessel rises with the fourth power of its radius"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","obj":"CV-6.4","dok":3,"approach":["Note what changed: the chamber radius is larger and the wall is thinner, while pressure is unchanged.","Recall the law of Laplace: wall stress is proportional to pressure times radius, divided by wall thickness.","Apply it: a larger radius and thinner wall raise wall stress, and higher wall stress raises myocardial oxygen demand."],"why":"The law of Laplace says that wall stress equals pressure times radius divided by twice the wall thickness. A dilated ventricle has a larger radius and, in this case, a thinner wall, so each fiber must carry more tension to make the same pressure. Wall stress is a major driver of myocardial oxygen use, which is one reason a dilated heart works less efficiently.","whyNot":["This is the Frank-Starling relationship. It describes how filling changes stroke volume. It does not explain the rise in wall stress.","These are Starling forces across capillaries. They explain capillary fluid exchange and edema.","Correct.","This is Poiseuille's law for flow through a tube. It explains how vessel radius controls flow and resistance."],"study":{"topics":["Law of Laplace","Myocardial oxygen demand"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-E1"]}},{"id":"CV-C22-Q4","stem":"A barium swallow shows a dilated esophagus that tapers smoothly to a narrow point at a lower esophageal sphincter that fails to relax. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this finding?","options":["Loss of ganglion cells in the myenteric plexus","Fibrous replacement of smooth muscle from systemic sclerosis","Peptic stricture from long-standing acid reflux","Circumferential growth of squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K2","obj":"CV-18-K.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the pattern of achalasia: dilated esophagus, smooth taper, and a lower esophageal sphincter that does not relax.","Recall that sphincter relaxation depends on inhibitory neurons of the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus.","Link it to the diagnosis: chronic Chagas disease destroys these enteric neurons, causing megaesophagus and megacolon."],"why":"Chronic T. cruzi infection damages neurons of the enteric nervous system, especially the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus. Loss of inhibitory neurons that release nitric oxide and VIP means the lower esophageal sphincter cannot relax, and peristalsis fails. The esophagus slowly dilates above the closed sphincter (megaesophagus). The same process in the colon produces megacolon.","whyNot":["Correct.","Systemic sclerosis replaces esophageal smooth muscle with fibrosis, which leaves the lower sphincter weak and open and causes reflux.","A peptic stricture narrows the distal esophagus, but the sphincter still relaxes and dysphagia is usually to solids first.","Carcinoma usually causes progressive dysphagia to solids first, with weight loss and an irregular narrowing on barium study."],"study":{"topics":["Chagas megaesophagus","Enteric nervous system"],"comps":["CV-18-K2","CV-17"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C23","system":"CV","dx":"Lyme carditis with third-degree AV block (Borrelia burgdorferi)","codes":["CV-18-K3","CV-18-E2"],"case":"A 26-year-old man from Connecticut comes to the physician because of lightheadedness and one episode of syncope. He went camping in wooded areas in June. Five weeks ago, he had an expanding erythematous rash with central clearing on his thigh that resolved without treatment. Pulse is 42/min. ECG shows P waves at 86/min with no relationship to narrow QRS complexes at 42/min. Two-tier serologic testing (enzyme immunoassay followed by Western blot) is positive for IgM and IgG antibodies against Borrelia burgdorferi. Diagnosis: Lyme carditis with third-degree AV block.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C23-Q1","stem":"His escape QRS complexes are narrow, which places the block above the bundle branches. In Lyme carditis, the block most often occurs in the structure that normally delays conduction between the atria and ventricles. In which of the following locations is this structure found?","options":["Subepicardium where the superior vena cava meets the right atrium","Interatrial septum near the opening of the coronary sinus","Posterior wall of the left atrium between the pulmonary veins","Muscular interventricular septum near the cardiac apex"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-7","obj":"CV-7.1","dok":3,"approach":["Use the narrow escape QRS: the block is at or above the bundle of His.","Identify the structure that delays the impulse between atria and ventricles: the AV node.","Locate it: the lower interatrial septum, in the triangle of Koch near the coronary sinus opening, just above the septal tricuspid leaflet."],"why":"The AV node sits in the lower part of the interatrial septum, within the triangle of Koch, close to the opening of the coronary sinus and the septal leaflet of the tricuspid valve. It slows conduction so the ventricles fill before they contract. Lyme carditis most often blocks conduction at this level. A junctional escape rhythm then takes over, giving narrow QRS complexes at about 40 to 60/min.","whyNot":["This is the location of the SA node, the normal pacemaker. His P waves at 86/min show the SA node is working.","Correct.","The area between the pulmonary veins is where many atrial fibrillation triggers arise. It contains no AV conduction tissue.","The septum near the apex holds bundle branch and Purkinje fibers. Block there would give a wide, slow ventricular escape rhythm."],"study":{"topics":["AV node and triangle of Koch","Escape rhythms"],"comps":["CV-7","CV-18-K3"]}},{"id":"CV-C23-Q2","stem":"He recalls removing a small tick from his thigh about 2 days after the trip began. An infected Ixodes tick removed within about 24 hours of attachment rarely transmits B. burgdorferi. Which of the following best explains why transmission usually requires 36 to 48 hours or more of feeding?","options":["Spirochetes must first multiply inside the host's red blood cells","Spirochetes must move from the tick midgut to its salivary glands","The tick must molt into its adult stage while it is still attached","The tick must ingest the human's blood before it becomes infected"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall where B. burgdorferi lives in an unfed tick: the midgut.","Recall the trigger: the incoming blood meal activates the spirochetes, which multiply, change surface proteins, and migrate to the salivary glands.","Conclude that this migration takes time, so prompt tick removal lowers the risk."],"why":"In an unfed Ixodes tick, B. burgdorferi sits in the midgut. Once the tick starts feeding, the spirochetes multiply, switch surface proteins (OspA down, OspC up), and travel to the salivary glands. Only then are they injected with saliva. This process usually needs 36 to 48 hours or more of attachment, so checking for ticks daily and removing them promptly prevents most infections.","whyNot":["Babesia, which the same tick can carry, infects red blood cells. B. burgdorferi is an extracellular spirochete.","Correct.","Ticks molt between stages only after they drop off the host. Nymphs, which are small and easy to miss, transmit most human cases.","The tick acquired the spirochete earlier, as a larva or nymph feeding on infected mice. It does not need human blood to become infected."],"study":{"topics":["Lyme disease transmission","Ixodes tick life cycle"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K3"]}},{"id":"CV-C23-Q3","stem":"His Western blot shows IgG bands as well as IgM bands. Switching from IgM to IgG against the spirochete's protein antigens requires T-cell help. Which of the following interactions is required for this class switching?","options":["Bacterial lipopolysaccharide binding TLR4 on macrophages","Peptide on MHC class I binding CD8 on cytotoxic T cells","CD40 ligand on helper T cells binding CD40 on B cells","Complement C5a binding its receptor on neutrophils"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-5","obj":"IM-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note the shift from IgM to IgG over the weeks after infection.","Recall that class switching to protein antigens is T-cell dependent.","Name the contact signal: CD40 ligand (CD154) on activated helper T cells binding CD40 on B cells, together with cytokines."],"why":"B cells first make IgM. To switch to IgG, a B cell presents protein peptides on MHC class II to a helper T cell. The T cell then binds CD40 on the B cell through CD40 ligand and releases cytokines. These signals turn on activation-induced cytidine deaminase and class switch recombination. Without CD40 ligand, as in X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome, patients make IgM but little IgG.","whyNot":["B. burgdorferi lacks classic lipopolysaccharide, and TLR4 on macrophages drives innate inflammation. Its lipoproteins signal mainly through TLR2.","MHC class I with CD8 activates cytotoxic T cells, which kill infected cells. It does not drive B cell class switching.","Correct.","C5a attracts and activates neutrophils. It plays no direct role in class switching."],"study":{"topics":["Class switch recombination","T-dependent antibody responses"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-K3"]}},{"id":"CV-C23-Q4","stem":"If his infection had not been treated, which of the following manifestations would most likely have developed months after the rash?","options":["Migratory polyarthritis with subcutaneous nodules and chorea","Sudden monoarthritis of the big toe with urate crystals in fluid","Episodic swelling of one or a few large joints, often the knee","Symmetric small-joint arthritis with morning stiffness of the hands"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K3","obj":"CV-18-K.4","dok":2,"approach":["Place the patient on the Lyme timeline: early localized (erythema migrans), early disseminated (carditis, facial palsy), late (arthritis).","Recall the late form: attacks of arthritis in one or a few large joints, most often the knee.","Rule out look-alikes from other diseases by their distinct features."],"why":"Untreated Lyme disease can progress over months to late disease. The most common late finding in the United States is Lyme arthritis, with intermittent attacks of swelling in one or a few large joints, especially the knee. The swelling is often large and less painful than it looks. Carditis and facial nerve palsy belong to the earlier disseminated stage, which this patient is in now.","whyNot":["This combination is acute rheumatic fever after group A streptococcal pharyngitis.","This is acute gout from monosodium urate crystals.","Correct.","Symmetric small-joint arthritis with morning stiffness describes rheumatoid arthritis."],"study":{"topics":["Stages of Lyme disease","Lyme arthritis"],"comps":["CV-18-K3","CV-17"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C24","system":"CV","dx":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever (Rickettsia rickettsii)","codes":["CV-18-K4","CV-18-I12"],"case":"A 9-year-old girl in rural North Carolina is brought to the emergency department in July because of 5 days of fever, severe headache, and myalgias. Her parents found a tick on her scalp 8 days ago after she played with the family dog. Temperature is 39.8 °C (103.6 °F), pulse is 132/min, and blood pressure is 86/50 mm Hg. A rash that began as pink macules on the wrists and ankles has spread to the trunk and become petechial, including the palms and soles. There is edema of the hands and feet. Platelet count is 64,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000), and serum sodium is 129 mEq/L (135 to 145). Diagnosis: Rocky Mountain spotted fever caused by Rickettsia rickettsii. Doxycycline is started.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C24-Q1","stem":"Her petechial rash, edema of the hands and feet, and thrombocytopenia all result from infection of one principal host cell type. Which of the following cells does R. rickettsii primarily infect?","options":["Monocytes in blood and tissues","Red blood cells in the circulation","Neutrophils in blood and tissues","Endothelial cells of small vessels"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K4","obj":"CV-18-K.5","dok":3,"approach":["List the findings: petechiae, edema, low blood pressure, and low platelets.","Find the common cause: damage to small blood vessels (a vasculitis).","Name the target: R. rickettsii infects vascular endothelial cells (and vascular smooth muscle) of small vessels."],"why":"R. rickettsii enters and multiplies in the endothelial cells of small vessels throughout the body. Infected endothelium is injured, which produces a small-vessel vasculitis. Leaky vessels cause edema and low blood volume, bleeding into the skin causes petechiae, and platelets are used up at sites of endothelial damage. The same vessel damage in the brain, lungs, and kidneys explains the severe forms of the disease.","whyNot":["Ehrlichia chaffeensis infects monocytes, forming morulae in their cytoplasm.","Babesia infects red blood cells, causing hemolytic anemia.","Anaplasma phagocytophilum infects neutrophils (granulocytes).","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Rickettsial vasculitis","Tick-borne intracellular bacteria"],"comps":["CV-18-K4","CV-17"]}},{"id":"CV-C24-Q2","stem":"Two sets of routine blood cultures show no growth after 5 days. Which of the following properties of the organism best explains this finding?","options":["It is a spirochete too thin to see on Gram stain","It has a mycolic acid cell wall that needs weeks to grow","It is a virus that replicates only in host cell nuclei","It is a bacterium that grows only inside host cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism: R. rickettsii.","Recall its key property: an obligate intracellular gram-negative bacterium.","Apply it: it cannot grow on the cell-free media used for routine blood cultures, so diagnosis relies on serology, PCR, or skin biopsy staining."],"why":"Rickettsiae are small gram-negative bacteria that stain poorly and grow only inside living host cells. They take nutrients and energy molecules from the host cell, so they do not grow in routine blood culture bottles. Diagnosis uses indirect immunofluorescent antibody testing, PCR, or immunohistochemistry on a skin biopsy. Treatment must start on clinical suspicion because early tests are often negative.","whyNot":["Thin spirochetes such as Treponema and Borrelia are hard to see on Gram stain. R. rickettsii is a gram-negative coccobacillus.","Mycobacteria have mycolic acid walls and grow slowly. That would not fit this acute tick-borne illness.","R. rickettsii is a gram-negative bacterium that multiplies mainly in the host cell cytoplasm.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Obligate intracellular bacteria","Diagnosis of Rocky Mountain spotted fever"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K4"]}},{"id":"CV-C24-Q3","stem":"Within endothelial cells, R. rickettsii escapes the phagosome and spreads directly into adjacent cells without re-entering the bloodstream. Which of the following host processes does it use for this movement?","options":["Clathrin-coated pits taking it up from the cell surface","Polymerization of host actin into a tail that pushes it","Dynein motors carrying it along microtubules","SNARE-mediated fusion of vesicles with the plasma membrane"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize cell-to-cell spread in the cytoplasm, like Listeria.","Recall which cytoskeletal protein builds a moving tail: actin.","Apply it: the bacterium recruits host actin to its surface and is pushed by actin polymerization into a neighboring cell."],"why":"Spotted fever group rickettsiae, including R. rickettsii, recruit host actin to one end of the bacterium. Continuous actin polymerization forms a tail that pushes the bacterium through the cytoplasm and into protrusions that enter the next cell. This lets infection spread along the endothelium while the bacteria stay hidden from antibodies. Listeria monocytogenes and Shigella use the same host system.","whyNot":["Clathrin-coated pits bring cargo into the cell. They do not move bacteria from one cell to the next.","Correct.","Dynein moves cargo toward the minus ends of microtubules. Some viruses use it to reach the nucleus, but rickettsiae do not use it to spread between cells.","SNARE proteins fuse vesicles with membranes during secretion. They do not move cytoplasmic bacteria."],"study":{"topics":["Actin-based motility","Cell-to-cell spread of intracellular bacteria"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K4"]}},{"id":"CV-C24-Q4","stem":"Her serum albumin concentration is 3.9 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0), and her jugular veins are flat. Which of the following changes in capillary fluid exchange best explains the edema of her hands and feet?","options":["Increased capillary permeability to fluid and protein","Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure from venous congestion","Impaired lymphatic drainage from lymph node obstruction","Decreased plasma oncotic pressure from hypoalbuminemia"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.5","dok":3,"approach":["List the Starling factors: capillary hydrostatic pressure, plasma oncotic pressure, capillary permeability, and lymph drainage.","Use the clues: flat neck veins rule out venous congestion, and normal albumin rules out low oncotic pressure.","Connect the remaining factor to endothelial infection: damaged endothelium makes capillaries leaky."],"why":"Injured endothelium in rickettsial vasculitis lets fluid and protein leak into the interstitium. In Starling terms, the filtration coefficient rises and the reflection coefficient for protein falls. Fluid leaves the vessels even though hydrostatic and oncotic pressures are not abnormal. This causes edema and, at the same time, low blood volume and hypotension.","whyNot":["Correct.","Venous congestion would raise jugular venous pressure. Her neck veins are flat.","Lymphatic blockage causes lymphedema over weeks to months, usually from surgery, cancer, or filarial infection.","Low albumin would lower oncotic pressure, but her albumin is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces and edema","Capillary permeability"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H4"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C25","system":"CV","dx":"Dilated cardiomyopathy from chronic alcohol use (alcoholic cardiomyopathy)","codes":["CV-18-E1"],"case":"A 52-year-old right-handed man comes to the physician because of 4 months of progressive dyspnea on exertion, orthopnea, and ankle swelling. He has consumed 10 to 12 beers daily for 15 years. His pulse is 104/min and blood pressure is 112/74 mm Hg. Physical examination shows jugular venous distention, bibasilar crackles, an S3 gallop, and a laterally displaced apical impulse. Coronary angiography shows no significant stenosis. Echocardiography shows dilation of all four chambers with relatively thin walls, diffuse hypokinesis, and a left ventricular ejection fraction of 25% (reference 55% to 70%). Diagnosis: dilated cardiomyopathy caused by chronic alcohol use (alcoholic cardiomyopathy).","questions":[{"id":"CV-C25-Q1","stem":"Ethanol and its metabolite acetaldehyde are directly toxic to myocytes and weaken their contraction. In a healthy ventricular myocyte, which of the following directly triggers release of most of the calcium that activates contraction?","options":["Calcium binding calmodulin and activating myosin light-chain kinase","IP3 binding to receptors on the sarcoplasmic reticulum after Gq signaling","Calcium entering through L-type channels and opening ryanodine receptors","T-tubule voltage sensors mechanically pulling ryanodine receptors open"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-5","obj":"CV-5.2","dok":2,"approach":["The question asks what starts calcium release in cardiac muscle specifically.","Cardiac muscle uses calcium-induced calcium release: a small trigger influx releases a much larger store.","Rule out the skeletal muscle (mechanical coupling) and smooth muscle (IP3, calmodulin) mechanisms."],"why":"During the plateau of the ventricular action potential, L-type (dihydropyridine) calcium channels in the T tubules open. This small calcium influx opens ryanodine receptors (RyR2) on the sarcoplasmic reticulum, which release most of the calcium that binds troponin C. This is calcium-induced calcium release. Because the trigger influx and the sarcoplasmic reticulum store set how much calcium is released, they help set the force of each beat.","whyNot":["Calcium-calmodulin activation of myosin light-chain kinase drives smooth muscle contraction. Cardiac muscle is regulated by calcium binding to troponin C on the thin filament.","IP3-mediated calcium release is a main pathway in smooth muscle after Gq-coupled receptor activation. It plays only a minor role in ventricular myocytes.","Correct.","Direct mechanical coupling of the T-tubule voltage sensor (DHP receptor) to RyR1 is how skeletal muscle releases calcium. Cardiac muscle needs calcium entry to open RyR2."],"study":{"topics":["Excitation-contraction coupling in cardiac muscle","Cardiac versus skeletal versus smooth muscle calcium handling"],"comps":["CV-5","CV-6"]}},{"id":"CV-C25-Q2","stem":"In his liver, alcohol dehydrogenase converts ethanol to acetaldehyde, and aldehyde dehydrogenase converts acetaldehyde to acetate. Both reactions reduce the same cofactor. Which of the following ratios is most likely increased in his hepatocytes?","options":["NADPH to NADP+","NADH to NAD+","ATP to ADP","FADH2 to FAD"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-E1","obj":"CV-18-E.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the two dehydrogenase steps of ethanol metabolism.","Both use NAD+ as the electron acceptor, producing NADH.","Large alcohol loads therefore raise the NADH to NAD+ ratio."],"why":"Alcohol dehydrogenase and aldehyde dehydrogenase both transfer electrons to NAD+, so heavy drinking raises the NADH to NAD+ ratio. The high ratio pushes pyruvate toward lactate and oxaloacetate toward malate, which slows gluconeogenesis. It also slows fatty acid oxidation and favors glycerol-3-phosphate formation, which promotes fatty liver.","whyNot":["NADPH is used, not produced, by the microsomal ethanol-oxidizing system (CYP2E1), which oxidizes NADPH to NADP+. NADPH is mainly made by the pentose phosphate pathway.","Correct.","ATP and ADP describe energy charge. They are not the electron carriers reduced by these dehydrogenase reactions.","FAD is the cofactor for enzymes such as succinate dehydrogenase. Neither alcohol dehydrogenase nor aldehyde dehydrogenase uses FAD."],"study":{"topics":["Ethanol metabolism","Effects of a high NADH to NAD+ ratio"],"comps":["CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C25-Q3","stem":"A new grade 2/6 holosystolic murmur is heard best at the apex and radiates to the left axilla. Which of the following best explains this murmur?","options":["Mitral valve, leaflets pulled apart by a dilated ventricle","Aortic valve, cusps unable to close in a dilated aortic root","Tricuspid valve, leaflets pulled apart by a dilated right ventricle","Pulmonary valve, cusps unable to close in a dilated pulmonary trunk"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-18-D2","obj":"CV-18-D.4","dok":3,"approach":["A holosystolic murmur means blood leaks backward through an atrioventricular valve during systole.","Apex location with radiation to the axilla points to the mitral valve.","In dilated cardiomyopathy, annular dilation and outward displacement of the papillary muscles stop the leaflets from meeting (functional regurgitation)."],"why":"Mitral regurgitation produces a holosystolic murmur best heard at the apex that radiates to the left axilla. In dilated cardiomyopathy the leaflets are structurally normal, but the mitral annulus stretches and the papillary muscles are pulled outward and apically as the ventricle enlarges. The leaflets can no longer meet in systole, so blood leaks back into the left atrium. This is called functional or secondary mitral regurgitation.","whyNot":["Correct.","Aortic regurgitation from a dilated root causes an early diastolic decrescendo murmur at the left sternal border, not a holosystolic apical murmur.","Tricuspid regurgitation is also holosystolic and common in dilated cardiomyopathy, but it is heard best at the left lower sternal border and gets louder with inspiration. It does not radiate to the axilla.","Pulmonary regurgitation causes a diastolic murmur at the left upper sternal border, usually with pulmonary hypertension."],"study":{"topics":["Valve auscultation areas","Functional mitral regurgitation"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-18-D2"]}},{"id":"CV-C25-Q4","stem":"Eight months later, he develops sudden weakness of the right face and right arm and nonfluent speech with intact comprehension. Echocardiography shows a thrombus at the left ventricular apex. An embolus from this thrombus has most likely occluded which of the following arteries?","options":["Left middle cerebral artery","Basilar artery","Left anterior cerebral artery","Right middle cerebral artery"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Localize the deficit: right face and arm weakness with nonfluent speech and intact comprehension (expressive aphasia) means a left (dominant) frontal lesion in a right-handed person.","Face and arm motor cortex and Broca area are supplied by the middle cerebral artery.","Trace the embolus: left ventricle, aorta, left common carotid, left internal carotid, then straight into the left middle cerebral artery."],"why":"A poorly contracting, dilated ventricle allows blood to pool (stasis), so mural thrombi form, often at the apex. An embolus leaving the left ventricle enters the aorta and can reach either carotid. The middle cerebral artery is the direct continuation of the internal carotid and is the most common site for cardiac emboli. The left middle cerebral artery supplies the lateral motor cortex for the right face and arm and, in most right-handed people, the Broca speech area.","whyNot":["Correct.","Basilar artery occlusion affects the brainstem and occipital lobes, causing cranial nerve deficits, bilateral signs, or reduced consciousness.","The left anterior cerebral artery supplies the medial motor cortex, so it causes right leg more than arm weakness and spares the face.","A right middle cerebral artery embolus would cause left-sided face and arm weakness and usually neglect, not aphasia."],"study":{"topics":["Cardioembolic stroke","Cerebral arterial territories"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-H1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C26","system":"CV","dx":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with left ventricular outflow tract obstruction","codes":["CV-18-E1"],"case":"A 19-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after he collapsed during a sprint drill at college soccer practice and regained consciousness within 1 minute. He has had chest tightness with strenuous exercise for several months. His paternal uncle died suddenly at age 34 years. Physical examination shows a harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases in intensity when he stands from a squatting position and decreases with sustained handgrip. ECG shows left ventricular hypertrophy with deep, narrow Q waves in the lateral leads. Echocardiography shows asymmetric septal thickening of 24 mm (reference up to about 11 mm), a small left ventricular cavity, systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve, and a resting outflow gradient of 50 mm Hg. Diagnosis: hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with left ventricular outflow tract obstruction.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C26-Q1","stem":"Genetic testing finds a pathogenic variant in MYH7, the gene for beta-myosin heavy chain. His father carries the same variant. If the patient later has children with a partner who does not carry the variant, which of the following is the probability that each child inherits it?","options":["25%","75%","100%","50%"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the inheritance pattern: hypertrophic cardiomyopathy from sarcomere gene variants is autosomal dominant.","A heterozygous parent passes the variant allele to each child with a probability of one half.","Penetrance affects whether a carrier gets disease, not whether the allele is inherited."],"why":"Most familial hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is autosomal dominant and is caused by variants in sarcomere genes, most often MYH7 (beta-myosin heavy chain) and MYBPC3 (cardiac myosin-binding protein C). The patient is heterozygous, so each child has a 50% chance of inheriting the variant. Penetrance is incomplete and age-dependent, and severity varies even within one family, so relatives who carry the same variant may have mild disease or none. First-degree relatives are offered genetic testing and echocardiographic screening.","whyNot":["25% is the chance that a child of two carrier parents is affected in an autosomal recessive disorder.","75% is the chance of inheriting at least one variant allele when both parents are heterozygous for a dominant trait.","100% would apply only if the patient were homozygous for the variant, which is rare.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Autosomal dominant inheritance and penetrance","Sarcomere genes in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"comps":["CV-14"]}},{"id":"CV-C26-Q2","stem":"A teammate's screening echocardiogram shows a left ventricular wall thickness of 13 mm. Which of the following additional findings would most strongly favor physiologic athlete's heart over hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","options":["Thickening confined to the septum, sparing the free wall","A small left ventricular cavity, under 45 mm at end-diastole","Regression of wall thickness after 3 months of detraining","Impaired early diastolic relaxation on Doppler imaging"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-12","obj":"CV-12.5","dok":3,"approach":["A wall thickness of 13 to 15 mm is a gray zone where athlete's heart and mild hypertrophic cardiomyopathy overlap.","Athlete's heart is a reversible adaptation to training with normal relaxation and an enlarged cavity.","Pick the finding that only a physiologic adaptation would show."],"why":"Training causes a physiologic, reversible increase in cardiac mass, usually with an enlarged left ventricular cavity and normal diastolic function. When training stops for about 3 months, the wall thickness falls in athlete's heart but not in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, whose hypertrophy comes from a sarcomere defect. Regression with detraining therefore points strongly toward a physiologic adaptation.","whyNot":["Asymmetric septal thickening is the classic pattern of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Athletic hypertrophy is usually symmetric.","A small cavity under about 45 mm favors hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Trained athletes usually have a normal or enlarged cavity, often over 55 mm.","Correct.","Impaired relaxation favors hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Athlete's heart typically has normal or enhanced diastolic filling."],"study":{"topics":["Athlete's heart versus hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Cardiac adaptation to training"],"comps":["CV-12","CV-18-E1"]}},{"id":"CV-C26-Q3","stem":"His syncope occurred during an intense sprint. Which of the following pairs of changes during exercise most increases his outflow tract obstruction?","options":["Decreased heart rate and increased end-diastolic volume","Decreased contractility and increased afterload","Increased afterload and increased end-diastolic volume","Increased contractility and decreased afterload"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-E1","obj":"CV-18-E.2","dok":3,"approach":["In this disease the obstruction is dynamic: it worsens whenever the left ventricular cavity gets smaller or ejects faster.","During exercise, sympathetic drive raises contractility and heart rate, and dilation of muscle arterioles lowers systemic vascular resistance.","Stronger, faster ejection into a lower resistance pulls the anterior mitral leaflet toward the septum and narrows the outflow tract."],"why":"Exercise raises sympathetic tone, which increases contractility and heart rate, and metabolic vasodilation in working muscle lowers systemic vascular resistance. Stronger contraction empties the small ventricle further, and lower afterload speeds ejection. Both bring the thickened septum and the anterior mitral leaflet (systolic anterior motion) closer together, raising the outflow gradient. Cardiac output then cannot rise to meet demand, and syncope or a ventricular arrhythmia can follow.","whyNot":["A slower heart rate and a larger end-diastolic volume widen the outflow tract and lessen the obstruction, as with squatting or leg raising.","Decreased contractility and increased afterload both reduce the obstruction. This is why handgrip, which raises afterload, softens his murmur.","Higher afterload and a larger ventricular volume both reduce the obstruction. Squatting produces both and softens the murmur.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Dynamic outflow obstruction","Cardiovascular response to exercise"],"comps":["CV-12","CV-6"]}},{"id":"CV-C26-Q4","stem":"If his collapse had been fatal, which of the following findings on microscopic examination of the interventricular septum would most likely be seen at autopsy?","options":["Lymphocytic infiltrate surrounding necrotic myocytes","Hypertrophied myocytes arranged in a chaotic, swirling pattern","Amorphous pink extracellular deposits with apple-green birefringence","Coagulation necrosis with neutrophils and loss of nuclei"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-E1","obj":"CV-18-E.2","dok":2,"approach":["Link the diagnosis to its histologic hallmark.","Sarcomere protein defects produce hypertrophied myocytes that lose their parallel arrangement (myofiber disarray).","Rule out the patterns of myocarditis, amyloidosis, and acute infarction."],"why":"The characteristic histology of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is myocyte disarray: large, hypertrophied myocytes that branch and run in a disorganized, swirling pattern, most marked in the septum, with interstitial and replacement fibrosis. Disarray and fibrosis create an unstable electrical substrate. Ventricular arrhythmias from this substrate are the main cause of sudden death in young athletes with this disease.","whyNot":["A lymphocytic infiltrate with adjacent myocyte necrosis is the picture of viral or other lymphocytic myocarditis.","Correct.","Amorphous pink deposits with apple-green birefringence on Congo red staining define amyloidosis, which causes restrictive cardiomyopathy.","Coagulation necrosis with neutrophils is seen about 1 to 3 days after a myocardial infarction."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of the cardiomyopathies","Sudden cardiac death in young athletes"],"comps":["CV-18-E1","CV-2"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C27","system":"CV","dx":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy from hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis","codes":["CV-18-E1"],"case":"A 71-year-old man comes to the physician because of 6 months of progressive dyspnea on exertion, leg swelling, and early satiety. He underwent surgery for bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome 5 years ago. Blood pressure is 102/68 mm Hg. Physical examination shows jugular venous distention that increases with inspiration and hepatomegaly. ECG shows low QRS voltage in the limb leads. Echocardiography shows a left ventricular wall thickness of 17 mm (reference up to about 11 mm), a normal-sized ventricle, an ejection fraction of 55%, markedly enlarged atria, and no pericardial effusion. Serum and urine studies show no monoclonal protein. A technetium pyrophosphate scan shows intense cardiac uptake, and genetic testing finds a pathogenic transthyretin (TTR) variant. Diagnosis: restrictive cardiomyopathy from hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C27-Q1","stem":"Transthyretin is made mainly in the liver and normally circulates as a tetramer that carries thyroxine and retinol-binding protein. Which of the following mechanisms best explains how his TTR variant causes disease?","options":["Excess intestinal iron uptake deposits iron within myocytes","Low alpha-galactosidase A activity lets glycosphingolipids build up in lysosomes","Unstable tetramers release monomers that misfold into beta-sheet fibrils","Clonal plasma cells secrete free light chains that form fibrils"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-15-E","obj":"IM-15-E.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the protein: transthyretin is a transport tetramer made by the liver.","Pathogenic variants make the tetramer less stable, so it falls apart into monomers.","The monomers misfold into insoluble beta-pleated sheet fibrils that deposit in the heart and nerves."],"why":"Hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis is autosomal dominant. A single amino acid substitution destabilizes the transthyretin tetramer, which dissociates into monomers that misfold and assemble into beta-pleated sheet amyloid fibrils. Fibrils deposit in the myocardial interstitium, the carpal tunnel, and peripheral nerves. Wild-type transthyretin can do the same with age, mostly in older men, without any variant.","whyNot":["Increased iron absorption is hereditary hemochromatosis (usually HFE variants), which can cause a dilated or restrictive cardiomyopathy through iron overload, not amyloid.","Alpha-galactosidase A deficiency is Fabry disease, an X-linked lysosomal storage disorder that can thicken the ventricle. It does not form amyloid fibrils.","Correct.","Free light chains from a plasma cell clone cause AL amyloidosis. His negative serum and urine studies for monoclonal protein and the TTR variant point away from it."],"study":{"topics":["Protein misfolding and amyloid","Hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-E1"]}},{"id":"CV-C27-Q2","stem":"Two months later, he reports dysphagia to solid foods. A barium swallow shows smooth extrinsic compression of the anterior wall of the esophagus at the level of the heart. Enlargement of which of the following chambers most likely explains this finding?","options":["Left atrium","Left ventricle","Right atrium","Right ventricle"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","obj":"CV-3.2","dok":2,"approach":["Restrictive cardiomyopathy enlarges the atria because they must push blood into stiff ventricles.","Recall which surface of the heart each chamber forms.","The left atrium forms most of the base (posterior surface) and sits directly in front of the esophagus."],"why":"The left atrium forms most of the posterior surface (base) of the heart and lies just anterior to the esophagus, separated from it by the pericardium. When the left atrium enlarges, it can indent the esophagus and cause trouble swallowing solids. The same relationship is why transesophageal echocardiography gives such clear views of the left atrium.","whyNot":["Correct.","The left ventricle forms the apex, the left border, and much of the inferior (diaphragmatic) surface.","The right atrium forms the right border of the heart and receives the venae cavae. It does not lie against the esophagus.","The right ventricle forms most of the anterior (sternocostal) surface, the side farthest from the esophagus."],"study":{"topics":["Surfaces and borders of the heart","Relations of the left atrium"],"comps":["CV-3"]}},{"id":"CV-C27-Q3","stem":"His echocardiogram shows thick ventricular walls, yet his ECG shows low QRS voltage. Which of the following best explains this combination of findings?","options":["Concentric myocyte hypertrophy from long-standing pressure overload","Extracellular deposits thickening the wall without adding myocytes","Fluid in the pericardial sac that damps the cardiac electrical signal","Thinning and scarring of the wall after a large transmural infarction"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-E1","obj":"CV-18-E.3","dok":3,"approach":["QRS voltage reflects the mass of electrically active myocytes.","Wall thickness on echo reflects everything in the wall, including extracellular material.","Thick walls with low voltage mean the thickening is from something electrically silent."],"why":"Amyloid fibrils accumulate between myocytes, so the wall thickens while the amount of working, electrically active muscle stays the same or falls. The ECG therefore shows low voltage despite a thick wall on echo, a classic clue to infiltrative cardiomyopathy. The deposits also make the ventricle stiff, which causes the restrictive filling, enlarged atria, and rising neck veins on inspiration.","whyNot":["True myocyte hypertrophy from pressure overload adds electrically active muscle and raises QRS voltage.","Correct.","A pericardial effusion can lower QRS voltage, but his echocardiogram shows no effusion, and an effusion would not thicken the walls.","A large infarction thins and scars the wall. His walls are thick, and nothing in the case suggests a prior infarction."],"study":{"topics":["Infiltrative cardiomyopathy","Restrictive physiology"],"comps":["CV-18-E1","CV-7"]}},{"id":"CV-C27-Q4","stem":"His earlier carpal tunnel syndrome resulted from amyloid deposits in the carpal tunnel compressing a nerve. Which of the following deficits would this compression most likely cause?","options":["Weak finger abduction with sensory loss in the little finger","Weak thumb adduction with a positive Froment sign","Weak thumb opposition with index fingertip sensory loss","Weak wrist extension with wrist drop"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-20-I2","obj":"NE-20-I.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the nerve that passes through the carpal tunnel: the median nerve.","Recall what the median nerve supplies distal to the wrist: the thenar muscles and palmar skin of the thumb, index, middle, and half of the ring finger.","Match the deficit to that distribution."],"why":"The median nerve runs through the carpal tunnel under the flexor retinaculum. Amyloid in the retinaculum and tendon sheaths narrows the tunnel and compresses it. Distal to the wrist, the median nerve supplies the thenar muscles, including opponens pollicis, and the palmar skin of the lateral three and a half digits. Bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome often appears years before the heart disease in transthyretin amyloidosis and is a useful early clue.","whyNot":["Weak finger abduction (dorsal interossei) and a numb little finger point to the ulnar nerve, which passes through the Guyon canal, not the carpal tunnel.","Weak thumb adduction (adductor pollicis) with a positive Froment sign is an ulnar nerve deficit.","Correct.","Wrist drop comes from radial nerve injury, usually in the arm, and the radial nerve does not pass through the carpal tunnel."],"study":{"topics":["Median nerve at the wrist","Extracardiac clues to amyloidosis"],"comps":["CV-18-E1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C28","system":"CV","dx":"Acute viral myocarditis from coxsackievirus B","codes":["CV-18-K6"],"case":"A 23-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of 2 days of sharp chest pain, fatigue, and dyspnea. Ten days earlier she had fever, sore throat, and diarrhea that resolved spontaneously. Temperature is 38.1 °C (100.6 °F), pulse is 118/min, and blood pressure is 98/64 mm Hg. Serum troponin I concentration is 3.2 ng/mL (reference below 0.04 ng/mL). ECG shows sinus tachycardia with diffuse nonspecific ST-segment and T-wave changes. Echocardiography shows global hypokinesis with an ejection fraction of 35% (reference 55% to 70%). Coronary angiography is normal. Endomyocardial biopsy shows a lymphocytic infiltrate with adjacent myocyte necrosis, and PCR of the tissue detects coxsackievirus B3. Diagnosis: acute viral myocarditis caused by coxsackievirus B.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C28-Q1","stem":"Coxsackievirus B is an enterovirus in the picornavirus family. After it enters a cell, its genome is translated directly by host ribosomes into one large polyprotein that viral proteases then cleave. Which of the following genome types allows this?","options":["Negative-sense single-stranded RNA","Positive-sense single-stranded RNA","Linear double-stranded DNA","Segmented double-stranded RNA"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.6","dok":2,"approach":["Ask what a genome must be for ribosomes to read it right away.","Only positive-sense RNA can act directly as messenger RNA.","Picornaviruses are small, nonenveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA viruses."],"why":"Picornaviruses, including coxsackieviruses, have a positive-sense single-stranded RNA genome that works as messenger RNA as soon as it enters the cytoplasm. Host ribosomes translate it into one polyprotein, which viral proteases cleave into structural proteins and the RNA-dependent RNA polymerase. The virus is nonenveloped, spreads mainly by the fecal-oral route, and enters cardiomyocytes through the coxsackievirus and adenovirus receptor (CAR).","whyNot":["Negative-sense RNA cannot be translated directly. These viruses (for example influenza and rabies) must carry their own RNA polymerase to make a positive strand first.","Correct.","Double-stranded DNA must be transcribed into messenger RNA before translation, usually in the nucleus, as in adenoviruses and herpesviruses.","Segmented double-stranded RNA is the genome of reoviruses such as rotavirus, which must transcribe messenger RNA with a packaged polymerase."],"study":{"topics":["Picornavirus structure and replication","Viral genome types"],"comps":["CV-17"]}},{"id":"CV-C28-Q2","stem":"Infected cardiomyocytes display viral peptides on MHC class I molecules. Which of the following cells recognizes these complexes and directly kills the myocytes, contributing to the injury seen on her biopsy?","options":["Antibody-secreting plasma cell","CD4+ helper T cell","Natural killer (NK) cell","CD8+ cytotoxic T cell"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.6","dok":2,"approach":["Peptide on MHC class I is read by a T-cell receptor plus the CD8 coreceptor.","CD8+ T cells kill their targets with perforin and granzymes or Fas ligand.","Match the effector cell to both the antigen presentation and the killing."],"why":"Every nucleated cell, including cardiomyocytes, presents peptides from intracellular proteins on MHC class I. CD8+ cytotoxic T cells recognize viral peptide on MHC class I with their T-cell receptor and kill the infected cell through perforin and granzymes or Fas ligand. This clears the virus but also destroys myocytes, so much of the damage in viral myocarditis comes from the immune response itself.","whyNot":["Plasma cells secrete antibody against free virus and surface antigens. They do not recognize peptide-MHC complexes or kill cells themselves.","CD4+ helper T cells recognize peptide on MHC class II, which is found on antigen-presenting cells, and they mainly direct other cells rather than kill directly.","NK cells kill cells that have lost MHC class I (missing self) or are coated with antibody. They do not recognize specific peptide-MHC complexes.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["MHC class I and CD8+ T cells","Immune injury in viral myocarditis"],"comps":["CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C28-Q3","stem":"Myocarditis often causes arrhythmias. Inflammation that damages the intercalated discs can slow the spread of action potentials from one myocyte to the next. Which of the following junctions in the disc directly carries this current?","options":["Tight junctions","Fasciae adherentes","Gap junctions","Desmosomes"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","obj":"CV-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["List the parts of the intercalated disc: fasciae adherentes, desmosomes, and gap junctions.","Separate mechanical junctions from the electrical junction.","Gap junctions (connexin 43 channels) let ions flow between neighboring cells."],"why":"Gap junctions are channels built from connexins (mainly connexin 43 in ventricular muscle) that let ions pass directly between neighboring myocytes. This electrical coupling lets the myocardium act as a functional syncytium. Inflammation and scarring disturb gap junctions, which slows and fragments conduction and helps create reentrant arrhythmias.","whyNot":["Tight junctions seal epithelial and endothelial cells. They are not part of the intercalated disc.","Fasciae adherentes anchor actin filaments of the sarcomeres to the cell membrane and transmit force, not current.","Correct.","Desmosomes anchor intermediate filaments (desmin) and hold cells together under strain. They do not pass current."],"study":{"topics":["Intercalated disc structure","Cardiac muscle histology"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-7"]}},{"id":"CV-C28-Q4","stem":"One year later, her ejection fraction is 25% and all four chambers are dilated. Which of the following processes best explains this outcome?","options":["Interstitial amyloid deposits stiffening an otherwise normal ventricle","Atherosclerotic narrowing of the coronaries causing repeated infarcts","Inherited sarcomere protein defect causing myocyte disarray","Ongoing immune injury causing myocyte loss and replacement fibrosis"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the new picture as dilated cardiomyopathy following myocarditis.","Most myocarditis resolves, but in some patients viral persistence or autoimmune injury continues.","Ongoing myocyte loss is replaced by fibrous tissue, and the weakened ventricle dilates."],"why":"Viral myocarditis is a recognized cause of dilated cardiomyopathy. In a minority of patients, persistent viral genome or an autoimmune response against cardiac proteins such as myosin keeps injuring myocytes after the acute infection. Lost myocytes are replaced by fibrosis, the remaining muscle cannot keep up, and the ventricle remodels into a dilated, poorly contracting chamber.","whyNot":["Amyloid causes a stiff, thick-walled restrictive cardiomyopathy with a normal-sized ventricle, not four-chamber dilation, and is not a result of myocarditis.","Her angiography was normal and she is young, so ischemic cardiomyopathy from coronary disease is very unlikely.","Sarcomere gene defects with myocyte disarray cause hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, which is inherited and would not follow an infection.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Outcomes of myocarditis","Causes of dilated cardiomyopathy"],"comps":["CV-18-K6","CV-18-E1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C29","system":"CV","dx":"Dengue hemorrhagic fever (dengue with warning signs) in a second dengue infection","codes":["CV-18-K5"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the emergency department on day 5 of a febrile illness because of abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, and bleeding gums. The illness began 4 days after she returned from a 2-week trip to Southeast Asia, with sudden fever to 39.8 °C (103.6 °F), severe headache, retro-orbital pain, and intense myalgias and arthralgias. Her fever resolved today. She had confirmed dengue while living in the Caribbean 4 years ago. Laboratory studies show a platelet count of 32,000/µL (reference 150,000 to 400,000/µL), leukocyte count of 2,900/µL (reference 4,500 to 11,000/µL), and hematocrit of 47% (reference 36% to 46%), up from 38% on day 2. Ultrasonography shows ascites and a right pleural effusion. Dengue NS1 antigen is positive. Diagnosis: dengue hemorrhagic fever (dengue with warning signs) during a second dengue infection.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C29-Q1","stem":"Her travel companion asks how to avoid this infection on a future trip. Which of the following characteristics of the vector should guide prevention?","options":["Aedes mosquitoes biting mainly during the daytime","Triatomine bugs biting the face during sleep at night","Anopheles mosquitoes biting mainly from dusk to dawn","Ixodes ticks needing to stay attached for more than a day"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.5","dok":2,"approach":["Name the vector of dengue virus: Aedes mosquitoes, mainly Aedes aegypti.","Recall its behavior: it bites during the day and breeds in small containers of standing water near homes.","Prevention therefore means daytime repellent and covering skin, not only bed nets at night."],"why":"Dengue virus is a flavivirus spread by Aedes mosquitoes, mainly Aedes aegypti and also Aedes albopictus. Aedes aegypti bites mostly during the day, with peaks in the early morning and late afternoon, and breeds in water-holding containers around homes. Daytime repellent, long sleeves, and removing standing water matter more than bed nets alone.","whyNot":["Correct.","Triatomine (kissing) bugs transmit Trypanosoma cruzi, the cause of Chagas disease, through feces rubbed into the bite or mucosa.","Night-biting Anopheles mosquitoes transmit malaria, where bed nets are a mainstay of prevention.","Ixodes ticks transmit Lyme disease, and transmission usually requires attachment for more than a day."],"study":{"topics":["Arthropod vectors of cardiovascular infections","Dengue virus transmission"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K5"]}},{"id":"CV-C29-Q2","stem":"Her first dengue infection was with a different serotype. Which of the following mechanisms best explains why this second infection is more severe?","options":["Antigenic drift produces a new viral hemagglutinin that her antibodies miss","Non-neutralizing IgG boosts viral entry into Fc receptor-bearing monocytes","Virus-induced loss of CD4+ T cells leaves the new infection unchecked","Cross-reactive IgE from the first infection triggers mast cell degranulation"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.5","dok":3,"approach":["Note the key history: a prior infection with a different dengue serotype.","Antibodies from the first serotype bind the new serotype but do not neutralize it.","The antibody-coated virus is taken up through Fc gamma receptors on monocytes and macrophages, where it replicates (antibody-dependent enhancement)."],"why":"There are four dengue serotypes. Infection gives lasting immunity to that serotype only. In a later infection with a different serotype, cross-reactive IgG binds the virus without neutralizing it, and the Fc portion directs it into Fc gamma receptor-bearing monocytes and macrophages. This antibody-dependent enhancement raises viral load and, with cross-reactive T cells and cytokine release, drives the capillary leak of dengue hemorrhagic fever.","whyNot":["Antigenic drift of hemagglutinin describes influenza. Her two dengue infections were caused by different, established serotypes, not a drifted strain.","Correct.","Progressive loss of CD4+ T cells is the hallmark of HIV infection, not dengue.","IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation is type I hypersensitivity (allergy and anaphylaxis). It is not the basis of severe dengue."],"study":{"topics":["Antibody-dependent enhancement","Dengue serotypes and immunity"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-K5"]}},{"id":"CV-C29-Q3","stem":"Between day 2 and day 5, her hematocrit rose while ascites and a pleural effusion appeared. She has no history of heart, liver, or kidney disease, and her neck veins are flat. Which of the following changes in capillary fluid exchange best explains these findings?","options":["Obstructed lymphatic drainage of the pleura and peritoneum","Decreased plasma oncotic pressure from impaired albumin synthesis","Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure from venous congestion","Increased capillary permeability to plasma and protein"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.5","dok":3,"approach":["A rising hematocrit with new effusions means plasma is leaving the vessels while red cells stay behind.","Check each Starling force against the case: flat neck veins argue against high venous pressure, and she has no liver or kidney disease.","Dengue injures the endothelium, so permeability rises and the reflection coefficient for protein falls."],"why":"Fluid movement across capillaries depends on hydrostatic and oncotic pressures and on how permeable the wall is to water and protein. In dengue, viral and immune effects on the endothelium make capillaries leak plasma and protein into serous cavities. Plasma volume falls, red cells are concentrated, and the hematocrit rises. This leak usually peaks around the time the fever breaks and can lead to shock.","whyNot":["Lymphatic obstruction causes protein-rich edema over weeks, usually from tumor, surgery, or filariasis. Nothing in the case suggests it.","Failing albumin synthesis occurs with chronic liver disease and develops over weeks because albumin has a half-life of about 3 weeks, which does not fit a 3-day change.","Venous congestion raises capillary hydrostatic pressure in heart failure, but her neck veins are flat and she has no heart disease.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces","Capillary leak in dengue"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H4"]}},{"id":"CV-C29-Q4","stem":"Which of the following complications is she at greatest risk of developing over the next 24 to 48 hours?","options":["Hypovolemic shock from continued plasma leakage","Rupture of an enlarged spleen","Acute kidney failure from immune complex glomerulonephritis","Ischemic stroke from a cardioembolic clot"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K5","obj":"CV-18-K.6","dok":2,"approach":["Place her in the course of dengue: the fever has just broken and warning signs have appeared, which marks the critical phase.","The critical phase usually lasts about 24 to 48 hours and is driven by capillary leak.","Ongoing leak shrinks plasma volume and can progress to shock."],"why":"Dengue has a febrile phase, a critical phase, and a recovery phase. The critical phase begins around defervescence, usually about days 3 to 7 of illness, and lasts about 24 to 48 hours. Plasma leakage during this window can reduce circulating volume enough to cause hypovolemic shock (dengue shock syndrome), which is the main cause of death. Her rising hematocrit, effusions, and warning signs put her at high risk.","whyNot":["Correct.","Splenic rupture is a rare event and is classically linked to infectious mononucleosis.","Immune complex glomerulonephritis is not a typical early complication of dengue. Kidney injury in dengue usually follows shock.","Cardioembolic stroke requires a cardiac source of clot, such as atrial fibrillation, which she does not have."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of dengue","Dengue shock syndrome"],"comps":["CV-18-K5","CV-18-H5"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C30","system":"CV","dx":"Wet beriberi: thiamine deficiency with high-output heart failure","codes":["CV-18-E1"],"case":"A 36-year-old man comes to the physician 4 months after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass surgery because of dyspnea, leg swelling, and burning paresthesias in both feet. He has vomited after most meals for 6 weeks. His pulse is 116/min and blood pressure is 112/46 mm Hg. Physical examination shows warm extremities, bounding pulses, jugular venous distention, and pitting edema to the knees. Echocardiography shows dilated ventricles, an ejection fraction of 65%, and a cardiac output of 10 L/min (reference 4 to 8 L/min). Serum lactate concentration is 4.6 mmol/L (reference 0.5 to 2.2 mmol/L). Whole-blood thiamine concentration is low. Diagnosis: wet beriberi, thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency causing high-output heart failure.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C30-Q1","stem":"Thiamine pyrophosphate is the active form of thiamine and a cofactor for several enzymes. Decreased activity of which of the following enzymes best explains his increased serum lactate concentration?","options":["Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase","Pyruvate carboxylase","Pyruvate dehydrogenase","Lactate dehydrogenase"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.5","dok":3,"approach":["Identify which listed enzyme needs thiamine pyrophosphate.","Pyruvate dehydrogenase links glycolysis to the citric acid cycle by converting pyruvate to acetyl-CoA.","When it slows, pyruvate builds up and is converted to lactate by lactate dehydrogenase."],"why":"The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex needs thiamine pyrophosphate, lipoic acid, CoA, FAD, and NAD+. Without enough thiamine, pyruvate cannot enter the citric acid cycle as acetyl-CoA. Pyruvate accumulates and is reduced to lactate, causing lactic acidosis, and aerobic ATP production falls in high-demand tissues such as heart, nerve, and brain.","whyNot":["Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase is a glycolytic enzyme that uses NAD+, not thiamine. Reduced activity would slow glycolysis and lower pyruvate and lactate production.","Pyruvate carboxylase uses biotin, not thiamine. Its deficiency also raises lactate, but his deficiency is thiamine.","Correct.","Lactate dehydrogenase makes lactate from pyruvate. Reduced activity would lower lactate, and it does not need thiamine."],"study":{"topics":["Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex cofactors","Causes of lactic acidosis"],"comps":["CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C30-Q2","stem":"To confirm a functional deficiency, a laboratory measures an enzyme's activity in his erythrocytes before and after adding thiamine pyrophosphate. A large increase with the added cofactor confirms deficiency. Which of the following enzymes and pathways does this test measure?","options":["Transketolase, pentose phosphate pathway","Glutathione reductase, glutathione recycling","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase, pentose phosphate pathway","Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, citric acid cycle"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.5","dok":3,"approach":["List the thiamine-dependent enzymes: pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase, and transketolase.","Red blood cells have no mitochondria, so the three mitochondrial dehydrogenases are absent.","That leaves transketolase in the nonoxidative pentose phosphate pathway."],"why":"Transketolase is a thiamine pyrophosphate-dependent enzyme in the nonoxidative branch of the pentose phosphate pathway, which red blood cells use. The erythrocyte transketolase activity test measures activity with and without added thiamine pyrophosphate. A large increase with added cofactor shows that the enzyme was short of thiamine, which confirms functional deficiency.","whyNot":["Correct.","Glutathione reductase uses FAD, so a similar activation test with added FAD assesses riboflavin (vitamin B2) status, not thiamine.","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase is in the pentose phosphate pathway but uses NADP+, not thiamine pyrophosphate.","Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase does need thiamine pyrophosphate, but it is mitochondrial and red blood cells have no mitochondria."],"study":{"topics":["Thiamine-dependent enzymes","Pentose phosphate pathway"],"comps":["CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C30-Q3","stem":"His deficiency also reduces mitochondrial ATP production in his cardiomyocytes. In the cross-bridge cycle, which of the following steps is directly caused by ATP binding to the myosin head?","options":["Release of calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Shift of tropomyosin off the myosin-binding sites on actin","Power stroke that slides actin toward the sarcomere center","Release of the myosin head from actin"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-5","obj":"CV-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["Walk through the cross-bridge cycle step by step.","ATP binding detaches myosin from actin, ATP hydrolysis cocks the head, and phosphate release triggers the power stroke.","Calcium release and the tropomyosin shift come from excitation and calcium binding to troponin, not from ATP binding to myosin."],"why":"In the cross-bridge cycle, myosin stays tightly bound to actin until ATP binds the myosin head, which lowers its affinity for actin and releases it. Hydrolysis of ATP to ADP and phosphate then re-cocks the head, and release of phosphate after rebinding triggers the power stroke. Without ATP, myosin stays locked to actin (rigor). ATP is also needed for SERCA to pump calcium back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum so the muscle can relax, so an energy-starved heart struggles with both contraction and relaxation.","whyNot":["Calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum is triggered by calcium entry through L-type channels opening ryanodine receptors.","Tropomyosin moves off the myosin-binding sites when calcium binds troponin C.","The power stroke is triggered by release of inorganic phosphate after the cocked head binds actin, not by ATP binding.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cross-bridge cycle","Myocardial energy use"],"comps":["CV-5"]}},{"id":"CV-C30-Q4","stem":"His cardiac output is high, yet he has heart failure with edema. Which of the following mechanisms best explains the high-output state?","options":["Sympathetic vasoconstriction raising afterload and filling pressure","Peripheral vasodilation lowering systemic vascular resistance","A left-to-right shunt through an acquired septal defect","Increased blood viscosity from a rising hematocrit"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-B","obj":"CV-18-B.6","dok":3,"approach":["Read the clues: warm extremities, bounding pulses, a low diastolic pressure, and a wide pulse pressure.","These point to low systemic vascular resistance.","Low resistance raises venous return and cardiac output, while the energy-poor heart and kidney sodium retention lead to congestion."],"why":"In wet beriberi, impaired oxidative metabolism in tissues is thought to cause local metabolic vasodilation, so peripheral arterioles dilate. Systemic vascular resistance falls, venous return and cardiac output rise, and the pulse pressure widens. The kidneys sense low effective arterial filling and retain sodium and water through the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. The heart, short of energy, cannot keep pace, so filling pressures rise and edema forms. Thiamine replacement usually reverses the high-output state quickly.","whyNot":["Sympathetic vasoconstriction raises resistance and lowers output. It describes low-output heart failure with cool, pale extremities.","Correct.","A left-to-right shunt raises pulmonary flow, not systemic output, and nothing in the case suggests a septal defect.","Higher blood viscosity raises resistance and would tend to lower cardiac output, and his hematocrit is not reported as high."],"study":{"topics":["High-output heart failure","Wet versus dry beriberi"],"comps":["CV-18-E1","CV-13","CV-11"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C31","system":"CV","dx":"Malignant pericardial effusion from metastatic lung adenocarcinoma","codes":["CV-18-F1","CV-18-F2"],"case":"A 61-year-old woman who has never smoked comes to the physician because of a 3-week history of worsening shortness of breath, fatigue, and a dull sensation of fullness in her chest. Six months ago, biopsy of a right upper lobe lung mass showed adenocarcinoma. Pulse is 102/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 118/76 mm Hg. Physical examination shows distant heart sounds. Systolic pressure falls by 6 mm Hg with inspiration (normal fall is under 10 mm Hg). An ECG shows low QRS voltage, and a chest x-ray shows an enlarged, water-bottle-shaped cardiac silhouette. Echocardiography shows a large circumferential pericardial effusion with no collapse of the right atrium or right ventricle. Pericardiocentesis drains 800 mL of bloody fluid. Diagnosis: malignant pericardial effusion from metastatic lung adenocarcinoma.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C31-Q1","stem":"Pericardiocentesis through the left fifth intercostal space immediately adjacent to the sternum is considered. Which of the following anatomic features allows a needle at this site to enter the pericardial sac without passing through the left lung?","options":["The oblique fissure, which separates the left upper and lower lobes here","The cardiac notch of the left lung, with a matching notch in the pleura","The costodiaphragmatic recess, which holds no lung during quiet breathing","The lingula, which curls behind the heart and out of the needle path"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","obj":"CV-3.1","dok":2,"approach":["Picture what lies under the left fifth intercostal space beside the sternum.","Recall that the anterior border of the left lung and its pleura curve away from the midline at the level of the fourth and fifth costal cartilages.","That gap leaves part of the pericardium covered only by the chest wall, so a needle can enter the sac without crossing lung."],"why":"The anterior border of the left lung bends laterally to form the cardiac notch, and the left pleural sac has a smaller notch in the same place. This leaves a small area of pericardium with no lung or pleura in front of it, sometimes called the bare area. A needle placed in the left fifth or sixth intercostal space close to the sternum can enter the pericardial sac here. Staying close to the sternum also matters because the internal thoracic artery runs just lateral to the sternal border.","whyNot":["The oblique fissure is a cleft between two lobes. A needle placed into a fissure still passes through pleura and lung tissue on either side.","Correct.","The costodiaphragmatic recess lies much lower, near the eighth to tenth ribs at the lateral chest wall, and it is still lined by parietal pleura. It is the target for draining a pleural effusion.","The lingula is the tongue-shaped lower part of the left upper lobe. It lies in front of the heart, below the cardiac notch, so it would be in the way of a needle aimed lower."],"study":{"topics":["Surface anatomy of the lungs and pleura","Pericardiocentesis approaches"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-F2"]}},{"id":"CV-C31-Q2","stem":"Her 800-mL effusion developed without hypotension. In contrast, about 200 mL of blood accumulating within minutes after a penetrating cardiac wound can cause fatal tamponade. Which of the following properties of the pericardium best explains this difference?","options":["The fibrous pericardium stretches when filled slowly, keeping sac pressure low","The serous pericardium absorbs slowly forming fluid as fast as it is produced","The right ventricle thickens over weeks and resists compression from outside","The pericardium stiffens over several weeks and shields the chambers from fluid"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-F2","obj":"CV-18-F.4","dok":3,"approach":["Compare the two cases: a large volume tolerated versus a small volume that kills. The difference is the rate of filling.","Recall the pericardial pressure and volume curve: the tough fibrous pericardium is stiff over minutes but stretches over weeks.","Tamponade depends on the pressure inside the sac exceeding the filling pressures of the heart, and pressure is what slow filling keeps low."],"why":"The fibrous pericardium is dense collagen that barely stretches over minutes. When fluid enters quickly, pressure in the sac rises steeply, exceeds right heart filling pressure, and the chambers cannot fill. When fluid builds over weeks, the sac gradually enlarges, so pressure rises little even with a large volume. This is why a slowly growing malignant effusion can reach 1 L or more before tamponade, while acute hemopericardium of about 200 mL can be fatal.","whyNot":["Correct.","If the serous layer removed fluid as fast as it formed, no effusion would build up. Her 800 mL effusion shows that formation outpaced removal.","The right ventricle does not hypertrophy as a defense against outside pressure. Right ventricular hypertrophy follows pressure overload from inside, as in pulmonary hypertension.","A stiffer pericardium would raise sac pressure faster, making tamponade more likely. Stiffening and scarring describe constrictive pericarditis, which limits filling."],"study":{"topics":["Pericardial pressure and volume relationship","Rate of fluid accumulation and tamponade"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-F2"]}},{"id":"CV-C31-Q3","stem":"Molecular testing of her tumor shows a small deletion in one allele of the EGFR gene. The deletion keeps the receptor tyrosine kinase active in the absence of bound growth factor. Which of the following best describes this mutation?","options":["Loss of function in a tumor suppressor gene, so both copies must be lost","Loss of function in a DNA mismatch repair gene, causing unstable repeats","Gain of function from a translocation that fuses two separate genes","Gain of function in a proto-oncogene, so one altered copy is enough"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the effect: the receptor is active without its ligand, so the change adds function.","Classify the gene: a normal gene whose overactivity drives cell growth is a proto-oncogene.","Recall that activating changes in proto-oncogenes act dominantly at the cell level, so one altered allele is enough."],"why":"EGFR is a receptor tyrosine kinase. Normally, binding of a growth factor causes the receptors to pair up and phosphorylate each other, which turns on growth signals such as the RAS and MAPK pathway. Small in-frame deletions in the kinase region keep the receptor active without ligand. Because the altered protein is overactive, one mutated copy drives growth even with a normal second copy. EGFR mutations are more common in lung adenocarcinomas of people who have never smoked.","whyNot":["Tumor suppressors such as TP53 and RB1 usually need both copies lost (the two-hit model). Her mutation adds activity to a growth receptor, which is the opposite effect.","Mismatch repair defects, as in Lynch syndrome, cause microsatellite instability. They do not switch on a single receptor directly.","A fusion gene from a chromosomal rearrangement, such as EML4 and ALK in some lung adenocarcinomas, also adds function. Her change is a small deletion within one gene.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Proto-oncogenes and tumor suppressor genes","Receptor tyrosine kinase signaling"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-F1"]}},{"id":"CV-C31-Q4","stem":"Cytologic examination of the pericardial fluid shows clusters of large atypical cells, some forming small glands. Reactive mesothelial cells can have a similar appearance. Which of the following immunohistochemical staining patterns best supports metastatic lung adenocarcinoma?","options":["S-100 and HMB-45 positive, pan-cytokeratin negative","CK20 and CDX2 positive, CK7 negative","TTF-1 and napsin A positive, calretinin negative","Calretinin positive and TTF-1 negative"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-F1","obj":"CV-18-F.6","dok":3,"approach":["Name the two cell types to separate: reactive mesothelium versus metastatic lung adenocarcinoma.","Recall the markers: TTF-1 and napsin A mark lung adenocarcinoma, while calretinin marks mesothelial cells.","Pick the pattern that is positive for the lung markers and negative for the mesothelial marker."],"why":"Lung and breast cancers are the most common sources of metastasis to the pericardium, and malignant effusions are often bloody. TTF-1 (thyroid transcription factor 1) and napsin A are expressed by most lung adenocarcinomas. Calretinin is a calcium-binding protein expressed by mesothelial cells and mesothelioma. A tumor cell population that is TTF-1 and napsin A positive and calretinin negative supports metastatic lung adenocarcinoma.","whyNot":["S-100 and HMB-45 positivity with no cytokeratin points to melanoma, which can also spread to the pericardium.","CK20 and CDX2 positive with CK7 negative is the typical colorectal adenocarcinoma pattern. Lung adenocarcinoma is usually CK7 positive and CK20 negative.","Correct.","This pattern marks mesothelial cells. It fits reactive mesothelium or mesothelioma."],"study":{"topics":["Metastatic tumors of the pericardium","Immunohistochemistry of carcinoma versus mesothelium"],"comps":["CV-18-F1","CV-18-F2"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C32","system":"CV","dx":"Tuberculous pericardial effusion progressing to cardiac tamponade","codes":["CV-18-F2","CV-18-F3"],"case":"A 38-year-old man with untreated HIV infection (CD4 count 140 cells/µL, reference 500 to 1500) is brought to the emergency department because of lightheadedness and shortness of breath for 2 days. He has had night sweats, weight loss, and cough for 6 weeks. Temperature is 38.2 °C (100.8 °F), pulse is 124/min, and blood pressure is 86/64 mm Hg. Physical examination shows jugular venous distention to the angle of the jaw and muffled heart sounds. Systolic pressure falls by 18 mm Hg with each inspiration (normal fall is under 10 mm Hg). Echocardiography shows a large pericardial effusion with diastolic collapse of the right atrium and right ventricle. The drained fluid is an exudate with a high adenosine deaminase level, and culture later grows Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Diagnosis: tuberculous pericardial effusion progressing to cardiac tamponade.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C32-Q1","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains the large fall in his systolic pressure during each inspiration?","options":["Inspiration raises intrathoracic pressure and squeezes the venae cavae shut","Inspiration triggers vagal slowing that lowers cardiac output with each breath","Inspiration lowers right ventricular filling, so less blood reaches the lungs","Inspiration adds right ventricular filling that bows the septum leftward"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","obj":"CV-15.3","dok":3,"approach":["Name the sign: a fall in systolic pressure of more than 10 mm Hg on inspiration is pulsus paradoxus.","Recall normal breathing effects: inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure and increases venous return to the right heart.","Apply the fixed space: with the sac full, the right ventricle can only expand by pushing the septum into the left ventricle, which then fills less and ejects less."],"why":"Inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure, which draws more blood into the right atrium and right ventricle. Normally the free walls of the heart expand to take this volume. In tamponade the fluid-filled sac fixes the total volume of the heart, so the extra right ventricular filling bows the interventricular septum toward the left ventricle. Left ventricular filling and stroke volume fall, so systolic pressure drops more than 10 mm Hg. This ventricular interdependence is the main cause of pulsus paradoxus.","whyNot":["Inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure. Raised intrathoracic pressure that limits venous return happens during a Valsalva maneuver or positive pressure ventilation.","Inspiration speeds the heart rate slightly by reducing vagal tone (respiratory sinus arrhythmia). Vagal slowing comes with expiration.","Inspiration increases right ventricular filling. The problem is that the extra right-sided volume crowds the left ventricle.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pulsus paradoxus","Ventricular interdependence"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-18-F2"]}},{"id":"CV-C32-Q2","stem":"Before the fluid is drained, a right heart catheter is placed. Normal values are right atrial (RA) pressure 2 to 6 mm Hg and pulmonary capillary wedge pressure 6 to 12 mm Hg. Which of the following sets of pressures (mm Hg) is most likely to be found?","options":["RA 5, RV 42/6, wedge 26","RA 17, RV 64/17, wedge 10","RA 19, RV 34/19, wedge 19","RA 2, RV 18/2, wedge 3"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-F2","obj":"CV-18-F.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that in tamponade the pressure in the sac squeezes every chamber equally during diastole.","Predict that right atrial pressure, right ventricular diastolic pressure, and wedge pressure all rise to about the same value as the pericardial pressure.","Pick the set with high, equal diastolic pressures on both sides of the heart."],"why":"In tamponade the chambers can only fill until their pressure matches the pressure in the pericardial sac. Because the fluid surrounds all four chambers, the diastolic pressures in the right atrium, right ventricle, and left atrium (measured as the wedge pressure) all rise and become nearly equal, often around 15 to 20 mm Hg. Equal, raised diastolic pressures across the heart are the classic catheter finding. Constrictive pericarditis also equalizes pressures, but the clinical picture of a large effusion and diastolic chamber collapse points to tamponade.","whyNot":["A high wedge pressure with a normal right atrial pressure points to left heart failure or mitral valve disease, with the left side congested and the right side spared.","A high right ventricular systolic pressure with a normal wedge pressure fits pulmonary hypertension or a pulmonary embolus with right heart strain. The left-sided filling pressure is not raised to match.","Correct.","Low pressures everywhere fit hypovolemia. His distended neck veins show that his right-sided pressure is high."],"study":{"topics":["Hemodynamics of cardiac tamponade","Right heart catheter pressures"],"comps":["CV-18-F2","CV-6"]}},{"id":"CV-C32-Q3","stem":"A pericardial biopsy specimen shows caseating granulomas. Which of the following cytokines, secreted by CD4+ T lymphocytes, activates his macrophages to kill this organism and form granulomas?","options":["Transforming growth factor beta","Interferon gamma","Interleukin 4","Interleukin 12"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the response: granulomas are a cell-mediated (type IV) response to an intracellular organism.","Identify the helper cell: Th1 CD4 T cells drive this response.","Name the Th1 cytokine that activates macrophages: interferon gamma."],"why":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis survives inside macrophages. Macrophages present its antigens and release interleukin 12, which pushes CD4 T cells toward the Th1 type. Th1 cells release interferon gamma, which activates macrophages to kill ingested bacilli and to transform into epithelioid cells and giant cells that form granulomas. With few CD4 cells, people with HIV form poor granulomas and are prone to spread of tuberculosis outside the lungs, including to the pericardium.","whyNot":["Transforming growth factor beta comes from regulatory T cells and other cells. It dampens immune responses and promotes fibrosis.","Correct.","Interleukin 4 comes from Th2 cells. It drives IgE class switching and allergic responses and does not activate macrophages to kill mycobacteria.","Interleukin 12 is made by macrophages and dendritic cells. It steers T cells toward Th1, but it comes from the macrophage side of the loop."],"study":{"topics":["Th1 response and granuloma formation","Tuberculosis in HIV infection"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-17"]}},{"id":"CV-C32-Q4","stem":"The organism stains poorly with Gram stain but retains carbol fuchsin after decolorization with acid-alcohol. Which of the following components of its cell envelope is responsible for this staining property?","options":["Polysaccharide capsule","Lipoteichoic acid","Lipopolysaccharide","Mycolic acid"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.7","dok":2,"approach":["Name the stain: holding carbol fuchsin after an acid-alcohol wash means the organism is acid-fast (Ziehl-Neelsen stain).","Recall what makes mycobacteria acid-fast: a waxy, lipid-rich cell wall.","Pick the long-chain fatty acid that forms that waxy layer."],"why":"Mycobacteria have a cell wall rich in mycolic acids, very long-chain fatty acids linked to arabinogalactan and peptidoglycan. This waxy layer keeps out the crystal violet and iodine of the Gram stain, so the organism stains poorly. Once carbol fuchsin is driven in with heat, the same layer holds the dye against acid-alcohol, which is what acid-fast means. Mycolic acids also help the organism resist drying and survive inside macrophages.","whyNot":["A polysaccharide capsule, as in Streptococcus pneumoniae, blocks phagocytosis. Capsules are shown with special stains and do not cause acid-fastness.","Lipoteichoic acid is found in Gram-positive cell walls. It anchors the wall to the membrane and triggers inflammation, and it does not make cells acid-fast.","Lipopolysaccharide is the endotoxin of the outer membrane of Gram-negative bacteria. Mycobacteria lack it.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Acid-fast stain","Mycobacterial cell wall"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-16"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C33","system":"CV","dx":"Acute viral pericarditis (coxsackievirus B)","codes":["CV-18-F3"],"case":"A 24-year-old man comes to the physician because of sharp central chest pain for 2 days. The pain worsens when he lies supine or inspires deeply, improves when he sits up and leans forward, and radiates to the ridge of the left trapezius muscle. One week ago he had a sore throat, fever, and diarrhea, and his roommate had a similar illness. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F). Auscultation with the patient leaning forward shows a scratchy, three-component sound at the left sternal border. An ECG shows diffuse ST-segment elevation with PR depression. Serum troponin is within the reference range. Echocardiography shows a small pericardial effusion with normal wall motion. Stool PCR detects coxsackievirus B. Diagnosis: acute viral pericarditis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C33-Q1","stem":"Pain from his inflamed parietal pericardium is referred to the skin over the trapezius ridge. Which of the following nerves carries this pain?","options":["Phrenic nerve, from spinal segments C3 to C5","Upper thoracic sympathetic afferents, from T1 to T4","Vagus nerve, through its cardiac branches","Intercostobrachial nerve, from spinal segment T2"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","obj":"CV-3.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the pain source: the parietal pericardium, which has sensory fibers, unlike the visceral layer.","Recall its nerve supply: the phrenic nerve, which runs between the fibrous pericardium and the mediastinal pleura.","Link segments to skin: C3 to C5 also supply the skin over the trapezius ridge and shoulder, so the brain maps the pain there."],"why":"The fibrous pericardium and the parietal layer of serous pericardium receive sensory fibers from the phrenic nerves, which arise from C3 to C5. The skin over the trapezius ridge is supplied by the same cervical segments through the supraclavicular nerves. Pain from the parietal pericardium is therefore referred to that area. Trapezius ridge pain is a useful clue that separates pericarditis from myocardial ischemia.","whyNot":["Correct.","Sympathetic afferents from T1 to T4 carry pain from the heart muscle itself. That explains ischemic pain referred to the chest wall and medial left arm.","The vagus nerve carries parasympathetic fibers to the heart and visceral sensations such as stretch, which take part in reflexes. It does not carry the pain of pericarditis.","The intercostobrachial nerve supplies skin of the axilla and medial upper arm. It carries no fibers from the pericardium."],"study":{"topics":["Innervation of the pericardium","Referred pain"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-F3"]}},{"id":"CV-C33-Q2","stem":"His ECG shows ST-segment elevation in most leads, reflecting an injury current in the outer myocardium. Which of the following features of the heart wall best explains how pericardial inflammation affects the myocardium?","options":["The epicardium is the visceral serous layer and lies on the myocardium","The fibrous pericardium is fused to the myocardium with no space between them","The parietal serous layer is attached directly to the outer myocardium","The endocardium continues around the valve rings onto the outer heart surface"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","obj":"CV-2.1","dok":3,"approach":["List the layers from outside in: fibrous pericardium, parietal serous layer, pericardial cavity, visceral serous layer (epicardium), myocardium, endocardium.","Notice which inflamed layer touches heart muscle: the visceral serous layer is the epicardium itself.","Conclude that inflammation of the epicardium spreads to the subepicardial myocardium, producing diffuse ST elevation."],"why":"The serous pericardium has two layers. The parietal layer lines the inside of the fibrous pericardium, and the visceral layer covers the heart as the epicardium. The epicardium is a layer of mesothelium over loose connective tissue and fat that carries the coronary vessels, and it sits directly on the myocardium. Inflammation of the epicardium irritates the outer myocardium beneath it. Because the whole surface is involved, ST elevation appears in most leads, not in one coronary territory.","whyNot":["Correct.","The fibrous pericardium is the outer sac. It is separated from the heart by the two serous layers and the pericardial cavity.","The parietal serous layer lines the fibrous pericardium. It faces the heart across the pericardial cavity and does not touch the myocardium.","The endocardium lines the inside of the chambers and covers the valves. It does not wrap onto the outer surface of the heart."],"study":{"topics":["Layers of the heart wall","ECG changes in pericarditis"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-7"]}},{"id":"CV-C33-Q3","stem":"Purified RNA from this virus, without any viral proteins, can initiate infection when introduced into a cell. Which of the following features of the virus best explains this finding?","options":["Negative-sense single-stranded RNA genome packaged with its own polymerase","Double-stranded RNA genome split into segments within a nonenveloped capsid","Positive-sense single-stranded RNA genome that serves directly as mRNA","RNA genome that is copied into DNA by a reverse transcriptase it carries"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the virus: coxsackievirus B is an enterovirus in the picornavirus family.","Recall its genome: nonenveloped, positive-sense, single-stranded RNA.","Reason it out: host ribosomes can translate a positive-sense genome right away, making the viral polymerase, so naked RNA is infectious."],"why":"Picornaviruses such as coxsackievirus have a positive-sense single-stranded RNA genome. It reads like mRNA, so host ribosomes translate it into one large polyprotein that is cut into viral proteins, including the RNA-dependent RNA polymerase. Because the virus needs nothing from the virion to start, the purified RNA alone is infectious. Coxsackievirus B spreads by the fecal-oral route and is a leading cause of viral pericarditis and myocarditis.","whyNot":["Negative-sense RNA must first be copied into positive-sense mRNA by the polymerase carried in the virion, so the naked RNA alone cannot start infection. Influenza and measles viruses work this way.","Double-stranded RNA viruses such as rotavirus must bring their own polymerase to make mRNA, so their naked genome is not infectious.","Correct.","Retroviruses such as HIV need the reverse transcriptase packaged in the virion to make DNA, so their purified RNA alone does not start infection."],"study":{"topics":["Picornavirus genome and replication","Positive-sense and negative-sense RNA viruses"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-F3"]}},{"id":"CV-C33-Q4","stem":"The scratchy, three-component sound at his left sternal border is a pericardial friction rub. Which of the following types of exudate on the pericardial surfaces is most likely responsible for this sound?","options":["Purulent exudate packed with neutrophils and bacteria","Serous fluid with few cells that pools between the layers","Fibrinous exudate that roughens the serous surfaces","Caseous material from necrotizing granulomas"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-F3","obj":"CV-18-F.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the usual pattern of viral pericarditis: fibrinous or serofibrinous inflammation.","Link the pattern to the sound: deposits of fibrin make the smooth serous surfaces rough.","Rough layers rubbing together with each heartbeat produce the scratchy rub."],"why":"Acute viral pericarditis usually produces a fibrinous or serofibrinous exudate. Fibrin deposits make the normally smooth, moist serous layers dry and shaggy, sometimes called bread and butter pericarditis. As the heart moves, these rough surfaces rub, producing a scratchy sound that can have three parts linked to atrial systole, ventricular systole, and early ventricular filling. The same fibrinous pattern is seen in pericarditis after a myocardial infarction and in uremia.","whyNot":["Purulent pericarditis follows bacterial spread, for example from pneumonia or a chest wound. It fits a very ill patient with high fever, and his course and virus test point to a viral cause.","Thin serous fluid separates the layers and keeps them smooth. A large serous effusion tends to muffle heart sounds and can make a rub fade.","Correct.","Caseous pericarditis comes from tuberculosis or fungal infection and develops over weeks. His acute illness after a viral prodrome does not fit."],"study":{"topics":["Morphologic types of pericarditis","Pericardial friction rub"],"comps":["CV-18-F3","CV-16"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C34","system":"CV","dx":"Bicuspid aortic valve with coarctation of the aorta in Turner syndrome","codes":["CV-18-G1"],"case":"A 17-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of short stature and absent breast development. Her height is 142 cm. Physical examination shows a broad chest with widely spaced nipples and a low posterior hairline. Blood pressure is 152/88 mm Hg in the right arm and 104/70 mm Hg in the right leg (normally the leg pressure equals or exceeds the arm pressure). Femoral pulses are weak and delayed. Auscultation shows an ejection click and a soft midsystolic murmur at the right upper sternal border and a separate systolic murmur over the left upper back. Karyotype is 45,X. Echocardiography shows a bicuspid aortic valve with mild stenosis, a mildly dilated ascending aorta, and coarctation of the aorta just distal to the left subclavian artery. Diagnosis: bicuspid aortic valve with coarctation of the aorta in Turner syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C34-Q1","stem":"In a 46,XX female, most genes on one X chromosome are inactivated in each cell. Which of the following best explains why loss of the second X chromosome causes this patient's short stature?","options":["Her single X is always paternal, and imprinting silences its growth genes","Some X genes, such as SHOX, escape inactivation and need two working copies","Her cells inactivate the single remaining X, so no X genes are expressed","Her remaining X carries an expanded trinucleotide repeat that silences growth genes"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.5","dok":3,"approach":["Note the puzzle: if one X is normally silenced, a female with one X should match a 46,XX female.","Resolve it: some X genes escape inactivation, including those in the pseudoautosomal region shared with Y, so normal cells use two active copies.","Apply it: with one X, these genes are expressed at half dose. Half a dose of SHOX causes short stature."],"why":"X inactivation silences most of one X chromosome in each female cell, but a set of genes escapes it. These include genes in the pseudoautosomal regions, which are shared with the Y chromosome so that males also carry two copies. SHOX lies in pseudoautosomal region 1 and is needed in two copies for normal long bone growth. A 45,X female has only one copy, and this haploinsufficiency explains much of the short stature in Turner syndrome. Loss of other escaping genes is thought to contribute to the other features.","whyNot":["In most cases of Turner syndrome the single X comes from the mother, because the paternal sex chromosome is the one lost. Imprinting does not explain the short stature.","Correct.","A cell with one X keeps it active. X inactivation leaves one active X in every cell, and a cell with no active X could not survive.","Trinucleotide repeat expansion causes disorders such as fragile X syndrome and Huntington disease. Turner syndrome is a loss of a whole chromosome."],"study":{"topics":["X inactivation and genes that escape it","Turner syndrome genetics"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-G1"]}},{"id":"CV-C34-Q2","stem":"A chest x-ray shows notching of the inferior borders of several posterior ribs, caused by enlarged collateral arteries that bypass the narrowed segment. Which of the following collateral pathways is responsible for the rib notching?","options":["Vertebral arteries joining to form the basilar artery","Internal thoracic arteries to the intercostal arteries","Celiac trunk to the superior mesenteric artery through pancreatic arcades","Superior epigastric arteries to the inferior epigastric arteries"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.1","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the block: the coarctation lies just distal to the left subclavian artery, so the subclavian arteries are proximal to it and the descending aorta is distal.","Find a bridge: the internal thoracic artery (a subclavian branch) feeds anterior intercostal arteries, which connect to posterior intercostal arteries from the descending aorta.","Link to the x-ray: the enlarged, tortuous posterior intercostal arteries run in the costal grooves and erode the lower rib borders."],"why":"With coarctation distal to the left subclavian artery, blood reaches the lower body by detouring around the narrowing. The internal thoracic arteries arise from the subclavian arteries and give off anterior intercostal arteries. These connect with the posterior intercostal arteries, which carry blood backward into the descending aorta beyond the coarctation. The posterior intercostal arteries enlarge and press on the costal grooves on the lower edge of the ribs, producing notching. The first two ribs are usually spared because their intercostal arteries come from the costocervical trunk.","whyNot":["The vertebral arteries arise from the subclavian arteries proximal to the coarctation and supply the brain. Their junction as the basilar artery is normal anatomy and does not bypass the aorta.","Correct.","Pancreaticoduodenal arcades connect the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery. They enlarge with narrowing of one of those vessels and lie in the abdomen.","The internal thoracic artery also continues as the superior epigastric artery, which joins the inferior epigastric artery in the rectus sheath. This collateral carries blood to the lower body but runs in the abdominal wall, so it does not notch ribs."],"study":{"topics":["Collateral circulation in coarctation of the aorta","Intercostal arteries"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-G1"]}},{"id":"CV-C34-Q3","stem":"If her aortic stenosis worsens over the years, her left ventricle will face a higher systolic pressure, and its wall will thicken while the chamber size stays normal. Which of the following relationships best explains why this thickening benefits the ventricle?","options":["Fick principle: flow equals oxygen uptake divided by arteriovenous O2 difference","Poiseuille law: resistance to flow rises steeply as the radius of a tube falls","Frank-Starling law: more stretch of the fibers raises the force of contraction","Law of Laplace: wall stress rises with pressure and radius, falls with thickness"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","obj":"CV-6.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the load: aortic stenosis causes pressure overload of the left ventricle.","Recall the law that links pressure, chamber size, and wall thickness: Laplace, wall stress equals pressure times radius divided by twice the wall thickness.","Apply it: a thicker wall brings stress back toward normal, which is why pressure overload produces concentric hypertrophy."],"why":"By the law of Laplace, wall stress is about pressure times radius divided by twice the wall thickness. When systolic pressure rises, as with aortic stenosis or coarctation, stress on the wall rises. Adding sarcomeres in parallel thickens the wall and brings stress back toward normal without enlarging the chamber, a pattern called concentric hypertrophy. The cost is a stiffer ventricle with impaired filling and a higher oxygen demand.","whyNot":["The Fick principle is used to measure cardiac output or organ blood flow from oxygen uptake. It says nothing about wall stress.","Poiseuille law explains why a narrow valve or vessel greatly increases resistance to flow. It explains why the stenosis raises pressure, but it does not explain the response of the ventricular wall.","The Frank-Starling law links end-diastolic volume to stroke volume. It explains beat-to-beat matching of output to filling and does not explain why a thicker wall lowers stress.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Law of Laplace","Concentric and eccentric hypertrophy"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-D1"]}},{"id":"CV-C34-Q4","stem":"A dilated ascending aorta is common with a bicuspid aortic valve and increases the later risk of aneurysm and dissection. The main abnormality lies in the layer that normally gives the aorta its elastic recoil. Which of the following is most likely to be seen on histologic examination of this layer?","options":["Fragmented elastic lamellae and loss of smooth muscle in the media","Granulomas with giant cells surrounding a fragmented elastic lamina","Lipid-filled foam cells under a fibrous cap in the intima","Obliterative endarteritis of the vasa vasorum in the adventitia"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-G1","obj":"CV-18-G.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the structure of an elastic artery: the media of the aorta holds many layers of elastic lamellae with smooth muscle between them.","Identify the lesion of bicuspid valve aortopathy: noninflammatory medial degeneration.","Pick the option that describes loss of elastic tissue and smooth muscle in the media with no inflammation."],"why":"The aorta is an elastic artery. Its thick media contains dozens of concentric elastic lamellae with smooth muscle cells and matrix between them, which let it stretch in systole and recoil in diastole. In aortas linked to a bicuspid valve, as in Marfan syndrome, the media shows medial degeneration: elastic lamellae fragment, smooth muscle cells are lost, and pools of proteoglycan-rich matrix collect. The weakened wall dilates and can tear, causing dissection.","whyNot":["Correct.","Granulomatous inflammation with giant cells near the internal elastic lamina describes giant cell arteritis or Takayasu arteritis. Her aortic dilation has no inflammation.","Foam cells and a fibrous cap in the intima describe an atherosclerotic plaque. Atherosclerosis mainly affects the abdominal aorta and is the main cause of abdominal aortic aneurysm.","Obliterative endarteritis of the vasa vasorum is the lesion of tertiary syphilis. It starves the media of blood and gives a tree-bark appearance to the aortic root."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of elastic arteries","Medial degeneration of the aorta"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-18-I2"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C35","system":"CV","dx":"Patent ductus arteriosus with congenital rubella syndrome","codes":["CV-18-G2"],"case":"A 3-month-old girl is brought to the physician because of poor feeding, sweating during feeds, and poor weight gain. Her mother was never vaccinated and had a fever and a fine rash during the first trimester. The infant has bilateral cataracts and failed her newborn hearing screen. Pulse is 168/min (normal about 100 to 160), and blood pressure is 82/34 mm Hg, a wide pulse pressure for her age. Peripheral pulses are bounding. A continuous, machine-like murmur is heard best below the left clavicle. Echocardiography shows a large patent ductus arteriosus with left-to-right flow and an enlarged left atrium and left ventricle. Diagnosis: patent ductus arteriosus with congenital rubella syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C35-Q1","stem":"Serologic testing for rubella is performed. Which of the following results best indicates that the infant was infected in utero rather than carrying antibody passed from her mother?","options":["Rubella IgG in her serum at the same level as her mother's","Rubella IgA in her mother's breast milk","Rubella-specific IgM in her own serum","Rubella IgG in her serum that falls steadily over her first 6 months"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-G2","obj":"CV-18-G.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall which antibody class crosses the placenta: only IgG, through neonatal Fc receptors.","Reason that any IgM in the infant must have been made by the infant herself.","Conclude that rubella-specific IgM in her serum shows her own immune response to infection."],"why":"IgG is the only antibody class carried across the placenta, so every newborn whose mother is immune has maternal rubella IgG. IgM is a large pentamer that does not cross. Rubella-specific IgM in an infant's serum therefore means the infant's own B cells responded to the virus, which confirms congenital infection. Rubella IgG that persists beyond the age when maternal IgG should have disappeared, roughly 6 to 12 months, also supports the diagnosis.","whyNot":["IgG equal to the mother's level is what passive placental transfer produces. It cannot separate infection from maternal antibody.","IgA in breast milk is made by the mother and protects the infant's gut. It says nothing about whether the infant was infected.","Correct.","A steady fall in IgG over the first months is the expected decay of maternal antibody (half-life about 3 to 4 weeks). It points away from the infant making her own antibody."],"study":{"topics":["Placental transfer of IgG","Diagnosing congenital infection"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-17"]}},{"id":"CV-C35-Q2","stem":"During surgical ligation of the ductus, the left recurrent laryngeal nerve, which curves under the aortic arch beside the ductus, is at risk. The ductus and the low course of this nerve on the left are both explained by the same pharyngeal arch artery. Which of the following arch arteries is it?","options":["Left second arch artery","Left sixth arch artery","Left fourth arch artery","Left third arch artery"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","obj":"CV-1.7","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the derivatives: the sixth arch arteries form the proximal pulmonary arteries, and the distal part of the left sixth forms the ductus arteriosus.","Recall that the recurrent laryngeal nerves are sixth arch nerves that hook under the sixth arch arteries as the heart descends.","On the left the distal sixth artery persists as the ductus, so the nerve stays hooked under it beside the aortic arch."],"why":"The recurrent laryngeal nerves supply the muscles of the sixth pharyngeal arch and loop beneath the sixth arch arteries. On the right, the distal sixth arch artery and the fifth disappear, so the right nerve moves up and hooks around the right subclavian artery, a fourth arch derivative. On the left, the distal sixth arch artery persists as the ductus arteriosus, which becomes the ligamentum arteriosum. The left nerve therefore stays hooked under the aortic arch next to the ductus, where it can be injured in ductus surgery.","whyNot":["The second arch artery mostly regresses and leaves small remnants such as the stapedial artery. It does not form the ductus.","Correct.","The left fourth arch artery forms part of the aortic arch. The left nerve passes under the arch, but its low course is set by the sixth arch artery, the ductus.","The third arch arteries form the common carotid arteries and the start of the internal carotid arteries. They are unrelated to the ductus."],"study":{"topics":["Pharyngeal arch artery derivatives","Course of the recurrent laryngeal nerves"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-3"]}},{"id":"CV-C35-Q3","stem":"Before birth, patency of the ductus is maintained by prostaglandin E2, produced by both the placenta and the ductus wall. Which of the following precursors and enzymes produce prostaglandin E2?","options":["Arachidonic acid, by 5-lipoxygenase","Sphingosine, by sphingosine kinase","Cholesterol, by side-chain cleavage enzyme","Arachidonic acid, by cyclooxygenase"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-1","obj":"CV-1.6","dok":2,"approach":["Classify the molecule: prostaglandins are eicosanoids, 20-carbon signals made from a membrane fatty acid.","Name the precursor: arachidonic acid, released from membrane phospholipids by phospholipase A2.","Split the pathways: cyclooxygenase makes prostaglandins and thromboxanes, while 5-lipoxygenase makes leukotrienes."],"why":"Phospholipase A2 releases arachidonic acid, a 20-carbon omega-6 fatty acid, from membrane phospholipids. Cyclooxygenase (COX-1 and COX-2) turns it into prostaglandin H2, which other enzymes convert to prostaglandin E2, prostacyclin, and thromboxane A2. PGE2 relaxes the smooth muscle of the ductus. After birth the placental source is gone and the lungs break down circulating PGE2, so the level falls and the ductus constricts. This is why COX inhibitors can close a patent ductus and why PGE1 is given to keep it open in duct-dependent heart defects.","whyNot":["5-lipoxygenase turns arachidonic acid into leukotrienes, which cause bronchoconstriction and attract neutrophils. It does not make prostaglandins.","Sphingosine kinase makes sphingosine 1-phosphate, a lipid signal that guides lymphocyte movement. It is unrelated to prostaglandin synthesis.","Side-chain cleavage of cholesterol is the first step in steroid hormone synthesis. Prostaglandins are made from a fatty acid.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Eicosanoid synthesis","Prostaglandins and the ductus arteriosus"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-G2"]}},{"id":"CV-C35-Q4","stem":"Suppose her ductus is never closed. Years later, pulmonary vascular resistance rises until flow through the ductus reverses (Eisenmenger syndrome). Her toes become cyanotic and clubbed while her fingers remain pink. Which of the following anatomic facts best explains this pattern?","options":["The ductus joins the aorta at its root, before the coronary arteries arise","The ductus joins the aorta just distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery","The ductus joins the arch between the brachiocephalic trunk and the left common carotid","The ductus joins the right subclavian artery close to where that vessel begins"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-18-G2","obj":"CV-18-G.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the shunt: with reversed flow, deoxygenated blood moves from the pulmonary artery into the aorta through the ductus.","Locate where it enters: the ductus joins the aorta just beyond the origin of the left subclavian artery.","Trace the distribution: branches that leave the aorta before that point (to the head and arms) carry oxygenated blood, while the descending aorta carries mixed blood to the lower body."],"why":"The ductus arteriosus connects the pulmonary trunk near its bifurcation to the aorta just distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery. When pulmonary pressure exceeds aortic pressure, deoxygenated blood enters the aorta at this point. The brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, and left subclavian arteries have already branched off and still carry well-oxygenated blood to the head and arms. The descending aorta carries the mixed blood to the legs, so cyanosis and clubbing appear in the toes but not the fingers (differential cyanosis).","whyNot":["Entry at the aortic root would send deoxygenated blood to every branch, including the coronary arteries, causing cyanosis of both hands and feet.","Correct.","Entry between the brachiocephalic trunk and the left common carotid would also darken the left head and arm. The ductus enters further along, past all three arch branches.","The ductus does not connect to the right subclavian artery. If it did, the right arm would turn blue."],"study":{"topics":["Branches of the aortic arch","Eisenmenger syndrome and differential cyanosis"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-G2"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C36","system":"CV","dx":"Ostium secundum atrial septal defect in Holt-Oram syndrome","codes":["CV-18-G3"],"case":"A 29-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 1-year history of shortness of breath when climbing stairs and recent episodes of palpitations. Her father and brother have similar thumb abnormalities, and her father had a congenital heart defect repaired as a child. Physical examination shows bilateral hypoplastic thumbs. The second heart sound is widely split and does not vary with respiration. A soft midsystolic murmur is heard at the left upper sternal border. An ECG shows incomplete right bundle branch block. Echocardiography shows a 14-mm defect in the center of the atrial septum at the fossa ovalis with left-to-right flow, an enlarged right atrium and right ventricle, and a pulmonary-to-systemic flow ratio (Qp:Qs) of 2.1 (normal 1.0). Genetic testing shows a TBX5 mutation. Diagnosis: ostium secundum atrial septal defect in Holt-Oram syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C36-Q1","stem":"She asks about the risk to her future children. Based on her family history, which of the following is the inheritance pattern of her condition and the risk to each child?","options":["Autosomal dominant, 50% for each child","X-linked dominant, 50% for each child","Mitochondrial, nearly 100% for each child","Autosomal recessive, 25% for each child"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.5","dok":3,"approach":["Read the pedigree: affected people appear in two generations, and her father passed it to a son (her brother).","Rule out patterns: father-to-son transmission excludes X-linked inheritance, and a father cannot pass on mitochondrial DNA.","Conclude autosomal dominant: an affected parent passes the mutant allele to each child with a 50% chance."],"why":"Holt-Oram syndrome is autosomal dominant and is caused by mutations in TBX5, a transcription factor needed for development of the upper limb and the heart. Her family shows affected people in each generation and transmission from father to son, which fits autosomal dominant inheritance. Each child of an affected parent has a 50% chance of inheriting the mutant allele. Severity can vary within a family, so an affected child may have different heart or limb findings from hers.","whyNot":["Correct.","A father passes his X chromosome only to daughters, so father-to-son transmission rules out any X-linked pattern.","Mitochondria are passed down through the egg, so an affected father cannot pass on a mitochondrial disorder.","Autosomal recessive traits usually appear in siblings of unaffected carrier parents, not in parent and child across generations. Her child would be at risk only if her partner were also a carrier."],"study":{"topics":["Pedigree analysis","Holt-Oram syndrome"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-G3"]}},{"id":"CV-C36-Q2","stem":"A secundum defect forms when the flap that should cover the foramen ovale is too short, often from excessive resorption. Which of the following embryonic structures normally forms this flap, the floor of the fossa ovalis?","options":["Septum secundum","Sinus venosus","Endocardial cushions","Septum primum"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","obj":"CV-1.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the two atrial septa: the thin septum primum grows first, and the thick septum secundum grows to its right.","Recall the opening: the foramen ovale is a gap in the septum secundum that the septum primum covers from the left like a flap valve.","Link to the adult heart: the septum primum becomes the floor of the fossa ovalis, and the septum secundum forms its rim (limbus)."],"why":"The septum primum grows down toward the endocardial cushions. Before it fuses, holes appear in its upper part and merge into the ostium secundum. The thicker septum secundum then grows on its right side but leaves an opening, the foramen ovale. The septum primum acts as a flap valve over this opening, letting blood pass from right to left in the fetus. After birth, rising left atrial pressure presses the flap against the septum secundum, and it becomes the floor of the fossa ovalis. If the septum primum is resorbed too much, the flap is too short and a secundum defect remains.","whyNot":["The septum secundum forms the thick upper rim (limbus) of the fossa ovalis. A small septum secundum can also cause a secundum defect, but it is not the flap.","The sinus venosus forms the smooth part of the right atrium. A defect near the opening of the superior vena cava is a sinus venosus defect, which lies outside the fossa ovalis.","The endocardial cushions close the ostium primum and form part of the atrioventricular valves. Failure here causes an ostium primum defect or an atrioventricular septal defect, often in Down syndrome.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Atrial septation","Types of atrial septal defect"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-G3"]}},{"id":"CV-C36-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her second heart sound is widely split and does not vary with respiration?","options":["Shunt flow keeps right ventricular volume about equal in both breathing phases","Delayed left ventricular activation makes the aortic valve close after the pulmonic valve","High pulmonary artery pressure makes the pulmonic valve close early with every beat","Right bundle branch block delays right ventricular contraction only on inspiration"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-4","obj":"CV-4.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall normal splitting: inspiration increases venous return to the right heart, delaying pulmonic valve closure, so the split widens.","Add the defect: the large left-to-right shunt keeps the right ventricle loaded, so pulmonic closure is late in every beat.","Explain the fixed part: when inspiration brings more systemic venous blood to the right atrium, less blood crosses from the left atrium, so right ventricular volume barely changes."],"why":"In an atrial septal defect the two atria act as one shared chamber. Shunted blood adds to right ventricular volume, prolonging right ventricular ejection and delaying pulmonic valve closure, so S2 is widely split. During inspiration, more systemic venous blood returns to the right atrium, but this raises right atrial pressure slightly and reduces the left-to-right flow across the defect. During expiration the shunt increases again. Right ventricular filling stays about the same through the breathing cycle, so the split is fixed.","whyNot":["Correct.","Delayed left ventricular activation, as in left bundle branch block, delays aortic closure and causes paradoxical splitting, which narrows on inspiration.","High pulmonary artery pressure makes P2 loud. It does not explain a split that stays wide and fixed, and her shunt flow is still left to right.","Right bundle branch block widens the split, but the split still increases on inspiration because the breathing effect on venous return remains. Block affects every beat, not only inspiration."],"study":{"topics":["Splitting of S2","Physiology of atrial septal defect"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-18-G3"]}},{"id":"CV-C36-Q4","stem":"Before her defect is closed, she develops a deep vein thrombosis of the calf. Immediately after straining to lift a heavy box, she has sudden weakness of the right face and arm from an embolic stroke. Which of the following events best explains how a venous thrombus reached her brain?","options":["Left atrial pressure briefly rose above right atrial pressure","Pulmonary capillaries widened enough for the clot to pass through","Right atrial pressure briefly rose above left atrial pressure","The clot crossed from the pulmonary artery to the aorta by a ductus"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-G3","obj":"CV-18-G.3","dok":3,"approach":["Name the event: a venous clot causing a stroke is a paradoxical embolism, which needs a path from the right heart to the left heart.","Recall the usual direction: left atrial pressure is normally higher, so her shunt runs left to right.","Apply the trigger: straining, then releasing it, sends a surge of venous blood to the right atrium, so right atrial pressure briefly exceeds left atrial pressure and the clot crosses the defect."],"why":"Normally left atrial pressure is a few mm Hg higher than right atrial pressure, so flow across an atrial septal defect is left to right. Straining against a closed glottis (a Valsalva maneuver) holds back venous return, and when the strain is released, pooled venous blood rushes into the right atrium. For a few beats right atrial pressure exceeds left atrial pressure, and blood, along with any clot it carries, crosses into the left atrium and the systemic arteries. Coughing can have the same effect. This is a paradoxical embolism.","whyNot":["Left atrial pressure higher than right atrial pressure is the usual state and drives left-to-right flow. It would carry blood away from the systemic side, not bring a venous clot to it.","Pulmonary capillaries are about 5 to 10 µm wide and trap venous clots, which is how pulmonary emboli form. They do not widen enough to let a clot through.","Correct.","Her echocardiogram shows an atrial septal defect only. A ductus is a different shunt, and she has no evidence of one."],"study":{"topics":["Paradoxical embolism","Atrial pressures and shunt direction"],"comps":["CV-18-G3","CV-18-H1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C37","system":"CV","dx":"Tetralogy of Fallot","codes":["CV-18-G4"],"case":"A 4-month-old boy is brought to a pediatric clinic because his lips turn blue when he feeds or cries. During one episode he became limp and deeply cyanotic, then improved when his mother held his knees to his chest. Physical examination shows a harsh systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border and a single second heart sound. Oxygen saturation is 82% (95 to 100%) on room air. Chest x-ray shows a boot-shaped heart with decreased pulmonary vascular markings. Echocardiography shows a large ventricular septal defect, an aorta that overrides the septum, narrowing of the right ventricular outflow tract, and right ventricular hypertrophy. The diagnosis is tetralogy of Fallot.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C37-Q1","stem":"The four structural findings on this infant's echocardiogram trace back to one error during septation of the outflow tract. Which of the following developmental errors best explains all four findings?","options":["Failure of the endocardial cushions to fuse","Failure of the conotruncal ridges to spiral","Excess resorption of the septum primum","Anterosuperior shift of the outflow septum"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","obj":"CV-1.4","dok":2,"approach":["List the four findings: VSD, overriding aorta, right ventricular outflow narrowing, right ventricular hypertrophy.","Ask which single shift could narrow the right outflow and leave the aorta sitting over a septal gap.","Match that to displacement of the infundibular (outflow) septum toward the right ventricular side."],"why":"In tetralogy of Fallot the infundibular (outflow) septum, built largely from neural crest derived conotruncal tissue, is displaced anteriorly and superiorly. This narrows the right ventricular outflow tract and leaves a malaligned ventricular septal defect with the aorta riding over it. Right ventricular hypertrophy follows because the ventricle pumps against the outflow obstruction. One displacement therefore explains all four findings.","whyNot":["Endocardial cushion fusion failure produces atrioventricular septal defects (ostium primum ASD, inlet VSD, a common AV valve), classically in trisomy 21. It does not narrow the outflow tract.","Failure of the conotruncal ridges to spiral produces transposition of the great arteries, where the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk from the left.","Excess resorption of the septum primum leaves a secundum atrial septal defect, which causes a left-to-right shunt at the atrial level and no outflow obstruction.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Outflow tract septation","Conotruncal defects"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-G4"]}},{"id":"CV-C37-Q2","stem":"A different newborn has tetralogy of Fallot with complete pulmonary atresia. On day 2 of life his cyanosis suddenly worsens because a fetal channel is closing. That channel develops from which embryonic aortic arch?","options":["Left fourth aortic arch","Right fourth aortic arch","Left sixth aortic arch","Third aortic arch"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","obj":"CV-1.7","dok":3,"approach":["With pulmonary atresia, no blood leaves the right ventricle for the lungs, so lung flow must come from the aorta.","The fetal channel that carries aortic blood to the pulmonary artery is the ductus arteriosus, which closes in the first days of life.","Recall that the ductus arteriosus is the distal part of the left sixth aortic arch."],"why":"When the pulmonary valve is atretic, all pulmonary blood flow in the newborn depends on the ductus arteriosus carrying blood from the aorta into the pulmonary artery. The ductus begins to constrict within hours of birth and usually closes functionally over the first few days as arterial oxygen rises and prostaglandin E2 falls, so lung flow drops and cyanosis worsens. The ductus arteriosus forms from the distal part of the left sixth aortic arch, while the proximal sixth arches form the proximal pulmonary arteries.","whyNot":["The left fourth arch forms part of the adult arch of the aorta. It does not form a channel that closes after birth.","The right fourth arch forms the proximal right subclavian artery, a permanent vessel that does not close after birth.","Correct.","The third arches form the common carotid arteries and the first part of the internal carotid arteries. They have no role in neonatal pulmonary flow."],"study":{"topics":["Aortic arch derivatives","Ductus-dependent pulmonary flow"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-G2"]}},{"id":"CV-C37-Q3","stem":"Further testing of the 4-month-old shows a serum calcium of 6.9 mg/dL (8.8 to 10.8 mg/dL), a low parathyroid hormone level, and no thymic shadow on chest x-ray. Which genetic finding is most likely?","options":["Trisomy 21 on karyotype","Microdeletion at 22q11.2","Microdeletion at 7q11.23","Monosomy X on karyotype"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.5","dok":2,"approach":["Group the findings: a conotruncal heart defect, low calcium from low parathyroid hormone, and an absent thymus.","Link parathyroid and thymus loss to failed development of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches.","Name the deletion syndrome that combines pouch defects with conotruncal defects."],"why":"A 22q11.2 microdeletion (DiGeorge syndrome) disrupts neural crest migration and development of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches. The pouches form the parathyroid glands and thymus, so infants have hypocalcemia from hypoparathyroidism and T-cell deficiency from thymic hypoplasia. Neural crest cells also build the conotruncal septum, so tetralogy of Fallot and truncus arteriosus are common. The deletion is found by FISH or chromosomal microarray, since it is too small to see on a routine karyotype.","whyNot":["Trisomy 21 is most strongly linked to atrioventricular septal defects. It does not cause hypoparathyroidism or an absent thymus.","Correct.","A 7q11.23 deletion causes Williams syndrome, with supravalvular aortic stenosis and high, not low, calcium.","Monosomy X (Turner syndrome) is linked to coarctation of the aorta and bicuspid aortic valve, with normal parathyroid and thymic development."],"study":{"topics":["22q11.2 deletion syndrome","Pharyngeal pouch derivatives"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-1"]}},{"id":"CV-C37-Q4","stem":"During the spell described in the case, his color improved when his knees were held to his chest. Which of the following best explains this improvement?","options":["It lowered outflow resistance in the narrowed right ventricle","It raised systemic resistance and cut the right-to-left shunt","It reduced venous return and lengthened ventricular filling","It lowered pulmonary resistance and drew blood to the lungs"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-G4","obj":"CV-18-G.5","dok":3,"approach":["Shunt direction across the VSD depends on the balance between right ventricular outflow resistance and systemic vascular resistance.","Flexing the legs compresses the femoral arteries and raises systemic vascular resistance.","Higher systemic resistance pushes less blood right to left and sends more right ventricular output through the pulmonary valve."],"why":"In tetralogy of Fallot, blood leaving the right ventricle chooses between the narrowed outflow tract and the VSD into the aorta. When systemic vascular resistance falls or outflow spasm worsens, more deoxygenated blood shunts right to left and a hypercyanotic spell follows. Pressing the knees to the chest (the infant version of squatting) kinks the femoral arteries and raises systemic vascular resistance, which reduces the right-to-left shunt and sends more blood to the lungs. It also increases venous return to the right heart.","whyNot":["Knee-chest positioning does not relax the infundibular obstruction. Drugs such as beta blockers are used to reduce infundibular spasm.","Correct.","Squatting and knee-chest positioning increase venous return from the legs. Reduced venous return would lower right ventricular output to the lungs and would not help.","Knee-chest positioning acts on the systemic side. Pulmonary vascular resistance is already low here, and the fixed outflow narrowing, not pulmonary vascular tone, limits lung flow."],"study":{"topics":["Tet spells and squatting","Shunt direction and vascular resistance"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-G4"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C38","system":"CV","dx":"Systemic embolism to the superior mesenteric artery from a left atrial thrombus","codes":["CV-18-H1"],"case":"A 76-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department with sudden, severe periumbilical pain that began 3 hours ago. She has atrial fibrillation and stopped taking her anticoagulant 2 months ago. Pulse is 118/min and irregularly irregular, and blood pressure is 142/86 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a soft, mildly tender abdomen, and the pain is out of proportion to these findings. Serum lactate is 5.8 mmol/L (0.5 to 2.2 mmol/L). CT angiography shows an embolus blocking the superior mesenteric artery a few centimeters beyond its origin from the aorta. Echocardiography shows remaining thrombus in the left atrial appendage. The diagnosis is acute mesenteric ischemia from a cardiac embolus.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C38-Q1","stem":"Given the site where the embolus has lodged, which of the following segments of bowel is most likely to keep its normal arterial supply?","options":["Sigmoid colon","Terminal ileum","Ascending colon","Cecum"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.1","dok":3,"approach":["An embolus in the proximal superior mesenteric artery (SMA) cuts off the branches that arise beyond it.","List the SMA territory: jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and about the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon.","Pick the segment supplied by a different trunk, the inferior mesenteric artery."],"why":"The SMA supplies midgut structures: the small bowel beyond the duodenum, the cecum and appendix, the ascending colon, and about the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon. The sigmoid colon is hindgut and receives blood from the sigmoid branches of the inferior mesenteric artery, so an SMA embolus leaves it perfused. Knowing the boundary between midgut and hindgut supply predicts which bowel will be ischemic.","whyNot":["Correct.","The terminal ileum is supplied by the ileocolic artery, a branch of the SMA, so it is at high risk here.","The ascending colon is supplied by the right colic and ileocolic branches of the SMA, so it is at risk. It would stay perfused if the inferior mesenteric artery were the vessel blocked.","The cecum is supplied by the ileocolic artery from the SMA and is at risk with this embolus."],"study":{"topics":["Mesenteric arterial territories","Midgut versus hindgut supply"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-H1"]}},{"id":"CV-C38-Q2","stem":"Which of the following factors is mainly responsible for formation of the thrombus that later broke off and became her embolus?","options":["Endothelial injury from a jet of regurgitant blood","Hyperviscosity from an increased red cell mass","Antibodies against phospholipid-binding proteins","Stasis of blood in the poorly emptying appendage"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H1","obj":"CV-18-H.1","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the source: a left atrial appendage thrombus in a patient with atrial fibrillation.","Apply Virchow triad and ask which arm atrial fibrillation disturbs.","Loss of organized atrial contraction leaves blood pooling in the appendage, which is stasis."],"why":"In atrial fibrillation the atria quiver and no longer contract in an organized way. Blood pools in the left atrial appendage, a blind pouch, and stasis lets clotting factors accumulate and keeps platelets in contact with the wall. A thrombus forms there and pieces can break off into the systemic circulation, where they lodge in the brain, gut, kidneys, spleen, or limbs. This is why anticoagulation is used in atrial fibrillation to prevent stroke and other systemic emboli.","whyNot":["Jet lesions from regurgitant valves injure endothelium, but nothing in the case points to valve disease. Endothelial injury is the main factor in arterial thrombosis on a plaque.","Hyperviscosity from polycythemia can promote thrombosis, but nothing in her case suggests a raised red cell mass. Her atrial fibrillation explains the thrombus.","Antiphospholipid syndrome causes arterial and venous thrombi, but nothing in the case suggests it, such as lupus, pregnancy losses, or a prolonged PTT. Atrial fibrillation fully explains her thrombus.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Virchow triad","Atrial fibrillation and systemic emboli"],"comps":["CV-18-H1","CV-18-H6"]}},{"id":"CV-C38-Q3","stem":"Her increased lactate comes from ischemic intestinal cells that keep making ATP through glycolysis without oxygen. Which of the following reactions allows glycolysis to continue in these cells?","options":["Oxidation of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA in mitochondria","Conversion of lactate back to glucose in hepatocytes","Reduction of pyruvate to lactate, regenerating NAD+","Transamination of pyruvate to alanine by alanine aminotransferase"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.4","dok":3,"approach":["Glycolysis needs NAD+ at the glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase step.","Without oxygen, the electron transport chain cannot reoxidize NADH.","Lactate dehydrogenase reduces pyruvate to lactate and returns NAD+ so glycolysis can keep running."],"why":"Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase uses NAD+ and produces NADH. Normally mitochondria reoxidize NADH through the electron transport chain, but that requires oxygen. In ischemic tissue, lactate dehydrogenase reduces pyruvate to lactate and converts NADH back to NAD+, so glycolysis continues and yields a net 2 ATP per glucose. The lactate spills into the blood, which is why a raised serum lactate is a clue to bowel ischemia.","whyNot":["Pyruvate dehydrogenase feeds pyruvate into the citric acid cycle. This path depends on oxygen being available to reoxidize NADH and is what fails in ischemia.","Gluconeogenesis from lactate (the Cori cycle) happens in the liver and uses ATP. It clears lactate and does not keep glycolysis running in the ischemic gut.","Correct.","Alanine aminotransferase transfers an amino group from glutamate to pyruvate to make alanine. It does not regenerate NAD+, so it cannot sustain anaerobic glycolysis."],"study":{"topics":["Anaerobic glycolysis","Lactate dehydrogenase and NAD+"],"comps":["CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C38-Q4","stem":"On day 2 she develops fever, hypotension, and leukocytosis after gram-negative bacteria cross the necrotic bowel wall into the blood. Which of the following host recognition events most directly initiates this inflammatory response?","options":["Toll-like receptor 2 binding lipoteichoic acid","Toll-like receptor 3 binding double-stranded RNA","Toll-like receptor 9 binding unmethylated CpG DNA","Toll-like receptor 4 binding lipid A"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-H5","obj":"CV-18-H.8","dok":2,"approach":["Gram-negative bacteria carry lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin) in their outer membrane.","Lipid A is the toxic part of lipopolysaccharide.","Lipid A is recognized by toll-like receptor 4 (with CD14 and MD-2) on macrophages, which triggers TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6 release."],"why":"Once the bowel wall dies, gut gram-negative bacteria and their lipopolysaccharide reach the circulation. Lipid A binds the TLR4 complex (with CD14 and MD-2) on macrophages and other innate cells. Signaling through NF-kappa B releases TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6, which cause fever, vasodilation, and leukocytosis and can progress to septic shock.","whyNot":["TLR2 recognizes lipoteichoic acid and lipoproteins, typical of gram-positive bacteria. It would be the main receptor in a gram-positive infection.","TLR3 recognizes double-stranded RNA inside endosomes and responds mainly to viral infection.","TLR9 recognizes unmethylated CpG DNA inside endosomes. It contributes to responses against many microbes, but lipid A is the main trigger of gram-negative septic shock.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Toll-like receptors","Endotoxin and septic shock"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-H5"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C39","system":"CV","dx":"Hemorrhage from a ruptured spleen with hypovolemia","codes":["CV-18-H2"],"case":"A 19-year-old college student is brought to the emergency department 40 minutes after he was tackled during an intramural rugby game. Two weeks ago he had sore throat, fever, fatigue, and swollen posterior cervical lymph nodes, and a heterophile antibody test was positive. He reports left upper quadrant pain and pain at the tip of his left shoulder. Pulse is 124/min, and blood pressure is 88/56 mm Hg. Physical examination shows cool, pale skin and left upper quadrant tenderness. Hemoglobin is 13.6 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5 g/dL). Bedside ultrasound shows a large amount of free fluid in the abdomen. The diagnosis is hemorrhage from a ruptured spleen with hypovolemia.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C39-Q1","stem":"His heart rate rose within minutes of the bleed. Which of the following changes in the carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptor reflex best explains this finding?","options":["Increased stretch receptor firing with decreased sympathetic outflow","Decreased stretch receptor firing with increased sympathetic outflow","Increased stretch receptor firing with increased vagal outflow","Decreased stretch receptor firing with increased vagal outflow"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","obj":"CV-6.5","dok":2,"approach":["Blood loss lowers arterial pressure, so the arterial wall stretches less.","Less stretch means fewer impulses from the carotid sinus (via cranial nerve IX) and aortic arch (via cranial nerve X) to the medulla.","The medulla responds by reducing vagal tone and raising sympathetic outflow, which speeds the heart and constricts vessels."],"why":"Baroreceptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch fire in proportion to arterial wall stretch. A fall in pressure after hemorrhage reduces their firing, which removes their normal inhibition of the vasomotor center. Sympathetic outflow rises and vagal outflow falls, so heart rate, contractility, arteriolar tone, and venous tone all increase. This is why tachycardia with cool, pale skin is an early sign of significant blood loss.","whyNot":["Increased firing with less sympathetic outflow is also the response to a rise in arterial pressure, the opposite of his situation.","Correct.","Increased stretch receptor firing happens when pressure rises. The extra vagal outflow would slow the heart, which is the response to hypertension.","Firing does fall after hemorrhage, but the reflex lowers vagal outflow. Raised vagal outflow would slow his heart."],"study":{"topics":["Baroreceptor reflex","Compensation for hemorrhage"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H5"]}},{"id":"CV-C39-Q2","stem":"His hemoglobin concentration is near normal even though he has lost a large volume of blood. Which of the following best explains this finding?","options":["Whole blood was lost, so its concentration has not changed yet","Aldosterone has expanded his plasma volume by retaining sodium","Red cells lost into the abdomen have already been reabsorbed","Erythropoietin has already increased red cell production"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H2","obj":"CV-18-H.5","dok":3,"approach":["Hemoglobin is a concentration (grams per deciliter), not a total amount.","In acute hemorrhage, cells and plasma are lost together in the same proportion.","Hemoglobin falls only after interstitial fluid shifts into vessels or IV fluid is given, which takes hours."],"why":"In acute bleeding, red cells and plasma leave together, so the volume falls but the concentration of hemoglobin stays close to baseline at first. Over the next hours, lower capillary hydrostatic pressure pulls interstitial fluid into the capillaries, and IV fluids add more volume, which dilutes the remaining red cells. The full drop in hemoglobin often appears over about 24 to 72 hours. A near normal early hemoglobin therefore does not rule out major hemorrhage, and pulse and blood pressure are better early guides.","whyNot":["Correct.","Aldosterone-driven sodium retention takes hours and adds plasma volume. Any added plasma would dilute red cells and lower hemoglobin, not keep it normal.","Blood in the peritoneal cavity is absorbed slowly over days to weeks, too late to affect a hemoglobin drawn 40 minutes after injury.","Erythropoietin rises within hours, but new red cells take several days to reach the blood, seen as a reticulocyte rise at about 3 to 5 days."],"study":{"topics":["Hemoglobin in acute blood loss","Transcapillary refill"],"comps":["CV-18-H2","CV-11"]}},{"id":"CV-C39-Q3","stem":"The virus that caused his illness two weeks ago enters B cells through CD21. A blood smear at that time showed many large atypical lymphocytes. Which of the following cell types are these atypical lymphocytes most likely to be?","options":["CD4+ helper T cells infected through the CD4 molecule","Infected B cells maturing into antibody-secreting plasma cells","Cytotoxic CD8+ T cells reacting to infected B cells","Monocytes that carry viral inclusion bodies"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.6","dok":2,"approach":["Heterophile-positive infectious mononucleosis is caused by Epstein-Barr virus, which infects B cells through CD21.","The body controls infected B cells with a large cytotoxic T-cell response.","The atypical lymphocytes on the smear are these activated CD8+ T cells."],"why":"Epstein-Barr virus binds CD21 (complement receptor 2) on B cells and drives them to proliferate. The host responds with a strong expansion of cytotoxic CD8+ T cells that kill infected B cells. These activated T cells are large with abundant cytoplasm that scallops around neighboring red cells, and they are the atypical lymphocytes seen in mononucleosis. The same lymphoid expansion enlarges the spleen and makes it fragile, which is why contact sports are avoided for several weeks after the illness.","whyNot":["Infection of CD4+ T cells through the CD4 molecule describes HIV. Epstein-Barr virus targets B cells through CD21.","Infected B cells are present, but they make up a small fraction of circulating lymphocytes. The atypical cells are mostly the T cells responding to them.","Correct.","Monocytes are not the atypical cells in mononucleosis. Large cells with owl-eye inclusions suggest cytomegalovirus infection of tissue cells."],"study":{"topics":["Epstein-Barr virus","Atypical lymphocytes"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-17"]}},{"id":"CV-C39-Q4","stem":"He undergoes splenectomy. A blood smear several weeks later shows erythrocytes containing small, round, dark purple nuclear remnants. Loss of which of the following splenic functions best explains this finding?","options":["Removal of red cell inclusions by red pulp macrophages","Production of IgM by marginal zone B cells","Synthesis of erythropoietin by splenic interstitial cells","Storage of about a third of platelets in the spleen"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-10","obj":"CV-10.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the inclusions as Howell-Jolly bodies, which are DNA remnants in red cells.","Recall that red cells normally squeeze through the cords and sinusoids of the red pulp.","Red pulp macrophages remove inclusions during this passage, so the inclusions persist after splenectomy."],"why":"Blood entering the red pulp passes through the splenic cords and must squeeze through narrow slits in the sinusoid walls. Macrophages there remove old or damaged red cells and pluck out inclusions such as nuclear remnants, a process called pitting. Without a spleen, red cells keep their Howell-Jolly bodies, so these bodies on a smear are a marker of absent or poorly functioning splenic tissue.","whyNot":["Correct.","Marginal zone B cells make IgM against encapsulated bacteria. Losing them explains the infection risk after splenectomy, not nuclear remnants in red cells.","Erythropoietin is made mainly by peritubular interstitial cells in the kidney, with a small share from the liver. The spleen is not a source.","The spleen holds about a third of the platelet mass. Losing this pool causes a rise in platelet count after splenectomy, not red cell inclusions."],"study":{"topics":["Red pulp and white pulp function","Howell-Jolly bodies"],"comps":["CV-10"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C40","system":"CV","dx":"Splenic infarction in sickle cell trait at high altitude","codes":["CV-18-H3"],"case":"A 38-year-old man with sickle cell trait develops sudden, sharp left upper quadrant pain on the second day of a ski trip at about 3,000 m (about 9,800 ft) elevation. He has never had a similar episode. Temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 104/min, and blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows left upper quadrant tenderness. Lactate dehydrogenase is 410 U/L (140 to 280 U/L). Hemoglobin electrophoresis shows about 38% hemoglobin S and 60% hemoglobin A. Contrast CT shows a wedge-shaped area of the spleen that does not enhance, with its base at the capsule. The diagnosis is splenic infarction.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C40-Q1","stem":"This splenic infarct is pale. A lung infarct after a pulmonary embolus is usually dark red and hemorrhagic. Which of the following features of the lung best explains this difference?","options":["Its cells consume more oxygen than splenic cells do","Its arteries are end arteries with no collateral flow","The embolus also blocks the pulmonary venous outflow","Bronchial arteries keep perfusing the dead tissue"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-18-H3","obj":"CV-18-H.4","dok":3,"approach":["Pale infarcts form in solid organs with end-arterial supply, such as the spleen, kidney, and heart.","Red infarcts form where blood keeps entering the dead tissue.","The lung has a dual supply: pulmonary arteries and bronchial arteries from the systemic circulation."],"why":"The spleen and kidney are supplied by end arteries, so when one branch is blocked the territory loses its blood and dies pale, in a wedge with its base at the capsule. The lung receives blood from both the pulmonary arteries and the bronchial arteries. After a pulmonary embolus, bronchial blood keeps flowing into the dying, loose lung tissue and leaks out, giving a hemorrhagic red infarct. The same dual supply explains why most pulmonary emboli in healthy lungs do not cause infarction at all.","whyNot":["Oxygen demand affects how fast cells die, but it does not decide whether blood fills the infarct.","End arteries without collaterals describe the spleen and kidney and lead to pale infarcts. The lung has a dual supply.","A pulmonary embolus lodges in a pulmonary artery and does not block venous outflow, so it cannot explain the blood-filled infarct this way.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Dual blood supply of the lung","End arteries"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-H3"]}},{"id":"CV-C40-Q2","stem":"If tissue from the infarcted area were examined 2 days after the onset of pain, which of the following microscopic patterns would most likely be seen?","options":["Liquefactive necrosis with a cyst forming","Fat necrosis with chalky deposits of calcium soaps","Coagulative necrosis with preserved cell outlines","Caseous necrosis with surrounding granulomas"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H3","obj":"CV-18-H.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the cause of cell death: ischemia in a solid organ.","Recall that ischemic death in solid organs other than the brain gives coagulative necrosis.","In coagulative necrosis, proteins denature, so cell outlines and tissue architecture remain while nuclei fade."],"why":"Ischemia in solid organs such as the spleen, kidney, and heart causes coagulative necrosis. Denaturation of structural proteins and enzymes slows breakdown of the dead cells, so ghost outlines of cells and the basic architecture persist for days while nuclei disappear. Neutrophils gather at the edge of the infarct within 1 to 2 days, and the area is slowly replaced by a fibrous scar.","whyNot":["Liquefactive necrosis with cyst formation is the pattern of ischemic infarcts in the brain and of abscesses.","Fat necrosis with calcium soaps is typical of acute pancreatitis or trauma to fatty tissue such as the breast.","Correct.","Caseous necrosis with granulomas suggests tuberculosis or some fungal infections, not an ischemic infarct."],"study":{"topics":["Patterns of necrosis","Morphology of infarcts"],"comps":["CV-18-H3"]}},{"id":"CV-C40-Q3","stem":"Low oxygen tension at altitude triggered sickling in his spleen. Which of the following molecular events most directly drives the sickling?","options":["Excess alpha chains that form insoluble aggregates","Valine contacts linking deoxygenated HbS molecules","Spectrin deficiency that makes the membrane rigid","Unstable globin that precipitates as Heinz bodies"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-H3","obj":"CV-18-H.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the sickle mutation: a point mutation in the beta-globin gene that replaces glutamate with valine at position 6.","Valine is hydrophobic and, in deoxygenated hemoglobin S, fits into a pocket on a neighboring hemoglobin molecule.","These contacts build long polymers that deform the red cell."],"why":"Hemoglobin S results from a missense point mutation in the beta-globin gene that places valine instead of glutamate at position 6. When hemoglobin S gives up its oxygen, the hydrophobic valine binds a complementary site on an adjacent molecule, and the molecules stack into long polymers that distort and stiffen the cell. In sickle cell trait, about 40% of hemoglobin is hemoglobin S, so sickling needs severe stress such as high altitude, dehydration, or acidosis. The spleen is vulnerable because blood moves slowly through its red pulp, where oxygen and pH are low.","whyNot":["Excess alpha chains precipitate in beta-thalassemia because too little beta globin is made. They damage red cell precursors and do not form sickle polymers.","Correct.","Spectrin deficiency causes hereditary spherocytosis, where red cells lose membrane and become spheres.","Heinz bodies are precipitated hemoglobin. They form when oxidant stress damages hemoglobin in G6PD deficiency, or with unstable hemoglobin variants. They do not cause sickling."],"study":{"topics":["Hemoglobin S polymerization","Sickle cell trait"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C40-Q4","stem":"Which of the following would most likely produce a red (hemorrhagic) infarct instead of a pale one like this patient's?","options":["Embolic occlusion of a renal artery branch","Atherosclerotic occlusion of a splenic artery branch","Torsion of the testis on its spermatic cord","Thrombosis of a coronary artery branch"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H3","obj":"CV-18-H.4","dok":3,"approach":["Pale infarcts follow arterial occlusion in solid organs with end-arterial supply.","Red infarcts form when blood keeps entering dead tissue, as with venous occlusion.","Torsion twists the cord and compresses the thin-walled veins before the thicker arteries, so blood enters but cannot leave."],"why":"In testicular torsion the spermatic cord twists. Veins have thin walls and low pressure, so they collapse first while the arteries keep pushing some blood in. The testis becomes congested, then infarcts with blood trapped inside, producing a red infarct. Ovarian torsion and volvulus of the bowel behave the same way, while arterial occlusion in the kidney, heart, and spleen gives pale infarcts.","whyNot":["The kidney has end arteries, so a branch embolus produces a pale wedge-shaped infarct.","An occluded splenic artery branch gives a pale infarct like this patient's, since the spleen is supplied by end arteries.","Correct.","The heart muscle is solid tissue supplied by end arteries, so a coronary thrombosis typically produces a pale infarct."],"study":{"topics":["Red versus pale infarcts","Venous occlusion and infarction"],"comps":["CV-18-H3","CV-9"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C41","system":"CV","dx":"Generalized edema from nephrotic syndrome (minimal change disease)","codes":["CV-18-H4"],"case":"A 5-year-old boy is brought to the clinic because his eyes have been puffy every morning for a week, and now his legs and scrotum are swollen. He had a mild cold 2 weeks ago. Blood pressure is 98/60 mm Hg. Physical examination shows periorbital edema, scrotal edema, and pitting edema of both legs. Urinalysis shows 4+ protein and no red cells. The urine protein-to-creatinine ratio is 6.2 mg/mg (under 0.2 mg/mg). Serum albumin is 1.6 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0 g/dL), total cholesterol is 380 mg/dL (under 170 mg/dL), and serum creatinine is 0.3 mg/dL (0.3 to 0.7 mg/dL). The diagnosis is nephrotic syndrome, most likely minimal change disease, with generalized edema.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C41-Q1","stem":"Which of the following changes in Starling forces at his systemic capillaries is the main cause of fluid accumulation in his tissues?","options":["Decreased plasma oncotic pressure","Increased capillary permeability to protein","Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure","Decreased interstitial hydrostatic pressure"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.5","dok":2,"approach":["Find the key lab: serum albumin of 1.6 g/dL from heavy urinary protein loss.","Albumin provides most of the plasma oncotic pressure that holds fluid inside capillaries.","With less oncotic pull, net filtration rises and fluid collects in loose tissues such as the eyelids and scrotum."],"why":"Net filtration across a capillary depends on capillary hydrostatic pressure pushing fluid out and plasma oncotic pressure pulling it back. Albumin supplies most of the plasma oncotic pressure, so heavy proteinuria with a low serum albumin lowers this pull and more fluid stays in the interstitium. Edema shows first where tissue is loose, such as around the eyes in the morning. Renal sodium retention also adds to the edema in nephrotic syndrome, but the low oncotic pressure is the classic Starling explanation.","whyNot":["Correct.","Increased permeability to protein drives edema in inflammation, burns, and sepsis. The leak in his disease is at the glomerulus, while his systemic capillaries are normal.","Raised capillary hydrostatic pressure drives edema in heart failure and venous obstruction. Nothing in his case points to high venous pressure.","A fall in interstitial hydrostatic pressure would favor filtration slightly, but it is not the main problem here. Interstitial pressure actually rises as edema fluid collects."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces","Hypoalbuminemia and edema"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H4"]}},{"id":"CV-C41-Q2","stem":"A woman develops chronic arm swelling after removal of her axillary lymph nodes. Compared with this boy's edema fluid, her edema fluid is most likely to have which of the following features?","options":["A higher protein concentration","A lower protein concentration","More neutrophils and fibrin strands","Similar protein but more red blood cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-10","obj":"CV-10.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that lymphatics return filtered fluid and the protein that leaks out of capillaries back to the blood.","When lymphatics are blocked, the protein stays behind in the interstitium.","His edema comes from low plasma protein with normal capillaries, so his fluid is a low-protein transudate."],"why":"Each day about 2 to 3 L of filtered fluid, along with plasma proteins that slowly leak from capillaries, returns to the blood through lymphatics. Lymphatic obstruction, as after axillary node dissection or in filariasis, traps that protein in the tissue, raising interstitial oncotic pressure and producing protein-rich edema that can lead to fibrosis over time. In nephrotic syndrome, plasma albumin is low and capillary walls are intact, so the edema fluid is a protein-poor transudate.","whyNot":["Correct.","A lower protein concentration describes his transudate. Lymphatic obstruction does the opposite because protein can no longer be cleared from the tissue.","Neutrophils and fibrin are features of an inflammatory exudate, such as in infection or acute inflammation. Simple lymphatic obstruction is not inflammatory at the start.","Red cells in edema fluid suggest hemorrhage or trauma. Lymphatic obstruction changes protein content, not red cell content."],"study":{"topics":["Lymphatic function","Lymphedema versus transudate"],"comps":["CV-10","CV-18-H4"]}},{"id":"CV-C41-Q3","stem":"Three weeks later he develops fever and diffuse abdominal tenderness. Paracentesis yields cloudy ascitic fluid with a neutrophil count of 1,500/µL (under 250/µL). Which of the following organisms is the most likely cause?","options":["Candida albicans","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Staphylococcus aureus","Clostridioides difficile"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-H4","obj":"CV-18-H.6","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the infection as spontaneous bacterial peritonitis in a child with nephrotic ascites.","Recall that nephrotic children lose IgG and complement factors in the urine, which weakens opsonization.","Choose the encapsulated organism classically linked to peritonitis in nephrotic children."],"why":"Children with nephrotic syndrome lose IgG and some complement factors in the urine. Encapsulated bacteria resist phagocytosis unless they are coated by antibody and complement, so weak opsonization raises the risk of invasive infection by them. Streptococcus pneumoniae is the classic cause of spontaneous peritonitis in nephrotic children, and gram-negative rods such as Escherichia coli are the other main cause.","whyNot":["Candida peritonitis is mainly seen with bowel perforation, peritoneal dialysis, or long antibiotic courses, not as a spontaneous infection in nephrotic syndrome.","Correct.","Staphylococcus aureus causes skin, line, and bone infections. It is an unusual cause of spontaneous peritonitis and is not the organism linked to loss of opsonins here.","Clostridioides difficile causes colitis after antibiotic use. It does not cause spontaneous peritonitis."],"study":{"topics":["Opsonization and encapsulated bacteria","Infections in nephrotic syndrome"],"comps":["CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C41-Q4","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains his total cholesterol concentration of 380 mg/dL?","options":["Increased intestinal uptake of dietary cholesterol","Reduced use of cholesterol for bile acid synthesis","Loss-of-function mutation in the LDL receptor gene","Increased hepatic synthesis of apoB lipoproteins"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.1","dok":2,"approach":["Link the hyperlipidemia to the other nephrotic findings: low albumin and low plasma oncotic pressure.","Recall that the liver responds by increasing protein synthesis, including albumin and lipoproteins.","More apoB-containing lipoproteins (VLDL and LDL) leave the liver, so cholesterol rises."],"why":"In nephrotic syndrome the liver responds to protein loss and low oncotic pressure by making more proteins, including apoB-containing lipoproteins such as VLDL, which becomes LDL. Clearance of lipoproteins is also reduced, partly because lipoprotein lipase activity falls. The combined result is high cholesterol and often high triglycerides, which usually settle once the proteinuria resolves.","whyNot":["Intestinal absorption of cholesterol is not the driver in nephrotic syndrome. The extra lipoproteins come from the liver.","Reduced conversion of cholesterol to bile acids can raise cholesterol, but it is not the mechanism linked to protein loss in nephrotic syndrome.","An LDL receptor mutation causes familial hypercholesterolemia, which is present from birth and would not appear suddenly with proteinuria and edema.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hyperlipidemia in nephrotic syndrome","Lipoprotein metabolism"],"comps":["CV-13"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C42","system":"CV","dx":"Acute arterial thrombosis of the superficial femoral artery","codes":["CV-18-H6"],"case":"A 64-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because his right leg suddenly became painful, pale, and cool this morning. For the past year he has had calf pain after walking two blocks. He has type 2 diabetes and a 45-pack-year smoking history. Physical examination shows that the right femoral pulse is present, but the right popliteal and pedal pulses are absent. His heart rhythm is regular, and echocardiography shows no cardiac thrombus. Platelet count is 260,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000/µL). Angiography shows a thrombus on an ulcerated atherosclerotic plaque that blocks the right superficial femoral artery. The diagnosis is acute arterial thrombosis of the superficial femoral artery.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C42-Q1","stem":"The first step in forming his thrombus was adhesion of platelets to the subendothelium exposed by the ulcerated plaque. Which of the following interactions mediates this adhesion under the high shear stress of arterial flow?","options":["P2Y12 receptor binding adenosine diphosphate","Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa receptor binding fibrinogen","Tissue factor binding factor VIIa","Glycoprotein Ib binding von Willebrand factor"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H6","obj":"CV-18-H.1","dok":2,"approach":["Separate the steps of platelet plug formation: adhesion, activation, then aggregation.","Adhesion is the step where a resting platelet first sticks to exposed collagen.","At high arterial shear, von Willebrand factor bound to collagen captures platelets through glycoprotein Ib."],"why":"When a plaque ulcerates, subendothelial collagen is exposed. Von Willebrand factor from plasma and endothelium binds the collagen and unfolds under the high shear of arterial flow. Platelets then stick to it through their glycoprotein Ib receptor, which is the adhesion step. The adherent platelets activate, release ADP and thromboxane A2, and then aggregate, building a platelet-rich arterial thrombus.","whyNot":["ADP binding the P2Y12 receptor amplifies platelet activation and recruits more platelets. It acts after the platelets have already adhered.","Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa binding fibrinogen links platelets to one another. This is aggregation, the step that follows adhesion and activation.","Tissue factor binding factor VIIa starts the coagulation cascade that makes thrombin and fibrin. It is not the receptor interaction that makes platelets stick to the vessel wall.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Platelet adhesion and von Willebrand factor","Arterial thrombus formation"],"comps":["CV-18-H6","CV-18-I"]}},{"id":"CV-C42-Q2","stem":"Before today, his leg tolerated a year of gradual narrowing of the superficial femoral artery. Collateral branches of which of the following arteries most likely carried blood around the narrowing to the region of the knee?","options":["Superficial circumflex iliac artery","Deep femoral artery","Inferior epigastric artery","Obturator artery"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.3","dok":3,"approach":["The superficial femoral artery continues as the popliteal artery behind the knee.","Look for an artery that arises above the narrowing and has branches that reach the knee.","The deep femoral (profunda femoris) artery sends perforating branches and the descending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral artery down to join the genicular arteries."],"why":"The femoral artery divides into the superficial femoral artery and the deep femoral (profunda femoris) artery. Branches of the deep femoral artery, including its perforating arteries and the descending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral artery, connect with genicular branches of the popliteal artery around the knee. When the superficial femoral artery narrows slowly, these channels enlarge and carry blood around the blockage. This is why chronic narrowing caused only calf pain on walking, while sudden thrombosis today overwhelmed the collaterals.","whyNot":["The superficial circumflex iliac artery supplies skin near the anterior superior iliac spine and does not reach the knee.","Correct.","The inferior epigastric artery runs up the anterior abdominal wall and joins the superior epigastric artery. It is a collateral around aortic or iliac blockage, not around the thigh.","The obturator artery supplies the medial thigh muscles and the hip. Its branches do not reach the knee anastomosis."],"study":{"topics":["Arteries of the thigh","Genicular anastomosis"],"comps":["CV-9"]}},{"id":"CV-C42-Q3","stem":"He is started on daily aspirin. Which of the following best explains why one dose of aspirin impairs a platelet for the rest of its life span of about 7 to 10 days?","options":["Platelets have no nucleus to make new cyclooxygenase","Aspirin is stored inside platelet dense granules","Platelets lack the enzymes that clear salicylate","Aspirin binds glycoprotein IIb/IIIa for about 10 days"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-H6","obj":"CV-18-H.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the target: aspirin irreversibly acetylates cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1).","Without COX-1, platelets cannot turn arachidonic acid into thromboxane A2, a key signal for aggregation.","A platelet has no nucleus, so it cannot replace the blocked enzyme, and the effect lasts until new platelets are made."],"why":"Aspirin acetylates a serine in the active site of COX-1, which blocks the enzyme permanently. Platelets use COX-1 to convert arachidonic acid into thromboxane A2, which promotes vasoconstriction and platelet aggregation. Platelets have no nucleus and cannot make meaningful amounts of new enzyme, so each exposed platelet stays inhibited for its life span. Normal function returns only as the marrow releases new platelets, about 10% of the total each day.","whyNot":["Correct.","Dense granules store ADP, calcium, and serotonin. Aspirin is not stored there.","Salicylate is cleared by the liver and kidneys within hours. The lasting effect comes from the permanently acetylated enzyme, not from drug left in the platelet.","Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa is blocked by drugs such as abciximab and eptifibatide. Aspirin does not bind this receptor."],"study":{"topics":["Thromboxane A2 synthesis","Aspirin and COX-1"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-H6"]}},{"id":"CV-C42-Q4","stem":"He undergoes bypass surgery and receives unfractionated heparin. Six days later his platelet count is 95,000/µL, and a new thrombus forms in a vein of his left arm. Which of the following mechanisms best explains these events?","options":["Heparin directly clumps platelets without any antibody","IgE against heparin causes mast cell degranulation","IgG to heparin-platelet factor 4 activates platelets","Heparin depletes antithrombin, leaving thrombin unchecked"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-H6","obj":"CV-18-H.2","dok":3,"approach":["Note the timing and pattern: a platelet fall of more than 50% about 5 to 10 days after starting heparin, plus new thrombosis.","This is heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT), an antibody-mediated reaction.","IgG binds heparin-platelet factor 4 complexes, and the immune complexes activate platelets through Fc gamma receptors."],"why":"In heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, heparin binds platelet factor 4 (PF4) and creates a new antigen. The patient makes IgG against heparin-PF4 complexes, and the antibody-coated complexes bind Fc gamma IIa receptors on platelets and activate them. Activated platelets release procoagulant particles and are cleared, so the count falls while clots form in veins and arteries. The usual onset is about 5 to 10 days after first exposure, the time needed to make IgG.","whyNot":["A mild, early fall in platelets from direct heparin effect can occur within the first 1 to 2 days, but it is harmless and does not cause thrombosis.","IgE-mediated reactions cause hives, wheezing, or anaphylaxis, not a falling platelet count with clots.","Correct.","Heparin works by boosting antithrombin. Any small drop in antithrombin does not explain a large fall in platelets."],"study":{"topics":["Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","Fc receptor-mediated platelet activation"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-H6"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C43","system":"CV","dx":"Abdominal aortic aneurysm (infrarenal)","codes":["CV-18-I1"],"case":"A 72-year-old man comes to the physician for a routine health maintenance examination. He has hypertension and has smoked one pack of cigarettes daily for 50 years. He has no abdominal or back pain. Pulse is 74/min, and blood pressure is 152/88 mm Hg (normal below 120/80 mm Hg). Physical examination shows a nontender, pulsatile mass just above the umbilicus. Ultrasonography shows fusiform dilation of the abdominal aorta below the renal arteries, with an outer diameter of 5.8 cm (normal about 2 cm). The renal arteries arise from normal-caliber aorta above the dilation. The diagnosis is infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA).","questions":[{"id":"CV-C43-Q1","stem":"He undergoes open surgical repair. During the operation, an unpaired artery arising from the anterior wall of the aneurysm at about the level of the third lumbar vertebra is ligated. If collateral flow to this artery's territory is poor, which of the following segments of bowel is at greatest risk for ischemia after surgery?","options":["Ascending colon","Jejunum","Sigmoid colon","Second part of the duodenum"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.1","dok":3,"approach":["Name the vessel: an unpaired anterior branch of the aorta at L3, below the renal arteries, is the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA).","Recall the IMA territory: distal transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, and upper rectum.","Pick the segment in that territory that is classically injured after aortic repair: the sigmoid colon."],"why":"The inferior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at about L3, so it arises from the segment that an infrarenal AAA usually involves. It supplies the left colon, including the sigmoid colon and upper rectum. When it is tied off during repair, the left colon depends on collaterals from the superior mesenteric artery (through the marginal artery) and from the internal iliac arteries. If these are poor, ischemic colitis develops, most often in the sigmoid colon.","whyNot":["The ascending colon is supplied by the ileocolic and right colic branches of the superior mesenteric artery, which are not tied off in an infrarenal repair.","The jejunum is supplied by jejunal branches of the superior mesenteric artery, which arises at about L1, above the renal arteries and above this aneurysm.","Correct.","The second part of the duodenum is supplied by the superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries, from the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery."],"study":{"topics":["Inferior mesenteric artery territory","Branches of the abdominal aorta by vertebral level"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I1"]}},{"id":"CV-C43-Q2","stem":"Which of the following processes best explains the progressive weakening of the aortic wall in this patient's aneurysm?","options":["Obliterative endarteritis of the vasa vasorum","Granulomatous inflammation with giant cells in the media","An intimal tear that lets blood split the media","Protease breakdown of medial elastin and collagen"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I1","obj":"CV-18-I.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the setting: an older male smoker with hypertension and an infrarenal aneurysm has the typical atherosclerosis-associated AAA.","Recall what thins the media in AAA: inflammatory cells release matrix metalloproteinases that digest elastin and collagen.","Rule out mechanisms that belong to other aortic diseases (syphilis, dissection, arteritis)."],"why":"Typical AAAs arise in older smokers with atherosclerosis. Macrophages and other inflammatory cells in the aortic wall release matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) that break down elastin and collagen in the media, and their inhibitors are reduced. Thick intimal plaque also slows diffusion of nutrients to the inner media. The thinned, weakened wall slowly dilates under arterial pressure.","whyNot":["This describes tertiary syphilis, which narrows the vasa vasorum and causes aneurysms of the ascending aorta and arch, not the infrarenal aorta.","Granulomas with giant cells describe giant cell arteritis or Takayasu arteritis, which mainly involve the thoracic aorta and its branches.","An intimal tear with blood tracking through the media describes aortic dissection, which is a different disease from a fusiform aneurysm.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of abdominal aortic aneurysm","Matrix metalloproteinases in vessel wall remodeling"],"comps":["CV-18-I1","CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C43-Q3","stem":"For a cylindrical vessel, Laplace's law states that wall tension equals transmural pressure times radius. If the pressure inside this patient's aneurysm equals that in a normal 2-cm aorta, which of the following is closest to the ratio of wall tension in the aneurysm to normal wall tension?","options":["About 1.7 times","About 3 times","About 8 times","About 70 times"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","obj":"CV-6.4","dok":3,"approach":["Write Laplace's law for a cylinder: tension = pressure x radius.","Compare radii: 5.8 cm divided by 2 cm is 2.9, so the radius is about 3 times normal.","With pressure unchanged, tension rises in direct proportion to radius: about 3 times."],"why":"Laplace's law for a cylinder says wall tension equals pressure times radius. The aneurysm's radius is about 2.9 times normal, so at the same pressure the wall tension is about 3 times normal. Because the aneurysm wall is also thinner, the stress on each unit of wall (tension divided by thickness) rises even more. This growing load on a weakening wall is why rupture risk climbs steeply as an aneurysm enlarges, and why elective repair is advised at about 5.5 cm in men.","whyNot":["About 1.7 times would come from taking the square root of the radius ratio, which has no basis in Laplace's law.","Correct.","About 8 times comes from squaring the radius ratio, which would apply to cross-sectional area, not wall tension.","About 70 times comes from raising the radius ratio to the fourth power, which belongs to Poiseuille's law for resistance and flow."],"study":{"topics":["Law of Laplace","Aneurysm rupture risk and size"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-I1"]}},{"id":"CV-C43-Q4","stem":"Before the repair is scheduled, he develops fever and new back pain. One week earlier he had diarrhea after eating undercooked chicken. Temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F). Blood cultures grow a motile, oxidase-negative, gram-negative rod that does not ferment lactose and produces hydrogen sulfide. CT scan shows rapid enlargement of the aneurysm with surrounding soft-tissue thickening. Which of the following is the most likely causal organism?","options":["Klebsiella pneumoniae","Salmonella enterica","Escherichia coli","Shigella sonnei"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-I1","obj":"CV-18-I.4","dok":2,"approach":["List the lab clues: gram-negative rod, oxidase negative, motile, lactose nonfermenter, hydrogen sulfide producer.","Add the history: poultry-borne diarrhea followed by bacteremia in an older adult with a diseased aorta.","Match to the organism known for seeding atherosclerotic or aneurysmal arteries: nontyphoidal Salmonella."],"why":"Nontyphoidal Salmonella is a motile, oxidase-negative, gram-negative rod that does not ferment lactose and makes hydrogen sulfide. It is commonly acquired from poultry and eggs. In adults over about 50, Salmonella bacteremia can seed atherosclerotic or aneurysmal arterial walls and cause an infected (mycotic) aneurysm, which tends to enlarge quickly and rupture.","whyNot":["Klebsiella ferments lactose and is nonmotile, so it does not fit these culture results.","Correct.","Escherichia coli usually ferments lactose and does not produce hydrogen sulfide.","Shigella does not ferment lactose but is nonmotile and does not produce hydrogen sulfide. It has no animal reservoir and rarely enters the bloodstream."],"study":{"topics":["Nontyphoidal Salmonella","Infected (mycotic) aneurysm","Enteric gram-negative rod identification"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-I1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C44","system":"CV","dx":"Acute Stanford type A aortic dissection in Marfan syndrome","codes":["CV-18-I2"],"case":"A 34-year-old man with Marfan syndrome is brought to the emergency department because of sudden, severe, tearing chest pain that radiates to the area between his shoulder blades. He had bilateral ectopia lentis as a teenager. He is 196 cm tall. Blood pressure is 158/92 mm Hg in the right arm and 124/76 mm Hg in the left arm. Physical examination shows arachnodactyly. A soft early diastolic murmur is heard along the left sternal border. CT angiography shows an intimal flap that begins in the ascending aorta just above the aortic valve and extends beyond the origin of the left subclavian artery. The diagnosis is acute Stanford type A aortic dissection.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C44-Q1","stem":"His Marfan syndrome is caused by a mutation in FBN1. Normal fibrillin-1 microfibrils sequester a growth factor in a latent form in the extracellular matrix. Increased signaling by which of the following factors contributes to degeneration of this patient's aortic media?","options":["Platelet-derived growth factor","Tumor necrosis factor alpha","Transforming growth factor beta","Vascular endothelial growth factor"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.3","dok":2,"approach":["Name the gene product: FBN1 encodes fibrillin-1, a scaffold for elastic fibers.","Recall the second role of fibrillin-1: it binds and sequesters latent TGF-beta in the matrix.","Connect the defect to disease: abnormal fibrillin releases too much active TGF-beta, which damages the aortic wall."],"why":"Marfan syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in FBN1 on chromosome 15. Fibrillin-1 forms microfibrils that support elastic fibers and also hold transforming growth factor beta (TGF-beta) in a latent complex. Mutant fibrillin fails to sequester TGF-beta, and the excess signaling contributes to loss of smooth muscle and elastic tissue in the aortic media. This medial weakness leads to aortic root dilation and dissection.","whyNot":["Platelet-derived growth factor drives smooth muscle migration in atherosclerosis but is not stored by fibrillin-1.","Tumor necrosis factor alpha is an inflammatory cytokine, and Marfan aortic disease is characteristically noninflammatory.","Correct.","Vascular endothelial growth factor drives new vessel growth and is not held latent by fibrillin microfibrils."],"study":{"topics":["Fibrillin-1 and TGF-beta signaling","Marfan syndrome genetics"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-I2"]}},{"id":"CV-C44-Q2","stem":"The false lumen of an aortic dissection forms within the aortic wall, usually between the middle and outer thirds of one layer. In a normal adult aorta, which of the following best describes the main components of that layer?","options":["Fenestrated elastic laminae with smooth muscle between them","Endothelium resting on a thin layer of loose connective tissue","Loose collagen containing vasa vasorum, nerves, and fibroblasts","Longitudinal smooth muscle bundles with few elastic fibers"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","obj":"CV-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the layer: a dissection plane runs within the tunica media.","Recall that the aorta is an elastic artery whose media is its thickest layer.","Describe that media: many concentric fenestrated elastic laminae with smooth muscle cells, collagen, and ground substance between them."],"why":"In a dissection, blood enters through an intimal tear and splits the tunica media, most often between its middle and outer thirds. The aorta is an elastic artery. Its media is the thickest layer and contains dozens of concentric, fenestrated elastic laminae with smooth muscle cells, collagen, and proteoglycan ground substance between them. These laminae store energy during systole and recoil in diastole, and their loss is what makes the media vulnerable to splitting.","whyNot":["Correct.","This describes the tunica intima. The intimal tear is the entry point, but the false lumen runs in the media.","This describes the tunica adventitia, the outer layer that carries vasa vasorum and nerves to the outer wall.","Longitudinal smooth muscle bundles are typical of the adventitia of large veins such as the inferior vena cava, not the aortic wall."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of elastic arteries","Layers of the aortic wall"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-18-I2"]}},{"id":"CV-C44-Q3","stem":"Two hours later, he becomes confused. Pulse is 128/min, and blood pressure is 84/60 mm Hg. Jugular venous distention is present, and heart sounds are muffled. Systolic pressure decreases by 18 mm Hg with each inspiration. Which of the following mechanisms best explains the decrease in systolic pressure during inspiration?","options":["Inspiration lowers venous return to the right heart, reducing output","Lung expansion compresses the false lumen and narrows the true lumen","Inspiration raises vagal tone, slowing the heart and lowering output","Right ventricular filling rises and bows the septum to the left"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","obj":"CV-15.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize cardiac tamponade: hypotension, distended neck veins, and muffled heart sounds after a type A dissection ruptures into the pericardium.","Recall the effect of inspiration: lower intrathoracic pressure increases venous return to the right ventricle.","Apply the fixed pericardial volume: the right ventricle can only expand by bowing the septum left, so left ventricular filling and stroke volume fall."],"why":"A type A dissection can rupture back into the pericardial sac, causing hemopericardium and tamponade. The tight pericardium fixes the total volume of the heart. During inspiration, falling intrathoracic pressure draws more blood into the right ventricle, which can only enlarge by pushing the interventricular septum into the left ventricle. Left ventricular filling and stroke volume fall, so systolic pressure drops by more than 10 mm Hg with inspiration (pulsus paradoxus). Pooling of blood in the expanding pulmonary veins adds to the fall.","whyNot":["Inspiration increases venous return to the right heart. It does not reduce it.","There is no recognized mechanism by which lung expansion compresses the false lumen to cause a cyclic fall in pressure.","Inspiration lowers vagal tone and slightly speeds the heart (sinus arrhythmia), the opposite of this option.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pulsus paradoxus","Cardiac tamponade physiology","Effects of respiration on venous return"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-18-F"]}},{"id":"CV-C44-Q4","stem":"A resected segment of his ascending aorta is examined microscopically. Which of the following findings in the media is most characteristic of the process that predisposed him to dissection?","options":["Fibrinoid necrosis with neutrophils across the vessel wall","Granulomas with multinucleated giant cells throughout the media","Plasma cells around narrowed, thickened vasa vasorum","Elastic fiber breakup, smooth muscle loss, and mucoid pools"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I2","obj":"CV-18-I.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the predisposing condition: Marfan syndrome weakens the aortic media.","Name the lesion: cystic medial degeneration.","Describe it: elastic fiber fragmentation, smooth muscle loss, and pools of proteoglycan-rich ground substance, with little inflammation."],"why":"Marfan syndrome and long-standing hypertension both predispose to dissection through cystic medial degeneration. The media shows fragmented and separated elastic fibers, loss of smooth muscle cells, and small cleftlike or cystic spaces filled with proteoglycan-rich (mucoid) ground substance. Inflammation is characteristically absent. The weakened media splits easily once blood enters through an intimal tear.","whyNot":["Fibrinoid necrosis with neutrophils describes necrotizing vasculitis such as polyarteritis nodosa, which affects medium-sized arteries.","Granulomas with giant cells describe giant cell arteritis or Takayasu arteritis.","Plasma cells around narrowed vasa vasorum describe syphilitic aortitis, which causes aneurysms of the ascending aorta but rarely dissection.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cystic medial degeneration","Risk factors for aortic dissection"],"comps":["CV-18-I2","CV-14"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C45","system":"CV","dx":"Atherosclerotic carotid artery disease with amaurosis fugax","codes":["CV-18-I3"],"case":"A 66-year-old woman comes to the physician because of an episode of painless vision loss in her right eye yesterday. She describes a shade coming down over the eye that lasted about 5 minutes and then lifted. She has type 2 diabetes mellitus and hypertension and smoked for 30 years. Blood pressure is 148/90 mm Hg (normal below 120/80 mm Hg). A bruit is heard over the right side of the neck near the angle of the mandible. Serum LDL cholesterol is 168 mg/dL (optimal below 100 mg/dL). Duplex ultrasonography shows an ulcerated plaque at the origin of the right internal carotid artery that narrows the lumen diameter by about 50%. The diagnosis is atherosclerotic carotid artery disease with amaurosis fugax, a warning sign of high stroke risk.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C45-Q1","stem":"Her transient vision loss was most likely caused by a small embolus from the plaque that lodged in a branch of which of the following arteries?","options":["Right posterior cerebral artery","Right superficial temporal artery","Right ophthalmic artery","Left middle cerebral artery"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Note that vision was lost in one eye only, which points to the retina rather than the visual cortex or optic radiations.","Trace the path: plaque at the right internal carotid origin, up the internal carotid, into its first major intracranial branch.","That branch is the ophthalmic artery, which gives off the central retinal artery."],"why":"Monocular vision loss means the retina or optic nerve of that eye lost blood flow. The internal carotid artery has no branches in the neck. Its first major branch inside the skull is the ophthalmic artery, which gives off the central retinal artery. An embolus from an ulcerated plaque at the internal carotid origin can travel into this branch and cause transient monocular blindness (amaurosis fugax).","whyNot":["The posterior cerebral artery supplies the occipital cortex. Its ischemia causes a homonymous hemianopia that affects the visual field of both eyes, and it usually arises from the basilar artery, not the carotid.","The superficial temporal artery is a branch of the external carotid artery and does not supply the retina. It is the classic site of giant cell arteritis.","Correct.","The left middle cerebral artery cannot receive an embolus from a right carotid plaque, and middle cerebral territory loss affects both eyes' visual fields, not one eye."],"study":{"topics":["Branches of the internal carotid artery","Amaurosis fugax"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C45-Q2","stem":"At the plaque, the lumen radius is about half of normal. If flow is laminar and blood viscosity and segment length are unchanged, the resistance of the narrowed segment has increased by approximately which of the following factors?","options":["About 16 times","About 256 times","About 4 times","About 2 times"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall Poiseuille's law: resistance is proportional to viscosity times length divided by radius to the fourth power.","Halving the radius divides the denominator by 2 to the fourth power, which is 16.","So resistance in that segment rises about 16 times."],"why":"Poiseuille's law states that resistance equals 8 times viscosity times length, divided by pi times the radius to the fourth power. Because radius is raised to the fourth power, halving the radius raises resistance 16 times. A 50% diameter narrowing is the same as halving the radius. This steep relationship explains why modest further plaque growth or a thrombus on an ulcerated plaque can sharply cut flow.","whyNot":["Correct.","About 256 times is the increase when the radius falls to one quarter of normal (4 to the fourth power).","About 4 times would be true if resistance depended on radius squared (cross-sectional area).","About 2 times would be true only if resistance were inversely proportional to radius itself."],"study":{"topics":["Poiseuille's law","Vascular resistance and radius"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C45-Q3","stem":"Macrophages in her plaque become foam cells by taking up oxidized LDL. Which of the following best explains why these cells continue to take up cholesterol after they are heavily loaded?","options":["LDL receptor numbers rise as the cell's cholesterol increases","Scavenger receptors are not downregulated by cholesterol","SREBP-2 is activated by high levels of cell cholesterol","HDL delivers extra stored cholesterol into macrophages"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.1","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the receptor: oxidized LDL enters macrophages through scavenger receptors such as SR-A and CD36.","Contrast with the LDL receptor, which is shut down by high intracellular cholesterol through SREBP-2.","Conclude that scavenger receptor uptake has no feedback brake, so lipid keeps accumulating."],"why":"Oxidized LDL in the intima is recognized by macrophage scavenger receptors, such as SR-A and CD36, which are pattern recognition receptors of innate immunity. Unlike the LDL receptor, these receptors are not downregulated when the cell fills with cholesterol. Uptake continues, cholesteryl ester droplets build up, and the cell becomes a foam cell. Loaded macrophages also release cytokines that recruit more inflammatory cells.","whyNot":["LDL receptor expression falls when cell cholesterol rises, because SREBP-2 is held inactive. This feedback is the reason native LDL does not overload cells.","Correct.","SREBP-2 is activated when cell cholesterol is low and suppressed when it is high.","HDL removes cholesterol from macrophages (reverse cholesterol transport). It does not load them."],"study":{"topics":["Scavenger receptors and foam cells","Macrophages in atherosclerosis"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C45-Q4","stem":"Her plaque formed at the carotid bifurcation, a common site of atherosclerosis. Which of the following local conditions best explains why arterial branch points are prone to plaque formation?","options":["Absent vasa vasorum causing ischemia of the inner wall","Low and oscillating shear stress on endothelial cells","Lower pressure at branches that draws plasma lipids inward","High, steady laminar shear stress on endothelial cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I3","obj":"CV-18-I.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall where plaques form: branch points, ostia, and the outer wall of the carotid bulb.","Link flow pattern to endothelial behavior: steady laminar flow is protective, disturbed flow is not.","Choose low, oscillating shear stress, which turns on proinflammatory, adhesive endothelial genes."],"why":"Steady laminar flow with high shear stress induces protective endothelial genes, including endothelial nitric oxide synthase, which keep the surface anti-inflammatory and less permeable. At branch points such as the carotid bulb, flow separates and recirculates, so shear stress is low and changes direction. Endothelium there makes less nitric oxide, expresses adhesion molecules such as VCAM-1, and lets more LDL into the intima. This is why plaques cluster at bifurcations and ostia.","whyNot":["The inner wall of all arteries is nourished by diffusion from the lumen, so lack of vasa vasorum there is not specific to branch points.","Correct.","Pressure differences between branches do not draw lipids into the wall. LDL entry depends on endothelial permeability.","High, steady laminar shear stress protects the endothelium and is found in straight segments where plaques are less common."],"study":{"topics":["Shear stress and endothelial function","Sites of atherosclerosis"],"comps":["CV-18-I3","CV-11"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C46","system":"CV","dx":"Atherosclerosis risk in metabolic syndrome with atherogenic dyslipidemia","codes":["CV-18-I3"],"case":"A 48-year-old man is evaluated at a workplace health screening. He has a sedentary lifestyle and a diet high in fast food. Waist circumference is 108 cm (risk threshold above 102 cm in men). Blood pressure is 142/90 mm Hg (normal below 120/80 mm Hg). Fasting laboratory studies show glucose 118 mg/dL (normal 70 to 99 mg/dL), triglycerides 260 mg/dL (normal below 150 mg/dL), HDL cholesterol 34 mg/dL (desired above 40 mg/dL in men), and LDL cholesterol 138 mg/dL (optimal below 100 mg/dL). High-sensitivity C-reactive protein (hs-CRP) is 4.2 mg/L (low risk below 1.0 mg/L). Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio is 45 mg/g (normal below 30 mg/g). The diagnosis is metabolic syndrome with atherogenic dyslipidemia, which raises his risk of atherosclerosis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C46-Q1","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains this patient's elevated fasting triglyceride concentration?","options":["Increased hepatic VLDL secretion from excess fatty acids","Loss of hepatic LDL receptors slowing the clearance of LDL","Reduced hepatic secretion of apoB-100 particles","Increased intestinal absorption of dietary cholesterol"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.1","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the driver: central obesity with high glucose points to insulin resistance.","Follow the fatty acids: insulin normally suppresses adipocyte lipolysis, so resistant fat cells release more free fatty acids to the liver.","The liver packages the extra fatty acids as triglyceride in VLDL, so fasting triglycerides rise."],"why":"In insulin resistance, insulin fails to suppress hormone-sensitive lipase in adipocytes, so more free fatty acids flow to the liver. The liver re-esterifies them into triglyceride and secretes more VLDL with apoB-100. Reduced lipoprotein lipase activity slows VLDL clearance and adds to the rise. Through cholesteryl ester transfer protein, triglyceride then moves from VLDL into HDL and LDL, which explains his low HDL and the small, dense LDL typical of this pattern.","whyNot":["Correct.","Loss of LDL receptors raises LDL cholesterol, as in familial hypercholesterolemia, but does not by itself raise fasting triglycerides.","Reduced apoB-100 secretion would lower VLDL and triglycerides. Insulin resistance does the opposite.","Dietary cholesterol absorption mainly affects LDL cholesterol. Fasting triglycerides come from hepatic VLDL, not from chylomicrons."],"study":{"topics":["Insulin resistance and VLDL","Atherogenic dyslipidemia"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C46-Q2","stem":"In a research study, infusion of acetylcholine into his brachial artery causes much less forearm vasodilation than in healthy controls. Infusion of sodium nitroprusside, which releases nitric oxide directly, causes a normal response. Which of the following steps is most likely impaired?","options":["Alpha-1 receptor signaling in forearm arterioles","Smooth muscle guanylyl cyclase response to nitric oxide","Release of nitric oxide by the endothelium","Smooth muscle beta-2 receptor signaling through cAMP"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the acetylcholine pathway: acetylcholine acts on endothelial muscarinic receptors to make nitric oxide.","Use the control: nitroprusside bypasses the endothelium and relaxes smooth muscle normally, so the smooth muscle response is intact.","Locate the defect in the endothelium: reduced release and availability of nitric oxide (endothelial dysfunction)."],"why":"Acetylcholine binds endothelial M3 receptors, raises endothelial calcium, and activates endothelial nitric oxide synthase. Nitric oxide diffuses into smooth muscle, activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, raises cGMP, and relaxes the vessel. Nitroprusside supplies nitric oxide without the endothelium, so a normal response to it proves the smooth muscle pathway works. A blunted acetylcholine response with a normal nitroprusside response is the standard test result for endothelial dysfunction, an early step in atherosclerosis that is common in insulin resistance.","whyNot":["Alpha-1 receptors cause vasoconstriction. Acetylcholine-induced dilation does not depend on them.","A smooth muscle guanylyl cyclase defect would also blunt the response to nitroprusside, which was normal.","Correct.","Beta-2 receptors respond to epinephrine, not acetylcholine, and a beta-2 defect would not explain this test result."],"study":{"topics":["Endothelial nitric oxide","Endothelial dysfunction"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C46-Q3","stem":"Visceral adipose tissue in central obesity contains many activated macrophages. Which of the following cytokines released from this tissue most directly stimulates hepatic synthesis of C-reactive protein in this patient?","options":["Interleukin 4","Interleukin 10","Interleukin 2","Interleukin 6"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize CRP as an acute phase protein made by hepatocytes.","Recall the main cytokine that induces acute phase protein synthesis: interleukin 6.","Link visceral fat macrophages and adipocytes as an ongoing source of interleukin 6 in obesity."],"why":"C-reactive protein is an acute phase protein made by hepatocytes. Interleukin 6 is the main signal that drives its synthesis, with help from interleukin 1 and tumor necrosis factor. In central obesity, activated macrophages and adipocytes in visceral fat release interleukin 6 steadily, producing low-grade chronic inflammation. An hs-CRP above about 3 mg/L marks higher cardiovascular risk.","whyNot":["Interleukin 4 is a Th2 cytokine that drives IgE class switching and does not induce CRP.","Interleukin 10 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that dampens acute phase responses.","Interleukin 2 is a T-cell growth factor and does not drive hepatic acute phase protein synthesis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Acute phase response","Inflammation in obesity"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C46-Q4","stem":"Two years later, a kidney biopsy is performed because of increasing albuminuria. It shows small arterioles with walls thickened by homogeneous, glassy, eosinophilic material and narrowed lumens. Which of the following processes most likely produced this arteriolar change?","options":["Lipid-laden macrophages collecting beneath the endothelium","Concentric smooth muscle growth with duplicated basement membrane","Calcium deposition in the media of muscular arteries","Plasma proteins leaking into the wall, plus excess matrix"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I3","obj":"CV-18-I.1","dok":2,"approach":["Name the lesion: glassy pink arteriolar wall thickening is hyaline arteriolosclerosis.","Link it to his conditions: chronic hypertension and diabetes or prediabetes.","Recall the mechanism: plasma protein leakage through injured endothelium plus extra matrix from smooth muscle cells."],"why":"Hyaline arteriolosclerosis is a form of arteriosclerosis seen with long-standing hypertension, diabetes, and aging. Chronic hemodynamic and metabolic stress injures the endothelium, so plasma proteins leak into the arteriolar wall. Smooth muscle cells also lay down extra extracellular matrix. The result is homogeneous pink wall thickening that narrows the lumen and causes ischemic injury to the kidney (nephrosclerosis).","whyNot":["Lipid-laden macrophages beneath the endothelium form fatty streaks and atheromas in large and medium arteries, not arterioles.","Concentric, onion-skin smooth muscle growth with duplicated basement membrane is hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis, seen in severe (malignant) hypertension.","Medial calcification is Monckeberg medial sclerosis, which affects muscular arteries rather than arterioles and does not narrow the lumen.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hyaline arteriolosclerosis","Types of arteriosclerosis"],"comps":["CV-18-I3","CV-18-A"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C47","system":"CV","dx":"Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","codes":["CV-18-I4"],"case":"A 31-year-old man comes to the physician for evaluation after his father died of a myocardial infarction at age 46. He does not smoke and is not obese. Physical examination shows nodular thickening of both Achilles tendons and bilateral corneal arcus. Fasting serum LDL cholesterol is 292 mg/dL (optimal below 100 mg/dL), triglycerides are 120 mg/dL (normal below 150 mg/dL), and HDL cholesterol is 48 mg/dL (desired above 40 mg/dL in men). Thyroid and kidney function test results are normal. Genetic testing shows one pathogenic variant in the LDL receptor gene (LDLR). The diagnosis is heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C47-Q1","stem":"His wife also has heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia caused by a different pathogenic LDLR variant. Which of the following is the probability that their first child inherits a pathogenic variant from each parent and has the severe form of the disease?","options":["25%","50%","75%","100%"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the inheritance: familial hypercholesterolemia is autosomal dominant, and having two pathogenic alleles causes a more severe disease.","Each heterozygous parent passes the variant to a child with a probability of 1/2.","Multiply: 1/2 x 1/2 = 1/4, or 25%."],"why":"Familial hypercholesterolemia is autosomal dominant with a gene dosage effect. Each heterozygous parent passes the pathogenic allele half the time, so the chance that a child inherits both is 1/2 x 1/2, or 25%. A child with two pathogenic alleles (homozygous or compound heterozygous) has very little LDL receptor function, LDL cholesterol often above 500 mg/dL, and coronary disease that can begin in childhood. Each child also has a 50% chance of being heterozygous and a 25% chance of being unaffected.","whyNot":["Correct.","50% is the chance that a child is heterozygous, carrying exactly one pathogenic allele.","75% is the chance that a child has at least one pathogenic allele (affected in either form).","100% would apply only if a parent had two pathogenic alleles and could pass nothing else."],"study":{"topics":["Autosomal dominant inheritance with gene dosage","Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-I4"]}},{"id":"CV-C47-Q2","stem":"In his hepatocytes, fewer functional LDL receptors means less LDL-derived cholesterol reaches the cell interior. Which of the following best describes the hepatocyte response to the decreased intracellular cholesterol?","options":["SREBP-2 is activated, increasing HMG-CoA reductase","HMG-CoA reductase is ubiquitinated and degraded faster","SREBP-2 stays in the ER, lowering HMG-CoA reductase","ACAT activity rises, storing more cholesteryl ester"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.3","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the sensor: SCAP in the endoplasmic reticulum senses membrane cholesterol.","Low cholesterol lets SCAP carry SREBP-2 to the Golgi, where it is cleaved and released.","The released SREBP-2 enters the nucleus and raises transcription of HMG-CoA reductase and the LDL receptor."],"why":"When cholesterol in the endoplasmic reticulum membrane is low, SCAP escorts SREBP-2 to the Golgi. Two proteases (S1P and S2P) cleave it, and the freed transcription factor enters the nucleus and binds sterol regulatory elements. This raises synthesis of HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis, and of the LDL receptor. In heterozygotes, the normal allele is upregulated this way, which is why they respond to statins.","whyNot":["Correct.","Faster ubiquitination and degradation of HMG-CoA reductase is triggered by high sterol levels, not low ones.","SREBP-2 stays in the endoplasmic reticulum when cell cholesterol is high, not low.","ACAT esterifies cholesterol for storage when the cell has excess cholesterol. Low cholesterol reduces its activity."],"study":{"topics":["SREBP-2 regulation","HMG-CoA reductase control","LDL receptor pathway"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I4"]}},{"id":"CV-C47-Q3","stem":"An elastic-stained section of his father's left anterior descending artery shows a plaque with foam cells and a lipid core. The plaque lies between the endothelium and a dark, wavy band, and beneath the band are layers of smooth muscle cells. Which of the following correctly identifies the layer containing the plaque and the band?","options":["Media; the band is the external elastic lamina","Intima; the band is the internal elastic lamina","Adventitia; the band is the external elastic lamina","Media; the band is the internal elastic lamina"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","obj":"CV-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the layers of a muscular artery: intima, internal elastic lamina, media, external elastic lamina, adventitia.","Place the plaque: it sits between the endothelium and the first elastic band, so it is in the intima.","Identify the band: the elastic layer just under the intima, above the smooth muscle media, is the internal elastic lamina."],"why":"Coronary arteries are muscular arteries. From the lumen outward they have an intima (endothelium and subendothelial connective tissue), a wavy internal elastic lamina, a media of circular smooth muscle, an external elastic lamina, and an adventitia. Atherosclerotic plaques form in the intima, above the internal elastic lamina. As a plaque grows, the internal elastic lamina can fragment and the underlying media thins.","whyNot":["The external elastic lamina lies outside the media, so it cannot be the band directly beneath the plaque with smooth muscle below it.","Correct.","The adventitia is the outermost layer, and atheromas do not form there.","The media is the smooth muscle layer beneath the band. The plaque is above the band, so it is in the intima."],"study":{"topics":["Layers of a muscular artery","Location of atherosclerotic plaque"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C47-Q4","stem":"His father's fatal infarction most likely followed rupture of a coronary plaque with a thin fibrous cap. T-helper 1 cells in such plaques release a cytokine that decreases collagen synthesis by intimal smooth muscle cells and activates macrophages. Which of the following cytokines is most likely responsible?","options":["Interleukin 5","Transforming growth factor beta","Interferon gamma","Interleukin 10"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the cell: T-helper 1 cells.","Recall their signature cytokine: interferon gamma, which activates macrophages.","Link it to cap thinning: interferon gamma lowers smooth muscle collagen production while activated macrophages release matrix metalloproteinases."],"why":"The fibrous cap is made of collagen laid down by intimal smooth muscle cells. Th1 cells in the plaque release interferon gamma, which reduces smooth muscle collagen synthesis and activates macrophages. Activated macrophages release matrix metalloproteinases that digest the cap. A thin cap over a large lipid core is the typical vulnerable plaque that ruptures and triggers thrombosis and myocardial infarction.","whyNot":["Interleukin 5 is a Th2 cytokine that drives eosinophil growth and does not reduce cap collagen.","Transforming growth factor beta stimulates collagen synthesis and tends to stabilize plaques.","Correct.","Interleukin 10 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that dampens macrophage activation."],"study":{"topics":["Vulnerable plaque","Th1 cells and interferon gamma"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-C"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C48","system":"CV","dx":"Peripheral arterial disease with intermittent claudication","codes":["CV-18-I6"],"case":"A 63-year-old woman comes to the physician because of cramping pain in her right calf that begins after she walks about two blocks and resolves within a few minutes of standing still. She has smoked for 40 years. Physical examination shows normal femoral pulses bilaterally. The right popliteal, dorsalis pedis, and posterior tibial pulses are diminished. The skin of the right lower leg is shiny with sparse hair. Resting ankle-brachial index (ABI) is 0.62 on the right (normal 1.0 to 1.4) and 1.05 on the left. The diagnosis is peripheral arterial disease of the right leg with intermittent claudication.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C48-Q1","stem":"Based on the pulse findings and the location of her pain, stenosis of which of the following arteries is most likely?","options":["Anterior tibial artery","Superficial femoral artery","Deep artery of the thigh (profunda femoris)","Common iliac artery"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.3","dok":3,"approach":["Use the pulses: a normal femoral pulse places the lesion below the common femoral artery, and a weak popliteal pulse places it above the popliteal artery.","The artery between those two points that continues to the popliteal is the superficial femoral artery in the adductor canal.","Confirm with symptoms: calf claudication fits a femoropopliteal lesion, one level above the painful muscles."],"why":"The femoral artery continues down the thigh as the superficial femoral artery, passes through the adductor canal, and becomes the popliteal artery at the adductor hiatus. A normal femoral pulse with a weak popliteal pulse localizes the narrowing to this segment. The superficial femoral artery in the adductor canal is the most common site of symptomatic atherosclerosis in the leg, and it typically causes calf claudication.","whyNot":["The anterior tibial artery arises below the popliteal artery, so narrowing there would not weaken the popliteal pulse.","Correct.","The deep artery of the thigh supplies the thigh muscles and does not continue to the popliteal artery, so narrowing there would not weaken the popliteal pulse.","A common iliac lesion would weaken the femoral pulse and usually causes buttock and thigh claudication."],"study":{"topics":["Arteries of the lower limb","Localizing PAD by pulses and symptoms"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I6"]}},{"id":"CV-C48-Q2","stem":"Her calf blood flow at rest is normal, yet pain develops with walking. Which of the following best explains this pattern?","options":["Arterioles offset the stenosis at rest but not during exercise","Walking triggers sympathetic constriction of the calf arterioles","Exercise lowers arterial pressure, so less blood reaches the calf","Calf contraction collapses the stenosis and stops flow completely"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-12","obj":"CV-12.3","dok":3,"approach":["Think of the circuit as two resistances in series: the fixed stenosis and the adjustable arterioles downstream.","At rest, the arterioles dilate enough to keep total resistance and flow normal.","During exercise the arterioles are already dilated, so total resistance cannot fall enough, and flow cannot rise to meet demand."],"why":"The stenosis adds a fixed resistance in series with the calf arterioles. At rest, the arterioles dilate to compensate, so resting flow stays normal. During walking, muscle metabolites call for flow several times higher, but the arterioles have little dilation left and the stenosis caps the flow. The pressure drop across the stenosis also grows as flow increases. Oxygen supply falls short of demand, anaerobic metabolites build up, and pain begins. It eases when demand drops with rest.","whyNot":["Correct.","During exercise, local metabolic vasodilation in working muscle overrides sympathetic constriction.","Arterial systolic pressure rises during walking. It does not fall.","Muscle contraction does not close a proximal arterial stenosis. The problem is limited flow reserve, not complete collapse."],"study":{"topics":["Flow reserve in claudication","Exercise hyperemia"],"comps":["CV-12","CV-11"]}},{"id":"CV-C48-Q3","stem":"She drinks alcohol heavily and eats few vegetables. Fasting plasma homocysteine is 24 µmol/L (normal 5 to 15 µmol/L), mean corpuscular volume (MCV) is 106 fL (normal 80 to 100 fL), and serum methylmalonic acid is within the reference range. Deficiency of which of the following vitamins most likely explains her elevated homocysteine concentration?","options":["Cobalamin (vitamin B12)","Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","Folate (vitamin B9)","Thiamine (vitamin B1)"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that homocysteine is remethylated to methionine by methionine synthase, which needs N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate and vitamin B12.","Use methylmalonic acid to separate the two: it rises in B12 deficiency and stays normal in folate deficiency.","Combine with the large red cells and poor diet with heavy drinking: folate deficiency."],"why":"Methionine synthase moves a methyl group from N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate, through methylcobalamin, to homocysteine to make methionine. A lack of folate or B12 therefore raises homocysteine and causes a macrocytic anemia. Only B12 deficiency also raises methylmalonic acid, because B12 is a cofactor for methylmalonyl-CoA mutase. High homocysteine injures endothelium and promotes thrombosis, and it is a recognized risk factor for atherosclerosis.","whyNot":["B12 deficiency would raise both homocysteine and methylmalonic acid. Her methylmalonic acid is normal.","B6 deficiency can raise homocysteine through cystathionine beta-synthase, but it causes a microcytic (sideroblastic) anemia, not a high MCV.","Correct.","Thiamine is a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase and transketolase and does not affect homocysteine metabolism."],"study":{"topics":["Homocysteine metabolism","Folate versus B12 deficiency"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C48-Q4","stem":"Which of the following serious outcomes is she most likely to have over the next 5 years?","options":["Pulmonary embolism","Major amputation of the right leg","Rupture of an abdominal aortic aneurysm","Myocardial infarction or stroke"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I6","obj":"CV-18-I.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize that PAD is a marker of widespread atherosclerosis.","Recall that most patients with claudication stay stable in the leg, and few need amputation.","Conclude that coronary and cerebrovascular events are the most common serious outcomes and the leading cause of death."],"why":"Peripheral arterial disease reflects atherosclerosis throughout the arterial tree, so patients with it usually also have coronary and carotid disease. In people with claudication, myocardial infarction and stroke are far more common than limb loss and are the main causes of death. Only a small minority progress to critical limb ischemia or major amputation over five years. This is why PAD is treated as a coronary risk equivalent.","whyNot":["Pulmonary embolism arises from venous thrombosis and is not a typical consequence of arterial atherosclerosis.","Major amputation occurs in only a small minority of patients with claudication over five years, and the risk is lower in those who stop smoking.","PAD raises the chance of having an AAA, but rupture is much less common than myocardial infarction or stroke in this group.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Prognosis of peripheral arterial disease","Atherosclerosis as a systemic disease"],"comps":["CV-18-I6","CV-18-C"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C49","system":"CV","dx":"Giant cell (temporal) arteritis with large-vessel involvement","codes":["CV-18-I5"],"case":"A 74-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 3-week history of new right-sided headache, jaw claudication, and scalp tenderness. Twice this week she has had transient blurred vision in the right eye. She also has aching and morning stiffness of both shoulders. Physical examination shows a thickened, tender right superficial temporal artery with a diminished pulse. Blood pressure is 142/80 mm Hg in the right arm and 118/74 mm Hg in the left arm. Laboratory studies show an erythrocyte sedimentation rate of 88 mm/h (less than 42 for her age and sex) and a C-reactive protein concentration of 64 mg/L (less than 10). Temporal artery biopsy shows granulomatous inflammation of the media with multinucleated giant cells and fragmentation of the internal elastic lamina. Diagnosis: giant cell (temporal) arteritis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C49-Q1","stem":"Permanent vision loss in this disease most often results from occlusion of the posterior ciliary arteries, which supply the optic nerve head. These arteries are branches of an artery that arises from which of the following vessels?","options":["Superficial temporal artery","Basilar artery","External carotid artery","Internal carotid artery"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Key clue: one-eye vision loss from ischemia of the optic nerve head.","The posterior ciliary arteries are branches of the ophthalmic artery.","The ophthalmic artery is a branch of the internal carotid artery, so the eye is fed by a different carotid system than the scalp and jaw."],"why":"Arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy is the usual cause of blindness in giant cell arteritis. It results from inflammatory occlusion of the posterior ciliary arteries, which come off the ophthalmic artery. The ophthalmic artery is the first major branch of the internal carotid artery after it leaves the cavernous sinus. The biopsied superficial temporal artery belongs to the external carotid system, so this disease can threaten both carotid territories at once.","whyNot":["The superficial temporal artery is the inflamed artery that was biopsied. It is a terminal branch of the external carotid and does not give rise to the vessels that feed the eye.","The basilar artery supplies the brainstem and, through the posterior cerebral arteries, the occipital cortex. Vision loss from that system affects the visual fields of both eyes, while her symptoms are in one eye.","The external carotid artery supplies the scalp, face, and chewing muscles through branches such as the superficial temporal and maxillary arteries. It explains her scalp tenderness and jaw claudication, while the optic nerve head is supplied from the internal carotid system.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Ophthalmic artery and its branches","Arteritic ischemic optic neuropathy"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I5"]}},{"id":"CV-C49-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains her increased erythrocyte sedimentation rate?","options":["Hemoglobin polymerizes and makes the red cells rigid and sickle shaped","Red cells gain water and swell into spheres that resist stacking","Fibrinogen masks red cell surface charge, letting cells form rouleaux","Albumin binds red cells and adds to their mutual surface repulsion"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-I5","obj":"CV-18-I.12","dok":2,"approach":["Key clue: ESR rises in inflammation, so the change must come from acute-phase proteins.","IL-6 drives the liver to make fibrinogen, the main plasma protein that speeds sedimentation.","Fibrinogen lowers the negative-charge repulsion between red cells, so they stack and fall faster."],"why":"IL-6 from the inflamed arteries signals hepatocytes to make acute-phase proteins, chiefly fibrinogen. Red cells normally carry a negative surface charge (the zeta potential) that keeps them apart. Large, asymmetric plasma proteins such as fibrinogen reduce this repulsion, so red cells stack into rouleaux. A stack has more mass for its surface area than a single cell, so it falls faster and the ESR rises.","whyNot":["Sickled cells are rigid and cannot form rouleaux, so sickle cell anemia tends to give a low ESR. Polymerization of hemoglobin S also has no link to inflammation.","Spherocytes stack poorly, so swollen spherical cells lower the ESR, as in hereditary spherocytosis.","Correct.","Albumin opposes rouleaux formation, so a high albumin tends to slow the ESR and a low albumin speeds it. It cannot explain a high ESR in active inflammation."],"study":{"topics":["Erythrocyte sedimentation rate","Acute-phase proteins and IL-6"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C49-Q3","stem":"She also reports aching and fatigue of the left arm when she carries groceries. Imaging shows narrowing of the artery that continues as the left axillary artery. This narrowed artery arises from which of the following?","options":["Brachiocephalic trunk","Arch of the aorta","Left common carotid artery","Thyrocervical trunk"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","obj":"CV-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Key clue: lower left arm pressure plus arm claudication means a stenosis upstream of the left axillary artery.","The artery that becomes the axillary artery is the subclavian artery.","The left subclavian is the third branch of the aortic arch; the right subclavian comes from the brachiocephalic trunk."],"why":"The subclavian artery becomes the axillary artery at the outer border of the first rib. On the left, the subclavian artery arises directly from the arch of the aorta as its third branch, after the brachiocephalic trunk and the left common carotid artery. Giant cell arteritis involves the aorta and its main branches in a sizable share of patients, and subclavian narrowing gives arm claudication and unequal arm pressures, as in this woman.","whyNot":["The brachiocephalic trunk gives rise to the right subclavian and right common carotid arteries. It would be the answer for a narrowing that lowered pressure in the right arm.","Correct.","The left common carotid artery is the second arch branch and supplies the head and neck. It does not give rise to the artery of the arm.","The thyrocervical trunk is a branch of the subclavian artery, so it lies downstream of the narrowed vessel."],"study":{"topics":["Branches of the aortic arch","Large-vessel giant cell arteritis"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-I5"]}},{"id":"CV-C49-Q4","stem":"In this disease, adventitial dendritic cells present an unidentified antigen to CD4+ T lymphocytes, which then drive inflammation in the media. Which of the following T-lymphocyte responses is mainly responsible for the granulomatous inflammation in her artery wall?","options":["CD8 T cells releasing perforin and granzymes that kill endothelial cells","Th2 cells releasing IL-4 and IL-5 that recruit eosinophils","Th1 cells releasing interferon gamma that activates macrophages","Regulatory T cells releasing IL-10 that quiets macrophages"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.3","dok":3,"approach":["Key clue: the biopsy shows granulomas with multinucleated giant cells and no mention of eosinophils or immune complexes.","Granulomas are a delayed (type IV) hypersensitivity pattern driven by CD4 T cells.","Th1 cells release interferon gamma, which activates macrophages that gather into granulomas and fuse into giant cells."],"why":"Giant cell arteritis is a T cell-mediated (type IV) hypersensitivity reaction in the artery wall. Activated dendritic cells in the adventitia prime CD4 T cells, which polarize mainly to Th1 and Th17 cells. Interferon gamma from Th1 cells activates macrophages, which form granulomas, fuse into multinucleated giant cells, and release enzymes and growth factors. These damage the elastic laminae and cause intimal thickening that narrows the lumen and causes ischemia, as in her jaw and eye.","whyNot":["CD8 T cells kill cells that display foreign peptide on MHC class I, as in viral infection and transplant rejection. Cytotoxic killing does not build granulomas.","Th2 cells and eosinophils drive allergic inflammation and the eosinophil-rich vasculitis of eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis, which occurs with asthma. Her biopsy does not describe eosinophils.","Correct.","Regulatory T cells and IL-10 suppress inflammation. Lack of them can allow autoimmunity, but they do not produce granulomas."],"study":{"topics":["Type IV hypersensitivity","Th1 cells and granuloma formation"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-I5"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C50","system":"CV","dx":"Secondary Raynaud phenomenon caused by systemic sclerosis","codes":["CV-18-I8"],"case":"A 46-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 10-year history of episodes in which her fingers turn white, then blue, then red on exposure to cold or emotional stress. Over the past 3 years, the skin of her fingers and face has become thick and tight. She also has heartburn and a sensation of solid food sticking behind the sternum. Physical examination shows tight, shiny skin over the fingers distal to the metacarpophalangeal joints, with normal skin over the upper arms and trunk. There is a healing pitted ulcer on the right index fingertip, firm subcutaneous calcium deposits in two finger pads, and telangiectasias on the face and lips. Nailfold capillaroscopy shows enlarged capillary loops with areas of capillary dropout. Antinuclear antibody assay is positive. Diagnosis: secondary Raynaud phenomenon caused by systemic sclerosis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C50-Q1","stem":"During an attack, her fingers turn red as they rewarm after the blue phase. Which of the following best explains the red phase?","options":["Reactive hyperemia from local vasodilator metabolites","Polycythemia that raises the hemoglobin content of skin capillaries","Increased sympathetic alpha-adrenergic tone in the digital arteries","Rising venous pressure that traps deoxygenated blood in the skin"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.6","dok":2,"approach":["Match each color to blood flow: white is no flow, blue is slow flow with deoxygenated blood, red is flow returning.","During ischemia, metabolites build up in the tissue.","When the spasm ends, these metabolites dilate arterioles and flow overshoots: reactive hyperemia."],"why":"The white phase is vasospasm of the digital arteries that stops flow. The blue phase follows as a small amount of slowly moving blood gives up its oxygen. While flow is low, vasodilator metabolites such as adenosine, carbon dioxide, hydrogen ions, and potassium build up in the tissue. When the spasm relaxes, these metabolites hold the arterioles open, and flow rises above its resting level. This overshoot, called reactive hyperemia, makes the fingers red and often throbbing.","whyNot":["Correct.","Polycythemia can make skin look ruddy all the time, but it would not appear in a pattern that follows each cold attack. Her color change comes from a change in flow.","Sympathetic alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction triggered by cold or stress causes the white phase, not the red one.","Slow flow with deoxygenated blood in the skin explains the blue phase. The red phase means fresh arterial blood is flowing in again."],"study":{"topics":["Raynaud color sequence","Reactive hyperemia and local metabolic control"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-I8"]}},{"id":"CV-C50-Q2","stem":"Which of the following autoantibodies is most likely to be found in her serum?","options":["Anti-double-stranded DNA antibody","Anti-Jo-1 antibody","Anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl-70) antibody","Anticentromere antibody"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-I8","obj":"CV-18-I.10","dok":3,"approach":["Key clue: skin thickening only on the fingers and face, with calcinosis, Raynaud phenomenon, esophageal dysmotility, and telangiectasias.","This pattern is the limited cutaneous form of systemic sclerosis (CREST).","Limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis is most strongly linked to anticentromere antibody."],"why":"Her skin changes stop at the fingers and face, with calcinosis, Raynaud phenomenon, esophageal dysmotility, sclerodactyly, and telangiectasias. These five features make up CREST syndrome, the limited cutaneous form of systemic sclerosis. Raynaud phenomenon often comes years before skin changes in this form, as it did for her. Anticentromere antibody is the autoantibody most closely tied to this form, which carries a later risk of pulmonary arterial hypertension.","whyNot":["Anti-double-stranded DNA is specific for systemic lupus erythematosus and tracks with lupus nephritis. It is not typical of systemic sclerosis.","Anti-Jo-1 is a marker of inflammatory myositis (polymyositis or dermatomyositis), often with interstitial lung disease. She has no muscle weakness.","Anti-Scl-70 is linked to diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis, in which skin thickening spreads to the upper arms and trunk and interstitial lung disease is common. Her trunk and upper arms are spared.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Limited vs diffuse systemic sclerosis","Autoantibodies in connective tissue disease"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-I8"]}},{"id":"CV-C50-Q3","stem":"The fibrosis in her skin and esophagus is driven largely by transforming growth factor beta (TGF-beta), which stimulates fibroblasts to deposit excess collagen. Which of the following best describes how the TGF-beta receptor transmits this signal into the cell?","options":["Serine/threonine phosphorylation of SMAD proteins","Tyrosine autophosphorylation that recruits RAS","Activation of Gs, which raises cyclic AMP","Associated JAK kinases that phosphorylate STAT proteins"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the mediator: TGF-beta is the main profibrotic cytokine in systemic sclerosis.","Recall its receptor class: TGF-beta receptors are serine/threonine kinases.","The activated receptor phosphorylates SMAD proteins, which carry the signal to the nucleus."],"why":"TGF-beta binds the type II receptor, a serine/threonine kinase, which then phosphorylates and activates the type I receptor. The type I receptor phosphorylates SMAD2 and SMAD3. These join SMAD4, move into the nucleus, and switch on genes for collagen and other matrix proteins. In systemic sclerosis, persistent TGF-beta signaling keeps fibroblasts producing matrix, which thickens the skin and stiffens the esophageal wall.","whyNot":["Correct.","Tyrosine autophosphorylation and RAS recruitment describe receptor tyrosine kinases such as the receptors for insulin, EGF, and PDGF.","Gs coupling and cyclic AMP are used by receptors such as the beta-adrenergic receptors and the glucagon receptor, not by TGF-beta receptors.","JAK-STAT signaling is used by many cytokine receptors, such as those for IL-6, interferons, and erythropoietin. TGF-beta uses SMAD proteins instead."],"study":{"topics":["TGF-beta and SMAD signaling","Receptor classes and second messengers"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I8"]}},{"id":"CV-C50-Q4","stem":"Fingertip ulceration is rare in primary Raynaud phenomenon. Which of the following changes in her digital arteries best explains why her attacks cause tissue loss?","options":["Immune complex leukocytoclastic vasculitis of postcapillary venules","Intimal proliferation and fibrosis that keep the lumen narrow","Cholesterol-rich atheroma with a thin fibrous cap","Cold-reacting IgM antibodies that agglutinate red cells in the fingers"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I8","obj":"CV-18-I.10","dok":3,"approach":["Compare primary and secondary Raynaud: primary is pure vasospasm in a normal vessel; secondary adds structural vessel disease.","In systemic sclerosis, endothelial injury leads to intimal proliferation and fibrosis of small arteries, and capillaries are lost (seen on nailfold capillaroscopy).","A vessel that is already narrow closes completely during spasm, so ischemia is deeper and lasts longer, causing ulcers."],"why":"In primary Raynaud phenomenon the digital arteries are structurally normal, so blood flow returns fully after each spasm and ulcers are rare. In systemic sclerosis, endothelial injury triggers intimal proliferation and fibrosis of small arteries, and capillaries drop out, as her nailfold capillaroscopy shows. The lumen stays narrow between attacks, so each spasm shuts the vessel completely and ischemia lasts longer. The result is pitting scars, ulcers, and sometimes gangrene of the fingertips.","whyNot":["Leukocytoclastic vasculitis from immune complexes affects postcapillary venules and gives palpable purpura, as in IgA vasculitis or cryoglobulinemia. It does not explain cold-triggered digital ischemia.","Correct.","Atheroma forms mainly in large and medium arteries, not in small digital arteries, and it is not the lesion of systemic sclerosis.","Cold agglutinins cause acrocyanosis and hemolysis in cold exposure. They are not part of systemic sclerosis, and her antibody findings point to an autoimmune connective tissue disease."],"study":{"topics":["Primary vs secondary Raynaud phenomenon","Vasculopathy of systemic sclerosis"],"comps":["CV-18-I8","CV-16"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C51","system":"CV","dx":"Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger disease)","codes":["CV-18-I9"],"case":"A 36-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of cramping pain in the arch of his right foot when he walks. For the past 2 weeks he has had pain in his right toes at rest that is worse at night. He has smoked about 30 cigarettes daily since the age of 15 years. Last year he had tender, erythematous cords along superficial veins of both forearms that resolved spontaneously. He does not have diabetes mellitus. Physical examination shows a small dry ulcer at the tip of the right great toe. Femoral and popliteal pulses are normal; the right dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are absent. Serum LDL cholesterol concentration is 96 mg/dL (less than 100). Angiography shows segmental occlusions of the tibial arteries with corkscrew-shaped collateral vessels. Diagnosis: thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger disease).","questions":[{"id":"CV-C51-Q1","stem":"A biopsy specimen of an involved segment of his tibial artery obtained during the acute phase is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Transmural fibrinoid necrosis with lesions at different stages of healing","Lipid-rich atheroma with cholesterol clefts beneath a fibrous cap","Inflamed thrombus with microabscesses that spreads to the nearby vein and nerve","Granulomas with multinucleated giant cells centered on the internal elastic lamina"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I9","obj":"CV-18-I.11","dok":2,"approach":["Key clues: young heavy smoker, distal small and medium arteries, migratory superficial thrombophlebitis, and normal lipids.","Buerger disease is a segmental inflammation of arteries, veins, and nerves of the limbs.","Its acute lesion is a cellular, inflamed thrombus with microabscesses, with inflammation that extends to the neighboring vein and nerve."],"why":"Thromboangiitis obliterans is a segmental, thrombosing inflammation of small and medium arteries of the hands and feet that nearly always occurs in heavy tobacco users. In the acute phase, the lumen holds a highly cellular thrombus with small microabscesses, sometimes with giant cells, and the inflammation spreads to the adjacent vein and nerve. The internal elastic lamina is usually preserved. Later the thrombus organizes and the artery, vein, and nerve are bound together by fibrosis. Nerve involvement helps explain his severe rest pain.","whyNot":["Transmural fibrinoid necrosis with lesions of different ages is the pattern of polyarteritis nodosa. That disease does not involve veins and is not tied to smoking.","Atheroma is the lesion of atherosclerotic peripheral arterial disease, usually in older patients with risk factors such as diabetes and high LDL. His age, normal lipids, and phlebitis point elsewhere.","Correct.","Granulomas with giant cells centered on a fragmented internal elastic lamina describe giant cell arteritis, which affects older adults and the carotid branches and aorta."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of thromboangiitis obliterans","Comparing vasculitis lesions"],"comps":["CV-18-I9","CV-18-I12"]}},{"id":"CV-C51-Q2","stem":"Angiography shows that the longest occlusion is in the artery that descends in the anterior compartment of the leg with the deep fibular nerve. Flow in which of the following arteries is most directly reduced as a result?","options":["Dorsalis pedis artery","Medial plantar artery","Posterior tibial artery","Fibular artery"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the occluded vessel: the artery of the anterior compartment, running with the deep fibular nerve, is the anterior tibial artery.","Follow it distally: it crosses the front of the ankle and continues on the dorsum of the foot.","Its continuation is the dorsalis pedis artery."],"why":"The popliteal artery divides into the anterior tibial artery and the tibioperoneal trunk. The anterior tibial artery passes into the anterior compartment, runs down with the deep fibular nerve, and crosses the front of the ankle midway between the malleoli. On the dorsum of the foot it continues as the dorsalis pedis artery, whose pulse is felt just lateral to the extensor hallucis longus tendon. An occlusion of the anterior tibial artery therefore removes direct flow to the dorsalis pedis.","whyNot":["Correct.","The medial plantar artery is a terminal branch of the posterior tibial artery in the sole, so it lies in a different arterial line.","The posterior tibial artery is a branch of the tibioperoneal trunk in the posterior compartment. It runs with the tibial nerve and is felt behind the medial malleolus.","The fibular artery also comes from the tibioperoneal trunk and runs in the posterior compartment near the fibula. It is not downstream of the anterior tibial artery."],"study":{"topics":["Arteries of the leg and foot","Peripheral pulse sites"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I9"]}},{"id":"CV-C51-Q3","stem":"One segment of a tibial artery has its radius reduced by half by thrombus and wall inflammation. If the pressure difference across that segment and blood viscosity remain constant, which of the following best approximates flow through the segment compared with baseline?","options":["About 25 percent of baseline","About 50 percent of baseline","About 12 percent of baseline","About 6 percent of baseline"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.1","dok":3,"approach":["Name the law: Poiseuille's law says resistance is inversely proportional to the radius to the fourth power.","Halving the radius gives (1/2) to the fourth power, which is 1/16.","Resistance rises 16-fold, so flow at the same pressure difference falls to 1/16, about 6 percent."],"why":"By Poiseuille's law, flow equals the pressure difference times pi times the radius to the fourth power, divided by 8 times viscosity times length. Because radius enters to the fourth power, halving it raises resistance 16-fold. With the same pressure difference, flow falls to 1/16 of baseline, which is about 6 percent. This steep relationship is why modest narrowing of small distal arteries can leave toes without enough blood at rest.","whyNot":["About 25 percent (1/4) would follow if flow depended on cross-sectional area (radius squared). That underestimates the effect of narrowing on resistance.","About 50 percent would follow if flow changed in direct proportion to radius. Resistance depends far more steeply on radius than that.","About 12 percent (1/8) would follow if flow depended on the radius cubed. Poiseuille's law uses the fourth power.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Poiseuille's law","Resistance and vessel radius"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-I6"]}},{"id":"CV-C51-Q4","stem":"His carboxyhemoglobin concentration is 8 percent (less than 2 percent in nonsmokers). Which of the following best describes how carbon monoxide further reduces oxygen delivery to his ischemic toes?","options":["It takes up heme sites and shifts the curve left, so less oxygen unloads","It oxidizes heme iron to the ferric form, producing methemoglobin","It shifts the oxyhemoglobin curve to the right, so oxygen unloads too early","It lowers the dissolved oxygen in plasma, reducing the arterial PO2"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-I9","obj":"CV-18-I.11","dok":3,"approach":["Key fact: carbon monoxide binds the iron of heme with an affinity about 200 times or more that of oxygen.","Bound CO lowers oxygen content by taking heme sites.","It also makes the remaining heme groups hold oxygen more tightly (a left shift), so less oxygen is released in tissues."],"why":"Carbon monoxide binds ferrous heme far more tightly than oxygen does, so each CO molecule takes a site that oxygen could have used, lowering oxygen content. CO also changes the shape of the hemoglobin molecule so the remaining heme groups hold their oxygen more tightly, shifting the dissociation curve to the left. Tissues with already poor flow, such as his toes, then get even less oxygen. The arterial PO2 stays normal, because it reflects only dissolved oxygen.","whyNot":["Correct.","Oxidizing heme iron to the ferric (Fe3+) form produces methemoglobin, as with nitrites or dapsone. Carbon monoxide binds ferrous (Fe2+) heme without oxidizing it.","A right shift helps oxygen unload in tissues. Carbon monoxide shifts the curve in the opposite direction.","The arterial PO2 depends on alveolar oxygen and lung function, which carbon monoxide does not change. Oxygen content falls because hemoglobin sites are occupied."],"study":{"topics":["Carbon monoxide and hemoglobin","Oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I9"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C52","system":"CV","dx":"Polyarteritis nodosa associated with hepatitis B","codes":["CV-18-I12"],"case":"A 52-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of fever, myalgias, and a 6-kg weight loss. For 3 weeks he has had crampy abdominal pain that begins about 30 minutes after meals. One week ago he developed numbness of the left ring and little fingers, and yesterday he awoke with a right foot drop. Blood pressure is 168/98 mm Hg; 1 year ago it was 124/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows tender erythematous nodules and livedo reticularis over both legs. Serum creatinine concentration is 1.9 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3). Urinalysis shows mild proteinuria with no red blood cell casts. Hepatitis B surface antigen is positive, and ANCA is negative. A chest x-ray shows no abnormalities. Angiography shows multiple small aneurysms and segmental narrowing of the renal and mesenteric arteries. Diagnosis: polyarteritis nodosa associated with hepatitis B.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C52-Q1","stem":"His arterial injury is attributed to a viral protein circulating in his blood. Which of the following immune mechanisms best explains the damage to his arteries?","options":["Sensitized T cells form granulomas around viral antigen in the arterial media","Antigen-antibody complexes deposit in vessel walls and activate complement","Antibodies bind antigens fixed on the surface of normal endothelial cells","IgE bound to mast cells triggers histamine release within the vessel wall"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.3","dok":2,"approach":["Key clue: hepatitis B surface antigen circulates in large amounts in his blood.","Soluble antigen plus antibody forms circulating immune complexes.","Complexes that lodge in vessel walls fix complement and recruit neutrophils: type III hypersensitivity."],"why":"In hepatitis B-associated polyarteritis nodosa, circulating hepatitis B surface antigen combines with antibody to form immune complexes. These deposit in the walls of medium-sized arteries, activate complement, and draw in neutrophils, whose enzymes and oxidants cause fibrinoid necrosis. This is type III (immune complex) hypersensitivity. The weakened wall then forms the small aneurysms and narrowings seen on his angiogram.","whyNot":["T cell granulomas are type IV hypersensitivity, the mechanism of giant cell arteritis. Polyarteritis nodosa shows neutrophils and fibrinoid necrosis without granulomas.","Correct.","Antibody against an antigen fixed on cells or matrix is type II hypersensitivity, as in anti-glomerular basement membrane disease. Here the antigen circulates in the blood.","IgE on mast cells is type I hypersensitivity, which causes allergy and anaphylaxis. It does not cause necrotizing arteritis."],"study":{"topics":["Type III hypersensitivity","Hepatitis B and vasculitis"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-I12"]}},{"id":"CV-C52-Q2","stem":"Three years ago, after donating blood, he was told that he tested positive for hepatitis B surface antigen. Today, tests for IgM antibody to hepatitis B core antigen and for antibody to hepatitis B surface antigen are negative. Which of the following best describes the status of his hepatitis B infection?","options":["Acute infection in its early phase","Past infection with lasting immunity","Chronic infection","Window period of a resolving infection"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-I12","obj":"CV-18-I.14","dok":3,"approach":["Key clue: surface antigen has been present for years, far longer than 6 months.","IgM anti-core is a marker of recent infection, and it is negative.","Persistent surface antigen without anti-surface antibody means chronic infection."],"why":"Hepatitis B surface antigen that persists for more than 6 months defines chronic infection. IgM antibody to core antigen appears in acute infection and fades over months, so a negative result fits long-standing infection. Antibody to surface antigen appears when the infection clears or after vaccination, and he has none. Ongoing antigen production in chronic infection supplies the antigen for the immune complexes in his arteries.","whyNot":["Acute infection would show IgM antibody to core antigen, and surface antigen would not have been present 3 years ago.","Past infection with immunity shows antibody to surface antigen and IgG anti-core, with no surface antigen in the blood.","Correct.","In the window period surface antigen has already disappeared and antibody to surface antigen has not yet appeared, with IgM anti-core as the main marker. His surface antigen is positive."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatitis B serology","Acute vs chronic hepatitis B"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-I12"]}},{"id":"CV-C52-Q3","stem":"His postprandial abdominal pain is caused by narrowing of the artery that supplies the jejunum and ileum. This artery arises from which of the following locations?","options":["Anterior aorta at about the T12 vertebra","Anterior aorta at about the L1 vertebra","Lateral aorta at about the L1 to L2 vertebrae","Anterior aorta at about the L3 vertebra"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the artery: the jejunum and ileum are midgut, supplied by the superior mesenteric artery.","Recall the unpaired anterior branches of the abdominal aorta and their levels: celiac trunk T12, superior mesenteric L1, inferior mesenteric L3.","The superior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at about L1."],"why":"The jejunum and ileum are midgut structures supplied by the superior mesenteric artery. It arises from the anterior surface of the abdominal aorta at about the L1 vertebral level, just below the celiac trunk, and passes behind the neck of the pancreas. Narrowing of its branches limits the increase in intestinal blood flow that normally follows a meal, which causes postprandial pain and food avoidance with weight loss, a pattern called intestinal angina.","whyNot":["About T12 is the level of the celiac trunk, which supplies the foregut (stomach, liver, spleen, and proximal duodenum).","Correct.","Paired lateral branches at about L1 to L2 are the renal arteries. They are also often involved in polyarteritis nodosa, but they do not supply the small bowel.","About L3 is the level of the inferior mesenteric artery, which supplies the hindgut from the distal transverse colon to the upper rectum."],"study":{"topics":["Branches of the abdominal aorta","Midgut blood supply"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I12"]}},{"id":"CV-C52-Q4","stem":"His blood pressure has increased as aneurysms and stenoses have developed in his renal arteries. Which of the following is the initiating event in this increase in blood pressure?","options":["Macula densa cells sense a rise in tubular sodium chloride and trigger renin release","Atrial myocytes release natriuretic peptide in response to a larger blood volume","Carotid baroreceptors increase their firing and withdraw sympathetic tone","Juxtaglomerular cells sense a lower afferent arteriolar pressure and release renin"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-A2","obj":"CV-18-A.2","dok":3,"approach":["Key clue: narrowed renal arteries lower the pressure reaching the glomeruli.","Juxtaglomerular cells in the afferent arteriole act as pressure sensors and release renin when pressure falls.","Renin starts the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, which raises vascular resistance and sodium retention."],"why":"Narrowing of renal arteries lowers the pressure in the afferent arterioles downstream. The juxtaglomerular cells in the afferent arteriole wall sense this lower stretch and release renin. Renin converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, which angiotensin-converting enzyme turns into angiotensin II. Angiotensin II constricts arterioles and stimulates aldosterone release, so sodium retention and vasoconstriction raise systemic blood pressure. This renovascular hypertension is common in polyarteritis nodosa.","whyNot":["A fall in sodium chloride delivery to the macula densa stimulates renin release. A rise in sodium chloride delivery suppresses renin.","Atrial natriuretic peptide promotes sodium excretion and vasodilation, so it lowers blood pressure.","Increased baroreceptor firing withdraws sympathetic tone, which lowers blood pressure. It is a response to high pressure, not its cause.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","Renovascular hypertension"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-A2","CV-18-I12"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C53","system":"CV","dx":"Kawasaki disease with coronary artery dilation","codes":["CV-18-I12"],"case":"A 3-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of fever for 6 days and marked irritability. His temperature is 39.8 °C (103.6 °F). Physical examination shows bilateral nonexudative conjunctival injection with limbal sparing, red cracked lips, and a strawberry tongue. There is a polymorphous erythematous rash over the trunk, erythema and edema of the hands and feet, and a single firm 2-cm left cervical lymph node. Laboratory studies show a C-reactive protein concentration of 110 mg/L (less than 10) and a leukocyte count of 19,600/µL (about 6,000 to 17,000 at his age). A rapid streptococcal antigen test and throat culture are negative. Echocardiography shows dilation of the proximal left anterior descending coronary artery. Diagnosis: Kawasaki disease.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C53-Q1","stem":"Kawasaki disease targets medium-sized muscular arteries such as the coronary arteries. Histologic examination of a normal segment of his left anterior descending artery is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Endothelium with fenestrations and a basal lamina, with no media at all","A media built mostly of many fenestrated elastic lamellae","A smooth muscle media bounded by a distinct internal elastic lamina","A thin media, wide lumen, and valves formed by folds of intima"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","obj":"CV-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Classify the vessel: coronary arteries are medium-sized muscular (distributing) arteries.","Recall the muscular artery wall: a thick media of smooth muscle between a prominent internal elastic lamina and an external elastic lamina.","Rule out the elastic artery, vein, and capillary patterns."],"why":"Muscular arteries, such as the coronary, renal, and mesenteric arteries, have a media made mostly of layered smooth muscle. A distinct, wavy internal elastic lamina separates the intima from the media, and an external elastic lamina often marks the outer edge of the media. In Kawasaki disease, inflammation damages this smooth muscle and elastic tissue, and the weakened segment balloons into an aneurysm.","whyNot":["Endothelium with pores (fenestrations) on a basal lamina describes a fenestrated capillary, found in places such as the kidney and endocrine glands.","Many fenestrated elastic lamellae in the media mark an elastic (conducting) artery such as the aorta or pulmonary trunk. Kawasaki disease usually spares these.","Correct.","A thin media, a wide lumen, and valves describe a medium-sized vein, especially in the limbs."],"study":{"topics":["Muscular vs elastic artery histology","Vessel wall layers"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-8"]}},{"id":"CV-C53-Q2","stem":"Scarlet fever was considered in the differential diagnosis because it can also cause a red tongue, rash, and later desquamation. The rash of scarlet fever is caused by a streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin that acts as a superantigen. Which of the following best describes how a superantigen activates T lymphocytes?","options":["It cross-links IgE on mast cells and basophils, releasing histamine","It links MHC class II to the T cell receptor beta chain outside the groove","It is processed and presented in the MHC class II groove like a normal peptide","It binds CD14 and TLR4 on macrophages in the same way as endotoxin"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the toxin: streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin A of Streptococcus pyogenes is a superantigen.","A superantigen skips antigen processing and binds the outside of MHC class II and the variable region of the T cell receptor beta chain.","Because many T cells share beta chain variable regions, a huge number are activated at once, releasing large amounts of cytokines."],"why":"A normal peptide antigen is processed, loaded into the groove of an MHC molecule, and recognized only by the rare T cell clones whose receptors fit it. A superantigen is not processed. It binds intact to the outer surface of MHC class II on antigen-presenting cells and to the variable region of the T cell receptor beta chain. This links any T cell with a matching beta chain, activating a large fraction of all T cells. The surge of cytokines such as IL-2, interferon gamma, and TNF causes fever, rash, and, with toxic shock toxins, shock.","whyNot":["Cross-linking IgE on mast cells and basophils is type I hypersensitivity, triggered by allergens, not streptococcal toxins.","Correct.","Processing and presentation in the MHC groove is how conventional peptide antigens work. Only a tiny fraction of T cells respond to any one peptide.","CD14 and TLR4 recognize lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin) from gram-negative bacteria and activate innate immunity. Streptococci are gram-positive and lack lipopolysaccharide."],"study":{"topics":["Superantigens","Streptococcus pyogenes exotoxins"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C53-Q3","stem":"Over the next 2 weeks, the dilated segment enlarges into an aneurysm with an internal diameter 3 times that of the normal segment just upstream. Total blood flow through the artery is unchanged. Which of the following best describes the mean blood velocity in the aneurysm compared with the velocity upstream?","options":["About the same as upstream","About one third as fast","About three times as fast","About one ninth as fast"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.3","dok":3,"approach":["Apply the continuity principle: flow equals velocity times cross-sectional area, and flow is the same in both segments.","Area depends on the diameter squared, so tripling the diameter makes the area 9 times larger.","Velocity falls to 1/9, and slow flow in the aneurysm favors thrombus formation."],"why":"When the same volume of blood per minute passes through segments in series, flow equals velocity times cross-sectional area in each segment. Cross-sectional area depends on the radius (or diameter) squared, so a 3-fold wider lumen has 9 times the area. Average velocity there drops to about one ninth of the upstream value. Slow, swirling flow in the aneurysm, together with injured endothelium and the high platelet counts of later Kawasaki disease, promotes thrombus that can block the artery and cause myocardial infarction.","whyNot":["Velocity stays the same only if the cross-sectional area is unchanged. Here the area is much larger.","One third as fast would be true if velocity depended on diameter directly. Area, and so velocity, depends on diameter squared.","Velocity rises, not falls, where a vessel narrows. Three times faster would fit a segment whose area was one third of normal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Continuity of flow and velocity","Thrombosis in coronary aneurysms"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H6"]}},{"id":"CV-C53-Q4","stem":"In the third week of illness, the skin of his fingertips and toes begins to desquamate. Which of the following laboratory findings is most likely at this stage?","options":["Positive antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody (ANCA)","Rising antistreptolysin O titer","Platelet count well above the reference range","Low complement C3 and C4 levels"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I12","obj":"CV-18-I.15","dok":2,"approach":["Place him on the timeline: peeling of the fingers and toes marks the subacute phase, about weeks 2 to 4.","Recall the hallmark laboratory change of this phase: reactive thrombocytosis.","Rule out markers of other diseases: ANCA vasculitis, streptococcal infection, and immune complex disease."],"why":"Kawasaki disease runs in phases. In the acute febrile phase, platelet counts are usually normal. In the subacute phase, about weeks 2 to 4, the fingers and toes peel and the platelet count rises, often to 500,000 to more than 1,000,000/µL (reference about 150,000 to 450,000). Platelet counts then fall back toward normal during convalescence. The high platelet count adds to the risk of thrombosis inside coronary aneurysms in this phase.","whyNot":["ANCA is a marker of small-vessel vasculitides such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis and microscopic polyangiitis. It is negative in Kawasaki disease.","A rising antistreptolysin O titer points to a recent Streptococcus pyogenes infection, as in rheumatic fever. His streptococcal tests were negative.","Correct.","Low C3 and C4 suggest complement consumption by immune complexes, as in lupus or cryoglobulinemia. Complement is not low in Kawasaki disease."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of Kawasaki disease","Laboratory findings in vasculitis"],"comps":["CV-18-I12","CV-18-H6"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C54","system":"CV","dx":"Multifocal infantile hemangiomas with liver involvement","codes":["CV-18-J1"],"case":"A 10-week-old girl is brought to the physician for a well-child examination. She was born at 31 weeks' gestation. Two weeks after birth, her parents noticed small red marks on her scalp, back, and right thigh, which have since grown rapidly. She is feeding well and gaining weight. Physical examination shows seven bright red, raised, lobulated lesions 0.5 to 2 cm in diameter that partially blanch with pressure. Because she has five or more cutaneous lesions, abdominal ultrasonography is performed and shows several small, well-defined, highly vascular lesions scattered throughout the liver. Diagnosis: multifocal infantile hemangiomas of the skin with liver involvement.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C54-Q1","stem":"The skin lesions consist of tightly packed, capillary-sized channels lined by plump endothelial cells. In a normal continuous capillary, which of the following cells surrounds the endothelium and lies within the same basal lamina?","options":["Myoepithelial cell","Fibroblast","Smooth muscle cell","Pericyte"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","obj":"CV-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Key clue: the channels are capillary-sized, so compare them with the normal capillary wall.","A capillary wall has endothelium, a basal lamina, and scattered contractile cells with long processes.","These cells, enclosed by the endothelial basal lamina, are pericytes."],"why":"A continuous capillary is a tube of endothelial cells joined by tight junctions and resting on a basal lamina. Pericytes wrap their long processes around the endothelium and sit inside a split in that basal lamina. They have contractile proteins, help stabilize the vessel, and can give rise to smooth muscle cells and fibroblasts after injury. Proliferating infantile hemangiomas contain both endothelial cells and pericytes arranged in capillary-sized lobules.","whyNot":["Myoepithelial cells wrap glandular secretory units, such as sweat and breast glands, not blood vessels.","Fibroblasts lie in the surrounding connective tissue outside the basal lamina and do not wrap the endothelium.","Smooth muscle cells form the media of arteries, arterioles, and veins. Capillaries have no muscular media.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Capillary structure","Pericytes"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-18-J1"]}},{"id":"CV-C54-Q2","stem":"At 14 weeks of age, the liver lesions have increased in size and number. Her serum TSH concentration is now increased and her free T4 concentration decreased for her age, although her newborn thyroid screen was normal. An enzyme produced by the hemangioma tissue is inactivating thyroid hormone faster than her thyroid gland can replace it. Which of the following reactions does this enzyme catalyze?","options":["Removing an inner-ring iodine, converting T4 to reverse T3","Removing an outer-ring iodine, converting T4 into active T3","Blocking iodide uptake by the sodium-iodide symporter","Blocking thyroid peroxidase coupling of iodotyrosines"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-J1","obj":"CV-18-J.1","dok":3,"approach":["Key clue: normal newborn screen, then low free T4 with high TSH once large liver hemangiomas appear, so the hormone is being destroyed outside the thyroid.","Hemangioma tissue can express type 3 iodothyronine deiodinase.","Type 3 deiodinase removes an inner-ring iodine, turning T4 into inactive reverse T3 (and T3 into inactive T2)."],"why":"Infantile hemangiomas, especially large or numerous liver lesions, can express high levels of type 3 iodothyronine deiodinase. This enzyme removes an iodine from the inner ring of thyroid hormone, converting T4 into inactive reverse T3 and T3 into inactive T2. When the lesions break down hormone faster than the thyroid can make it, free T4 falls and TSH rises. This is called consumptive hypothyroidism, and it matters in infancy because thyroid hormone is needed for brain development.","whyNot":["Correct.","Removing an outer-ring iodine is done by type 1 and type 2 deiodinases and activates the hormone by making T3. It would not lower her thyroid hormone activity.","Blocking the sodium-iodide symporter reduces hormone synthesis within the gland, as perchlorate or thiocyanate can. The problem here is breakdown of hormone outside the thyroid.","Blocking thyroid peroxidase reduces hormone synthesis within the gland, as thionamide drugs do. Her gland works normally, as shown by her normal newborn screen."],"study":{"topics":["Thyroid hormone deiodinases","Consumptive hypothyroidism"],"comps":["CV-18-J1","CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C54-Q3","stem":"Over the next month the liver lesions enlarge. Her pulse is 180/min (about 100 to 160 at her age). Physical examination shows bounding pulses, a wide pulse pressure, and hepatomegaly. Which of the following is the initiating change in this high-output state?","options":["Reduced venous return as the enlarged liver presses on the vena cava","Shunting through the lesions that lowers systemic vascular resistance","Lower contractility that reduces stroke volume and causes reflex tachycardia","Higher systemic vascular resistance from a surge of catecholamine release"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-J1","obj":"CV-18-J.1","dok":3,"approach":["Key clues: bounding pulses, wide pulse pressure, and tachycardia point to high cardiac output with low resistance.","Large vascular liver lesions act as low-resistance shunts between arteries and veins.","Lower systemic vascular resistance lowers diastolic pressure and increases venous return, so the heart must pump more blood each minute."],"why":"Large or numerous liver hemangiomas can contain arteriovenous and portovenous shunts. Blood takes this low-resistance path, so systemic vascular resistance falls. Diastolic pressure drops, which widens the pulse pressure and makes pulses bounding. More blood returns quickly to the heart, and cardiac output rises with tachycardia to keep arterial pressure up. If the demand outstrips the heart, she develops high-output heart failure with an enlarged liver.","whyNot":["Reduced venous return lowers cardiac output by the Frank-Starling mechanism. A high-output state needs more venous return, not less.","Correct.","Lower contractility reduces stroke volume and cardiac output. It would give weak pulses and a narrow pulse pressure, not bounding pulses with a wide pulse pressure.","Higher systemic vascular resistance would narrow the pulse pressure and lower cardiac output. Her findings show the opposite."],"study":{"topics":["High-output heart failure","Systemic vascular resistance and pulse pressure"],"comps":["CV-18-J1","CV-11","CV-18-B1"]}},{"id":"CV-C54-Q4","stem":"Her parents ask what the skin lesions will do over time if they cause no complications. Which of the following is the most likely course?","options":["More growth for a few months, then slow regression over several years","Slow conversion into angiosarcoma during early childhood","Steady shrinking that is complete within a few more weeks","Growth in proportion to her body throughout life, without any regression"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-J1","obj":"CV-18-J.1","dok":2,"approach":["Key clues: lesions absent at birth, appeared in the first weeks, and are growing fast.","This is the natural history of infantile hemangioma: rapid growth in early infancy, then slow involution.","Rule out the patterns of vascular malformations and malignant vascular tumors."],"why":"Infantile hemangiomas are absent or faint at birth and appear in the first weeks of life. Most of their growth happens in the first 3 to 5 months. Growth then slows and stops, and the lesions involute slowly over several years, with most regression finished by about age 4. Some leave behind thin, wrinkled skin, fibrofatty tissue, or telangiectasias. Female sex and preterm birth, both present here, are known risk factors.","whyNot":["Correct.","Infantile hemangiomas are benign and do not turn into angiosarcoma. Angiosarcoma is a rare malignant tumor, usually of older adults.","Infantile hemangiomas do not disappear within weeks. Involution takes years.","Growth in proportion to the child with no regression describes a vascular malformation, such as a capillary malformation (port-wine stain), which is present at birth."],"study":{"topics":["Natural history of infantile hemangioma","Hemangioma vs vascular malformation"],"comps":["CV-18-J1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C01","system":"GI","dx":"Mumps parotitis in an unvaccinated college student","codes":["GI-13-A1"],"case":"A 19-year-old man comes to the student health clinic because of a 2-day history of painful swelling at both angles of the jaw and pain with chewing. Fever, malaise, and headache began 3 days earlier. He did not receive childhood vaccinations, and several students in his residence hall have had a similar illness. Temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg. Physical examination shows tender, bilateral parotid swelling that obscures the angles of the mandible. The parotid duct openings are erythematous, and no pus is expressed with gland massage. The testes are normal. Serum amylase concentration is 310 U/L (normal 25 to 125), and serum lipase concentration is 48 U/L (normal 10 to 140). RT-PCR of a buccal swab detects mumps virus. Diagnosis: mumps parotitis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C01-Q1","stem":"During the same week, a 76-year-old man who is dehydrated 4 days after a colectomy develops painful swelling of the right parotid gland and a temperature of 38.6 °C (101.5 °F). Which of the following findings would best distinguish his condition from that of the college student?","options":["Raised serum amylase with a normal lipase concentration","Pus expressed from the parotid duct opening","Swelling that obscures the angle of the mandible","Pain in the gland that worsens with chewing"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-A1","obj":"GI-13-A.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the older patient's setting: postoperative, dehydrated, one gland involved. This points to acute bacterial parotitis.","List what both conditions share: a swollen, tender parotid, pain with chewing, fever, and a raised salivary amylase.","Pick the finding seen only with bacterial infection: pus at the duct opening."],"why":"Acute bacterial parotitis develops when salivary flow falls, as with dehydration after surgery, and bacteria ascend the parotid (Stensen) duct. Staphylococcus aureus is the usual cause. The gland fills with neutrophils, so massaging it pushes pus out of the duct opening. Mumps is a viral infection of the gland tissue, so the duct openings may be red, but no pus comes out.","whyNot":["Any inflamed salivary gland leaks salivary-type amylase into the blood while lipase stays normal. This pattern points to the salivary glands but does not tell viral from bacterial disease.","Correct.","Marked swelling hides the angle of the mandible whenever the parotid enlarges, whatever the cause.","Chewing stimulates salivary secretion and stretches the inflamed gland capsule, so it hurts in both mumps and bacterial parotitis."],"study":{"topics":["Mumps versus bacterial parotitis","Risk factors for bacterial parotitis"],"comps":["GI-13-A1","GI-12"]}},{"id":"GI-C01-Q2","stem":"His roommate, a 22-year-old man, has recurrent painful swelling below the left side of the mandible that begins within minutes of eating and subsides over the next hour. Radiography shows a radiopaque calculus in the floor of the mouth. Which of the following features of the affected gland best explains why calculi form most often in this gland?","options":["Many small ducts that open along the sublingual fold","Mainly serous, amylase-rich saliva drained through a short duct","A duct that opens opposite the upper second molar tooth","Thicker, mucin-rich saliva drained upward through a long duct"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-A","obj":"GI-13-A.2","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the swelling: below the mandible with a stone in the floor of the mouth means the submandibular gland and its duct.","Recall the two features that favor stone formation in this gland: viscous saliva and a long duct that runs upward against gravity.","Link slow flow of thick saliva to precipitation of calcium salts."],"why":"About 80 to 90% of salivary stones form in the submandibular gland. Its saliva is a mixed serous and mucous secretion that is more viscous and more alkaline, with more calcium, than parotid saliva. Its duct (Wharton duct) is long and runs upward and forward to open beside the frenulum of the tongue, so saliva drains against gravity and stagnates. A stone blocks outflow, so the gland swells and hurts when eating stimulates secretion, and stagnant saliva can become infected (sialadenitis).","whyNot":["The sublingual gland drains through many small ducts in the sublingual fold. Short ducts with little stasis make stones in this gland rare.","This describes the parotid gland. Thin serous saliva flows easily, which is one reason parotid stones are uncommon.","The parotid (Stensen) duct opens on the inner cheek opposite the upper second molar. A stone there would cause swelling in front of the ear.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Sialolithiasis","Salivary gland ducts"],"comps":["GI-13-A","GI-3"]}},{"id":"GI-C01-Q3","stem":"His raised serum amylase comes from the inflamed glands. Which of the following best describes the secretory units that make up nearly all of these glands?","options":["Serous acini that secrete a watery, amylase-rich fluid","Mucous acini that secrete a viscous, mucin-rich fluid","Exocrine acini with scattered islets of endocrine cells","Mixed acini of mucous cells capped by serous demilunes"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the glands involved: the parotid glands.","Recall that the parotid is an almost purely serous gland.","Connect serous cells to their product, salivary amylase, which explains the raised serum amylase."],"why":"The parotid gland is made almost entirely of serous acini. Serous cells have basophilic cytoplasm, round central nuclei, and apical secretory granules, and they make a watery fluid rich in salivary amylase. When mumps virus inflames the parotid, this amylase leaks into the blood. The submandibular gland is mixed but mostly serous, and the sublingual gland is mostly mucous.","whyNot":["Correct.","Mucous acini, with pale, foamy cytoplasm and flattened basal nuclei, make up most of the sublingual gland and the minor salivary glands of the palate.","Acini mixed with islets of Langerhans describe the pancreas. Mumps can inflame the pancreas, but his normal lipase points away from it.","Mucous tubules capped by serous demilunes are typical of the submandibular and sublingual glands."],"study":{"topics":["Serous versus mucous salivary glands","Salivary amylase"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-3"]}},{"id":"GI-C01-Q4","stem":"Mumps virus first replicates in the upper respiratory mucosa. After recovery, his saliva contains the main antibody class that protects mucosal surfaces. Which of the following mediates the transport of this antibody into his saliva?","options":["Neonatal Fc receptor carrying IgG across the epithelium","Polymeric Ig receptor on the basolateral epithelial surface","M cell transcytosis of antigen to underlying lymphoid follicles","Passive leakage of plasma IgM between epithelial cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.1","dok":2,"approach":["Name the antibody in saliva and other mucosal secretions: secretory IgA.","Recall how it crosses the epithelium: plasma cells under the epithelium release dimeric IgA joined by a J chain, and epithelial cells carry it across.","Match this to the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor."],"why":"Plasma cells in the gland stroma and mucosa secrete IgA as a dimer held together by a J chain. The polymeric immunoglobulin receptor on the basolateral surface of epithelial cells binds the dimer and carries it across the cell by transcytosis. At the apical surface the receptor is cleaved, and the piece that stays attached, called secretory component, protects IgA from digestion in the secretions. Secretory IgA blocks viruses and bacteria from attaching to mucosal cells.","whyNot":["The neonatal Fc receptor carries IgG across the placenta and protects IgG from breakdown, which gives IgG its long half-life. It is not the route for antibody in saliva.","Correct.","M cells over Peyer patches carry antigen from the gut lumen to lymphoid tissue to start mucosal immune responses. They move antigen inward, not antibody outward.","IgM can also be carried by the polymeric Ig receptor, but it is a minor secretory antibody, and it moves by active transcytosis, not leakage."],"study":{"topics":["Secretory IgA","Polymeric immunoglobulin receptor"],"comps":["GI-11"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C02","system":"GI","dx":"Pleomorphic adenoma of the parotid gland","codes":["GI-13-K"],"case":"A 46-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a painless lump in front of her right ear that has grown slowly over 3 years. She has no facial weakness, numbness, or pain. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 72/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a 2.5-cm, firm, mobile, nontender mass in the superficial part of the right parotid gland. Facial nerve function is normal on both sides. There is no cervical lymphadenopathy. Fine-needle aspiration shows epithelial and myoepithelial cells in a myxoid and chondroid stroma. She undergoes superficial parotidectomy. Diagnosis: pleomorphic adenoma of the right parotid gland.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C02-Q1","stem":"Her surgeon removes the tumor with a margin of normal gland tissue and avoids simple enucleation. Which of the following features of this tumor best explains this choice?","options":["Perineural invasion along branches of the facial nerve","Cystic spaces lined by oncocytes over lymphoid stroma","Finger-like projections through an incomplete capsule","Infiltrating mixture of mucus-secreting and squamoid cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K","obj":"GI-13-K.12","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the most common salivary tumor: a slow-growing, mobile parotid mass with epithelial, myoepithelial, and chondromyxoid tissue.","Recall that this benign tumor is not fully encapsulated.","Connect the small extensions of tumor beyond the capsule to recurrence when the tumor is shelled out."],"why":"Pleomorphic adenoma is the most common salivary gland tumor, and most arise in the parotid. It is benign and mixes epithelial and myoepithelial cells with myxoid, hyaline, or cartilage-like stroma. The capsule is incomplete, and small tongues of tumor push into the surrounding gland. Shelling out the main mass leaves these projections behind, so recurrence is common unless a rim of normal tissue is removed. Long-standing tumors carry a small risk of malignant change.","whyNot":["Perineural invasion is the hallmark of adenoid cystic carcinoma, a malignant tumor that often causes pain or facial weakness. Her nerve function is normal and the aspirate shows a benign mixed tumor.","Oncocytic cysts over dense lymphoid stroma describe Warthin tumor, the second most common benign parotid tumor, which is strongly linked to smoking. Its cytology differs from this aspirate.","Correct.","Mucus-secreting, squamoid, and intermediate cells describe mucoepidermoid carcinoma, the most common malignant salivary tumor."],"study":{"topics":["Pleomorphic adenoma","Salivary gland tumors"],"comps":["GI-13-K","GI-3"]}},{"id":"GI-C02-Q2","stem":"During the operation, the surgeon identifies and preserves a nerve that branches within the gland, between its superficial and deep parts. Injury to this nerve would most likely cause which of the following?","options":["Weak right eye closure and a drooping mouth corner","Deviation of the jaw to the right on opening the mouth","Numbness of the skin over the right angle of the mandible","Reduced secretion of saliva from the right parotid gland"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the nerve that divides into its branches inside the parotid gland: the facial nerve (CN VII).","Recall what the facial nerve does after it leaves the stylomastoid foramen: it supplies the muscles of facial expression.","Predict weakness of the orbicularis oculi and the muscles of the mouth on the same side."],"why":"After leaving the stylomastoid foramen, the facial nerve enters the parotid gland and divides into its temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, and cervical branches within it. The plane of the nerve separates the superficial part of the gland from the deep part. These branches carry only motor fibers to the muscles of facial expression, so injury weakens eye closure and lets the corner of the mouth droop on that side. This is why a parotid mass that causes facial weakness raises concern for a malignant tumor.","whyNot":["Correct.","The muscles of mastication, including the lateral pterygoid that opens the jaw, are supplied by CN V3. Weakness of one lateral pterygoid makes the jaw deviate toward the weak side.","Skin over the angle of the mandible is supplied by the great auricular nerve (C2 and C3), which can be cut during parotid surgery but runs on the surface of the gland, not through it.","Secretomotor fibers to the parotid come from CN IX through the otic ganglion and reach the gland with the auriculotemporal nerve. The facial nerve branches in the gland do not carry them."],"study":{"topics":["Facial nerve in the parotid gland","Parotid gland anatomy"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-13-K"]}},{"id":"GI-C02-Q3","stem":"Five years later, she receives radiation therapy for an unrelated oropharyngeal cancer, and both parotid glands are in the radiation field. Over the next year, her mouth stays dry, and she develops many new carious lesions along the gum line. Loss of which of the following salivary functions most directly explains the new dental caries?","options":["Haptocorrin binding of dietary vitamin B12","Amylase digestion of dietary starch into maltose","Lingual lipase hydrolysis of dietary triglycerides","Bicarbonate buffering of acid from plaque bacteria"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize that radiation has destroyed the serous acini, so salivary flow has fallen.","Recall how caries form: plaque bacteria ferment sugars to acid, which dissolves enamel.","Choose the salivary function that neutralizes this acid."],"why":"Saliva protects the teeth in several ways. Its bicarbonate buffers the acid that plaque bacteria such as Streptococcus mutans make from dietary sugar, and its calcium and phosphate help remineralize enamel. It also contains IgA, lysozyme, and lactoferrin that limit bacterial growth, and its flow washes the teeth. When salivary flow falls, as after radiation or in Sjogren syndrome, acid stays on the enamel and caries develop quickly, often at the gum line.","whyNot":["Haptocorrin (R binder) protects vitamin B12 from stomach acid. It has no role in protecting enamel.","Salivary amylase starts starch digestion. Its loss would not promote caries, and breaking starch into sugars actually gives plaque bacteria more substrate.","Lingual lipase begins fat digestion, mainly in infants. It has no protective role for the teeth.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Protective functions of saliva","Xerostomia and dental caries"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-2"]}},{"id":"GI-C02-Q4","stem":"Before surgery, her swallowing is evaluated. When a swallowed bolus enters the pharynx, her lower esophageal sphincter relaxes before the peristaltic wave reaches it. Which of the following most directly mediates this relaxation?","options":["Vagal excitatory neurons releasing acetylcholine","Sympathetic fibers releasing norepinephrine onto beta-2 receptors","Vagal inhibitory neurons releasing nitric oxide and VIP","Gastrin released from antral G cells after the meal"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the pharyngeal phase of swallowing is a reflex coordinated by the swallowing center in the medulla.","Recognize that the sphincter must open ahead of the bolus, which needs active inhibition of its tonic contraction.","Identify the inhibitory transmitters released by vagal pathways in the myenteric plexus: nitric oxide and VIP."],"why":"Swallowing starts voluntarily in the mouth, and the pharyngeal and esophageal phases then run as a reflex organized in the medulla. Through the vagus nerve, the swallowing center activates inhibitory neurons in the myenteric plexus of the esophagus and stomach. These neurons release nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide, which relax the smooth muscle of the lower esophageal sphincter and the proximal stomach (receptive relaxation). Loss of these inhibitory neurons causes achalasia.","whyNot":["Acetylcholine from vagal excitatory neurons contracts esophageal smooth muscle and drives peristalsis. It does not relax the sphincter.","Sympathetic input has little role in swallowing and does not produce the rapid relaxation that precedes the bolus.","Correct.","Gastrin increases lower esophageal sphincter tone, and it is released only after food reaches the stomach."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of swallowing","Lower esophageal sphincter control"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-F1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C03","system":"GI","dx":"Acute alcohol-related pancreatitis complicated by a pseudocyst","codes":["GI-13-B1"],"case":"A 42-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of a 1-day history of severe epigastric pain that radiates to the back, with nausea and vomiting. He has drunk about 10 beers daily for 15 years and drank heavily for 2 days before the pain began. Temperature is 38.1 °C (100.6 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Physical examination shows epigastric tenderness with guarding and decreased bowel sounds. Serum lipase concentration is 1850 U/L (normal 10 to 140), and serum calcium concentration is 8.6 mg/dL (normal 8.5 to 10.5). Ultrasonography shows no gallstones. CT shows an edematous pancreas with peripancreatic fluid. Diagnosis: acute alcohol-related pancreatitis. Five weeks later, he has early satiety and epigastric fullness, and CT shows a 7-cm, well-defined fluid collection in the lesser sac behind the stomach.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C03-Q1","stem":"Which of the following is the key initiating event in the pathogenesis of his pancreatic injury?","options":["Activation of trypsinogen to trypsin within acinar cells","Release of insulin and glucagon from destroyed islet cells","Reflux of bile into the pancreatic duct through a common channel","IgG4-positive plasma cells infiltrating and destroying acini"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-B1","obj":"GI-13-B.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that pancreatic enzymes are normally made as inactive zymogens and become active only in the duodenum.","Recognize that any cause of acute pancreatitis, alcohol included, leads to activation of these enzymes inside the gland.","Identify trypsin as the key enzyme, because it activates the other zymogens."],"why":"Acute pancreatitis begins when trypsinogen is activated to trypsin inside acinar cells or ducts. Trypsin then activates the other zymogens, such as phospholipase A2 and elastase, and these enzymes digest the gland, its fat, and its blood vessels. Alcohol promotes this by thickening pancreatic secretions so they plug small ducts, by directly injuring acinar cells, and by causing abnormal enzyme release. Gallstones and alcohol cause most cases, with hypertriglyceridemia, hypercalcemia, ERCP, mumps, trauma, and some drugs such as thiazides as less common causes.","whyNot":["Correct.","Islet hormones do not digest tissue. Islet damage may follow severe necrosis, but it is a result of the injury, not its cause.","Bile reflux through a shared channel is one proposed way that a gallstone at the ampulla triggers pancreatitis. Even then, the injury runs through trypsin activation, and he has no gallstones.","IgG4-positive plasma cells mark autoimmune pancreatitis, which causes a painless, diffusely enlarged pancreas and responds to glucocorticoids."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of acute pancreatitis","Causes of acute pancreatitis"],"comps":["GI-13-B1","GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C03-Q2","stem":"The fluid collection seen 5 weeks later is drained, and part of its wall is examined microscopically. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Papillary mucinous epithelium lining dilated main ducts","Columnar mucinous epithelium over ovarian-type stroma","Glycogen-rich cuboidal epithelium lining many small cysts","Fibrous granulation tissue with no epithelial lining"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-B1","obj":"GI-13-B.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the complication: a well-defined fluid collection in the lesser sac several weeks after acute pancreatitis is a pseudocyst.","Recall why it is called a pseudocyst: the wall is reactive tissue, not epithelium.","Exclude the cystic neoplasms, which all have an epithelial lining."],"why":"A pancreatic pseudocyst forms when enzyme-rich fluid and necrotic debris from pancreatitis are walled off, often in the lesser sac. Over about 4 weeks the body builds a wall of granulation tissue that matures into fibrous tissue. There is no epithelial lining, which is what makes it a pseudocyst. Large pseudocysts can press on the stomach and cause early satiety, and they can become infected, bleed, or rupture.","whyNot":["Papillary mucin-producing epithelium in dilated ducts describes an intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm, a precursor of pancreatic cancer.","Mucinous epithelium over ovarian-type stroma defines a mucinous cystic neoplasm, which occurs almost only in women, usually in the body or tail, and has malignant potential.","Glycogen-rich cuboidal cells lining many small cysts describe a serous cystadenoma, a benign tumor usually seen in older women.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pancreatic pseudocyst","Complications of acute pancreatitis"],"comps":["GI-13-B1","GI-13-K6"]}},{"id":"GI-C03-Q3","stem":"His initial CT shows peripancreatic fluid confined behind the posterior parietal peritoneum. Which of the following structures is most likely to be surrounded by this fluid?","options":["Proximal first part of the duodenum","Lower pole of the spleen","Second part of the duodenum","Proximal jejunum"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize that fluid behind the posterior parietal peritoneum lies in the retroperitoneum, where most of the pancreas sits.","Sort the options into intraperitoneal and retroperitoneal structures.","Pick the only retroperitoneal one: the descending duodenum, which curves around the pancreatic head."],"why":"The pancreas, except for its tail, is a secondarily retroperitoneal organ. The duodenum from its second to fourth parts, the ascending and descending colon, and part of the rectum share this location, along with the kidneys, ureters, adrenal glands, aorta, and inferior vena cava. Inflammatory fluid from pancreatitis spreads through these retroperitoneal spaces and can surround the duodenum and reach the flanks.","whyNot":["The proximal first part of the duodenum (the bulb, about 2 cm) has a mesentery and is intraperitoneal. The rest of the duodenum is retroperitoneal.","The spleen is intraperitoneal, held by the gastrosplenic and splenorenal ligaments. The pancreatic tail reaches it within the splenorenal ligament.","Correct.","The jejunum hangs on the mesentery and is intraperitoneal. Fluid would reach it only by spreading into the peritoneal cavity."],"study":{"topics":["Retroperitoneal organs","Peritoneal relations of the duodenum"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-4"]}},{"id":"GI-C03-Q4","stem":"When he resumes eating, a meal delivers fatty acids and amino acids to his duodenum. Which of the following hormones, with its source cell, most directly increases pancreatic enzyme secretion in response?","options":["Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide from K cells","Gastrin from G cells","Cholecystokinin from I cells","Secretin from S cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the stimulus: fat and protein products in the duodenum.","Recall which duodenal hormone responds to fat and amino acids and which responds to acid.","Match enzyme secretion from acinar cells to cholecystokinin, made by I cells."],"why":"Fatty acids, monoglycerides, and amino acids in the duodenum and jejunum stimulate I cells to release cholecystokinin. CCK acts on acinar cells, largely through vagal reflexes, to release digestive enzymes, and it contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi. It also slows gastric emptying. Acid entering the duodenum instead triggers secretin from S cells, which drives bicarbonate secretion from duct cells. Patients with acute pancreatitis are often kept from eating at first so that this stimulation is avoided.","whyNot":["GIP from K cells responds to glucose and fat and increases insulin release. It does not drive enzyme secretion.","Gastrin from antral G cells responds to peptides and amino acids in the stomach and stimulates gastric acid secretion.","Correct.","Secretin from duodenal S cells responds mainly to acid and stimulates duct cells to secrete bicarbonate-rich fluid, not enzymes."],"study":{"topics":["Cholecystokinin","GI hormones and their source cells"],"comps":["GI-6"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C04","system":"GI","dx":"Hypertriglyceridemia-induced acute pancreatitis with steatotic liver disease","codes":["GI-13-B1"],"case":"A 38-year-old woman with obesity and type 2 diabetes mellitus is brought to the emergency department because of an 8-hour history of severe epigastric pain radiating to the back and repeated vomiting. She rarely drinks alcohol. Her BMI is 36 kg/m2. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 108/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Physical examination shows epigastric tenderness. Her plasma is visibly milky. Laboratory studies show serum lipase 2400 U/L (normal 10 to 140), triglycerides 2150 mg/dL (normal below 150), glucose 248 mg/dL (normal fasting 70 to 99), ALT 68 U/L (normal 10 to 40), and AST 49 U/L (normal 10 to 40). Ultrasonography shows no gallstones and a diffusely echogenic liver. Diagnosis: hypertriglyceridemia-induced acute pancreatitis with metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C04-Q1","stem":"On hospital day 2, she has perioral tingling, and her serum calcium concentration is 7.4 mg/dL (normal 8.5 to 10.5). Which of the following best explains this finding?","options":["Reduced intestinal calcium absorption from vitamin D deficiency","Calcium binding to fatty acids released in peripancreatic fat","Increased urinary calcium loss from renal tubular injury","Calcium uptake into bone driven by excess calcitonin"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-B1","obj":"GI-13-B.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that pancreatic lipase leaks into the fat around the pancreas during acute pancreatitis.","Follow the chemistry: lipase splits triglyceride into free fatty acids, which bind calcium.","Link calcium soap formation to a falling serum calcium."],"why":"In acute pancreatitis, activated lipase escapes into peripancreatic and omental fat and splits triglycerides into free fatty acids. The fatty acids combine with calcium to form insoluble calcium soaps, seen as chalky white spots of fat necrosis. This takes calcium out of the blood, and a low calcium is a sign of more severe disease. Other classic findings are epigastric pain radiating to the back, lipase above 3 times normal, and in hemorrhagic disease, periumbilical (Cullen) or flank (Grey Turner) bruising.","whyNot":["Vitamin D deficiency lowers calcium over weeks to months. It does not explain a fall within 2 days of acute pancreatitis.","Correct.","Tubular injury is not a usual cause of hypocalcemia here. Calcium falls because it is deposited in necrotic fat.","Calcitonin has only a small effect on serum calcium in adults and is not raised in pancreatitis."],"study":{"topics":["Fat necrosis and hypocalcemia","Findings in acute pancreatitis"],"comps":["GI-13-B1"]}},{"id":"GI-C04-Q2","stem":"Dietary triglyceride reaches her blood in chylomicrons that enterocytes assemble with apolipoprotein B-48. A child with a mutation that prevents enterocytes from loading lipid onto apolipoprotein B would most likely have which of the following?","options":["Eruptive xanthomas, lipemia retinalis, and recurrent pancreatitis","Steatorrhea, acanthocytes, and ataxia from vitamin E deficiency","Tendon xanthomas and early coronary artery disease","Enlarged orange tonsils and very low HDL cholesterol"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.3","dok":3,"approach":["Trace fat absorption: micelles deliver fatty acids and monoglycerides to enterocytes, which re-form triglyceride and package it with apoB-48 into chylomicrons that enter lacteals.","Recognize the defect: without microsomal triglyceride transfer protein, chylomicrons cannot form (abetalipoproteinemia).","Predict the result: fat stays in enterocytes and is lost in the stool, and fat-soluble vitamins, especially vitamin E, are not absorbed."],"why":"After bile salt micelles deliver fatty acids and monoglycerides to the enterocyte, the cell re-forms triglyceride in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum. Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein loads this lipid onto apoB-48 to form chylomicrons, which leave through lacteals into the lymph. In abetalipoproteinemia, MTP is absent, so no chylomicrons or VLDL form. Fat builds up in enterocytes and is lost as steatorrhea, red cells develop spiky membranes (acanthocytes), and vitamin E deficiency damages the spinal cord and retina.","whyNot":["This triad comes from lipoprotein lipase or apoC-II deficiency. Chylomicrons form normally but are not cleared from the blood, much like her own hypertriglyceridemia. In the child described, chylomicrons cannot form at all.","Correct.","Tendon xanthomas and early coronary disease come from familial hypercholesterolemia, a defect in the LDL receptor or apoB-100 binding.","Orange tonsils with very low HDL describe Tangier disease, a defect in the ABCA1 cholesterol transporter."],"study":{"topics":["Chylomicron formation","Abetalipoproteinemia"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-8"]}},{"id":"GI-C04-Q3","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms most directly links her insulin resistance to the accumulation of triglyceride in her hepatocytes?","options":["Increased export of hepatocyte triglyceride as VLDL","Decreased hepatic fatty acid synthesis from glucose","Increased fatty acid release from adipose tissue lipolysis","Increased hepatic beta-oxidation of fatty acids in mitochondria"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-C","obj":"GI-13-C.8","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that insulin normally suppresses lipolysis in adipose tissue.","Predict what happens when adipose tissue resists insulin: more free fatty acids flow to the liver.","Recognize that the liver re-forms these fatty acids into triglyceride faster than it can burn or export them."],"why":"In obesity with insulin resistance, insulin fails to restrain hormone-sensitive lipase in adipose tissue, so fat cells release large amounts of free fatty acids. The liver takes them up and re-forms them into triglyceride, and high insulin levels also drive new fatty acid synthesis in hepatocytes. When intake exceeds what the liver can oxidize or export as VLDL, triglyceride collects as steatosis. Simple steatosis is fat alone. Steatohepatitis (MASH) adds hepatocyte ballooning and inflammation and can progress to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma.","whyNot":["VLDL export removes triglyceride from the liver. More export would reduce fat, not cause it.","Hepatic fatty acid synthesis from glucose rises, not falls, in insulin-resistant states with high insulin levels.","Correct.","Beta-oxidation burns fatty acids. More of it would lower liver fat, so it cannot explain the accumulation."],"study":{"topics":["MASLD pathogenesis","Insulin and lipolysis"],"comps":["GI-13-C","GI-8"]}},{"id":"GI-C04-Q4","stem":"In a healthy person, the pancreas secretes trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, and procarboxypeptidase as inactive precursors. Which of the following explains how all three become active in the duodenal lumen?","options":["Enteropeptidase activates trypsin, which activates the rest","Gastric acid entering the duodenum cleaves each zymogen","Bile salts unfold each zymogen to expose its active site","Brush-border aminopeptidases remove the inhibitory peptide from each"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the brush-border enzyme of the duodenum that starts protein digestion: enteropeptidase (enterokinase).","Recognize that it acts on trypsinogen only.","Follow the cascade: trypsin then activates the other zymogens and more trypsinogen."],"why":"Enteropeptidase on the duodenal brush border cleaves trypsinogen to trypsin. Trypsin then activates chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase, proelastase, and more trypsinogen, so one brush-border enzyme switches on the whole cascade. Keeping these enzymes inactive until they reach the duodenum protects the pancreas, and a trypsin inhibitor made by acinar cells adds a second safeguard. Pancreatitis, as in this patient, starts when this protection fails and trypsin is activated inside the gland.","whyNot":["Correct.","Pepsinogen in the stomach is activated by acid. Pancreatic zymogens are not, and duodenal acid is neutralized by bicarbonate.","Bile salts emulsify fat and help pancreatic lipase work, with colipase. They do not activate protease zymogens.","Aminopeptidases on the brush border finish digesting small peptides into amino acids. They do not activate the pancreatic zymogens."],"study":{"topics":["Activation of pancreatic proteases","Protein digestion"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-B1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C05","system":"GI","dx":"Chronic pancreatitis with exocrine insufficiency in cystic fibrosis","codes":["GI-13-B1","GI-13-K6","GI-13-E3"],"case":"A 26-year-old woman with cystic fibrosis comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of bulky, greasy, foul-smelling stools and a 6-kg (13-lb) weight loss. She has had three episodes of acute pancreatitis since adolescence and has had intermittent epigastric pain since then. She does not drink alcohol. Her BMI is 17.8 kg/m2. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 108/68 mm Hg. Physical examination shows digital clubbing, scattered crackles, and mild epigastric tenderness. Fecal elastase concentration is 62 µg/g of stool (normal above 200), and fecal fat excretion is 21 g/day (normal below 7). Fasting plasma glucose concentration is 132 mg/dL (normal 70 to 99). CT shows an atrophic pancreas with multiple calcifications. Diagnosis: chronic pancreatitis with exocrine insufficiency caused by cystic fibrosis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C05-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her raised fasting plasma glucose concentration?","options":["Excess glucagon secretion from an alpha-cell tumor of the pancreas","Autoimmune T-cell destruction of pancreatic beta cells","Peripheral insulin resistance caused by obesity","Loss of islets as fibrosis replaces the pancreatic tissue"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-B1","obj":"GI-13-B.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize chronic pancreatitis from repeated attacks, steatorrhea, and an atrophic, calcified pancreas.","Recall that ongoing inflammation replaces acini and later islets with fibrous tissue.","Link islet loss to diabetes, which appears after exocrine failure."],"why":"Chronic pancreatitis is ongoing inflammation that replaces the pancreas with fibrous tissue, with duct strictures and calcifications. Causes include alcohol, cystic fibrosis, duct obstruction, and hereditary mutations in PRSS1 and SPINK1. Acini are lost first, causing steatorrhea and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency. As fibrosis advances, islets are also destroyed, so insulin and glucagon both fall, and diabetes develops. In cystic fibrosis this is called cystic fibrosis-related diabetes.","whyNot":["A glucagonoma raises glucose and causes weight loss and a migratory necrolytic rash. It would not explain her calcified, atrophic pancreas.","Autoimmune beta-cell destruction is type 1 diabetes. It spares the exocrine pancreas and does not explain her calcified, fibrotic gland.","Insulin resistance from obesity drives type 2 diabetes. She is underweight, with a BMI of 17.8.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Chronic pancreatitis","Pancreatogenic diabetes"],"comps":["GI-13-B1","GI-10"]}},{"id":"GI-C05-Q2","stem":"Her CFTR defect has reduced the delivery of pancreatic enzymes to her duodenum. If her malabsorption goes untreated, which of the following is most likely to develop?","options":["Prolonged prothrombin time from vitamin K deficiency","Megaloblastic anemia from folate deficiency","Microcytic anemia from failed duodenal iron absorption","Wernicke encephalopathy from thiamine deficiency"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-10","obj":"GI-10.6","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the defect: faulty CFTR reduces chloride and bicarbonate secretion by duct cells, so thick secretions plug the ducts and destroy acini.","Predict the result: too little lipase, so fat is not digested and is lost in the stool.","Recognize that vitamins A, D, E, and K travel with fat, so they are malabsorbed too."],"why":"CFTR is a chloride channel in the apical membrane of pancreatic duct cells that also drives bicarbonate and water secretion. When it fails, pancreatic secretions are scant and thick, ducts plug, and acini are destroyed. Without lipase, dietary fat is not digested, so fat and the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K are lost in the stool. Vitamin K deficiency reduces carboxylation of factors II, VII, IX, and X, which prolongs the prothrombin time. CFTR defects in the gut and liver also cause meconium ileus in newborns and focal biliary cirrhosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Folate is water soluble and absorbed in the jejunum without pancreatic enzymes, so folate deficiency is not expected from pancreatic insufficiency.","Iron is absorbed in the duodenum without needing pancreatic enzymes. Iron deficiency suggests blood loss or duodenal mucosal disease such as celiac disease.","Thiamine is water soluble. Its deficiency is typical of alcohol use disorder or malnutrition, not pancreatic insufficiency."],"study":{"topics":["Cystic fibrosis and the pancreas","Fat-soluble vitamin deficiency"],"comps":["GI-10","GI-8"]}},{"id":"GI-C05-Q3","stem":"Her chronic pancreatitis increases her lifetime risk of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma. In this cancer, the most frequently mutated oncogene encodes a protein whose mutation causes which of the following?","options":["Constitutive tyrosine kinase activity from a gene fusion","Impaired GTP hydrolysis that keeps it in the active state","Loss of a CDK4/6 inhibitor that restrains the G1 to S transition","Overexpression of an antiapoptotic mitochondrial membrane protein"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-K6","obj":"GI-13-K.9","dok":2,"approach":["Name the oncogene mutated in about 90% of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinomas: KRAS.","Recall that KRAS is a small G protein that is active when bound to GTP.","Predict the effect of the usual point mutation: GTP is not hydrolyzed, so growth signaling stays on."],"why":"KRAS encodes a small GTPase that relays signals from growth factor receptors to the MAP kinase pathway. Normally it switches itself off by hydrolyzing GTP to GDP. Point mutations, often at codon 12, block this GTPase activity, so KRAS stays bound to GTP and keeps signaling. This is usually the earliest mutation in pancreatic precursor lesions. Pancreatic cancer is linked to smoking and chronic pancreatitis, and a tumor in the head of the pancreas typically causes painless jaundice with a palpable gallbladder (Courvoisier sign), weight loss, migratory thrombophlebitis, and a raised CA 19-9.","whyNot":["A fusion that creates a constantly active tyrosine kinase describes BCR-ABL in chronic myeloid leukemia.","Correct.","This describes loss of CDKN2A (p16), which is also very common in pancreatic cancer. CDKN2A is a tumor suppressor gene, not an oncogene.","Overexpression of the antiapoptotic protein BCL2 follows the t(14;18) translocation in follicular lymphoma."],"study":{"topics":["KRAS in pancreatic cancer","Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma"],"comps":["GI-13-K6","GI-13-B1"]}},{"id":"GI-C05-Q4","stem":"A biopsy specimen of her pancreas shows dense fibrosis with scattered remaining islets of Langerhans and a few residual acini. On hematoxylin and eosin staining, which of the following features identifies the enzyme-producing cells?","options":["Pale cords of cells arranged around fenestrated capillaries","Tall columnar cells with apical droplets of mucin","Cells with flattened basal nuclei and pale, foamy cytoplasm","Basophilic basal cytoplasm and apical eosinophilic granules"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that acinar cells make large amounts of secreted protein.","Link protein synthesis to abundant rough endoplasmic reticulum, which stains blue at the base of the cell.","Link stored enzyme to zymogen granules, which stain pink at the apex."],"why":"Pancreatic acinar cells are pyramidal cells built for protein export. Their basal cytoplasm is packed with rough endoplasmic reticulum, whose ribosomal RNA stains strongly basophilic, and their apical cytoplasm holds eosinophilic zymogen granules full of digestive enzyme precursors. Islets of Langerhans are pale-staining clusters of endocrine cells arranged in cords around a dense network of fenestrated capillaries. In chronic pancreatitis the islets tend to survive longer than the acini, which is why exocrine failure comes before diabetes.","whyNot":["Pale cords of cells around fenestrated capillaries describe islets of Langerhans, which release insulin and glucagon into the blood rather than enzymes into ducts.","Tall columnar cells with apical mucin describe goblet cells or mucous duct epithelium, not enzyme-secreting acini.","Flattened basal nuclei and pale, foamy cytoplasm describe mucous acini, such as those in the sublingual gland.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of the exocrine pancreas","Pancreatic islets"],"comps":["GI-2"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C06","system":"GI","dx":"Alcohol-related hepatitis with a Mallory-Weiss tear","codes":["GI-13-C4"],"case":"A 51-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of repeated hematemesis after a night of heavy drinking and forceful retching. He has drunk about a pint of vodka daily for 20 years and has had 3 weeks of increasing jaundice, anorexia, and right upper quadrant pain. Temperature is 38.0 °C (100.4 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Physical examination shows scleral icterus and tender hepatomegaly. Laboratory studies show leukocyte count 14,800/mm3 (normal 4500 to 11,000), AST 186 U/L (normal 10 to 40), ALT 72 U/L (normal 10 to 40), total bilirubin 8.4 mg/dL (normal 0.1 to 1.2), and prothrombin time 17.5 seconds (normal 11 to 13.5). Endoscopy shows a 1.5-cm mucosal laceration at the gastroesophageal junction and no varices. Diagnosis: alcohol-related hepatitis with a Mallory-Weiss tear.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C06-Q1","stem":"A liver biopsy specimen from this patient is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Ballooned hepatocytes with Mallory-Denk bodies and neutrophils","Granulomatous destruction of small interlobular bile ducts","Ground-glass hepatocytes containing hepatitis B surface antigen","Portal lymphoid follicles with mild macrovesicular steatosis"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C4","obj":"GI-13-C.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize alcohol-related hepatitis: heavy drinking, fever, tender hepatomegaly, jaundice, leukocytosis, and an AST more than twice the ALT.","Recall the three histologic features of alcoholic hepatitis.","Match ballooning, Mallory-Denk bodies, and neutrophils to the key."],"why":"Alcohol-related liver disease moves from steatosis to alcoholic hepatitis to cirrhosis. In alcoholic hepatitis, injured hepatocytes swell (ballooning degeneration), damaged keratin filaments clump into eosinophilic Mallory-Denk bodies, and neutrophils gather around dying cells. An AST to ALT ratio above 2 is typical because alcohol injures mitochondria, which hold much of the AST, and because alcohol-related pyridoxal phosphate deficiency lowers ALT more than AST.","whyNot":["Correct.","Granulomatous destruction of small bile ducts is the lesion of primary biliary cholangitis, which mainly affects middle-aged women and causes cholestasis with a high alkaline phosphatase.","Ground-glass hepatocytes packed with HBsAg in their endoplasmic reticulum mark chronic hepatitis B infection, not alcohol injury.","Lymphoid follicles in portal tracts with mild steatosis are typical of chronic hepatitis C infection."],"study":{"topics":["Alcohol-related hepatitis histology","AST to ALT ratio"],"comps":["GI-13-C4","GI-13-C2"]}},{"id":"GI-C06-Q2","stem":"On hospital day 2, he becomes confused and has horizontal nystagmus, bilateral lateral rectus palsies, and an unsteady, wide-based gait. A deficiency of the cofactor for which of the following enzymes is most likely responsible?","options":["Aminolevulinate synthase in heme synthesis","Pyruvate and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenases","Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase in propionate metabolism","Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases in collagen synthesis"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize Wernicke encephalopathy: confusion, ophthalmoplegia, and ataxia in a person with heavy alcohol use.","Identify the missing vitamin: thiamine, whose active form is thiamine pyrophosphate.","Recall which enzymes need thiamine pyrophosphate: pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase, and transketolase."],"why":"Heavy alcohol use reduces thiamine intake, absorption, and storage. Thiamine pyrophosphate is the cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, which feed and run the citric acid cycle, and for transketolase. Without it, neurons in high-demand areas such as the mammillary bodies and the region around the third ventricle cannot make enough ATP. Giving glucose without thiamine can make this worse, so thiamine is given first or with glucose.","whyNot":["Aminolevulinate synthase needs pyridoxal phosphate (vitamin B6). B6 deficiency, also common with alcohol use, causes sideroblastic anemia and seizures.","Correct.","Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase needs vitamin B12. B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia and subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord, which develop slowly.","Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases need vitamin C. Scurvy causes bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhages, and poor wound healing."],"study":{"topics":["Thiamine and Wernicke encephalopathy","Vitamin cofactors"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-13-C4"]}},{"id":"GI-C06-Q3","stem":"His prothrombin time stays prolonged after he receives parenteral vitamin K. Which of the following best explains the prolonged prothrombin time?","options":["Platelet sequestration in an enlarged spleen","Interference with the clotting assay by antiphospholipid antibodies","Reduced hepatocyte synthesis of clotting factors","Decreased release of von Willebrand factor from endothelium"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that the prothrombin time measures the extrinsic and common pathways, factors VII, X, V, II, and fibrinogen, all made in the liver.","Use the failed vitamin K response: if vitamin K deficiency were the cause, the prothrombin time would correct.","Conclude that the injured hepatocytes cannot make enough clotting factor protein."],"why":"Hepatocytes make most clotting factors, including fibrinogen and factors II, V, VII, IX, and X. When liver cell function fails, factor levels fall, and the prothrombin time rises early because factor VII has the shortest half-life, about 4 to 6 hours. A prolonged prothrombin time that does not correct with vitamin K shows that the liver cannot make the proteins, rather than lacking the vitamin to activate them. Low serum albumin reflects the same loss of synthetic function, and failure of the urea cycle can also raise blood ammonia.","whyNot":["Low platelets cause mucosal bleeding and a long bleeding time, but they do not prolong the prothrombin time, which is measured in plasma.","Antiphospholipid antibodies mainly prolong the aPTT in the laboratory while causing clotting in the patient. They do not explain liver-related coagulopathy.","Correct.","Von Willebrand factor helps platelets adhere and carries factor VIII. Its deficiency prolongs the bleeding time and sometimes the aPTT, not the prothrombin time."],"study":{"topics":["Synthetic functions of the liver","Prothrombin time in liver disease"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C2"]}},{"id":"GI-C06-Q4","stem":"Six months later, after another episode of forceful vomiting, he has severe retrosternal chest pain, and crepitus is palpated in the soft tissues of the neck. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","options":["Herniation of the gastric fundus through the hiatus","Transmural rupture of the distal esophagus","Rupture of submucosal esophageal varices","Recurrent mucosal laceration at the gastroesophageal junction"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G","obj":"GI-13-G.12","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize crepitus in the neck as subcutaneous emphysema, which means air has escaped from a hollow organ.","Recall that a mucosal tear does not let air out, but a full-thickness tear does.","Identify Boerhaave syndrome: transmural rupture of the distal esophagus after forceful vomiting."],"why":"Mallory-Weiss tears are lacerations of the mucosa and submucosa at the gastroesophageal junction caused by retching. They bleed but usually heal on their own. Boerhaave syndrome is a full-thickness rupture, usually of the left posterolateral wall of the distal esophagus. Air and gastric contents escape into the mediastinum, causing severe chest pain, pneumomediastinum, and subcutaneous emphysema that can be felt in the neck. It is an emergency with a high death rate from mediastinitis.","whyNot":["A hiatal hernia can cause reflux and chest discomfort but does not release air into the soft tissues of the neck.","Correct.","Variceal rupture causes massive hematemesis in patients with portal hypertension. It does not let air into the tissues, and his endoscopy showed no varices.","Another Mallory-Weiss tear would cause hematemesis but no air leak, because the muscular wall stays intact."],"study":{"topics":["Mallory-Weiss versus Boerhaave syndrome","Esophageal rupture"],"comps":["GI-13-G","GI-13-J1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C07","system":"GI","dx":"Hereditary hemochromatosis with hepatocellular carcinoma","codes":["GI-13-C2","GI-13-K4"],"case":"A 58-year-old man comes to the physician because of 2 months of right upper quadrant discomfort and a 7-kg (15-lb) weight loss. He has had type 2 diabetes mellitus for 6 years and chronic hand pain. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 80/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. Physical examination shows grayish-bronze skin, a firm, nodular liver edge 4 cm below the right costal margin, and enlarged second and third metacarpophalangeal joints. Serum ferritin is 2850 ng/mL (normal 24 to 336), transferrin saturation is 82% (normal 20 to 50), and alpha-fetoprotein is 960 ng/mL (normal below 10). Hepatitis B and C serologies are negative. CT shows a cirrhotic liver with a 6-cm arterially enhancing right lobe mass. He is homozygous for HFE C282Y. Diagnosis: hereditary hemochromatosis with cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C07-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the excess iron in his body?","options":["High hepcidin, trapping iron in enterocytes and macrophages","Increased iron delivery from repeated transfusions of red blood cells","Loss of transferrin, leaving iron unbound in the plasma","Low hepcidin, allowing high ferroportin activity in enterocytes"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-10","obj":"GI-10.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the body has no regulated way to excrete iron, so iron balance is set by absorption.","Recall that hepcidin from the liver controls absorption by degrading ferroportin, the iron exporter on enterocytes and macrophages.","Recognize that the HFE mutation lowers hepcidin, so ferroportin stays active and iron absorption stays high."],"why":"Hereditary hemochromatosis is autosomal recessive, and most cases are homozygous for the HFE C282Y mutation. HFE normally helps hepatocytes sense iron and make hepcidin. With low hepcidin, ferroportin on duodenal enterocytes keeps exporting iron into the blood even when stores are full. Over decades, iron builds up in the liver, pancreas, heart, skin, and joints, causing cirrhosis, diabetes, bronze skin, cardiomyopathy, arthropathy, and a greatly raised risk of hepatocellular carcinoma.","whyNot":["High hepcidin blocks iron export and traps iron in cells. This is the pattern of anemia of chronic disease, with low serum iron and low transferrin saturation.","Transfusional iron overload causes secondary hemochromatosis, as in thalassemia major. He has had no transfusions, and his genotype explains his disease.","Transferrin is not lost in hemochromatosis. His transferrin saturation is high because there is too much iron for the transferrin present.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hepcidin and ferroportin","Hereditary hemochromatosis"],"comps":["GI-10","GI-7"]}},{"id":"GI-C07-Q2","stem":"A 45-year-old farmer from a region where stored peanuts and maize are often contaminated with Aspergillus flavus also develops hepatocellular carcinoma. A characteristic mutation caused by the mold toxin is most likely found in which of the following genes?","options":["TP53, which arrests the cell cycle after DNA damage","APC, which targets beta-catenin for degradation","BRCA2, which repairs double-strand DNA breaks","KRAS, which relays growth factor signals as a GTPase"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-K4","obj":"GI-13-K.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the toxin: Aspergillus flavus makes aflatoxin B1.","Recall how it becomes a carcinogen: liver cytochrome P450 converts it to an epoxide that binds guanine in DNA.","Recall the signature result: a G to T change at codon 249 of TP53."],"why":"Aflatoxin B1 is a mycotoxin in moldy grain and nuts. Liver enzymes activate it to a reactive epoxide that forms DNA adducts at guanine, producing a characteristic G to T transversion at codon 249 of the TP53 tumor suppressor gene. Loss of p53 removes the checkpoint that halts damaged cells. Other major risk factors for hepatocellular carcinoma are chronic hepatitis B, which can cause cancer even without cirrhosis, hepatitis C, alcohol, and hemochromatosis. Alpha-fetoprotein is often raised, and the tumor spreads mainly through the blood.","whyNot":["Correct.","APC loss starts most colorectal cancers. Beta-catenin pathway mutations occur in some liver tumors, but they are not the aflatoxin signature.","BRCA2 mutations raise the risk of breast, ovarian, prostate, and pancreatic cancer. They are inherited, not caused by aflatoxin.","KRAS mutations drive pancreatic and many colorectal cancers. They are not the hallmark of aflatoxin exposure."],"study":{"topics":["Aflatoxin and TP53","Hepatocellular carcinoma risk factors"],"comps":["GI-13-K4","GI-13-C2"]}},{"id":"GI-C07-Q3","stem":"His 50-year-old brother is also homozygous for HFE C282Y but has normal iron studies. Ten years ago, the brother had a Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, in which food passes from a small gastric pouch directly into the jejunum. Which of the following best explains his normal iron stores?","options":["Food no longer passes through the terminal ileum","Colonic transit of dietary iron is faster","Intrinsic factor from the gastric body is lost","Food no longer passes through the duodenum"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the main site of iron absorption: the duodenum and upper jejunum.","Recognize that a Roux-en-Y bypass sends food past the duodenum.","Predict that less iron is absorbed, which offsets the effect of his mutation."],"why":"Dietary iron is absorbed mainly in the duodenum. There, ferric iron is reduced to ferrous iron, taken up by DMT1, and exported to the blood by ferroportin. A Roux-en-Y bypass routes food from the gastric pouch to the jejunum, so it skips the duodenum, and the loss of gastric acid also reduces conversion of ferric to ferrous iron. Iron deficiency is a common late effect of this surgery, and here it offsets his genetic tendency to absorb too much iron. Folate is absorbed in the jejunum, and vitamin B12 and bile acids in the terminal ileum.","whyNot":["The terminal ileum absorbs vitamin B12 and bile acids, not iron, and a Roux-en-Y bypass does not exclude it.","Very little iron is absorbed in the colon, so faster colonic transit would not change his iron balance.","Intrinsic factor is needed for vitamin B12 absorption, not iron absorption.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Sites of nutrient absorption","Iron absorption"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-10"]}},{"id":"GI-C07-Q4","stem":"Three years before this diagnosis, he had fever, diarrhea, and severe right lower quadrant pain 4 days after eating undercooked pork. At surgery, the appendix was normal, and the mesenteric lymph nodes near the terminal ileum were enlarged and inflamed. A gram-negative rod was isolated from stool cultured at 25 °C (77 °F). Which of the following organisms was most likely responsible?","options":["Shigella dysenteriae","Escherichia coli O157:H7","Yersinia enterocolitica","Listeria monocytogenes"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.3","dok":2,"approach":["Link the clues: undercooked pork, right lower quadrant pain mimicking appendicitis, mesenteric lymphadenitis, and growth at cold temperature.","Recognize Yersinia enterocolitica, a gram-negative rod that invades through Peyer patches of the terminal ileum.","Note that iron overload favors this iron-hungry organism."],"why":"Yersinia enterocolitica is a gram-negative rod spread by undercooked pork, unpasteurized milk, and contaminated water. It invades through M cells over Peyer patches in the terminal ileum and spreads to nearby mesenteric lymph nodes, causing pain in the right lower quadrant that can mimic appendicitis. It grows at refrigerator temperatures, which is used for cold enrichment in the laboratory. It depends on outside sources of iron, so patients with iron overload, such as in hemochromatosis, are at higher risk of severe infection.","whyNot":["Shigella causes bloody diarrhea with colitis spread person to person. It does not cause mesenteric adenitis mimicking appendicitis.","E. coli O157:H7 spreads in undercooked beef and makes Shiga toxin, causing bloody diarrhea and hemolytic uremic syndrome.","Correct.","Listeria is a gram-positive rod spread by deli meats and soft cheeses. It also grows in the cold, but it causes sepsis and meningitis in newborns, pregnant women, and older adults, not mesenteric adenitis."],"study":{"topics":["Yersinia enterocolitica","Iron overload and infection"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-L1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C08","system":"GI","dx":"Wilson disease presenting as acute hepatitis with hemolysis","codes":["GI-13-C4"],"case":"A 17-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department because of a 1-week history of jaundice and fatigue. Her school performance has declined, and a hand tremor began 6 months ago. She takes no medications. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 106/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 110/68 mm Hg. Physical examination shows scleral icterus, hepatomegaly, and golden-brown corneal limbal rings on slit-lamp examination. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 8.9 g/dL (normal 12.0 to 16.0), reticulocytes 9% (normal 0.5 to 2.5), a negative direct antiglobulin test, total bilirubin 11.6 mg/dL (normal 0.1 to 1.2), direct bilirubin 3.1 mg/dL (normal 0 to 0.3), AST 182 U/L and ALT 96 U/L (normal 10 to 40), ceruloplasmin 8 mg/dL (normal 20 to 40), and 24-hour urine copper 410 µg (normal below 40). Diagnosis: Wilson disease with hepatitis and hemolysis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C08-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her low serum ceruloplasmin concentration?","options":["Ceruloplasmin is consumed by binding free hemoglobin","Apoceruloplasmin lacks copper and is rapidly degraded","Ceruloplasmin leaks into the urine through damaged glomeruli","Defective intestinal copper export limits ceruloplasmin synthesis"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-10","obj":"GI-10.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the gene: ATP7B, a copper-transporting ATPase in hepatocytes, mutated in Wilson disease (autosomal recessive).","Recall its two jobs: loading copper onto apoceruloplasmin and excreting excess copper into bile.","Predict that ceruloplasmin without copper is unstable and degraded, so serum ceruloplasmin falls while free copper rises."],"why":"ATP7B moves copper into the Golgi of hepatocytes, where it is built into ceruloplasmin, and into bile for excretion. When ATP7B fails, ceruloplasmin is secreted without copper and is quickly broken down, so serum ceruloplasmin is low. Copper that cannot leave in bile builds up in the liver, causing hepatitis and later cirrhosis. It then spills into the blood as free copper, which deposits in the cornea (Kayser-Fleischer rings) and basal ganglia, is excreted in large amounts in urine, and damages red cell membranes to cause Coombs-negative hemolysis.","whyNot":["Free hemoglobin is bound by haptoglobin, which falls in hemolysis. Ceruloplasmin does not bind hemoglobin.","Correct.","Urinary loss of ceruloplasmin is not the cause. Her high urine copper reflects free copper, not lost ceruloplasmin.","Defective intestinal copper export is caused by ATP7A mutations in Menkes disease, which leads to copper deficiency, kinky hair, and low ceruloplasmin. She has copper overload, with high urine copper and corneal rings."],"study":{"topics":["Wilson disease and ATP7B","Copper metabolism"],"comps":["GI-10","GI-13-C4"]}},{"id":"GI-C08-Q2","stem":"Copper is normally removed from the body in bile. In a different patient, obstruction at which of the following sites would most likely block both biliary copper excretion and drainage of the main pancreatic duct?","options":["Cystic duct","Minor duodenal papilla","Common hepatic duct","Ampulla of Vater"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.3","dok":2,"approach":["Trace bile: canaliculi, bile ductules, right and left hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct, then the common bile duct after the cystic duct joins.","Recall where the common bile duct meets the main pancreatic duct: the hepatopancreatic ampulla (of Vater), surrounded by the sphincter of Oddi.","Choose the site shared by both ducts."],"why":"Bile leaves the liver through the right and left hepatic ducts, which form the common hepatic duct at the porta hepatis. The cystic duct joins it to form the common bile duct, which runs behind the duodenum and through the head of the pancreas. The common bile duct joins the main pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla, which opens at the major duodenal papilla in the second part of the duodenum, controlled by the sphincter of Oddi. A gallstone or tumor at the ampulla therefore blocks both bile and pancreatic juice.","whyNot":["A stone in the cystic duct blocks only the gallbladder, causing biliary colic or cholecystitis. Bile from the liver still reaches the duodenum.","The minor papilla drains the accessory pancreatic duct, a minor route in most people. Bile does not pass through it.","Blockage of the common hepatic duct stops bile flow but lies above the pancreas, so pancreatic drainage is unaffected.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Biliary tree anatomy","Hepatopancreatic ampulla"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-13-D2"]}},{"id":"GI-C08-Q3","stem":"Before the copper studies returned, the differential diagnosis included autoimmune hepatitis. Which of the following findings would have most supported autoimmune hepatitis?","options":["Hypergammaglobulinemia and interface hepatitis with plasma cells","Centrilobular necrosis after a large acetaminophen ingestion","Steatohepatitis in a patient with obesity and insulin resistance","Hepatitis that began 2 months after starting isoniazid therapy"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C4","obj":"GI-13-C.9","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the profile of autoimmune hepatitis: usually young women, with autoantibodies such as ANA and anti-smooth muscle antibody.","Recall its laboratory and biopsy hallmarks: raised IgG and interface hepatitis rich in plasma cells.","Separate it from drug and toxin injury, which follows an exposure."],"why":"Autoimmune hepatitis is a T-cell mediated attack on hepatocytes, most common in young women. Serum IgG is high, and ANA or anti-smooth muscle antibodies are often present. Biopsy shows interface hepatitis, in which lymphocytes and many plasma cells spill from the portal tracts into the nearby hepatocytes. It responds to glucocorticoids. Drug and toxin hepatitis follows an exposure and has its own patterns, such as zone 3 necrosis from acetaminophen and hepatitis from isoniazid.","whyNot":["Correct.","Acetaminophen overdose causes centrilobular (zone 3) necrosis through its toxic metabolite NAPQI. It would point to drug injury, and she takes no medications.","Steatohepatitis with obesity and insulin resistance describes MASH, a metabolic liver disease, not an autoimmune one.","Isoniazid can cause hepatitis weeks to months after it is started. This would point to a drug cause."],"study":{"topics":["Autoimmune hepatitis","Drug and toxin hepatitis"],"comps":["GI-13-C4","GI-11"]}},{"id":"GI-C08-Q4","stem":"Most of her serum bilirubin is unconjugated. Which of the following best explains this pattern?","options":["Inherited reduction in UGT1A1 gene transcription","Heme breakdown outpaces hepatic glucuronidation capacity","Bacterial deconjugation of bilirubin in the colon with reabsorption","Defective canalicular export of bilirubin glucuronides by MRP2"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize hemolysis: anemia, high reticulocyte count, and a negative direct antiglobulin test with copper overload.","Trace bilirubin: heme oxygenase and biliverdin reductase make unconjugated bilirubin, which travels on albumin to the liver for conjugation by UGT1A1.","Conclude that heavy heme breakdown, with an injured liver, produces more bilirubin than the liver can conjugate."],"why":"Red cell breakdown releases heme, which macrophages convert to biliverdin with heme oxygenase and then to unconjugated bilirubin with biliverdin reductase. Unconjugated bilirubin is insoluble in water and travels to the liver bound to albumin. Hepatocytes conjugate it with glucuronic acid using UGT1A1 and export it into bile through MRP2. In the gut, bacteria convert it to urobilinogen and stercobilin. Her copper-driven hemolysis makes bilirubin faster than her injured liver can conjugate it, so unconjugated bilirubin dominates.","whyNot":["Reduced UGT1A1 transcription is Gilbert syndrome, a harmless cause of mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia with stress or fasting. It does not cause anemia or a bilirubin this high.","Correct.","Gut bacteria can deconjugate some bilirubin, and a little is reabsorbed, which matters mainly in newborns. In adults most is reduced to urobilinogen and stercobilin, so this cannot produce her high unconjugated bilirubin.","Defective MRP2 is Dubin-Johnson syndrome, which raises conjugated bilirubin and gives the liver a black color."],"study":{"topics":["Bilirubin metabolism","Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C3"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C09","system":"GI","dx":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (PiZZ) with cirrhosis","codes":["GI-13-C2"],"case":"A 44-year-old man comes to the physician because of 3 months of increasing abdominal girth, ankle swelling, and exertional dyspnea. He has smoked one pack of cigarettes daily for 20 years and rarely drinks alcohol. Temperature is 36.7 °C (98.1 °F), pulse is 88/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Physical examination shows scleral icterus, spider angiomas, palmar erythema, shifting dullness, and splenomegaly. Laboratory studies show albumin 2.9 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.0), total bilirubin 2.6 mg/dL (normal 0.1 to 1.2), prothrombin time 15.8 seconds (normal 11 to 13.5), platelet count 88,000/mm3 (normal 150,000 to 400,000), and alpha-1 antitrypsin 22 mg/dL (normal 100 to 200). Viral hepatitis serologies are negative. Chest CT shows panacinar emphysema, worst in the lower lobes. His genotype is PiZZ. Diagnosis: alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency with cirrhosis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C09-Q1","stem":"His liver biopsy specimen shows round, PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules in periportal hepatocytes. Which of the following best explains the formation of these globules?","options":["Glycogen stored because of a debranching enzyme defect","Hyaline aggregates of damaged keratin intermediate filaments","Misfolded protein retained in the endoplasmic reticulum","Lysosomes filled with oxidized lipid from membrane injury"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-10","obj":"GI-10.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the PiZZ variant: a single amino acid change (Glu342Lys) that makes alpha-1 antitrypsin misfold.","Recall what happens to misfolded secretory proteins: they polymerize and stay in the endoplasmic reticulum.","Use the stain: PAS-positive and resistant to diastase means glycoprotein, not glycogen."],"why":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin is a glycoprotein made in hepatocytes and secreted into the blood, where it inhibits neutrophil elastase. The Z variant misfolds and forms polymers that cannot leave the endoplasmic reticulum. These polymers collect as PAS-positive globules that diastase cannot digest, because they are glycoprotein rather than glycogen. The retained protein injures hepatocytes and can lead to cirrhosis, while the low serum level leaves the lung alveoli open to elastase, causing panacinar emphysema that smoking makes much worse.","whyNot":["Glycogen in storage diseases such as Cori disease is PAS positive but is digested by diastase.","Hyaline clumps of damaged keratin filaments are Mallory-Denk bodies, typical of alcoholic hepatitis.","Correct.","Lysosomes filled with oxidized lipid are lipofuscin, a brown wear-and-tear pigment that builds up with age."],"study":{"topics":["Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","PAS with diastase stain"],"comps":["GI-10","GI-13-C2"]}},{"id":"GI-C09-Q2","stem":"Which of the following cells is most directly responsible for the fibrous septa that have converted his liver to cirrhosis?","options":["Kupffer cells within the sinusoidal lumen","Periportal hepatocytes containing the abnormal protein","Hepatic stellate cells in the space of Disse","Fenestrated sinusoidal endothelial cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C2","obj":"GI-13-C.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that cirrhosis is bridging fibrosis with regenerative nodules.","Identify the cell that stores vitamin A in the space of Disse and turns into a collagen-producing myofibroblast after injury.","Name the hepatic stellate (Ito) cell."],"why":"Hepatic stellate cells sit in the space of Disse between hepatocytes and sinusoidal endothelium, where they normally store vitamin A. Chronic injury releases cytokines such as TGF-beta from Kupffer cells and damaged hepatocytes, which activate stellate cells into contractile myofibroblasts. These cells lay down type I and III collagen, forming septa that bridge portal tracts and central veins. Surviving hepatocytes regrow as nodules. The main causes of cirrhosis are alcohol, chronic hepatitis B and C, fatty liver disease, and metabolic diseases such as this one.","whyNot":["Kupffer cells are resident macrophages. They release cytokines that activate stellate cells, but they do not make the collagen.","Hepatocytes holding misfolded protein are injured and die, which drives the process, but hepatocytes do not deposit the fibrous septa.","Correct.","Sinusoidal endothelial cells lose their fenestrations in cirrhosis (capillarization), but they are not the main source of collagen."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatic stellate cells","Pathogenesis of cirrhosis"],"comps":["GI-13-C2","GI-2"]}},{"id":"GI-C09-Q3","stem":"Which of the following findings in this patient results mainly from portal hypertension rather than from loss of hepatocyte function?","options":["Spider angiomas on the chest","Scleral icterus","Prolonged prothrombin time","Congestive splenomegaly"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C2","obj":"GI-13-C.4","dok":3,"approach":["Sort the findings of cirrhosis into two groups: those from failing hepatocytes and those from high portal pressure.","Hepatocyte failure: jaundice, low albumin, coagulopathy, hyperestrogenism, and encephalopathy.","Portal hypertension: splenomegaly, ascites, varices, and caput medusae."],"why":"Cirrhosis causes two kinds of problems. Loss of hepatocyte function leads to jaundice, low albumin, a prolonged prothrombin time, and hepatic encephalopathy, and poor breakdown of estrogen causes spider angiomas, palmar erythema, gynecomastia, and testicular atrophy. Scarring also blocks blood flow through the liver and raises portal pressure. Blood backs up through the splenic vein, so the spleen enlarges and traps platelets, and collateral veins open in the esophagus, rectum, and abdominal wall.","whyNot":["Spider angiomas come from excess estrogen because the failing liver cannot break it down.","Jaundice reflects failing uptake, conjugation, and excretion of bilirubin by hepatocytes.","A prolonged prothrombin time reflects poor hepatocyte synthesis of clotting factors.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Findings in cirrhosis","Portal hypertension"],"comps":["GI-13-C2","GI-13-C5"]}},{"id":"GI-C09-Q4","stem":"Blood from his enlarged spleen normally joins blood from the superior mesenteric vein behind the neck of the pancreas. Which of the following sequences describes the normal path of this blood after that point?","options":["Portal vein, sinusoids, central veins, hepatic veins, inferior vena cava","Portal vein, sinusoids, central veins, portal triads, inferior vena cava","Portal vein, central veins, sinusoids, hepatic veins, inferior vena cava","Portal vein, sinusoids, hepatic veins, central veins, inferior vena cava"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the splenic and superior mesenteric veins join behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein.","Follow portal blood into the liver: branches in the portal triads open into sinusoids, which run toward the central vein of each lobule.","Central veins drain into hepatic veins, which empty into the inferior vena cava."],"why":"The portal vein forms behind the neck of the pancreas from the splenic and superior mesenteric veins, with the inferior mesenteric vein usually draining into the splenic vein. It enters the liver at the porta hepatis and branches with the hepatic artery and bile ducts in the portal triads. Blood from both vessels mixes in the sinusoids, flows past hepatocytes toward the central vein, and leaves through the hepatic veins into the inferior vena cava. In cirrhosis, resistance in the sinusoids raises pressure all the way back to the spleen.","whyNot":["Correct.","Portal triads are where blood enters the lobule. Blood does not return to them after the central veins.","Central veins lie downstream of the sinusoids. Blood reaches them only after passing hepatocytes.","Central veins drain into the hepatic veins, not the other way around."],"study":{"topics":["Portal venous system","Blood flow through the hepatic lobule"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-2"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C10","system":"GI","dx":"Carbon tetrachloride hepatotoxicity in an industrial worker","codes":["GI-13-C4"],"case":"A 36-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department because of a 2-day history of nausea, vomiting, and right upper quadrant pain, followed by yellowing of the eyes. Three days ago, she spilled carbon tetrachloride while degreasing metal parts in a poorly ventilated workshop. She drinks heavily on weekends. Temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 102/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 114/72 mm Hg. Physical examination shows scleral icterus and tender hepatomegaly. Laboratory studies show ALT 3850 U/L (normal 10 to 40), AST 4620 U/L (normal 10 to 40), total bilirubin 4.2 mg/dL (normal 0.1 to 1.2), prothrombin time 18.0 seconds (normal 11 to 13.5), and creatinine 1.9 mg/dL (normal 0.6 to 1.1). Serum acetaminophen is undetectable, and viral hepatitis serologies are negative. Liver biopsy shows centrilobular necrosis with fatty change. Diagnosis: carbon tetrachloride hepatotoxicity.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C10-Q1","stem":"Which of the following is the initial step in this solvent's injury to her hepatocytes?","options":["Direct dissolution of hepatocyte membranes by unchanged solvent","Glucuronidation to a reactive acyl glucuronide metabolite","Cytochrome P450 conversion to a trichloromethyl free radical","Immune attack after the solvent binds hepatocyte proteins as a hapten"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that carbon tetrachloride itself is fairly inert and must be activated by the liver.","Identify the activating enzyme: cytochrome P450, mainly CYP2E1, which forms the trichloromethyl radical.","Follow the damage: the radical starts lipid peroxidation of membranes."],"why":"Carbon tetrachloride is the classic example of free radical liver injury. Cytochrome P450, mostly CYP2E1 in zone 3 hepatocytes, converts it to the trichloromethyl free radical. The radical reacts with fatty acids in membranes and starts a chain of lipid peroxidation that damages the endoplasmic reticulum, mitochondria, and plasma membrane. Damage to the endoplasmic reticulum halts apoprotein synthesis, so triglyceride cannot be exported as lipoprotein, and fat collects. Severe injury ends in centrilobular necrosis. Alcohol induces CYP2E1, so heavy drinkers are more vulnerable.","whyNot":["Carbon tetrachloride can depress the nervous system as a solvent, but liver injury needs metabolic activation. Damage centered in zone 3, where CYP2E1 is concentrated, shows this.","Glucuronidation is a phase II reaction that usually detoxifies compounds. Acyl glucuronides form from carboxylic acid drugs, not from carbon tetrachloride.","Correct.","Hapten-driven immune injury causes some drug hepatitis that appears after a delay and often with rash or eosinophilia. Carbon tetrachloride injury is direct and dose-dependent."],"study":{"topics":["Carbon tetrachloride and free radical injury","Lipid peroxidation"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C4"]}},{"id":"GI-C10-Q2","stem":"Her physician notes that chronic heavy alcohol use also raises the risk of liver injury from acetaminophen. Which of the following best explains this added risk?","options":["More glucuronide conjugates that precipitate within bile canaliculi","More NAPQI formed by CYP2E1 and less glutathione to conjugate it","Higher glutathione levels that convert NAPQI into a toxic adduct","Slower CYP2E1 activity that prolongs exposure to the parent drug"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the usual fate of acetaminophen: most is safely conjugated with glucuronide or sulfate (phase II).","Recall that a small fraction is oxidized by CYP2E1 (phase I) to the toxic metabolite NAPQI, which glutathione conjugates and neutralizes.","Apply chronic alcohol: CYP2E1 is induced and glutathione stores are low, so more NAPQI is made and less is neutralized."],"why":"Phase I reactions, mainly by cytochrome P450 enzymes, add or expose reactive groups. Phase II reactions join the drug to glucuronide, sulfate, or glutathione so it can be excreted. Acetaminophen is mostly handled by glucuronidation and sulfation, and a small amount is oxidized by CYP2E1 to NAPQI. Glutathione normally conjugates NAPQI. When glutathione runs out, NAPQI binds cell proteins and kills zone 3 hepatocytes. Chronic alcohol use induces CYP2E1 and lowers glutathione stores, so injury occurs at lower doses. N-acetylcysteine treats overdose by restoring glutathione.","whyNot":["Glucuronide conjugates are water soluble and excreted in urine and bile. They are the safe pathway, not the source of injury.","Correct.","Glutathione neutralizes NAPQI. Higher glutathione would protect the liver, which is why N-acetylcysteine is the antidote.","Chronic alcohol induces CYP2E1 rather than slowing it. The parent drug itself is not the toxic agent."],"study":{"topics":["Phase I and phase II metabolism","Acetaminophen toxicity"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C4"]}},{"id":"GI-C10-Q3","stem":"She progresses to acute liver failure and receives a liver transplant. Two weeks later, Doppler ultrasonography shows thrombosis of the hepatic artery of the graft, with normal portal venous flow. Which of the following structures in the graft is at greatest risk of ischemic necrosis?","options":["Periportal (zone 1) hepatocytes","Centrilobular (zone 3) hepatocytes","Sinusoidal Kupffer cells","Intrahepatic bile duct epithelium"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the liver's dual supply: most blood flow comes from the portal vein, the rest from the hepatic artery, and both mix in the sinusoids.","Recognize that with portal flow intact, hepatocytes still receive blood through the sinusoids.","Identify the structure supplied only by the hepatic artery: the bile ducts, through the peribiliary plexus."],"why":"The liver gets about 75% of its blood flow from the portal vein and about 25% from the hepatic artery, and the two sources each supply roughly half of its oxygen. Both empty into the sinusoids, so hepatocytes can survive on portal blood alone, which is why liver infarction is uncommon. The bile ducts are different. They are supplied only by arterial branches that form a plexus around them. After hepatic artery thrombosis, especially in a transplanted liver that has no collateral arteries, the bile ducts become ischemic and can necrose, leak, or narrow.","whyNot":["Zone 1 hepatocytes sit nearest the portal triad and receive the most oxygenated sinusoidal blood. With portal flow intact they are well supplied.","Zone 3 hepatocytes are the most sensitive to a general drop in hepatic blood flow or oxygen, as in shock. Here portal blood still reaches the sinusoids, and the bile ducts depend entirely on the artery.","Kupffer cells line the sinusoids and are bathed in portal blood. They are not at special risk.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Dual blood supply of the liver","Arterial supply of the bile ducts"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-3"]}},{"id":"GI-C10-Q4","stem":"Her biopsy shows necrosis centered on the central veins, with surviving hepatocytes around the portal triads. Which of the following best explains why hepatocytes in the injured zone are most affected?","options":["Lowest oxygen tension and most cytochrome P450 activity","Most Kupffer cells, which release the toxic free radical","Highest oxygen tension and greatest glutathione content","Closest contact with portal blood carrying the absorbed toxin"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["Picture the hepatic acinus: zone 1 lies next to the portal triad, and zone 3 surrounds the central vein.","Recall that blood loses oxygen as it flows from zone 1 to zone 3.","Recall that zone 3 hepatocytes hold the most cytochrome P450, so toxins that need activation do their damage there."],"why":"In the hepatic acinus, blood enters at the portal triad (zone 1) and flows through the sinusoids to the central vein (zone 3). Hepatocytes extract oxygen along the way, so zone 3 cells live at the lowest oxygen tension and are first to die in ischemia. Zone 3 cells also have the most cytochrome P450, including CYP2E1, so toxins that are activated by P450, such as carbon tetrachloride and acetaminophen, injure them most. Zone 1 cells, rich in oxygen, are the first hit by toxins that arrive directly in portal blood and need no activation.","whyNot":["Correct.","Kupffer cells are found throughout the sinusoids and do not activate carbon tetrachloride. The radical forms inside hepatocytes.","High oxygen and plenty of glutathione describe zone 1 hepatocytes, which survived here.","Zone 1 cells meet portal blood first. Toxins that need no activation injure zone 1 most."],"study":{"topics":["Zones of the hepatic acinus","Zone 3 vulnerability"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-9"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C11","system":"GI","dx":"Autoimmune hepatitis type 1","codes":["GI-13-C4"],"case":"A 24-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of fatigue, joint aches, and yellow discoloration of her eyes. She takes levothyroxine for Hashimoto thyroiditis and no other medications or supplements, and she does not drink alcohol. Temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 82/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 116/72 mm Hg. Physical examination shows scleral icterus and mild tender hepatomegaly. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin concentration of 13.2 g/dL (normal 12.0 to 16.0), ALT 642 U/L (normal 7 to 56), AST 588 U/L (normal 10 to 40), alkaline phosphatase 148 U/L (normal 40 to 130), total bilirubin 4.1 mg/dL (normal 0.1 to 1.2), and serum IgG 2,650 mg/dL (normal 700 to 1,600). Serologic tests for hepatitis A, B, and C are negative. A liver biopsy is performed. Diagnosis: autoimmune hepatitis type 1.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C11-Q1","stem":"Serum autoantibody testing is performed. Antibodies against which of the following antigens are most likely to be present?","options":["Cytochrome P450 2D6","Filamentous actin","Tissue transglutaminase","Gastric H+/K+ ATPase"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.4","dok":2,"approach":["Note the young woman with another autoimmune disease, high IgG, and a hepatitis pattern with negative viral serology: autoimmune hepatitis.","Recall that type 1 is marked by antinuclear and anti-smooth muscle antibodies, and type 2 by anti-LKM1.","Name the antigen that anti-smooth muscle antibodies recognize."],"why":"Type 1 autoimmune hepatitis is defined by antinuclear antibodies and anti-smooth muscle antibodies. Anti-smooth muscle antibodies mainly recognize filamentous actin (F-actin), which is why they are also called anti-actin antibodies. The antibodies are markers of the disease; the liver damage itself is driven mainly by T cells. A high serum IgG is another typical finding.","whyNot":["Anti-liver-kidney microsomal type 1 antibodies target cytochrome P450 2D6. They define autoimmune hepatitis type 2, which is seen mostly in children and adolescents.","Correct.","Antibodies to tissue transglutaminase mark celiac disease, which injures the small bowel and causes malabsorption.","Antibodies to the H+/K+ ATPase of parietal cells mark autoimmune gastritis, which causes atrophic body gastritis and pernicious anemia."],"study":{"topics":["Autoantibodies in autoimmune hepatitis","Type 1 versus type 2 autoimmune hepatitis"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-C4"]}},{"id":"GI-C11-Q2","stem":"Her physician considers drug-induced liver injury, but she has taken no acetaminophen, isoniazid, or other hepatotoxic drugs. Which of the following findings on the liver biopsy is most likely and best distinguishes her disease from acetaminophen toxicity?","options":["Interface hepatitis with portal plasma cell infiltrates","Centrilobular coagulative necrosis with little inflammation","Granulomatous destruction of interlobular bile ducts","Macrovesicular steatosis with Mallory-Denk bodies"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C4","obj":"GI-13-C.9","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize autoimmune hepatitis from the high IgG, other autoimmunity, and negative viral tests.","Recall its lesion: inflammation spilling from the portal tract into the lobule (interface hepatitis) rich in plasma cells.","Contrast this with acetaminophen injury, which is zone 3 necrosis with sparse inflammation."],"why":"Autoimmune hepatitis shows interface hepatitis: lymphocytes and many plasma cells cross the limiting plate and destroy periportal hepatocytes. The plasma cells match the high serum IgG. Acetaminophen and other toxic injuries kill zone 3 hepatocytes around the central vein and cause little inflammation. Isoniazid hepatitis looks like viral hepatitis on biopsy, so history and serology matter as well.","whyNot":["Correct.","Coagulative necrosis around the central vein (zone 3) with little inflammation is the pattern of acetaminophen toxicity. Zone 3 hepatocytes have the most cytochrome P450 2E1 and the least oxygen.","Granulomatous destruction of small interlobular bile ducts is the lesion of primary biliary cholangitis, which causes a cholestatic pattern with a high alkaline phosphatase.","Large fat droplets with Mallory-Denk bodies point to alcohol-related or metabolic liver disease. She does not drink, and her pattern is a high-transaminase hepatitis."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of autoimmune hepatitis","Acetaminophen zone 3 necrosis"],"comps":["GI-13-C4","GI-2"]}},{"id":"GI-C11-Q3","stem":"Urinalysis shows bilirubin and an increased concentration of urobilinogen. Which of the following best explains the increased urine urobilinogen in this patient?","options":["Accelerated heme breakdown from red cell hemolysis","Complete obstruction of bile flow into the duodenum","Renal conversion of filtered conjugated bilirubin","Impaired hepatic reuptake of absorbed urobilinogen"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that gut bacteria make urobilinogen, some of which is absorbed into portal blood and normally taken back up by the liver.","Hepatocyte injury reduces that reuptake, so more urobilinogen passes into the systemic blood and urine.","The bilirubin in her urine shows a conjugated component, which fits liver injury and argues against pure hemolysis."],"why":"Bile still reaches the gut in hepatocellular injury, so bacteria keep making urobilinogen. Part of it is absorbed into portal blood, and a healthy liver takes most of it back up and re-excretes it in bile. Injured hepatocytes clear it poorly, so more spills into the urine. Conjugated bilirubin leaking from damaged cells is water soluble and also appears in the urine. Hemolysis raises urobilinogen without urine bilirubin, and complete obstruction makes urobilinogen disappear.","whyNot":["Hemolysis raises urine urobilinogen by increasing bilirubin production, but urine bilirubin stays negative because the extra bilirubin is unconjugated. Her hemoglobin is normal and her urine contains bilirubin.","Complete obstruction stops bilirubin from reaching the gut, so no urobilinogen is formed and urine urobilinogen falls to zero.","Urobilinogen is made by gut bacteria from bilirubin in the intestinal lumen. The kidney does not convert bilirubin to urobilinogen.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Urine bilirubin and urobilinogen patterns","Enterohepatic circulation of urobilinogen"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C1"]}},{"id":"GI-C11-Q4","stem":"Her prothrombin time is 18 seconds (normal 11 to 13.5), and the platelet count is 240,000/mm3 (normal 150,000 to 400,000). The prothrombin time does not change after an intravenous dose of vitamin K. Which of the following best explains the prolonged prothrombin time?","options":["Reduced hepatocyte synthesis of clotting factors","Consumption of clotting factors in disseminated coagulation","Impaired micellar absorption of vitamin K from the gut","Circulating autoantibody inhibitor directed at factor VIII"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.5","dok":3,"approach":["The prothrombin time depends mostly on factor VII, plus factors X, V, II, and fibrinogen, all made by hepatocytes.","Separate a vitamin K problem from a synthesis problem by giving vitamin K by vein: only a vitamin K deficit corrects.","No correction with a normal platelet count means the liver cannot make the factors."],"why":"Hepatocytes make almost all clotting factors. Factor VII has the shortest half-life, about 6 hours, so the prothrombin time rises within a day or so of severe liver injury. Giving vitamin K by vein corrects a prolonged prothrombin time caused by poor absorption, but not one caused by failing hepatocytes. A prothrombin time that does not correct marks severe hepatocyte dysfunction, along with a low albumin and a high ammonia.","whyNot":["Correct.","Disseminated intravascular coagulation consumes factors and platelets together. Her platelet count is normal, and nothing suggests a trigger.","Poor absorption of vitamin K would be corrected by giving vitamin K intravenously, which bypasses the gut. Her prothrombin time did not change.","Factor VIII is in the intrinsic pathway, so an inhibitor prolongs the PTT, not the prothrombin time."],"study":{"topics":["Synthetic function of the liver","Prothrombin time in liver failure"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C12","system":"GI","dx":"Primary biliary cholangitis","codes":["GI-13-C1"],"case":"A 52-year-old woman comes to the physician because of an 8-month history of generalized itching that is worse at night, and fatigue. She also has dry eyes and a dry mouth. She does not drink alcohol and takes no medications. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows excoriations on the arms and back, yellow plaques on both upper eyelids, and mild hepatomegaly. Laboratory studies show alkaline phosphatase 486 U/L (normal 40 to 130), GGT 390 U/L (normal 9 to 48), ALT 68 U/L (normal 7 to 56), total bilirubin 1.4 mg/dL (normal 0.1 to 1.2), and total cholesterol 310 mg/dL (desirable below 200). Ultrasonography shows normal extrahepatic bile ducts without dilation. Serologic tests for autoantibodies are sent. Diagnosis: primary biliary cholangitis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C12-Q1","stem":"A liver biopsy is performed. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Bile ductular proliferation with bile lakes and portal edema","Concentric onion-skin fibrosis around medium-sized bile ducts","Granulomatous destruction of small interlobular bile ducts","Periportal hepatocytes with PAS-positive cytoplasmic globules"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C1","obj":"GI-13-C.2","dok":2,"approach":["A middle-aged woman with pruritus, a high alkaline phosphatase, and normal extrahepatic ducts has intrahepatic cholestasis.","Primary biliary cholangitis targets the small interlobular ducts inside the portal tracts.","Its signature lesion is lymphocytic and granulomatous destruction of these ducts (the florid duct lesion)."],"why":"Primary biliary cholangitis is an autoimmune attack on small intrahepatic bile ducts. Lymphocytes and sometimes granulomas surround and destroy the interlobular ducts, and the ducts slowly disappear (ductopenia). Primary sclerosing cholangitis is different: it affects larger intrahepatic and extrahepatic ducts with onion-skin fibrosis, is linked to ulcerative colitis, and raises the risk of cholangiocarcinoma. Her dry eyes and mouth fit the Sjogren syndrome that often accompanies primary biliary cholangitis.","whyNot":["Ductular proliferation, portal edema, and bile lakes are the picture of large duct (extrahepatic) obstruction. Her ultrasound shows no duct dilation.","Concentric onion-skin fibrosis around larger ducts is the lesion of primary sclerosing cholangitis, which usually affects men with ulcerative colitis and causes beaded ducts on cholangiography.","Correct.","PAS-positive globules in periportal hepatocytes are misfolded alpha-1 antitrypsin, seen in alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency."],"study":{"topics":["Primary biliary versus primary sclerosing cholangitis"],"comps":["GI-13-C1","GI-11"]}},{"id":"GI-C12-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains her serum cholesterol concentration and eyelid plaques?","options":["Defective LDL receptor-mediated uptake by hepatocytes","Increased intestinal absorption of dietary cholesterol","Retention of cholesterol normally excreted in bile","Loss of lipoprotein lipase activity in capillary beds"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C1","obj":"GI-13-C.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the cholestatic pattern: high alkaline phosphatase and GGT, pruritus, normal extrahepatic ducts.","Recall that bile is the body's main route for removing cholesterol, as free cholesterol and as bile acids.","When bile flow fails, biliary lipids back up into blood and are deposited in skin as xanthomas."],"why":"The liver gets rid of cholesterol by secreting it into bile, both unchanged and after converting it to bile acids. In cholestasis this outflow is blocked, and biliary cholesterol and phospholipid reflux into the blood, partly as an abnormal particle called lipoprotein X. Serum cholesterol rises and is deposited in the skin as xanthelasma and xanthomas. A raised GGT along with the alkaline phosphatase confirms the alkaline phosphatase comes from the liver, not bone. Retained bile acids also contribute to the itching.","whyNot":["LDL receptor defects (familial hypercholesterolemia) cause high LDL from birth with tendon xanthomas. Her cholesterol rose with a cholestatic liver disease.","Cholesterol absorption needs bile salt micelles. Cholestasis lowers intestinal bile salts, so absorption falls rather than rises.","Correct.","Lipoprotein lipase deficiency raises chylomicrons and triglycerides and causes eruptive xanthomas and pancreatitis, not cholestatic hypercholesterolemia."],"study":{"topics":["Laboratory pattern of cholestasis","Xanthomas in cholestasis"],"comps":["GI-13-C1","GI-8"]}},{"id":"GI-C12-Q3","stem":"Her disease progresses over several years with persistent cholestasis. Which of the following is she at greatest risk of developing as a result of reduced bile flow?","options":["Perifollicular hemorrhages","Impaired dark adaptation","Angular cheilitis and glossitis","Megaloblastic anemia"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.2","dok":3,"approach":["Bile salts form micelles that carry dietary fat and the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K to the enterocyte.","Chronic cholestasis therefore causes deficiency of these four vitamins, not of water-soluble vitamins.","Match the deficiency to its sign: vitamin A deficiency impairs rhodopsin regeneration, causing night blindness."],"why":"Vitamins A, D, E, and K dissolve in fat and are absorbed inside bile salt micelles. Low bile flow in long-standing cholestasis causes fat malabsorption and deficiency of all four. Vitamin A is needed to make rhodopsin, so deficiency first shows as poor vision in dim light. The other deficiencies are osteomalacia (D), hemolytic anemia with neuropathy (E), and a prolonged prothrombin time (K). Water-soluble vitamins are absorbed without micelles.","whyNot":["Perifollicular hemorrhages are a sign of scurvy (vitamin C deficiency). Vitamin C is water soluble and absorbed without bile.","Correct.","Angular cheilitis and glossitis point to riboflavin or other B vitamin deficiency, which does not depend on bile salts.","Megaloblastic anemia comes from folate or vitamin B12 deficiency. These water-soluble vitamins do not need bile for absorption; B12 needs intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum."],"study":{"topics":["Fat-soluble vitamin malabsorption","Vitamin A and night blindness"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C12-Q4","stem":"The autoantibody that is most characteristic of her disease is detected. Which of the following is the target antigen of this antibody?","options":["Thyroid peroxidase in follicular cells","Intrinsic factor secreted by gastric parietal cells","Double-stranded DNA in cell nuclei","E2 subunit of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.4","dok":2,"approach":["The characteristic antibody in primary biliary cholangitis is antimitochondrial antibody, present in about 95% of patients.","Recall its target: the E2 component of pyruvate dehydrogenase, an enzyme of the inner mitochondrial membrane.","Rule out antibodies that belong to other autoimmune diseases."],"why":"Antimitochondrial antibodies are the serologic hallmark of primary biliary cholangitis. Their main target is the E2 subunit of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex (PDC-E2). Bile duct epithelial cells display this antigen in a form that attracts an immune attack, which is thought to explain why the damage centers on small bile ducts. A positive antimitochondrial antibody with a cholestatic pattern is usually enough for the diagnosis without biopsy.","whyNot":["Anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies mark Hashimoto thyroiditis. They can occur with other autoimmune diseases but are not characteristic of this one.","Antibodies to intrinsic factor are found in autoimmune gastritis and cause vitamin B12 malabsorption and pernicious anemia.","Anti-double-stranded DNA antibodies are specific for systemic lupus erythematosus and track with lupus nephritis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Antimitochondrial antibody","Autoantibodies in GI disease"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-C1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C13","system":"GI","dx":"Gilbert syndrome unmasked by fasting","codes":["GI-13-C3"],"case":"A 19-year-old man comes to the physician because his roommate noticed that his eyes looked yellow. Over the past 3 days he had a febrile respiratory illness and ate very little. He has no abdominal pain, and his urine is normal in color. He takes no medications and does not drink alcohol. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 76/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 118/70 mm Hg. Physical examination shows mild scleral icterus and no hepatosplenomegaly. Laboratory studies show total bilirubin 3.2 mg/dL (normal 0.1 to 1.2), direct bilirubin 0.2 mg/dL (normal 0 to 0.3), hemoglobin 15.1 g/dL (normal 13.5 to 17.5), and a reticulocyte count of 1% (normal 0.5 to 2.5). Haptoglobin, LDH, ALT, AST, and alkaline phosphatase are within normal limits. Diagnosis: Gilbert syndrome unmasked by fasting.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C13-Q1","stem":"His newborn nephew develops severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia that persists despite phototherapy and progresses to kernicterus. The nephew is found to have Crigler-Najjar syndrome type I. Which of the following best explains the difference in severity between the two conditions?","options":["Normal versus accelerated red cell destruction","Normal versus defective canalicular MRP2 transport","Partial versus absent hepatic UGT1A1 activity","Normal versus deficient biliverdin reductase activity"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-C3","obj":"GI-13-C.6","dok":3,"approach":["Both patients have isolated unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia without hemolysis, so the problem is conjugation.","UGT1A1 attaches glucuronic acid to bilirubin. Gilbert syndrome has mildly reduced activity; Crigler-Najjar type I has none.","Relate the amount of residual enzyme to how high bilirubin rises and whether it reaches the brain."],"why":"Gilbert syndrome is common and benign. Reduced expression of UGT1A1, often from a promoter variant, leaves roughly a third of normal activity, so bilirubin rises only mildly and mainly during fasting, stress, or illness. Crigler-Najjar type I has no UGT1A1 activity, so unconjugated bilirubin climbs high in the newborn period, crosses the immature blood-brain barrier, and causes kernicterus. Crigler-Najjar type II has partial deficiency and a milder course.","whyNot":["Neither condition involves hemolysis. Both are defects in bilirubin conjugation; this patient's hemoglobin, reticulocyte count, haptoglobin, and LDH are normal.","MRP2 moves conjugated bilirubin into bile. Its defect causes Dubin-Johnson syndrome, a conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, which neither patient has.","Correct.","Biliverdin reductase converts biliverdin to bilirubin. A deficiency would lower bilirubin production, not cause unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia."],"study":{"topics":["Gilbert versus Crigler-Najjar syndromes","UGT1A1 function"],"comps":["GI-13-C3","GI-9"]}},{"id":"GI-C13-Q2","stem":"His urine remains normal in color despite the hyperbilirubinemia. Which of the following describes the form of his excess bilirubin in plasma that best explains this finding?","options":["Conjugated to glucuronate and loosely bound to albumin","Free in plasma and reabsorbed by proximal tubules","Bound to haptoglobin and cleared by macrophages","Bound to albumin and not filtered"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.1","dok":2,"approach":["His bilirubin is almost all indirect (unconjugated), since the direct fraction is normal.","Unconjugated bilirubin is water insoluble and travels in plasma tightly bound to albumin.","The albumin-bilirubin complex is too large to pass the glomerular filter, so no bilirubin enters the urine."],"why":"Macrophages break heme into biliverdin with heme oxygenase, and biliverdin reductase converts it to unconjugated bilirubin. Unconjugated bilirubin is not water soluble, so it is carried to the liver bound to albumin and cannot be filtered by the kidney. That is why jaundice from unconjugated bilirubin is called acholuric. After hepatocytes conjugate it with UGT1A1, MRP2 pumps it into bile, and gut bacteria turn it into urobilinogen and stercobilin.","whyNot":["Conjugated bilirubin is water soluble and only loosely bound to albumin, so when it is high it is filtered and appears in urine. His excess bilirubin is unconjugated.","Unconjugated bilirubin in plasma is almost entirely bound to albumin. It is not filtered and then reabsorbed.","Haptoglobin binds free hemoglobin, not bilirubin. Hemoglobin-haptoglobin complexes are cleared by macrophages.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Bilirubin transport on albumin","Bilirubin metabolism pathway"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C3"]}},{"id":"GI-C13-Q3","stem":"Once his bilirubin is conjugated, hepatocytes secrete it into bile canaliculi. Within the hepatic lobule, which of the following best describes the flow of this bile?","options":["Into the sinusoid lumen, then to the central vein","Toward the portal triad, opposite to sinusoidal blood flow","Toward the central vein, in the same direction as sinusoidal blood flow","Into the space of Disse, then into hepatic lymphatic vessels"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["Picture the classic lobule: portal triads at the corners and a central vein in the middle.","Blood from the portal vein and hepatic artery flows through sinusoids from the triad to the central vein.","Canaliculi between hepatocytes carry bile in the opposite direction, through the canals of Hering to the bile ductules of the portal triad."],"why":"In the hepatic lobule, blood enters at the portal triad and flows through sinusoids to the central vein. Bile canaliculi, formed by grooves between adjacent hepatocytes and sealed by tight junctions, carry bile the other way. They drain through the canals of Hering into the bile ductules of the portal triad. The two flows run in opposite directions and never mix in health.","whyNot":["Bile is kept apart from blood by tight junctions around the canaliculi, and it drains toward the portal triad rather than into the sinusoids.","Correct.","Blood moves from the portal triad toward the central vein. Bile moves the other way, so it does not travel with the blood toward the central vein.","The space of Disse lies between hepatocytes and sinusoidal endothelium and drains to lymphatics, but it carries plasma filtrate, not bile."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatic lobule organization","Bile canaliculi and canals of Hering"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-3"]}},{"id":"GI-C13-Q4","stem":"During his 3 days of poor intake, his serum beta-hydroxybutyrate concentration rose. Which of the following best explains why his liver, which produced these ketone bodies, did not oxidize them for its own energy?","options":["Ketone bodies cannot cross the hepatocyte plasma membrane","Hepatocytes lack succinyl-CoA:3-ketoacid CoA transferase","Hepatic mitochondria lack carnitine palmitoyltransferase I","Hepatocytes lack mitochondrial HMG-CoA synthase activity"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.5","dok":2,"approach":["In fasting, the liver oxidizes fatty acids to acetyl-CoA and turns the excess into acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate.","To use acetoacetate, a tissue must first convert it to acetoacetyl-CoA with succinyl-CoA:3-ketoacid CoA transferase (thiophorase).","The liver lacks this enzyme, so it exports ketones to other tissues."],"why":"In fasting, liver glycogen runs low within about a day, and blood glucose then depends on gluconeogenesis from alanine, lactate, and glycerol. At the same time, the liver oxidizes fatty acids and converts the extra acetyl-CoA into ketone bodies. Muscle, heart, and brain use ketones after converting acetoacetate to acetoacetyl-CoA with thiophorase. The liver lacks thiophorase, so it can make ketones but not use them, and they are exported as fuel.","whyNot":["Ketone bodies move freely across cell membranes. That is how they leave the liver for muscle and brain.","Correct.","Carnitine palmitoyltransferase I brings fatty acids into hepatic mitochondria. The liver needs it to make ketones in the first place.","Hepatocytes have mitochondrial HMG-CoA synthase. It is the rate-limiting enzyme of ketone body synthesis, which is why the liver can make ketones."],"study":{"topics":["Ketogenesis and ketone use","Fed and fasting metabolism"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-9"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C14","system":"GI","dx":"Dubin-Johnson syndrome","codes":["GI-13-C"],"case":"A 26-year-old woman comes to the physician because of yellow eyes for 4 weeks. She has had brief episodes of mild jaundice since adolescence. Three months ago she started an oral contraceptive. She has no pruritus or abdominal pain and does not drink alcohol. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows mild scleral icterus; the liver and spleen are not enlarged. Laboratory studies show total bilirubin 3.8 mg/dL (normal 0.1 to 1.2), direct bilirubin 2.6 mg/dL (normal 0 to 0.3), and a hemoglobin concentration of 13.6 g/dL (normal 12.0 to 16.0). ALT, AST, alkaline phosphatase, and GGT are within normal limits. Ultrasonography shows a normal gallbladder and no bile duct dilation. A liver biopsy is performed. Diagnosis: Dubin-Johnson syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C14-Q1","stem":"Which of the following biopsy findings is most likely and distinguishes her condition from Rotor syndrome?","options":["Coarse dark pigment within hepatocyte lysosomes","Hemosiderin granules in periportal hepatocytes and bile ducts","Copper-associated protein granules in periportal hepatocytes","Normal hepatocyte morphology with no pigment accumulation"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-C","obj":"GI-13-C.11","dok":2,"approach":["Isolated conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with normal liver enzymes and ducts suggests an inherited excretion defect.","Dubin-Johnson syndrome (MRP2 defect) and Rotor syndrome look alike in the blood.","Only Dubin-Johnson syndrome leaves dark pigment in the liver."],"why":"Dubin-Johnson syndrome is an autosomal recessive defect in MRP2, the canalicular pump that moves conjugated bilirubin and other organic anions into bile. Conjugated bilirubin backs up into the blood. A dark, melanin-like pigment collects in hepatocyte lysosomes and makes the liver look black. Rotor syndrome causes a similar but milder conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with a normal, unpigmented liver. Estrogens and pregnancy reduce biliary excretion further, which is why her jaundice worsened on an oral contraceptive.","whyNot":["Correct.","Hemosiderin in periportal hepatocytes is the pattern of hereditary hemochromatosis, which raises transaminases and ferritin rather than causing isolated conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.","Periportal copper and copper-associated protein are seen in Wilson disease and in long-standing cholestasis. Neither fits her isolated conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with normal enzymes.","A normal, unpigmented liver is the finding in Rotor syndrome, the milder look-alike of Dubin-Johnson syndrome."],"study":{"topics":["Dubin-Johnson versus Rotor syndrome","MRP2 and canalicular transport"],"comps":["GI-13-C","GI-9"]}},{"id":"GI-C14-Q2","stem":"Which of the following urinalysis results is most likely in this patient?","options":["Bilirubin absent; urobilinogen increased","Bilirubin absent; urobilinogen normal","Bilirubin present; urobilinogen absent","Bilirubin present; urobilinogen normal"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Her raised bilirubin is mostly direct (conjugated), and conjugated bilirubin is water soluble and filtered at the glomerulus, so urine bilirubin is present.","Urobilinogen depends on bilirubin reaching the gut. Her ducts are open, so bacteria still make urobilinogen.","With normal liver cells to reclaim it, urine urobilinogen stays about normal."],"why":"Conjugated bilirubin is water soluble and only loosely bound to albumin, so it passes into the urine when its blood level rises. In Dubin-Johnson syndrome bile still reaches the intestine, and hepatocytes are otherwise healthy, so urobilinogen formation and reuptake are about normal. This combination separates it from hemolysis (no urine bilirubin, high urobilinogen) and complete obstruction (urine bilirubin with absent urobilinogen).","whyNot":["No urine bilirubin with high urobilinogen is the pattern of hemolysis, in which the extra bilirubin is unconjugated. Her hemoglobin is normal and her bilirubin is mostly direct.","Absent urine bilirubin fits normal people and unconjugated causes such as Gilbert syndrome. Her bilirubin is mostly conjugated.","Bilirubin with no urobilinogen means bile is not reaching the gut, as in complete obstruction. Her ducts are normal and bile still flows.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Urine findings in conjugated hyperbilirubinemia"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C"]}},{"id":"GI-C14-Q3","stem":"Her normal ultrasound argued against biliary obstruction. Progressive obstruction by a tumor at which of the following sites would cause jaundice together with a distended gallbladder?","options":["Common hepatic duct proximal to the cystic duct junction","Cystic duct at the neck of the gallbladder","Distal common bile duct within the pancreatic head","Right hepatic duct at the porta hepatis"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.3","dok":3,"approach":["Trace bile: right and left hepatic ducts to the common hepatic duct, which joins the cystic duct to form the common bile duct.","The common bile duct runs through the head of the pancreas to the ampulla of Vater, where it joins the pancreatic duct under the sphincter of Oddi.","Only a block below the cystic duct junction both stops all bile flow and lets bile back up into the gallbladder."],"why":"Bile leaves the liver through the right and left hepatic ducts at the porta hepatis, which join as the common hepatic duct. The cystic duct from the gallbladder joins it to form the common bile duct. This duct passes behind the duodenum and through the pancreatic head to the ampulla of Vater. Slow obstruction of the distal common bile duct, as by pancreatic head cancer, blocks all bile flow and lets the gallbladder fill and distend. This is the basis of Courvoisier sign.","whyNot":["Obstruction of the common hepatic duct above the cystic duct causes jaundice, but bile cannot back up into the gallbladder, so it stays small.","Cystic duct obstruction distends the gallbladder, but bile from the liver still flows down the common bile duct, so there is no jaundice.","Correct.","Blocking one hepatic duct leaves the other lobe draining normally, so jaundice usually does not occur, and the gallbladder is not distended."],"study":{"topics":["Biliary tree anatomy","Sites of bile duct obstruction"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-13-C1"]}},{"id":"GI-C14-Q4","stem":"Which of the following sets of laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?","options":["Normal albumin, normal PT, normal ammonia","Normal albumin, prolonged PT, high ammonia","Low albumin, normal PT, normal ammonia","Low albumin, prolonged PT, high ammonia"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.5","dok":2,"approach":["Dubin-Johnson syndrome affects one transport step: moving conjugated bilirubin into bile.","Synthetic and metabolic work of the liver (albumin, clotting factors, urea cycle) is untouched.","So albumin, prothrombin time, and ammonia stay normal, unlike in liver failure."],"why":"The liver makes albumin and most clotting factors and removes ammonia by turning it into urea. When hepatocytes fail, albumin falls, the prothrombin time lengthens, and ammonia rises, leading to edema, bleeding, and encephalopathy. Dubin-Johnson syndrome is a single transporter defect, so these functions are normal. This is why it is a benign condition that needs no treatment.","whyNot":["Correct.","A prolonged PT with high ammonia but normal albumin can occur early in acute liver failure because albumin has a long half-life. Her normal transaminases rule out such injury.","Low albumin with normal liver function suggests protein loss in the urine or gut, or malnutrition, not an inherited excretion defect.","Low albumin, a prolonged PT, and high ammonia together signal failing hepatocytes, as in cirrhosis or acute liver failure. Her hepatocytes are otherwise healthy."],"study":{"topics":["Liver synthetic and metabolic function","Benign inherited hyperbilirubinemias"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C15","system":"GI","dx":"Alcohol-related cirrhosis with spontaneous bacterial peritonitis and hepatorenal syndrome","codes":["GI-13-C2","GI-13-I1","GI-13-I2"],"case":"A 58-year-old man with a 30-year history of heavy alcohol use is brought to the emergency department because of a 1-week history of increasing abdominal girth, fever, mild diffuse abdominal pain, and decreased urine output. Temperature is 38.2 °C (100.8 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg. Physical examination shows jaundice, spider angiomas, gynecomastia, and tense ascites with mild diffuse tenderness; there is no rigidity or guarding. Serum albumin is 2.4 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5), and creatinine is 2.6 mg/dL (normal 0.7 to 1.3; 0.9 three months ago). Urine sodium is below 10 mEq/L, and urinalysis shows no protein, blood, or casts. Paracentesis shows ascitic albumin 0.8 g/dL and 620 neutrophils/mm3. Diagnosis: alcohol-related cirrhosis with spontaneous bacterial peritonitis and hepatorenal syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C15-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the serum-ascites albumin gradient in this patient?","options":["High sinusoidal pressure from portal hypertension","Exudation of protein from inflamed peritoneum","Peritoneal seeding by metastatic carcinoma","Low oncotic pressure from glomerular albumin loss"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-13-I1","obj":"GI-13-I.1","dok":3,"approach":["Calculate the gradient: serum albumin 2.4 minus ascitic albumin 0.8 equals 1.6 g/dL.","A gradient of 1.1 g/dL or more means the fluid formed from high hydrostatic pressure in the portal circulation.","In cirrhosis, fibrosis raises sinusoidal pressure; low albumin and sodium retention add to fluid build-up."],"why":"The serum-ascites albumin gradient reflects the hydrostatic pressure pushing fluid out of the sinusoids. A value of 1.1 g/dL or more indicates portal hypertension, as in cirrhosis, Budd-Chiari syndrome, or heart failure. Ascites in cirrhosis forms from high sinusoidal pressure plus low oncotic pressure from hypoalbuminemia. Splanchnic vasodilation lowers effective arterial volume, which activates renin-angiotensin-aldosterone and makes the kidney retain sodium and water.","whyNot":["Correct.","Peritoneal inflammation, as in tuberculous peritonitis, adds protein to the fluid and gives a gradient below 1.1 g/dL. Infection of portal hypertensive ascites does not change the gradient much.","Peritoneal carcinomatosis makes a protein-rich exudate with a gradient below 1.1 g/dL.","Nephrotic syndrome causes ascites through low oncotic pressure, but the gradient is below 1.1 g/dL. His urinalysis shows no protein."],"study":{"topics":["Serum-ascites albumin gradient","Mechanism of ascites in cirrhosis"],"comps":["GI-13-I1","GI-13-C5"]}},{"id":"GI-C15-Q2","stem":"Culture of the ascitic fluid grows Escherichia coli only. Which of the following best explains how this organism most likely reached the ascitic fluid?","options":["Direct inoculation during an earlier paracentesis","Rupture of a pyogenic abscess through the liver capsule","Translocation of gut bacteria into lymph and blood","Leakage of colonic flora through a perforated diverticulum"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-I2","obj":"GI-13-I.2","dok":3,"approach":["Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis is infection of ascites with no surgical source, diagnosed by 250 or more neutrophils/mm3.","It is usually one enteric gram-negative organism, most often E. coli or Klebsiella.","In cirrhosis, gut bacteria cross a leaky bowel wall into mesenteric lymph and blood and seed ascites that has poor opsonic activity."],"why":"Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis develops in cirrhotic ascites without a perforation or abscess. Bacterial overgrowth, a leaky gut wall, and impaired immune defenses let gut bacteria move into mesenteric lymph nodes and the blood. Ascites with a low protein level has little complement and opsonic activity, so the bacteria grow. Secondary peritonitis comes from a perforated or inflamed viscus, is polymicrobial, and causes a rigid abdomen. SBP is a common trigger of hepatorenal syndrome.","whyNot":["Infection from a needle puncture is rare and would more likely involve skin flora such as staphylococci, not a single enteric gram-negative rod.","A ruptured liver abscess is a cause of secondary peritonitis and would usually show an abscess on imaging and often mixed organisms.","Correct.","A perforated viscus causes secondary peritonitis: polymicrobial flora including anaerobes, and a rigid abdomen with guarding. He has a single organism and no rigidity."],"study":{"topics":["Spontaneous versus secondary peritonitis","Bacterial translocation"],"comps":["GI-13-I2","GI-12"]}},{"id":"GI-C15-Q3","stem":"His creatinine does not improve after 2 days of intravenous albumin, and kidney ultrasonography is normal. Which of the following best explains his decline in renal function?","options":["Immune complex glomerulonephritis from bacterial antigens","Renal vasoconstriction driven by splanchnic vasodilation","Allergic interstitial nephritis with eosinophiluria","Ischemic acute tubular necrosis with shed epithelial casts"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-13-C2","obj":"GI-13-C.5","dok":3,"approach":["Kidney failure in advanced cirrhosis with low urine sodium, bland urine, and no response to volume expansion is hepatorenal syndrome.","Portal hypertension causes splanchnic arterial vasodilation (nitric oxide), which lowers effective arterial blood volume.","The body responds with renin-angiotensin, sympathetic, and vasopressin activation that intensely constricts renal arteries."],"why":"Hepatorenal syndrome is a functional kidney failure. Splanchnic vessels dilate in portal hypertension, so the effective arterial volume falls even though total body fluid is high. Strong renin-angiotensin, sympathetic, and vasopressin activity then constricts the renal arteries and lowers the glomerular filtration rate. The kidneys are normal on histology and recover after liver transplant. Low urine sodium shows the tubules still work.","whyNot":["Glomerulonephritis would show hematuria, red cell casts, or protein in the urine. His urinalysis is clean.","Correct.","Interstitial nephritis is usually a reaction to a drug and shows white cells and white cell casts in the urine. Nothing suggests a drug exposure, and his urinalysis is bland.","Acute tubular necrosis damages tubules, so urine sodium is high and the urine contains muddy brown casts. His urine sodium is below 10 mEq/L and the sediment is bland."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatorenal syndrome","Splanchnic vasodilation in cirrhosis"],"comps":["GI-13-C2","GI-13-C5"]}},{"id":"GI-C15-Q4","stem":"Physical examination also shows dilated veins radiating from the umbilicus. The collateral veins that carry portal blood to these surface veins reach the anterior abdominal wall within which of the following peritoneal folds?","options":["Falciform ligament","Gastrosplenic ligament","Gastrohepatic ligament","Splenorenal ligament"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-4","obj":"GI-4.4","dok":2,"approach":["Dilated periumbilical veins (caput medusae) form when portal blood flows out through paraumbilical veins.","The paraumbilical veins run with the ligamentum teres, the remnant of the umbilical vein.","The ligamentum teres lies in the free lower edge of the falciform ligament, which runs from the liver to the umbilicus."],"why":"The falciform ligament attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and carries the ligamentum teres and the small paraumbilical veins in its free edge. In portal hypertension these veins open up and carry portal blood to the superficial veins around the umbilicus, producing caput medusae. Each of the other ligaments carries a different set of vessels: gastric arteries in the gastrohepatic, short gastrics in the gastrosplenic, and the splenic vessels with the pancreatic tail in the splenorenal.","whyNot":["Correct.","The gastrosplenic ligament carries the short gastric and left gastroepiploic vessels.","The gastrohepatic ligament, part of the lesser omentum, carries the left and right gastric vessels along the lesser curvature.","The splenorenal ligament carries the splenic artery and vein and the tail of the pancreas."],"study":{"topics":["Peritoneal ligaments and their contents","Caput medusae"],"comps":["GI-4","GI-5"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C16","system":"GI","dx":"Hypervitaminosis A with noncirrhotic portal hypertension","codes":["GI-13-C5"],"case":"A 44-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of increasing abdominal girth, dry skin, hair loss, and bone pain. For 2 years he has taken about 100,000 IU of vitamin A daily to improve his skin. He does not drink alcohol. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows dry, scaly skin, splenomegaly, and shifting dullness. Platelet count is 92,000/mm3 (normal 150,000 to 400,000). ALT is 74 U/L (normal 7 to 56), and albumin is 3.8 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5). Doppler ultrasonography shows ascites, splenomegaly, and patent portal and hepatic veins. A liver biopsy shows perisinusoidal fibrosis and hypertrophied fat-storing cells containing lipid droplets, without nodular cirrhosis. Diagnosis: hypervitaminosis A with noncirrhotic portal hypertension.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C16-Q1","stem":"He also reports daily headaches and episodes of blurred vision. Which of the following findings is most likely on further evaluation?","options":["Low serum ceruloplasmin concentration","Raised serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D concentration","Low serum vitamin B12 concentration","Raised cerebrospinal fluid opening pressure"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.7","dok":2,"approach":["Link the chronic high vitamin A intake to his skin, bone, and liver findings.","Recall that vitamin A toxicity raises intracranial pressure (pseudotumor cerebri), causing headache, papilledema, and blurred vision.","Raised intracranial pressure shows up as a high cerebrospinal fluid opening pressure on lumbar puncture."],"why":"Chronic vitamin A excess causes dry skin, hair loss, bone pain, liver injury, and raised intracranial pressure. The headache and transient blurring of vision come from idiopathic intracranial hypertension, with papilledema and a high opening pressure. Vitamin A is also strongly teratogenic. Vitamin D toxicity looks different: hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, kidney stones, and polyuria.","whyNot":["Low ceruloplasmin suggests Wilson disease, which causes liver and neuropsychiatric disease, but not this vitamin A picture or raised intracranial pressure.","Raised 25-hydroxyvitamin D would point to vitamin D excess, which causes hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, and kidney stones. He took vitamin A only.","Vitamin B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia and neuropathy, not headache with visual blurring from raised intracranial pressure.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin A toxicity","Vitamin A versus vitamin D toxicity"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-13-C5"]}},{"id":"GI-C16-Q2","stem":"Which of the following cells is responsible for the fibrosis in this patient's liver, and where is it located?","options":["Endothelial cells lining hepatic sinusoids","Kupffer cells within the sinusoid lumen","Cholangiocytes lining the canals of Hering","Stellate cells in the space of Disse"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["The biopsy describes fat-storing cells next to the sinusoids. These are hepatic stellate (Ito) cells.","Stellate cells sit in the space of Disse and normally store vitamin A in lipid droplets.","When activated, they become myofibroblasts and deposit collagen, causing perisinusoidal fibrosis."],"why":"Hepatic stellate (Ito) cells live in the space of Disse, between hepatocytes and the sinusoidal endothelium. At rest they store vitamin A in lipid droplets. Excess vitamin A loads and activates them, and activated stellate cells turn into myofibroblasts that lay down collagen in the space of Disse. This perisinusoidal fibrosis raises resistance to sinusoidal flow and can cause portal hypertension before cirrhosis develops. The same cell drives fibrosis in alcohol-related and viral liver disease.","whyNot":["Sinusoidal endothelial cells are fenestrated and lack a basement membrane. They lose their fenestrae in fibrosis but do not make the scar.","Kupffer cells are resident macrophages in the sinusoids. They release cytokines that activate stellate cells but do not lay down most of the collagen.","Cholangiocytes line the bile ductules and canals of Hering. They proliferate in biliary disease but are not the main collagen-producing cells.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatic stellate cells","Space of Disse"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-13-C2"]}},{"id":"GI-C16-Q3","stem":"Which of the following conditions causes portal hypertension at the same anatomic level as in this patient?","options":["Budd-Chiari syndrome","Portal vein thrombosis","Alcohol-related cirrhosis","Constrictive pericarditis"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C5","obj":"GI-13-C.10","dok":3,"approach":["His portal and hepatic veins are patent, so the block is inside the liver.","The biopsy shows perisinusoidal fibrosis, an intrahepatic cause.","Choose the option that also raises resistance inside the liver: cirrhosis."],"why":"Portal hypertension is classified by where the resistance lies. Prehepatic causes block the portal vein, as in thrombosis. Intrahepatic causes raise resistance within the liver, as in cirrhosis, schistosomiasis, and perisinusoidal fibrosis from vitamin A. Posthepatic causes block outflow, as in Budd-Chiari syndrome, right heart failure, and constrictive pericarditis. All levels can cause splenomegaly and varices; ascites is typical of sinusoidal and posthepatic causes.","whyNot":["Budd-Chiari syndrome is thrombosis of the hepatic veins, which blocks outflow after the liver. This is posthepatic portal hypertension.","Portal vein thrombosis blocks flow before blood reaches the liver. This is prehepatic portal hypertension.","Correct.","Constrictive pericarditis raises right atrial and hepatic vein pressure. This is posthepatic portal hypertension."],"study":{"topics":["Classification of portal hypertension"],"comps":["GI-13-C5","GI-5"]}},{"id":"GI-C16-Q4","stem":"His splenomegaly results from raised pressure transmitted back from the portal vein. Which of the following describes the normal course of the vein that carries this pressure to the spleen?","options":["Joins the inferior vena cava behind the second part of the duodenum","Joins the superior mesenteric vein behind the pancreatic neck","Joins the inferior mesenteric vein at the duodenojejunal flexure","Joins the left renal vein anterior to the abdominal aorta"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["The spleen drains through the splenic vein, which runs behind the pancreas.","The splenic vein joins the superior mesenteric vein behind the neck of the pancreas to form the portal vein.","High portal pressure is therefore transmitted directly back to the spleen, causing congestive splenomegaly."],"why":"The splenic vein runs along the back of the pancreas and joins the superior mesenteric vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein. The portal vein carries this blood to the liver sinusoids, which drain through central veins to the hepatic veins and the inferior vena cava. When resistance rises in the liver, pressure backs up through the splenic vein into the spleen. The enlarged spleen traps platelets, explaining his thrombocytopenia.","whyNot":["The splenic vein drains to the portal system, not the inferior vena cava. Portal blood reaches the vena cava only after passing through the liver.","Correct.","The inferior mesenteric vein usually drains into the splenic vein, not the reverse, and the junction is behind the pancreas.","The left renal vein crosses anterior to the aorta, but the splenic vein does not join it. A splenorenal connection exists only as a surgical shunt or a spontaneous collateral."],"study":{"topics":["Portal venous system anatomy","Congestive splenomegaly"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-13-C5"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C17","system":"GI","dx":"Esophageal variceal hemorrhage in hepatitis C cirrhosis","codes":["GI-13-J1","GI-13-C5","GI-13-L7"],"case":"A 61-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 1 hour after vomiting a large amount of bright red blood. He has no abdominal or chest pain. He has chronic hepatitis C infection, acquired through injection drug use 30 years ago and never treated, and cirrhosis diagnosed 2 years ago. Temperature is 36.6 °C (97.9 °F), pulse is 124/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 84/50 mm Hg. Physical examination shows jaundice, splenomegaly, and ascites. Hemoglobin is 7.8 g/dL (normal 13.5 to 17.5), and platelet count is 68,000/mm3 (normal 150,000 to 400,000). Upper endoscopy shows large actively bleeding varices in the distal esophagus. Diagnosis: esophageal variceal hemorrhage in hepatitis C cirrhosis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C17-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why these veins dilated?","options":["Erosive esophagitis weakening the walls of submucosal veins","Azygos blood diverted via esophageal veins into the portal vein","Portal blood diverted via the left gastric vein to the azygos","Pressure transmitted upward from an obstructed superior vena cava"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-J1","obj":"GI-13-J.1","dok":3,"approach":["Cirrhosis raises portal pressure, so portal blood seeks lower-pressure systemic routes.","The left gastric (coronary) vein connects with esophageal veins that drain to the azygos system.","Extra flow dilates the thin submucosal veins of the distal esophagus, which can rupture and bleed massively."],"why":"In portal hypertension, blood from the portal system flows backward through the left gastric vein into the esophageal venous plexus, which drains to the azygos system. The thin-walled submucosal veins of the distal esophagus dilate into varices. When they rupture, bleeding is painless and often massive. Variceal hemorrhage is one of the leading causes of death in cirrhosis, and low platelets and clotting factors make it worse.","whyNot":["Esophagitis damages mucosa and can bleed, but it does not cause the vein dilation of varices.","Flow goes from high to low pressure. In portal hypertension, blood moves from the portal side toward the systemic azygos side, not the reverse.","Correct.","Superior vena cava obstruction can cause downhill varices in the upper esophagus, but he has cirrhosis with portal hypertension and distal varices."],"study":{"topics":["Esophageal varices","Portosystemic collaterals"],"comps":["GI-13-J1","GI-5"]}},{"id":"GI-C17-Q2","stem":"Proctoscopy shows anorectal varices. These varices connect a portal tributary with the middle and inferior rectal veins. Which of the following is the portal tributary involved?","options":["Inferior vesical vein","Median sacral vein","Internal pudendal vein","Superior rectal vein"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.4","dok":2,"approach":["Portosystemic anastomoses form where portal and systemic veins meet.","The middle and inferior rectal veins drain to the internal iliac system (systemic).","The portal partner in the rectum is the superior rectal vein, a tributary of the inferior mesenteric vein."],"why":"The rectum has a portal and a systemic venous drainage. The superior rectal vein drains to the inferior mesenteric vein and then the portal system. The middle rectal vein drains to the internal iliac vein, and the inferior rectal vein drains through the internal pudendal vein to the internal iliac vein. In portal hypertension, blood flows from the superior rectal vein into these systemic veins, forming anorectal varices. The other main sites are the esophagus (left gastric to azygos) and the umbilicus (paraumbilical to abdominal wall veins).","whyNot":["The inferior vesical vein drains the bladder to the internal iliac vein. It is systemic and not part of this anastomosis.","The median sacral vein drains to the common iliac vein. It is systemic and not part of this anastomosis.","The internal pudendal vein receives the inferior rectal vein and drains to the internal iliac vein. It is on the systemic side.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Portosystemic anastomoses","Venous drainage of the rectum"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-13-J1"]}},{"id":"GI-C17-Q3","stem":"A year earlier he had palpable purpura on both legs, arthralgias, and hematuria with proteinuria. Serum C4 was low. Which of the following best explains these findings?","options":["Cold-precipitating IgM rheumatoid factor-IgG complexes","Antibodies to type IV collagen in basement membranes","Galactose-deficient IgA1 complexes in small vessel walls","Autoantibodies to neutrophil cytoplasmic myeloperoxidase"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L7","obj":"GI-13-L.9","dok":3,"approach":["Chronic hepatitis C stimulates B cells, which can make monoclonal IgM with rheumatoid factor activity.","This IgM binds IgG (often with HCV antigen) to form complexes that precipitate in the cold: mixed cryoglobulins.","The complexes deposit in small vessels and glomeruli and fix complement, causing purpura, arthralgia, glomerulonephritis, and low C4."],"why":"Most adults infected with HCV, about 75 to 85%, develop chronic infection, and a minority progress to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma over decades. Chronic infection also drives B cells to make IgM rheumatoid factor that binds IgG and viral antigen. These mixed cryoglobulins precipitate in cooler tissues and deposit in small vessels and glomeruli, consuming complement. The result is palpable purpura, arthralgias, and membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Anti-glomerular basement membrane disease affects the kidney and lung and does not cause purpura with low complement and rheumatoid factor.","IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein purpura) causes palpable purpura and nephritis, but usually in children, with normal complement and no rheumatoid factor.","Myeloperoxidase ANCA vasculitis (microscopic polyangiitis) is pauci-immune, so complement is not consumed."],"study":{"topics":["HCV mixed cryoglobulinemia","Outcomes of chronic HCV"],"comps":["GI-13-L7","GI-11"]}},{"id":"GI-C17-Q4","stem":"No vaccine is available to prevent infection with this virus. Which of the following features of the virus best explains this?","options":["Dependence on a helper virus for envelope proteins","Rapid variation of envelope glycoprotein epitopes","Lack of a lipid envelope on the virion","Integration of viral DNA into the host genome"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.5","dok":2,"approach":["HCV is an enveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA flavivirus.","Its RNA-dependent RNA polymerase has no proofreading, so mutations build up fast.","The envelope glycoproteins change constantly, so antibodies made against one variant miss the next, and vaccine design has failed."],"why":"Hepatitis C virus is a flavivirus with an RNA genome. Its RNA-dependent RNA polymerase makes many errors and cannot proofread, so each patient carries a swarm of related variants (quasispecies). The hypervariable regions of the envelope glycoproteins keep changing and escape neutralizing antibodies. This explains both the high rate of chronic infection and the lack of a vaccine. Hepatitis B, by contrast, is a partially double-stranded DNA hepadnavirus with a stable surface antigen used in its vaccine.","whyNot":["Dependence on a helper virus describes hepatitis D, which needs HBsAg from hepatitis B. Vaccinating against hepatitis B prevents it.","Correct.","Lack of an envelope describes hepatitis A and E, which spread by the fecal-oral route. Hepatitis A has an effective vaccine.","Integration of viral DNA describes hepatitis B, which nonetheless has an effective vaccine. HCV is an RNA virus with no DNA stage."],"study":{"topics":["Comparison of hepatitis viruses","HCV antigenic variation"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-L7"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C18","system":"GI","dx":"Acute hepatitis B in an adult","codes":["GI-13-L7"],"case":"A 34-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 10-day history of fatigue, nausea, right upper quadrant discomfort, and dark urine. Three months ago he had unprotected sexual intercourse with a new partner. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 88/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 122/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows jaundice and tender hepatomegaly. Laboratory studies show ALT 1,840 U/L (normal 7 to 56), AST 1,210 U/L (normal 10 to 40), alkaline phosphatase 160 U/L (normal 40 to 130), total bilirubin 6.2 mg/dL (normal 0.1 to 1.2), direct bilirubin 4.5 mg/dL (normal 0 to 0.3), and prothrombin time 13 seconds (normal 11 to 13.5). HBsAg and IgM anti-HBc are positive; anti-HBs is negative. Tests for hepatitis A IgM and hepatitis C antibody are negative. Diagnosis: acute hepatitis B.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C18-Q1","stem":"Eight weeks later his symptoms have resolved. Serologic testing now shows HBsAg negative, anti-HBs negative, and IgM anti-HBc positive. Which of the following best describes his status?","options":["Chronic infection with low replication","Window period of acute infection","Resolved infection with immunity","Early acute infection before antibodies appear"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L7","obj":"GI-13-L.8","dok":3,"approach":["HBsAg has cleared, but anti-HBs has not yet appeared.","In this gap, IgM anti-HBc is the only positive marker.","This is the window period, which still means recent acute infection."],"why":"In acute hepatitis B, HBsAg appears first and IgM anti-HBc follows with symptoms. As the infection clears, HBsAg disappears, and there can be a gap of weeks before anti-HBs becomes detectable. During this window period, IgM anti-HBc is the only marker, so it must be tested to avoid missing the infection. Recovery is later marked by anti-HBs and IgG anti-HBc. HBsAg lasting more than 6 months defines chronic infection.","whyNot":["Chronic infection requires HBsAg persisting longer than 6 months, with IgG anti-HBc. His HBsAg has already cleared.","Correct.","Resolved infection shows anti-HBs with IgG anti-HBc. He has no anti-HBs yet, and his anti-HBc is still IgM.","Early acute infection shows HBsAg before any antibody. His HBsAg is gone and IgM anti-HBc is present."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatitis B serology","Window period"],"comps":["GI-13-L7","GI-11"]}},{"id":"GI-C18-Q2","stem":"Which of the following is mainly responsible for the hepatocyte injury in this patient?","options":["Toxic accumulation of HBsAg in hepatocyte endoplasmic reticulum","Complement-mediated lysis by antibody bound to infected cells","CD8 T cells recognizing viral peptides on MHC class I","Direct cytopathic lysis of hepatocytes by replicating virus"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.6","dok":2,"approach":["HBV itself does little direct damage to the cells it infects.","Infected hepatocytes present viral peptides on MHC class I.","Cytotoxic CD8 T cells recognize these peptides and kill the cells, which raises ALT and also clears the virus."],"why":"The liver damage in hepatitis B comes mainly from the immune response. Cytotoxic CD8 T cells recognize HBV peptides on MHC class I and kill infected hepatocytes by perforin, granzymes, and Fas ligand. Adults with a strong T cell response have marked ALT elevation but usually clear the virus. Newborns mount a weak response, have little injury, and most become chronically infected. Circulating HBsAg immune complexes can also cause polyarteritis nodosa and membranous nephropathy.","whyNot":["HBsAg fills the endoplasmic reticulum in chronic carriers (ground-glass hepatocytes), but these cells are not killed by it.","Antibodies such as anti-HBs neutralize free virus, but antibody and complement are not the main cause of hepatocyte death.","Correct.","HBV is not directly cytopathic. Many people carry high viral loads with little liver damage when the immune response is weak, as in neonatal infection."],"study":{"topics":["Immunopathogenesis of hepatitis B"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-L7"]}},{"id":"GI-C18-Q3","stem":"His partner completed the hepatitis B vaccine series 5 years ago. Her serology shows HBsAg negative, anti-HBs positive, and total anti-HBc negative. Which of the following best explains the absence of anti-HBc?","options":["Anti-HBc disappears within months after any exposure","Vaccine-induced anti-HBs neutralizes the core protein","The vaccine is a live virus that lacks the core gene","The vaccine contains only recombinant HBsAg"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.7","dok":2,"approach":["Ask which viral proteins the vaccine contains.","The vaccine is recombinant HBsAg only, with no core protein.","So vaccination produces anti-HBs alone, while natural infection produces both anti-HBs and anti-HBc."],"why":"The hepatitis B vaccine is a subunit vaccine of recombinant HBsAg produced in yeast. It teaches the immune system to make anti-HBs, which neutralizes the virus. Because no core antigen is given, vaccinated people never make anti-HBc. Anti-HBs alone therefore means vaccine immunity, while anti-HBs together with IgG anti-HBc means recovery from natural infection.","whyNot":["IgG anti-HBc usually persists for life after natural infection. That is why it marks past infection.","Anti-HBs binds the surface antigen. It does not neutralize or remove core protein, which is inside the virion.","The hepatitis B vaccine is not a live virus. It is a subunit vaccine of surface antigen made in yeast.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatitis B vaccine serology"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-L7"]}},{"id":"GI-C18-Q4","stem":"His serum bilirubin is raised in both the conjugated and unconjugated fractions. Which of the following best explains this pattern?","options":["Inherited absence of hepatic UGT1A1 activity","Extrahepatic obstruction of the common bile duct by a stone","Accelerated heme breakdown from immune-mediated hemolysis","Hepatocyte injury impairing conjugation and excretion"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Indirect bilirubin is total minus direct: 6.2 minus 4.5 equals 1.7 mg/dL, so both fractions are high.","ALT above 1,000 U/L with only a small alkaline phosphatase rise is a hepatocellular pattern.","Injured hepatocytes take up and conjugate less bilirubin, and conjugated bilirubin leaks back into blood from damaged cells and canaliculi."],"why":"In hepatitis, injured hepatocytes cannot take up, conjugate, and excrete bilirubin normally. Some unconjugated bilirubin is not processed, and conjugated bilirubin leaks back into the blood because excretion into bile is the slowest step. The result is a mixed hyperbilirubinemia with bilirubin in the urine. A very high ALT with a modest alkaline phosphatase distinguishes this from obstructive jaundice, where conjugated bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase dominate.","whyNot":["Absent UGT1A1 (Crigler-Najjar type I) causes severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia from birth with no conjugated rise.","A common duct stone raises mainly conjugated bilirubin with a high alkaline phosphatase and pain. His transaminases are far higher than his alkaline phosphatase, which points to hepatocyte injury.","Hemolysis raises mainly unconjugated bilirubin, with no bilirubin in the urine. His dark urine and high direct fraction show conjugated bilirubin.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Mixed hyperbilirubinemia","Hepatocellular versus obstructive jaundice"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-L7"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C19","system":"GI","dx":"Amebic liver abscess","codes":["GI-13-L"],"case":"A 36-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 3-week history of fever and right upper quadrant pain that radiates to the right shoulder. He returned 4 months ago from a year of agricultural work in rural southern Mexico, where he had bloody diarrhea for about a week. Temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), pulse is 108/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg. Physical examination shows tender hepatomegaly and tenderness over the right lower intercostal spaces. Leukocyte count is 16,500/mm3 (normal 4,500 to 11,000) with no eosinophilia. Alkaline phosphatase is 210 U/L (normal 40 to 130). CT scan of the abdomen shows a single 7-cm abscess in the right lobe of the liver. Serum antibodies to Entamoeba histolytica are positive. Diagnosis: amebic liver abscess.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C19-Q1","stem":"Aspiration of the abscess is performed. Which of the following findings in the aspirated fluid is most likely?","options":["Reddish brown odorless fluid with no bacteria on Gram stain","Foul-smelling pus containing gram-negative rods and anaerobes","Clear fluid containing daughter cysts and protoscolex hooklets","Green bile-stained fluid with cholesterol crystals"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L","obj":"GI-13-L.10","dok":3,"approach":["Travel to an endemic area, prior bloody diarrhea, a single right lobe abscess, and positive amebic serology point to amebic abscess.","Amebae dissolve liver tissue into a thick, odorless, reddish brown fluid often called anchovy paste.","The fluid is usually sterile on Gram stain and culture; trophozoites are found mainly in the abscess wall."],"why":"Amebic liver abscess is a cavity of liquefied liver tissue made by Entamoeba histolytica trophozoites. The contents are reddish brown, odorless, and usually free of bacteria and neutrophils, because amebae lyse neutrophils. Pyogenic abscesses are often multiple, follow biliary infection or portal spread from bowel disease, and contain foul pus with enteric gram-negative rods or anaerobes. Travel history and serology help separate the two.","whyNot":["Correct.","Foul pus with mixed bacteria is the content of a pyogenic abscess, which usually comes from biliary disease or portal spread and grows E. coli, Klebsiella, or anaerobes.","Clear fluid with daughter cysts and hooklets is a hydatid cyst of Echinococcus granulosus, usually found in people exposed to dogs and sheep.","Bile-stained fluid suggests a biloma from bile duct injury, not an abscess after amebic dysentery."],"study":{"topics":["Amebic versus pyogenic liver abscess"],"comps":["GI-13-L","GI-12"]}},{"id":"GI-C19-Q2","stem":"Which of the following would most likely have been found in an intestinal biopsy specimen during his diarrheal illness?","options":["Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites containing red cells","Acid-fast oocysts on the brush border without ulceration","Rhabditiform larvae in crypts with eosinophilic infiltrates","Flagellated trophozoites attached to villi without invasion"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.7","dok":2,"approach":["The liver abscess and earlier bloody diarrhea point to invasive Entamoeba histolytica.","Trophozoites invade the colonic mucosa and spread sideways in the submucosa, making flask-shaped ulcers.","The trophozoites often contain ingested red blood cells, which separates them from harmless amebae."],"why":"Entamoeba histolytica is spread by cysts in fecally contaminated food and water. Trophozoites attack the colon, most often the cecum and ascending colon, and make narrow-necked, broad-based flask-shaped ulcers. They ingest red blood cells, and this is a key way to recognize them. From the ulcers, trophozoites can enter portal venous blood and reach the liver.","whyNot":["Correct.","Acid-fast oocysts on the brush border are Cryptosporidium, which causes watery diarrhea, severe in people with advanced HIV infection.","Larvae in the crypts are Strongyloides, which penetrates skin and can cause hyperinfection with corticosteroid use.","Flagellated trophozoites stuck to villi without invasion describe Giardia, which causes fatty, nonbloody diarrhea from contaminated water."],"study":{"topics":["Entamoeba histolytica","Comparison of intestinal protozoa"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-L"]}},{"id":"GI-C19-Q3","stem":"Which of the following is the most likely route by which trophozoites traveled from his colon to his liver?","options":["Common bile duct ascending from the duodenum","Superior mesenteric vein to portal vein","Ileocolic lymphatics to the thoracic duct","Inferior vena cava to the hepatic veins"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["Amebic ulcers are usually in the cecum and ascending colon, which drain to the superior mesenteric vein.","The superior mesenteric vein joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein.","Portal blood enters the liver first, so amebae lodge in the hepatic sinusoids."],"why":"Venous blood from the cecum and ascending colon drains through the ileocolic and right colic veins to the superior mesenteric vein. It joins the splenic vein to form the portal vein, which delivers the blood to the liver sinusoids before it reaches the hepatic veins and inferior vena cava. Trophozoites in this blood are trapped in the liver. Flow from the superior mesenteric vein tends to stream into the right lobe, which helps explain why most amebic abscesses form there.","whyNot":["Spread up the common bile duct is the route of ascending cholangitis and many pyogenic abscesses, not amebic abscess.","Correct.","Lymph from the colon drains to the thoracic duct and then the systemic veins, which would take organisms to the lungs first, not directly to the liver.","Blood in the inferior vena cava flows toward the heart. Hepatic veins drain the liver into the vena cava, not the other way."],"study":{"topics":["Portal venous drainage of the colon","Route of amebic liver abscess"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-12"]}},{"id":"GI-C19-Q4","stem":"During his diarrheal illness, stool studies would most likely have shown which of the following?","options":["Watery stool, high osmotic gap, stops with fasting","Watery stool, low osmotic gap, persists with fasting","Bloody stool, low osmotic gap, persists with fasting","Greasy stool, high fecal fat, stops with fasting"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.6","dok":2,"approach":["Invasive amebic colitis damages the mucosa and causes bloody stool: an inflammatory diarrhea.","Inflammatory diarrhea does not come from unabsorbed solutes, so the stool osmotic gap is low and fasting does not stop it.","Compare with osmotic diarrhea (high gap, stops with fasting) and secretory diarrhea (low gap, watery, persists)."],"why":"Diarrhea is grouped by mechanism. Osmotic diarrhea comes from unabsorbed solutes, so the stool osmotic gap is high (above about 100 to 125 mOsm/kg) and the diarrhea stops with fasting. Secretory diarrhea comes from active chloride secretion, as with cholera toxin, so the gap is low (below about 50 mOsm/kg) and it persists with fasting. Inflammatory diarrhea comes from mucosal damage, with blood in the stool, a low gap, and persistence with fasting. Amebic colitis is a classic inflammatory cause.","whyNot":["A high osmotic gap that stops with fasting is osmotic diarrhea, such as from lactose that is not absorbed.","Watery diarrhea with a low gap that continues during fasting is secretory diarrhea, as from cholera toxin raising cAMP and opening CFTR chloride channels. It is not bloody.","Correct.","Greasy stool with high fecal fat is malabsorption, as in Giardia infection or pancreatic insufficiency, not invasive colitis."],"study":{"topics":["Types of diarrhea","Stool osmotic gap"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-12"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C20","system":"GI","dx":"Lynch syndrome right-sided colon adenocarcinoma with liver metastasis","codes":["GI-13-K7","GI-13-K4"],"case":"A 46-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of fatigue and shortness of breath on exertion. Her mother had endometrial cancer at the age of 49 years, and a maternal uncle had colon cancer at the age of 44 years. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows pallor. Hemoglobin is 8.6 g/dL (normal 12.0 to 16.0), mean corpuscular volume is 71 fL (normal 80 to 100), and serum ferritin is 6 ng/mL (normal 12 to 150). A test for fecal occult blood is positive. Colonoscopy shows an ulcerated mass in the ascending colon; biopsy shows poorly differentiated mucinous adenocarcinoma with many tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes. CT scan of the abdomen shows two solid masses in the liver. Diagnosis: Lynch syndrome with adenocarcinoma of the ascending colon.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C20-Q1","stem":"Which of the following molecular findings is most likely in her tumor?","options":["Microsatellite instability from mismatch repair loss","Germline STK11 mutation with hamartomatous polyps","Biallelic APC loss with chromosomal instability","Defective nucleotide excision repair of thymine dimers"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-10","obj":"GI-10.4","dok":3,"approach":["Early colon cancer and endometrial cancer in close relatives fit Lynch syndrome (hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer).","Lynch syndrome is a germline defect in a DNA mismatch repair gene such as MLH1 or MSH2.","Without mismatch repair, replication errors build up in short repeated sequences, causing microsatellite instability."],"why":"Lynch syndrome is autosomal dominant. Patients inherit one faulty mismatch repair gene (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, or PMS2), and the second copy is lost in the tumor. Errors made when DNA polymerase slips on repetitive sequences are not fixed, so microsatellites change length, and mutations pile up in growth control genes. These cancers tend to be right-sided, mucinous, and full of lymphocytes. Sporadic cancers usually follow the APC, KRAS, TP53 adenoma to carcinoma sequence instead.","whyNot":["Correct.","STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, with hamartomatous polyps and mucocutaneous pigmentation.","APC loss followed by KRAS activation and TP53 loss is the chromosomal instability pathway of most sporadic cancers and of familial adenomatous polyposis, which presents with hundreds of polyps.","Nucleotide excision repair defects cause xeroderma pigmentosum, with skin cancers after sunlight exposure, not hereditary colon cancer."],"study":{"topics":["Mismatch repair and Lynch syndrome","Pathways to colorectal cancer"],"comps":["GI-10","GI-13-K7"]}},{"id":"GI-C20-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her tumor presented with iron deficiency anemia rather than bowel obstruction?","options":["Annular napkin-ring growth typical of right colon tumors","Hemolysis induced by cytokines secreted by the tumor","Wide lumen and liquid stool in the right colon","Impaired duodenal iron absorption from liver metastases"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K7","obj":"GI-13-K.10","dok":3,"approach":["A microcytic anemia with low ferritin and occult blood points to slow bleeding from the colon.","The cecum and ascending colon have a wide lumen and liquid contents, so bulky tumors rarely block them.","These exophytic tumors ooze blood and present with iron deficiency anemia."],"why":"Right-sided colon cancers tend to be bulky, polypoid masses in a wide segment filled with liquid stool. They bleed slowly and rarely obstruct, so iron deficiency anemia is a common first sign. Left-sided cancers often grow around the wall in a napkin-ring pattern in a narrow segment with formed stool, causing a change in bowel habit and obstruction. Iron deficiency anemia in an older man or a postmenopausal woman should prompt a search for colon cancer. CEA is used to watch for recurrence after treatment, not for screening.","whyNot":["Annular napkin-ring growth is typical of left-sided cancers, which narrow the lumen and cause a change in bowel habit and obstruction.","Her anemia is microcytic with a very low ferritin, which shows iron loss from bleeding, not hemolysis.","Correct.","Iron is absorbed in the duodenum, and liver metastases do not block this. The low ferritin reflects chronic blood loss."],"study":{"topics":["Right versus left colon cancer","Colorectal cancer risk factors"],"comps":["GI-13-K7","GI-10"]}},{"id":"GI-C20-Q3","stem":"Which of the following is the most likely nature of the liver masses?","options":["Multiple cavernous hemangiomas","Primary hepatocellular carcinoma","Hepatocellular adenomas from estrogen exposure","Colorectal adenocarcinoma metastases"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K4","obj":"GI-13-K.7","dok":2,"approach":["Metastases are the most common malignant tumors of the liver, far outnumbering primary liver cancer.","Colon cancer drains through the portal vein, so the liver is the first capillary bed it reaches.","Multiple solid liver masses in a patient with colon cancer are metastases until proven otherwise."],"why":"Metastatic tumors are much more common in the liver than primary cancers. Cancers of the colon, stomach, and pancreas reach the liver through portal venous blood, and breast and lung cancers reach it through the hepatic artery. Metastases are usually multiple. Among benign lesions, cavernous hemangioma is the most common, and hepatocellular adenoma is linked to estrogen or anabolic steroid exposure and carries a risk of rupture.","whyNot":["Cavernous hemangioma is the most common benign liver tumor, but multiple solid masses in a patient with colon cancer are much more likely to be metastases.","Hepatocellular carcinoma usually arises in a liver with cirrhosis or chronic HBV or HCV infection. Nothing in her history suggests either, and she has a known primary tumor.","Hepatocellular adenomas are linked to oral contraceptives and anabolic steroids and can rupture. With a known colon cancer, metastasis is far more likely.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Liver metastases","Benign liver tumors"],"comps":["GI-13-K4","GI-5"]}},{"id":"GI-C20-Q4","stem":"During her evaluation she develops septic shock from a urinary tract infection, with a blood pressure near 72/40 mm Hg for several hours. Two days later she has left-sided abdominal pain and bloody stools, and colonoscopy shows ischemic mucosa limited to the splenic flexure. Which of the following best explains the vulnerability of this segment?","options":["It is supplied by end arteries arising directly from the aorta","It lies where superior and inferior mesenteric supply meet","It receives its blood only from the superior rectal artery","It drains to the systemic rather than the portal venous system"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.2","dok":3,"approach":["Ischemia after a period of hypotension affects watershed zones, where two arterial territories meet.","The superior mesenteric artery (middle colic) supplies the colon up to near the splenic flexure; the inferior mesenteric artery (left colic) supplies beyond it.","At the junction, the marginal artery is often thin, so this segment is the first to suffer when pressure falls."],"why":"The superior mesenteric artery supplies the midgut, from the duodenum to the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon. The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the hindgut beyond that. The marginal artery links the two along the colon, but at the splenic flexure the connection is often thin. When blood pressure falls, these border zones get the least flow. The splenic flexure and the rectosigmoid junction are the classic watershed sites for ischemic colitis.","whyNot":["The splenic flexure is supplied by colic branches of the mesenteric arteries through the marginal artery, not by end arteries straight from the aorta.","Correct.","The superior rectal artery, the end of the inferior mesenteric artery, supplies the upper rectum, not the splenic flexure.","The splenic flexure drains to the inferior mesenteric vein and the portal system. Venous drainage does not explain ischemia from low blood pressure."],"study":{"topics":["Colonic blood supply","Watershed ischemic colitis"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-13-J3"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C21","system":"GI","dx":"Familial adenomatous polyposis","codes":["GI-13-K7"],"case":"A 19-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of intermittent bright red blood in his stools and loose bowel movements. His father died of colon cancer at the age of 41 years, and his paternal aunt had a colectomy at the age of 25 years. Physical examination shows no abnormalities. Hemoglobin concentration is 11.6 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5). Colonoscopy shows more than 400 sessile and pedunculated polyps, most 2 to 8 mm in diameter, carpeting the colon and rectum. Biopsy specimens of several polyps show tubular adenomas with low-grade dysplasia. Diagnosis: familial adenomatous polyposis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C21-Q1","stem":"Five years after prophylactic colectomy, he develops a firm mass in the abdominal wall that is diagnosed as a desmoid tumor. Radiographs show osteomas of the mandible, and he has several epidermoid cysts on his back. Which of the following is the most likely genetic basis of his entire presentation?","options":["Biallelic germline mutations in MUTYH","Heterozygous germline mutation in STK11","Heterozygous germline mutation in APC","Heterozygous germline mutation in MLH1"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-10","obj":"GI-10.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize osteomas, epidermoid cysts, and desmoid tumors with colonic polyposis as Gardner syndrome.","Recall that Gardner syndrome is a variant of familial adenomatous polyposis caused by the same gene.","Use the family history: an affected father and aunt fit autosomal dominant inheritance of one mutant allele."],"why":"Gardner syndrome is familial adenomatous polyposis plus extracolonic growths such as osteomas, epidermoid cysts, desmoid tumors, and extra teeth. Both come from a heterozygous germline mutation in APC, a tumor suppressor gene on chromosome 5q, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern. The extracolonic lesions mark a variant of the same disease and do not point to a different gene. Turcot syndrome is the other variant, with brain tumors such as medulloblastoma.","whyNot":["MUTYH-associated polyposis also produces many adenomas, but it is autosomal recessive, so an affected father and aunt would be unexpected. The classic Gardner lesions (osteomas, epidermoid cysts, desmoid tumors) point to APC.","STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, with hamartomatous polyps and dark macules on the lips and buccal mucosa. It does not produce hundreds of adenomas.","Correct.","MLH1 mutations cause Lynch syndrome, in which a few adenomas progress quickly to cancer, often in the right colon. Lynch syndrome does not carpet the colon with hundreds of polyps."],"study":{"topics":["Gardner and Turcot variants of FAP","Hereditary polyposis syndromes"],"comps":["GI-10","GI-13-K7"]}},{"id":"GI-C21-Q2","stem":"One polyp in the rectum measures 3.5 cm. Microscopic examination shows long, slender, finger-like projections of dysplastic columnar epithelium over thin fibrovascular cores, making up most of the lesion. Compared with his 5-mm polyps, which of the following best describes this lesion?","options":["Villous adenoma with a higher risk of invasive carcinoma","Tubular adenoma with a similar risk of invasive carcinoma","Hyperplastic polyp with no meaningful malignant risk","Hamartomatous polyp with a low risk of invasive carcinoma"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K7","obj":"GI-13-K.11","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the architecture: finger-like fronds of dysplastic epithelium define a villous adenoma.","Recall that malignant risk in adenomas rises with size, villous component, and grade of dysplasia.","Combine the two: a large villous adenoma carries more risk than a small tubular one."],"why":"Adenomas are classified by their architecture. Tubular adenomas are mostly branching glands, villous adenomas are mostly long fronds, and tubulovillous adenomas are a mix. The risk of invasive carcinoma rises with size (especially above 2 cm), with the villous fraction, and with high-grade dysplasia. This 3.5-cm villous lesion is the polyp most likely to already contain cancer.","whyNot":["Correct.","Tubular adenomas are made of branching tubular glands. A small tubular adenoma carries a lower risk than a large villous one.","Hyperplastic polyps show serrated crypts without dysplasia, are usually small and rectosigmoid, and have little malignant potential.","Hamartomatous polyps, such as juvenile and Peutz-Jeghers polyps, are disorganized normal tissue without dysplastic fronds."],"study":{"topics":["Classification of colonic polyps","Adenoma size and villous risk"],"comps":["GI-13-K7"]}},{"id":"GI-C21-Q3","stem":"Upper endoscopy for surveillance shows several adenomas surrounding the major duodenal papilla. A biopsy of normal-appearing mucosa next to the papilla is taken. Which of the following histologic features best identifies the segment of bowel sampled?","options":["Aggregated lymphoid follicles in the submucosa","Straight crypts rich in goblet cells without villi","Mucous glands in the submucosa","Tubular glands lined by parietal and chief cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the major duodenal papilla in the second part of the duodenum.","Recall the histologic marker of the duodenum: Brunner glands in the submucosa.","Match the other features to the ileum, colon, and stomach and exclude them."],"why":"The major papilla opens into the second part of the duodenum, so the biopsy is duodenal. The duodenum is the only segment with Brunner glands, mucous glands in the submucosa that secrete alkaline mucus to protect against gastric acid. Periampullary adenomas and carcinomas are a leading cause of death in FAP after colectomy, which is why the duodenum is surveyed.","whyNot":["Aggregated lymphoid follicles (Peyer patches) mark the ileum.","Straight crypts packed with goblet cells and no villi describe the colon.","Correct.","Parietal and chief cells line the fundic glands of the gastric body and fundus."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of the small intestine segments"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-3"]}},{"id":"GI-C21-Q4","stem":"Without colectomy, one of his adenomas would be expected to progress to carcinoma. Which of the following sequences of somatic events best describes that progression?","options":["TP53 loss, then KRAS activation, then loss of the second APC allele","KRAS activation, then loss of both APC alleles, then TP53 loss","MLH1 loss, then microsatellite instability, then TGFBR2 mutation","Second APC allele loss, then KRAS activation, then TP53 loss"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-10","obj":"GI-10.4","dok":3,"approach":["Note that he already carries one mutant APC allele in every cell.","Recall that the adenoma to carcinoma sequence starts with APC loss, followed by KRAS activation and then TP53 loss.","Separate this from the mismatch repair pathway of Lynch syndrome."],"why":"In familial adenomatous polyposis every colonic cell starts with one mutant APC allele, so a single somatic hit to the second allele frees beta-catenin and starts an adenoma. KRAS activation then drives growth of the adenoma, and loss of TP53 late in the sequence allows transformation to carcinoma. This is the chromosomal instability pathway. Lynch syndrome tumors follow a separate route, in which loss of mismatch repair (MLH1, MSH2) causes microsatellite instability.","whyNot":["TP53 loss is a late event in the sequence. It permits the change from a large adenoma to invasive carcinoma.","APC loss is the gatekeeper event and comes first in this pathway, before KRAS activation.","This is the mismatch repair pathway of Lynch syndrome. Frameshift mutations build up in genes with repeat sequences such as TGFBR2.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Adenoma to carcinoma sequence","Lynch syndrome and microsatellite instability"],"comps":["GI-10","GI-13-K7"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C22","system":"GI","dx":"Acute calculous cholecystitis from cholesterol stones","codes":["GI-13-D1","GI-13-D2"],"case":"A 38-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of a 14-hour history of constant right upper quadrant pain that radiates to the right shoulder blade, with nausea and vomiting. She has had three shorter episodes of similar pain over the past year. Temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), pulse is 106/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg. Deep palpation of the right upper quadrant causes inspiratory arrest. Leukocyte count is 15,800/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000). Serum total bilirubin is 1.0 mg/dL (0.1 to 1.2), and lipase is normal. Ultrasonography shows multiple gallstones, one impacted in the gallbladder neck, a gallbladder wall 6 mm thick (normal under 3), and pericholecystic fluid. Diagnosis: acute calculous cholecystitis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C22-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the initial injury to the gallbladder wall in this patient?","options":["Primary bacterial infection ascending from the duodenal lumen","Chemical irritation by bile trapped behind the stone","Wall ischemia and bile stasis during a critical illness","Reflux of activated pancreatic enzymes into the gallbladder"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-D1","obj":"GI-13-D.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note the stone impacted in the gallbladder neck, which blocks outflow into the cystic duct.","Recall that acute calculous cholecystitis begins as chemical inflammation from trapped bile.","Treat bacterial infection as a later, secondary event."],"why":"In acute calculous cholecystitis a stone obstructs the gallbladder neck or cystic duct. Trapped bile becomes more concentrated, and phospholipase from the injured mucosa converts lecithin to lysolecithin, which, with bile acids, damages the mucosa and releases prostaglandins. The gallbladder distends, its wall becomes edematous, and inflammation spreads to the parietal peritoneum, which produces the Murphy sign. Bacteria infect the stagnant bile later in many patients.","whyNot":["Bacteria can be cultured from the bile in many cases, but infection follows the obstruction and chemical injury rather than starting it.","Correct.","This describes acalculous cholecystitis, which occurs in critically ill patients after trauma, burns, major surgery, or sepsis. She has an impacted stone and no critical illness.","Pancreatic enzyme reflux does not cause cholecystitis, and her normal lipase argues against pancreatic involvement."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of acute cholecystitis","Calculous versus acalculous cholecystitis"],"comps":["GI-13-D1"]}},{"id":"GI-C22-Q2","stem":"She has taken a combined estrogen-progestin oral contraceptive for 12 years. Analysis of the stones removed at cholecystectomy shows that they are composed mainly of cholesterol. Which of the following mechanisms most directly links her estrogen exposure to this type of stone?","options":["Increased hemolysis with excess unconjugated bilirubin in bile","Bacterial beta-glucuronidase activity in the bile ducts","Increased conversion of cholesterol to bile acids","Increased hepatic secretion of cholesterol into bile"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-D2","obj":"GI-13-D.3","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the stone type: cholesterol stones form when bile is supersaturated with cholesterol.","Recall that estrogen increases hepatic cholesterol uptake and secretion into bile.","Exclude the mechanisms that produce pigment stones."],"why":"Cholesterol stays dissolved in bile only when enough bile acids and lecithin are present to carry it in micelles and vesicles. Estrogen increases hepatic uptake of lipoprotein cholesterol and its secretion into bile, which supersaturates the bile. Progesterone adds gallbladder hypomotility and stasis. This is why female sex, pregnancy, and estrogen therapy are risk factors, along with obesity and rapid weight loss.","whyNot":["Chronic hemolysis, as in sickle cell disease or hereditary spherocytosis, causes black pigment stones made of calcium bilirubinate.","Bacterial beta-glucuronidase deconjugates bilirubin in infected ducts and forms brown pigment stones, which are common in East Asia and with liver flukes.","Converting cholesterol to bile acids lowers the cholesterol saturation of bile and protects against stones.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cholesterol versus pigment gallstones","Risk factors for cholesterol stones"],"comps":["GI-13-D2","GI-9"]}},{"id":"GI-C22-Q3","stem":"Each of her episodes began about 30 minutes after a meal of fried food. Release of which of the following best explains why such meals precipitate her pain?","options":["Gastrin from G cells of the gastric antrum","Cholecystokinin from duodenal I cells","Secretin from S cells of the duodenal mucosa","Motilin from enteroendocrine cells of the duodenum"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.7","dok":2,"approach":["Link fat in the duodenum to the hormone that contracts the gallbladder.","Recall that cholecystokinin from I cells contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi.","Apply it: contraction against a stone in the neck raises pressure and causes pain."],"why":"Fatty acids and amino acids entering the duodenum stimulate I cells of the duodenum and jejunum to release cholecystokinin. CCK contracts gallbladder smooth muscle and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi to deliver bile for fat digestion. When a stone blocks the neck, contraction against the obstruction raises pressure in the gallbladder and causes pain. This is why biliary pain typically follows fatty meals.","whyNot":["Gastrin stimulates gastric acid secretion and mucosal growth and has no major role in gallbladder contraction.","Correct.","Secretin responds to acid in the duodenum and stimulates bicarbonate-rich fluid from pancreatic and bile duct cells. It does not contract the gallbladder.","Motilin drives the migrating motor complex during fasting, and its release is suppressed by eating."],"study":{"topics":["Cholecystokinin actions","Gallbladder filling and emptying"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C22-Q4","stem":"During laparoscopic cholecystectomy, the surgeon clips the artery that crosses the hepatocystic triangle to reach the gallbladder. This artery is most commonly a branch of which of the following?","options":["Right hepatic artery","Gastroduodenal artery","Left gastric artery","Splenic artery"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the artery in the hepatocystic (Calot) triangle as the cystic artery.","Recall that it usually arises from the right hepatic artery.","Trace it back: right hepatic, proper hepatic, common hepatic, celiac trunk."],"why":"The cystic artery usually arises from the right hepatic artery within the hepatocystic triangle, which is bounded by the cystic duct, the common hepatic duct, and the inferior surface of the liver. The right hepatic artery comes from the proper hepatic artery, the continuation of the common hepatic artery, which is one of the three branches of the celiac trunk. Because the right hepatic artery often runs close to the cystic duct, surgeons identify these structures before clipping.","whyNot":["Correct.","The gastroduodenal artery arises from the common hepatic artery and runs behind the first part of the duodenum. It is the vessel eroded by a posterior duodenal ulcer.","The left gastric artery supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach and the distal esophagus.","The splenic artery runs along the upper border of the pancreas to the spleen and gives short gastric and left gastro-omental branches."],"study":{"topics":["Branches of the celiac trunk","Cystic artery and hepatocystic triangle"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-3"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C23","system":"GI","dx":"Mirizzi syndrome","codes":["GI-13-D2","GI-13-C1"],"case":"A 67-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 1-week history of yellow skin, itching, dark urine, and pale stools. He has had episodes of right upper quadrant pain after meals for 2 years. Temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 136/82 mm Hg. He is jaundiced. Serum studies show total bilirubin of 6.4 mg/dL (0.1 to 1.2), direct bilirubin of 5.0 mg/dL (0 to 0.3), alkaline phosphatase of 420 U/L (40 to 130), and ALT of 76 U/L (10 to 40). Ultrasonography shows a 3-cm stone impacted in the gallbladder neck, with dilated intrahepatic ducts and a dilated common hepatic duct. The common bile duct below the cystic duct junction is of normal caliber and contains no stones. Diagnosis: Mirizzi syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C23-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains this patient's jaundice?","options":["Stone obstruction of the distal common bile duct","Obstruction of the cystic duct alone","Extrinsic compression of the common hepatic duct","Malignant stricture at the hepatic duct confluence"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-D2","obj":"GI-13-D.5","dok":2,"approach":["Note the dilated ducts above the stone and the normal common bile duct below it.","Place the obstruction at the common hepatic duct, beside the gallbladder neck.","Recognize Mirizzi syndrome: a stone in the gallbladder neck or cystic duct presses on the common hepatic duct from outside."],"why":"In Mirizzi syndrome a large stone lodges in the gallbladder neck or cystic duct, and the stone and surrounding inflammation press on the adjacent common hepatic duct. Bile cannot leave the liver, so conjugated bilirubin backs up into the blood and urine, and stools turn pale. The ducts above the compression dilate while the common bile duct below stays normal. Over time the stone can erode into the duct and form a cholecystobiliary fistula.","whyNot":["A stone in the distal common bile duct (choledocholithiasis) would dilate the common bile duct as well, and ultrasonography shows a normal, stone-free common bile duct.","Cystic duct obstruction alone causes biliary colic or cholecystitis without jaundice, because bile from the liver still drains through the common hepatic and common bile ducts.","Correct.","A tumor at the confluence of the right and left hepatic ducts would leave the common hepatic duct below it undilated. Here the common hepatic duct is dilated down to the impacted stone."],"study":{"topics":["Mirizzi syndrome","Levels of biliary obstruction"],"comps":["GI-13-D2","GI-13-C1"]}},{"id":"GI-C23-Q2","stem":"Three days later, while awaiting surgery, he has shaking chills and becomes confused. Temperature is 39.6 °C (103.3 °F), pulse is 118/min, and blood pressure is 86/50 mm Hg. Which of the following best explains this change?","options":["Ascending cholangitis","Biliary colic","Gallstone pancreatitis","Gallstone ileus"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-D2","obj":"GI-13-D.4","dok":3,"approach":["Note fever, jaundice, and right upper quadrant disease (Charcot triad) plus hypotension and confusion (Reynolds pentad).","Recall that bacteria multiply in obstructed, stagnant bile within the duct system.","Match the stone's location in the biliary tree to its complication."],"why":"Obstruction of the bile ducts lets enteric bacteria such as Escherichia coli, Klebsiella, and Enterococcus ascend and multiply in stagnant bile. Rising duct pressure pushes bacteria into the blood and causes sepsis. Fever, jaundice, and right upper quadrant pain form the Charcot triad, and adding hypotension and confusion forms the Reynolds pentad, which calls for urgent biliary drainage. Cholangitis requires obstruction of the duct system, which his compressed common hepatic duct provides.","whyNot":["Correct.","Biliary colic is transient pain from a stone briefly blocking the cystic duct, without fever or shock.","Gallstone pancreatitis needs a stone at or near the ampulla, where it blocks the pancreatic duct. It presents with epigastric pain radiating to the back and a high lipase.","Gallstone ileus follows a cholecystoenteric fistula. A large stone passes into the bowel and obstructs it, usually near the ileocecal valve, causing vomiting and distension."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of gallstones by location","Charcot triad and Reynolds pentad"],"comps":["GI-13-D2","GI-13-B1"]}},{"id":"GI-C23-Q3","stem":"Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography is planned to place a stent above the compression. To cannulate the bile duct, the endoscope must be advanced into which of the following parts of the duodenum?","options":["Horizontal part","Ascending part","Superior part","Descending part"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the common bile duct joins the main pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla.","Locate the ampulla's opening, the major duodenal papilla, on the posteromedial wall of the descending (second) part.","Name the duodenal parts in order: superior, descending, horizontal, ascending."],"why":"The common bile duct passes behind the first part of the duodenum and through the head of the pancreas, then joins the main pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla (of Vater). The ampulla opens at the major duodenal papilla on the posteromedial wall of the descending part. The minor papilla, for the accessory pancreatic duct, lies a little above it in the same part.","whyNot":["The horizontal part crosses the inferior vena cava and aorta at about L3, passing under the superior mesenteric vessels. No ducts open into it.","The ascending part rises to the duodenojejunal flexure, held by the suspensory muscle of the duodenum (ligament of Treitz), and has no papilla.","The superior part, which includes the duodenal cap, has no papilla. The common bile duct and gastroduodenal artery pass behind it.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Parts of the duodenum","Major duodenal papilla"],"comps":["GI-3"]}},{"id":"GI-C23-Q4","stem":"His obstruction also keeps bile acids from reaching the intestine. In health, the liver secretes several times more bile acid into the intestine each day than it synthesizes, and only a small fraction appears in stool. Which of the following best accounts for this conservation?","options":["Bacterial conversion of bile acids to cholesterol in the colon","Sodium-coupled active uptake in the terminal ileum","Passive diffusion of conjugated bile acids in the duodenum","Reabsorption of bile acids from the gallbladder into blood"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize that a small bile acid pool must be recycled several times a day.","Recall that conjugated bile acids are reclaimed by an active sodium-dependent transporter in the terminal ileum.","Follow them through portal blood back to hepatocytes for resecretion."],"why":"About 95% of secreted bile acids are reabsorbed, mostly by the apical sodium-dependent bile acid transporter in the terminal ileum. They return in portal blood bound to albumin, and hepatocytes take them up and secrete them again, so the pool cycles several times a day. The liver synthesizes only enough new bile acid to replace the small amount lost in stool. Ileal disease or resection breaks this circuit, shrinking the pool and causing steatorrhea and a higher risk of cholesterol gallstones.","whyNot":["Colonic bacteria deconjugate and dehydroxylate bile acids into secondary bile acids such as deoxycholate. They do not convert bile acids back into cholesterol.","Correct.","Conjugated bile acids are ionized at intestinal pH and cross the proximal intestine poorly. This keeps them in the lumen, where they form micelles for fat absorption.","The gallbladder stores and concentrates bile by absorbing sodium and water. It does not return bile acids to the blood."],"study":{"topics":["Enterohepatic circulation of bile acids"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-9"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C24","system":"GI","dx":"Gallbladder adenocarcinoma in a porcelain gallbladder","codes":["GI-13-D1","GI-13-K"],"case":"A 68-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of yellow skin, generalized itching, a dull right upper quadrant ache, and a 7-kg (15-lb) weight loss. She was told she had gallstones 15 years ago and declined surgery. She is jaundiced, and a firm mass is palpable below the right costal margin. Serum total bilirubin is 9.1 mg/dL (0.1 to 1.2), direct bilirubin is 7.6 mg/dL (0 to 0.3), and alkaline phosphatase is 610 U/L (40 to 130). CT scan shows gallstones, a continuous rim of calcification in the gallbladder wall, and an irregular mass in the gallbladder neck that invades the adjacent liver and narrows the common hepatic duct, with dilated intrahepatic ducts. Biopsy shows adenocarcinoma. Diagnosis: gallbladder adenocarcinoma in a porcelain gallbladder.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C24-Q1","stem":"Her serum calcium concentration is 9.3 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5). Which of the following best describes the calcification in her gallbladder wall?","options":["Calcification within a cluster of cholesterol polyps","Dystrophic calcification from chronic inflammation","Calcified wall of a hepatic echinococcal cyst","Metastatic calcification from a high serum calcium"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-D1","obj":"GI-13-D.2","dok":2,"approach":["Note the normal serum calcium, which excludes metastatic calcification.","Link calcium deposits in damaged tissue with a normal serum calcium to dystrophic calcification.","Connect long-standing gallstones to chronic cholecystitis, and the porcelain gallbladder to cancer risk."],"why":"Long-standing gallstones cause repeated injury and chronic cholecystitis, with fibrosis of the gallbladder wall. Calcium salts deposit in the damaged wall while serum calcium stays normal, which is dystrophic calcification. A calcified (porcelain) gallbladder marks long-standing chronic inflammation and is a classic risk factor for gallbladder adenocarcinoma.","whyNot":["Cholesterol polyps are small, lipid-laden projections of the mucosa (cholesterolosis). They do not calcify the whole wall.","Correct.","Echinococcal (hydatid) cysts can calcify, but they form in the liver and would not outline the gallbladder wall.","Metastatic calcification occurs in normal tissue when serum calcium or phosphate is high, as in hyperparathyroidism. Her serum calcium is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Porcelain gallbladder","Dystrophic versus metastatic calcification"],"comps":["GI-13-D1","GI-13-K"]}},{"id":"GI-C24-Q2","stem":"Which of the following sets of findings in her urine and stool is most likely?","options":["Urine bilirubin absent, urine urobilinogen normal, brown stools","Urine bilirubin absent, urine urobilinogen high, dark stools","Urine bilirubin present, urine urobilinogen high, brown stools","Urine bilirubin present, urine urobilinogen low, pale stools"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K","obj":"GI-13-K.13","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize progressive jaundice with a high direct bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase as obstructive (posthepatic) jaundice.","Conjugated bilirubin is water soluble, so it spills into urine when it backs up into blood.","With bile blocked from the gut, bacteria make little urobilinogen and stercobilin, so urine urobilinogen falls and stools turn pale."],"why":"Gallbladder adenocarcinoma near the neck, like cholangiocarcinoma, obstructs the bile ducts and causes progressive, often painless jaundice with weight loss. Conjugated bilirubin regurgitates into the blood and, being water soluble, appears in urine and darkens it. Little bilirubin reaches the intestine, so gut bacteria produce little urobilinogen (lowering urine urobilinogen) and little stercobilin (giving pale, clay-colored stools). Cholangiocarcinoma has different risk factors, including primary sclerosing cholangitis and liver flukes such as Clonorchis sinensis.","whyNot":["This pattern fits Gilbert syndrome or another mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia. It does not fit a high direct bilirubin.","This pattern fits hemolysis. Unconjugated bilirubin is bound to albumin and does not enter urine, while increased bilirubin delivery to the gut raises urobilinogen.","Bilirubin in urine with increased urobilinogen fits hepatocellular disease such as hepatitis, where some bilirubin still reaches the gut. Duct obstruction lowers urobilinogen.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Obstructive jaundice","Gallbladder cancer and cholangiocarcinoma"],"comps":["GI-13-K","GI-9","GI-13-C1"]}},{"id":"GI-C24-Q3","stem":"At surgery, the tumor has spread from the gallbladder neck along the free edge of the lesser omentum into the transverse fissure on the visceral surface of the liver. Which of the following groups of structures, passing through this fissure, is at greatest risk of encasement?","options":["Right, middle, and left hepatic veins and the inferior vena cava","Splenic artery, splenic vein, and the tail of the pancreas","Portal vein, hepatic arteries, and hepatic ducts","Left gastric artery, left gastric vein, and esophageal branches"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the transverse fissure on the visceral surface of the liver as the porta hepatis.","Recall that the portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile duct run in the free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament) to the porta.","Exclude vessels that leave the liver posteriorly near the bare area."],"why":"The porta hepatis is the transverse fissure on the visceral surface of the liver, between the quadrate and caudate lobes. The portal vein and hepatic arteries enter the liver there, and the right and left hepatic ducts leave it and join to form the common hepatic duct. Bile then flows down the common hepatic duct, joins the cystic duct to form the common bile duct, and meets the pancreatic duct at the ampulla of Vater under the sphincter of Oddi.","whyNot":["The hepatic veins drain directly into the inferior vena cava on the posterior surface of the liver near the bare area, away from the porta hepatis.","The splenic vessels run along the pancreas to the splenic hilum, far from the liver.","Correct.","The left gastric vessels run along the lesser curvature of the stomach toward the esophagus, away from the free edge of the lesser omentum."],"study":{"topics":["Porta hepatis","Path of bile from liver to duodenum"],"comps":["GI-3"]}},{"id":"GI-C24-Q4","stem":"Her gallstones formed in part because bile is concentrated during storage in the gallbladder. Which of the following processes concentrates gallbladder bile?","options":["Active sodium absorption with water following osmotically","Active secretion of bile acids by the gallbladder epithelium","Cholecystokinin-stimulated secretion of chloride and water","Filtration of bile water through the gallbladder serosa"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that gallbladder epithelium absorbs sodium actively, with chloride and water following.","Note that bile acids, cholesterol, lecithin, and bilirubin stay behind and become more concentrated.","Link prolonged storage and concentration to stone formation."],"why":"The gallbladder epithelium actively absorbs sodium, and chloride and water follow. Bile acids, cholesterol, lecithin, and bilirubin are left behind, so gallbladder bile is often about five times more concentrated than hepatic bile and can be concentrated more than tenfold. When emptying is infrequent, as in fasting, pregnancy, or rapid weight loss, concentrated bile stays longer in the gallbladder, and cholesterol and calcium bilirubinate can crystallize.","whyNot":["Correct.","Bile acids are made and secreted by hepatocytes. The gallbladder stores them and does not secrete them.","Cholecystokinin contracts the gallbladder to empty it. Secretion of chloride and water would dilute bile, as secretin does in the bile ducts.","Bile water is not filtered across the serosa. It leaves bile through the mucosa, following absorbed sodium."],"study":{"topics":["Gallbladder concentration of bile","Stasis and gallstone formation"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-D2"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C25","system":"GI","dx":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis with pernicious anemia","codes":["GI-13-E1","GI-13-G7"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman with Hashimoto thyroiditis comes to the physician because of fatigue and tingling in both feet for 6 months. Vibration sense is decreased in the toes. Hemoglobin concentration is 9.2 g/dL (12 to 16), mean corpuscular volume is 114 fL (80 to 100), and a peripheral blood smear shows hypersegmented neutrophils. Serum vitamin B12 concentration is 105 pg/mL (200 to 900). Serum antibodies to intrinsic factor and to parietal cells are present. Upper endoscopy shows thin, flat mucosa with loss of rugal folds in the gastric body and fundus and a normal antrum. Biopsy specimens of the body show loss of oxyntic glands, a lymphocytic and plasma cell infiltrate, and intestinal metaplasia. Diagnosis: autoimmune atrophic gastritis with pernicious anemia.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C25-Q1","stem":"Serum gastrin concentration is 850 pg/mL (under 100). Persistent elevation of this hormone places her at increased risk for tumors arising from which of the following cell types?","options":["Parietal cells of the fundic glands","Chief cells of the fundic glands","Enterochromaffin-like cells","Somatostatin-secreting antral D cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-13-E1","obj":"GI-13-E.1","dok":3,"approach":["Link loss of parietal cells to achlorhydria.","Without acid, antral D cells release less somatostatin, and G cells secrete gastrin unchecked.","Gastrin is trophic for enterochromaffin-like cells, which become hyperplastic and can form carcinoid tumors."],"why":"Destruction of parietal cells removes gastric acid. Acid normally stimulates antral D cells to release somatostatin, which restrains G cells, so achlorhydria lets gastrin rise steeply. Gastrin acts on CCK2 receptors of enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells in the body mucosa, stimulating histamine release and cell growth. Chronic stimulation causes ECL cell hyperplasia and, in some patients, multiple small gastric neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumors. Achlorhydria also impairs iron and B12 absorption and allows bacterial overgrowth.","whyNot":["Parietal cells have gastrin receptors, but they are the target of the autoimmune attack and are destroyed.","Chief cells, which secrete pepsinogen, are lost along with parietal cells when the oxyntic glands atrophy.","Correct.","Antral D cells release somatostatin in response to acid. With achlorhydria they are less active, which is one reason gastrin rises."],"study":{"topics":["Achlorhydria and hypergastrinemia","ECL cell hyperplasia and carcinoid tumors"],"comps":["GI-13-E1","GI-2"]}},{"id":"GI-C25-Q2","stem":"Serum methylmalonic acid concentration is 1.1 µmol/L (0.07 to 0.27), and homocysteine is 31 µmol/L (5 to 15). Reduced activity of which of the following enzymes accounts for the result that distinguishes her anemia from folate deficiency?","options":["Methionine synthase","Propionyl-CoA carboxylase","Dihydrofolate reductase","Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note that both methylmalonic acid and homocysteine are high.","Recall that B12 is a cofactor for methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, while folate is needed only in the methionine synthase reaction.","Conclude that the high methylmalonic acid points to B12 deficiency through loss of mutase activity."],"why":"Vitamin B12 is the cofactor for two enzymes. Methionine synthase uses methylcobalamin to move a methyl group from N5-methyltetrahydrofolate to homocysteine, so a lack of either B12 or folate raises homocysteine. Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase uses adenosylcobalamin to convert methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA, and only B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid. Abnormal fatty acid handling from this block is thought to contribute to the myelin damage behind her neuropathy.","whyNot":["Methionine synthase activity falls in both B12 and folate deficiency, so a high homocysteine does not separate the two.","Propionyl-CoA carboxylase uses biotin. Its deficiency causes propionic acidemia.","Dihydrofolate reductase regenerates tetrahydrofolate and is the target of methotrexate and trimethoprim. It does not need B12.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["B12 versus folate deficiency","B12-dependent enzymes"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-13-E1"]}},{"id":"GI-C25-Q3","stem":"Which of the following serum markers is most likely to be decreased as a direct result of the glandular loss in her gastric body?","options":["Chromogranin A","Pepsinogen I","Secretin","Cholecystokinin"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the cells of the oxyntic glands of the body and fundus: parietal cells, chief cells, mucous neck cells, and ECL cells.","Recall that chief cells secrete pepsinogen, mainly pepsinogen I in the body and fundus.","Predict that atrophy of these glands lowers acid, intrinsic factor, and pepsinogen I, while antral G cells are spared."],"why":"Autoimmune gastritis destroys the oxyntic glands of the body and fundus. Parietal cells make hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor, and chief cells make pepsinogen, so all three fall. Pepsinogen I comes almost entirely from chief and mucous neck cells of the body and fundus, so a low serum pepsinogen I (or a low pepsinogen I to II ratio) is a marker of body atrophy. Antral G cells survive and secrete more gastrin.","whyNot":["Chromogranin A is released by neuroendocrine cells. It rises in this disease because hypergastrinemia expands the ECL cell population.","Correct.","Secretin comes from duodenal S cells, which are not affected by gastric atrophy.","Cholecystokinin comes from I cells of the duodenum and jejunum, outside the stomach."],"study":{"topics":["Gastric gland cell types and secretions"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-13-G7"]}},{"id":"GI-C25-Q4","stem":"Her brother has chronic gastritis caused by Helicobacter pylori infection. Compared with her disease, his gastritis is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Antibodies against the parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase","Hypergastrinemia with enterochromaffin-like cell hyperplasia","Body-predominant atrophy with achlorhydria","Antrum-predominant inflammation with normal acid output"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-G7","obj":"GI-13-G.8","dok":2,"approach":["Recall where each type starts: H. pylori in the antrum, autoimmune gastritis in the body and fundus.","Predict acid output: antral infection spares parietal cells, so acid is normal or high.","Assign autoantibodies, achlorhydria, and marked hypergastrinemia to the autoimmune type."],"why":"H. pylori colonizes the antrum and causes chronic active gastritis with neutrophils, lymphoid follicles, and later intestinal metaplasia. Parietal cells in the body are spared early, so acid output is normal or increased, which predisposes to duodenal ulcer. H. pylori gastritis raises the risk of intestinal-type gastric adenocarcinoma and MALT lymphoma. Autoimmune gastritis targets the body and fundus, with antibodies to parietal cells and intrinsic factor, achlorhydria, and pernicious anemia.","whyNot":["Antibodies to the H+/K+-ATPase of parietal cells are the hallmark of autoimmune gastritis.","Marked hypergastrinemia with ECL cell hyperplasia follows achlorhydria, which is typical of autoimmune gastritis.","Body-predominant atrophy with achlorhydria is the pattern of autoimmune gastritis. Long-standing H. pylori pangastritis can reach this stage, but it is not the typical pattern.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["H. pylori versus autoimmune gastritis"],"comps":["GI-13-G7","GI-12"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C26","system":"GI","dx":"Dermatitis herpetiformis with gluten-sensitive enteropathy","codes":["GI-13-E2"],"case":"A 27-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of intensely itchy blisters on his elbows, knees, and buttocks, with bloating and loose stools. Examination shows grouped vesicles and excoriated papules on the extensor surfaces. Hemoglobin concentration is 10.8 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), mean corpuscular volume is 73 fL (80 to 100), and serum ferritin is 7 ng/mL (24 to 336). Direct immunofluorescence of perilesional skin shows granular IgA deposits in the dermal papillae. Serum IgA antibodies to tissue transglutaminase are markedly increased. Biopsy specimens from the second part of the duodenum show villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes. Diagnosis: dermatitis herpetiformis with gluten-sensitive enteropathy.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C26-Q1","stem":"Which of the following steps makes dietary gliadin peptides strongly immunogenic in this patient?","options":["Deamidation by tissue transglutaminase, increasing binding to HLA-DQ2","Crosslinking of T cell receptor V beta chains by gliadin as a superantigen","Binding of gliadin to IgE on intestinal mast cells, causing degranulation","Activation of the lectin complement pathway by gliadin on the epithelium"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that gliadin is rich in glutamine and proline and resists complete digestion.","Tissue transglutaminase converts glutamine residues to negatively charged glutamate (deamidation).","Deamidated peptides bind HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 tightly and activate gluten-specific CD4 T cells."],"why":"Gliadin peptides resist digestion and cross the epithelium. In the lamina propria, tissue transglutaminase deamidates their glutamine residues to glutamate. The added negative charges let the peptides bind tightly in the groove of HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8 on antigen-presenting cells, which present them to CD4 T cells. These T cells drive inflammation, largely through interferon-gamma, and help B cells make IgA antibodies to tissue transglutaminase, endomysium, and deamidated gliadin. In dermatitis herpetiformis, IgA targets epidermal transglutaminase and deposits in the dermal papillae.","whyNot":["Correct.","Superantigens such as toxic shock syndrome toxin bind outside the antigen groove and activate many T cells nonspecifically. Gliadin is processed and presented in the HLA groove.","IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation describes a food allergy such as wheat allergy, a type I reaction. Celiac disease is T cell mediated.","Complement activation by gliadin is not the driving mechanism. The disease depends on antigen presentation by HLA-DQ2 or DQ8."],"study":{"topics":["Celiac disease immunopathogenesis","HLA-DQ2 and DQ8"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-E2"]}},{"id":"GI-C26-Q2","stem":"Most of the intraepithelial lymphocytes in his duodenal biopsy are CD8+ T cells. If he does not follow a gluten-free diet, chronic activation of this cell population most increases his risk of which of the following malignancies?","options":["Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma","Gastric extranodal marginal zone B-cell lymphoma","Burkitt lymphoma of the terminal ileum","Adenocarcinoma of the sigmoid colon"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-E2","obj":"GI-13-E.2","dok":2,"approach":["Note that celiac disease expands intraepithelial T lymphocytes in the small bowel.","Recall that long-standing, untreated celiac disease predisposes to a T-cell lymphoma arising from these cells.","Separate it from B-cell lymphomas and carcinomas with other causes."],"why":"In celiac disease, intraepithelial CD8+ T cells expand and are activated by IL-15 from the stressed epithelium. Persistent stimulation in untreated or refractory disease can lead to clonal expansion and enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma, usually in the jejunum. Other findings of celiac disease are villous atrophy and crypt hyperplasia, worst in the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum, iron deficiency anemia, and dermatitis herpetiformis. A strict gluten-free diet lowers the lymphoma risk.","whyNot":["Correct.","Gastric MALT lymphoma is a B-cell lymphoma driven by chronic Helicobacter pylori infection.","Burkitt lymphoma is a B-cell lymphoma with a MYC translocation, linked to Epstein-Barr virus, and often found in the ileocecal region in sporadic cases. It is not linked to celiac disease.","Colonic adenocarcinoma follows the adenoma to carcinoma sequence and is not a typical complication of celiac disease. Celiac disease does raise the risk of small-bowel adenocarcinoma."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of celiac disease","Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma"],"comps":["GI-13-E2","GI-11"]}},{"id":"GI-C26-Q3","stem":"His biopsy is compared with a full-thickness section of normal bowel wall. The section shows tall, closely spaced circular mucosal folds covered by long villi, with no glands in the submucosa and no aggregated lymphoid follicles. From which of the following segments was this section most likely taken?","options":["Ascending colon","Jejunum","Ileum","Duodenum"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.4","dok":3,"approach":["Use the absence of submucosal glands to exclude the duodenum.","Use the absence of aggregated lymphoid follicles (Peyer patches) to exclude the ileum, and the villi to exclude the colon.","Recall that tall, closely spaced plicae circulares are most prominent in the jejunum."],"why":"Each region of the bowel has a histologic signature. The duodenum has Brunner glands in the submucosa, the ileum has Peyer patches, and the colon has crypts full of goblet cells and no villi. The jejunum has the tallest and most numerous plicae circulares and long villi, without those other markers. Together with the distal duodenum, it is where celiac disease is most severe.","whyNot":["The colon has no villi and no plicae circulares. Its mucosa is flat, with straight crypts rich in goblet cells.","Correct.","The ileum has Peyer patches, aggregated lymphoid follicles in the mucosa and submucosa, which are absent here.","The duodenum has Brunner glands in its submucosa, which are absent here."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of the small intestine segments"],"comps":["GI-2"]}},{"id":"GI-C26-Q4","stem":"His serum vitamin B12 concentration is normal. Which of the following pairs of absorption sites best explains why he is deficient in iron but not in vitamin B12?","options":["Iron in the jejunum; vitamin B12 in the duodenum","Iron in the duodenum; vitamin B12 in the terminal ileum","Iron in the stomach; vitamin B12 in the proximal ileum","Iron in the terminal ileum; vitamin B12 in the proximal jejunum"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.4","dok":2,"approach":["Note that celiac disease is worst in the proximal small bowel and often spares the ileum.","Recall that iron is absorbed mainly in the duodenum and B12 in the terminal ileum.","Predict iron deficiency with preserved B12 absorption."],"why":"Iron is absorbed mainly in the duodenum and upper jejunum through DMT1 after reduction to the ferrous form. Folate is absorbed mainly in the jejunum, and vitamin B12 bound to intrinsic factor and bile acids are absorbed in the terminal ileum. Celiac disease damages the proximal small bowel most, so iron deficiency is common, folate deficiency can follow, and B12 deficiency is less common. Ileal resection, as in Crohn disease, produces the opposite pattern, with B12 and bile acid loss.","whyNot":["The jejunum is the main site of folate absorption. B12 is not absorbed in the duodenum, where it is released from haptocorrin before binding intrinsic factor.","Correct.","The stomach releases iron and B12 from food with acid and pepsin but absorbs neither, and B12 uptake occurs in the terminal ileum.","The terminal ileum absorbs B12 and bile acids and absorbs little iron."],"study":{"topics":["Sites of nutrient absorption"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-13-E2"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C27","system":"GI","dx":"Adult-type lactase deficiency","codes":["GI-13-E4"],"case":"A 31-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 1-year history of bloating, cramping abdominal pain, flatulence, and watery diarrhea that begin 1 to 2 hours after he drinks milk or eats ice cream. Symptoms do not occur when he avoids dairy foods. His weight is stable. Physical examination shows no abnormalities. Stool is negative for occult blood and leukocytes. Serum IgA antibodies to tissue transglutaminase are absent. After an oral load of 25 g of lactose, breath hydrogen concentration rises by 45 ppm (a rise of 20 ppm or more is abnormal). Duodenal biopsy specimens show normal villi. Diagnosis: adult-type (primary) lactase deficiency.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C27-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the rise in breath hydrogen after the lactose load?","options":["Hepatic metabolism of excess absorbed glucose","Hydrolysis of lactose by gastric acid in the stomach","Colonic bacterial fermentation of undigested lactose","Mucosal oxidation of absorbed galactose in the jejunum"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-E4","obj":"GI-13-E.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize that human cells do not produce hydrogen gas.","Undigested lactose passes to the colon, where bacteria ferment it to short-chain fatty acids, hydrogen, methane, and carbon dioxide.","Hydrogen is absorbed into blood and exhaled, so breath hydrogen rises."],"why":"Lactase on the brush border of the small intestine splits lactose into glucose and galactose. In adult-type lactase deficiency, lactase expression declines after weaning, so lactose stays in the lumen, holds water osmotically, and reaches the colon. Colonic bacteria ferment it, producing gas (hydrogen, carbon dioxide, methane) that causes bloating and flatulence, and the hydrogen is exhaled. Normal villi separate this primary form from secondary lactase deficiency after viral enteritis or celiac disease, in which the brush border is damaged.","whyNot":["Absorbed glucose is metabolized to carbon dioxide and water. Breath hydrogen comes from bacterial fermentation.","Gastric acid does not meaningfully hydrolyze lactose. Its digestion depends on brush-border lactase.","Correct.","Human cells oxidize galactose and glucose to carbon dioxide and water. They do not produce hydrogen gas."],"study":{"topics":["Primary and secondary lactase deficiency","Hydrogen breath test"],"comps":["GI-13-E4","GI-7"]}},{"id":"GI-C27-Q2","stem":"He begins drinking milk pretreated with lactase enzyme and has no symptoms. Which of the following apical transporters absorbs both monosaccharides released from this lactose?","options":["Insulin-responsive glucose transporter (GLUT4)","Proton-coupled peptide transporter (PepT1)","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 1 (SGLT1)","Facilitative fructose transporter (GLUT5)"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the products of lactose hydrolysis: glucose and galactose.","Recall that both enter the enterocyte with sodium through SGLT1 on the apical membrane.","Note that they then leave across the basolateral membrane through GLUT2."],"why":"Lactose yields glucose and galactose, and both are taken up at the brush border by SGLT1, which couples their entry to sodium moving down the gradient kept by the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase. Fructose enters by facilitated diffusion through GLUT5. All three monosaccharides exit to the blood through GLUT2. Because SGLT1 also carries sodium and water follows, glucose plus sodium in oral rehydration solution increases water absorption even when cholera toxin is driving secretion.","whyNot":["GLUT4 is the insulin-responsive transporter of skeletal muscle and adipose tissue and has no role in intestinal absorption.","PepT1 absorbs dipeptides and tripeptides with hydrogen ions and does not carry sugars.","Correct.","GLUT5 carries fructose by facilitated diffusion and does not carry glucose or galactose well."],"study":{"topics":["Monosaccharide transporters","Oral rehydration solution"],"comps":["GI-7"]}},{"id":"GI-C27-Q3","stem":"Before diagnosis, a stool sample collected after a dairy meal shows sodium 32 mmol/L, potassium 28 mmol/L, and osmolality 290 mOsm/kg. Which of the following best describes his diarrhea?","options":["Osmotic diarrhea that stops with fasting","Secretory diarrhea from raised enterocyte cAMP","Inflammatory diarrhea with fecal leukocytes","Secretory diarrhea that persists with fasting"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.6","dok":3,"approach":["Calculate the stool osmotic gap: 290 minus 2 times (32 + 28) = 170 mOsm/kg.","A gap above about 125 means unmeasured solutes such as lactose hold water in the lumen. A gap below about 50 means electrolytes do, as in secretory diarrhea.","Osmotic diarrhea stops when the offending solute is no longer eaten, as with fasting."],"why":"The stool osmotic gap estimates solutes other than sodium and potassium salts. Here it is 170 mOsm/kg, so most stool osmolality comes from unmeasured solutes, the undigested lactose and its fermentation products. Osmotic diarrhea stops with fasting because the solute is no longer delivered. Secretory diarrhea, such as cholera toxin raising cAMP and opening CFTR chloride channels, has a small gap and continues during fasting. Inflammatory diarrhea shows blood and leukocytes in stool.","whyNot":["Correct.","Raised cAMP opening CFTR chloride channels is the mechanism of cholera toxin, which causes secretory diarrhea with a small gap.","Inflammatory diarrhea produces blood and leukocytes in stool, and his stool is negative for both.","Secretory diarrhea has a small osmotic gap (under about 50) because secreted sodium and chloride account for stool osmolality, and it continues during fasting."],"study":{"topics":["Stool osmotic gap","Osmotic versus secretory diarrhea"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-13-E4"]}},{"id":"GI-C27-Q4","stem":"Two years later, he has taken 150 mg of elemental zinc daily for 10 months to prevent colds. He now has an unsteady gait and decreased vibration and position sense in the legs. Hemoglobin concentration is 9.8 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), and absolute neutrophil count is 900/mm3 (1,500 to 8,000). Serum vitamin B12 concentration is normal. Deficiency of which of the following minerals is most likely?","options":["Iron","Magnesium","Copper","Selenium"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize anemia, neutropenia, and a posterior column myelopathy with a normal B12.","Link this combination to copper deficiency.","Identify excess zinc as the cause: zinc induces metallothionein in enterocytes, which traps dietary copper."],"why":"High zinc intake induces metallothionein in enterocytes. Metallothionein binds copper more tightly than zinc and holds it in the cell, and the copper is lost in stool when the enterocyte is shed. Copper is a cofactor for enzymes such as cytochrome c oxidase and for iron handling (ceruloplasmin and hephaestin oxidize iron for transport), so deficiency causes anemia, neutropenia, and a myelopathy that mimics B12 deficiency. Gastric bypass surgery and malabsorption are other causes.","whyNot":["Iron deficiency causes a microcytic anemia but not neutropenia or a myelopathy.","Magnesium deficiency causes muscle cramps, tetany, arrhythmias, and low calcium and potassium.","Correct.","Selenium deficiency causes cardiomyopathy (Keshan disease) and muscle weakness, without anemia and neutropenia."],"study":{"topics":["Copper deficiency from excess zinc","Trace mineral deficiencies"],"comps":["GI-8"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C28","system":"GI","dx":"Short bowel syndrome after ileal resection for Crohn disease","codes":["GI-13-E"],"case":"A 34-year-old woman with Crohn disease comes to the physician because of 6 to 8 loose, greasy, foul-smelling stools daily and a 9-kg (20-lb) weight loss. Over the past 3 years, repeated resections for strictures and fistulas removed most of her ileum, including the terminal ileum and ileocecal valve, leaving about 150 cm of small bowel joined to an intact colon. Hemoglobin concentration is 10.1 g/dL (12 to 16), and mean corpuscular volume is 108 fL (80 to 100). Serum vitamin B12 concentration is 120 pg/mL (200 to 900). Fecal fat excretion is 22 g/day (under 7). Two weeks ago she passed a kidney stone composed of calcium oxalate. Diagnosis: short bowel syndrome after ileal resection.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C28-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her calcium oxalate kidney stone?","options":["Increased hepatic synthesis of oxalate from glyoxylate","Uric acid supersaturation from loss of bicarbonate in stool","Increased intestinal calcium absorption causing hypercalciuria","Unabsorbed fatty acids binding calcium in the gut lumen"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-E","obj":"GI-13-E.5","dok":3,"approach":["Link fat malabsorption to excess fatty acids in the lumen.","Fatty acids bind calcium, so less calcium is available to bind dietary oxalate.","Free, soluble oxalate is absorbed in the retained colon, and urinary oxalate rises."],"why":"Dietary calcium normally binds oxalate in the gut lumen, forming an insoluble salt that leaves in stool. With fat malabsorption, unabsorbed fatty acids bind calcium (saponification), leaving oxalate free and soluble. Unabsorbed bile acids and fatty acids also increase colonic permeability, so the colon absorbs much more oxalate. This enteric hyperoxaluria causes calcium oxalate stones and requires an intact colon. Her ileal resection also explains the steatorrhea (bile acid loss) and B12 deficiency (loss of the receptor for intrinsic factor and B12).","whyNot":["Increased hepatic oxalate production occurs in primary hyperoxaluria, an inherited enzyme defect. Her problem starts in the gut.","Loss of bicarbonate and volume in diarrhea does favor uric acid stones, but her stone is calcium oxalate.","Calcium absorption falls with fat malabsorption, because calcium is bound by fatty acids and vitamin D is poorly absorbed.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Short bowel syndrome","Enteric hyperoxaluria"],"comps":["GI-13-E","GI-7"]}},{"id":"GI-C28-Q2","stem":"Her resected ileum showed transmural inflammation, fissuring ulcers, and noncaseating granulomas. Which of the following immune mechanisms best explains the granulomas?","options":["IL-4 and IL-5 from Th2 cells recruiting eosinophils","Histamine release from mast cells triggered by IgE","Complement activation by immune complexes in vessel walls","Interferon-gamma from Th1 cells activating macrophages"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that granulomas are collections of activated macrophages (epithelioid cells) and giant cells.","Macrophage activation is driven by interferon-gamma from Th1 cells, supported by IL-12 from macrophages.","Place this in Crohn disease: NOD2 variants impair handling of commensal bacteria, and Th1 and Th17 responses drive transmural inflammation."],"why":"In Crohn disease, NOD2 variants impair intracellular sensing of bacterial peptidoglycan, so commensal bacteria that cross the epithelium are cleared poorly. Persistent antigen drives macrophages and dendritic cells to make IL-12 and IL-23, which push CD4 T cells toward Th1 and Th17 responses. Interferon-gamma from Th1 cells activates macrophages, which become epithelioid cells and form granulomas, while IL-17 recruits neutrophils. The inflammation extends through the full thickness of the wall, causing strictures and fistulas.","whyNot":["A Th2 response with IL-4, IL-5, and eosinophils fits allergic disease and helminth infection and does not form these granulomas.","IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation is an immediate (type I) hypersensitivity reaction and does not form granulomas.","Immune complex deposition (type III hypersensitivity) causes vasculitis and glomerulonephritis. It does not form granulomas.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Crohn disease immunopathogenesis","NOD2 and Th1 and Th17 responses"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-G9"]}},{"id":"GI-C28-Q3","stem":"Despite adequate total calories, she develops dry, scaly dermatitis, hair thinning, and poor wound healing. The plasma triene to tetraene ratio is 0.5 (under 0.2). Lack of which of the following dietary fatty acids is the primary cause?","options":["Palmitic acid","Linoleic acid","Oleic acid","Arachidonic acid"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.1","dok":3,"approach":["Link severe fat malabsorption to essential fatty acid deficiency.","Recall that humans cannot insert double bonds beyond carbon 9, so linoleic acid (omega-6) and alpha-linolenic acid (omega-3) must come from the diet.","Read the high triene to tetraene ratio: without linoleic acid, the body makes Mead acid (a triene) instead of arachidonic acid (a tetraene)."],"why":"Linoleic and alpha-linolenic acids are the essential fatty acids because humans lack the desaturases that add double bonds beyond carbon 9. Chronic steatorrhea can cause deficiency even when calories are adequate. Without linoleic acid, arachidonic acid falls and oleic acid is converted to Mead acid, raising the triene to tetraene ratio above about 0.2. Deficiency causes scaly dermatitis, hair loss, and poor wound healing.","whyNot":["Palmitic acid is the main product of fatty acid synthase and can be made from carbohydrate, so it is not essential.","Correct.","Oleic acid is made by desaturating stearic acid at carbon 9. In essential fatty acid deficiency, oleic acid is the precursor of Mead acid.","Arachidonic acid is made from linoleic acid, so it is needed in the diet only when linoleic acid is lacking. Its fall here follows the linoleic acid deficiency."],"study":{"topics":["Essential fatty acids","Essential fatty acid deficiency"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-13-E"]}},{"id":"GI-C28-Q4","stem":"A semi-elemental enteral formula containing mostly dipeptides and tripeptides is chosen to improve her protein absorption. Which of the following transport mechanisms moves these peptides into her enterocytes?","options":["Proton cotransport through PepT1","Endocytosis by microfold (M) cells","Sodium cotransport through SGLT1","Facilitated diffusion through GLUT5"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that protein digestion yields free amino acids and small peptides.","Free amino acids enter mainly with sodium, while dipeptides and tripeptides enter with hydrogen ions through PepT1.","Inside the enterocyte, peptidases split them into amino acids that exit to the blood."],"why":"Brush-border peptidases leave a mix of free amino acids and dipeptides and tripeptides. Free amino acids enter through several sodium-linked transporters, while di- and tripeptides enter through PepT1, which uses the hydrogen ion gradient kept by the apical Na+/H+ exchanger. Because peptides use a separate carrier, they are absorbed well even when single amino acid transporters are defective. In Hartnup disease, loss of a neutral amino acid transporter causes tryptophan deficiency and pellagra-like findings, yet tryptophan in peptides is still absorbed.","whyNot":["Correct.","M cells over Peyer patches sample particles and microbes for the immune system and do not absorb dietary protein in meaningful amounts.","SGLT1 carries glucose and galactose with sodium. It does not carry peptides.","GLUT5 carries fructose by facilitated diffusion."],"study":{"topics":["Amino acid and peptide absorption","Hartnup disease"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-8"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C29","system":"GI","dx":"Bacterial overgrowth in a blind loop after Billroth II gastrectomy","codes":["GI-13-E"],"case":"A 71-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of bulky, foul-smelling, greasy stools, bloating, numbness in both feet, and a 6-kg (13-lb) weight loss. Twenty years ago he had a partial gastrectomy with Billroth II gastrojejunostomy for a bleeding peptic ulcer. Hemoglobin concentration is 10.2 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), and mean corpuscular volume is 110 fL (80 to 100). Serum vitamin B12 concentration is 140 pg/mL (200 to 900), and serum folate is 24 ng/mL (2.7 to 17). Fecal fat excretion is 18 g/day (under 7). Culture of jejunal aspirate grows more than 100,000 colony-forming units/mL of mixed anaerobic and aerobic bacteria. Diagnosis: bacterial overgrowth in the afferent (blind) loop.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C29-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the combination of a low serum vitamin B12 and a high serum folate concentration?","options":["Bacterial uptake of B12 and bacterial synthesis of folate","Ethanol-induced malabsorption of both B12 and folate","Ileal mucosal damage with preserved jejunal folate uptake","Autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor with a high-folate diet"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-E","obj":"GI-13-E.6","dok":3,"approach":["Note the stasis in the afferent loop and the heavy bacterial growth in the aspirate.","Recall that anaerobic bacteria take up B12 in the lumen before it reaches the ileum.","Recall that many intestinal bacteria synthesize folate, which is absorbed and raises serum folate."],"why":"Food does not pass through the afferent loop of a Billroth II, so stasis lets colonic-type bacteria multiply. These bacteria take up vitamin B12 in the lumen, so less reaches the ileum, causing B12 deficiency with macrocytic anemia and neuropathy. Many of the same bacteria make folate, which the jejunum absorbs, so serum folate is normal or high. Bacterial deconjugation of bile acids adds steatorrhea. This pattern helps separate bacterial overgrowth from other causes of malabsorption, which usually lower folate too.","whyNot":["Correct.","Alcohol use lowers folate, which is the opposite of what is seen here.","Ileal mucosal disease would lower B12, but it does not raise folate, and the culture points to overgrowth as the cause.","Pernicious anemia lowers B12 through loss of intrinsic factor, but it does not explain a high folate, and nothing here points to an autoimmune process."],"study":{"topics":["Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth","Blind loop syndrome"],"comps":["GI-13-E","GI-8"]}},{"id":"GI-C29-Q2","stem":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency is also considered. After a 25-g oral dose of D-xylose, urinary xylose excretion over 5 hours is 2.1 g (normal above 4), and fecal elastase is 380 µg/g (normal above 200). Which of the following is the best interpretation of these results?","options":["Pancreatic lipase output is too low for fat digestion","The defect lies in the small-bowel lumen or mucosa","Xylose uptake requires bile acid micelles in the lumen","Xylose absorption requires pancreatic amylase activity"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-13-E","obj":"GI-13-E.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that D-xylose is absorbed directly by the small-bowel mucosa without pancreatic enzymes or bile.","A low urinary xylose therefore points to mucosal disease or bacterial consumption of xylose in the lumen.","A normal fecal elastase confirms normal pancreatic enzyme output."],"why":"D-xylose is a pentose that needs no digestion, so its absorption tests the surface and lumen of the small bowel. In pancreatic insufficiency, xylose absorption is normal while fat is poorly digested, and fecal elastase is low. In mucosal disease such as celiac disease, or in bacterial overgrowth, urinary xylose is low. In overgrowth the bacteria consume xylose in the lumen, so the result typically improves after antibiotics. A related enzyme problem, congenital enteropeptidase deficiency, blocks activation of trypsinogen and therefore of the other pancreatic proteases.","whyNot":["Low pancreatic lipase output would come with a low fecal elastase, and his elastase is normal. Pancreatic insufficiency also leaves D-xylose absorption normal.","Correct.","D-xylose is water soluble and is absorbed without micelles. Bile acids are needed for fat absorption.","D-xylose is a monosaccharide and needs no amylase. That is why it separates mucosal from pancreatic causes."],"study":{"topics":["D-xylose test","Pancreatic versus mucosal malabsorption"],"comps":["GI-13-E","GI-13-E3"]}},{"id":"GI-C29-Q3","stem":"In normal vitamin B12 absorption, pancreatic juice mixes with gastric contents in the duodenum. Which of the following is the role of pancreatic proteases in this process?","options":["Cleaving intrinsic factor so B12 can enter ileal cells","Releasing B12 from the proteins in which it is ingested","Degrading haptocorrin so B12 can bind intrinsic factor","Converting cyanocobalamin to active coenzyme forms"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.3","dok":2,"approach":["Trace B12 in order: release from food, binding to haptocorrin, release from haptocorrin, binding to intrinsic factor, ileal uptake.","Place the pancreatic proteases at the duodenal step.","Predict that pancreatic insufficiency can impair B12 absorption by leaving B12 trapped on haptocorrin."],"why":"Gastric acid and pepsin release B12 from food proteins, and free B12 binds haptocorrin (R binder) from saliva and the stomach. In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases degrade haptocorrin, releasing B12 to bind intrinsic factor from parietal cells. The intrinsic factor and B12 complex is taken up in the terminal ileum by the cubilin receptor. In the blood, B12 travels on transcobalamin II to the tissues.","whyNot":["Intrinsic factor stays intact. The whole intrinsic factor and B12 complex binds the cubilin receptor in the terminal ileum.","Release of B12 from food proteins is done by gastric acid and pepsin, which is why low acid states can impair B12 absorption.","Correct.","Conversion to methylcobalamin and adenosylcobalamin happens inside cells after absorption."],"study":{"topics":["Steps of vitamin B12 absorption"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-13-E1"]}},{"id":"GI-C29-Q4","stem":"His steatorrhea results in part from bacterial deconjugation of bile acids in the afferent loop. Which of the following best explains why deconjugated bile acids leave the jejunal lumen too early?","options":["They are packaged into chylomicrons with dietary lipids","They are degraded by pancreatic lipase to free cholesterol","They are less ionized and diffuse passively across the mucosa","They bind the sodium-dependent bile acid transporter of the jejunum"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that conjugation with glycine or taurine lowers the pKa of bile acids, keeping them ionized at intestinal pH.","Ionized bile acids cannot cross the mucosa passively, so they stay in the lumen to form micelles until the terminal ileum reclaims them.","Deconjugated bile acids are less ionized, so they diffuse out early and the luminal concentration falls."],"why":"In the normal enterohepatic circulation, conjugated bile acids stay in the lumen through the jejunum, form micelles that carry fats to the mucosa, and are reabsorbed actively in the terminal ileum. Bacteria in a blind loop deconjugate them. Unconjugated bile acids have a higher pKa, so more are un-ionized, and they are absorbed passively in the proximal bowel or precipitate. The luminal bile acid concentration falls below what is needed for micelles, and fat is malabsorbed.","whyNot":["Chylomicrons carry triglycerides, cholesterol esters, and fat-soluble vitamins. Bile acids are not packaged into them.","Pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes triglycerides. It does not degrade bile acids, and bile acids are not turned back into cholesterol.","Correct.","The apical sodium-dependent bile acid transporter is found in the terminal ileum. The jejunum lacks it."],"study":{"topics":["Bile acid conjugation and micelles","Enterohepatic circulation"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-9"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C30","system":"GI","dx":"Achalasia","codes":["GI-13-F1"],"case":"A 45-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 1-year history of difficulty swallowing both solids and liquids, regurgitation of undigested food at night, and a 5-kg (11-lb) weight loss. Barium swallow shows a dilated esophagus that tapers smoothly to a bird-beak narrowing at the gastroesophageal junction. Esophageal manometry shows absent peristalsis in the esophageal body and a high resting pressure of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) with incomplete relaxation on swallowing. Upper endoscopy shows retained food and no mass at the gastroesophageal junction. Diagnosis: achalasia.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C30-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the failure of her lower esophageal sphincter to relax?","options":["Loss of excitatory myenteric neurons releasing acetylcholine","Hypertrophy of LES muscle driven by excess gastrin","Smooth muscle atrophy and fibrosis of the LES","Loss of myenteric neurons releasing nitric oxide and VIP"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-13-F1","obj":"GI-13-F.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note incomplete LES relaxation with aperistalsis on manometry.","Recall that LES relaxation during swallowing depends on inhibitory myenteric neurons that release nitric oxide and VIP.","Conclude that loss of these neurons leaves excitatory cholinergic tone unopposed."],"why":"LES tone at rest comes from the muscle itself and from excitatory cholinergic neurons. When a person swallows, inhibitory neurons of the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus release nitric oxide and VIP, which relax the sphincter. In achalasia these inhibitory neurons are selectively lost, probably through an immune attack, so the LES stays closed and its pressure rises. In Chagas disease, Trypanosoma cruzi destroys the same plexus and causes secondary achalasia.","whyNot":["Excitatory cholinergic neurons maintain LES tone and are relatively preserved. Losing them would lower LES pressure.","Gastrin can raise LES tone, but excess gastrin does not cause failed relaxation, and nothing here suggests hypergastrinemia.","Smooth muscle atrophy and fibrosis occur in systemic sclerosis, which lowers LES pressure and causes reflux.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Achalasia pathophysiology","Enteric neurotransmitters"],"comps":["GI-13-F1","GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C30-Q2","stem":"In a healthy person, manometry after a swallow shows a contraction that travels down the esophageal body in sequence. In this patient, it shows only weak contractions that occur at the same time along its length. Loss of which of the following normal mechanisms best explains this difference?","options":["Receptive relaxation of the proximal stomach","Slow-wave pacing generated by interstitial cells of Cajal","Motilin-driven migrating motor complexes","Relaxation below the bolus with contraction above it"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that peristalsis needs contraction above the bolus and relaxation below it, coordinated by the myenteric plexus.","Relaxation ahead of the bolus comes from inhibitory neurons, and longer inhibition in more distal segments sets the timing of the wave.","Without inhibitory neurons, all segments contract at once, so the bolus is not propelled."],"why":"Peristalsis is a reflex of the myenteric plexus. Excitatory neurons (acetylcholine, substance P) contract the muscle above a bolus, while inhibitory neurons (nitric oxide, VIP) relax the muscle below it. In the smooth muscle esophagus, inhibition that lasts longer in more distal segments delays their contraction and creates a sequential wave. Losing the inhibitory neurons in achalasia removes this delay, so contractions become simultaneous or absent. Elsewhere in the gut, slow waves from interstitial cells of Cajal set contraction frequency, and motilin drives the migrating motor complex during fasting.","whyNot":["Receptive relaxation is a vagal reflex that relaxes the proximal stomach as food arrives. It does not coordinate the esophageal wave.","Slow waves from interstitial cells of Cajal set the maximum contraction frequency in the stomach and intestine. The order of a swallow-induced esophageal wave depends on the myenteric plexus.","Motilin drives the migrating motor complex that sweeps the stomach and small intestine between meals. It does not produce the swallow-induced esophageal wave.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Peristalsis and the myenteric plexus","Slow waves and the migrating motor complex"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-F1"]}},{"id":"GI-C30-Q3","stem":"She declines treatment. Fifteen years later, she has a 4-month history of worsening dysphagia, now with difficulty swallowing her own saliva, and a further 8-kg (18-lb) weight loss. Which of the following tumors is most likely?","options":["Squamous cell carcinoma of the mid esophagus","Adenocarcinoma of the distal esophagus","Leiomyoma of the distal esophagus","Gastrointestinal stromal tumor of the cardia"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K1","obj":"GI-13-K.1","dok":2,"approach":["Link long-standing untreated achalasia to stasis of food in a dilated esophagus.","Chronic stasis and irritation of the squamous lining raise the risk of squamous cell carcinoma, usually in the middle of the dilated esophagus.","Separate this from adenocarcinoma, which arises from Barrett esophagus after chronic reflux."],"why":"Food and fluid retained in the dilated esophagus cause chronic inflammation of the squamous lining. Over many years this raises the risk of squamous cell carcinoma, usually in the mid esophagus. Other risk factors for squamous cell carcinoma are alcohol, tobacco, caustic injury, and Plummer-Vinson syndrome. Adenocarcinoma arises in the distal third from Barrett esophagus, with GERD and obesity as risk factors. Both cancers present with progressive dysphagia, first to solids and then to liquids, with weight loss.","whyNot":["Correct.","Distal adenocarcinoma arises from Barrett esophagus after chronic reflux. An untreated achalasia sphincter tends to block reflux.","Leiomyomas are benign, slow-growing smooth muscle tumors and are not linked to achalasia.","Gastrointestinal stromal tumors arise from interstitial cells of Cajal, most often in the stomach, and are not linked to achalasia."],"study":{"topics":["Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma versus adenocarcinoma"],"comps":["GI-13-K1","GI-13-G2"]}},{"id":"GI-C30-Q4","stem":"She later undergoes pneumatic balloon dilation, with the balloon centered on the most distal of the three normal narrowings of the esophagus. Which of the following forms this narrowing, and at what vertebral level?","options":["Median arcuate ligament at T12","Right crus of the diaphragm at T10","Aortic arch and left main bronchus at T4","Cricopharyngeus muscle at C6"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-4","obj":"GI-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Name the three normal narrowings from top to bottom: cricopharyngeus, aortic arch and left main bronchus, diaphragmatic hiatus.","Recall that the most distal one is where the esophagus passes through the diaphragm at T10.","Identify the right crus as the muscle whose fibers form the esophageal hiatus."],"why":"The esophagus begins at the cricopharyngeus at about C6, descends through the superior and posterior mediastinum behind the trachea and left atrium, and passes through the esophageal hiatus at T10 to join the stomach. Fibers of the right crus of the diaphragm surround the hiatus and act as an external sphincter that adds to LES pressure. The three narrowings, at the cricopharyngeus, at the crossing of the aortic arch and left main bronchus, and at the hiatus, are common sites for swallowed objects to lodge and for caustic injury.","whyNot":["The median arcuate ligament forms the aortic hiatus at T12, which transmits the aorta and thoracic duct.","Correct.","The aortic arch and left main bronchus cross the esophagus in the thorax at about the T4 level, forming the middle narrowing.","The cricopharyngeus forms the upper esophageal sphincter at about C6, the most proximal narrowing."],"study":{"topics":["Course and narrowings of the esophagus"],"comps":["GI-4","GI-13-F1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C31","system":"GI","dx":"Adhesive small bowel obstruction after appendectomy","codes":["GI-13-I2","GI-13-F2"],"case":"A 38-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of a 2-day history of crampy periumbilical pain, repeated bilious vomiting, and abdominal distension. He has passed no stool or flatus for 24 hours. He underwent open appendectomy for perforated appendicitis 3 years ago. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mmHg. The abdomen is distended, with a healed right lower quadrant scar and high-pitched, tinkling bowel sounds. Serum studies show Na+ 136 mEq/L (135 to 145), K+ 3.1 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.0), Cl- 88 mEq/L (98 to 106), and HCO3- 34 mEq/L (22 to 28). CT scan shows dilated small bowel proximal to a transition point in the right lower quadrant, with collapsed bowel distally and no hernia or mass. The diagnosis is small bowel obstruction from postoperative adhesions.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C31-Q1","stem":"Which of the following processes most likely produced the fibrous band that is obstructing this patient's bowel?","options":["Implantation of ectopic endometrial tissue on the peritoneum at surgery","Organization of persistent serosal fibrin by fibroblasts into collagen","Granulomatous reaction to retained suture material forming a mass","Hypertrophic scarring of the skin incision tethering the underlying ileum"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-I2","obj":"GI-13-I.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note the setting: earlier surgery for perforated appendicitis, so the peritoneum was both cut and inflamed.","Injured mesothelium leaks a fibrin-rich exudate that glues nearby serosal surfaces together.","Fibrin that is not cleared becomes scaffolding for fibroblasts and new vessels, which lay down a collagen band that can kink or trap a loop of bowel."],"why":"Surgery and peritonitis injure the mesothelium and cause a fibrinous exudate between serosal surfaces. Normally, mesothelial cells release tissue plasminogen activator, and plasmin dissolves this fibrin within days. Injury and inflammation reduce this fibrinolysis, so the fibrin persists and is organized by fibroblasts into collagen bands. These adhesions are the most common cause of small bowel obstruction in adults who have had abdominal surgery.","whyNot":["Endometriosis can cause pelvic adhesions, but it comes from retrograde menstruation or metaplasia in women. It does not arise from a surgical procedure in a man.","Correct.","A suture granuloma forms a small nodule of macrophages around foreign material. It does not form the long fibrous bands that obstruct bowel.","Skin scars stay in the dermis. They do not reach through the abdominal wall muscles to bind the bowel."],"study":{"topics":["Formation of peritoneal adhesions","Causes of small bowel obstruction"],"comps":["GI-13-I2","GI-13-F2"]}},{"id":"GI-C31-Q2","stem":"Which of the following findings in this patient best distinguishes his condition from adynamic ileus?","options":["High-pitched bowel sounds and a CT transition point","Abdominal distension with repeated bilious vomiting","Absence of stool and flatus for the past 24 hours","Serum potassium concentration below the reference range"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-F2","obj":"GI-13-F.2","dok":3,"approach":["Both mechanical obstruction and adynamic ileus cause distension, vomiting, and no passage of stool or gas, so those findings cannot separate them.","In mechanical obstruction, the bowel above the block contracts hard, which makes high-pitched, tinkling sounds.","Imaging shows dilated loops only up to one point, with collapsed bowel beyond it. In ileus, the bowel is quiet and gas is spread diffusely through small bowel and colon."],"why":"Mechanical obstruction has a physical block. The bowel proximal to it works against the block, so bowel sounds are high pitched and rushing, and CT shows a transition point with dilated loops above and collapsed bowel below. Adynamic ileus is a failure of motility with no physical block. Bowel sounds are reduced or absent, and gas fills small bowel and colon diffusely, with no transition point.","whyNot":["Correct.","Distension and vomiting occur in both conditions because contents build up in either case. They do not tell you whether a physical block is present.","Obstipation occurs in both conditions. It tells you contents are not moving forward, without telling you why.","Low potassium is a known cause of adynamic ileus, so it would point away from mechanical obstruction. Here it is a result of vomiting."],"study":{"topics":["Mechanical obstruction versus adynamic ileus","Bowel sounds and imaging in obstruction"],"comps":["GI-13-F2","GI-13-I2"]}},{"id":"GI-C31-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains why this patient's kidneys have not corrected his elevated serum bicarbonate concentration?","options":["Loss of pancreatic bicarbonate in the vomited intestinal fluid","Hypoaldosteronism that limits distal hydrogen ion secretion","Release of bicarbonate from bone as it buffers lost gastric acid","Chloride and volume depletion that preserve bicarbonate"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.7","dok":3,"approach":["Read the labs: high HCO3-, low Cl-, and low K+ after days of vomiting point to a hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis.","Vomiting removes HCl, which generates the alkalosis. Something must also stop the kidney from excreting the extra bicarbonate.","Volume loss activates renin and aldosterone, and low chloride limits bicarbonate secretion in the collecting duct, so the kidney keeps reabsorbing bicarbonate."],"why":"Vomiting is triggered when the chemoreceptor trigger zone in the area postrema and visceral afferents from the distended gut activate the medullary vomiting center. Each liter of vomited gastric juice removes H+ and Cl-, which raises plasma bicarbonate. Volume depletion then activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, which increases Na+ reabsorption with H+ and K+ secretion. Low chloride also limits the collecting duct exchanger that secretes bicarbonate in return for chloride. The kidney therefore holds on to bicarbonate until volume and chloride are replaced.","whyNot":["Losing bicarbonate-rich intestinal or pancreatic fluid lowers serum bicarbonate and causes metabolic acidosis. His bicarbonate is high.","Low aldosterone reduces H+ and K+ secretion and causes a hyperkalemic acidosis. Volume loss raises aldosterone in this patient.","Bone buffers acid in chronic metabolic acidosis, releasing carbonate and calcium. Losing acid does not draw bicarbonate out of bone.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Metabolic alkalosis from vomiting","Vomiting center and chemoreceptor trigger zone"],"comps":["GI-6","UR-7"]}},{"id":"GI-C31-Q4","stem":"At laparoscopy, the obstructing band attaches a loop of ileum to the parietal peritoneum of the anterior abdominal wall. Which of the following layers forms the outer surface of the ileal wall at the site of attachment?","options":["Muscularis externa with inner circular and outer longitudinal layers","Adventitia of dense connective tissue blending into nearby tissues","Serosa of mesothelium over a thin layer of loose connective tissue","Submucosa of dense irregular tissue containing the Meissner plexus"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.1","dok":2,"approach":["Decide whether the ileum is intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal. It hangs on a mesentery, so it is intraperitoneal.","Intraperitoneal gut is covered by visceral peritoneum, which forms the serosa.","Adhesions join serosal (mesothelial) surfaces, so the band attaches to the serosa."],"why":"The gut wall has four layers: mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and an outer serosa or adventitia. Intraperitoneal segments such as the jejunum and ileum are covered by serosa, which is a mesothelium on a thin layer of loose connective tissue. Adhesions form between injured mesothelial surfaces, which is why they link loops of bowel to one another and to the parietal peritoneum.","whyNot":["The muscularis externa lies deep to the serosa. It holds the myenteric plexus between its two layers but is not the outer surface.","Adventitia covers gut that has no peritoneal covering, such as most of the esophagus and the retroperitoneal parts of the colon and duodenum. The ileum is intraperitoneal.","Correct.","The submucosa lies between the mucosa and the muscularis externa. It is deep inside the wall and is not exposed to the peritoneal cavity."],"study":{"topics":["Layers of the gut wall","Serosa versus adventitia"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-3"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C32","system":"GI","dx":"Strangulated indirect inguinal hernia","codes":["GI-13-F3"],"case":"A 67-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of a painful right groin swelling for 10 hours and three episodes of vomiting. For 2 years, he has had a right groin bulge that appeared with straining and disappeared when he lay down. Temperature is 38.1 °C (100.6 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 128/80 mmHg. Physical examination shows a tense, tender, erythematous right inguinoscrotal mass that cannot be reduced. The abdomen is distended. Leukocyte count is 15,800/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000), and serum lactate concentration is 3.6 mmol/L (0.5 to 2.2). At emergency surgery, the hernia sac contains a dark, edematous loop of ileum. The diagnosis is a strangulated indirect inguinal hernia.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C32-Q1","stem":"Which of the following sequences of events most likely produced the dark, edematous loop of ileum?","options":["Arterial occlusion at the neck of the sac, then venous thrombosis","Mucosal invasion by bacteria, then ischemia of the serosal surface","Venous outflow blocked at the neck, then arterial inflow lost","Embolus to the superior mesenteric artery, then mucosal infarction"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-F3","obj":"GI-13-F.3","dok":2,"approach":["An irreducible hernia is incarcerated. Fever, tenderness, and a high lactate mean the trapped bowel has lost its blood supply, so it is strangulated.","The narrow neck of the sac squeezes the mesentery. Thin-walled, low-pressure veins collapse before thick-walled arteries do.","Blood keeps flowing in but cannot leave, so the loop swells until tissue pressure exceeds arterial pressure and the bowel infarcts."],"why":"In an incarcerated hernia, the neck of the sac first compresses the mesenteric veins. Venous outflow stops while arterial inflow continues, so the loop becomes congested and edematous. The swelling tightens the loop in the neck even more until arterial inflow fails. The result is a hemorrhagic infarct, which is why strangulated bowel looks dark red to black. Gangrene then allows bacteria to cross the wall, causing peritonitis and sepsis.","whyNot":["Arteries have thick walls and high pressure, so they are compressed last. The narrow neck squeezes the low-pressure veins first, which is why the loop became congested and edematous before it infarcted.","Bacteria cross the wall after ischemia has damaged the mucosal barrier. They follow ischemia and are a late step in strangulation.","Correct.","An embolus to the superior mesenteric artery infarcts long segments of small bowel and right colon. It does not explain a single loop trapped in a groin sac."],"study":{"topics":["Incarceration versus strangulation","Hemorrhagic infarction of bowel"],"comps":["GI-13-F3","GI-13-J3"]}},{"id":"GI-C32-Q2","stem":"At surgery, the hernia sac lies within the internal spermatic fascia beside the ductus deferens and extends into the scrotum. Which of the following best describes the course of this sac?","options":["Through the linea semilunaris, at the level of the arcuate line","Through the deep ring, lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels","Through the inguinal triangle, medial to the inferior epigastric vessels","Through the femoral ring, below the inguinal ligament"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-4","obj":"GI-4.5","dok":2,"approach":["A sac inside the spermatic cord coverings that reaches the scrotum followed the path of the testis through the deep ring.","The deep inguinal ring sits lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, so an indirect hernia begins lateral to them.","A direct hernia pushes through Hesselbach triangle medial to the vessels and lies outside the internal spermatic fascia."],"why":"An indirect inguinal hernia enters the deep inguinal ring, which lies lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, and follows the spermatic cord through the canal. It is covered by the same layers as the cord, including internal spermatic fascia, and can reach the scrotum. It often reflects a patent processus vaginalis. A direct hernia bulges through the weak posterior wall of the canal in Hesselbach triangle, bounded by the inferior epigastric vessels, the lateral border of the rectus abdominis, and the inguinal ligament.","whyNot":["This is a Spigelian hernia through the semilunar line on the anterior abdominal wall. It does not travel with the spermatic cord.","Correct.","This is the path of a direct hernia. It bulges through the posterior wall of the canal and usually stays outside the internal spermatic fascia, so it rarely reaches the scrotum.","This is a femoral hernia. It passes below the inguinal ligament, medial to the femoral vein, and presents in the upper thigh."],"study":{"topics":["Indirect versus direct inguinal hernia","Hesselbach triangle"],"comps":["GI-4","GI-13-F3"]}},{"id":"GI-C32-Q3","stem":"The loop of ileum could slide into the hernia sac because it is suspended from the posterior abdominal wall by a mesentery. Which of the following segments of the gastrointestinal tract has the same relationship to the peritoneum as the ileum?","options":["Ascending colon","Transverse colon","Second part of the duodenum","Descending colon"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.2","dok":2,"approach":["An organ with a mesentery is intraperitoneal and mobile.","Recall the retroperitoneal gut: duodenum parts 2 to 4, ascending and descending colon, and part of the rectum. These are fixed to the posterior wall.","The transverse colon hangs on the transverse mesocolon, so it is intraperitoneal like the ileum."],"why":"Intraperitoneal organs are wrapped in visceral peritoneum and attached by a mesentery, which lets them move. The jejunum, ileum, transverse colon, and sigmoid colon all have mesenteries, so they can enter hernia sacs or twist. Secondarily retroperitoneal structures, such as the ascending and descending colon and most of the duodenum, lost their mesentery during development and are fixed to the posterior abdominal wall.","whyNot":["The ascending colon is secondarily retroperitoneal and covered by peritoneum only on its front and sides.","Correct.","The second part of the duodenum is retroperitoneal and fixed beside the head of the pancreas. Only the first part of the duodenum is partly intraperitoneal.","The descending colon is secondarily retroperitoneal. Its mesentery fused with the posterior wall during development, so it is fixed in place."],"study":{"topics":["Intraperitoneal versus retroperitoneal organs"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-4"]}},{"id":"GI-C32-Q4","stem":"Before the loop became strangulated, contractions of the ileum proximal to the hernia were increased. Which of the following sets the maximum frequency of these contractions?","options":["Spike potentials fired by the submucosal (Meissner) plexus","Migrating motor complexes released by motilin during fasting","Vagal efferent bursts from the dorsal motor nucleus","Slow waves generated by the interstitial cells of Cajal"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Gut smooth muscle contracts only when spike potentials ride on top of a slow wave.","Slow waves are rhythmic membrane depolarizations made by the interstitial cells of Cajal, the pacemakers of the gut.","So the slow-wave rate is the ceiling for contraction frequency. Nerves and hormones change how many slow waves trigger a contraction and how strong it is."],"why":"Interstitial cells of Cajal produce slow waves at a set rate in each region, about 3 per minute in the stomach, about 12 in the duodenum, and about 8 to 9 in the terminal ileum. A contraction occurs only when a slow wave reaches threshold and triggers spike potentials, so the slow-wave rate is the maximum contraction rate. The myenteric plexus coordinates peristalsis by contracting the segment above a bolus and relaxing the segment below it. Proximal to an obstruction, this reflex is strongly driven, causing colicky pain.","whyNot":["The submucosal plexus mainly controls secretion and local blood flow. It does not set the rhythm of smooth muscle contraction.","Motilin triggers the migrating motor complex every 90 to 120 minutes during fasting to sweep debris along. It does not set the minute-to-minute contraction rate.","Vagal input changes the strength of contractions through the enteric nervous system, but the gut keeps its slow-wave rhythm even after vagotomy.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Slow waves and interstitial cells of Cajal","Peristaltic reflex"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-F2"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C33","system":"GI","dx":"Ileocolic intussusception with a Meckel diverticulum lead point","codes":["GI-13-F4","GI-13-H3"],"case":"A 4-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department because of 12 hours of episodes of severe abdominal pain during which he cries and draws his knees to his chest. Between episodes he is lethargic. He has vomited four times and has passed one stool of dark red blood mixed with mucus. Temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 130/min, respirations are 26/min, and blood pressure is 98/60 mmHg. Physical examination shows a sausage-shaped mass in the right upper quadrant. Abdominal ultrasonography shows a target sign. Air enema reduction fails. At surgery, the terminal ileum has telescoped into the cecum and ascending colon, with an inverted Meckel diverticulum at its tip. The diagnosis is ileocolic intussusception with a Meckel diverticulum lead point.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C33-Q1","stem":"Histologic examination of the resected diverticulum shows only ileal-type mucosa. Which of the following mechanisms best explains the dark red, mucoid stool?","options":["Venous congestion of the intussusceptum from trapped mesentery","Shiga toxin destroying the epithelial cells of the colonic mucosa","Acid from ectopic gastric mucosa ulcerating the adjacent ileum","Rupture of dilated submucosal vessels in the wall of the cecum"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-F4","obj":"GI-13-F.4","dok":3,"approach":["The biopsy rules out ectopic gastric mucosa as the bleeding source.","In intussusception, the inner segment (intussusceptum) pulls its mesentery in with it, and the outer segment squeezes that mesentery.","Veins are compressed first, so the inner bowel becomes congested, edematous, and ischemic. Its mucosa sloughs and releases blood and mucus, giving currant jelly stool."],"why":"Intussusception is telescoping of one bowel segment into the next, most often ileocolic in young children. A lead point, such as hypertrophied Peyer patches after a viral illness or a Meckel diverticulum in an older child, is pulled forward by peristalsis. The mesentery of the inner segment is trapped, so venous outflow stops, and the mucosa becomes congested and ischemic. Blood mixed with mucus from the sloughing mucosa produces currant jelly stool. Without reduction, the segment infarcts. Volvulus differs because the bowel twists on its mesentery, as in sigmoid volvulus in older adults or midgut volvulus with malrotation in infants.","whyNot":["Correct.","Shiga toxin from EHEC causes bloody diarrhea and can lead to hemolytic uremic syndrome. It does not cause a palpable mass or a target sign.","Ectopic gastric mucosa in a Meckel diverticulum causes painless bleeding from an ulcer in nearby ileum. This diverticulum had no gastric mucosa, and his bleeding came with colicky pain.","Dilated, fragile submucosal vessels (angiodysplasia) bleed painlessly in older adults. They are not a cause of bleeding in a child with intussusception."],"study":{"topics":["Intussusception and currant jelly stool","Intussusception versus volvulus"],"comps":["GI-13-F4","GI-13-H3"]}},{"id":"GI-C33-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes the origin and wall structure of the lesion that served as this patient's lead point?","options":["Patent urachus joining the ileum to the bladder dome","False diverticulum of mucosa herniating where vasa recta enter","Duplication cyst from failed recanalization of the ileum","True diverticulum from a persistent vitelline duct"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-H3","obj":"GI-13-H.3","dok":2,"approach":["A Meckel diverticulum is a remnant of the vitelline (omphalomesenteric) duct, which joins the midgut to the yolk sac in the embryo.","The duct normally obliterates early in development. If its intestinal end persists, it forms an outpouching on the antimesenteric border of the ileum.","Because it is a remnant of the whole gut tube, it contains all layers of the wall, so it is a true diverticulum."],"why":"Meckel diverticulum is the most common congenital anomaly of the gastrointestinal tract. It is a true diverticulum containing mucosa, submucosa, and muscularis, on the antimesenteric border of the ileum. The rule of twos describes it: about 2% of people, about 2 feet (60 cm) from the ileocecal valve, about 2 inches long, often with 2 types of ectopic tissue (gastric and pancreatic), and usually symptomatic by age 2. It presents as painless rectal bleeding from ectopic gastric mucosa, or as obstruction when it acts as a lead point for intussusception or a band for volvulus.","whyNot":["The urachus is the allantois remnant joining the bladder to the umbilicus. A patent urachus leaks urine at the umbilicus and has no link to the ileum.","False diverticula of mucosa and submucosa form where vasa recta pierce the muscle, as in sigmoid diverticulosis in older adults. They lack a muscle layer.","Duplication cysts are rare tubular or cystic structures attached to the mesenteric side of the bowel. They are not remnants of the vitelline duct.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Meckel diverticulum and the rule of twos","True versus false diverticula"],"comps":["GI-13-H3","GI-1"]}},{"id":"GI-C33-Q3","stem":"At the onset of his illness, the pain was dull and poorly localized around the umbilicus. Which of the following best explains this location?","options":["Foregut structure supplied by the celiac trunk, with afferents to about T5 to T9","Hindgut structure supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery, with afferents to about L1 to L2","Midgut structure supplied by the superior mesenteric artery, with afferents to about T10","Parietal peritoneum supplied by somatic intercostal nerves, with afferents to about T12"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-1","obj":"GI-1.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the involved bowel: terminal ileum, cecum, and ascending colon.","These are midgut derivatives, supplied by the superior mesenteric artery.","Visceral afferents from the midgut enter the cord at about T10, so the pain is felt in the T10 dermatome around the umbilicus."],"why":"Visceral pain fibers travel back with sympathetic nerves and enter the spinal cord at the levels that supplied the embryonic gut segment. Pain is then referred to the dermatomes of those levels. Foregut pain (to the second part of the duodenum, celiac trunk) is felt in the epigastrium. Midgut pain (to the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon, superior mesenteric artery) is felt around the umbilicus. Hindgut pain (inferior mesenteric artery) is felt in the suprapubic region. Visceral pain is dull and poorly localized because the gut is innervated on both sides of the midline by few afferent fibers.","whyNot":["The foregut ends at the second part of the duodenum. Its pain is felt in the epigastrium, and none of this boy's involved bowel is foregut.","The hindgut begins at the distal third of the transverse colon. Its pain is felt in the suprapubic region.","Correct.","Parietal peritoneum gives sharp, well-localized somatic pain over the inflamed area. It explains sharp, localized pain later in an illness. His early pain was dull and central."],"study":{"topics":["Foregut, midgut, and hindgut derivatives","Referred visceral pain"],"comps":["GI-1","GI-5"]}},{"id":"GI-C33-Q4","stem":"During the same week, his 10-month-old sister develops vomiting and profuse watery diarrhea during a winter outbreak at her day care center. Stool testing identifies a nonenveloped virus with a segmented, double-stranded RNA genome. Which of the following mechanisms best explains her diarrhea?","options":["Loss of villus-tip enterocytes, with villus blunting and malabsorption","ADP-ribosylation of Gs protein, raising enterocyte cAMP and chloride loss","Invasion of colonic mucosa through M cells, with ulceration and bleeding","Inactivation of the 60S ribosomal subunit in colonic epithelial cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the virus: nonenveloped, segmented double-stranded RNA, infant, winter, day care. This is rotavirus.","Rotavirus infects the mature absorptive enterocytes at the tips of small intestinal villi.","Loss of these cells blunts villi and reduces absorption and disaccharidase activity, so fluid and unabsorbed sugar stay in the lumen. The viral protein NSP4 also increases secretion."],"why":"Rotavirus, a reovirus with a segmented double-stranded RNA genome, is the classic cause of severe watery diarrhea in infants and young children, especially in winter. It kills mature enterocytes at the villus tips, which blunts the villi and leaves immature crypt cells that absorb poorly. Its NSP4 protein acts as an enterotoxin that raises intracellular calcium and chloride secretion. Norovirus, a single-stranded RNA calicivirus, causes outbreaks in all ages, and the enteroviruses (coxsackievirus, echovirus) are single-stranded RNA picornaviruses spread by the fecal-oral route.","whyNot":["Correct.","This is the mechanism of cholera toxin and the heat-labile toxin of enterotoxigenic E. coli, which are bacteria. It does not describe a double-stranded RNA virus.","M cell invasion with mucosal ulceration describes Shigella and invasive bacteria, which cause bloody diarrhea with fever.","Ribosome inactivation is the action of Shiga toxin and Shiga-like toxin. It causes bloody diarrhea. Her illness is watery and viral."],"study":{"topics":["Rotavirus diarrhea","Comparison of viral causes of enteritis"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-L3"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C34","system":"GI","dx":"Chagas disease megacolon","codes":["GI-13-F5"],"case":"A 52-year-old woman comes to the physician because of worsening constipation and abdominal distension. She had normal bowel habits until 8 years ago and now has a bowel movement once every 2 to 3 weeks. She grew up in a rural area of Bolivia in a house with an adobe and thatch roof and moved to the United States 10 years ago. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 62/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 122/76 mmHg. Physical examination shows a distended, tympanitic abdomen. An electrocardiogram shows right bundle branch block. Abdominal radiograph shows a massively dilated sigmoid colon and rectum. Serologic testing is positive for IgG antibodies against Trypanosoma cruzi. The diagnosis is megacolon caused by chronic Chagas disease.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C34-Q1","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms most likely underlies this patient's megacolon?","options":["Mechanical blockage by a stenosing rectal adenocarcinoma","Transmural colitis with cytokine-mediated loss of muscle tone","Congenital absence of ganglion cells from a RET mutation","Acquired inflammatory destruction of enteric ganglion cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-F5","obj":"GI-13-F.5","dok":2,"approach":["Normal bowel function for decades, followed by slowly progressive constipation, means the problem was acquired.","Childhood in an area with triatomine bugs, positive T. cruzi serology, and a conduction block point to chronic Chagas disease.","In chronic Chagas disease, parasite-driven inflammation destroys enteric neurons, especially in the myenteric plexus, so the colon cannot propel stool and dilates."],"why":"Trypanosoma cruzi is spread by triatomine (reduviid) bugs in rural Latin America. Years after infection, parasite persistence and immune-mediated inflammation destroy neurons of the enteric plexuses and the cardiac conduction system. Loss of enteric neurons causes megaesophagus and megacolon, and cardiac involvement causes dilated cardiomyopathy and conduction blocks such as right bundle branch block. Hirschsprung disease differs because ganglion cells are absent from birth, so symptoms start in the newborn. Toxic megacolon differs because it is an acute, life-threatening dilation during severe colitis.","whyNot":["A rectal cancer can cause obstruction and proximal dilation, but the dilated segment here includes the rectum itself, and serology explains the cause.","This describes toxic megacolon, an acute complication of ulcerative colitis or C. difficile colitis with fever and systemic toxicity. Her course is slow and painless.","This is Hirschsprung disease. It presents in the newborn with delayed passage of meconium, which does not fit normal bowel habits until middle age.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Causes of megacolon","Chronic Chagas disease"],"comps":["GI-13-F5","IN-12"]}},{"id":"GI-C34-Q2","stem":"Her physician contrasts her disease with Hirschsprung disease, in which the aganglionic segment always begins at the anus and extends a variable distance proximally. Which of the following best explains this distribution in Hirschsprung disease?","options":["Rectal neurons arise from sacral somites, which fail to form in the embryo","Rectal ganglion cells degenerate after birth from ischemia","Neural crest cells migrate craniocaudally and reach the rectum last","Endoderm of the cloaca fails to form the intrinsic enteric plexuses"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-1","obj":"GI-1.5","dok":3,"approach":["Enteric neurons and glia come from neural crest cells, mainly vagal neural crest.","These cells enter the foregut and migrate down the gut in a craniocaudal direction, so the rectum is colonized last.","If migration stops early (most often from a loss-of-function RET mutation), the most distal bowel is left without ganglion cells, in one continuous segment from the anus upward."],"why":"Vagal neural crest cells enter the foregut and migrate toward the hindgut during the embryonic period, forming the myenteric and submucosal plexuses. RET, a receptor tyrosine kinase activated by GDNF, drives their survival and migration. When migration stops short, the distal colon and rectum lack ganglion cells. The aganglionic segment stays contracted and acts as a functional obstruction, so the normal bowel above it dilates. This is why the rectum is always involved and why the defect extends proximally in one continuous segment.","whyNot":["Somites form muscle, bone, and dermis of the body wall. Enteric neurons do not come from somites.","Ganglion cells in Hirschsprung disease never arrived, so they are absent at birth. There is no postnatal degeneration.","Correct.","Endoderm forms the epithelial lining of the gut. Enteric neurons come from neural crest, which is ectoderm."],"study":{"topics":["Neural crest origin of enteric neurons","Hirschsprung disease and RET"],"comps":["GI-1","GI-13-F5"]}},{"id":"GI-C34-Q3","stem":"Anorectal manometry shows that balloon distension of her rectum fails to cause relaxation of the internal anal sphincter. Which of the following best explains this finding?","options":["Injury to the pudendal nerve that supplies the external sphincter","Fibrosis of the levator ani muscle that limits pelvic floor descent","Loss of intramural neurons mediating internal sphincter relaxation","Increased sympathetic tone carried by the lumbar splanchnic nerves"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.6","dok":3,"approach":["Rectal distension normally triggers reflex relaxation of the internal anal sphincter (the rectoanal inhibitory reflex).","This reflex runs through intrinsic enteric neurons in the wall of the rectum and sphincter, which release nitric oxide and VIP.","Chagas disease destroys those neurons, so the reflex is lost, just as it is in Hirschsprung disease."],"why":"Defecation begins when stool stretches the rectal wall. Enteric neurons then relax the internal anal sphincter, which is smooth muscle, and the urge to defecate is felt. The external anal sphincter is skeletal muscle under voluntary control through the pudendal nerve (S2 to S4), so a person can delay defecation. The gastrocolic reflex increases colonic motility after a meal. Loss of the intrinsic neurons removes internal sphincter relaxation, so stool cannot pass easily and the colon above dilates.","whyNot":["Pudendal nerve injury weakens the external sphincter and causes incontinence. It does not affect reflex relaxation of the internal sphincter.","Levator ani dysfunction can cause dyssynergic defecation, but this muscle is skeletal and does not control the internal sphincter's reflex relaxation.","Correct.","Sympathetic input raises internal sphincter tone, but it does not abolish the intrinsic relaxation reflex. Chagas disease destroys the enteric neurons that run that reflex."],"study":{"topics":["Defecation reflex and rectoanal inhibitory reflex","Internal versus external anal sphincter"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-F5"]}},{"id":"GI-C34-Q4","stem":"A biopsy is planned to examine the neural plexus that coordinates contraction of the muscular coat of her colon. Which of the following locations must the tissue sample include?","options":["Between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers","Lamina propria directly beneath the surface epithelium","Between the muscularis mucosae and the inner circular muscle","Between the outer longitudinal muscle and the serosa"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.1","dok":2,"approach":["The plexus that controls motility of the muscular wall is the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus.","It sits in the muscularis externa, between its inner circular and outer longitudinal layers.","A superficial suction biopsy reaches only mucosa and submucosa, so a full-thickness sample is needed to see this plexus."],"why":"The gut wall has mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia. The submucosal (Meissner) plexus lies in the submucosa and mainly regulates secretion and blood flow. The myenteric (Auerbach) plexus lies between the circular and longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa and controls motility. In Chagas disease the myenteric plexus is the main target, which is why motility fails.","whyNot":["Correct.","The lamina propria contains capillaries, lymphocytes, and lymphatics. It does not contain either enteric plexus.","This is the submucosa, which holds the submucosal (Meissner) plexus. That plexus mainly controls secretion and local blood flow.","The tissue between the longitudinal muscle and serosa is subserosal connective tissue. No major enteric plexus lies there."],"study":{"topics":["Location of the myenteric and submucosal plexuses","Layers of the gut wall"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-6"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C35","system":"GI","dx":"Zenker diverticulum","codes":["GI-13-F"],"case":"A 74-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 1-year history of progressive difficulty swallowing. He often regurgitates undigested food that he ate several hours earlier, and his wife reports bad breath. He notices a gurgling sound in his neck when he drinks and has had nighttime coughing spells. He has lost 5 kg (11 lb) over this period. Temperature is 36.7 °C (98.1 °F), pulse is 78/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 136/82 mmHg. Physical examination shows no abnormalities. A barium swallow shows a contrast-filled pouch extending posteriorly from the pharynx just above the cricopharyngeus muscle. The diagnosis is Zenker diverticulum.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C35-Q1","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains the formation of this patient's pouch?","options":["Persistent embryonic connection between the foregut and the trachea","Pulsion of mucosa through a weak area above a poorly relaxing sphincter","Failure of lower esophageal sphincter relaxation from loss of inhibitory neurons","Traction on the full esophageal wall by scarred mediastinal lymph nodes"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-F","obj":"GI-13-F.6","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the pouch: posterior hypopharynx, just above the cricopharyngeus, in Killian triangle.","The cricopharyngeus (upper esophageal sphincter) does not open fully during swallowing, so pressure in the hypopharynx rises.","Over years, that pressure pushes mucosa and submucosa out through the weak area. This is a false (pulsion) diverticulum."],"why":"Zenker diverticulum forms in Killian triangle, a weak area of the posterior pharyngeal wall between the thyropharyngeal part of the inferior constrictor and the cricopharyngeus. Poor relaxation of the cricopharyngeus raises pressure during each swallow, and mucosa and submucosa bulge out without a muscle layer. Food collects in the pouch, causing dysphagia, regurgitation of undigested food, halitosis, gurgling, and aspiration. It occurs mostly in older adults.","whyNot":["A persistent foregut to trachea connection is a tracheoesophageal fistula, a congenital defect that presents in newborns with choking and feeding problems.","Correct.","This is achalasia. It affects the lower esophageal sphincter and causes a dilated esophagus above a narrow distal segment, far below this pouch.","Traction diverticula form in the midesophagus when scarred lymph nodes (for example after tuberculosis or histoplasmosis) pull out all layers of the wall. They are true diverticula and usually cause no symptoms."],"study":{"topics":["Zenker diverticulum and Killian triangle","Pulsion versus traction diverticula"],"comps":["GI-13-F","GI-4"]}},{"id":"GI-C35-Q2","stem":"In a normal swallow, the muscle at the lower edge of this patient's pouch relaxes to let the bolus pass. Which of the following best describes the phase of swallowing in which this occurs and how it is controlled?","options":["Reflex pharyngeal phase coordinated by the medulla","Esophageal phase controlled by vagal nitric oxide release","Esophageal phase controlled by the myenteric plexus alone","Voluntary oral phase controlled by the motor cortex"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["The muscle below the pouch is the cricopharyngeus, which forms the upper esophageal sphincter.","It relaxes when the bolus reaches the pharynx, during the involuntary pharyngeal phase.","That phase is a reflex triggered by touch receptors in the pharynx and run by the swallowing center in the medulla through cranial nerves IX and X."],"why":"Chewing uses the muscles of mastication, supplied by CN V3. Swallowing then begins with a voluntary oral phase in which the tongue pushes the bolus back. When the bolus touches the pharynx, the pharyngeal phase starts as a reflex coordinated by the medullary swallowing center. The soft palate closes the nasopharynx, the larynx rises and the epiglottis covers it, breathing stops, and the upper esophageal sphincter relaxes. In the esophageal phase, peristalsis carries the bolus down, and vagal fibers that release nitric oxide and VIP relax the lower esophageal sphincter and proximal stomach.","whyNot":["Correct.","Vagal nitric oxide relaxes the lower esophageal sphincter at the end of the esophagus. The pouch sits at the upper sphincter.","The myenteric plexus helps run peristalsis in the smooth muscle of the lower esophagus. The cricopharyngeus is skeletal muscle controlled from the brainstem.","The oral phase moves the bolus to the back of the mouth and is voluntary. Opening of the upper esophageal sphincter happens after it, as a reflex."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of swallowing","Upper versus lower esophageal sphincter"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-4"]}},{"id":"GI-C35-Q3","stem":"During surgery, a biopsy specimen is taken from the lining of the pouch. Which of the following types of epithelium is most likely to be seen?","options":["Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Simple columnar epithelium with many goblet cells","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["The pouch is a false diverticulum, so its lining is the mucosa of the area it came from.","It arises from the hypopharynx just above the esophagus.","The hypopharynx and esophagus are lined by moist, nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium that resists abrasion from food."],"why":"The oropharynx, hypopharynx, and esophagus carry rough food boluses, so they are lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium kept moist by mucous glands. The stomach and intestines are lined by simple columnar epithelium for secretion and absorption. The anal canal is columnar above the pectinate line and stratified squamous below it. Because the pouch is mucosa pushed out of the hypopharynx, it keeps the same squamous lining.","whyNot":["Keratinized epithelium covers dry surfaces such as skin and the hard palate. The pharynx and esophagus are normally nonkeratinized.","Correct.","Columnar epithelium with goblet cells lines the intestine. In the esophagus it appears only as Barrett metaplasia near the gastroesophageal junction.","Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium lines the nasopharynx and airways. The hypopharynx, where this pouch arises, is a food passage with squamous lining."],"study":{"topics":["Epithelium of the gastrointestinal tract"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-13-F"]}},{"id":"GI-C35-Q4","stem":"An endoscope passed into this patient's esophagus would meet a natural narrowing just below the opening of the pouch. Which of the following best describes this narrowing and its approximate vertebral level?","options":["Aortic arch and left main bronchus at about T4 to T5","Esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm at about T10","Thoracic inlet behind the manubrium at about T1","Cricopharyngeus muscle at about C6"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-4","obj":"GI-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["The pouch arises just above the cricopharyngeus, so the narrowing below its opening is the start of the esophagus.","The esophagus begins at the cricopharyngeus at about the level of C6 and the cricoid cartilage.","The other two narrowings are where the aortic arch and left main bronchus cross it and where it passes through the diaphragm at T10."],"why":"The esophagus begins at the lower border of the cricoid cartilage, about C6, where the cricopharyngeus forms the upper esophageal sphincter. It descends through the superior and posterior mediastinum and has three natural narrowings: at the cricopharyngeus, where the aortic arch and then the left main bronchus cross it, and at the esophageal hiatus at T10. These are the sites where swallowed objects tend to lodge and where an endoscope meets resistance. The cricopharyngeal narrowing is the tightest, and a Zenker pouch sits just above it.","whyNot":["This is the middle (thoracic) narrowing, well below the neck. The pouch arises in the neck.","This is the lowest narrowing where the esophagus enters the abdomen. It is far below the pouch.","The thoracic inlet is the opening at the top of the thorax. It is not one of the three classic esophageal narrowings.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Course and narrowings of the esophagus"],"comps":["GI-4","GI-13-F"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C36","system":"GI","dx":"Collagenous colitis","codes":["GI-13-G4"],"case":"A 63-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of 6 to 8 watery, nonbloody stools daily, including at night, with mild crampy abdominal pain and a 3-kg (6.6-lb) weight loss. She takes naproxen daily for osteoarthritis of the knees. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 76/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 128/78 mmHg. Physical examination shows no abnormalities. Hemoglobin concentration is 13.1 g/dL (12.0 to 16.0), and C-reactive protein is 3 mg/L (below 10). Stool testing for Clostridioides difficile toxin is negative. Serum IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase antibodies are negative with a normal total IgA. Colonoscopy shows normal-appearing mucosa throughout. Biopsy specimens confirm collagenous colitis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C36-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings is most likely to be seen on this patient's colonic biopsy specimens?","options":["Crypt abscesses with crypt distortion and basal plasmacytosis","Noncaseating granulomas with transmural lymphoid aggregates","Thickened collagen band beneath the surface epithelium","Hyalinized small vessels with atypical stromal fibroblasts"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G4","obj":"GI-13-G.4","dok":2,"approach":["Chronic watery diarrhea with a normal-looking colon at colonoscopy points to microscopic colitis.","Microscopic colitis has two forms. The collagenous form has a thick subepithelial collagen band (over about 10 µm), and the lymphocytic form has excess intraepithelial lymphocytes without the band.","Collagenous colitis also has more intraepithelial lymphocytes and lamina propria inflammation, but the collagen band defines it."],"why":"Microscopic colitis causes chronic, nonbloody watery diarrhea, often nocturnal, mostly in middle-aged and older women. The colon looks normal, so the diagnosis needs biopsies. Collagenous colitis shows a collagen band under the surface epithelium thicker than about 10 µm, with surface damage and inflammation in the lamina propria. It is linked to autoimmune disease, celiac disease, and drugs such as NSAIDs and proton pump inhibitors. Other noninfectious causes of colitis include radiation and NSAID injury.","whyNot":["Crypt abscesses with crypt architectural distortion and basal plasmacytosis are features of ulcerative colitis. That disease causes bloody diarrhea and visible mucosal inflammation.","Noncaseating granulomas and transmural inflammation define Crohn disease, which usually shows ulcers or strictures on colonoscopy.","Correct.","Hyalinized vessels and atypical fibroblasts are features of chronic radiation injury. Nothing in her history suggests radiation therapy."],"study":{"topics":["Microscopic colitis: collagenous and lymphocytic","Noninfectious colitis"],"comps":["GI-13-G4","GI-13-G9"]}},{"id":"GI-C36-Q2","stem":"Before colonoscopy, irritable bowel syndrome was included in the differential diagnosis. Which of the following features of this patient's presentation most strongly argued against that diagnosis?","options":["Nocturnal bowel movements and weight loss","Cramping abdominal pain that eases after defecation","Loose stools that have persisted for several months","Normal hemoglobin and C-reactive protein levels"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-13-F","obj":"GI-13-F.7","dok":3,"approach":["Irritable bowel syndrome is recurrent abdominal pain linked to defecation, with a change in stool frequency or form, and no structural disease.","Its symptoms come from altered motility and visceral hypersensitivity during waking hours, so they do not wake the patient from sleep and do not cause weight loss.","Nocturnal diarrhea and weight loss are alarm features that point to organic disease and call for further testing."],"why":"Irritable bowel syndrome is a disorder of gut and brain interaction. Altered motility, visceral hypersensitivity, and changes in central pain processing cause recurrent pain that is related to defecation, with altered stool form. There is no inflammation or structural lesion. Diarrhea that wakes a patient at night, weight loss, bleeding, anemia, and onset after age 50 are alarm features that suggest an organic cause such as microscopic colitis, inflammatory bowel disease, or cancer. Gastroparesis, a motility disorder with measurably delayed gastric emptying, causes early satiety and vomiting from delayed gastric emptying, usually from diabetic autonomic neuropathy.","whyNot":["Correct.","Pain that improves after defecation is one of the core features of irritable bowel syndrome, so it supports that diagnosis.","Irritable bowel syndrome is defined by symptoms lasting months, so chronic loose stools fit it.","Normal blood counts and inflammatory markers are expected in irritable bowel syndrome, so they support that diagnosis."],"study":{"topics":["Irritable bowel syndrome and alarm features","Irritable bowel syndrome versus gastroparesis"],"comps":["GI-13-F","GI-13-G4"]}},{"id":"GI-C36-Q3","stem":"Celiac disease was considered because it is associated with microscopic colitis. In a patient with celiac disease, which of the following steps allows dietary gliadin peptides to activate the T-cell response in the small intestine?","options":["Presentation of native gliadin on HLA-B27 to CD8 T cells","Tissue transglutaminase deamidation for HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 binding","Activation of the classical complement pathway by bound gliadin","Cross-linking of IgE on mucosal mast cells by gliadin peptides"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.2","dok":2,"approach":["Gliadin, from wheat gluten, is rich in proline and glutamine, so it resists digestion and crosses the epithelium as long peptides.","Tissue transglutaminase in the lamina propria converts some glutamines to glutamate (deamidation), adding negative charges.","These charged peptides bind tightly to HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 on antigen-presenting cells, which activate gluten-specific CD4 T cells."],"why":"Celiac disease needs gluten, the right HLA class II genes, and tissue transglutaminase. Deamidated gliadin binds HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 strongly and is presented to CD4 T cells, which release interferon-gamma and drive villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytosis. Gliadin bound to tissue transglutaminase also helps B cells make IgA antibodies against tissue transglutaminase and endomysium, which are the screening tests. Because selective IgA deficiency is more common in celiac disease and can cause a false negative IgA test, total IgA is checked at the same time.","whyNot":["HLA-B27 is a class I allele linked to the seronegative spondyloarthropathies. Celiac disease is linked to the class II alleles HLA-DQ2 and DQ8.","Correct.","Complement activation is not the driver of celiac disease. The injury comes from gluten-specific CD4 T cells and cytotoxic intraepithelial lymphocytes.","IgE cross-linking on mast cells causes wheat allergy, an immediate hypersensitivity with hives or anaphylaxis. Celiac disease is a T cell-mediated disorder."],"study":{"topics":["Celiac disease immunopathogenesis","Celiac serology and IgA deficiency"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-E2"]}},{"id":"GI-C36-Q4","stem":"Stool was tested for C. difficile toxin because she had received clindamycin for a dental infection 6 weeks before her symptoms began. If C. difficile had been the cause, which of the following mechanisms would have produced her diarrhea?","options":["Heat-stable toxin activating guanylate cyclase in enterocytes","Preformed superantigen toxin acting on the vagal afferents","Toxins A and B inactivating Rho GTPases of colonocytes","Spread between enterocytes by polymerizing host actin"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L1","obj":"GI-13-L.1","dok":2,"approach":["Clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, and cephalosporins suppress normal colonic flora, which lets C. difficile spores germinate and grow.","C. difficile makes toxin A (enterotoxin) and toxin B (cytotoxin).","Both toxins glucosylate Rho family GTPases, which breaks down the actin cytoskeleton, opens tight junctions, kills colonocytes, and triggers inflammation."],"why":"C. difficile is a spore-forming, anaerobic gram-positive rod. After antibiotics disrupt the normal flora, it grows in the colon and releases toxins A and B. These toxins inactivate Rho GTPases, so colonocytes lose their cytoskeleton and tight junctions and die. Neutrophils, fibrin, and mucus erupt from damaged crypts and form yellow-white pseudomembranes. Patients have watery diarrhea, cramps, and fever, and severe cases can progress to toxic megacolon. Diagnosis is by stool toxin testing or by PCR for the toxin genes.","whyNot":["Heat-stable toxin of enterotoxigenic E. coli raises cGMP and causes traveler's diarrhea. It does not cause antibiotic-associated colitis.","Preformed Staphylococcus aureus enterotoxins cause vomiting within hours of eating contaminated food. They do not cause weeks of diarrhea after antibiotics.","Correct.","Actin-based spread between cells is used by Listeria and Shigella. C. difficile does not invade cells; it damages them with toxins from the lumen."],"study":{"topics":["Clostridioides difficile toxins and pseudomembranous colitis"],"comps":["GI-13-L1","GI-12"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C37","system":"GI","dx":"Acute appendicitis with pelvic abscess","codes":["GI-13-G1"],"case":"A 24-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of 3 days of abdominal pain. The pain began around the umbilicus and moved to the right lower quadrant within a day. She did not seek care until fever and nausea developed. Temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mmHg. Physical examination shows right lower quadrant tenderness with guarding. Rectal examination shows tenderness anteriorly. Leukocyte count is 18,200/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000) with 88% neutrophils (40 to 70). A urine pregnancy test is negative. CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis shows a dilated appendix with an appendicolith, periappendiceal fat stranding, and a 5-cm rim-enhancing fluid collection in the pelvis. The diagnosis is perforated acute appendicitis with a pelvic abscess.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C37-Q1","stem":"Which of the following sequences of events best explains how the appendicolith led to perforation in this patient?","options":["Arterial thrombosis, then mucosal necrosis and stone formation","Luminal obstruction, rising pressure, ischemia, then invasion","Viral ulceration of the mucosa, then fibrosis with a stricture","Bacterial invasion of the wall, then lymphoid hyperplasia"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G1","obj":"GI-13-G.1","dok":2,"approach":["Start with the appendicolith. It blocks the lumen of a narrow, blind-ended tube.","Mucus keeps being secreted, so pressure rises. Veins collapse first, then arterial flow falls, and the wall becomes ischemic.","Ischemic mucosa lets luminal bacteria invade the wall, causing suppurative inflammation, gangrene, and perforation."],"why":"Appendicitis usually starts with obstruction of the lumen by a fecalith or by lymphoid hyperplasia after an infection. Continued secretion raises pressure, which blocks venous outflow and then arterial inflow. Ischemia lets bacteria invade the wall, producing neutrophilic inflammation through the muscularis. Early pain is visceral and felt around the umbilicus. When inflammation reaches the parietal peritoneum, pain localizes to McBurney point. Untreated, the gangrenous wall can perforate, causing an abscess or peritonitis.","whyNot":["Fecaliths form from hardened stool before the illness. Arterial thrombosis is not the first event in appendicitis.","Correct.","Viral illness can cause lymphoid hyperplasia that blocks the lumen, but mucosal ulceration followed by fibrosis does not describe acute appendicitis.","This reverses the order. Lymphoid hyperplasia can be a first step that blocks the lumen, and bacterial invasion comes after ischemia."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of acute appendicitis","Visceral to parietal pain shift"],"comps":["GI-13-G1","GI-1"]}},{"id":"GI-C37-Q2","stem":"At laparoscopy, the inflamed appendix is hidden by adhesions behind the cecum. Which of the following landmarks most reliably leads the surgeon to the base of the appendix?","options":["Deepest haustral fold of the cecal wall","Line of omental appendices along the ascending colon","Last Peyer patch on the antimesenteric ileum","Convergence point of the three taeniae coli"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["The tip of the appendix can lie in many places (retrocecal, pelvic, subcecal), but its base is fixed on the cecum.","The three taeniae coli are bands of longitudinal muscle on the colon.","Followed down the cecum, they converge at the base of the appendix, where they become its continuous longitudinal muscle layer."],"why":"The colon differs from the small intestine by its taeniae coli, haustra, and omental appendices (fat tags). The taeniae are three bands of the outer longitudinal muscle. They meet at the base of the appendix, which on the skin surface lies near McBurney point, one third of the way from the anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus. Because the tip of the appendix varies in position, following the taeniae is the reliable way to find the base.","whyNot":["Haustra are sacculations of the colon wall between taeniae. They change with contraction and do not mark the appendix base.","Omental appendices are fat tags along the colon. They help identify colon but do not point to the appendix, and the appendix itself lacks them.","Peyer patches are lymphoid follicles in the ileal mucosa on the antimesenteric side. They are not visible from the outside and do not lead to the appendix.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Features of the colon versus small intestine","Appendix location and McBurney point"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-13-G1"]}},{"id":"GI-C37-Q3","stem":"The same month, her 15-year-old brother undergoes surgery for suspected appendicitis. At surgery, the appendix is normal, but the terminal ileum is thickened and the mesenteric lymph nodes are enlarged. Stool culture grows a gram-negative coccobacillus that grows well after cold enrichment at 4 °C (39.2 °F). Which of the following exposures most likely caused his infection?","options":["Eating fried rice left at room temperature","Eating raw oysters harvested in summer","Eating undercooked ground beef at a picnic","Preparing raw pork chitterlings at home"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.3","dok":3,"approach":["A gram-negative organism that grows in the cold and causes terminal ileitis with mesenteric adenitis is Yersinia enterocolitica.","It invades through M cells over Peyer patches and spreads to mesenteric lymph nodes, causing right lower quadrant pain that mimics appendicitis.","Its main reservoir is pigs, so pork products, especially chitterlings, and unpasteurized milk are typical sources."],"why":"Yersinia enterocolitica is a gram-negative coccobacillus that can grow at refrigerator temperatures. It crosses the ileal mucosa through M cells, multiplies in Peyer patches, and spreads to mesenteric lymph nodes. The result is fever, diarrhea, and right lower quadrant pain from mesenteric adenitis, a pseudoappendicitis that can lead to surgery. Pigs are the main reservoir, and handling raw chitterlings is a classic source, along with contaminated milk.","whyNot":["Fried rice left at room temperature is the classic source of Bacillus cereus, a gram-positive rod whose preformed toxin causes vomiting within hours. It does not cause terminal ileitis or mesenteric adenitis.","Raw oysters carry Vibrio species, which are curved gram-negative rods linked to warm seawater. They do not cause mesenteric adenitis.","Undercooked ground beef is the classic source of EHEC O157:H7, which makes Shiga toxin and causes bloody diarrhea that can progress to hemolytic uremic syndrome.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Yersinia enterocolitica and pseudoappendicitis","Foodborne bacterial sources"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-L1"]}},{"id":"GI-C37-Q4","stem":"The abscess formed while she was mostly sitting and walking during the days before admission. In which of the following peritoneal spaces is this collection most likely located?","options":["Rectouterine pouch","Vesicouterine pouch","Hepatorenal recess","Right subphrenic space"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-4","obj":"GI-4.6","dok":2,"approach":["Fluid and pus in the peritoneal cavity flow to the lowest space for the patient's position.","In an upright woman, the lowest point of the peritoneal cavity is the rectouterine pouch (of Douglas) between the uterus and rectum.","This fits the CT finding of a pelvic collection and the anterior tenderness on rectal examination."],"why":"Gravity decides where infected fluid collects. When upright, the lowest peritoneal space is the rectouterine pouch in women and the rectovesical pouch in men. When supine, the lowest space in the upper abdomen is the hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch). Fluid can also track up the right paracolic gutter to the subphrenic spaces. A collection in the rectouterine pouch lies just in front of the rectum, so it causes anterior rectal tenderness and can be drained through the rectum or vagina.","whyNot":["Correct.","The vesicouterine pouch between the bladder and uterus is shallow and higher than the rectouterine pouch, so fluid passes it by.","The hepatorenal recess is the most dependent upper abdominal space when a person lies supine. It lies in the upper abdomen, far above the pelvis.","The subphrenic spaces lie under the diaphragm. Pus reaches them by tracking up the paracolic gutters, and they are not pelvic."],"study":{"topics":["Dependent peritoneal spaces","Pelvic peritoneal pouches"],"comps":["GI-4","GI-13-G1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C38","system":"GI","dx":"Barrett esophagus","codes":["GI-13-G2"],"case":"A 58-year-old man comes to the physician because of 15 years of heartburn and regurgitation that wake him at night. BMI is 33 kg/m2. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 74/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 134/84 mmHg. Physical examination shows central obesity and no other abnormalities. Upper endoscopy shows a small sliding hiatal hernia and salmon-colored mucosa extending 4 cm above the gastroesophageal junction into the distal esophagus. Multiple biopsy specimens are taken. Histologic examination confirms Barrett esophagus without dysplasia.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C38-Q1","stem":"Surveillance endoscopy with biopsy is planned for this patient. Which of the following findings on a future biopsy would indicate the next step in the progression toward cancer?","options":["Low-grade dysplasia in the columnar epithelium","Gastric cardiac-type mucosa without goblet cells","At least 15 eosinophils per high-power field in the squamous mucosa","Basal cell hyperplasia of the remaining squamous mucosa"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G2","obj":"GI-13-G.2","dok":2,"approach":["Barrett esophagus is intestinal metaplasia, which is the first step in the sequence toward esophageal adenocarcinoma.","The sequence runs from metaplasia to low-grade dysplasia, then high-grade dysplasia, then invasive adenocarcinoma.","Surveillance biopsies look for dysplasia, which is the next step and the main predictor of cancer risk."],"why":"Chronic acid and bile reflux injures the squamous epithelium of the distal esophagus. It heals with columnar epithelium containing goblet cells (intestinal metaplasia), which resists acid better. Over years, the metaplastic cells can gain genetic changes, such as TP53 mutation, that lead to low-grade and then high-grade dysplasia and finally adenocarcinoma. The risk of cancer rises with the grade of dysplasia, which is why patients with Barrett esophagus have endoscopic surveillance with biopsies.","whyNot":["Correct.","Columnar mucosa without goblet cells is cardiac-type mucosa. It is a less specific finding and is not a step beyond intestinal metaplasia.","Many eosinophils in squamous mucosa define eosinophilic esophagitis, an allergic disorder. It is not part of the Barrett sequence.","Basal cell hyperplasia and elongated papillae are signs of reflux injury to squamous mucosa. They precede metaplasia and do not mark progression toward cancer."],"study":{"topics":["Barrett metaplasia to adenocarcinoma sequence"],"comps":["GI-13-G2","GI-13-K1"]}},{"id":"GI-C38-Q2","stem":"Which of the following histologic findings in the salmon-colored mucosa established this patient's diagnosis?","options":["Keratinized squamous cells in place of nonkeratinized ones","Columnar epithelium with goblet cells in place of squamous","Parietal and chief cells forming gastric glands in the submucosa","Atypical squamous cells extending into the lamina propria"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["Know the normal lining: the esophagus has nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, and the stomach starts with simple columnar epithelium at the gastroesophageal junction.","Salmon-colored mucosa above the junction means columnar epithelium has replaced the squamous lining.","Goblet cells in that columnar lining show intestinal-type metaplasia, which defines Barrett esophagus."],"why":"The esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, which looks pale pink at endoscopy. The stomach and intestine are lined by simple columnar epithelium, which looks red and velvety. Normally the two meet at the gastroesophageal junction (the Z-line). In Barrett esophagus, chronic reflux causes the distal squamous lining to be replaced by columnar epithelium with goblet cells, an intestinal pattern that is not normally found in the esophagus or stomach.","whyNot":["Reflux does not make the esophageal lining keratinize. In Barrett esophagus the squamous lining is replaced by columnar epithelium.","Correct.","Gastric glands deep in the submucosa would be a heterotopia. Barrett esophagus is a change in the surface epithelium.","Atypical squamous cells invading the lamina propria describe squamous cell carcinoma, which is not the result of Barrett metaplasia."],"study":{"topics":["Epithelium of the esophagus and gastroesophageal junction","Intestinal metaplasia"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-13-G2"]}},{"id":"GI-C38-Q3","stem":"This patient's reflux injury depends on acid secreted by gastric parietal cells. Blocking which of the following would most completely reduce acid output regardless of the stimulus?","options":["Histamine H2 receptors on parietal cells","Muscarinic M3 receptors on parietal cells","H+/K+ ATPase in the parietal cell apical membrane","Gastrin (CCK-B) receptors on enterochromaffin-like cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.3","dok":3,"approach":["Parietal cells respond to three stimuli: acetylcholine (M3), gastrin (CCK-B), and histamine (H2).","Each receptor acts through its own second messenger (calcium for M3 and CCK-B, cAMP for H2), so blocking one leaves the others working.","All three pathways converge on the H+/K+ ATPase, the final step that pumps H+ into the lumen in exchange for K+."],"why":"Acid secretion has a cephalic phase (sight, smell, and taste of food acting through the vagus), a gastric phase (stretch and peptides in the stomach, which release gastrin), and an intestinal phase. Acetylcholine and gastrin raise intracellular calcium, and histamine from enterochromaffin-like cells raises cAMP. These signals move H+/K+ ATPase pumps to the apical membrane and activate them. Because every stimulus works through this pump, blocking it reduces acid output the most. Somatostatin from D cells inhibits acid secretion.","whyNot":["Blocking H2 receptors removes the histamine signal and also blunts gastrin's effect, which works largely through histamine. Acetylcholine can still stimulate parietal cells directly.","Blocking M3 receptors removes vagal stimulation, mainly during the cephalic phase. Gastrin and histamine can still drive secretion.","Correct.","Blocking gastrin receptors removes one stimulus, but acetylcholine and histamine released by other signals can still drive the parietal cell."],"study":{"topics":["Parietal cell stimulation and the proton pump","Phases of gastric acid secretion"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-G8"]}},{"id":"GI-C38-Q4","stem":"Which of the following is the most likely type and location of the cancer this patient is at increased risk of developing?","options":["Squamous cell carcinoma of the middle third","Adenocarcinoma of the distal third","Adenocarcinoma of the upper third","Squamous cell carcinoma of the upper third"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K1","obj":"GI-13-K.1","dok":2,"approach":["Barrett esophagus is glandular (columnar) metaplasia, so the cancer that follows is a glandular cancer, adenocarcinoma.","Barrett esophagus forms in the distal esophagus near the gastroesophageal junction, so the tumor arises there.","Obesity raises intra-abdominal pressure and reflux, which adds to the same risk."],"why":"Esophageal adenocarcinoma arises from Barrett esophagus in the distal third. Its risk factors are chronic reflux, Barrett esophagus, obesity, and smoking. Squamous cell carcinoma arises in the upper and middle thirds and is linked to alcohol, tobacco, achalasia, caustic injury, and Plummer-Vinson syndrome. Both types present with progressive dysphagia, first to solids and then to liquids, with weight loss.","whyNot":["Squamous cell carcinoma most often arises in the middle third, linked to alcohol and tobacco. Barrett esophagus leads to a glandular tumor.","Correct.","Adenocarcinoma follows the columnar metaplasia, which forms in the distal esophagus. The upper third keeps its squamous lining.","Squamous cell carcinoma of the upper esophagus is linked to alcohol, tobacco, and Plummer-Vinson syndrome. It does not arise from Barrett metaplasia."],"study":{"topics":["Esophageal adenocarcinoma versus squamous cell carcinoma"],"comps":["GI-13-K1","GI-13-G2"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C39","system":"GI","dx":"Angiodysplasia of the cecum with sigmoid diverticulosis","codes":["GI-13-J4","GI-13-G3"],"case":"A 79-year-old woman comes to the physician because of three episodes of painless passage of maroon blood per rectum over the past 6 months and increasing fatigue. She also reports exertional lightheadedness. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 126/78 mmHg. Physical examination shows conjunctival pallor and a harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the right upper sternal border that radiates to the carotid arteries. Hemoglobin concentration is 8.6 g/dL (12.0 to 16.0), mean corpuscular volume is 72 fL (80 to 100), and serum ferritin is 8 ng/mL (15 to 200). Colonoscopy shows several small, flat, cherry-red lesions in the cecum and numerous diverticula in the sigmoid colon without inflammation. Echocardiography shows severe calcific aortic stenosis. The diagnosis is cecal angiodysplasia with sigmoid diverticulosis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C39-Q1","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best links this patient's heart disease to the bleeding from her colonic lesions?","options":["Vitamin K deficiency from reduced colonic bacterial synthesis","Antibodies against platelet glycoprotein IIb/IIIa","Septic emboli from the valve eroding mucosal vessels","Shear-induced loss of large von Willebrand factor multimers"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-J4","obj":"GI-13-J.4","dok":3,"approach":["Angiodysplasia with aortic stenosis is Heyde syndrome.","High-velocity, turbulent flow through the narrow valve stretches large von Willebrand factor multimers, exposing a site that ADAMTS13 cleaves.","Loss of the largest multimers reduces platelet adhesion, which matters most in fragile, thin-walled vessels such as angiodysplasias, so they bleed."],"why":"Angiodysplasias are dilated, thin-walled, tortuous submucosal vessels, mostly in the cecum and right colon of older adults. They are thought to form when repeated contraction of the colon wall partly blocks submucosal veins, and the high wall tension of the wide cecum adds to this. They cause painless, often recurrent lower GI bleeding and iron deficiency. In aortic stenosis, shear stress causes an acquired von Willebrand syndrome, which makes the lesions bleed more. Replacing the valve often reduces the bleeding. Angiodysplasia is also linked to end-stage kidney disease.","whyNot":["Vitamin K deficiency raises the PT and causes bleeding from many sites. Aortic stenosis does not cause it.","Antibodies against glycoprotein IIb/IIIa occur in immune thrombocytopenia, which lowers the platelet count and causes petechiae. Nothing links them to aortic stenosis.","Septic emboli come from infective endocarditis, which causes fever and vegetations. Her valve is calcified, and her lesions are vascular ectasias.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Angiodysplasia and Heyde syndrome","Acquired von Willebrand syndrome"],"comps":["GI-13-J4","CV-18-I1"]}},{"id":"GI-C39-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes the outpouchings in this patient's sigmoid colon?","options":["Mucosa and submucosa herniating where vasa recta pierce the muscle","All wall layers bulging at a persistent embryonic duct remnant","Dilated submucosal veins draining into the inferior mesenteric vein","Mucosal folds raised over areas of submucosal lymphoid hyperplasia"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G3","obj":"GI-13-G.3","dok":2,"approach":["Sigmoid diverticula in an older adult are acquired false diverticula.","Low-fiber diet and strong segmental contractions raise pressure in the narrow sigmoid colon.","That pressure pushes mucosa and submucosa out through weak points in the muscularis externa where the vasa recta enter the wall."],"why":"Diverticulosis comes from high intraluminal pressure acting on weak spots in the colon wall. The sigmoid colon is the narrowest part of the colon, so segmental contractions there produce the highest pressures. Mucosa and submucosa herniate through the muscularis where vasa recta penetrate it, which is why the vessel is stretched over the dome of the pouch. That vessel can rupture into the lumen and cause painless bleeding, often large. Diverticulitis is inflammation of a pouch after obstruction or microperforation and causes left lower quadrant pain, fever, abscess, or fistula.","whyNot":["Correct.","A full-thickness outpouching from an embryonic duct remnant is a Meckel diverticulum, which arises from the ileum.","Dilated submucosal veins in the anal canal are hemorrhoids, and dilated vessels in the right colon are angiodysplasia. Neither forms outpouchings.","Lymphoid hyperplasia raises small nodules on the mucosa. It does not create pouches that extend outside the colon wall."],"study":{"topics":["Diverticulosis versus diverticulitis","False diverticula and vasa recta"],"comps":["GI-13-G3","GI-2"]}},{"id":"GI-C39-Q3","stem":"A month later, she has a brisk episode of bleeding. Angiography shows active extravasation of contrast from a vessel in the cecum. Which of the following arteries is the most likely source?","options":["Sigmoid branch of the inferior mesenteric artery","Left colic branch of the inferior mesenteric artery","Ileocolic branch of the superior mesenteric artery","Middle colic branch of the superior mesenteric artery"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.2","dok":2,"approach":["The cecum is a midgut derivative, so it is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery.","Among the branches of the superior mesenteric artery, the ileocolic artery supplies the terminal ileum, cecum, and appendix.","The middle colic artery supplies the transverse colon, and the branches of the inferior mesenteric artery supply the distal colon."],"why":"The superior mesenteric artery supplies the midgut, from the second part of the duodenum to the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon. Its colic branches are the ileocolic (terminal ileum, cecum, appendix), right colic (ascending colon), and middle colic (transverse colon). The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the distal transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, and upper rectum through its left colic, sigmoid, and superior rectal branches. The marginal artery links the two systems. Its weak points, the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction, are the watershed zones for ischemic colitis.","whyNot":["The sigmoid arteries supply the sigmoid colon. They would be the source if one of her sigmoid diverticula bled.","The left colic artery supplies the distal transverse and descending colon. It does not reach the cecum.","Correct.","The middle colic artery supplies the transverse colon. It does not supply the cecum."],"study":{"topics":["Arterial supply of the colon","Watershed zones of the colon"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-1"]}},{"id":"GI-C39-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best explains the low mean corpuscular volume of this patient's erythrocytes?","options":["Delayed nuclear maturation from impaired DNA synthesis","Defective globin chain synthesis from an inherited gene mutation","Lead inhibition of ferrochelatase and ALA dehydratase","Too little iron for heme synthesis, so extra divisions occur"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.6","dok":3,"approach":["Low hemoglobin, low MCV, and low ferritin together mean iron deficiency. Ferritin reflects body iron stores.","Repeated blood loss from the colon removes iron faster than the gut can absorb it, which drains her stores over months.","Without iron, the precursor cannot finish making heme and hemoglobin, so it divides extra times before maturing, and the red cells end up small and pale."],"why":"Iron is absorbed as ferrous iron in the duodenum and is needed for the final step of heme synthesis, when ferrochelatase inserts it into protoporphyrin IX. When iron runs out, hemoglobin builds up slowly in red cell precursors, so they go through extra divisions and give rise to small, pale (microcytic, hypochromic) cells. Low ferritin separates iron deficiency from other microcytic anemias. In an older adult, iron deficiency calls for a search for gastrointestinal blood loss. Other mineral deficiencies show different findings: zinc causes poor wound healing and dysgeusia, and copper causes anemia, neutropenia, and myelopathy.","whyNot":["Impaired DNA synthesis from folate or vitamin B12 deficiency delays nuclear maturation and makes large cells (macrocytic anemia). Her cells are small.","Thalassemia causes microcytosis from reduced globin synthesis, but ferritin is normal or high. Her low ferritin shows iron deficiency.","Lead poisoning can cause microcytic anemia with basophilic stippling, but ferritin is not low, and nothing suggests lead exposure.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Iron deficiency anemia from chronic GI blood loss","Mineral deficiency findings"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-7"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C40","system":"GI","dx":"Helicobacter pylori duodenal ulcer with bleeding","codes":["GI-13-G5"],"case":"A 45-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of black, tarry stools for 1 day and one episode of vomiting coffee-ground material. For 3 months, he has had burning epigastric pain that improves with meals and returns 2 to 3 hours later, often waking him at night. He does not take NSAIDs. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 98/62 mmHg. Hemoglobin concentration is 9.4 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), serum urea nitrogen is 38 mg/dL (7 to 20), and serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.2). Upper endoscopy shows a 1.5-cm ulcer on the posterior wall of the duodenal bulb with a visible vessel. Antral biopsy specimens show curved bacilli in the surface mucus. The diagnosis is a bleeding Helicobacter pylori-associated duodenal ulcer.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C40-Q1","stem":"Which of the following arteries is most likely the source of this patient's bleeding?","options":["Gastroduodenal artery","Left gastric artery","Splenic artery","Superior mesenteric artery"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G5","obj":"GI-13-G.5","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the ulcer: posterior wall of the first part of the duodenum.","The gastroduodenal artery runs directly behind the first part of the duodenum.","A posterior duodenal ulcer that erodes deeply can open this artery and cause major bleeding. An anterior ulcer is more likely to perforate and release free air."],"why":"Peptic ulcers can bleed, perforate, or obstruct. The vessel at risk depends on the ulcer's location. A posterior duodenal bulb ulcer can erode the gastroduodenal artery, a branch of the common hepatic artery, causing hematemesis and melena. An anterior duodenal ulcer is more likely to perforate into the peritoneal cavity and cause free air under the diaphragm. An ulcer on the lesser curvature of the stomach can erode the left gastric artery. Duodenal ulcers cause pain that improves with meals and are almost never malignant, while gastric ulcer pain worsens with meals, and gastric ulcers need biopsy to exclude cancer.","whyNot":["Correct.","The left gastric artery runs along the lesser curvature of the stomach. It is the usual source of bleeding from lesser curvature gastric ulcers.","The splenic artery runs along the top of the pancreas behind the stomach. A posterior gastric ulcer could erode it, but it does not lie behind the duodenum.","The superior mesenteric artery passes behind the neck of the pancreas and in front of the third part of the duodenum. It is not related to the duodenal bulb."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of peptic ulcer disease","Gastric versus duodenal ulcers"],"comps":["GI-13-G5","GI-5"]}},{"id":"GI-C40-Q2","stem":"A urea breath test is used to confirm eradication of the organism after treatment. Which of the following properties of the organism allows both this test and its survival in the stomach?","options":["Spore coat that resists gastric acid","Catalase that breaks down hydrogen peroxide","Polysaccharide capsule that blocks phagocytosis","Urease that converts urea to ammonia and CO2"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.6","dok":2,"approach":["In the breath test, the patient swallows urea labeled with carbon-13 or carbon-14.","If H. pylori is present, its urease splits the urea into ammonia and labeled CO2, which is absorbed and breathed out.","The same ammonia buffers acid around the organism, letting it survive in the mucus layer."],"why":"Helicobacter pylori is a curved, flagellated, microaerophilic gram-negative rod. Its flagella let it swim into the mucus layer, and its urease makes ammonia that neutralizes acid around it. Urease also explains the diagnostic tests: the urea breath test and the rapid urease test on biopsy tissue. Stool antigen testing and biopsy with histology or culture also detect it. The breath test and stool antigen detect active infection, so they are used to confirm eradication.","whyNot":["H. pylori does not form spores. Spore-forming bacteria include Bacillus and Clostridium species.","H. pylori is catalase positive, but catalase protects against oxidants. It does not neutralize acid or produce the labeled CO2 measured in the test.","A capsule helps bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae avoid phagocytosis. It does not protect against acid or explain the breath test.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Helicobacter pylori virulence factors","Tests for H. pylori"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-G7"]}},{"id":"GI-C40-Q3","stem":"Infection of this patient's gastric antrum increases the acid load delivered to his duodenum. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this increase?","options":["Increased secretin release from duodenal S cells","Destruction of parietal cells in the gastric body and fundus","Reduced somatostatin from antral D cells, releasing gastrin","Autonomous gastrin secretion by a pancreatic tumor"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.3","dok":3,"approach":["Normally, acid in the antrum stimulates D cells to release somatostatin, which inhibits gastrin release from G cells.","Antral-predominant H. pylori gastritis damages D cells, so somatostatin falls.","Gastrin is no longer held back, so parietal cells secrete more acid, and more acid reaches the duodenum."],"why":"Somatostatin is the main brake on acid secretion. It acts locally on G cells, enterochromaffin-like cells, and parietal cells. When H. pylori infects the antrum, it reduces D cells and somatostatin, so gastrin and acid output rise. The excess acid entering the duodenum causes gastric metaplasia there, which H. pylori can then colonize, and the duodenal mucosa ulcerates. Infection centered in the gastric body has the opposite effect, causing atrophy and low acid.","whyNot":["Secretin is released by acid in the duodenum. It stimulates pancreatic bicarbonate and inhibits gastrin, so it lowers acid.","Destruction of parietal cells lowers acid output. This happens in autoimmune gastritis and in H. pylori gastritis centered in the gastric body.","Correct.","A gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome) causes very high gastrin and multiple ulcers. It is a tumor, and this patient's biopsy shows infection."],"study":{"topics":["Somatostatin and gastrin regulation","H. pylori and duodenal ulcers"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-G5"]}},{"id":"GI-C40-Q4","stem":"Endoscopy also shows a second ulcer on the posterior wall of the gastric body. If this ulcer perforated, into which of the following spaces would gastric contents first leak?","options":["Hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch)","Omental bursa (lesser sac)","Right subphrenic space","Left paracolic gutter"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-4","obj":"GI-4.3","dok":2,"approach":["The lesser sac (omental bursa) lies directly behind the stomach and lesser omentum, in front of the pancreas.","A perforation through the posterior wall of the stomach therefore opens into the lesser sac first.","Fluid can then pass through the omental (epiploic) foramen into the greater sac."],"why":"The peritoneal cavity has a large greater sac and a smaller lesser sac behind the stomach. The lesser sac is bounded in front by the stomach and lesser omentum and behind by the pancreas and posterior abdominal wall. It opens into the greater sac only through the omental (epiploic) foramen, which lies behind the free edge of the lesser omentum, the hepatoduodenal ligament. That ligament carries the portal vein, proper hepatic artery, and common bile duct. A posterior gastric perforation therefore fills the lesser sac first and may stay walled off there.","whyNot":["The hepatorenal recess is part of the greater sac. Lesser sac fluid can reach it only by passing through the omental foramen.","Correct.","The right subphrenic space lies between the liver and diaphragm in the greater sac. Contents from an anterior perforation can reach it.","The left paracolic gutter lies beside the descending colon in the greater sac. Fluid reaches it only after it spreads."],"study":{"topics":["Lesser sac and omental foramen","Hepatoduodenal ligament contents"],"comps":["GI-4","GI-13-G5"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C41","system":"GI","dx":"Curling ulcer after severe burns","codes":["GI-13-G7"],"case":"A 34-year-old man is admitted to the burn unit after a house fire with full-thickness and partial-thickness burns covering 45% of his total body surface area. He required large-volume fluid resuscitation for hypotension during the first 24 hours. On hospital day 4, he vomits blood and passes black stools. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg. Hemoglobin has fallen from 14.1 g/dL to 9.6 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5). Upper endoscopy shows multiple shallow erosions in the gastric body and a 1.5-cm ulcer on the posterior wall of the duodenal bulb. Diagnosis: Curling ulcer (acute stress ulceration) after severe burns.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C41-Q1","stem":"Which of the following is the most important mechanism of mucosal injury in this patient?","options":["Vagally mediated increase in parietal cell acid output","Mucosal ischemia from hypovolemia and splanchnic constriction","Reduced prostaglandin synthesis from cyclooxygenase-1 inhibition","Urease-producing bacterial colonization of the antral mucosa"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G7","obj":"GI-13-G.6","dok":2,"approach":["Note the setting: a severe burn with hypotension, and ulcers that appear within days.","Recall that Curling ulcers follow burns, while Cushing ulcers follow brain injury.","Link burn shock to low splanchnic blood flow, which weakens the mucosal barrier."],"why":"After a major burn, plasma is lost from the circulation and the body shunts blood away from the gut. Low mucosal blood flow cuts the supply of oxygen and bicarbonate to the epithelium, slows cell renewal, and weakens the mucus and bicarbonate barrier. Even normal amounts of acid can then damage the mucosa and produce multiple acute erosions and ulcers within days. Curling ulcers are classically described in the proximal duodenum.","whyNot":["Vagal overstimulation that raises acid output is the mechanism of a Cushing ulcer, which follows head injury or raised intracranial pressure. This patient had burns and shock, with no brain injury.","Correct.","Loss of protective prostaglandins from cyclooxygenase-1 inhibition is how NSAIDs injure the mucosa. Nothing in his history points to NSAID use.","Helicobacter pylori causes chronic antral gastritis and most ordinary duodenal ulcers over months to years. It does not explain ulcers that appear within days of a burn."],"study":{"topics":["Stress ulcers: Curling and Cushing","Gastric mucosal defenses"],"comps":["GI-13-G5","GI-13-G7"]}},{"id":"GI-C41-Q2","stem":"On hospital day 6, he develops brisk hematemesis and his blood pressure falls to 78/40 mm Hg. Repeat endoscopy shows a visible vessel spurting from the base of the posterior duodenal bulb ulcer. Which of the following arteries is most likely bleeding?","options":["Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","Left gastric artery","Gastroduodenal artery","Splenic artery"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.1","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the ulcer: posterior wall of the first part of the duodenum.","Recall which artery runs directly behind the duodenal bulb.","The gastroduodenal artery, a branch of the common hepatic artery, lies there."],"why":"The celiac trunk gives off the common hepatic artery, which gives off the gastroduodenal artery. This vessel descends directly behind the first part of the duodenum before dividing into the right gastroepiploic and superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. An ulcer that penetrates the posterior wall of the duodenal bulb can erode it and cause life-threatening bleeding. Anterior duodenal ulcers tend to perforate into the peritoneal cavity instead.","whyNot":["The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery comes from the superior mesenteric artery and supplies the lower duodenum and pancreatic head. It does not run behind the first part of the duodenum.","The left gastric artery runs along the lesser curvature of the stomach. It is the usual source of bleeding from a lesser curvature gastric ulcer.","Correct.","The splenic artery runs along the upper border of the pancreas behind the stomach. A posterior gastric ulcer can erode it, but it does not lie behind the duodenal bulb."],"study":{"topics":["Celiac trunk branches","Bleeding versus perforating duodenal ulcers"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-13-G5"]}},{"id":"GI-C41-Q3","stem":"After the bleeding is controlled, enteral feeding with a peptide-based formula is started through a nasogastric tube. Gastric acid output rises during the feeding. Which of the following best explains the effect of the peptides on acid secretion?","options":["Stimulation of antral G cells to release gastrin","Stimulation of antral D cells to release somatostatin","Stimulation of duodenal S cells to release secretin","Stimulation of duodenal I cells to release cholecystokinin"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the phase: food in the stomach means the gastric phase.","Recall that peptides and amino acids in the antrum are a direct stimulus for G cells.","Follow gastrin to histamine release and parietal cell activation."],"why":"In the gastric phase, which produces most of the acid in a meal, peptides and amino acids in the antrum stimulate G cells to release gastrin into the blood. Gastrin acts mainly on enterochromaffin-like cells, which release histamine onto nearby parietal cells, and it also stimulates parietal cells directly. Gastric distension adds vagovagal and local cholinergic reflexes. All of these signals converge on the H+/K+ ATPase, the final step that pumps acid into the lumen.","whyNot":["Correct.","Somatostatin from D cells inhibits gastrin release and parietal cell secretion. D cells are stimulated mainly by acid in the lumen, which acts as a brake on secretion.","Secretin is released when acid enters the duodenum. It stimulates pancreatic bicarbonate and inhibits gastrin release, so it lowers gastric acid output.","Cholecystokinin is released by fats and amino acids in the duodenum. It stimulates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic enzymes and slows gastric emptying, and its overall effect on acid secretion is inhibitory."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of gastric acid secretion","Gastrin, histamine, and somatostatin"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-4"]}},{"id":"GI-C41-Q4","stem":"Biopsies taken from the gastric body during endoscopy show intact oxyntic glands. If these glands were instead destroyed by autoimmune gastritis, which of the following sets of findings would be expected?","options":["Low acid, low intrinsic factor, low gastrin","Low acid, low intrinsic factor, high gastrin","Low acid, normal intrinsic factor, low gastrin","High acid, low intrinsic factor, high gastrin"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that oxyntic glands of the body and fundus hold parietal and chief cells.","Parietal cells make both HCl and intrinsic factor, so both fall when they are destroyed.","The antrum is spared, and loss of acid removes somatostatin braking, so gastrin rises."],"why":"Autoimmune gastritis targets parietal cells and intrinsic factor in the body and fundus. Losing parietal cells removes both hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor, which leads to achlorhydria and, over years, vitamin B12 deficiency. Chief cells are also lost, so pepsinogen I falls. The antral G cells are spared, and without acid to trigger somatostatin release, gastrin climbs high and drives enterochromaffin-like cell hyperplasia.","whyNot":["The antral G cells are spared in autoimmune gastritis. Without acid to stimulate somatostatin release, gastrin rises.","Correct.","Intrinsic factor comes from the same parietal cells that make acid, so it falls along with acid. Gastrin rises, since the antral G cells are spared and lose acid feedback.","Acid comes from parietal cells, which are destroyed. High acid with high gastrin is the pattern of a gastrin-secreting tumor (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome)."],"study":{"topics":["Gastric gland cell types","Autoimmune gastritis"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-13-E1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C42","system":"GI","dx":"Reflux esophagitis with a sliding hiatal hernia in pregnancy","codes":["GI-13-G8","GI-13-G6"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman at 30 weeks' gestation comes to the physician because of a 6-week history of burning retrosternal pain after meals and when lying down, sour regurgitation at night, and pain with swallowing. Her symptoms began at about 20 weeks' gestation and have worsened. Her body mass index before pregnancy was 31 kg/m2. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the fetal heart rate is normal. Because of persistent odynophagia, upper endoscopy is performed. It shows a 3-cm sliding hiatal hernia and several linear erosions in the distal 5 cm of the esophagus. Diagnosis: reflux esophagitis with a sliding hiatal hernia during pregnancy.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C42-Q1","stem":"In addition to her hiatal hernia and higher intra-abdominal pressure, which of the following most directly contributes to her reflux?","options":["Motilin-mediated increase in migrating motor complexes","Increased vagal cholinergic tone to the LES muscle","Estrogen-mediated increase in parietal cell acid output","Progesterone-mediated decrease in LES resting tone"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-13-G8","obj":"GI-13-G.9","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the risk factors named: pregnancy, obesity, and hiatal hernia.","Recall that progesterone relaxes smooth muscle throughout the gut.","Apply it to the lower esophageal sphincter: lower resting tone lets acid reflux."],"why":"Reflux happens when the antireflux barrier fails through transient sphincter relaxations, a low resting sphincter pressure, or a hiatal hernia. In pregnancy, rising progesterone relaxes the smooth muscle of the lower esophageal sphincter and lowers its resting pressure. The enlarging uterus raises intra-abdominal pressure and pushes gastric contents upward. Her obesity and sliding hernia add to the same problem, which is why symptoms worsen as pregnancy advances.","whyNot":["Migrating motor complexes sweep the fasting gut clean and would speed clearance. Progesterone tends to slow gut motility.","Vagal cholinergic input contracts the lower esophageal sphincter and raises its tone. More of it would protect against reflux.","Pregnancy hormones do not meaningfully raise gastric acid output. Reflux in pregnancy comes from a weak barrier at the gastroesophageal junction.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["GERD mechanisms","Hormonal effects on gut smooth muscle"],"comps":["GI-13-G8","GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C42-Q2","stem":"Biopsies are taken from the mid and distal esophagus. Which of the following findings would most strongly support reflux esophagitis over eosinophilic esophagitis?","options":["A sharply punched-out ulcer in the mid esophagus near the aortic arch","Dense eosinophils, 15 or more per high-power field, in mid and distal biopsies","Scattered eosinophils, under 15 per high-power field, in distal biopsies only","Concentric mucosal rings and linear furrows along the esophageal length"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G6","obj":"GI-13-G.7","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that both conditions can show eosinophils and basal cell hyperplasia.","Separate them by eosinophil count and by where they are found.","Reflux injures the distal esophagus most, with only a few eosinophils."],"why":"Acid reflux injures the squamous epithelium closest to the stomach. Biopsies show basal zone hyperplasia, elongated lamina propria papillae, and a few intraepithelial eosinophils and neutrophils, mostly in the distal esophagus. Eosinophilic esophagitis is an allergic, Th2-driven disease with dense eosinophils (15 or more per high-power field) along the whole esophagus. A normal mid-esophageal biopsy with mild distal changes points to reflux.","whyNot":["A discrete mid-esophageal ulcer where the aortic arch narrows the lumen suggests pill esophagitis, from a tablet such as doxycycline lodging there.","Fifteen or more eosinophils per high-power field in both the mid and distal esophagus is the histologic definition of eosinophilic esophagitis, often in a patient with atopy and food impaction.","Correct.","Stacked rings (trachealization) and linear furrows are the classic endoscopic look of eosinophilic esophagitis."],"study":{"topics":["Reflux versus eosinophilic esophagitis","Pill esophagitis"],"comps":["GI-13-G6","GI-13-G8"]}},{"id":"GI-C42-Q3","stem":"In her type of hiatal hernia, which part of the stomach is displaced into the thorax together with the gastroesophageal junction?","options":["Antrum","Pylorus","Fundus","Cardia"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.1","dok":2,"approach":["Define a sliding hernia: the gastroesophageal junction moves up through the hiatus.","Name the stomach region that surrounds the gastroesophageal junction.","That region, the cardia, goes up with it."],"why":"The stomach has five regions: cardia, fundus, body, antrum, and pylorus. The cardia is the small zone that surrounds the opening of the esophagus. In a sliding hiatal hernia, the most common type, the gastroesophageal junction and cardia slide up through the esophageal hiatus into the thorax. This removes the help that the crura normally give the sphincter and favors reflux. In a paraesophageal hernia, the junction stays put and the fundus herniates beside the esophagus.","whyNot":["The antrum is the distal stomach near the pylorus. It is not displaced in a small sliding hernia.","The pylorus is the outlet to the duodenum, far from the hiatus. It stays in the abdomen.","The fundus rolls up beside an esophagus that stays in place in a paraesophageal (rolling) hernia. In a sliding hernia the junction itself moves up.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Regions of the stomach","Sliding versus paraesophageal hernia"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-13-F3"]}},{"id":"GI-C42-Q4","stem":"Refluxed acid is normally cleared from her esophagus by secondary peristalsis triggered by distension. Which of the following best describes how the enteric nervous system propels the bolus?","options":["Cholinergic contraction above and nitrergic relaxation below the bolus","Simultaneous contraction of the whole length of the esophageal body","Nitrergic relaxation above and cholinergic contraction below the bolus","Motilin-driven contraction that moves from the distal to the proximal end"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the law of the intestine: contract behind the bolus, relax in front of it.","Name the transmitters: acetylcholine and substance P contract, nitric oxide and VIP relax.","Match the option with the right transmitter on the right side."],"why":"Peristalsis is coordinated by the myenteric plexus. When a bolus stretches the wall, excitatory neurons release acetylcholine and substance P to contract the circular muscle on the oral side. Inhibitory neurons release nitric oxide and VIP to relax the muscle on the anal side. The ring of contraction moves down the tube and pushes the bolus ahead of it. Secondary peristalsis uses this local reflex to clear refluxed acid back into the stomach.","whyNot":["Correct.","Simultaneous contractions do not propel anything. This is the abnormal pattern of distal esophageal spasm.","This pattern would push the bolus backward toward the mouth. The contraction must be behind the bolus.","Motilin drives the fasting migrating motor complex in the stomach and small bowel, and it moves in the oral to anal direction."],"study":{"topics":["Peristalsis and the myenteric plexus","Esophageal acid clearance"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-2"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C43","system":"GI","dx":"Ulcerative colitis with toxic megacolon","codes":["GI-13-G9","GI-13-F5"],"case":"A 24-year-old man with ulcerative colitis diagnosed 3 years ago is brought to the emergency department because of 10 days of bloody diarrhea, 12 stools daily, and 2 days of increasing abdominal swelling and pain. He stopped his maintenance medication 2 months ago. Temperature is 39.0 °C (102.2 °F), pulse is 124/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. The abdomen is distended and diffusely tender with reduced bowel sounds. Leukocyte count is 16,800/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000). An abdominal x-ray shows the transverse colon dilated to 8 cm (normal under 6 cm). Diagnosis: ulcerative colitis complicated by toxic megacolon.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C43-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings was most likely seen on colonoscopic biopsy when his disease was first diagnosed?","options":["Continuous mucosal inflammation from the rectum with crypt abscesses","Caseating granulomas with ulcers clustered in the ileocecal region","Skip lesions with transmural inflammation and noncaseating granulomas","Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites that contain ingested red cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G9","obj":"GI-13-G.10","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the distribution of ulcerative colitis: rectum first, then continuous proximal spread.","Recall its depth: mucosa and submucosa only.","Match the histology: neutrophils in crypts form crypt abscesses."],"why":"Ulcerative colitis always involves the rectum and extends proximally in one continuous stretch. Inflammation is limited to the mucosa and submucosa. Neutrophils invade crypts to form crypt abscesses, and chronic disease distorts crypt architecture. Ulcers undermine the mucosa, and islands of regenerating mucosa between them form pseudopolyps. Granulomas and skip lesions argue for Crohn disease.","whyNot":["Correct.","Caseating granulomas in the ileocecal region suggest intestinal tuberculosis.","Skip lesions, transmural inflammation, and noncaseating granulomas are the pattern of Crohn disease.","Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites that ingest red cells are typical of amebic colitis from Entamoeba histolytica."],"study":{"topics":["Ulcerative colitis versus Crohn disease"],"comps":["GI-13-G9","GI-11"]}},{"id":"GI-C43-Q2","stem":"He recovers after medical treatment. Which of the following long-term complications is more characteristic of his disease than of Crohn disease?","options":["Vitamin B12 deficiency","Enterocutaneous fistula","Calcium oxalate nephrolithiasis","Primary sclerosing cholangitis"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G9","obj":"GI-13-G.11","dok":3,"approach":["Sort complications by mechanism: transmural or ileal disease points to Crohn disease.","Recall which extraintestinal problem is linked most strongly to ulcerative colitis.","Primary sclerosing cholangitis fits; it occurs in a large share of patients with ulcerative colitis."],"why":"Most patients with primary sclerosing cholangitis have inflammatory bowel disease, usually ulcerative colitis. The bile ducts become inflamed and fibrosed, giving a beaded pattern on cholangiography, a cholestatic rise in alkaline phosphatase, and p-ANCA positivity in many cases. It raises the risk of cholangiocarcinoma and of colorectal cancer. Fistulas, strictures, B12 deficiency, and oxalate stones all come from transmural or ileal disease in Crohn disease.","whyNot":["Vitamin B12 is absorbed in the terminal ileum, which is often diseased or resected in Crohn disease. Ulcerative colitis spares the small bowel, apart from mild backwash ileitis.","Fistulas need transmural inflammation, which is a feature of Crohn disease. Ulcerative colitis stays in the mucosa.","Oxalate stones follow fat malabsorption in ileal Crohn disease. Unabsorbed fat binds calcium, which frees oxalate to be absorbed in the colon.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of IBD","Primary sclerosing cholangitis"],"comps":["GI-13-G9","GI-13-C1"]}},{"id":"GI-C43-Q3","stem":"His younger brother has Crohn disease of the terminal ileum and carries a loss-of-function NOD2 variant. Which of the following best explains how this variant predisposes to Crohn disease?","options":["Excess binding of bacterial lipopolysaccharide by surface TLR4","Impaired cytosolic sensing of bacterial peptidoglycan","Deficient transport of secretory IgA across the epithelium","Loss of FOXP3 expression in regulatory T cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that NOD2 is an intracellular pattern recognition receptor.","Name its ligand: muramyl dipeptide from bacterial peptidoglycan.","Link weak sensing to poor clearance of commensal bacteria and a chronic Th1 and Th17 response."],"why":"NOD2 is a cytosolic receptor in Paneth cells, monocytes, and macrophages that recognizes muramyl dipeptide from bacterial peptidoglycan. Loss-of-function variants weaken antimicrobial defensin release and bacterial killing, so commensal bacteria cross the epithelium and persist. The immune system answers with an exaggerated Th1 response (interferon-gamma) and Th17 response (IL-17) that drives transmural inflammation and noncaseating granulomas. Crohn disease often affects the terminal ileum, where Paneth cells are most dense.","whyNot":["Toll-like receptor 4 senses lipopolysaccharide at the cell surface. NOD2 is a separate, cytosolic receptor, and the Crohn variants reduce its function.","Correct.","Secretory IgA is carried across the epithelium by the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor. NOD2 is an innate sensor inside the cell and has no role in IgA transport.","FOXP3 mutations cause IPEX syndrome, with severe enteropathy, type 1 diabetes, and dermatitis in infant boys. They are not the NOD2 mechanism."],"study":{"topics":["NOD2 and innate immunity","Th1 and Th17 in Crohn disease"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-G9"]}},{"id":"GI-C43-Q4","stem":"Two years later he has a total colectomy with an end ileostomy. Over the next several months, the ileostomy output averages 2 L/day. He then develops poor healing of the stoma site, reduced taste, and scaly, red plaques around the mouth and on the hands. Deficiency of which of the following is the most likely cause?","options":["Selenium","Iron","Zinc","Copper"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.6","dok":2,"approach":["Note the cause: large, ongoing intestinal fluid losses.","Group the findings: poor wound healing, dysgeusia, and periorificial and acral dermatitis.","Match the triad to the mineral lost in intestinal fluid."],"why":"Zinc is lost in large amounts in diarrhea and high-output stomas. It is a cofactor for many enzymes, including those needed for cell division, collagen formation, and taste. Deficiency causes poor wound healing, loss of taste and smell, hair loss, and a scaly, red dermatitis around the mouth, anus, and on the hands and feet, called acrodermatitis. The inherited form, acrodermatitis enteropathica, comes from a defect in intestinal zinc uptake.","whyNot":["Selenium deficiency causes cardiomyopathy (Keshan disease) and muscle weakness. Skin and taste changes are not its pattern.","Iron deficiency causes microcytic anemia, fatigue, glossitis, and spoon-shaped nails. It does not cause periorificial dermatitis.","Correct.","Copper deficiency causes anemia, neutropenia, and a myelopathy that resembles vitamin B12 deficiency. It does not cause this rash or taste loss."],"study":{"topics":["Zinc deficiency","Mineral deficiencies"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-7"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C44","system":"GI","dx":"Internal hemorrhoids with a coexisting anal fissure","codes":["GI-13-J2"],"case":"A 33-year-old woman comes to the physician 3 months after a vaginal delivery because of bright red blood on the toilet paper after bowel movements and a soft swelling that protrudes from the anus during defecation and reduces spontaneously. She has had constipation with hard stools since late pregnancy. For the past 3 weeks, she has also had sharp, tearing pain during defecation that lasts for an hour afterward. Vital signs are within normal limits. Inspection shows a small linear tear in the posterior midline of the anoderm. Anoscopy shows engorged vascular cushions above the pectinate line. Diagnosis: grade II internal hemorrhoids with an acute anal fissure.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C44-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the development of her hemorrhoids?","options":["Portal hypertension diverting blood through rectal portosystemic anastomoses","Straining that engorges and displaces the anal vascular cushions","Obstruction and infection of the anal glands at the pectinate line","Weakness of the pelvic floor allowing full-thickness rectal descent"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-J2","obj":"GI-13-J.2","dok":2,"approach":["Note her risks: constipation, straining, and recent pregnancy.","Recall that hemorrhoids are normal vascular cushions that become enlarged and slide down.","Choose the mechanism that links straining to cushion engorgement."],"why":"Everyone has anal cushions, made of submucosal vessels and connective tissue that help seal the anal canal. Straining with hard stools and the pressure of a pregnant uterus on pelvic veins engorge these cushions, and their supporting tissue stretches so they slide downward. Internal hemorrhoids arise above the pectinate line. They cause painless bright red bleeding and may prolapse; grade II hemorrhoids return on their own. External hemorrhoids lie below the line and hurt when they thrombose.","whyNot":["Portal hypertension opens anorectal varices at the portosystemic anastomosis, which are a different lesion from hemorrhoids. She has no liver disease.","Correct.","Obstructed, infected anal glands produce an anorectal abscess, which causes constant throbbing pain and fever.","Full-thickness descent of the rectal wall through the anus is rectal prolapse, which shows concentric mucosal folds. Her swelling is engorged vascular cushions."],"study":{"topics":["Hemorrhoid pathogenesis","Internal versus external hemorrhoids"],"comps":["GI-13-J2","GI-5"]}},{"id":"GI-C44-Q2","stem":"Her hemorrhoids are painless, but her fissure is very painful. Which of the following best explains this difference?","options":["Sympathetic afferents above the pectinate line and vagal afferents below","Absent sensory fibers above the pectinate line and pelvic splanchnic fibers below","Pudendal somatic fibers above the pectinate line and visceral afferents below","Visceral afferents above the pectinate line and pudendal somatic fibers below"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.5","dok":2,"approach":["Locate each lesion: internal hemorrhoids above the pectinate line, fissure below it.","Recall that the area above the line is endoderm-derived gut with visceral innervation.","The area below is ectoderm-derived skin supplied by the inferior rectal branches of the pudendal nerve."],"why":"The pectinate line marks where gut endoderm meets ectoderm. Above it, the mucosa has visceral innervation that senses stretch but not cutting or tearing, so internal hemorrhoids are painless. Below it, the anoderm is supplied by somatic fibers in the inferior rectal nerve, a branch of the pudendal nerve, so a fissure there is sharply painful. The same line divides the epithelium, the arterial supply, portal versus systemic venous drainage, and internal iliac versus superficial inguinal lymph drainage.","whyNot":["The vagus does not supply the anal canal. Visceral fibers above the line travel with the pelvic splanchnic and inferior hypogastric nerves, and the area below is somatic.","The mucosa above the line does have visceral afferents that sense stretch, and pelvic splanchnic nerves carry visceral fibers.","This reverses the anatomy. The pudendal nerve supplies the skin-lined anal canal below the line.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pectinate line anatomy","Anal canal innervation"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-2"]}},{"id":"GI-C44-Q3","stem":"Eight months later, she returns because of 2 days of constant, throbbing perianal pain that is worse when sitting. Temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F). There is a tender, red, fluctuant swelling beside the anal verge. Which of the following is the most likely origin of this new problem?","options":["Thrombosis within the inferior rectal venous plexus","Strangulation of a prolapsed, irreducible internal hemorrhoid","Infected, obstructed anal glands at the pectinate line","Extension of the posterior fissure into the rectal wall"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G","obj":"GI-13-G.13","dok":2,"approach":["Note the new features: fever, constant pain, and a fluctuant swelling.","Recognize an anorectal abscess.","Recall that most begin in anal glands that open into the crypts at the pectinate line."],"why":"Anal glands open into the anal crypts at the pectinate line. When a gland duct becomes blocked, bacteria multiply and form an abscess that spreads into the perianal or ischioanal tissues. The result is constant throbbing pain, fever, and a tender fluctuant swelling. After drainage, many abscesses leave a fistula between the crypt and the perianal skin. Recurrent abscesses and fistulas should raise concern for Crohn disease.","whyNot":["A thrombosed external hemorrhoid is a painful, bluish, firm lump at the anal verge. It does not cause fever or fluctuance.","A strangulated internal hemorrhoid is a painful, dark, irreducible mass protruding through the anus. It is not a fluctuant collection beside the anus.","Correct.","Fissures stay in the anoderm below the pectinate line. A fissure does not track into the rectum to form a fluctuant mass."],"study":{"topics":["Anorectal abscess and fistula","Anal fissure"],"comps":["GI-13-G","GI-13-G9"]}},{"id":"GI-C44-Q4","stem":"Her delivery was prolonged, with forceps assistance. Stretch injury to both pudendal nerves during such a delivery would most directly impair which of the following components of fecal continence?","options":["Reflex relaxation of the internal anal sphincter to rectal filling","Resting tone of the internal anal sphincter between defecations","Rectal stretch sensation carried by pelvic splanchnic afferents","Voluntary contraction of the external anal sphincter"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.6","dok":3,"approach":["List the parts of continence: rectal sensation, internal sphincter reflex, and external sphincter control.","Recall which nerve supplies each part.","The pudendal nerve supplies the voluntary external sphincter, so its loss causes urge incontinence."],"why":"When stool enters the rectum, stretch receptors signal fullness and the internal anal sphincter relaxes by a reflex through the myenteric plexus. Continence then depends on voluntary contraction of the external anal sphincter and puborectalis, which are skeletal muscles supplied by the pudendal nerve (S2 to S4). Pudendal injury from a prolonged or instrumented delivery weakens this voluntary squeeze. The person feels the urge but cannot hold stool until she reaches a toilet.","whyNot":["Reflex relaxation of the internal sphincter when the rectum fills is mediated by the myenteric plexus in the gut wall. It survives pudendal injury.","The internal sphincter is smooth muscle with tone maintained by its own properties and autonomic input. The pudendal nerve does not supply it.","Stretch of the rectum is sensed by visceral afferents traveling with the pelvic splanchnic nerves. These are separate from the pudendal nerve.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Defecation reflex","Fecal incontinence after childbirth"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-5"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C45","system":"GI","dx":"Esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula and duodenal atresia in trisomy 21","codes":["GI-13-H1"],"case":"A newborn girl born at 37 weeks' gestation is noted to have frothy oral secretions, and she chokes, coughs, and becomes cyanotic at her first feed. The pregnancy was complicated by polyhydramnios. She has upslanting palpebral fissures, a flat facial profile, and single transverse palmar creases. A nasogastric tube cannot be passed beyond 10 cm, and a chest x-ray shows it coiled in a blind upper esophageal pouch. An abdominal x-ray shows two large gas-filled bubbles in the upper abdomen with no gas beyond them. Karyotype is 47,XX,+21. Diagnosis: esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula and duodenal atresia in a newborn with trisomy 21.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C45-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the presence of gas in her stomach?","options":["A fistula joins the trachea to the distal esophagus","Bacteria in the stomach produce gas by fermenting milk","A fistula joins the trachea to the proximal esophageal pouch","Swallowed air passes through a narrowed but open esophagus"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-H1","obj":"GI-13-H.1","dok":3,"approach":["Note the blind upper pouch: swallowed air cannot reach the stomach that way.","Ask how air enters the stomach anyway: through the airway.","A fistula from the trachea to the lower esophagus carries inspired air into the stomach."],"why":"The most common type of esophageal atresia, about 85% of cases, has a blind upper pouch and a fistula from the trachea to the distal esophagus. Each breath and cry pushes air through the fistula into the stomach, so the abdomen contains gas. Saliva fills the blind pouch and spills into the larynx, causing drooling, choking, and cyanosis with feeds. The fetus could not swallow amniotic fluid, which caused polyhydramnios. Look for VACTERL anomalies: vertebral, anal, cardiac, tracheoesophageal, renal, and limb defects.","whyNot":["Correct.","The newborn gut has very few bacteria, and she has not been fed successfully. Fermentation does not explain gas within the first hours of life.","A fistula from the trachea to the upper pouch can send saliva into the lungs. Without a distal connection, air could not reach the stomach, and the abdomen would be gasless.","The case states the esophagus ends in a blind pouch, so no air can pass from the upper esophagus to the stomach."],"study":{"topics":["Types of esophageal atresia","VACTERL association"],"comps":["GI-13-H1","GI-1"]}},{"id":"GI-C45-Q2","stem":"Abnormal development of which of the following structures most likely produced her esophageal defect?","options":["Pleuroperitoneal membrane","Aorticopulmonary septum","Tracheoesophageal septum","Urorectal septum"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-1","obj":"GI-1.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the lesion as a faulty separation of the foregut into trachea and esophagus.","Recall that the respiratory diverticulum grows from the ventral foregut in week 4.","The tracheoesophageal septum divides the two; when it deviates or fails, atresia and fistula result."],"why":"In week 4, the respiratory diverticulum buds from the ventral wall of the foregut. Tracheoesophageal ridges grow toward each other and fuse into the tracheoesophageal septum, which separates the ventral trachea from the dorsal esophagus. If the septum deviates posteriorly or the division is incomplete, the esophagus may end blindly and a channel may remain between the trachea and esophagus. These defects often occur with other VACTERL anomalies.","whyNot":["Failure of a pleuroperitoneal membrane to close leaves a congenital diaphragmatic hernia, usually on the left, with bowel in the chest.","The aorticopulmonary septum divides the truncus arteriosus. Its defects cause persistent truncus arteriosus or transposition of the great arteries.","Correct.","The urorectal septum divides the cloaca into the rectum and urogenital sinus. Defects cause anorectal malformations and fistulas to the urinary tract."],"study":{"topics":["Foregut division","Tracheoesophageal fistula embryology"],"comps":["GI-1","GI-13-H1"]}},{"id":"GI-C45-Q3","stem":"At surgery, the second part of the duodenum ends blindly just distal to the major duodenal papilla, with no pancreatic tissue around it. Failure of which of the following developmental processes most likely explains this finding?","options":["Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut around the SMA","Recanalization of the solid-stage duodenal lumen","Migration of the ventral pancreatic bud around the duodenum","Obliteration of the vitelline duct at the ileum"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-1","obj":"GI-1.6","dok":2,"approach":["Note the findings: a blind-ending duodenum with a double bubble and no distal gas.","Recall that the duodenal lumen fills with cells in weeks 5 to 6 and opens again by about the end of week 8.","Failed reopening leaves atresia, which is strongly linked to Down syndrome."],"why":"Epithelial cells briefly fill the lumen of the duodenum during weeks 5 to 6. The lumen normally reopens by vacuolization by about the end of the second month. If this recanalization fails, the duodenum is blocked, usually near or just beyond the major papilla, so vomitus contains bile. The stomach and proximal duodenum fill with gas to make the double bubble sign, and the fetus cannot absorb swallowed fluid, adding to polyhydramnios. About a quarter to a third of infants with duodenal atresia have Down syndrome.","whyNot":["Malrotation leaves the cecum high and allows Ladd bands or volvulus to compress the duodenum. It does not create a blind-ending duodenum.","Correct.","Abnormal migration of the ventral pancreatic bud forms an annular pancreas, a ring of pancreas around the duodenum. The case states there is no pancreatic tissue around the obstruction.","A persistent vitelline duct remnant forms a Meckel diverticulum in the ileum. It does not cause duodenal obstruction at birth."],"study":{"topics":["Duodenal atresia","Gut recanalization"],"comps":["GI-1","GI-13-H"]}},{"id":"GI-C45-Q4","stem":"Saliva pools in her blind upper pouch. In a healthy newborn, once a bolus of milk reaches the pharynx, which of the following controls its passage into the esophagus?","options":["Reflex program of the medullary swallowing center","Local myenteric reflexes independent of the brainstem","Sympathetic outflow from the thoracic spinal cord","Voluntary commands from the primary motor cortex"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["Divide swallowing into oral, pharyngeal, and esophageal phases.","Recall that only the oral phase is voluntary.","The pharyngeal phase is a reflex run by the medullary swallowing center."],"why":"Swallowing begins voluntarily when the tongue pushes the bolus back. Contact with the pharynx triggers a fixed reflex sequence controlled by the swallowing center in the medulla. The soft palate seals the nasopharynx, the larynx rises and the epiglottis covers it, breathing stops briefly, and the upper esophageal sphincter relaxes. Primary peristalsis then carries the bolus down, and vagal inhibitory nerves relax the lower esophageal sphincter ahead of it.","whyNot":["Correct.","The pharynx and upper esophagus are skeletal muscle controlled by brainstem motor nuclei. The myenteric plexus governs the smooth muscle of the lower esophagus.","Sympathetic outflow does not drive swallowing. The pharyngeal phase uses cranial nerves IX, X, and the nucleus ambiguus.","Only the oral phase of swallowing is voluntary. Once the bolus touches the pharynx, the sequence cannot be stopped by will."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of swallowing","Medullary swallowing center"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-3"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C46","system":"GI","dx":"Plummer-Vinson syndrome","codes":["GI-13-H2"],"case":"A 52-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 5 months of progressive difficulty swallowing solid food, with a sensation that food sticks in her throat. She also reports fatigue. She had a Roux-en-Y gastric bypass 8 years ago and stopped taking supplements 3 years ago. Physical examination shows pallor, a smooth red tongue, cracks at the corners of the mouth, and spoon-shaped fingernails. Hemoglobin is 8.6 g/dL (12.0 to 16.0), mean corpuscular volume is 68 fL (80 to 100), and ferritin is 5 ng/mL (15 to 150). A barium swallow shows a thin shelf-like membrane in the upper esophagus just below the cricopharyngeus. Diagnosis: Plummer-Vinson syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C46-Q1","stem":"This patient has an increased long-term risk of which of the following?","options":["Gastrointestinal stromal tumor of the stomach","Extranodal marginal zone lymphoma of the stomach","Squamous cell carcinoma of the upper esophagus","Adenocarcinoma arising in Barrett esophagus"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-H2","obj":"GI-13-H.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the lesion: an upper esophageal web with iron deficiency anemia.","Recall the cancer linked to Plummer-Vinson syndrome.","Contrast it with the lower esophageal Schatzki ring, a benign ring at the squamocolumnar junction."],"why":"Plummer-Vinson syndrome combines iron deficiency anemia, dysphagia, and webs in the upper esophagus. Webs are thin mucosal folds that cause dysphagia mainly for solids. The syndrome carries a higher risk of squamous cell carcinoma of the hypopharynx and upper esophagus, so patients need follow-up. A Schatzki ring is a different lesion in the lower esophagus at the squamocolumnar junction that causes intermittent solid food dysphagia, often with a hiatal hernia.","whyNot":["A gastrointestinal stromal tumor arises from interstitial cells of Cajal, usually with a KIT mutation. It has no link to iron deficiency or webs.","Gastric MALT lymphoma (extranodal marginal zone lymphoma) is driven by chronic Helicobacter pylori infection. It is not linked to esophageal webs.","Correct.","Distal esophageal adenocarcinoma arises from Barrett metaplasia after long-standing reflux. A Schatzki ring sits at that level but carries no comparable cancer risk."],"study":{"topics":["Esophageal webs versus Schatzki rings","Plummer-Vinson syndrome"],"comps":["GI-13-H2","GI-13-K1"]}},{"id":"GI-C46-Q2","stem":"She is treated with intravenous iron. Six months later, her ferritin is normal, but hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL with an absolute neutrophil count of 900/mm3 (1,500 to 8,000). She has a new unsteady gait with loss of vibration and position sense in the feet. She has been taking a high-dose zinc supplement daily since her treatment began. Serum vitamin B12 and methylmalonic acid levels are normal. Deficiency of which of the following most likely explains these new findings?","options":["Folate","Copper","Vitamin E","Vitamin B12"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.6","dok":3,"approach":["Group the findings: anemia, neutropenia, and a dorsal column myelopathy.","B12 deficiency looks similar, but her B12 and methylmalonic acid are normal.","Link high zinc intake and gastric bypass to copper deficiency."],"why":"Copper deficiency produces anemia, neutropenia, and a myelopathy that closely mimics subacute combined degeneration from B12 deficiency. Two of her risks point there. Gastric bypass bypasses the stomach and proximal small bowel, where copper is absorbed. High zinc intake induces metallothionein in enterocytes, which binds copper tightly so it is lost when the cells shed into the lumen. Stopping zinc and replacing copper corrects the blood counts, though nerve damage may not fully recover.","whyNot":["Folate deficiency causes a megaloblastic anemia but no myelopathy.","Correct.","Vitamin E deficiency causes spinocerebellar degeneration and hemolytic anemia. It does not cause neutropenia, and it is not linked to zinc excess.","Vitamin B12 deficiency also causes anemia, neutropenia, and dorsal column myelopathy, but her normal B12 and methylmalonic acid levels rule it out."],"study":{"topics":["Copper deficiency","Zinc and copper interaction"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-7"]}},{"id":"GI-C46-Q3","stem":"Biopsy of the web would most likely show which of the following lining epithelia?","options":["Nonkeratinized stratified squamous","Simple columnar with interspersed goblet cells","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar","Keratinized stratified squamous"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the web: the upper esophagus.","Recall that a web is a fold of mucosa, covered by the normal local epithelium.","The esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium."],"why":"An esophageal web is a thin fold of mucosa that projects into the lumen, so it is covered by the normal esophageal lining: nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium over lamina propria. This lining resists abrasion from swallowed food. The stomach and intestine are lined by simple columnar epithelium. At the gastroesophageal junction, chronic acid reflux can replace squamous cells with columnar cells and goblet cells, which is Barrett metaplasia and the precursor of adenocarcinoma.","whyNot":["Correct.","Simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells is intestinal metaplasia, the change of Barrett esophagus in the distal esophagus.","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines the trachea and bronchi.","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium covers the skin. The esophagus is normally nonkeratinized."],"study":{"topics":["Esophageal epithelium","Barrett metaplasia"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-13-G2"]}},{"id":"GI-C46-Q4","stem":"Her bypass routes food from a small gastric pouch directly into the jejunum. Diversion of food away from which of the following segments most directly explains her original anemia?","options":["Terminal ileum","Ascending colon","Duodenum","Distal jejunum"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the anemia: microcytic with very low ferritin, so iron deficiency.","Recall where iron is absorbed: mostly the duodenum and proximal jejunum.","Note that the Roux-en-Y bypass diverts food past the duodenum."],"why":"Iron is absorbed mainly in the duodenum. There, acid helps keep it soluble, duodenal cytochrome b reduces ferric to ferrous iron, and DMT1 carries it into enterocytes. A Roux-en-Y gastric bypass sends food from a small gastric pouch straight into the jejunum, skipping the duodenum. The small pouch also makes little acid. Folate is absorbed in the jejunum, and B12 and bile acids in the terminal ileum.","whyNot":["The terminal ileum absorbs vitamin B12 and bile acids. Loss of it would cause a macrocytic anemia, and it still receives food after this operation.","The colon absorbs water and electrolytes and very little iron.","Correct.","The distal jejunum continues to receive food after a Roux-en-Y bypass. It is not the main site of iron uptake."],"study":{"topics":["Sites of nutrient absorption","Iron absorption"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-8"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C47","system":"GI","dx":"Infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","codes":["GI-13-H4"],"case":"A 4-week-old boy, his parents' first child, is brought to the physician because of forceful vomiting after every feed for 5 days. The vomitus is milk without green or yellow staining, and he is eager to feed again right after vomiting. He was born at term and fed well until 3 weeks of age. At 2 weeks, he received a course of oral erythromycin after a pertussis exposure. He weighs less than he did at birth. His mucous membranes are dry. A firm, mobile, olive-sized mass is felt in the epigastrium. Ultrasonography shows pyloric muscle thickness of 5 mm (normal under 3 mm). Diagnosis: infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C47-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why his vomiting began at 3 weeks of age rather than at birth?","options":["Gradual twisting of the midgut around the mesenteric root","Delayed failure of recanalization of the pyloric lumen","Progressive scarring after an ulcer in the pyloric channel","Postnatal hypertrophy of the pyloric circular muscle"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-H4","obj":"GI-13-H.4","dok":2,"approach":["Note the timing: normal feeding, then onset at 2 to 6 weeks.","Recall that the pylorus is open at birth and the muscle thickens over the first weeks.","Choose the option that develops after birth and makes a palpable mass."],"why":"In infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis, the circular smooth muscle of the pylorus thickens after birth. The channel gradually narrows until milk cannot leave the stomach, which usually happens between 2 and 6 weeks of age. The thick muscle is the olive felt in the epigastrium, and visible peristaltic waves may cross the upper abdomen. Risk is higher in first-born boys and after macrolide exposure, especially erythromycin given in the first 2 weeks of life.","whyNot":["Midgut volvulus with malrotation causes sudden bilious vomiting because the twist is below the papilla. It does not produce an olive-shaped mass.","Failed recanalization causes atresia, which blocks the gut completely and causes vomiting from the first feeds.","Peptic scarring of the pylorus is a cause of gastric outlet obstruction in adults with chronic ulcer disease. It does not occur at this age.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pyloric stenosis","Neonatal causes of vomiting"],"comps":["GI-13-H4","GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C47-Q2","stem":"Which of the following sets of serum findings is most likely in this infant?","options":["Chloride high, potassium low, bicarbonate low","Chloride normal, potassium high, bicarbonate high","Chloride low, potassium high, bicarbonate low","Chloride low, potassium low, bicarbonate high"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.7","dok":3,"approach":["Note that the vomitus is pure gastric juice: HCl.","Losing H+ leaves bicarbonate behind, and losing Cl- lowers serum chloride.","Volume loss raises aldosterone, and bicarbonate in the urine carries K+ out, so potassium falls."],"why":"Each parietal cell that secretes H+ into the lumen returns an equal amount of bicarbonate to the blood. Normally that bicarbonate is neutralized when acid enters the duodenum, but vomiting removes the acid so bicarbonate stays in the blood, causing metabolic alkalosis. Chloride is lost with the acid. Volume depletion activates aldosterone, which saves sodium at the cost of potassium and H+, so the kidney wastes potassium and keeps the alkalosis going. The result is hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, corrected with saline and potassium before surgery.","whyNot":["High chloride, low potassium, and low bicarbonate is a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, the pattern of diarrhea, which loses intestinal bicarbonate.","Vomiting gastric juice always lowers chloride, and potassium falls through renal losses. Neither stays normal or rises.","High potassium with metabolic acidosis suggests renal failure or adrenal insufficiency, such as congenital adrenal hyperplasia with salt wasting.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Acid-base effects of vomiting","Contraction alkalosis"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-H4"]}},{"id":"GI-C47-Q3","stem":"His vomitus contains no bile because bile enters the gut distal to the obstruction. In which part of the duodenum does bile normally enter?","options":["Fourth (ascending) part","Second (descending) part","First (superior) part","Third (horizontal, inferior) part"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.1","dok":2,"approach":["Connect nonbilious vomiting to an obstruction proximal to the bile entry point.","Recall where the common bile duct and pancreatic duct open.","The major duodenal papilla is on the posteromedial wall of the second part."],"why":"The duodenum has four parts: superior, descending, horizontal, and ascending. The common bile duct and main pancreatic duct join at the hepatopancreatic ampulla and open at the major duodenal papilla in the second, descending part. Any block above this point, such as a thick pylorus, leaves vomitus free of bile. A block below it, such as midgut volvulus or most duodenal atresias, causes bilious vomiting. This is a key bedside sign for sorting neonatal obstruction.","whyNot":["The fourth part rises to the duodenojejunal flexure at the ligament of Treitz. It also lies beyond the papilla.","Correct.","The first part, including the duodenal bulb, lies before the papilla. Bile does not enter here.","The third part crosses in front of the aorta and behind the superior mesenteric vessels. It lies beyond the papilla, and obstruction here causes bilious vomiting."],"study":{"topics":["Parts of the duodenum","Bilious versus nonbilious vomiting"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-1"]}},{"id":"GI-C47-Q4","stem":"The thickened muscle in this condition has reduced neuronal nitric oxide synthase in the enteric nerve plexus that controls this muscle. Which of the following identifies this plexus and its location?","options":["Myenteric plexus within the muscularis externa","Submucosal plexus within the submucosa","Submucosal plexus within the muscularis externa","Myenteric plexus within the submucosa"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the thickened layer: circular smooth muscle, part of the muscularis externa.","Recall which plexus sits between the circular and longitudinal muscle.","That is the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus, which controls motility."],"why":"The gut wall has four layers: mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia. The submucosal (Meissner) plexus lies in the submucosa and regulates secretion and blood flow. The myenteric (Auerbach) plexus lies between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa and controls motility. Its nitrergic neurons normally relax the pylorus, and reduced nitric oxide signaling there has been described in pyloric stenosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","The submucosal (Meissner) plexus lies in the submucosa and controls secretion and local blood flow. The thickened layer here is the muscle coat.","The submucosal plexus is not located in the muscularis externa.","The myenteric plexus does not sit in the submucosa. It lies between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers."],"study":{"topics":["Layers of the gut wall","Enteric nervous system"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-6"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C48","system":"GI","dx":"Biliary atresia","codes":["GI-13-H"],"case":"A 6-week-old girl is brought to the physician because of yellow discoloration of the skin and sclerae since 2 weeks of age and pale gray stools. She was born at term and is breastfed. She is gaining weight. The liver edge is firm and palpable 4 cm below the right costal margin. Serum total bilirubin is 8.4 mg/dL (0.1 to 1.0), with direct bilirubin of 6.9 mg/dL (under 0.3). A hepatobiliary scan shows uptake of tracer by the liver but no excretion into the intestine after 24 hours. An intraoperative cholangiogram shows obliterated extrahepatic bile ducts. Diagnosis: biliary atresia.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C48-Q1","stem":"Without surgical drainage of bile, which of the following is the most likely outcome for this infant?","options":["Resolution of jaundice once breastfeeding is stopped","Biliary cirrhosis within the first years of life","Spontaneous reopening of the bile ducts by age 1 year","Kernicterus from bilirubin deposition in basal ganglia"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-H","obj":"GI-13-H.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize biliary atresia as progressive inflammatory obliteration of the extrahepatic ducts.","Bile backs up into the liver, injuring ducts and hepatocytes.","Ongoing injury causes fibrosis and biliary cirrhosis."],"why":"Biliary atresia is a progressive, inflammatory destruction of the extrahepatic bile ducts in the first weeks of life. Bile cannot leave the liver, so conjugated bilirubin rises, stools lose their color, and bile acids injure the liver. Portal fibrosis and bile duct proliferation progress to biliary cirrhosis and portal hypertension, often within the first 1 to 2 years without treatment. A Kasai procedure works best when done early, and biliary atresia is the most common reason for liver transplantation in children.","whyNot":["Breast milk jaundice is an unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia in a well infant with normal stools. It does not cause conjugated jaundice with acholic stools.","Correct.","The obliteration in biliary atresia is progressive and does not reverse. Without a hepatoportoenterostomy (Kasai procedure) or transplant, the damage continues.","Kernicterus is caused by unconjugated bilirubin, which is lipid soluble and crosses the blood-brain barrier. Her bilirubin is mostly conjugated."],"study":{"topics":["Biliary atresia","Neonatal conjugated hyperbilirubinemia"],"comps":["GI-13-H","GI-13-C1"]}},{"id":"GI-C48-Q2","stem":"The obliterated ducts develop from which of the following embryonic structures?","options":["Dorsal pancreatic bud of the foregut endoderm","Septum transversum splanchnic mesoderm","Hepatic diverticulum of foregut endoderm","Vitelline duct of the midgut endoderm"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-1","obj":"GI-1.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the liver and biliary tree bud from the foregut in week 4.","The hepatic diverticulum gives liver cords and, through its stalk, the bile ducts.","Its cystic part forms the gallbladder and cystic duct."],"why":"In week 4, the hepatic diverticulum grows from the endoderm of the distal foregut into the septum transversum. Its cranial part forms hepatocytes and intrahepatic ducts, while its caudal cystic part forms the gallbladder and cystic duct. The stalk connecting it to the duodenum becomes the common bile duct. The ventral pancreatic bud also arises near this stalk and rotates behind the duodenum, which explains annular pancreas when it migrates abnormally.","whyNot":["The dorsal pancreatic bud forms most of the pancreas, including the accessory duct. The bile ducts arise from the hepatic diverticulum.","The septum transversum gives rise to the connective tissue of the liver and the central tendon of the diaphragm. It does not form duct epithelium.","Correct.","The vitelline duct connects the midgut to the yolk sac and normally disappears. A remnant forms a Meckel diverticulum."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatobiliary embryology","Pancreatic development"],"comps":["GI-1","GI-13-H"]}},{"id":"GI-C48-Q3","stem":"Which of the following urine findings is most likely in this infant?","options":["Bilirubin present, urobilinogen absent","Bilirubin absent, urobilinogen increased","Bilirubin present, urobilinogen increased","Bilirubin absent, urobilinogen absent"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Note the conjugated hyperbilirubinemia: conjugated bilirubin is water soluble and passes into urine.","Note the acholic stools: no bile reaches the gut.","Without bilirubin in the gut, bacteria make no urobilinogen to reabsorb and excrete."],"why":"Conjugated bilirubin is water soluble and loosely bound to albumin, so when it builds up in blood it is filtered into the urine, making it dark. Urobilinogen is made by gut bacteria from bilirubin that reaches the intestine, and some of it is reabsorbed and excreted in urine. With complete obstruction, no bilirubin reaches the gut, so urobilinogen disappears from urine and the stools turn pale. Hemolysis shows the opposite pattern: unconjugated bilirubin that cannot enter urine, and high urine urobilinogen.","whyNot":["Correct.","Absent urine bilirubin with high urobilinogen is the pattern of hemolysis. The excess bilirubin is unconjugated, which cannot pass into urine.","Bilirubin and urobilinogen can both be raised in some hepatocellular injury, but urobilinogen needs bile to reach the gut. Her ducts are blocked.","Her conjugated bilirubin is high and water soluble, so it spills into the urine. Urine bilirubin cannot be absent."],"study":{"topics":["Bilirubin metabolism","Types of jaundice"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C1"]}},{"id":"GI-C48-Q4","stem":"Surgery is delayed. At 9 weeks of age, she bleeds for a long time from a heel-stick site. Prothrombin time is 24 seconds (11 to 14) and corrects within 24 hours after a parenteral dose of vitamin K. Which of the following best explains the prolonged prothrombin time?","options":["Consumption of clotting factors in widespread microthrombi","An inhibitory antibody against coagulation factor VIII","Poor vitamin K absorption without bile acid micelles","Reduced hepatic synthesis of clotting factor proteins"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.2","dok":3,"approach":["Note the fix: parenteral vitamin K corrects the prothrombin time.","Recall that vitamin K is fat soluble and needs bile acid micelles to be absorbed.","Without bile in the gut, vitamin K deficiency develops, and factors II, VII, IX, and X are not carboxylated."],"why":"Vitamins A, D, E, and K are fat soluble and are absorbed within bile acid micelles. With no bile reaching the gut, they are poorly absorbed. Vitamin K is needed for gamma-carboxylation of factors II, VII, IX, and X and proteins C and S, so deficiency prolongs the prothrombin time and causes bleeding. Parenteral vitamin K bypasses the gut, which is why the clotting test corrects. Over time she is also at risk for night blindness, rickets, and vitamin E neuropathy and hemolysis.","whyNot":["Disseminated intravascular coagulation uses up factors and platelets, usually in a severely ill patient. Vitamin K would not correct it.","A factor VIII inhibitor prolongs the partial thromboplastin time and leaves the prothrombin time normal. Vitamin K does not affect it.","Correct.","Hepatocyte failure lowers clotting factor production, but that does not correct with vitamin K. The quick correction shows the liver can still make the factors."],"study":{"topics":["Fat-soluble vitamin deficiency","Vitamin K and clotting factors"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-7"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C49","system":"GI","dx":"Omphalocele with cleft lip and palate in trisomy 13","codes":["GI-13-H"],"case":"A newborn boy is examined shortly after birth at 38 weeks' gestation. Prenatal ultrasonography showed an abdominal wall defect and a single fused forebrain consistent with holoprosencephaly. He has a midline abdominal wall defect about 5 cm wide at the base of the umbilical cord, through which loops of small bowel and part of the liver protrude. He also has a bilateral cleft lip with a cleft palate, small eyes, and an extra digit on each hand. Karyotype is 47,XY,+13. Diagnosis: omphalocele with cleft lip and palate in a newborn with trisomy 13.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C49-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes his abdominal wall defect, as compared with gastroschisis?","options":["Viscera covered by intact skin protruding through a weak umbilical ring","Uncovered viscera passing through a full-thickness defect right of the cord","Exposed bladder mucosa through a defect in the lower abdominal wall","Viscera in a sac of peritoneum and amnion, with the cord on the sac"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-13-H","obj":"GI-13-H.6","dok":3,"approach":["Note the defect is midline at the base of the cord and contains liver.","Recall that an omphalocele keeps a membrane covering, while gastroschisis does not.","Link the trisomy to omphalocele, which is often associated with chromosomal anomalies."],"why":"An omphalocele is a midline defect at the umbilical ring through which viscera protrude inside a sac of peritoneum and amnion, with the umbilical cord inserting on the sac. It is often linked to trisomy 13 or 18, Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, and heart defects. Gastroschisis is a defect beside the cord, usually on the right, with uncovered bowel that floats in amniotic fluid and becomes thick and matted. Gastroschisis is rarely associated with other anomalies, but it carries a risk of intestinal atresia.","whyNot":["A skin-covered bulge at the umbilicus is an umbilical hernia, common in infants and usually closing by itself.","Uncovered bowel beside a normally inserted cord, usually to the right, is gastroschisis. It is seldom linked to trisomies.","Exposed bladder mucosa in the lower abdominal wall is bladder exstrophy, a defect of the infraumbilical body wall and cloacal membrane.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Omphalocele versus gastroschisis","Abdominal wall defects"],"comps":["GI-13-H","GI-1"]}},{"id":"GI-C49-Q2","stem":"He has trouble feeding, and milk comes out of his nose when he tries to suck. Failure of fusion of which of the following structures most directly explains this problem?","options":["Two medial nasal prominences","Lateral palatine shelves","Maxillary and mandibular prominences","Maxillary and medial nasal prominences"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-H","obj":"GI-13-H.7","dok":3,"approach":["Note the symptom: milk passes from mouth to nose, so the oral and nasal cavities connect.","The roof of the mouth is the secondary palate.","The secondary palate forms from fusion of the two lateral palatine shelves."],"why":"The upper lip forms when each maxillary prominence fuses with the merged medial nasal prominences; failure gives cleft lip. The secondary palate forms when the lateral palatine shelves grow up above the tongue and fuse with each other and the nasal septum; failure gives cleft palate. With a cleft palate, the infant cannot seal the oral cavity to make suction, and milk passes into the nose. Cleft lip and palate are common in trisomy 13, often with holoprosencephaly.","whyNot":["The two medial nasal prominences fuse to form the intermaxillary segment, including the philtrum and primary palate. A failure here causes a median cleft lip, which is rare and does not explain nasal regurgitation.","Correct.","Failure of the maxillary and mandibular prominences to merge widens the mouth (macrostomia). It does not create an opening into the nose.","Failure of the maxillary and medial nasal prominences to fuse produces cleft lip. A cleft lip alone does not open the mouth into the nasal cavity behind the gums."],"study":{"topics":["Cleft lip versus cleft palate","Palate development"],"comps":["GI-13-H","GI-1"]}},{"id":"GI-C49-Q3","stem":"Failure of which of the following normal developmental events most directly explains the bowel loops within the defect?","options":["Return of the herniated midgut loop","Recanalization of the intestinal lumen","Obliteration of the vitelline duct","Rotation of the midgut around the superior mesenteric artery"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-1","obj":"GI-1.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the midgut normally herniates into the umbilical cord in week 6.","It rotates and normally returns to the abdomen by week 10.","If it fails to return, the bowel stays in a sac at the umbilicus: an omphalocele."],"why":"In week 6, the midgut grows faster than the abdominal cavity and herniates into the umbilical cord. While outside, it rotates 90 degrees counterclockwise around the superior mesenteric artery, and it completes 270 degrees as it returns to the abdomen around week 10. If the bowel does not return, it remains outside within the cord covering, forming an omphalocele. A large omphalocele that contains liver also reflects failure of the lateral body folds to close the abdominal wall. Malrotation often coexists.","whyNot":["Correct.","Failed recanalization causes atresia or stenosis of the gut lumen. It does not move bowel outside the abdomen.","A persistent vitelline duct remnant causes a Meckel diverticulum or an umbilical fistula draining ileal contents. It does not leave bowel loops outside the abdominal wall.","Failed or incomplete rotation is malrotation, which leaves the bowel inside the abdomen in an abnormal position with Ladd bands and a risk of volvulus."],"study":{"topics":["Physiologic midgut herniation","Midgut rotation and malrotation"],"comps":["GI-1","GI-13-H"]}},{"id":"GI-C49-Q4","stem":"After repair of his defects, a swallow study is performed. As he swallows, the lower esophageal sphincter relaxes before the bolus arrives. Which of the following mediates this relaxation?","options":["Vagal neurons releasing nitric oxide and VIP","Vagal neurons releasing acetylcholine onto muscarinic receptors","Sympathetic neurons releasing norepinephrine","Circulating gastrin from antral G cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note that the sphincter opens before the bolus arrives, so the signal must be neural.","Recall that the vagus carries both excitatory and inhibitory fibers to the sphincter.","Inhibitory fibers act through myenteric neurons that release nitric oxide and VIP."],"why":"The lower esophageal sphincter is tonically contracted at rest to block reflux. When a swallow begins, the medullary swallowing center sends signals down the vagus to inhibitory myenteric neurons. These release nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide, which relax the sphincter and the proximal stomach before the bolus arrives. In achalasia, these inhibitory neurons are lost, so the sphincter fails to relax.","whyNot":["Correct.","Acetylcholine acting on muscarinic receptors contracts the sphincter muscle and maintains its tone between swallows.","Sympathetic input has only a minor role in the lower esophageal sphincter and does not produce swallow-induced relaxation.","Gastrin raises lower esophageal sphincter tone if anything. It does not relax the sphincter during a swallow."],"study":{"topics":["Lower esophageal sphincter control","Receptive relaxation"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-F1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C50","system":"GI","dx":"Acute mesenteric ischemia from an atrial fibrillation embolus","codes":["GI-13-J3"],"case":"A 76-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 4 hours after the sudden onset of severe abdominal pain around the umbilicus, followed by one episode of vomiting and a loose stool. She has atrial fibrillation and stopped taking her anticoagulant 1 month ago. Temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 118/min and irregularly irregular, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 142/84 mm Hg. She is writhing in pain, but the abdomen is soft and only mildly tender without guarding. Serum lactate is 5.6 mmol/L (0.5 to 2.2). CT angiography shows an embolus in the superior mesenteric artery distal to its origin. Diagnosis: acute mesenteric ischemia from an embolus.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C50-Q1","stem":"Which of the following histories, if present instead, would most suggest chronic mesenteric ischemia?","options":["Crampy lower abdominal pain relieved by defecation","Painful bloody diarrhea after an episode of shock","Sudden severe pain beginning shortly after cardioversion","Pain after meals, fear of eating, and weight loss"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-J3","obj":"GI-13-J.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that chronic mesenteric ischemia comes from atherosclerosis of several mesenteric arteries.","Flow is enough at rest but not after meals, when gut demand rises.","Look for pain after eating, food fear, and weight loss: intestinal angina."],"why":"Chronic mesenteric ischemia is usually caused by atherosclerotic narrowing of two or more of the celiac trunk, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric arteries. After a meal, blood flow to the gut must rise, and when it cannot, the patient has pain 15 to 60 minutes after eating. Patients learn to avoid food and lose weight. Acute mesenteric ischemia, as in this patient, is most often an embolus from atrial fibrillation to the superior mesenteric artery, with severe pain out of proportion to the exam and a rising lactate.","whyNot":["Crampy pain relieved by defecation, with no alarm features, suggests irritable bowel syndrome, a functional disorder without ischemia.","Painful bloody diarrhea after low blood pressure suggests ischemic colitis at a watershed area of the colon, such as the splenic flexure.","Sudden pain soon after cardioversion suggests an embolus dislodged from the left atrium, which is acute mesenteric ischemia like hers.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Acute versus chronic mesenteric ischemia","Ischemic colitis"],"comps":["GI-13-J3","GI-5"]}},{"id":"GI-C50-Q2","stem":"At laparotomy, the segments supplied by the occluded artery are dusky. Which of the following segments is most likely to remain well perfused?","options":["Cecum and appendix","Terminal ileum","Descending colon","Ascending colon"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the occluded vessel: the superior mesenteric artery.","Recall its territory: distal duodenum to the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon.","Choose the segment in the inferior mesenteric territory."],"why":"The superior mesenteric artery supplies the midgut, from the distal duodenum through the jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and about the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon. The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the hindgut, from the distal transverse colon to the upper rectum, including the descending colon. Emboli often lodge beyond the first jejunal or middle colic branches, so the proximal jejunum may be spared. The marginal artery links the two territories, but its weakest points at the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction are prone to ischemic colitis after hypotension.","whyNot":["The cecum and appendix are supplied by the ileocolic artery, a branch of the superior mesenteric artery.","The terminal ileum is supplied by the ileocolic and ileal branches of the superior mesenteric artery. It is at high risk.","Correct.","The ascending colon is supplied by the right colic and ileocolic branches of the superior mesenteric artery."],"study":{"topics":["Mesenteric arterial territories","Watershed areas of the colon"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-1"]}},{"id":"GI-C50-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her pain was first felt around the umbilicus?","options":["Foregut visceral afferents enter the cord at T10, the segment of umbilical skin","Midgut visceral afferents enter the cord at T10, the segment of umbilical skin","Hindgut visceral afferents enter the cord at T10, the segment of umbilical skin","Intercostal nerves in the parietal peritoneum near the umbilicus are irritated"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-1","obj":"GI-1.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the ischemic bowel as a midgut derivative.","Recall that visceral pain is poorly localized and referred by embryologic origin.","Midgut pain is referred to the periumbilical region, the T10 dermatome."],"why":"Visceral pain fibers from the gut travel with sympathetic nerves and enter the spinal cord at the segments that supplied the gut tube in the embryo. The brain cannot localize this input well and refers it to the skin served by the same segments. Foregut pain is felt in the epigastrium, midgut pain around the umbilicus at about T10, and hindgut pain in the suprapubic region. Pain becomes sharp and localized only when the parietal peritoneum is inflamed, which is why early mesenteric ischemia causes severe pain with a soft abdomen.","whyNot":["Foregut visceral pain, as from the stomach or duodenum, enters higher thoracic segments and is referred to the epigastrium.","Correct.","Hindgut visceral pain, as from the sigmoid colon, enters lower segments and is referred to the suprapubic region.","Irritation of the parietal peritoneum gives sharp, well-localized pain over the diseased organ with guarding. Her soft abdomen shows the peritoneum is not yet involved."],"study":{"topics":["Referred visceral pain","Foregut, midgut, and hindgut"],"comps":["GI-1","GI-5"]}},{"id":"GI-C50-Q4","stem":"She undergoes embolectomy and resection of a short ileal segment and has no caloric intake for the next 3 days. On the third day, which of the following is the main source of her blood glucose?","options":["Hepatic conversion of circulating ketone bodies into new glucose","Hepatic glycogenolysis releasing glucose from stored glycogen","Muscle glycogenolysis releasing free glucose into the blood","Hepatic gluconeogenesis from amino acids, lactate, and glycerol"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.5","dok":2,"approach":["Note the timing: 3 days without food.","Liver glycogen lasts about a day, so after that the liver must make new glucose.","Gluconeogenesis uses alanine and other amino acids from muscle, lactate, and glycerol from fat."],"why":"In the first hours of fasting, the liver keeps blood glucose up by breaking down its glycogen. Those stores are largely gone after about 24 to 48 hours. The liver then relies on gluconeogenesis, using alanine and other amino acids from muscle protein, lactate from red cells and muscle (the Cori cycle), and glycerol released from fat. At the same time, fatty acid oxidation feeds hepatic ketogenesis. The liver exports ketones to the brain and muscle but cannot use them, because it lacks the enzyme succinyl-CoA:acetoacetate CoA transferase (thiophorase).","whyNot":["Ketone bodies come from acetyl-CoA and cannot be converted into glucose. The brain uses them in place of glucose as fasting continues.","Liver glycogen is the main source in the first 12 to 24 hours of fasting, but it is largely used up within about 1 to 2 days.","Muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase, so its glycogen supplies glucose only to the muscle itself. It cannot release free glucose into the blood.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Fasting metabolism","Gluconeogenesis substrates"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-9"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C51","system":"GI","dx":"Necrotizing enterocolitis in a premature infant","codes":["GI-13-J3"],"case":"A 12-day-old boy born at 29 weeks' gestation is evaluated in the neonatal intensive care unit because of feeding intolerance, abdominal distension, and bloody stools. He has been fed a cow's milk-based preterm formula since day 4 of life. Temperature is 36.0 °C (96.8 °F), pulse is 182/min, respirations are 64/min, and blood pressure is 50/30 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a distended, tender abdomen with erythema of the abdominal wall and absent bowel sounds. Gastric aspirates are bilious. Laboratory studies show a platelet count of 82,000/mm3 (150,000 to 450,000) and an arterial pH of 7.24 (7.35 to 7.45). Diagnosis: necrotizing enterocolitis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C51-Q1","stem":"An abdominal radiograph is obtained. Which of the following radiographic findings is most likely?","options":["Linear and bubbly gas within the bowel wall","Gas confined to a dilated stomach and duodenal bulb","Scattered calcifications along the peritoneal surfaces","Dilated loops with a bubbly mass in the right lower quadrant"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-J3","obj":"GI-13-J.5","dok":2,"approach":["Name the core process: ischemic injury of immature mucosa followed by bacterial invasion.","Ask what invading bacteria produce inside damaged bowel: gas from fermentation.","Pick the finding that shows gas inside the bowel wall, called pneumatosis intestinalis."],"why":"Necrotizing enterocolitis starts with mucosal injury in the immature bowel of a premature infant, aided by reduced blood flow, an immature barrier, and formula feeding. Bacteria cross the damaged mucosa and ferment luminal substrate, producing hydrogen gas that tracks into the submucosa and subserosa. On radiograph this gas appears as linear or bubbly lucencies in the bowel wall, called pneumatosis intestinalis, the hallmark finding. Gas can also enter the portal venous system, and free air means the bowel has perforated.","whyNot":["Correct.","This double bubble sign points to duodenal atresia, which presents within the first day of life with bilious vomiting and is linked to Down syndrome.","Peritoneal calcifications suggest meconium peritonitis from a bowel perforation that happened before birth.","A bubbly right lower quadrant mass of thick meconium with dilated proximal loops suggests meconium ileus, an early sign of cystic fibrosis seen in the first days of life."],"study":{"topics":["Necrotizing enterocolitis","Pneumatosis intestinalis"],"comps":["GI-13-J3","GI-13-J"]}},{"id":"GI-C51-Q2","stem":"His mother was unable to provide breast milk. Human milk lowers the risk of this disease, partly because it contains secretory IgA directed against microbes from the mother's own intestine. In which of the following sites were the B cells that produce this milk IgA most likely first activated?","options":["Germinal centers in the mother's splenic white pulp","Axillary lymph nodes that drain the mother's breast","Mesenteric lymph nodes of the infant","Peyer patches in the mother's ileum"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.1","dok":3,"approach":["Note that the milk IgA targets the mother's gut microbes, so the B cells met those antigens in her gut.","Recall how gut antigens are sampled: M cells over Peyer patches pass them to underlying lymphoid follicles.","Link this to homing of IgA-committed B cells from the gut to other mucosal sites, including the lactating breast."],"why":"M cells in the epithelium over Peyer patches take up luminal antigens and pass them to dendritic cells and B cells in the follicles below. Activated B cells switch to IgA and gain homing receptors that send them to mucosal tissues, including the lactating mammary gland, which is often called the enteromammary link. Plasma cells in the breast then secrete dimeric IgA that the polymeric Ig receptor carries into milk. The infant receives antibody aimed at exactly the microbes he is likely to meet from his mother, which helps protect the immature gut.","whyNot":["The spleen mounts systemic responses to blood-borne antigens, mainly IgM and IgG, and these B cells do not home to the breast.","Axillary nodes drain lymph from breast tissue, but the IgA plasma cells in milk-producing tissue are mostly gut-primed cells that homed there.","A premature infant makes very little IgA of his own, and milk antibodies come from the mother.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Gut mucosal immunity and M cells","Secretory IgA in breast milk"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-J3"]}},{"id":"GI-C51-Q3","stem":"He undergoes resection of a necrotic segment of ileum. Microscopy shows mucosal necrosis and gas-filled cysts in the layer between the muscularis mucosae and the muscularis externa. Which of the following neural structures normally lies within this layer?","options":["Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus","Submucosal (Meissner) plexus","Prevertebral sympathetic ganglion cells","Parasympathetic preganglionic cell bodies"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the layer: the tissue between the muscularis mucosae and the muscularis externa is the submucosa.","Recall which enteric plexus lies in the submucosa and which lies within the muscle coat.","Exclude neurons whose cell bodies lie outside the gut wall."],"why":"The gut wall has four layers: mucosa (with its thin muscularis mucosae), submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia. The submucosa holds large vessels, lymphatics, and the submucosal (Meissner) plexus, which mainly controls secretion and local blood flow. In necrotizing enterocolitis, gas cysts often collect in the submucosa and subserosa. If necrosis extends through the muscularis externa and serosa, the bowel perforates.","whyNot":["The myenteric plexus lies between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa and mainly controls motility.","Correct.","Prevertebral ganglia, such as the celiac and superior mesenteric ganglia, sit outside the gut wall near the aorta. Only their postganglionic fibers reach the bowel.","Parasympathetic preganglionic cell bodies lie in the brainstem (vagal nuclei) and sacral spinal cord (S2 to S4). Their fibers synapse on enteric neurons in the wall."],"study":{"topics":["Layers of the gut wall","Enteric nervous system plexuses"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C51-Q4","stem":"Feeds are stopped for 10 days. In healthy people between meals, a cyclic wave of strong contractions sweeps undigested debris and bacteria from the stomach through the small intestine about every 90 minutes. Which of the following pairs this motor pattern with the hormone that triggers it?","options":["Mass movement, triggered by gastrin","Receptive relaxation, triggered by vasoactive intestinal peptide","Segmentation, triggered by cholecystokinin","Migrating motor complex, triggered by motilin"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Note the timing (fasting) and the purpose (clearing debris and bacteria from the stomach and small bowel).","Name the pattern: the migrating motor complex.","Recall the hormone that starts its strongest phase: motilin from M cells of the upper small intestine."],"why":"During fasting, the stomach and small intestine cycle through the migrating motor complex about every 90 to 120 minutes. Its phase III is a burst of strong peristaltic contractions that sweeps residue and bacteria toward the colon, acting as a housekeeper that limits bacterial overgrowth. Motilin, released from M cells of the duodenum and jejunum, rises in step with these bursts and triggers the gastric phase III. Eating interrupts the cycle and replaces it with the fed pattern.","whyNot":["Mass movements push colonic contents toward the rectum a few times a day, often after a meal as part of the gastrocolic reflex.","Receptive relaxation lets the proximal stomach accept a meal and is driven by vagal release of VIP and nitric oxide during swallowing.","Segmentation mixes chyme with digestive juices in the fed state. Cholecystokinin is released by fat and protein after a meal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Migrating motor complex","Motilin"],"comps":["GI-6"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C52","system":"GI","dx":"Gastric MALT lymphoma","codes":["GI-13-K2"],"case":"A 63-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of epigastric discomfort and early satiety. She has lost 3 kg (6.6 lb). Physical examination shows mild epigastric tenderness and no lymphadenopathy. Hemoglobin is 11.4 g/dL (12 to 16). Upper endoscopy shows thickened, nodular mucosa in the gastric antrum with a shallow ulcer. Biopsy shows chronic active gastritis with curved bacilli in the surface mucus and a dense infiltrate of small, atypical lymphocytes that invade and destroy gastric glands (lymphoepithelial lesions). The lymphoma cells are CD20 positive. CT shows no disease beyond the stomach. Diagnosis: gastric mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) lymphoma with Helicobacter pylori infection.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C52-Q1","stem":"Flow cytometry of the biopsy shows that the lymphoma cells are CD5 negative and CD10 negative. Which of the following best describes the cell of origin of this tumor?","options":["Interstitial cells of Cajal with a KIT mutation","Germinal center B cells with BCL2 overexpression","Marginal zone B cells of gastric lymphoid tissue","Mantle zone B cells with cyclin D1 overexpression"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-K2","obj":"GI-13-K.3","dok":2,"approach":["Use the markers: CD20 positive means a B cell tumor; CD5 negative and CD10 negative exclude mantle cell and follicular lymphoma.","Recall that chronic H. pylori gastritis creates lymphoid follicles in a stomach that normally has none.","Match the tumor to the B cells in the marginal zone around these acquired follicles."],"why":"The normal stomach has no organized lymphoid tissue. Chronic H. pylori infection induces lymphoid follicles, and MALT lymphoma arises from the marginal zone B cells around them, so it is an extranodal marginal zone B cell lymphoma. The cells are CD20 positive and typically CD5 and CD10 negative. Lymphoepithelial lesions, where tumor cells invade and destroy glands, are characteristic.","whyNot":["Gastrointestinal stromal tumor arises from interstitial cells of Cajal, is CD117 (KIT) positive, and is a spindle cell tumor without CD20.","Follicular lymphoma comes from germinal center B cells, is CD10 positive, and carries t(14;18), which overexpresses BCL2.","Correct.","Mantle cell lymphoma is CD5 positive and carries t(11;14), which overexpresses cyclin D1."],"study":{"topics":["MALT lymphoma","B cell lymphoma markers"],"comps":["GI-13-K2","GI-11"]}},{"id":"GI-C52-Q2","stem":"She receives a proton pump inhibitor, clarithromycin, and amoxicillin. Six months later, a urea breath test is negative and repeat biopsies show regression of the lymphoma. Which of the following best explains this response?","options":["Antibodies against the organism lyse tumor cells by complement","Growth depends on help from H. pylori-specific T cells","Clarithromycin is directly cytotoxic to the lymphoma cells","Lower gastric acid removes a growth signal from the B cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that early MALT lymphoma is still driven by the chronic antigen that started it.","Identify the source of the growth signal: CD4 T cells that recognize H. pylori antigens and help the B cells.","Predict that removing the antigen removes T cell help, so the tumor shrinks."],"why":"In early gastric MALT lymphoma, the tumor B cells still depend on stimulation from H. pylori-specific CD4 T cells, which supply CD40 ligand and cytokines. When the infection is cured, the antigen is gone, T cell help stops, and many tumors regress. Tumors that carry t(11;18), which joins API2 and MALT1, activate NF-kB on their own and usually do not respond to eradication alone.","whyNot":["Antibodies to H. pylori bind the bacteria. The tumor B cells do not display H. pylori antigens for these antibodies to target.","Correct.","Clarithromycin is given to kill H. pylori. It has no meaningful direct effect on lymphoma cells.","Acid suppression helps the antibiotics work and heals the ulcer, but acid does not drive B cell growth."],"study":{"topics":["H. pylori and MALT lymphoma","T cell help for B cells"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-K2"]}},{"id":"GI-C52-Q3","stem":"H. pylori infection also raises her risk of gastric adenocarcinoma. Which of the following sequences most likely precedes the H. pylori-associated type of this cancer?","options":["Foveolar hyperplasia with giant rugal folds and protein loss","Reflux esophagitis, then columnar metaplasia at the cardia","Atrophic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia","Germline CDH1 loss, then signet ring cells infiltrating singly"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K2","obj":"GI-13-K.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the two Lauren types of gastric adenocarcinoma: intestinal and diffuse.","Link chronic H. pylori gastritis to the intestinal type.","Trace the stepwise path of mucosal injury that leads to gland-forming cancer."],"why":"Intestinal-type gastric adenocarcinoma follows years of chronic injury. H. pylori gastritis, sometimes with dietary nitrosamines, causes atrophy of the glands, then intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells, then dysplasia, and finally an invasive carcinoma that forms glands. The diffuse type has a weaker link to environmental injury and grows as single signet ring cells that thicken the wall.","whyNot":["Giant rugal folds with protein loss and foveolar hyperplasia describe Menetrier disease, a hypertrophic gastropathy that is not the usual path to H. pylori-related cancer.","Reflux injury and columnar metaplasia lead to Barrett esophagus and adenocarcinoma of the distal esophagus and gastroesophageal junction. H. pylori is not a risk factor for these.","Correct.","Loss of E-cadherin (CDH1) causes diffuse-type carcinoma, with signet ring cells and a rigid linitis plastica stomach. Germline CDH1 mutations cause hereditary diffuse gastric cancer."],"study":{"topics":["Intestinal vs diffuse gastric adenocarcinoma","Atrophic gastritis and intestinal metaplasia"],"comps":["GI-13-K2","GI-13-G7"]}},{"id":"GI-C52-Q4","stem":"Her gastritis is most severe in the antrum. Another patient has autoimmune gastritis that destroys the mucosa of the gastric body and fundus but spares the antrum. Which of the following secretion changes is most likely in that patient?","options":["Low serum gastrin, high acid, normal intrinsic factor","Low pepsinogen, normal acid, low serum gastrin","Low acid and intrinsic factor, high serum gastrin","Low serum gastrin and acid, normal intrinsic factor"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.3","dok":3,"approach":["Map the cells: body and fundus glands contain parietal cells (HCl and intrinsic factor) and chief cells (pepsinogen); G cells live in the antrum.","Predict loss of acid and intrinsic factor when body and fundus mucosa is destroyed.","Apply feedback: with no acid, antral D cells release less somatostatin, so the spared G cells oversecrete gastrin."],"why":"Autoimmune gastritis targets parietal cells and intrinsic factor in the body and fundus. Losing parietal cells removes HCl and intrinsic factor, which leads to achlorhydria and, over time, vitamin B12 deficiency (pernicious anemia). Chief cells are also lost, so pepsinogen I falls. The antrum is spared, and without acid to trigger somatostatin, G cells release large amounts of gastrin. In her antral-predominant H. pylori gastritis, by contrast, acid output is often normal or high.","whyNot":["High acid with low gastrin does not fit loss of parietal cells. With no parietal cells, acid falls.","Pepsinogen does fall, but parietal cells are destroyed along with chief cells, so acid cannot stay normal, and gastrin rises.","Correct.","Acid falls, but gastrin rises because acid feedback is lost, and intrinsic factor falls with the parietal cells."],"study":{"topics":["Gastric gland cell types","Autoimmune gastritis"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-13-G7"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C53","system":"GI","dx":"Small intestinal carcinoid tumor with carcinoid syndrome","codes":["GI-13-K3"],"case":"A 59-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of episodes of facial flushing and watery diarrhea several times daily. He also has intermittent wheezing. Physical examination shows a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration, and a firm, nodular, enlarged liver. A 24-hour urine 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA) level is 96 mg (2 to 8). CT shows a 2-cm mass in the distal ileum, enlarged mesenteric lymph nodes, and multiple liver masses. Biopsy of a liver mass shows nests of uniform cells that stain for chromogranin A and synaptophysin. Diagnosis: metastatic well-differentiated neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumor of the ileum with carcinoid syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C53-Q1","stem":"Echocardiography shows thickened, fixed tricuspid and pulmonic valve leaflets, while the mitral and aortic valves are normal. Which of the following best explains why the left-sided valves are spared?","options":["Pulmonary monoamine oxidase inactivates serotonin","Higher left-sided pressures wash away fibrous plaques","Hepatic metastases release serotonin into the bile","Left-sided endocardium lacks serotonin receptors"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K3","obj":"GI-13-K.5","dok":3,"approach":["Trace tumor products from liver metastases: hepatic veins, inferior vena cava, right heart.","Ask what happens to serotonin as blood passes through the lungs.","Conclude that only right-sided valves see high serotonin levels."],"why":"Liver metastases release serotonin and other vasoactive products directly into the hepatic veins, so they reach the right heart without first-pass clearance. Serotonin stimulates fibroblasts to lay down plaque-like fibrous tissue on the tricuspid and pulmonic valves, causing regurgitation and stenosis. The lungs contain monoamine oxidase and clear much of the serotonin before blood reaches the left heart, so the mitral and aortic valves are usually spared. Left-sided lesions appear with a right-to-left shunt, such as a patent foramen ovale, or with a bronchial carcinoid.","whyNot":["Correct.","Valve plaques reflect exposure to tumor products. Pressure differences do not remove them.","Serotonin from liver metastases enters hepatic venous blood. Biliary release would not explain the valve pattern.","Both sides of the heart have similar tissue. The difference is how much serotonin each side receives."],"study":{"topics":["Carcinoid heart disease","Serotonin metabolism"],"comps":["GI-13-K3","CV-18-D6"]}},{"id":"GI-C53-Q2","stem":"Before the tumor spread to the liver, he had no flushing, although the ileal tumor was already secreting serotonin. Which of the following venous pathways carried this serotonin to the site where it was inactivated?","options":["Ileal veins, inferior mesenteric vein, left renal vein, inferior vena cava","Ileal veins, superior mesenteric vein, portal vein, sinusoids","Ileal veins, right gonadal vein, inferior vena cava","Ileal veins, splenic vein, hepatic veins, then portal vein"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify that ileal venous blood drains to the liver before reaching the systemic circulation.","Trace the vessels: superior mesenteric vein, joining the splenic vein behind the neck of the pancreas to form the portal vein.","Recall that hepatocytes inactivate serotonin as portal blood passes through the sinusoids."],"why":"Veins of the ileum drain into the superior mesenteric vein. Behind the neck of the pancreas the superior mesenteric vein joins the splenic vein to form the hepatic portal vein, which enters the liver and branches into sinusoids. Hepatocytes break down serotonin with monoamine oxidase, so little reaches the central veins, hepatic veins, and inferior vena cava. Once tumor grows in the liver, its products enter the hepatic veins directly and bypass this first-pass clearance, which is why the syndrome usually appears after liver metastasis.","whyNot":["The inferior mesenteric vein drains the descending colon, sigmoid colon, and upper rectum and usually joins the splenic vein. It does not drain to the left renal vein, and the ileum does not drain into it.","Correct.","The gonadal veins drain the testes or ovaries. The gut drains through the portal system.","The ileum does not drain to the splenic vein, and blood flows from the portal vein into the liver and out through the hepatic veins, in that order."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatic portal system","First-pass hepatic clearance"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-13-K3"]}},{"id":"GI-C53-Q3","stem":"He also develops a scaly, hyperpigmented rash on the sun-exposed skin of his neck and forearms, a red, swollen tongue, and difficulty concentrating. Deficiency of which of the following cofactors best explains these new findings?","options":["NAD+ and NADP+","Ascorbate","Thiamine pyrophosphate","Biotin"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize pellagra: photosensitive dermatitis, diarrhea, glossitis, and mental changes.","Recall that tryptophan is a precursor of both serotonin and niacin.","Link niacin to its cofactor forms, NAD+ and NADP+."],"why":"Carcinoid tumors can divert a large share of dietary tryptophan into serotonin. Less tryptophan is left to make niacin, so niacin and its cofactors NAD+ and NADP+ fall. Many dehydrogenases in energy metabolism need these electron carriers, and tissues with high turnover, such as skin, gut, and brain, show the deficiency first. The result is pellagra: dermatitis on sun-exposed skin, diarrhea, glossitis, and confusion or dementia.","whyNot":["Correct.","Ascorbate deficiency causes scurvy, with bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhages, and poor wound healing from impaired collagen hydroxylation.","Thiamine pyrophosphate deficiency causes Wernicke encephalopathy and beriberi (neuropathy or heart failure), without a photosensitive rash.","Biotin deficiency, seen with heavy raw egg white intake, causes a scaly rash around the face, hair loss, and lethargy, without a sun-exposed pattern."],"study":{"topics":["Pellagra and niacin","Tryptophan metabolism"],"comps":["GI-8"]}},{"id":"GI-C53-Q4","stem":"He is kept fasting for 48 hours, and the large-volume watery diarrhea continues. Stool sodium is 95 mEq/L, stool potassium is 45 mEq/L, and measured stool osmolality is 290 mOsm/kg. Which of the following best classifies his diarrhea?","options":["Osmotic, with a stool osmotic gap of about 10 mOsm/kg","Osmotic, with a stool osmotic gap of about 150 mOsm/kg","Secretory, with a stool osmotic gap of about 150 mOsm/kg","Secretory, with a stool osmotic gap of about 10 mOsm/kg"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.6","dok":3,"approach":["Calculate the gap: 290 minus 2 times (95 plus 45) equals 290 minus 280, or about 10 mOsm/kg.","Interpret it: a gap below about 50 means electrolytes account for the stool water, which fits secretory diarrhea.","Confirm with fasting: secretory diarrhea continues without food, while osmotic diarrhea stops."],"why":"The stool osmotic gap is 290 minus 2 times the sum of stool sodium and potassium. Here it is about 10 mOsm/kg. A small gap, below about 50, means the water in stool is held by secreted electrolytes, which is secretory diarrhea. A large gap, above about 100, means an unmeasured solute such as unabsorbed lactose is holding water, which is osmotic diarrhea. Serotonin and other tumor products drive intestinal secretion and speed motility, so his diarrhea persists during fasting.","whyNot":["The gap is small, which points away from an unabsorbed solute. Osmotic diarrhea also stops with fasting.","A gap of 150 with diarrhea that stops on fasting would fit osmotic diarrhea, such as lactose intolerance. His numbers do not give this gap, and his diarrhea continued.","His values give a gap of about 10, and a gap of 150 would mean an unmeasured solute was present.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Stool osmotic gap","Secretory vs osmotic diarrhea"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-13-K3"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C54","system":"GI","dx":"Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 with a pancreatic gastrinoma","codes":["GI-13-K3"],"case":"A 41-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 5-month history of burning epigastric pain, watery diarrhea, and loose, greasy stools. She has had two kidney stones, and her brother had a parathyroid adenoma removed. Upper endoscopy shows prominent, thickened gastric folds and multiple ulcers, including ulcers in the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum. Laboratory studies show a serum calcium of 11.4 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5) and a fasting serum gastrin of 1,250 pg/mL (less than 100), with a gastric pH of 1.5. CT shows a 2-cm mass in the head of the pancreas. Genetic testing shows a germline MEN1 mutation. Diagnosis: multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 with a pancreatic gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome).","questions":[{"id":"GI-C54-Q1","stem":"Before the CT, a secretin stimulation test was done to confirm the cause of her hypergastrinemia. Which of the following results is most likely?","options":["Serum gastrin falls by half","Serum gastrin falls below 100 pg/mL","Serum gastrin rises sharply","Serum gastrin does not change"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K3","obj":"GI-13-K.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that secretin normally inhibits gastrin release from antral G cells.","Recall that gastrinoma cells respond to secretin in the opposite way.","Predict a paradoxical rise in gastrin, which confirms a gastrinoma."],"why":"In healthy people, secretin suppresses gastrin release from G cells. Gastrinoma cells express secretin receptors that stimulate gastrin release, so intravenous secretin causes a sharp, paradoxical rise in serum gastrin, commonly defined as an increase of 120 pg/mL or more. Her low gastric pH with very high gastrin already argues against causes of high gastrin with low acid, such as atrophic gastritis or proton pump inhibitor use, and the secretin test confirms an autonomous tumor.","whyNot":["Secretin does not raise gastrin release from normal G cells, so a fall or no change is expected when high gastrin comes from normal G cells, as in atrophic gastritis or proton pump inhibitor use. A gastrinoma responds with a rise.","Normalization of gastrin would mean the gastrin source is suppressible normal tissue, which does not fit a tumor.","Correct.","An unchanged level would not support a gastrinoma. Tumor cells respond to secretin with a rise."],"study":{"topics":["Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","Secretin stimulation test"],"comps":["GI-13-K3","GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C54-Q2","stem":"In a healthy person, a meal that delivers acid and fat into the duodenum triggers hormones that slow gastric emptying, neutralize the acid, and contract the gallbladder. Which of the following hormone and source cell pairs are released in response?","options":["Motilin from M cells and GIP from K cells","Gastrin from G cells and motilin from M cells","Secretin from I cells and CCK from S cells","Secretin from S cells and CCK from I cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.4","dok":2,"approach":["Acid in the duodenum is the main stimulus for secretin, from S cells.","Fat and protein in the duodenum release cholecystokinin (CCK), from I cells.","Check the actions: secretin brings bicarbonate; CCK contracts the gallbladder, releases pancreatic enzymes, and slows gastric emptying."],"why":"Acid entering the duodenum releases secretin from S cells, which drives pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate secretion to neutralize the acid. Fatty acids and amino acids release cholecystokinin from I cells, which contracts the gallbladder, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion, and slows gastric emptying. Fat and glucose also release GIP from K cells, which boosts insulin release.","whyNot":["GIP does rise after fat, but motilin is a fasting hormone that drives the migrating motor complex and falls after a meal.","Gastrin rises during the gastric phase in response to peptides and vagal input, and acid in the gut suppresses it. Motilin acts in the fasting state.","These hormones are correct, but their source cells are reversed. S cells make secretin and I cells make CCK.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Gastrointestinal hormones","Duodenal phase of digestion"],"comps":["GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C54-Q3","stem":"The thickened gastric folds seen at endoscopy reflect the trophic effect of gastrin on the mucosa of the gastric body. Which of the following best describes the hyperplastic cell type responsible for her high acid output?","options":["Chief cells, which also secrete gastric lipase","Parietal cells, which also secrete intrinsic factor","Surface mucous cells, which also secrete bicarbonate","Antral G cells, which also secrete somatostatin"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the acid-secreting cell of the gastric body glands.","Recall the other product of that same cell.","Link gastrin's growth effect to an expanded parietal cell mass and thick rugal folds."],"why":"Gastrin acts on parietal cells directly and through histamine from enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells. Over time it also acts as a growth factor, expanding the parietal cell and ECL cell mass and thickening the folds of the body and fundus. Parietal cells secrete HCl and intrinsic factor, which binds vitamin B12 for absorption in the ileum. A larger parietal cell mass explains her very high acid output and her low gastric pH.","whyNot":["Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and gastric lipase. They do not secrete acid.","Correct.","Surface mucous cells secrete mucus and bicarbonate that protect the mucosa. They do not secrete acid.","Antral G cells secrete gastrin, while somatostatin comes from D cells. Her gastrin comes from the tumor, and G cells do not secrete acid."],"study":{"topics":["Gastric gland cell types","Trophic effects of gastrin"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C54-Q4","stem":"Her stools contain excess fat even though her pancreas secretes normal amounts of digestive enzymes. Which of the following best explains her steatorrhea?","options":["Low duodenal pH inactivates pancreatic lipase","Gastrin directly inhibits pancreatic lipase release","Bile salt synthesis by hepatocytes is suppressed","Excess CCK release depletes bile in the gallbladder"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that pancreatic lipase, with colipase and bile salts, digests triglycerides in the duodenum at a near-neutral pH.","Note that a gastrinoma delivers far more acid than pancreatic bicarbonate can neutralize.","Predict that acid inactivates lipase and impairs micelle formation, so fat goes unabsorbed."],"why":"Fat digestion depends on pancreatic lipase and colipase acting at the surface of fat droplets that bile salts have emulsified. These steps work at a near-neutral pH, which pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate normally provide. In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome the acid load overwhelms this buffering, so lipase is inactivated and bile acids precipitate and cannot form micelles. Acid injury to the small bowel mucosa adds to the malabsorption, and the large fluid load adds to the diarrhea.","whyNot":["Correct.","Gastrin does not block lipase release. Her pancreas releases normal amounts of enzyme.","Bile salt synthesis is normal. The problem is that bile acids precipitate in an acid duodenum.","CCK is released normally by fat. Gallbladder contraction delivers bile, and bile is not depleted in this condition."],"study":{"topics":["Fat digestion and pancreatic lipase","Steatorrhea in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-K3"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C55","system":"GI","dx":"Oral leukoplakia in a smoker with chronic periodontitis","codes":["GI-13-K5","GI-13-L4"],"case":"A 61-year-old man comes to the dentist because of a painless white patch on his tongue that he noticed 3 months ago. He has smoked 1.5 packs of cigarettes daily for 40 years and drinks 4 to 5 beers daily. Examination shows a 1.5-cm white, well-demarcated plaque on the right lateral border of the anterior two thirds of the tongue that does not rub off with gauze. His gingiva is red and swollen and bleeds on probing, with deep periodontal pockets and several loose molars. Dental radiographs show loss of alveolar bone around the molars. Diagnosis: oral leukoplakia and chronic periodontitis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C55-Q1","stem":"A biopsy of the tongue plaque is performed. Which of the following histologic findings would most strongly indicate a risk of progression to invasive squamous cell carcinoma?","options":["Budding yeast and pseudohyphae within keratin","Ballooned superficial cells with EBV inclusions","Thickened surface keratin with no atypia","Epithelial dysplasia with atypical mitoses"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K5","obj":"GI-13-K.8","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize leukoplakia as a clinical term for a white plaque that cannot be scraped off and has no other cause.","Recall that its cancer risk depends on what the biopsy shows.","Pick the finding that marks a precancerous change in the squamous epithelium."],"why":"Leukoplakia is a clinical description, and biopsy shows a range from simple hyperkeratosis to dysplasia to carcinoma. Dysplasia, with atypical nuclei, loss of normal maturation, and abnormal mitoses above the basal layer, is a precursor of invasive squamous cell carcinoma. Tobacco and alcohol act together as the main risks for oral squamous cell carcinoma, and lesions on the lateral tongue and floor of the mouth carry the highest risk.","whyNot":["Yeast and pseudohyphae mean candidiasis, whose white plaques scrape off. Candida can also colonize leukoplakia, but it is not the marker of cancer risk.","Ballooned cells with EBV point to oral hairy leukoplakia, a benign lesion of the lateral tongue in immunosuppressed patients such as those with HIV.","Hyperkeratosis without atypia is the most common biopsy result and carries a low risk of cancer.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Oral leukoplakia","Epithelial dysplasia"],"comps":["GI-13-K5","GI-13-K"]}},{"id":"GI-C55-Q2","stem":"Which of the following organisms and mechanisms best explains the destruction of tooth-supporting tissue in his gum disease?","options":["Candida albicans, by invading the epithelium with hyphae","Porphyromonas gingivalis, through inflammatory bone resorption","Streptococcus mutans, by fermenting sucrose to lactic acid","Actinomyces israelii, by forming sulfur granules in sinus tracts"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L4","obj":"GI-13-L.5","dok":3,"approach":["Separate the two plaque diseases: caries destroys tooth mineral, and periodontitis destroys the ligament and bone that hold the tooth.","Recall the key anaerobe of subgingival plaque in periodontitis.","Link its products to host inflammation and osteoclast activation."],"why":"Plaque at the gum line first causes gingivitis, which is reversible with good oral hygiene. When the subgingival biofilm shifts toward gram-negative anaerobes such as Porphyromonas gingivalis, the inflammation deepens. Bacterial proteases (gingipains) and lipopolysaccharide drive a host response with cytokines and RANKL that activates osteoclasts. The periodontal ligament and alveolar bone are lost, pockets deepen, and teeth loosen.","whyNot":["Candida causes oral thrush with white plaques that scrape off. It is not the driver of periodontal bone loss.","Correct.","Streptococcus mutans makes acid from sucrose that dissolves enamel and causes dental caries, which can lead to a tooth abscess. It does not destroy the periodontal ligament and bone.","Actinomyces israelii causes cervicofacial actinomycosis, a slowly spreading infection with draining sinus tracts and sulfur granules, often after dental work."],"study":{"topics":["Periodontitis","Porphyromonas gingivalis"],"comps":["GI-13-L4","GI-9"]}},{"id":"GI-C55-Q3","stem":"The lesion is excised. Afterward, he has loss of both general sensation and taste over the right anterior two thirds of the tongue, and tongue protrusion is normal. Injury to which of the following nerves best explains these deficits?","options":["Glossopharyngeal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Lingual nerve","Chorda tympani in the middle ear"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-4","obj":"GI-4.2","dok":3,"approach":["Map the anterior two thirds: general sensation by the lingual nerve (V3), taste by chorda tympani fibers (VII).","Recall that chorda tympani fibers join the lingual nerve in the infratemporal fossa and run with it to the tongue.","Pick the one nerve that carries both modalities at the site of surgery."],"why":"General sensation from the anterior two thirds of the tongue travels in the lingual nerve, a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3). Taste from the same area travels in the chorda tympani (facial nerve), whose fibers join the lingual nerve and run with it along the side of the tongue. Injury to the lingual nerve near the tongue therefore removes both touch and taste. Motor function is intact because the hypoglossal nerve supplies the tongue muscles.","whyNot":["The glossopharyngeal nerve carries both taste and general sensation from the posterior third of the tongue.","The hypoglossal nerve is motor. A lesion makes the tongue deviate toward the injured side on protrusion, without sensory loss.","Correct.","A chorda tympani lesion in the middle ear removes taste from the anterior two thirds and reduces submandibular and sublingual secretion, but general sensation is spared."],"study":{"topics":["Innervation of the tongue","Lingual nerve and chorda tympani"],"comps":["GI-4"]}},{"id":"GI-C55-Q4","stem":"His tobacco and alcohol use also increase his risk of squamous cell carcinoma at another site in the digestive tract that is lined by the same type of epithelium as the lateral tongue. Which of the following sites has this epithelium?","options":["Gastric cardia","Anal canal above the pectinate line","Duodenal bulb","Middle third of the esophagus"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the lining of the lateral tongue: nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium.","Find the gut segment with the same lining.","Link shared epithelium and shared carcinogen exposure to shared squamous cell carcinoma risk."],"why":"The lateral tongue, floor of mouth, and esophagus are lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium. Tobacco smoke and alcohol bathe this whole upper aerodigestive lining, so a patient with oral squamous lesions is also at higher risk for esophageal squamous cell carcinoma, which most often arises in the middle third. Below the gastroesophageal junction the lining changes to simple columnar epithelium, where adenocarcinomas arise instead.","whyNot":["The gastric cardia is lined by simple columnar epithelium. Cancers here are adenocarcinomas.","Above the pectinate line the anal canal has columnar epithelium. Stratified squamous epithelium lies below the line.","The duodenum is lined by simple columnar epithelium with villi and goblet cells.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Epithelium of the digestive tract","Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-13-K1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C56","system":"GI","dx":"Clostridioides difficile pseudomembranous colitis","codes":["GI-13-L1"],"case":"A 72-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 4 days of profuse watery diarrhea, about 10 stools daily, with lower abdominal cramps. Eight days ago she finished a course of clindamycin for a dental abscess. Temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. The abdomen is mildly distended and diffusely tender. Leukocyte count is 19,800/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000). A stool PCR assay is positive for the toxin B gene. Flexible sigmoidoscopy shows multiple raised yellow-white plaques on erythematous colonic mucosa. Diagnosis: Clostridioides difficile pseudomembranous colitis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C56-Q1","stem":"A biopsy of one of the plaques is taken. Which of the following best describes the composition of the plaques?","options":["Fibrin, mucin, and neutrophils from damaged crypts","Necrotic squamous cells with budding yeast","Granulomas with central caseous necrosis","A thick collagen band under intact surface epithelium"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L1","obj":"GI-13-L.1","dok":2,"approach":["Connect the clinical setting (recent clindamycin, toxin B gene) to toxin-mediated colitis.","Recall how toxin damage to crypts produces the yellow-white plaques.","Pick the exudate that erupts from injured crypts."],"why":"Antibiotics such as clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, and cephalosporins disrupt the normal colonic flora and let C. difficile overgrow. Its toxins A and B injure the surface epithelium and crypts, which release a mushroom-shaped exudate of fibrin, mucin, neutrophils, and cell debris. These pseudomembranes form the yellow-white plaques seen at endoscopy. Severe disease can progress to toxic megacolon and perforation.","whyNot":["Correct.","Necrotic squamous cells with yeast describe Candida plaques, which form on squamous mucosa such as the mouth and esophagus.","Caseating granulomas suggest tuberculosis, which can involve the ileocecal region and does not form surface pseudomembranes.","A thick subepithelial collagen band describes collagenous colitis, a cause of chronic watery diarrhea with a normal-looking colon."],"study":{"topics":["Pseudomembranous colitis","C. difficile risk factors"],"comps":["GI-13-L1"]}},{"id":"GI-C56-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes how the toxins of this organism injure colonic epithelial cells?","options":["Cross-linking MHC class II and T cell receptors as a superantigen","Glucosylation of Rho GTPases, disrupting the actin cytoskeleton","ADP-ribosylation of the Gs alpha subunit, raising cyclic AMP in enterocytes","Cleavage of 28S ribosomal RNA, halting protein synthesis"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that C. difficile toxins A and B are glucosyltransferases.","Identify their targets, the Rho family GTPases that control the actin cytoskeleton.","Predict cell rounding, loss of tight junctions, cell death, and inflammation."],"why":"Toxins A and B enter colonocytes and add glucose to Rho, Rac, and Cdc42, the small GTPases that organize actin. The actin cytoskeleton collapses, tight junctions open, and cells round up and die. The damage triggers strong neutrophil recruitment, fluid loss, and pseudomembranes. Toxin B is the more potent cytotoxin, and stool PCR targets the toxin B gene because nearly all disease-causing strains carry it, including strains that lack toxin A.","whyNot":["Staphylococcus aureus enterotoxins act as superantigens. Preformed toxin in food causes vomiting within a few hours.","Correct.","Cholera toxin and the heat-labile toxin of enterotoxigenic E. coli ADP-ribosylate Gs, raise cAMP, and open CFTR, causing large-volume watery diarrhea without cell death.","Shiga toxin and Shiga-like toxins of EHEC cleave 28S rRNA in the 60S subunit and stop protein synthesis."],"study":{"topics":["C. difficile toxins A and B","Mechanisms of bacterial enterotoxins"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-L1"]}},{"id":"GI-C56-Q3","stem":"On the third day her abdominal distension worsens. An abdominal radiograph shows a markedly dilated transverse colon that has lost its normal haustral markings. Which of the following structures normally produces the haustra?","options":["Transverse rectal folds","Plicae circulares","Taeniae coli","Omental appendices"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the radiograph: a dilated colon without haustra in severe colitis suggests toxic megacolon.","Recall what haustra are: sacculations of the colon wall seen only in the large intestine.","Pick the colon feature that gathers the wall into these sacs: the taeniae coli."],"why":"In the colon, the outer longitudinal muscle is gathered into three bands, the taeniae coli. The taeniae are shorter than the colon wall, so the wall bunches into sacs between them, called haustra. Haustra, taeniae coli, and omental appendices together mark large intestine on gross inspection and imaging. In toxic megacolon, inflammation spreads into the muscle layer, muscle tone is lost, and the dilated colon loses its haustral pattern, which raises the risk of perforation.","whyNot":["Transverse rectal folds are shelves of mucosa, submucosa, and circular muscle inside the rectum, which has no haustra.","Plicae circulares are permanent circular folds of mucosa and submucosa in the small intestine. On radiograph they cross the full width of the bowel, unlike haustral markings.","Correct.","Omental appendices are small fat-filled pouches of peritoneum on the outer colon surface. They help identify the colon but do not form the haustra."],"study":{"topics":["Gross features of the colon","Toxic megacolon"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-13-L1"]}},{"id":"GI-C56-Q4","stem":"Which of the following sets of stool findings best fits the type of diarrhea she has?","options":["Leukocytes and lactoferrin present; continues with fasting","No leukocytes; gap above 100 mOsm/kg; stops with fasting","No leukocytes; high fecal fat; stops with fasting","No leukocytes; gap below 50 mOsm/kg; continues with fasting"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.6","dok":2,"approach":["Classify her illness: toxins destroy the mucosa and recruit neutrophils, which is inflammatory diarrhea.","Recall the stool marker of inflammation: leukocytes, or lactoferrin and calprotectin released by neutrophils.","Recall that inflammatory diarrhea does not depend on eating, so it continues with fasting."],"why":"Diarrhea is grouped as secretory, osmotic, or inflammatory. In inflammatory diarrhea, damaged mucosa leaks fluid, protein, and cells, and neutrophils enter the lumen, so stool contains leukocytes and neutrophil proteins such as lactoferrin and calprotectin. Fever and leukocytosis are common. Because the problem is mucosal injury, the diarrhea continues during fasting.","whyNot":["Correct.","A large osmotic gap that resolves with fasting describes osmotic diarrhea, such as lactose intolerance or laxative use.","High fecal fat that stops with fasting suggests fat malabsorption, such as pancreatic insufficiency.","A small gap without leukocytes that continues with fasting describes pure secretory diarrhea, such as cholera."],"study":{"topics":["Inflammatory diarrhea","Fecal lactoferrin and calprotectin"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-13-L1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C57","system":"GI","dx":"Shigella dysentery","codes":["GI-13-L1"],"case":"A 27-year-old man who works at a child care center comes to the physician because of 2 days of fever, crampy lower abdominal pain, and frequent small-volume stools containing blood and mucus. He has painful straining to pass stool. Several children at the center have had a similar illness this week. Temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), pulse is 102/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. The lower abdomen is tender. Stool microscopy shows many neutrophils and erythrocytes. Stool culture grows Shigella sonnei. Diagnosis: Shigella dysentery (shigellosis).","questions":[{"id":"GI-C57-Q1","stem":"Three weeks after the diarrhea resolves, he develops a painful, swollen right knee, bilateral conjunctivitis, and dysuria. Synovial fluid culture is sterile. Which of the following host factors most increases the risk of this complication?","options":["HLA-B27","HLA-DQ2","HLA-DR3","HLA-DR4"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L1","obj":"GI-13-L.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize reactive arthritis: sterile arthritis, conjunctivitis, and urethritis weeks after an enteric or genital infection.","Recall the enteric triggers: Shigella, Salmonella, Campylobacter, and Yersinia.","Link reactive arthritis to its MHC class I association."],"why":"Reactive arthritis is a sterile inflammatory arthritis that follows infection elsewhere, most often enteric infection with Shigella, Salmonella, Campylobacter, or Yersinia, or genital infection with Chlamydia. It typically affects large joints of the legs and may come with conjunctivitis and urethritis. Many patients carry HLA-B27, the same allele linked to ankylosing spondylitis and the other seronegative spondyloarthropathies.","whyNot":["Correct.","HLA-DQ2 (with DQ8) is linked to celiac disease.","HLA-DR3 is linked to type 1 diabetes, Graves disease, and systemic lupus erythematosus.","HLA-DR4 is linked to rheumatoid arthritis and type 1 diabetes."],"study":{"topics":["Reactive arthritis","Complications of enteric infection"],"comps":["GI-13-L1","MS-13-B7"]}},{"id":"GI-C57-Q2","stem":"The infection spread quickly among the children by person-to-person contact without a common food source. Which of the following features of the organism best explains this pattern?","options":["A reservoir in the intestines of poultry flocks","A reservoir in reptiles such as pet turtles","Flagellar motility toward the mucosal surface","Infection with as few as 10 to 100 organisms"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.2","dok":2,"approach":["Note the pattern: spread by direct contact among young children, with no food vehicle.","Recall which enteric pathogen needs only a tiny inoculum.","Link low infectious dose to easy fecal-oral spread on hands and surfaces."],"why":"Shigella resists gastric acid well, so as few as 10 to 100 organisms can cause disease. A tiny inoculum on hands or toys is enough, which makes person-to-person spread easy in child care centers and other crowded settings. Humans are its only reservoir. Shigella is nonmotile and does not produce H2S, which helps separate it from Salmonella in the laboratory.","whyNot":["A poultry reservoir fits Campylobacter jejuni and Salmonella, which usually spread through undercooked chicken.","Reptiles, such as pet turtles, are a reservoir for nontyphoidal Salmonella, which needs a much larger dose to cause disease.","Shigella is nonmotile. Salmonella and Campylobacter are motile.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Shigella vs Salmonella vs Campylobacter","Infectious dose"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-L1"]}},{"id":"GI-C57-Q3","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains why his stools are frequent and small in volume and contain blood, mucus, and neutrophils, unlike the large-volume stools of cholera?","options":["Cyclic AMP-driven chloride secretion through CFTR","Invasion and destruction of the colonic mucosa","Unabsorbed solute holding water in the lumen","Serotonin-driven increase in intestinal motility"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.6","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the clues to inflammatory diarrhea: blood, mucus, neutrophils, fever, tenesmus.","Recall that Shigella enters through M cells and spreads cell to cell in the colonic epithelium.","Link an inflamed, irritable rectosigmoid colon to frequent small stools."],"why":"Shigella invades the colonic epithelium, spreads from cell to cell, and kills the cells, which produces ulcers and intense neutrophil infiltration. The damaged mucosa leaks blood, mucus, and inflammatory cells into the lumen. An inflamed rectum and sigmoid colon hold little stool and cause urgency and tenesmus, so stools are frequent and small. Cholera, by contrast, leaves the mucosa intact and makes the small intestine secrete liters of fluid.","whyNot":["Cholera toxin raises cAMP and opens CFTR, causing large-volume, watery stools without blood or neutrophils.","Correct.","Unabsorbed solute, such as lactose, causes osmotic diarrhea that stops with fasting and has no blood or neutrophils.","Serotonin drives secretion and speeds transit in carcinoid syndrome, causing watery diarrhea without mucosal destruction."],"study":{"topics":["Inflammatory vs secretory diarrhea","Shigella invasion"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-12"]}},{"id":"GI-C57-Q4","stem":"His roommate returns from travel with prolonged fever and is found to have typhoid fever with ulcers in the terminal ileum. Which of the following histologic features would identify a biopsy as ileum rather than jejunum or duodenum?","options":["Mucous glands deep in the submucosa","Tall, closely spaced circular folds with villi","Large lymphoid aggregates in the submucosa","Crypts rich in goblet cells and no villi"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the identifying feature of each small bowel segment.","Match the ileum to Peyer patches, the large lymphoid aggregates that extend into the submucosa.","Link Salmonella Typhi to invasion through M cells over Peyer patches, which explains the ileal ulcers."],"why":"Peyer patches are clusters of lymphoid follicles in the mucosa and submucosa of the ileum, covered by M cells that sample luminal antigens. Salmonella Typhi enters through these M cells, multiplies in macrophages, and spreads through the blood. Inflammation and necrosis of the Peyer patches produce ulcers in the terminal ileum, which can bleed or perforate.","whyNot":["Submucosal mucous glands are Brunner glands, which identify the duodenum.","Tall, closely spaced plicae circulares are the hallmark of the jejunum.","Correct.","Goblet cell-rich crypts without villi identify the colon."],"study":{"topics":["Peyer patches","Histology of the small intestine"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-11"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C58","system":"GI","dx":"Candida esophagitis and oral thrush in advanced HIV infection","codes":["GI-13-L2","GI-13-L5"],"case":"A 38-year-old man with HIV infection who stopped antiretroviral therapy 2 years ago comes to the physician because of 3 weeks of painful swallowing and a sore mouth. He has lost 6 kg (13.2 lb). Temperature is 37.6 °C (99.7 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 116/72 mm Hg. Physical examination shows creamy white plaques on the buccal mucosa and palate that scrape off and leave a red, bleeding base. CD4+ T-lymphocyte count is 62/mm3 (500 to 1,500). Upper endoscopy shows numerous adherent white plaques along the length of the esophagus. Diagnosis: Candida esophagitis and oral thrush in advanced HIV infection.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C58-Q1","stem":"Biopsy specimens are taken from the esophageal plaques. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Enlarged endothelial cells with inclusions at the base of linear ulcers","Multinucleated squamous cells with intranuclear inclusions at ulcer edges","Budding yeast and pseudohyphae in a fibrinous exudate","More than 15 eosinophils per high-power field in the epithelium"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L2","obj":"GI-13-L.3","dok":2,"approach":["Match the endoscopic pattern: white plaques point to Candida, punched-out ulcers to HSV, and long linear ulcers to CMV.","Recall the tissue form of Candida albicans.","Pick yeast with pseudohyphae in the plaque material."],"why":"Candida albicans grows in tissue as budding yeast with pseudohyphae, best shown with PAS or silver stains. In the esophagus it forms white plaques of fungus, fibrin, and debris that stick to the squamous lining. In patients with HIV and low CD4 counts, Candida is the most common cause of infectious esophagitis, and esophageal candidiasis is an AIDS-defining illness.","whyNot":["Inclusions in endothelial and stromal cells at the base of long, shallow linear ulcers describe CMV esophagitis.","Multinucleated squamous cells with ground-glass intranuclear inclusions at the edges of small, punched-out ulcers describe HSV esophagitis.","Correct.","Many intraepithelial eosinophils describe eosinophilic esophagitis, an allergic disorder with rings and furrows."],"study":{"topics":["Infectious esophagitis","Candida morphology"],"comps":["GI-13-L2","GI-12"]}},{"id":"GI-C58-Q2","stem":"Which of the following conditions also predisposes to oral infection by this organism by impairing local mucosal defenses?","options":["Splenectomy after abdominal trauma","Inhaled corticosteroid use without rinsing","Deficiency of terminal complement components","Deficiency of C1 esterase inhibitor"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L5","obj":"GI-13-L.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that mucosal control of Candida depends on T cells (especially Th17 cells) and a balanced oral flora.","Look for a condition that suppresses local immunity in the mouth.","Exclude defects that mainly raise risk from encapsulated bacteria, Neisseria, or swelling."],"why":"Oral thrush is overgrowth of Candida albicans, which normally lives in the mouth in small numbers. It appears when local defenses or the competing flora fail: in young infants, with inhaled corticosteroids that deposit in the mouth, after broad-spectrum antibiotics, in diabetes, in denture wearers, and in HIV with falling CD4 counts. Steroid from an inhaler suppresses local immunity, which is why patients are told to rinse after each use.","whyNot":["Splenectomy raises the risk of sepsis from encapsulated bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae.","Correct.","Terminal complement (C5 to C9) deficiency raises the risk of recurrent Neisseria infections.","C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency causes hereditary angioedema, with recurrent swelling and no infection risk."],"study":{"topics":["Oral thrush risk factors","Mucosal antifungal immunity"],"comps":["GI-13-L5","IM-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C58-Q3","stem":"At endoscopy, the paler esophageal lining meets the redder gastric lining at an irregular line near the gastroesophageal junction. Which of the following epithelial transitions normally occurs at this line?","options":["Keratinized stratified squamous to simple cuboidal","Pseudostratified columnar to simple columnar","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous to goblet cell-rich columnar","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous to simple columnar"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the normal esophageal lining: nonkeratinized stratified squamous.","Recall the normal gastric lining: simple columnar mucus-secreting cells.","Recognize that goblet cells above this line would be abnormal intestinal metaplasia."],"why":"The esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, which resists abrasion from swallowed food. At the squamocolumnar junction, or Z line, it changes abruptly to the simple columnar, mucus-secreting epithelium of the stomach. With chronic acid reflux, columnar epithelium with goblet cells can replace the distal esophageal squamous lining. This intestinal metaplasia is Barrett esophagus, the precursor of esophageal adenocarcinoma.","whyNot":["The esophagus is nonkeratinized in humans, and the stomach is lined by columnar cells.","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines the trachea and bronchi.","Columnar epithelium with goblet cells at or above the junction is Barrett metaplasia, an abnormal change caused by chronic reflux.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Gastroesophageal junction histology","Barrett esophagus"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-13-G2"]}},{"id":"GI-C58-Q4","stem":"During swallowing, the lower esophageal sphincter relaxes as the peristaltic wave approaches it. Which of the following mediates this relaxation?","options":["Norepinephrine released from postganglionic sympathetic fibers","Nitric oxide and VIP from vagal inhibitory pathways","Gastrin released from antral G cells","Acetylcholine acting on muscarinic receptors"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that swallowing is coordinated by the swallowing center in the medulla.","Recall that vagal fibers synapse on enteric inhibitory neurons at the sphincter.","Identify their transmitters: nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide."],"why":"Swallowing begins with a voluntary oral phase, then a reflex pharyngeal phase and esophageal phase coordinated by the medulla through the vagus nerves. As peristalsis starts, vagal fibers activate inhibitory neurons of the myenteric plexus at the lower esophageal sphincter. These neurons release nitric oxide and VIP, which relax the sphincter smooth muscle so the bolus can enter the stomach. Loss of these inhibitory neurons causes achalasia.","whyNot":["Sympathetic input does not drive the swallow-induced relaxation of the sphincter.","Correct.","Gastrin does not mediate the swallow-induced relaxation of the sphincter.","Acetylcholine from excitatory vagal pathways contracts esophageal smooth muscle and maintains sphincter tone."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of swallowing","Lower esophageal sphincter control"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-F1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C59","system":"GI","dx":"Norovirus gastroenteritis in a cruise ship outbreak","codes":["GI-13-L3"],"case":"A 67-year-old woman comes to the ship's physician during a cruise because of sudden nausea, repeated vomiting, and watery, nonbloody diarrhea that began 14 hours ago. More than 100 other passengers and crew members have had similar symptoms over the past 3 days. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 108/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. Mucous membranes are dry. Serum sodium is 136 mEq/L (135 to 145), and serum creatinine is 1.4 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1). Reverse transcription PCR of stool is positive for norovirus. Diagnosis: norovirus gastroenteritis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C59-Q1","stem":"On the same voyage, a crew member eats fried rice that was cooked the day before, left at room temperature, and then reheated. Two hours later he develops nausea and repeated vomiting. Which of the following is the most likely cause of his illness?","options":["Bacillus cereus","Rotavirus","Vibrio cholerae","Enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L3","obj":"GI-13-L.4","dok":2,"approach":["Use the time to onset: vomiting within a few hours means a toxin already formed in the food.","Use the food: rice left warm lets spores germinate and make toxin.","Match reheated rice to Bacillus cereus."],"why":"Bacillus cereus spores survive cooking. When cooked rice sits at room temperature, the spores germinate and the bacteria make a heat-stable emetic toxin. Reheating does not destroy this toxin, so vomiting begins within about 1 to 6 hours of eating. A separate diarrheal form appears later, after about 8 to 16 hours.","whyNot":["Correct.","Rotavirus causes watery diarrhea in unvaccinated infants and young children after an incubation of about 2 days.","Vibrio cholerae causes profuse rice-water stools after drinking contaminated water, with an incubation of about 1 to 3 days.","Enterotoxigenic E. coli is the classic cause of traveler's diarrhea, with watery stools 1 to 3 days after eating contaminated food or water."],"study":{"topics":["Watery gastroenteritis by setting","Bacillus cereus"],"comps":["GI-13-L3","GI-12"]}},{"id":"GI-C59-Q2","stem":"The outbreak continued despite wide use of alcohol-based hand sanitizer. Which of the following best describes the causative virus?","options":["Segmented double-stranded RNA reovirus","Enveloped single-stranded RNA coronavirus","Nonenveloped double-stranded DNA enteric adenovirus","Nonenveloped single-stranded RNA calicivirus"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.4","dok":3,"approach":["Classify norovirus: a calicivirus with a positive-sense single-stranded RNA genome.","Recall that it lacks an envelope.","Link the missing envelope to resistance to alcohol and survival on surfaces."],"why":"Norovirus is a nonenveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus in the calicivirus family. Without a lipid envelope, it resists alcohol-based hand sanitizers, survives on surfaces, and tolerates freezing and mild heat. A very small dose causes infection, and patients shed large amounts in vomit and stool. These traits make it the leading cause of outbreaks on cruise ships and in nursing homes, schools, and restaurants, where soap and water and bleach-based cleaning are needed.","whyNot":["Rotavirus is a segmented double-stranded RNA reovirus. It mainly affects unvaccinated infants and young children.","Enveloped viruses are inactivated readily by alcohol. Coronaviruses are mainly respiratory pathogens.","Enteric adenovirus types 40 and 41 cause diarrhea mostly in young children, not adult outbreaks.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Norovirus","Viral causes of gastroenteritis"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-L3"]}},{"id":"GI-C59-Q3","stem":"She is given an oral rehydration solution containing glucose and sodium chloride. Which of the following best explains why adding glucose increases intestinal water absorption?","options":["SGLT1 couples sodium uptake to glucose at the brush border","Glucose enters by GLUT5 and pulls water in by osmosis","Glucose closes CFTR chloride channels in crypt cells","Glucose raises insulin, which adds aquaporins to enterocytes"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall how glucose crosses the apical membrane: SGLT1, with two sodium ions per glucose.","Note that SGLT1 keeps working during most infectious diarrheas.","Link solute absorption to water following it across the epithelium."],"why":"SGLT1 on the brush border carries two sodium ions with each glucose or galactose molecule into the enterocyte. Glucose leaves the cell through GLUT2 on the basolateral side, and sodium is pumped out by Na+/K+-ATPase. The absorbed solute creates an osmotic gradient, and water follows. This transporter remains largely functional in most acute diarrheal illnesses, so a solution with both glucose and sodium restores fluid even while diarrhea continues.","whyNot":["Correct.","GLUT5 transports fructose by facilitated diffusion, without sodium. Glucose enters by SGLT1.","Glucose does not close CFTR. Cholera toxin opens CFTR by raising cAMP.","Insulin does not control water uptake in the intestine, and enterocyte glucose uptake does not depend on insulin."],"study":{"topics":["Monosaccharide absorption","Oral rehydration solution"],"comps":["GI-7"]}},{"id":"GI-C59-Q4","stem":"Her symptoms persist for 3 weeks, and she undergoes upper endoscopy with biopsy of the proximal small intestine. Which of the following features identifies the specimen as duodenum rather than jejunum?","options":["Peyer patches in the mucosa and submucosa","Mucous glands in the submucosa","Villi lined by simple columnar epithelium","Goblet cells scattered among absorptive cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["List what all small bowel segments share: villi, simple columnar epithelium, and goblet cells.","Find the feature unique to the duodenum.","Link Brunner glands to their alkaline mucus, which protects against acid from the stomach."],"why":"Brunner glands are coiled mucous glands in the submucosa of the duodenum. They secrete alkaline, bicarbonate-rich mucus that helps neutralize acid from the stomach and protects the duodenal mucosa. The jejunum lacks these glands and has tall, closely spaced plicae circulares, and the ileum has Peyer patches.","whyNot":["Peyer patches identify the ileum.","Correct.","Villi with simple columnar epithelium line the whole small intestine.","Goblet cells occur throughout the small intestine and colon, so they do not identify a segment."],"study":{"topics":["Brunner glands","Histology of the small intestine"],"comps":["GI-2"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C60","system":"GI","dx":"Primary herpetic gingivostomatitis in a toddler","codes":["GI-13-L6"],"case":"A 2-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of 3 days of fever, irritability, drooling, and refusal to eat or drink. Temperature is 39.4 °C (102.9 °F), pulse is 140/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 94/58 mm Hg. Physical examination shows swollen, erythematous gingiva that bleed easily, clusters of small vesicles and shallow ulcers on the gingiva, anterior tongue, and lips, and tender submandibular and anterior cervical lymph nodes. Mucous membranes are dry. A Tzanck smear of a vesicle shows multinucleated giant cells, and PCR is positive for herpes simplex virus type 1 (HSV-1). Diagnosis: primary herpetic gingivostomatitis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C60-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings in this patient most reliably distinguishes his illness from herpangina?","options":["Occurrence in a child younger than 5 years","Vesicles that progress to shallow ulcers","Gingivitis with lip and anterior oral vesicles","High fever with irritability and refusal to drink"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L6","obj":"GI-13-L.7","dok":3,"approach":["Recall herpangina: coxsackie A virus with vesicles on the soft palate, uvula, and tonsillar pillars.","Recall primary HSV-1: inflamed, bleeding gums and vesicles in the front of the mouth and on the lips.","Pick the feature found in only one of the two."],"why":"Both illnesses strike young children with fever, sore mouth, and vesicles that become ulcers. Primary HSV-1 gingivostomatitis involves the front of the mouth: swollen, bleeding gingiva and vesicles on the gums, tongue, buccal mucosa, and lips. Herpangina, caused by coxsackie A viruses, places vesicles at the back of the mouth on the soft palate, uvula, and tonsillar pillars, and the gums are spared. Hand-foot-and-mouth disease (coxsackie A16) adds vesicles on the palms and soles.","whyNot":["Both occur mostly in young children.","Vesicles become ulcers in both illnesses.","Correct.","Both illnesses can cause high fever, irritability, and poor intake."],"study":{"topics":["Herpetic gingivostomatitis vs herpangina","Oral vesicular lesions"],"comps":["GI-13-L6"]}},{"id":"GI-C60-Q2","stem":"After the illness resolves, the virus remains latent and can later reactivate as cold sores. Latent virus that infected the anterior tongue and lower gingiva most likely resides in neurons of which of the following ganglia?","options":["Trigeminal ganglion","Geniculate ganglion","Submandibular ganglion","Inferior ganglion of the glossopharyngeal nerve"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-4","obj":"GI-4.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that HSV travels up sensory axons and stays latent in their cell bodies.","Map general sensation: anterior two thirds of the tongue by the lingual nerve and lower gingiva by branches of V3.","Find the ganglion that holds these sensory cell bodies."],"why":"HSV-1 enters the endings of sensory neurons in the mucosa and travels by retrograde axonal transport to their cell bodies, where it stays latent. General sensation from the anterior two thirds of the tongue travels in the lingual nerve, and the lower gums are supplied by other branches of the mandibular nerve (V3). The cell bodies of these neurons lie in the trigeminal ganglion. Reactivation sends virus back down the axons, usually causing cold sores on the lip.","whyNot":["Correct.","The geniculate ganglion holds the cell bodies of chorda tympani taste fibers (facial nerve). The gingiva has no taste fibers, so general sensory neurons are the better fit.","The submandibular ganglion is a parasympathetic ganglion for the submandibular and sublingual glands, with no sensory cell bodies.","The inferior glossopharyngeal ganglion holds sensory neurons for the posterior third of the tongue and the pharynx."],"study":{"topics":["Sensory innervation of the tongue and gums","HSV latency"],"comps":["GI-4","GI-13-L6"]}},{"id":"GI-C60-Q3","stem":"Because of his painful mouth and fever, he drinks little, and his salivary flow falls. If salivary flow stayed low for months, loss of which of the following salivary functions would most directly promote dental caries?","options":["Lubrication of food by mucins","Starch digestion by salivary amylase","Fat digestion by lingual lipase","Bicarbonate buffering of plaque acid"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that caries forms when plaque bacteria make acid that dissolves enamel.","Ask which salivary function opposes this acid.","Pick bicarbonate buffering, which also allows calcium and phosphate to remineralize enamel."],"why":"Dental caries forms when bacteria such as Streptococcus mutans ferment sugars into acid that dissolves enamel. Saliva protects teeth in several ways: bicarbonate buffers plaque acid, calcium and phosphate remineralize early lesions, flow washes away sugars, and IgA, lysozyme, and lactoferrin limit bacteria. When flow stays low, as in Sjogren syndrome, after head and neck radiation, or with anticholinergic drugs, caries develops rapidly, often at the gum line.","whyNot":["Mucins lubricate food for swallowing, and their loss makes chewing and swallowing harder, but buffering of acid is the key defense against caries.","Salivary amylase starts starch digestion in the mouth, but losing it does not promote caries, and pancreatic amylase completes starch digestion.","Lingual lipase begins fat digestion, mainly in infants, and has no role in protecting enamel.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Functions of saliva","Dental caries and dry mouth"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-L4"]}},{"id":"GI-C60-Q4","stem":"Secretory IgA in his saliva helps neutralize viruses on the oral mucosa. Which of the following steps delivers this antibody into saliva in a protease-resistant form?","options":["Synthesis of IgA by the salivary acinar cells","Leakage of monomeric serum IgA across tight junctions","Transcytosis by the polymeric Ig receptor","Transport by the neonatal Fc receptor on acinar cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that plasma cells beneath the epithelium make dimeric IgA joined by a J chain.","Identify the receptor that binds this dimer on the basolateral surface and carries it across the cell.","Note that cleavage at the apical surface leaves a piece of the receptor attached that shields IgA from proteases."],"why":"Plasma cells in the gland interstitium secrete dimeric IgA linked by a J chain. The polymeric Ig receptor on the basolateral surface of epithelial cells binds the dimer and carries it across the cell by transcytosis. At the apical surface the receptor is cleaved, and a part of it, the secretory component, stays bound to the IgA. Secretory component protects the antibody from proteases in saliva and the gut lumen. Selective IgA deficiency removes this barrier and predisposes to recurrent sinus, lung, and gut infections.","whyNot":["IgA is made by plasma cells, not by epithelial acinar cells. The epithelium only transports it.","Serum IgA is mostly monomeric and does not cross intact epithelium in meaningful amounts. Without secretory component it would be degraded.","Correct.","The neonatal Fc receptor transports IgG across the placenta and protects IgG from breakdown. It does not carry IgA into secretions."],"study":{"topics":["Secretory IgA and the polymeric Ig receptor","Mucosal immunity"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-9"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C01","system":"MS","dx":"Osteomalacia after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass","codes":["MS-13-A1"],"case":"A 46-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 9-month history of aching pain in her ribs, hips, and lower legs and increasing difficulty climbing stairs and rising from a chair. She had a Roux-en-Y gastric bypass 7 years ago and stopped taking her prescribed supplements 3 years ago. Physical examination shows tenderness to pressure over the ribs and tibiae, weakness of the hip flexors, and a waddling gait. Laboratory studies show: calcium 8.3 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5), phosphate 2.1 mg/dL (2.5 to 4.5), alkaline phosphatase 312 U/L (40 to 120), parathyroid hormone 131 pg/mL (10 to 65), and 25-hydroxyvitamin D 7 ng/mL (30 to 100). Serum creatinine and aminotransferase activities are within the reference ranges. Diagnosis: osteomalacia due to vitamin D deficiency.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C01-Q1","stem":"Radiographs of the pelvis and femurs are obtained. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Bilateral transverse lucent bands in the femoral necks","Coarse trabeculae with cortical thickening and bone enlargement","Subchondral cysts with osteophytes and joint space narrowing","Multiple lytic lesions with sharply punched-out margins"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-A1","obj":"MS-13-A.3","dok":2,"approach":["Link the low phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase, high PTH, and low 25-hydroxyvitamin D to defective mineralization of osteoid in an adult.","Recall that unmineralized osteoid cannot carry normal loads, so it develops incomplete stress fractures that do not heal.","Pick the radiographic sign of these pseudofractures, called Looser zones."],"why":"Osteomalacia is defective mineralization of new osteoid after the growth plates have closed. Soft, poorly mineralized bone develops Looser zones, also called pseudofractures. These are narrow lucent bands that run across the cortex, often on both sides, at the femoral necks, pubic rami, ribs, and lateral border of the scapula. The rest of the skeleton often looks diffusely osteopenic.","whyNot":["Correct.","This describes Paget disease of bone, which also raises alkaline phosphatase. In Paget disease, calcium, phosphate, and PTH are usually normal, and the bone is enlarged and dense.","These are findings of osteoarthritis, a disease of articular cartilage. They do not explain diffuse bone pain or the mineral abnormalities here.","Punched-out lytic lesions suggest multiple myeloma. Myeloma causes bone pain, but it tends to raise calcium and does not lower 25-hydroxyvitamin D."],"study":{"topics":["Looser zones in osteomalacia","Laboratory pattern of vitamin D deficiency"],"comps":["MS-13-A1","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C01-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains her vitamin D deficiency?","options":["Increased hepatic catabolism of vitamin D by induced cytochrome P450","Reduced 25-hydroxylation of cholecalciferol in the liver","Reduced 1-alpha-hydroxylation of 25-hydroxyvitamin D in the kidney","Poor absorption of fat-soluble vitamin D from the small bowel"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-A1","obj":"MS-13-A.2","dok":3,"approach":["Note that 25-hydroxyvitamin D is very low, so the problem is in the supply of vitamin D that reaches the liver.","Normal creatinine and aminotransferases argue against kidney or liver disease, and no enzyme-inducing drug is listed.","Roux-en-Y bypass sends food past the duodenum and proximal jejunum and delays mixing with bile, so fat-soluble vitamins are poorly absorbed."],"why":"Vitamin D is fat soluble. It is absorbed with dietary fat in bile salt micelles in the small intestine. After Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, food skips the duodenum and proximal jejunum and meets bile only further down in the common channel, so less vitamin D is absorbed. Without supplements, her stores ran down over years. A low 25-hydroxyvitamin D level shows that the body is short of substrate.","whyNot":["Enzyme-inducing anticonvulsants such as phenytoin speed vitamin D breakdown. She takes no such drug.","Severe liver disease can impair 25-hydroxylation, but her aminotransferases are normal, and the liver has a large reserve for this step.","Kidney failure lowers calcitriol, but 25-hydroxyvitamin D stays normal. Her creatinine is normal and her 25-hydroxyvitamin D is very low.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Risk factors for vitamin D deficiency","Fat-soluble vitamin absorption after bariatric surgery"],"comps":["MS-13-A1","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C01-Q3","stem":"She is started on oral cholecalciferol. Her physician plans to follow serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D rather than 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D to judge when her stores are restored. Which of the following best explains this choice?","options":["It is formed directly in the skin by ultraviolet B light","It is long-lived and its synthesis tracks supply","Its production in the kidney is stimulated by PTH","It binds the vitamin D receptor with the highest affinity"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.1","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the pathway: 7-dehydrocholesterol in the skin to cholecalciferol, then hepatic 25-hydroxylation, then renal 1-alpha-hydroxylation.","Ask which step is tightly controlled by hormones and which simply follows the amount of substrate.","The best marker of stores is the long-lived form whose level follows supply."],"why":"UVB light converts 7-dehydrocholesterol in the skin to cholecalciferol, which also comes from the diet. The liver adds a 25-hydroxyl group with little feedback control, so 25-hydroxyvitamin D rises and falls with supply and circulates for about 2 to 3 weeks. The kidney then makes calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D) under tight control by PTH, phosphate, and FGF23. Calcitriol lasts only hours, and in deficiency the high PTH can keep it normal or even high, so it is a poor measure of stores.","whyNot":["The skin makes cholecalciferol (vitamin D3) from 7-dehydrocholesterol. The 25-hydroxylation step happens in the liver.","Correct.","PTH stimulates renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase, which makes calcitriol. 25-hydroxyvitamin D is made in the liver.","Calcitriol is the form that binds the vitamin D receptor most strongly and acts as the hormone."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin D activation pathway","Why 25-hydroxyvitamin D measures vitamin D status"],"comps":["MS-8","MS-13-A1"]}},{"id":"MS-C01-Q4","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms most directly accounts for her low serum phosphate concentration?","options":["Rapid uptake of phosphate into remineralizing bone","Insulin-driven shift of phosphate into cells","PTH excess reducing renal phosphate reabsorption","FGF23 excess reducing renal phosphate reabsorption"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.2","dok":3,"approach":["Vitamin D deficiency lowers intestinal calcium absorption, and the falling calcium stimulates the parathyroid glands.","Secondary hyperparathyroidism follows, and PTH is high in this case.","PTH removes sodium-phosphate cotransporters from the proximal tubule, so phosphate is lost in the urine."],"why":"In vitamin D deficiency the gut absorbs less calcium, and PTH rises to defend serum calcium. PTH increases bone resorption by raising RANKL on osteoblasts, increases calcium reabsorption in the distal tubule, and stimulates renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase. It also lowers phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule. The result is a calcium that is low or low-normal and a clearly low phosphate. Reduced gut absorption of phosphate adds to this, but PTH-driven urinary loss is the main cause.","whyNot":["Hungry bone after parathyroidectomy or early in vitamin D treatment pulls phosphate into bone. Her labs were drawn before any treatment.","Insulin moves phosphate into cells during refeeding. Nothing in her history suggests refeeding.","Correct.","FGF23 excess causes phosphate wasting in X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets and tumor-induced osteomalacia. In those disorders PTH and 25-hydroxyvitamin D are usually normal."],"study":{"topics":["Secondary hyperparathyroidism","PTH actions on the kidney"],"comps":["MS-8","MS-13-A1"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C02","system":"MS","dx":"Nutritional rickets in an exclusively breastfed toddler","codes":["MS-13-A2"],"case":"An 18-month-old boy is brought to the physician because he is not yet walking steadily and his legs have become bowed. He has been exclusively breastfed and has received no vitamin supplements. He has darkly pigmented skin and lives in a northern city with long winters. Physical examination shows frontal bossing, beading of the costochondral junctions, widened wrists, and bowed legs. Laboratory studies show: calcium 8.1 mg/dL (8.8 to 10.8), phosphate 3.1 mg/dL (3.8 to 6.5), alkaline phosphatase 960 U/L (about 150 to 420), parathyroid hormone 148 pg/mL (10 to 65), and 25-hydroxyvitamin D 6 ng/mL (30 to 100). Diagnosis: nutritional rickets due to vitamin D deficiency.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C02-Q1","stem":"A 2-year-old girl seen in the same clinic also has bowed legs and widened wrists. Her mother and maternal grandmother are short and have bowed legs. Which of the following sets of laboratory findings would most strongly suggest X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets in the girl?","options":["Calcium low, phosphate low, 25-hydroxyvitamin D low, PTH high","Calcium normal, phosphate low, 25-hydroxyvitamin D normal, PTH normal","Calcium high, phosphate high, 25-hydroxyvitamin D high, PTH suppressed","Calcium low, phosphate high, 25-hydroxyvitamin D normal, PTH very high"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-A2","obj":"MS-13-A.4","dok":3,"approach":["X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets comes from loss of PHEX function, which raises FGF23 from osteocytes.","FGF23 causes renal phosphate wasting and suppresses 1-alpha-hydroxylase, so phosphate is low while vitamin D stores are normal.","Calcium stays near normal, so PTH is not strongly stimulated. Pick the pattern with an isolated low phosphate."],"why":"In X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets, loss of PHEX function raises FGF23. FGF23 removes sodium-phosphate cotransporters from the proximal tubule and suppresses renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase, so phosphate is low and calcitriol is low or inappropriately normal. Calcium, 25-hydroxyvitamin D, and PTH are usually normal or near normal. The skeletal signs look like nutritional rickets, but the disease does not respond to ordinary vitamin D doses. Affected mothers pass it to half their children, which fits this family.","whyNot":["This is the pattern of nutritional vitamin D deficiency, as in the boy in this case.","Correct.","High calcium, suppressed PTH, and high 25-hydroxyvitamin D point to vitamin D toxicity, which does not cause rickets.","Low calcium with high phosphate and very high PTH is the pattern of chronic kidney disease, which causes renal osteodystrophy."],"study":{"topics":["X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets","FGF23 and renal phosphate handling"],"comps":["MS-13-A2","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C02-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains why the long bones of his legs have bowed?","options":["Failure of newly formed osteoid to mineralize","Reduced synthesis of type I collagen by osteoblasts","Reduced chondrocyte proliferation in the growth plate","Reduced bone mass with a normally mineralized matrix"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-A2","obj":"MS-13-A.1","dok":2,"approach":["Low calcium and low phosphate leave too little mineral for hydroxyapatite crystals to form.","Osteoblasts keep making osteoid, but it stays soft and unmineralized.","Soft bone bends under the weight of a child who is starting to walk."],"why":"Vitamin D deficiency lowers intestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate, and secondary hyperparathyroidism adds renal phosphate loss. With too little mineral, the osteoid that osteoblasts lay down cannot form hydroxyapatite. Unmineralized osteoid is flexible, so weight-bearing bones bow. The same defect is called rickets when it occurs before the growth plates close and osteomalacia after they close.","whyNot":["Correct.","Reduced or abnormal type I collagen causes osteogenesis imperfecta, with brittle bones that fracture. The collagen matrix is made normally in rickets.","Reduced chondrocyte proliferation from FGFR3 gain of function causes achondroplasia, with short limbs and normal mineral levels.","This describes osteoporosis. The bone that remains is normally mineralized, and calcium, phosphate, and PTH are normal."],"study":{"topics":["Mineralization defect in rickets and osteomalacia"],"comps":["MS-13-A2","MS-2"]}},{"id":"MS-C02-Q3","stem":"A radiograph of his wrist shows widened, cupped, and frayed distal radial and ulnar metaphyses. A biopsy of the growth plate would most likely show which of the following?","options":["A thin, disorganized zone of proliferating chondrocytes","Dense calcified cartilage cores persisting in the diaphysis","A thick, disorganized zone of hypertrophic chondrocytes","Early fusion of the plate with bony bridging"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the zones from the epiphysis toward the shaft: reserve, proliferative, hypertrophic, calcification, and ossification.","Hypertrophic chondrocytes need enough phosphate to undergo apoptosis and let their matrix calcify.","Without mineralization the hypertrophic cells pile up, so the plate becomes thick and irregular, seen as a widened, cupped metaphysis."],"why":"In a normal growth plate, chondrocytes divide in the proliferative zone, enlarge in the hypertrophic zone, and then die as the matrix around them calcifies and is replaced by bone. Low phosphate blocks apoptosis of hypertrophic chondrocytes and mineralization of their matrix. The hypertrophic zone expands and becomes disorganized, and the metaphysis widens and cups on radiographs.","whyNot":["A thin proliferative zone is the defect in achondroplasia, where FGFR3 gain of function slows chondrocyte proliferation.","Persistent calcified cartilage cores in dense bone are seen in osteopetrosis, where osteoclasts fail to resorb.","Correct.","Premature closure follows growth plate fractures or early exposure to sex steroids. Rickets widens the plate."],"study":{"topics":["Growth plate zones","Growth plate in rickets compared with achondroplasia"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-13-A2"]}},{"id":"MS-C02-Q4","stem":"Two days after a febrile illness, he has spasms of the hands and feet, and tapping over the facial nerve makes the corner of his mouth twitch. Which of the following best explains his increased neuromuscular excitability?","options":["Increased acetylcholine release from motor nerve terminals","Lower threshold for opening of neuronal sodium channels","Increased calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Reduced acetylcholinesterase activity at the end plate"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.5","dok":3,"approach":["Link carpopedal spasm and a Chvostek sign to hypocalcemia.","Extracellular calcium normally binds the outer membrane surface near sodium channels and raises the voltage needed to open them.","When calcium falls, sodium channels open closer to the resting potential, so nerves fire on their own."],"why":"Extracellular calcium stabilizes excitable membranes. Calcium ions bind negative charges on the outside of the membrane near sodium channel gates, which makes the channels harder to open. In hypocalcemia, a smaller depolarization opens sodium channels, so peripheral nerves fire repeatedly and cause tetany. The Chvostek sign (facial twitch when the facial nerve is tapped) and the Trousseau sign (carpal spasm with a blood pressure cuff) show this excitability.","whyNot":["Calcium entry into the nerve terminal triggers acetylcholine release, so low extracellular calcium reduces release.","Correct.","In skeletal muscle, sarcoplasmic reticulum release is triggered by the T-tubule voltage sensor and does not depend on extracellular calcium.","Acetylcholinesterase inhibition, as with organophosphates, causes fasciculations and weakness. Hypocalcemia does not affect the enzyme."],"study":{"topics":["Hypocalcemia and membrane excitability","Chvostek and Trousseau signs"],"comps":["MS-10","MS-13-A2"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C03","system":"MS","dx":"Scurvy in an older man with alcohol use disorder","codes":["MS-13-A3"],"case":"A 67-year-old man is brought to the physician by his daughter because of 2 months of fatigue, bleeding gums, easy bruising, and a painful, swollen right knee. He has alcohol use disorder, lives alone, and for the past year has eaten mostly toast, tea, and canned meat with no fruit or vegetables. A shin laceration from 6 weeks ago has not healed. Physical examination shows swollen, purple, friable gums, large ecchymoses on the thighs, and a tense right knee effusion. Laboratory studies show: hemoglobin 10.6 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), platelet count 238,000/mm3 (150,000 to 400,000), prothrombin time 12.5 seconds (11 to 13.5), and partial thromboplastin time 31 seconds (25 to 35). Plasma ascorbic acid is 0.1 mg/dL (0.4 to 2.0). Diagnosis: scurvy.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C03-Q1","stem":"Examination of his skin is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Perifollicular hemorrhages with coiled hairs","Palpable purpura with IgA deposits in vessel walls","Rough follicular papules without bleeding","Spider angiomas with radiating small vessels"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-A3","obj":"MS-13-A.6","dok":2,"approach":["Bleeding with a normal platelet count, PT, and PTT points to weak vessel walls, not a clotting factor or platelet problem.","A diet without fruit or vegetables and a very low ascorbic acid level identify vitamin C deficiency.","Weak collagen around hair follicles leads to small bleeds there, and abnormal keratinization produces coiled, corkscrew hairs."],"why":"Scurvy weakens the collagen that supports small blood vessels. Early skin signs are follicular hyperkeratosis with perifollicular hemorrhages, often on the legs, and coiled corkscrew hairs. Larger bleeds cause ecchymoses, swollen bleeding gums, and hemarthrosis. Coagulation tests are normal because platelets and the clotting cascade are intact. Risk is highest in people who eat no fruit or vegetables, including many with alcohol use disorder.","whyNot":["Correct.","Palpable purpura with IgA deposits is IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein purpura), seen mostly in children after an upper respiratory infection.","Follicular papules without bleeding fit vitamin A deficiency. Bleeding around the follicle is what points to vitamin C.","Spider angiomas come from high estrogen in chronic liver disease. They are common with heavy alcohol use but do not explain bleeding with normal coagulation tests."],"study":{"topics":["Clinical findings of scurvy"],"comps":["MS-13-A3","MS-6"]}},{"id":"MS-C03-Q2","stem":"Which of the following steps in collagen synthesis is most directly impaired in this patient?","options":["Cleavage of terminal propeptides from procollagen","Cross-linking of lysine residues by lysyl oxidase","Glycosylation of hydroxylysine residues in procollagen","Hydroxylation of proline residues in procollagen"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-A3","obj":"MS-13-A.5","dok":2,"approach":["Vitamin C is the cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases in the rough endoplasmic reticulum.","Hydroxyproline forms hydrogen bonds that hold the three alpha chains together in a stable triple helix.","Without it the triple helix is unstable at body temperature and is degraded, so vessel walls and wounds are weak."],"why":"Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases need iron in the ferrous state, and ascorbate keeps the iron reduced. Without vitamin C, proline and lysine residues in procollagen are poorly hydroxylated. Underhydroxylated chains form unstable triple helices that are degraded inside the cell, so less collagen is secreted. Capillary walls and healing wounds depend on new collagen, so they bleed and fail to heal.","whyNot":["Procollagen peptidases remove propeptides outside the cell. Defects cause the dermatosparaxis type of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","Lysyl oxidase acts outside the cell and needs copper. Its failure is seen in Menkes disease.","Glycosylation does not use vitamin C. It falls only as a downstream effect, because fewer hydroxylysine residues are available.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin C and prolyl hydroxylation","Steps of collagen synthesis"],"comps":["MS-6","MS-13-A3"]}},{"id":"MS-C03-Q3","stem":"Aspiration of his right knee yields grossly bloody fluid. Which of the following structures of this synovial joint is the most likely source of the bleeding?","options":["Inner portion of the medial meniscus","Articular hyaline cartilage of the femoral condyles","Calcified cartilage deep to the tidemark","Synovial membrane lining the joint capsule"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.6","dok":2,"approach":["Bleeding needs a vascular tissue that faces the joint cavity.","Articular hyaline cartilage and the inner menisci have no blood vessels and are nourished by synovial fluid.","The synovial membrane has a rich capillary network under its lining cells, so fragile capillaries there bleed into the joint."],"why":"A synovial joint has articular hyaline cartilage on the bone ends, a cavity filled with synovial fluid, and a capsule lined by synovial membrane. The synovial membrane is a thin layer of synoviocytes that make hyaluronan and lubricin, lying on a loose, highly vascular subintima. In scurvy the walls of these capillaries are weak, so they bleed into the cavity and cause hemarthrosis. Articular cartilage has no vessels at all.","whyNot":["Only the outer third of each meniscus has a blood supply. The inner portion is avascular.","Articular hyaline cartilage is avascular and gets its nutrients by diffusion from synovial fluid, so it cannot bleed.","The calcified cartilage under the tidemark is avascular and is covered by the articular cartilage, so it does not face the cavity.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Structure of synovial joints","Vascular supply of joint tissues"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-4"]}},{"id":"MS-C03-Q4","stem":"Additional studies show a serum calcium of 7.4 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5) with a normal albumin, magnesium 0.8 mg/dL (1.7 to 2.2), and parathyroid hormone 12 pg/mL (10 to 65). His calcium stays low despite intravenous calcium until magnesium is replaced. Which of the following best explains his hypocalcemia?","options":["Reduced osteocalcin carboxylation from vitamin K depletion","Reduced lysyl oxidase activity from copper depletion","Impaired PTH secretion and action from magnesium depletion","Reduced renal calcium reabsorption from vitamin C depletion"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.6","dok":3,"approach":["Low calcium should drive PTH up, yet his PTH is low-normal, so the parathyroid response is blunted.","Severe hypomagnesemia, common in alcohol use disorder, impairs PTH release and makes bone and kidney resistant to PTH.","Calcium corrects only after magnesium is replaced, which confirms the link."],"why":"Magnesium is needed for the parathyroid glands to secrete PTH and for target tissues to respond to it. In severe magnesium depletion, PTH stays inappropriately low and calcium falls. Alcohol use disorder causes magnesium loss through poor intake, diarrhea, and renal wasting. Calcium given alone does not correct the problem until magnesium is restored. Mild hypomagnesemia can stimulate PTH, and the block appears with severe depletion.","whyNot":["Vitamin K carboxylates osteocalcin and clotting factors. Deficiency affects bone quality and clotting, and his PT is normal.","Copper is the cofactor for lysyl oxidase, which cross-links collagen. Copper deficiency weakens connective tissue but does not lower calcium.","Correct.","Vitamin C has no direct role in renal calcium handling."],"study":{"topics":["Magnesium and PTH secretion","Mineral and cofactor roles in bone"],"comps":["MS-8","MS-13-A3"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C04","system":"MS","dx":"Ankylosing spondylitis","codes":["MS-13-B1"],"case":"A 26-year-old man comes to the physician because of 2 years of low back and buttock pain with morning stiffness that lasts about 90 minutes. The pain wakes him in the second half of the night, improves with exercise, and does not improve with rest. He has also had heel pain. Physical examination shows tenderness over both sacroiliac joints and at the Achilles tendon insertions. Forward flexion of the lumbar spine is reduced, and chest expansion is 2.0 cm (normal 5 cm or more). Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 38 mm/h (0 to 15), and rheumatoid factor is negative. Radiographs show bilateral sacroiliitis with erosions and sclerosis. HLA-B27 testing is positive. Diagnosis: ankylosing spondylitis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C04-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the normal function of the HLA molecule linked to his disease?","options":["Presents endocytosed peptides to CD4 T cells","Presents lipid antigens to NKT cells","Presents cytosolic peptides to CD8 T cells","Binds immune complexes as a complement protein"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-13-B1","obj":"MS-13-B.1","dok":2,"approach":["HLA-B is one of the three classical MHC class I loci (A, B, and C).","Class I molecules are found on nearly all nucleated cells and display peptides made inside the cell.","Peptides on class I molecules are recognized by CD8 cytotoxic T cells."],"why":"HLA-B27 is an MHC class I molecule. Like other class I molecules, it carries peptides from cytosolic proteins, cut by the proteasome and loaded in the endoplasmic reticulum, to the cell surface for CD8 T cells. How it causes spondyloarthritis is not fully known. Leading ideas are presentation of cross-reactive microbial peptides, misfolding of HLA-B27 heavy chains that triggers cell stress, and IL-23 and IL-17 driven inflammation at entheses. The result is enthesitis and sacroiliitis that heal with new bone.","whyNot":["This is the function of MHC class II molecules (HLA-DR, DP, and DQ). HLA-DR4 is linked to rheumatoid arthritis.","CD1d, a nonclassical molecule, presents lipids to NKT cells. HLA-B27 presents peptides.","Correct.","Complement proteins C2, C4, and factor B are encoded in the MHC class III region. HLA-B27 is not a complement protein."],"study":{"topics":["HLA-B27 and seronegative spondyloarthropathies","MHC class I antigen presentation"],"comps":["MS-13-B1","MS-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C04-Q2","stem":"Twelve years later, a high-pitched, blowing, early diastolic decrescendo murmur is heard along the sternal border while he leans forward. Which of the following hemodynamic findings is most likely?","options":["Wide pulse pressure with a low diastolic pressure","Large v waves in the jugular venous pulse","Pulsus paradoxus with equal diastolic chamber pressures","Narrow pulse pressure with a delayed carotid upstroke"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-13-B1","obj":"MS-13-B.2","dok":3,"approach":["An early diastolic decrescendo murmur along the sternal border is aortic regurgitation.","Aortitis in ankylosing spondylitis dilates the aortic root and pulls the cusps apart.","Blood flows back into the ventricle during diastole, so diastolic pressure falls, and the larger stroke volume raises systolic pressure."],"why":"Ankylosing spondylitis can inflame the aortic root and the base of the valve. The root dilates and the cusps fail to close, causing aortic regurgitation. Backflow during diastole drops aortic diastolic pressure, while the left ventricle ejects a larger stroke volume, raising systolic pressure. The result is a wide pulse pressure with bounding pulses. Other extra-articular features of the disease include anterior uveitis, restrictive lung disease, and fractures of the fused spine.","whyNot":["Correct.","Large v waves suggest tricuspid regurgitation, which causes a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border.","Pulsus paradoxus with equal diastolic pressures in all chambers is cardiac tamponade.","A narrow pulse pressure with a slow, delayed upstroke is aortic stenosis, which causes a systolic ejection murmur."],"study":{"topics":["Extra-articular features of ankylosing spondylitis","Hemodynamics of aortic regurgitation"],"comps":["MS-13-B1"]}},{"id":"MS-C04-Q3","stem":"As the disease advances, his spine fuses in the forward-flexed posture that eases his pain. Which of the following changes in spinal curvature is most likely?","options":["Increased lumbar lordosis and reduced thoracic kyphosis","Loss of lumbar lordosis and increased thoracic kyphosis","Lateral thoracic curvature with vertebral rotation","Increased cervical lordosis with normal thoracic curvature"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-3","obj":"MS-3.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the normal curves: cervical and lumbar lordosis, and thoracic and sacral kyphosis.","Bony bridges called syndesmophytes join the vertebrae in whatever posture the patient holds.","A forward-flexed posture flattens the lumbar spine and bends the thoracic spine further forward."],"why":"The adult spine has two curves that are convex forward (cervical and lumbar lordosis) and two that are convex backward (thoracic and sacral kyphosis). In ankylosing spondylitis, inflammation at the vertebral corners and the annulus fibrosus heals with syndesmophytes that fuse the spine, the bamboo spine on radiographs. Patients lean forward for comfort, so the lumbar lordosis is lost and thoracic kyphosis increases. Severe thoracic deformity, like severe scoliosis, stiffens the chest wall and limits lung expansion.","whyNot":["Excess lumbar lordosis occurs with pregnancy, obesity, or weak abdominal muscles. Inflammation and fusion in this disease flatten the lumbar spine.","Correct.","Lateral curvature with vertebral rotation is scoliosis, which is usually idiopathic and starts in adolescence.","The cervical spine also stiffens and the head is pushed forward, so cervical lordosis is lost."],"study":{"topics":["Normal spinal curvatures","Spinal posture in ankylosing spondylitis"],"comps":["MS-3","MS-13-B1"]}},{"id":"MS-C04-Q4","stem":"His reduced chest expansion is partly due to inflammation and fusion of the posterior synovial joints that let the ribs rise during inspiration. These joints are formed with which of the following vertebral features?","options":["Transverse foramina of the cervical vertebrae","Bifid spinous processes of the cervical vertebrae","Mammillary processes of the lumbar vertebrae","Costal facets of the thoracic vertebrae"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-3","obj":"MS-3.1","dok":2,"approach":["The ribs move at joints with the vertebrae during breathing, which lifts and widens the chest.","Only thoracic vertebrae carry costal facets on their bodies and transverse processes for the rib heads and tubercles.","Fusion of these synovial joints locks the ribs and limits chest expansion."],"why":"Each typical rib meets the thoracic spine at two synovial joints. The rib head joins costal facets on the vertebral bodies (costovertebral joint), and the rib tubercle joins the facet on the transverse process (costotransverse joint). Rib elevation at these joints expands the chest during inspiration. Ankylosing spondylitis inflames and fuses these joints, so chest expansion falls and breathing depends more on the diaphragm.","whyNot":["Transverse foramina carry the vertebral arteries in the cervical spine. Ribs do not attach there.","Bifid cervical spines anchor the ligamentum nuchae and neck muscles, not ribs.","Mammillary processes on lumbar vertebrae anchor deep back muscles. Lumbar vertebrae have no rib joints.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Regional features of vertebrae","Costovertebral joints and breathing"],"comps":["MS-3","MS-4"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C05","system":"MS","dx":"Septic prepatellar bursitis from Staphylococcus aureus","codes":["MS-13-B2"],"case":"A 41-year-old woman who installs floor tile comes to the emergency department because of 3 days of painful swelling over the front of her right knee. She works kneeling most of the day and had several small abrasions on that knee last week. Temperature is 38.3 °C (100.9 °F), pulse 98/min, respirations 16/min, and blood pressure 126/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a warm, red, fluctuant swelling directly over the patella. She can flex the knee to 120 degrees with pain only at full flexion, and there is no joint effusion. Aspiration of the swelling yields cloudy fluid with 52,000 leukocytes/mm3, 90% neutrophils. Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in clusters, and culture grows a catalase-positive, coagulase-positive organism. Diagnosis: septic prepatellar bursitis caused by Staphylococcus aureus.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C05-Q1","stem":"Which of the following is the most likely route by which the organism reached the bursa?","options":["Direct entry through overlying skin abrasions","Contiguous spread from osteomyelitis of the patella","Spread from the adjacent knee joint cavity","Hematogenous seeding during transient bacteremia"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B2","obj":"MS-13-B.3","dok":3,"approach":["The prepatellar bursa lies between the skin and the patella, just under the skin surface.","It does not communicate with the knee joint, which is why knee motion is preserved and there is no effusion.","Superficial bursae such as the prepatellar and olecranon bursae are usually infected by organisms entering through skin breaks over them."],"why":"Septic bursitis most often involves the superficial prepatellar and olecranon bursae, which take repeated pressure and minor skin injury. Kneeling work causes chronic friction, the cause of most prepatellar bursitis, and abrasions let skin flora such as Staphylococcus aureus enter the bursa directly. Septic arthritis, by contrast, usually follows bacteremia. Deeper bursae, such as the subacromial and trochanteric bursae, are more often inflamed by overuse than infected. Preserved knee motion without an effusion helps tell bursitis from septic arthritis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Patellar osteomyelitis is rare and nothing here suggests it. Her skin abrasions over the bursa explain the infection.","The prepatellar bursa does not communicate with the knee joint, and her knee has no effusion.","Hematogenous seeding is the usual route for septic arthritis and for childhood osteomyelitis. It is uncommon for superficial bursae."],"study":{"topics":["Bursitis by site and cause","Prepatellar bursa anatomy"],"comps":["MS-13-B2","MS-12"]}},{"id":"MS-C05-Q2","stem":"The bursal fluid contains IgG antibodies against the organism, yet neutrophils ingest the bacteria poorly. Which of the following bacterial products best explains this finding?","options":["M protein","IgA protease","Exfoliative toxin","Protein A"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-12","obj":"MS-12.1","dok":2,"approach":["Gram-positive cocci in clusters that are catalase and coagulase positive identify Staphylococcus aureus.","IgG opsonizes bacteria when its Fc region binds Fc receptors on neutrophils.","Staphylococcal protein A binds the Fc region of IgG, so the antibody is held the wrong way round and cannot opsonize."],"why":"Protein A is a cell wall protein of Staphylococcus aureus that binds the Fc portion of IgG. Antibody bound by its Fc end cannot link the bacterium to neutrophil Fc receptors, and complement activation is reduced, so phagocytosis fails. Other factors help S. aureus cause bone and joint infection: adhesins that bind collagen and fibronectin, and biofilm on prosthetic hardware. Together they make it the most common cause of osteomyelitis and septic arthritis.","whyNot":["M protein is the main antiphagocytic protein of Streptococcus pyogenes, a catalase-negative organism that grows in chains.","IgA protease is made by Neisseria, Haemophilus influenzae, and Streptococcus pneumoniae. It cleaves IgA at mucosal surfaces and does not act on IgG.","Exfoliative toxin from S. aureus splits desmoglein 1 and causes scalded skin syndrome. It does not block opsonization.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Staphylococcus aureus virulence factors"],"comps":["MS-12","MS-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C05-Q3","stem":"If the infection had spread into her knee joint, the articular cartilage could have been destroyed within days. Which of the following most directly causes this rapid cartilage loss?","options":["Immune complexes activating complement in synovium","Proteases and oxidants released by neutrophils","Pannus with RANKL-activated osteoclasts","CD8 T cells lysing chondrocytes"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.6","dok":3,"approach":["Septic arthritis fills the joint with neutrophils, much like the 52,000 cells/mm3 in her bursal fluid.","Activated neutrophils release elastase, collagenases, and reactive oxygen species.","These degrade cartilage matrix within days, much faster than any chronic autoimmune process."],"why":"In septic arthritis, bacteria in the joint cavity recruit large numbers of neutrophils. Neutrophil elastase, matrix metalloproteinases, and reactive oxygen species break down aggrecan and type II collagen. Cytokines such as IL-1 and TNF also stimulate chondrocytes to release their own proteases. Because cartilage has no blood supply and repairs poorly, damage can be permanent within days, which is why an infected joint is drained urgently.","whyNot":["Immune complex deposition with complement activation is type III hypersensitivity, as in SLE arthritis, which is usually nonerosive.","Correct.","Pannus with RANKL-driven osteoclasts erodes cartilage and bone in rheumatoid arthritis over months to years.","CD8 T cells kill cells that show foreign peptides on class I molecules, as in viral infection. They are not the main effectors in acute bacterial joint infection."],"study":{"topics":["Cartilage destruction in septic arthritis"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-12"]}},{"id":"MS-C05-Q4","stem":"After drainage and antibiotics, the skin incision over the bursa heals. Which of the following best describes the main collagen in the granulation tissue at 1 week and in the mature scar months later?","options":["Type III at 1 week, replaced by type I in the scar","Type II at 1 week, replaced by type I in the scar","Type I at 1 week, replaced by type III in the scar","Type IV at 1 week, replaced by type III in the scar"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Granulation tissue forms first, with new capillaries and fibroblasts laying down a thin, flexible collagen.","That early collagen is type III, the type in reticular fibers and vessel walls.","During remodeling, fibroblasts replace it with stronger type I collagen, the type in tendon, ligament, and bone."],"why":"Early granulation tissue is rich in type III collagen, which forms thin fibers suited to a new, vascular scaffold. Over weeks to months, matrix metalloproteinases remove it and fibroblasts lay down type I collagen, which has high tensile strength. A mature scar reaches only about 70 to 80 percent of the strength of normal skin. Type II collagen belongs to hyaline cartilage and the nucleus pulposus, and type IV to basement membranes.","whyNot":["Correct.","Type II collagen is found in hyaline cartilage, the vitreous, and the nucleus pulposus, not in healing skin.","This reverses the real sequence. Type I collagen is the strong, late collagen of a mature scar.","Type IV collagen forms basement membranes. It does not make up the bulk of granulation tissue or scar."],"study":{"topics":["Collagen types and their tissues","Collagen in wound healing"],"comps":["MS-6"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C06","system":"MS","dx":"Fibromyalgia","codes":["MS-13-B3"],"case":"A 43-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 10 months of pain in her neck, shoulders, back, hips, and legs on both sides of the body. She also reports fatigue, waking unrefreshed despite 8 hours in bed, and trouble concentrating at work. Physical examination shows tenderness to light pressure at many soft tissue sites. The joints show no swelling, muscle strength is 5/5 throughout, and deep tendon reflexes are 2+ and symmetric. Laboratory studies show: erythrocyte sedimentation rate 8 mm/h (0 to 20), C-reactive protein 0.3 mg/dL (0 to 0.8), creatine kinase 88 U/L (30 to 170), thyroid-stimulating hormone 1.9 μU/mL (0.5 to 4.5), and a normal complete blood count. Diagnosis: fibromyalgia.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C06-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her widespread pain?","options":["Denervation of muscle fibers by motor neuron loss","CD8 T cell infiltration of skeletal muscle fibers","Increased central amplification of sensory input","Inflammation of bursae around the shoulders and hips"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B3","obj":"MS-13-B.4","dok":2,"approach":["Widespread pain for more than 3 months with fatigue, unrefreshing sleep, and poor concentration fits fibromyalgia.","Normal ESR, CRP, CK, strength, and joints rule out inflammation and muscle injury.","The pain comes from increased sensitivity of central pain pathways, so ordinary pressure is felt as pain."],"why":"Fibromyalgia is a disorder of central pain sensitization. Pathways in the spinal cord and brain amplify incoming signals, and descending pain inhibition is weaker, so pressure that should feel normal is painful. There is no tissue inflammation or muscle damage, which is why inflammatory markers, CK, and the joint examination are normal. It is more common in women and is linked with poor sleep, fatigue, and mood disorders.","whyNot":["Motor neuron loss causes weakness, atrophy, fasciculations, and reflex changes. Her strength and reflexes are normal.","CD8 T cell invasion of muscle fibers is polymyositis, which causes proximal weakness and a high CK.","Correct.","Bursitis around the shoulder and hip girdles is typical of polymyalgia rheumatica, which occurs after age 50 with a high ESR."],"study":{"topics":["Central sensitization in fibromyalgia"],"comps":["MS-13-B3","MS-10"]}},{"id":"MS-C06-Q2","stem":"During the neurologic examination, tapping her patellar tendon produces brisk extension of the knee. Which of the following receptors and spinal cord segments mediate this reflex?","options":["Golgi tendon organ with group Ib afferents, mainly L4","Golgi tendon organ with group Ib afferents, mainly S1","Muscle spindle with group Ia afferents, mainly L4","Muscle spindle with group Ia afferents, mainly S1"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.3","dok":2,"approach":["Tapping the tendon briefly stretches the quadriceps.","Stretch is sensed by muscle spindles, whose group Ia afferents synapse directly on alpha motor neurons to the same muscle.","The patellar reflex runs through L2 to L4, mainly L4. The Achilles reflex tests S1."],"why":"The patellar reflex is a monosynaptic stretch reflex. A tendon tap stretches the quadriceps and its intrafusal fibers, Ia afferents fire, and they excite alpha motor neurons in the L2 to L4 segments, mainly L4, so the quadriceps contracts. Gamma motor neurons set spindle sensitivity. Golgi tendon organs sit at the muscle-tendon junction, sense tension through Ib afferents, and inhibit the same muscle. Normal reflexes in fibromyalgia fit the absence of nerve or muscle disease.","whyNot":["Golgi tendon organs sense tension and inhibit the same muscle through Ib afferents. They do not drive the knee jerk.","Both parts are wrong. Ib afferents from tendon organs mediate inhibition, and S1 serves the Achilles reflex.","Correct.","Muscle spindles and Ia fibers are right, but S1 is the level of the Achilles reflex."],"study":{"topics":["Muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs","Reflexes and their root levels"],"comps":["MS-10"]}},{"id":"MS-C06-Q3","stem":"She begins a graded aerobic walking and cycling program. After 3 months, which of the following changes in her leg muscles is most likely?","options":["Higher glycolytic enzyme activity in type II fibers","Greater mitochondrial and capillary density","Hypertrophy of type II fibers with more myofibrils","Conversion of type I fibers into type IIx fibers"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.6","dok":2,"approach":["Endurance exercise is sustained, low-force work that depends on oxidative metabolism.","Type I fibers are slow, oxidative, and fatigue resistant, and trained muscle adapts by increasing oxidative capacity.","Resistance training, not aerobic training, causes type II fiber hypertrophy."],"why":"Repeated aerobic exercise increases mitochondrial number and size, oxidative enzymes, myoglobin, and capillary density in trained muscle, mainly in type I and type IIa fibers. These changes let the muscle burn more fatty acid and produce less lactate at a given workload. Heavy resistance training instead enlarges type II fibers by adding myofibrils. Type I fibers look red because of their myoglobin and dense capillary supply.","whyNot":["Endurance training raises oxidative capacity. Sprint and resistance training are the stimuli for higher glycolytic capacity.","Correct.","Type II fiber hypertrophy is the main response to heavy resistance training.","Endurance training shifts fibers toward more oxidative types, such as type IIx toward type IIa. It does not convert type I fibers into type IIx."],"study":{"topics":["Muscle fiber types","Training adaptations in muscle"],"comps":["MS-7","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C06-Q4","stem":"Her antinuclear antibody test is positive at a titer of 1:80, and she is worried that she has systemic lupus erythematosus. Which of the following antibody results would be most specific for that diagnosis?","options":["Anti-Scl-70","Anti-Jo-1","Anti-CCP","Anti-Smith"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.3","dok":2,"approach":["A low-titer ANA is common in healthy people, especially women, and is not specific.","Disease-specific antibodies are used to confirm a suspected connective tissue disease.","Anti-Smith and anti-dsDNA are highly specific for SLE. The other choices point to other diseases."],"why":"ANA is sensitive for SLE but not specific, and a titer of 1:80 is found in more than 1 in 10 healthy people. Anti-Smith antibodies, against small nuclear ribonucleoproteins, are highly specific for SLE, though only a minority of patients have them. Anti-dsDNA is also specific and tracks disease activity and lupus nephritis. Her normal inflammatory markers and examination already make SLE unlikely.","whyNot":["Anti-Scl-70 (anti-topoisomerase I) marks diffuse systemic sclerosis, with skin thickening and interstitial lung disease.","Anti-Jo-1 marks inflammatory myositis with interstitial lung disease.","Anti-CCP is the most specific antibody for rheumatoid arthritis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Autoantibodies in rheumatic diseases"],"comps":["MS-11"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C07","system":"MS","dx":"Acute gout of the first metatarsophalangeal joint","codes":["MS-13-B4"],"case":"A 58-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of sudden, severe pain in his right great toe that woke him at 3 AM. The evening before, he drank several beers and ate a large steak dinner. He has hypertension treated with hydrochlorothiazide. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse 92/min, respirations 14/min, and blood pressure 148/88 mm Hg. The first metatarsophalangeal joint is red, hot, swollen, and too tender to touch. Serum urate is 9.2 mg/dL (3.5 to 7.2). Aspiration of the joint yields cloudy fluid with 28,000 leukocytes/mm3, mostly neutrophils. Gram stain and culture are negative. Diagnosis: acute gout of the first metatarsophalangeal joint.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C07-Q1","stem":"Polarized light microscopy of the joint fluid is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Needle-shaped crystals with strong negative birefringence","Rhomboid crystals with weak positive birefringence","Needle-shaped crystals with strong positive birefringence","Flat rectangular plates with notched corners"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B4","obj":"MS-13-B.6","dok":2,"approach":["Sudden, severe first MTP pain after alcohol and a large meat meal, with high urate and a thiazide, points to gout.","Monosodium urate forms long, thin needles.","Urate crystals are strongly negatively birefringent: yellow when parallel to the compensator axis and blue when perpendicular."],"why":"Gout is confirmed by finding monosodium urate crystals in joint fluid, often inside neutrophils. They are needle shaped and strongly negatively birefringent, so they look yellow when their long axis lies parallel to the compensator and blue when perpendicular. Other features of gout include tophi (urate deposits in soft tissue), punched-out bone erosions with overhanging edges, and uric acid kidney stones. Serum urate can be normal during an attack, so crystals are the standard for diagnosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Weakly positively birefringent rhomboid crystals are calcium pyrophosphate, seen in pseudogout.","Urate crystals are needle shaped but negatively birefringent. Strong positive birefringence does not fit urate.","Flat plates with notched corners are cholesterol crystals, found in chronic effusions such as long-standing rheumatoid arthritis."],"study":{"topics":["Gout findings and urate crystals"],"comps":["MS-13-B4"]}},{"id":"MS-C07-Q2","stem":"Before this attack, his 24-hour urinary uric acid excretion on a regular diet was 410 mg/day (people who overproduce urate typically excrete more than 800 mg/day). Which of the following best explains his hyperuricemia?","options":["Increased cell turnover releasing nucleic acid purines","Reduced renal urate excretion from thiazide therapy","Excess urate production from glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency","Increased de novo purine synthesis from HGPRT deficiency"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-B4","obj":"MS-13-B.5","dok":3,"approach":["Compare urinary uric acid with serum urate. A high serum level with normal urinary output means the kidney is not clearing urate well.","About 90 percent of gout comes from underexcretion.","Thiazides cause volume contraction and compete with urate for tubular secretion, so net reabsorption rises."],"why":"Uric acid is the end product of purine breakdown: xanthine oxidase converts hypoxanthine to xanthine and xanthine to uric acid. Serum urate rises from overproduction or, far more often, from reduced renal excretion. Thiazide and loop diuretics cause volume contraction and compete with urate at organic anion transporters in the proximal tubule. A normal 24-hour urinary uric acid with a high serum urate shows underexcretion here, and alcohol and purine-rich meat added to the load that night.","whyNot":["High cell turnover, as in tumor lysis or myeloproliferative disease, causes overproduction with high urinary uric acid.","Correct.","Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency (von Gierke disease) raises urate and appears in childhood with hypoglycemia and hepatomegaly.","HGPRT deficiency (Lesch-Nyhan syndrome) blocks purine salvage, raises de novo synthesis, and causes overproduction with high urinary uric acid, usually in boys with neurologic disease."],"study":{"topics":["Causes of hyperuricemia","Purine breakdown and xanthine oxidase"],"comps":["MS-13-B4","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C07-Q3","stem":"Neutrophils fill the joint within hours after crystals are shed into it. Which of the following events in synovial macrophages most directly starts this inflammation?","options":["Sensitized CD4 T cells releasing interferon gamma","Immune complexes fixing complement in vessel walls","NLRP3 inflammasome activation releasing IL-1 beta","IgE cross-linking on mast cells releasing histamine"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.6","dok":3,"approach":["Urate crystals are a danger signal recognized by innate immunity, with no need for prior sensitization.","Macrophages take up the crystals, which activates the NLRP3 inflammasome and caspase-1.","Caspase-1 cleaves pro-IL-1 beta to IL-1 beta, which recruits neutrophils and drives the rapid, intense attack."],"why":"Synovial macrophages take up monosodium urate crystals, which activate the NLRP3 inflammasome. This complex activates caspase-1, which cleaves pro-IL-1 beta into active IL-1 beta. IL-1 beta activates endothelium, triggers more cytokines, and brings in large numbers of neutrophils within hours. Because this is innate immunity, no prior exposure is needed, which explains the sudden onset of attacks.","whyNot":["Sensitized CD4 T cells releasing interferon gamma is type IV hypersensitivity, which develops over days.","Immune complex deposition with complement is type III hypersensitivity, as in SLE or serum sickness.","Correct.","IgE-mediated mast cell release is type I hypersensitivity, as in allergy and anaphylaxis."],"study":{"topics":["NLRP3 inflammasome in gout"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-13-B4"]}},{"id":"MS-C07-Q4","stem":"A 74-year-old woman seen the same day has an acutely swollen knee. Her joint fluid shows rhomboid crystals with weak positive birefringence, and a knee radiograph shows linear calcification of the menisci. Which of the following conditions is most strongly associated with her disorder?","options":["Tumor lysis syndrome","Thiazide diuretic use","Hemochromatosis","Lesch-Nyhan syndrome"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B","obj":"MS-13-B.15","dok":3,"approach":["Rhomboid, weakly positively birefringent crystals with chondrocalcinosis identify calcium pyrophosphate deposition, or pseudogout.","Pseudogout usually affects older adults, most often the knee and wrist.","Its classic metabolic associations are hemochromatosis, hyperparathyroidism, and hypomagnesemia. The other choices raise urate and cause gout."],"why":"Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals form in cartilage and cause chondrocalcinosis, the linear calcification of menisci and articular cartilage seen on radiographs. Acute attacks mimic gout but usually strike the knee or wrist of older adults. Hemochromatosis and primary hyperparathyroidism are classic associations, as are hypomagnesemia and hypophosphatasia. Gout instead favors the first MTP joint and is linked to hyperuricemia.","whyNot":["Tumor lysis releases nucleic acids that raise urate and can cause gout and uric acid nephropathy.","Thiazides reduce urate excretion and promote gout. They are not a recognized cause of calcium pyrophosphate deposition.","Correct.","Lesch-Nyhan syndrome causes urate overproduction and gout."],"study":{"topics":["Pseudogout compared with gout"],"comps":["MS-13-B","MS-13-B4"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C08","system":"MS","dx":"Dermatomyositis with ovarian cancer","codes":["MS-13-B5"],"case":"A 54-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 3 months of increasing difficulty climbing stairs, rising from a chair, and lifting her arms to wash her hair. She has also had a rash on her face and hands, abdominal bloating, early satiety, and a 6-kg (13-lb) weight loss. Physical examination shows a violaceous rash on the upper eyelids with periorbital edema and raised, scaly, violet papules over the knuckles. Strength is 4-/5 in the hip flexors and deltoids and 5/5 distally. Serum creatine kinase is 2,850 U/L (30 to 170). Serum CA-125 is 410 U/mL (under 35), and imaging shows a left adnexal mass. Biopsy confirms high-grade serous ovarian carcinoma. Diagnosis: dermatomyositis associated with ovarian cancer.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C08-Q1","stem":"A deltoid muscle biopsy shows atrophy of fibers at the edges of the fascicles and inflammation around perimysial vessels. Which of the following immune mechanisms best explains this pattern?","options":["CD8 T cell lysis of fibers that express MHC class I","Granulomas formed by activated macrophages","Antibody blockade of acetylcholine receptors","Complement attack on intramuscular capillaries"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-13-B5","obj":"MS-13-B.7","dok":3,"approach":["Atrophy at the edges of fascicles, called perifascicular atrophy, is the signature of dermatomyositis.","In dermatomyositis, antibodies and the membrane attack complex injure endomysial capillaries, which drop out.","Fibers at the fascicle edges are farthest from the remaining blood supply, so they become ischemic and shrink."],"why":"Dermatomyositis is a complement-driven injury of small vessels. Membrane attack complex is deposited on endomysial capillaries, which are lost. The watershed fibers at the edge of each fascicle become ischemic and atrophic, giving perifascicular atrophy, with CD4 T cells and B cells around perimysial vessels. Polymyositis instead shows CD8 T cells invading fibers within the endomysium. Both cause proximal weakness and a high CK, and both can come with anti-Jo-1 antibodies and interstitial lung disease. Dermatomyositis in adults is linked to cancers such as ovarian, lung, and gastric carcinoma.","whyNot":["CD8 T cell invasion of fibers that express MHC class I is the mechanism of polymyositis, which lacks perifascicular atrophy.","Granulomas in muscle suggest sarcoidosis. They do not produce perifascicular atrophy.","Antibodies against acetylcholine receptors cause myasthenia gravis, with fatigable ptosis and a normal CK.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Dermatomyositis compared with polymyositis"],"comps":["MS-13-B5","MS-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C08-Q2","stem":"She develops difficulty starting a swallow, with coughing and nasal regurgitation of liquids. Which of the following histologic features describes the normal muscle tissue responsible for this symptom?","options":["Branched fibers with central nuclei and intercalated discs","Fusiform cells with central nuclei and no striations","Striated fibers with central nuclei and dyads","Long striated fibers with many peripheral nuclei"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.2","dok":3,"approach":["Trouble starting a swallow with nasal regurgitation points to weakness of the pharynx and upper esophagus.","These muscles are skeletal muscle, so inflammatory myositis can weaken them.","Skeletal muscle fibers are long, striated, and multinucleated, with nuclei at the periphery."],"why":"The pharyngeal constrictors and the upper third of the esophagus are skeletal muscle, the middle third is mixed, and the lower third is smooth muscle. Myositis weakens the striated muscles, so the result is difficulty starting a swallow, coughing, and nasal regurgitation. Skeletal muscle fibers are long, multinucleated cells with peripheral nuclei and triads of T tubules and sarcoplasmic reticulum. Cardiac muscle has branched cells with central nuclei and intercalated discs, and smooth muscle has fusiform cells with a central nucleus and no striations.","whyNot":["Branched fibers with central nuclei and intercalated discs are cardiac muscle.","Fusiform cells without striations are smooth muscle, found in the lower esophagus. Weakness there slows passage of food through the chest and does not impair starting a swallow.","Central nuclei with dyads describe cardiac muscle, where each T tubule pairs with one sarcoplasmic reticulum cisterna.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-13-B5"]}},{"id":"MS-C08-Q3","stem":"In polymyositis, by contrast, biopsy shows CD8 T cells surrounding and invading non-necrotic muscle fibers. Which of the following types of hypersensitivity best describes the injury in polymyositis?","options":["Type II, antibodies against cell surface antigens","Type IV, T cell-mediated cytotoxicity","Type III, immune complex deposition","Type I, IgE-mediated mast cell release"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.5","dok":2,"approach":["Classify by effector: IgE (type I), antibody against fixed antigen (type II), immune complexes (type III), and T cells (type IV).","In polymyositis, muscle fibers display MHC class I, and CD8 T cells recognize and kill them.","Injury carried out by T cells without antibody is type IV hypersensitivity."],"why":"Type IV hypersensitivity is injury caused by T cells, either CD4 T cells that release cytokines or CD8 T cells that kill target cells directly. In polymyositis, muscle fibers up-regulate MHC class I, and CD8 T cells with perforin and granzymes invade and destroy them. Other musculoskeletal examples: rheumatic fever polyarthritis and Lambert-Eaton syndrome are type II, and SLE arthritis is type III.","whyNot":["Type II reactions use antibodies against fixed cell or matrix antigens, as in rheumatic fever and Lambert-Eaton syndrome.","Correct.","Type III reactions come from circulating immune complexes, as in SLE arthritis.","Type I reactions are immediate IgE-driven responses such as anaphylaxis."],"study":{"topics":["Hypersensitivity types in musculoskeletal disease"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-13-B5"]}},{"id":"MS-C08-Q4","stem":"In her intact deltoid fibers, a motor nerve impulse still triggers contraction. Which of the following events directly links T-tubule depolarization to calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum in these fibers?","options":["Calcium entry through L-type channels triggering RyR2","IP3 binding to receptors on the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Dihydropyridine receptor shape change opening RyR1","Calcium-calmodulin activation of myosin light chain kinase"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.1","dok":2,"approach":["Skeletal muscle couples voltage to calcium release mechanically.","The dihydropyridine receptor in the T tubule acts as a voltage sensor and is physically linked to ryanodine receptor 1 in the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","Calcium then binds troponin C, and SERCA later pumps it back to end the contraction."],"why":"In skeletal muscle, the action potential spreads down the T tubules and changes the shape of the dihydropyridine receptor, an L-type calcium channel that works mainly as a voltage sensor. It is mechanically coupled to ryanodine receptor 1 on the terminal cisternae, which opens and releases calcium. No calcium entry from outside the cell is required. Calcium binds troponin C, tropomyosin moves aside, and cross-bridges form. SERCA pumps calcium back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum for relaxation.","whyNot":["Calcium-induced calcium release through L-type channels and RyR2 is how cardiac muscle couples excitation to contraction.","IP3-mediated release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum contributes to smooth muscle contraction.","Correct.","Calcium-calmodulin activation of myosin light chain kinase drives smooth muscle contraction. Skeletal muscle uses troponin C."],"study":{"topics":["Excitation-contraction coupling in skeletal muscle"],"comps":["MS-7"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C09","system":"MS","dx":"Polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis","codes":["MS-13-B6"],"case":"A 72-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 6 weeks of aching and stiffness in both shoulders, her neck, and both hips. Morning stiffness lasts about 2 hours, and she needs help getting out of bed. For 2 weeks she has also had a new right-sided headache, scalp tenderness when combing her hair, and jaw pain when chewing. Temperature is 37.6 °C (99.7 °F). Physical examination shows painful, limited active shoulder abduction, with full strength when pain is controlled. The right temporal artery is tender and thickened with a reduced pulse. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 86 mm/h (0 to 30), C-reactive protein is 5.8 mg/dL (0 to 0.8), and serum creatine kinase is 62 U/L (30 to 170). Diagnosis: polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C09-Q1","stem":"Without prompt treatment, she is at greatest risk for permanent vision loss. Which of the following mechanisms most likely causes this complication?","options":["Occlusion of the posterior ciliary arteries","Compression of the optic chiasm by a pituitary mass","Acute closure of the anterior chamber angle","Thrombosis of the central retinal vein"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B6","obj":"MS-13-B.9","dok":3,"approach":["Headache, scalp tenderness, jaw claudication, and an abnormal temporal artery in a patient with polymyalgia rheumatica indicate giant cell arteritis.","The arteritis involves branches of the external carotid and ophthalmic arteries.","Narrowing of the short posterior ciliary arteries cuts off blood to the optic nerve head, causing ischemic optic neuropathy and sudden vision loss."],"why":"Polymyalgia rheumatica occurs in adults over 50, more often women, with shoulder and hip girdle pain and stiffness, a high ESR, and a normal CK. About 15 to 20 percent of patients also have giant cell arteritis, a granulomatous vasculitis of large and medium arteries. Narrowing of the posterior ciliary arteries, branches of the ophthalmic artery, starves the optic nerve head and causes arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy. Vision loss is sudden and often permanent.","whyNot":["Correct.","A pituitary mass compresses the chiasm and causes a slowly developing bitemporal hemianopia.","Acute angle-closure glaucoma causes a painful red eye with a fixed, mid-dilated pupil. It is not caused by arteritis.","Central retinal vein occlusion causes painless vision loss with widespread retinal hemorrhages and is linked to hypertension and diabetes."],"study":{"topics":["Polymyalgia rheumatica and giant cell arteritis","Blood supply of the optic nerve head"],"comps":["MS-13-B6"]}},{"id":"MS-C09-Q2","stem":"A colleague suggests polymyositis as an alternative diagnosis. Which of the following findings in this patient argues most strongly against inflammatory myositis?","options":["Elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate","Onset after the age of 50 years","Symmetric proximal girdle involvement","Normal serum creatine kinase"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B6","obj":"MS-13-B.8","dok":2,"approach":["Inflammatory myositis destroys muscle fibers, which releases creatine kinase into the blood.","Polymyalgia rheumatica inflames bursae, tendon sheaths, and joints around the girdles, and spares the muscle fibers.","A normal CK with full strength points away from myositis."],"why":"Polymyositis and dermatomyositis cause true proximal weakness from myofiber necrosis, so CK is high, often many times normal, and biopsy shows inflammatory infiltrates. In polymyalgia rheumatica, the apparent weakness comes from pain, and CK and muscle biopsy are normal. Steroid myopathy is another cause of proximal weakness with a normal CK, but it is painless and follows corticosteroid use.","whyNot":["ESR can be high in both conditions, so it does not separate them.","Polymyalgia rheumatica requires age over 50, but myositis also occurs at this age.","Both disorders affect the shoulder and hip girdles on both sides.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Polymyalgia rheumatica compared with inflammatory myositis"],"comps":["MS-13-B6","MS-13-B5"]}},{"id":"MS-C09-Q3","stem":"Ultrasound of her shoulder shows inflammation of the subacromial bursa around the tendon that passes under the acromion and starts abduction of the arm. Which of the following nerves innervates the muscle of this tendon?","options":["Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Axillary nerve","Upper subscapular nerve"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-5","obj":"MS-5.1","dok":2,"approach":["The tendon under the acromion that starts abduction belongs to supraspinatus.","Supraspinatus starts abduction for about the first 15 degrees, and deltoid takes over after that.","Supraspinatus and infraspinatus are both supplied by the suprascapular nerve."],"why":"Supraspinatus runs from the supraspinous fossa under the acromion to the greater tubercle. It starts abduction and is supplied by the suprascapular nerve (C5 to C6), as is infraspinatus, a lateral rotator. Teres minor, also a lateral rotator, is supplied by the axillary nerve, and subscapularis, a medial rotator, by the subscapular nerves. Its course under the acromion makes supraspinatus the rotator cuff tendon most often injured, and the subacromial bursa next to it is often inflamed in polymyalgia rheumatica.","whyNot":["Correct.","The long thoracic nerve supplies serratus anterior. Injury causes a winged scapula.","The axillary nerve supplies deltoid, which abducts the arm after the first 15 degrees, and teres minor.","The subscapular nerves supply subscapularis, a medial rotator, and teres major."],"study":{"topics":["Rotator cuff muscles and nerves"],"comps":["MS-5","MS-4"]}},{"id":"MS-C09-Q4","stem":"She is treated with high-dose prednisone. Five months later her girdle pain has resolved, but she has painless, progressive difficulty rising from a chair. Serum creatine kinase is 48 U/L (30 to 170). A muscle biopsy would most likely show which of the following?","options":["Endomysial lymphocytes with necrotic fibers","Ragged red fibers on Gomori trichrome stain","Type II fiber atrophy without inflammation","Grouped atrophy with fiber type grouping"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.4","dok":3,"approach":["Painless proximal weakness with a normal CK after months of high-dose corticosteroids suggests steroid myopathy.","Corticosteroids increase protein breakdown and reduce protein synthesis, mostly in fast glycolytic fibers.","The biopsy shows selective type II fiber atrophy with no necrosis or inflammation, the same pattern seen with disuse."],"why":"Long-term glucocorticoids cause catabolic muscle wasting that falls mostly on type II (fast, glycolytic) fibers. The result is painless proximal weakness, especially of the hip flexors, with a normal CK because fibers shrink without dying. Biopsy shows type II fiber atrophy without inflammation. Neurogenic disease instead shows grouped atrophy and fiber type grouping, and inflammatory myopathy shows necrosis and infiltrates with a high CK.","whyNot":["Endomysial lymphocytes with necrotic fibers indicate inflammatory myositis, which would raise CK.","Ragged red fibers reflect abnormal mitochondria in mitochondrial myopathies such as MERRF.","Correct.","Grouped atrophy and fiber type grouping mean denervation and reinnervation, as in spinal muscular atrophy, usually with fasciculations."],"study":{"topics":["Steroid myopathy","Neurogenic compared with myopathic weakness"],"comps":["MS-10","MS-13-B6"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C10","system":"MS","dx":"Reactive arthritis after Chlamydia trachomatis urethritis","codes":["MS-13-B7"],"case":"A 24-year-old man comes to the physician because of 5 days of pain and swelling in his right knee and left ankle. Three weeks ago he had dysuria and a urethral discharge, and a nucleic acid amplification test of urine was positive for Chlamydia trachomatis. He also reports red, irritated eyes and pain in both heels. Temperature is 37.8 °C (100.0 °F). Physical examination shows bilateral conjunctival injection, a warm knee effusion, a swollen left ankle, and tenderness at the Achilles tendon and plantar fascia insertions. Knee aspiration yields fluid with 16,000 leukocytes/mm3, mostly neutrophils, and no crystals. Gram stain and culture are negative. HLA-B27 testing is positive. Diagnosis: reactive arthritis after Chlamydia trachomatis urethritis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C10-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the arthritis in this patient?","options":["Replication of viable bacteria within the synovium","Immune reaction after a distant infection","Autoantibodies against citrullinated synovial proteins","Deposition of crystals within the joint cavity"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-13-B7","obj":"MS-13-B.10","dok":3,"approach":["Arthritis that starts weeks after urethritis, with conjunctivitis and enthesitis, fits reactive arthritis.","The synovial culture is negative and no crystals are present, so the joint is neither infected nor affected by crystal disease.","In HLA-B27 positive hosts, the immune response to bacterial antigens from a distant site drives a sterile arthritis."],"why":"Reactive arthritis is a sterile inflammatory arthritis that follows infection elsewhere, usually Chlamydia trachomatis urethritis or enteric infection with Shigella, Salmonella, Yersinia, or Campylobacter. Bacterial antigens, and sometimes nonviable bacterial fragments, reach the joint, and HLA-B27 carriers mount a persistent immune response. Typical features are asymmetric arthritis of the lower limbs, conjunctivitis, urethritis, enthesitis, dactylitis, and keratoderma blennorrhagicum on the soles.","whyNot":["Live bacteria in the joint is septic arthritis, which would give a positive Gram stain or culture. In sexually active young adults, Neisseria gonorrhoeae is a common cause.","Correct.","Antibodies to citrullinated peptides drive rheumatoid arthritis, a symmetric small joint arthritis linked to HLA-DR4.","Crystal arthritis (gout or pseudogout) is excluded because no crystals are present."],"study":{"topics":["Reactive arthritis pathogenesis","HLA-B27 associated arthritis"],"comps":["MS-13-B7","MS-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C10-Q2","stem":"A 34-year-old kindergarten teacher develops symmetric pain and swelling of the MCP joints, PIP joints, and wrists. Two weeks earlier, several children in her class had bright red cheeks followed by a lacy rash on the arms. Which of the following is the most likely cause of her arthritis?","options":["Recent rubella vaccination","Parvovirus B19 infection","HLA-B27 associated reactive arthritis","Disseminated Neisseria gonorrhoeae"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.7","dok":2,"approach":["Bright red cheeks followed by a lacy rash in children is erythema infectiosum, or fifth disease.","In adults, especially women, parvovirus B19 often causes a symmetric small joint arthritis.","Symmetric hand arthritis after exposure to children with fifth disease points to parvovirus."],"why":"Parvovirus B19 causes fifth disease in children. Adults, especially women, often develop a symmetric arthritis of the hands, wrists, and knees that can mimic rheumatoid arthritis and usually resolves within weeks. Rubella infection, and less often rubella vaccination, causes a similar short-lived arthralgia in adult women. Reactive arthritis follows enteric or chlamydial infection in HLA-B27 carriers and is usually asymmetric and in the lower limbs.","whyNot":["Rubella vaccination can cause brief arthralgia in adult women, but no vaccine is mentioned and her exposure fits fifth disease.","Correct.","Reactive arthritis is usually asymmetric and affects large lower-limb joints after genitourinary or enteric infection.","Disseminated gonococcal infection causes tenosynovitis, pustular skin lesions, and migratory arthralgia or purulent monoarthritis in sexually active young adults."],"study":{"topics":["Postinfectious and postvaccine arthritis"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-13-B7"]}},{"id":"MS-C10-Q3","stem":"His 52-year-old father has scaly plaques on the elbows, pitted fingernails, and swelling of several distal interphalangeal joints. A radiograph of the father's hands would most likely show which of the following?","options":["Periarticular osteopenia with erosions at the MCP joints","Osteophytes with joint space narrowing at the DIP joints","Punched-out erosions with overhanging edges","Pencil-in-cup erosions at the DIP joints"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B","obj":"MS-13-B.13","dok":3,"approach":["Skin plaques, nail pitting, and DIP arthritis identify psoriatic arthritis.","Psoriatic arthritis combines erosion with new bone formation, and severe erosion at the DIP joint gives a pencil-in-cup shape.","Rheumatoid arthritis spares the DIP joints and shows periarticular osteopenia with marginal erosions at the MCP and PIP joints."],"why":"Psoriatic arthritis is a seronegative spondyloarthropathy. It typically involves the DIP joints, causes dactylitis (sausage digits) and nail pitting, and can involve the spine, a pattern linked to HLA-B27. Radiographs show erosions with new bone formation nearby, and advanced disease produces pencil-in-cup deformities. Rheumatoid arthritis is a symmetric MCP and PIP arthritis that spares the DIP joints, with periarticular osteopenia, marginal erosions, and positive rheumatoid factor or anti-CCP.","whyNot":["Periarticular osteopenia with MCP erosions is the pattern of rheumatoid arthritis, which spares the DIP joints.","Osteophytes at the DIP joints (Heberden nodes) mark osteoarthritis, which does not cause nail pitting or skin plaques.","Punched-out erosions with overhanging edges are typical of chronic tophaceous gout.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Psoriatic arthritis compared with rheumatoid arthritis"],"comps":["MS-13-B","MS-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C10-Q4","stem":"His heel pain arises at the attachment of the plantar aponeurosis to the calcaneus. Long-term failure of this support, together with the spring ligament and tibialis posterior, would most likely produce which of the following deformities?","options":["Pes planus from medial longitudinal arch collapse","Pes cavus from an exaggerated longitudinal arch","Hallux valgus from lateral deviation of the great toe","Talipes equinovarus with plantarflexion and inversion"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-3","obj":"MS-3.5","dok":2,"approach":["The plantar aponeurosis acts like a tie-beam between the calcaneus and the toes, holding up the longitudinal arches.","The medial longitudinal arch is also held by the spring (plantar calcaneonavicular) ligament under the head of the talus and by the tibialis posterior tendon.","When these supports fail, the medial arch collapses and the foot becomes flat."],"why":"The foot has medial and lateral longitudinal arches and a transverse arch. The medial longitudinal arch is the highest and depends on the plantar aponeurosis, the spring ligament under the head of the talus, and the tibialis posterior tendon. When these weaken, the arch collapses and the foot becomes flat (pes planus), often with the heel turned outward. Pes cavus is the opposite, an abnormally high arch.","whyNot":["Correct.","Pes cavus is an abnormally high arch, often from neuromuscular disease such as Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease. Loss of arch support does the opposite.","Hallux valgus is lateral deviation of the great toe at the first MTP joint, linked to narrow footwear. It does not come from loss of arch support.","Talipes equinovarus (clubfoot) is a congenital deformity present at birth."],"study":{"topics":["Arches of the foot","Pes planus"],"comps":["MS-3","MS-13-B7"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C11","system":"MS","dx":"Lateral epicondylitis (tendinopathy of the extensor carpi radialis brevis origin)","codes":["MS-13-B8"],"case":"A 46-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of pain over the outer aspect of his right elbow. He installs drywall and uses a power screwdriver for most of the workday. The pain is worse when he grips tools or lifts a cup with the palm facing down. He recalls no injury. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows tenderness 1 cm distal to the right lateral epicondyle. Resisted extension of the wrist with the elbow extended reproduces the pain. Elbow range of motion is full, and there is no swelling or warmth. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 8 mm/h (normal 0 to 15). Ultrasonography shows a thickened, hypoechoic common extensor tendon origin. Diagnosis: lateral epicondylitis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C11-Q1","stem":"Eight months later, his symptoms persist despite rest and physical therapy, and the diseased tendon origin is surgically debrided. Which of the following findings is most likely on microscopic examination of the excised tissue?","options":["Disorganized collagen, new vessels, and scant inflammation","Urate crystal deposits rimmed by foreign body giant cells","Sheets of neutrophils with liquefactive necrosis of the tendon","Noncaseating granulomas with multinucleated giant cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B8","obj":"MS-13-B.11","dok":3,"approach":["Note the clues: months of overuse pain, no warmth or swelling, and a normal sedimentation rate.","Recognize chronic tendinopathy as a degenerative process (tendinosis) more than an inflammatory one.","Pick the histology of failed repair: disorganized collagen, fibroblasts, and small vessels with few inflammatory cells."],"why":"Lateral epicondylitis is overuse tendinopathy at the origin of the extensor carpi radialis brevis. Repeated small tears outpace repair, so the tendon shows tendinosis: collagen fibers that are thin, loosely packed, and out of line, more ground substance, many fibroblasts, and ingrowth of small vessels. Inflammatory cells are scarce, which is why the term epicondylitis is a misnomer and why his sedimentation rate is normal.","whyNot":["Correct.","Urate crystals with giant cells form a tophus in chronic gout. Gout causes episodic, warm, inflamed joints, which he does not have.","Neutrophils with liquefactive necrosis describe an abscess, as in septic bursitis or a tendon sheath infection. He has no warmth, swelling, or systemic signs.","Noncaseating granulomas suggest sarcoidosis or a foreign body reaction. Overuse tendinopathy does not form granulomas."],"study":{"topics":["Tendinosis histology","Lateral and medial epicondylitis"],"comps":["MS-13-B8","MS-6"]}},{"id":"MS-C11-Q2","stem":"Analysis of the debrided tendon shows that much of its normal collagen has been replaced by thinner fibrils of the collagen type that predominates in early granulation tissue. This collagen type is also the main component of which of the following?","options":["Lamellar bone of the lateral epicondyle","Reticular fiber network of lymph nodes","Hyaline cartilage of the articular surface","Lamina densa of epithelial basement membranes"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the collagen of early granulation tissue as type III.","Recall where type III collagen is normally found: reticular fibers, blood vessel walls, and early scar.","Match type III collagen to the reticular framework of lymphoid organs."],"why":"Healthy tendon is built mostly of thick, parallel type I collagen fibrils, which give it high tensile strength. Repair tissue in tendinopathy contains more type III collagen, which forms thinner, weaker fibrils, the same collagen laid down first in granulation tissue. Type III collagen also forms reticular fibers, the fine supporting mesh of lymph nodes, spleen, bone marrow, and liver, and it is abundant in blood vessel walls.","whyNot":["Bone matrix is mainly type I collagen, the same collagen that normally makes up healthy tendon.","Correct.","Hyaline cartilage contains mainly type II collagen, which is also found in the nucleus pulposus and vitreous humor.","The lamina densa of basement membranes is made of type IV collagen, which forms a sheet-like network. Defects in type IV collagen cause Alport syndrome."],"study":{"topics":["Collagen types and tissues","Granulation tissue and scar"],"comps":["MS-6"]}},{"id":"MS-C11-Q3","stem":"He asks why the tendon is still painful even though he took 6 weeks off work. Which of the following features of the tendon best explains its slow healing?","options":["Continuous bathing of the tendon in synovial fluid","A matrix built mainly of type II collagen","Sparse blood supply and low cell density","Absence of resident collagen-producing cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that repair needs cells to make new matrix and blood to deliver nutrients and more cells.","Tendons have few cells (tenocytes) and a poor blood supply, especially near the bony origin.","Choose the option that combines low vascularity with low cellularity."],"why":"Tendon is dense regular connective tissue with relatively few tenocytes (specialized fibroblasts) spread between thick collagen bundles. Its blood supply enters mainly at the muscle junction, the bone attachment, and the surrounding paratenon, and the region near the bony origin is poorly vascular. With few cells to make new matrix and little blood to bring nutrients and repair cells, tendon heals over months. Articular cartilage has no blood vessels at all and heals even more poorly.","whyNot":["Bathing in synovial fluid hampers healing of intra-articular structures such as the anterior cruciate ligament. The common extensor origin lies outside the elbow joint.","Type II collagen is the main collagen of hyaline cartilage. Tendon matrix is mainly type I collagen.","Correct.","Tendons do contain collagen-producing cells, the tenocytes. They are few in number, so repair is slow, but they are present."],"study":{"topics":["Tendon structure and blood supply","Healing of tendon and cartilage"],"comps":["MS-6","MS-13-H"]}},{"id":"MS-C11-Q4","stem":"To measure grip strength, the physician asks him to squeeze a dynamometer with the wrist held first in slight extension and then in full flexion. Grip force is much lower with the wrist fully flexed, on both the affected and the unaffected side. Which of the following best explains the lower force with the wrist flexed?","options":["Finger flexors contract at a higher velocity, which lowers force","Finger flexor sarcomeres are too short for optimal filament overlap","Finger flexor sarcomeres are stretched beyond optimal filament overlap","Fewer finger flexor motor units can be recruited in this position"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.3","dok":3,"approach":["The long finger flexors cross the front of the wrist, so wrist flexion shortens them.","Apply the length-tension relationship: force is greatest at a sarcomere length with optimal actin and myosin overlap.","At very short lengths, thin filaments overlap each other and thick filaments press on the Z discs, so fewer cross-bridges form."],"why":"The flexor digitorum superficialis and profundus pass in front of the wrist. Flexing the wrist shortens them before the grip starts, so their sarcomeres work below optimal length, where thin filaments overlap and fewer cross-bridges can form. This is called active insufficiency. During a normal grip, the wrist extensors, including the extensor carpi radialis brevis, hold the wrist slightly extended to keep the finger flexors near optimal length, which is why gripping loads his painful tendon.","whyNot":["Velocity lowers force only when a muscle is shortening. A dynamometer grip is nearly isometric, so velocity is close to zero in both wrist positions.","Correct.","Overstretching reduces overlap between thick and thin filaments and lowers force, but wrist flexion shortens the finger flexors. Wrist extension combined with finger extension would stretch them.","Motor unit recruitment depends on the drive from the motor neuron pool and does not fall because of wrist position. The loss of force appears on the healthy side too."],"study":{"topics":["Length-tension relationship","Active insufficiency of finger flexors"],"comps":["MS-7","MS-5"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C12","system":"MS","dx":"Autosomal recessive osteopetrosis from carbonic anhydrase II deficiency","codes":["MS-13-C1"],"case":"An 18-month-old boy is brought to the physician because of a fracture of the right femur after he fell from a sitting position. He has had poor weight gain since infancy. His parents are first cousins. His length and weight are below the 3rd percentile. Laboratory studies show a serum sodium concentration of 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145), chloride 114 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106), and bicarbonate 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 28), with a urine pH of 6.8. Radiographs show diffusely increased bone density and a bone-within-bone appearance of the vertebrae. CT of the head shows calcifications in the basal ganglia. Diagnosis: autosomal recessive osteopetrosis caused by carbonic anhydrase II deficiency.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C12-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the increased bone density in this patient?","options":["Osteoclast precursors cannot form because RANKL is absent","Osteoblasts lay down excess osteoid that mineralizes too rapidly","Osteoclasts cannot acidify the space beneath the ruffled border","Excess calcitonin secretion suppresses osteoclast activity"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-C1","obj":"MS-13-C.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the reaction carbonic anhydrase II catalyzes: carbon dioxide and water to carbonic acid, a source of hydrogen ions.","Osteoclasts pump these hydrogen ions into the resorption pit to dissolve hydroxyapatite.","Without the enzyme, bone is not resorbed and accumulates, even though osteoclasts are present."],"why":"Osteoclasts dissolve bone mineral by secreting acid into the sealed space under the ruffled border (Howship lacuna). Carbonic anhydrase II in the osteoclast makes the hydrogen ions that the vacuolar proton pump moves into this space. Without it, mineral is not dissolved, old bone and calcified cartilage are not removed, and the skeleton becomes dense but brittle. The same enzyme is needed in the renal tubules, which explains his normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with an inappropriately alkaline urine.","whyNot":["RANKL deficiency is a real but rare cause of osteopetrosis in which osteoclasts are nearly absent. It does not cause renal tubular acidosis, which points to carbonic anhydrase II deficiency here.","Osteopetrosis is a disease of too little resorption. Osteoblast bone formation is not the primary problem.","Correct.","Calcitonin has only a minor role in human bone, and calcitonin excess, as in medullary thyroid carcinoma, does not cause osteopetrosis."],"study":{"topics":["Osteoclast acid secretion","Causes of osteopetrosis"],"comps":["MS-13-C1","MS-2"]}},{"id":"MS-C12-Q2","stem":"At the age of 3 years, he has trouble finding toys across a room. His pupils react sluggishly to light, and funduscopic examination shows pale optic discs. Which of the following is the most likely cause of his visual loss?","options":["Compression of the optic nerves in narrowed bony canals","Calcification of the occipital cortex causing cortical blindness","Autoimmune demyelination of the optic nerves","Retinal ganglion cell loss from chronic metabolic acidosis"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-C1","obj":"MS-13-C.2","dok":3,"approach":["Pale optic discs and a sluggish pupillary light reflex localize the problem to the optic nerves.","In osteopetrosis, unresorbed bone narrows the skull foramina and canals.","Link narrowing of the optic canals to compression and atrophy of the optic nerves."],"why":"Bone that cannot be remodeled keeps thickening, so the canals and foramina of the skull base become narrow. Nerves passing through them are compressed, causing optic atrophy and vision loss, hearing loss, and facial nerve palsy. Encroachment of bone on the marrow space can also cause anemia or pancytopenia with extramedullary hematopoiesis and hepatosplenomegaly. These complications are most frequent and most severe in the infantile autosomal recessive forms.","whyNot":["Correct.","Cortical blindness spares the pupillary light reflex and leaves the optic discs normal, because the lesion lies behind the lateral geniculate body.","Optic neuritis from demyelination usually affects older children and adults and is often painful. It is unrelated to osteopetrosis.","Metabolic acidosis from renal tubular acidosis affects growth and bone, but it does not destroy retinal ganglion cells."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of osteopetrosis","Skull foramina and cranial nerves"],"comps":["MS-13-C1"]}},{"id":"MS-C12-Q3","stem":"A bone biopsy shows many large multinucleated cells lying in shallow pits on the bone surface. These cells arise from which of the following?","options":["Mesenchymal stromal cells of the bone marrow","Chondrocytes of the growth plate","Osteocytes released from their lacunae","Monocyte and macrophage precursors"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the cells: large, multinucleated, and sitting in pits on the bone surface. These are osteoclasts.","Recall that osteoclasts form by fusion of hematopoietic precursors.","Separate this lineage from the mesenchymal lineage that gives osteoblasts and osteocytes."],"why":"Osteoclasts form when monocyte and macrophage precursors fuse under the influence of M-CSF and RANKL, which osteoblasts and stromal cells supply. The mature cell is multinucleated, seals onto bone, and resorbs it with acid and cathepsin K in a Howship lacuna. In carbonic anhydrase II deficiency the osteoclasts are present but cannot make enough acid. Because osteoclasts come from blood-forming stem cells, hematopoietic stem cell transplantation can correct some severe infantile forms of osteopetrosis.","whyNot":["Mesenchymal stromal cells give rise to osteoblasts, chondrocytes, and fat cells. Osteoblasts are mononuclear and secrete type I collagen and alkaline phosphatase.","Growth plate chondrocytes build the cartilage model for long bone growth. They do not resorb bone.","Osteocytes are osteoblasts trapped in mineralized matrix. They are small, single-nucleus cells that sense mechanical load and secrete FGF23.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Bone cell lineages","Osteoclast formation"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-13-C1"]}},{"id":"MS-C12-Q4","stem":"At the age of 2 years, he develops diffuse muscle weakness during an episode of gastroenteritis with diarrhea. Deep tendon reflexes are decreased. Serum potassium concentration is 2.4 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0). Which of the following best explains his weakness?","options":["Muscle cells are hyperpolarized farther from threshold","Less acetylcholine is released at each motor end plate","Nerve sodium channels open with less depolarization","Sustained depolarization inactivates muscle sodium channels"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.5","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the cause of the low potassium: urinary potassium loss from his renal tubular acidosis, made worse by diarrhea.","Apply the Nernst equation: lower extracellular potassium makes the resting membrane potential more negative.","A more negative resting potential sits farther from threshold, so muscle fibers are harder to fire and he is weak."],"why":"The resting membrane potential of nerve and muscle depends mostly on the potassium gradient across the membrane. When extracellular potassium falls, the gradient grows and the resting potential becomes more negative (hyperpolarized), so a larger stimulus is needed to reach threshold. Muscle fibers fire less easily, which causes weakness and low reflexes. Renal tubular acidosis from carbonic anhydrase II deficiency wastes potassium in the urine, and diarrhea adds more loss. High potassium also causes weakness, but by a different route: sustained depolarization keeps sodium channels inactivated.","whyNot":["Correct.","Reduced acetylcholine release causes weakness in Lambert-Eaton syndrome and hypermagnesemia. Low potassium acts on the muscle membrane itself.","A lower threshold for sodium channel opening is the effect of hypocalcemia, which causes tetany and spasms instead of weakness.","Sustained depolarization with sodium channel inactivation is how hyperkalemia causes weakness. His potassium is low."],"study":{"topics":["Potassium and resting membrane potential","Hypokalemia and hyperkalemia weakness"],"comps":["MS-10","MS-13-C1"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C13","system":"MS","dx":"Marfan syndrome","codes":["MS-13-D1"],"case":"A 17-year-old boy comes to the physician for a preparticipation sports examination before joining his high school basketball team. His father died suddenly at the age of 36 years. He is 198 cm (6 ft 6 in) tall, and his arm span is 208 cm. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows long, thin fingers, pectus excavatum, a high-arched palate, and mild thoracic scoliosis. A midsystolic click is heard at the apex. Slit-lamp examination shows upward and outward displacement of both lenses. Echocardiography shows a dilated aortic root and mitral valve prolapse. Diagnosis: Marfan syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C13-Q1","stem":"Genetic testing shows a heterozygous missense mutation in FBN1. Which of the following mechanisms contributes most to the progressive dilation of his aortic root?","options":["Haploinsufficiency of elastin from a 7q11.23 deletion","Excess TGF-beta activity from defective microfibrils","Reduced lysyl oxidase cross-linking from copper deficiency","Defective triple helix formation in type I collagen"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-D1","obj":"MS-13-D.1","dok":3,"approach":["FBN1 encodes fibrillin-1, the main protein of extracellular microfibrils.","Microfibrils normally bind latent TGF-beta and keep it inactive.","Defective microfibrils release too much active TGF-beta, which weakens the aortic media."],"why":"Marfan syndrome is autosomal dominant, caused by mutations in FBN1 on chromosome 15. Fibrillin-1 microfibrils are the scaffold for elastic fibers and also hold latent TGF-beta in the matrix. When fibrillin-1 is abnormal, TGF-beta is activated in excess, which promotes matrix breakdown and loss of smooth muscle in the aortic media (cystic medial degeneration). The aortic root dilates, and the risk of aortic dissection rises. Excess TGF-beta activity also contributes to the skeletal overgrowth.","whyNot":["Deletion at 7q11.23 that includes the elastin gene causes Williams syndrome, which narrows the aorta above the valve (supravalvular aortic stenosis).","Correct.","Lysyl oxidase needs copper, and its failure in Menkes disease weakens collagen and elastin. Copper metabolism is normal in Marfan syndrome.","Defective type I collagen triple helices cause osteogenesis imperfecta, with fractures and blue sclerae."],"study":{"topics":["Fibrillin-1 and TGF-beta","Cystic medial degeneration"],"comps":["MS-13-D1","MS-6"]}},{"id":"MS-C13-Q2","stem":"Homocystinuria can produce a similar tall, thin body habitus. Which of the following findings would most strongly favor homocystinuria in a tall patient with long fingers?","options":["Pectus excavatum with a high-arched palate and scoliosis","Arm span exceeding height with long, slender fingers and toes","Prolapse of the mitral valve with a midsystolic apical click","Downward lens displacement and a prior venous thrombosis"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-D1","obj":"MS-13-D.2","dok":3,"approach":["List what both disorders share: tall stature, long limbs and digits, and chest deformity.","Find the features that separate them: lens direction, thrombosis, and intellectual disability.","Pick the combination that only homocystinuria explains."],"why":"Both disorders cause a marfanoid habitus and lens dislocation. In Marfan syndrome the lens usually moves up and out, and the main threats are aortic root dilation, aortic dissection, and mitral valve prolapse. In homocystinuria, most often from cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency, the lens usually moves down and in, and high homocysteine damages endothelium, causing arterial and venous thrombosis. Intellectual disability is also common in homocystinuria and is not a feature of Marfan syndrome.","whyNot":["Pectus deformity, a high-arched palate, and scoliosis occur in both disorders as part of the skeletal overgrowth.","Long limbs and digits are part of the marfanoid habitus seen in both disorders, so they do not separate them.","Mitral valve prolapse is a typical cardiovascular finding in Marfan syndrome. It points away from homocystinuria.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Marfan syndrome versus homocystinuria","Marfan cardiovascular complications"],"comps":["MS-13-D1"]}},{"id":"MS-C13-Q3","stem":"The same gene defect affects the yellow ligaments that connect the laminae of adjacent vertebrae and recoil as the spine straightens after flexion. Which of the following best describes the normal fibers responsible for this recoil?","options":["Type II collagen fibrils embedded in aggrecan","Type III collagen fibers forming a reticular mesh","Type I collagen fibrils cross-linked by lysyl oxidase","Elastin cores built on fibrillin microfibrils"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the yellow ligaments as the ligamenta flava, which are rich in elastic fibers.","Recall how an elastic fiber is built: an amorphous elastin core around a sheath of microfibrils.","Link the microfibril protein to the FBN1 defect."],"why":"The ligamenta flava are yellow because they are rich in elastic fibers, which let them stretch during flexion and recoil during extension. An elastic fiber forms when tropoelastin is laid down on a scaffold of fibrillin-1 microfibrils and then cross-linked into an elastin core. Fibrillin microfibrils also bind latent TGF-beta and hold it inactive in the matrix. Elastic fibers are also prominent in the aortic media and the suspensory ligaments of the lens, which explains the aortic and lens findings in Marfan syndrome.","whyNot":["Type II collagen with aggrecan forms hyaline cartilage, which resists compression.","Type III collagen forms reticular fibers in lymphoid organs and vessel walls. They support cells but do not provide elastic recoil.","Type I collagen fibrils give tensile strength to tendon, ligament, and bone, but collagen does not stretch and recoil the way elastic fibers do.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Elastic fiber structure","Ligamenta flava"],"comps":["MS-6","MS-13-D1"]}},{"id":"MS-C13-Q4","stem":"When he folds his thumb across his palm and closes his fingers over it, the tip of the thumb extends well beyond the ulnar border of the hand. Movement at the first carpometacarpal joint contributes to this sign. Which of the following best describes this joint?","options":["Hinge joint between the trapezoid and first metacarpal","Condyloid joint between the scaphoid and first metacarpal","Saddle joint between the trapezium and first metacarpal","Plane joint between the capitate and first metacarpal"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-4","obj":"MS-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the bones of the first carpometacarpal joint: the trapezium and the base of the first metacarpal.","Recall that both surfaces are concave in one direction and convex in the other.","Classify this as a saddle joint, which allows flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, and opposition."],"why":"The first carpometacarpal joint lies between the trapezium and the base of the first metacarpal. Each surface is shaped like a saddle, so the joint moves in two planes and adds some rotation, which allows opposition of the thumb. In Marfan syndrome, long digits and joint laxity let the thumb tip pass beyond the ulnar border of the closed hand, the thumb sign. Other synovial joint types include the pivot (atlantoaxial), hinge (interphalangeal), condyloid (radiocarpal), ball and socket (shoulder), and plane (intercarpal).","whyNot":["The trapezoid articulates with the second metacarpal. Hinge joints, such as the elbow and interphalangeal joints, move in one plane.","The scaphoid does not articulate with the first metacarpal. Condyloid joints, such as the radiocarpal and metacarpophalangeal joints, have an oval head in an oval socket.","Correct.","The capitate articulates with the third metacarpal. Plane joints, such as the intercarpal joints, allow only gliding."],"study":{"topics":["Synovial joint types","Carpometacarpal joints"],"comps":["MS-4","MS-3"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C14","system":"MS","dx":"Duchenne muscular dystrophy","codes":["MS-13-D2"],"case":"A 4-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of frequent falls and difficulty climbing stairs over the past year. He walked at 18 months of age. His mother's brother used a wheelchair from the age of 11 years and died of respiratory failure at 20 years. Physical examination shows a waddling gait, firm, enlarged calves, and weakness of the hip and shoulder girdle muscles. To rise from the floor, he pushes on his knees and thighs with his hands. Serum creatine kinase activity is 16,800 U/L (normal 40 to 200). Diagnosis: Duchenne muscular dystrophy.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C14-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings is most likely on biopsy of his enlarged calf muscle?","options":["Perifascicular atrophy with perivascular inflammation","Uniformly enlarged fibers with normal architecture","Necrotic fibers replaced by fat and fibrous tissue","Endomysial CD8 T cells invading nonnecrotic fibers"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-D2","obj":"MS-13-D.3","dok":3,"approach":["Notice that the calves are large while he is weak.","Recall that absent dystrophin makes the sarcolemma tear with contraction, so fibers die repeatedly.","Dead fibers are replaced by fat and connective tissue, which enlarges the calf without adding strength (pseudohypertrophy)."],"why":"Dystrophin links the actin cytoskeleton through the dystroglycan complex to the extracellular matrix. Without it, the sarcolemma tears during contraction, calcium floods in, and fibers undergo necrosis. Regeneration cannot keep up, so the biopsy shows fibers of varied size, necrotic and regenerating fibers, and progressive replacement by fat and fibrous tissue. The calves enlarge from this fibrofatty tissue, which is pseudohypertrophy. Creatine kinase leaking from damaged fibers explains the very high serum level.","whyNot":["Perifascicular atrophy with perivascular inflammation is typical of dermatomyositis, an inflammatory myopathy with a heliotrope rash and Gottron papules.","Uniformly large, normal fibers describe true hypertrophy, as after resistance training. His large calves are weak, which points to pseudohypertrophy.","Correct.","CD8 T cells invading fibers are typical of polymyositis, which presents in adults. A family history in a maternal uncle points to an X-linked dystrophy."],"study":{"topics":["Muscle biopsy in Duchenne dystrophy","Pseudohypertrophy"],"comps":["MS-13-D2","MS-9"]}},{"id":"MS-C14-Q2","stem":"Genetic testing shows a deletion of several exons of the DMD gene whose combined length is not a multiple of three nucleotides. Which of the following best explains why this deletion causes his severe phenotype?","options":["It removes the promoter so that no mRNA is transcribed","It shifts the reading frame, creating an early stop codon","It produces a protein that blocks the normal allele's product","It yields a shortened protein that keeps both binding ends"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-9","obj":"MS-9.4","dok":3,"approach":["Codons are read three nucleotides at a time.","Deleting a number of nucleotides that is not divisible by three shifts the reading frame downstream.","A frameshift soon creates a stop codon, so no functional dystrophin is made, which gives Duchenne dystrophy."],"why":"The reading frame rule explains most of the difference between Duchenne and Becker dystrophy. A frameshift deletion scrambles every codon after the break and soon reaches a premature stop codon, so the mRNA is degraded and no functional dystrophin is made. An in-frame deletion removes some codons but keeps the rest of the message in register, so a shorter, partly working dystrophin is made, which gives the milder Becker form. The DMD gene is one of the largest human genes, which is why new mutations are common.","whyNot":["The deletion described lies within the coding exons. Promoter deletions are uncommon, and the key here is that the deleted length is not divisible by three.","Correct.","A boy has only one X chromosome and therefore one DMD allele. Dominant-negative effects require a normal allele whose product can be disrupted.","A shortened protein that keeps the actin-binding and dystroglycan-binding ends results from an in-frame deletion and causes Becker dystrophy."],"study":{"topics":["Reading frame rule","Duchenne versus Becker genetics"],"comps":["MS-9","MS-13-D2"]}},{"id":"MS-C14-Q3","stem":"At the age of 17 years, he has morning headaches and daytime sleepiness. When he lies supine, his abdomen moves inward as he inhales. Weakness of which of the following muscles, with its nerve supply, best explains this finding?","options":["Diaphragm, phrenic nerves from C3 to C5","Sternocleidomastoid, spinal accessory nerve (CN XI)","External intercostals, intercostal nerves from T1 to T11","Rectus abdominis, thoracoabdominal nerves from T7 to T11"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-5","obj":"MS-5.5","dok":2,"approach":["Normally the diaphragm descends during inspiration and pushes the abdominal wall outward.","Inward movement of the abdomen during inspiration (paradoxical breathing) means the diaphragm is weak and is being pulled up by negative chest pressure.","Name the diaphragm's nerve supply: the phrenic nerves from C3 to C5."],"why":"The diaphragm is the main muscle of inspiration. When it contracts, it descends and pushes the abdominal contents down and the abdominal wall out. When it is weak, the accessory muscles expand the chest, the negative pressure pulls the diaphragm upward, and the abdomen moves in, especially when lying supine. Diaphragm weakness causes hypoventilation during sleep, with morning headaches from carbon dioxide retention and daytime sleepiness. Respiratory failure and dilated cardiomyopathy are the main causes of death in Duchenne dystrophy.","whyNot":["Correct.","The sternocleidomastoid is an accessory muscle that raises the sternum in forced inspiration. Paradoxical abdominal motion points to the diaphragm.","The external intercostals help raise the ribs in inspiration, but their weakness does not make the abdomen move inward when he lies supine.","The rectus abdominis flexes the trunk and helps forced expiration. Its weakness does not cause inward abdominal motion during inspiration."],"study":{"topics":["Diaphragm innervation","Paradoxical breathing"],"comps":["MS-5","MS-13-D2"]}},{"id":"MS-C14-Q4","stem":"His findings are compared with those of an unrelated 16-year-old boy who first had exercise-induced calf cramps at the age of 11 years, still walks independently, and has a serum creatine kinase activity of 4,200 U/L. Which of the following results of dystrophin immunostaining is most likely in the older boy's muscle?","options":["No staining at the sarcolemma of nearly all fibers","Normal, continuous staining at the sarcolemma of all fibers","Faint, patchy staining at the sarcolemma of most fibers","Strong staining confined to the myofiber nuclei"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-D2","obj":"MS-13-D.4","dok":3,"approach":["Later onset, slower course, and walking in the teens point to Becker dystrophy.","Becker dystrophy makes a reduced amount of a shortened dystrophin.","Immunostaining therefore shows reduced, uneven staining along the fiber membrane, instead of the absent staining of Duchenne."],"why":"Becker muscular dystrophy is the milder form of the same X-linked disease. Symptoms start later, often in late childhood or adolescence, many patients walk into adulthood, and creatine kinase is high but usually lower than in Duchenne. Because some shortened dystrophin is made, immunostaining of the sarcolemma is faint and patchy, and Western blot shows protein of reduced size or amount. In Duchenne dystrophy, staining is absent from nearly all fibers. Genetic testing then shows whether the deletion keeps or shifts the reading frame.","whyNot":["Absent staining in nearly all fibers is the Duchenne pattern, as in the younger boy. The older boy's later onset and preserved walking point to Becker dystrophy.","Normal dystrophin staining would point to another myopathy, such as a limb-girdle dystrophy, which can look similar clinically.","Correct.","Dystrophin is a cytoskeletal protein that sits under the sarcolemma. It is not a nuclear protein."],"study":{"topics":["Becker muscular dystrophy","Dystrophin immunostaining"],"comps":["MS-13-D2","MS-9"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C15","system":"MS","dx":"Myotonic dystrophy type 1","codes":["MS-13-D2"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 3-year history of difficulty releasing her grip on door handles and tools and frequent tripping. She had cataract surgery in both eyes at the age of 29 years. Her mother developed cataracts in her 50s and has no weakness. Physical examination shows bilateral ptosis, a long, thin face with wasting of the temporal muscles, weakness of neck flexion, and bilateral foot drop. After a firm handshake, she opens her hand slowly. Percussion of the thenar eminence causes a sustained contraction. An ECG shows a PR interval of 240 msec (normal 120 to 200). Diagnosis: myotonic dystrophy type 1.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C15-Q1","stem":"Two years later, she gives birth to a boy after a pregnancy complicated by polyhydramnios. At birth he is severely hypotonic, has facial weakness, and needs ventilatory support. Which of the following best explains why his disease is more severe than his mother's?","options":["Inheritance of a second mutant DMPK allele from the father","Further expansion of the CTG repeat during transmission","Imprinting that silences the normal paternal DMPK allele","High mutant mitochondrial DNA load inherited from the oocyte"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-9","obj":"MS-9.5","dok":3,"approach":["Notice the pattern across three generations: cataracts only in the grandmother, adult disease in the mother, and severe newborn disease in the son.","Earlier onset and greater severity in each generation is anticipation.","Anticipation in myotonic dystrophy comes from an unstable CTG repeat that grows when passed on, most dramatically through the mother."],"why":"Myotonic dystrophy type 1 is autosomal dominant and caused by an expanded CTG repeat in the untranslated end of the DMPK gene on chromosome 19. The repeat is unstable and tends to grow in each generation, so onset is earlier and disease is more severe (anticipation). The largest expansions, which cause congenital myotonic dystrophy with hypotonia and breathing failure, are passed on almost always by an affected mother. The expanded RNA traps splicing regulators, causing missplicing of the muscle chloride channel and other proteins, which explains myotonia, weakness, cataracts, and conduction block.","whyNot":["Nothing suggests the father is affected, and one expanded allele is enough to cause disease. A second mutant allele is not needed to explain the severity.","Correct.","Imprinting explains disorders such as Prader-Willi and Angelman syndromes. One expanded DMPK allele is enough to cause disease, whatever the parent of origin.","Mitochondrial disorders are passed through the mother and vary with mutant load, but myotonic dystrophy is a nuclear gene disorder with autosomal dominant inheritance."],"study":{"topics":["Trinucleotide repeat expansion","Anticipation in myotonic dystrophy"],"comps":["MS-9","MS-13-D2"]}},{"id":"MS-C15-Q2","stem":"Wasting of her temporal and masseter muscles gives her face a narrow, hollow appearance. From which pharyngeal arch do these muscles develop, and which nerve supplies them?","options":["Fourth arch, superior laryngeal nerve (CN X)","Third arch, glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Second arch, facial nerve (CN VII)","First arch, mandibular nerve (CN V3)"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-1","obj":"MS-1.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the temporalis and masseter as muscles of mastication.","Recall that the muscles of mastication come from the first pharyngeal arch.","Each arch keeps its own cranial nerve, and the first arch nerve is the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve."],"why":"Each pharyngeal arch forms muscles that keep the nerve of that arch. The first arch forms the muscles of mastication (temporalis, masseter, medial and lateral pterygoids), plus the mylohyoid, anterior belly of the digastric, tensor tympani, and tensor veli palatini, all supplied by the mandibular nerve (CN V3). Wasting of the temporalis and masseter is typical of myotonic dystrophy and produces the long, narrow face. Her ptosis comes from weakness of the levator palpebrae superioris, which is supplied by the oculomotor nerve.","whyNot":["The fourth arch forms the cricothyroid and most pharyngeal constrictors, supplied by branches of the vagus nerve.","The third arch forms only the stylopharyngeus, supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve.","The second arch forms the muscles of facial expression, the stapedius, the stylohyoid, and the posterior belly of the digastric, supplied by the facial nerve.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pharyngeal arch derivatives","Muscles of mastication"],"comps":["MS-1","MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C15-Q3","stem":"Her weakness of neck flexion is accompanied by thin sternocleidomastoid muscles. Which of the following correctly describes the nerve supply and an action of one of these muscles?","options":["Accessory nerve; turns the face toward the opposite side","Accessory nerve; turns the face toward the same side as the muscle","Ansa cervicalis; depresses the hyoid bone during swallowing","Facial nerve; tenses the skin of the anterior neck"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-5","obj":"MS-5.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the sternocleidomastoid's attachments: mastoid process above, sternum and clavicle below.","Contracting one side pulls the mastoid toward the same-side clavicle, which tilts the head toward that side and turns the face to the other side.","Name its motor nerve: the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI)."],"why":"The sternocleidomastoid runs from the mastoid process to the manubrium and the medial clavicle. One muscle acting alone tilts the head toward its own side and rotates the face toward the opposite side. Both muscles together flex the neck. Motor supply is the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI), which also supplies the trapezius. Weakness and wasting of the sternocleidomastoid with neck flexion weakness is a typical early finding in myotonic dystrophy type 1.","whyNot":["Correct.","This reverses the rotation. Because the muscle pulls the mastoid process down and forward, the face turns away from the contracting side.","The ansa cervicalis supplies the infrahyoid muscles, such as the sternohyoid and omohyoid, which depress the hyoid.","The facial nerve supplies the platysma, which tenses the skin of the neck. The platysma is a muscle of facial expression."],"study":{"topics":["Sternocleidomastoid action","Accessory nerve"],"comps":["MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C15-Q4","stem":"Her skeletal muscles stay contracted after a voluntary grip, yet her ventricles relax fully between beats even during the tachycardia of exercise. Which of the following properties of cardiac muscle prevents its contractions from summating into tetany?","options":["A refractory period that lasts almost as long as contraction","Absence of ryanodine receptors on the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Gap junctions that make the myocardium contract as a syncytium","Contraction triggered only by calcium entering from outside the cell"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.4","dok":3,"approach":["Tetany needs a new action potential to arrive before the muscle relaxes.","The cardiac action potential has a long plateau from calcium entry through L-type channels.","Its refractory period lasts through most of the contraction, so a second contraction cannot be added on top of the first."],"why":"In skeletal muscle the action potential lasts a few milliseconds, much shorter than the twitch, so repeated stimuli can summate into tetanus. In ventricular muscle the plateau of the action potential keeps sodium channels inactivated for most of systole, so the absolute refractory period lasts nearly as long as the contraction itself. A new beat cannot start until the muscle is relaxing, which protects filling. Her myotonia is different: missplicing of the skeletal muscle chloride channel leaves the membrane prone to repetitive firing, so the muscle keeps contracting after the voluntary effort ends.","whyNot":["Correct.","Cardiac muscle has ryanodine receptors (RyR2). Calcium entering through L-type channels opens them, releasing calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum (calcium-induced calcium release).","Gap junctions do spread excitation so the ventricles contract as a unit, but they do not stop one contraction from summating with the next.","Most of the calcium for cardiac contraction comes from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Calcium entering from outside mainly acts as a trigger for that release."],"study":{"topics":["Cardiac refractory period","Calcium-induced calcium release"],"comps":["MS-7"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C16","system":"MS","dx":"Type I osteogenesis imperfecta in a toddler evaluated for abuse","codes":["MS-13-D3"],"case":"A 20-month-old girl is brought to the emergency department because she would not use her right arm after a fall from a low couch. Her father had several fractures in childhood and began to lose his hearing in his 30s. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows blue-gray sclerae, a swollen, tender right upper arm, and mildly lax joints. Radiographs show a transverse fracture of the middle third of the right humeral shaft. A skeletal survey shows generalized osteopenia, a healed fracture of the left tibia, and many small extra bones within the lambdoid suture. A child protection evaluation is started. Diagnosis: osteogenesis imperfecta type I.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C16-Q1","stem":"Which of the following additional findings on the skeletal survey would most strongly suggest nonaccidental trauma instead of her bone disease?","options":["Compression and wedging of several vertebral bodies","Anterior bowing of both femurs and both tibias","Thin cortices and slender shafts of the long bones","Corner fractures of the distal femoral metaphyses"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-D3","obj":"MS-13-D.6","dok":3,"approach":["List the features already pointing to osteogenesis imperfecta: blue sclerae, wormian bones, osteopenia, and an affected father.","Recall the fracture patterns that are highly specific for abuse: classic metaphyseal lesions and posterior rib fractures.","Pick the finding produced by shaking or twisting forces that bone fragility alone rarely explains."],"why":"Osteogenesis imperfecta and child abuse both cause multiple fractures, and the two must be told apart carefully. Clues for osteogenesis imperfecta include blue or gray sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta, wormian bones, osteopenia, bowing, and a family history of fractures or early hearing loss. Clues for abuse include classic metaphyseal lesions (corner or bucket-handle fractures), posterior rib fractures, fractures of different ages without a fitting history, and bruising in protected areas, usually in bone of normal density. Both can occur together, so the evaluation continues even when osteogenesis imperfecta is confirmed.","whyNot":["Vertebral compression fractures are common in osteogenesis imperfecta because of low bone mass.","Long bone bowing results from repeated microfractures in weak bone and is typical of osteogenesis imperfecta.","Thin cortices and slender shafts reflect too little type I collagen matrix. They support osteogenesis imperfecta.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Osteogenesis imperfecta versus abuse","Classic metaphyseal lesions"],"comps":["MS-13-D3","MS-13-H"]}},{"id":"MS-C16-Q2","stem":"Her primary teeth are gray-brown and translucent and wear down quickly. Which of the following best explains these dental findings?","options":["Defective amelogenin in the developing enamel","Abnormal type I collagen in the dentin matrix","Excess fluoride built into enamel crystals","Tetracycline bound to developing tooth mineral"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-D3","obj":"MS-13-D.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that the organic matrix of dentin, like bone, is mainly type I collagen.","Osteogenesis imperfecta is a type I collagen disorder, so dentin is abnormal (dentinogenesis imperfecta).","Weak dentin looks translucent and gray-brown, and the enamel on top chips off, so teeth wear quickly."],"why":"Osteogenesis imperfecta is caused by mutations in COL1A1 or COL1A2, the genes for type I collagen. Every tissue rich in type I collagen is affected: bone (fractures with little trauma), sclera (thin, so the dark choroid shows through as blue), dentin (dentinogenesis imperfecta), ligaments (joint laxity), and the middle ear ossicles (conductive hearing loss, often starting in early adulthood, as in her father). Enamel contains no collagen, though it chips away from the weak dentin beneath it.","whyNot":["Amelogenin defects cause amelogenesis imperfecta, a disorder of enamel with normal dentin and normal bones.","Correct.","Excess fluoride causes dental fluorosis, with white or brown mottling of enamel. It does not weaken bone in childhood.","Tetracycline taken while teeth are forming stains them yellow to brown in bands. It does not cause fractures or blue sclerae."],"study":{"topics":["Osteogenesis imperfecta features","Dentinogenesis imperfecta"],"comps":["MS-13-D3","MS-6"]}},{"id":"MS-C16-Q3","stem":"Genetic testing shows a heterozygous COL1A1 mutation that creates a premature stop codon, and the mutant mRNA is destroyed by nonsense-mediated decay. Which of the following best explains why her disease is milder than osteogenesis imperfecta caused by a glycine substitution in the triple helix?","options":["Mutant chains enter triple helices and disrupt most of them","The mutation affects only collagen made by osteoblasts","Collagen is made in half the usual amount but is normal","The normal allele doubles its output and fully compensates"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-9","obj":"MS-9.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note that her mutant mRNA is destroyed, so no mutant chains are made.","Only the normal allele produces chains, so collagen is reduced in amount but normal in structure (haploinsufficiency).","Compare glycine substitutions, where abnormal chains are built into helices and damage many molecules (dominant negative effect)."],"why":"Type I collagen has two pro-alpha1 chains (COL1A1) and one pro-alpha2 chain (COL1A2). Glycine must occupy every third position because only this smallest amino acid fits in the center of the triple helix. A glycine substitution produces a chain that still joins helices but kinks them, and because each molecule contains two alpha1 chains, about three quarters of the molecules are abnormal. This dominant negative effect causes the severe forms. A null allele removed by nonsense-mediated decay leaves only normal chains in half the amount, which gives the milder type I form.","whyNot":["This is the dominant negative mechanism of glycine substitutions, which cause the more severe forms. Her mutant mRNA is destroyed, so no mutant chains are made.","Type I collagen made by fibroblasts and odontoblasts is also affected, which explains her blue sclerae, lax joints, and abnormal teeth.","Correct.","The remaining normal allele does not fully make up the loss. If it did, she would have no fractures."],"study":{"topics":["Dominant negative versus null alleles","Collagen triple helix structure"],"comps":["MS-9","MS-6"]}},{"id":"MS-C16-Q4","stem":"Which of the following deficits should be looked for most carefully in her injured right limb?","options":["Inability to spread the fingers apart","Inability to abduct the arm at the shoulder","Inability to extend the wrist and fingers","Inability to flex the elbow with the forearm supinated"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-3","obj":"MS-3.4","dok":3,"approach":["Locate the fracture: the middle third of the humeral shaft.","Recall that the radial nerve runs in the radial groove on the back of the humeral shaft at this level.","Predict radial nerve injury: weak wrist and finger extension (wrist drop)."],"why":"The radial nerve winds around the back of the middle of the humerus in the radial groove, with the deep brachial artery. A midshaft fracture can stretch or trap it, causing wrist drop and loss of finger extension at the metacarpophalangeal joints, with sensory loss over the back of the hand. Other classic pairings: surgical neck fracture with the axillary nerve, supracondylar fracture with the median nerve and brachial artery, and medial epicondyle fracture with the ulnar nerve.","whyNot":["Inability to spread the fingers reflects weak interossei from ulnar nerve injury, as with medial epicondyle fractures.","Loss of shoulder abduction suggests axillary nerve injury, which goes with fractures of the surgical neck of the humerus or shoulder dislocation.","Correct.","Weak elbow flexion with the forearm supinated suggests musculocutaneous nerve injury, which is rare with humeral shaft fractures."],"study":{"topics":["Nerves at risk with humeral fractures","Radial nerve course"],"comps":["MS-3","MS-11"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C17","system":"MS","dx":"Classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome with recurrent shoulder dislocation","codes":["MS-13-D"],"case":"A 22-year-old woman comes to the emergency department 1 hour after her left shoulder dislocated while she reached overhead to place a box on a shelf. This is her fourth shoulder dislocation since the age of 16 years. She bruises easily, and her mother has similar skin and joint problems. Neither has had arterial or bowel rupture. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows a squared-off left shoulder with the humeral head palpable anteriorly. Her skin is soft and stretches easily, and there are wide, thin, shiny scars over both knees and the forehead. She can place her palms flat on the floor with her knees straight. Diagnosis: classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C17-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her skin and joint findings?","options":["Glycine substitutions in the type I collagen triple helix","Mutant type V collagen disrupting type I fibril assembly","Mutant type IV collagen weakening basement membranes","Mutant type III collagen weakening arteries and bowel wall"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-D","obj":"MS-13-D.7","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the classic type of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome: stretchy skin, wide atrophic scars, lax joints, and no arterial or bowel rupture.","Recall that classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is most often caused by mutations in type V collagen (COL5A1 or COL5A2).","Type V collagen helps control the size of type I fibrils, so its loss makes dermal and ligament fibrils irregular and weak."],"why":"Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is a group of disorders of collagen structure or processing. In the classic type, mutant type V collagen fails to regulate the assembly of type I collagen fibrils, so dermis and ligaments contain irregular, weak fibrils. The result is soft, stretchy skin that splits easily and heals with wide, thin scars, joint hypermobility with dislocations, and easy bruising from fragile small vessels. The vascular type, caused by type III collagen mutations, carries the risk of arterial, bowel, and uterine rupture.","whyNot":["Glycine substitutions in type I collagen cause the severe forms of osteogenesis imperfecta, with fractures and blue sclerae.","Correct.","Type IV collagen mutations cause Alport syndrome, with kidney disease, hearing loss, and eye findings.","Type III collagen mutations cause vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, with thin, translucent skin and rupture of arteries, bowel, or uterus. Her family has none of these."],"study":{"topics":["Types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Collagen types and disease"],"comps":["MS-13-D","MS-6"]}},{"id":"MS-C17-Q2","stem":"Because of her easy bruising, a nutritional cause of weak collagen is considered before genetic testing. In vitamin C deficiency, which of the following steps of collagen synthesis is impaired?","options":["Cross-linking of collagen fibrils by lysyl oxidase","Glycosylation of hydroxylysine residues in the rough ER","Cleavage of propeptides from procollagen outside the cell","Hydroxylation of proline and lysine in the rough ER"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the order of collagen synthesis: chain synthesis, hydroxylation, glycosylation, triple helix formation, secretion, propeptide cleavage, then cross-linking.","Identify the step that needs ascorbate: prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases.","Predict the result: unstable helices and fragile vessels, causing bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhage, and easy bruising."],"why":"Collagen chains are made in the rough endoplasmic reticulum as repeats of Gly-X-Y. Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases then add hydroxyl groups, and they need vitamin C (and iron) as cofactors. Hydroxyproline stabilizes the triple helix, and hydroxylysine is needed for glycosylation and later cross-linking. In scurvy, underhydroxylated collagen forms unstable helices, so vessel walls are fragile and wounds heal poorly. Other steps fail in other diseases: propeptide cleavage in some Ehlers-Danlos types and cross-linking in Menkes disease.","whyNot":["Lysyl oxidase needs copper. Its failure in Menkes disease, a disorder of copper transport, weakens collagen and elastin.","Glycosylation of hydroxylysine follows hydroxylation and does not itself require vitamin C.","Procollagen peptidases cleave the propeptides outside the cell. Defects in this step cause rare types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Collagen synthesis steps","Scurvy"],"comps":["MS-6","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C17-Q3","stem":"After reduction of her shoulder, which of the following findings would most likely indicate a nerve injury caused by the dislocation?","options":["Lateral shoulder numbness and weak arm abduction","Weak elbow flexion and lateral forearm numbness","Weak wrist extension and numbness of the dorsal hand","Scapular winging when pushing against a wall"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-4","obj":"MS-4.3","dok":3,"approach":["In an anterior dislocation, the humeral head moves forward and down, below the coracoid process.","The axillary nerve wraps around the surgical neck of the humerus just below the joint capsule and is stretched.","Predict axillary nerve signs: weak deltoid abduction and numbness over the lateral shoulder."],"why":"The glenohumeral joint trades stability for range of motion. The glenoid fossa is shallow, so the joint depends on the rotator cuff muscles and the capsule and ligaments to hold the humeral head in place. With lax capsular collagen in Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, the head dislocates easily, most often anteriorly. The axillary nerve passes through the quadrangular space and around the surgical neck, so anterior dislocation can stretch it. Injury weakens the deltoid and teres minor and numbs the skin over the lower deltoid.","whyNot":["Correct.","Musculocutaneous nerve injury can occur with dislocation, but it is much less common than axillary nerve injury.","Weak wrist extension with dorsal hand numbness points to radial nerve injury, usually from a fracture of the humeral shaft.","Scapular winging comes from long thoracic nerve injury, which affects the serratus anterior. This nerve runs on the chest wall and is not stretched by anterior dislocation."],"study":{"topics":["Glenohumeral stability","Axillary nerve injury"],"comps":["MS-4","MS-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C17-Q4","stem":"Six weeks later, she inverts her right ankle while stepping off a curb. There is swelling and tenderness just anterior and inferior to the lateral malleolus, with no tenderness over the bones. The anterior drawer test shows increased forward movement of the foot. Radiographs show no fracture. Which of the following best describes this injury?","options":["Sprain of the anterior talofibular ligament","Strain of the fibularis brevis musculotendinous unit","Contusion of the soft tissue over the lateral malleolus","Dislocation of the talocrural joint"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-H1","obj":"MS-13-H.3","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the tenderness: anterior and inferior to the lateral malleolus, the site of the anterior talofibular ligament.","A positive anterior drawer test means this ligament is torn or stretched.","An injury to a ligament is a sprain, while a strain affects muscle or tendon."],"why":"A sprain is a stretch or tear of a ligament. A strain is an injury to a muscle or tendon. A contusion is bleeding into soft tissue from a direct blow, and a dislocation is complete loss of contact between joint surfaces. Inversion with the foot pointed down most often injures the anterior talofibular ligament, and the anterior drawer test checks it. Ligaments heal slowly because they are dense collagen with few fibroblasts and limited blood supply, and the repair scar is weaker than the original ligament. In Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, the lax collagen raises the risk of sprains.","whyNot":["Correct.","A fibularis brevis strain would cause pain along the tendon behind the malleolus or at its insertion on the fifth metatarsal, with pain on resisted eversion. A positive anterior drawer test points to a ligament.","A contusion follows a direct blow and causes bruising without joint instability. Her anterior drawer test is abnormal.","A talocrural dislocation would cause obvious deformity and usually a fracture. Her joint surfaces remain in contact."],"study":{"topics":["Sprain, strain, contusion, dislocation","Lateral ankle ligaments"],"comps":["MS-13-H","MS-4"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C18","system":"MS","dx":"Achondroplasia","codes":["MS-13-D"],"case":"A 10-month-old boy is brought to the physician for a well-child examination. He was born at term to a 27-year-old mother and a 45-year-old father, both of average height, and there is no family history of short stature. His length is below the 1st percentile, and his head circumference is above the 97th percentile. Physical examination shows short arms and legs with redundant skin folds, a trunk of normal length, a prominent forehead, a flat nasal bridge, and short fingers with a gap between the middle and ring fingers. Radiographs show short tubular bones. Diagnosis: achondroplasia.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C18-Q1","stem":"Which of the following patterns of limb shortening is most characteristic of his disorder?","options":["Hands and feet shortened most","Forearms and lower legs shortened most","Trunk shortened more than the limbs","Upper arms and thighs shortened most"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-D","obj":"MS-13-D.8","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that achondroplasia impairs endochondral bone growth at the growth plates.","The proximal long bones (humerus and femur) normally grow the most in length, so they lose the most.","Name the pattern: rhizomelic shortening, with a trunk of near-normal length."],"why":"Achondroplasia is the most common skeletal dysplasia. All long bones are short, but the proximal segments, the humerus and femur, are shortened most (rhizomelia). The trunk is close to normal length, the head is large with frontal bossing and a flat nasal bridge, and the hands are short with a trident shape. Intelligence is normal. Motor milestones can be delayed in infancy because of low muscle tone and the large head, which is different from cognitive delay.","whyNot":["The hands are short in achondroplasia, but the most marked shortening is in the proximal segments.","Shortening that is worst in the forearms and lower legs is mesomelic. It is seen in other skeletal dysplasias.","A trunk shorter than the limbs points to a spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia, which affects the spine. In achondroplasia the trunk is close to normal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Achondroplasia features","Rhizomelic shortening"],"comps":["MS-13-D","MS-2"]}},{"id":"MS-C18-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains how this child acquired his disorder?","options":["Both parents carry one copy of a recessive mutant allele","A new FGFR3 gain-of-function mutation in a sperm cell","An FGFR3 loss-of-function allele inherited with low penetrance","Maternal uniparental disomy of chromosome 4"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-9","obj":"MS-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Achondroplasia is autosomal dominant with essentially full penetrance, so an affected parent would show it.","Both parents are of average height, so the mutation is new.","Most new achondroplasia mutations arise in the father's germ cells and become more frequent with advanced paternal age."],"why":"Achondroplasia is caused by gain-of-function mutations in FGFR3, most often the same glycine-to-arginine change at position 380. FGFR3 normally slows chondrocyte proliferation in the growth plate, and the overactive receptor slows it too much. About 80% of cases are new mutations, and these arise almost always on the paternal chromosome, with risk rising with paternal age. Recurrence risk for these parents is low, but each child of an affected person has a 50% chance of inheriting it.","whyNot":["Achondroplasia is autosomal dominant, so carrier parents of average height do not explain it. Two affected parents can have a child with the severe homozygous form.","Correct.","The FGFR3 change in achondroplasia increases receptor activity, and penetrance is essentially complete, so a parent carrying it would be affected.","Uniparental disomy causes disease mainly through imprinting or by unmasking a recessive allele. It is not how achondroplasia arises."],"study":{"topics":["FGFR3 and achondroplasia","Advanced paternal age and new mutations"],"comps":["MS-9","MS-13-D"]}},{"id":"MS-C18-Q3","stem":"If a biopsy of his proximal tibial growth plate were examined, which of the following would most likely be seen?","options":["Retained calcified cartilage cores within dense trabeculae","Widened hypertrophic zone with unmineralized cartilage","Short, disorganized columns in the proliferative zone","Early closure of the plate with bony bridging across it"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.5","dok":3,"approach":["List the growth plate zones from the epiphysis: reserve, proliferative, hypertrophic, calcification, and ossification.","Overactive FGFR3 inhibits chondrocyte proliferation.","Predict a short, poorly organized proliferative zone, so less cartilage is made for bone to replace."],"why":"Long bones grow in length when chondrocytes in the proliferative zone divide and stack into columns, then enlarge in the hypertrophic zone before the matrix calcifies and is replaced by bone. In achondroplasia, overactive FGFR3 slows proliferation and hypertrophy, so the columns are short and disorganized and bone length suffers. Intramembranous bone formation is spared, so the skull vault and the width of the long bones develop normally, which helps explain the large head. In rickets, the problem is mineralization, so the hypertrophic zone widens with uncalcified cartilage.","whyNot":["Retained calcified cartilage within dense bone is seen in osteopetrosis, where osteoclasts fail to remove it.","A widened hypertrophic zone with unmineralized cartilage is the growth plate picture of rickets from vitamin D deficiency.","Correct.","Early fusion of the growth plate follows trauma, infection, or excess sex steroids. It does not explain disproportionate shortening from birth."],"study":{"topics":["Growth plate zones","Achondroplasia versus rickets"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-9"]}},{"id":"MS-C18-Q4","stem":"His parents ask whether his limbs formed abnormally early in pregnancy. His limbs have every normal segment and differ only in length. In the embryo, loss of which of the following signals during limb bud outgrowth would produce limbs that end abruptly, with proximal segments present and distal segments missing?","options":["HOX transcription factors in limb mesenchyme","WNT7a from the dorsal ectoderm","Sonic hedgehog from the zone of polarizing activity","FGF from the apical ectodermal ridge"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-1","obj":"MS-1.3","dok":3,"approach":["Link each signal center to its axis: the apical ectodermal ridge for proximal to distal, the zone of polarizing activity for anterior to posterior, and the dorsal ectoderm for dorsal to ventral.","Truncation of the distal limb means proximal to distal outgrowth stopped.","The apical ectodermal ridge drives that outgrowth through FGF signaling."],"why":"The apical ectodermal ridge is a thickened rim of ectoderm at the tip of the limb bud. It secretes FGFs that keep the underlying mesenchyme dividing, so the limb grows out from proximal to distal. Removing the ridge early truncates the limb near the body, and later removal truncates it more distally. Sonic hedgehog from the zone of polarizing activity sets the anterior to posterior pattern (extra expression causes polydactyly), HOX genes give each segment its identity, and thalidomide disrupts outgrowth to cause phocomelia. His limbs are complete but short because his defect lies in later growth plate function.","whyNot":["HOX genes give segments their identity. Their mutations change the shape or number of segments, as in synpolydactyly, instead of stopping outgrowth.","WNT7a from the dorsal ectoderm sets the dorsal to ventral pattern, such as nails on the back of the digits.","Sonic hedgehog from the zone of polarizing activity patterns the limb from thumb side to little finger side. Its ectopic expression causes extra digits.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Limb bud signaling centers","Limb defects"],"comps":["MS-1"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C19","system":"MS","dx":"Primary osteoarthritis of the knee","codes":["MS-13-E1"],"case":"A 67-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 3-year history of pain in her right knee. The pain worsens with walking and climbing stairs and eases with rest. She has about 15 minutes of stiffness after waking and after sitting for long periods. She worked as a mail carrier for 30 years. Her body mass index is 34 kg/m2. Vital signs are within normal limits. Examination of the right knee shows bony enlargement, crepitus with movement, medial joint line tenderness, a mild varus deformity, and a small, cool effusion. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 12 mm/h (normal 0 to 30). Synovial fluid analysis shows a leukocyte count of 700/mm3 (noninflammatory, below 2,000) and no crystals. Radiographs show medial joint space narrowing, osteophytes, and subchondral sclerosis. Diagnosis: primary osteoarthritis of the right knee.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C19-Q1","stem":"Three years later, she undergoes total knee arthroplasty. The medial femoral condyle has a smooth, polished, ivory-like surface. Which of the following best explains this appearance?","options":["Inflamed synovial pannus spreading across the cartilage","Exposed subchondral bone polished by friction","Chalky urate deposits coating the articular cartilage","Calcium pyrophosphate crystals deposited within cartilage"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-E1","obj":"MS-13-E.1","dok":3,"approach":["Trace the sequence of osteoarthritis: proteoglycan loss, fibrillation and cracking of cartilage, then full-thickness cartilage loss.","Once cartilage is gone, bone rubs on bone.","The rubbing polishes and thickens the subchondral bone, called eburnation."],"why":"Osteoarthritis starts with chondrocyte injury from age, load, and obesity. Chondrocytes release enzymes that break down proteoglycan and type II collagen, the cartilage softens, splits along its surface (fibrillation), and wears away. When subchondral bone is exposed, friction polishes it into a smooth, ivory-like surface (eburnation), and the bone beneath thickens (sclerosis) and may form cysts. Osteophytes grow at the joint margins. Her medial compartment carries the most load, especially with a varus knee, so it wears first.","whyNot":["Pannus is inflamed, proliferating synovium that erodes cartilage in rheumatoid arthritis. Her synovial fluid is noninflammatory.","Correct.","Chalky white urate deposits on joint surfaces are seen in chronic gout. Her synovial fluid contained no crystals.","Calcium pyrophosphate deposition (chondrocalcinosis) occurs in pseudogout. It does not produce a polished bone surface."],"study":{"topics":["Osteoarthritis pathogenesis","Eburnation and osteophytes"],"comps":["MS-13-E1","MS-6"]}},{"id":"MS-C19-Q2","stem":"Examination of her hands is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Ulnar deviation and swelling of the metacarpophalangeal joints","Sausage-shaped swelling of an entire digit with nail pitting","Hard bony enlargement of the distal interphalangeal joints","Soft, warm, symmetric swelling of both wrists"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-E1","obj":"MS-13-E.2","dok":2,"approach":["Osteoarthritis in older women often involves the hands as well as weight-bearing joints.","Bony osteophytes at the distal interphalangeal joints are Heberden nodes, and at the proximal interphalangeal joints Bouchard nodes.","Separate these hard, cool swellings from the soft, warm synovitis of inflammatory arthritis."],"why":"Osteoarthritis causes pain that worsens with use, morning stiffness under 30 minutes, and asymmetric involvement of weight-bearing joints such as the knee and hip. In the hands it targets the distal and proximal interphalangeal joints and the base of the thumb, producing hard bony swellings: Heberden nodes at the distal joints and Bouchard nodes at the proximal joints. The metacarpophalangeal joints and wrists are usually spared. Radiographs show joint space narrowing, osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis, and cysts.","whyNot":["Ulnar deviation with metacarpophalangeal swelling is typical of rheumatoid arthritis, which also causes prolonged morning stiffness.","A sausage digit with nail pitting points to psoriatic arthritis.","Correct.","Soft, warm, symmetric wrist swelling indicates synovitis from an inflammatory arthritis such as rheumatoid arthritis."],"study":{"topics":["Osteoarthritis findings","Heberden and Bouchard nodes"],"comps":["MS-13-E1","MS-13-A"]}},{"id":"MS-C19-Q3","stem":"Loss of proteoglycan is one of the earliest changes in her articular cartilage. Which of the following components of normal articular cartilage is mainly responsible for resisting compressive loads?","options":["Water bound by the sulfated chains of aggrecan","Elastic fibers that recoil after the load is removed","A dense capillary network supplying the chondrocytes","Type I collagen fibers aligned parallel to the surface"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the structure of aggrecan: a core protein with many chondroitin sulfate and keratan sulfate chains, attached in large numbers to hyaluronan.","The negatively charged sulfate groups attract cations and water, which swell the matrix.","Bound water resists compression like a cushion, and losing proteoglycan lets cartilage deform and wear."],"why":"Articular cartilage is hyaline cartilage made of type II collagen and proteoglycan, mainly aggrecan. Each aggrecan molecule carries many chondroitin sulfate and keratan sulfate chains and attaches to hyaluronan to form huge aggregates. Their dense negative charge draws in water, which resists compression and is pushed out and drawn back with each load. Cartilage has no blood vessels, so chondrocytes rely on diffusion from synovial fluid, which helps explain why damaged cartilage repairs so poorly.","whyNot":["Correct.","Elastic fibers are found in elastic cartilage of the ear and epiglottis. Hyaline articular cartilage relies on bound water to resist compression.","Articular cartilage is avascular. Chondrocytes receive nutrients by diffusion from synovial fluid.","Articular cartilage collagen is type II, and collagen fibrils mainly resist tension. Type I collagen is found in bone, tendon, and fibrocartilage."],"study":{"topics":["Cartilage matrix and aggrecan","Why cartilage heals poorly"],"comps":["MS-6","MS-13-E1"]}},{"id":"MS-C19-Q4","stem":"To reduce her knee pain, she begins a 12-week program of progressive resistance exercise for the quadriceps. Which of the following changes in the trained muscle is most likely?","options":["Higher mitochondrial density, mainly in type I fibers","Enlargement of existing fibers, mainly type II","Formation of many new fibers by fiber splitting","Conversion of most type II fibers to type I fibers"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.6","dok":2,"approach":["Separate the two training types: endurance training improves oxidative capacity, and resistance training builds strength.","Strength gains come mostly from adding myofibrils to existing fibers.","Type II fibers show the most hypertrophy with resistance training."],"why":"Type I fibers are slow, oxidative, red, and rich in mitochondria and myoglobin, and they resist fatigue. Type II fibers are fast and glycolytic, generate high force, and fatigue quickly. Heavy resistance training enlarges existing fibers by adding actin and myosin filaments, with the largest gains in type II fibers, so strength increases. Endurance training instead increases mitochondria, capillaries, and oxidative enzymes, mainly in type I fibers. Early strength gains in the first weeks also come from better neural recruitment.","whyNot":["More mitochondria in type I fibers is an adaptation to endurance training, such as long-distance running or cycling.","Correct.","In adult humans, strength training enlarges existing fibers. New fiber formation contributes little or nothing.","Training changes fiber subtypes only slightly, mostly within type II fibers. It does not convert most fast fibers into slow fibers."],"study":{"topics":["Muscle fiber types","Adaptations to training"],"comps":["MS-7"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C20","system":"MS","dx":"Postmenopausal osteoporosis with vertebral compression fracture","codes":["MS-13-E2"],"case":"A 71-year-old woman comes to the physician because of sudden midback pain that began 5 days ago when she lifted a bag of groceries. She has lost 4 cm of height over 10 years. Menopause occurred at the age of 47 years, and she has never taken hormone therapy. She has smoked one pack of cigarettes daily for 40 years. Her body mass index is 19 kg/m2. Vital signs are within normal limits. Examination shows thoracic kyphosis and point tenderness over the T12 spinous process. Serum studies: calcium 9.4 mg/dL (normal 8.5 to 10.5), phosphorus 3.6 mg/dL (normal 2.5 to 4.5), alkaline phosphatase 82 U/L (normal 40 to 120), and PTH 38 pg/mL (normal 10 to 65). A radiograph shows anterior wedging of the T12 vertebral body. Diagnosis: postmenopausal osteoporosis with a vertebral compression fracture.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C20-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the accelerated bone loss that began after her menopause?","options":["Defective mineralization of osteoid from vitamin D deficiency","Less calcitonin secretion, which releases osteoclasts from inhibition","Lower PTH secretion, which slows osteoblast-driven bone formation","More RANKL and less osteoprotegerin, increasing osteoclast formation"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-13-E2","obj":"MS-13-E.3","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the hormonal change: loss of estrogen at menopause.","Recall that estrogen restrains bone resorption by lowering RANKL and raising osteoprotegerin.","Without estrogen, more osteoclasts form and live longer, so resorption outpaces formation."],"why":"Osteoblasts and stromal cells express RANKL, which binds RANK on osteoclast precursors and drives their formation and activity. Osteoprotegerin is a decoy receptor that blocks RANKL. Estrogen lowers RANKL, raises osteoprotegerin, reduces bone-resorbing cytokines such as IL-1, IL-6, and TNF, and shortens osteoclast life span. After menopause these brakes are lost, and trabecular bone, as in the vertebrae, thins fastest. Her early menopause, smoking, and low body weight add to the risk, as do low peak bone mass and aging.","whyNot":["Defective mineralization is osteomalacia. In osteoporosis the bone that remains is normally mineralized, and her calcium, phosphorus, and alkaline phosphatase are normal.","Calcitonin plays only a small role in adult bone. People who lose it after thyroidectomy do not develop osteoporosis from that loss.","Low PTH would reduce bone resorption and would not explain bone loss. Her PTH is normal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Estrogen and RANKL","Risk factors for osteoporosis"],"comps":["MS-13-E2","MS-2"]}},{"id":"MS-C20-Q2","stem":"Dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry shows a lumbar spine T-score of minus 3.1. Which of the following best describes the meaning of this value?","options":["3.1 standard deviations below the mean for women of her age","31 percent below the mean bone mass for women her age","31 percent below the young adult female mean bone mass","3.1 standard deviations below the young adult female mean"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-E2","obj":"MS-13-E.4","dok":3,"approach":["A T-score compares bone density with the peak value of healthy young adults of the same sex.","It is expressed in standard deviations.","A Z-score is the measure that compares with people of the same age."],"why":"The T-score is the number of standard deviations a patient's bone density lies above or below the mean for healthy young adults of the same sex. A T-score of minus 1 or higher is normal, between minus 1 and minus 2.5 is osteopenia, and minus 2.5 or lower is osteoporosis. A fragility fracture of the spine or hip establishes osteoporosis on its own, whatever the T-score. Typical findings are vertebral compression, hip, and distal radius fractures, height loss, and kyphosis, with normal serum calcium, phosphate, and alkaline phosphatase.","whyNot":["Comparison with people of the same age and sex is the Z-score, which is used for premenopausal women, men under 50, and children.","This mixes two errors: the comparison group of the Z-score and a percentage instead of standard deviations.","T-scores are expressed in standard deviations. A percentage loss cannot be read directly from the score.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["DEXA T-score and Z-score","Osteoporosis findings"],"comps":["MS-13-E2"]}},{"id":"MS-C20-Q3","stem":"Which of the following sets of serum findings would have pointed to osteomalacia from vitamin D deficiency instead of osteoporosis?","options":["Low calcium, low phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase, high PTH","Normal calcium, normal phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase, normal PTH","High calcium, low phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase, high PTH","Low calcium, high phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase, high PTH"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-13-C","obj":"MS-13-C.3","dok":3,"approach":["Vitamin D deficiency lowers intestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate.","Low calcium triggers secondary hyperparathyroidism, and PTH makes the kidney waste phosphate.","Poorly mineralized osteoid stimulates osteoblasts, which raises alkaline phosphatase."],"why":"In osteoporosis, bone mass is low but its mineral is normal, so calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, and PTH are all normal, as in this patient. In vitamin D deficiency osteomalacia, calcium is low or low-normal, phosphate is low, PTH is high, and alkaline phosphatase is high. Primary hyperparathyroidism raises calcium and lowers phosphate. Renal osteodystrophy lowers calcium and raises phosphate because the failing kidney cannot excrete phosphate or make calcitriol. Paget disease raises alkaline phosphatase alone.","whyNot":["Correct.","An isolated high alkaline phosphatase with normal minerals and PTH is the pattern of Paget disease of bone.","High calcium with low phosphate and high PTH is primary hyperparathyroidism, usually from a parathyroid adenoma.","Low calcium with high phosphate and high PTH is renal osteodystrophy from chronic kidney disease."],"study":{"topics":["Lab patterns in metabolic bone disease","Osteomalacia"],"comps":["MS-13-C","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C20-Q4","stem":"She has taken warfarin for 6 years for atrial fibrillation. Which of the following modifications of a bone matrix protein is most likely reduced by this drug?","options":["Lysyl oxidase cross-linking of type I collagen fibrils","Hydroxylation of proline residues in type I collagen","Gamma-carboxylation of glutamate in osteocalcin","Phosphorylation of serine residues in osteopontin"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase, so active vitamin K is depleted.","Vitamin K is the cofactor for gamma-carboxylation of glutamate residues.","Apply this beyond clotting factors: osteoblasts make osteocalcin, a vitamin K dependent protein."],"why":"Vitamin K is needed by gamma-glutamyl carboxylase, which adds a carboxyl group to specific glutamate residues. This creates gamma-carboxyglutamate (Gla), which binds calcium. Clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X depend on it, and so does osteocalcin, a bone matrix protein made by osteoblasts that binds hydroxyapatite. Warfarin and vitamin K deficiency leave osteocalcin undercarboxylated. Other cofactors matter for bone and muscle too: copper for lysyl oxidase, vitamin C for collagen hydroxylation, and magnesium for PTH secretion.","whyNot":["Lysyl oxidase needs copper and is impaired in Menkes disease and copper deficiency.","Proline hydroxylation needs vitamin C and fails in scurvy. Warfarin does not affect it.","Correct.","Osteopontin phosphorylation is done by kinases and does not depend on vitamin K."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin K and osteocalcin","Minerals and cofactors in bone"],"comps":["MS-8","MS-13-E2"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C21","system":"MS","dx":"Paget disease of bone","codes":["MS-13-E3"],"case":"A 68-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 1-year history of a deep, aching pain in his right hip and pelvis that is worse at night. He has also noticed that his hats no longer fit. Physical examination shows frontal bossing and warm skin over the right anterior thigh. Laboratory studies show: calcium 9.6 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5), phosphate 3.4 mg/dL (2.5 to 4.5), alkaline phosphatase 410 U/L (40 to 130), and parathyroid hormone 38 pg/mL (10 to 65). Radiographs show a thickened cortex and coarse trabeculae in the right hemipelvis and proximal femur, and patchy areas of increased density in the skull. Diagnosis: Paget disease of bone.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C21-Q1","stem":"A bone biopsy is taken from the right iliac crest. Which of the following histologic findings is most likely?","options":["Trabeculae with retained cores of calcified cartilage","Mosaic of lamellar bone joined by irregular cement lines","Wide seams of unmineralized osteoid covering normal trabeculae","Thin, widely spaced trabeculae with normal mineralization"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-E3","obj":"MS-13-E.5","dok":2,"approach":["Notice an older man with bone pain, enlarging skull, and an isolated high alkaline phosphatase with normal calcium, phosphate, and PTH.","That pattern points to Paget disease, a disorder of disorganized remodeling.","Predict the tissue result: repeated rounds of resorption and hasty repair leave a mosaic of lamellar bone."],"why":"Paget disease starts with overactive, oversized osteoclasts that resorb bone. Osteoblasts then lay down new bone quickly and in a disorganized way. The many rounds of resorption and repair leave lamellar bone pieces joined by irregular cement lines, the classic mosaic pattern. The bone is thick but weak, and the high osteoblast activity raises alkaline phosphatase while calcium, phosphate, and PTH stay normal.","whyNot":["Retained cartilage cores reflect failed osteoclast resorption in osteopetrosis. Paget disease is the opposite at the start, with too much osteoclast activity.","Correct.","Excess unmineralized osteoid is the hallmark of osteomalacia from vitamin D deficiency. That causes low or low-normal calcium and phosphate with high PTH, which this patient does not have.","This is osteoporosis: less bone of normal quality. Osteoporosis does not thicken the cortex, enlarge the skull, or raise alkaline phosphatase on its own."],"study":{"topics":["Paget disease phases and mosaic bone","Isolated alkaline phosphatase elevation"],"comps":["MS-13-E3","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C21-Q2","stem":"Two years later, he reports shortness of breath on exertion. His pulse is 104/min and bounding, and the skin over the affected pelvis and skull is warm. Echocardiography shows a cardiac output of 9.5 L/min (4 to 8) with normal valves. Which of the following best explains these cardiac findings?","options":["Anemia from replacement of marrow by fibrous tissue","Hypercalcemia shortening the ventricular action potential","Systemic hypertension from calcification of large arteries","Arteriovenous shunting through hypervascular pagetic bone"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-13-E3","obj":"MS-13-E.6","dok":3,"approach":["Link warm skin over pagetic bone to increased blood flow through that bone.","Recall that very vascular pagetic bone acts like many small arteriovenous fistulas.","Low resistance lowers afterload and raises venous return, so the heart must pump a high output, which can lead to high-output failure."],"why":"In active Paget disease the remodeling bone and its marrow become highly vascular. When much of the skeleton is involved, the blood flowing through these beds acts like arteriovenous shunts. Systemic vascular resistance falls, venous return rises, and the heart responds with a high output and wide pulse pressure. Over time this load can produce high-output heart failure.","whyNot":["Severe anemia can cause high-output failure, but Paget disease does not typically cause marrow failure. Fibrous marrow in Paget disease is local, not widespread enough to cause anemia.","His calcium is normal. Hypercalcemia shortens the QT interval but does not raise cardiac output.","Arterial stiffening raises afterload and causes pressure overload. It does not raise cardiac output or warm the skin over the bones.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["High-output heart failure","Complications of Paget disease"],"comps":["MS-13-E3"]}},{"id":"MS-C21-Q3","stem":"Over the next year he develops left-sided sensorineural hearing loss and weakness of all the muscles of facial expression on the left. Narrowing of which of the following openings by thickened bone best explains both findings?","options":["Superior orbital fissure","Jugular foramen","Internal acoustic meatus","Stylomastoid foramen"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-3","obj":"MS-3.3","dok":2,"approach":["Name the nerves involved: facial weakness points to cranial nerve VII and sensorineural hearing loss to cranial nerve VIII.","Ask which skull opening carries both nerves together.","Cranial nerves VII and VIII enter the petrous temporal bone together through the internal acoustic meatus."],"why":"Cranial nerves VII and VIII leave the posterior cranial fossa together through the internal acoustic meatus in the petrous temporal bone. Thickening of the temporal bone in Paget disease can narrow this canal and compress both nerves. Hearing loss in Paget disease can also be conductive from involvement of the ossicles, but the combination with facial weakness places the lesion at the meatus.","whyNot":["This fissure carries cranial nerves III, IV, V1, and VI to the orbit. Compression would cause eye movement problems and forehead numbness, not hearing loss or facial weakness.","The jugular foramen carries cranial nerves IX, X, and XI and the internal jugular vein. Compression would cause swallowing and voice problems and trapezius weakness.","Correct.","Only the facial nerve exits here. Compression could cause facial weakness, but it could not explain sensorineural hearing loss."],"study":{"topics":["Skull foramina and their contents","Hearing loss in Paget disease"],"comps":["MS-3","MS-13-E3"]}},{"id":"MS-C21-Q4","stem":"He falls and fractures his left femoral shaft and is kept on bed rest for 3 weeks. His serum calcium rises to 11.8 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5). Which of the following sets of hormone changes is most likely?","options":["Low PTH and low calcitriol","Low PTH and high calcitriol","High PTH and high calcitriol","High PTH and low calcitriol"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the source of the extra calcium: immobilization speeds osteoclast resorption, and pagetic bone already turns over fast.","Calcium-sensing receptors on the parathyroid glands respond to the high calcium by cutting PTH secretion.","Without PTH, renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase is not stimulated, so calcitriol falls."],"why":"Bed rest removes the mechanical loading that keeps bone formation and resorption balanced, and resorption wins. In a patient whose bone already turns over rapidly, this can release enough calcium to cause hypercalcemia. The parathyroid glands sense high calcium and lower PTH. PTH normally stimulates 1-alpha-hydroxylase in the proximal tubule, so calcitriol also falls. This is a PTH-independent hypercalcemia.","whyNot":["Correct.","High calcitriol with low PTH occurs when calcitriol is made outside the kidney, as in sarcoidosis. Bed rest does not do this, and without PTH the renal enzyme is not stimulated.","This pattern is seen in primary hyperparathyroidism, where PTH drives the hypercalcemia. Here the calcium comes from bone resorption, so PTH should be suppressed.","High PTH with low calcitriol occurs in chronic kidney disease, where the kidney cannot make calcitriol. Calcium is usually low or normal in that setting."],"study":{"topics":["PTH and calcitriol regulation","Hypercalcemia of immobilization"],"comps":["MS-8","MS-13-E3"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C22","system":"MS","dx":"Avascular necrosis of the femoral head in SLE treated with glucocorticoids","codes":["MS-13-E4"],"case":"A 32-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of right groin pain that is worse with walking. For the past 2 years she has taken prednisone 20 to 40 mg daily for lupus nephritis. She does not drink alcohol. Physical examination shows pain and limited range of motion on internal rotation of the right hip. Radiographs of the hip are normal. MRI shows a band of low signal intensity outlining a wedge of subchondral bone in the anterosuperior right femoral head, with no collapse. Diagnosis: avascular necrosis of the right femoral head related to glucocorticoid therapy and SLE.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C22-Q1","stem":"A core decompression is performed, and tissue from the lesion is examined. Which of the following histologic findings is most likely?","options":["Mosaic lamellar bone with irregular cement lines and fibrovascular marrow","A fragment of dead bone surrounded by neutrophils and reactive new bone","Wide seams of unmineralized osteoid lining the trabeculae","Trabeculae with empty osteocyte lacunae and necrotic marrow fat"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-E4","obj":"MS-13-E.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize avascular necrosis as an infarct of bone and marrow from interrupted blood flow.","Ask what dead bone looks like: osteocytes die and leave empty lacunae, and marrow fat undergoes necrosis.","Choose the option that shows ischemic cell death without infection or a remodeling disorder."],"why":"Avascular necrosis is ischemic death of bone and marrow. Osteocytes die and their lacunae are left empty, and the marrow fat becomes necrotic. The dead subchondral bone cannot remodel well, so it can fracture and collapse, flattening the joint surface. Glucocorticoids, alcohol, SLE, sickle cell disease, decompression sickness, and femoral neck or scaphoid fractures are classic causes, and the femoral head is the most common site because its retinacular arteries have few collaterals.","whyNot":["This is Paget disease, which comes from disordered remodeling, not loss of blood supply. It occurs in older adults with high alkaline phosphatase.","A sequestrum with an involucrum is the picture of chronic osteomyelitis. That needs infection, and she has no fever or other signs of infection.","Wide osteoid seams mean poor mineralization, as in osteomalacia. Bone cells stay alive in osteomalacia.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Avascular necrosis causes and sites","Bone infarct histology"],"comps":["MS-13-E4","MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C22-Q2","stem":"Three years before her hip pain began, she had pain and swelling in the small joints of both hands. Which of the following findings in her hands is most likely?","options":["Sclerodactyly with fingertip calcinosis","Marginal bony erosions of the metacarpophalangeal joints","Reducible deformities without erosions on radiographs","Pencil-in-cup deformities of the distal joints"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the typical arthritis of SLE: symmetric small-joint polyarthritis.","Lupus synovitis does not erode bone; its deformities come from lax ligaments and joint capsules.","Pick the option that combines deformity with no erosions."],"why":"Arthritis is one of the most common features of SLE and usually involves the small joints of the hands symmetrically, like rheumatoid arthritis. Lupus synovitis does not form the invasive pannus that erodes cartilage and bone in rheumatoid arthritis. Some patients develop ulnar deviation and swan-neck deformities from lax tendons and capsules, which can be pushed back into place and show no erosions on radiographs (Jaccoud arthropathy).","whyNot":["Tight, thickened skin over the fingers with calcium deposits is a feature of systemic sclerosis, not SLE.","Marginal erosions come from invasive pannus in rheumatoid arthritis. Lupus arthritis does not form an erosive pannus.","Correct.","Pencil-in-cup erosions of the distal interphalangeal joints are typical of psoriatic arthritis, a seronegative spondyloarthropathy."],"study":{"topics":["SLE arthritis","Comparing inflammatory arthritides"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-13-B"]}},{"id":"MS-C22-Q3","stem":"During flares of her lupus, serum C3 and C4 fall and anti-double-stranded DNA titers rise, and her joint symptoms worsen. Which of the following mechanisms best explains her joint inflammation during these flares?","options":["Mast cell degranulation triggered by IgE cross-linking","Deposited circulating immune complexes that fix complement","Antibody binding to a fixed antigen on synovial lining cells","CD8+ T cells directly lysing muscle and synovial cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.5","dok":3,"approach":["Link low C3 and C4 to complement consumption.","Link anti-dsDNA antibodies to circulating antigen-antibody complexes.","Complexes that lodge in tissue and activate complement define type III hypersensitivity."],"why":"In SLE, antibodies bind nuclear antigens such as DNA to form circulating immune complexes. These complexes deposit in the synovium, skin, kidney, and vessel walls and activate the classical complement pathway, which consumes C3 and C4. Complement fragments such as C5a recruit neutrophils, which cause the inflammation. This is type III hypersensitivity, and falling complement with rising anti-dsDNA is a marker of active disease.","whyNot":["This is type I hypersensitivity, as in allergic reactions. It does not consume C3 and C4 or involve anti-DNA antibodies.","Correct.","Antibody against a fixed tissue antigen is type II hypersensitivity, as in the polyarthritis of rheumatic fever. Anti-DNA antibodies form circulating complexes instead.","Direct T-cell killing is type IV hypersensitivity, as in polymyositis. It does not lower complement levels."],"study":{"topics":["Hypersensitivity types","Immune complexes in SLE"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-13-E4"]}},{"id":"MS-C22-Q4","stem":"She also reports difficulty rising from a chair and climbing stairs. Examination shows symmetric weakness of the hip flexors and shoulder abductors without tenderness. Serum creatine kinase is 85 U/L (30 to 170). Which of the following findings on a muscle biopsy is most likely?","options":["Perifascicular atrophy with perimysial inflammatory cells","Endomysial CD8+ T cells with necrotic muscle fibers","Fiber type grouping with groups of atrophic fibers","Selective atrophy of type II fibers without necrosis"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.4","dok":3,"approach":["Notice proximal weakness with a normal creatine kinase in a patient on long-term glucocorticoids.","A normal CK argues against an inflammatory myopathy with fiber necrosis.","Glucocorticoid myopathy selectively shrinks type II (fast glycolytic) fibers."],"why":"Long-term glucocorticoids cause a proximal myopathy that mainly affects the hip and shoulder girdles. Steroids increase muscle protein breakdown and reduce synthesis, and type II fast glycolytic fibers are the most affected. Because the fibers shrink but do not die, creatine kinase stays normal. Disuse causes the same type II atrophy pattern.","whyNot":["Perifascicular atrophy is the hallmark of dermatomyositis, which raises CK and usually shows a skin rash.","This is polymyositis or lupus myositis. Active fiber necrosis releases creatine kinase, so CK would be high.","Fiber type grouping and grouped atrophy follow denervation and reinnervation, as in motor neuron disease. Neurogenic weakness also brings fasciculations and is often distal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Steroid myopathy","Neurogenic vs myopathic weakness"],"comps":["MS-10","MS-2"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C23","system":"MS","dx":"Chondrosarcoma arising from an osteochondroma","codes":["MS-13-F1"],"case":"A 41-year-old man comes to the physician because of an 8-month history of a slowly enlarging, painful mass over his left hip. Since childhood he has had multiple painless bony lumps near his knees, shoulders, and hips, and his father had similar lumps. Physical examination shows a firm, fixed mass over the left iliac wing and several hard, nontender bony prominences around both knees. Radiographs show multiple bony outgrowths from the metaphyses of long bones. MRI of the left ilium shows an outgrowth with a lobulated cartilage cap 4 cm thick. Biopsy shows a malignant cartilage-forming tumor. Diagnosis: secondary chondrosarcoma arising from an osteochondroma in hereditary multiple osteochondromas.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C23-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings in the resected tumor would most strongly support chondrosarcoma rather than osteosarcoma?","options":["Malignant chondrocytes in hyaline matrix with no tumor osteoid","Mononuclear stromal cells mixed with osteoclast-like giant cells","Pleomorphic spindle cells laying down lacelike osteoid directly","Sheets of small round blue cells with membranous CD99 staining"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F1","obj":"MS-13-F.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that bone sarcomas are named for the matrix the malignant cells make.","Chondrosarcoma cells make cartilage only; any osteoid made directly by tumor cells makes the tumor an osteosarcoma.","Choose the option with malignant cartilage and no tumor osteoid."],"why":"Chondrosarcoma is a malignant tumor whose cells make hyaline or myxoid cartilage matrix but no osteoid. It is a tumor of middle-aged and older adults and favors the pelvis, proximal femur, and shoulder girdle. It may arise on its own, from an enchondroma, or in the cartilage cap of an osteochondroma, as here. A thick cap (more than about 2 cm in an adult) and new pain in an old lesion are warning signs of malignant change.","whyNot":["Correct.","This describes giant cell tumor of bone, an epiphyseal tumor of young adults that does not make cartilage.","Osteoid made directly by malignant cells defines osteosarcoma. Osteosarcoma can contain cartilage, but tumor osteoid places it in that category.","Small round blue cells with CD99 are Ewing sarcoma, a tumor of children and adolescents, usually in the diaphysis or pelvis."],"study":{"topics":["Chondrosarcoma features","Matrix-based classification of bone tumors"],"comps":["MS-13-F1","MS-13-F4"]}},{"id":"MS-C23-Q2","stem":"Genetic testing shows a germline loss-of-function mutation in EXT1. Which of the following best describes the lesions he has had around his knees since childhood?","options":["Expansile lytic lesions of the epiphysis with thin cortical shells","Intramedullary nodules of hyaline cartilage in the bones of the hands","Bony stalks continuous with the marrow cavity and capped by cartilage","Small cortical nidus of osteoid surrounded by dense reactive bone"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F","obj":"MS-13-F.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize EXT1 or EXT2 mutations as the cause of hereditary multiple osteochondromas.","Recall the structure of an osteochondroma.","The cortex and marrow of the stalk are continuous with the parent bone, and a hyaline cartilage cap sits on top."],"why":"EXT1 and EXT2 encode enzymes that make heparan sulfate, which helps control growth-plate signaling. Loss of both copies in growth-plate chondrocytes lets cartilage grow out sideways from the metaphysis, forming an osteochondroma. The lesion is a bony stalk whose cortex and marrow are continuous with the parent bone, covered by a cartilage cap. Solitary osteochondromas rarely turn malignant, but the risk of chondrosarcoma is higher in the hereditary multiple form.","whyNot":["Expansile epiphyseal lytic lesions suggest giant cell tumor of bone, which occurs in young adults after the growth plates close.","This is enchondroma. Many enchondromas occur in Ollier disease and, with hemangiomas, in Maffucci syndrome; these are not caused by EXT mutations.","Correct.","This is osteoid osteoma, a painful tumor of young people that is not hereditary and does not form a palpable bony lump."],"study":{"topics":["Osteochondroma vs enchondroma","EXT1 and EXT2"],"comps":["MS-13-F","MS-9"]}},{"id":"MS-C23-Q3","stem":"Which of the following is the main fibrillar collagen in the cartilage cap of the benign lesions around his knees?","options":["Type II","Type III","Type I","Type IV"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the tissue: the cap of an osteochondroma is hyaline cartilage, like a growth plate.","Match hyaline cartilage to its collagen type.","Hyaline cartilage and the nucleus pulposus are made mainly of type II collagen."],"why":"The cap of an osteochondroma is hyaline cartilage that behaves like a growth plate, and its main collagen is type II. Type II collagen forms thin fibrils that hold proteoglycans such as aggrecan, giving cartilage its resistance to compression. Below the cap, the cartilage undergoes endochondral ossification into the type I collagen bone of the stalk.","whyNot":["Correct.","Type III collagen forms reticular fibers and is abundant in blood vessel walls and early granulation tissue.","Type I collagen is the main collagen of bone, tendon, ligament, and fibrocartilage. It forms the bony stalk of an osteochondroma, not the cap.","Type IV collagen is a network-forming collagen of basement membranes, not a fibrillar collagen of cartilage."],"study":{"topics":["Collagen types by tissue","Hyaline cartilage matrix"],"comps":["MS-6","MS-2"]}},{"id":"MS-C23-Q4","stem":"A second outgrowth on the neck of his right fibula enlarges. He develops right foot drop. Which of the following additional findings is most likely?","options":["Weak knee extension and numbness on the medial leg","Weak foot eversion and numbness on the dorsum of the foot","Weak plantar flexion and numbness on the sole of the foot","Weak hip adduction and numbness on the medial thigh"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-3","obj":"MS-3.5","dok":3,"approach":["Locate the lesion: the neck of the fibula.","The common fibular nerve wraps around the fibular neck, where it is exposed to compression and fracture.","Predict its deficits: the deep branch supplies dorsiflexors and the superficial branch supplies evertors and most of the dorsal foot skin."],"why":"The common fibular (peroneal) nerve winds around the lateral side of the fibular neck just under the skin, so masses, fractures, and casts there can compress it. Its deep branch supplies the anterior compartment dorsiflexors, so injury causes foot drop. Its superficial branch supplies the lateral compartment evertors and the skin of most of the dorsum of the foot, so eversion is weak and that skin is numb.","whyNot":["These point to the femoral nerve and its saphenous branch, which do not pass near the fibula.","Correct.","These are signs of tibial nerve injury. The tibial nerve runs deep in the posterior leg, away from the fibular neck.","These are obturator nerve signs. The obturator nerve supplies the medial thigh and runs nowhere near the fibula."],"study":{"topics":["Common fibular nerve injury","Leg compartments and their nerves"],"comps":["MS-3","MS-5"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C24","system":"MS","dx":"Ewing sarcoma of the femur","codes":["MS-13-F2"],"case":"A 13-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of a 6-week history of pain and swelling of the left thigh. He has had intermittent fevers and has lost 3 kg (6.6 lb). His temperature is 38.3 °C (100.9 °F). Physical examination shows a tender, warm mass over the mid left thigh. Leukocyte count is 12,800/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000), and erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 58 mm/h (0 to 15). A radiograph shows a permeative lytic lesion in the femoral diaphysis with layered, onion-skin periosteal new bone and a large soft tissue mass. Blood cultures are negative. Biopsy shows sheets of uniform small round blue cells. Molecular testing confirms Ewing sarcoma.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C24-Q1","stem":"Which of the following immunohistochemical staining patterns is most likely in this tumor?","options":["Nuclear myogenin with strong cytoplasmic desmin","Strong membranous CD45 with negative CD99","Cytoplasmic synaptophysin with negative CD99","Membranous CD99 with negative myogenin"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F2","obj":"MS-13-F.2","dok":2,"approach":["List the small round blue cell tumors of childhood: Ewing sarcoma, rhabdomyosarcoma, lymphoma, and neuroblastoma.","A diaphyseal bone tumor with onion-skin periosteum, fever, and high ESR in a teenager fits Ewing sarcoma.","Ewing sarcoma cells show strong membranous CD99 and lack muscle, leukocyte, and neuroendocrine markers."],"why":"Ewing sarcoma is a tumor of children and adolescents made of sheets of uniform small round blue cells. It usually arises in the diaphysis of long bones or in the pelvis, and the periosteum lifts in layers to form onion-skin new bone. Strong membranous CD99 staining supports the diagnosis, and the EWSR1-FLI1 fusion from t(11;22) confirms it. Fever, leukocytosis, and a high ESR are common and can mimic osteomyelitis, which is why biopsy and cultures matter.","whyNot":["Myogenin and desmin mark skeletal muscle differentiation in rhabdomyosarcoma, another small round blue cell tumor of children, usually in the head and neck or genitourinary tract.","CD45 is the common leukocyte antigen. A CD45-positive small round cell tumor is a lymphoma.","Synaptophysin marks neuroendocrine tumors such as neuroblastoma, which usually arises in the adrenal medulla of young children.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Small round blue cell tumors","Ewing sarcoma markers"],"comps":["MS-13-F2","MS-13-F5"]}},{"id":"MS-C24-Q2","stem":"Molecular testing shows a t(11;22)(q24;q12) translocation. Which of the following best describes the product of this rearrangement?","options":["An anti-apoptotic protein overexpressed from an immunoglobulin promoter","A constitutively active cytoplasmic tyrosine kinase","A chimeric transcription factor that activates abnormal target genes","A truncated tumor suppressor that can no longer bind E2F"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-9","obj":"MS-9.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the genes at the breakpoints: EWSR1 on chromosome 22 and FLI1 on chromosome 11.","FLI1 is an ETS family transcription factor.","The fusion joins the EWSR1 activation domain to the FLI1 DNA-binding domain, making a new transcription factor."],"why":"The t(11;22) translocation fuses the EWSR1 gene on chromosome 22 to FLI1 on chromosome 11. The fusion protein keeps the DNA-binding domain of FLI1, an ETS family transcription factor, and gains a strong activation region from EWSR1. It binds DNA and turns on a set of genes that drive growth and block normal differentiation. Detecting this fusion confirms the diagnosis.","whyNot":["Moving BCL2 next to the immunoglobulin heavy chain promoter by t(14;18) causes follicular lymphoma.","A fused, always-on tyrosine kinase is the product of BCR-ABL1 from t(9;22) in chronic myeloid leukemia.","Correct.","Loss of RB function frees E2F and drives osteosarcoma and retinoblastoma. It is a loss of a tumor suppressor, not a fusion gene."],"study":{"topics":["EWSR1-FLI1 fusion","Translocations in cancer"],"comps":["MS-9","MS-13-F2"]}},{"id":"MS-C24-Q3","stem":"Which of the following embryonic tissues gives rise to the bone in which this tumor arose?","options":["Sclerotome of the paraxial mesoderm","Neural crest mesenchyme","Somatic layer of lateral plate mesoderm","Intermediate mesoderm of the nephrogenic cord"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the bone: the femur, part of the appendicular skeleton.","Recall that limb buds grow out from the body wall, which is lined by the somatic layer of lateral plate mesoderm.","Limb bones, cartilage, and tendons come from this layer; somites supply limb muscle instead."],"why":"The limb buds form when somatic lateral plate mesoderm and its overlying ectoderm grow out from the body wall. This mesoderm forms the limb bones, cartilages, tendons, and ligaments. Limb muscles come from myotome cells that migrate in from the somites. The axial skeleton comes from sclerotome, and most of the facial skeleton comes from neural crest.","whyNot":["Sclerotome forms the vertebrae and ribs, the axial skeleton. Limb bones do not come from somites.","Neural crest mesenchyme forms much of the bone and cartilage of the face and anterior skull, not the limbs.","Correct.","Intermediate mesoderm forms the kidneys and gonads and does not form bone."],"study":{"topics":["Embryonic origins of the skeleton","Limb bud development"],"comps":["MS-6","MS-1"]}},{"id":"MS-C24-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best describes how the region of the femur where this tumor arose first ossified during development?","options":["A primary center within a hyaline cartilage model","A secondary center within a hyaline cartilage model","Direct ossification within a sheet of mesenchyme","Cavitation of interzone mesenchyme"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-1","obj":"MS-1.5","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the tumor: the femoral diaphysis.","Recall that long bones form by endochondral ossification on a hyaline cartilage model.","The first, or primary, ossification center appears in the diaphysis before birth."],"why":"The femur forms by endochondral ossification. Mesenchyme first makes a hyaline cartilage model. Early in development a periosteal bone collar forms around the middle of the shaft, the central cartilage calcifies, and blood vessels bring in osteoprogenitor cells to form the primary ossification center in the diaphysis. Secondary centers appear later in the epiphyses, and the growth plate between them allows lengthening.","whyNot":["Correct.","Secondary ossification centers form in the epiphyses, mostly around birth or after. The tumor is in the shaft.","Intramembranous ossification forms the flat bones of the skull vault and most of the clavicle, not the femoral shaft.","Cavitation of interzone mesenchyme creates synovial joint cavities. It does not make bone."],"study":{"topics":["Endochondral vs intramembranous ossification","Primary and secondary ossification centers"],"comps":["MS-1","MS-2"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C25","system":"MS","dx":"Osteoid osteoma of the tibia","codes":["MS-13-F3"],"case":"A 17-year-old boy comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of pain in his right shin. The pain is worst at night and wakes him from sleep. It goes away within an hour of taking ibuprofen. He has no fever or weight loss. Physical examination shows mild tenderness over the anterior right tibia without swelling or warmth. Leukocyte count and erythrocyte sedimentation rate are within normal limits. CT shows a round 1.2 cm lucent nidus within the anterior cortex of the tibial diaphysis, surrounded by dense sclerotic bone. Diagnosis: osteoid osteoma.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C25-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the pattern of his pain and its response to ibuprofen?","options":["Osteoclasts in the sclerotic rim release bradykinin locally","Prostaglandin E2 made by cyclooxygenase in the nidus","Mast cells around the nidus release histamine","Neutrophils in the nidus release leukotriene B4"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F3","obj":"MS-13-F.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note the classic history: night pain from a small cortical lesion that is relieved by NSAIDs.","NSAIDs work by blocking cyclooxygenase.","So the pain must depend on a cyclooxygenase product, prostaglandin E2 made by the nidus."],"why":"Osteoid osteoma is a benign bone-forming tumor under about 2 cm, usually in the cortex of the femur or tibia of adolescents and young men. The nidus of osteoid and woven bone makes large amounts of prostaglandin E2, which causes the pain. NSAIDs block cyclooxygenase and so relieve the pain dramatically. Osteoblastoma has similar tissue but is larger than 2 cm, favors the vertebrae, and its pain is not relieved by NSAIDs.","whyNot":["Bradykinin is made from kininogen by kallikrein in plasma, and NSAIDs do not block its production.","Correct.","Histamine release is not blocked by NSAIDs, and the nidus is not a mast cell lesion.","Leukotrienes come from the lipoxygenase pathway, which NSAIDs do not block. The nidus is not a neutrophilic abscess."],"study":{"topics":["Osteoid osteoma vs osteoblastoma","Prostaglandins and pain"],"comps":["MS-13-F3","MS-13-F"]}},{"id":"MS-C25-Q2","stem":"The nidus is made of irregular trabeculae of woven bone rimmed by osteoblasts. Compared with the normal lamellar bone of the surrounding cortex, which of the following features best describes woven bone?","options":["Randomly oriented collagen with more osteocytes per volume","Concentric lamellae around central vascular canals","Parallel collagen sheets that change direction in each layer","Fewer osteocytes with greater resistance to bending"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that woven bone is the first bone made in development, fracture repair, and fast-growing lesions.","It is laid down quickly, so its collagen is disorganized and many osteoblasts are trapped as osteocytes.","Lamellar bone is laid down slowly in ordered layers and forms osteons in compact bone."],"why":"Woven bone is immature bone made quickly. Its collagen fibers run in random directions, it contains more osteocytes, and it is less mineralized and weaker than lamellar bone. Lamellar bone has collagen in orderly layers. In compact bone, these lamellae form osteons around Haversian canals, with osteocytes in lacunae connected by canaliculi and Volkmann canals linking the osteons. In adults, woven bone points to rapid bone formation such as a healing fracture, Paget disease, or a bone-forming tumor.","whyNot":["Correct.","Concentric lamellae around a Haversian canal form an osteon, the unit of mature compact lamellar bone.","Collagen in parallel sheets that alternate direction between layers is the structure of lamellar bone and gives it strength.","Woven bone is weaker than lamellar bone, not stronger, and it has more osteocytes, not fewer."],"study":{"topics":["Woven vs lamellar bone","Compact bone structure"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-13-F3"]}},{"id":"MS-C25-Q3","stem":"Giant cell tumor of bone was included in the radiologist's differential diagnosis. In giant cell tumor of bone, which of the following cell populations are the neoplastic cells?","options":["Multinucleated osteoclast-like giant cells with RANK","Synovial cells that overexpress CSF1","Osteoblasts that secrete prostaglandin E2","Mononuclear stromal cells that express RANKL"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F","obj":"MS-13-F.8","dok":3,"approach":["Recall giant cell tumor of bone: an epiphyseal, soap-bubble lytic lesion in young adults.","The striking giant cells are osteoclast-like cells, but they are not the tumor cells.","The neoplastic mononuclear stromal cells express RANKL, which recruits and activates the giant cells."],"why":"Giant cell tumor of bone is a locally aggressive tumor of the epiphysis in young adults, usually around the knee, and appears as an expansile soap-bubble lytic lesion. The neoplastic cells are mononuclear stromal cells that express RANKL. RANKL binds RANK on monocyte precursors and drives them to fuse into the osteoclast-like giant cells that destroy bone. This is why the RANKL-blocking antibody denosumab can shrink these tumors.","whyNot":["The giant cells are reactive. They are recruited and activated by RANKL from the stromal cells, and they cause the bone destruction.","CSF1 overexpression drives tenosynovial giant cell tumor, which arises from synovium or tendon sheaths, not inside bone.","Prostaglandin-secreting osteoblasts describe the nidus of this patient's osteoid osteoma.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Giant cell tumor of bone","RANKL and osteoclasts"],"comps":["MS-13-F","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C25-Q4","stem":"Osteoblasts in the nidus secrete osteocalcin, a protein that binds the mineral phase of bone. Which of the following post-translational modifications allows osteocalcin to bind this mineral?","options":["Oxidative deamination of lysine, requiring copper","Gamma-carboxylation of glutamate, requiring vitamin K","Hydroxylation of proline residues, requiring vitamin C","Phosphorylation of serine, requiring magnesium"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the mineral of bone: calcium hydroxyapatite.","Calcium-binding proteins such as clotting factors and osteocalcin use gamma-carboxyglutamate residues to bind calcium.","Gamma-carboxylation needs reduced vitamin K as a cofactor."],"why":"Osteocalcin is made by osteoblasts and is one of the most abundant noncollagen proteins in bone. Vitamin K dependent gamma-glutamyl carboxylase adds a second carboxyl group to specific glutamate residues. These gamma-carboxyglutamate residues bind calcium and let osteocalcin attach to hydroxyapatite. The same modification activates clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X, which is why warfarin can also lower carboxylated osteocalcin.","whyNot":["Copper-dependent lysyl oxidase forms the cross-links that strengthen collagen and elastin. It does not modify osteocalcin.","Correct.","Prolyl hydroxylation stabilizes the collagen triple helix. Its loss in vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy, not a defect in calcium binding by osteocalcin.","Magnesium supports kinases and PTH secretion, but the calcium-binding residues of osteocalcin come from carboxylation, not phosphorylation."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin K and gamma-carboxylation","Bone matrix proteins"],"comps":["MS-8","MS-6"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C26","system":"MS","dx":"Osteosarcoma of the distal femur after hereditary retinoblastoma","codes":["MS-13-F4"],"case":"A 15-year-old girl comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of pain and swelling above her right knee. She was treated for bilateral retinoblastoma in infancy. Physical examination shows a firm, tender mass over the distal right thigh. Serum alkaline phosphatase is 520 U/L (for age, about 50 to 260). A radiograph shows a destructive, mixed lytic and sclerotic lesion in the distal femoral metaphysis with a triangle of elevated periosteum at its edge and radiating spicules of new bone in the soft tissue. Biopsy shows pleomorphic malignant cells producing lacelike osteoid. Diagnosis: osteosarcoma.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C26-Q1","stem":"This tumor has a second, smaller age peak later in life. Which of the following groups is at increased risk for this tumor in later adulthood?","options":["Adults with multiple enchondromas","Adults with hereditary multiple osteochondromas","Older adults with Paget disease of bone","Older adults with long-standing gout"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F4","obj":"MS-13-F.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the bimodal age pattern of osteosarcoma: adolescents during the growth spurt and older adults.","In older adults, osteosarcoma usually follows a condition with high bone turnover or DNA damage.","Paget disease and prior radiation are the classic predisposing conditions."],"why":"Osteosarcoma is the most common primary malignant bone tumor apart from myeloma. Its main peak is in adolescents, where it arises in the metaphysis around the knee during the growth spurt. A second, smaller peak occurs in older adults, usually on a background of Paget disease or previous radiation. The defining feature is osteoid made directly by malignant cells.","whyNot":["Multiple enchondromas in Ollier disease and Maffucci syndrome raise the risk of chondrosarcoma.","Hereditary multiple osteochondromas raise the risk of chondrosarcoma in the cartilage caps, not osteosarcoma.","Correct.","Gout causes erosive arthritis and tophi but does not lead to bone sarcoma."],"study":{"topics":["Osteosarcoma epidemiology","Paget disease and sarcoma risk"],"comps":["MS-13-F4","MS-13-E3"]}},{"id":"MS-C26-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains her increased risk for this tumor?","options":["A somatic EWSR1-FLI1 fusion created by prior radiation exposure","A germline TP53 mutation that removes apoptosis after DNA damage","A gain-of-function RB1 mutation that keeps E2F tightly bound","A second hit in RB1 that frees E2F to push cells into S phase"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-F4","obj":"MS-13-F.5","dok":3,"approach":["Bilateral retinoblastoma in infancy means a germline loss-of-function RB1 mutation in every cell.","Tumors arise when the second RB1 copy is lost in a cell, the two-hit model.","Without RB protein, E2F is free to drive the G1 to S transition."],"why":"Children with hereditary retinoblastoma inherit or are born with one nonfunctional RB1 allele in every cell. When a bone cell loses the second allele, it has no working RB protein. RB normally binds E2F and holds the cell in G1 until cyclin D and CDK4/6 phosphorylate it. Without RB, E2F drives cells into S phase unchecked. Osteosarcoma is the most common second cancer in these patients. Its imaging shows a Codman triangle of lifted periosteum and a sunburst pattern of tumor bone, and it spreads through the blood to the lungs.","whyNot":["EWSR1-FLI1 defines Ewing sarcoma, a small round cell tumor that does not make osteoid.","Germline TP53 loss in Li-Fraumeni syndrome also raises osteosarcoma risk, but her history of bilateral retinoblastoma points to a germline RB1 mutation.","Bound E2F holds cells in G1, which would block growth. Retinoblastoma and osteosarcoma come from loss of RB function.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["RB1 and the two-hit hypothesis","Osteosarcoma imaging and spread"],"comps":["MS-9","MS-13-F4"]}},{"id":"MS-C26-Q3","stem":"A 71-year-old man comes to the physician because of back pain. Radiographs show multiple dense, sclerotic lesions in the lumbar vertebrae and pelvis. Which of the following is the most likely primary tumor?","options":["Prostatic adenocarcinoma","Renal cell carcinoma","Squamous cell lung carcinoma","Follicular thyroid carcinoma"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F","obj":"MS-13-F.9","dok":2,"approach":["In an older adult with multiple bone lesions, think of metastases before a primary bone tumor.","Classify the lesions: dense and sclerotic means osteoblastic.","Prostate cancer is the classic cause of osteoblastic metastases in men."],"why":"Metastases are far more common than primary malignant bone tumors in adults, because carcinomas of the prostate, breast, lung, kidney, and thyroid often spread to the red marrow of the axial skeleton. Prostate cancer causes osteoblastic (sclerotic) lesions by releasing factors that stimulate osteoblasts. Kidney, thyroid, and lung cancers cause mainly lytic lesions, and breast cancer often causes mixed lesions. Metastases can cause pathologic fractures, and lytic lesions often cause hypercalcemia.","whyNot":["Correct.","Renal cell carcinoma produces purely lytic, often expansile and very vascular bone metastases.","Lung cancer metastases to bone are mainly lytic.","Thyroid carcinoma that spreads to bone causes lytic lesions."],"study":{"topics":["Bone metastases patterns","Hypercalcemia of malignancy"],"comps":["MS-13-F","MS-13-F4"]}},{"id":"MS-C26-Q4","stem":"The tumor arose in the metaphysis next to the distal femoral growth plate, where new bone is laid down on calcified cartilage. In a child with severe vitamin D deficiency, which of the following changes would be seen in this growth plate?","options":["Early fusion of the growth plate with loss of the reserve zone","A wide, disorganized hypertrophic zone that fails to mineralize","A thin proliferative zone with short, sparse chondrocyte columns","Thick cores of calcified cartilage retained within trabeculae"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.5","dok":3,"approach":["List the zones from epiphysis to metaphysis: reserve, proliferative, hypertrophic, calcification, and ossification.","Vitamin D deficiency lowers calcium and phosphate, so cartilage matrix cannot calcify.","Hypertrophic chondrocytes are not cleared on time, so the plate widens and becomes irregular, which is rickets."],"why":"In the growth plate, chondrocytes rest, divide in columns, swell in the hypertrophic zone, and then the matrix around them calcifies. Calcified cartilage is invaded by vessels and used as a scaffold for new bone in the metaphysis. In rickets, low calcium and phosphate prevent this calcification. Hypertrophic chondrocytes pile up, the plate becomes wide and disorganized, and the ends of the bones flare. In achondroplasia, by contrast, the proliferative zone is underactive.","whyNot":["Early closure of the growth plate follows excess estrogen, as in precocious puberty, and limits final height.","Correct.","Too little chondrocyte proliferation is seen in achondroplasia, where activating FGFR3 mutations slow the proliferative zone.","Retained calcified cartilage within trabeculae happens when osteoclasts cannot resorb it, as in osteopetrosis."],"study":{"topics":["Growth plate zones","Rickets vs achondroplasia"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-13-A2"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C27","system":"MS","dx":"Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma (sarcoma botryoides)","codes":["MS-13-F5"],"case":"A 3-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of a 1-week history of vaginal bleeding and a mass protruding from the vagina. Physical examination shows a soft, polypoid, grapelike mass at the vaginal opening. Biopsy shows a dense layer of primitive tumor cells just beneath the surface epithelium. Some tumor cells have abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm with cross-striations. The tumor cells stain for desmin and myogenin. Diagnosis: embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma, botryoid type (sarcoma botryoides).","questions":[{"id":"MS-C27-Q1","stem":"The pathologist also considered alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma, which is more common in the extremities of adolescents. Which of the following genetic findings would indicate the alveolar type?","options":["PAX3 or PAX7 fused to FOXO1","SS18 fused to SSX1, SSX2, or SSX4","COL1A1 fused to PDGFB","EWSR1 fused to FLI1"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F5","obj":"MS-13-F.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the two main types of rhabdomyosarcoma in children: embryonal (younger children, head and neck or genitourinary tract) and alveolar (older children, extremities).","The alveolar type is defined by a fusion of a PAX gene with FOXO1.","Match each other fusion to its sarcoma to rule it out."],"why":"Rhabdomyosarcoma is the most common soft tissue sarcoma of childhood. The embryonal type affects young children, often in the head and neck or genitourinary tract, and its botryoid form grows as grapelike masses under the mucosa of hollow organs such as the vagina and bladder. The alveolar type affects adolescents, often in the extremities, and carries PAX3 or PAX7 fused to FOXO1 from t(2;13) or t(1;13). Both types stain for desmin and myogenin, which mark skeletal muscle differentiation.","whyNot":["Correct.","This fusion from t(X;18) defines synovial sarcoma, a soft tissue sarcoma of young adults near large joints.","This fusion drives dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans, a slow-growing sarcoma of the dermis.","This fusion from t(11;22) defines Ewing sarcoma, a CD99-positive small round cell tumor that lacks myogenin."],"study":{"topics":["Rhabdomyosarcoma types","Sarcoma fusion genes"],"comps":["MS-13-F5","MS-9"]}},{"id":"MS-C27-Q2","stem":"The tumor cells resemble embryonic skeletal myoblasts. In normal development, the deep intrinsic muscles of the back arise from which part of the somite, and which nerves supply them?","options":["Hypaxial myotome, supplied by dorsal rami","Epaxial myotome, supplied by ventral rami","Epaxial myotome, supplied by dorsal rami","Sclerotome, supplied by ventral rami"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-1","obj":"MS-1.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that somites divide into sclerotome, myotome, and dermatome.","The myotome splits into a dorsal epaxial part and a ventral hypaxial part.","Epaxial muscles become the deep back muscles and are supplied by dorsal rami of spinal nerves."],"why":"Somites form from paraxial mesoderm and divide into sclerotome, which forms the vertebrae and ribs, myotome, which forms skeletal muscle, and dermatome, which forms the dermis of the back. The epaxial part of the myotome forms the deep (intrinsic) back muscles, supplied by dorsal rami. The hypaxial part forms the muscles of the body wall and limbs, supplied by ventral rami. Rhabdomyosarcoma cells copy this myoblast program, which is why they express desmin and myogenin even in sites such as the bladder and vagina, whose walls are made of smooth muscle.","whyNot":["Hypaxial myotome forms the body wall and limb muscles, and these are supplied by ventral rami.","Epaxial muscles are supplied by dorsal rami. Ventral rami supply the hypaxial muscles of the body wall and limbs.","Correct.","Sclerotome forms the vertebrae and ribs, not muscle."],"study":{"topics":["Somite derivatives","Epaxial vs hypaxial muscles"],"comps":["MS-1","MS-4"]}},{"id":"MS-C27-Q3","stem":"Her mother developed breast cancer at age 31 years, and a maternal uncle had adrenocortical carcinoma at age 8 years. Germline testing of the child is most likely to show a loss-of-function mutation in which of the following genes?","options":["APC","RB1","TP53","BRCA1"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-9","obj":"MS-9.7","dok":3,"approach":["Look at the family pattern: several different cancers at young ages in one maternal line.","Childhood sarcoma, early breast cancer, and adrenocortical carcinoma are the core cancers of Li-Fraumeni syndrome.","Li-Fraumeni syndrome is caused by germline loss of TP53."],"why":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome is an autosomal dominant cancer syndrome caused by germline TP53 mutations. p53 normally senses DNA damage and halts the cell cycle in G1 through p21 or triggers apoptosis. Without it, damaged cells keep dividing and gain more mutations. Affected families have soft tissue sarcomas such as rhabdomyosarcoma, osteosarcoma, early breast cancer, brain tumors, leukemia, and adrenocortical carcinoma, often in childhood or young adulthood.","whyNot":["APC loss causes familial adenomatous polyposis with hundreds of colon polyps.","Germline RB1 loss causes hereditary retinoblastoma and later osteosarcoma. It does not explain early breast cancer and childhood adrenocortical carcinoma.","Correct.","BRCA1 raises the risk of breast and ovarian cancer but not childhood adrenocortical carcinoma or rhabdomyosarcoma."],"study":{"topics":["Li-Fraumeni syndrome","p53 function"],"comps":["MS-9","MS-13-F"]}},{"id":"MS-C27-Q4","stem":"A 58-year-old woman is found to have a large retroperitoneal mass. Biopsy shows intersecting fascicles of spindle cells with blunt-ended, cigar-shaped nuclei. Which of the following immunostaining profiles is most likely in this woman's tumor?","options":["Desmin and myogenin positive, smooth muscle actin negative","Smooth muscle actin and desmin positive, myogenin negative","CD99 positive, desmin and myogenin negative","S100 and SOX10 positive, desmin negative"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F","obj":"MS-13-F.10","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize spindle cells with cigar-shaped nuclei in the retroperitoneum of an older adult as leiomyosarcoma.","Leiomyosarcoma shows smooth muscle markers: smooth muscle actin and desmin.","Myogenin is a skeletal muscle transcription factor, present in rhabdomyosarcoma and absent in leiomyosarcoma."],"why":"Leiomyosarcoma is a malignant smooth muscle tumor of adults that arises most often in the uterus and retroperitoneum. Its spindle cells have blunt, cigar-shaped nuclei and stain for smooth muscle actin and desmin. Desmin is found in both smooth and skeletal muscle, so myogenin, a skeletal muscle transcription factor, is the marker that separates rhabdomyosarcoma from leiomyosarcoma. Both sarcomas spread mainly through the blood, most often to the lungs.","whyNot":["This profile marks skeletal muscle differentiation, as in the child's rhabdomyosarcoma.","Correct.","Strong CD99 without muscle markers suggests Ewing sarcoma, a small round cell tumor of young people.","S100 and SOX10 mark nerve sheath tumors and melanoma, which do not show muscle differentiation."],"study":{"topics":["Soft tissue sarcoma markers","Leiomyosarcoma"],"comps":["MS-13-F","MS-13-F5"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C28","system":"MS","dx":"Staphylococcus aureus septic arthritis of the knee in rheumatoid arthritis","codes":["MS-13-G1"],"case":"A 64-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of a 2-day history of severe pain and swelling of the right knee. She has had rheumatoid arthritis for 15 years and takes methotrexate and prednisone 7.5 mg daily. One week ago she had a draining, infected skin ulcer over her right ankle. She has had no joint injections or surgery. Her temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), pulse is 108/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 118/70 mm Hg. The right knee is hot and swollen with a large effusion, and passive motion causes severe pain. Blood cultures grow gram-positive cocci in clusters that are coagulase positive. Diagnosis: Staphylococcus aureus septic arthritis of the right knee.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C28-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains how infection became established in this joint?","options":["Spread from osteomyelitis of the adjacent distal femur","Direct inoculation through a penetrating wound of the knee","Deposition of staphylococcal antigen and antibody complexes","Bacteremia seeding a synovium damaged by inflammation"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-13-G1","obj":"MS-13-G.1","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the likely source: an infected skin ulcer with Staphylococcus aureus now in the blood.","Most septic arthritis comes from hematogenous seeding, and the vascular synovium has no limiting basement membrane.","Rheumatoid arthritis adds risk through damaged, inflamed synovium and through immunosuppressive drugs."],"why":"Most septic arthritis in adults results from bacteremia. Synovial capillaries are rich and lack a limiting basement membrane, so circulating bacteria can enter the joint. Joints already damaged by rheumatoid arthritis are especially vulnerable, and methotrexate and glucocorticoids weaken host defense. Staphylococcus aureus is the most common cause overall, and its adhesins for collagen and fibronectin help it attach in the joint. Other risk factors are prosthetic joints, injection drug use, and diabetes.","whyNot":["Contiguous spread from bone does occur, but nothing in her history or exam suggests femoral osteomyelitis.","Direct inoculation follows trauma, surgery, or joint injection. She had none of these.","Immune complex deposition causes a sterile arthritis, as in SLE or serum sickness. Here live bacteria are growing in the blood.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Septic arthritis pathogenesis","Risk factors for septic arthritis"],"comps":["MS-13-G1","MS-12"]}},{"id":"MS-C28-Q2","stem":"Arthrocentesis of the right knee is performed. Which of the following synovial fluid findings is most likely?","options":["WBC 14,000/mm3, 70% neutrophils, no crystals","WBC 96,000/mm3, 95% neutrophils, no crystals","WBC 22,000/mm3, 85% neutrophils, needle-shaped crystals","WBC 1,100/mm3, 20% neutrophils, no crystals"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-G1","obj":"MS-13-G.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the synovial fluid categories by WBC count: normal under 200/mm3, noninflammatory under 2,000, inflammatory 2,000 to about 50,000, septic usually over 50,000.","Septic fluid is dominated by neutrophils and has no crystals; Gram stain and culture are often positive.","In a patient with rheumatoid arthritis, a very high count separates infection from a flare."],"why":"Synovial fluid analysis sorts an acute hot joint into categories. Normal fluid has fewer than 200 WBC/mm3, noninflammatory fluid fewer than 2,000, and inflammatory fluid from rheumatoid arthritis or crystals usually 2,000 to 50,000. Septic fluid usually has more than 50,000 WBC/mm3, mostly neutrophils, and Gram stain or culture is often positive. Crystal analysis is always done, because gout and infection can occur together.","whyNot":["This is an inflammatory fluid, as in a rheumatoid arthritis flare. It is too low for a typical bacterial joint infection.","Correct.","Needle-shaped crystals, negatively birefringent under polarized light, mean gout.","This is a noninflammatory fluid, under 2,000 WBC/mm3, as in osteoarthritis or trauma."],"study":{"topics":["Synovial fluid analysis","Acute monoarthritis evaluation"],"comps":["MS-13-G1","MS-13-B4"]}},{"id":"MS-C28-Q3","stem":"The orthopedic team drains the joint urgently. If drainage is delayed, which of the following mechanisms will most directly cause rapid loss of her articular cartilage?","options":["Urate crystals activating the NLRP3 inflammasome","Osteoclast resorption driven by synovial RANKL","Antibody-dependent killing of chondrocytes","Proteases and oxidants released by neutrophils"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize that a septic joint fills with huge numbers of neutrophils.","Neutrophils release elastase, collagenases, matrix metalloproteinases, and reactive oxygen species.","These enzymes digest the cartilage matrix within days, along with bacterial toxins and enzymes."],"why":"In septic arthritis, bacteria trigger a massive influx of neutrophils into the joint. Neutrophils release elastase, collagenases, and other proteases and reactive oxygen species. Avascular articular cartilage cannot repair itself, so its collagen and proteoglycans are destroyed within days. Pressure from the effusion also reduces blood flow to the joint. Prompt drainage and antibiotics are needed to save the joint.","whyNot":["Urate crystals activate the NLRP3 inflammasome and IL-1 beta release in gout. No crystals are involved in this infection.","RANKL-driven osteoclasts cause the slow bone erosions of rheumatoid arthritis over months, not cartilage loss within days.","Antibodies against chondrocytes are not the mechanism of joint destruction in bacterial infection.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Neutrophil-mediated tissue damage","Septic arthritis complications"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-13-G1"]}},{"id":"MS-C28-Q4","stem":"The aspirated fluid is thin and watery and lacks the stringy quality of normal synovial fluid. Which of the following cells and secreted molecules normally give synovial fluid its viscosity?","options":["Fibroblast-like synoviocytes secreting hyaluronan","Macrophage-like synoviocytes secreting chondroitin sulfate","Chondrocytes secreting type II collagen","Mast cells secreting heparin"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the two cell types of the synovial lining: macrophage-like type A cells and fibroblast-like type B cells.","Type B cells secrete hyaluronan and lubricin into the joint space.","Hyaluronan makes the fluid viscous; in septic fluid, neutrophil enzymes and reactive oxygen species break it down."],"why":"Synovial joints are lined by a synovial membrane with two cell types. Macrophage-like type A synoviocytes remove debris, and fibroblast-like type B synoviocytes secrete hyaluronan and lubricin. Hyaluronan, a long glycosaminoglycan, gives the fluid its viscosity, and lubricin lowers friction between cartilage surfaces. Synovial fluid also feeds the avascular articular cartilage. In septic arthritis, enzymes and oxidants break down hyaluronan, so the fluid loses its viscosity.","whyNot":["Correct.","Macrophage-like (type A) synoviocytes clear debris from the joint. Chondroitin sulfate is a proteoglycan side chain of cartilage matrix, not the main viscous molecule of synovial fluid.","Chondrocytes make the collagen and proteoglycans of articular cartilage, which stay in the cartilage matrix.","Mast cells contain heparin, but it does not give synovial fluid its viscosity."],"study":{"topics":["Synovial joint histology","Synovial fluid composition"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-13-G1"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C29","system":"MS","dx":"Disseminated gonococcal infection with septic arthritis of the wrist","codes":["MS-13-G1"],"case":"A 22-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 5-day history of fever and joint pain. The pain moved between her knees, elbows, and wrists and is now in her right wrist, which has become swollen. She has a new sexual partner and has no genital symptoms. Her temperature is 38.2 °C (100.8 °F). Physical examination shows a swollen, warm right wrist and pain along the dorsal wrist tendons with finger movement. There are four small pustules on red bases on her forearms and ankles. Synovial fluid from the wrist has 48,000 WBC/mm3 (normal under 200), 90% neutrophils, and no crystals; Gram stain is negative. Nucleic acid amplification testing of the fluid detects Neisseria gonorrhoeae. Diagnosis: disseminated gonococcal infection with septic arthritis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C29-Q1","stem":"She was treated for gonococcal cervicitis 18 months ago. Which of the following properties of the organism best explains why that infection did not protect her from this one?","options":["Antigenic variation of its pilus proteins","Survival within phagolysosomes of macrophages","Superantigen-induced anergy of helper T cells","A polysaccharide capsule that resists opsonization"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-12","obj":"MS-12.2","dok":2,"approach":["Ask why a person can get gonorrhea again after infection.","Neisseria gonorrhoeae keeps changing its surface proteins, so antibodies from the last infection do not recognize the new strain.","Its pili undergo antigenic variation by gene recombination."],"why":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae is a gram-negative diplococcus that attaches to mucosa with pili. By recombining silent pilin genes into the expressed site, it keeps changing the antigenic makeup of its pili, and it also varies its Opa proteins. Antibodies made during one infection therefore do not block the next, so reinfection is common and there is no vaccine. IgA protease cleaves mucosal IgA and helps the organism attach. Disseminated infection causes the triad of tenosynovitis, pustular skin lesions, and migratory polyarthralgia, or a purulent monoarthritis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Intracellular survival in macrophages is the strategy of organisms such as Mycobacterium tuberculosis.","Superantigens are made by Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes, not by gonococci.","Neisseria gonorrhoeae has no polysaccharide capsule. Neisseria meningitidis does, and vaccines target it."],"study":{"topics":["Neisseria gonorrhoeae virulence factors","Disseminated gonococcal infection"],"comps":["MS-12","MS-13-G1"]}},{"id":"MS-C29-Q2","stem":"Arthrocentesis of the right radiocarpal joint is performed from a dorsal approach. Which of the following carpal bones form the main articular surface that meets the distal radius in this joint?","options":["Lunate and pisiform","Scaphoid and lunate","Capitate and hamate","Scaphoid and trapezium"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-3","obj":"MS-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the proximal row from lateral to medial: scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform.","The distal radius articulates with the scaphoid and lunate.","The triquetrum meets the articular disc of the ulna, not the radius directly."],"why":"The radiocarpal (wrist) joint is formed by the distal radius and the articular disc above, and the scaphoid, lunate, and triquetrum below. The distal radius itself articulates with the scaphoid and lunate, while the triquetrum contacts the articular disc. The ulna does not articulate directly with the carpal bones. A fall on an outstretched hand transmits force from the scaphoid to the radius, which explains why scaphoid and distal radius fractures are common.","whyNot":["The pisiform lies on the palmar surface of the triquetrum and does not reach the radius.","Correct.","The capitate and hamate are in the distal row and take part in the midcarpal and carpometacarpal joints.","The trapezium is in the distal row. It articulates with the scaphoid and the first metacarpal, not with the radius."],"study":{"topics":["Carpal bones and the wrist joint","Upper limb bones"],"comps":["MS-3","MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C29-Q3","stem":"Early in her illness, pain at the radial side of the wrist was reproduced when she made a fist over her flexed thumb and the wrist was bent toward the ulnar side, and de Quervain tenosynovitis was considered. Inflammation of which of the following tendons produces that sign?","options":["Extensor pollicis longus and extensor indicis","Extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis","Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis","Flexor carpi radialis and flexor digitorum superficialis"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B8","obj":"MS-13-B.12","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the maneuver as the Finkelstein test, which stretches the tendons of the first dorsal compartment.","The first dorsal compartment holds the abductor pollicis longus and the extensor pollicis brevis.","Pain over the radial styloid with this test means de Quervain tenosynovitis."],"why":"De Quervain tenosynovitis is inflammation and thickening of the sheath of the first dorsal compartment, which holds the abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis at the radial styloid. Repetitive thumb and wrist use and late pregnancy or new parenthood are common settings. Folding the thumb into the palm and bending the wrist toward the ulna (Finkelstein test) stretches these tendons and reproduces pain. Gonococcal tenosynovitis, by contrast, is an infection and comes with fever and skin lesions.","whyNot":["These tendons run in the third and fourth dorsal compartments. The extensor pollicis longus can rupture after distal radius fractures.","These wrist extensors share the second dorsal compartment and do not move the thumb, so the Finkelstein maneuver does not mainly stress them.","Correct.","These are flexors on the palmar side of the wrist. Ulnar deviation with the thumb in the fist stretches the radial-side extensors, not these."],"study":{"topics":["De Quervain tenosynovitis","Dorsal compartments of the wrist"],"comps":["MS-13-B8","MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C29-Q4","stem":"Which of the following is the most likely original site of the infection that spread to her wrist?","options":["Pustular lesions on the distal extremities","Asymptomatic endocervical infection","Dorsal tendon sheaths of the wrist","Osteomyelitis of the distal radius"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-13-G1","obj":"MS-13-G.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that gonococci first infect mucosa: cervix, urethra, rectum, or pharynx.","Mucosal infection in women is often silent, which lets bacteria reach the blood before treatment.","Bacteremia then seeds the joints, tendon sheaths, and skin."],"why":"Disseminated gonococcal infection starts as a mucosal infection, most often of the cervix in women, that is frequently asymptomatic. Gonococci invade the bloodstream and seed tendon sheaths, skin, and joints. Most septic arthritis arises this way, by hematogenous seeding of the vascular synovium, rather than by direct inoculation. In sexually active young adults, Neisseria gonorrhoeae is a leading cause of septic arthritis, while Staphylococcus aureus dominates in older adults and those with damaged joints.","whyNot":["The skin pustules come from bacteria spread through the blood. They are a result of dissemination, not its source.","Correct.","Tenosynovitis is part of disseminated infection. The tendon sheaths were seeded from the blood.","Contiguous spread from bone is a rare route here, and nothing suggests radial osteomyelitis."],"study":{"topics":["Disseminated gonococcal infection pathogenesis","Routes of joint infection"],"comps":["MS-13-G1","MS-12"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C30","system":"MS","dx":"Type II necrotizing fasciitis from Streptococcus pyogenes","codes":["MS-13-G2"],"case":"A 34-year-old previously healthy man comes to the emergency department because of a 1-day history of severe pain in the left lower leg. Two days ago he scraped his shin while playing soccer. His temperature is 39.4 °C (102.9 °F), pulse is 124/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 88/52 mm Hg. The anterior left leg is swollen and tense, with dusky red skin and several bullae. Pain is far greater than the skin changes suggest, and the skin is numb at the center. Serum creatinine is 2.1 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3). At surgery, the fascia is gray and necrotic and separates easily from the overlying tissue. Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in chains, and culture grows beta-hemolytic group A streptococci. Diagnosis: type II necrotizing fasciitis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C30-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why his pain was far greater than the visible skin changes early in the illness?","options":["Infection of dermal lymphatics with a raised sharp border","Toxin cleavage of desmoglein 1 in the upper epidermis","Clostridial gas dissecting through skeletal muscle","Infection spreading along deep fascia under intact skin"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-13-G2","obj":"MS-13-G.3","dok":3,"approach":["Place the infection: necrotizing fasciitis spreads along the deep fascia, beneath the skin.","The skin looks almost normal at first while vessels and nerves in the deeper layer are destroyed.","Later, thrombosis of perforating vessels causes dusky skin, bullae, and numbness from nerve death."],"why":"Necrotizing fasciitis spreads quickly along fascial planes, which have a poor blood supply. Early on the skin over the infection looks almost normal, so severe pain is out of proportion to the exam. As bacteria and toxins cause thrombosis of the vessels that perforate the fascia, the skin becomes dusky, forms bullae, and loses sensation as nerves die. Type I is polymicrobial and common in diabetes and the perineum (Fournier gangrene); type II is caused by Streptococcus pyogenes, often in healthy people after minor injury. Crepitus is more typical of type I with gas-forming organisms.","whyNot":["Infection of the upper dermis and lymphatics with a sharp, raised border is erysipelas. It is painful, but the visible skin changes match the pain.","Exfoliative toxin cleaving desmoglein 1 causes staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome, with superficial peeling, not deep tissue destruction.","Gas gangrene is a clostridial infection of muscle with crepitus. His Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in chains, not large gram-positive rods.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Necrotizing fasciitis types","Skin and soft tissue infection depth"],"comps":["MS-13-G2","MS-12"]}},{"id":"MS-C30-Q2","stem":"He becomes more hypotensive despite fluids and develops a diffuse red rash. Which of the following actions of this organism's pyrogenic exotoxins best explains this shock?","options":["Binding MHC II and T-cell receptors outside the peptide groove","Cleaving SNARE proteins to block release of neurotransmitters","Inserting into cholesterol in host membranes to form large pores","ADP-ribosylating elongation factor 2 to stop protein synthesis"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-12","obj":"MS-12.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize streptococcal toxic shock syndrome: shock and organ failure with an invasive group A streptococcal infection.","Pyrogenic exotoxins act as superantigens.","They link MHC class II to T-cell receptor V beta regions without processing, activating many T cells and causing a cytokine storm."],"why":"Streptococcus pyogenes is a beta-hemolytic gram-positive coccus in chains. Its M protein resists phagocytosis by blocking complement deposition, and hyaluronidase and streptokinase help it spread through tissue planes. Its streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins are superantigens. They bind MHC class II on antigen-presenting cells and the V beta region of T-cell receptors outside the normal peptide groove, activating a large share of T cells at once. The resulting flood of IL-2, TNF-alpha, and IL-1 causes fever, capillary leak, shock, and organ failure.","whyNot":["Correct.","SNARE cleavage is how tetanus and botulinum toxins act. They paralyze rather than cause shock.","Pore formation describes streptolysin O, which lyses cells and red blood cells but does not drive massive cytokine release.","This is how diphtheria toxin and Pseudomonas exotoxin A kill cells, not the cause of streptococcal toxic shock."],"study":{"topics":["Superantigens","Streptococcus pyogenes virulence factors"],"comps":["MS-12","MS-13-G2"]}},{"id":"MS-C30-Q3","stem":"During debridement, the surgeon opens the anterior compartment of the leg, which contains the dorsiflexors of the ankle. Which of the following developmental events explains why the extensor compartment of the leg lies anteriorly while the extensor compartment of the arm lies posteriorly?","options":["Medial rotation of both limbs by about 90 degrees","Lateral rotation of the lower limb and medial rotation of the upper limb","Medial rotation of the lower limb and lateral rotation of the upper limb","Lateral rotation of both limbs by about 90 degrees"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-1","obj":"MS-1.4","dok":2,"approach":["Start with the early limb buds: flexor surfaces face ventrally and extensor surfaces face dorsally.","The upper limb rotates laterally about 90 degrees, so extensors end up posterior and the thumb lateral.","The lower limb rotates medially about 90 degrees, so extensors end up anterior and the big toe medial."],"why":"Early limb buds have a ventral flexor surface and a dorsal extensor surface. In the seventh and eighth weeks the upper limb rotates laterally about 90 degrees, so its extensors face posteriorly and the elbow points back. The lower limb rotates medially about 90 degrees, so its extensors face anteriorly, the knee points forward, and the big toe lies medially. This medial rotation also twists the dermatomes of the leg into a spiral pattern.","whyNot":["If both limbs rotated medially, both would have extensors facing anteriorly and thumbs facing medially.","This reverses the true directions. If the lower limb rotated laterally, its extensors would face posteriorly like the arm's.","Correct.","If both limbs rotated laterally, both would have extensors facing posteriorly, which is not true of the leg."],"study":{"topics":["Limb rotation in development","Leg compartments"],"comps":["MS-1","MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C30-Q4","stem":"Over the following months, the scar over his leg is remodeled and gains tensile strength as the collagen first laid down in his granulation tissue is replaced. Which collagen type mainly replaces it?","options":["Type II","Type III","Type IV","Type I"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the early matrix: granulation tissue fibroblasts first make mostly type III collagen, the collagen of reticular fibers.","Remodeling degrades this collagen and replaces it with the collagen of dense connective tissue such as dermis, tendon, and ligament.","That strong, cross-linked fibrillar collagen is type I."],"why":"Granulation tissue forms within days of injury and contains new capillaries, fibroblasts, macrophages, and a loose matrix. The first collagen deposited is mostly type III, the same type found in reticular fibers and vessel walls. During remodeling, matrix metalloproteinases break down this collagen and fibroblasts replace it with type I collagen, which is cross-linked to strengthen the scar. Even so, a mature scar reaches only about 70 to 80% of the strength of normal skin.","whyNot":["Type II collagen is found in hyaline cartilage and the nucleus pulposus, not in healing skin.","Type III collagen is the early collagen of granulation tissue. It is the collagen being replaced, not the replacement.","Type IV collagen forms basement membranes under the new epithelium and around vessels. It does not give a scar its tensile strength.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Wound healing phases","Collagen types by tissue"],"comps":["MS-6","MS-12"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C31","system":"MS","dx":"Wet gangrene of the foot in diabetes with peripheral artery disease","codes":["MS-13-G4"],"case":"A 68-year-old man with a 20-year history of type 2 diabetes mellitus comes to the emergency department because of a 4-day history of swelling, discoloration, and foul-smelling drainage from the second and third toes of his right foot. The symptoms began after a blister formed from new shoes. He has smoked one pack of cigarettes daily for 45 years and has calf pain when walking one block. Temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 138/82 mm Hg. The toes are black, moist, and swollen, with purulent drainage, and erythema extends onto the forefoot without a clear line of demarcation. Pedal pulses are not palpable. Ankle-brachial index is 0.48 (normal 1.0 to 1.4). Hemoglobin A1c is 9.6% (normal below 5.7%), and leukocyte count is 16,800/mm3 (normal 4,500 to 11,000). Diagnosis: wet gangrene of the right forefoot.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C31-Q1","stem":"In patients with arterial occlusion alone, an ischemic toe usually becomes dry, shrunken, and sharply demarcated from viable tissue. Which of the following processes most likely accounts for the moist, swollen, poorly demarcated appearance of this patient's toes?","options":["Fat necrosis from lipase release and calcium soap formation","Coagulative necrosis from arterial occlusion with protein denaturation","Caseous necrosis from a granulomatous response to mycobacteria","Liquefactive necrosis from bacterial infection and neutrophil enzymes"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-G4","obj":"MS-13-G.6","dok":3,"approach":["Both dry and wet gangrene begin as ischemic coagulative necrosis, so look for what has been added on top.","Fever, pus, leukocytosis, and spreading erythema point to bacterial infection of the dead tissue.","Neutrophil and bacterial enzymes digest the tissue, which is liquefactive necrosis, so the lesion becomes wet and spreads."],"why":"Dry gangrene is coagulative necrosis after arterial occlusion. The tissue mummifies and a clear line separates dead from living tissue. Wet gangrene happens when bacteria invade that dead tissue. Neutrophils and bacterial enzymes then liquefy it, so it becomes moist, swollen, foul smelling, and poorly demarcated, and infection can spread up the limb. Diabetes adds poor blood flow, neuropathy that hides minor injuries, and impaired neutrophil function, which together make this complication more likely.","whyNot":["Fat necrosis follows lipase release, as in acute pancreatitis or trauma to breast tissue. It is not the process in an infected ischemic toe.","Coagulative necrosis is the ischemic process behind both forms of gangrene. On its own it produces dry gangrene, so it does not explain the wet appearance.","Caseous necrosis is the cheesy necrosis of granulomas in tuberculosis and some fungal infections. It does not cause a wet, purulent toe.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Dry versus wet gangrene","Patterns of necrosis"],"comps":["MS-13-G4","MS-13-G"]}},{"id":"MS-C31-Q2","stem":"Three years earlier, this patient had osteomyelitis of the calcaneus after a nail went through the rubber sole of his athletic shoe while he was doing yard work. Bone culture at that time most likely grew which of the following organisms?","options":["Pasteurella multocida","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Salmonella enterica","Staphylococcus epidermidis"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-12","obj":"MS-12.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the setting: a puncture wound of the foot through the sole of a shoe.","Recall that the moist foam and rubber inside athletic shoes harbor Pseudomonas aeruginosa.","Match the setting to the organism carried in on the nail."],"why":"A nail passing through a sneaker sole picks up Pseudomonas aeruginosa, which grows in the warm, damp foam of the shoe, and drives it straight into the calcaneus. This is the classic organism for osteomyelitis after a puncture through a shoe sole, and it is also linked to osteomyelitis in people who inject drugs. Pseudomonas is an aerobic, oxidase-positive, gram-negative rod.","whyNot":["Pasteurella multocida is the organism for osteomyelitis after a cat or dog bite, carried in from the animal's mouth.","Correct.","Salmonella is the classic cause of osteomyelitis in people with sickle cell disease, who have poor splenic function and infarcted bone.","Staphylococcus epidermidis causes infection on prosthetic joints and other implanted hardware, where its biofilm protects it."],"study":{"topics":["Osteomyelitis organisms by setting"],"comps":["MS-12","MS-13-G3"]}},{"id":"MS-C31-Q3","stem":"Atherosclerosis and long-standing hyperglycemia have damaged the endothelium of this patient's leg arteries, so it releases less nitric oxide. In normal arteriolar smooth muscle, nitric oxide causes relaxation mainly through which of the following?","options":["cGMP-dependent activation of myosin light chain phosphatase","cAMP-dependent inhibition of troponin C calcium binding","IP3-mediated calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Calmodulin-dependent activation of myosin light chain kinase"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.5","dok":2,"approach":["Nitric oxide diffuses into smooth muscle and activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, which makes cGMP.","Smooth muscle contraction depends on phosphorylated myosin light chains, so relaxation needs those phosphates removed.","cGMP-dependent protein kinase lowers cytosolic calcium and activates myosin light chain phosphatase, which removes them."],"why":"Smooth muscle contracts when calcium binds calmodulin, the complex activates myosin light chain kinase, and the phosphorylated light chain lets myosin cycle on actin. Nitric oxide from endothelium raises cGMP in the smooth muscle cell. cGMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers cytosolic calcium and activates myosin light chain phosphatase. The light chains are dephosphorylated and the vessel relaxes. Loss of this signal in diseased arteries favors vasoconstriction and adds to poor flow in peripheral artery disease.","whyNot":["Correct.","Smooth muscle has no troponin. Troponin C is the calcium sensor in skeletal and cardiac muscle, and nitric oxide acts through cGMP rather than cAMP.","IP3 releases calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum after alpha-1 or other Gq receptor activation. That raises calcium and causes contraction.","Calcium-calmodulin activation of myosin light chain kinase is the step that starts smooth muscle contraction, the opposite of what nitric oxide does."],"study":{"topics":["Smooth muscle contraction and relaxation","Nitric oxide and cGMP"],"comps":["MS-7","CV-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C31-Q4","stem":"He undergoes a transmetatarsal amputation. Two weeks later, the open stump is filled with red, granular, easily bleeding tissue. Which of the following collagen types is most abundant in the newly formed tissue at this stage?","options":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type VII"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the red, granular, bleeding tissue as granulation tissue in an early healing wound.","Recall that fibroblasts in granulation tissue first lay down a loose collagen meshwork.","That early collagen is type III, which is later replaced by stronger type I as the scar matures."],"why":"Granulation tissue is new capillaries, fibroblasts, and loose matrix that fills a wound in the first days to weeks. Its fibroblasts first make type III collagen, the thin, flexible collagen of reticular fibers and blood vessel walls. Over weeks to months the scar remodels, and type I collagen replaces most of the type III, which is why tensile strength rises slowly over time.","whyNot":["Type I collagen dominates the mature scar, bone, tendon, and ligament. It replaces type III later in healing.","Type II collagen is found in hyaline cartilage and the nucleus pulposus. It is not made in skin wound healing.","Correct.","Type VII collagen forms the anchoring fibrils that attach the epidermis to the dermis. It is defective in dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa and is not the main collagen of granulation tissue."],"study":{"topics":["Collagen types","Wound healing and granulation tissue"],"comps":["MS-6","IN-10"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C32","system":"MS","dx":"Gas gangrene after a contaminated crush injury","codes":["MS-13-G4"],"case":"A 34-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 30 hours after his right lower leg was crushed under farm machinery. A deep laceration on the lateral leg, contaminated with soil, was cleaned and sutured at a local clinic. Since then he has had rapidly increasing, severe pain in the leg. Temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), pulse is 124/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 88/52 mm Hg. The calf and lateral leg are tensely swollen with bronze-to-purple skin, hemorrhagic bullae, a thin, brown, foul-smelling discharge, and crepitus on palpation. Hemoglobin is 8.9 g/dL (normal 13.5 to 17.5), down from 14.6 g/dL at the time of injury. A radiograph shows gas dissecting along muscle planes. Gram stain of the discharge shows large gram-positive rods with blunt ends and few neutrophils. Diagnosis: gas gangrene (clostridial myonecrosis).","questions":[{"id":"MS-C32-Q1","stem":"Spores of the causative organism are common in soil, yet most contaminated surface wounds never develop this infection. Which of the following features of this wound most directly allowed the spores to germinate and multiply?","options":["Large numbers of neutrophils recruited into the injured muscle","Exposed collagen fibers in the torn fascial layers","Low oxidation-reduction potential in devitalized muscle","High glucose concentration in the wound exudate"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-13-G4","obj":"MS-13-G.7","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the organism as Clostridium perfringens, a spore-forming obligate anaerobe.","Spores germinate only where oxygen tension and redox potential are low.","A deep crush wound with dead muscle, poor blood supply, and soil, then closed with sutures, creates exactly that environment."],"why":"Clostridium perfringens spores sit harmlessly in soil and on skin. They germinate only in an anaerobic setting. Crushed, devitalized muscle has no blood flow, foreign material uses up oxygen, and closing the wound seals it off. Once growing, the organism releases toxins that kill nearby muscle and lower the redox potential further, so the infection spreads through muscle within hours, with gas, severe pain, bronze skin, hemolysis, and shock.","whyNot":["Neutrophils fight infection. In gas gangrene the toxins actually destroy neutrophils, which is why the Gram stain shows few of them.","Exposed collagen helps organisms such as Staphylococcus aureus adhere. It does not create the anaerobic conditions spores need to germinate.","Correct.","Glucose supports bacterial growth in many settings, but it does not trigger germination of anaerobic spores. Low oxygen is the key requirement."],"study":{"topics":["Clostridial myonecrosis pathogenesis","Anaerobic wound infection"],"comps":["MS-13-G4","MS-12"]}},{"id":"MS-C32-Q2","stem":"His hemoglobin fell from 14.6 to 8.9 g/dL in 30 hours without external bleeding. Which of the following actions of the organism's major toxin best explains both the hemolysis and the muscle necrosis?","options":["Hydrolysis of phosphatidylcholine in host cell membranes","ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2 in host cells","Cleavage of SNARE proteins at presynaptic nerve terminals","Cross-linking of MHC class II molecules and T-cell receptors"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-12","obj":"MS-12.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the main toxin of Clostridium perfringens as alpha toxin.","Alpha toxin is a phospholipase C, also called lecithinase.","Breaking down membrane lecithin lyses any cell it reaches, including red cells and muscle fibers."],"why":"Clostridium perfringens alpha toxin is a phospholipase C that splits phosphatidylcholine (lecithin) and sphingomyelin in cell membranes. Red cells lyse, which explains the falling hemoglobin. Muscle fibers, endothelial cells, and leukocytes are destroyed, which explains the myonecrosis, the spreading edema, and the scarce neutrophils. The same lecithinase activity is the basis of the Nagler reaction on egg yolk agar in the laboratory.","whyNot":["Correct.","ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2 stops protein synthesis. This is how diphtheria toxin and Pseudomonas exotoxin A kill cells.","SNARE cleavage blocks neurotransmitter release. This is how tetanus and botulinum toxins act, made by other clostridia.","Cross-linking MHC class II to T-cell receptors is the action of superantigens such as toxic shock syndrome toxin from Staphylococcus aureus."],"study":{"topics":["Clostridium perfringens alpha toxin","Bacterial exotoxin mechanisms"],"comps":["MS-12","MS-13-G4"]}},{"id":"MS-C32-Q3","stem":"Surgical exploration shows that the laceration severed the nerve running through the lateral compartment of the leg, between the fibularis longus and fibularis brevis muscles. Which of the following deficits is most likely to result from this nerve injury?","options":["Weak plantarflexion with numbness over the sole of the foot","Weak dorsiflexion with numbness limited to the first web space","Weak knee extension with numbness over the medial leg","Weak eversion with numbness over the dorsum of the foot"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-5","obj":"MS-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["Name the nerve of the lateral compartment: the superficial fibular nerve.","Its muscles, fibularis longus and brevis, evert the foot.","Its skin territory is most of the dorsum of the foot, sparing the first web space."],"why":"The common fibular nerve splits at the neck of the fibula. The superficial branch runs in the lateral compartment and supplies fibularis longus and brevis, the main evertors of the foot. It then becomes cutaneous and supplies most of the dorsum of the foot. Cutting it weakens eversion and numbs the dorsum, while dorsiflexion is preserved because the deep fibular nerve in the anterior compartment is intact.","whyNot":["This is a tibial nerve injury. The tibial nerve runs in the posterior compartment and supplies the plantarflexors and the sole.","This is a deep fibular nerve injury, which affects the anterior compartment dorsiflexors and the skin of the first web space.","This is a femoral nerve injury in the thigh, which weakens the quadriceps and numbs the medial leg through the saphenous nerve.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Nerves of the leg compartments","Superficial fibular nerve"],"comps":["MS-5","NE-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C32-Q4","stem":"Serum creatine kinase (reference 40 to 200 U/L) and potassium (reference 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L) are measured on arrival. Which of the following sets of results is most likely?","options":["Creatine kinase 120 U/L; potassium 3.1 mEq/L","Creatine kinase 21,000 U/L; potassium 6.4 mEq/L","Creatine kinase 120 U/L; potassium 6.4 mEq/L","Creatine kinase 21,000 U/L; potassium 3.1 mEq/L"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-13-H1","obj":"MS-13-H.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize widespread muscle necrosis from the crush injury and the toxin as rhabdomyolysis.","Dying muscle releases its intracellular contents: creatine kinase, potassium, phosphate, and myoglobin.","Expect a very high creatine kinase with hyperkalemia."],"why":"Muscle cells hold large amounts of creatine kinase and most of the body's potassium. When many fibers die, as with a crush injury and clostridial myonecrosis, both leak into the blood. Creatine kinase rises to many times normal, and potassium climbs, which can cause fatal arrhythmias. Hemolysis here adds more potassium. Released myoglobin also reaches the kidney, where it can cause acute kidney injury, and it turns the urine dipstick positive for blood without red cells on microscopy.","whyNot":["A normal creatine kinase rules out major muscle necrosis, and a low potassium is the opposite of what cell breakdown produces.","Correct.","Hyperkalemia fits, but a normal creatine kinase is not possible with this much dead muscle.","The creatine kinase fits, but necrotic muscle and lysed red cells release potassium, so it rises rather than falls."],"study":{"topics":["Rhabdomyolysis","Hyperkalemia from cell breakdown"],"comps":["MS-13-H","MS-8"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C33","system":"MS","dx":"Salmonella osteomyelitis in a child with sickle cell disease","codes":["MS-13-G3"],"case":"A 6-year-old boy with sickle cell disease (hemoglobin SS) is brought to the physician because of a 5-day history of fever and pain above his left knee. He has refused to bear weight for 2 days. Two weeks ago he had 3 days of diarrhea. Temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), pulse is 128/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. The distal left thigh is warm, swollen, and tender. Hemoglobin is 7.4 g/dL (his baseline is 8.0 g/dL), leukocyte count is 19,500/mm3 (normal 5,000 to 14,500), and C-reactive protein is 9.2 mg/dL (normal below 1.0). MRI shows marrow edema in the distal femoral metaphysis with a small subperiosteal collection. Blood cultures grow gram-negative rods that do not ferment lactose and produce hydrogen sulfide. Diagnosis: Salmonella osteomyelitis of the distal femur.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C33-Q1","stem":"In children, hematogenous osteomyelitis most often begins in the metaphysis of a long bone, as in this patient. Which of the following features of the growing bone best explains this location?","options":["Rich supply of epiphyseal arteries crossing the open growth plate","High density of osteoclasts along the diaphyseal endosteum","Exposure of the metaphyseal cortex inside the joint capsule","Slow flow in looping capillaries beneath the growth plate"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-13-G3","obj":"MS-13-G.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the blood supply of the metaphysis in a child: nutrient artery branches end in hairpin loops near the growth plate.","These loops open into wide sinusoidal veins where blood flow slows sharply.","Bacteria in the blood settle where flow is slow and phagocytes are few, so infection starts there."],"why":"In a growing long bone, branches of the nutrient artery run up to the growth plate, turn back in tight loops, and empty into large venous sinusoids. Blood slows down in these sinusoids, and the area has few phagocytes and gaps in the vessel lining. Circulating bacteria lodge there and multiply. After infancy the growth plate blocks spread into the epiphysis, so the infection stays in the metaphysis or tracks out under the periosteum, as in this boy. In sickle cell disease, bone infarcts can also seed infection in the diaphysis.","whyNot":["After infancy, vessels do not cross the growth plate. The plate acts as a barrier, which is why childhood infection stays on the metaphyseal side.","Osteoclasts resorb bone, but they do not trap circulating bacteria. Their activity does not explain where infection starts.","In some joints, such as the hip, the metaphysis lies inside the capsule. That explains spread into the joint, but not why infection starts in the metaphysis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hematogenous osteomyelitis in children","Metaphyseal blood supply"],"comps":["MS-13-G3","MS-2"]}},{"id":"MS-C33-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains why children with this patient's underlying disease are especially prone to invasive infection with this organism?","options":["Deficiency of terminal complement components","Selective deficiency of secretory IgA","Splenic dysfunction from repeated infarction","Thymic hypoplasia with low T-cell counts"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-12","obj":"MS-12.3","dok":2,"approach":["Link the setting: osteomyelitis caused by Salmonella in sickle cell disease.","Recall that sickled cells infarct the spleen early in childhood, leaving functional asplenia.","Without a working spleen, bacteremia from the gut is cleared poorly and seeds infarcted bone."],"why":"Repeated sickling in the spleen causes infarction, so most children with hemoglobin SS have little splenic function by early childhood. The spleen normally filters bacteria from the blood and makes opsonizing antibody. Salmonella can enter from the gut, sometimes through areas of bowel injured by sickling, and without splenic clearance it reaches the bone. Infarcted bone with poor blood flow gives it a place to grow. This is why Salmonella is a classic cause of osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease, alongside Staphylococcus aureus.","whyNot":["Terminal complement deficiency (C5 to C9) causes recurrent Neisseria infections. It is not part of sickle cell disease.","IgA deficiency causes sinus and lung infections and Giardia diarrhea. It does not explain Salmonella bone infection in sickle cell disease.","Correct.","Thymic hypoplasia, as in DiGeorge syndrome, causes viral, fungal, and opportunistic infections from low T cells. It is not linked to sickle cell disease."],"study":{"topics":["Functional asplenia in sickle cell disease","Osteomyelitis organisms by setting"],"comps":["MS-12","HE-9-B"]}},{"id":"MS-C33-Q3","stem":"In children without sickle cell disease, hematogenous osteomyelitis is most often caused by gram-positive cocci in clusters that are catalase and coagulase positive. Which of the following virulence factors most directly allows that organism to attach to exposed bone matrix?","options":["Protein A that binds the Fc portion of IgG antibodies","Surface adhesins that bind collagen and fibronectin","Superantigen toxin that activates T cells nonspecifically","M protein that blocks complement deposition on the cell"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-12","obj":"MS-12.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism as Staphylococcus aureus.","Separate its virulence factors by job: attachment, immune evasion, or toxin effects.","Attachment to bone depends on surface adhesins that bind the matrix proteins collagen and fibronectin."],"why":"Staphylococcus aureus carries surface proteins that bind host matrix molecules, including collagen-binding and fibronectin-binding adhesins. These let it stick to bone matrix and to injured tissue, which is a key reason it is the most common cause of osteomyelitis and septic arthritis. Once attached, it can form biofilm, especially on hardware, which shields it from antibiotics and phagocytes.","whyNot":["Protein A binds the Fc portion of IgG so the antibody cannot opsonize the bacterium. It helps evade phagocytosis, not attach to bone.","Correct.","Superantigens such as toxic shock syndrome toxin cause massive cytokine release and shock. They do not mediate attachment to bone.","M protein is a virulence factor of Streptococcus pyogenes, which is catalase negative and grows in chains."],"study":{"topics":["Staphylococcus aureus virulence factors"],"comps":["MS-12","MS-13-G3"]}},{"id":"MS-C33-Q4","stem":"Treatment is delayed, and 3 months later he has a draining sinus over the distal thigh. Radiographs show a dense, sharply separated fragment of cortical bone lying in a cavity, surrounded by a shell of new subperiosteal bone. Which of the following best describes the dense fragment and its significance?","options":["Brodie abscess; a walled-off cavity with a sclerotic bony rim","Bone infarct; sterile necrosis from sickling that later calcifies","Sequestrum; avascular dead bone that shields bacteria from drugs","Involucrum; reactive periosteal new bone that walls off the infected site"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-G3","obj":"MS-13-G.5","dok":3,"approach":["Separate the two structures in chronic osteomyelitis: the dead fragment and the new bone shell around it.","The dense, separated fragment is dead bone, the sequestrum, and the shell is the involucrum.","Dead bone has no blood supply, so antibiotics and leukocytes cannot reach bacteria inside it, and it usually must be removed."],"why":"In chronic osteomyelitis, pus under the periosteum strips away the blood supply of a piece of cortex, and that piece dies and separates. This dead fragment is the sequestrum. The lifted periosteum lays down a shell of new bone, the involucrum, around it. Because the sequestrum has no blood flow, antibiotics and immune cells cannot reach the bacteria living on it, so surgical removal is usually needed for cure. A draining sinus is common, and years of drainage can lead to squamous cell carcinoma in the tract.","whyNot":["A Brodie abscess is a localized subacute abscess, usually in the metaphysis, with a sclerotic rim and no separated dead fragment.","Sickle cell disease does cause bone infarcts, but a fragment lying in a cavity with an involucrum and a draining sinus is infected dead bone.","Correct.","The involucrum is the shell of new bone in this film, not the dense fragment inside it."],"study":{"topics":["Chronic osteomyelitis: sequestrum and involucrum"],"comps":["MS-13-G3"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C34","system":"MS","dx":"Tuberculous spondylitis (Pott disease)","codes":["MS-13-G3"],"case":"A 41-year-old woman who moved to the United States 3 years ago from a region where tuberculosis is endemic comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of progressive mid-back pain, night sweats, and a 6-kg (13-lb) weight loss. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Examination shows a sharp, angular kyphosis at the thoracolumbar junction with point tenderness over T11 and T12. She holds the right hip slightly flexed, and passive extension of the hip is painful. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 64 mm/h (normal 0 to 20). An interferon-gamma release assay is positive. MRI shows destruction of the T11 and T12 vertebral bodies, narrowing of the disc between them, and a right psoas abscess. CT-guided biopsy shows caseating granulomas with acid-fast bacilli. Diagnosis: tuberculous spondylitis (Pott disease).","questions":[{"id":"MS-C34-Q1","stem":"A chest radiograph shows a small calcified peripheral lung nodule and a calcified hilar lymph node, with no cavities or active lung disease. Which of the following best describes how the organism most likely reached her vertebrae?","options":["Hematogenous spread from an earlier lung infection","Direct extension from a pulmonary cavity through the pleura","Ingestion of organisms with spread through the portal vein","Direct inoculation during an earlier spinal procedure"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-12","obj":"MS-12.7","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the calcified nodule and hilar node as a healed primary (Ghon) complex.","During primary infection, Mycobacterium tuberculosis enters the blood and seeds distant organs, where it can stay dormant for years.","Vertebral bodies, with their rich marrow blood flow, are a common site, and reactivation later causes Pott disease."],"why":"In primary tuberculosis, bacilli spread from the lung to the hilar nodes and then into the blood, seeding many organs. Most foci are walled off by granulomas and stay dormant. Years later, a vertebral focus can reactivate. Caseating granulomas destroy the front of the vertebral bodies next to the disc, the infection spreads under the anterior longitudinal ligament to the next vertebra, and the bodies collapse forward into a gibbus. Pus tracking down along the psoas forms a psoas abscess, which explains her painful hip extension.","whyNot":["Correct.","Direct extension needs active disease next to the spine. Her lungs show only healed calcified lesions with no cavity.","Swallowed organisms cause intestinal tuberculosis. Portal blood drains to the liver and is not how the spine is seeded.","Direct inoculation causes infection only after surgery or injection into the spine, and she has had no such procedure."],"study":{"topics":["Pott disease pathogenesis","Primary and reactivation tuberculosis"],"comps":["MS-12","PU-13"]}},{"id":"MS-C34-Q2","stem":"The infection began in the T12 vertebral body next to the disc it shares with T11. During embryonic development, the T12 vertebral body formed from which of the following?","options":["Paraxial dermomyotome cells that migrate around the neural tube","Caudal half of one sclerotome joined to cranial half of the next","One whole sclerotome lying at the level of the twelfth spinal nerve","Cranial halves of two adjacent sclerotomes joined across a disc"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-1","obj":"MS-1.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that vertebral bodies come from sclerotome, while dermomyotome forms dermis and muscle.","Each sclerotome splits into a cranial and a caudal half.","In resegmentation, the caudal half of one sclerotome fuses with the cranial half of the next to form one vertebral body."],"why":"Sclerotome cells from the somites surround the notochord and neural tube. Each sclerotome splits into halves, and every vertebral body forms from the caudal half of one sclerotome joined to the cranial half of the one below. Because of this shift, each vertebra spans two body segments, the segmental muscles from the myotomes bridge the disc and can move one vertebra on the next, and the spinal nerves exit near the discs. The notochord between the bodies persists as the nucleus pulposus.","whyNot":["Dermomyotome forms dermis and skeletal muscle. Vertebrae come from sclerotome.","Correct.","A vertebral body does not come from a single whole sclerotome. Resegmentation combines halves of two sclerotomes.","The halves that fuse are a caudal half and a cranial half. Two cranial halves do not join."],"study":{"topics":["Somite derivatives","Vertebral resegmentation"],"comps":["MS-1","MS-3"]}},{"id":"MS-C34-Q3","stem":"Which of the following immune mechanisms is mainly responsible for the caseating granulomas and the destruction of her vertebral bodies?","options":["Type I, IgE-mediated degranulation of tissue mast cells and basophils","Type II, IgG antibodies binding antigens fixed on the cell surface","Type III, deposition of immune complexes that activate complement","Type IV, Th1 cell activation of macrophages by interferon-gamma"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.5","dok":2,"approach":["Granulomas form when T cells repeatedly activate macrophages against an antigen they cannot clear.","This T cell driven response is delayed-type, or type IV, hypersensitivity.","The positive interferon-gamma release assay shows the same Th1 memory response."],"why":"Macrophages that ingest Mycobacterium tuberculosis present its antigens and release IL-12, which drives CD4 T cells to become Th1 cells. Th1 cells release interferon-gamma, which activates macrophages to kill bacilli and to form epithelioid cells and giant cells, building a granuloma. TNF helps hold the granuloma together. The central caseous necrosis and much of the bone destruction come from this host response rather than from bacterial toxins, which is the type IV hypersensitivity pattern.","whyNot":["Type I hypersensitivity causes allergy and anaphylaxis within minutes. It does not form granulomas.","Type II hypersensitivity is antibody against cell or tissue antigens, as in the polyarthritis of rheumatic fever and in Lambert-Eaton syndrome.","Type III hypersensitivity is immune complex disease, as in the arthritis of SLE. It causes vasculitis and complement-driven inflammation, not caseating granulomas.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hypersensitivity types","Granuloma formation"],"comps":["MS-11","IM-15-C"]}},{"id":"MS-C34-Q4","stem":"In adults, both tuberculous and pyogenic vertebral infections typically involve two adjacent vertebral bodies and the disc between them, as in this patient. Which of the following best explains this pattern?","options":["A single segmental artery supplies endplates on both sides of a disc","Infection begins in the posterior arch and spreads forward into the bodies","The adult disc has a rich blood supply that seeds the bones on either side","Organisms spread through the spinal canal from the epidural space"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-G3","obj":"MS-13-G.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that the adult intervertebral disc is avascular, so bacteria cannot lodge in it first.","Blood-borne organisms settle in the richly supplied bone just under the vertebral endplates.","Branches of one segmental artery supply the endplates above and below a disc, so both vertebrae are seeded and the disc is destroyed between them."],"why":"In adults the disc has no blood vessels and gets nutrients by diffusion. Bacteria arriving in the blood therefore lodge in the vascular bone beside the endplates. A segmental artery divides to supply the lower endplate of one vertebra and the upper endplate of the next, so the infection usually involves both bodies and then spreads into the disc between them. This differs from children, where hematogenous osteomyelitis favors the metaphyses of long bones, and from diabetic foot osteomyelitis, which spreads directly from a skin ulcer.","whyNot":["Correct.","Vertebral osteomyelitis starts in the vascular vertebral bodies. The posterior arch is usually spared until late.","The adult disc is avascular. Infection reaches it from the bone on either side, not the reverse.","Epidural abscess is a complication that spreads from the infected bodies. It is not the starting point."],"study":{"topics":["Vertebral osteomyelitis and discitis","Blood supply of the vertebrae"],"comps":["MS-13-G3","MS-3"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C35","system":"MS","dx":"Trichinellosis","codes":["MS-13-G"],"case":"A 58-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 10-day history of muscle pain, fever, and swelling around his eyes. Three weeks ago he ate homemade pork sausage made from a pig raised and slaughtered on a neighbor's farm, and a few days later he had abdominal cramps and diarrhea for 3 days. His calves, thighs, shoulders, and jaw muscles ache, and chewing is painful. Temperature is 38.6 °C (101.5 °F), pulse is 98/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg. Examination shows bilateral periorbital edema, subconjunctival hemorrhages, splinter hemorrhages, and tender proximal and calf muscles. Leukocyte count is 14,200/mm3 (normal 4,500 to 11,000) with 32% eosinophils (normal 1 to 4%). Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 46 mm/h (normal 0 to 20), and serum creatine kinase is 2,850 U/L (normal 40 to 200). Serologic testing confirms the diagnosis of trichinellosis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C35-Q1","stem":"Which of the following stages of the parasite's life cycle is mainly responsible for this patient's current muscle symptoms?","options":["Newborn larvae encysting within skeletal muscle cells","Eggs hatching in the duodenum with larvae migrating through the liver","Larvae penetrating the skin and migrating to the lungs","Adult worms in the intestinal mucosa releasing eggs in the stool"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-13-G","obj":"MS-13-G.8","dok":2,"approach":["Lay out the timeline: diarrhea in the first week (intestinal phase), then muscle pain from the second week on (muscle phase).","Recall that Trichinella spiralis females are viviparous and release live larvae, not eggs.","Those larvae travel in the blood to skeletal muscle, enter muscle cells, and encyst, causing myositis."],"why":"A person is infected by eating encysted larvae in undercooked pork or game. Stomach acid frees the larvae, which mature into adults in the small intestine and cause the early diarrhea. About a week later, females release newborn larvae that enter lymph and blood and invade striated muscle. Each larva enters a muscle cell and turns it into a nurse cell, where it coils and encysts. This muscle invasion causes myalgia, high creatine kinase, eosinophilia, and the periorbital edema and splinter hemorrhages of the systemic phase.","whyNot":["Correct.","Egg hatching with migration through the liver and lungs describes Ascaris. Trichinella has no free egg stage.","Skin penetration with lung migration is how hookworms and Strongyloides infect people. Trichinella is acquired by eating meat.","Trichinella females release live larvae, and eggs are not passed in the stool. The intestinal stage also explains the early diarrhea, not the muscle pain."],"study":{"topics":["Trichinella spiralis life cycle","Parasitic myositis"],"comps":["MS-13-G","MS-12"]}},{"id":"MS-C35-Q2","stem":"A deltoid muscle biopsy shows coiled larvae inside host cells. The larvae also invade cardiac muscle, but they do not encyst there. Which of the following features of the surrounding uninfected fibers identifies the tissue as skeletal muscle rather than cardiac muscle?","options":["Branched cells joined end to end by gap junctions","Multinucleated fibers with nuclei at the periphery","Fusiform cells with dense bodies and no striations","Central nuclei with intercalated discs between cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["List the histologic features that separate the three muscle types.","Skeletal muscle fibers are long, unbranched, and multinucleated, with nuclei pushed to the edge.","Cardiac muscle has central nuclei, branching, and intercalated discs; smooth muscle has no striations."],"why":"Skeletal muscle fibers form by fusion of many myoblasts, so each fiber has many nuclei, and these sit just under the sarcolemma at the periphery. The fibers are striated and do not branch. Cardiac muscle cells are single cells with one or two central nuclei, they branch, and they join end to end at intercalated discs that contain gap junctions and desmosomes. Smooth muscle cells are spindle shaped, with one central nucleus, dense bodies, and no striations.","whyNot":["Branching cells linked by gap junctions describe cardiac muscle, which conducts electrical impulses from cell to cell.","Correct.","Fusiform cells with dense bodies and no striations describe smooth muscle.","Central nuclei and intercalated discs identify cardiac muscle."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle"],"comps":["MS-2"]}},{"id":"MS-C35-Q3","stem":"Before serologic results were available, his physician considered polymyalgia rheumatica because of his muscle pain and stiffness. Which of the following findings from this case most strongly argues against polymyalgia rheumatica?","options":["Tenderness of the shoulder and thigh muscles","Age of 58 years","Serum creatine kinase of 2,850 U/L","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate of 46 mm/h"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B5","obj":"MS-13-B.8","dok":3,"approach":["Recall what polymyalgia rheumatica looks like: adults over 50, girdle pain and stiffness, high ESR, and normal muscle enzymes.","Its inflammation is in the bursae and synovium around the shoulders and hips, so muscle fibers are not damaged.","A high creatine kinase means myofiber necrosis, which points to myositis and away from polymyalgia rheumatica."],"why":"Creatine kinase leaks from damaged muscle fibers. In polymyalgia rheumatica the inflammation sits in the bursae and joints around the shoulder and hip girdles, so creatine kinase stays normal and strength is preserved. Inflammatory myositis, whether autoimmune or parasitic as here, damages muscle fibers and raises creatine kinase, often many times above normal. A muscle biopsy in myositis shows inflammatory cells and necrotic fibers, while biopsy is normal in polymyalgia rheumatica.","whyNot":["Pain around the shoulder and hip girdles is the main complaint in polymyalgia rheumatica, so this finding fits both diagnoses.","Polymyalgia rheumatica occurs in adults over 50, so his age fits the diagnosis.","Correct.","An elevated ESR is expected in polymyalgia rheumatica and does not argue against it."],"study":{"topics":["Polymyalgia rheumatica versus myositis","Creatine kinase in muscle disease"],"comps":["MS-13-B5","MS-13-B6"]}},{"id":"MS-C35-Q4","stem":"He is treated with albendazole and a 2-month tapering course of high-dose prednisone. Four months later, his muscle pain is gone, but he has trouble rising from a chair and climbing stairs. Serum creatine kinase is 95 U/L. Which of the following findings is most likely on a muscle biopsy now?","options":["Selective atrophy of type II fibers without inflammation","Perifascicular atrophy with perivascular inflammation","Endomysial CD8 T cells invading nonnecrotic fibers","Grouped atrophy with large clusters of a single fiber type"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.4","dok":3,"approach":["Note the new proximal weakness after weeks of high-dose glucocorticoids, with a normal creatine kinase.","Normal creatine kinase argues against active myofiber necrosis or inflammation.","Glucocorticoids cause a myopathy marked by atrophy of type II (fast, glycolytic) fibers."],"why":"High-dose glucocorticoids break down muscle protein and reduce protein synthesis, and type II fibers are hit hardest. The result is proximal weakness with a normal creatine kinase and a biopsy showing small type II fibers without inflammation or necrosis. Disuse causes the same type II atrophy. Neurogenic disease looks different: denervated fibers become reinnervated by neighboring motor units, producing fiber type grouping and grouped atrophy, often with fasciculations.","whyNot":["Correct.","Perifascicular atrophy with perivascular inflammation is the pattern of dermatomyositis, which has a high creatine kinase and skin findings.","CD8 T cells invading fibers is the pattern of polymyositis, which raises creatine kinase.","Grouped atrophy and fiber type grouping mean denervation and reinnervation, as in spinal muscular atrophy or motor neuron disease."],"study":{"topics":["Steroid myopathy","Neurogenic versus myopathic biopsy patterns"],"comps":["MS-10","MS-13-B5"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C36","system":"MS","dx":"Tibial shaft fracture with anterior compartment syndrome","codes":["MS-13-H1"],"case":"A 23-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after a motorcycle collision. Radiographs show a closed, displaced fracture of the right tibial shaft, and the leg is splinted. Eight hours later she has severe, increasing pain in the front of the leg that is not relieved by intravenous opioids. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 128/76 mm Hg. The anterior leg is tense and firm. Passive plantarflexion of the toes causes severe pain. The dorsalis pedis pulse is 2+ and capillary refill is normal. Sensation is decreased in the first web space of the foot. Anterior compartment pressure is 48 mm Hg (normal below 10). Diagnosis: acute compartment syndrome of the anterior compartment of the leg.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C36-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why the muscles in this compartment are ischemic even though the dorsalis pedis pulse is palpable?","options":["Reflex spasm of the femoral artery has reduced inflow to the limb","Bone fragments have partly torn the anterior tibial artery wall","Marrow fat emboli have occluded arterioles within the muscle","Tissue pressure collapses capillaries and veins but not arteries"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-13-H1","obj":"MS-13-H.4","dok":3,"approach":["Compare pressures: compartment pressure 48 mm Hg, arterial systolic pressure about 128 mm Hg.","Capillary and venous pressures are far lower, roughly 10 to 30 mm Hg, so they are overcome first.","Blood still flows through the large artery to the foot, but the muscle capillaries collapse and the tissue becomes ischemic."],"why":"Bleeding and edema from the fracture fill a closed fascial compartment that cannot expand. As pressure rises above venous and capillary pressure, venous outflow and capillary flow stop, while the main artery, with far higher pressure, stays open. The muscle and nerves in the compartment become ischemic even though the distal pulse is normal. That is why severe pain, pain with passive stretch, and sensory changes appear early, and loss of pulse is a late and unreliable sign. Untreated, the dead muscle scars and contracts into a Volkmann ischemic contracture.","whyNot":["Femoral artery spasm would reduce flow to the entire limb and weaken the distal pulse. It would not single out the anterior compartment.","A torn artery would reduce or abolish the distal pulse and would cause ischemia of the whole foot, not just one compartment.","Fat embolism after long bone fractures mainly affects the lungs, brain, and skin. It does not produce a tense, painful compartment.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Compartment syndrome mechanism"],"comps":["MS-13-H1","CV-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C36-Q2","stem":"If the compartment pressure is not relieved, injury to the nerve running through this compartment is most likely to cause which of the following motor deficits?","options":["Loss of plantarflexion and foot inversion","Loss of foot eversion with intact dorsiflexion","Loss of ankle dorsiflexion and toe extension","Loss of knee extension and the patellar reflex"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-5","obj":"MS-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["Name the nerve of the anterior compartment: the deep fibular nerve, which runs with the anterior tibial artery.","Its numb skin area, the first web space, is already affected in this patient.","The muscles it supplies, tibialis anterior and the long toe extensors, dorsiflex the ankle and extend the toes, so their loss causes foot drop."],"why":"The deep fibular nerve runs in the anterior compartment and supplies tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus, and fibularis tertius. These muscles dorsiflex the ankle and extend the toes. Its only skin area on the dorsum is the first web space. Ischemic damage to this nerve and these muscles leaves a foot drop with a high-stepping gait, which is a common lasting result of untreated anterior compartment syndrome.","whyNot":["Loss of plantarflexion and inversion follows tibial nerve injury in the posterior compartment.","Isolated loss of eversion follows superficial fibular nerve injury in the lateral compartment.","Correct.","Loss of knee extension and the patellar reflex follows femoral nerve injury in the thigh."],"study":{"topics":["Deep fibular nerve and foot drop","Compartments of the leg"],"comps":["MS-5","NE-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C36-Q3","stem":"She undergoes fasciotomy and intramedullary nailing of the tibia. She smokes one pack of cigarettes daily and continues to smoke after surgery. Five months later, radiographs show a persistent fracture line with little bridging bone. Which of the following steps of fracture healing was most likely impaired?","options":["Formation of the initial hematoma around the fracture ends","Vascular ingrowth that converts cartilage callus to bone","Recruitment of neutrophils and macrophages to the site","Osteoclast remodeling of woven bone into lamellar bone"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-H1","obj":"MS-13-H.1","dok":3,"approach":["Sequence healing: hematoma and inflammation, soft callus, hard callus, then remodeling.","No bridging bone at 5 months means the soft callus has not turned into hard bony callus.","That conversion needs new blood vessels, which smoking and damaged surrounding muscle both reduce."],"why":"Fracture healing starts with a hematoma and inflammation in the first days. Over the next weeks, a soft callus of granulation tissue, fibrocartilage, and cartilage bridges the gap. New blood vessels then grow into this callus, and osteoblasts replace the cartilage with woven bone, forming the hard callus. Months to years of remodeling turn woven bone into lamellar bone. Nicotine constricts vessels and slows angiogenesis, and the muscle injury from compartment syndrome reduces local blood supply, so the step that depends on blood vessels fails and union is delayed. Infection, instability, diabetes, and malnutrition also delay healing.","whyNot":["The hematoma forms within hours of the fracture. Smoking does not stop it from forming.","Correct.","Inflammatory cell recruitment happens in the first days and is not the step that leaves a gap without bone at 5 months.","Remodeling acts on bone that has already bridged the gap. Here no bridging bone has formed."],"study":{"topics":["Stages of fracture healing","Causes of delayed union"],"comps":["MS-13-H1","MS-2"]}},{"id":"MS-C36-Q4","stem":"While the compartment pressure was high, oxygen delivery to the anterior compartment muscles fell sharply. After stored phosphocreatine was used up, which of the following processes allowed these muscle cells to keep making ATP?","options":["Beta-oxidation of fatty acids in mitochondria","Oxidation of ketone bodies through the citric acid cycle","Release of free glucose from glycogen by glucose-6-phosphatase","Conversion of pyruvate to lactate to regenerate NAD+"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.3","dok":2,"approach":["Without oxygen, the electron transport chain stops, so all mitochondrial fuel oxidation stops.","Glycolysis can still make ATP, but it needs NAD+ to keep running.","Lactate dehydrogenase converts pyruvate to lactate and regenerates NAD+, so anaerobic glycolysis continues."],"why":"Muscle first uses its small store of phosphocreatine, which creatine kinase converts to ATP within seconds. After that, without oxygen, it relies on anaerobic glycolysis of glucose and its own glycogen. Glycolysis makes 2 ATP per glucose but uses NAD+ at the glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase step. Lactate dehydrogenase reduces pyruvate to lactate and regenerates that NAD+, so glycolysis keeps going. Lactate builds up, the tissue becomes acidic, and the output is far too low to keep the muscle alive for long.","whyNot":["Beta-oxidation feeds acetyl-CoA and NADH to mitochondria, which need oxygen to make ATP. It stops in ischemia.","Ketone oxidation also depends on the citric acid cycle and oxidative phosphorylation, which require oxygen.","Muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase. It breaks glycogen down to glucose-6-phosphate and uses it inside the cell rather than releasing free glucose.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Anaerobic glycolysis in muscle","Muscle fuel use"],"comps":["MS-8","MS-7"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C37","system":"MS","dx":"Supracondylar humerus fracture in a child","codes":["MS-13-H1"],"case":"A 7-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department 1 hour after she fell from playground monkey bars onto her outstretched right hand with the elbow extended. She has severe elbow pain and will not move the arm. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. The right elbow is markedly swollen with an S-shaped deformity of the distal arm. The right radial pulse is faint compared with the left, and capillary refill in the fingers is 3 seconds (normal 2 seconds or less). Radiographs show a completely displaced extension-type supracondylar fracture of the humerus with the distal fragment displaced posteriorly and laterally. Diagnosis: displaced extension-type supracondylar fracture of the right humerus.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C37-Q1","stem":"If the compression of the brachial artery is not relieved, which of the following is the most likely long-term complication in this patient's limb?","options":["Nonunion from interruption of a retrograde intraosseous blood supply","Fixed wrist and finger flexion from forearm muscle fibrosis","Avascular necrosis of the proximal humeral fracture fragment","Fat embolism syndrome with hypoxemia, confusion, and petechiae"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-H1","obj":"MS-13-H.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the faint radial pulse as compromise of the brachial artery by the displaced fracture.","Prolonged poor flow, often with swelling, causes ischemia of the deep forearm flexor muscles.","Dead muscle is replaced by scar that shortens, leaving the wrist and fingers fixed in flexion: Volkmann ischemic contracture."],"why":"In an extension-type supracondylar fracture, the sharp end of the proximal fragment can kink or tear the brachial artery in the front of the elbow. Ischemia, often with forearm compartment syndrome, kills the deep flexor muscles, especially flexor digitorum profundus and flexor pollicis longus. Fibrous scar then replaces and shortens them, producing Volkmann ischemic contracture with a clawed, flexed wrist and fingers. Restoring flow quickly prevents it.","whyNot":["Nonunion from a retrograde blood supply is the classic risk of scaphoid waist fractures. Supracondylar fractures in children heal well.","Correct.","Avascular necrosis from a disrupted blood supply is typical of femoral neck fractures and of the proximal scaphoid, not the distal humerus in a child.","Fat embolism syndrome follows fractures of large long bones such as the femur, mostly in adults. It does not follow arterial compression."],"study":{"topics":["Supracondylar fracture complications","Volkmann ischemic contracture"],"comps":["MS-13-H1","MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C37-Q2","stem":"With the distal fragment displaced posterolaterally, the sharp end of the proximal fragment is driven forward and medially into the cubital fossa, where it compresses the brachial artery. Injury to the nerve that runs beside this artery would most likely cause which of the following deficits?","options":["Inability to oppose the thumb and flex the index fingertip","Weak elbow flexion with numbness of the lateral forearm","Inability to extend the wrist and fingers","Weak finger abduction with clawing of the ring and little fingers"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-5","obj":"MS-5.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the contents of the cubital fossa from lateral to medial: biceps tendon, brachial artery, median nerve.","The nerve beside the brachial artery is the median nerve, so a medially driven spike threatens both.","Median nerve injury at the elbow weakens thumb opposition and flexion of the thumb and index finger."],"why":"In the cubital fossa, the median nerve lies just medial to the brachial artery, which lies medial to the biceps tendon. When the distal fragment shifts posterolaterally, the proximal fragment pushes into these medial structures. Median nerve injury at this level weakens the thenar muscles, so the thumb cannot oppose, and weakens flexor pollicis longus and the index part of flexor digitorum profundus through the anterior interosseous branch, so the child cannot make an OK sign. Posteromedial displacement instead puts the radial nerve at risk.","whyNot":["Correct.","Weak elbow flexion with lateral forearm numbness comes from musculocutaneous nerve injury, which is rare in this fracture.","Wrist drop comes from radial nerve injury, which is more likely when the distal fragment shifts posteromedially.","Clawing of the ring and little fingers with weak finger abduction comes from ulnar nerve injury, more typical of flexion-type fractures or medial pin placement."],"study":{"topics":["Cubital fossa contents","Median nerve injury at the elbow"],"comps":["MS-5","NE-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C37-Q3","stem":"At follow-up after reduction and pinning, the physician taps the biceps tendon to test the reflex. Which of the following receptors and spinal cord segments carry the afferent limb of this reflex?","options":["Golgi tendon organ, Ib afferent, C7","Golgi tendon organ, Ib afferent, C5 to C6","Muscle spindle, Ia afferent, C5 to C6","Muscle spindle, Ia afferent, C7"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.3","dok":2,"approach":["A tendon tap briefly stretches the muscle, which is the stimulus for the muscle spindle.","Spindle Ia afferents synapse directly on alpha motor neurons, producing a monosynaptic stretch reflex.","The biceps reflex is carried by C5 and C6; the triceps reflex by C7."],"why":"Tapping the biceps tendon stretches the muscle quickly. Muscle spindles, which lie parallel to the muscle fibers, sense this stretch and fire through large Ia afferents. These synapse directly on alpha motor neurons for the biceps in the C5 and C6 segments, and the muscle contracts. Golgi tendon organs sit in series with the muscle at the tendon junction and sense tension through Ib afferents, which inhibit the same muscle rather than produce a brisk jerk.","whyNot":["Golgi tendon organs do not mediate the stretch reflex, and C7 carries the triceps reflex.","The segments are right, but Golgi tendon organs sense tension and inhibit the muscle. They do not drive the tendon jerk.","Correct.","The receptor is right, but C7 is the root for the triceps reflex. The biceps reflex is C5 to C6."],"study":{"topics":["Muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs","Reflex roots"],"comps":["MS-10","NE-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C37-Q4","stem":"While the brachial artery was compressed, her forearm muscles relied on anaerobic glycolysis and released lactate. After blood flow is restored, which of the following best describes the main fate of this lactate?","options":["Converted to alanine in the liver and returned to muscle","Converted to glucose in the liver, at a net cost of ATP","Excreted unchanged by the kidneys as the main route of clearance","Converted to glucose in skeletal muscle for glycogen storage"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.3","dok":2,"approach":["Lactate from muscle travels in the blood to the liver.","The liver turns it back into pyruvate and then into glucose by gluconeogenesis, which returns to muscle. This loop is the Cori cycle.","Making glucose costs more ATP in the liver than anaerobic glycolysis gave the muscle, so the cycle has a net energy cost."],"why":"In the Cori cycle, muscle converts glucose to lactate and gains 2 ATP per glucose. The liver takes up the lactate, converts it to pyruvate with lactate dehydrogenase, and builds glucose by gluconeogenesis at a cost of 6 ATP equivalents per glucose. The glucose returns to muscle. The cycle therefore costs the body about 4 ATP per turn, but it keeps glycolysis going in muscle and prevents lactate from piling up in the blood.","whyNot":["The alanine cycle runs the other way: muscle sends alanine to the liver, which uses it to make glucose.","Correct.","Only a small amount of lactate is lost in urine. The liver, and to a lesser degree the kidney cortex, clears most of it by metabolism.","Skeletal muscle does not carry out meaningful gluconeogenesis and lacks glucose-6-phosphatase, so it cannot make free glucose from lactate."],"study":{"topics":["Cori cycle","Gluconeogenesis"],"comps":["MS-8"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C38","system":"MS","dx":"Myositis ossificans of the quadriceps","codes":["MS-13-H1"],"case":"A 19-year-old man who plays college soccer comes to the physician because of a firm, painful lump in his right thigh. Five weeks ago an opponent's knee struck the front of his thigh, causing a large bruise and swelling. Since then the pain has lessened, but the lump has grown harder and he cannot fully bend his knee. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 64/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 118/70 mm Hg. Examination shows a 6-cm firm, mildly tender mass deep in the anterior mid-thigh. Knee flexion is limited to 90 degrees (normal about 135 degrees). A radiograph shows a well-circumscribed mass within the quadriceps with a rim of mineralization at its periphery and a lucent center, separated from the femoral cortex by a thin radiolucent line. Diagnosis: myositis ossificans of the vastus intermedius.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C38-Q1","stem":"Because the lesion is growing in a young adult, a biopsy is considered to exclude osteosarcoma. Which of the following histologic patterns would most strongly support myositis ossificans?","options":["Mature bone centrally with atypical cells infiltrating at the margins","Sheets of small round blue cells with scant cytoplasm and glycogen","Malignant cells laying down osteoid that invades the surrounding muscle","Mature bone at the periphery and proliferating fibroblasts centrally"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-H1","obj":"MS-13-H.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that myositis ossificans matures from the outside in, which is called the zonal pattern.","Its center holds immature, proliferating fibroblasts, the middle zone holds osteoid and woven bone, and the rim holds mature bone.","Osteosarcoma shows the reverse: its most immature, atypical cells are at the edge, invading the tissue around it."],"why":"Myositis ossificans is a benign reaction to muscle injury in which mesenchymal cells form bone in the muscle. It matures in zones. The center is cellular, with fibroblast-like cells that can look busy, the middle zone has osteoid and woven bone, and the periphery has the most mature bone, which is why the radiograph shows a mineralized rim. Osteosarcoma is the reverse: it may be most mineralized centrally, and its atypical cells make malignant osteoid that infiltrates the surrounding muscle. Recognizing this zonal pattern prevents an unnecessary radical operation.","whyNot":["Mature bone centrally with atypical cells at the edges is the reverse zonal pattern, which points to osteosarcoma.","Small round blue cells with glycogen describe Ewing sarcoma, which usually arises in the shaft of a long bone or the pelvis.","Malignant osteoid infiltrating the surrounding muscle is the hallmark of osteosarcoma.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Myositis ossificans versus osteosarcoma"],"comps":["MS-13-H","MS-13-F"]}},{"id":"MS-C38-Q2","stem":"A lesion of the nerve that supplies the injured muscle, just below the inguinal ligament, would most likely cause which of the following?","options":["Foot drop with numbness over the dorsum of the foot","Weak hip adduction with numbness over the medial thigh","Weak knee extension with numbness over the medial leg","Contralateral pelvic drop during stance on the affected leg"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-5","obj":"MS-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["The vastus intermedius is part of the quadriceps, which is supplied by the femoral nerve.","The quadriceps extends the knee, so femoral nerve injury weakens knee extension and reduces the patellar reflex.","Its sensory branches cover the anterior thigh, and its saphenous branch covers the medial leg."],"why":"The femoral nerve (L2 to L4) enters the thigh under the inguinal ligament, lateral to the femoral artery, and supplies the quadriceps and sartorius. Injury there weakens knee extension, so the knee may buckle, and reduces the patellar reflex. Its anterior cutaneous branches supply the front of the thigh, and its saphenous branch supplies the skin of the medial leg and foot.","whyNot":["Foot drop with dorsal foot numbness follows common fibular nerve injury, often at the fibular neck.","Weak adduction with medial thigh numbness follows obturator nerve injury, which supplies the medial compartment of the thigh.","Correct.","A pelvic drop on the opposite side during stance is the Trendelenburg sign of superior gluteal nerve injury, from weak gluteus medius and minimus."],"study":{"topics":["Femoral nerve","Lower limb nerve injuries"],"comps":["MS-5","NE-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C38-Q3","stem":"In this lesion, mesenchymal progenitor cells in the injured muscle differentiate into osteoblasts under the control of a master transcription factor. Haploinsufficiency of that same transcription factor causes which of the following conditions?","options":["Cleidocranial dysplasia with absent clavicles","Osteogenesis imperfecta with blue sclerae and fractures","Campomelic dysplasia with bowed long bones","Achondroplasia with shortening of proximal limbs"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-9","obj":"MS-9.1","dok":2,"approach":["Name the master transcription factor for osteoblast differentiation: RUNX2.","Recall what happens when one copy of RUNX2 is lost.","Too little RUNX2 impairs intramembranous bone in particular, giving cleidocranial dysplasia."],"why":"RUNX2 switches mesenchymal cells into the osteoblast lineage and turns on bone matrix genes. Losing one copy (haploinsufficiency) leaves too little RUNX2 for normal bone formation, which shows most in bones formed by intramembranous ossification, such as the clavicles and skull vault. The result is cleidocranial dysplasia, with absent or small clavicles, delayed closure of the fontanelles and sutures, and dental anomalies. SOX9 plays the matching role for chondrocytes.","whyNot":["Correct.","Osteogenesis imperfecta comes from mutations in COL1A1 or COL1A2, the genes for type I collagen.","Campomelic dysplasia comes from haploinsufficiency of SOX9, the master transcription factor for chondrocytes.","Achondroplasia comes from a gain-of-function mutation in FGFR3, which slows chondrocyte proliferation in the growth plate."],"study":{"topics":["RUNX2 and SOX9","Cleidocranial dysplasia"],"comps":["MS-9","MS-1"]}},{"id":"MS-C38-Q4","stem":"A biopsy of the middle zone of the lesion shows osteoblasts rapidly laying down new bone on strands of osteoid. Which of the following best describes the structure of this newly formed bone?","options":["Hyaline cartilage with chondrocytes in lacunae and type II collagen","Lamellar bone with osteons around central Haversian canals","Spongy lamellar bone with trabeculae surrounding red marrow","Woven bone with haphazard collagen fibers and many osteocytes"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Bone that forms quickly, as in repair, the embryo, or reactive lesions, is laid down first as woven bone.","Woven bone has randomly arranged collagen and many large osteocytes and is weaker than lamellar bone.","Over months it is remodeled into lamellar bone, which is organized into osteons in compact bone."],"why":"Whenever bone forms fast, osteoblasts lay down woven bone: collagen fibers run in random directions, osteocytes are numerous and irregular, and the bone is mechanically weak. Later, it is remodeled into lamellar bone, in which collagen runs in parallel layers. In compact bone the lamellae wrap around Haversian canals to form osteons, linked by Volkmann canals, and in spongy bone they form trabeculae around marrow. The middle zone of a lesion only weeks old is still making woven bone.","whyNot":["Cartilage may appear in some lesions, but the stem describes bone being formed, and the earliest bone is woven.","Osteons with Haversian canals are the mature lamellar structure of compact bone. They take months to form by remodeling.","Spongy bone is also lamellar and mature. It is not the first bone laid down in a reactive lesion.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Woven versus lamellar bone","Compact and spongy bone"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-13-H"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C39","system":"MS","dx":"Rheumatoid arthritis with Felty syndrome","codes":["MS-11"],"case":"A 62-year-old woman with a 15-year history of rheumatoid arthritis comes to the physician because of two episodes of cellulitis in the past year and increasing fatigue. Temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 126/78 mm Hg. Examination shows symmetric swelling of the metacarpophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints with sparing of the distal interphalangeal joints, ulnar deviation of the fingers, swan-neck deformities, and firm subcutaneous nodules over both olecranon processes. The spleen is palpable 5 cm below the left costal margin. Hemoglobin is 10.8 g/dL (normal 12.0 to 16.0), leukocyte count is 2,600/mm3 (normal 4,500 to 11,000), absolute neutrophil count is 700/mm3 (normal 1,500 to 8,000), and platelet count is 198,000/mm3 (normal 150,000 to 400,000). Rheumatoid factor and anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibodies are positive. Diagnosis: rheumatoid arthritis with Felty syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C39-Q1","stem":"She carries HLA-DRB1*04 (HLA-DR4) alleles, whose shared epitope binds certain modified self-peptides especially well and presents them to CD4 T cells. Her antibodies target the same modified peptides. Which of the following posttranslational modifications creates these self-peptides?","options":["Deamidation of glutamine residues by tissue transglutaminase","Hydroxylation of lysine residues by lysyl hydroxylase","Phosphorylation of tyrosine residues by receptor kinases","Enzymatic conversion of arginine residues to citrulline"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.1","dok":3,"approach":["Link the anti-CCP antibody to its target: proteins containing citrulline.","Citrulline is made after translation when peptidylarginine deiminase enzymes convert arginine residues.","HLA-DR4 shared epitope alleles present citrullinated peptides well, which drives T cell help for autoantibody production."],"why":"Inflammation, and in the lungs smoking, activates peptidylarginine deiminase enzymes that convert arginine residues in proteins such as fibrinogen, vimentin, and collagen into citrulline. In people with HLA-DR4 shared epitope alleles, these citrullinated peptides fit well into the MHC class II groove and activate CD4 T cells. The T cells help B cells make anti-CCP antibodies and drive synovitis through TNF, IL-1, and IL-6. The inflamed synovium becomes pannus, and RANKL-activated osteoclasts erode cartilage and bone.","whyNot":["Glutamine deamidation by tissue transglutaminase creates gliadin peptides that bind HLA-DQ2 and DQ8 in celiac disease.","Lysine hydroxylation is a normal step in collagen synthesis that allows cross-linking. It is not the target of rheumatoid arthritis autoantibodies.","Tyrosine phosphorylation is a signaling step in receptor tyrosine kinase pathways. It does not create the rheumatoid autoantigens.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis","Citrullination and HLA-DR4"],"comps":["MS-11","IM-3"]}},{"id":"MS-C39-Q2","stem":"Her sister has hand osteoarthritis. Which of the following radiographic findings in this patient's hands would best distinguish her disease from her sister's?","options":["Pencil-in-cup deformities with fluffy periosteal new bone","Osteophytes and subchondral sclerosis with asymmetric joint space loss","Marginal bony erosions with periarticular osteopenia","Punched-out erosions with overhanging edges of cortical bone"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.2","dok":3,"approach":["Rheumatoid arthritis is an inflammatory synovitis, and osteoarthritis is degeneration of cartilage with bony overgrowth.","Inflamed synovium erodes bone at the joint margins where cartilage does not cover it, and cytokines thin the nearby bone.","Osteoarthritis builds bone, with osteophytes and sclerosis, instead of eroding it."],"why":"In rheumatoid arthritis, pannus first attacks the bare areas at the joint margins, where bone sits inside the capsule without cartilage cover, producing marginal erosions. Cytokines and disuse thin the bone around the joint, which is periarticular osteopenia. Joint space narrowing is even across the joint, and the metacarpophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints are hit symmetrically, with the distal joints spared. Osteoarthritis instead shows uneven joint space loss, subchondral sclerosis, cysts, and osteophytes, often at the distal interphalangeal joints.","whyNot":["Pencil-in-cup deformities with periosteal new bone are typical of psoriatic arthritis, which favors the distal interphalangeal joints.","Osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis, and uneven narrowing are the features of osteoarthritis, her sister's disease.","Correct.","Punched-out erosions with overhanging edges are the classic radiographic sign of chronic tophaceous gout."],"study":{"topics":["Rheumatoid arthritis versus osteoarthritis"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-13-E"]}},{"id":"MS-C39-Q3","stem":"She also reports 2 years of gritty, dry eyes and a dry mouth that makes it hard to swallow crackers. Which of the following autoantibodies, if present, would most support the additional diagnosis suggested by these symptoms?","options":["Anti-Scl-70","Anti-SSA/Ro","Anti-Smith","Anti-Jo-1"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.3","dok":2,"approach":["Dry eyes and dry mouth point to Sjogren syndrome, which often occurs secondary to rheumatoid arthritis.","Match Sjogren syndrome to its autoantibodies: anti-SSA (Ro) and anti-SSB (La).","Rule out antibodies tied to myositis, diffuse scleroderma, and SLE."],"why":"Sjogren syndrome is lymphocytic destruction of the lacrimal and salivary glands, causing dry eyes and dry mouth. It can occur alone or alongside rheumatoid arthritis and other connective tissue diseases. Anti-SSA (Ro) and anti-SSB (La) antibodies support the diagnosis. Anti-SSA also matters in pregnancy, because it crosses the placenta and can cause neonatal lupus with congenital heart block.","whyNot":["Anti-Scl-70 (anti-topoisomerase I) is linked to diffuse systemic sclerosis.","Correct.","Anti-Smith is highly specific for SLE.","Anti-Jo-1 is linked to inflammatory myositis, often with interstitial lung disease."],"study":{"topics":["Autoantibodies in rheumatic disease","Sjogren syndrome"],"comps":["MS-11","IM-15-D"]}},{"id":"MS-C39-Q4","stem":"In her metacarpophalangeal joints, inflamed synovium has spread over the surface of the articular cartilage as pannus. The cartilage beneath it thins even in areas without direct erosion. Which of the following normally supplies nutrients to this articular cartilage?","options":["Capillaries running through the articular cartilage matrix","Lymphatic vessels in the deep calcified cartilage layer","Perichondrial vessels covering the articular surface","Diffusion from the synovial fluid in the joint cavity"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that articular hyaline cartilage has no blood vessels, nerves, lymphatics, or perichondrium.","Its chondrocytes get nutrients by diffusion from synovial fluid, helped by joint loading and movement.","Pannus covering the surface blocks this supply and releases enzymes, so the cartilage starves and breaks down."],"why":"Synovial joints are lined by a synovial membrane whose cells make synovial fluid, rich in hyaluronan and lubricin. The articular surface is hyaline cartilage with no blood supply and no perichondrium, so its chondrocytes depend on diffusion of nutrients from this fluid, pumped in and out as the joint is loaded. In rheumatoid arthritis, pannus spreading over the cartilage cuts off this diffusion and releases proteases and cytokines, so the cartilage degrades even before the bone is eroded.","whyNot":["Articular cartilage is avascular. It has no capillaries in its matrix, which is one reason it heals poorly.","Cartilage has no lymphatic vessels. The calcified deep layer anchors it to bone.","Articular cartilage has no perichondrium, unlike most other hyaline cartilage, such as the cartilage of the trachea.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Synovial joint structure","Articular cartilage nutrition"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-6"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C40","system":"MS","dx":"Limited systemic sclerosis (CREST)","codes":["MS-11"],"case":"A 52-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 2-year history of heartburn and difficulty swallowing solid food. For 8 years, her fingers have turned white, then blue, then red when exposed to cold. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 76/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg. Examination shows thickened, tight, shiny skin of the fingers distal to the metacarpophalangeal joints and around the mouth, with no skin thickening on the arms or trunk. There are telangiectasias on the face and lips, small firm white nodules at the fingertips, and healed pitted scars on several fingertips. Antinuclear antibody testing is positive at a titer of 1:640. Esophageal manometry shows weak peristalsis in the distal esophagus and a low lower esophageal sphincter pressure. Diagnosis: limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis (CREST syndrome).","questions":[{"id":"MS-C40-Q1","stem":"Over the next several years, which of the following changes in her hands is most likely to develop?","options":["Fixed flexion contractures of the fingers from skin fibrosis","Ulnar deviation with erosions at the metacarpophalangeal joints","Reducible deformities without erosions from lax tendons","Bony enlargement of the distal interphalangeal joints"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the disease process: fibrosis of the skin and tissues around the joints, with Raynaud phenomenon and calcinosis.","Tight, thickened skin and fibrotic tissue over the fingers cannot stretch.","The fingers become fixed in flexion: sclerodactyly with flexion contractures."],"why":"In systemic sclerosis, activated fibroblasts lay down excess collagen in the skin and in tendon sheaths and joint capsules. Over the fingers this produces sclerodactyly, and as the tissues tighten the fingers are pulled into fixed flexion contractures. Calcinosis deposits form at the fingertips, and ischemia from Raynaud phenomenon leaves pitted scars or ulcers. Joint involvement is usually from this fibrosis rather than erosive synovitis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Ulnar deviation with marginal erosions is the pattern of rheumatoid arthritis, an erosive synovitis.","Reducible deformities without erosions from lax tendons and capsules are typical of SLE arthritis (Jaccoud arthropathy).","Bony enlargement of the distal interphalangeal joints (Heberden nodes) is a sign of osteoarthritis."],"study":{"topics":["Musculoskeletal features of systemic sclerosis"],"comps":["MS-11","IM-15-D"]}},{"id":"MS-C40-Q2","stem":"Given the limited distribution of her skin thickening and her other findings, which of the following autoantibodies is most likely to be found on further testing?","options":["Anti-Jo-1","Anticentromere","Anti-topoisomerase I","Anti-U1 RNP"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.3","dok":2,"approach":["Separate the two forms of systemic sclerosis by skin extent: limited (distal limbs and face) and diffuse (proximal limbs and trunk).","Limited disease with calcinosis, Raynaud phenomenon, esophageal dysmotility, sclerodactyly, and telangiectasia is CREST.","Anticentromere antibodies go with the limited form; anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl-70) goes with the diffuse form."],"why":"Systemic sclerosis is split by how far the skin thickening extends. In the limited form, skin changes stay distal to the elbows and knees and on the face, the course is slow, and anticentromere antibodies are typical. The major late risk is pulmonary arterial hypertension. In the diffuse form, skin thickens on the upper arms and trunk early, anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl-70) antibodies are typical, and interstitial lung disease and scleroderma renal crisis are more common.","whyNot":["Anti-Jo-1 is linked to inflammatory myositis with interstitial lung disease and mechanic's hands.","Correct.","Anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl-70) is linked to diffuse systemic sclerosis, with skin thickening on the trunk and proximal limbs.","Anti-U1 RNP is linked to mixed connective tissue disease, which combines features of SLE, scleroderma, and myositis."],"study":{"topics":["Limited versus diffuse systemic sclerosis","Scleroderma autoantibodies"],"comps":["MS-11","IM-15-D"]}},{"id":"MS-C40-Q3","stem":"During an attack of Raynaud phenomenon, cold increases sympathetic alpha-1 adrenergic constriction of her digital arteries. Vascular smooth muscle can hold this contraction for long periods while using far less ATP than skeletal muscle needs to hold the same force. Which of the following best explains this economy of energy?","options":["Latch-state cross-bridges that detach slowly after dephosphorylation","Calcium binding to troponin C to keep myosin-binding sites exposed","Rigor cross-bridges that persist because cellular ATP is depleted","Rising myosin light chain kinase activity throughout the contraction"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.5","dok":2,"approach":["Note the clue: force held for a long time with little ATP use.","Recall that smooth muscle can keep its force after the myosin light chains have been dephosphorylated.","Dephosphorylated myosin heads still bound to actin detach very slowly, which is the latch state, so tension is held at low energy cost."],"why":"Smooth muscle starts contracting when calcium binds calmodulin and the complex activates myosin light chain kinase. The kinase phosphorylates the regulatory light chain, and myosin begins cycling on actin. Once force has built up, myosin light chain phosphatase can remove the phosphate from heads that are still attached to actin. These heads detach very slowly, so they keep holding tension while using little ATP. This is the latch state, and it lets vascular smooth muscle maintain tone for long periods at a low energy cost. In systemic sclerosis, damaged and narrowed digital arteries make this normal cold response severe enough to cause ulcers and pitted scars.","whyNot":["Correct.","Smooth muscle has no troponin. Calcium acts through calmodulin and myosin light chain kinase instead.","Rigor bonds form only when ATP runs out, as after death. Her smooth muscle has ATP and relaxes when the stimulus ends.","Light chain kinase activity and light chain phosphorylation fall during a sustained contraction even though force is maintained. That fall is part of the latch state, so rising kinase activity does not explain the low ATP use."],"study":{"topics":["Latch state in smooth muscle","Raynaud phenomenon"],"comps":["MS-7","CV-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C40-Q4","stem":"A skin biopsy from her finger shows a thickened dermis packed with dense collagen bundles that have replaced hair follicles and sweat glands. Which of the following collagen types makes up the majority of this excess collagen?","options":["Type II","Type III","Type I","Type IV"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the dense dermal collagen as fibrosis from activated fibroblasts.","Recall which collagen makes up most of normal dermis, tendon, and mature scar: type I.","Fibroblasts in systemic sclerosis overproduce the same dermal collagen, so type I dominates."],"why":"Type I collagen is the main collagen of dermis, bone, tendon, ligament, and mature scar, and it forms thick, strong fibers. In systemic sclerosis, TGF-beta and other signals drive fibroblasts to make far too much collagen, and the dermis fills with dense type I bundles that crowd out skin appendages. Type III collagen is also increased, especially early, but type I makes up most of the excess.","whyNot":["Type II collagen is found in hyaline cartilage and the nucleus pulposus. It is not a skin collagen.","Type III collagen rises in early fibrosis and in granulation tissue, but it makes up a smaller share than type I.","Correct.","Type IV collagen forms basement membranes, such as those under the epidermis and around vessels. It does not fill the dermis."],"study":{"topics":["Collagen types","Fibrosis in systemic sclerosis"],"comps":["MS-6","IM-15-D"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C41","system":"MS","dx":"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome with small cell lung cancer","codes":["MS-10"],"case":"A 62-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of progressive difficulty climbing stairs and rising from a chair. He also reports a dry mouth and erectile dysfunction. He has smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 45 years. Physical examination shows symmetric weakness of the hip and shoulder girdle muscles and mild bilateral ptosis. Patellar and biceps reflexes are absent at rest but can be elicited after 10 seconds of maximal voluntary contraction of the tested muscle. Sensation is intact. CT of the chest shows a large right hilar mass with mediastinal lymphadenopathy, and bronchoscopic biopsy shows small cell carcinoma. Diagnosis: Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome associated with small cell lung cancer.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C41-Q1","stem":"Nerve conduction studies are performed. Which of the following electrodiagnostic findings is most likely in this patient?","options":["Low resting motor amplitude that rises markedly after brief exercise","Normal resting motor amplitude with a decrement at 3 Hz stimulation","Reduced sensory nerve action potentials with slowed conduction velocity","Fibrillation potentials with large, long-duration motor units"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.2","dok":3,"approach":["Notice the clues: proximal weakness, autonomic symptoms, absent reflexes that return after exercise, and a smoker with small cell cancer.","Recall that this disorder reduces acetylcholine release, so each nerve impulse produces a small end plate potential.","Repeated activity builds up calcium in the nerve terminal and boosts release, so the response grows with exercise or fast stimulation."],"why":"In Lambert-Eaton syndrome, antibodies block presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels, so too little acetylcholine is released with each impulse. The resting compound muscle action potential is small. After brief maximal exercise or fast repetitive stimulation, calcium accumulates in the terminal and release improves, so the amplitude rises, often by more than 100%. Myasthenia gravis is the opposite pattern: a normal resting amplitude that falls with repeated stimulation, because the problem is fewer postsynaptic receptors.","whyNot":["Correct.","This is the classic pattern of myasthenia gravis. Release is normal, but receptors are lost, so the safety margin fails as release falls with repeated 3 Hz stimulation.","Low sensory amplitudes and slowed conduction point to a peripheral neuropathy. Sensation is normal here, and Lambert-Eaton syndrome spares sensory nerves.","Fibrillations with large, long motor units are signs of denervation and reinnervation, as in motor neuron disease. They are not expected in a presynaptic junction disorder."],"study":{"topics":["Lambert-Eaton syndrome versus myasthenia gravis","Repetitive nerve stimulation patterns"],"comps":["MS-10","MS-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C41-Q2","stem":"Serum testing shows antibodies against P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels. Which of the following best classifies the immune mechanism of this patient's weakness?","options":["Type I hypersensitivity from IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation","Type IV hypersensitivity from CD8+ T cells attacking muscle fibers","Type II hypersensitivity from IgG cross-reacting with a nerve antigen","Type III hypersensitivity from immune complexes deposited at end plates"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.5","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the target: an antibody against a fixed membrane protein on the nerve terminal.","Link the source: small cell carcinoma cells are neuroendocrine and express the same calcium channel, so the immune response to the tumor cross-reacts with nerve.","An antibody against a cell-surface antigen that changes cell function is type II hypersensitivity."],"why":"Small cell lung cancer cells express voltage-gated calcium channels. The immune response to the tumor makes IgG that also binds the channels on motor and autonomic nerve terminals. The antibodies cross-link and remove the channels, so less calcium enters and less acetylcholine is released. Antibody binding to a fixed cell-surface antigen is type II hypersensitivity, the same class as myasthenia gravis and the molecular mimicry of rheumatic fever.","whyNot":["Type I reactions are immediate IgE-mediated allergic responses such as anaphylaxis and play no role in this weakness.","Type IV injury by CD8+ T cells invading muscle fibers is the mechanism of polymyositis. Muscle fibers are normal in this disorder.","Correct.","Type III injury comes from circulating immune complexes that lodge in vessel walls, as in SLE arthritis and glomerulonephritis. Here the antibody binds a fixed membrane protein."],"study":{"topics":["Hypersensitivity types in musculoskeletal disease","Paraneoplastic syndromes of small cell lung cancer"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-10"]}},{"id":"MS-C41-Q3","stem":"Six weeks later, he reports aching pain in both wrists and ankles. Examination shows clubbing of the fingers and warm, tender distal forearms and lower legs. Radiographs show smooth, symmetric periosteal new bone along the shafts of the distal radius, ulna, tibia, and fibula. Which of the following best explains these findings?","options":["PTH-related peptide causing subperiosteal bone resorption","Osteolytic metastases with a reactive periosteal bone response","Calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition in the joints","Paraneoplastic new bone formation linked to the lung tumor"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F","obj":"MS-13-F.11","dok":2,"approach":["Group the findings: clubbing, symmetric periosteal new bone on long bone shafts, and joint pain.","Recognize this triad as hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy.","Recall that it is a paraneoplastic syndrome of lung cancer and is not caused by tumor in the bone."],"why":"Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy is the triad of digital clubbing, periosteal new bone along the diaphyses of long bones, and arthritis of nearby joints. It is a paraneoplastic syndrome of lung cancer, more often non-small cell types, but it can occur with any lung tumor. The new bone is laid down symmetrically, which separates it from focal metastases. The leading explanation is that growth factors such as VEGF and PDGF act on the distal limbs.","whyNot":["PTH-related peptide is made mostly by squamous cell carcinoma and causes hypercalcemia. Subperiosteal resorption is a sign of hyperparathyroidism and removes bone rather than adding it.","Metastases are usually focal and asymmetric, often in the axial skeleton, and appear as lytic or blastic lesions within bone rather than smooth symmetric periosteal layers.","Calcium pyrophosphate deposition causes chondrocalcinosis and pseudogout attacks, often in the knee or wrist, but does not produce clubbing or periosteal new bone.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy","Paraneoplastic syndromes of lung cancer"],"comps":["MS-13-F","MS-13-C"]}},{"id":"MS-C41-Q4","stem":"Treatment with amifampridine (3,4-diaminopyridine), a blocker of voltage-gated potassium channels in the nerve terminal, improves his strength. Which of the following best explains this improvement?","options":["Prolonged terminal depolarization increases calcium entry","Increased number of postsynaptic nicotinic receptors","Protection of SNARE proteins from proteolytic cleavage","Inhibited acetylcholinesterase prolongs acetylcholine in the cleft"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.1","dok":3,"approach":["Trace the steps: the action potential depolarizes the terminal, voltage-gated calcium channels open, calcium triggers SNARE-mediated vesicle fusion, and acetylcholine binds nicotinic receptors.","Locate the defect in this disease at calcium entry.","Blocking potassium channels slows repolarization, so the remaining calcium channels stay open longer."],"why":"Potassium efflux repolarizes the nerve terminal after each action potential. When those channels are blocked, the terminal stays depolarized longer, and the calcium channels that survive the antibody attack stay open longer. More calcium enters, more vesicles fuse through the SNARE complex, and the end plate potential reaches threshold more often. This treats the exact step that fails in Lambert-Eaton syndrome.","whyNot":["Correct.","Potassium channel blockers do not change receptor number. Receptor loss is the defect in myasthenia gravis, and here the receptors are normal.","Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins and blocks vesicle fusion. That is the defect in botulism, and SNARE proteins are intact here.","This is how pyridostigmine works. It helps most in myasthenia gravis, where the problem is postsynaptic, and it is not how a potassium channel blocker acts."],"study":{"topics":["Neuromuscular transmission steps","Localizing junction disorders"],"comps":["MS-10"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C42","system":"MS","dx":"McArdle disease","codes":["MS-8"],"case":"A 19-year-old woman comes to the physician because of lifelong exercise intolerance. Within a few minutes of intense exercise she develops muscle pain, stiffness, and fatigue. If she rests briefly and then resumes at a slower pace, she can continue with much less discomfort. Two days ago, after sprint drills, she had painful cramps and passed dark red-brown urine. Her parents are healthy and are first cousins. Physical examination today is normal. Serum creatine kinase is 8,400 U/L (reference 30 to 170). A forearm exercise test shows no rise in venous lactate, while venous ammonia rises. Genetic testing shows biallelic pathogenic variants in PYGM, the gene for muscle glycogen phosphorylase. Diagnosis: McArdle disease (glycogen storage disease type V).","questions":[{"id":"MS-C42-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her symptoms ease when she rests briefly and then continues exercising?","options":["Debranching enzyme releases free glucose from muscle glycogen","Muscle uptake of blood glucose and free fatty acids increases","Muscle glucose-6-phosphatase releases glucose into the cytosol","Lysosomal acid maltase begins degrading stored muscle glycogen"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the defect: muscle cannot break down its own glycogen.","Ask what other fuels muscle can use: glucose from the blood (supplied by the liver, whose phosphorylase is a different enzyme) and fatty acids.","After a few minutes, blood flow and fuel delivery rise, so muscle shifts to these outside fuels. This is the second wind."],"why":"McArdle disease is an autosomal recessive deficiency of muscle glycogen phosphorylase, so muscle cannot mobilize its glycogen at the start of intense exercise. After several minutes, exercising muscle receives more blood flow and takes up more glucose released by the liver and more free fatty acids from adipose tissue. With these outside fuels, she can continue, which is the second wind. The same block explains the flat lactate curve, the cramps, and myoglobinuria after intense exertion.","whyNot":["Debranching enzyme acts only after phosphorylase has shortened the outer chains, and it releases very little free glucose. It cannot bypass the missing phosphorylase.","Correct.","Muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase, which is why muscle glycogen cannot supply blood glucose. This enzyme is found in liver and kidney.","Acid maltase is the lysosomal enzyme missing in Pompe disease. Its small lysosomal pathway cannot supply energy for exercise."],"study":{"topics":["McArdle disease and the second wind","Glycogen storage diseases"],"comps":["MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C42-Q2","stem":"During one of her cramps, needle electromyography of the contracted muscle is electrically silent. The cramp is attributed to energy failure in the fibers. Which step in the contraction cycle most directly fails when muscle ATP is depleted?","options":["Depolarization of T tubules that activates DHP receptors","Calcium binding to troponin C to expose sites on actin","Release of ADP from myosin at the end of the stroke","Release of myosin heads from actin after ATP binding"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.2","dok":2,"approach":["An electrically silent contraction means the muscle is stuck in a contracted state without new action potentials.","Walk through the cross-bridge cycle and find the step that needs a fresh ATP molecule.","ATP binding releases myosin from actin. Without ATP, heads stay attached, as in rigor mortis."],"why":"In the cross-bridge cycle, ATP binding to the myosin head releases it from actin. Hydrolysis then cocks the head, the head binds actin, and phosphate release drives the power stroke. When ATP runs out, myosin stays bound to actin and the fiber cannot relax, which is the mechanism of rigor mortis. An electrically silent contracture in McArdle disease reflects this kind of energy failure, unlike an ordinary cramp, which shows electrical activity.","whyNot":["T tubule depolarization is electrical. The electromyogram is silent, so no new depolarizations are driving this contraction.","Calcium binding to troponin C does not use ATP. It starts contraction but does not explain why fibers stay locked once ATP is gone.","ADP release does not need new ATP. It happens at the end of the power stroke and leaves the head bound to actin, so it does not explain failure to relax.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cross-bridge cycle","Rigor and ATP depletion"],"comps":["MS-7","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C42-Q3","stem":"A muscle biopsy is fixed while the fibers are in a contracted state, and electron microscopy shows shortened sarcomeres. Compared with a relaxed sarcomere, which of the following keeps the same length?","options":["H zone","I band","A band","Distance between Z lines"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the sliding filament model: thin filaments slide past thick filaments, and neither filament changes length.","The A band equals the length of the thick filaments, so it stays the same.","Bands that contain only one kind of filament (I band, H zone) shrink as overlap increases, and the Z lines move closer together."],"why":"The A band is the full length of the thick myosin filaments, and thick filaments do not shorten. During contraction, thin filaments slide toward the M line, so the overlap grows. The I band (thin filaments only) and the H zone (thick filaments only) both shorten, and the Z lines are pulled closer, shortening the sarcomere.","whyNot":["The H zone contains only thick filaments and narrows, and can disappear, as thin filaments slide toward the M line.","The I band contains only thin filaments and shortens as they slide further into the A band.","Correct.","The Z line to Z line distance is the sarcomere length itself, which shortens with contraction."],"study":{"topics":["Sarcomere bands","Sliding filament model"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-7"]}},{"id":"MS-C42-Q4","stem":"She notes that she can perform a single 5-second maximal jump without any symptoms. Which of the following energy sources mainly supports this brief effort?","options":["Anaerobic breakdown of muscle glycogen to lactate","Phosphocreatine transfer of phosphate to ADP","Oxidation of free fatty acids in mitochondria","Oxidation of ketone bodies released by the liver"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.3","dok":2,"approach":["Match exercise duration to fuel: the first seconds use stored ATP and phosphocreatine, the next minute or so relies on anaerobic glycolysis, and longer work uses oxidative metabolism.","Her glycogen breakdown is blocked, yet a 5-second effort is normal.","That points to the phosphocreatine system, which does not need glycogen."],"why":"In the first several seconds of maximal effort, muscle regenerates ATP from phosphocreatine through creatine kinase, which transfers phosphate to ADP. This system does not depend on glycogen, so it works normally in McArdle disease. Symptoms appear when the effort lasts long enough to need anaerobic glycolysis from muscle glycogen, the pathway she cannot start.","whyNot":["Anaerobic glycolysis from muscle glycogen powers efforts lasting from several seconds to about 2 minutes. It is the pathway blocked in her disease.","Correct.","Fatty acid oxidation is slow to start and supports prolonged, lower-intensity work, not a single maximal burst.","Ketone bodies are used mainly during fasting by heart, muscle, and brain. They are too slow and too scarce to fuel a 5-second maximal effort."],"study":{"topics":["Energy systems by exercise duration","Creatine kinase and phosphocreatine"],"comps":["MS-8"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C43","system":"MS","dx":"Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II deficiency","codes":["MS-8"],"case":"A 26-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 6 hours after finishing a 5-hour hike during which he ate nothing. He has diffuse muscle pain, weakness, and dark brown urine. He had two similar episodes, one after a long bicycle ride and one during a febrile illness. He can sprint without symptoms. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 98/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 128/76 mm Hg. Thigh and calf muscles are tender. Serum studies show creatine kinase 62,000 U/L (reference 30 to 200), potassium 6.1 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.0), and creatinine 2.4 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3). Urine dipstick is strongly positive for blood, and microscopy shows 0 to 2 red blood cells per high-power field. Genetic testing shows biallelic CPT2 variants. Diagnosis: carnitine palmitoyltransferase II deficiency with exertional rhabdomyolysis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C43-Q1","stem":"Which of the following steps of fatty acid metabolism is deficient in this patient?","options":["Transfer of long-chain acyl groups from CoA to carnitine","Activation of long-chain fatty acids to acyl-CoA in the cytosol","Dehydrogenation of medium-chain acyl-CoA in the matrix","Return of acyl groups from carnitine to CoA in the matrix"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the carnitine shuttle: CPT I on the outer mitochondrial membrane moves long-chain acyl groups onto carnitine, a translocase carries acylcarnitine across the inner membrane, and CPT II on the inner membrane puts the acyl group back onto CoA.","Place CPT II at the last step, inside the matrix.","Link the defect to his triggers: prolonged exercise and fasting, when muscle depends most on long-chain fatty acid oxidation."],"why":"CPT II sits on the matrix side of the inner mitochondrial membrane and converts long-chain acylcarnitine back to acyl-CoA so beta-oxidation can begin. When it is deficient, long-chain fatty acids cannot be oxidized. At rest and during short bursts, muscle runs on glucose and glycogen, so he is well. During prolonged exercise, fasting, cold, or fever, muscle relies on fatty acids, energy fails, and fibers break down.","whyNot":["This is the CPT I reaction on the outer membrane. CPT I deficiency mainly affects the liver and causes fasting hypoketotic hypoglycemia, and his gene test points to CPT II.","Acyl-CoA synthetase activates fatty acids in the cytosol before the shuttle. It is upstream of the defect.","Medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency (MCAD) causes fasting hypoketotic hypoglycemia, mainly in infants, and is a beta-oxidation defect after the shuttle.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Carnitine shuttle","CPT II deficiency"],"comps":["MS-8","MS-13-H"]}},{"id":"MS-C43-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains the rise in his serum creatinine?","options":["Interstitial nephritis from a drug hypersensitivity","Immune complex deposition in glomerular capillaries","Heme pigment toxicity and casts in renal tubules","Hemoglobin released by intravascular hemolysis"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-H","obj":"MS-13-H.5","dok":3,"approach":["Read the urine: dipstick positive for blood but almost no red cells means a free heme pigment.","With a very high creatine kinase, the pigment is myoglobin from muscle breakdown.","Myoglobin filtered by the kidney injures tubules and forms casts, causing acute kidney injury."],"why":"In rhabdomyolysis, injured muscle releases potassium, creatine kinase, and myoglobin. Myoglobin is small enough to be filtered. In the tubules its heme causes oxidant injury, and it precipitates into casts, especially in acidic, concentrated urine, while volume depletion lowers renal blood flow. The dipstick detects heme, so it reads positive for blood even though few red cells are seen. Hyperkalemia comes from release of intracellular potassium and worsens as kidney function falls.","whyNot":["Drug-induced interstitial nephritis follows a new medication and often brings fever, rash, and white cells in the urine. Nothing in his history points to a drug, and the very high creatine kinase points to muscle breakdown.","Immune complex glomerulonephritis causes hematuria with red cells and red cell casts, not a heme-positive urine with no red cells.","Correct.","Hemolysis also gives a heme-positive urine with few red cells, but it would not raise creatine kinase to 62,000 U/L. The muscle pain and tenderness point to myoglobin."],"study":{"topics":["Rhabdomyolysis and myoglobinuria","Pigment nephropathy"],"comps":["MS-13-H","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C43-Q3","stem":"Which of the following features of skeletal muscle fibers best explains why long, moderate-intensity exercise triggers his episodes while sprinting does not?","options":["Type I fibers have low myoglobin and few mitochondria","Type I fibers rely on fatty acid oxidation for steady work","Type II fibers are recruited first during low-intensity work","Type II fibers store glycogen that powers hours of steady work"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the two fiber types: type I are slow, red, oxidative, and fatigue resistant; type II are fast, glycolytic, and fatigable.","Long steady exercise mainly uses type I fibers, which burn fatty acids in their many mitochondria.","His defect blocks long-chain fatty acid oxidation, so the fibers doing endurance work run short of energy."],"why":"Type I fibers are slow, red, and rich in mitochondria and myoglobin. They resist fatigue because they generate ATP oxidatively, and during prolonged exercise much of that fuel is fatty acids. Small motor units with type I fibers are recruited first, so they carry most of the load in long, moderate work. When CPT II is missing, these fibers cannot use their main sustained fuel. Sprinting relies on type II fibers powered by phosphocreatine and glycolysis, which are unaffected.","whyNot":["Type I fibers are red because they are rich in myoglobin, and they have many mitochondria. This description is reversed.","Correct.","By the size principle, small motor units with type I fibers are recruited first. Type II units are added as force demands rise.","Type II fibers do store glycogen, but they use it for short, intense bursts and fatigue quickly. They do not power hours of steady work."],"study":{"topics":["Type I and type II muscle fibers","Motor unit recruitment"],"comps":["MS-7","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C43-Q4","stem":"His sprints are powered largely by anaerobic glycolysis, and the lactate produced is carried to the liver. Which of the following describes what the liver does with this lactate and the net energy cost of the cycle?","options":["Converts it to ketone bodies, with a net gain of 2 ATP","Converts it to glucose, with a net gain of 2 ATP per glucose","Converts it to alanine, with a net cost of 2 ATP","Converts it to glucose, with a net cost of 4 ATP per glucose"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.3","dok":2,"approach":["Name the cycle: muscle lactate goes to the liver, is made into glucose, and glucose returns to muscle. This is the Cori cycle.","Count ATP: glycolysis in muscle yields 2 ATP per glucose, and gluconeogenesis from 2 lactate uses 6 high-energy phosphates.","The difference is a net cost of 4 ATP, paid by the liver."],"why":"In the Cori cycle, lactate from anaerobic glycolysis travels to the liver, is oxidized to pyruvate, and is rebuilt into glucose by gluconeogenesis. The glucose returns to muscle. Muscle gains 2 ATP per glucose, but the liver spends 6 ATP equivalents to make one glucose from 2 lactate, so the cycle costs 4 ATP overall. The liver can afford this because it burns fatty acids for energy, and the cycle lets muscle keep working anaerobically.","whyNot":["Ketone bodies are made from acetyl-CoA during fat oxidation, not from lactate, and their synthesis does not yield ATP in this way.","Glucose is the right product, but the cycle costs energy. Gluconeogenesis uses more ATP than glycolysis yields.","Alanine carries amino groups from muscle to the liver in the glucose-alanine cycle. The liver converts alanine to glucose, not lactate to alanine.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cori cycle","Gluconeogenesis energy cost"],"comps":["MS-8"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C44","system":"MS","dx":"MERRF mitochondrial myopathy","codes":["MS-9"],"case":"A 17-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of 2 years of sudden jerking movements of his arms, unsteady gait, and fatigue and weakness with exertion. Six months ago he had a generalized tonic-clonic seizure. His mother has bilateral sensorineural hearing loss, and a maternal uncle had seizures and died at the age of 34 years. Physical examination shows multifocal myoclonus, gait ataxia, and mild proximal weakness. Serum lactate at rest is 4.8 mmol/L (reference 0.5 to 2.2). Gomori trichrome stain of a quadriceps biopsy shows ragged red fibers. Genetic testing of muscle shows the m.8344A>G variant in the mitochondrial tRNA-lysine gene in 85% of mitochondrial DNA copies. Diagnosis: myoclonic epilepsy with ragged red fibers (MERRF).","questions":[{"id":"MS-C44-Q1","stem":"His 20-year-old sister carries the same variant but has only mild exercise intolerance. Which of the following best explains the difference in severity between the siblings?","options":["Different proportions of mutant mitochondrial DNA in their tissues","Expansion of a trinucleotide repeat in the more severely affected child","Random inactivation of the X chromosome carrying the variant","Genomic imprinting that silences the maternal copy of the gene"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-9","obj":"MS-9.6","dok":2,"approach":["Note the pattern: the variant is in mitochondrial DNA and passes through the mother to all her children.","Each cell holds many mitochondrial genomes, some normal and some mutant. This mix is heteroplasmy.","The proportion of mutant copies varies between siblings and between tissues, and disease appears when it crosses a threshold."],"why":"Mitochondria come from the egg, so a mitochondrial DNA variant passes from a mother to all her children, while fathers do not transmit it. Each cell contains hundreds to thousands of mitochondrial genomes. When mutant and normal copies coexist (heteroplasmy), they are distributed randomly at cell division and into each egg. A sibling with a high mutant load in brain and muscle is severely affected, while one with a lower load may have mild or no symptoms. Ragged red fibers are muscle fibers packed with abnormal mitochondria that stain red under the sarcolemma on Gomori trichrome.","whyNot":["Correct.","Trinucleotide repeat expansion explains anticipation in disorders such as myotonic dystrophy and Huntington disease. It is a nuclear gene mechanism and does not apply to a mitochondrial point variant.","X inactivation explains variable expression in females with X-linked disorders. Mitochondrial genes are not on the X chromosome.","Imprinting silences one parental copy of certain nuclear genes, as in Prader-Willi and Angelman syndromes. Mitochondrial DNA is not imprinted."],"study":{"topics":["Mitochondrial inheritance and heteroplasmy","Ragged red fibers"],"comps":["MS-9"]}},{"id":"MS-C44-Q2","stem":"His muscle fibers produce too little ATP, which slows the cross-bridge cycle. In a normal cycle, which of the following events directly produces the power stroke?","options":["Binding of ATP to the myosin head","Hydrolysis of ATP by the myosin head","Release of inorganic phosphate from myosin","Detachment of the myosin head from the actin filament"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.2","dok":2,"approach":["List the cycle in order: ATP binds and myosin detaches, ATP is hydrolyzed and the head is cocked, the head binds actin, phosphate is released and the power stroke occurs, then ADP leaves.","Separate the step that stores energy (hydrolysis cocks the head) from the step that uses it (phosphate release).","The power stroke follows phosphate release."],"why":"Hydrolysis of ATP stores energy in the myosin head by cocking it into a high-energy position while ADP and phosphate remain bound. When the cocked head binds actin, release of inorganic phosphate triggers the power stroke, which pulls the thin filament toward the M line. ADP is released at the end of the stroke, leaving the head tightly bound until a new ATP arrives. Low ATP supply in mitochondrial myopathy limits how fast this cycle can repeat, causing early fatigue.","whyNot":["ATP binding releases the head from actin. It ends one cycle and does not generate force.","Hydrolysis cocks the head and stores energy, but the head is not yet bound to actin, so no force is produced.","Correct.","Detachment follows ATP binding and allows the head to reset. It does not move the thin filament."],"study":{"topics":["Cross-bridge cycle sequence"],"comps":["MS-7"]}},{"id":"MS-C44-Q3","stem":"A supervised endurance training program is recommended. In healthy skeletal muscle, which of the following adaptations does sustained endurance training most characteristically produce?","options":["Hypertrophy of type II fibers with more myofibrils","More mitochondria and capillaries in trained fibers","Greater phosphocreatine stores in type II muscle fibers","Conversion of type I fibers into type II fibers"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.6","dok":2,"approach":["Separate training types: endurance training improves oxidative capacity, and resistance training enlarges fibers.","Oxidative capacity depends on mitochondria and on oxygen delivery through capillaries.","Pick the option that increases both."],"why":"Endurance training increases the number and size of mitochondria, the amount of oxidative enzymes, and capillary density around muscle fibers. These changes raise oxidative capacity and fatigue resistance without much change in fiber size. Resistance training, by contrast, mainly causes hypertrophy of type II fibers by adding myofibrils. In mitochondrial myopathy, carefully supervised endurance training is used to build oxidative capacity in the fibers that remain.","whyNot":["Type II fiber hypertrophy is the main adaptation to resistance training, not endurance training.","Correct.","Phosphocreatine supports brief maximal efforts. Endurance training does not mainly work by enlarging this short-term store.","Training does not convert type I fibers to type II. Endurance training tends to shift fibers toward more oxidative properties."],"study":{"topics":["Training adaptations of muscle fibers"],"comps":["MS-7"]}},{"id":"MS-C44-Q4","stem":"Which of the following findings would most support a myopathic rather than a neurogenic cause of his weakness?","options":["Proximal weakness with elevated serum CK","Fasciculations in the tongue and thighs","Grouped atrophy of angular muscle fibers","Fiber type grouping on ATPase staining"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.4","dok":3,"approach":["Neurogenic disease damages the motor neuron or axon, so whole motor units lose their nerve supply.","Denervation leads to fasciculations, groups of atrophic fibers, and fiber type grouping after reinnervation.","Myopathic disease damages the fibers themselves, so weakness is proximal and the leaking fibers raise creatine kinase."],"why":"When a muscle fiber is damaged, creatine kinase leaks into the blood, and the large proximal muscles weaken first. This is the myopathic pattern, which fits a mitochondrial myopathy. Neurogenic disease, such as spinal muscular atrophy from SMN1 deletion, denervates whole motor units. Denervated fibers shrink in groups, surviving neurons reinnervate neighboring fibers and convert them to one type (fiber type grouping), and unstable motor units produce fasciculations.","whyNot":["Correct.","Fasciculations are spontaneous firing of whole motor units and point to lower motor neuron disease.","Grouped atrophy of small angular fibers means a whole motor unit lost its nerve. It is a neurogenic sign.","Fiber type grouping forms when surviving motor neurons reinnervate denervated fibers and convert them to their own type. It is a neurogenic sign."],"study":{"topics":["Neurogenic versus myopathic weakness"],"comps":["MS-10","MS-9"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C45","system":"MS","dx":"L4-L5 intervertebral disc herniation","codes":["MS-13-E"],"case":"A 44-year-old woman comes to the physician 3 days after she felt a sudden pain in her lower back while lifting a heavy box. The pain now radiates down the back of her left thigh and the lateral side of her leg to the top of her foot and big toe. Coughing worsens the pain. A straight-leg raise on the left reproduces the leg pain at 40 degrees. Neurologic examination shows weak extension of the left big toe and weak ankle dorsiflexion, with decreased sensation over the dorsum of the foot and the first web space. Patellar and Achilles reflexes are normal and symmetric. MRI of the lumbar spine shows a left posterolateral disc herniation at the L4-L5 level. Diagnosis: L4-L5 intervertebral disc herniation.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C45-Q1","stem":"Which of the following nerve roots is most likely compressed by this herniation?","options":["L5","L4","S1","L3"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-13-E","obj":"MS-13-E.9","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that a lumbar root exits below the pedicle of its own vertebra, so the L4 root leaves through the L4-L5 foramen above the disc.","A posterolateral herniation bulges into the lateral recess and hits the root traveling down to exit at the next level.","At L4-L5 that root is L5, which fits weak big toe extension, weak dorsiflexion, numbness of the foot dorsum, and normal reflexes."],"why":"With age, the nucleus pulposus dries out and the annulus fibrosus develops tears. The nucleus usually herniates posterolaterally, where the posterior longitudinal ligament is thinnest. In the lumbar spine, the root named for the upper vertebra has already exited above the disc, so a posterolateral L4-L5 herniation compresses the L5 root. L5 controls big toe extension and dorsiflexion and supplies skin on the dorsum of the foot, and it has no major reflex.","whyNot":["Correct.","L4 exits above the L4-L5 disc and is spared by a typical posterolateral herniation. Its compression would weaken knee extension and reduce the patellar reflex.","S1 is compressed by an L5-S1 herniation. It weakens plantarflexion, reduces the Achilles reflex, and causes numbness of the lateral foot.","L3 compression causes anterior thigh pain and weak knee extension and hip flexion. It would come from an L2-L3 herniation or a far lateral herniation at L3-L4."],"study":{"topics":["Lumbar disc herniation and root level","Spondylosis versus spondylolisthesis"],"comps":["MS-13-E","MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C45-Q2","stem":"If the herniation had occurred one level lower, at L5-S1, which reflex would most likely be reduced, and which afferent fibers carry that reflex?","options":["Patellar reflex, carried by Ia afferents from muscle spindles","Achilles reflex, carried by Ia afferents from muscle spindles","Achilles reflex, carried by Ib afferents from Golgi tendon organs","Patellar reflex, carried by Ib afferents from Golgi tendon organs"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.3","dok":3,"approach":["An L5-S1 posterolateral herniation compresses the S1 root.","Match reflexes to roots: patellar is L4, Achilles is S1.","A tendon tap stretches the muscle, which activates spindle Ia afferents, so the stretch reflex runs through Ia fibers."],"why":"Tapping the Achilles tendon briefly stretches the gastrocnemius and soleus. Muscle spindles sense the stretch and fire Ia afferents that synapse directly on alpha motor neurons, mostly at S1, causing plantarflexion. An S1 root lesion interrupts this arc and reduces the reflex. Golgi tendon organs sense tension, signal through Ib afferents, and inhibit the same muscle; they do not drive the stretch reflex.","whyNot":["The patellar reflex runs through L4 and would be affected by an L3-L4 lesion or a far lateral L4-L5 herniation, not an L5-S1 herniation.","Correct.","The level is right, but Golgi tendon organs and Ib afferents sense tension and inhibit the muscle. The tendon jerk is a spindle Ia reflex.","Both parts are wrong: the patellar reflex is L4, and tendon reflexes use spindle Ia afferents, not Ib fibers."],"study":{"topics":["Muscle spindle versus Golgi tendon organ","Reflex root levels"],"comps":["MS-10"]}},{"id":"MS-C45-Q3","stem":"Her physician must distinguish this L5 radiculopathy from a common fibular nerve injury at the fibular neck. Which of the following findings would best support the radiculopathy?","options":["Weakness of foot eversion against resistance","Weakness of ankle dorsiflexion and toe extension","Contralateral pelvic drop in single-leg stance","Reduced sensation over the dorsum of the foot"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-5","obj":"MS-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["Both lesions can cause foot drop, weak eversion, and numbness of the foot dorsum, so these do not separate them.","The L5 root also feeds nerves that the common fibular nerve does not carry, such as the superior gluteal nerve to gluteus medius and minimus.","Weak hip abduction shows as a Trendelenburg sign, with the pelvis dropping on the side opposite the weak muscle."],"why":"The common fibular nerve supplies the anterior and lateral leg compartments and the skin of the dorsum of the foot, so its injury causes foot drop, weak eversion, and dorsal foot numbness. An L5 root lesion causes the same findings but also weakens L5 muscles supplied by other nerves. The superior gluteal nerve (L4 to S1) innervates gluteus medius and minimus, which hold the pelvis level during single-leg stance. Weakness of these muscles lets the pelvis drop on the opposite side, which places the lesion above the fibular neck.","whyNot":["Weak eversion occurs with both lesions because the superficial fibular nerve supplies the fibularis muscles, which receive L5 and S1 fibers.","Weak dorsiflexion occurs with both lesions because the deep fibular nerve supplies tibialis anterior, which receives L4 and L5 fibers.","Correct.","Numbness of the foot dorsum occurs with both lesions because the superficial fibular nerve carries this L5 skin area."],"study":{"topics":["Lower limb nerve injuries","Root lesion versus peripheral nerve lesion"],"comps":["MS-5","MS-13-E"]}},{"id":"MS-C45-Q4","stem":"The herniated material passed through a radial tear in the annulus fibrosus. Which collagen types predominate in the outer annulus fibrosus and in the nucleus pulposus, respectively?","options":["Type I and type IV","Type II and type I","Type I and type II","Type III and type II"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["The outer annulus fibrosus is fibrocartilage built to resist tension, like tendon and ligament.","Tension-resisting tissues use type I collagen.","The nucleus pulposus is a hydrated gel that resists compression, like hyaline cartilage, and uses type II collagen."],"why":"The outer annulus fibrosus is made of concentric lamellae of fibrocartilage that resist tension, and its main collagen is type I, as in tendon, ligament, and bone. The nucleus pulposus is a gel rich in proteoglycans with a fine network of type II collagen, the type found in hyaline cartilage. As the disc ages, the nucleus loses proteoglycans and water, and the annulus develops tears through which the nucleus can herniate.","whyNot":["Type IV collagen forms basement membranes. It is not the main collagen of the nucleus pulposus.","This reverses the two tissues. Type II collagen is the main collagen of the nucleus, not the outer annulus.","Correct.","Type III collagen forms reticular fibers and is prominent in vessels and early granulation tissue. It is not the main collagen of the annulus."],"study":{"topics":["Collagen types by tissue","Intervertebral disc structure"],"comps":["MS-6","MS-13-E"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C46","system":"MS","dx":"Oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis with anterior uveitis","codes":["MS-13-B"],"case":"A 4-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of a 10-week history of swelling of her right knee and a limp that is worst in the morning. She has had no fever or rash. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F). Physical examination shows a warm right knee with a large effusion and a mild flexion contracture, and a swollen left ankle. No other joints are involved. Laboratory studies show a positive antinuclear antibody at a titer of 1:320, negative rheumatoid factor, a normal leukocyte count, and an erythrocyte sedimentation rate of 28 mm/h (reference 0 to 20). Slit-lamp examination shows inflammatory cells in the anterior chamber of the right eye, although the eye is not red or painful. Diagnosis: oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis with anterior uveitis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C46-Q1","stem":"Her parents ask why she needs slit-lamp eye examinations every few months for several years. Which of the following features of this condition best explains this recommendation?","options":["Uveitis occurs mainly in the systemic form of the disease","Uveitis usually begins only after the arthritis remits","Uveitis is usually painful but responds to topical lubricants","Uveitis is often silent until it damages vision"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B","obj":"MS-13-B.14","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the subtype: arthritis in 4 or fewer joints for more than 6 weeks in a young ANA-positive girl is oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis.","Recall that this subtype carries the highest risk of chronic anterior uveitis, especially when ANA is positive.","The uveitis usually causes no redness or pain, so only regular slit-lamp screening finds it before damage occurs."],"why":"Juvenile idiopathic arthritis is arthritis that starts before age 16 and lasts at least 6 weeks with no other cause. The oligoarticular form affects 4 or fewer joints, often the knee, and is most common in young girls. ANA positivity marks a high risk of chronic anterior uveitis, which is typically silent, with no redness or pain. Untreated, it can cause cataract, band keratopathy, glaucoma, and vision loss, so children are screened regularly with slit-lamp examination.","whyNot":["Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis presents with daily spiking fever and a salmon-colored rash, and uveitis is uncommon in that form. The oligoarticular ANA-positive form has the highest risk.","Uveitis can appear before, with, or after the arthritis, and it can stay active when the joints are quiet. Its timing does not depend on remission.","The uveitis of oligoarticular disease is usually painless, and it needs anti-inflammatory treatment, not lubricants.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Juvenile idiopathic arthritis subtypes","Uveitis screening in JIA"],"comps":["MS-13-B","MS-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C46-Q2","stem":"At age 9 years, she develops pain in front of her right ear when chewing and a click when opening her mouth. MRI shows anterior displacement of the articular disc of the right temporomandibular joint. Which of the following nerves carries most of the pain sensation from this joint?","options":["Chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve","Great auricular nerve from C2 and C3","Buccal branch of the facial nerve","Auriculotemporal branch of CN V3"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-4","obj":"MS-4.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the temporomandibular joint as a synovial joint with an articular disc that allows hinge and gliding motion.","Apply Hilton law: the nerve to the muscles that move a joint also supplies the joint. The muscles of mastication are supplied by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (CN V3).","The auriculotemporal and masseteric branches of CN V3 supply the joint capsule."],"why":"The temporomandibular joint lies between the mandibular condyle and the temporal bone, with an articular disc dividing it into upper (gliding) and lower (hinge) compartments. When the disc slips forward, the condyle snaps back under it during opening, causing a click and pain. Sensation from the joint comes mainly from the auriculotemporal nerve, with help from the masseteric nerve, both branches of CN V3. Juvenile idiopathic arthritis often involves this joint and can disturb mandibular growth.","whyNot":["The chorda tympani carries taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and parasympathetic fibers to the submandibular and sublingual glands. It does not supply the joint.","The great auricular nerve supplies skin over the parotid gland and earlobe, not the joint capsule.","The buccal branch of the facial nerve is motor to buccinator and muscles of the upper lip. It carries no joint sensation.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Temporomandibular joint anatomy","Hilton law"],"comps":["MS-4"]}},{"id":"MS-C46-Q3","stem":"Her 35-year-old mother has had 4 months of symmetric pain and swelling of the metacarpophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints of both hands, with 2 hours of morning stiffness. Which of the following antibodies is most specific for the mother's most likely disease?","options":["Anti-dsDNA antibody","Anti-CCP antibody","Anticentromere antibody","Anti-Jo-1 antibody"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.3","dok":2,"approach":["Symmetric small joint arthritis of the hands with long morning stiffness in an adult suggests rheumatoid arthritis.","Rheumatoid factor is sensitive but not specific.","Antibodies to cyclic citrullinated peptides are the most specific marker."],"why":"Rheumatoid arthritis typically involves the metacarpophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints symmetrically, with morning stiffness lasting more than an hour. Anti-CCP antibodies recognize citrullinated proteins and are highly specific for rheumatoid arthritis. They can appear years before symptoms and predict more erosive disease. Rheumatoid factor is also common in rheumatoid arthritis but appears in many other conditions.","whyNot":["Anti-dsDNA is specific for SLE, where arthritis is usually nonerosive and comes with rash, serositis, cytopenias, or nephritis.","Correct.","Anticentromere antibodies mark limited systemic sclerosis, with Raynaud phenomenon, sclerodactyly, and telangiectasias.","Anti-Jo-1 is a myositis antibody linked to interstitial lung disease, mechanic's hands, and arthritis in antisynthetase syndrome, with proximal muscle weakness."],"study":{"topics":["Autoantibodies in rheumatic disease"],"comps":["MS-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C46-Q4","stem":"Radiographs of the mother's hands show marginal erosions at the metacarpophalangeal joints. Which of the following mechanisms most directly produces these erosions?","options":["RANKL-driven osteoclasts at the edge of synovial pannus","Anti-dsDNA immune complexes in synovial vessels","Uric acid crystals activating the NLRP3 inflammasome","Mechanical wear of cartilage with subchondral sclerosis"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.1","dok":3,"approach":["In rheumatoid arthritis, CD4+ T cells reacting to citrullinated peptides (linked to HLA-DR4) drive synovial inflammation through TNF, IL-1, and IL-6.","The inflamed synovium grows into a pannus that spreads over cartilage and bone at the joint margins.","Synovial cells and T cells express RANKL, which activates osteoclasts that cut marginal erosions."],"why":"In rheumatoid arthritis, HLA-DR4 associated CD4+ T cells respond to citrullinated proteins and activate macrophages and B cells. Cytokines such as TNF, IL-1, and IL-6 cause synovial hyperplasia and inflammation, forming a pannus of proliferating synovium and granulation tissue. Pannus releases enzymes that destroy cartilage, and RANKL from synovial fibroblasts and T cells activates osteoclasts. Bone is eroded first at the joint margins, where synovium touches bone not covered by cartilage.","whyNot":["Correct.","Immune complexes cause the arthritis of SLE, which is typically nonerosive. Anti-dsDNA is not part of rheumatoid arthritis.","Uric acid crystals activate the NLRP3 inflammasome in gout. Gout erosions have overhanging edges and occur near tophi, often in the first metatarsophalangeal joint.","Mechanical wear with subchondral sclerosis and osteophytes describes osteoarthritis, which favors the distal interphalangeal joints and is not erosive in this way."],"study":{"topics":["Rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis","Pannus and RANKL"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-8"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C47","system":"MS","dx":"Lyme arthritis of the knee","codes":["MS-12"],"case":"An 11-year-old girl in Connecticut is brought to the physician because of intermittent swelling of her left knee over the past 2 months. Each episode lasts about a week, and the knee is only mildly painful. She can walk normally. Last summer she spent several weeks at a wooded camp in Wisconsin and had an expanding red rash on her thigh that faded without treatment. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 88/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Examination shows a large, mildly warm effusion of the left knee with full range of motion. Other joints are normal. Serum enzyme immunoassay for Borrelia burgdorferi is positive and confirmed by IgG immunoblot. Diagnosis: Lyme arthritis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C47-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes how she most likely acquired this infection?","options":["Bite of an adult Dermacentor tick attached for a few minutes","Bite of a larval Ixodes tick that hatched already infected","Bite of a body louse, with its feces rubbed into the skin","Bite of a nymphal Ixodes tick attached for 36 hours or more"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-12","obj":"MS-12.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism: Borrelia burgdorferi, a spirochete.","Recall the cycle: Ixodes larvae hatch uninfected and pick up the organism from white-footed mice, then molt into nymphs.","Nymphs are small, are active in late spring and summer, and must stay attached for about 36 hours or more before the spirochete is transmitted."],"why":"Borrelia burgdorferi is maintained in white-footed mice. Ixodes scapularis larvae hatch uninfected because the spirochete is rarely passed through the egg; they acquire it when feeding on infected mice. After molting, the nymph transmits the spirochete during its next blood meal. Spirochetes multiply in the tick gut and move to the salivary glands over a day or more, so transmission usually needs about 36 hours or more of attachment. Nymphs are tiny and often unnoticed, which is why many patients do not recall a bite.","whyNot":["Dermacentor ticks transmit Rocky Mountain spotted fever (Rickettsia rickettsii), not Lyme disease, and a few minutes of attachment is too short for Borrelia.","Ixodes larvae hatch uninfected because transovarial transmission of B. burgdorferi is rare. They acquire the spirochete by feeding on infected mice.","Body lice transmit Borrelia recurrentis (louse-borne relapsing fever) when crushed lice are rubbed into the skin, and also epidemic typhus. They do not transmit Lyme disease.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Borrelia burgdorferi transmission cycle","Ixodes life cycle"],"comps":["MS-12"]}},{"id":"MS-C47-Q2","stem":"Arthrocentesis of the left knee yields cloudy yellow fluid. Analysis shows a leukocyte count of 24,000/mm3 (normal under 200/mm3) with 75% neutrophils, a negative Gram stain, and no crystals on polarized light microscopy. Which of the following best classifies this fluid?","options":["Septic, as in staphylococcal joint infection","Inflammatory, as in rheumatoid arthritis","Hemorrhagic, as in traumatic hemarthrosis","Noninflammatory, as in osteoarthritis"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-G","obj":"MS-13-G.2","dok":3,"approach":["Use leukocyte count cutoffs: under 2,000/mm3 is noninflammatory, 2,000 to about 50,000/mm3 is inflammatory, and septic fluid is usually over 50,000/mm3 with mostly neutrophils.","Her count of 24,000/mm3 falls in the inflammatory range, and the Gram stain is negative.","No crystals means this is not gout or pseudogout. Inflammatory fluid fits Lyme arthritis."],"why":"Synovial fluid is grouped by leukocyte count. Noninflammatory fluid (under 2,000/mm3) is seen in osteoarthritis and trauma. Inflammatory fluid (about 2,000 to 50,000/mm3) is seen in rheumatoid arthritis, crystal arthritis, reactive arthritis, and Lyme arthritis, which often has counts of 10,000 to 50,000/mm3. Septic fluid usually exceeds 50,000/mm3 with over 90% neutrophils and often a positive Gram stain or culture. The ranges overlap, so culture, crystal search, and the clinical picture are always used together.","whyNot":["Septic fluid usually has more than 50,000 leukocytes/mm3 with over 90% neutrophils, and bacteria are often seen on Gram stain. Her count is lower, the stain is negative, and she is nearly afebrile.","Correct.","Hemorrhagic fluid is bloody and follows trauma or a bleeding disorder. Her fluid is cloudy yellow, not bloody.","Noninflammatory fluid has fewer than 2,000 leukocytes/mm3 and is clear. Her count is far higher."],"study":{"topics":["Synovial fluid analysis","Approach to the hot swollen joint"],"comps":["MS-13-G","MS-12"]}},{"id":"MS-C47-Q3","stem":"Before the effusion was linked to Lyme disease, she was examined for a soccer injury of the knee. Which of the following findings would have indicated a tear of the anterior cruciate ligament?","options":["Lateral joint opening with varus stress at 30 degrees of flexion","Posterior tibial translation with the knee flexed 90 degrees","Anterior tibial translation with the knee flexed 20 to 30 degrees","Medial joint line opening with valgus stress at 30 degrees of flexion"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-4","obj":"MS-4.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the ACL runs from the anterior tibia to the lateral femoral condyle and stops the tibia from sliding forward on the femur.","A torn ACL lets the tibia translate anteriorly, which the Lachman test (20 to 30 degrees of flexion) and the anterior drawer test (90 degrees) detect.","Match the other tests: valgus stress for MCL, varus stress for LCL, posterior drawer for PCL."],"why":"The anterior cruciate ligament attaches to the anterior intercondylar area of the tibia and the medial side of the lateral femoral condyle. It resists anterior translation of the tibia on the femur. The Lachman test, done at 20 to 30 degrees of flexion, is the most sensitive bedside test for an ACL tear, and the anterior drawer test at 90 degrees checks the same motion. The MCL is fused to the medial meniscus, which is why the two are often injured together.","whyNot":["Lateral opening with varus stress tests the lateral collateral ligament, which resists varus force.","Posterior translation at 90 degrees is a positive posterior drawer test, indicating a PCL tear.","Correct.","Medial opening with valgus stress tests the medial collateral ligament, which resists valgus force."],"study":{"topics":["Knee ligament tests","Knee ligaments and menisci"],"comps":["MS-4"]}},{"id":"MS-C47-Q4","stem":"Two months later, her 34-year-old mother develops symmetric swelling and stiffness of the small joints of both hands. One week earlier, the mother's 5-year-old son had bright red cheeks followed by a lacy rash on his arms. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the mother's arthritis?","options":["Parvovirus B19 associated arthritis","Early Lyme arthritis from a tick bite","Disseminated gonococcal arthritis","HLA-B27 associated reactive arthritis"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.7","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the child's illness: slapped-cheek rash followed by a lacy rash is erythema infectiosum, caused by parvovirus B19.","Recall that adults, especially women, infected with parvovirus B19 often develop symmetric small joint arthritis.","Distinguish it from reactive arthritis and Lyme arthritis, which are asymmetric and favor large joints of the legs."],"why":"Parvovirus B19 causes erythema infectiosum in children. Adults, especially women, often have little or no rash but develop acute symmetric polyarthritis of the hands, wrists, and knees that resembles rheumatoid arthritis. The arthritis is immune-mediated and usually resolves within weeks. Rubella infection and, less often, rubella vaccination can cause a similar transient arthritis or arthralgia in adult women.","whyNot":["Correct.","Lyme arthritis is a late finding that affects one or a few large joints, mainly the knee. It does not cause symmetric small joint arthritis after a child's rash.","Disseminated gonococcal infection causes migratory arthralgia, tenosynovitis, and pustular skin lesions, or a purulent monoarthritis. It does not follow a child's rash.","Reactive arthritis follows enteric or chlamydial infection, is linked to HLA-B27, and is usually an asymmetric arthritis of the large joints of the legs."],"study":{"topics":["Postinfectious arthritis","Parvovirus B19 in adults"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-12"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C48","system":"MS","dx":"Adhesive capsulitis with Dupuytren contracture in diabetes","codes":["MS-13-B"],"case":"A 56-year-old woman with a 12-year history of type 2 diabetes mellitus comes to the physician because of a 5-month history of pain and progressive stiffness of the right shoulder. She can no longer reach behind her back or brush her hair. She has had no injury. Her hemoglobin A1c is 8.9% (reference under 5.7). Examination shows marked loss of both active and passive external rotation and abduction of the right shoulder; strength is normal within the available range. In the left palm there is a firm nodule with a cord extending to the ring finger, which cannot be fully extended at the metacarpophalangeal joint. Radiographs of the right shoulder are normal. Diagnosis: adhesive capsulitis of the right shoulder and Dupuytren contracture of the left hand.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C48-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her shoulder cannot be moved passively by the examiner?","options":["Full-thickness tear of the supraspinatus tendon","Denervation of the deltoid by axillary nerve injury","Calcium pyrophosphate deposits in the labrum","Inflammation and fibrosis of the joint capsule"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B","obj":"MS-13-B.16","dok":3,"approach":["Compare active and passive motion: muscle, tendon, or nerve problems reduce active motion, but the examiner can still move the arm passively.","When passive motion is also lost, something inside the joint or capsule is mechanically blocking movement.","With normal radiographs and diabetes, the best fit is a thickened, contracted capsule."],"why":"Adhesive capsulitis is inflammation followed by fibrosis and contraction of the glenohumeral joint capsule. The capsule shrinks and loses its folds, so both active and passive motion are limited, especially external rotation and abduction. Radiographs are normal, which separates it from arthritis. Diabetes is a major risk factor, and the condition often coexists with other fibrosing hand disorders such as Dupuytren contracture.","whyNot":["A rotator cuff tear weakens active abduction, but the examiner can usually move the arm passively through a full range.","Axillary nerve injury weakens deltoid abduction but does not stop passive movement. Her strength is also normal.","Calcium pyrophosphate deposition causes acute pseudogout or chronic arthropathy, with chondrocalcinosis often visible on radiographs. Her radiographs are normal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Adhesive capsulitis","Active versus passive range of motion"],"comps":["MS-13-B","MS-4"]}},{"id":"MS-C48-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains the contracture of her left ring finger?","options":["Synovial inflammation of the flexor tendon sheaths","Myofibroblast proliferation in the palmar fascia","Fibrosis of the metacarpophalangeal joint capsule","Thickening of the A1 pulley over the flexor tendons"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-E","obj":"MS-13-E.11","dok":2,"approach":["Find the location of the lesion: a palpable nodule and cord in the palm, superficial to the tendons.","That tissue is the palmar aponeurosis.","Contractile myofibroblasts in this fascia form the nodules and cords that pull the ring and little fingers into flexion."],"why":"Dupuytren contracture is a fibroproliferative disorder of the palmar fascia. Myofibroblasts, which contain smooth muscle actin, form nodules and then cords of dense collagen that shorten over time. The cords pull the ring and little fingers into flexion at the metacarpophalangeal and then the proximal interphalangeal joints. Risk factors include diabetes, family history, alcohol use, and smoking.","whyNot":["Flexor tenosynovitis causes pain and swelling along the tendon sheath, as in rheumatoid arthritis or infection, without a fascial cord in the palm.","Correct.","Capsular fibrosis of the joint itself is not the source of a palpable palmar cord.","Thickening of the A1 pulley causes trigger finger, with catching or locking during finger flexion rather than a fixed palmar cord."],"study":{"topics":["Dupuytren contracture","Hand disorders in diabetes"],"comps":["MS-13-E","MS-13-B"]}},{"id":"MS-C48-Q3","stem":"Two years later, she falls on her outstretched, abducted, externally rotated right arm and sustains an anterior dislocation of the glenohumeral joint. After reduction, which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Winging of the scapula with weak protraction of the arm","Wrist drop with numbness over the dorsum of the hand","Weak abduction with numbness of the lateral shoulder","Weak elbow flexion with numbness of the lateral forearm"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-4","obj":"MS-4.3","dok":3,"approach":["The glenohumeral joint has a shallow glenoid and depends on the rotator cuff and capsule for stability, so it dislocates easily, usually anteriorly and inferiorly.","The displaced humeral head stretches the axillary nerve as it winds around the surgical neck.","Predict loss of deltoid function (abduction from about 15 to 90 degrees) and numbness over the lateral shoulder."],"why":"The glenoid fossa is shallow and covers only part of the humeral head, so stability comes mainly from the rotator cuff muscles and the capsule and labrum. The anterior-inferior capsule is weakest, so most dislocations are anterior. The axillary nerve passes through the quadrangular space and around the surgical neck of the humerus, where the displaced head can stretch it. Injury weakens the deltoid and teres minor and causes numbness over the lateral shoulder (the regimental badge area). Supraspinatus can still start abduction for the first 15 degrees.","whyNot":["Scapular winging comes from long thoracic nerve injury, usually after surgery or trauma along the chest wall, not from anterior dislocation.","Wrist drop comes from radial nerve injury, typically at the spiral groove with a midshaft humeral fracture.","Correct.","Weak elbow flexion with lateral forearm numbness comes from musculocutaneous nerve injury, which is much less common with dislocation than axillary nerve injury."],"study":{"topics":["Glenohumeral stability","Axillary nerve injury"],"comps":["MS-4","MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C48-Q4","stem":"The myofibroblasts in her palmar cords express alpha-smooth muscle actin and contract in a way that resembles smooth muscle. In smooth muscle, which of the following events directly allows myosin to begin cross-bridge cycling?","options":["Rise in cGMP after nitric oxide enters the cell","Dephosphorylation of myosin light chains by a phosphatase","Phosphorylation of myosin light chains by their kinase","Binding of calcium to troponin C on thin filaments"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that smooth muscle has no troponin, so regulation is on the myosin side.","Calcium binds calmodulin, and calcium-calmodulin activates myosin light chain kinase.","Phosphorylated myosin light chains allow cross-bridge cycling. The phosphatase reverses this and relaxes the cell."],"why":"In smooth muscle, a rise in cytosolic calcium leads to calcium binding calmodulin. The calcium-calmodulin complex activates myosin light chain kinase, which phosphorylates the regulatory light chains of myosin and allows cross-bridges to form and cycle. Myosin light chain phosphatase removes the phosphate and causes relaxation. If myosin is dephosphorylated while still attached, the cross-bridges cycle slowly and hold tension at low energy cost, which is the latch state.","whyNot":["Nitric oxide raises cGMP, which activates protein kinase G and favors relaxation by lowering calcium and activating the phosphatase.","Myosin light chain phosphatase removes the phosphate and causes relaxation, not contraction.","Correct.","Troponin C regulates skeletal and cardiac muscle. Smooth muscle lacks troponin."],"study":{"topics":["Smooth muscle contraction and relaxation","Myosin light chain kinase"],"comps":["MS-7"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C49","system":"MS","dx":"Renal osteodystrophy in chronic kidney disease","codes":["MS-13-C"],"case":"A 67-year-old man with chronic kidney disease from diabetic nephropathy comes to the physician because of aching pain in his hips, lower back, and ribs for 6 months. He also has difficulty rising from a chair. He is not yet on dialysis. His estimated glomerular filtration rate is 14 mL/min/1.73 m2 (reference over 90). Serum studies show calcium 8.1 mg/dL (reference 8.5 to 10.5), phosphate 6.8 mg/dL (2.5 to 4.5), parathyroid hormone 640 pg/mL (10 to 65), alkaline phosphatase 310 U/L (40 to 130), 25-hydroxyvitamin D 34 ng/mL (30 to 100), and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D 9 pg/mL (18 to 72). Radiographs of the hands show subperiosteal bone resorption along the radial sides of the middle phalanges. Diagnosis: renal osteodystrophy with secondary hyperparathyroidism.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C49-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains his elevated parathyroid hormone concentration?","options":["Phosphate retention and calcitriol deficiency","Excess calcitriol from extrarenal hydroxylase","Autonomous secretion by a parathyroid adenoma","Ectopic PTH-related peptide from a carcinoma"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-13-C","obj":"MS-13-C.4","dok":3,"approach":["Look at the direction of each value: calcium is low, phosphate is high, PTH is high, and calcitriol is low.","High PTH with low calcium means the parathyroids are responding appropriately, which is secondary hyperparathyroidism.","Failing kidneys retain phosphate and make less calcitriol, and both lower serum calcium and stimulate PTH."],"why":"As kidney function falls, phosphate excretion drops and serum phosphate rises. High phosphate binds calcium and also stimulates the parathyroids directly. The diseased kidney makes less calcitriol, so intestinal calcium absorption falls, and FGF23 from osteocytes rises and further suppresses calcitriol. Low calcium, high phosphate, and low calcitriol all drive PTH up. Chronic high PTH increases bone turnover and resorption, causing osteitis fibrosa cystica with subperiosteal resorption, while low calcitriol impairs mineralization.","whyNot":["Correct.","Extrarenal calcitriol production, as in sarcoidosis, raises calcium and suppresses PTH. His calcitriol is low.","A parathyroid adenoma causes primary hyperparathyroidism, with high calcium and low or normal phosphate. His calcium is low and phosphate is high.","PTH-related peptide causes hypercalcemia and suppresses native PTH. His PTH is high and his calcium is low."],"study":{"topics":["Renal osteodystrophy","Secondary hyperparathyroidism"],"comps":["MS-13-C","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C49-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes how parathyroid hormone increases osteoclast activity in his skeleton?","options":["Releases calcitonin, which stimulates osteoclast resorption","Raises osteoprotegerin secretion, which activates osteoclasts","Binds receptors on osteoclasts to directly increase resorption","Induces osteoblast RANKL, activating osteoclast precursors"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that PTH receptors are on osteoblasts and osteocytes, not osteoclasts.","PTH makes osteoblasts express RANKL and less osteoprotegerin.","RANKL binds RANK on osteoclast precursors and drives their maturation and activity, so PTH acts on osteoclasts indirectly."],"why":"Osteoclasts do not have PTH receptors. PTH binds receptors on osteoblasts and osteocytes, which then increase RANKL and decrease osteoprotegerin. RANKL binds RANK on osteoclast precursors and, with M-CSF, drives them to fuse and resorb bone. Osteoprotegerin is a decoy receptor that binds RANKL and limits this process. PTH also increases calcium reabsorption in the distal tubule, increases phosphate excretion, and activates renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase, although the last effect fails in advanced kidney disease.","whyNot":["Calcitonin from thyroid C cells inhibits osteoclasts. PTH does not cause calcitonin release.","Osteoprotegerin is a decoy receptor that blocks RANKL and inhibits osteoclasts. PTH lowers it.","This is a common misconception. Osteoclasts lack PTH receptors, so the effect is indirect through osteoblasts.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["PTH action on bone","RANKL and osteoprotegerin"],"comps":["MS-8","MS-2"]}},{"id":"MS-C49-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains why his 25-hydroxyvitamin D concentration is normal while his 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D concentration is low?","options":["Reduced 1-alpha-hydroxylation in the kidney","Reduced intestinal absorption of dietary vitamin D","Reduced 25-hydroxylation of vitamin D in the liver","Reduced synthesis of vitamin D3 in sun-exposed skin"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.1","dok":2,"approach":["Trace activation: 7-dehydrocholesterol in skin becomes vitamin D3 with UVB light, the liver adds a 25-hydroxyl group, and the kidney adds the 1-alpha-hydroxyl group.","A normal 25-hydroxyvitamin D means skin synthesis, intake, and liver conversion are adequate.","The block must be at the final renal step."],"why":"Vitamin D3 is made in the skin when UVB converts 7-dehydrocholesterol, or it comes from the diet. The liver converts it to 25-hydroxyvitamin D, the main circulating form, whose level reflects total vitamin D supply. The proximal tubule then adds a 1-alpha-hydroxyl group to form active calcitriol. In chronic kidney disease, loss of renal mass and high FGF23 reduce 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity, so calcitriol falls even when 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal.","whyNot":["Correct.","Poor intestinal absorption, as in fat malabsorption, reduces supply and would lower 25-hydroxyvitamin D.","Reduced hepatic 25-hydroxylation, as in severe liver disease, would lower 25-hydroxyvitamin D.","Reduced skin synthesis lowers the supply of vitamin D and would lower 25-hydroxyvitamin D, which is normal here."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin D activation","Vitamin D status testing"],"comps":["MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C49-Q4","stem":"His serum alkaline phosphatase is elevated because bone turnover is high. Which of the following best describes the bone cells that are the main source of this enzyme?","options":["Multinucleated cells of monocyte and macrophage origin","Mesenchymal-derived cells that secrete osteoid","Mechanosensing cells within mineralized lacunae","Hypertrophic chondrocytes within a growth plate"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that bone alkaline phosphatase is a marker of bone formation.","Bone formation is carried out by osteoblasts, which come from mesenchymal cells and secrete type I collagen osteoid.","In high-turnover bone disease, increased resorption is coupled with increased osteoblast activity, so alkaline phosphatase rises."],"why":"Osteoblasts arise from mesenchymal stem cells. They secrete type I collagen osteoid and alkaline phosphatase, which helps mineralize the matrix by raising local phosphate. In secondary hyperparathyroidism, resorption and formation both speed up, so osteoblast activity and serum alkaline phosphatase rise. Osteoclasts, the multinucleated resorbing cells, come from the monocyte and macrophage lineage and use acid and cathepsin K in Howship lacunae.","whyNot":["This describes osteoclasts, which resorb bone with acid and cathepsin K. They are a source of tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase, not alkaline phosphatase.","Correct.","This describes osteocytes, which sense mechanical load and secrete FGF23 and sclerostin. They are not the main source of serum alkaline phosphatase.","Growth plates are closed in adults. Hypertrophic chondrocytes are found only in growing bones."],"study":{"topics":["Bone cell types","Markers of bone turnover"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-8"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C50","system":"MS","dx":"Slipped capital femoral epiphysis presenting as knee pain","codes":["MS-13-E"],"case":"A 13-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of a 6-week history of dull pain in his left knee and a limp. The pain is worse after sports. He recalls no injury. His body mass index is at the 98th percentile for age and sex. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F). Examination of the left knee shows no swelling, tenderness, or instability. Passive flexion of the left hip produces obligatory external rotation, and internal rotation of the hip is markedly limited and painful. Anteroposterior and frog-leg lateral radiographs of the pelvis show posterior and inferior displacement of the left femoral head relative to the femoral neck. Diagnosis: slipped capital femoral epiphysis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C50-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings in this patient best distinguishes his disorder from Legg-Calve-Perthes disease?","options":["Limited and painful internal rotation of the hip","Displacement of the femoral head at the physis","Hip disease presenting as pain in the knee","Limp without fever or a history of injury"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-E","obj":"MS-13-E.8","dok":3,"approach":["List what the two disorders share: a limp, hip or knee pain, loss of internal rotation, and no fever.","Recall what differs: Legg-Calve-Perthes disease is idiopathic osteonecrosis of the femoral head in children about 4 to 8 years old, while this disorder is a slip of the epiphysis through the physis in an obese adolescent.","Pick the finding that reflects the slip itself: the femoral head displaced on the neck at the growth plate."],"why":"In slipped capital femoral epiphysis, the femoral neck moves anteriorly and superiorly while the epiphysis stays in the acetabulum, so the head looks displaced posteriorly and inferiorly on radiographs. The slip happens through the weak hypertrophic zone of the physis during the growth spurt, and obesity adds shear force. Legg-Calve-Perthes disease is idiopathic osteonecrosis of the femoral head, usually at about 4 to 8 years of age, and radiographs show a flattened or fragmented head that has not slipped. Developmental dysplasia of the hip is found in newborns with the Ortolani and Barlow maneuvers.","whyNot":["Loss of internal rotation is a sign of hip joint disease in general. It is also seen in Legg-Calve-Perthes disease, so it does not separate the two.","Correct.","Both disorders can present as knee pain, because nerves to the hip also supply the knee. It does not separate the two.","Both disorders usually cause a limp without fever or a clear injury. Fever with refusal to bear weight would instead suggest septic arthritis."],"study":{"topics":["Pediatric hip disorders by age","Slipped capital femoral epiphysis versus Legg-Calve-Perthes disease"],"comps":["MS-13-E"]}},{"id":"MS-C50-Q2","stem":"Before hip radiographs were obtained, his knee pain was thought to come from a common adolescent knee disorder. Which of the following findings would have instead supported Osgood-Schlatter disease?","options":["Tender swelling over the tibial tubercle","Knee locking with a loose fragment from the femoral condyle","Hip pain with limited internal rotation of the hip","Anterior knee pain worsened by sitting with the knees bent"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-E","obj":"MS-13-E.10","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that Osgood-Schlatter disease is a traction apophysitis where the patellar tendon pulls on the tibial tubercle apophysis.","It is common in active adolescents during growth spurts.","The key finding is a tender, sometimes enlarged, tibial tubercle."],"why":"In Osgood-Schlatter disease, repeated pull of the quadriceps through the patellar tendon irritates the tibial tubercle apophysis in growing adolescents. Running and jumping worsen the pain, and the tubercle is tender and often prominent. Chondromalacia patellae is softening of patellar cartilage with anterior knee pain, often worse after long sitting. Osteochondritis dissecans is separation of a fragment of subchondral bone and cartilage, often from the medial femoral condyle, which can cause locking.","whyNot":["Correct.","Locking with a loose fragment suggests osteochondritis dissecans, which separates subchondral bone and cartilage.","Limited hip internal rotation points to a hip disorder such as this patient's, not to Osgood-Schlatter disease.","Anterior knee pain after long sitting with bent knees suggests chondromalacia patellae, which involves the patellar cartilage, not the tibial tubercle."],"study":{"topics":["Adolescent knee disorders"],"comps":["MS-13-E","MS-4"]}},{"id":"MS-C50-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains why disease of his hip was felt as pain in the knee?","options":["The superior and inferior gluteal nerves supply both joints","The lateral femoral cutaneous and saphenous nerves supply both joints","The pudendal and genitofemoral nerves supply both joints","The obturator and femoral nerves supply both joints"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-4","obj":"MS-4.2","dok":2,"approach":["Apply Hilton law: the nerves that supply the muscles moving a joint also supply that joint.","The femoral and obturator nerves send branches to the hip capsule and also to the knee.","Pain from the hip carried in these nerves can be perceived in the knee, their shared territory."],"why":"Hilton law states that a nerve supplying the muscles acting on a joint also supplies the joint and the skin over it. The hip capsule receives branches from the femoral and obturator nerves, and both nerves also send articular branches to the knee. Because the brain cannot always tell which end of the nerve's territory the signal came from, hip pain is often felt in the thigh or knee. This is why a child or adolescent with knee pain and a normal knee exam needs a hip exam.","whyNot":["The gluteal nerves supply the gluteal muscles and do not supply the knee joint.","These are cutaneous nerves to the skin of the thigh and medial leg. They do not carry pain from the hip joint.","The pudendal nerve supplies the perineum, and the genitofemoral nerve supplies the cremaster and skin of the upper anterior thigh. Neither supplies both joints.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hilton law","Referred pain from the hip"],"comps":["MS-4","MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C50-Q4","stem":"After surgical fixation, he begins physical therapy with active knee extension exercises. As the quadriceps shortens, which of the following keeps its muscle spindles able to detect further stretch?","options":["Ib afferent firing from Golgi tendon organs","Renshaw cell inhibition of alpha motor neurons","Gamma motor neuron activation of intrafusal fibers","Alpha motor neuron activation of extrafusal fibers"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.3","dok":2,"approach":["Muscle spindles lie parallel to extrafusal fibers and sense stretch through Ia afferents.","When the muscle shortens, the spindle would go slack and stop firing.","Gamma motor neurons contract the ends of intrafusal fibers, taking up the slack so the spindle stays sensitive."],"why":"Muscle spindles are made of intrafusal fibers lying in parallel with the main extrafusal fibers. When the muscle shortens, the spindle would go slack and its Ia afferents would fall silent. Gamma motor neurons fire along with alpha motor neurons (alpha-gamma coactivation), contracting the ends of the intrafusal fibers and keeping the central sensory region taut. This keeps the spindle able to signal length changes during movement.","whyNot":["Golgi tendon organs lie in series with the muscle and sense tension through Ib afferents. They do not reset spindle sensitivity.","Renshaw cells provide recurrent inhibition of alpha motor neurons. They do not act on intrafusal fibers.","Correct.","Alpha motor neurons contract the extrafusal fibers, which shortens the muscle and unloads the spindle rather than keeping it taut."],"study":{"topics":["Muscle spindle and gamma motor neurons"],"comps":["MS-10"]}}]}];
window.NPLEX_DIAGNOSTIC = [{"id":"DX-CV-C01","system":"CV","dx":"Acute anterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction from left anterior descending artery occlusion","codes":["CV-18-C3","CV-8"],"case":"A 61-year-old man with hypertension and a 40 pack-year smoking history comes to the emergency department 4 hours after crushing substernal chest pain began. The pain radiates to his left arm, and he is diaphoretic. Pulse is 102/min, and blood pressure is 104/68 mm Hg. The ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads V1 to V4. Serum troponin I is 8.2 ng/mL (reference below 0.04 ng/mL). Echocardiography shows no motion of the anterior wall, anterior septum, and apex of the left ventricle, with an ejection fraction of 40% (normal 55 to 70%). Diagnosis: acute anterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI).","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C01-Q1","from":[4],"stem":"Occlusion of which of the following arteries most likely explains the ST-segment elevation in leads V1 to V4 and the loss of anterior wall, septal, and apical motion?","options":["Left anterior descending artery","Right coronary artery","Left circumflex artery","Posterior interventricular artery"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-8","obj":"CV-8.1","dok":2,"approach":["Match the leads to the wall: V1 and V2 face the septum, and V3 and V4 face the anterior wall.","Recall which artery runs down the anterior interventricular groove to the apex.","That artery, the LAD, supplies the anterior left ventricle, the apex, and the anterior two-thirds of the septum."],"why":"The left anterior descending artery is a branch of the left main coronary artery that runs in the anterior interventricular groove toward the apex. It supplies the anterior wall of the left ventricle, the apex, and the anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum through its septal branches. ST elevation in V1 to V4 with anterior and apical wall motion loss is the classic pattern of LAD occlusion, the most common site of coronary occlusion in MI.","whyNot":["Correct.","The right coronary artery supplies the right ventricle and, in most people, the inferior wall, the SA node, and the AV node. Its occlusion causes ST elevation in the inferior leads II, III, and aVF.","The left circumflex artery runs in the left atrioventricular groove and supplies the lateral and posterior left ventricle. Its occlusion shows up in the lateral leads I, aVL, V5, and V6.","The posterior interventricular (posterior descending) artery usually arises from the right coronary artery and supplies the inferior wall and the posterior third of the septum, which leads V1 to V4 do not face."],"study":{"topics":["Coronary artery territories","ECG leads and the walls they face"],"comps":["CV-8","CV-7","CV-18-C3"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C01-Q2","from":[17],"stem":"Troponin I is a structural protein of the myocyte contractile apparatus. Which of the following processes is most likely responsible for its increased serum concentration in this patient?","options":["Caspase-driven apoptosis of myocytes in the ischemic zone","Hepatic synthesis of troponin as an acute phase reactant","Exocytosis of troponin by viable myocytes under stress","Membrane rupture in myocytes that have died by necrosis"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C3","obj":"CV-18-C.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note that troponin normally sits inside the myocyte, bound to the thin filaments.","Ask what lets an intracellular protein reach the blood: loss of plasma membrane integrity.","Membrane breakdown is the hallmark of irreversible injury and necrosis."],"why":"After about 20 to 40 minutes of severe ischemia, myocyte injury becomes irreversible. ATP loss, calcium influx, and activation of phospholipases and proteases damage the plasma membrane, so the cell contents leak out. Troponin I and troponin T are cardiac-specific, so their leakage into blood marks myocyte necrosis. Troponin begins to rise within about 2 to 4 hours and stays elevated for about 7 to 10 days.","whyNot":["Apoptosis keeps the plasma membrane intact, and the cell fragments are eaten by macrophages without spilling their contents. The cell death of an infarct is mainly coagulative necrosis.","The liver does not make cardiac troponin. Acute phase reactants made by the liver include C-reactive protein and fibrinogen.","Myocytes do not package troponin into vesicles for secretion. A large rise like his reflects leakage from dead cells.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Reversible and irreversible cell injury","Cardiac biomarkers after MI"],"comps":["CV-18-C3","CV-5"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C01-Q3","from":[26],"stem":"Two days after the onset of pain, he dies of ventricular fibrillation. Which of the following microscopic findings is most likely in the infarcted anterior wall at autopsy?","options":["Granulation tissue with new capillaries and fibroblasts","Dense collagen scar replacing the lost muscle fibers","Macrophages clearing dead myocytes at the infarct border","Coagulative necrosis with a dense neutrophil infiltrate"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C3","obj":"CV-18-C.5","dok":3,"approach":["Note the timing: about 48 hours after the infarct began.","Recall the Robbins sequence: neutrophils peak at about 1 to 3 days, macrophages at about 3 to 7 days, granulation tissue at about 7 to 14 days, then scar.","At 2 days, expect dead myocytes with preserved outlines and many neutrophils."],"why":"Myocardial infarcts undergo coagulative necrosis: dead myocytes keep their outlines while their nuclei fade. Neutrophils are drawn into the dead tissue and peak at about 1 to 3 days. Macrophages take over at about 3 to 7 days, granulation tissue forms at about 7 to 14 days, and a collagen scar matures over the following weeks. At 2 days the expected picture is coagulative necrosis with a dense neutrophil infiltrate.","whyNot":["Granulation tissue forms at the infarct margins at about 7 to 14 days.","A collagen scar replaces the infarct over several weeks and is largely complete by about 2 months.","Macrophages arrive and begin removing dead myocytes at about 3 to 7 days. At 2 days the infiltrate is still mainly neutrophils.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Timeline of changes after MI","Coagulative necrosis"],"comps":["CV-18-C3","CV-18-H3"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C01-Q4","from":[8],"stem":"During the first hours, potassium released from injured cells depolarizes the resting membrane potential of myocytes at the infarct border from about -90 mV to about -65 mV. Which of the following best explains the slowed impulse conduction through these ventricular myocytes?","options":["L-type calcium channels close early, so the phase 2 plateau is lost","Many fast sodium channels stay inactivated, so phase 0 is slower","Funny current channels open, so phase 4 depolarization speeds up","Delayed rectifier potassium channels fail, so phase 3 is prolonged"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-5","obj":"CV-5.1","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the ventricular myocyte upstroke (phase 0): rapid sodium influx through voltage-gated fast sodium channels.","Recall that these channels inactivate with sustained depolarization and recover only at a negative resting potential.","A less negative resting potential leaves fewer channels available, so the upstroke is smaller and slower, and conduction slows."],"why":"The rapid upstroke of the ventricular myocyte action potential comes from sodium rushing in through voltage-gated fast sodium channels. These channels close an inactivation gate when the membrane stays depolarized and need a negative resting potential to reset. When extracellular potassium rises in ischemic tissue, the resting potential becomes less negative, many sodium channels stay inactivated, and phase 0 becomes smaller and slower. Slow conduction in the border zone sets up reentry, which is why ventricular fibrillation is a common cause of early death after MI.","whyNot":["Calcium entry through L-type channels holds up the plateau and triggers contraction. It does not set how fast the upstroke spreads in ventricular myocytes.","Correct.","The funny current drives spontaneous phase 4 depolarization in SA node cells. Working ventricular myocytes have a stable phase 4 and rely on sodium channels for their upstroke.","Potassium efflux in phase 3 repolarizes the cell and sets the refractory period. It has no role in the speed of the upstroke."],"study":{"topics":["Ventricular myocyte action potential phases","Sodium channel inactivation"],"comps":["CV-5","CV-7","CV-18-C3"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C02","system":"CV","dx":"Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia with coronary atherosclerosis and stable angina","codes":["CV-18-I4","CV-18-I3","CV-13"],"case":"A 32-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of chest pressure that occurs when he climbs stairs and resolves with rest within 5 minutes. His father had a myocardial infarction at age 44 years. Physical examination shows firm nodules on both Achilles tendons. Fasting serum studies show LDL cholesterol 310 mg/dL (desirable below 100 mg/dL), HDL cholesterol 44 mg/dL (40 or higher), and triglycerides 120 mg/dL (below 150 mg/dL). An exercise stress test shows ischemic ST-segment depression. Coronary angiography shows 75% atherosclerotic narrowing of the proximal right coronary artery. Genetic testing shows one pathogenic variant in the LDL receptor gene and one normal copy. Diagnosis: heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia with coronary atherosclerosis and stable angina.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C02-Q1","from":[16],"stem":"His LDL cholesterol is markedly increased, but his triglyceride concentration is normal. Which of the following is the direct consequence of his LDL receptor defect?","options":["The liver makes and secretes more VLDL than usual","Lipoprotein lipase in capillaries is less active","Less LDL is taken up and cleared by hepatocytes","HDL can no longer carry cholesterol back to the liver"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-I4","obj":"CV-18-I.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note the pattern: isolated high LDL with normal triglycerides.","Recall that most LDL leaves the blood by receptor-mediated endocytosis into hepatocytes through the LDL receptor.","A defective receptor means LDL stays in the blood longer and its level rises."],"why":"Hepatocytes remove most LDL from the blood by binding its apolipoprotein B-100 to the LDL receptor and taking it in by receptor-mediated endocytosis. In familial hypercholesterolemia, an autosomal dominant defect in the LDL receptor slows this clearance, so plasma LDL rises, often 2 to 3 times normal in heterozygotes. The excess LDL deposits in tendons as xanthomas and in arteries as atheroma, which explains his early coronary disease.","whyNot":["More VLDL output would raise fasting triglycerides as well as LDL. His triglycerides are normal, and an LDL receptor defect does not drive VLDL synthesis.","Low lipoprotein lipase activity causes high triglycerides from retained chylomicrons and VLDL (familial chylomicronemia). It does not raise LDL alone.","Correct.","Reverse cholesterol transport by HDL uses other receptors, such as scavenger receptor B1. His HDL is normal."],"study":{"topics":["LDL receptor and receptor-mediated endocytosis","Familial hypercholesterolemia"],"comps":["CV-18-I4","CV-13","CV-14"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C02-Q2","from":[14],"stem":"He begins statin therapy, and 8 weeks later his LDL cholesterol concentration has decreased by 45%. Which of the following best explains this decrease?","options":["It blocks 7-alpha-hydroxylase, so the liver converts more cholesterol to bile","It blocks HMG-CoA reductase, so his normal allele makes more LDL receptors","It blocks cholesterol esterase in the gut, so less dietary cholesterol is absorbed","It blocks lipoprotein lipase, so less LDL is formed from VLDL in the blood"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.3","dok":3,"approach":["Name the statin target: HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting step of cholesterol synthesis (HMG-CoA to mevalonate).","Follow the effect in the hepatocyte: less cholesterol inside the cell activates SREBP, which turns on LDL receptor gene transcription.","Apply it to him: his one normal LDL receptor allele can still respond, so LDL clearance rises."],"why":"Statins inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme that converts HMG-CoA to mevalonate in hepatic cholesterol synthesis. When hepatocyte cholesterol falls, the transcription factor SREBP increases expression of the LDL receptor gene, so the liver pulls more LDL out of the blood. In heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia the normal allele can be upregulated, so statins work well. Patients with two nonfunctional LDL receptor alleles respond poorly for this reason.","whyNot":["7-alpha-hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis. Blocking it would leave more cholesterol in the liver, and statins do not target it.","Correct.","Reducing intestinal cholesterol absorption is how ezetimibe works, through the NPC1L1 transporter. Statins act on hepatic cholesterol synthesis.","Blocking lipoprotein lipase would raise triglycerides and is not how statins work. Statins inhibit the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis."],"study":{"topics":["HMG-CoA reductase and SREBP regulation","How statins lower LDL"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I4"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C02-Q3","from":[15],"stem":"Which of the following best describes the lipoprotein particle and the route by which dietary triglyceride leaves his small intestine after a fatty meal?","options":["Chylomicrons, through lacteals and the thoracic duct","Chylomicrons, through the portal vein to the liver","VLDL particles, through lacteals and the thoracic duct","LDL particles, through the portal vein to the liver"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-10","obj":"CV-10.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the particle the enterocyte builds for dietary triglyceride: the chylomicron, with apolipoprotein B-48.","Recall that chylomicrons are too large for blood capillaries and enter the central lacteal of each villus.","Trace the lymph: lacteals, cisterna chyli, thoracic duct, and the left venous angle."],"why":"Enterocytes package absorbed dietary triglyceride and cholesterol into chylomicrons containing apolipoprotein B-48. Chylomicrons are too large to pass into blood capillaries, so they enter the lacteals and reach the blood through the thoracic duct at the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins. In the blood, lipoprotein lipase removes their triglyceride for muscle and fat, and the remnants are taken up by the liver.","whyNot":["Correct.","Chylomicrons are too large to enter intestinal capillaries, so they do not travel in the portal vein. Short- and medium-chain fatty acids are the dietary fats that go by the portal route.","VLDL is made by the liver to export triglyceride the liver itself synthesizes. It does not carry dietary fat out of the intestine.","LDL is formed in the blood from VLDL after its triglyceride is removed. It does not carry dietary fat from the gut."],"study":{"topics":["Chylomicron formation and lymphatic transport","Lipoprotein classes"],"comps":["CV-10","CV-13"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C02-Q4","from":[25],"stem":"Macrophages in his coronary plaque are filled with cholesterol despite his defective LDL receptors. Which of the following best explains how cholesterol accumulated in his plaque?","options":["Oxidized intimal LDL is taken up by macrophage scavenger receptors","Adventitial fibroblasts multiply, and LDL enters the wall from the vasa vasorum","Medial smooth muscle calcifies, and the stiff wall traps LDL from the plasma within it","Fibrin forms on normal endothelium, and LDL is trapped in the clot as it organizes"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that atherosclerosis begins with endothelial injury or dysfunction in the intima.","LDL enters the intima and is oxidized; monocytes enter and become macrophages.","Macrophages take up oxidized LDL through scavenger receptors, which are separate from the LDL receptor, so foam cells form even in familial hypercholesterolemia."],"why":"Atherosclerosis starts when injured or dysfunctional endothelium lets LDL into the intima, where it is oxidized. Recruited monocytes become macrophages that take up oxidized LDL through scavenger receptors, which do not depend on the LDL receptor and are not turned off by cholesterol in the cell. Engorged macrophages become foam cells, and smooth muscle cells migrate from the media into the intima and lay down collagen. The result is a fibrous cap over a lipid core. High LDL speeds up every step.","whyNot":["Correct.","Atherosclerosis is a disease of the intima. LDL enters from the lumen through injured endothelium, and the adventitia is not the site of plaque.","Calcification of the media without lipid or inflammation is Monckeberg medial sclerosis. It does not narrow the lumen or form foam cells.","Thrombus usually forms on a plaque after its cap is disrupted. It does not start atherosclerosis on healthy endothelium."],"study":{"topics":["Atherosclerosis pathogenesis","Scavenger receptors and foam cells"],"comps":["CV-18-I3","CV-2","CV-18-I4"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C03","system":"CV","dx":"Rheumatic mitral stenosis with a past subacute endocarditis and current acute Staphylococcus aureus endocarditis","codes":["CV-18-D5","CV-18-D3","CV-18-K1"],"case":"A 34-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of a 4-day history of fever. At age 9 years, she had fever, migratory polyarthritis, and a new heart murmur about 3 weeks after an untreated sore throat. She has known mitral stenosis with a valve area of 1.3 cm2 (normal 4 to 6 cm2). Three years ago, 2 weeks after a tooth extraction, she had 6 weeks of low-grade fever and fatigue, and a vegetation was found on her mitral valve; that infection was cured. She currently injects heroin. Her temperature is 39.4 °C (102.9 °F). Physical examination shows a new holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases in intensity with inspiration. Blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus. Diagnosis: rheumatic mitral stenosis, a past subacute endocarditis, and acute S. aureus endocarditis.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C03-Q1","from":[19],"stem":"Which of the following organisms most likely caused the pharyngitis that preceded her childhood illness?","options":["Streptococcus agalactiae (group B)","Streptococcus pyogenes (group A)","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-D5","obj":"CV-18-D.8","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the childhood picture as acute rheumatic fever: carditis and migrating polyarthritis a few weeks after pharyngitis.","Recall that rheumatic fever follows throat infection with group A beta-hemolytic streptococci.","Name the organism: Streptococcus pyogenes."],"why":"Acute rheumatic fever is a delayed, nonsuppurative complication of pharyngitis caused by group A beta-hemolytic Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes). It usually starts about 2 to 4 weeks after the sore throat, most often in children aged 5 to 15. Repeated or severe attacks scar the valves, and the mitral valve is affected most often. Prompt antibiotic treatment of streptococcal pharyngitis prevents rheumatic fever.","whyNot":["Group B streptococci colonize the vagina and cause sepsis and meningitis in newborns. They do not cause pharyngitis followed by rheumatic fever.","Correct.","Pneumococcus causes pneumonia, otitis media, and meningitis. It is not linked to rheumatic fever.","S. aureus can cause many infections, but it does not trigger rheumatic fever. Her current endocarditis is a separate, later problem."],"study":{"topics":["Acute rheumatic fever","Group A streptococcus"],"comps":["CV-18-D5","CV-17"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C03-Q2","from":[22],"stem":"No bacteria were present in her heart during the childhood illness, yet her mitral valve was damaged and later scarred. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this valve damage?","options":["Circulating immune complexes deposit in the valve and fix complement","IgE on mast cells triggers degranulation within the valve tissue","Streptococci invade the valve leaflets and form a small abscess","Antibodies to streptococcal M protein bind valve proteins"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.2","dok":2,"approach":["Note that the valve is sterile, so the damage is immune mediated.","Recall molecular mimicry: streptococcal M protein resembles proteins in heart valves and myocardium.","Antibodies made against the bacterium bind the patient's own tissue, which is type II hypersensitivity."],"why":"Rheumatic fever is an example of molecular mimicry. Antibodies made against the streptococcal M protein cross-react with similar proteins in the heart, including valve glycoproteins and myosin, which is classified as type II (antibody-mediated) hypersensitivity. CD4 T cells that react to the same antigens add to the inflammation. Aschoff bodies in the myocardium are the characteristic lesion, and repeated attacks leave the mitral valve thick, fused, and stenotic.","whyNot":["Immune complex deposition is type III hypersensitivity. After streptococcal infection it explains poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, not rheumatic carditis.","IgE and mast cell degranulation is type I hypersensitivity, as in anaphylaxis and allergic asthma. It does not scar heart valves.","Rheumatic fever is nonsuppurative: the valve is sterile. Bacteria growing on a valve would be infective endocarditis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Molecular mimicry","Hypersensitivity types I to IV"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-D5"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C03-Q3","from":[20],"stem":"Her endocarditis 3 years ago began 2 weeks after a tooth extraction and followed an indolent 6-week course on her scarred mitral valve. Which of the following best explains that infection?","options":["Enterococcus faecalis from the bowel reached the valve after a colonoscopy","Candida albicans from a central line grew on the surface of the valve","Oral viridans streptococci adhered to fibrin on the scarred valve","Staphylococcus aureus from the skin invaded the normal leaflets of the valve"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-D3","obj":"CV-18-D.5","dok":3,"approach":["Note the clues: a slow, low-grade illness (subacute), a previously damaged valve, and recent dental work.","Recall that oral viridans streptococci enter the blood during dental procedures.","They adhere to the sterile fibrin and platelet deposits that form on damaged valve endothelium, using surface dextran and adhesins."],"why":"Viridans streptococci, such as Streptococcus sanguinis and Streptococcus mutans, are normal mouth flora that enter the blood during dental work. They are low in virulence, so they usually infect valves that are already damaged, where small sterile fibrin and platelet deposits give them a place to attach. Dextran on their surface helps them stick. The illness develops over weeks to months, which is the classic subacute endocarditis pattern.","whyNot":["Enterococci cause subacute endocarditis after gastrointestinal or genitourinary procedures. Her infection followed dental work.","Fungal endocarditis is linked to central lines, prosthetic valves, and injection drug use. She had none of these at that time, and the trigger was a tooth extraction.","Correct.","S. aureus is highly virulent, can infect normal valves, and causes an acute illness over days. Her infection was slow and sat on a valve that was already damaged."],"study":{"topics":["Acute and subacute endocarditis","Viridans streptococci"],"comps":["CV-18-D3","CV-18-K1","CV-17"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C03-Q4","from":[24],"stem":"Which of the following valves is most likely the site of her current vegetation?","options":["Pulmonary valve","Tricuspid valve","Aortic valve","Mitral valve"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-18-K1","obj":"CV-18-K.1","dok":3,"approach":["Note that injected bacteria enter the venous blood and reach the right heart valves first.","A holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration is right-sided regurgitation.","Inspiration raises venous return to the right heart, so right-sided murmurs get louder. The valve is the tricuspid."],"why":"In people who inject drugs, bacteria and particles enter the veins and strike the tricuspid valve first, and S. aureus is the most common organism. Damage to the leaflets causes tricuspid regurgitation, a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border. Inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure, which increases venous return to the right heart and makes right-sided murmurs louder. Pieces of a tricuspid vegetation travel to the lungs as septic emboli.","whyNot":["Pulmonary valve endocarditis is rare. Its murmurs are heard at the left upper sternal border.","Correct.","Aortic regurgitation gives a diastolic murmur along the left sternal border, and left-sided murmurs do not get louder with inspiration.","The mitral valve is the most common site of endocarditis in people who do not inject drugs. A mitral regurgitation murmur is loudest at the apex and does not increase with inspiration."],"study":{"topics":["Endocarditis in people who inject drugs","Murmurs and their locations"],"comps":["CV-18-K1","CV-4"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C04","system":"CV","dx":"Hypertensive heart disease with concentric left ventricular hypertrophy, hypertensive nephrosclerosis, and an aneurysm of the ascending aorta and arch","codes":["CV-18-A2","CV-18-I1"],"case":"A 71-year-old man with 30 years of poorly controlled hypertension comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of hoarseness and difficulty swallowing solid food. Blood pressure is 172/98 mm Hg. Serum creatinine concentration is 1.7 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3 mg/dL) with mild proteinuria. Echocardiography shows a non-dilated left ventricle with a wall thickness of 15 mm (normal 6 to 11 mm), an ejection fraction of 62% (normal 55 to 70%), and impaired relaxation in diastole. CT shows a 6.2 cm aneurysm of the ascending aorta and aortic arch. Diagnosis: hypertensive heart disease with concentric left ventricular hypertrophy, hypertensive nephrosclerosis, and an aneurysm of the ascending aorta and arch.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C04-Q1","from":[11],"stem":"Which of the following processes best explains his echocardiographic findings?","options":["Dilated cardiomyopathy with failing contraction","Eccentric hypertrophy from chronic volume overload","Concentric hypertrophy from pressure overload","Constrictive pericarditis that limits filling"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-A2","obj":"CV-18-A.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the load: long-standing hypertension raises afterload, which is a pressure overload.","Apply the Laplace relationship: wall stress rises with pressure and falls as the wall thickens.","Myocytes add sarcomeres in parallel, the wall thickens without dilation, and the stiff wall relaxes poorly."],"why":"Chronic hypertension forces the left ventricle to generate high pressure. By the Laplace relationship, wall stress rises with pressure and radius and falls with wall thickness, so myocytes add sarcomeres in parallel and the wall thickens to bring stress back down. This is concentric hypertrophy. The thick, stiff wall fills poorly in diastole, so ejection fraction stays normal at first while relaxation is impaired. Over many years, the hypertrophied heart can dilate and fail.","whyNot":["Dilated cardiomyopathy gives a large, thin-walled ventricle with a low ejection fraction. His ejection fraction is normal and the cavity is not dilated.","Volume overload, as in mitral or aortic regurgitation, adds sarcomeres in series. The chamber dilates and the wall is not much thicker than normal.","Correct.","A stiff pericardium limits filling, but the myocardium itself is not thickened. His problem is in the ventricular wall."],"study":{"topics":["Concentric and eccentric hypertrophy","Law of Laplace"],"comps":["CV-18-A2","CV-6"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C04-Q2","from":[6],"stem":"Compression of which of the following pairs of structures by the aneurysm most likely explains his hoarseness and dysphagia?","options":["Thoracic duct and left sympathetic trunk","Left brachiocephalic vein and left vagus nerve","Left phrenic nerve and trachea","Left recurrent laryngeal nerve and esophagus"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","obj":"CV-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Link hoarseness to the vocal cords, which are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerves.","Recall that the left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the aortic arch next to the ligamentum arteriosum.","Link trouble swallowing to the esophagus, which lies just to the right of the arch."],"why":"The left vagus nerve gives off the left recurrent laryngeal nerve as it crosses the aortic arch. The nerve hooks under the arch beside the ligamentum arteriosum and climbs back up in the groove between the trachea and esophagus to supply most of the muscles of the larynx. An arch aneurysm can stretch or compress it, causing left vocal cord paralysis and hoarseness. The esophagus lies right next to the arch and the descending aorta, so a large aneurysm can also compress it and cause dysphagia.","whyNot":["Compression of the sympathetic trunk can cause Horner syndrome, and duct injury can cause chylothorax. Neither causes hoarseness or trouble swallowing.","A compressed brachiocephalic vein causes swelling of the left arm and face. It does not explain trouble swallowing solid food.","The phrenic nerve controls the diaphragm, so compressing it paralyzes a hemidiaphragm. It does not cause hoarseness, and tracheal compression causes cough and stridor, not trouble swallowing.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Relations of the aortic arch","Recurrent laryngeal nerve course"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-I1"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C04-Q3","from":[27],"stem":"His increased serum creatinine concentration and mild proteinuria are attributed to long-standing hypertension without a malignant phase. Which of the following changes is most likely present in the walls of his small renal arterioles?","options":["Hyaline arteriolosclerosis","Necrotizing granulomatous vasculitis","Hyperplastic (onion-skin) arteriolosclerosis","Monckeberg medial calcific sclerosis"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-A2","obj":"CV-18-A.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note the setting: years of moderate (benign) hypertension, not a hypertensive emergency.","Recall that chronic pressure pushes plasma proteins into arteriolar walls and stimulates extra matrix.","The result is pink, glassy wall thickening that narrows the lumen: hyaline arteriolosclerosis, which causes nephrosclerosis."],"why":"In long-standing benign hypertension, and also in diabetes, plasma proteins leak through injured endothelium into the walls of small arterioles, and smooth muscle cells lay down extra basement membrane. The wall becomes thick, pink, and glassy on H&E stain, and the lumen narrows. In the kidney, the resulting ischemia shrinks glomeruli and tubules. This is benign nephrosclerosis, which causes a slow rise in creatinine and mild proteinuria.","whyNot":["Correct.","Granulomatous inflammation that destroys vessel walls is seen in vasculitis, such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis. It is not caused by hypertension.","Concentric layers of smooth muscle and basement membrane, often with fibrinoid necrosis, are seen in malignant hypertension, usually with pressures above 180/120 mm Hg and acute organ damage.","Monckeberg sclerosis is calcification in the media of medium-sized muscular arteries in older people. It does not narrow the lumen or damage the kidney."],"study":{"topics":["Arteriolosclerosis types","Hypertensive nephrosclerosis"],"comps":["CV-18-A2","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C04-Q4","from":[3],"stem":"Three weeks later, he develops sudden tearing chest pain and collapses. Blood pressure is 76/50 mm Hg. Physical examination shows jugular venous distention and muffled heart sounds. The ascending aorta has dissected and ruptured. Into which of the following spaces is blood most likely accumulating?","options":["Left pleural cavity","Superior mediastinum","Posterior mediastinum","Pericardial cavity"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","obj":"CV-3.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize Beck triad: low blood pressure, distended neck veins, and muffled heart sounds point to cardiac tamponade.","Recall that the ascending aorta begins inside the pericardial sac.","Blood from an ascending aortic rupture fills the pericardial cavity, between the parietal and visceral layers of serous pericardium."],"why":"The pericardial cavity is the thin space between the parietal and visceral layers of serous pericardium. Because the fibrous pericardium does not stretch quickly, sudden bleeding into this space raises its pressure and squeezes the heart, especially the thin-walled right side, so the ventricles cannot fill in diastole. Stroke volume and blood pressure fall, venous pressure rises, and the fluid muffles the heart sounds. The same picture follows a stab wound to the heart or rupture of the free wall after a myocardial infarction.","whyNot":["Rupture of the descending thoracic aorta often bleeds into the left pleural cavity. That causes hypotension and a hemothorax, but not distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds.","The aortic arch and great vessels lie in the superior mediastinum, outside the pericardial sac. Bleeding there does not produce Beck triad.","Bleeding there collects around the descending aorta and esophagus, outside the pericardial sac, so it does not cause tamponade. His rupture is in the ascending aorta, which lies inside the sac.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pericardium layers","Cardiac tamponade and Beck triad"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-F2","CV-18-I2"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C05","system":"CV","dx":"Tetralogy of Fallot","codes":["CV-18-G4","CV-1"],"case":"A 4-month-old girl is brought to the physician because of episodes of perioral cyanosis during crying and feeding. She was born at term after a normal pregnancy. Physical examination shows a harsh systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border. Oxygen saturation is 82% on room air (normal 95 to 100%). Chest x-ray shows a boot-shaped heart with reduced lung markings. Echocardiography shows a large ventricular septal defect, an aorta that sits over the defect, narrowing of the right ventricular outflow tract below the pulmonary valve, and right ventricular hypertrophy. Diagnosis: tetralogy of Fallot.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C05-Q1","from":[29],"stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains her cyanosis?","options":["Two parallel circuits with no mixing because the great arteries are switched","Right-to-left shunt across the VSD because flow to the lungs is obstructed","Left-to-right shunt across the VSD because left ventricular pressure is higher","Left-to-right shunt across a patent ductus because aortic pressure is higher"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-G4","obj":"CV-18-G.5","dok":2,"approach":["Note the low saturation with reduced lung markings: less blood is reaching the lungs.","Identify the obstruction: right ventricular outflow narrowing raises right ventricular pressure.","With a large VSD, deoxygenated blood takes the easier path into the overriding aorta, a right-to-left shunt."],"why":"In tetralogy of Fallot, narrowing of the right ventricular outflow tract makes it hard for blood to reach the lungs. Right ventricular pressure rises to match or exceed left ventricular pressure, so deoxygenated blood crosses the large VSD into the overriding aorta. This right-to-left shunt sends unoxygenated blood to the body and causes cyanosis. The more severe the outflow obstruction, the larger the shunt and the worse the cyanosis.","whyNot":["Parallel circuits are the problem in transposition of the great arteries, where the aorta arises from the right ventricle. Her echo shows an overriding aorta, not transposition.","Correct.","A left-to-right shunt sends oxygenated blood back to the lungs. It increases lung markings and does not cause cyanosis early on, as in an isolated VSD.","Flow from the aorta into the pulmonary artery through a patent ductus returns oxygenated blood to the lungs. It does not cause cyanosis unless pulmonary hypertension later reverses it."],"study":{"topics":["Tetralogy of Fallot","Right-to-left and left-to-right shunts"],"comps":["CV-18-G4","CV-18-G3"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C05-Q2","from":[],"stem":"During an episode of severe cyanosis, her mother draws the infant's knees up to her chest, and the cyanosis improves. Older children with this defect squat for the same reason. Which of the following best explains this improvement?","options":["It relaxes the outflow tract muscle, so pulmonary resistance falls","It raises systemic resistance, so less blood crosses the VSD","It lowers venous return, so the right ventricle ejects less blood","It lowers systemic vascular resistance, so more blood enters the aorta"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-G4","obj":"CV-18-G.5","dok":3,"approach":["See the VSD as a fork: right ventricular blood goes to the lungs or into the aorta, whichever path resists less.","Flexing the legs kinks the femoral arteries and raises systemic vascular resistance.","Higher aortic resistance pushes more right ventricular blood through the narrowed outflow tract to the lungs, so oxygenation improves."],"why":"The direction of flow across her VSD depends on the balance between resistance to pulmonary flow and resistance to systemic flow. Pulling the knees to the chest, or squatting, compresses the femoral arteries and raises systemic vascular resistance. With a higher pressure in the aorta, less deoxygenated blood crosses from the right ventricle into the aorta and more goes to the lungs. Anything that lowers systemic resistance, such as exercise or a hot bath, does the opposite.","whyNot":["Spasm of the outflow tract can trigger a spell, but leg position does not relax it. The benefit comes from the systemic side of the circulation.","Correct.","Squatting tends to increase venous return from the legs. Less right ventricular filling would also not send more blood to the lungs.","Lower systemic resistance makes the aorta an easier path for right ventricular blood, which increases the right-to-left shunt and worsens cyanosis."],"study":{"topics":["Tet spells and squatting","Systemic vascular resistance and shunt direction"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-G4"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C05-Q3","from":[1],"stem":"Before surgery, a catheter is advanced from the inferior vena cava into the right atrium and then directly into the hypertrophied ventricle with the narrowed outflow tract. Which of the following valves does the catheter cross?","options":["Tricuspid valve","Aortic valve","Mitral valve","Pulmonary valve"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-4","obj":"CV-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the chamber: the hypertrophied chamber with a narrowed outflow tract in tetralogy of Fallot is the right ventricle.","The catheter is moving from the right atrium to the right ventricle.","Name the right atrioventricular valve: the tricuspid valve."],"why":"The tricuspid valve is the right atrioventricular valve, with three leaflets held by chordae tendineae to papillary muscles of the right ventricle. A venous catheter passes from the inferior vena cava into the right atrium and through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle. In tetralogy of Fallot, right ventricular pressure equals left ventricular and aortic pressure because the large VSD joins the two ventricles and the outflow tract is narrow.","whyNot":["Correct.","The aortic valve guards the exit from the left ventricle. It is reached from the arterial side or by crossing the septum.","The mitral valve lies between the left atrium and left ventricle. A catheter from the inferior vena cava would reach it only by crossing the atrial septum.","The pulmonary valve guards the exit from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk. The catheter would cross it only after leaving the right ventricle."],"study":{"topics":["Heart valves and chambers","Right heart catheterization path"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-3"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C05-Q4","from":[5],"stem":"Before birth, oxygenated blood from the placenta entered her right atrium through the inferior vena cava and passed directly to the left atrium. Which of the following correctly names this structure and the reason it normally closes after birth?","options":["Ductus arteriosus; rising oxygen and falling prostaglandin E2 constrict it","Foramen ovale; falling oxygen levels constrict the septum primum","Foramen ovale; left atrial pressure rises above right atrial pressure","Ductus venosus; flow in the umbilical vein stops when the cord is clamped"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","obj":"CV-1.6","dok":2,"approach":["Trace fetal blood: inferior vena cava to right atrium, then across the atrial septum to the left atrium.","Name the opening in the atrial septum: the foramen ovale.","At birth, lung blood flow rises, more blood returns to the left atrium, and left atrial pressure pushes the septum primum flap shut."],"why":"In the fetus, the crista dividens (the lower edge of the septum secundum) directs oxygen-rich inferior vena cava blood through the foramen ovale into the left atrium, so it bypasses the airless lungs. At birth the lungs expand, pulmonary resistance falls, pulmonary venous return to the left atrium rises, and placental flow into the right side stops. Left atrial pressure then exceeds right atrial pressure and presses the septum primum against the septum secundum. The opening later seals to form the fossa ovalis.","whyNot":["This is how the ductus arteriosus closes, but the ductus joins the pulmonary trunk to the aorta. It does not carry blood between the atria.","The foramen ovale is the correct structure, but it closes by pressure, with no muscle to constrict. Oxygen level is the trigger for closing the ductus arteriosus.","Correct.","The ductus venosus carries umbilical vein blood past the liver into the inferior vena cava. It does not connect the two atria."],"study":{"topics":["Fetal circulation","Changes in the circulation at birth"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-G3"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C06","system":"CV","dx":"Chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy (dilated) with left and then right heart failure","codes":["CV-18-K2","CV-18-E1","CV-18-B"],"case":"A 47-year-old woman who grew up in a mud-walled house in rural Bolivia comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of worsening dyspnea with paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea and a 1-month history of leg swelling. Physical examination shows bibasilar crackles, an S3 gallop, jugular venous distention, tender hepatomegaly, and pitting edema of the legs. Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 25% (normal 55 to 70%) and an estimated pulmonary artery systolic pressure of 58 mm Hg (normal 15 to 30 mm Hg). Coronary angiography is normal. Serum albumin is 4.0 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0 g/dL). Two serologic tests for Trypanosoma cruzi are positive. Diagnosis: chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy with heart failure.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C06-Q1","from":[21],"stem":"By which of the following routes was Trypanosoma cruzi most likely transmitted to her during childhood?","options":["Tsetse fly saliva injected during a bite","Sand fly saliva injected during a bite","Triatomine bug feces rubbed into a bite","Anopheles mosquito saliva injected during a bite"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.3","dok":2,"approach":["Link the setting, a rural home with mud walls in Latin America, to the insects that live in wall cracks.","Name the vector of Trypanosoma cruzi: triatomine (reduviid, kissing) bugs.","Recall the route: the bug defecates while feeding, and the person rubs infected feces into the bite or the conjunctiva."],"why":"Trypanosoma cruzi is carried by triatomine (reduviid or kissing) bugs that hide in cracks in mud walls and thatched roofs and feed at night. The parasite is in the bug's feces, not its saliva, and enters through the bite wound or mucous membranes when the person scratches or rubs the area. Years later, about 20 to 30% of infected people develop chronic disease, most often a dilated cardiomyopathy, with megaesophagus or megacolon in some.","whyNot":["Tsetse flies in Africa carry Trypanosoma brucei, which causes African sleeping sickness. They do not live in the Americas.","Sand flies transmit Leishmania species, which cause cutaneous and visceral leishmaniasis.","Correct.","Female Anopheles mosquitoes transmit Plasmodium species, which cause malaria."],"study":{"topics":["Chagas disease","Arthropod vectors of protozoa"],"comps":["CV-18-K2","CV-17"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C06-Q2","from":[12],"stem":"Her dyspnea preceded the leg edema, jugular venous distention, and hepatomegaly by several months. Which of the following mechanisms best explains how her left ventricular failure led to these right-sided findings?","options":["Pulmonary venous congestion raised right ventricular afterload","Occlusion of the right coronary artery caused a right ventricular infarct","Low plasma oncotic pressure from protein loss let fluid leave the capillaries","A stiff, thickened pericardium limited filling of the right ventricle"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-B","obj":"CV-18-B.2","dok":3,"approach":["Start on the left: a failing left ventricle raises left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure, which causes her breathlessness and crackles.","Follow the pressure backward: pulmonary artery pressure rises (58 mm Hg), raising right ventricular afterload.","The right ventricle fails, so systemic venous pressure rises, causing raised jugular venous pressure, liver congestion, and leg edema."],"why":"Left ventricular failure is the most common cause of right ventricular failure. When the left ventricle cannot empty, left atrial and pulmonary venous pressures rise, and the pulmonary arteries must work at higher pressure to push blood through the lungs. The thin-walled right ventricle tolerates this afterload poorly and eventually fails. Systemic venous pressure then rises, causing raised jugular venous pressure, a congested liver, and dependent edema.","whyNot":["Correct.","Her coronary angiogram is normal, so there was no right coronary occlusion.","Her albumin is normal. Low oncotic pressure, as in nephrotic syndrome or cirrhosis, causes edema without raised jugular venous pressure.","Constrictive pericarditis limits filling of both ventricles and would not explain a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 25%."],"study":{"topics":["Left-sided and right-sided heart failure","Pulmonary hypertension from left heart disease"],"comps":["CV-18-B","CV-18-A1","CV-15"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C06-Q3","from":[7],"stem":"In a healthy heart, an increase in end-diastolic volume increases stroke volume, but her dilated ventricle gains little stroke volume from the same increase in filling. Which of the following is the main cellular basis of this length-tension relationship in a normal ventricle?","options":["Stretch shortens the sarcomeres, so more myosin heads bind to the thin filaments","Stretch releases norepinephrine from cardiac nerve endings, which raises contractility","Stretch lowers wall tension, so afterload falls and the ventricle empties more fully","Stretch sets better filament overlap and makes troponin C more sensitive to calcium"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-5","obj":"CV-5.5","dok":3,"approach":["Name the principle: the Frank-Starling mechanism, where preload sets stroke volume.","Go to the sarcomere: stretch moves resting sarcomere length toward the optimum and increases the calcium sensitivity of the myofilaments.","Apply it to her: a failing ventricle has a flattened curve, so extra filling mostly raises filling pressure and congestion."],"why":"When the ventricle fills more, each myocyte is stretched. In the normal working range this brings the sarcomere closer to its optimal length, and it increases the calcium sensitivity of troponin C, so the same calcium transient produces more force. The ventricle therefore ejects more, matching output to venous return beat by beat. In a dilated, failing ventricle the curve is flat, so extra preload adds congestion with little gain in stroke volume.","whyNot":["Filling stretches the sarcomeres, it does not shorten them. In the normal working range, stretch brings sarcomere length closer to the optimum for force.","Norepinephrine raises contractility through beta-1 receptors, but stretch does not release it. The Frank-Starling effect works in a denervated, transplanted heart.","By the Laplace relationship, a larger chamber has higher wall tension for the same pressure, so afterload on each fiber rises with dilation.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Frank-Starling mechanism","Length-dependent activation"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-5"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C06-Q4","from":[30],"stem":"Her low cardiac output decreases perfusion pressure in the renal afferent arterioles, and juxtaglomerular cells release renin. Which of the following sequences correctly describes the subsequent steps leading to aldosterone release?","options":["Renin converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, then ACE converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II","Renin converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin II, then ACE converts angiotensin II to aldosterone","Renin converts angiotensin I to angiotensinogen, then ACE converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin II","ACE converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, then renin converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","obj":"CV-6.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the trigger: low afferent arteriolar pressure and less sodium chloride at the macula densa cause renin release.","Place each enzyme: renin cleaves liver-made angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, and ACE, mainly on lung endothelium, converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II.","Angiotensin II acts on the zona glomerulosa to release aldosterone."],"why":"Juxtaglomerular cells in the afferent arteriole release renin when renal perfusion pressure falls, when the macula densa senses less sodium chloride, or with sympathetic stimulation through beta-1 receptors. Renin cleaves angiotensinogen, made by the liver, to angiotensin I. Angiotensin-converting enzyme, abundant on pulmonary capillary endothelium, converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II. Angiotensin II constricts arterioles and stimulates the zona glomerulosa to release aldosterone, which increases sodium and water retention and worsens edema in heart failure.","whyNot":["Correct.","Renin makes angiotensin I, not angiotensin II. Aldosterone is a steroid made from cholesterol in the adrenal cortex, not a product of ACE.","Angiotensinogen is the starting substrate made by the liver. It is not a product of renin.","The enzymes are reversed. Renin acts first, on angiotensinogen, and ACE acts second."],"study":{"topics":["Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","Neurohormonal activation in heart failure"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-B"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C07","system":"CV","dx":"Lyme carditis with complete (third-degree) atrioventricular block","codes":["CV-18-K3","CV-7"],"case":"A 26-year-old man from Connecticut comes to the emergency department in August because of a 2-day history of lightheadedness and one episode of syncope. Five weeks ago, after hiking, he had an expanding erythematous rash with central clearing on his thigh that resolved without treatment. Pulse is 42/min and blood pressure is 108/66 mm Hg. The ECG shows regular P waves at 84/min and regular, narrow QRS complexes (0.08 s; normal below 0.12 s) at 42/min, with no fixed relationship between them. Serologic testing confirms Lyme disease. Diagnosis: Lyme carditis with complete (third-degree) atrioventricular block.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C07-Q1","from":[23],"stem":"Which of the following organisms is the most likely cause of his condition?","options":["Ehrlichia chaffeensis","Treponema pallidum","Borrelia burgdorferi","Rickettsia rickettsii"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-K3","obj":"CV-18-K.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize erythema migrans: an expanding red rash with central clearing after a tick exposure.","Link it to Lyme disease, which in the northeastern United States is carried by Ixodes ticks.","Recall that early disseminated Lyme disease can cause carditis, most often as AV block."],"why":"Lyme disease is caused by the spirochete Borrelia burgdorferi, carried by Ixodes ticks in the northeastern and upper midwestern United States. Erythema migrans marks early local infection. Weeks later the organism can spread to the heart, nerves, and joints. Lyme carditis most often causes AV block that can change quickly in degree, and it usually resolves with antibiotics, sometimes after temporary pacing.","whyNot":["Ehrlichia is spread by the lone star tick and causes fever, low white cell and platelet counts, and raised liver enzymes. It does not cause erythema migrans or heart block.","T. pallidum causes syphilis. Its main cardiovascular effect is tertiary syphilitic aortitis, which damages the vasa vasorum of the ascending aorta years after infection.","Correct.","R. rickettsii causes Rocky Mountain spotted fever: fever, headache, and a rash that starts on the wrists and ankles and spreads to the trunk, including the palms and soles."],"study":{"topics":["Lyme disease stages","Spirochetes"],"comps":["CV-18-K3","CV-17"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C07-Q2","from":[2],"stem":"His P waves continue at a regular rate of 84/min although none are conducted to the ventricles. Which of the following correctly names the structure that sets this atrial rate and its location?","options":["AV node, in the interatrial septum near the coronary sinus opening","Bundle of His, in the membranous part of the interventricular septum","Purkinje fibers, under the endocardium of the right ventricle","SA node, in the right atrial wall near the superior vena cava"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-7","obj":"CV-7.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note that each P wave is an atrial depolarization, so something above the block is firing at 84/min.","Recall the fastest intrinsic pacemaker: the SA node, at about 60 to 100/min.","Locate it: in the wall of the right atrium where the superior vena cava enters, at the top of the crista terminalis."],"why":"The SA node lies in the wall of the right atrium where the superior vena cava enters, at the upper end of the crista terminalis. Its cells depolarize on their own faster than any other cardiac tissue, at about 60 to 100/min, so they set the rate of the whole heart in health. In complete heart block, the SA node keeps driving the atria normally, but its impulses cannot reach the ventricles.","whyNot":["The AV node is the structure most often blocked in Lyme carditis. It does not set the atrial rate, and its own rate is only about 40 to 60/min.","The bundle of His carries impulses from the AV node to the bundle branches. It does not drive atrial depolarization.","Purkinje fibers spread the impulse through the ventricles. Their intrinsic rate is about 20 to 40/min, and they do not drive the atria.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Conduction system anatomy","Intrinsic pacemaker rates"],"comps":["CV-7","CV-3"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C07-Q3","from":[9],"stem":"His QRS complexes are narrow (0.08 s) and occur at 42/min, independent of the P waves. Which of the following best describes the origin and spread of this escape rhythm?","options":["Atrial depolarization spreads backward from a focus in the AV node","Ventricular depolarization spreads from a focus above the bundle branch split","Ventricular depolarization spreads cell to cell from a Purkinje fiber focus","Ventricular repolarization spreads from a focus above the bundle branch split"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-7","obj":"CV-7.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that the QRS complex records ventricular depolarization.","A narrow QRS means the impulse entered the ventricles through the His bundle and spread rapidly through both bundle branches and the Purkinje system.","A rate of about 40 to 60/min with a narrow QRS fits a junctional escape pacemaker in the AV junction or His bundle."],"why":"The QRS complex records depolarization of the ventricles. When the impulse starts at or above the His bundle, it spreads through the fast His-Purkinje system and the QRS stays narrow, below 0.12 s. An escape focus in the ventricular muscle or distal Purkinje fibers has to spread slowly from cell to cell and gives a wide QRS. His narrow QRS at 42/min points to a junctional escape rhythm below a block in the AV node, the usual site of block in Lyme carditis.","whyNot":["Atrial depolarization is shown by the P wave. His P waves come from the SA node and are unrelated to the QRS complexes.","Correct.","An escape beat from the Purkinje fibers or ventricular muscle spreads slowly through muscle, giving a wide QRS of 0.12 s or more and a rate of about 20 to 40/min.","Ventricular repolarization is shown by the T wave. The QRS complex records ventricular depolarization."],"study":{"topics":["ECG waves and intervals","Escape rhythms"],"comps":["CV-7"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C07-Q4","from":[10],"stem":"Intravenous atropine is administered, and his P-wave rate increases from 84/min to 112/min. Atropine blocked which of the following effects of vagal acetylcholine on SA node cells?","options":["Activation of nicotinic receptors that open sodium channels in nodal cells","Activation of M2 receptors that open potassium channels and slow phase 4","Activation of M3 receptors that raise IP3 and release stored calcium","Activation of beta-1 receptors that raise cAMP and speed up phase 4"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-7","obj":"CV-7.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that atropine blocks muscarinic receptors, so the rate rise means vagal tone was slowing the SA node.","Name the cardiac muscarinic receptor: M2, coupled to Gi.","M2 activation opens acetylcholine-sensitive potassium channels and lowers cAMP, which reduces the funny current and slows phase 4 depolarization."],"why":"Vagal fibers release acetylcholine onto M2 muscarinic receptors in the SA and AV nodes. Through Gi, the beta-gamma subunits open acetylcholine-sensitive potassium channels, which make the resting potential more negative, and the alpha subunit lowers cAMP, which reduces the funny current. Both effects slow phase 4 depolarization, so the node takes longer to reach threshold and the rate falls. Atropine blocks M2 receptors and removes this brake.","whyNot":["Nicotinic receptors are found at autonomic ganglia and the neuromuscular junction. Postganglionic vagal fibers act on muscarinic receptors at the SA node.","Correct.","M3 receptors act through Gq and IP3 in smooth muscle and glands. The heart uses M2 receptors, which act through Gi.","Beta-1 activation by norepinephrine is the sympathetic effect, and it speeds the heart. Atropine does not block beta-1 receptors."],"study":{"topics":["Autonomic control of heart rate","SA node action potential"],"comps":["CV-7","CV-6"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C08","system":"CV","dx":"Wet beriberi (thiamine deficiency) with high-output heart failure, and deep vein thrombosis, in a woman with pancreatic cancer","codes":["CV-13","CV-18-I10","CV-14"],"case":"A 68-year-old woman with metastatic adenocarcinoma of the pancreas has had poor oral intake and frequent vomiting for 3 months. She now has dyspnea, leg edema, and numbness and burning pain in both feet. Her pulse is 110/min, and blood pressure is 112/48 mm Hg. Physical examination shows warm skin and bounding peripheral pulses. Echocardiography shows an end-diastolic volume of 160 mL and an end-systolic volume of 60 mL. Serum lactate is 4.1 mmol/L (0.5 to 2.2 mmol/L). She is admitted to the hospital and confined to bed. On hospital day 4, her left calf becomes swollen. Ultrasound shows a deep vein thrombosis in the left femoral and popliteal veins. She is later found to be heterozygous for factor V Leiden. Diagnosis: wet beriberi with high-output heart failure, and a deep vein thrombosis.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C08-Q1","from":[13],"stem":"Which of the following is closest to her cardiac output?","options":["1.1 L/min","6.6 L/min","11.0 L/min","17.6 L/min"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","obj":"CV-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["Find stroke volume: end-diastolic volume minus end-systolic volume, 160 mL minus 60 mL, which is 100 mL.","Multiply by heart rate: 100 mL times 110/min is 11,000 mL/min.","Convert and judge: 11.0 L/min is about twice the normal resting output of about 5 L/min, which fits high-output failure."],"why":"Cardiac output equals stroke volume times heart rate. Stroke volume is the end-diastolic volume minus the end-systolic volume, here 100 mL, so her output is 100 mL times 110/min, or 11.0 L/min. Her ejection fraction is 100 divided by 160, about 63%, so the pump itself is not weak. In wet beriberi, peripheral vasodilation lowers systemic vascular resistance and the heart must pump a very high output, which over time leads to failure with congestion.","whyNot":["This misplaces the decimal when converting units. Stroke volume is 100 mL, and 100 mL times 110/min is 11,000 mL/min, which is 11.0 L/min.","This multiplies the end-systolic volume (60 mL) by the heart rate. End-systolic volume is the blood left in the ventricle after the beat, not the amount ejected.","Correct.","This multiplies the end-diastolic volume (160 mL) by the heart rate. Not all of the end-diastolic volume is ejected."],"study":{"topics":["Cardiac output and stroke volume","High-output heart failure"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-11"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C08-Q2","from":[18],"stem":"Her increased serum lactate and her cardiac and neurologic findings result from deficiency of a cofactor required by several enzymes of energy metabolism. Which of the following reactions is most directly impaired?","options":["Pyruvate to acetyl-CoA","Alanine to pyruvate by transamination","Pyruvate to oxaloacetate","Methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.5","dok":3,"approach":["Name the missing cofactor in wet beriberi: thiamine pyrophosphate, made from vitamin B1.","List the enzymes that need it: pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase, and transketolase.","Link pyruvate dehydrogenase failure to her findings: pyruvate is shunted to lactate, and aerobic ATP production falls in heart and nerve."],"why":"Thiamine pyrophosphate is the cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, which converts pyruvate to acetyl-CoA so it can enter the citric acid cycle. It is also needed by alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase and transketolase. Without it, pyruvate builds up and is turned into lactate, and tissues that rely on glucose oxidation, such as nerves and heart, cannot make enough ATP. Poor intake, vomiting, and alcohol use are common causes of deficiency, and giving glucose before thiamine can make it worse.","whyNot":["Correct.","Transamination needs pyridoxal phosphate from vitamin B6. B6 deficiency causes neuropathy, cheilosis, and sideroblastic anemia.","Pyruvate carboxylase needs biotin. Biotin deficiency is rare and causes dermatitis and hair loss.","This reaction needs vitamin B12 (adenosylcobalamin). B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia and spinal cord disease, not high-output heart failure."],"study":{"topics":["Thiamine-dependent enzymes","Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-E1"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C08-Q3","from":[28],"stem":"Which of the following components of Virchow triad best explain the formation of her deep vein thrombosis?","options":["Endothelial injury and turbulent flow","Endothelial injury and venous stasis","Venous stasis and hypercoagulability","Turbulent flow and hypercoagulability"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I10","obj":"CV-18-I.8","dok":2,"approach":["List the triad: endothelial injury, abnormal blood flow (stasis or turbulence), and hypercoagulability.","Match her risks: bed rest causes venous stasis; pancreatic cancer and factor V Leiden cause hypercoagulability.","Pick the pair that matches the case."],"why":"Virchow triad names three factors that lead to thrombosis: endothelial injury, abnormal blood flow, and hypercoagulability. In the deep leg veins, slow flow from immobility is the main flow problem, because the calf muscle pump stops working. Mucin-secreting adenocarcinomas such as pancreatic cancer release procoagulant factors, including tissue factor, and factor V Leiden adds an inherited hypercoagulable state. Her deep vein thrombosis therefore reflects stasis and hypercoagulability.","whyNot":["These two drive thrombosis in arteries and the heart, such as on a ruptured plaque or an aneurysm. In the deep leg veins of an immobile patient, slow flow is the main problem.","Stasis applies to her, but nothing in the case points to injury of the vein wall, such as trauma, surgery, or a catheter. This pair also leaves out her cancer and factor V Leiden.","Correct.","Her cancer and factor V Leiden make her blood hypercoagulable, but blood in the deep leg veins of a bedbound patient moves slowly, which is stasis, not turbulence."],"study":{"topics":["Virchow triad","Deep vein thrombosis risk factors"],"comps":["CV-18-I10","CV-18-H6"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C08-Q4","from":[],"stem":"Factor V Leiden is a single amino acid substitution in factor V. Which of the following best explains how this variant increases her risk of thrombosis?","options":["Factor V is made in greater amounts by the liver","Factor V is no longer able to bind antithrombin","Factor Va binds tissue factor more tightly","Factor Va resists activated protein C"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-H6","obj":"CV-18-H.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the normal brake: activated protein C, with protein S, cleaves and inactivates factors Va and VIIIa.","Identify the variant: factor V Leiden changes arginine 506, the site where activated protein C cuts factor Va.","Factor Va stays active longer, so more thrombin is made and clots form more easily."],"why":"Factor V Leiden is a point mutation in the factor V gene that replaces arginine with glutamine at position 506, one of the sites where activated protein C cleaves factor Va. Factor Va therefore resists inactivation, keeps supporting the prothrombinase complex, and more thrombin is made. It is the most common inherited thrombophilia in people of European descent and is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern. Heterozygotes have a several-fold higher risk of venous thrombosis, which adds to risks such as cancer and immobility.","whyNot":["The Leiden variant does not raise the amount of factor V. Increased levels of a clotting factor describe other risks, such as high factor VIII.","Antithrombin mainly inhibits thrombin and factor Xa, and heparin speeds this up. Antithrombin deficiency is a separate inherited thrombophilia.","Tissue factor binds factor VII to start the extrinsic pathway. Factor V does not bind tissue factor.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Factor V Leiden","Protein C pathway"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-H6"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C01","system":"GI","dx":"MASH cirrhosis with portal hypertension, ascites, esophageal varices, and Barrett esophagus","codes":["GI-13-C2","GI-13-C5","GI-13-J1","GI-13-G2"],"case":"A 57-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of increasing abdominal girth. He has obesity (BMI 38 kg/m2), type 2 diabetes, 20 years of heartburn, and known cirrhosis from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis (MASH). He does not drink alcohol. Physical examination shows shifting dullness, spider angiomas, and a palpable spleen tip. Laboratory studies show albumin 2.6 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0), platelet count 82,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000), and INR 1.8 (0.8 to 1.2). Paracentesis yields clear, straw-colored fluid with a serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG) of 1.8 g/dL (1.1 or higher points to portal hypertension) and no increase in neutrophils. Screening endoscopy shows large distal esophageal varices and a tongue of salmon-colored mucosa extending 4 cm above the gastroesophageal junction. Diagnosis: MASH cirrhosis with portal hypertension, ascites, esophageal varices, and Barrett esophagus.","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C01-Q1","from":[32],"stem":"His varices form where portal blood is diverted into systemic veins at the lower esophagus. Which of the following portal tributaries carries this blood backward into the esophageal submucosal veins?","options":["Left gastric vein","Superior rectal vein","Paraumbilical veins","Inferior mesenteric vein"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-13-J1","obj":"GI-13-J.1","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the varices: distal esophagus.","Name the anastomosis there: portal left gastric (coronary) vein meets systemic esophageal veins that drain to the azygos system.","With portal hypertension, blood flows backward through the left gastric vein into the thin-walled submucosal veins."],"why":"The left gastric (coronary) vein drains the lesser curvature of the stomach into the portal vein, and its esophageal branches connect with esophageal veins that drain to the azygos system. When portal pressure rises, blood is pushed backward through the left gastric vein into these submucosal esophageal veins. They dilate into varices that can rupture and cause massive hematemesis, a leading cause of death in cirrhosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","The superior rectal vein is the portal side of the anorectal anastomosis with the middle and inferior rectal veins. In portal hypertension it feeds anorectal varices, not esophageal ones.","The paraumbilical veins run in the falciform ligament and connect with superficial veins of the abdominal wall. When they dilate, they produce caput medusae around the umbilicus.","The inferior mesenteric vein drains the left colon and upper rectum into the splenic vein. It does not reach the esophagus."],"study":{"topics":["Portosystemic anastomoses","Esophageal varices"],"comps":["GI-13-J1","GI-5","GI-13-C5"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C01-Q2","from":[56],"stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains his ascites?","options":["Blocked thoracic duct releasing milky, fat-rich lymph","Tumor implants on the peritoneum leaking protein-rich fluid","Bacterial infection of the peritoneum producing an exudate","High sinusoidal pressure with low plasma albumin"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-I1","obj":"GI-13-I.1","dok":2,"approach":["Read the SAAG: 1.1 g/dL or higher means portal hypertension drives the fluid.","Add the low albumin from reduced hepatic synthesis, which lowers plasma oncotic pressure.","Combine the two Starling forces: high hydrostatic pressure out, low oncotic pressure holding fluid in."],"why":"Scarring in cirrhosis raises resistance in the sinusoids, so hydrostatic pressure in the sinusoids and splanchnic capillaries rises and fluid leaks into the peritoneal cavity. The failing liver also makes less albumin, which lowers plasma oncotic pressure. Splanchnic vasodilation lowers effective arterial volume and activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, so the kidneys retain sodium and water that keep the ascites coming back. A SAAG of 1.1 g/dL or more confirms that portal hypertension is the driver.","whyNot":["Chylous ascites from lymphatic disruption (lymphoma, trauma, surgery) is milky and high in triglycerides. His fluid is clear and straw colored.","Peritoneal carcinomatosis produces protein-rich fluid with a SAAG below 1.1 g/dL. His SAAG of 1.8 g/dL points to portal hypertension instead.","Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis is an infection of ascites that is already present, and it raises the ascitic neutrophil count. His fluid has no increase in neutrophils, and infection does not explain why the ascites formed.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Ascites and SAAG","Starling forces"],"comps":["GI-13-I1","GI-13-C5"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C01-Q3","from":[46],"stem":"His INR of 1.8 reflects falling hepatic synthesis of clotting factors. Deficiency of which of the following factors prolongs the prothrombin time (PT) earliest as liver function declines?","options":["Factor VIII, made mostly by sinusoidal endothelium","Factor IX, which the prothrombin time measures directly","Factor VII, which has the shortest half-life","Factor XII, which begins the extrinsic coagulation pathway"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-C2","obj":"GI-13-C.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall what the PT measures: the extrinsic and common pathways (VII, X, V, II, fibrinogen).","Recall that the liver makes these factors, including the vitamin K-dependent factors II, VII, IX, and X.","Pick the factor that falls first when synthesis drops: the one with the shortest half-life."],"why":"Hepatocytes make most clotting factors, including the vitamin K-dependent factors II, VII, IX, and X and the anticoagulant proteins C and S. When synthesis fails, the factor with the shortest plasma half-life falls first. Factor VII lasts only about 4 to 6 hours, and because it starts the extrinsic pathway, the PT and INR rise early in liver failure. Vitamin K does not fully correct this, because the problem is synthesis, not vitamin supply.","whyNot":["Factor VIII is made mainly by sinusoidal and other endothelial cells, and its level is often normal or high in cirrhosis. It is measured by the aPTT, not the PT.","Factor IX belongs to the intrinsic pathway and is measured by the aPTT. A low factor IX level prolongs the aPTT, as in hemophilia B.","Correct.","Factor XII starts the intrinsic (contact) pathway, and its deficiency prolongs the aPTT without bleeding. The extrinsic pathway begins with tissue factor and factor VII."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatic synthesis of clotting factors","PT versus aPTT"],"comps":["GI-13-C2","GI-9"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C01-Q4","from":[57],"stem":"Biopsy of the salmon-colored segment shows columnar epithelium with goblet cells where stratified squamous epithelium should be. This change is a precursor of which of the following tumors?","options":["Primary lymphoma of the esophageal wall","Small cell carcinoma of the esophagus","Adenocarcinoma of the distal esophagus","Squamous cell carcinoma of the mid esophagus"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G2","obj":"GI-13-G.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize intestinal metaplasia (goblet cells) in the distal esophagus: Barrett esophagus from chronic reflux.","Follow the sequence: metaplasia, then dysplasia, then carcinoma.","A glandular precursor gives a glandular cancer: adenocarcinoma near the gastroesophageal junction."],"why":"Years of acid and bile reflux replace the distal esophageal squamous lining with intestinal-type columnar epithelium containing goblet cells. This metaplastic epithelium can become dysplastic and then progress to adenocarcinoma, which arises in the distal esophagus and gastroesophageal junction. Obesity adds to his risk because it promotes reflux. Patients with Barrett esophagus are followed with surveillance endoscopy to catch dysplasia early.","whyNot":["Primary esophageal lymphoma is very rare and does not arise from intestinal metaplasia.","Small cell carcinoma of the esophagus is a rare neuroendocrine tumor with no link to Barrett metaplasia.","Correct.","Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma arises from the native squamous lining, usually in the middle third. Its main risks are tobacco, alcohol, very hot drinks, and achalasia, not columnar metaplasia."],"study":{"topics":["Barrett esophagus","Esophageal cancer types"],"comps":["GI-13-G2","GI-13-K1","GI-13-G8"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C02","system":"GI","dx":"Adenocarcinoma of the head of the pancreas with obstructive jaundice","codes":["GI-13-K6","GI-13-C1"],"case":"A 68-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of itching, dark urine, pale greasy stools, and a 7-kg weight loss. She has no abdominal pain. Physical examination shows jaundice and a smooth, nontender, pear-shaped mass below the right costal margin. Laboratory studies show total bilirubin 9.4 mg/dL (0.1 to 1.2) with direct bilirubin 7.9 mg/dL (0 to 0.3), alkaline phosphatase 610 U/L (40 to 120), and CA 19-9 1,250 U/mL (under 37). CT shows a 3-cm mass in the head of the pancreas, dilated common bile and pancreatic ducts, a distended gallbladder without stones, and tumor contact with the portal vein. Biopsy confirms ductal adenocarcinoma of the pancreatic head.","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C02-Q1","from":[58],"stem":"Which of the following best explains why her gallbladder is enlarged and nontender?","options":["Slow tumor blockage of the distal bile duct stretches a healthy gallbladder","A stone lodged in the cystic duct inflames and swells the gallbladder wall","Years of stone-related inflammation have thickened and enlarged the gallbladder","Tumor has spread into the gallbladder wall and formed a bulky mass there"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K6","obj":"GI-13-K.9","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the sign: painless jaundice with a palpable, nontender gallbladder (Courvoisier sign).","Ask what lets a gallbladder distend: a healthy, compliant wall and slowly rising pressure below it.","Match the cause: gradual distal bile duct blockage by a pancreatic head tumor."],"why":"The pancreatic head surrounds the distal common bile duct, so a tumor there slowly narrows the duct. Bile backs up gradually, and a gallbladder with a normal, compliant wall stretches until it can be felt. Because the process is slow and not inflammatory, the gallbladder is not tender. Gallstone disease scars the wall, so obstruction from a stone rarely produces a large palpable gallbladder.","whyNot":["Correct.","A stone impacted in the cystic duct causes acute cholecystitis, which makes the gallbladder tender (Murphy sign) and causes pain and fever. Her gallbladder is painless and has no stones.","Chronic stone disease scars the gallbladder wall, so it becomes small and stiff and cannot distend. This is why a palpable, painless gallbladder points away from stones.","Pancreatic head cancer blocks the bile duct; it does not usually invade the gallbladder. CT shows a distended gallbladder without a wall mass."],"study":{"topics":["Courvoisier sign","Obstructive jaundice"],"comps":["GI-13-K6","GI-13-C1"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C02-Q2","from":[34],"stem":"At surgery, the surgeon places a finger in the omental (epiploic) foramen and the thumb on its anterior border. Which of the following groups of structures runs in this free edge of the lesser omentum?","options":["Inferior vena cava, hepatic veins, and cystic duct","Hepatic artery proper, portal vein, and bile duct","Splenic artery, splenic vein, and pancreatic duct","Left gastric artery, portal vein, and thoracic duct"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-4","obj":"GI-4.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the free edge of the lesser omentum: the hepatoduodenal ligament, the anterior border of the omental foramen.","Recall its contents, the portal triad: bile duct (right), hepatic artery proper (left), portal vein (posterior).","Check each option against that list."],"why":"The hepatoduodenal ligament is the free right edge of the lesser omentum and forms the front wall of the omental foramen. It carries the common bile duct on the right, the hepatic artery proper on the left, and the portal vein behind both. Surgeons use this relation to find the bile duct, and squeezing the ligament (the Pringle maneuver) stops inflow to the liver during bleeding.","whyNot":["The inferior vena cava lies behind the omental foramen, forming its posterior wall, and the hepatic veins drain into it above the liver. Neither runs in the hepatoduodenal ligament.","Correct.","The splenic artery and vein run along the upper and back surface of the pancreas, and the pancreatic duct runs inside the gland. None of them lies in the lesser omentum.","The left gastric artery runs in the lesser omentum along the lesser curvature, not in its free edge, and the thoracic duct is in the posterior mediastinum."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatoduodenal ligament","Omental foramen"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-4","GI-5"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C02-Q3","from":[31],"stem":"If the tumor occludes the portal vein, venous pressure will rise in which of the following groups of organs?","options":["Lower limbs, pelvic walls, and bladder","Kidneys, adrenal glands, and gonads","Stomach, spleen, and intestines","Head, neck, and upper limbs"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall how the portal vein forms: the superior mesenteric vein joins the splenic vein behind the neck of the pancreas.","List what those veins drain: the abdominal gut from stomach to upper rectum, plus the spleen, pancreas, and gallbladder.","Choose the option made only of portal territory."],"why":"The portal vein forms behind the neck of the pancreas, where the superior mesenteric vein joins the splenic vein, so pancreatic head tumors often touch or block it. It carries blood from the stomach, small and large intestines, spleen, pancreas, and gallbladder to the liver, which processes absorbed nutrients and drugs before they reach the systemic circulation. Blockage raises pressure throughout this territory and can cause splenomegaly, bowel congestion, and gastric varices.","whyNot":["The lower limbs, pelvic walls, and bladder drain through the iliac veins into the inferior vena cava.","The kidneys, adrenal glands, and gonads drain into the inferior vena cava or the left renal vein, which are systemic veins.","Correct.","The head, neck, and upper limbs drain into the superior vena cava."],"study":{"topics":["Portal venous system","Portal vein formation"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-13-C5"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C02-Q4","from":[38],"stem":"Her pale, greasy stools reflect blocked flow of bile and pancreatic juice. Normally, fat and protein entering the duodenum release a hormone that contracts the gallbladder and stimulates pancreatic acinar enzyme secretion. Which of the following cells releases this hormone?","options":["S cells of the duodenal mucosa","I cells of the duodenum and jejunum","G cells of the gastric antrum","K cells of the duodenum and upper jejunum"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the hormone from its two actions: gallbladder contraction plus acinar enzyme secretion is cholecystokinin (CCK).","Recall its trigger: fatty acids and amino acids in the duodenum.","Recall its source: I cells of the duodenum and jejunum."],"why":"Cholecystokinin (CCK) is released by I cells in the duodenal and jejunal mucosa when fatty acids and amino acids arrive. It contracts the gallbladder, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, stimulates acinar cells to secrete lipase, amylase, and proteases, and slows gastric emptying. In this patient the hormone still works, but the tumor blocks both ducts, so bile and enzymes cannot reach the gut and fat is poorly digested.","whyNot":["S cells release secretin when acid enters the duodenum. Secretin stimulates bicarbonate and water secretion from pancreatic duct cells, not gallbladder contraction.","Correct.","G cells release gastrin, which stimulates gastric acid secretion. Gastrin does not contract the gallbladder.","K cells release glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP), which raises insulin release after a meal. It does not drive gallbladder contraction or enzyme secretion."],"study":{"topics":["CCK","GI hormones and their cells"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-7"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C03","system":"GI","dx":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome from a duodenal gastrinoma","codes":["GI-13-K3","GI-13-G5"],"case":"A 46-year-old man comes to the physician because of watery diarrhea with greasy, foul-smelling stools. He has had three duodenal ulcers in 2 years. Helicobacter pylori was found on gastric biopsy at his first endoscopy and treated, and a negative stool antigen test confirmed eradication, yet ulcers returned, including one in the proximal jejunum. Off acid-suppressing drugs, fasting serum gastrin is 1,150 pg/mL (under 100) with a gastric pH of 1.2. Endoscopy shows prominent, thickened gastric folds. Imaging finds a 1.5-cm mass in the duodenal wall. Diagnosis: Zollinger-Ellison syndrome from a duodenal gastrinoma.","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C03-Q1","from":[39,51],"stem":"His first gastric biopsy showed curved organisms in the mucus layer of the antrum. Which of the following best describes this organism and how it survives gastric acid?","options":["Gram-positive, facultative anaerobic rod; catalase removes peroxide","Gram-positive coccus in chains; a toxin increases acid output","Gram-negative, strictly anaerobic rod; spores resist the acid","Gram-negative, microaerophilic spiral rod; urease makes ammonia"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism: curved rods in the antral mucus layer of an ulcer patient are Helicobacter pylori.","Recall its features: gram negative, microaerophilic, helical or spiral rod with flagella.","Recall its acid survival tool: urease splits urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide, buffering the acid around it."],"why":"Helicobacter pylori is a gram-negative, microaerophilic, flagellated spiral rod that lives in the mucus layer over the gastric epithelium, mostly in the antrum. Its urease splits urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide, and the ammonia neutralizes acid in its immediate surroundings while also injuring epithelial cells. Flagella let it swim through mucus, and the CagA and VacA proteins add to tissue damage. The same urease is the basis of the urea breath test and the rapid urease biopsy test.","whyNot":["Helicobacter pylori is gram negative, not gram positive. It does make catalase, but catalase protects against neutrophil peroxide, not against gastric acid.","Helicobacter pylori is a curved rod, not a coccus, and it does not survive by raising acid output. Gram-positive cocci in chains are streptococci.","Helicobacter pylori is microaerophilic, not a strict anaerobe, and it does not form spores. Spore formation is a feature of gram-positive rods such as Clostridium and Bacillus.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Helicobacter pylori virulence","Gram-negative spiral organisms"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-G5","GI-13-G7"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C03-Q2","from":[43],"stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains his greasy, foul-smelling stools?","options":["Excess acid in the duodenum inactivates pancreatic lipase","Gastrin acts on enterocytes and drives chloride secretion","Gastrin overstimulates the gallbladder and causes bile acid loss","Gastrin blocks CCK receptors on pancreatic acinar cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-13-K3","obj":"GI-13-K.4","dok":3,"approach":["Start with the cause: autonomous gastrin secretion drives huge acid output.","Follow the acid: it overwhelms pancreatic bicarbonate, so the duodenal pH stays low.","Apply enzyme pH limits: lipase is inactivated in acid, and bile acids precipitate, so fat is not digested."],"why":"The gastrinoma releases gastrin continuously, so parietal cells secrete large volumes of acid. This acid overwhelms the bicarbonate from the pancreas, and the duodenal pH stays low. Pancreatic lipase is inactivated at low pH, and bile acids precipitate, so fewer micelles form and fat is poorly digested and absorbed. The result is steatorrhea, alongside the watery diarrhea caused by the sheer volume of gastric secretion.","whyNot":["Correct.","Gastrin does not stimulate intestinal chloride secretion. Secretory diarrhea from chloride secretion is the mechanism of cholera toxin and VIP-secreting tumors, and it does not cause fat malabsorption.","Gastrin is a weak stimulus for gallbladder contraction at most. Bile acid diarrhea follows ileal disease or resection, which he does not have.","Gastrin and CCK share a receptor family, but gastrin does not block acinar CCK receptors. Enzyme secretion is still present; the enzymes are inactivated after they reach the acidic duodenum."],"study":{"topics":["Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","Fat digestion and pH"],"comps":["GI-13-K3","GI-7"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C03-Q3","from":[37],"stem":"Gastrin is the main hormonal stimulus of gastric acid secretion. Besides acting on parietal cells directly, it raises acid output by making a neighboring cell release a paracrine mediator that binds H2 receptors on parietal cells. Which of the following cells releases this mediator?","options":["D cell of the oxyntic mucosa","Chief cell at the gland base","Mucous neck cell of the gland","Enterochromaffin-like cell"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.3","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the mediator from the receptor: H2 receptors bind histamine.","Name the gastric cell that releases histamine: the enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cell.","Link it to gastrin: gastrin binds CCK2 receptors on ECL cells and triggers histamine release."],"why":"Gastrin from antral G cells, or here from the tumor, binds CCK2 (CCK-B) receptors on parietal cells and on enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells. ECL cells respond by releasing histamine, which binds H2 receptors on nearby parietal cells and raises cAMP, strongly increasing H+/K+-ATPase activity. This is why H2 blockers reduce gastrin-driven acid secretion. Long-term high gastrin also makes ECL cells and parietal cells multiply.","whyNot":["D cells release somatostatin, which inhibits gastrin release and acid secretion. Somatostatin does not act on H2 receptors.","Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and gastric lipase. They do not release histamine.","Mucous neck cells secrete mucus and are stem-like precursors. They do not release histamine.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Regulation of gastric acid secretion","Gastrin and ECL cells"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-2"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C03-Q4","from":[33],"stem":"To confirm that his thickened folds come from hyperplasia of the acid-secreting cells, the endoscopist biopsies the region where those cells are concentrated. Which of the following regions is most likely biopsied?","options":["Antrum near the pylorus","Gastric body and fundus","Cardia near the esophagus","First part of the duodenum"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Name the acid-secreting cell: the parietal cell.","Recall where parietal cells live: the oxyntic glands of the gastric body and fundus.","Remember that gastrin is trophic to this mucosa, so chronic hypergastrinemia thickens its folds."],"why":"Parietal cells, which secrete hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor, are concentrated in the oxyntic glands of the gastric body and fundus, along with chief cells that secrete pepsinogen. The antrum holds G cells that make gastrin, and the cardia holds mainly mucous glands. Constant high gastrin from a gastrinoma makes parietal cells and ECL cells multiply, so the body and fundus mucosa thickens into prominent folds.","whyNot":["The antral glands contain G cells and mucous cells and have few parietal cells. Gastrin from the antrum acts on the body and fundus.","Correct.","The cardiac glands near the gastroesophageal junction are mostly mucus secreting and contain few parietal cells.","The duodenum has no parietal cells. Its Brunner glands secrete alkaline mucus."],"study":{"topics":["Gastric gland cell types","Regions of the stomach"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-3"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C04","system":"GI","dx":"Celiac disease with iron deficiency anemia and coexisting primary biliary cholangitis","codes":["GI-13-E2","GI-13-C1"],"case":"A 41-year-old woman comes to the physician because of an 8-month history of bloating, loose bulky stools, fatigue, and a 5-kg weight loss, and a 1-year history of generalized itching. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 9.6 g/dL (12.0 to 16.0), MCV 71 fL (80 to 100), and ferritin 5 ng/mL (15 to 150). Tissue transglutaminase IgA is strongly positive. Duodenal biopsy shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes. Alkaline phosphatase is 390 U/L (40 to 120) with normal bilirubin, antimitochondrial antibody (AMA) is positive, and ultrasound shows no bile duct dilation. Diagnosis: celiac disease with iron deficiency anemia and coexisting primary biliary cholangitis (PBC), an autoimmune liver disease that is more common in people with celiac disease.","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C04-Q1","from":[41],"stem":"Because of where celiac disease damages the bowel, which of the following is she most likely to malabsorb early in her disease?","options":["Fat and fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K","Vitamin B12 bound to intrinsic factor","Short-chain fatty acids from colonic fermentation","Conjugated bile acids returning to the liver"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-E2","obj":"GI-13-E.2","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the injury: celiac disease is worst in the duodenum and proximal jejunum.","Recall what those segments absorb: most dietary fat and fat-soluble vitamins, plus iron, calcium, and folate.","Exclude options absorbed in the terminal ileum or colon."],"why":"Gluten-driven immune injury flattens the villi most severely in the duodenum and proximal jejunum, where most dietary fat and the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K are absorbed. The loss of surface area causes bulky, fatty stools and deficiencies such as low vitamin D with bone loss and low vitamin K with easy bruising. Her PBC can add to fat malabsorption by reducing bile flow late in its course. The terminal ileum and colon are usually spared early in celiac disease.","whyNot":["Correct.","The vitamin B12 and intrinsic factor complex is absorbed in the terminal ileum, which celiac disease usually spares early on. B12 deficiency suggests ileal disease, as in Crohn disease.","Short-chain fatty acids are made and absorbed in the colon, which celiac disease does not damage.","Conjugated bile acids are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum. Their loss follows ileal disease or resection."],"study":{"topics":["Celiac disease malabsorption","Fat-soluble vitamins"],"comps":["GI-13-E2","GI-6","GI-8"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C04-Q2","from":[40],"stem":"Her iron deficiency persists even though her diet contains enough iron. Which of the following best explains why celiac disease often causes iron deficiency?","options":["Iron is absorbed mostly in the terminal ileum, where gluten acts","Iron absorption needs intrinsic factor made by parietal cells","Iron is absorbed mostly in the duodenum, where damage is worst","Iron absorption needs bile acid micelles to cross the mucosa"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the absorption site for iron (and calcium): the duodenum and proximal jejunum.","Recall the site of maximal celiac injury: the same proximal segment.","Connect them: proximal villous atrophy causes iron deficiency that does not respond well to oral iron."],"why":"Dietary nonheme iron is reduced from Fe3+ to Fe2+ at the brush border and enters duodenal enterocytes through DMT1, then leaves through ferroportin. Active, calcitriol-driven calcium absorption also happens mainly in the duodenum. Because celiac disease damages the duodenum most, iron deficiency anemia is one of its most common presentations, sometimes without diarrhea. Unexplained iron deficiency that does not respond to oral iron is a classic reason to test for celiac disease.","whyNot":["The terminal ileum absorbs vitamin B12 and bile acids, not most of the iron. Celiac damage is also worst proximally.","Intrinsic factor is needed for vitamin B12 absorption, not iron absorption.","Correct.","Bile acid micelles carry fats and fat-soluble vitamins. Iron is taken up by a membrane transporter without micelles."],"study":{"topics":["Iron absorption","Nutrient absorption sites"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-8","GI-13-E2"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C04-Q3","from":[47],"stem":"Her liver adjusts iron absorption to her depleted iron stores by changing its output of a peptide hormone. Which of the following changes occurs, and what is its effect?","options":["Hepcidin rises, so ferroportin is degraded and absorption falls","Hepcidin falls, so more ferroportin stays in cell membranes","Transferrin falls, so less iron is carried away from enterocytes","Erythropoietin falls, so the marrow's demand for iron decreases"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.6","dok":3,"approach":["Name the liver hormone that controls iron: hepcidin.","Recall its action: it binds ferroportin and causes its internalization and degradation.","Apply to low stores: hepcidin falls, ferroportin stays, and enterocytes and macrophages release more iron into blood."],"why":"Hepcidin, made by hepatocytes, is the main regulator of body iron. It binds ferroportin, the only known iron exporter, on enterocytes and macrophages and causes it to be degraded, which traps iron inside those cells. When iron stores are low, hepcidin falls, ferroportin stays in the membrane, and absorption rises. In hereditary hemochromatosis, hepcidin is inappropriately low despite iron overload, so absorption continues unchecked.","whyNot":["Hepcidin rises when iron stores are full or with inflammation, which lowers iron absorption. With a ferritin of 5 ng/mL, her stores are empty, so hepcidin is suppressed.","Correct.","Transferrin is a carrier protein, not a regulating hormone, and its level rises in iron deficiency (high TIBC).","Erythropoietin is made mainly by the kidney, not the liver, and anemia raises it."],"study":{"topics":["Hepcidin and ferroportin","Iron homeostasis"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-9"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C04-Q4","from":[60],"stem":"Her itching and high alkaline phosphatase come from autoimmune injury in the liver. Which of the following cells are the main target of this injury?","options":["Kupffer cells lining the hepatic sinusoids","Stellate cells in the space of Disse","Hepatocytes around the central veins","Small intrahepatic bile duct cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C1","obj":"GI-13-C.2","dok":2,"approach":["Read the pattern: itching and high alkaline phosphatase with normal duct imaging is intrahepatic cholestasis.","Add the AMA: this marks primary biliary cholangitis.","Name the target: epithelial cells (cholangiocytes) of small intrahepatic bile ducts."],"why":"Primary biliary cholangitis is an autoimmune disease in which T cells destroy the epithelial cells (cholangiocytes) of small intrahepatic bile ducts. Bile backs up, causing itching, high alkaline phosphatase, and later jaundice, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency, and cirrhosis. Antimitochondrial antibodies against the E2 subunit of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex are found in more than 90% of patients. It mainly affects middle-aged women and often occurs with other autoimmune diseases.","whyNot":["Kupffer cells are resident macrophages. They take part in liver inflammation but are not the target of PBC.","Stellate cells store vitamin A and become collagen-producing myofibroblasts in fibrosis. They cause scarring but are not the target of the autoimmune attack.","Hepatocytes are the main target in autoimmune and viral hepatitis, which raise ALT and AST far more than alkaline phosphatase.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Primary biliary cholangitis","Cholestasis patterns"],"comps":["GI-13-C1","GI-11"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C05","system":"GI","dx":"Crohn disease of the terminal ileum and colon","codes":["GI-13-G9","GI-13-K7"],"case":"A 54-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of right lower quadrant cramping, nonbloody diarrhea, and an 8-kg weight loss. Her temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F). Physical examination shows a tender mass in the right lower quadrant. Colonoscopy shows deep linear ulcers and a cobblestone pattern in the terminal ileum and in patches of the ascending colon, with normal mucosa between them and a normal rectum. A 1.4-cm tubular adenoma is removed from her sigmoid colon, which is not inflamed. Ileal biopsies show transmural inflammation with non-caseating granulomas. Diagnosis: Crohn disease of the terminal ileum and colon.","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C05-Q1","from":[53],"stem":"Which of the following best describes the main cells in her ileal granulomas and the cytokine that drives their formation?","options":["Plasma cells and B cells, activated by IL-4 from Th2 cells","Neutrophils and mast cells, activated by IL-5 from Th2 cells","Epithelioid macrophages, driven by IFN-gamma from Th1 cells","Eosinophils and NK cells, activated by IL-17 from Th17 cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize a granuloma: a collection of activated macrophages.","Recall the cell types: epithelioid macrophages, often with multinucleated giant cells, ringed by CD4 T cells.","Recall the signal: Th1 cells release IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages (type IV hypersensitivity)."],"why":"Granulomas form through a type IV, cell-mediated response. Macrophages present antigen and release IL-12, which drives CD4 T cells toward the Th1 type. Th1 cells release IFN-gamma, which turns macrophages into epithelioid cells that can fuse into multinucleated giant cells, surrounded by a cuff of lymphocytes. In Crohn disease the granulomas are non-caseating, which helps separate it from tuberculosis.","whyNot":["Plasma cells and B cells make antibodies, and IL-4 drives class switching to IgE. Antibody responses do not build granulomas.","Neutrophils form abscesses and mast cells drive immediate hypersensitivity. IL-5 recruits and activates eosinophils, and none of these form granulomas.","Correct.","Eosinophils and NK cells are not the core of granulomas. IL-17 recruits neutrophils and contributes to Crohn inflammation, but the granuloma itself is organized by Th1 cells."],"study":{"topics":["Granuloma formation","Type IV hypersensitivity"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-G9"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C05-Q2","from":[55],"stem":"Six months later she has pneumaturia and recurrent urinary tract infections with enteric organisms. Which of the following features of her disease, in contrast to ulcerative colitis, best explains this complication?","options":["Continuous mucosal inflammation spreading up from the rectum","Crypt abscesses confined to the mucosa and submucosa","Loss of haustra that leaves a short, tube-shaped colon","Transmural inflammation that tunnels into nearby organs"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G9","obj":"GI-13-G.10","dok":3,"approach":["Read the new clue: pneumaturia with enteric organisms means a fistula from bowel to bladder.","Ask what lets inflammation reach the bladder: inflammation through the full thickness of the bowel wall.","Match to the disease: transmural, patchy (skip) inflammation is Crohn disease; mucosal, continuous inflammation from the rectum is ulcerative colitis."],"why":"Crohn disease inflames the full thickness of the bowel wall in patches (skip lesions) anywhere from mouth to anus, most often the terminal ileum. Deep fissuring ulcers can tunnel through the wall and form fistulas to bowel, bladder, vagina, or skin, and they can also cause abscesses and strictures. Ulcerative colitis involves only the mucosa and submucosa, starting at the rectum and spreading continuously, so fistulas are not a feature of it.","whyNot":["Continuous inflammation spreading upward from the rectum is the pattern of ulcerative colitis. Her rectum is normal and her disease is patchy.","Inflammation limited to the mucosa and submucosa is typical of ulcerative colitis, and it does not reach other organs.","A shortened colon without haustra (lead-pipe colon) is a late feature of ulcerative colitis and does not create tracts to the bladder.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Crohn disease versus ulcerative colitis","Fistulas in Crohn disease"],"comps":["GI-13-G9"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C05-Q3","from":[35],"stem":"Two years later, about 80 cm of her terminal ileum is removed because of a stricture. For which of the following vitamins will she most likely need lifelong monitoring and replacement?","options":["Vitamin B12, taken up with intrinsic factor by ileal receptors","Vitamin C, taken up only after binding gastric intrinsic factor","Folate, taken up mainly by the terminal ileum after digestion","Thiamine, taken up mainly by the terminal ileum with bile acids"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify what was removed: the terminal ileum.","Recall what only the terminal ileum absorbs: the vitamin B12 and intrinsic factor complex (and bile acids).","Predict the result: B12 deficiency and megaloblastic anemia once liver stores run out over years."],"why":"Parietal cells secrete intrinsic factor, which binds vitamin B12 in the duodenum after pancreatic proteases free it from haptocorrin. The B12 and intrinsic factor complex is absorbed only in the terminal ileum through cubilin receptors. Removing a long stretch of terminal ileum, or severe ileal Crohn disease, therefore causes B12 malabsorption. Liver stores last years, so deficiency appears late, which is why monitoring and replacement are lifelong.","whyNot":["Correct.","Vitamin C does not bind intrinsic factor. Intrinsic factor is specific for vitamin B12.","Folate is absorbed mainly in the duodenum and jejunum, so it is not lost with ileal resection.","Thiamine is absorbed mainly in the jejunum and does not depend on bile acids."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin B12 absorption","Ileal resection effects"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-8"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C05-Q4","from":[59],"stem":"The sigmoid tubular adenoma arose in a segment free of colitis, so it follows the usual sporadic pathway. Which of the following sequences of genetic changes drives that pathway from normal mucosa to carcinoma?","options":["TP53 loss early, with APC loss only late in the process","APC loss, then KRAS activation, then TP53 loss","MLH1 or MSH2 loss, causing microsatellite instability","BRCA2 loss, causing failed homologous recombination repair"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-10","obj":"GI-10.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the lesion: a tubular adenoma in uninflamed colon follows the adenoma-carcinoma sequence.","Recall the order: APC loss starts the adenoma, KRAS activation enlarges it, and TP53 loss allows invasive cancer.","Separate it from the mismatch repair pathway and from colitis-associated cancer."],"why":"Most sporadic colorectal cancers follow the chromosomal instability pathway. Loss of both APC alleles turns on Wnt signaling and starts the adenoma, activating KRAS mutations let it grow, and loss of SMAD4 and TP53 allows the shift to invasive carcinoma. Adenomas take years to progress, which is why removing them prevents cancer. Cancers arising in colitis, as can happen in long-standing Crohn colitis, tend to lose TP53 early instead.","whyNot":["Early TP53 loss with late APC loss is the order seen in colitis-associated cancer, which arises in chronically inflamed mucosa without a preceding adenoma. Her adenoma arose outside the colitis.","Correct.","Loss of mismatch repair genes causes microsatellite instability. This happens in Lynch syndrome and in sporadic cancers with MLH1 promoter silencing, together about 15% of colorectal cancers, so it is not the usual pathway.","BRCA2 loss is linked to breast, ovarian, prostate, and pancreatic cancers. It is not a step in the colorectal adenoma sequence."],"study":{"topics":["Adenoma-carcinoma sequence","Colorectal cancer pathways"],"comps":["GI-13-K7","GI-10"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C06","system":"GI","dx":"Hemolytic uremic syndrome from enterohemorrhagic Escherichia coli O157:H7","codes":["GI-13-L1","GI-13-L3"],"case":"A 6-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of pallor and decreased urine output. Six days ago he developed abdominal cramps and watery diarrhea that became bloody; the illness began 4 days after he ate an undercooked hamburger at a barbecue. He has had no fever. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 7.8 g/dL (11.5 to 15.5), platelet count 42,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000), creatinine 2.6 mg/dL (0.3 to 0.7), and a high LDH. The direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test is negative, and the blood smear shows schistocytes. Stool testing detects Shiga toxin, and culture grows Escherichia coli O157:H7. Diagnosis: hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) caused by enterohemorrhagic E. coli (EHEC).","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C06-Q1","from":[52],"stem":"The toxin in his stool binds the glycolipid Gb3 on endothelial cells of the colon and glomeruli and enters them. Which of the following is the action of its A subunit inside the cell?","options":["ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2 in the cytosol","Links MHC class II to T-cell receptors outside the groove","Cleaves synaptobrevin and blocks vesicle release","Removes one adenine from 28S rRNA in the 60S subunit"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the toxin: EHEC O157:H7 makes Shiga toxin (Shiga-like toxin, verotoxin).","Recall its enzyme action: the A subunit is an N-glycosidase that removes a single adenine from 28S rRNA of the 60S ribosomal subunit.","Connect to disease: protein synthesis stops, endothelial cells die, and microthrombi form in glomeruli."],"why":"Shiga toxin is an AB5 toxin. Its B pentamer binds Gb3 (globotriaosylceramide), which is plentiful on glomerular and colonic endothelial cells. Inside the cell, the A subunit acts as an N-glycosidase that removes one specific adenine from the 28S rRNA of the 60S ribosomal subunit, so aminoacyl-tRNA can no longer bind and protein synthesis stops. Endothelial injury exposes a surface that triggers platelet-fibrin microthrombi, producing HUS.","whyNot":["ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2 is how diphtheria toxin and Pseudomonas exotoxin A stop protein synthesis.","Linking MHC class II to T-cell receptors outside the peptide groove is the action of superantigens such as TSST-1, which cause toxic shock.","Cleaving synaptobrevin is the action of tetanus toxin and some botulinum toxins, which block neurotransmitter release.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Shiga toxin mechanism","EHEC and HUS"],"comps":["GI-13-L1","GI-12"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C06-Q2","from":[54],"stem":"While his culture was pending, the health department asked whether the family keeps backyard poultry or pet reptiles. That question screens for a different cause of bloody diarrhea. Which of the following identifies that organism and explains why the question fits?","options":["Shigella, which lives only in humans and spreads person to person","Vibrio cholerae, which lives in poultry and causes bloody stools","Salmonella, which has animal hosts and can enter the blood","Shigella, which has animal hosts and spreads mainly through eggs"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L3","obj":"GI-13-L.2","dok":3,"approach":["Spot the exposure clue: poultry, eggs, and reptiles are animal reservoirs.","Recall which enteric pathogen has those reservoirs: nontyphoidal Salmonella.","Contrast with Shigella: humans are the only reservoir, a very small dose causes infection, and spread is person to person."],"why":"Nontyphoidal Salmonella lives in animals, especially poultry, eggs, and reptiles, so public health teams ask about these exposures. It invades the intestinal mucosa and can enter the bloodstream, particularly in infants, older adults, and people with sickle cell disease or weak immunity. Shigella has no animal reservoir, spreads person to person with a very small infectious dose, and usually stays in the colon. Both are gram-negative rods that do not ferment lactose.","whyNot":["This statement about Shigella is true, but it explains why Shigella spreads in daycares and households. It does not explain a question about poultry and reptiles.","Vibrio cholerae lives in brackish and salt water and in shellfish, and it causes watery, not bloody, diarrhea.","Correct.","Shigella has no animal reservoir, so poultry and eggs are not its usual source."],"study":{"topics":["Salmonella versus Shigella","Reservoirs of enteric pathogens"],"comps":["GI-13-L3","GI-12"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C06-Q3","from":[42],"stem":"Shiga toxin and cholera toxin are both AB5 toxins whose B pentamer binds a cell-surface glycolipid. Cholera toxin, however, causes profuse rice-water diarrhea without blood. Which of the following is the mechanism of its A subunit?","options":["ADP-ribosylates Gs alpha, so cAMP rises and CFTR secretes chloride","Activates guanylyl cyclase, so cGMP rises and NaCl absorption falls","ADP-ribosylates Gi alpha, so adenylyl cyclase loses its inhibition","Forms pores in enterocyte membranes, so the cells lyse"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the cholera toxin target: the alpha subunit of the stimulatory G protein (Gs).","Follow the signal: ADP-ribosylation blocks GTP hydrolysis, so adenylyl cyclase stays on and cAMP rises.","Follow the effect: protein kinase A opens CFTR, chloride and water pour into the lumen, and the cells survive, so the stool is watery without blood."],"why":"Cholera toxin's B pentamer binds the ganglioside GM1 on enterocytes, and its A1 fragment ADP-ribosylates the Gs alpha subunit. This blocks GTP hydrolysis, so Gs stays active and adenylyl cyclase keeps making cAMP. Protein kinase A then opens CFTR chloride channels and reduces NaCl absorption, and water follows into the lumen. The enterocytes stay alive and intact, so the diarrhea is watery and noninflammatory, unlike the bloody diarrhea of EHEC.","whyNot":["Correct.","Raising cGMP through guanylyl cyclase is the action of the heat-stable toxin of enterotoxigenic E. coli, not cholera toxin.","ADP-ribosylation of Gi alpha is the action of pertussis toxin. It raises cAMP in airway cells and lymphocytes, not by locking Gs on.","Forming pores in enterocyte membranes is the action of Clostridium perfringens enterotoxin, which damages the cells. Cholera toxin leaves enterocytes alive and intact."],"study":{"topics":["Cholera toxin mechanism","Secretory diarrhea"],"comps":["GI-13-L3","GI-12","GI-6"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C06-Q4","from":[],"stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains his anemia?","options":["IgG coats red cells, which splenic macrophages then remove","Red cells are sliced by microthrombi in small vessels","Toxin in the blood suppresses red cell production in marrow","Complement lyses red cells that lack CD55 and CD59"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-L1","obj":"GI-13-L.2","dok":2,"approach":["Read the clues: schistocytes, high LDH, low platelets, negative Coombs test.","Name the pattern: microangiopathic hemolytic anemia.","Link to the toxin: endothelial injury triggers platelet-fibrin microthrombi that shear red cells, mainly in glomeruli."],"why":"Shiga toxin injures endothelial cells, especially in the glomeruli. Platelets stick to the damaged surface and form platelet-fibrin microthrombi, which use up platelets and narrow small vessels. Red cells are sliced as they squeeze past these thrombi, producing schistocytes, high LDH, and anemia, while blocked glomerular capillaries cause acute kidney injury. This triad of microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury defines HUS.","whyNot":["IgG-coated red cells cause warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia, which gives a positive direct Coombs test and spherocytes. His Coombs test is negative.","Correct.","Marrow suppression lowers red cell production without fragmented cells or a high LDH. His schistocytes and high LDH point to destruction in the circulation.","Red cells lacking CD55 and CD59 are found in paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, an acquired stem cell disorder unrelated to EHEC."],"study":{"topics":["Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia","HUS pathogenesis"],"comps":["GI-13-L1"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C07","system":"GI","dx":"Acute hepatitis A in a man with Gilbert syndrome","codes":["GI-13-L7","GI-13-C3"],"case":"A 23-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 5-day history of fever, nausea, right upper quadrant pain, and dark urine. Three weeks ago he ate raw oysters on a trip, and two friends from the trip are also ill. His temperature is 38.2 °C (100.8 °F). Physical examination shows jaundice and a tender liver edge. Laboratory studies show ALT 2,150 U/L (7 to 56), total bilirubin 6.8 mg/dL (0.1 to 1.2) with direct bilirubin 4.1 mg/dL (0 to 0.3), and INR 1.1 (0.8 to 1.2). IgM anti-HAV is positive. HBsAg and anti-HBc are negative, and anti-HBs is positive. His records show three episodes of mild jaundice since age 19 during fasting or illness, each with unconjugated bilirubin of 2.4 to 3.0 mg/dL, normal ALT, and normal hemoglobin and reticulocyte counts. Diagnosis: acute hepatitis A.","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C07-Q1","from":[49],"stem":"Which of the following is the most likely course of his hepatitis A infection?","options":["Full recovery with lasting immunity and no carrier state","Chronic infection in most patients, with slow scarring over decades","Chronic infection in about 5% of adults, with later cirrhosis","Latent virus in hepatocytes that reactivates during later stress"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L7","obj":"GI-13-L.9","dok":2,"approach":["Use the clues: raw shellfish and a cluster of ill travelers point to fecal-oral spread, and IgM anti-HAV confirms acute hepatitis A.","Recall the natural history of HAV: acute, self-limited illness.","Exclude options describing chronic hepatitis B or C or latent viruses."],"why":"Hepatitis A is a nonenveloped single-stranded RNA picornavirus spread by the fecal-oral route through contaminated food, water, and shellfish. It causes an acute illness that resolves in nearly all patients, with no chronic infection and no carrier state, and recovery gives lifelong immunity (IgG anti-HAV). Rare patients develop fulminant liver failure, mostly older adults or people with existing liver disease. Hepatitis E is also spread fecal-orally but can become chronic in people with weak immunity.","whyNot":["Correct.","Chronic infection in most patients describes hepatitis C, which spreads mainly through blood.","Chronic infection in about 5% of adults infected in adulthood describes hepatitis B, which spreads through blood and sexual contact.","Hepatitis A, an RNA virus, does not establish latency. Latency and reactivation are features of herpesviruses."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatitis A natural history","Comparison of hepatitis viruses"],"comps":["GI-13-L7","GI-12"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C07-Q2","from":[45],"stem":"Which of the following defects best explains the jaundice episodes recorded since age 19?","options":["Short red cell survival from a membrane skeleton defect","Promoter variant lowering UGT1A1 activity to about 30%","Defective MRP2 transport of conjugated bilirubin into bile","Complete absence of UGT1A1 activity from a coding mutation"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-C3","obj":"GI-13-C.6","dok":3,"approach":["Characterize the episodes: mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia triggered by fasting or illness, with normal ALT.","Exclude hemolysis: normal hemoglobin and reticulocytes.","Name the condition and defect: Gilbert syndrome, reduced (not absent) UGT1A1 activity, most often from an extra TA repeat in the promoter."],"why":"Gilbert syndrome is the most common inherited cause of hyperbilirubinemia. In most people of European descent it comes from an extra TA repeat in the UGT1A1 promoter, which lowers expression of bilirubin UDP-glucuronosyltransferase to about 30% of normal. Bilirubin stays normal or mildly high and rises with fasting, illness, stress, or exertion. Liver enzymes and liver structure are normal, and no treatment is needed.","whyNot":["A red cell membrane defect such as hereditary spherocytosis causes hemolysis with anemia and a high reticulocyte count. His hemoglobin and reticulocytes were normal.","Correct.","An MRP2 defect is Dubin-Johnson syndrome, which raises conjugated bilirubin and darkens the liver. His past episodes were unconjugated.","Complete absence of UGT1A1 activity is Crigler-Najjar syndrome type I, which causes severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia and kernicterus in the newborn period."],"study":{"topics":["Gilbert syndrome","Inherited hyperbilirubinemias"],"comps":["GI-13-C3","GI-9","GI-10"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C07-Q3","from":[44],"stem":"During his earlier episodes his urine stayed normal in color, but now it is dark with bilirubin. Which of the following changes allows bilirubin to appear in his urine now?","options":["Albumin binding increased in plasma, so it passes the glomerulus","Glycine was attached in hepatocytes, so it became lipid soluble","Glucuronic acid was added in hepatocytes, so it dissolves in water","Sulfate was attached by gut bacteria, so it is reabsorbed from bowel"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Compare the episodes: before, unconjugated bilirubin; now, a large conjugated (direct) fraction.","Recall the change: UGT1A1 attaches glucuronic acid, making bilirubin water soluble.","Apply to the kidney: conjugated bilirubin that leaks back into blood from injured hepatocytes is filtered and appears in urine."],"why":"Unconjugated bilirubin is lipid soluble and tightly bound to albumin, so it is not filtered and never appears in urine, which is why his Gilbert episodes left his urine normal. In the hepatocyte, UDP-glucuronosyltransferase attaches glucuronic acid, making conjugated (direct) bilirubin that is water soluble and normally secreted into bile. Hepatitis injures hepatocytes and bile canaliculi, so conjugated bilirubin leaks back into blood, is filtered by the kidney, and darkens the urine.","whyNot":["Albumin binding keeps unconjugated bilirubin out of the urine, because the albumin complex is too large to pass the glomerular barrier.","Glycine and taurine are attached to bile acids, not bilirubin, and conjugation makes molecules more water soluble, not lipid soluble.","Correct.","Gut bacteria turn conjugated bilirubin into urobilinogen; they do not sulfate it. Urobilinogen reabsorbed from the gut is a small, normal urine component that does not make urine dark with bilirubin."],"study":{"topics":["Bilirubin conjugation","Bilirubinuria"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C3"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C07-Q4","from":[50],"stem":"He asks whether he could also acquire hepatitis D. Based on his serologic results, which of the following best describes his risk?","options":["At risk, because HDV replicates on its own once inside hepatocytes","Protected, because HDV needs HBsAg made by HBV for its envelope","Protected, because HDV spreads only by the fecal-oral route","At risk, because antibodies against HBsAg do not neutralize HDV"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.5","dok":3,"approach":["Read the serology: anti-HBs positive with HBsAg and anti-HBc negative means vaccinated against HBV.","Recall what HDV needs: HBsAg from an active HBV infection to build its envelope.","Conclude: no HBV infection, so no HDV infection."],"why":"Hepatitis D virus is a small, defective RNA virus. It can replicate its genome inside hepatocytes, but it uses HBsAg made by hepatitis B virus as its envelope, so it can only infect people who also have HBV. It can arrive at the same time as HBV (coinfection, which usually clears) or infect someone with chronic HBV (superinfection, which causes the most severe and rapidly progressive hepatitis). Because he is immune to HBV from vaccination, he is protected from HDV as well.","whyNot":["HDV can copy its own RNA genome using host RNA polymerase, but it cannot make an envelope or leave the cell without HBsAg from HBV. Without HBV it cannot spread.","Correct.","HDV spreads like HBV, through blood, sexual contact, and from mother to child. The fecal-oral route belongs to hepatitis A and E.","The HDV envelope is made of HBsAg, so anti-HBs neutralizes HDV particles. HBV vaccination therefore also prevents HDV infection."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatitis D","Hepatitis B serology"],"comps":["GI-13-L7","GI-12"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C08","system":"GI","dx":"Hirschsprung disease (congenital aganglionic megacolon)","codes":["GI-13-F5","NE-20-E3"],"case":"A 2-day-old boy born at term is evaluated because he has not passed meconium. He now has abdominal distension and bilious vomiting. His mother has phenylketonuria (PKU) and kept a phenylalanine-restricted diet before and during the pregnancy; his father has no family history of PKU. Rectal examination shows a tight rectum, and withdrawal of the finger releases an explosive gush of stool and gas. Contrast enema shows a narrow rectum and sigmoid colon below a cone-shaped transition zone and a dilated colon above it. Suction rectal biopsy shows no ganglion cells in the submucosal plexus and thickened nerve fibers. Diagnosis: Hirschsprung disease (congenital aganglionic megacolon).","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C08-Q1","from":[36],"stem":"Which of the following developmental failures best explains the missing ganglion cells?","options":["Splanchnic mesoderm did not form the muscularis externa","Endoderm did not recanalize the gut lumen after its solid stage","Neural crest cells did not migrate all the way to the distal colon","The midgut loop did not rotate around the superior mesenteric artery"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-13-F5","obj":"GI-13-F.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify what is missing: ganglion cells of the enteric nervous system.","Recall their origin: neural crest cells that enter the foregut early in development and migrate in a head-to-tail wave toward the end of the hindgut.","Apply: migration that stops early leaves the most distal segment (rectum and sigmoid) without ganglia."],"why":"The enteric nervous system comes from neural crest cells, mainly from the vagal region, that enter the foregut and migrate down the gut toward the anus. In Hirschsprung disease this migration stops short, so a continuous distal segment starting at the rectum has no ganglion cells in the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. That segment cannot relax, so it acts as a functional obstruction, and the normal bowel above it dilates. Risk is higher in boys and in Down syndrome.","whyNot":["Splanchnic mesoderm forms the gut muscle and connective tissue. A muscle defect does not explain absent ganglion cells.","Failed recanalization of the gut lumen, best known in the duodenum, causes stenosis or atresia. That is a structural blockage, and ganglion cells are normal.","Correct.","Failed midgut rotation causes malrotation with a risk of volvulus. It does not affect ganglion cells in the rectum."],"study":{"topics":["Enteric nervous system development","Hirschsprung disease"],"comps":["GI-13-F5","GI-1"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C08-Q2","from":[],"stem":"The aganglionic segment stays tightly contracted. Loss of which of the following neurons best explains why it cannot relax?","options":["Sympathetic fibers that release norepinephrine onto the bowel","Excitatory motor neurons that release acetylcholine and substance P","Vagal preganglionic fibers that release acetylcholine onto ganglia","Inhibitory motor neurons that release nitric oxide and VIP"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that gut smooth muscle relaxes in front of a bolus by an enteric reflex.","Identify the neurons that make it relax: intrinsic inhibitory motor neurons releasing nitric oxide and VIP.","Apply: with no ganglion cells, these neurons are absent, so the segment cannot relax and stays narrow."],"why":"Peristalsis needs the muscle ahead of a bolus to relax while the muscle behind it contracts. Relaxation comes from inhibitory motor neurons in the myenteric plexus that release nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP). Without ganglion cells, these neurons are missing, so the segment cannot relax, and thick extrinsic cholinergic nerve fibers in the area add to its tone. The internal anal sphincter also fails to relax when the rectum fills.","whyNot":["Sympathetic fibers come from outside the gut and are still present in the aganglionic segment, so their loss does not explain the problem.","Losing excitatory motor neurons would reduce contraction and cause a flaccid segment, not a tightly contracted one.","Vagal fibers do not supply the rectum or sigmoid colon; pelvic splanchnic nerves carry parasympathetic input there.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Enteric motor neurons","Peristaltic reflex"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-F5"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C08-Q3","from":[],"stem":"Genetic testing finds a heterozygous variant in the gene most often mutated in this disease. Which of the following variants is most likely?","options":["Loss-of-function variant in RET","Gain-of-function variant in RET","Loss-of-function variant in APC","Gain-of-function variant in KRAS"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-1","obj":"GI-1.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the receptor that guides enteric neural crest cells: the RET receptor tyrosine kinase, activated by GDNF.","Ask which direction of change blocks migration: loss of function.","Separate it from the gain-of-function RET variants of MEN2."],"why":"Enteric neural crest cells need signaling through the RET receptor tyrosine kinase, activated by glial cell line-derived neurotrophic factor (GDNF), to survive, multiply, and migrate along the gut. Loss-of-function RET variants are the most common genetic finding in Hirschsprung disease, especially in familial and long-segment cases, and penetrance is incomplete. Gain-of-function RET variants have the opposite effect and cause MEN2.","whyNot":["Correct.","Gain-of-function RET variants cause multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 and medullary thyroid cancer. A few MEN2A variants occur with Hirschsprung disease, but the usual cause is loss of RET signaling, which impairs neural crest migration into the gut.","Loss-of-function APC variants cause familial adenomatous polyposis and start the colorectal adenoma sequence.","Gain-of-function KRAS variants drive growth in many cancers, including colorectal and pancreatic cancer, and are not a cause of Hirschsprung disease."],"study":{"topics":["RET and Hirschsprung disease","Loss versus gain of function"],"comps":["GI-10","GI-13-F5"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C08-Q4","from":[48],"stem":"His newborn screen shows a normal phenylalanine level. Which of the following best describes him with respect to his mother's disorder?","options":["Obligate carrier of one tyrosine hydroxylase variant; PKU is X-linked","Obligate carrier of one homogentisate oxidase variant; PKU is autosomal recessive","50% chance of carrying one PAH variant; PKU is autosomal recessive","Obligate carrier of one PAH variant; PKU is autosomal recessive"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-20-E3","obj":"NE-20-E.3","dok":3,"approach":["Name the defect: PKU is deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH), which converts phenylalanine to tyrosine.","Recall the inheritance: autosomal recessive, so his affected mother has two PAH variants.","Apply: he must inherit one maternal variant, and his normal screen means he is not affected, so he is a carrier."],"why":"Classic PKU is an autosomal recessive deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH), which needs tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) to convert phenylalanine to tyrosine. An affected mother passes one PAH variant to every child, so this baby is an obligate carrier, and his normal screen shows he is not affected. Untreated PKU causes intellectual disability, seizures, light skin and hair, and a musty odor, and tyrosine becomes an essential amino acid. Keeping maternal phenylalanine low before and during pregnancy prevents maternal PKU embryopathy (microcephaly, heart defects, growth restriction).","whyNot":["Tyrosine hydroxylase makes L-DOPA from tyrosine and is not the PKU enzyme, and PKU is autosomal, not X-linked.","Homogentisate oxidase deficiency causes alkaptonuria, not PKU.","His mother has two PAH variants, so every child inherits one of them. His chance of carrying one is 100%, not 50%.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Phenylketonuria","Autosomal recessive inheritance"],"comps":["NE-20-E3","GI-8"]}}]}];
